window.PAIRS = {"run": "pilot-rate-ladder-frame", "created": "2026-11-11T18:25:51+00:00", "langs": ["en", "zh"], "pairs": [{"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0000", "lang": "en", "target_id": "google-glass", "target_name": "Google Glass", "facts": ["Unveiled at Google I/O 2012 with skydivers livestreaming their jump through it onto the Moscone Center roof.", "The Explorer Edition sold to invited users in 2013 for $1,500.", "Wearers were nicknamed 'Glassholes'; bars, cinemas, and casinos banned the device over its camera.", "The consumer programme ended in January 2015; an Enterprise Edition continued until Google stopped selling it in 2023.", "Its display was a 640-by-360 prism in the corner of one eye."], "a": {"item_id": "4b59aaf692b9", "text": "You paid fifteen hundred dollars to become the only man in the bar the bartender watches more closely than the drunk guy. You wanted to look like the future. You look like someone about to be asked to leave by a stranger already hiding behind a pillar.\n\nYou walk in and say \"OK, Glass\" to a room that never asked, then repeat it louder over the jukebox. Nobody's impressed. Nobody's wondering what you're building. They're wondering if they're in your footage. It's the most attention you've ever gotten, and it's fear.\n\nYou wanted conversation starters. You got \"Are you recording me?\" and you said \"No,\" and then you winked. With you, that's a confession.\n\nGo home, Explorer. The only thing you've discovered is how fast a bar empties."}, "b": {"item_id": "2e15bc79f728", "text": "Look at the guy at the bar wearing Google Glass. He paid fifteen hundred dollars to be an Explorer, which is what you call a man who volunteers to be the test dummy for his own loneliness. He's not drinking, he's documenting. He keeps saying \"OK Glass\" like he's summoning a genie who only grants weather updates. Every time a woman walks past, he winks, and she can't tell if he's flirting or taking her mugshot. He wants everyone to know he's ahead of the future. Meanwhile, the future is a grown man in a bar talking to his own face, reading his texts out loud, and nobody here asked him what he's looking at. Buddy, you're not looking at us. You're looking at emails the rest of us can't see, and honestly, that's the most lonely thing in this room."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0001", "lang": "en", "target_id": "graphene", "target_name": "Graphene", "facts": ["Isolated in 2004 by Andre Geim and Konstantin Novoselov at Manchester by peeling graphite with Scotch tape; Nobel Prize in Physics, 2010.", "The EU's Graphene Flagship, launched in 2013, committed €1 billion over ten years.", "A 2018 study in Advanced Materials tested 'graphene' from 60 producers and found none with more than 50% graphene content; most was graphite flakes.", "Sold in tennis rackets, running shoes, and headphones as a performance ingredient.", "Geim also won an Ig Nobel Prize in 2000 for levitating a frog with magnets."], "a": {"item_id": "5342b17b4a3e", "text": "Every graphene founder has the same pitch: \"Stronger than steel, one atom thick, and it's going to change everything.\" Wonderful. Your funding timeline is also one atom thick, and it's also not holding anything up.\n\nYou've been \"five years away\" since two guys at Manchester peeled pencil residue off with Scotch tape and won a Nobel Prize. That's the greatest return on office supplies in history, and you've spent a billion-euro Flagship budget trying to repeat it.\n\nAnd what has the revolution delivered? A tennis racket. A jacket. A keynote speaker in a lanyard saying \"paradigm shift\" over a stock photo of honeycomb. In the lab it works flawlessly, one square centimeter at a time, on a Tuesday, if nobody breathes.\n\nBut you're not fools. You figured out the material doesn't have to scale as long as the grant renewal does. You didn't discover a wonder material. You discovered \"potential,\" and it's renewable."}, "b": {"item_id": "dd1e6a2334b9", "text": "Let's hear it for the graphene evangelists, the only people who've been \"five years away\" since 2004. Two guys in Manchester peel pencil lead with Scotch tape, win a Nobel, and an entire industry decides the tape was the business plan. Every press release is the same Mad Lib: \"Wonder material could revolutionize [batteries/condoms/desalination/your feelings].\" *Could.* That word has funded more postdocs than the NSF. You promised us space elevators and delivered a tennis racket with a sticker on it. Startup founders slap \"graphene-enhanced\" on a phone case the way divorced dads put a spoiler on a Honda Civic. And the grant committees keep paying, because admitting the miracle is stuck on the production line means admitting you are too. Twenty years of changing everything, and the only thing you've actually changed is the abstract."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0002", "lang": "en", "target_id": "ugg", "target_name": "UGG boots", "facts": ["Sheepskin boots Australian surfers wore to warm up after sessions; Brian Smith started selling them in California in 1978.", "Deckers Outdoor bought the brand in 1995 and holds 'UGG' as a trademark in the US and elsewhere; in Australia, 'ugg' is a generic word any bootmaker may use.", "In 2019, a US jury found an Australian maker liable for selling boots labelled 'ugg' into the United States.", "Oprah Winfrey featured them in her 'Favorite Things' lists repeatedly in the 2000s, which drove the first boom.", "UGG is Deckers' largest brand, with over $2 billion in annual sales."], "a": {"item_id": "1531edc90308", "text": "You wear UGGs in July. In Phoenix. Respect. Most people need a therapist to reach that level of denial, and you just needed a mall.\n\nYou're not cold. Nobody's cold. Your feet are poaching in a dead sheep's final revenge, and you call it \"cozy.\" That's not cozy, that's a slow cooker with a ponytail.\n\nWhat are you after? Fall. You want autumn so badly you're dragging it into June like a hostage. You've been holding the same pumpkin spice latte since 2019.\n\nAnd what does it get you? A private, humid sauna for each foot. A smell that enters rooms four seconds before you do. And a look that says \"I'm wearing denim shorts and sheepskin,\" which is two different seasons arguing.\n\nYour boyfriend isn't holding your hand in public. He's holding his breath."}, "b": {"item_id": "2082912cfaf3", "text": "Let's hear it for the people wearing UGGs in July. You're not dressing for the weather. You're dressing for 2005, still waiting for Paris Hilton to text back. Australian surfers made these to warm their feet after the ocean. You wear them to warm your feet after the Trader Joe's parking lot. In August. In Phoenix. What are you after? \"Comfort,\" you say. Babe, your ankles have been collapsing outward since the Bush administration. You walk like a Clydesdale that's given up. And what does it get you? One summer rainstorm and you're squelching through Target like a wet golden retriever who made bad choices. People don't ask where you got them. They ask if you're okay. Congratulations: you've turned footwear into a wellness check."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0003", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hawaiian-pizza", "target_name": "Hawaiian pizza", "facts": ["Invented in 1962 by Sam Panopoulos, a Greek immigrant, at the Satellite Restaurant in Chatham, Ontario, Canada; named after the brand of canned pineapple he used.", "Iceland's president Guðni Th. Jóhannesson said in 2017 that he would ban pineapple on pizza if he could, then clarified that a president has no such power.", "Hawaii is no longer a major pineapple producer: Del Monte shut its Hawaiian pineapple operations in November 2006, two years ahead of its announced 2008 exit, the state's last cannery closed in 2007, and most canned pineapple now comes from Thailand, Indonesia, and the Philippines.", "Panopoulos, whose restaurant also served Chinese dishes, told the Toronto Sun in 2010 that 'in those days people didn't mix the tastes like sweet and sour, except for Chinese food'."], "a": {"item_id": "236a9662f31e", "text": "Ordering Hawaiian pizza is how you tell the table you've never been asked to leave a party. You're the guy who says \"I'm not a food snob, I just like what I like,\" then defends canned pineapple like it owes you money. You want to be the rebel without the risk. The pineapple makes you seem fun, the ham makes you seem like you have a plan, and the whole order lets you feel bold while the pizzeria guy reads it as \"fine.\" Your pizza was invented in Ontario in 1962, and you order it like you were there. You've never been to Hawaii. You've been to a strip-mall pizza place and called it a vacation. Next time, get the pepperoni. Nobody has to explain their pepperoni."}, "b": {"item_id": "1cab9b7c0cdd", "text": "Hawaiian pizza people don't order dinner; they order a personality. You can't just eat it, you have to announce it. Every group order has one of you, arms crossed, waiting for someone to gasp so you can say \"sweet and savory\" like you invented the concept. You didn't. A Canadian did, in Ontario, in 1962. You're not rebelling, you're doing Canada's homework.\n\nAnd it's always the same rebel: someone whose wildest night was asking for medium salsa. Pineapple is your face tattoo, the edgiest thing you'll ever do, and it wipes off with a napkin.\n\nWhat does it get you? Your own box. Nobody touches it. You came for controversy and left with exactly what you wanted: eight slices, the same argument you've had since 2009, and proof that nobody will ever steal your food."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0004", "lang": "en", "target_id": "esperanto", "target_name": "Esperanto", "facts": ["Published in Warsaw in 1887 by L. L. Zamenhof, a Białystok-born eye doctor, under the pen name Doktoro Esperanto, 'one who hopes'.", "Speaker estimates range from 100,000 to two million; roughly a thousand people grow up with it as a native language.", "William Shatner starred in Incubus (1966), a horror film shot entirely in Esperanto, with pronunciation Esperantists still mock.", "Its Wikipedia has over 350,000 articles, and its Duolingo course for English speakers had 1.36 million learners by July 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "3c7c5dbcf1f4", "text": "You know the Esperanto people. They wear the green star on a lanyard and say it's \"so logical\" like they personally designed it. Ask what they're after and they'll say world peace, then admit they've been stuck on the accusative *-n* since 2016. Their plan was that one day they'd meet a Hungarian, a Brazilian, and a Japanese guy at a bus stop and all speak the same language. What they actually get is a pen pal in Lyon who stopped writing in 2019 and a Discord server with four hundred members and one active voice. They're not learning a language. They're waiting for a train Zamenhof promised in 1887, and it has never once left the station."}, "b": {"item_id": "d94ef91d85f7", "text": "Learning Esperanto is for people who looked at the twentieth century and thought, \"The real problem was conjugation.\" Zamenhof built it to end war, and his followers have bravely ended exactly one thing: their dinner invitations. You meet them at the World Congress, green star pinned to a fleece vest, shouting \"Saluton!\" at a Brazilian who also learned it on Duolingo. The big perk is Pasporta Servo: free couches worldwide, paid for by hearing your host explain why English is \"linguistic imperialism.\" Practical payoff? You can finally watch William Shatner's *Incubus* without subtitles. One hundred thirty years later, the dream of uniting humanity survives in a Telegram group of forty men named Bjorn."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0005", "lang": "en", "target_id": "stinky-tofu", "target_name": "Stinky tofu (臭豆腐)", "facts": ["Tofu soaked for hours to a day or two in a brine that has itself fermented for months from vegetables such as bamboo shoots and mustard greens, often with dried shrimp or meat; the smell is the brine's, not the bean's.", "Changsha's version is black, deep-fried and doused in chili; Taiwan's night-market version is golden and served with pickled cabbage; Shaoxing's, brined with amaranth stalks, is fried pale yellow or steamed with other 'stinky' pickles.", "Legend credits Wang Zhihe, a Qing-era exam candidate who left his unsold tofu too long in 1669; a Beijing fermented-bean-curd brand still carries his name.", "Street vendors are its main outlet, and the smell carries for a block."], "a": {"item_id": "04135b916762", "text": "Look at him under the night market lights, eating stinky tofu like he's auditioning for a documentary about bravery. Three blocks away, people are checking their shoes. He isn't eating it for flavor. He's eating it so his friends will say \"you're so brave,\" and he can answer \"it's an acquired taste,\" which is what people say when the only thing they've acquired is a reputation. Watch him wipe the chili sauce off his wrist, pretending the pickled cabbage is his favorite part. He waited twenty minutes in line just to become the guy who did it. And when he gets home, his jacket smells like a fermentation tank, and he calls it a souvenir."}, "b": {"item_id": "63f160a3e349", "text": "Let's talk about the stinky tofu people. You smelled it from three blocks away, a smell like a sewer filed a noise complaint, and your brain said: *that's where we're eating.* Your entire personality is \"it tastes better than it smells.\" That's not a review. That's a hostage statement.\n\nYou stand on a Taipei night market curb with a paper boat, chopsticks in one hand, phone in the other, filming your own bravery for followers who can't smell it, which is the only reason they're still following you. Tourists do it once for the story. Locals do it every Tuesday, then board the MRT, where forty strangers silently agree to hate them.\n\nAnd the pickled cabbage on top? That's you pretending you came for vegetables. You didn't. You came to be the deadliest passenger on the scooter."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0006", "lang": "en", "target_id": "juicero", "target_name": "Juicero", "facts": ["A Wi-Fi-connected cold-press juicer launched in 2016 at $699, later cut to $399, that squeezed proprietary bags of chopped produce sold by subscription.", "Raised about $120 million from investors including Google Ventures and Kleiner Perkins.", "In April 2017, Bloomberg reporters squeezed the packs by hand and got 7.5 ounces of juice in 90 seconds, faster than the machine's 8 ounces in about two minutes.", "Each pack carried a QR code that the press scanned and checked online before squeezing; it refused expired packs and would not run at all unless connected to Wi-Fi.", "Shut down in September 2017 and offered refunds on the machine."], "a": {"item_id": "cdfa0d41c93b", "text": "The people who bought a Juicero weren't buying juice. They were buying the feeling of being someone with a juice situation. You know the type. They wanted to be a wellness person without the bother of actually squeezing anything, so they paid four hundred dollars to outsource the squeezing to a machine. Then the reporters showed up and squeezed the little packets by hand, and it turned out the machine was just a very expensive hand. The buyers were left holding a four-hundred-dollar receipt for their own fingers. And when the company folded, they still had the thing. Still on the counter. Still doing nothing. Like a trophy for a sport they quit on day one, with a little Wi-Fi light blinking at them every morning like it was disappointed too."}, "b": {"item_id": "fa27493ecc18", "text": "You paid $400 to feel like someone with a \"morning routine.\" Not to have juice. To have a routine. You wanted to stand in your kitchen at 6 a.m. in a gray linen set, slide a sealed pouch into a machine, and feel like a Series B founder with kidneys.\n\nThen Bloomberg showed you could squeeze the pouch with your bare hands and get the same juice. And you weren't mad at the company. You were mad at your hands. You'd hired a middleman for the one job your body was already built for.\n\nYou let a gadget check the internet before it would let you drink a beet, and you called that wellness. Your grandmother wrung out dishrags with more commitment to nutrition, and she never needed a QR code to know when the carrots expired."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0007", "lang": "en", "target_id": "juicero", "target_name": "Juicero", "facts": ["A Wi-Fi-connected cold-press juicer launched in 2016 at $699, later cut to $399, that squeezed proprietary bags of chopped produce sold by subscription.", "Raised about $120 million from investors including Google Ventures and Kleiner Perkins.", "In April 2017, Bloomberg reporters squeezed the packs by hand and got 7.5 ounces of juice in 90 seconds, faster than the machine's 8 ounces in about two minutes.", "Each pack carried a QR code that the press scanned and checked online before squeezing; it refused expired packs and would not run at all unless connected to Wi-Fi.", "Shut down in September 2017 and offered refunds on the machine."], "a": {"item_id": "fa27493ecc18", "text": "You paid $400 to feel like someone with a \"morning routine.\" Not to have juice. To have a routine. You wanted to stand in your kitchen at 6 a.m. in a gray linen set, slide a sealed pouch into a machine, and feel like a Series B founder with kidneys.\n\nThen Bloomberg showed you could squeeze the pouch with your bare hands and get the same juice. And you weren't mad at the company. You were mad at your hands. You'd hired a middleman for the one job your body was already built for.\n\nYou let a gadget check the internet before it would let you drink a beet, and you called that wellness. Your grandmother wrung out dishrags with more commitment to nutrition, and she never needed a QR code to know when the carrots expired."}, "b": {"item_id": "596abf5c014a", "text": "You paid four hundred dollars so a startup could check your juice's expiration date over Wi-Fi. That's not wellness. That's asking a server for permission to have breakfast. These are people who looked at a bag of chopped kale and thought, \"This needs a subscription.\" People who wanted to be someone who juices without ever touching a fruit. Then Bloomberg showed the whole world you could squeeze the pack with your bare hands. Faster. For free. Your grandmother has been doing that with a lemon since 1952. You didn't buy a juicer; you bought the privilege of paying seven dollars a pouch to a company Google Ventures funded and still couldn't keep alive past 2017. Congratulations: you're the only people who ever got a refund from Silicon Valley and still walked away looking poorer."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0008", "lang": "en", "target_id": "jackfruit", "target_name": "Jackfruit", "facts": ["The largest fruit that grows on a tree; single fruits can weigh over 40 kg.", "The national fruit of Bangladesh and the state fruit of Kerala and Tamil Nadu.", "Unripe flesh is sold as a vegan 'pulled pork' substitute, although it has only 1-2 g of protein per 100 g.", "Cutting one releases a sticky white latex, so cooks oil their knives and hands first.", "A member of the mulberry family (Moraceae), a relative of breadfruit and figs."], "a": {"item_id": "73836848e6ca", "text": "You spent two weeks in Kerala, watched a grandmother cook jackfruit the way her family has for centuries, and came home with an LLC. Congratulations, you colonized a lunch.\n\nNow you're under a tent called \"Jack's Back,\" charging fourteen dollars for \"pulled pork\" that is ninety percent your cousin's barbecue sauce and ten percent a can you opened at 6 a.m. You call it disrupting BBQ. Texas hasn't noticed.\n\nWhat does it get you? Twenty-four hundred followers. A Whole Foods cashier who says \"Oh, jackfruit!\" like a secret handshake. And one carnivore uncle at Thanksgiving who says \"not bad\" in the voice of a hostage.\n\nMeanwhile, that grandmother in Kerala is still just calling it dinner. No pitch deck. No tent. Somehow, no sauce."}, "b": {"item_id": "fc39af40bf91", "text": "Let's talk about the geniuses selling jackfruit as \"pulled pork.\" These are people who looked at a fruit that half of Kerala has been feeding to cousins for free since forever, slapped it in a brine pouch, and charged eleven dollars for the privilege of being disappointed in a Whole Foods.\n\nTheir whole business model is barbecue sauce. That's it. They're a sauce company with a fruit alibi. And when you mention it has about two grams of protein, they get *defensive*. Buddy, you sold me a pork sandwich with the nutritional profile of a pear.\n\nBut I get the appeal. You don't need a lab, a patent, or a scientist named Klaus. You just need a food truck, a beard, and the confidence to say \"same texture!\" to people who haven't eaten pork since 2011 and genuinely can't remember."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0009", "lang": "en", "target_id": "nfts", "target_name": "NFTs", "facts": ["Beeple's 'Everydays: The First 5000 Days' sold at Christie's for $69.3 million in March 2021.", "OpenSea, the largest NFT marketplace, processed a record $2.7 billion in trades on 1 May 2022 and just $9.34 million on 28 August 2022, a 99% drop, according to DappRadar data.", "A 2023 dappGambl study found that 95% of the NFT collections it examined had a market value of zero.", "The token usually records only a URL; the image itself sits on an ordinary server or IPFS, not on the blockchain.", "Collins Dictionary named 'NFT' its word of the year for 2021."], "a": {"item_id": "41fe71c9494d", "text": "Let me tell you about the guy who bought a cartoon monkey in 2021. Not a monkey. A JPEG of a monkey, with a receipt that says he owns it. He pays four figures in gas fees to a stranger in Discord named \"Thanos_Fan_69\" who promised him \"utility.\" What utility? He gets a little ape on his Twitter profile. That's it. That's the utility. He changed his profile picture to a rectangle of a monkey wearing sunglasses, and now he's a \"founder.\" Founder of what? A group chat with eleven thousand people who all say \"wagmi\" like it's a prayer they don't understand. Every morning he checks the floor price like a man checking a pulse. Diamond hands, he says. Diamond hands, the bathroom mirror says back. Ten-dollar haircut, no sleep."}, "b": {"item_id": "de2993927ffe", "text": "Let's hear it for the 2021 NFT buyer. You paid four hundred dollars in gas fees to become the legal owner of something your coworker right-click saved during the meeting where you explained it. You didn't want art. You've never wanted art. You wanted a hexagon around your Twitter picture so strangers would know you had a Discord password. You typed \"WAGMI\" to eleven thousand anonymous cartoon monkeys and believed it. We are not all gonna make it, Kyle. The roadmap promised a metaverse yacht party; it delivered a founder named \"0xDegenSensei\" who deleted his account and moved to Portugal. But hey, you got what you paid for: a floor price you check like a heart monitor, and a profile picture you still can't bring yourself to change, because then it would be over."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0010", "lang": "en", "target_id": "microsoft-word", "target_name": "Microsoft Word", "facts": ["Launched in 1983 as 'Multi-Tool Word' for Xenix and MS-DOS.", "Clippy, the animated paperclip assistant, arrived in Office 97 and was removed in Office 2007; Microsoft itself ran ads mocking it in 2001.", "Its .docx format, Office Open XML, was fast-tracked to ISO standard 29500 in 2008 as a roughly 6,000-page specification, amid accusations that national committees had been packed.", "Older spell checkers, Word's included, offered 'Cupertino' as a correction for a mistyped 'cooperation', and the resulting 'Cupertino effect' made it into published NATO, EU and UN-agency documents."], "a": {"item_id": "374d6b6e70bc", "text": "You chose Word for your thesis because \"LaTeX has a learning curve.\" Bold, from someone who then spent a semester learning how to make page 1 start after the roman numerals. You wanted to focus on the research. Instead you're a hostage negotiator for Figure 3.2, which flees to the next page whenever you breathe on the paragraph above it. Your committee asked for one more citation, and the bibliography reflowed like a tectonic event. The phrase \"Error! Reference source not found\" appears in your thesis eleven times. Your file is called Thesis_FINAL_v9_USE_THIS_ONE.docx, and it's 400 megabytes because you pasted one chart from Excel. You came for a doctorate. You're leaving with a codependent relationship with Section Break (Next Page) and a flinch every time someone says \"Normal style.\""}, "b": {"item_id": "c9f44b95f5be", "text": "You've got 214 pages in a single file and a belief that this time it will behave. You are a person who typed \"1.1\" by hand and then pressed the space bar forty times to line up the title with the page number. You call that a table of contents.\n\nYou wanted to avoid learning LaTeX because you're \"not a coding person,\" so instead you learned section breaks, roman numerals, and the one sacred rule: never touch Chapter 3. You moved one figure on page 12 and now your bibliography is in Comic Sans, somehow.\n\nYour file is called Thesis_FINAL_v7_REAL_advisorEdits_USE THIS ONE. You've emailed it to yourself as a backup, which is your entire version control strategy.\n\nAll that sacrifice to look professional, and your defense slides will be in PowerPoint. You haven't escaped. You've just switched the hostage."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0011", "lang": "en", "target_id": "nfts", "target_name": "NFTs", "facts": ["Beeple's 'Everydays: The First 5000 Days' sold at Christie's for $69.3 million in March 2021.", "OpenSea, the largest NFT marketplace, processed a record $2.7 billion in trades on 1 May 2022 and just $9.34 million on 28 August 2022, a 99% drop, according to DappRadar data.", "A 2023 dappGambl study found that 95% of the NFT collections it examined had a market value of zero.", "The token usually records only a URL; the image itself sits on an ordinary server or IPFS, not on the blockchain.", "Collins Dictionary named 'NFT' its word of the year for 2021."], "a": {"item_id": "e8eddc47095c", "text": "Nothing says \"I'm early\" like paying $300 in gas fees to join a Discord with forty thousand other people who are also early. You didn't buy art, Kyle. You bought a seat in a group chat where everyone types \"gm\" at strangers like they're greeting a medieval village. You told your mom it was \"a community.\" You changed your Twitter avatar to a hexagon and started saying \"ngmi\" to coworkers who just asked about the Wi-Fi password. Your whole investment thesis is \"number go up,\" and your exit strategy is finding someone dumber, who is also in the Discord, who is also looking for you. You check the floor price at red lights. You've cried over a Discord mod's emoji reaction. You spent your tax refund to be called \"anon\" by a guy named CryptoChad420, and the only thing you walked away with is proof that you were there when it peaked."}, "b": {"item_id": "c0aeb5d8a3ed", "text": "Let's hear it for the 2021 NFT buyer. You paid four hundred dollars in gas fees, then made it your Twitter avatar so strangers could right-click save it for free. You joined a Discord with eleven thousand guys named \"Chad.eth,\" called them family, and stayed family right up until the floor price dropped and family started doxxing the dev. You said \"WAGMI.\" We did not all make it. You told your mom you were \"early.\" Early to what? The rug pull? You weren't buying art. You were buying the right to say \"few understand\" at parties, which was true. Nobody understood, especially you. Jimmy Fallon bought one too. When your financial strategy matches Jimmy Fallon's, that's not a community, that's a support group. The roadmap promised a metaverse. It delivered you, at Thanksgiving, still owing your cousin."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0012", "lang": "en", "target_id": "macau", "target_name": "Macau", "facts": ["A Portuguese settlement from the 1550s until the handover to China in December 1999.", "Casino revenue passed the Las Vegas Strip's in 2006, and at its 2013 peak, about $45 billion, was roughly seven times the Strip's.", "One company, STDM, held the casino monopoly for four decades until licences opened to competition in 2002.", "Among the most densely populated places on Earth, at roughly 20,000 people per square kilometre.", "The Cotai Strip stands on land reclaimed from the sea between the islands of Taipa and Coloane."], "a": {"item_id": "6405f7045e3c", "text": "You lost it in Vegas, or Manila, or on a Tuesday online, and now you're on the ferry from Hong Kong with the confidence of a man who has finally \"figured out baccarat.\" You haven't. Baccarat has no figuring out. It's a coin flip in a tuxedo.\n\nYou're not here for money, you're here for a story where you come back a legend. Meanwhile, the guy next to you, who's been awake since Thursday, is bowing to a card like it's his ancestor, and he's winning, which is why you think it's contagious.\n\nYour plan: one hour, minimum table. By hour six, you're in a gondola at the Venetian, inside a mall, under a painted sky, asking a singing man from Guangdong if he takes WeChat.\n\nThe only thing you win back is a Portuguese egg tart, and even that's on the credit card."}, "b": {"item_id": "fe0ea9392503", "text": "Every man on the 6 a.m. TurboJET from Hong Kong has the same plan: lose the rent in Macau, win it back before the boat docks. He lost his bonus in Vegas and figures the baccarat tables here are the same tables, just with more gold leaf. He says \"I'm just here to see the Venetian,\" then walks straight past the gondolas, because gondolas aren't on the way to the table. He's after the feeling of being the guy who came back. What he gets is a room with no clocks and a VIP host named Kelvin who calls him \"boss\" in the voice of a man already calculating his credit limit. Hour six, he's on the ferry home, watching the Cotai skyline shrink, doing math on a comp slip."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0013", "lang": "en", "target_id": "almond-milk", "target_name": "Almond milk", "facts": ["California grows about 80% of the world's almonds.", "A single almond takes about 1.1 US gallons (roughly 4 litres) of water to grow, a figure Mother Jones published in 2014 that spread during California's drought.", "A 2015 US class action alleged Almond Breeze contained only about 2% almonds; it partly survived a motion to dismiss, was stayed in 2017 pending a separate Blue Diamond labelling settlement, and the plaintiffs then dropped it.", "Pollinating California's almond orchards each February requires trucking in most of the commercial honeybee colonies in the United States.", "Medieval European cooks used almond milk as a Lenten substitute for dairy, centuries before the current fashion."], "a": {"item_id": "2f3ff40c74e4", "text": "You switched to almond milk to save the planet, which is a bold move for someone who read one headline about cows. Congratulations: you found the crop that needs about a gallon of water per almond and grows almost entirely in a California drought. You didn't solve the problem, you relocated it to Fresno, where you can't see it.\n\nAnd the bees. Orchards truck in most of the country's honeybees every spring, like a tour that never ends. You didn't go vegan, you went on a bee-logistics subscription.\n\nBut let's be honest, it was never about the planet. It was about the moment the barista asks \"dairy?\" and you say \"almond, please\" like a governor granting a pardon. You paid sixty cents extra for a halo with a surcharge.\n\nMeanwhile, your oat-milk friend is quietly doing it better, and nobody's clapping for them. That's what really bothers you."}, "b": {"item_id": "f6708fabd3f6", "text": "You switched to almond milk to save the planet. Bold move, picking the one crop that drinks California like it's an open bar. Every latte you order, a farmer in the Central Valley watches his well go dry and thinks, \"At least Kayleigh's being sustainable.\"\n\nAnd you didn't save a cow. You just outsourced the suffering to bees, trucked cross-country every February to work themselves to death in an orchard, while you post about dairy being cruel.\n\nYou're not after a healthier Earth. You're after the barista hearing you say it. That little pause before \"almond, please\" is the biggest environmental impact you've ever had.\n\nAnd now the oat milk people look at you the way you used to look at skim drinkers. You picked a side in the milk war, and you lost."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0014", "lang": "en", "target_id": "luosifen", "target_name": "Luosifen (螺蛳粉)", "facts": ["A rice-noodle dish from Liuzhou, Guangxi; the smell comes from pickled bamboo shoots (酸笋), not from the river snails, which only flavour the broth.", "The instant version boomed during the 2020 lockdowns; Liuzhou's government reported packaged luosifen sales of over 10 billion yuan that year.", "Added to China's national intangible cultural heritage list in 2021.", "Liuzhou opened a luosifen industry college in 2020 to train workers for the trade."], "a": {"item_id": "2de91fadb67e", "text": "Nobody in this shared kitchen has ever been brave. You're the one who tears open the sachet at 11pm and lets that sour-funk fill the whole flat, then stands over the pot with your eyes closed like it's a religious rite. You wanted to be the guy who eats the wild thing and doesn't flinch. Your roommates are in their rooms now, pretending it's a fire alarm. Six months ago you said you'd \"get into\" Liuzhou food. Now you own three packs of pickled bamboo shoots, a chili oil you've never read the label on, and a standing grudge against the extractor fan. You're not really eating snail noodles. You're collecting a reaction."}, "b": {"item_id": "4f071ba2f3bc", "text": "You know the type. Ordered thirty packs on Taobao \"for emergencies,\" and the emergency is that it's Tuesday. They wait until 11 p.m., when the kitchen's empty, like that's a courtesy and not an alibi. They snip open the sour bamboo shoot packet with the focus of a bomb technician, then act shocked when the smoke alarm stays quiet but the flatmates don't. Their entire personality is one sentence, \"it tastes way better than it smells,\" delivered like a TED talk to people holding their breath. They'll tell you it's from Liuzhou, it's authentic, it's *culture*. Mate, you microwaved the tofu skin. And what does it get them? Twenty minutes of joy, a hallway that's filing for divorce, and a group chat renamed \"WHO.\" Congratulations: your hoodie is now a Liuzhou consulate until spring."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0015", "lang": "en", "target_id": "windows-8", "target_name": "Windows 8", "facts": ["Released in October 2012; removed the Start button and booted into a full-screen grid of tiles designed for touch screens most PCs did not have.", "The 8.1 update in 2013 put a Start button back, Windows 10 (2015) restored the Start menu, and Microsoft skipped the name Windows 9 altogether.", "Microsoft dropped the 'Metro' name in August 2012 after reports that German retailer Metro AG had objected on trademark grounds; Microsoft called 'Metro' a code name and denied any litigation, and the style was afterwards known informally as 'Modern UI'.", "The 'Charms bar' hid Search, Share, and Settings behind an invisible swipe from the right edge.", "Windows RT, the ARM edition launched alongside it, could not run ordinary Windows programs; Microsoft wrote down $900 million of unsold Surface RT tablets in 2013."], "a": {"item_id": "f8677486c44b", "text": "You put Windows 8 on a desktop. A tower. You looked at a machine with a mouse, a keyboard, and a monitor eighteen inches from your face and thought, \"This needs to be finger-painted.\" Who are you? You're the guy who bought a Zune and still calls it \"ahead of its time.\" You wanted to feel like the future. Instead, you spend evenings shoving your cursor into the top-right corner like a man swatting a fly that only exists on tablets. Your weather app is full-screen. Your 27-inch monitor is now a clock tile. When 8.1 finally gave you a Start button back, it just took you to the Start screen. So, inevitably, you installed Classic Shell. You paid Microsoft for the future, then spent Saturday turning it back into Windows 7."}, "b": {"item_id": "131d66f0a7cf", "text": "Nobody installs Windows 8 on a desktop out of passion. You saw a perfectly good Start button and thought, \"Honestly, too much of this.\" You wanted touch controls on a machine with no touchscreen, so now you're swiping at a monitor like it owes you money. Shutting down means hunting through the charms bar, hovering in the corner like a nervous burglar, and praying the hot corner doesn't ambush you mid-email. You bought Start8, felt guilty, then bought a second utility to fix the first. Your Start screen is a grid of tiles for apps you'll never open, and the one you actually use is buried under Weather, which is always sunny in your sad little world."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0016", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hydrogen-cars", "target_name": "Hydrogen fuel-cell cars", "facts": ["Toyota launched the Mirai in 2014; 'mirai' means 'future' in Japanese.", "Nearly all US hydrogen filling stations are in California; Shell closed all seven of its California passenger-car stations in February 2024.", "More than 95% of the world's hydrogen is made from fossil fuels, mostly by steam-reforming natural gas.", "California pump prices reached about $36 per kilogram in 2023-24, over $200 for a Mirai fill; Toyota has handed buyers fuel cards worth $15,000.", "Mirai owners filed a class action against Toyota in July 2024 over hydrogen shortages and station outages; a second suit in 2025 sought at least $5.7 billion."], "a": {"item_id": "94ec4c185be2", "text": "Nobody buys a hydrogen car by accident. You're the person who drives forty minutes past three gas stations to reach the one hydrogen pump in the state, and it's out of order. You're proud of your tailpipe because it drips water. You bring this up at dinner parties, unprompted, like you personally invented rain. You wanted to be the early adopter who makes everyone else feel behind, and you are, because the only other person at the station is a guy in a Prius asking whether you're lost. Most of that hydrogen is made from natural gas, so your eco-friendly car runs on fossil fuel with a better marketing team. Congratulations. You're the most environmentally conscious man in a line of one."}, "b": {"item_id": "1f255592ab99", "text": "Let's talk about the guy who bought a hydrogen car. He wanted to be greener than the Tesla people and smugger than the Prius people, and found the one way to do both while living like a medieval pilgrim. His life is now a map of eleven pumps, and nine say \"offline.\" He loves telling you it \"only emits water.\" Yeah, so does he, every time the app says \"station unavailable.\" He brags that refueling takes five minutes, after the forty-minute drive and the two-hour line behind three other Mirai owners, all quietly weeping. Toyota handed him a fifteen-thousand-dollar fuel card and he burned through it before the warranty paperwork dried. Now he owns a sixty-thousand-dollar car worth a used futon, all to prove he's an early adopter of a future that met him and moved to Japan."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0017", "lang": "en", "target_id": "nfts", "target_name": "NFTs", "facts": ["Beeple's 'Everydays: The First 5000 Days' sold at Christie's for $69.3 million in March 2021.", "OpenSea, the largest NFT marketplace, processed a record $2.7 billion in trades on 1 May 2022 and just $9.34 million on 28 August 2022, a 99% drop, according to DappRadar data.", "A 2023 dappGambl study found that 95% of the NFT collections it examined had a market value of zero.", "The token usually records only a URL; the image itself sits on an ordinary server or IPFS, not on the blockchain.", "Collins Dictionary named 'NFT' its word of the year for 2021."], "a": {"item_id": "ff6086240a0d", "text": "Let's talk about the guy who bought a JPEG of a bored cartoon ape in 2021 for the price of a Honda. He's not an art collector. Art collectors own things. He owns a receipt. His whole personality now fits in a profile picture, and that picture is a cartoon ape wearing a bandana, the closest he's ever come to a self-portrait. He says \"wagmi\" unironically. He calls strangers \"ser.\" He joined a Discord to get whitelisted for a second Discord. Every morning he checks the floor price like it's his pulse, and he has never once looked at the thing itself, because the thing was never the point. The point was being the guy who got in early, six months before he became the guy who got out late."}, "b": {"item_id": "de2993927ffe", "text": "Let's hear it for the 2021 NFT buyer. You paid four hundred dollars in gas fees to become the legal owner of something your coworker right-click saved during the meeting where you explained it. You didn't want art. You've never wanted art. You wanted a hexagon around your Twitter picture so strangers would know you had a Discord password. You typed \"WAGMI\" to eleven thousand anonymous cartoon monkeys and believed it. We are not all gonna make it, Kyle. The roadmap promised a metaverse yacht party; it delivered a founder named \"0xDegenSensei\" who deleted his account and moved to Portugal. But hey, you got what you paid for: a floor price you check like a heart monitor, and a profile picture you still can't bring yourself to change, because then it would be over."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0018", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hawaiian-pizza", "target_name": "Hawaiian pizza", "facts": ["Invented in 1962 by Sam Panopoulos, a Greek immigrant, at the Satellite Restaurant in Chatham, Ontario, Canada; named after the brand of canned pineapple he used.", "Iceland's president Guðni Th. Jóhannesson said in 2017 that he would ban pineapple on pizza if he could, then clarified that a president has no such power.", "Hawaii is no longer a major pineapple producer: Del Monte shut its Hawaiian pineapple operations in November 2006, two years ahead of its announced 2008 exit, the state's last cannery closed in 2007, and most canned pineapple now comes from Thailand, Indonesia, and the Philippines.", "Panopoulos, whose restaurant also served Chinese dishes, told the Toronto Sun in 2010 that 'in those days people didn't mix the tastes like sweet and sour, except for Chinese food'."], "a": {"item_id": "000c462b9442", "text": "Ordering Hawaiian pizza is a cry for help from someone who wants to be called a rebel. You've never been to Hawaii. The dish is from Ontario. You're defending a Canadian restaurant accident like it's your homeland.\n\nYou order it for the office party, then wait for someone to gasp. Nobody does, so you announce, \"I know, I know, pineapple doesn't belong on pizza,\" to a room that wasn't talking. You've been waiting years for this fight. Nobody's coming.\n\nYou call yourself adventurous. It's the only risk you've ever taken, and you retake it every Friday. Your entire personality is a defense of a topping that never hired a lawyer.\n\nAnd what do you get? Three leftover slices, because everyone else quietly ordered pepperoni, and a reputation as the \"random\" one at work. You wanted to be dangerous. You're just Greg, with ham."}, "b": {"item_id": "1cab9b7c0cdd", "text": "Hawaiian pizza people don't order dinner; they order a personality. You can't just eat it, you have to announce it. Every group order has one of you, arms crossed, waiting for someone to gasp so you can say \"sweet and savory\" like you invented the concept. You didn't. A Canadian did, in Ontario, in 1962. You're not rebelling, you're doing Canada's homework.\n\nAnd it's always the same rebel: someone whose wildest night was asking for medium salsa. Pineapple is your face tattoo, the edgiest thing you'll ever do, and it wipes off with a napkin.\n\nWhat does it get you? Your own box. Nobody touches it. You came for controversy and left with exactly what you wanted: eight slices, the same argument you've had since 2009, and proof that nobody will ever steal your food."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0019", "lang": "en", "target_id": "javascript", "target_name": "JavaScript", "facts": ["Written by Brendan Eich at Netscape in ten days in May 1995; named Mocha, then LiveScript, then JavaScript to ride Java's marketing, although the two are unrelated.", "typeof null returns 'object', a bug from the first version that has been kept for compatibility ever since.", "In March 2016 the author of left-pad, an 11-line npm package, unpublished it and broke builds across the industry, including Babel and React.", "[] + {} evaluates to '[object Object]'; {} + [] typed at a console evaluates to 0.", "Standardized by Ecma as 'ECMAScript' in 1997 because the name JavaScript is a trademark, first Sun's and now Oracle's."], "a": {"item_id": "7c79a2ca0b0b", "text": "You wrote your backend in JavaScript, your frontend in JavaScript, your build scripts in JavaScript, and your smart fridge dashboard in JavaScript, and you call it \"full-stack\" because \"only-stack\" sounds like a cry for help. You said you wanted to avoid context switching. Congratulations: you now switch between four frameworks that all launched this quarter.\n\nYou're not a polyglot. You're someone who learned one language and renamed every problem until it fit.\n\nWhat did it get you? A node_modules folder heavier than your product, a notepad app that ships its own browser and eats two gigs of RAM, and a TypeScript migration to apologize for the original decision. Your Hello World has 1,400 dependencies, one of them maintained by a guy who once deleted eleven lines and broke the internet.\n\nBut you're hireable. Every posting wants you. That's not a career, that's a hostage situation with stock options."}, "b": {"item_id": "9f280ef92811", "text": "Let's hear it for the people who write everything in JavaScript. You learned one language at a bootcamp and decided the rest of computing could adapt. Backend? Node. Desktop? Electron: a to-do list that eats four gigs of RAM because it ships its own copy of Chrome. Mobile? React Native. Your toaster is probably a Next.js app by now.\n\nYou call it \"full-stack.\" Your node_modules folder calls it a landfill. You npm-installed a package to pad a string, and when its author deleted it, half the internet fell over.\n\nEvery year you rewrite your whole app in a new framework, then bolt on TypeScript so you can pretend you chose a real language.\n\nAnd what does it get you? A résumé that says \"versatile,\" and a career spent asking why undefined is not a function."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0020", "lang": "en", "target_id": "luosifen", "target_name": "Luosifen (螺蛳粉)", "facts": ["A rice-noodle dish from Liuzhou, Guangxi; the smell comes from pickled bamboo shoots (酸笋), not from the river snails, which only flavour the broth.", "The instant version boomed during the 2020 lockdowns; Liuzhou's government reported packaged luosifen sales of over 10 billion yuan that year.", "Added to China's national intangible cultural heritage list in 2021.", "Liuzhou opened a luosifen industry college in 2020 to train workers for the trade."], "a": {"item_id": "2de91fadb67e", "text": "Nobody in this shared kitchen has ever been brave. You're the one who tears open the sachet at 11pm and lets that sour-funk fill the whole flat, then stands over the pot with your eyes closed like it's a religious rite. You wanted to be the guy who eats the wild thing and doesn't flinch. Your roommates are in their rooms now, pretending it's a fire alarm. Six months ago you said you'd \"get into\" Liuzhou food. Now you own three packs of pickled bamboo shoots, a chili oil you've never read the label on, and a standing grudge against the extractor fan. You're not really eating snail noodles. You're collecting a reaction."}, "b": {"item_id": "6782cb950544", "text": "You know who you are. You ordered twelve packs off Taobao, waited until every roommate was home, then tore open the sour bamboo shoot sachet like you were declassifying something. You're not hungry. You're making a point. Every slurp comes with the speech: \"Smells bad, tastes good.\" Buddy, we only get the first half. Nobody signed up for the tasting, just the hallway. And you keep saying \"it's authentic Liuzhou\" like geography is an apology. What does it get you? A spotless pot nobody will borrow, a group chat that goes silent the second you type \"anyone want some?\", and roommates who suddenly prefer dating people with their own apartments. You came for comfort food. You left with a reputation, a window permanently propped open, and a fridge shelf everyone treats like a crime scene."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0021", "lang": "en", "target_id": "chicago-deep-dish", "target_name": "Chicago deep-dish pizza", "facts": ["Created at Pizzeria Uno in Chicago in 1943; credit is disputed among founder Ike Sewell, his partner Ric Riccardo, and cook Rudy Malnati.", "Assembled upside down: sliced mozzarella on the dough, then toppings, then crushed tomatoes on top, so the cheese does not scorch during the long bake.", "Jon Stewart called it 'tomato soup in a bread bowl' and 'a casserole' on The Daily Show in 2013.", "Chicagoans' everyday pizza is thin 'tavern-style' cut into squares; GrubHub order data reported by WBEZ in 2013 showed city residents ordering non-deep-dish pizza about 10 to 1 over deep dish, with deep dish and stuffed making up under 9% of orders."], "a": {"item_id": "999fc0b6671a", "text": "Let's talk about the people who insist deep-dish is pizza. You're from the city that named its own comedy theater Second City and still acts shocked when nobody ranks you first. You're not defending food. You're nursing a grudge against New York you've carried since losing the World's Fair argument and the skyline argument. You'll wait forty-five minutes at Lou Malnati's, order before you're seated like it's the DMV, then ask for a knife and fork and call it \"the real thing.\" Jon Stewart roasted you on national TV and you took it personally, because deep down you knew he was right. You ship it on dry ice to your cousin in Phoenix like an organ donation. And what does the label get you? Navy Pier tourists nodding politely, a two-hour nap, and the Midwest's loudest participation trophy."}, "b": {"item_id": "506e0b98fda7", "text": "Let's talk about the people who call it pizza. Mostly Chicagoans, who haven't eaten it since their cousin visited in 2019. You order tavern-cut thin crust every Friday, but the second a New Yorker opens his mouth, you're a martyr for the Gino's East gift shop.\n\nAnd the tourists. You stood outside for an hour in a Michigan Avenue wind to be told your pie takes 45 minutes. You said \"when in Chicago\" like it was a vow. You got a photo of one slice held up by a spatula, a nap in a hotel bed at 4 p.m., and the right to say you \"did it.\"\n\nWhat do you get for calling it pizza? An argument with a stranger from Brooklyn, which is the only reason you came. Congratulations. Mayor Daley would be proud."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0022", "lang": "en", "target_id": "windows-vista", "target_name": "Windows Vista", "facts": ["Released to consumers in January 2007, after the 'Longhorn' project was reset in 2004 and headline features such as the WinFS file system were dropped.", "User Account Control prompted so often that Apple's 'Get a Mac' campaign ran an ad about 'Cancel or Allow'.", "'Windows Vista Capable' stickers went on PCs that could only run Home Basic, without the Aero interface, and led to a class-action lawsuit.", "Windows 7 (2009), widely praised, was largely a tuned-up Vista on the same kernel line; Vista support ended in April 2017."], "a": {"item_id": "1925db42cfe5", "text": "Every guy who upgraded to Vista on day one has the same story. He's standing in line at midnight, January 30th, 2007, holding a box like it's the last Wii. He wants to tell people he was first. Nobody asked. He got the Ultimate edition because he's the kind of man who says \"Ultimate\" about a salad. He turned on Aero, watched his window borders go translucent, and called it \"the future.\" Then his printer stopped working. He found out its driver was from 2004, and the printer was not interested in the future. So he clicked \"Allow\" on a UAC prompt. Then he clicked it again. He's clicked Allow four hundred times now, and he's the only one who thinks that's security."}, "b": {"item_id": "ff3ee1cef20f", "text": "You installed Vista on launch day. Nobody needed you to. You just wanted to be the guy at the office who says \"oh, you're still on XP?\" in the voice of someone judging a flip phone.\n\nYou were after transparent window borders. You spent three hundred dollars on the upgrade, then another eighty on RAM, so your title bars could be see-through. Your computer now has the glass look of a luxury hotel and the speed of its elevator.\n\nDay one got you a printer with a driver that was \"coming soon,\" and a sticker on your laptop that said \"Vista Capable,\" which turned out to be a legal term. You clicked Allow so many times your finger filed for workers' comp. You plugged in a flash drive to make it faster, and called it ReadyBoost like that's a normal sentence.\n\nBy March you were hunting for your old XP disc, and it was waiting for you, no hard feelings."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0023", "lang": "en", "target_id": "carbon-nanotubes", "target_name": "Carbon nanotubes", "facts": ["Reported by Sumio Iijima in Nature in 1991.", "Promised as the cable for a space elevator; a 2006 analysis showed that ordinary defects cut the strength of a macroscopic cable far below the ideal value.", "Long, rigid multi-walled nanotubes behaved like asbestos fibres in a 2008 mouse study in Nature Nanotechnology.", "Sorting metallic from semiconducting tubes has blocked nanotube electronics for three decades; MIT's 16-bit RV16X-NANO processor (2019) is still a lab demonstration.", "Today they are sold mainly as a conductive additive in lithium-ion battery electrodes."], "a": {"item_id": "09b6c02ce449", "text": "Every CNT pitch deck has the same slide: a space elevator hanging off the equator, a cable strong enough to hold a city, and a sunrise nobody asked for. The founder skips the part where that cable is \"in development\" the way the moon is in development. He has a PhD, wears a lab coat to investor dinners, and has said \"five years from commercial scale\" since 2009. He's been five years away for fifteen years. Investors adore him because he says \"tensile strength\" like a man who has never had to explain a yield curve. What it gets him: a Series C, a conference booth beside a guy selling graphene sunglasses, and a LinkedIn banner of himself squinting at a black smear that is somehow his life's work. The tubes are doing fine. They've never once asked for a Series C."}, "b": {"item_id": "d717e15fa133", "text": "You've been \"two years from commercialization\" since the Clinton administration. Your founder has a PhD, a patent, and the confidence of a man who's never had to ship anything in a quantity larger than a vial.\n\nSlide three is the space elevator. Everyone knows it's a joke, including you, but you keep it in because it's the only slide where the investors stop checking their phones.\n\nYou say \"TAM\" and mean \"every industry that has ever existed.\" Batteries, concrete, tennis rackets, sunscreen. You've pitched so many markets you've never actually sold to one.\n\nWhat are you after? A seed round, a lab with your name on it, and one more year before anyone asks about price per kilogram.\n\nWhat do you get? A warm handshake, a \"fascinating science,\" and a follow-up email that says \"keep us posted.\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0024", "lang": "en", "target_id": "windows-8", "target_name": "Windows 8", "facts": ["Released in October 2012; removed the Start button and booted into a full-screen grid of tiles designed for touch screens most PCs did not have.", "The 8.1 update in 2013 put a Start button back, Windows 10 (2015) restored the Start menu, and Microsoft skipped the name Windows 9 altogether.", "Microsoft dropped the 'Metro' name in August 2012 after reports that German retailer Metro AG had objected on trademark grounds; Microsoft called 'Metro' a code name and denied any litigation, and the style was afterwards known informally as 'Modern UI'.", "The 'Charms bar' hid Search, Share, and Settings behind an invisible swipe from the right edge.", "Windows RT, the ARM edition launched alongside it, could not run ordinary Windows programs; Microsoft wrote down $900 million of unsold Surface RT tablets in 2013."], "a": {"item_id": "8dd545adc448", "text": "You put Windows 8 on a desktop. A tower. Under a desk. With a mouse. You looked at an operating system built for fingertips and thought, \"I'll just reach.\" Who are you? You're the guy who buys a touchscreen glove to use a keyboard. You wanted to feel modern, and now you spend your evenings shoving a cursor into the top-right corner like you're trying to scratch your monitor's back. You wanted productivity. You got a full-screen weather app swallowing your entire twenty-four-inch monitor to tell you it's raining, and you can't figure out how to close it. You lost the Start button and called it \"a fresh start.\" Your coworkers asked how you shut your computer down, and you said, \"I don't. It's always on, like a lifestyle.\" You're not using a PC. You're waving at it, waiting for the Charms bar to love you back."}, "b": {"item_id": "131d66f0a7cf", "text": "Nobody installs Windows 8 on a desktop out of passion. You saw a perfectly good Start button and thought, \"Honestly, too much of this.\" You wanted touch controls on a machine with no touchscreen, so now you're swiping at a monitor like it owes you money. Shutting down means hunting through the charms bar, hovering in the corner like a nervous burglar, and praying the hot corner doesn't ambush you mid-email. You bought Start8, felt guilty, then bought a second utility to fix the first. Your Start screen is a grid of tiles for apps you'll never open, and the one you actually use is buried under Weather, which is always sunny in your sad little world."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0025", "lang": "en", "target_id": "google-sheets", "target_name": "Google Sheets", "facts": ["Launched in June 2006 as Google Spreadsheets, built on XL2Web, a product Google acquired from 2Web Technologies.", "A spreadsheet is capped at 10 million cells (raised from 5 million in 2022).", "Functions such as IMPORTHTML, IMPORTXML, and GOOGLEFINANCE pull live web data straight into cells.", "Free with a Google account; files live in Google Drive, and collaborators edit the same sheet simultaneously with coloured cursors.", "In a browser, offline editing works only in Chrome or Edge, with the Google Docs Offline extension installed and offline access switched on while still connected; the Android and iOS apps work offline natively."], "a": {"item_id": "85e5be0c8934", "text": "So you run the whole team out of one Google Sheet. You have forty tabs, and one is called \"FINAL FINAL use this one.\" Every task gets a color, and the palette only makes sense to you. You built a Gantt chart out of merged cells, so the project is technically on schedule but unreadable to anyone born after the chart. Your status dropdown has six options, four of which mean \"fine.\" You want control, so you are the only one allowed near the VLOOKUPs, and you leave comments that just say \"see my note\" with no note. What it gets you is a team that makes its own copy, pastes the real numbers into a tab called \"actual,\" and checks version history only to find out who ruined your formatting."}, "b": {"item_id": "2067d213f836", "text": "You're not running a team. You're running a tab called \"MASTER - DO NOT TOUCH,\" which Brad touched in 2021. You wanted a CRM, a project tracker, and an HR system, and you built all three out of IMPORTRANGE and spite. Every morning you watch an Anonymous Capybara scroll through payroll and just decide that's fine. Someone sorts column B without selecting column C, and suddenly your top performer is assigned to Linda's maternity leave. Your entire career is \"Last edit was 4 minutes ago.\" You don't do performance reviews, you do Version History. Your conditional formatting has more colors than your company has policies. Your Apps Script was written by an intern who now works at a bakery. Every time someone emails \"Request edit access,\" you feel like a king. And you are a king, ruling over 47 frozen rows and one #REF! that nobody can explain."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0026", "lang": "en", "target_id": "chicago-deep-dish", "target_name": "Chicago deep-dish pizza", "facts": ["Created at Pizzeria Uno in Chicago in 1943; credit is disputed among founder Ike Sewell, his partner Ric Riccardo, and cook Rudy Malnati.", "Assembled upside down: sliced mozzarella on the dough, then toppings, then crushed tomatoes on top, so the cheese does not scorch during the long bake.", "Jon Stewart called it 'tomato soup in a bread bowl' and 'a casserole' on The Daily Show in 2013.", "Chicagoans' everyday pizza is thin 'tavern-style' cut into squares; GrubHub order data reported by WBEZ in 2013 showed city residents ordering non-deep-dish pizza about 10 to 1 over deep dish, with deep dish and stuffed making up under 9% of orders."], "a": {"item_id": "6aea7052f5a6", "text": "Every time someone tells me Chicago deep-dish is \"technically not a pizza,\" I know exactly who I'm dealing with. He's a guy from Hoboken who ate one slice in Naples in 2009 and has been waiting fifteen years to bring it up. He's not hungry. He's shopping for a fight. He wants the whole table to hear that real pizza is thin and foldable, and he says \"foldable\" like it's a personality. Meanwhile he's eating with a knife and fork, which he thinks makes him sophisticated. Chicago never asked for his permission. Chicago has been feeding people since before he was born, while he was still folding pepperoni on a napkin. So let him win the argument. It's the only thing he's ever ordered that came out exactly how he wanted."}, "b": {"item_id": "999fc0b6671a", "text": "Let's talk about the people who insist deep-dish is pizza. You're from the city that named its own comedy theater Second City and still acts shocked when nobody ranks you first. You're not defending food. You're nursing a grudge against New York you've carried since losing the World's Fair argument and the skyline argument. You'll wait forty-five minutes at Lou Malnati's, order before you're seated like it's the DMV, then ask for a knife and fork and call it \"the real thing.\" Jon Stewart roasted you on national TV and you took it personally, because deep down you knew he was right. You ship it on dry ice to your cousin in Phoenix like an organ donation. And what does the label get you? Navy Pier tourists nodding politely, a two-hour nap, and the Midwest's loudest participation trophy."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0027", "lang": "en", "target_id": "beanie-babies", "target_name": "Beanie Babies", "facts": ["Launched by Ty Inc. in 1993 and sold only through small gift shops, with designs 'retired' to manufacture scarcity.", "At the height of the craze Beanie Babies accounted for about 10% of eBay's sales, and eBay's 1998 IPO filing said the site at times had over 30,000 Beanie Baby auctions running simultaneously in the quarter ending June 1998.", "In 1999 a divorcing couple divided their Beanie Baby collection one by one on a courtroom floor, in a photograph that ran worldwide.", "In 1999 Ty announced that Beanie Babies would be retired at the end of the year, then reversed itself after asking the public to vote and seeing an overwhelming vote to keep them.", "Most 'rare' Beanies that price guides valued in the thousands now sell for a few dollars."], "a": {"item_id": "1a45d6768831", "text": "Let's talk about the Beanie Baby investors, the folks who bought eleven Peanut the Elephants and never let a child within arm's reach of one. You know the type. He's got a binder. Every bear has a hang tag, a tush tag, and a plastic sleeve, and he inspects the tush tag for misprints like it's the Dead Sea Scrolls. He says \"retired\" the way a doctor says \"terminal.\" His retirement plan is one Princess bear and a prayer. Ty Inc. never promised him a dime, but he's still waiting for the big payout. Meanwhile his daughter sleeps with a bald rabbit named Hope, because the good stuff is sealed in the basement, appreciating, never once hugged. Congratulations. You own the most expensive animals in your house that nobody's allowed to touch."}, "b": {"item_id": "6a4b77c7b767", "text": "Let's hear it for the Beanie Baby investors, the only people who watched the 1990s stock market boom and thought, \"Too volatile. I'll put it all in Princess the Bear.\" These are folks who bought tag protectors before they bought life insurance. Who read a price guide sold at the same counter as the bears and called it due diligence. Ty would announce a \"retirement\" and you'd sprint to the Hallmark store like a rich uncle just died.\n\nTwenty-five years later, your portfolio is in a Rubbermaid tub in your mom's garage, and your exit strategy is an eBay listing: \"RARE Peanut, tag error, $40,000 OBO.\" Zero watchers. The only person who got rich off Beanie Babies got convicted of tax evasion. And honestly? He earned it. You handed it to him."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0028", "lang": "en", "target_id": "google-sheets", "target_name": "Google Sheets", "facts": ["Launched in June 2006 as Google Spreadsheets, built on XL2Web, a product Google acquired from 2Web Technologies.", "A spreadsheet is capped at 10 million cells (raised from 5 million in 2022).", "Functions such as IMPORTHTML, IMPORTXML, and GOOGLEFINANCE pull live web data straight into cells.", "Free with a Google account; files live in Google Drive, and collaborators edit the same sheet simultaneously with coloured cursors.", "In a browser, offline editing works only in Chrome or Edge, with the Google Docs Offline extension installed and offline access switched on while still connected; the Android and iOS apps work offline natively."], "a": {"item_id": "a5780556db66", "text": "Let's talk about the brave souls who run their whole team out of one Google Sheet. Tab one says \"Tasks,\" tab nine says \"Tasks (FINAL),\" and tab twelve says \"Tasks (FINAL) copy 2.\" They color-code everything, then color-code the color codes. Their status dropdown has eleven options, and \"Done\" is still somehow \"In Progress.\" They believe a #REF! error is a personality trait. They lie awake wondering if the VLOOKUP in cell F212 will survive Brenda touching column D. They're not managing people. They're managing a spreadsheet that manages people, and they're so proud of it that they've locked the header row so nobody can ruin their life. Their dream is a pivot table. Their reality is a comment thread with forty-one replies that all say \"bump.\""}, "b": {"item_id": "b196e566b5fd", "text": "You're not a manager, you're a guy who discovered conditional formatting and mistook it for leadership. You call it \"our single source of truth,\" which is adorable, because there are nine tabs, and three of them are named \"Copy of Q3 (DO NOT USE).\"\n\nOne IMPORTRANGE holds your entire department together, and you're the only one who knows. When it breaks, \"Loading...\" becomes your team's mission statement.\n\nYou wanted visibility, and you got it: an anonymous capybara hovering over your cell while someone you hired quietly overwrites your formula with a hardcoded 47. You wanted accountability, so you @-tagged Greg in a comment eleven times. Greg has never opened a comment. Greg's row is yellow. Yellow means something. Nobody remembers what.\n\nYou say you'll \"move to a real tool next quarter\" while building a dropdown menu like it's a cathedral.\n\nYour career highlight is a pivot table. Your legacy is \"Request edit access.\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0029", "lang": "en", "target_id": "slack", "target_name": "Slack", "facts": ["Spun out in 2013 from the internal chat tool of Tiny Speck's failed game Glitch; founder Stewart Butterfield had earlier spun Flickr out of another failed game.", "The name is a backronym: 'Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge'.", "Salesforce bought it for $27.7 billion in 2021.", "Filed an EU antitrust complaint against Microsoft in 2020 over the bundling of Teams with Office."], "a": {"item_id": "ccebb9af0d58", "text": "You run your company on Slack because you wanted a culture and settled for a green dot. You've made forty-seven channels and #general is a graveyard of work anniversaries and a guy named Greg's lunch photos. Your big decisions get made with a thumbs-up emoji, and a raised-hands reaction counts as alignment. You type \"@here\" like you're summoning a god, then ask whether anyone's seen the doc you pinned in a channel you archived in March.\n\nYou love being \"async\" until you need an answer, and then it's \"quick huddle?\" Nobody has ever had a quick huddle. You say you hate meetings, so you built a meeting that never ends, sends push notifications, and has a gif keyboard.\n\nAnd the payoff? Your best engineer set her status to \"🌴 away\" in 2022 and nobody's checked if she's gone."}, "b": {"item_id": "a66553ea7cb4", "text": "Nobody runs a company on Slack. They run a company that lives inside a Slack, and the founder has to be in forty channels so he can feel the building is still standing. Look at him: green dot on all day, status set to \"deep work,\" huddling with himself in an empty room. He starts every morning with \"quick question\" and ends it with \"let's circle back,\" which is corporate for \"I'm going to let this thread die.\" He doesn't answer you. He reacts with a little eyeball. An eyeball. That's his whole management style. And #random? Forty-one people in #random, all too scared to leave. Good morning, everybody. @here. Please read the pinned message."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0030", "lang": "en", "target_id": "beanie-babies", "target_name": "Beanie Babies", "facts": ["Launched by Ty Inc. in 1993 and sold only through small gift shops, with designs 'retired' to manufacture scarcity.", "At the height of the craze Beanie Babies accounted for about 10% of eBay's sales, and eBay's 1998 IPO filing said the site at times had over 30,000 Beanie Baby auctions running simultaneously in the quarter ending June 1998.", "In 1999 a divorcing couple divided their Beanie Baby collection one by one on a courtroom floor, in a photograph that ran worldwide.", "In 1999 Ty announced that Beanie Babies would be retired at the end of the year, then reversed itself after asking the public to vote and seeing an overwhelming vote to keep them.", "Most 'rare' Beanies that price guides valued in the thousands now sell for a few dollars."], "a": {"item_id": "45f7594d8a38", "text": "Let's hear it for the Beanie Baby investors, the only people who bought a retirement plan at a Hallmark store. You put tag protectors on a heart-shaped piece of cardboard and called it diversification. You drove to four McDonald's for Teenie Beanies, threw away the Happy Meals, and told your kids it was for their college fund. Now that kid is thirty, still paying off loans, and his inheritance is a Rubbermaid tub of Princess the Bears. Every few months you see an eBay listing asking $500,000 for one with a tag typo and think, \"Vindicated.\" Babe, that's not a market. That's another guy in another garage, also lying to his wife. Ty Warner told you which ones were \"retired,\" and you believed him. Ty Warner later pleaded guilty to hiding money offshore. He hoarded too. He just hoarded the right thing."}, "b": {"item_id": "7f33e3df1a62", "text": "You bought sixty Princess bears because a guy on a message board said they'd put your kids through college. Congratulations: your kids are now in their thirties and still can't afford college, but they do know what a \"tush tag\" is.\n\nIn 1998 you drove to three Hallmark stores before dawn, fought a woman named Deb over a shipment, and called it \"market research.\" You mocked people buying stocks. \"Paper,\" you said. \"Not tangible.\" Meanwhile the dot-com crash hurt less than your discovery that every other person on Earth also had sixty Princess bears.\n\nYou still keep them in tag protectors, because you believe a stranger will one day inspect the corner of a cardboard heart and wire you retirement money.\n\nYour eBay listing says \"RARE.\" It has said \"RARE\" since 2004. Your lawyer asked what you wanted from the divorce, and you said \"the Peace bear.\" You didn't mean it as a joke. That's the tragedy."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0031", "lang": "en", "target_id": "slack", "target_name": "Slack", "facts": ["Spun out in 2013 from the internal chat tool of Tiny Speck's failed game Glitch; founder Stewart Butterfield had earlier spun Flickr out of another failed game.", "The name is a backronym: 'Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge'.", "Salesforce bought it for $27.7 billion in 2021.", "Filed an EU antitrust complaint against Microsoft in 2020 over the bundling of Teams with Office."], "a": {"item_id": "c5f48d20bb73", "text": "You didn't build a company, you built a chat room with payroll. Your \"culture\" is a custom emoji of your CTO's face, and that's the only thing anyone has shipped this quarter.\n\nYou said Slack would kill meetings. Now every \"quick question\" is a huddle that starts with \"can you hear me?\" and ends with \"let's move this to a thread.\"\n\nYou @channel the whole company to announce a bagel delivery, then act shocked that nobody reads your @channels. You've got 112 channels for 14 employees. Three of them are for work. One is #wins, and it's just you, reacting to your own post with a rocket.\n\nYou aren't managing anybody. You're watching a green dot and calling it trust. Someone's dot went gray at 5:01 and you took it personally.\n\nAnd the best part? \"Sorry, just seeing this!\" is now your company's mission statement."}, "b": {"item_id": "a66553ea7cb4", "text": "Nobody runs a company on Slack. They run a company that lives inside a Slack, and the founder has to be in forty channels so he can feel the building is still standing. Look at him: green dot on all day, status set to \"deep work,\" huddling with himself in an empty room. He starts every morning with \"quick question\" and ends it with \"let's circle back,\" which is corporate for \"I'm going to let this thread die.\" He doesn't answer you. He reacts with a little eyeball. An eyeball. That's his whole management style. And #random? Forty-one people in #random, all too scared to leave. Good morning, everybody. @here. Please read the pinned message."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0032", "lang": "en", "target_id": "jackfruit", "target_name": "Jackfruit", "facts": ["The largest fruit that grows on a tree; single fruits can weigh over 40 kg.", "The national fruit of Bangladesh and the state fruit of Kerala and Tamil Nadu.", "Unripe flesh is sold as a vegan 'pulled pork' substitute, although it has only 1-2 g of protein per 100 g.", "Cutting one releases a sticky white latex, so cooks oil their knives and hands first.", "A member of the mulberry family (Moraceae), a relative of breadfruit and figs."], "a": {"item_id": "652321571654", "text": "You people found a fruit that has fed entire countries for centuries, moved it to Portland, and renamed it \"pulled pork\" so a guy named Dylan could feel something at lunch.\n\nLet's be honest about the business model. You didn't discover a meat alternative. You discovered that if you drown anything in enough barbecue sauce, a vegetarian will cry \"it's just like the real thing!\" about a food they haven't tasted in eleven years. You're not selling jackfruit. You're selling sauce and plausible deniability, for fourteen dollars.\n\nIn Kerala, this is a Tuesday. In your food truck, it's \"heritage, plant-forward, and ethically shredded.\"\n\nAnd what does it get you? One loyal customer, Brenda, who tells everyone it's \"honestly better than meat,\" and who stands in line behind the guy ordering brisket."}, "b": {"item_id": "fc39af40bf91", "text": "Let's talk about the geniuses selling jackfruit as \"pulled pork.\" These are people who looked at a fruit that half of Kerala has been feeding to cousins for free since forever, slapped it in a brine pouch, and charged eleven dollars for the privilege of being disappointed in a Whole Foods.\n\nTheir whole business model is barbecue sauce. That's it. They're a sauce company with a fruit alibi. And when you mention it has about two grams of protein, they get *defensive*. Buddy, you sold me a pork sandwich with the nutritional profile of a pear.\n\nBut I get the appeal. You don't need a lab, a patent, or a scientist named Klaus. You just need a food truck, a beard, and the confidence to say \"same texture!\" to people who haven't eaten pork since 2011 and genuinely can't remember."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0033", "lang": "en", "target_id": "windows-vista", "target_name": "Windows Vista", "facts": ["Released to consumers in January 2007, after the 'Longhorn' project was reset in 2004 and headline features such as the WinFS file system were dropped.", "User Account Control prompted so often that Apple's 'Get a Mac' campaign ran an ad about 'Cancel or Allow'.", "'Windows Vista Capable' stickers went on PCs that could only run Home Basic, without the Aero interface, and led to a class-action lawsuit.", "Windows 7 (2009), widely praised, was largely a tuned-up Vista on the same kernel line; Vista support ended in April 2017."], "a": {"item_id": "df697b757759", "text": "Oh, you upgraded to Vista on day one? Of course you did. You're the guy who lined up at midnight for a store that closed at nine. You wanted the translucent Aero borders so badly you bought a new graphics card just to watch your taskbar shimmer. You clicked \"Allow\" on forty UAC prompts before your coffee cooled, because you think a security dialog is just a loading screen with opinions. And your printer? Dead. Your scanner? Gone. You're not a pioneer. You're a beta tester who paid full price and slapped a \"Vista Capable\" sticker on your tower like it was a Michelin star. Enjoy the sidebar gadgets. Your weather widget is the only thing in your house still telling you the truth."}, "b": {"item_id": "4f7f18f8662a", "text": "Let's hear it for the day-one Vista crowd, the people who lined up at CompUSA on January 30th, 2007 because a billboard said \"The Wow Starts Now.\" You paid $399 for Ultimate so Flip 3D could shuffle the same three windows you used to Alt-Tab through. Your laptop had a \"Vista Capable\" sticker and 512 megs of RAM, which is like slapping \"Marathon Capable\" on a hamster. You wanted to look like the future; you had Aero turned off by Wednesday. Your printer quit. Your Sound Blaster went mute. Your \"Ultimate Extras\" turned out to be a poker game and a promise. And every time you tried to show off, the screen went dark and asked, \"Are you sure?\" Buddy, nobody was sure. Not even Microsoft. They were already working on Windows 7."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0034", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hawaiian-pizza", "target_name": "Hawaiian pizza", "facts": ["Invented in 1962 by Sam Panopoulos, a Greek immigrant, at the Satellite Restaurant in Chatham, Ontario, Canada; named after the brand of canned pineapple he used.", "Iceland's president Guðni Th. Jóhannesson said in 2017 that he would ban pineapple on pizza if he could, then clarified that a president has no such power.", "Hawaii is no longer a major pineapple producer: Del Monte shut its Hawaiian pineapple operations in November 2006, two years ahead of its announced 2008 exit, the state's last cannery closed in 2007, and most canned pineapple now comes from Thailand, Indonesia, and the Philippines.", "Panopoulos, whose restaurant also served Chinese dishes, told the Toronto Sun in 2010 that 'in those days people didn't mix the tastes like sweet and sour, except for Chinese food'."], "a": {"item_id": "1be3426edb6c", "text": "People who order Hawaiian pizza want you to know they're brave. They say \"I know, I know\" before anyone has said a word, because for them the pineapple isn't a topping, it's a personality. This is a person whose entire rebellious phase fits in a box for $14.99. They've never gotten a tattoo, never quit a job, never sent food back, but once a month they look a teenage cashier dead in the eye and say \"pineapple\" like they're defusing a bomb.\n\nAnd they always want to split one with you. Half Hawaiian, half \"normal.\" That's not a compromise. That's a hostage negotiation.\n\nBest part? It isn't even Hawaiian. A Greek guy invented it in Ontario. So congratulations: your one act of rebellion is a Canadian pretending he's on vacation."}, "b": {"item_id": "32c9917cf9ee", "text": "You order Hawaiian pizza the way a man buys a motorcycle at forty-five: you need everyone to know you're dangerous. Nobody asked. You say \"I know, I know, don't judge me\" to a room that wasn't judging you, and now it is. That's the plan. It's the only controversy you can afford. You'll never skydive, never quit the job, never tell the group chat what you actually think, but you will die on this hill, and the hill is a Friday night in your own kitchen.\n\nYou think you're ordering a tropical getaway. It was invented in Ontario in 1962 by a Greek guy running a restaurant called the Satellite. You're not on vacation. You're the loyal defender of a Canadian accident.\n\nYou wanted to be a maverick. You got a twenty-minute argument with your brother-in-law and a second pizza, pepperoni, that someone quietly ordered to cover for you."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0035", "lang": "en", "target_id": "oat-milk", "target_name": "Oat milk", "facts": ["Oatly started in Sweden in the 1990s, built on Lund University research by Rickard Öste on lactose intolerance.", "Oatly's slogan 'It's like milk but made for humans' was banned in Sweden in 2015 after the dairy lobby LRF Mjölk sued; Oatly then published the lawsuit as advertising.", "Oatly listed on Nasdaq in May 2021 at a valuation near $10 billion; the shares later lost more than 90% of their value.", "Commercial oat milk is made by breaking oat starch into maltose with amylase enzymes, and many brands, Oatly among them, add rapeseed oil; the result has more sugar and a higher glycaemic index than almond or soy milk."], "a": {"item_id": "6f1acdf77ea0", "text": "Oat milk people, let's be honest: you're not lactose intolerant. I've watched you eat a whole burrata at brunch. You switched because almond milk got canceled for drinking California dry, and you needed a new personality before anyone noticed you'd had the old one for six years.\n\nYou pay eighty cents extra to drink breakfast. Your grandma ate oats so she could afford milk. You're paying more for oats so you can say \"I just find it creamier.\"\n\nAnd you tell the barista \"barista edition, if you have it,\" like you're ordering a vintage. Same person who reads every label hunting for seed oils. Guess what's in your porridge latte, Kayleigh? Rapeseed oil.\n\nYou didn't save the planet. You flew to Lisbon twice this year. You just found a way to sigh at a cow."}, "b": {"item_id": "a99ceee7d226", "text": "Nobody orders oat milk for the oats. You order it so the barista knows you've done your research. You say \"just make it oat\" like you're showing a passport. You're not lactose intolerant. You're \"not really doing dairy right now,\" a phrase you've never once said about bread. You want your cup to announce your values before you've said a word. Then you spend four minutes explaining your gut to a kid named Jayden at 7 a.m. Your tote bag has a sea turtle on it. You pay a dollar extra and hold the cup up for a photo, waiting for someone to ask what's in it so you can say \"oat\" and watch them nod like you just told them you ran a marathon."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0036", "lang": "en", "target_id": "beanie-babies", "target_name": "Beanie Babies", "facts": ["Launched by Ty Inc. in 1993 and sold only through small gift shops, with designs 'retired' to manufacture scarcity.", "At the height of the craze Beanie Babies accounted for about 10% of eBay's sales, and eBay's 1998 IPO filing said the site at times had over 30,000 Beanie Baby auctions running simultaneously in the quarter ending June 1998.", "In 1999 a divorcing couple divided their Beanie Baby collection one by one on a courtroom floor, in a photograph that ran worldwide.", "In 1999 Ty announced that Beanie Babies would be retired at the end of the year, then reversed itself after asking the public to vote and seeing an overwhelming vote to keep them.", "Most 'rare' Beanies that price guides valued in the thousands now sell for a few dollars."], "a": {"item_id": "6a4b77c7b767", "text": "Let's hear it for the Beanie Baby investors, the only people who watched the 1990s stock market boom and thought, \"Too volatile. I'll put it all in Princess the Bear.\" These are folks who bought tag protectors before they bought life insurance. Who read a price guide sold at the same counter as the bears and called it due diligence. Ty would announce a \"retirement\" and you'd sprint to the Hallmark store like a rich uncle just died.\n\nTwenty-five years later, your portfolio is in a Rubbermaid tub in your mom's garage, and your exit strategy is an eBay listing: \"RARE Peanut, tag error, $40,000 OBO.\" Zero watchers. The only person who got rich off Beanie Babies got convicted of tax evasion. And honestly? He earned it. You handed it to him."}, "b": {"item_id": "7f33e3df1a62", "text": "You bought sixty Princess bears because a guy on a message board said they'd put your kids through college. Congratulations: your kids are now in their thirties and still can't afford college, but they do know what a \"tush tag\" is.\n\nIn 1998 you drove to three Hallmark stores before dawn, fought a woman named Deb over a shipment, and called it \"market research.\" You mocked people buying stocks. \"Paper,\" you said. \"Not tangible.\" Meanwhile the dot-com crash hurt less than your discovery that every other person on Earth also had sixty Princess bears.\n\nYou still keep them in tag protectors, because you believe a stranger will one day inspect the corner of a cardboard heart and wire you retirement money.\n\nYour eBay listing says \"RARE.\" It has said \"RARE\" since 2004. Your lawyer asked what you wanted from the divorce, and you said \"the Peace bear.\" You didn't mean it as a joke. That's the tragedy."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0037", "lang": "en", "target_id": "durian", "target_name": "Durian", "facts": ["Banned on Singapore's MRT trains and in many Southeast Asian hotels because of the smell.", "A 2017 genome study in Nature Genetics traced the odour to a duplicated family of MGL genes that pump out volatile sulfur compounds.", "China buys most of the world's durian exports, chiefly from Thailand, in a trade worth billions of dollars a year.", "Alfred Russel Wallace wrote in 1856 that eating it was 'a new sensation worth a voyage to the East to experience'."], "a": {"item_id": "0bf8c83200e0", "text": "You're not eating durian on the train because you love it. You love being the person who eats durian on the train. There's a sign right there, with a little picture, drawn by a government, specifically for you. You looked at it and thought, \"Not me, I'm cultured.\"\n\nYou went to Penang once. Now you say \"king of fruits\" like you were knighted alongside it. You tell coworkers \"you just have to get past the first bite,\" which is also what people say about marriages they're stuck in.\n\nAnd what did you win? A carriage to yourself. A lifetime ban from three hotel chains. A sixty-dollar fruit you're pretending to enjoy because a man on a plastic stool called you brave.\n\nSir, he says that to every tourist. He's selling by the kilo."}, "b": {"item_id": "5e02113e1bb8", "text": "You know who eats durian on the train? A man who has never once been told no. He's not eating fruit. He's eating a personality. He reads the NO DURIAN sign like it's a personal challenge, then cracks that spiky husk with the confidence of someone who watched one video. Then he looks up at the carriage and says, \"Honestly? Tastes like custard.\" Sir, nobody asked for a dessert review. You're not tasting custard. You're tasting attention. Every bite is a little flag planted in the aisle. And the worst part? Ten stops later he's still sniffing his fingers, grinning, like the smell is a medal he earned."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0038", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hsq", "target_name": "HSQ (e-beam resist)", "facts": ["Hydrogen silsesquioxane, sold by Dow Corning as FOx ('flowable oxide') and designed as a spin-on dielectric for chip interconnects; lithographers adopted it as a negative-tone resist.", "Exposure converts its cage structure into a cross-linked, silica-like network, so the developed pattern is essentially SiO2.", "Holds the resolution records for electron-beam lithography: isolated features around 5 nm, dense lines below 10 nm half-pitch, with high-contrast 'salty' NaOH/NaCl development.", "Must be kept refrigerated (Dow Corning's datasheet gives a six-month shelf life at 5 °C); the solution ages into a jelly, and its effective sensitivity shifts with processing delays because cross-linking continues after exposure.", "DuPont, which inherited Dow Corning's FOx/XR-1541 HSQ line, discontinued it and ended production at the end of 2025; labs such as EPFL's CMi switched to third-party HSQ from EM Resist or DisChem, both formulated from AQM powder."], "a": {"item_id": "884dd445a1e2", "text": "\"We're switching to HSQ for the resolution.\" Sure you are. Because your PMMA wasn't humiliating enough, you wanted a resist that's also a deadline. You're chasing sub-10 nanometer lines so you can put one SEM image in a paper, the one nice picture out of forty-one fallen-over grating samples.\n\nYour resist lives in the freezer like a tiny, expensive yogurt, and by the time you've booked the beam it has gone off anyway. You'll expose at a dose that makes the e-beam tool bill you like a hotel minibar. Then you'll develop in salt water, because apparently you wanted to cook pasta in the cleanroom.\n\nAnd what do you get? Silicon dioxide you can't strip. A permanent souvenir of your ambition, fused to the wafer forever. Congratulations, you made glass lines. Your advisor wanted devices."}, "b": {"item_id": "b68838ef49bb", "text": "So you switched to HSQ for the resolution. Bold move for someone whose devices still die at wire bonding. You saw one SEM of 6-nanometer lines at a conference and decided PMMA was beneath you. Now you're writing at a dose so high your name sits on the e-beam calendar like a hostage note. Your resist lives in the fridge next to somebody's yogurt, and you check its age more often than your own. You develop in salt water now, the closest you've come to a beach in three years. And congratulations on those gorgeous lines. They're basically glass, so the only way to fix a mistake is HF, a fume hood, and a long call to your mother. All this for one inset figure the reviewer will ask you to make bigger."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0039", "lang": "en", "target_id": "carbon-nanotubes", "target_name": "Carbon nanotubes", "facts": ["Reported by Sumio Iijima in Nature in 1991.", "Promised as the cable for a space elevator; a 2006 analysis showed that ordinary defects cut the strength of a macroscopic cable far below the ideal value.", "Long, rigid multi-walled nanotubes behaved like asbestos fibres in a 2008 mouse study in Nature Nanotechnology.", "Sorting metallic from semiconducting tubes has blocked nanotube electronics for three decades; MIT's 16-bit RV16X-NANO processor (2019) is still a lab demonstration.", "Today they are sold mainly as a conductive additive in lithium-ion battery electrodes."], "a": {"item_id": "221482c6c19c", "text": "You open with a space elevator, which tells me the last customer you spoke to was a grad student. Your deck says \"100 times stronger than steel,\" and every investor nods, because they heard it in 2009 from the guy before you, and the guy before him. You've been \"two years from commercialization\" since the Clinton administration.\n\nWhat are you after? Eight million dollars and the word \"platform.\" What do you get? A polite \"fascinating, let's circle back,\" a tour of the fund's kombucha tap, and a term sheet from a tennis racket company.\n\nYour market slide lists batteries, aerospace, sensors, concrete, and medicine. That isn't a market, that's a thesaurus. And when the one skeptical partner asks about dispersion, you suddenly get very interested in your water glass.\n\nYour real product is optimism with a PhD, sold by the gram at gold prices, to people who just want to know why it still clumps."}, "b": {"item_id": "09b6c02ce449", "text": "Every CNT pitch deck has the same slide: a space elevator hanging off the equator, a cable strong enough to hold a city, and a sunrise nobody asked for. The founder skips the part where that cable is \"in development\" the way the moon is in development. He has a PhD, wears a lab coat to investor dinners, and has said \"five years from commercial scale\" since 2009. He's been five years away for fifteen years. Investors adore him because he says \"tensile strength\" like a man who has never had to explain a yield curve. What it gets him: a Series C, a conference booth beside a guy selling graphene sunglasses, and a LinkedIn banner of himself squinting at a black smear that is somehow his life's work. The tubes are doing fine. They've never once asked for a Series C."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0040", "lang": "en", "target_id": "emacs", "target_name": "Emacs", "facts": ["Began in 1976 as a set of macros for the TECO editor at MIT's AI Lab, written by Richard Stallman and others; GNU Emacs followed in 1985.", "Extensible in its own Lisp dialect, Emacs Lisp; the standing joke is that it is a fine operating system lacking only a good editor.", "Ships with a psychotherapist (M-x doctor), Tetris, a mail client, a calendar, and a web browser (eww).", "An old backronym is 'Eight Megabytes And Constantly Swapping', from when 8 MB was a lot of memory.", "'Emacs pinky' is the name for the hand strain blamed on its Control-key chords."], "a": {"item_id": "67c107490918", "text": "Let's hear it for the people who live inside Emacs. They read email in mu4e, browse the web in eww, and get therapy from M-x doctor, the only therapist who'll put up with them. Their whole life is in org-mode: a 4,000-line to-do file where \"fix init.el\" has been a TODO since 2011. They don't use a computer; they negotiate with it in Lisp. Every problem gets solved by writing a function, and every function creates a new problem. They've declared init.el bankruptcy three times and still owe. Ask what they've built and they'll proudly show you a mode for managing their other modes. And the payoff? A pinky that clicks like a castanet from holding Control since the Clinton administration, and the quiet dignity of being the only person alive who knows how to leave. C-x C-c. They never do."}, "b": {"item_id": "78fe88a6a79d", "text": "You people don't use a text editor. You adopted a pet that you have to feed Lisp.\n\nYou open Emacs to write one email, and three weeks later you've written a mail client, but you still haven't sent the email. Your init file is 4,000 lines long, and you understand maybe nine of them. The rest is a Stack Overflow answer from 2011 that you're afraid to remove.\n\nYou've got an Org-mode agenda so elaborate it has its own org chart. It tracks every task you'll never do, in beautiful nested bullets. You don't have a to-do list. You have a to-do *liturgy*.\n\nAnd the pinky! Thirty years of Control and your left hand looks like a lobster claw. You call it \"ergonomic.\" Your chiropractor calls it \"a recurring client.\"\n\nYou say it's an operating system. Sir, it's a hobby with a splash screen."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0041", "lang": "en", "target_id": "papyrus", "target_name": "Papyrus", "facts": ["Hand-drawn by Chris Costello in 1982 over about six months, when he was 23; he sold the rights to Letraset for $750.", "Bundled with macOS and with Microsoft Office, which is why it appears on so many menus and signs.", "James Cameron's Avatar (2009) used it for the film's logo and subtitles.", "A 2017 Saturday Night Live sketch starring Ryan Gosling is about a man unable to get over that choice."], "a": {"item_id": "ebcdad06fe33", "text": "You opened a day spa. You wanted serenity, mystery, the ancient wisdom of the East. So you opened Microsoft Word and scrolled until something felt like a cucumber on your eyelid. Congratulations: your sanctuary now shares branding with a movie about blue cat people, a sixth-grade report on the Nile, and every church bake sale since 1998. Ryan Gosling lost sleep over James Cameron doing this. You did it for \"Serenity Nails & Wax\" and slept fine.\n\nAnd the Mediterranean bistro owner who asked, \"How do I show my hummus is authentic?\" then picked the font that came free with a Dell. Nothing says \"Grandmother's recipe\" like your nephew's laptop.\n\nYou wanted exotic. You got pre-installed. Nobody relaxes at your spa. They're too busy remembering which school project they typed it on."}, "b": {"item_id": "a4df2dc21f25", "text": "Nobody picks Papyrus by accident. You went to a spa once, came out convinced you'd found your true calling, and now you want customers to feel like they're getting a massage in an ancient tomb that a Bali resort renovated. So you scroll past every honest typeface and land on the one that's been on every Avatar fan's laptop since 2009. Congratulations. You didn't choose a font. You chose a vibe with a decade-old hangover. Your sushi place is now hosting a sacred ritual, and the chef is confused about why the wasabi feels so spiritual."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0042", "lang": "en", "target_id": "juicero", "target_name": "Juicero", "facts": ["A Wi-Fi-connected cold-press juicer launched in 2016 at $699, later cut to $399, that squeezed proprietary bags of chopped produce sold by subscription.", "Raised about $120 million from investors including Google Ventures and Kleiner Perkins.", "In April 2017, Bloomberg reporters squeezed the packs by hand and got 7.5 ounces of juice in 90 seconds, faster than the machine's 8 ounces in about two minutes.", "Each pack carried a QR code that the press scanned and checked online before squeezing; it refused expired packs and would not run at all unless connected to Wi-Fi.", "Shut down in September 2017 and offered refunds on the machine."], "a": {"item_id": "4defd00efed2", "text": "Four hundred dollars. You paid four hundred dollars so a machine could check with your Wi-Fi before letting you have breakfast. You're the guy who says \"disrupt\" at Thanksgiving. You didn't want juice; you wanted to be the kind of person a juice startup would pitch to. And it worked! Investors handed that company a hundred and twenty million dollars, and you volunteered to be the exit strategy. Then Bloomberg showed the world you could squeeze the packets with your bare hands, faster, for free. So you paid four hundred dollars to get outperformed by your own grip. Your grandmother squeezed oranges every morning for sixty years. You subscribed to a bag. Somewhere tonight, a router outage stands between a venture capitalist and his kale, and honestly? The router's the smartest thing in that kitchen."}, "b": {"item_id": "fa27493ecc18", "text": "You paid $400 to feel like someone with a \"morning routine.\" Not to have juice. To have a routine. You wanted to stand in your kitchen at 6 a.m. in a gray linen set, slide a sealed pouch into a machine, and feel like a Series B founder with kidneys.\n\nThen Bloomberg showed you could squeeze the pouch with your bare hands and get the same juice. And you weren't mad at the company. You were mad at your hands. You'd hired a middleman for the one job your body was already built for.\n\nYou let a gadget check the internet before it would let you drink a beet, and you called that wellness. Your grandmother wrung out dishrags with more commitment to nutrition, and she never needed a QR code to know when the carrots expired."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0043", "lang": "en", "target_id": "google-sheets", "target_name": "Google Sheets", "facts": ["Launched in June 2006 as Google Spreadsheets, built on XL2Web, a product Google acquired from 2Web Technologies.", "A spreadsheet is capped at 10 million cells (raised from 5 million in 2022).", "Functions such as IMPORTHTML, IMPORTXML, and GOOGLEFINANCE pull live web data straight into cells.", "Free with a Google account; files live in Google Drive, and collaborators edit the same sheet simultaneously with coloured cursors.", "In a browser, offline editing works only in Chrome or Edge, with the Google Docs Offline extension installed and offline access switched on while still connected; the Android and iOS apps work offline natively."], "a": {"item_id": "2bbca6d87206", "text": "You're not a manager, you're a guy who got comfortable with IMPORTRANGE and mistook it for leadership. You said \"we don't need Jira, a sheet works fine,\" and now you have eleven tabs, three named \"Final,\" and one called \"Copy of Final (Dave's).\"\n\nWhat are you after? Control, disguised as transparency. You want everyone to see everything, as long as only you understand the formulas. Your team doesn't update the sheet. They open it, spot an anonymous capybara hovering over cell F14, and quietly leave.\n\nWhat does it get you? A color-coding system only you can decode, a Status column that has said \"In progress\" since March, and the rush of a stakeholder clicking \"Request edit access,\" the closest you'll ever come to being feared.\n\nMeanwhile, Version History knows who broke it. You know it too. You just won't say."}, "b": {"item_id": "a5780556db66", "text": "Let's talk about the brave souls who run their whole team out of one Google Sheet. Tab one says \"Tasks,\" tab nine says \"Tasks (FINAL),\" and tab twelve says \"Tasks (FINAL) copy 2.\" They color-code everything, then color-code the color codes. Their status dropdown has eleven options, and \"Done\" is still somehow \"In Progress.\" They believe a #REF! error is a personality trait. They lie awake wondering if the VLOOKUP in cell F212 will survive Brenda touching column D. They're not managing people. They're managing a spreadsheet that manages people, and they're so proud of it that they've locked the header row so nobody can ruin their life. Their dream is a pivot table. Their reality is a comment thread with forty-one replies that all say \"bump.\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0044", "lang": "en", "target_id": "keurig", "target_name": "Keurig", "facts": ["John Sylvan invented the K-Cup in the early 1990s and sold his stake for about $50,000 in 1997; in 2015 he told The Atlantic, 'I feel bad sometimes that I ever did it.'", "Keurig 2.0 (2014) read an ink mark on the lid and rejected unlicensed pods; sales fell and workarounds spread online.", "By 2014 enough K-Cups were sold each year to circle the Earth more than ten times; the pods were mostly unrecyclable #7 plastic until a switch to polypropylene by 2020.", "Keurig Green Mountain merged with Dr Pepper Snapple in 2018 to form Keurig Dr Pepper."], "a": {"item_id": "a034e62fdf17", "text": "You said you don't have time to grind beans, then spent ten minutes hunting for a pod in a drawer that's half foil lids and half regret. You bought the 72-count variety pack because you \"like options,\" and now you're drinking Hazelnut Creme Brûlée Cheesecake at 6 a.m. like a toddler who got the dessert menu. You pay fifty cents a cup for coffee that tastes like a hostage video: weak, brown, and apologizing. You press the \"strong\" button, which does nothing, and you believe in it anyway, like a guy pushing a crosswalk button.\n\nThe man who invented the K-Cup says he regrets it. The inventor. And you're still in the kitchen peeling foil off tiny plastic cups like you're losing at a very small lottery.\n\nYou bought the reusable filter to feel better about the landfill. It lives in a drawer now, with the gym membership and the sourdough starter."}, "b": {"item_id": "299bf476f938", "text": "You know who makes coffee in a Keurig? People who want to feel like a barista without the one thing a barista needs, which is caring. You're not making coffee. You're pressing a button and standing there for ninety seconds like the machine owes you a personality. Your mug says \"Coffee Snob,\" and you bought it in the same aisle as the 48-count variety pack, because you can't commit to one flavor, just like your gym membership. Hazelnut, Dark Magic, Donut Shop, and one mystery pod called Winter Blend that tastes like a wet cardboard box. You tell people you're \"not really a coffee person\" while on your third cup before noon. Your goal isn't flavor. It's reaching the office by 8:15 with something warm in your hand and a plausible excuse for why you still feel a little hollow."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0045", "lang": "en", "target_id": "keurig", "target_name": "Keurig", "facts": ["John Sylvan invented the K-Cup in the early 1990s and sold his stake for about $50,000 in 1997; in 2015 he told The Atlantic, 'I feel bad sometimes that I ever did it.'", "Keurig 2.0 (2014) read an ink mark on the lid and rejected unlicensed pods; sales fell and workarounds spread online.", "By 2014 enough K-Cups were sold each year to circle the Earth more than ten times; the pods were mostly unrecyclable #7 plastic until a switch to polypropylene by 2020.", "Keurig Green Mountain merged with Dr Pepper Snapple in 2018 to form Keurig Dr Pepper."], "a": {"item_id": "299bf476f938", "text": "You know who makes coffee in a Keurig? People who want to feel like a barista without the one thing a barista needs, which is caring. You're not making coffee. You're pressing a button and standing there for ninety seconds like the machine owes you a personality. Your mug says \"Coffee Snob,\" and you bought it in the same aisle as the 48-count variety pack, because you can't commit to one flavor, just like your gym membership. Hazelnut, Dark Magic, Donut Shop, and one mystery pod called Winter Blend that tastes like a wet cardboard box. You tell people you're \"not really a coffee person\" while on your third cup before noon. Your goal isn't flavor. It's reaching the office by 8:15 with something warm in your hand and a plausible excuse for why you still feel a little hollow."}, "b": {"item_id": "017955fe0eb2", "text": "You wanted coffee without a relationship, so you bought a plastic thimble of \"Hazelnut Crème Brûlée\" and let a machine rinse it for forty seconds. That's not a flavor, that's a candle with a caffeine problem.\n\nYou own a 96-count Costco box and still haven't found a pod that tastes like anything but hot mug. You hit the Strong button like it's a negotiation. It's the same coffee, just smaller and angrier.\n\nThe descale light has been on since March. You know it. It knows it. You're drinking limescale with notes of whatever pod you picked.\n\nYou bought the reusable filter once, to feel responsible. It lives in the drawer with the takeout menus. Every morning you throw away a tiny wet puck and call it a routine."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0046", "lang": "en", "target_id": "chicago-deep-dish", "target_name": "Chicago deep-dish pizza", "facts": ["Created at Pizzeria Uno in Chicago in 1943; credit is disputed among founder Ike Sewell, his partner Ric Riccardo, and cook Rudy Malnati.", "Assembled upside down: sliced mozzarella on the dough, then toppings, then crushed tomatoes on top, so the cheese does not scorch during the long bake.", "Jon Stewart called it 'tomato soup in a bread bowl' and 'a casserole' on The Daily Show in 2013.", "Chicagoans' everyday pizza is thin 'tavern-style' cut into squares; GrubHub order data reported by WBEZ in 2013 showed city residents ordering non-deep-dish pizza about 10 to 1 over deep dish, with deep dish and stuffed making up under 9% of orders."], "a": {"item_id": "6aea7052f5a6", "text": "Every time someone tells me Chicago deep-dish is \"technically not a pizza,\" I know exactly who I'm dealing with. He's a guy from Hoboken who ate one slice in Naples in 2009 and has been waiting fifteen years to bring it up. He's not hungry. He's shopping for a fight. He wants the whole table to hear that real pizza is thin and foldable, and he says \"foldable\" like it's a personality. Meanwhile he's eating with a knife and fork, which he thinks makes him sophisticated. Chicago never asked for his permission. Chicago has been feeding people since before he was born, while he was still folding pepperoni on a napkin. So let him win the argument. It's the only thing he's ever ordered that came out exactly how he wanted."}, "b": {"item_id": "8d0af0e1882c", "text": "Let's talk about the people who call it pizza. Chicagoans, mostly, from a city that spent a century being called the \"Second City\" and finally found a hill to die on. You don't love it. You're defending it.\n\nYou put your name in at 5:15 and eat at 7:00, then tell everyone it was worth the wait, because the alternative is admitting you spent your anniversary on a bench with a family from Ohio. You're after one thing: a fight with a New Yorker. And you win, in the sense that he stops texting you, which in Chicago counts as a civic victory.\n\nWhat do you get? A two-hour nap, a ruined evening, and a Goldbelly order shipped to Phoenix, where you eat it alone in a kitchen, defending a city you moved away from in 2011."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0047", "lang": "en", "target_id": "esperanto", "target_name": "Esperanto", "facts": ["Published in Warsaw in 1887 by L. L. Zamenhof, a Białystok-born eye doctor, under the pen name Doktoro Esperanto, 'one who hopes'.", "Speaker estimates range from 100,000 to two million; roughly a thousand people grow up with it as a native language.", "William Shatner starred in Incubus (1966), a horror film shot entirely in Esperanto, with pronunciation Esperantists still mock.", "Its Wikipedia has over 350,000 articles, and its Duolingo course for English speakers had 1.36 million learners by July 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "f5faac750f03", "text": "You spent six months learning a language designed to unite humanity, and your total audience is a retired Dutch man named Henk in a Telegram group. You didn't pick Esperanto because it's useful. You picked it because Spanish had a waiting list of people who were better at it than you. Here, you can be fluent by spring and the only competition is a guy who wears the green star pin to the grocery store.\n\nYou say you believe in world peace. You also correct people's accusatives in the comments section. Zamenhof dreamed of ending war, and a century later his legacy is you, alone, translating \"The Little Prince\" for a fourth time.\n\nYour big vacation is a World Congress, where you'll finally speak to thousands of people, all of whom are also there to speak to you."}, "b": {"item_id": "5681feb1725d", "text": "Esperanto learners looked at seven thousand living languages and said, \"None of these. I want one with zero places to use it.\" You wanted to unite humanity, so you picked the one hobby guaranteed to make humanity back away slowly at parties. You signed up to talk to everyone on Earth, and now you talk to a retired Dutch engineer named Joop and a Brazilian guy who keeps sending you poems. Your big payoff is Pasporta Servo, where strangers let you crash on their couch for free, the only time a hobby this niche has ever gotten anyone into a bed. A hundred and thirty years of pushing world peace, and the crowning achievement is still William Shatner's *Incubus*. Zamenhof wanted to end war. You just wanted to be fluent in something before your cousin finishes Duolingo French."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0048", "lang": "en", "target_id": "nuclear-fusion", "target_name": "Nuclear fusion power", "facts": ["Has been 'about 30 years away' since the 1950s; at the 1955 Geneva atoms-for-peace conference, Homi Bhabha predicted fusion power within two decades.", "ITER, agreed in 2006 for first plasma in 2016, re-baselined in 2024 to start research operations in 2034 and deuterium-tritium burning in 2039, at a cost estimated above €20 billion.", "NIF's December 2022 'ignition' shot released 3.15 MJ from 2.05 MJ of laser energy; the lasers drew about 300 MJ from the grid to fire.", "Private fusion companies had raised more than $7 billion by 2024."], "a": {"item_id": "f84181eac144", "text": "Let's hear it for the fusion people, the only scientists who've been \"ten years away\" since my grandfather had hair. You can hold plasma hotter than the sun for a few seconds, but you can't hold a deadline for one decade. Every press release: \"Net energy gain!\" Sure, if you don't count the electricity bill for the lasers. That's like me saying I'm profitable if you ignore rent. ITER got approved when Myspace was cool, and it's still assembling the donut. And the startups: \"We're different, we have venture capital.\" Great, now the sun is ten years away *and* has a pitch deck. Honestly, it's a beautiful racket: promise infinite clean energy, collect the grant, retire before anyone plugs in a toaster. You're not building a star. You're building a pension."}, "b": {"item_id": "1f957f86774f", "text": "Fusion scientists have the best job security in human history. You promise limitless energy in ten years. Ten years later, you promise it in ten years. It's the only career where missing the deadline *is* the business model. A pharmacist does that, she's arrested. A fusion CEO does that, he gets a Series C and a TED Talk.\n\nAnd ten is the perfect number. Far enough away that you'll be retired, close enough that the grant committee gets a little tingle. Nobody ever says \"twelve,\" because twelve doesn't fit on the slide.\n\nThey announced \"net energy gain\" by not counting the electricity that powered the lasers. That's like saying you won at the casino if you ignore the chips you bought.\n\nThey're building a star on Earth, and they can't even make it on time. The real one is free and shows up every morning."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0049", "lang": "en", "target_id": "pmma", "target_name": "PMMA (e-beam resist)", "facts": ["The same polymer as Plexiglas and acrylic glass, dissolved in anisole or chlorobenzene and spun into a thin film.", "The oldest electron-beam resist still in everyday use; IBM researchers reported it as one in the late 1960s.", "Positive tone: the beam breaks polymer chains and the exposed region dissolves in a MIBK:IPA developer.", "Resolves below 10 nm but has poor sensitivity, so doses of hundreds to over a thousand µC/cm² (depending on voltage) make writing slow.", "Poor dry-etch resistance, so it is used mostly for lift-off rather than as an etch mask."], "a": {"item_id": "b05c221e0d70", "text": "You're the person who says \"it's a classic\" about a process you inherited from a postdoc who left in 2009. Your lab notebook is just a bottle of 950K with someone else's handwriting on it. You say ZEP is \"too finicky\" while spending nine hours writing a 200 µm array because you chose a dose that could cook an egg.\n\nYou say you want 10 nm resolution, but your liftoff has the edge profile of a torn receipt, and you've been \"optimizing the bilayer\" since your qualifying exam. You treat MIBK:IPA 1:3 like a family recipe and cold development like a personality.\n\nMeanwhile the group next door switched resists, graduated, bought houses, and now send you LinkedIn requests. You stay loyal. Loyalty is great. It's just that your beam time costs more than their weddings."}, "b": {"item_id": "a93c5e595a57", "text": "You're still running PMMA? Bless you. You're the guy who spins 950K A4 at 4000 rpm like it's 2003 and then acts shocked when your thickness drifts. You bake at 180 for ninety seconds like it's a sourdough starter, and you develop in MIBK to IPA one-to-three while muttering about contrast. Your writes take eleven hours because you're hosing the sample with dose just to draw a line HSQ would have handled before lunch. Meanwhile the ZEP people are home by six, drinking something with a garnish. You're not a purist. You're a man who read one paper on proximity effect in 2009 and never recovered. Your lift-off is clean, I'll give you that. Honestly, it's the most reliable thing about you."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0050", "lang": "en", "target_id": "php", "target_name": "PHP", "facts": ["Created by Rasmus Lerdorf in 1994 as 'Personal Home Page Tools', a set of C programs for tracking visitors to his own résumé page; the name now officially stands for the recursive 'PHP: Hypertext Preprocessor'.", "Lerdorf, in his 2007 MySQL Conference talk 'PHP on Hormones': 'I'm not a real programmer. I throw together things until it works then I move on.'", "Runs WordPress, which W3Techs counted on 39.4% of all websites (58.1% of sites with a known CMS) on 9 November 2026.", "Core function names are inconsistent (strpos, str_replace, htmlspecialchars, nl2br) partly because, as Lerdorf confirmed, early function names were chosen to balance a hash table keyed on string length.", "Until PHP 8 (2020), the comparison 0 == 'foo' was true because the string was silently converted to a number."], "a": {"item_id": "453c78fa8180", "text": "Let's hear it for the PHP developers. You chose a language with a dollar sign on every variable, and you did it on purpose. Nobody stumbles into that. You saw that `array_search` takes its arguments in the opposite order from `in_array`, shrugged, and said, \"Honestly, I respect the chaos.\" Now you say \"modern PHP\" with a straight face, like you aren't still babysitting a WordPress site held together by a plugin called `mega_fix_final_v2`. Your dream is to be the one person who knows where the bodies are buried in a 900,000-line codebase, and you're proud of it. Your LinkedIn says \"Laravel enthusiast.\" Your weekends are spent on a staging server. And somewhere a recruiter is paying you more than the Rust guy, and you still feel a little guilty about it."}, "b": {"item_id": "4b50923aaa44", "text": "You've said \"PHP isn't what it used to be\" so often it's basically your LinkedIn headline. Nobody asked. You bring up Facebook at parties like it's your cousin's startup. You switched to Laravel and call it a fresh start, which is a man putting a tuxedo on a Honda to prove he's going places.\n\nWhat are you after? Respect. What do you get? A client named Brenda who wants her WordPress site \"just a little different\" and pays by Venmo, sometimes.\n\nYou tell recruiters \"we're on PHP 8,\" meaning staging is. Production is a file called final_v2_REAL.php, with a comment from a guy who quit in 2014: \"don't touch.\"\n\nYou've been meaning to learn Go since 2019. Your fear isn't that PHP will die. It's that it never will, and neither will your on-call rotation."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0051", "lang": "en", "target_id": "papyrus", "target_name": "Papyrus", "facts": ["Hand-drawn by Chris Costello in 1982 over about six months, when he was 23; he sold the rights to Letraset for $750.", "Bundled with macOS and with Microsoft Office, which is why it appears on so many menus and signs.", "James Cameron's Avatar (2009) used it for the film's logo and subtitles.", "A 2017 Saturday Night Live sketch starring Ryan Gosling is about a man unable to get over that choice."], "a": {"item_id": "a4df2dc21f25", "text": "Nobody picks Papyrus by accident. You went to a spa once, came out convinced you'd found your true calling, and now you want customers to feel like they're getting a massage in an ancient tomb that a Bali resort renovated. So you scroll past every honest typeface and land on the one that's been on every Avatar fan's laptop since 2009. Congratulations. You didn't choose a font. You chose a vibe with a decade-old hangover. Your sushi place is now hosting a sacred ritual, and the chef is confused about why the wasabi feels so spiritual."}, "b": {"item_id": "df3aac397e9d", "text": "You opened a \"Himalayan salt cave and wellness sanctuary\" with a small-business loan and a dropdown menu. Around 11 p.m., exhausted, you scrolled past the fonts that cost money and landed on the one that whispers \"ancient healing\" to everyone, including your nephew, who \"does graphics\" and approved it.\n\nYou wanted timeless. You got the exact look of every other spa, the falafel place two doors down, and a yoga studio run by a guy named Dave who says \"namaste\" like a threat.\n\nSomewhere, Ryan Gosling is staring at your awning with the same haunted silence he gave the Avatar title, and he's right. You didn't build a brand. You built a $14 \"Pharaoh's Bowl\" for people who think Epcot counts as a passport, and you told yourself it was authentic."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0052", "lang": "en", "target_id": "esperanto", "target_name": "Esperanto", "facts": ["Published in Warsaw in 1887 by L. L. Zamenhof, a Białystok-born eye doctor, under the pen name Doktoro Esperanto, 'one who hopes'.", "Speaker estimates range from 100,000 to two million; roughly a thousand people grow up with it as a native language.", "William Shatner starred in Incubus (1966), a horror film shot entirely in Esperanto, with pronunciation Esperantists still mock.", "Its Wikipedia has over 350,000 articles, and its Duolingo course for English speakers had 1.36 million learners by July 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "5681feb1725d", "text": "Esperanto learners looked at seven thousand living languages and said, \"None of these. I want one with zero places to use it.\" You wanted to unite humanity, so you picked the one hobby guaranteed to make humanity back away slowly at parties. You signed up to talk to everyone on Earth, and now you talk to a retired Dutch engineer named Joop and a Brazilian guy who keeps sending you poems. Your big payoff is Pasporta Servo, where strangers let you crash on their couch for free, the only time a hobby this niche has ever gotten anyone into a bed. A hundred and thirty years of pushing world peace, and the crowning achievement is still William Shatner's *Incubus*. Zamenhof wanted to end war. You just wanted to be fluent in something before your cousin finishes Duolingo French."}, "b": {"item_id": "f86448df0967", "text": "You learned a language built to unite humanity, and the only humanity you've united with is a guy named Dieter on a forum from 2004. You wanted world peace; you got a Duolingo streak and a green star pin you're afraid to wear to Target. Zamenhof dreamed of one tongue for all nations, and \"all nations\" turned out to be eleven retirees at a congress in Slovakia. You picked it because it's \"easy,\" which is what people say about the gym membership they never use. You've mastered the letter ĉ, a skill that has never come up in a job interview. Your best conversation partner is a stranger who says \"Saluton!\" and then switches to English out of mercy. Spanish would have gotten you a girlfriend. Esperanto got you a mailing list, a sticker on your laptop, and a very strong opinion about the accusative."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0053", "lang": "en", "target_id": "google-glass", "target_name": "Google Glass", "facts": ["Unveiled at Google I/O 2012 with skydivers livestreaming their jump through it onto the Moscone Center roof.", "The Explorer Edition sold to invited users in 2013 for $1,500.", "Wearers were nicknamed 'Glassholes'; bars, cinemas, and casinos banned the device over its camera.", "The consumer programme ended in January 2015; an Enterprise Edition continued until Google stopped selling it in 2023.", "Its display was a 640-by-360 prism in the corner of one eye."], "a": {"item_id": "b9dec6f155dd", "text": "Nothing says \"I'm approachable\" like paying fifteen hundred dollars to be a beta tester. You're not an early adopter, you're an unpaid Google intern who also buys his own drinks.\n\nYou came here to be noticed, and it worked, the way a guy who says \"I'm not a cop\" gets noticed. The bartender isn't asking what you're having, he's asking if you're recording. The woman you wanted to impress is explaining to her friend what a \"Glasshole\" is.\n\nThen you said \"OK Glass\" to a crowded room, waited, said it louder, and the only one who responded was the bouncer.\n\nYou wanted to look like the future. Instead you look like the first guy the future gets asked to leave."}, "b": {"item_id": "d051a1381bcf", "text": "Look at this guy. Paid fifteen hundred dollars to join the Explorer Program, and the only thing he's explored is the sidewalk outside every bar in San Francisco. He walks up and says \"OK Glass\" out loud, like a man ordering from a butler who isn't there. You came here to meet women, and you're the one person every woman in the room has already assumed is filming her. Buddy, when you wink at someone, she doesn't think you're flirting. She thinks you just took her picture. Because you did. You tap your temple like you're having a thought, and we all know you've never had one. Google gave up on you. Google, the company that kept Google+ alive for eight years, looked at you and said \"no.\" There's a word for you. It's Glasshole, and you earned it."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0054", "lang": "en", "target_id": "canberra", "target_name": "Canberra", "facts": ["Chosen as a compromise because neither Sydney nor Melbourne would accept the other as capital; Australia's constitution required the capital to be at least 100 miles from Sydney.", "Designed by the Chicago architects Walter Burley Griffin and Marion Mahony Griffin, who won an international competition in 1912.", "Its centrepiece, Lake Burley Griffin, is artificial, created by damming the Molonglo River in 1963-64.", "Parliament House (1988) is built into Capital Hill under a lawn meant for citizens to walk over their politicians; the lawn was fenced off in 2017 for security.", "Melbourne served as the capital until 1927 while Canberra was being built."], "a": {"item_id": "f332ff248cdb", "text": "You came to Canberra for the job. Everyone does. Nobody moves here for the weather, which has a grudge. You're here for a portfolio you can't explain at a barbecue, on a salary that makes your Sydney mates ask if you're still on the dole. In week one you said \"I'm only here two years.\" That was six years ago. You own a kayak you've used once, on Lake Burley Griffin, and you call it \"the lake\" like it's the only one. Your lanyard lives in your handbag like a passport. When your mum asks what you actually do, you say \"advice,\" which is the most Canberra word there is. Every second Friday you drive to Sydney to see friends, when really you just need to sit in traffic with people who aren't in a meeting about the meeting."}, "b": {"item_id": "a719d3fb6cc2", "text": "You moved to Canberra for the job, which is a brave way of saying you wanted an EL1 before thirty and Sydney wanted $900 a week for a room with a view of another room.\n\nYou said \"it's only for two years.\" Now you own a house in Gungahlin and have strong opinions about roundabouts.\n\nYour whole personality is your department. At parties nobody asks what you do. They ask which building, and everyone nods like you've named a regiment. You wear your lanyard into the Dickson noodle place like it's a war medal.\n\nYou came for career progression. You got a restructure every eighteen months, a boss who says \"going forward\" at farewells, and Sydney friends who promise to visit the way you promise to \"circle back.\"\n\nYou're not climbing the ladder. You're being machinery-of-governmented sideways."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0055", "lang": "en", "target_id": "canberra", "target_name": "Canberra", "facts": ["Chosen as a compromise because neither Sydney nor Melbourne would accept the other as capital; Australia's constitution required the capital to be at least 100 miles from Sydney.", "Designed by the Chicago architects Walter Burley Griffin and Marion Mahony Griffin, who won an international competition in 1912.", "Its centrepiece, Lake Burley Griffin, is artificial, created by damming the Molonglo River in 1963-64.", "Parliament House (1988) is built into Capital Hill under a lawn meant for citizens to walk over their politicians; the lawn was fenced off in 2017 for security.", "Melbourne served as the capital until 1927 while Canberra was being built."], "a": {"item_id": "4906cad02ec9", "text": "You moved to Canberra for the job. Brave. You looked at Sydney rents, did the maths, and decided your personality could be replaced by a security clearance. Now you tell strangers you \"can't really talk about what you do,\" and they're relieved, because it's a briefing note about another briefing note for Senate Estimates.\n\nYou came for the ladder: APS5, APS6, EL1, a lanyard that opens one extra door. You stayed because you bought in Gungahlin and the mortgage is now your closest friend.\n\nEvery Friday you say you're \"basically in Sydney,\" which means three hours up the Hume, past a giant Merino, to feel alive for one weekend. Then it's back to a Braddon brunch with the same six people from your branch.\n\nBut hey, you're close to the snow. You've been once. You'll mention it forever."}, "b": {"item_id": "a719d3fb6cc2", "text": "You moved to Canberra for the job, which is a brave way of saying you wanted an EL1 before thirty and Sydney wanted $900 a week for a room with a view of another room.\n\nYou said \"it's only for two years.\" Now you own a house in Gungahlin and have strong opinions about roundabouts.\n\nYour whole personality is your department. At parties nobody asks what you do. They ask which building, and everyone nods like you've named a regiment. You wear your lanyard into the Dickson noodle place like it's a war medal.\n\nYou came for career progression. You got a restructure every eighteen months, a boss who says \"going forward\" at farewells, and Sydney friends who promise to visit the way you promise to \"circle back.\"\n\nYou're not climbing the ladder. You're being machinery-of-governmented sideways."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0056", "lang": "en", "target_id": "brasilia", "target_name": "Brasília", "facts": ["Inaugurated on 21 April 1960 after about 41 months of construction, moving the capital inland from Rio de Janeiro.", "Lúcio Costa's Plano Piloto is laid out like an airplane (or a bird, or a cross), with Oscar Niemeyer's government buildings along the fuselage.", "Listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1987, less than 30 years after it was built.", "Planned for 500,000 residents; the Federal District now has about 3 million, most in satellite towns outside the plan.", "Built for the car: wide avenues, few pedestrian crossings, and distances that make walking impractical."], "a": {"item_id": "5894439abe59", "text": "You're an architecture student, a tourist, or an intern, and all three of you looked at the Congress from the Rodoviária and said, \"Oh, that's right there.\" It is not right there. It's a mirage with a dome. Forty minutes later you're crossing a lawn at noon in 12 percent humidity, quoting Niemeyer to yourself like it's a hydration plan.\n\nAnd you're proud! You even cut a little dirt shortcut through the grass, the famous caminho do desejo, the only urban planning in Brasília done by someone who actually had to get somewhere. You were walking to a ministry for one signature, and the man who signs is out on Thursdays. It's Thursday.\n\nCongratulations, pilgrim. You've experienced modernism up close: a sunburn, a nosebleed, and a guy in a Corolla honking at you at the tesourinha like you're the one who's lost."}, "b": {"item_id": "a98c294fb19a", "text": "Walking in Brasília is for three kinds of people: tourists who saw the Esplanada on a postcard and thought \"that looks close,\" architecture students who love Niemeyer from a safe distance, and the guy whose car is in the shop. You're not a pedestrian. You're an ant that wandered onto a blueprint. You want bread? The padaria is \"right there\" across the Eixão, so you sprint six lanes like you owe the government money. Which, let's be honest, is also your employer. Your sneakers are permanently red from the dirt trails carved by every other fool who refused to walk around a tesourinha. And on Sunday, when they close the Eixão, you finally stroll down the middle of the highway like a man who's won something. You haven't. Everyone else just stopped driving for one day."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0057", "lang": "en", "target_id": "klingon", "target_name": "Klingon", "facts": ["Created by linguist Marc Okrand for Star Trek III (1984), building on a few words the actor James Doohan improvised for the 1979 film.", "Deliberately alien grammar: object-verb-subject word order and no verb 'to be', so Hamlet's line became 'taH pagh taHbe'', 'continue or not continue'.", "The Klingon Language Institute (1992) published Hamlet 'in the original Klingon'; Bing Translator added Klingon in 2013 and Duolingo in 2018.", "Okrand's 1985 Klingon Dictionary listed roughly 1,500 words, and fluent speakers number only in the dozens: 20 to 30 in 2009, 30 to 50 by 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "c3c693f6b9ba", "text": "Nobody becomes fluent in Klingon by accident. You have to sit down and decide that the thing you want most in life is to clear your throat in a language invented for a TV show. Your friends learned Spanish for a job. You learned to say \"Qapla'\" to a bartender who has never once been impressed. You've translated Hamlet. Twice. The Klingons in the original show would read your Hamlet and call it too soft, which is the only review you've ever wanted. At conventions you're the one who corrects the guy in the Worf costume, and he has the better forehead. You're chasing a room where someone finally gets your jokes, and that room is mostly you, reading your own joke aloud, slightly out of breath from the gutturals."}, "b": {"item_id": "a7e3b2e8dd9c", "text": "Congratulations on mastering a language whose native speakers are fictional, homicidal, and would gut you over your accent. You've spent a decade on the warrior tongue of the galaxy, and your only battle was a Discord war over whether the apostrophe is a glottal stop. Marc Okrand made it up on a movie deadline, and you treat it like Latin.\n\nYou can order a drink in a language with no word for \"hello,\" which explains why no one's greeted you at a party since 2009. You translated Hamlet into it. Hamlet! A guy who can't commit to a decision, rendered for a species that would have stabbed Claudius in act one.\n\nAnd what does fluency get you? A convention hall of forty people correcting your verb prefixes, a girlfriend who learned exactly one phrase (the one for \"leave me alone\"), and a lifetime of dying honorably in a Hyatt conference room.\n\nQapla'. You'll need it."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0058", "lang": "en", "target_id": "graphene", "target_name": "Graphene", "facts": ["Isolated in 2004 by Andre Geim and Konstantin Novoselov at Manchester by peeling graphite with Scotch tape; Nobel Prize in Physics, 2010.", "The EU's Graphene Flagship, launched in 2013, committed €1 billion over ten years.", "A 2018 study in Advanced Materials tested 'graphene' from 60 producers and found none with more than 50% graphene content; most was graphite flakes.", "Sold in tennis rackets, running shoes, and headphones as a performance ingredient.", "Geim also won an Ig Nobel Prize in 2000 for levitating a frog with magnets."], "a": {"item_id": "9c280b4152bc", "text": "Every graphene pitch deck opens with the same slide: a honeycomb, a stock photo of a tennis racket, and the word \"revolution\" in a font you'd use for a dentist. These are the people who got a Nobel Prize for pulling a flake of pencil lead off with sticky tape, then spent fifteen years and four investor rounds explaining why that's the future. Their LinkedIn says \"materials scientist.\" Their Series A says \"platform.\" Their fundraising emails say \"graphene-enabled,\" which is what you write when the graphene in the sunscreen is too thin to find with a microscope. They want the Nobel, the magazine cover, and a sabbatical in Tahiti. What they get is one sentence in a press release that starts \"Experts say.\""}, "b": {"item_id": "dd1e6a2334b9", "text": "Let's hear it for the graphene evangelists, the only people who've been \"five years away\" since 2004. Two guys in Manchester peel pencil lead with Scotch tape, win a Nobel, and an entire industry decides the tape was the business plan. Every press release is the same Mad Lib: \"Wonder material could revolutionize [batteries/condoms/desalination/your feelings].\" *Could.* That word has funded more postdocs than the NSF. You promised us space elevators and delivered a tennis racket with a sticker on it. Startup founders slap \"graphene-enhanced\" on a phone case the way divorced dads put a spoiler on a Honda Civic. And the grant committees keep paying, because admitting the miracle is stuck on the production line means admitting you are too. Twenty years of changing everything, and the only thing you've actually changed is the abstract."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0059", "lang": "en", "target_id": "canberra", "target_name": "Canberra", "facts": ["Chosen as a compromise because neither Sydney nor Melbourne would accept the other as capital; Australia's constitution required the capital to be at least 100 miles from Sydney.", "Designed by the Chicago architects Walter Burley Griffin and Marion Mahony Griffin, who won an international competition in 1912.", "Its centrepiece, Lake Burley Griffin, is artificial, created by damming the Molonglo River in 1963-64.", "Parliament House (1988) is built into Capital Hill under a lawn meant for citizens to walk over their politicians; the lawn was fenced off in 2017 for security.", "Melbourne served as the capital until 1927 while Canberra was being built."], "a": {"item_id": "e7c430775b37", "text": "You moved to Canberra for the job. Brave. You looked at Sydney rents, did the maths, and decided your personality was negotiable. Now you're an EL1 who says \"stakeholder\" at barbecues and talks about Senate Estimates like a war you survived. Your lanyard has a richer social life than you do. You promised your Melbourne friends a visit; they've cancelled twice for weddings in Bali. You've developed opinions about Gungahlin. Your wild Saturday is Braddon brunch, followed by complaining it was \"basically Sydney prices.\" And what did it get you? A secure job, a three-bedroom in Tuggeranong, and a security clearance confirming the government checked thoroughly and found nothing interesting. You came for two years. That was 2009. Your kid now says \"on the Hill\" unprompted, and you've never been prouder or more quietly trapped."}, "b": {"item_id": "a719d3fb6cc2", "text": "You moved to Canberra for the job, which is a brave way of saying you wanted an EL1 before thirty and Sydney wanted $900 a week for a room with a view of another room.\n\nYou said \"it's only for two years.\" Now you own a house in Gungahlin and have strong opinions about roundabouts.\n\nYour whole personality is your department. At parties nobody asks what you do. They ask which building, and everyone nods like you've named a regiment. You wear your lanyard into the Dickson noodle place like it's a war medal.\n\nYou came for career progression. You got a restructure every eighteen months, a boss who says \"going forward\" at farewells, and Sydney friends who promise to visit the way you promise to \"circle back.\"\n\nYou're not climbing the ladder. You're being machinery-of-governmented sideways."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0060", "lang": "en", "target_id": "papyrus", "target_name": "Papyrus", "facts": ["Hand-drawn by Chris Costello in 1982 over about six months, when he was 23; he sold the rights to Letraset for $750.", "Bundled with macOS and with Microsoft Office, which is why it appears on so many menus and signs.", "James Cameron's Avatar (2009) used it for the film's logo and subtitles.", "A 2017 Saturday Night Live sketch starring Ryan Gosling is about a man unable to get over that choice."], "a": {"item_id": "1988c200f894", "text": "Let's talk about the restaurant owner who picked Papyrus for the sign. You bought a franchise of \"authentic\" sourdough, you have a chalkboard that says \"Gather,\" and you think a font that looks like it was carved on a tree stump is the closest thing to a hug a customer can get. You didn't choose it. It chose you, the moment you typed \"organic\" into a font search. Now your $14 avocado toast sits under a logo that reads like a yoga instructor's grocery list. Your spa is \"a sanctuary,\" but the sign out front says \"we're still figuring out our brand.\" You wanted the calm of a Bali retreat. You got the calm of a dentist's waiting room in 2009, and somehow it still costs you $400 a month in massages you can't afford."}, "b": {"item_id": "ebcdad06fe33", "text": "You opened a day spa. You wanted serenity, mystery, the ancient wisdom of the East. So you opened Microsoft Word and scrolled until something felt like a cucumber on your eyelid. Congratulations: your sanctuary now shares branding with a movie about blue cat people, a sixth-grade report on the Nile, and every church bake sale since 1998. Ryan Gosling lost sleep over James Cameron doing this. You did it for \"Serenity Nails & Wax\" and slept fine.\n\nAnd the Mediterranean bistro owner who asked, \"How do I show my hummus is authentic?\" then picked the font that came free with a Dell. Nothing says \"Grandmother's recipe\" like your nephew's laptop.\n\nYou wanted exotic. You got pre-installed. Nobody relaxes at your spa. They're too busy remembering which school project they typed it on."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0061", "lang": "en", "target_id": "canberra", "target_name": "Canberra", "facts": ["Chosen as a compromise because neither Sydney nor Melbourne would accept the other as capital; Australia's constitution required the capital to be at least 100 miles from Sydney.", "Designed by the Chicago architects Walter Burley Griffin and Marion Mahony Griffin, who won an international competition in 1912.", "Its centrepiece, Lake Burley Griffin, is artificial, created by damming the Molonglo River in 1963-64.", "Parliament House (1988) is built into Capital Hill under a lawn meant for citizens to walk over their politicians; the lawn was fenced off in 2017 for security.", "Melbourne served as the capital until 1927 while Canberra was being built."], "a": {"item_id": "5732fa32845b", "text": "You know the type who moves to Canberra for \"two years, max.\" They arrive with a Perth accent, a Bunnings-sized ambition, and a LinkedIn banner that says \"Shaping policy.\" Six months in, they're saying \"Manuka\" like it's the suburb they grew up in. They want an acting EL2 before they've learned which parkway goes where. They own a lanyard drawer and a plan to be a Senate estimates star by spring. What it gets them: a townhouse in Gungahlin, a friend group made entirely of people who also work at Finance, and weekends spent driving to Bungendore to feel like they've left. Two years later they're still here, telling their mates back home the job's \"nearly done,\" which is exactly what they said in 2016."}, "b": {"item_id": "e7c430775b37", "text": "You moved to Canberra for the job. Brave. You looked at Sydney rents, did the maths, and decided your personality was negotiable. Now you're an EL1 who says \"stakeholder\" at barbecues and talks about Senate Estimates like a war you survived. Your lanyard has a richer social life than you do. You promised your Melbourne friends a visit; they've cancelled twice for weddings in Bali. You've developed opinions about Gungahlin. Your wild Saturday is Braddon brunch, followed by complaining it was \"basically Sydney prices.\" And what did it get you? A secure job, a three-bedroom in Tuggeranong, and a security clearance confirming the government checked thoroughly and found nothing interesting. You came for two years. That was 2009. Your kid now says \"on the Hill\" unprompted, and you've never been prouder or more quietly trapped."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0062", "lang": "en", "target_id": "macau", "target_name": "Macau", "facts": ["A Portuguese settlement from the 1550s until the handover to China in December 1999.", "Casino revenue passed the Las Vegas Strip's in 2006, and at its 2013 peak, about $45 billion, was roughly seven times the Strip's.", "One company, STDM, held the casino monopoly for four decades until licences opened to competition in 2002.", "Among the most densely populated places on Earth, at roughly 20,000 people per square kilometre.", "The Cotai Strip stands on land reclaimed from the sea between the islands of Taipa and Coloane."], "a": {"item_id": "67e9bb83372e", "text": "You already lost the down payment, so naturally you're taking the TurboJET from Hong Kong to win it back, because nothing says financial recovery like a ferry with a buffet. You've got a system for baccarat. Baccarat! The only decision is \"Banker\" or \"Player,\" and you still fill a little scorecard with red and blue dots like the shoe is sending you messages. It isn't. That's just a deck of cards and your mortgage. You've got red underwear on for luck and a junket host named \"Uncle\" who is definitely not your uncle. By 4 a.m. you'll be drifting through the fake Venice canals in a gondola, humming, past the pawn shop that's already priced your Rolex. Then you'll stumble up to the Ruins of St. Paul's: a church that burned until only the front was left standing. Finally, something here that looks like you."}, "b": {"item_id": "5ababa96b17b", "text": "You're not going to Macau to gamble. You're going to Macau to be wrong in a more expensive location. You lost it in Vegas, in Atlantic City, on your cousin's \"can't-miss\" crypto, and now you've flown fourteen hours to a former Portuguese colony to chase it down at a baccarat table, with a dealer who's seen your exact face on ten thousand other men.\n\nYou'll tell people it's a \"research trip.\" You'll stand in a fake Venice, under a fake sky, floating on a fake canal, trying to win real money back. You see the problem there, right?\n\nAnd the pawn shops are right outside the casinos. Macau doesn't even hide the sequel. It built the walk of shame with a loan desk.\n\nYou'll leave with no money, a stranger's cigarette smoke in your jacket, and one Portuguese egg tart you bought as a victory snack. It's the only thing you won, and you'll eat it on the ferry, alone, doing math."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0063", "lang": "en", "target_id": "las-vegas", "target_name": "Las Vegas", "facts": ["Nevada legalized casino gambling in 1931, the year construction of the Hoover Dam began nearby.", "Most of the Strip lies not in the city of Las Vegas but in the unincorporated town of Paradise.", "The Sphere, opened in 2023 at a cost of about $2.3 billion, is the largest spherical building in the world.", "'What happens here, stays here' was launched by the local convention and visitors authority in 2003.", "Lake Mead, its water source, fell in July 2022 to about 1,040 feet, its lowest since the reservoir first filled in 1937, and dropped below that mark again in August 2026."], "a": {"item_id": "c0bc98e0b0fb", "text": "You flew to Vegas to get rich. Think about that. You looked at a city built on the mathematical certainty that you will lose and said, \"Yeah, but I've got a feeling.\"\n\nYou have a system, of course. The system is a YouTube video from 2011 and a lucky shirt. You're up forty dollars at blackjack and suddenly you're tipping the dealer like a man whose yacht is double-parked outside.\n\nBy 4 a.m. you're hunched at a Wheel of Fortune slot, pressing a button that does nothing, drinking a free vodka cranberry like it's a salary. You've taken out cash three times, and the ATM fee has now earned more than you have.\n\nYou'll fly home with a buffet hangover, a sunburn from a pool you were too hungover to enjoy, and a cash-out ticket for $3.47 you're too proud to redeem. And a story about how you were \"so close.\"\n\nYou came to beat the house. The house gave you a discount on the room because it knew you'd be back."}, "b": {"item_id": "b2bb31b0fbe2", "text": "You flew Spirit to Las Vegas to get rich. Sit with that. You paid extra for a carry-on and called it an investment strategy. You've got a blackjack \"system\" from a YouTube guy filming in his mom's kitchen. You told your wife it's \"basically math.\" Sure. So is a second mortgage.\n\nHere's what you won: a Caesars Rewards card, a \"free\" buffet you earned by losing eleven hundred dollars, and a room \"upgrade\" overlooking the Bellagio parking structure. You hit four hundred bucks on a Wheel of Fortune slot at 4 a.m. and fed it right back like it owed you child support.\n\nThe pit boss knows your name. That's not respect, that's inventory.\n\nAnd you'll fly home saying \"I basically broke even,\" which is Vegas for \"Please don't check the joint account.\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0064", "lang": "en", "target_id": "comic-sans", "target_name": "Comic Sans", "facts": ["Designed by Vincent Connare at Microsoft in 1994, modeled on the comic-book lettering in The Dark Knight Returns and Watchmen.", "Made for the speech bubbles of Rover, the cartoon dog in Microsoft Bob; it missed Bob's release and shipped instead in the Windows 95 Plus! pack.", "CERN's July 2012 Higgs boson discovery announcement was presented on slides set in it.", "The 'Ban Comic Sans' campaign, started by designers Dave and Holly Combs in 1999, ran until 2019, when they retired it and launched usecomicsans.com."], "a": {"item_id": "6867518453d2", "text": "You're the office manager who wrote \"Parking permits will now be enforced\" and thought, this needs to feel like a hug. You wanted approachable. You wanted everyone to know you're not a monster, you're Deb, you bring bundt cake. And it worked! Nobody's afraid of you. Nobody's afraid of the notice either.\n\nThe landlord who slides \"Rent is 30 days late\" under the door in Comic Sans isn't collecting rent. He's opening negotiations. The HR rep announcing layoffs in it isn't softening the blow, she's making a grown man feel like he got fired by the cartoon dog from Microsoft Bob. And the HOA president threatening fines over bin placement? Congratulations, you've made a tyrant look like a substitute teacher on movie day.\n\nYou wanted warmth and authority and picked the one choice that guarantees neither. CERN did it for the Higgs boson and still hasn't lived it down, and they had a particle going for them. You've got a fish-in-the-microwave memo."}, "b": {"item_id": "011c029d7bc6", "text": "Let's talk about the person who posts the boil-water advisory in Comic Sans. You had one job: make residents afraid of their faucets. Instead, E. coli looks like it's coming to a sleepover.\n\nThese people think \"approachable\" is a setting you can click. The office manager typing MANDATORY LAYOFFS in a font Microsoft made for a cartoon dog's speech bubble. Sharon, you're firing forty people in the voice of a golden retriever.\n\nWhat are they after? Bad news with zero blame. The authority of a memo, the forgiveness of a kindergarten cubby label.\n\nAnd what does it get them? CERN found the Higgs boson, the particle that gives the universe mass, and announced it in Comic Sans. Humanity's greatest physics breakthrough, and the internet's only question was: who let the dog type?"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0065", "lang": "en", "target_id": "monopoly", "target_name": "Monopoly", "facts": ["Descends from Lizzie Magie's The Landlord's Game, patented in 1904 to teach the evils of land monopoly in the spirit of Henry George.", "Charles Darrow sold his version to Parker Brothers in 1935 and was credited as the inventor; Parker bought Magie's patent for $500.", "The official rules require any unbought property to be auctioned, and 'Free Parking' pays nothing; the house rules that skip the auction and pile cash on Free Parking are why games drag.", "During the Second World War, British intelligence had maps, compasses, and real currency hidden in sets sent to prisoner-of-war camps."], "a": {"item_id": "10bac3efc0cc", "text": "You all gather around a table to prove one thing: that you'd evict your own mother for a purple property. Dad's the banker, which is why the bank has mysteriously never gone broke and he somehow always has a 500. Your sister trades like a hostage negotiator, offering you Baltic Avenue and emotional closure for Boardwalk. Uncle Rick insists Free Parking pays out the whole jackpot, a rule that exists nowhere except in his need to feel lucky once in his life. The twelve-year-old wants only the railroads, because he's the one person here with a clear understanding of passive income. Grandma says nothing for three hours, then owns everything. Six hours in, nobody's having fun, but nobody can quit, because quitting means admitting you're the one in jail. It ends the way it always does: someone flips the board, someone cries, and everyone's out of money, including the winner's relationships."}, "b": {"item_id": "d5aee5aa186d", "text": "Let's talk about families who still play Monopoly on holidays. You know who you are: you swore it'd be \"fun,\" then spent four hours watching your brother-in-law build hotels on Boardwalk like he's finally getting the respect his career never gave him. Dad's the banker, which means a man with zero accountability and a lap full of orange five-hundreds. Mom invented a Free Parking jackpot rule in 1994 and will defend it harder than her marriage. Your little cousin traded you Park Place for Baltic and \"a favor later,\" and somehow that's the most binding contract in your family. Nobody's ever finished a game. It ends the same way every year: someone flips the board, someone cries in jail, and Grandma pockets the Get Out of Jail Free card like she's been planning it since Nixon."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0066", "lang": "en", "target_id": "php", "target_name": "PHP", "facts": ["Created by Rasmus Lerdorf in 1994 as 'Personal Home Page Tools', a set of C programs for tracking visitors to his own résumé page; the name now officially stands for the recursive 'PHP: Hypertext Preprocessor'.", "Lerdorf, in his 2007 MySQL Conference talk 'PHP on Hormones': 'I'm not a real programmer. I throw together things until it works then I move on.'", "Runs WordPress, which W3Techs counted on 39.4% of all websites (58.1% of sites with a known CMS) on 9 November 2026.", "Core function names are inconsistent (strpos, str_replace, htmlspecialchars, nl2br) partly because, as Lerdorf confirmed, early function names were chosen to balance a hash table keyed on string length.", "Until PHP 8 (2020), the comparison 0 == 'foo' was true because the string was silently converted to a number."], "a": {"item_id": "da336777c19f", "text": "Let's hear it for the people who write PHP for a living. They've got a dollar sign tattooed on their soul, and they still say \"it's a lot better now\" with a straight face, like a hostage defending the guy holding him. Ask one why they stayed and they'll tell you the shared hosting is cheap and every client has a cousin who needs a website. Ask what they're really after and it's the moment someone on Reddit admits Laravel is \"actually pretty good.\" They've memorized which argument goes first in in_array and they're proud of it. They have a folder called \"legacy\" that's older than their marriage, and they open it on Fridays for fun. Respect."}, "b": {"item_id": "5aafe23afc0f", "text": "PHP developers are the only engineers who've been told their career is dead every year since 2009 and kept invoicing anyway. Your entire résumé is \"fixed a WordPress plugin for a dentist.\" You put a dollar sign in front of every variable because it's the closest you'll get to one. You still can't remember if it's needle-haystack or haystack-needle, and neither could the language, so you Google it daily like a man checking which door says \"push.\" Laravel devs, you're not better. You're PHP in a Patagonia vest. And you all still say \"Facebook was built on PHP,\" like a guy bragging his ex married a billionaire. You deploy over FTP, you hotfix live on production, and when someone at a party asks what you do, you say \"backend\" and pray they don't ask which."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0067", "lang": "en", "target_id": "microsoft-word", "target_name": "Microsoft Word", "facts": ["Launched in 1983 as 'Multi-Tool Word' for Xenix and MS-DOS.", "Clippy, the animated paperclip assistant, arrived in Office 97 and was removed in Office 2007; Microsoft itself ran ads mocking it in 2001.", "Its .docx format, Office Open XML, was fast-tracked to ISO standard 29500 in 2008 as a roughly 6,000-page specification, amid accusations that national committees had been packed.", "Older spell checkers, Word's included, offered 'Cupertino' as a correction for a mistyped 'cooperation', and the resulting 'Cupertino effect' made it into published NATO, EU and UN-agency documents."], "a": {"item_id": "8af5ead45179", "text": "You chose Word for your thesis because you \"didn't have time to learn LaTeX.\" Beautiful. Instead you learned section breaks, by force, at 3 a.m., trying to make page numbers switch from Roman to Arabic without page one of Chapter 2 becoming page xvii. You wanted to spend your time on research. Instead you spend it dragging a figure half an inch and watching four chapters collapse like a Jenga tower built by your enemies. Your committee will open the PDF and see \"Error! Reference source not found.\" in the middle of your key finding, which, honestly, is a fair summary of your methodology. Your folder contains thesis_FINAL_v9_REALfinal_USE_THIS.docx, because you are a scientist who trusts nothing except filenames. Five years of work, and your proudest skill is knowing which heading style secretly isn't Heading 2."}, "b": {"item_id": "1bc74d6b8981", "text": "Ah, the thesis typesetters. Four years of research, and the final week is spent discovering that Heading 2 and Heading 3 are secretly different shades of Times New Roman. You aren't writing anymore. You're toggling paragraph marks at page 214, hunting a rogue section break like a bomb tech who forgot which wire. You've renumbered Chapter 3 six times because the cross-references keep saying \"Error! Reference source not found.\" You want a table of contents that updates itself, and you're clicking Update Field on the wrong field. Your reward is a 300-page PDF whose List of Figures reads \"No table of figures entries found,\" and you tell your advisor it's a formatting quirk. Your advisor has also used Word. Your advisor is scrolling to the margins right now."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0068", "lang": "en", "target_id": "nuclear-fusion", "target_name": "Nuclear fusion power", "facts": ["Has been 'about 30 years away' since the 1950s; at the 1955 Geneva atoms-for-peace conference, Homi Bhabha predicted fusion power within two decades.", "ITER, agreed in 2006 for first plasma in 2016, re-baselined in 2024 to start research operations in 2034 and deuterium-tritium burning in 2039, at a cost estimated above €20 billion.", "NIF's December 2022 'ignition' shot released 3.15 MJ from 2.05 MJ of laser energy; the lasers drew about 300 MJ from the grid to fire.", "Private fusion companies had raised more than $7 billion by 2024."], "a": {"item_id": "f84181eac144", "text": "Let's hear it for the fusion people, the only scientists who've been \"ten years away\" since my grandfather had hair. You can hold plasma hotter than the sun for a few seconds, but you can't hold a deadline for one decade. Every press release: \"Net energy gain!\" Sure, if you don't count the electricity bill for the lasers. That's like me saying I'm profitable if you ignore rent. ITER got approved when Myspace was cool, and it's still assembling the donut. And the startups: \"We're different, we have venture capital.\" Great, now the sun is ten years away *and* has a pitch deck. Honestly, it's a beautiful racket: promise infinite clean energy, collect the grant, retire before anyone plugs in a toaster. You're not building a star. You're building a pension."}, "b": {"item_id": "d77192f17243", "text": "Fusion scientists are the only people who can say \"ten years away\" and get a standing ovation, because nobody in the room was alive for the last ten years of promises. They've been ten years away since Eisenhower. ITER has been \"under construction\" longer than most of its engineers have been married. The startup founders have a slide deck with a glowing donut on it and a Series C that needs a press release before Christmas. Their investors aren't buying energy. They're buying the feeling of being in the room when the sun gets bottled. And the politicians show up in hard hats, cut a ribbon in front of a magnet, and fly home. Nobody's getting rich off fusion. They're getting tenure, a TED slot, and a career spent being almost there."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0069", "lang": "en", "target_id": "brasilia", "target_name": "Brasília", "facts": ["Inaugurated on 21 April 1960 after about 41 months of construction, moving the capital inland from Rio de Janeiro.", "Lúcio Costa's Plano Piloto is laid out like an airplane (or a bird, or a cross), with Oscar Niemeyer's government buildings along the fuselage.", "Listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1987, less than 30 years after it was built.", "Planned for 500,000 residents; the Federal District now has about 3 million, most in satellite towns outside the plan.", "Built for the car: wide avenues, few pedestrian crossings, and distances that make walking impractical."], "a": {"item_id": "cfeb728094dd", "text": "You're the guy walking from the Rodoviária to the Esplanada in July, backpack on, telling everyone \"it's only four kilometers.\" Four kilometers in a city built for helicopters. You chose the walk because you wanted to feel like a person in a place that forgot people exist. Your fitness tracker buzzes with pride while your shirt turns into a bath towel. You crossed the Eixão at a red light like a cowboy, then spent twenty minutes in an underpass hunting for the exit to a superquadra you swore was right there. Every car honks like it's doing you a favor. You arrive at the ministry looking like a wet sandwich, and you call it exercise."}, "b": {"item_id": "7d68bd3bc869", "text": "You saw the Congress from the rodoviária and said, \"It's right there.\" It is right there. It has been right there for forty minutes. Every step you take, it stays exactly the same size, like a mirage that went to architecture school.\n\nYou came to appreciate the modernist vision, and you're appreciating it from the median of the Eixão, holding a phone at 4% battery, asking a stranger where the \"corner\" is. There are no corners. That was the point. You are the point's victim.\n\nYou cut across a lawn because the real path is a mile of detour. Congratulations: you've joined the city's only grassroots planning committee, a dirt line worn by a thousand other optimists.\n\nHumidity is 11%, your nose is bleeding, and your reward is a ministry building identical to the last six.\n\nJust take the Uber, tourist. Even the drivers stop at the crosswalk for you out of pity."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0070", "lang": "en", "target_id": "juicero", "target_name": "Juicero", "facts": ["A Wi-Fi-connected cold-press juicer launched in 2016 at $699, later cut to $399, that squeezed proprietary bags of chopped produce sold by subscription.", "Raised about $120 million from investors including Google Ventures and Kleiner Perkins.", "In April 2017, Bloomberg reporters squeezed the packs by hand and got 7.5 ounces of juice in 90 seconds, faster than the machine's 8 ounces in about two minutes.", "Each pack carried a QR code that the press scanned and checked online before squeezing; it refused expired packs and would not run at all unless connected to Wi-Fi.", "Shut down in September 2017 and offered refunds on the machine."], "a": {"item_id": "cdfa0d41c93b", "text": "The people who bought a Juicero weren't buying juice. They were buying the feeling of being someone with a juice situation. You know the type. They wanted to be a wellness person without the bother of actually squeezing anything, so they paid four hundred dollars to outsource the squeezing to a machine. Then the reporters showed up and squeezed the little packets by hand, and it turned out the machine was just a very expensive hand. The buyers were left holding a four-hundred-dollar receipt for their own fingers. And when the company folded, they still had the thing. Still on the counter. Still doing nothing. Like a trophy for a sport they quit on day one, with a little Wi-Fi light blinking at them every morning like it was disappointed too."}, "b": {"item_id": "a22f3d4810e1", "text": "You're the guy who read \"Bloomberg shows you can squeeze the pouch with your bare hands\" and thought, \"Sure, but can my hands connect to WiFi?\" You paid $400 to outsource the one step of juicemaking a toddler can do. You didn't want juice. You wanted to feel like a Series B. You wanted a dinner guest to ask, \"Is that the Juicero?\" so you could say, \"It's backed by Google Ventures,\" like that's a nutrient.\n\nAnd you paid seven dollars a pouch for pre-chopped kale with an expiration date, because you can't be trusted to smell things.\n\nWhen the company folded, you didn't lose a juicer. You lost your seat at the table where adults say \"disrupting breakfast\" with a straight face.\n\nNow you squeeze the pouch over a glass like a Capri Sun, doing the work your $400 was supposed to prevent. Cheers. Full circle."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0071", "lang": "en", "target_id": "keurig", "target_name": "Keurig", "facts": ["John Sylvan invented the K-Cup in the early 1990s and sold his stake for about $50,000 in 1997; in 2015 he told The Atlantic, 'I feel bad sometimes that I ever did it.'", "Keurig 2.0 (2014) read an ink mark on the lid and rejected unlicensed pods; sales fell and workarounds spread online.", "By 2014 enough K-Cups were sold each year to circle the Earth more than ten times; the pods were mostly unrecyclable #7 plastic until a switch to polypropylene by 2020.", "Keurig Green Mountain merged with Dr Pepper Snapple in 2018 to form Keurig Dr Pepper."], "a": {"item_id": "9c5b82448851", "text": "Keurig people. You wanted coffee but couldn't commit to a whole pot, like a man ordering one buffalo wing. You pay forty dollars a pound for grounds, as long as they arrive in a thimble you throw away. You bought the variety pack, drank every Breakfast Blend in a week, and now there are eleven hot cocoas and a chai you're saving for the apocalypse. You press \"Strong\" like you're negotiating with it. The guy who invented the pod says he regrets it. You don't. You display your pods on a spinning carousel like fine cigars, and the one time you peeled the foil off to recycle one, you told people about it for a month. You didn't want coffee. You wanted to feel like a hotel guest in your own kitchen. Congratulations: it tastes exactly like that."}, "b": {"item_id": "a034e62fdf17", "text": "You said you don't have time to grind beans, then spent ten minutes hunting for a pod in a drawer that's half foil lids and half regret. You bought the 72-count variety pack because you \"like options,\" and now you're drinking Hazelnut Creme Brûlée Cheesecake at 6 a.m. like a toddler who got the dessert menu. You pay fifty cents a cup for coffee that tastes like a hostage video: weak, brown, and apologizing. You press the \"strong\" button, which does nothing, and you believe in it anyway, like a guy pushing a crosswalk button.\n\nThe man who invented the K-Cup says he regrets it. The inventor. And you're still in the kitchen peeling foil off tiny plastic cups like you're losing at a very small lottery.\n\nYou bought the reusable filter to feel better about the landfill. It lives in a drawer now, with the gym membership and the sourdough starter."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0072", "lang": "en", "target_id": "papyrus", "target_name": "Papyrus", "facts": ["Hand-drawn by Chris Costello in 1982 over about six months, when he was 23; he sold the rights to Letraset for $750.", "Bundled with macOS and with Microsoft Office, which is why it appears on so many menus and signs.", "James Cameron's Avatar (2009) used it for the film's logo and subtitles.", "A 2017 Saturday Night Live sketch starring Ryan Gosling is about a man unable to get over that choice."], "a": {"item_id": "ebcdad06fe33", "text": "You opened a day spa. You wanted serenity, mystery, the ancient wisdom of the East. So you opened Microsoft Word and scrolled until something felt like a cucumber on your eyelid. Congratulations: your sanctuary now shares branding with a movie about blue cat people, a sixth-grade report on the Nile, and every church bake sale since 1998. Ryan Gosling lost sleep over James Cameron doing this. You did it for \"Serenity Nails & Wax\" and slept fine.\n\nAnd the Mediterranean bistro owner who asked, \"How do I show my hummus is authentic?\" then picked the font that came free with a Dell. Nothing says \"Grandmother's recipe\" like your nephew's laptop.\n\nYou wanted exotic. You got pre-installed. Nobody relaxes at your spa. They're too busy remembering which school project they typed it on."}, "b": {"item_id": "50326144f6b1", "text": "You had every font in the world available, and you picked the one that says \"authentic\" the way a gift shop pyramid says \"Egypt.\" You wanted ancient wisdom. You got a sign that tells customers your hot stone massage was learned from a video.\n\nYou're the owner who said \"my nephew says it looks organic\" and considered that a design process. You're the guy whose \"Mediterranean-inspired\" menu includes a wrap. You wanted to be exotic, and you ended up in the same category as a quarter-billion-dollar blockbuster that got publicly roasted on SNL by Ryan Gosling, who played a man in actual tears over this exact decision.\n\nEveryone else in your strip mall hired a designer. You hired a feeling. Your five-star review says \"clean,\" which is what people write when they're being kind about the sign."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0073", "lang": "en", "target_id": "excel", "target_name": "Microsoft Excel", "facts": ["First shipped for the Macintosh in 1985; the Windows version followed in 1987.", "Silently converts gene names such as SEPT2 and MARCH1 into dates; by August 2020 the HUGO Gene Nomenclature Committee had renamed 27 human genes (MARCH1 became MARCHF1, SEPT2 became SEPTIN2) and published guidelines in Nature Genetics to stop it.", "In October 2020, Public Health England lost 15,841 COVID-19 cases when a legacy .xls file hit its 65,536-row limit.", "Reinhart and Rogoff's 2010 paper 'Growth in a Time of Debt', cited for austerity policies, omitted five countries through a spreadsheet range error found by a graduate student in 2013.", "Sheets have been capped at 1,048,576 rows by 16,384 columns since Excel 2007."], "a": {"item_id": "09a718e03e88", "text": "You don't run a company on Excel. You run it on Dave. Dave built the macro in 2014, Dave left in 2017, and now you pray to a button labeled \"DO NOT CLICK.\"\n\nYou tell investors you're \"data-driven.\" You're data-hostage. Your inventory system is a file called Final_v7_REAL_USE_THIS_ONE, and it's locked because Karen opened it at lunch and went on vacation.\n\nYou said you didn't want to pay for software. Congratulations, you've paid for it in nine hours a week of someone typing \"VLOOKUP\" with the face of a hostage reading a statement.\n\nYour ops manager is proud of having forty-seven tabs. That's not a business model, that's a hoarder's garage with a color-coding system.\n\nAnd every quarter, one cell says #REF!, and the whole leadership team stares at it like it's a diagnosis."}, "b": {"item_id": "106d81fa2fcf", "text": "You run a company on Excel. Let's be clear about who you are: a guy who thinks \"enterprise software\" is a scam and \"Q3_Forecast_FINAL_v9_USE_THIS_ONE.xlsx\" is a system. You wanted control. You got Dave. Dave wrote a macro in 2014, and now Dave can never leave, die, or take a vacation, because payroll is a VLOOKUP only he understands. Your entire supply chain is one #REF! away from bankruptcy. You can't onboard a customer because the file is \"locked for editing by Karen,\" and Karen is at lunch. You saved forty grand a year on a real database and lost it all the day someone sorted column B without column C. Your org chart is merged cells. Your audit trail is yellow and slightly different yellow. And your growth strategy has a hard ceiling: 1,048,576 rows."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0074", "lang": "en", "target_id": "graphene", "target_name": "Graphene", "facts": ["Isolated in 2004 by Andre Geim and Konstantin Novoselov at Manchester by peeling graphite with Scotch tape; Nobel Prize in Physics, 2010.", "The EU's Graphene Flagship, launched in 2013, committed €1 billion over ten years.", "A 2018 study in Advanced Materials tested 'graphene' from 60 producers and found none with more than 50% graphene content; most was graphite flakes.", "Sold in tennis rackets, running shoes, and headphones as a performance ingredient.", "Geim also won an Ig Nobel Prize in 2000 for levitating a frog with magnets."], "a": {"item_id": "0a60dd7d6c08", "text": "Every graphene founder has the same pitch deck. Slide one: \"A Nobel Prize in 2010.\" Slide two: \"Five years from batteries.\" Slide three: a photo of a tennis racket, because nobody on the investor side has ever checked. Their LinkedIn says \"materials visionary.\" Their grant renewals say \"transformative.\" Their spin-out's name ends in \"-ene,\" and the logo looks like a hexagon that had a hopeful dream. They've been five years from the breakthrough since the Obama administration, and they bring up the Nobel at weddings. Meanwhile a postdoc is squinting at a sample under a microscope at 2 a.m., and the CEO is in Davos saying it will change everything. Everything has not changed. The postdoc has."}, "b": {"item_id": "5342b17b4a3e", "text": "Every graphene founder has the same pitch: \"Stronger than steel, one atom thick, and it's going to change everything.\" Wonderful. Your funding timeline is also one atom thick, and it's also not holding anything up.\n\nYou've been \"five years away\" since two guys at Manchester peeled pencil residue off with Scotch tape and won a Nobel Prize. That's the greatest return on office supplies in history, and you've spent a billion-euro Flagship budget trying to repeat it.\n\nAnd what has the revolution delivered? A tennis racket. A jacket. A keynote speaker in a lanyard saying \"paradigm shift\" over a stock photo of honeycomb. In the lab it works flawlessly, one square centimeter at a time, on a Tuesday, if nobody breathes.\n\nBut you're not fools. You figured out the material doesn't have to scale as long as the grant renewal does. You didn't discover a wonder material. You discovered \"potential,\" and it's renewable."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0075", "lang": "en", "target_id": "nuclear-fusion", "target_name": "Nuclear fusion power", "facts": ["Has been 'about 30 years away' since the 1950s; at the 1955 Geneva atoms-for-peace conference, Homi Bhabha predicted fusion power within two decades.", "ITER, agreed in 2006 for first plasma in 2016, re-baselined in 2024 to start research operations in 2034 and deuterium-tritium burning in 2039, at a cost estimated above €20 billion.", "NIF's December 2022 'ignition' shot released 3.15 MJ from 2.05 MJ of laser energy; the lasers drew about 300 MJ from the grid to fire.", "Private fusion companies had raised more than $7 billion by 2024."], "a": {"item_id": "4a10914dfbf0", "text": "Fusion scientists have been promising power in ten years since before the Beatles broke up. That's not a forecast, that's a rolling lease. The timeline is perfectly engineered: close enough to unlock the grant, far enough that the person signing the check will be retired, dead, or hosting a podcast about living forever.\n\nYou celebrated \"net energy gain\" by counting the energy the plasma gave back but not the 300 megajoules of wall power it took to fire the lasers. That's claiming you're profitable because you ignored rent, payroll, and the building.\n\nAnd the startup founders: you raised a billion dollars on a slide deck with a sun on it and the word \"inevitable.\" You say \"ten years\" with the same face my uncle uses for \"I'm good for it.\"\n\nMeanwhile, the grad student who joined your tokamak project is now a grandfather.\n\nTen years, baby. Always ten. Never nine."}, "b": {"item_id": "c5c23a966bf8", "text": "Let's hear it for the fusion people, who have been ten years away since your grandfather was ten years away. These are the only professionals who can miss a deadline for seventy straight years and get a bigger budget every time. Where else does that work? \"Sorry, honey, I'll be home for dinner in ten years.\" \"Great, here's twenty billion dollars.\"\n\nThey put the sun in a magnetic donut and act shocked when it won't sit still. ITER's been under construction so long the original welders have grandkids on the crew. And when they finally hit net gain, they celebrated like Moses with the tablets, then mumbled that it doesn't count the electricity for the lasers. That's not net gain. That's me saying I'm up at the casino if you don't count the buy-in.\n\nTen years, guys. Same as last time. Same tie, too."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0076", "lang": "en", "target_id": "beanie-babies", "target_name": "Beanie Babies", "facts": ["Launched by Ty Inc. in 1993 and sold only through small gift shops, with designs 'retired' to manufacture scarcity.", "At the height of the craze Beanie Babies accounted for about 10% of eBay's sales, and eBay's 1998 IPO filing said the site at times had over 30,000 Beanie Baby auctions running simultaneously in the quarter ending June 1998.", "In 1999 a divorcing couple divided their Beanie Baby collection one by one on a courtroom floor, in a photograph that ran worldwide.", "In 1999 Ty announced that Beanie Babies would be retired at the end of the year, then reversed itself after asking the public to vote and seeing an overwhelming vote to keep them.", "Most 'rare' Beanies that price guides valued in the thousands now sell for a few dollars."], "a": {"item_id": "0c59a1f0431d", "text": "You bought 400 of them in 1998 because a stranger at a Hallmark told you Princess the bear would put your kids through college. Your kids are now in their thirties and still paying off college.\n\nYou drove to three states for a \"retiring soon\" rumor started by a guy named Dale on a message board. You kept them in plastic cases, because your retirement plan couldn't be touched by children, and you told your actual children that, to their faces.\n\nYou didn't collect, you waited. For twenty-five years, you've been waiting for a market that was only ever you, Dale, and a divorce attorney splitting a basement.\n\nNow you've got a garage full of \"rare errors\" and an appraisal that says forty dollars. Total.\n\nTy Warner got the yacht. You got the tag protectors."}, "b": {"item_id": "1a45d6768831", "text": "Let's talk about the Beanie Baby investors, the folks who bought eleven Peanut the Elephants and never let a child within arm's reach of one. You know the type. He's got a binder. Every bear has a hang tag, a tush tag, and a plastic sleeve, and he inspects the tush tag for misprints like it's the Dead Sea Scrolls. He says \"retired\" the way a doctor says \"terminal.\" His retirement plan is one Princess bear and a prayer. Ty Inc. never promised him a dime, but he's still waiting for the big payout. Meanwhile his daughter sleeps with a bald rabbit named Hope, because the good stuff is sealed in the basement, appreciating, never once hugged. Congratulations. You own the most expensive animals in your house that nobody's allowed to touch."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0077", "lang": "en", "target_id": "google-glass", "target_name": "Google Glass", "facts": ["Unveiled at Google I/O 2012 with skydivers livestreaming their jump through it onto the Moscone Center roof.", "The Explorer Edition sold to invited users in 2013 for $1,500.", "Wearers were nicknamed 'Glassholes'; bars, cinemas, and casinos banned the device over its camera.", "The consumer programme ended in January 2015; an Enterprise Edition continued until Google stopped selling it in 2023.", "Its display was a 640-by-360 prism in the corner of one eye."], "a": {"item_id": "260f23e62b71", "text": "Nobody wears Google Glass to a bar unless they paid fifteen hundred dollars to be called an Explorer and need the bartender to notice. You're not here for the beer. You're here for the moment the woman by the jukebox squints and says, \"Wait, are you recording me?\" That's the conversation you've been waiting your whole life for. Meanwhile you can't hear her, because you're reading a fantasy football text in the corner of your eye. You wink at the bartender to photograph the tap handles, and he photographs you back, which is the first picture anyone will ever have of you looking this lonely. You say \"OK Glass\" to the girl at the register, and she says \"Sure, buddy\" like she's talking to a smoke detector. You are a beta tester who never got the invitation to be a person."}, "b": {"item_id": "4b59aaf692b9", "text": "You paid fifteen hundred dollars to become the only man in the bar the bartender watches more closely than the drunk guy. You wanted to look like the future. You look like someone about to be asked to leave by a stranger already hiding behind a pillar.\n\nYou walk in and say \"OK, Glass\" to a room that never asked, then repeat it louder over the jukebox. Nobody's impressed. Nobody's wondering what you're building. They're wondering if they're in your footage. It's the most attention you've ever gotten, and it's fear.\n\nYou wanted conversation starters. You got \"Are you recording me?\" and you said \"No,\" and then you winked. With you, that's a confession.\n\nGo home, Explorer. The only thing you've discovered is how fast a bar empties."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0078", "lang": "en", "target_id": "las-vegas", "target_name": "Las Vegas", "facts": ["Nevada legalized casino gambling in 1931, the year construction of the Hoover Dam began nearby.", "Most of the Strip lies not in the city of Las Vegas but in the unincorporated town of Paradise.", "The Sphere, opened in 2023 at a cost of about $2.3 billion, is the largest spherical building in the world.", "'What happens here, stays here' was launched by the local convention and visitors authority in 2003.", "Lake Mead, its water source, fell in July 2022 to about 1,040 feet, its lowest since the reservoir first filled in 1937, and dropped below that mark again in August 2026."], "a": {"item_id": "4f281fa0f753", "text": "Every year a guy drives eight hours from Bakersfield with two hundred dollars and a system. He got the system from a forum run by a man who also sells the system. He's not coming to have fun. He's coming to turn two hundred into a down payment on a boat. Forty minutes later he's at a blackjack table next to a woman in a sequined visor who's been feeding the same slot machine since Tuesday, and she's the only relaxed person in the building. He's down to forty dollars, and he calls it a learning experience, which is what everybody says right before they call their brother-in-law for a loan. Then he drives home, eight hours, very quiet, and tells his wife it was a networking trip."}, "b": {"item_id": "c0bc98e0b0fb", "text": "You flew to Vegas to get rich. Think about that. You looked at a city built on the mathematical certainty that you will lose and said, \"Yeah, but I've got a feeling.\"\n\nYou have a system, of course. The system is a YouTube video from 2011 and a lucky shirt. You're up forty dollars at blackjack and suddenly you're tipping the dealer like a man whose yacht is double-parked outside.\n\nBy 4 a.m. you're hunched at a Wheel of Fortune slot, pressing a button that does nothing, drinking a free vodka cranberry like it's a salary. You've taken out cash three times, and the ATM fee has now earned more than you have.\n\nYou'll fly home with a buffet hangover, a sunburn from a pool you were too hungover to enjoy, and a cash-out ticket for $3.47 you're too proud to redeem. And a story about how you were \"so close.\"\n\nYou came to beat the house. The house gave you a discount on the room because it knew you'd be back."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0079", "lang": "en", "target_id": "graphene", "target_name": "Graphene", "facts": ["Isolated in 2004 by Andre Geim and Konstantin Novoselov at Manchester by peeling graphite with Scotch tape; Nobel Prize in Physics, 2010.", "The EU's Graphene Flagship, launched in 2013, committed €1 billion over ten years.", "A 2018 study in Advanced Materials tested 'graphene' from 60 producers and found none with more than 50% graphene content; most was graphite flakes.", "Sold in tennis rackets, running shoes, and headphones as a performance ingredient.", "Geim also won an Ig Nobel Prize in 2000 for levitating a frog with magnets."], "a": {"item_id": "9c280b4152bc", "text": "Every graphene pitch deck opens with the same slide: a honeycomb, a stock photo of a tennis racket, and the word \"revolution\" in a font you'd use for a dentist. These are the people who got a Nobel Prize for pulling a flake of pencil lead off with sticky tape, then spent fifteen years and four investor rounds explaining why that's the future. Their LinkedIn says \"materials scientist.\" Their Series A says \"platform.\" Their fundraising emails say \"graphene-enabled,\" which is what you write when the graphene in the sunscreen is too thin to find with a microscope. They want the Nobel, the magazine cover, and a sabbatical in Tahiti. What they get is one sentence in a press release that starts \"Experts say.\""}, "b": {"item_id": "5342b17b4a3e", "text": "Every graphene founder has the same pitch: \"Stronger than steel, one atom thick, and it's going to change everything.\" Wonderful. Your funding timeline is also one atom thick, and it's also not holding anything up.\n\nYou've been \"five years away\" since two guys at Manchester peeled pencil residue off with Scotch tape and won a Nobel Prize. That's the greatest return on office supplies in history, and you've spent a billion-euro Flagship budget trying to repeat it.\n\nAnd what has the revolution delivered? A tennis racket. A jacket. A keynote speaker in a lanyard saying \"paradigm shift\" over a stock photo of honeycomb. In the lab it works flawlessly, one square centimeter at a time, on a Tuesday, if nobody breathes.\n\nBut you're not fools. You figured out the material doesn't have to scale as long as the grant renewal does. You didn't discover a wonder material. You discovered \"potential,\" and it's renewable."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0080", "lang": "en", "target_id": "latex", "target_name": "LaTeX", "facts": ["Leslie Lamport wrote LaTeX in the early 1980s and released it in 1984 as a macro layer on Donald Knuth's TeX (1978), which Knuth built because he disliked how the galley proofs of the new edition of his own book looked.", "TeX's version number converges on π (3.141592653 since 2021), and Knuth's TeX bug bounty started at one hexadecimal dollar, $2.56, doubling yearly until frozen at $327.68; he stopped mailing real cheques in 2008 after fraud and now credits accounts at his fictional Bank of San Serriffe, and only 9 of the 275 cheques he sent from 2006 on were ever cashed.", "Its best-known warning is 'Underfull \\hbox (badness 10000)'; overfull lines are reported as 'Overfull \\hbox (Npt too wide)', and figures float to wherever the algorithm decides, not where the author put them.", "A single missing brace can produce an error reported pages away from the mistake, and a document can need three compiler passes to get its references right."], "a": {"item_id": "88a7647c2b15", "text": "Let's talk about the guy who writes a two-page memo in LaTeX. Your memo is about the break room fridge, but it's in Computer Modern, so everyone reads it expecting it to end with a theorem. \"Fridge Policy, Section 1.1.\" Buddy, there's no Section 1.2. You spent forty minutes on a preamble longer than the memo. Twelve \\usepackage lines to say \"label your yogurt.\" You compiled it three times because the cross-reference said \"see Section ??\" on page one, referencing page one. You're not after clarity. You're after someone asking, \"Is this LaTeX?\" so you can say \"Oh, habit,\" like a man who still mentions his thesis at parties. And it gets you exactly one thing: a PDF nobody can edit, so when Karen fixes a typo, she retypes the whole thing in Word."}, "b": {"item_id": "f1bceb623799", "text": "You spent four hours on a two-page memo your manager will read as an Outlook preview on his phone, where it renders as a gray rectangle. But the margins were perfect.\n\nYou're the person who says \"I just find it cleaner\" about a document with three bullet points. You grabbed a template from a 2011 math department website, and now your logistics update looks like a proof that the Q3 budget is a compact set.\n\nYou chased an overfull hbox for forty minutes, a flaw no one on Earth could see, and fixed it by changing \"Thursday\" to \"this week.\" That's not writing. That's a hostage negotiation with a comma.\n\nYou wanted \"wow, rigorous.\" They got Computer Modern and a ransom note from a topologist. Your reward: \"Can you resend as a Word doc?\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0081", "lang": "en", "target_id": "carbon-nanotubes", "target_name": "Carbon nanotubes", "facts": ["Reported by Sumio Iijima in Nature in 1991.", "Promised as the cable for a space elevator; a 2006 analysis showed that ordinary defects cut the strength of a macroscopic cable far below the ideal value.", "Long, rigid multi-walled nanotubes behaved like asbestos fibres in a 2008 mouse study in Nature Nanotechnology.", "Sorting metallic from semiconducting tubes has blocked nanotube electronics for three decades; MIT's 16-bit RV16X-NANO processor (2019) is still a lab demonstration.", "Today they are sold mainly as a conductive additive in lithium-ion battery electrodes."], "a": {"item_id": "9ad1a4c3964e", "text": "Let's hear it for the carbon nanotube founders, the only people who've been \"five years from commercialization\" since 1991. You can spot them at any pitch day: a postdoc in a blazer that still smells like the clean room, opening with a space elevator and closing with a seed round. Bold move, promising a cable to orbit when your entire production run fits in a vial labeled \"DO NOT INHALE.\" They swear they'll replace silicon. Silicon has heard this every year since Clinton and keeps shipping. They say \"a hundred times stronger than steel\" the way your uncle says he could've gone pro. And what does it get them? A Nature cover, a term sheet, and a quiet pivot to \"conductive additive for batteries.\" The dream: a ladder to space. The exit: slightly better tennis rackets."}, "b": {"item_id": "a9505577820b", "text": "Every carbon nanotube pitch opens with a slide calling it the strongest material ever made, then a space elevator, then a hockey-stick graph that hits commercial scale in 2031. The founder is a former postdoc who spent three years coaxing one gram out of a black hairball and now calls the hairball \"proprietary dispersion.\" They want your money to build a factory that will produce exactly enough for a demo vial. What it gets them: a Series A, a TED talk, and a lifetime of saying \"we're basically in pilot\" at dinner parties. You get a framed bucky-paper certificate and the promise that the only obstacle left is \"just manufacturing.\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0082", "lang": "en", "target_id": "google-glass", "target_name": "Google Glass", "facts": ["Unveiled at Google I/O 2012 with skydivers livestreaming their jump through it onto the Moscone Center roof.", "The Explorer Edition sold to invited users in 2013 for $1,500.", "Wearers were nicknamed 'Glassholes'; bars, cinemas, and casinos banned the device over its camera.", "The consumer programme ended in January 2015; an Enterprise Edition continued until Google stopped selling it in 2023.", "Its display was a 640-by-360 prism in the corner of one eye."], "a": {"item_id": "b9dec6f155dd", "text": "Nothing says \"I'm approachable\" like paying fifteen hundred dollars to be a beta tester. You're not an early adopter, you're an unpaid Google intern who also buys his own drinks.\n\nYou came here to be noticed, and it worked, the way a guy who says \"I'm not a cop\" gets noticed. The bartender isn't asking what you're having, he's asking if you're recording. The woman you wanted to impress is explaining to her friend what a \"Glasshole\" is.\n\nThen you said \"OK Glass\" to a crowded room, waited, said it louder, and the only one who responded was the bouncer.\n\nYou wanted to look like the future. Instead you look like the first guy the future gets asked to leave."}, "b": {"item_id": "abe9296c28ae", "text": "Look at this guy. Paid fifteen hundred dollars to join Google's \"Explorer Program,\" and the only thing he's explored is the sidewalk outside every bar that bounced him. He walks in thinking he's Tony Stark and walks out on a list, because nobody can tell if he's filming, and honestly, neither can he. He's here to meet women, and he gets results: every conversation ends the second he says \"OK Glass.\" Nothing kills the mood like a man asking his own face for directions to her place. He wanted to be the future. Instead the internet had to invent a brand-new word just for him: Glasshole. Silicon Valley promised to connect him to everything. It couldn't connect him to a second date, but it did get him his very own slur."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0083", "lang": "en", "target_id": "windows-8", "target_name": "Windows 8", "facts": ["Released in October 2012; removed the Start button and booted into a full-screen grid of tiles designed for touch screens most PCs did not have.", "The 8.1 update in 2013 put a Start button back, Windows 10 (2015) restored the Start menu, and Microsoft skipped the name Windows 9 altogether.", "Microsoft dropped the 'Metro' name in August 2012 after reports that German retailer Metro AG had objected on trademark grounds; Microsoft called 'Metro' a code name and denied any litigation, and the style was afterwards known informally as 'Modern UI'.", "The 'Charms bar' hid Search, Share, and Settings behind an invisible swipe from the right edge.", "Windows RT, the ARM edition launched alongside it, could not run ordinary Windows programs; Microsoft wrote down $900 million of unsold Surface RT tablets in 2013."], "a": {"item_id": "f8677486c44b", "text": "You put Windows 8 on a desktop. A tower. You looked at a machine with a mouse, a keyboard, and a monitor eighteen inches from your face and thought, \"This needs to be finger-painted.\" Who are you? You're the guy who bought a Zune and still calls it \"ahead of its time.\" You wanted to feel like the future. Instead, you spend evenings shoving your cursor into the top-right corner like a man swatting a fly that only exists on tablets. Your weather app is full-screen. Your 27-inch monitor is now a clock tile. When 8.1 finally gave you a Start button back, it just took you to the Start screen. So, inevitably, you installed Classic Shell. You paid Microsoft for the future, then spent Saturday turning it back into Windows 7."}, "b": {"item_id": "a78f1ebf5710", "text": "Let's hand a medal to the brave souls still running Windows 8 on a desktop. You looked at a perfectly good monitor and thought, what it's missing is a Start screen full of tiles. You wanted the Charms bar, that secret strip you summon by swiping in from the right edge, so you could find Shutdown like you're opening a spy drawer. You keep telling people, \"I'm fine with the tiles.\" Nobody asked about the tiles. Your Solitaire is a live tile now, and it's been live since 2012. You're waiting for the Start button to return the way the rest of us wait for a bus cancelled in 2013. Then a coworker asks why your desktop looks like a phone that lost an argument with a fridge, and you say, \"It's a choice.\" It is a choice. You make it every morning, and the tiles judge you quietly."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0084", "lang": "en", "target_id": "php", "target_name": "PHP", "facts": ["Created by Rasmus Lerdorf in 1994 as 'Personal Home Page Tools', a set of C programs for tracking visitors to his own résumé page; the name now officially stands for the recursive 'PHP: Hypertext Preprocessor'.", "Lerdorf, in his 2007 MySQL Conference talk 'PHP on Hormones': 'I'm not a real programmer. I throw together things until it works then I move on.'", "Runs WordPress, which W3Techs counted on 39.4% of all websites (58.1% of sites with a known CMS) on 9 November 2026.", "Core function names are inconsistent (strpos, str_replace, htmlspecialchars, nl2br) partly because, as Lerdorf confirmed, early function names were chosen to balance a hash table keyed on string length.", "Until PHP 8 (2020), the comparison 0 == 'foo' was true because the string was silently converted to a number."], "a": {"item_id": "5aafe23afc0f", "text": "PHP developers are the only engineers who've been told their career is dead every year since 2009 and kept invoicing anyway. Your entire résumé is \"fixed a WordPress plugin for a dentist.\" You put a dollar sign in front of every variable because it's the closest you'll get to one. You still can't remember if it's needle-haystack or haystack-needle, and neither could the language, so you Google it daily like a man checking which door says \"push.\" Laravel devs, you're not better. You're PHP in a Patagonia vest. And you all still say \"Facebook was built on PHP,\" like a guy bragging his ex married a billionaire. You deploy over FTP, you hotfix live on production, and when someone at a party asks what you do, you say \"backend\" and pray they don't ask which."}, "b": {"item_id": "453c78fa8180", "text": "Let's hear it for the PHP developers. You chose a language with a dollar sign on every variable, and you did it on purpose. Nobody stumbles into that. You saw that `array_search` takes its arguments in the opposite order from `in_array`, shrugged, and said, \"Honestly, I respect the chaos.\" Now you say \"modern PHP\" with a straight face, like you aren't still babysitting a WordPress site held together by a plugin called `mega_fix_final_v2`. Your dream is to be the one person who knows where the bodies are buried in a 900,000-line codebase, and you're proud of it. Your LinkedIn says \"Laravel enthusiast.\" Your weekends are spent on a staging server. And somewhere a recruiter is paying you more than the Rust guy, and you still feel a little guilty about it."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0085", "lang": "en", "target_id": "crocs", "target_name": "Crocs", "facts": ["Founded in Boulder, Colorado, in 2002; the first clog debuted at the Fort Lauderdale boat show as a boating shoe.", "Made of 'Croslite', which Crocs describes as a proprietary closed-cell resin foam and which third parties identify as an injection-moulded EVA foam.", "Balenciaga sold a platform Crocs for about $850 in 2018.", "The stock fell more than 90% from its 2007 peak during the 2008 crisis; the company then boomed in the pandemic and gave away 860,000 pairs to healthcare workers in 2020.", "The holes are officially 'ventilation ports' and the charms that go in them are 'Jibbitz', a company Crocs bought in 2006."], "a": {"item_id": "d220d23f1394", "text": "You wear Crocs out of the house because you've decided comfort is a personality, and the whole Whole Foods needs to know. You didn't give up on fashion, you held a press conference about giving up. \"I don't care what people think,\" you tell a stranger in the cereal aisle, unprompted, which is caring what people think with extra steps.\n\nYou flip the heel strap forward for \"sport mode,\" as if you're about to sprint somewhere other than the Target parking lot. You say \"nurses wear them,\" and you're an accountant named Dale. You bought your kid a matching pair so you're not the only one, which is less parenting than hostage-taking.\n\nWhat it gets you: a stranger's sympathetic nod, a lifelong inability to be taken seriously at a wedding, and the smug glow of winning an argument nobody was having."}, "b": {"item_id": "820d0b21e9b5", "text": "People who wear Crocs in public want you to know they've stopped trying, but they also want credit for it. That's why they buy the Jibbitz. Nothing says \"I don't care what you think\" like spending forty dollars on tiny rubber tacos so everyone knows exactly what you think. Then there's the strap. You flipped it back into \"Sport Mode,\" which is a bold claim from a man walking into Applebee's. Your nurse friend wears them because she stands for twelve-hour shifts. You wear them for a twelve-minute Target run, hoping to borrow a little of her dignity. And what does it get you? Comfort, sure. Plus a lifetime of strangers asking if you're coming off a shift. You're not, Kyle. You're coming off a nap."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0086", "lang": "en", "target_id": "ugg", "target_name": "UGG boots", "facts": ["Sheepskin boots Australian surfers wore to warm up after sessions; Brian Smith started selling them in California in 1978.", "Deckers Outdoor bought the brand in 1995 and holds 'UGG' as a trademark in the US and elsewhere; in Australia, 'ugg' is a generic word any bootmaker may use.", "In 2019, a US jury found an Australian maker liable for selling boots labelled 'ugg' into the United States.", "Oprah Winfrey featured them in her 'Favorite Things' lists repeatedly in the 2000s, which drove the first boom.", "UGG is Deckers' largest brand, with over $2 billion in annual sales."], "a": {"item_id": "1531edc90308", "text": "You wear UGGs in July. In Phoenix. Respect. Most people need a therapist to reach that level of denial, and you just needed a mall.\n\nYou're not cold. Nobody's cold. Your feet are poaching in a dead sheep's final revenge, and you call it \"cozy.\" That's not cozy, that's a slow cooker with a ponytail.\n\nWhat are you after? Fall. You want autumn so badly you're dragging it into June like a hostage. You've been holding the same pumpkin spice latte since 2019.\n\nAnd what does it get you? A private, humid sauna for each foot. A smell that enters rooms four seconds before you do. And a look that says \"I'm wearing denim shorts and sheepskin,\" which is two different seasons arguing.\n\nYour boyfriend isn't holding your hand in public. He's holding his breath."}, "b": {"item_id": "8e467854d2d5", "text": "Some of you wear UGGs in July, and I respect a person who has never once been told no. You're not dressing for the weather. You're dressing for 2006. Somewhere in your heart, a paparazzi photo of Paris Hilton is still loading. You want people to think \"cozy.\" People think \"sleepover or custody hearing?\" And the walk: your ankles gave up years ago and went horizontal. You're not walking anymore, you're leaning toward your destination. Australian surfers wore these to warm up after the freezing ocean. You wear them to warm up after the Target parking lot. In August. Your feet sweat so hard the sheep is finally getting its revenge. And the second it drizzles, you're frozen under an awning like a cat who's been threatened with a bath."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0087", "lang": "en", "target_id": "almond-milk", "target_name": "Almond milk", "facts": ["California grows about 80% of the world's almonds.", "A single almond takes about 1.1 US gallons (roughly 4 litres) of water to grow, a figure Mother Jones published in 2014 that spread during California's drought.", "A 2015 US class action alleged Almond Breeze contained only about 2% almonds; it partly survived a motion to dismiss, was stayed in 2017 pending a separate Blue Diamond labelling settlement, and the plaintiffs then dropped it.", "Pollinating California's almond orchards each February requires trucking in most of the commercial honeybee colonies in the United States.", "Medieval European cooks used almond milk as a Lenten substitute for dairy, centuries before the current fashion."], "a": {"item_id": "f6708fabd3f6", "text": "You switched to almond milk to save the planet. Bold move, picking the one crop that drinks California like it's an open bar. Every latte you order, a farmer in the Central Valley watches his well go dry and thinks, \"At least Kayleigh's being sustainable.\"\n\nAnd you didn't save a cow. You just outsourced the suffering to bees, trucked cross-country every February to work themselves to death in an orchard, while you post about dairy being cruel.\n\nYou're not after a healthier Earth. You're after the barista hearing you say it. That little pause before \"almond, please\" is the biggest environmental impact you've ever had.\n\nAnd now the oat milk people look at you the way you used to look at skim drinkers. You picked a side in the milk war, and you lost."}, "b": {"item_id": "cf146e52ac24", "text": "You switched to almond milk to save the planet, which is adorable, because you did it the way you do everything: you read one headline about cows and stopped there. Meanwhile, almonds drink California's drought like a frat boy at a keg, and the bees get trucked in like seasonal labor so you can feel superior in a Tuesday latte. You didn't save the planet. You outsourced your guilt to a state that's on fire.\n\nAnd you told people. Nobody asked, Brent. You announced it at brunch like you'd donated a kidney. You pay an extra dollar fifty for a carton that's about 2% almonds, so you're basically funding a rumor of a nut. Then you flew to Tulum for your birthday.\n\nNow you've heard oat is the responsible choice, so you're quietly converting, and by spring you'll act like you were never on almond's side. That's not moral growth. That's a subscription to guilt with a new flavor every year."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0088", "lang": "en", "target_id": "comic-sans", "target_name": "Comic Sans", "facts": ["Designed by Vincent Connare at Microsoft in 1994, modeled on the comic-book lettering in The Dark Knight Returns and Watchmen.", "Made for the speech bubbles of Rover, the cartoon dog in Microsoft Bob; it missed Bob's release and shipped instead in the Windows 95 Plus! pack.", "CERN's July 2012 Higgs boson discovery announcement was presented on slides set in it.", "The 'Ban Comic Sans' campaign, started by designers Dave and Holly Combs in 1999, ran until 2019, when they retired it and launched usecomicsans.com."], "a": {"item_id": "4b3978a4ee09", "text": "Somebody in this building printed \"URGENT: FIRE DRILL MANDATORY\" in Comic Sans and taped it to the break room fridge. I want that person to know we all saw it and we all laughed, though not the way they hoped. They wanted the fire marshal's authority. What they got was a note that looks like a kindergarten teacher wrote it right after finding out about the fire. They want to be taken seriously while seeming approachable, like wearing a tuxedo with light-up sneakers. They think the wobbly letters say \"we're a team.\" Nobody reads it that way. We read it as a birthday party for the fire drill, with cake in the parking lot. Next time, pick a plain font and own being in charge. Nobody has ever evacuated a building faster because the notice looked cheerful."}, "b": {"item_id": "6867518453d2", "text": "You're the office manager who wrote \"Parking permits will now be enforced\" and thought, this needs to feel like a hug. You wanted approachable. You wanted everyone to know you're not a monster, you're Deb, you bring bundt cake. And it worked! Nobody's afraid of you. Nobody's afraid of the notice either.\n\nThe landlord who slides \"Rent is 30 days late\" under the door in Comic Sans isn't collecting rent. He's opening negotiations. The HR rep announcing layoffs in it isn't softening the blow, she's making a grown man feel like he got fired by the cartoon dog from Microsoft Bob. And the HOA president threatening fines over bin placement? Congratulations, you've made a tyrant look like a substitute teacher on movie day.\n\nYou wanted warmth and authority and picked the one choice that guarantees neither. CERN did it for the Higgs boson and still hasn't lived it down, and they had a particle going for them. You've got a fish-in-the-microwave memo."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0089", "lang": "en", "target_id": "emacs", "target_name": "Emacs", "facts": ["Began in 1976 as a set of macros for the TECO editor at MIT's AI Lab, written by Richard Stallman and others; GNU Emacs followed in 1985.", "Extensible in its own Lisp dialect, Emacs Lisp; the standing joke is that it is a fine operating system lacking only a good editor.", "Ships with a psychotherapist (M-x doctor), Tetris, a mail client, a calendar, and a web browser (eww).", "An old backronym is 'Eight Megabytes And Constantly Swapping', from when 8 MB was a lot of memory.", "'Emacs pinky' is the name for the hand strain blamed on its Control-key chords."], "a": {"item_id": "59215bf985f1", "text": "You people don't use Emacs, you move into it, like a guy who rents a storage unit and starts getting mail there. You read email in mu4e, browse in eww, run a shell in eshell, and when you finally crack, you M-x doctor and tell a Lisp therapist your feelings. Your init.el is four thousand lines, half commented out, and you've declared Emacs bankruptcy twice: the only bankruptcy that ever made someone feel superior. You spent a weekend cutting startup from 1.8 seconds to 0.9, on a program you never close. Your left pinky is shot from holding Control, a war wound from never touching a mouse. Your whole life is in Org-mode, including a habit tracker whose only entry is \"configure habit tracker.\" And that novel you built the perfect writing environment for? It's in there too. Under TODO."}, "b": {"item_id": "18478adad1eb", "text": "You know the type. Ask where he works and he says, \"In Emacs.\" He reads his email in there, chats in there, runs his shell in there. He's left only for dentist appointments, and he checked beforehand whether the dentist had a keybinding for it. He's tried to quit exactly once. He typed C-x C-c, stared at the prompt for a while, then spent three weekends rewriting his init file so he'd never have to try again. He doesn't need a text editor. He needs a hobby that sounds like a personality, and he's got forty-one packages doing the job. Ask him what he's working on. He'll say, \"My config.\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0090", "lang": "en", "target_id": "stinky-tofu", "target_name": "Stinky tofu (臭豆腐)", "facts": ["Tofu soaked for hours to a day or two in a brine that has itself fermented for months from vegetables such as bamboo shoots and mustard greens, often with dried shrimp or meat; the smell is the brine's, not the bean's.", "Changsha's version is black, deep-fried and doused in chili; Taiwan's night-market version is golden and served with pickled cabbage; Shaoxing's, brined with amaranth stalks, is fried pale yellow or steamed with other 'stinky' pickles.", "Legend credits Wang Zhihe, a Qing-era exam candidate who left his unsold tofu too long in 1669; a Beijing fermented-bean-curd brand still carries his name.", "Street vendors are its main outlet, and the smell carries for a block."], "a": {"item_id": "5267c589868e", "text": "You're not eating stinky tofu, you're auditioning. Every tourist at that night market stall holds a styrofoam tray like a trophy and announces, \"It tastes better than it smells,\" a line you rehearsed on the flight over. You're not hungry. You wanted a story for the group chat and a promotion to \"not like other tourists.\"\n\nAnd the locals? You queued forty minutes behind a man who knows the vendor's first name, just to stand at a curb stabbing cubes with a toothpick and calling it \"authentic.\" Your date is suddenly very interested in a shop window three stalls away.\n\nThe pickled cabbage is your fiber. The chili sauce is your courage. The nod from the auntie behind the counter is the only validation you've earned all year, and you'll mention it for a decade.\n\nWhat it gets you: one photo of you mid-chew, eyes closed like it's a religious experience, and a very quiet ride home on the MRT, with an entire car of strangers suddenly deciding to stand."}, "b": {"item_id": "04135b916762", "text": "Look at him under the night market lights, eating stinky tofu like he's auditioning for a documentary about bravery. Three blocks away, people are checking their shoes. He isn't eating it for flavor. He's eating it so his friends will say \"you're so brave,\" and he can answer \"it's an acquired taste,\" which is what people say when the only thing they've acquired is a reputation. Watch him wipe the chili sauce off his wrist, pretending the pickled cabbage is his favorite part. He waited twenty minutes in line just to become the guy who did it. And when he gets home, his jacket smells like a fermentation tank, and he calls it a souvenir."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0091", "lang": "en", "target_id": "ugg", "target_name": "UGG boots", "facts": ["Sheepskin boots Australian surfers wore to warm up after sessions; Brian Smith started selling them in California in 1978.", "Deckers Outdoor bought the brand in 1995 and holds 'UGG' as a trademark in the US and elsewhere; in Australia, 'ugg' is a generic word any bootmaker may use.", "In 2019, a US jury found an Australian maker liable for selling boots labelled 'ugg' into the United States.", "Oprah Winfrey featured them in her 'Favorite Things' lists repeatedly in the 2000s, which drove the first boom.", "UGG is Deckers' largest brand, with over $2 billion in annual sales."], "a": {"item_id": "3e67204b78e6", "text": "If you wear UGGs in July, I admire the commitment. You've decided being cozy is a personality and weather is just a rumor. You'll tell me they \"regulate temperature,\" a line you memorized like a hostage reading a statement. It's 94 degrees, you're in running shorts, and your feet are poaching in their own broth.\n\nWhat are you after? You want to look like you just rolled out of bed, for $180. Effortless, with a receipt. You want it to be fall at all times, even in Phoenix, even at a pool party.\n\nAnd what does it get you? A smell that enters the room two seconds before you do. Socks you can't wear because \"the inside is the whole point.\" And the loyalty of a 2007 version of you who never left the Juicy tracksuit.\n\nCongratulations. You're the only person standing on a sidewalk you could fry an egg on, saying, \"My feet get cold.\""}, "b": {"item_id": "8e467854d2d5", "text": "Some of you wear UGGs in July, and I respect a person who has never once been told no. You're not dressing for the weather. You're dressing for 2006. Somewhere in your heart, a paparazzi photo of Paris Hilton is still loading. You want people to think \"cozy.\" People think \"sleepover or custody hearing?\" And the walk: your ankles gave up years ago and went horizontal. You're not walking anymore, you're leaning toward your destination. Australian surfers wore these to warm up after the freezing ocean. You wear them to warm up after the Target parking lot. In August. Your feet sweat so hard the sheep is finally getting its revenge. And the second it drizzles, you're frozen under an awning like a cat who's been threatened with a bath."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0092", "lang": "en", "target_id": "jackfruit", "target_name": "Jackfruit", "facts": ["The largest fruit that grows on a tree; single fruits can weigh over 40 kg.", "The national fruit of Bangladesh and the state fruit of Kerala and Tamil Nadu.", "Unripe flesh is sold as a vegan 'pulled pork' substitute, although it has only 1-2 g of protein per 100 g.", "Cutting one releases a sticky white latex, so cooks oil their knives and hands first.", "A member of the mulberry family (Moraceae), a relative of breadfruit and figs."], "a": {"item_id": "73836848e6ca", "text": "You spent two weeks in Kerala, watched a grandmother cook jackfruit the way her family has for centuries, and came home with an LLC. Congratulations, you colonized a lunch.\n\nNow you're under a tent called \"Jack's Back,\" charging fourteen dollars for \"pulled pork\" that is ninety percent your cousin's barbecue sauce and ten percent a can you opened at 6 a.m. You call it disrupting BBQ. Texas hasn't noticed.\n\nWhat does it get you? Twenty-four hundred followers. A Whole Foods cashier who says \"Oh, jackfruit!\" like a secret handshake. And one carnivore uncle at Thanksgiving who says \"not bad\" in the voice of a hostage.\n\nMeanwhile, that grandmother in Kerala is still just calling it dinner. No pitch deck. No tent. Somehow, no sauce."}, "b": {"item_id": "4062eab29108", "text": "Every jackfruit seller has the same origin story. He opened a tin of young green jackfruit in brine, felt something stir, and decided the planet needed him at a farmers market saying, \"It's basically pulled pork.\" Now he has a logo, a folding table, and a beard that's doing a lot of work. He's selling a fruit that spent its whole life as a dessert ingredient, and he's rebranded it as the guy who gets to say \"trust me, it's meat\" to people holding a paper boat of barbecue sauce. What he really wants is Thanksgiving: his uncle takes one forkful, goes quiet, and asks what cut it is. What he gets is a forty-dollar tote bag and the specific pride of convincing a tree to be a pork shoulder."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0093", "lang": "en", "target_id": "klingon", "target_name": "Klingon", "facts": ["Created by linguist Marc Okrand for Star Trek III (1984), building on a few words the actor James Doohan improvised for the 1979 film.", "Deliberately alien grammar: object-verb-subject word order and no verb 'to be', so Hamlet's line became 'taH pagh taHbe'', 'continue or not continue'.", "The Klingon Language Institute (1992) published Hamlet 'in the original Klingon'; Bing Translator added Klingon in 2013 and Duolingo in 2018.", "Okrand's 1985 Klingon Dictionary listed roughly 1,500 words, and fluent speakers number only in the dozens: 20 to 30 in 2009, 30 to 50 by 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "64aab443b4b9", "text": "Being fluent in Klingon is a big deal. You've joined an elite community of maybe forty people worldwide, and you've met all of them at the same Holiday Inn conference room. You spent years mastering a language built for a warrior race, a culture of honor, combat and blood oaths, and you use it to argue on a forum about verb suffixes. You learned the language of the fiercest fighters in the galaxy, and the last thing you fought was a lactose sensitivity.\n\nKlingon has no word for \"hello.\" The standard greeting is \"nuqneH,\" which means \"what do you want?\" Perfect for you, because nobody has ever approached you without a reason.\n\nYou told people you read *Hamlet* \"in the original Klingon.\" Brother, Shakespeare had a girlfriend. You have a bat'leth over the futon.\n\nQapla'. That means \"success,\" a word you know only in Klingon."}, "b": {"item_id": "a8c91de7e3da", "text": "Ever meet someone fluent in Klingon? Congratulations, you've found the only person who learned a language with no word for \"please\" and thought, *yes, this is my personality.* They spent years mastering a tongue built by a linguist Paramount paid to make growling official. They own Hamlet in Klingon, and they'll recite *taH pagh taHbe'* at your wedding. Ask them to pass the salt and they'll tell you asking is for the weak. Their dream is a job as a Klingon interpreter that never comes through, and their ideal date is someone who can answer \"Qapla'\" without blinking. Then you say one sentence in English, and they sigh like a warrior whose honor just got spilled on the couch."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0094", "lang": "en", "target_id": "brasilia", "target_name": "Brasília", "facts": ["Inaugurated on 21 April 1960 after about 41 months of construction, moving the capital inland from Rio de Janeiro.", "Lúcio Costa's Plano Piloto is laid out like an airplane (or a bird, or a cross), with Oscar Niemeyer's government buildings along the fuselage.", "Listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1987, less than 30 years after it was built.", "Planned for 500,000 residents; the Federal District now has about 3 million, most in satellite towns outside the plan.", "Built for the car: wide avenues, few pedestrian crossings, and distances that make walking impractical."], "a": {"item_id": "5894439abe59", "text": "You're an architecture student, a tourist, or an intern, and all three of you looked at the Congress from the Rodoviária and said, \"Oh, that's right there.\" It is not right there. It's a mirage with a dome. Forty minutes later you're crossing a lawn at noon in 12 percent humidity, quoting Niemeyer to yourself like it's a hydration plan.\n\nAnd you're proud! You even cut a little dirt shortcut through the grass, the famous caminho do desejo, the only urban planning in Brasília done by someone who actually had to get somewhere. You were walking to a ministry for one signature, and the man who signs is out on Thursdays. It's Thursday.\n\nCongratulations, pilgrim. You've experienced modernism up close: a sunburn, a nosebleed, and a guy in a Corolla honking at you at the tesourinha like you're the one who's lost."}, "b": {"item_id": "84e52e157b23", "text": "Look at the guy walking from the Asa Sul to the Rodoviária at noon, two liters of water in his backpack, wearing the sneakers he bought for a 5K he never ran. He isn't walking to get anywhere. He's walking so he can post a photo of the Cathedral with a caption about \"mindfulness.\" Every superquadra here was designed without corners, so he's been lost for forty minutes and has decided that's a spiritual experience. He crosses the Eixão through the underpass like a man who's read a book about Lúcio Costa. The bus he could have taken comes every twenty-five minutes, but he'd rather arrive drenched in sweat and certain of his own virtue than dry and on time."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0095", "lang": "en", "target_id": "stinky-tofu", "target_name": "Stinky tofu (臭豆腐)", "facts": ["Tofu soaked for hours to a day or two in a brine that has itself fermented for months from vegetables such as bamboo shoots and mustard greens, often with dried shrimp or meat; the smell is the brine's, not the bean's.", "Changsha's version is black, deep-fried and doused in chili; Taiwan's night-market version is golden and served with pickled cabbage; Shaoxing's, brined with amaranth stalks, is fried pale yellow or steamed with other 'stinky' pickles.", "Legend credits Wang Zhihe, a Qing-era exam candidate who left his unsold tofu too long in 1669; a Beijing fermented-bean-curd brand still carries his name.", "Street vendors are its main outlet, and the smell carries for a block."], "a": {"item_id": "69fa8443e946", "text": "Look at the crowd at this night market stall. Everyone here is eating stinky tofu for the same reason: so they can tell people about it later. The tourist holds the skewer out like it's a live grenade, films himself, then says \"honestly, not bad\" with the face of a man being waterboarded. The couple on a date are splitting one piece, which is romantic only if you're both willing to be the one who gets dumped first. And the teenager who has never once been hungry just needs a story where he was brave enough to chew. You're not here for the snack. You're here for twenty minutes of street cred and three days of your hair smelling like a drain. Enjoy the MRT ride back. Everyone on it already knows where you've been."}, "b": {"item_id": "5267c589868e", "text": "You're not eating stinky tofu, you're auditioning. Every tourist at that night market stall holds a styrofoam tray like a trophy and announces, \"It tastes better than it smells,\" a line you rehearsed on the flight over. You're not hungry. You wanted a story for the group chat and a promotion to \"not like other tourists.\"\n\nAnd the locals? You queued forty minutes behind a man who knows the vendor's first name, just to stand at a curb stabbing cubes with a toothpick and calling it \"authentic.\" Your date is suddenly very interested in a shop window three stalls away.\n\nThe pickled cabbage is your fiber. The chili sauce is your courage. The nod from the auntie behind the counter is the only validation you've earned all year, and you'll mention it for a decade.\n\nWhat it gets you: one photo of you mid-chew, eyes closed like it's a religious experience, and a very quiet ride home on the MRT, with an entire car of strangers suddenly deciding to stand."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0096", "lang": "en", "target_id": "microsoft-teams", "target_name": "Microsoft Teams", "facts": ["Launched in 2017, bundled at no extra charge into Office 365, and replaced Skype for Business.", "After Slack's antitrust complaint, Microsoft unbundled Teams from Office in the EU in 2023 and worldwide in 2024; the European Commission still sent a statement of objections in 2024.", "Microsoft reported 320 million monthly active users in 2023.", "Microsoft shut down consumer Skype in May 2025 and sent its users to Teams."], "a": {"item_id": "294237b8889b", "text": "Let's hear it for the CIO who chose Microsoft Teams, a decision made the way you pick ketchup packets: it came free with the order. Nobody evaluated it. It showed up inside the Office 365 license like a toy in a cereal box, and this man stood up at the all-hands and called it a \"collaboration strategy.\" Sir, the European Commission had to force Microsoft to unbundle it. You lost a negotiation to a checkbox.\n\nAnd what did he really want? The little status dot. He doesn't read your work; he reads your color. Go yellow after five minutes of actual thinking and you're on a list. Congratulations: you saved forty grand on Slack and spent it all on adults jiggling their mouse like raccoons on a treadmill. Your files are lost somewhere in SharePoint, and your morale is permanently \"Away.\""}, "b": {"item_id": "539c5a8c88ae", "text": "Let's talk about the people who picked Teams. Dave from procurement saw one demo in 2019 and never recovered. He schedules a \"quick sync\" at 4:55 on a Friday, then spends the first ten minutes asking if you can see his screen, like he's just performed a miracle. He checks his green dot like a pulse. He wants you to know he's \"Available,\" which is corporate for \"I'm watching you.\" Every channel he's made is called \"General,\" and nobody has read any of them since 2019. His reply to your message is a thumbs-up, proof he read it and has nothing to add. That's the whole job for these people: keep you present, keep you unmuted, and keep you dinging."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0097", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hsq", "target_name": "HSQ (e-beam resist)", "facts": ["Hydrogen silsesquioxane, sold by Dow Corning as FOx ('flowable oxide') and designed as a spin-on dielectric for chip interconnects; lithographers adopted it as a negative-tone resist.", "Exposure converts its cage structure into a cross-linked, silica-like network, so the developed pattern is essentially SiO2.", "Holds the resolution records for electron-beam lithography: isolated features around 5 nm, dense lines below 10 nm half-pitch, with high-contrast 'salty' NaOH/NaCl development.", "Must be kept refrigerated (Dow Corning's datasheet gives a six-month shelf life at 5 °C); the solution ages into a jelly, and its effective sensitivity shifts with processing delays because cross-linking continues after exposure.", "DuPont, which inherited Dow Corning's FOx/XR-1541 HSQ line, discontinued it and ended production at the end of 2025; labs such as EPFL's CMi switched to third-party HSQ from EM Resist or DisChem, both formulated from AQM powder."], "a": {"item_id": "884dd445a1e2", "text": "\"We're switching to HSQ for the resolution.\" Sure you are. Because your PMMA wasn't humiliating enough, you wanted a resist that's also a deadline. You're chasing sub-10 nanometer lines so you can put one SEM image in a paper, the one nice picture out of forty-one fallen-over grating samples.\n\nYour resist lives in the freezer like a tiny, expensive yogurt, and by the time you've booked the beam it has gone off anyway. You'll expose at a dose that makes the e-beam tool bill you like a hotel minibar. Then you'll develop in salt water, because apparently you wanted to cook pasta in the cleanroom.\n\nAnd what do you get? Silicon dioxide you can't strip. A permanent souvenir of your ambition, fused to the wafer forever. Congratulations, you made glass lines. Your advisor wanted devices."}, "b": {"item_id": "96896ea695a3", "text": "You're switching to HSQ for resolution. Of course you are. You have a PhD in patience and a deficit in sleep, and one paper showed someone drawing 6-nanometer lines, so now you want to be that person at group meeting. Here's what HSQ gets you: a resist that gels in the bottle if you look at it wrong and, once exposed, cures into glass that stays in your device forever. Want it gone? Good luck. That's hydrofluoric acid territory. Your dose matrix is forty chips. Your TMAH developer smells like a crime scene. Six months from now you'll be at the SEM at 2 a.m., staring at lines that have slumped into a sad little heap, swearing you'll go back to PMMA. You won't. You'll put it in your thesis as a \"lesson learned.\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0098", "lang": "en", "target_id": "brasilia", "target_name": "Brasília", "facts": ["Inaugurated on 21 April 1960 after about 41 months of construction, moving the capital inland from Rio de Janeiro.", "Lúcio Costa's Plano Piloto is laid out like an airplane (or a bird, or a cross), with Oscar Niemeyer's government buildings along the fuselage.", "Listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1987, less than 30 years after it was built.", "Planned for 500,000 residents; the Federal District now has about 3 million, most in satellite towns outside the plan.", "Built for the car: wide avenues, few pedestrian crossings, and distances that make walking impractical."], "a": {"item_id": "a98c294fb19a", "text": "Walking in Brasília is for three kinds of people: tourists who saw the Esplanada on a postcard and thought \"that looks close,\" architecture students who love Niemeyer from a safe distance, and the guy whose car is in the shop. You're not a pedestrian. You're an ant that wandered onto a blueprint. You want bread? The padaria is \"right there\" across the Eixão, so you sprint six lanes like you owe the government money. Which, let's be honest, is also your employer. Your sneakers are permanently red from the dirt trails carved by every other fool who refused to walk around a tesourinha. And on Sunday, when they close the Eixão, you finally stroll down the middle of the highway like a man who's won something. You haven't. Everyone else just stopped driving for one day."}, "b": {"item_id": "cfeb728094dd", "text": "You're the guy walking from the Rodoviária to the Esplanada in July, backpack on, telling everyone \"it's only four kilometers.\" Four kilometers in a city built for helicopters. You chose the walk because you wanted to feel like a person in a place that forgot people exist. Your fitness tracker buzzes with pride while your shirt turns into a bath towel. You crossed the Eixão at a red light like a cowboy, then spent twenty minutes in an underpass hunting for the exit to a superquadra you swore was right there. Every car honks like it's doing you a favor. You arrive at the ministry looking like a wet sandwich, and you call it exercise."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0099", "lang": "en", "target_id": "stinky-tofu", "target_name": "Stinky tofu (臭豆腐)", "facts": ["Tofu soaked for hours to a day or two in a brine that has itself fermented for months from vegetables such as bamboo shoots and mustard greens, often with dried shrimp or meat; the smell is the brine's, not the bean's.", "Changsha's version is black, deep-fried and doused in chili; Taiwan's night-market version is golden and served with pickled cabbage; Shaoxing's, brined with amaranth stalks, is fried pale yellow or steamed with other 'stinky' pickles.", "Legend credits Wang Zhihe, a Qing-era exam candidate who left his unsold tofu too long in 1669; a Beijing fermented-bean-curd brand still carries his name.", "Street vendors are its main outlet, and the smell carries for a block."], "a": {"item_id": "5be442dd726d", "text": "Look at you, standing in a night-market line everyone else is fleeing, like you're queuing to get into a fire. You followed that smell on purpose. Three blocks. Upwind. You're the only person alive who reads a warning as a recommendation.\n\nAnd you always need a witness. \"It smells like a sewer but tastes amazing!\" Yes, we know. You've said it to every foreigner you've ever met, then filmed their face gagging for content. Your entire personality is a dare you keep winning against tourists.\n\nAnd what does it get you? A paper cup, a toothpick, some pickled cabbage doing its best, and a jacket that now smells like a porta-potty at a wedding. Then you board the MRT and clear the whole car. You call that bold. The rest of us call it a hazmat incident with chili sauce."}, "b": {"item_id": "69fa8443e946", "text": "Look at the crowd at this night market stall. Everyone here is eating stinky tofu for the same reason: so they can tell people about it later. The tourist holds the skewer out like it's a live grenade, films himself, then says \"honestly, not bad\" with the face of a man being waterboarded. The couple on a date are splitting one piece, which is romantic only if you're both willing to be the one who gets dumped first. And the teenager who has never once been hungry just needs a story where he was brave enough to chew. You're not here for the snack. You're here for twenty minutes of street cred and three days of your hair smelling like a drain. Enjoy the MRT ride back. Everyone on it already knows where you've been."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0100", "lang": "en", "target_id": "microsoft-word", "target_name": "Microsoft Word", "facts": ["Launched in 1983 as 'Multi-Tool Word' for Xenix and MS-DOS.", "Clippy, the animated paperclip assistant, arrived in Office 97 and was removed in Office 2007; Microsoft itself ran ads mocking it in 2001.", "Its .docx format, Office Open XML, was fast-tracked to ISO standard 29500 in 2008 as a roughly 6,000-page specification, amid accusations that national committees had been packed.", "Older spell checkers, Word's included, offered 'Cupertino' as a correction for a mistyped 'cooperation', and the resulting 'Cupertino effect' made it into published NATO, EU and UN-agency documents."], "a": {"item_id": "374d6b6e70bc", "text": "You chose Word for your thesis because \"LaTeX has a learning curve.\" Bold, from someone who then spent a semester learning how to make page 1 start after the roman numerals. You wanted to focus on the research. Instead you're a hostage negotiator for Figure 3.2, which flees to the next page whenever you breathe on the paragraph above it. Your committee asked for one more citation, and the bibliography reflowed like a tectonic event. The phrase \"Error! Reference source not found\" appears in your thesis eleven times. Your file is called Thesis_FINAL_v9_USE_THIS_ONE.docx, and it's 400 megabytes because you pasted one chart from Excel. You came for a doctorate. You're leaving with a codependent relationship with Section Break (Next Page) and a flinch every time someone says \"Normal style.\""}, "b": {"item_id": "c46efa5460bb", "text": "You spent six years mastering quantum field theory, yet you've centered every chapter title by hitting the spacebar until it looked right. You know what Heading 1 is, the same way you know what a dentist is: you've heard good things and you're avoiding it.\n\nYou wanted a thesis that looks like scholarship. You got a 214-page file called \"thesis_FINAL_v9_actualfinal_JUNE,\" where adding one comma moves Figure 4.2 to the shadow realm. Your table of contents is typed by hand, and you'll find out at the print shop.\n\nYou've also learned what a section break is, at 2 a.m., while trying to make page numbers go roman and then Arabic. You cried. A committee member will now spend four seconds on your formatting and ask you to change the margins.\n\nYou chose Word because everyone does. LaTeX people have already told you. Twice. At parties."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0101", "lang": "en", "target_id": "esperanto", "target_name": "Esperanto", "facts": ["Published in Warsaw in 1887 by L. L. Zamenhof, a Białystok-born eye doctor, under the pen name Doktoro Esperanto, 'one who hopes'.", "Speaker estimates range from 100,000 to two million; roughly a thousand people grow up with it as a native language.", "William Shatner starred in Incubus (1966), a horror film shot entirely in Esperanto, with pronunciation Esperantists still mock.", "Its Wikipedia has over 350,000 articles, and its Duolingo course for English speakers had 1.36 million learners by July 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "09a618d282b4", "text": "Who learns Esperanto? People who've concluded the world's real problem is that nobody agreed on the grammar. They wear the green star on their lapel. They say \"Saluton!\" to the barista and watch her face do nothing. Their dream is one planet, one neutral tongue, and their first step is finding eleven other people in their city who share it. That's how they end up in a Leeds community hall with a plate of biscuits and Gerald, who has been \"nearly fluent\" since 1994. They have a pen pal in Brazil they've never met, and they write to him in flawless accusative. What does it get them? A hug from a stranger at a congress, and the quiet pride of being fluent in a language that mostly exists in other Esperantists' inboxes."}, "b": {"item_id": "5681feb1725d", "text": "Esperanto learners looked at seven thousand living languages and said, \"None of these. I want one with zero places to use it.\" You wanted to unite humanity, so you picked the one hobby guaranteed to make humanity back away slowly at parties. You signed up to talk to everyone on Earth, and now you talk to a retired Dutch engineer named Joop and a Brazilian guy who keeps sending you poems. Your big payoff is Pasporta Servo, where strangers let you crash on their couch for free, the only time a hobby this niche has ever gotten anyone into a bed. A hundred and thirty years of pushing world peace, and the crowning achievement is still William Shatner's *Incubus*. Zamenhof wanted to end war. You just wanted to be fluent in something before your cousin finishes Duolingo French."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0102", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hydrogen-cars", "target_name": "Hydrogen fuel-cell cars", "facts": ["Toyota launched the Mirai in 2014; 'mirai' means 'future' in Japanese.", "Nearly all US hydrogen filling stations are in California; Shell closed all seven of its California passenger-car stations in February 2024.", "More than 95% of the world's hydrogen is made from fossil fuels, mostly by steam-reforming natural gas.", "California pump prices reached about $36 per kilogram in 2023-24, over $200 for a Mirai fill; Toyota has handed buyers fuel cards worth $15,000.", "Mirai owners filed a class action against Toyota in July 2024 over hydrogen shortages and station outages; a second suit in 2025 sought at least $5.7 billion."], "a": {"item_id": "5d75fbe56d0a", "text": "Hydrogen car owners are the only people who looked at Tesla drivers and thought, \"Not enough range anxiety.\" You didn't buy a car; you entered a relationship with the three working pumps in California and an app that tells you which one is \"offline\" today. You wanted to save the planet. Instead you burned through Toyota's free fuel card in a year at thirty-six bucks a kilo, filling up with hydrogen made from natural gas. Congratulations, you invented a gas car with extra steps. You love saying the only emission is water. Mostly it's your tears at a shuttered Shell station in Fremont. And resale? Your Mirai lost value faster than your friendships after you said \"hydrogen economy\" at brunch. You're not early adopters. You're unpaid beta testers who drive two hours to refuel."}, "b": {"item_id": "d1233bdc49cf", "text": "You bought a hydrogen car because a regular electric car was too easy. You needed a commute with plot. Everyone else plugs in at home while they sleep, but you drive forty minutes to the one station in Torrance, find the pump broken, and drive forty more to Long Beach, where it's \"temporarily closed,\" a phrase you now hear in your dreams.\n\nYou pay per kilogram like you're buying something from a guy in a parking lot, and the dealer threw in a fuel card like a hostage negotiator. You tell everyone, \"It only emits water,\" as if that's a personality and not the thing every kettle does.\n\nYou're not an early adopter. You're the last passenger on a train line that's been cancelled, wearing a \"Future\" T-shirt, checking an app to see if the future is open."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0103", "lang": "en", "target_id": "stinky-tofu", "target_name": "Stinky tofu (臭豆腐)", "facts": ["Tofu soaked for hours to a day or two in a brine that has itself fermented for months from vegetables such as bamboo shoots and mustard greens, often with dried shrimp or meat; the smell is the brine's, not the bean's.", "Changsha's version is black, deep-fried and doused in chili; Taiwan's night-market version is golden and served with pickled cabbage; Shaoxing's, brined with amaranth stalks, is fried pale yellow or steamed with other 'stinky' pickles.", "Legend credits Wang Zhihe, a Qing-era exam candidate who left his unsold tofu too long in 1669; a Beijing fermented-bean-curd brand still carries his name.", "Street vendors are its main outlet, and the smell carries for a block."], "a": {"item_id": "49bb8f6483c1", "text": "You're not eating stinky tofu, you're auditioning. Every tourist at the night market stands there with a toothpick and a paper tray, filming their own face for the \"I tried it!\" video, which is the only reason you ordered. Nobody craves this. You wanted a story, and now you've got one and a ten-minute ride on the MRT.\n\nAnd the locals are worse. \"You just have to get past the first bite,\" you say, the way people talk about marathons and their ex. You've turned a snack into a personality. You'll argue Taipei versus Changsha like it's a custody hearing.\n\nThen you walk home trailing a cloud like a divorced cologne ad, wondering why nobody sits next to you. You wanted to be adventurous. You got to be alone on a bus."}, "b": {"item_id": "5be442dd726d", "text": "Look at you, standing in a night-market line everyone else is fleeing, like you're queuing to get into a fire. You followed that smell on purpose. Three blocks. Upwind. You're the only person alive who reads a warning as a recommendation.\n\nAnd you always need a witness. \"It smells like a sewer but tastes amazing!\" Yes, we know. You've said it to every foreigner you've ever met, then filmed their face gagging for content. Your entire personality is a dare you keep winning against tourists.\n\nAnd what does it get you? A paper cup, a toothpick, some pickled cabbage doing its best, and a jacket that now smells like a porta-potty at a wedding. Then you board the MRT and clear the whole car. You call that bold. The rest of us call it a hazmat incident with chili sauce."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0104", "lang": "en", "target_id": "vim", "target_name": "Vim", "facts": ["Released by Bram Moolenaar in November 1991 as 'Vi IMitation', a vi clone he had begun writing for the Amiga in 1988 on top of Stevie; the expansion became 'Vi IMproved' with version 2.0 in December 1993.", "Licensed as charityware: the licence asks users to donate to ICCF Holland, a charity Moolenaar ran for children in Uganda.", "Stack Overflow's question 'How to exit the Vim editor?' passed one million visits in May 2017, which Stack Overflow marked with a blog post.", "Modal editing: in normal mode, typing letters runs commands rather than inserting text; :wq saves and quits, :q! quits and discards changes."], "a": {"item_id": "fc4027ce9a34", "text": "You told the new intern you use Vim before you told him your name. He just wanted to know where the printer was.\n\nYou say you never leave the home row. Correct. You never leave the house either.\n\nYou spent forty hours on your .vimrc to save eleven seconds a day, so you break even in 2031, assuming you don't switch to Neovim, which you will, and then announce it like a religious conversion.\n\nYou're not after speed. You're after the moment someone glances at your screen and asks, \"How do you even quit that?\" so you can say \"Oh, it's simple\" and type something that looks like a ransom note: `:wqa!`\n\nWhat does it get you? A macro that renames forty variables, a pinky built like a bodybuilder's, and one guy at a meetup who nods at you with exactly your kind of loneliness."}, "b": {"item_id": "321a52e03ab6", "text": "Every Vim person has to tell you about Vim. You say \"nice weather\" at brunch and they say, \"Have you tried modal editing?\" They'll sit through your wedding toast in Normal mode, just so they can feel superior while hitting Escape. Their whole personality is hjkl. They don't write code. They perform a sport nobody asked them to join. And the best part is they can't get out. Ask one how to quit and they'll smirk, type a colon, and say, \"Ah, you just need to know the commands.\" Twenty years later he's still typing :wq, still telling you it's faster. Faster than what? He's never left the room."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0105", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hydrogen-cars", "target_name": "Hydrogen fuel-cell cars", "facts": ["Toyota launched the Mirai in 2014; 'mirai' means 'future' in Japanese.", "Nearly all US hydrogen filling stations are in California; Shell closed all seven of its California passenger-car stations in February 2024.", "More than 95% of the world's hydrogen is made from fossil fuels, mostly by steam-reforming natural gas.", "California pump prices reached about $36 per kilogram in 2023-24, over $200 for a Mirai fill; Toyota has handed buyers fuel cards worth $15,000.", "Mirai owners filed a class action against Toyota in July 2024 over hydrogen shortages and station outages; a second suit in 2025 sought at least $5.7 billion."], "a": {"item_id": "1f255592ab99", "text": "Let's talk about the guy who bought a hydrogen car. He wanted to be greener than the Tesla people and smugger than the Prius people, and found the one way to do both while living like a medieval pilgrim. His life is now a map of eleven pumps, and nine say \"offline.\" He loves telling you it \"only emits water.\" Yeah, so does he, every time the app says \"station unavailable.\" He brags that refueling takes five minutes, after the forty-minute drive and the two-hour line behind three other Mirai owners, all quietly weeping. Toyota handed him a fifteen-thousand-dollar fuel card and he burned through it before the warranty paperwork dried. Now he owns a sixty-thousand-dollar car worth a used futon, all to prove he's an early adopter of a future that met him and moved to Japan."}, "b": {"item_id": "54c0cf51dcab", "text": "Let's talk about the people who buy hydrogen cars. They looked at electric cars and thought, \"Too convenient.\" They want to be the only person in the Costco lot who needs a fuel station that closed in 2023. Their dream is pulling up to the pump, exhaling water vapor, and feeling like a Bond villain who runs on tap water. Then the nearest station turns out to be 140 miles away, with a handwritten sign reading \"Temporarily Out of Service\" taped over the nozzle. The attendant, a man named Dave, has never heard of hydrogen. These buyers own a car that makes drinking water out of its tailpipe, and they still can't get a fill-up."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0106", "lang": "en", "target_id": "google-sheets", "target_name": "Google Sheets", "facts": ["Launched in June 2006 as Google Spreadsheets, built on XL2Web, a product Google acquired from 2Web Technologies.", "A spreadsheet is capped at 10 million cells (raised from 5 million in 2022).", "Functions such as IMPORTHTML, IMPORTXML, and GOOGLEFINANCE pull live web data straight into cells.", "Free with a Google account; files live in Google Drive, and collaborators edit the same sheet simultaneously with coloured cursors.", "In a browser, offline editing works only in Chrome or Edge, with the Google Docs Offline extension installed and offline access switched on while still connected; the Android and iOS apps work offline natively."], "a": {"item_id": "2067d213f836", "text": "You're not running a team. You're running a tab called \"MASTER - DO NOT TOUCH,\" which Brad touched in 2021. You wanted a CRM, a project tracker, and an HR system, and you built all three out of IMPORTRANGE and spite. Every morning you watch an Anonymous Capybara scroll through payroll and just decide that's fine. Someone sorts column B without selecting column C, and suddenly your top performer is assigned to Linda's maternity leave. Your entire career is \"Last edit was 4 minutes ago.\" You don't do performance reviews, you do Version History. Your conditional formatting has more colors than your company has policies. Your Apps Script was written by an intern who now works at a bakery. Every time someone emails \"Request edit access,\" you feel like a king. And you are a king, ruling over 47 frozen rows and one #REF! that nobody can explain."}, "b": {"item_id": "2bbca6d87206", "text": "You're not a manager, you're a guy who got comfortable with IMPORTRANGE and mistook it for leadership. You said \"we don't need Jira, a sheet works fine,\" and now you have eleven tabs, three named \"Final,\" and one called \"Copy of Final (Dave's).\"\n\nWhat are you after? Control, disguised as transparency. You want everyone to see everything, as long as only you understand the formulas. Your team doesn't update the sheet. They open it, spot an anonymous capybara hovering over cell F14, and quietly leave.\n\nWhat does it get you? A color-coding system only you can decode, a Status column that has said \"In progress\" since March, and the rush of a stakeholder clicking \"Request edit access,\" the closest you'll ever come to being feared.\n\nMeanwhile, Version History knows who broke it. You know it too. You just won't say."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0107", "lang": "en", "target_id": "macau", "target_name": "Macau", "facts": ["A Portuguese settlement from the 1550s until the handover to China in December 1999.", "Casino revenue passed the Las Vegas Strip's in 2006, and at its 2013 peak, about $45 billion, was roughly seven times the Strip's.", "One company, STDM, held the casino monopoly for four decades until licences opened to competition in 2002.", "Among the most densely populated places on Earth, at roughly 20,000 people per square kilometre.", "The Cotai Strip stands on land reclaimed from the sea between the islands of Taipa and Coloane."], "a": {"item_id": "fe0ea9392503", "text": "Every man on the 6 a.m. TurboJET from Hong Kong has the same plan: lose the rent in Macau, win it back before the boat docks. He lost his bonus in Vegas and figures the baccarat tables here are the same tables, just with more gold leaf. He says \"I'm just here to see the Venetian,\" then walks straight past the gondolas, because gondolas aren't on the way to the table. He's after the feeling of being the guy who came back. What he gets is a room with no clocks and a VIP host named Kelvin who calls him \"boss\" in the voice of a man already calculating his credit limit. Hour six, he's on the ferry home, watching the Cotai skyline shrink, doing math on a comp slip."}, "b": {"item_id": "5ababa96b17b", "text": "You're not going to Macau to gamble. You're going to Macau to be wrong in a more expensive location. You lost it in Vegas, in Atlantic City, on your cousin's \"can't-miss\" crypto, and now you've flown fourteen hours to a former Portuguese colony to chase it down at a baccarat table, with a dealer who's seen your exact face on ten thousand other men.\n\nYou'll tell people it's a \"research trip.\" You'll stand in a fake Venice, under a fake sky, floating on a fake canal, trying to win real money back. You see the problem there, right?\n\nAnd the pawn shops are right outside the casinos. Macau doesn't even hide the sequel. It built the walk of shame with a loan desk.\n\nYou'll leave with no money, a stranger's cigarette smoke in your jacket, and one Portuguese egg tart you bought as a victory snack. It's the only thing you won, and you'll eat it on the ferry, alone, doing math."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0108", "lang": "en", "target_id": "emacs", "target_name": "Emacs", "facts": ["Began in 1976 as a set of macros for the TECO editor at MIT's AI Lab, written by Richard Stallman and others; GNU Emacs followed in 1985.", "Extensible in its own Lisp dialect, Emacs Lisp; the standing joke is that it is a fine operating system lacking only a good editor.", "Ships with a psychotherapist (M-x doctor), Tetris, a mail client, a calendar, and a web browser (eww).", "An old backronym is 'Eight Megabytes And Constantly Swapping', from when 8 MB was a lot of memory.", "'Emacs pinky' is the name for the hand strain blamed on its Control-key chords."], "a": {"item_id": "78fe88a6a79d", "text": "You people don't use a text editor. You adopted a pet that you have to feed Lisp.\n\nYou open Emacs to write one email, and three weeks later you've written a mail client, but you still haven't sent the email. Your init file is 4,000 lines long, and you understand maybe nine of them. The rest is a Stack Overflow answer from 2011 that you're afraid to remove.\n\nYou've got an Org-mode agenda so elaborate it has its own org chart. It tracks every task you'll never do, in beautiful nested bullets. You don't have a to-do list. You have a to-do *liturgy*.\n\nAnd the pinky! Thirty years of Control and your left hand looks like a lobster claw. You call it \"ergonomic.\" Your chiropractor calls it \"a recurring client.\"\n\nYou say it's an operating system. Sir, it's a hobby with a splash screen."}, "b": {"item_id": "59215bf985f1", "text": "You people don't use Emacs, you move into it, like a guy who rents a storage unit and starts getting mail there. You read email in mu4e, browse in eww, run a shell in eshell, and when you finally crack, you M-x doctor and tell a Lisp therapist your feelings. Your init.el is four thousand lines, half commented out, and you've declared Emacs bankruptcy twice: the only bankruptcy that ever made someone feel superior. You spent a weekend cutting startup from 1.8 seconds to 0.9, on a program you never close. Your left pinky is shot from holding Control, a war wound from never touching a mouse. Your whole life is in Org-mode, including a habit tracker whose only entry is \"configure habit tracker.\" And that novel you built the perfect writing environment for? It's in there too. Under TODO."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0109", "lang": "en", "target_id": "luosifen", "target_name": "Luosifen (螺蛳粉)", "facts": ["A rice-noodle dish from Liuzhou, Guangxi; the smell comes from pickled bamboo shoots (酸笋), not from the river snails, which only flavour the broth.", "The instant version boomed during the 2020 lockdowns; Liuzhou's government reported packaged luosifen sales of over 10 billion yuan that year.", "Added to China's national intangible cultural heritage list in 2021.", "Liuzhou opened a luosifen industry college in 2020 to train workers for the trade."], "a": {"item_id": "6eae17d96ab5", "text": "You're the person who read one Xiaohongshu post and now thinks you're from Liuzhou. You've never been to Guangxi. You've been to a Taobao checkout page, twelve packs deep, at 11 p.m.\n\nYou open the bamboo shoot packet like you're defusing a bomb, then tell the whole floor, \"It's actually not that strong once you get used to it.\" Used to it? Nobody got used to it. They just stopped telling you. Your roommates have a group chat with your name removed from it.\n\nAnd what do you get for all this? A bowl of noodles, a sticky note on the fridge, and the quiet dignity of eating alone, window open, in February. You wanted to be adventurous. You got a nickname in the HR department.\n\nAlso, you added extra chili oil. Of course you did."}, "b": {"item_id": "6782cb950544", "text": "You know who you are. You ordered twelve packs off Taobao, waited until every roommate was home, then tore open the sour bamboo shoot sachet like you were declassifying something. You're not hungry. You're making a point. Every slurp comes with the speech: \"Smells bad, tastes good.\" Buddy, we only get the first half. Nobody signed up for the tasting, just the hallway. And you keep saying \"it's authentic Liuzhou\" like geography is an apology. What does it get you? A spotless pot nobody will borrow, a group chat that goes silent the second you type \"anyone want some?\", and roommates who suddenly prefer dating people with their own apartments. You came for comfort food. You left with a reputation, a window permanently propped open, and a fridge shelf everyone treats like a crime scene."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0110", "lang": "en", "target_id": "pmma", "target_name": "PMMA (e-beam resist)", "facts": ["The same polymer as Plexiglas and acrylic glass, dissolved in anisole or chlorobenzene and spun into a thin film.", "The oldest electron-beam resist still in everyday use; IBM researchers reported it as one in the late 1960s.", "Positive tone: the beam breaks polymer chains and the exposed region dissolves in a MIBK:IPA developer.", "Resolves below 10 nm but has poor sensitivity, so doses of hundreds to over a thousand µC/cm² (depending on voltage) make writing slow.", "Poor dry-etch resistance, so it is used mostly for lift-off rather than as an etch mask."], "a": {"item_id": "c3b97d78415e", "text": "Still using PMMA as your e-beam resist? Bless your heart. You're the guy who still owns a flip phone and says \"the cloud\" like it's a place you visited once. Every grad student in your lab has moved on to HSQ and ZEP, and you're over there spinning PMMA at 4000 rpm, baking it on a hotplate, and wondering why your features look like they were drawn by a sleepy toddler with a cheap pen. You want sub-20-nanometer lines, but you're getting proximity effect so bad your dense arrays blur together like a bad Instagram filter. Congratulations, you've achieved the dream: a lithography process that's slow, low-contrast, and somehow still considered \"classic.\" Your advisor calls it a \"reliable baseline.\" We all know what that really means."}, "b": {"item_id": "b05c221e0d70", "text": "You're the person who says \"it's a classic\" about a process you inherited from a postdoc who left in 2009. Your lab notebook is just a bottle of 950K with someone else's handwriting on it. You say ZEP is \"too finicky\" while spending nine hours writing a 200 µm array because you chose a dose that could cook an egg.\n\nYou say you want 10 nm resolution, but your liftoff has the edge profile of a torn receipt, and you've been \"optimizing the bilayer\" since your qualifying exam. You treat MIBK:IPA 1:3 like a family recipe and cold development like a personality.\n\nMeanwhile the group next door switched resists, graduated, bought houses, and now send you LinkedIn requests. You stay loyal. Loyalty is great. It's just that your beam time costs more than their weddings."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0111", "lang": "en", "target_id": "jackfruit", "target_name": "Jackfruit", "facts": ["The largest fruit that grows on a tree; single fruits can weigh over 40 kg.", "The national fruit of Bangladesh and the state fruit of Kerala and Tamil Nadu.", "Unripe flesh is sold as a vegan 'pulled pork' substitute, although it has only 1-2 g of protein per 100 g.", "Cutting one releases a sticky white latex, so cooks oil their knives and hands first.", "A member of the mulberry family (Moraceae), a relative of breadfruit and figs."], "a": {"item_id": "11d60bd9214d", "text": "Let's hear it for the jackfruit pulled-pork crowd. You found something grandmothers in Kerala have cooked for centuries, slapped \"plant-based\" on a can, and tripled the price. Bold move, gentrifying a curry.\n\nYour entire pitch is \"it shreds like pork.\" So does a wet paper towel. You know what makes it taste like pulled pork? The barbecue sauce. You're not selling a meat substitute. You're selling a sauce delivery system with a mission statement.\n\nAnd the protein! Two grams. You told a generation of vegans they're eating a brisket sandwich and handed them a fruit cup in a trench coat.\n\nBut hey, it works. You get the Whole Foods endcap, the podcast slot, and the warm glow of saving the planet one eighteen-dollar taco at a time. The pig's still alive. So is your margin."}, "b": {"item_id": "4062eab29108", "text": "Every jackfruit seller has the same origin story. He opened a tin of young green jackfruit in brine, felt something stir, and decided the planet needed him at a farmers market saying, \"It's basically pulled pork.\" Now he has a logo, a folding table, and a beard that's doing a lot of work. He's selling a fruit that spent its whole life as a dessert ingredient, and he's rebranded it as the guy who gets to say \"trust me, it's meat\" to people holding a paper boat of barbecue sauce. What he really wants is Thanksgiving: his uncle takes one forkful, goes quiet, and asks what cut it is. What he gets is a forty-dollar tote bag and the specific pride of convincing a tree to be a pork shoulder."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0112", "lang": "en", "target_id": "risk", "target_name": "Risk", "facts": ["Invented in 1957 by Albert Lamorisse, the French director of The Red Balloon, as La Conquête du Monde; Parker Brothers published it in the United States in 1959.", "The map has 42 territories, among them Kamchatka, Irkutsk, and Yakutsk, while the entire Middle East is one territory.", "Holding Australia, which has a single entry point, is the standard opening strategy.", "A full game can run for hours, and players eliminated early have nothing to do but watch."], "a": {"item_id": "b165154a062e", "text": "Let's talk about the people who play Risk to the end. You know the guy. He says \"one more turn\" at 11 p.m. with a shift at seven tomorrow. He isn't in it for the win. He wants to be the last man standing over a continent he's never visited. Every game he claims he's \"not really into it,\" then spends four hours quietly stacking armies in Kamchatka. His girlfriend left during the Siberia stretch. His brother-in-law has been eliminated for three hours and still sits there, out of spite, hoping someone rolls snake eyes. These are people who have never conquered anything real, yet they guard Australia like it owes them rent. When he finally wins, nobody claps. Everyone just has to drive home and pretend they liked him."}, "b": {"item_id": "831ac90358ed", "text": "You people who play Risk to the end. Not \"until it gets late.\" To the *end*. Six hours in, somebody's uncle has fortified Australia like a doomsday bunker, and he'll still be sitting there at 2 a.m., one way in, one way out, guarding a war that ended at dinner. You're not after victory. You're after that one moment when you cash in three cards for fifteen armies and dump them all into Irkutsk like Irkutsk owes you money. And what does finishing get you? Total world domination, achieved by rolling three dice against two for forty straight minutes while your girlfriend reads a book in the next room. You broke a sacred peace treaty with your own brother over Kamchatka. Congratulations. You conquered the entire planet. Now nobody in it will drive you home."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0113", "lang": "en", "target_id": "chicago-deep-dish", "target_name": "Chicago deep-dish pizza", "facts": ["Created at Pizzeria Uno in Chicago in 1943; credit is disputed among founder Ike Sewell, his partner Ric Riccardo, and cook Rudy Malnati.", "Assembled upside down: sliced mozzarella on the dough, then toppings, then crushed tomatoes on top, so the cheese does not scorch during the long bake.", "Jon Stewart called it 'tomato soup in a bread bowl' and 'a casserole' on The Daily Show in 2013.", "Chicagoans' everyday pizza is thin 'tavern-style' cut into squares; GrubHub order data reported by WBEZ in 2013 showed city residents ordering non-deep-dish pizza about 10 to 1 over deep dish, with deep dish and stuffed making up under 9% of orders."], "a": {"item_id": "a4b76bd10aa4", "text": "Let's talk about the people who insist deep-dish is pizza. Mostly Chicagoans with a Second City complex so severe they'll defend anything New York makes fun of. Jon Stewart called it a casserole in 2013, and half of Lincoln Park still hasn't emotionally recovered. These are folks who order, then wait forty-five minutes at Lou Malnati's, and call that patience instead of what it is: a hostage negotiation with a buzzer. They eat it with a knife and fork and still claim it's the same food you fold on a subway platform. And what does it get them? A Giordano's line full of tourists from Ohio, a nap at 2 p.m., and the right to lecture you about tavern-style, which, notice, is what they actually eat at home. Defending deep-dish isn't civic pride. It's Stockholm syndrome with a parking ticket."}, "b": {"item_id": "6aea7052f5a6", "text": "Every time someone tells me Chicago deep-dish is \"technically not a pizza,\" I know exactly who I'm dealing with. He's a guy from Hoboken who ate one slice in Naples in 2009 and has been waiting fifteen years to bring it up. He's not hungry. He's shopping for a fight. He wants the whole table to hear that real pizza is thin and foldable, and he says \"foldable\" like it's a personality. Meanwhile he's eating with a knife and fork, which he thinks makes him sophisticated. Chicago never asked for his permission. Chicago has been feeding people since before he was born, while he was still folding pepperoni on a napkin. So let him win the argument. It's the only thing he's ever ordered that came out exactly how he wanted."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0114", "lang": "en", "target_id": "luosifen", "target_name": "Luosifen (螺蛳粉)", "facts": ["A rice-noodle dish from Liuzhou, Guangxi; the smell comes from pickled bamboo shoots (酸笋), not from the river snails, which only flavour the broth.", "The instant version boomed during the 2020 lockdowns; Liuzhou's government reported packaged luosifen sales of over 10 billion yuan that year.", "Added to China's national intangible cultural heritage list in 2021.", "Liuzhou opened a luosifen industry college in 2020 to train workers for the trade."], "a": {"item_id": "4f071ba2f3bc", "text": "You know the type. Ordered thirty packs on Taobao \"for emergencies,\" and the emergency is that it's Tuesday. They wait until 11 p.m., when the kitchen's empty, like that's a courtesy and not an alibi. They snip open the sour bamboo shoot packet with the focus of a bomb technician, then act shocked when the smoke alarm stays quiet but the flatmates don't. Their entire personality is one sentence, \"it tastes way better than it smells,\" delivered like a TED talk to people holding their breath. They'll tell you it's from Liuzhou, it's authentic, it's *culture*. Mate, you microwaved the tofu skin. And what does it get them? Twenty minutes of joy, a hallway that's filing for divorce, and a group chat renamed \"WHO.\" Congratulations: your hoodie is now a Liuzhou consulate until spring."}, "b": {"item_id": "911bf6e3e8ce", "text": "You know the luosifen guy. He opens the bag at 11 p.m. and the whole floor knows before the water boils. He doesn't cook it, he deploys it. Sour bamboo, peanuts, and that sachet of chili oil he's been hoarding since his last trip to Guangxi, all laid out like a surgeon's tray. He wants you to ask what that smell is. That's the whole meal. Every time someone asks, he gets to say \"it's snail,\" with the calm of a man who has never once been left out of a group chat. Then he eats it standing by the window, eyes closed, like he's the only one who understands it. The window stays open all winter. Nobody's allowed to say it's bad. Nobody's allowed to say much of anything, because he's already on his second bowl, and his face says he's in Liuzhou and you're not."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0115", "lang": "en", "target_id": "comic-sans", "target_name": "Comic Sans", "facts": ["Designed by Vincent Connare at Microsoft in 1994, modeled on the comic-book lettering in The Dark Knight Returns and Watchmen.", "Made for the speech bubbles of Rover, the cartoon dog in Microsoft Bob; it missed Bob's release and shipped instead in the Windows 95 Plus! pack.", "CERN's July 2012 Higgs boson discovery announcement was presented on slides set in it.", "The 'Ban Comic Sans' campaign, started by designers Dave and Holly Combs in 1999, ran until 2019, when they retired it and launched usecomicsans.com."], "a": {"item_id": "ea624ef43e94", "text": "Let's talk about the people who announce layoffs in Comic Sans. You're not a monster. You're Brenda from Facilities, and you want everyone to feel cozy about the asbestos removal. You took a typeface Microsoft designed for a cartoon dog's speech bubble and used it to type \"Your position has been eliminated.\" That's not softening the blow. That's a clown serving a subpoena. CERN announced the Higgs boson in it, and the physicists who found the God particle spent the week explaining the font. You think it says \"approachable.\" It says \"I scrolled right past Arial and Times New Roman and chose a kindergarten birthday invitation.\" Nobody reads your notice. They screenshot it. Congratulations: your fire evacuation memo is trending, and the building is still on fire."}, "b": {"item_id": "886470815955", "text": "You scrolled past every font on that dropdown menu, past the ones built for hospitals, courts, and airports, and picked the one designed for a cartoon dog's speech bubbles. Congratulations. Your notice about the mandatory layoffs is now technically being narrated by Rover from Microsoft Bob.\n\nI know what you were after. You wanted to seem approachable. You're the office manager who says \"no worries!\" while ruining someone's Friday. You wanted the boil-water advisory to feel like a hug. Instead, your tenants read it in the voice of a birthday clown and assumed the water was fine and the clown was just excited.\n\nHere's what it got you: a screenshot on Reddit, a group chat full of strangers, and a legal department that now reads your memos aloud to each other for fun.\n\nThe pet had a better career than the font you chose to speak for it."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0116", "lang": "en", "target_id": "esperanto", "target_name": "Esperanto", "facts": ["Published in Warsaw in 1887 by L. L. Zamenhof, a Białystok-born eye doctor, under the pen name Doktoro Esperanto, 'one who hopes'.", "Speaker estimates range from 100,000 to two million; roughly a thousand people grow up with it as a native language.", "William Shatner starred in Incubus (1966), a horror film shot entirely in Esperanto, with pronunciation Esperantists still mock.", "Its Wikipedia has over 350,000 articles, and its Duolingo course for English speakers had 1.36 million learners by July 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "09a618d282b4", "text": "Who learns Esperanto? People who've concluded the world's real problem is that nobody agreed on the grammar. They wear the green star on their lapel. They say \"Saluton!\" to the barista and watch her face do nothing. Their dream is one planet, one neutral tongue, and their first step is finding eleven other people in their city who share it. That's how they end up in a Leeds community hall with a plate of biscuits and Gerald, who has been \"nearly fluent\" since 1994. They have a pen pal in Brazil they've never met, and they write to him in flawless accusative. What does it get them? A hug from a stranger at a congress, and the quiet pride of being fluent in a language that mostly exists in other Esperantists' inboxes."}, "b": {"item_id": "f86448df0967", "text": "You learned a language built to unite humanity, and the only humanity you've united with is a guy named Dieter on a forum from 2004. You wanted world peace; you got a Duolingo streak and a green star pin you're afraid to wear to Target. Zamenhof dreamed of one tongue for all nations, and \"all nations\" turned out to be eleven retirees at a congress in Slovakia. You picked it because it's \"easy,\" which is what people say about the gym membership they never use. You've mastered the letter ĉ, a skill that has never come up in a job interview. Your best conversation partner is a stranger who says \"Saluton!\" and then switches to English out of mercy. Spanish would have gotten you a girlfriend. Esperanto got you a mailing list, a sticker on your laptop, and a very strong opinion about the accusative."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0117", "lang": "en", "target_id": "esperanto", "target_name": "Esperanto", "facts": ["Published in Warsaw in 1887 by L. L. Zamenhof, a Białystok-born eye doctor, under the pen name Doktoro Esperanto, 'one who hopes'.", "Speaker estimates range from 100,000 to two million; roughly a thousand people grow up with it as a native language.", "William Shatner starred in Incubus (1966), a horror film shot entirely in Esperanto, with pronunciation Esperantists still mock.", "Its Wikipedia has over 350,000 articles, and its Duolingo course for English speakers had 1.36 million learners by July 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "3c7c5dbcf1f4", "text": "You know the Esperanto people. They wear the green star on a lanyard and say it's \"so logical\" like they personally designed it. Ask what they're after and they'll say world peace, then admit they've been stuck on the accusative *-n* since 2016. Their plan was that one day they'd meet a Hungarian, a Brazilian, and a Japanese guy at a bus stop and all speak the same language. What they actually get is a pen pal in Lyon who stopped writing in 2019 and a Discord server with four hundred members and one active voice. They're not learning a language. They're waiting for a train Zamenhof promised in 1887, and it has never once left the station."}, "b": {"item_id": "5681feb1725d", "text": "Esperanto learners looked at seven thousand living languages and said, \"None of these. I want one with zero places to use it.\" You wanted to unite humanity, so you picked the one hobby guaranteed to make humanity back away slowly at parties. You signed up to talk to everyone on Earth, and now you talk to a retired Dutch engineer named Joop and a Brazilian guy who keeps sending you poems. Your big payoff is Pasporta Servo, where strangers let you crash on their couch for free, the only time a hobby this niche has ever gotten anyone into a bed. A hundred and thirty years of pushing world peace, and the crowning achievement is still William Shatner's *Incubus*. Zamenhof wanted to end war. You just wanted to be fluent in something before your cousin finishes Duolingo French."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0118", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hydrogen-cars", "target_name": "Hydrogen fuel-cell cars", "facts": ["Toyota launched the Mirai in 2014; 'mirai' means 'future' in Japanese.", "Nearly all US hydrogen filling stations are in California; Shell closed all seven of its California passenger-car stations in February 2024.", "More than 95% of the world's hydrogen is made from fossil fuels, mostly by steam-reforming natural gas.", "California pump prices reached about $36 per kilogram in 2023-24, over $200 for a Mirai fill; Toyota has handed buyers fuel cards worth $15,000.", "Mirai owners filed a class action against Toyota in July 2024 over hydrogen shortages and station outages; a second suit in 2025 sought at least $5.7 billion."], "a": {"item_id": "54c0cf51dcab", "text": "Let's talk about the people who buy hydrogen cars. They looked at electric cars and thought, \"Too convenient.\" They want to be the only person in the Costco lot who needs a fuel station that closed in 2023. Their dream is pulling up to the pump, exhaling water vapor, and feeling like a Bond villain who runs on tap water. Then the nearest station turns out to be 140 miles away, with a handwritten sign reading \"Temporarily Out of Service\" taped over the nozzle. The attendant, a man named Dave, has never heard of hydrogen. These buyers own a car that makes drinking water out of its tailpipe, and they still can't get a fill-up."}, "b": {"item_id": "5d75fbe56d0a", "text": "Hydrogen car owners are the only people who looked at Tesla drivers and thought, \"Not enough range anxiety.\" You didn't buy a car; you entered a relationship with the three working pumps in California and an app that tells you which one is \"offline\" today. You wanted to save the planet. Instead you burned through Toyota's free fuel card in a year at thirty-six bucks a kilo, filling up with hydrogen made from natural gas. Congratulations, you invented a gas car with extra steps. You love saying the only emission is water. Mostly it's your tears at a shuttered Shell station in Fremont. And resale? Your Mirai lost value faster than your friendships after you said \"hydrogen economy\" at brunch. You're not early adopters. You're unpaid beta testers who drive two hours to refuel."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0119", "lang": "en", "target_id": "excel", "target_name": "Microsoft Excel", "facts": ["First shipped for the Macintosh in 1985; the Windows version followed in 1987.", "Silently converts gene names such as SEPT2 and MARCH1 into dates; by August 2020 the HUGO Gene Nomenclature Committee had renamed 27 human genes (MARCH1 became MARCHF1, SEPT2 became SEPTIN2) and published guidelines in Nature Genetics to stop it.", "In October 2020, Public Health England lost 15,841 COVID-19 cases when a legacy .xls file hit its 65,536-row limit.", "Reinhart and Rogoff's 2010 paper 'Growth in a Time of Debt', cited for austerity policies, omitted five countries through a spreadsheet range error found by a graduate student in 2013.", "Sheets have been capped at 1,048,576 rows by 16,384 columns since Excel 2007."], "a": {"item_id": "55712e9f94e9", "text": "You run a 40-million-dollar company on a file called \"Final_v7_REAL_use-this-one.\" You're not a CEO. You're a hostage negotiator for a spreadsheet.\n\nYou say you're \"scrappy.\" No. You're a guy who refuses to pay for software but will happily pay Brenda eleven hours a week to color cells yellow. Yellow means pending. Light yellow means pending but emotionally.\n\nYour entire infrastructure is a macro written by Dave, who left in 2016, and nobody knows where Dave went. You light a candle for Dave. You're afraid to scroll past column AZ because that's where Dave's curse lives.\n\nYou wanted control. You got a file locked for editing by \"Karen,\" who's on vacation in Portugal.\n\nAnd every quarter, your VLOOKUP returns #N/A, and you stare at it like it's a diagnosis you're choosing to ignore."}, "b": {"item_id": "106d81fa2fcf", "text": "You run a company on Excel. Let's be clear about who you are: a guy who thinks \"enterprise software\" is a scam and \"Q3_Forecast_FINAL_v9_USE_THIS_ONE.xlsx\" is a system. You wanted control. You got Dave. Dave wrote a macro in 2014, and now Dave can never leave, die, or take a vacation, because payroll is a VLOOKUP only he understands. Your entire supply chain is one #REF! away from bankruptcy. You can't onboard a customer because the file is \"locked for editing by Karen,\" and Karen is at lunch. You saved forty grand a year on a real database and lost it all the day someone sorted column B without column C. Your org chart is merged cells. Your audit trail is yellow and slightly different yellow. And your growth strategy has a hard ceiling: 1,048,576 rows."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0120", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hsq", "target_name": "HSQ (e-beam resist)", "facts": ["Hydrogen silsesquioxane, sold by Dow Corning as FOx ('flowable oxide') and designed as a spin-on dielectric for chip interconnects; lithographers adopted it as a negative-tone resist.", "Exposure converts its cage structure into a cross-linked, silica-like network, so the developed pattern is essentially SiO2.", "Holds the resolution records for electron-beam lithography: isolated features around 5 nm, dense lines below 10 nm half-pitch, with high-contrast 'salty' NaOH/NaCl development.", "Must be kept refrigerated (Dow Corning's datasheet gives a six-month shelf life at 5 °C); the solution ages into a jelly, and its effective sensitivity shifts with processing delays because cross-linking continues after exposure.", "DuPont, which inherited Dow Corning's FOx/XR-1541 HSQ line, discontinued it and ended production at the end of 2025; labs such as EPFL's CMi switched to third-party HSQ from EM Resist or DisChem, both formulated from AQM powder."], "a": {"item_id": "91f701649a72", "text": "You switched to HSQ for the resolution. Of course you did. PMMA gave you 20-nanometer lines, which is fine for science but not for the cover of a journal your advisor brings up at dinner parties. Now you're writing 8-nanometer lines that nobody can see without a $2 million microscope you booked three weeks ago.\n\nYou pay triple for a tiny bottle that expires faster than your funding. You keep it cold like insulin and spin it knowing it's already going bad. Your dose is ten times higher, so your \"quick write\" runs overnight, and the e-beam tool manager who used to love you is now drafting an email. You develop in TMAH like you're brewing something, and the lines collapse anyway.\n\nAnd the best part: it never comes off. You wanted resolution, and you got permanent glass sitting on your device. Congratulations, you made something incredibly small that doesn't work."}, "b": {"item_id": "b68838ef49bb", "text": "So you switched to HSQ for the resolution. Bold move for someone whose devices still die at wire bonding. You saw one SEM of 6-nanometer lines at a conference and decided PMMA was beneath you. Now you're writing at a dose so high your name sits on the e-beam calendar like a hostage note. Your resist lives in the fridge next to somebody's yogurt, and you check its age more often than your own. You develop in salt water now, the closest you've come to a beach in three years. And congratulations on those gorgeous lines. They're basically glass, so the only way to fix a mistake is HF, a fume hood, and a long call to your mother. All this for one inset figure the reviewer will ask you to make bigger."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0121", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hawaiian-pizza", "target_name": "Hawaiian pizza", "facts": ["Invented in 1962 by Sam Panopoulos, a Greek immigrant, at the Satellite Restaurant in Chatham, Ontario, Canada; named after the brand of canned pineapple he used.", "Iceland's president Guðni Th. Jóhannesson said in 2017 that he would ban pineapple on pizza if he could, then clarified that a president has no such power.", "Hawaii is no longer a major pineapple producer: Del Monte shut its Hawaiian pineapple operations in November 2006, two years ahead of its announced 2008 exit, the state's last cannery closed in 2007, and most canned pineapple now comes from Thailand, Indonesia, and the Philippines.", "Panopoulos, whose restaurant also served Chinese dishes, told the Toronto Sun in 2010 that 'in those days people didn't mix the tastes like sweet and sour, except for Chinese food'."], "a": {"item_id": "32c9917cf9ee", "text": "You order Hawaiian pizza the way a man buys a motorcycle at forty-five: you need everyone to know you're dangerous. Nobody asked. You say \"I know, I know, don't judge me\" to a room that wasn't judging you, and now it is. That's the plan. It's the only controversy you can afford. You'll never skydive, never quit the job, never tell the group chat what you actually think, but you will die on this hill, and the hill is a Friday night in your own kitchen.\n\nYou think you're ordering a tropical getaway. It was invented in Ontario in 1962 by a Greek guy running a restaurant called the Satellite. You're not on vacation. You're the loyal defender of a Canadian accident.\n\nYou wanted to be a maverick. You got a twenty-minute argument with your brother-in-law and a second pizza, pepperoni, that someone quietly ordered to cover for you."}, "b": {"item_id": "f3575fe20181", "text": "Ordering Hawaiian pizza tells the whole table who you are: the guy who needs everyone to know he has opinions about a pizza invented in Chatham, Ontario, in 1962. You say \"pineapple belongs on pizza\" like you're defending a thesis, and nobody asked. Your friends order pepperoni, and you say \"bold choice\" in a voice that's clearly a dare. When your slice arrives, you take the first bite slowly, close your eyes, and nod like a sommelier. Meanwhile, everyone else is quietly fishing the chunks onto a napkin like a crime scene. You're not an adventurous eater. You're a man who has built his whole personality on ham and fruit, and who would absolutely fight a stranger in a parking lot over it."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0122", "lang": "en", "target_id": "slack", "target_name": "Slack", "facts": ["Spun out in 2013 from the internal chat tool of Tiny Speck's failed game Glitch; founder Stewart Butterfield had earlier spun Flickr out of another failed game.", "The name is a backronym: 'Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge'.", "Salesforce bought it for $27.7 billion in 2021.", "Filed an EU antitrust complaint against Microsoft in 2020 over the bundling of Teams with Office."], "a": {"item_id": "a071abe43a17", "text": "Let's hear it for the founders running a company on Slack. You wanted a culture; you got 400 channels and a guy who reacts to layoffs with the 🙏 emoji. You're not a CEO. You're a moderator with equity.\n\nYour whole leadership style is staying green. You think that little dot means you're present. Your kids see the same dot.\n\nYou don't make decisions; you start threads. Then you @channel at 11 p.m. with \"quick q, no rush\" and watch 200 people learn how fast a heart can beat.\n\nStrategy lives in #general, accountability dies in a DM, and the truth lives in a private channel called #venting-2 that you'll never be invited to.\n\nThe dream was async freedom. The reality is eight hours a day watching \"Kevin is typing…\" like it's a heart monitor."}, "b": {"item_id": "a66553ea7cb4", "text": "Nobody runs a company on Slack. They run a company that lives inside a Slack, and the founder has to be in forty channels so he can feel the building is still standing. Look at him: green dot on all day, status set to \"deep work,\" huddling with himself in an empty room. He starts every morning with \"quick question\" and ends it with \"let's circle back,\" which is corporate for \"I'm going to let this thread die.\" He doesn't answer you. He reacts with a little eyeball. An eyeball. That's his whole management style. And #random? Forty-one people in #random, all too scared to leave. Good morning, everybody. @here. Please read the pinned message."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0123", "lang": "en", "target_id": "nuclear-fusion", "target_name": "Nuclear fusion power", "facts": ["Has been 'about 30 years away' since the 1950s; at the 1955 Geneva atoms-for-peace conference, Homi Bhabha predicted fusion power within two decades.", "ITER, agreed in 2006 for first plasma in 2016, re-baselined in 2024 to start research operations in 2034 and deuterium-tritium burning in 2039, at a cost estimated above €20 billion.", "NIF's December 2022 'ignition' shot released 3.15 MJ from 2.05 MJ of laser energy; the lasers drew about 300 MJ from the grid to fire.", "Private fusion companies had raised more than $7 billion by 2024."], "a": {"item_id": "4a10914dfbf0", "text": "Fusion scientists have been promising power in ten years since before the Beatles broke up. That's not a forecast, that's a rolling lease. The timeline is perfectly engineered: close enough to unlock the grant, far enough that the person signing the check will be retired, dead, or hosting a podcast about living forever.\n\nYou celebrated \"net energy gain\" by counting the energy the plasma gave back but not the 300 megajoules of wall power it took to fire the lasers. That's claiming you're profitable because you ignored rent, payroll, and the building.\n\nAnd the startup founders: you raised a billion dollars on a slide deck with a sun on it and the word \"inevitable.\" You say \"ten years\" with the same face my uncle uses for \"I'm good for it.\"\n\nMeanwhile, the grad student who joined your tokamak project is now a grandfather.\n\nTen years, baby. Always ten. Never nine."}, "b": {"item_id": "d77192f17243", "text": "Fusion scientists are the only people who can say \"ten years away\" and get a standing ovation, because nobody in the room was alive for the last ten years of promises. They've been ten years away since Eisenhower. ITER has been \"under construction\" longer than most of its engineers have been married. The startup founders have a slide deck with a glowing donut on it and a Series C that needs a press release before Christmas. Their investors aren't buying energy. They're buying the feeling of being in the room when the sun gets bottled. And the politicians show up in hard hats, cut a ribbon in front of a magnet, and fly home. Nobody's getting rich off fusion. They're getting tenure, a TED slot, and a career spent being almost there."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0124", "lang": "en", "target_id": "vision-pro", "target_name": "Apple Vision Pro", "facts": ["Released in February 2024 at $3,499, with an external battery pack on a cable rated for about two hours.", "'EyeSight' shows a rendering of the wearer's eyes on an outward-facing screen.", "Apple calls it 'spatial computing' and its App Store rules tell developers not to describe apps as AR, VR, XR, or MR.", "Reports during 2024 said Apple had cut production; analysts' first-year sales estimates were well under a million units.", "Weighs 600-650 g, before the battery."], "a": {"item_id": "90ecfc190a47", "text": "You paid thirty-five hundred dollars to ride the 6 train inside Joshua Tree. Everyone else has to smell the actual 6 train; you just hear it echoing through a desert like a ghost with a MetroCard. You want us to know you're an early adopter. We know. Your little cartoon eyes are blinking at us like a hostage video. That cable running to your pocket says \"the future\" and also \"IV drip.\" You pinch the air at Union Square and a stranger thinks you're ordering him to move. You've got a hundred-foot Mac screen floating over a man eating soup out of a Pringles can. And the battery dies in two hours, so you'll still get to experience the subway in full, unimmersive 3D. Your commute outlasts your device. Welcome back to reality. It's delayed."}, "b": {"item_id": "2e6584647c14", "text": "You paid thirty-five hundred dollars to look like a hostage who's thrilled about it. Everyone on the 6 train can see you pinching the air, conducting an invisible orchestra of Safari tabs. Nobody is impressed. A man eating a hot dog just watched you \"tap\" a button that doesn't exist, and he's the one who feels sorry for you.\n\nYou wanted to be the future. Instead you're a guy who spent a month's rent to avoid eye contact, then bought the version that fakes eye contact for strangers. Those digital eyes are looking at nobody. The real ones are watching a 100-foot screen floating over Mount Hood, while the actual mountain you're passing is a tunnel wall covered in rat hair.\n\nAnd what did it get you? A full seat in rush hour. The whole car looked at you and decided you're either a genius or a crime scene."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0125", "lang": "en", "target_id": "risk", "target_name": "Risk", "facts": ["Invented in 1957 by Albert Lamorisse, the French director of The Red Balloon, as La Conquête du Monde; Parker Brothers published it in the United States in 1959.", "The map has 42 territories, among them Kamchatka, Irkutsk, and Yakutsk, while the entire Middle East is one territory.", "Holding Australia, which has a single entry point, is the standard opening strategy.", "A full game can run for hours, and players eliminated early have nothing to do but watch."], "a": {"item_id": "623676ba302f", "text": "You people said \"world domination\" out loud and then scheduled it around dinner. Hour six, and the guy who was eliminated in hour two is still watching, because his ride is the winner.\n\nWho plays Risk to the end? The guy who turtled in Australia for four hours like a retiree in Boca, then acts shocked when no one respects his \"strong defensive position.\" The one who negotiates alliances with the gravity of the Treaty of Versailles, then breaks them over a single Kamchatka. Your whole personality is \"I'm a strategist,\" and your whole strategy is rolling three dice at your best friend.\n\nAnd the prize? You conquered the world. Congratulations: it's 2 a.m., your girlfriend's gone, and your brother won't speak to you. Napoleon at least got an island. You get to pick up 400 tiny plastic men."}, "b": {"item_id": "2847036f4a61", "text": "You people who play Risk to the end are a special breed. Six hours in, Dad's still holed up in Australia like it's a tax shelter, your cousin is defending Kamchatka like he was born there, and nobody remembers why we hate Steve, only that we do. You're not after world domination. You're after the exact moment your sister breaks the nonaggression pact in Ukraine, so you can bring it up at Thanksgiving for eleven years. And what does winning get you? A board covered entirely in your color, a table of people who no longer speak to you, and the hollow joy of conquering Earth with the luck of a man who only rolls double sixes when defending Irkutsk. Congratulations, Napoleon. It's 2 a.m. The pizza's cold. Your empire goes back in the box."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0126", "lang": "en", "target_id": "chicago-deep-dish", "target_name": "Chicago deep-dish pizza", "facts": ["Created at Pizzeria Uno in Chicago in 1943; credit is disputed among founder Ike Sewell, his partner Ric Riccardo, and cook Rudy Malnati.", "Assembled upside down: sliced mozzarella on the dough, then toppings, then crushed tomatoes on top, so the cheese does not scorch during the long bake.", "Jon Stewart called it 'tomato soup in a bread bowl' and 'a casserole' on The Daily Show in 2013.", "Chicagoans' everyday pizza is thin 'tavern-style' cut into squares; GrubHub order data reported by WBEZ in 2013 showed city residents ordering non-deep-dish pizza about 10 to 1 over deep dish, with deep dish and stuffed making up under 9% of orders."], "a": {"item_id": "a4b76bd10aa4", "text": "Let's talk about the people who insist deep-dish is pizza. Mostly Chicagoans with a Second City complex so severe they'll defend anything New York makes fun of. Jon Stewart called it a casserole in 2013, and half of Lincoln Park still hasn't emotionally recovered. These are folks who order, then wait forty-five minutes at Lou Malnati's, and call that patience instead of what it is: a hostage negotiation with a buzzer. They eat it with a knife and fork and still claim it's the same food you fold on a subway platform. And what does it get them? A Giordano's line full of tourists from Ohio, a nap at 2 p.m., and the right to lecture you about tavern-style, which, notice, is what they actually eat at home. Defending deep-dish isn't civic pride. It's Stockholm syndrome with a parking ticket."}, "b": {"item_id": "8d0af0e1882c", "text": "Let's talk about the people who call it pizza. Chicagoans, mostly, from a city that spent a century being called the \"Second City\" and finally found a hill to die on. You don't love it. You're defending it.\n\nYou put your name in at 5:15 and eat at 7:00, then tell everyone it was worth the wait, because the alternative is admitting you spent your anniversary on a bench with a family from Ohio. You're after one thing: a fight with a New Yorker. And you win, in the sense that he stops texting you, which in Chicago counts as a civic victory.\n\nWhat do you get? A two-hour nap, a ruined evening, and a Goldbelly order shipped to Phoenix, where you eat it alone in a kitchen, defending a city you moved away from in 2011."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0127", "lang": "en", "target_id": "google-sheets", "target_name": "Google Sheets", "facts": ["Launched in June 2006 as Google Spreadsheets, built on XL2Web, a product Google acquired from 2Web Technologies.", "A spreadsheet is capped at 10 million cells (raised from 5 million in 2022).", "Functions such as IMPORTHTML, IMPORTXML, and GOOGLEFINANCE pull live web data straight into cells.", "Free with a Google account; files live in Google Drive, and collaborators edit the same sheet simultaneously with coloured cursors.", "In a browser, offline editing works only in Chrome or Edge, with the Google Docs Offline extension installed and offline access switched on while still connected; the Android and iOS apps work offline natively."], "a": {"item_id": "85e5be0c8934", "text": "So you run the whole team out of one Google Sheet. You have forty tabs, and one is called \"FINAL FINAL use this one.\" Every task gets a color, and the palette only makes sense to you. You built a Gantt chart out of merged cells, so the project is technically on schedule but unreadable to anyone born after the chart. Your status dropdown has six options, four of which mean \"fine.\" You want control, so you are the only one allowed near the VLOOKUPs, and you leave comments that just say \"see my note\" with no note. What it gets you is a team that makes its own copy, pastes the real numbers into a tab called \"actual,\" and checks version history only to find out who ruined your formatting."}, "b": {"item_id": "2bbca6d87206", "text": "You're not a manager, you're a guy who got comfortable with IMPORTRANGE and mistook it for leadership. You said \"we don't need Jira, a sheet works fine,\" and now you have eleven tabs, three named \"Final,\" and one called \"Copy of Final (Dave's).\"\n\nWhat are you after? Control, disguised as transparency. You want everyone to see everything, as long as only you understand the formulas. Your team doesn't update the sheet. They open it, spot an anonymous capybara hovering over cell F14, and quietly leave.\n\nWhat does it get you? A color-coding system only you can decode, a Status column that has said \"In progress\" since March, and the rush of a stakeholder clicking \"Request edit access,\" the closest you'll ever come to being feared.\n\nMeanwhile, Version History knows who broke it. You know it too. You just won't say."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0128", "lang": "en", "target_id": "monopoly", "target_name": "Monopoly", "facts": ["Descends from Lizzie Magie's The Landlord's Game, patented in 1904 to teach the evils of land monopoly in the spirit of Henry George.", "Charles Darrow sold his version to Parker Brothers in 1935 and was credited as the inventor; Parker bought Magie's patent for $500.", "The official rules require any unbought property to be auctioned, and 'Free Parking' pays nothing; the house rules that skip the auction and pile cash on Free Parking are why games drag.", "During the Second World War, British intelligence had maps, compasses, and real currency hidden in sets sent to prisoner-of-war camps."], "a": {"item_id": "c969b39b3b6a", "text": "Nobody plays Monopoly with the family to win. You play to find out which relatives have been running a loan shark operation in their hearts. There's Uncle Gary, who buys every railroad and reads the rent aloud like a hostage negotiator. Your sister hasn't passed Go since 2009 and keeps \"borrowing\" from the bank in a whisper. Dad wants Boardwalk so badly he's already picked out the sweater for the victory photo. Grandma insists Free Parking is a jackpot, wins nothing, and gloats anyway. Everybody says \"one more round\" at 11 p.m., and everybody means it, because the real prize is watching your mother quietly mortgage Baltic Avenue to settle a grudge she's been carrying since your ninth birthday."}, "b": {"item_id": "10bac3efc0cc", "text": "You all gather around a table to prove one thing: that you'd evict your own mother for a purple property. Dad's the banker, which is why the bank has mysteriously never gone broke and he somehow always has a 500. Your sister trades like a hostage negotiator, offering you Baltic Avenue and emotional closure for Boardwalk. Uncle Rick insists Free Parking pays out the whole jackpot, a rule that exists nowhere except in his need to feel lucky once in his life. The twelve-year-old wants only the railroads, because he's the one person here with a clear understanding of passive income. Grandma says nothing for three hours, then owns everything. Six hours in, nobody's having fun, but nobody can quit, because quitting means admitting you're the one in jail. It ends the way it always does: someone flips the board, someone cries, and everyone's out of money, including the winner's relationships."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0129", "lang": "en", "target_id": "latex", "target_name": "LaTeX", "facts": ["Leslie Lamport wrote LaTeX in the early 1980s and released it in 1984 as a macro layer on Donald Knuth's TeX (1978), which Knuth built because he disliked how the galley proofs of the new edition of his own book looked.", "TeX's version number converges on π (3.141592653 since 2021), and Knuth's TeX bug bounty started at one hexadecimal dollar, $2.56, doubling yearly until frozen at $327.68; he stopped mailing real cheques in 2008 after fraud and now credits accounts at his fictional Bank of San Serriffe, and only 9 of the 275 cheques he sent from 2006 on were ever cashed.", "Its best-known warning is 'Underfull \\hbox (badness 10000)'; overfull lines are reported as 'Overfull \\hbox (Npt too wide)', and figures float to wherever the algorithm decides, not where the author put them.", "A single missing brace can produce an error reported pages away from the mistake, and a document can need three compiler passes to get its references right."], "a": {"item_id": "f1bceb623799", "text": "You spent four hours on a two-page memo your manager will read as an Outlook preview on his phone, where it renders as a gray rectangle. But the margins were perfect.\n\nYou're the person who says \"I just find it cleaner\" about a document with three bullet points. You grabbed a template from a 2011 math department website, and now your logistics update looks like a proof that the Q3 budget is a compact set.\n\nYou chased an overfull hbox for forty minutes, a flaw no one on Earth could see, and fixed it by changing \"Thursday\" to \"this week.\" That's not writing. That's a hostage negotiation with a comma.\n\nYou wanted \"wow, rigorous.\" They got Computer Modern and a ransom note from a topologist. Your reward: \"Can you resend as a Word doc?\""}, "b": {"item_id": "c2eb8de50987", "text": "Nobody writes a two-page memo in LaTeX because they have to. They do it because they have a dissertation's worth of hubris and a sincere belief that the margins are the message. This is the guy who types \\documentclass{article} at his cubicle like he's summoning a demon. He spends ninety minutes getting one bullet point to hang properly, then emails the PDF to a coworker who opens it on her phone and asks, \"Can you just do it in Word?\" He wanted to look like a scientist. What he got was a memo that says \"Q3 Update\" in Computer Modern, and a reply-all from Doug asking what font that is."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0130", "lang": "en", "target_id": "monopoly", "target_name": "Monopoly", "facts": ["Descends from Lizzie Magie's The Landlord's Game, patented in 1904 to teach the evils of land monopoly in the spirit of Henry George.", "Charles Darrow sold his version to Parker Brothers in 1935 and was credited as the inventor; Parker bought Magie's patent for $500.", "The official rules require any unbought property to be auctioned, and 'Free Parking' pays nothing; the house rules that skip the auction and pile cash on Free Parking are why games drag.", "During the Second World War, British intelligence had maps, compasses, and real currency hidden in sets sent to prisoner-of-war camps."], "a": {"item_id": "c969b39b3b6a", "text": "Nobody plays Monopoly with the family to win. You play to find out which relatives have been running a loan shark operation in their hearts. There's Uncle Gary, who buys every railroad and reads the rent aloud like a hostage negotiator. Your sister hasn't passed Go since 2009 and keeps \"borrowing\" from the bank in a whisper. Dad wants Boardwalk so badly he's already picked out the sweater for the victory photo. Grandma insists Free Parking is a jackpot, wins nothing, and gloats anyway. Everybody says \"one more round\" at 11 p.m., and everybody means it, because the real prize is watching your mother quietly mortgage Baltic Avenue to settle a grudge she's been carrying since your ninth birthday."}, "b": {"item_id": "b78ec11705ad", "text": "Family Monopoly night: for people who looked at Thanksgiving and thought, \"This needs more landlords.\" Dad always grabs the banker job, the only time he's held money he didn't immediately lose. Your sister trades you Baltic Avenue for your Park Place \"as a favor,\" and you realize she's been running the same con since the womb. Mom invented a Free Parking jackpot rule in 1994 and defends it like scripture. And Grandpa just sits in jail, every game, refusing to pay the fifty, finally at peace.\n\nWhat are you all after? The thrill of owning property, a feeling none of you will experience off this cardboard square. Four hours later, someone flips the board, the thimble disappears under the couch forever, and the only thing anybody actually collected was a grudge worth way more than $200."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0131", "lang": "en", "target_id": "papyrus", "target_name": "Papyrus", "facts": ["Hand-drawn by Chris Costello in 1982 over about six months, when he was 23; he sold the rights to Letraset for $750.", "Bundled with macOS and with Microsoft Office, which is why it appears on so many menus and signs.", "James Cameron's Avatar (2009) used it for the film's logo and subtitles.", "A 2017 Saturday Night Live sketch starring Ryan Gosling is about a man unable to get over that choice."], "a": {"item_id": "50326144f6b1", "text": "You had every font in the world available, and you picked the one that says \"authentic\" the way a gift shop pyramid says \"Egypt.\" You wanted ancient wisdom. You got a sign that tells customers your hot stone massage was learned from a video.\n\nYou're the owner who said \"my nephew says it looks organic\" and considered that a design process. You're the guy whose \"Mediterranean-inspired\" menu includes a wrap. You wanted to be exotic, and you ended up in the same category as a quarter-billion-dollar blockbuster that got publicly roasted on SNL by Ryan Gosling, who played a man in actual tears over this exact decision.\n\nEveryone else in your strip mall hired a designer. You hired a feeling. Your five-star review says \"clean,\" which is what people write when they're being kind about the sign."}, "b": {"item_id": "a4df2dc21f25", "text": "Nobody picks Papyrus by accident. You went to a spa once, came out convinced you'd found your true calling, and now you want customers to feel like they're getting a massage in an ancient tomb that a Bali resort renovated. So you scroll past every honest typeface and land on the one that's been on every Avatar fan's laptop since 2009. Congratulations. You didn't choose a font. You chose a vibe with a decade-old hangover. Your sushi place is now hosting a sacred ritual, and the chef is confused about why the wasabi feels so spiritual."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0132", "lang": "en", "target_id": "klingon", "target_name": "Klingon", "facts": ["Created by linguist Marc Okrand for Star Trek III (1984), building on a few words the actor James Doohan improvised for the 1979 film.", "Deliberately alien grammar: object-verb-subject word order and no verb 'to be', so Hamlet's line became 'taH pagh taHbe'', 'continue or not continue'.", "The Klingon Language Institute (1992) published Hamlet 'in the original Klingon'; Bing Translator added Klingon in 2013 and Duolingo in 2018.", "Okrand's 1985 Klingon Dictionary listed roughly 1,500 words, and fluent speakers number only in the dozens: 20 to 30 in 2009, 30 to 50 by 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "64f4f6a7ba00", "text": "Congratulations on being fluent in Klingon, a language with maybe thirty fluent speakers, and you've met all of them at the same Marriott. You spent four years mastering a tongue where the standard greeting, nuqneH, means \"What do you want?\" That's also what your family says when you call. You've read Hamlet \"restored to the original Klingon,\" a phrase only a man who has never been hugged could type. One guy raised his son speaking only Klingon; by age five the kid stopped answering him. That's the dream: a language so pure even toddlers refuse it. There's no word for \"please,\" which explains your dating approach. You learned an entire warrior race's language to tell the barista Qapla'. Success. Sure, buddy. Today is a good day to die... a virgin."}, "b": {"item_id": "a7e3b2e8dd9c", "text": "Congratulations on mastering a language whose native speakers are fictional, homicidal, and would gut you over your accent. You've spent a decade on the warrior tongue of the galaxy, and your only battle was a Discord war over whether the apostrophe is a glottal stop. Marc Okrand made it up on a movie deadline, and you treat it like Latin.\n\nYou can order a drink in a language with no word for \"hello,\" which explains why no one's greeted you at a party since 2009. You translated Hamlet into it. Hamlet! A guy who can't commit to a decision, rendered for a species that would have stabbed Claudius in act one.\n\nAnd what does fluency get you? A convention hall of forty people correcting your verb prefixes, a girlfriend who learned exactly one phrase (the one for \"leave me alone\"), and a lifetime of dying honorably in a Hyatt conference room.\n\nQapla'. You'll need it."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0133", "lang": "en", "target_id": "ugg", "target_name": "UGG boots", "facts": ["Sheepskin boots Australian surfers wore to warm up after sessions; Brian Smith started selling them in California in 1978.", "Deckers Outdoor bought the brand in 1995 and holds 'UGG' as a trademark in the US and elsewhere; in Australia, 'ugg' is a generic word any bootmaker may use.", "In 2019, a US jury found an Australian maker liable for selling boots labelled 'ugg' into the United States.", "Oprah Winfrey featured them in her 'Favorite Things' lists repeatedly in the 2000s, which drove the first boom.", "UGG is Deckers' largest brand, with over $2 billion in annual sales."], "a": {"item_id": "2082912cfaf3", "text": "Let's hear it for the people wearing UGGs in July. You're not dressing for the weather. You're dressing for 2005, still waiting for Paris Hilton to text back. Australian surfers made these to warm their feet after the ocean. You wear them to warm your feet after the Trader Joe's parking lot. In August. In Phoenix. What are you after? \"Comfort,\" you say. Babe, your ankles have been collapsing outward since the Bush administration. You walk like a Clydesdale that's given up. And what does it get you? One summer rainstorm and you're squelching through Target like a wet golden retriever who made bad choices. People don't ask where you got them. They ask if you're okay. Congratulations: you've turned footwear into a wellness check."}, "b": {"item_id": "f64065ff7ffc", "text": "Y'all know the year-round UGG people. It's ninety-four degrees in Phoenix, and there's a woman in the Target parking lot in full sheepskin, sweating like a rotisserie chicken and saying, \"They're just so comfortable.\" Comfortable? Your feet are wrapped in a sheep's coat in July. You're not dressing for comfort. You're dressing for a personality. You want people to think you have a cozy life, a Pinterest board, and a husband named Brett who keeps a sourdough starter. And what does it get you? Feet that smell like a wet Labrador and a standing order at the drive-thru, where the barista already knows your name and is quietly worried about you. You didn't choose footwear. You chose to hibernate from the neck down."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0134", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hawaiian-pizza", "target_name": "Hawaiian pizza", "facts": ["Invented in 1962 by Sam Panopoulos, a Greek immigrant, at the Satellite Restaurant in Chatham, Ontario, Canada; named after the brand of canned pineapple he used.", "Iceland's president Guðni Th. Jóhannesson said in 2017 that he would ban pineapple on pizza if he could, then clarified that a president has no such power.", "Hawaii is no longer a major pineapple producer: Del Monte shut its Hawaiian pineapple operations in November 2006, two years ahead of its announced 2008 exit, the state's last cannery closed in 2007, and most canned pineapple now comes from Thailand, Indonesia, and the Philippines.", "Panopoulos, whose restaurant also served Chinese dishes, told the Toronto Sun in 2010 that 'in those days people didn't mix the tastes like sweet and sour, except for Chinese food'."], "a": {"item_id": "1be3426edb6c", "text": "People who order Hawaiian pizza want you to know they're brave. They say \"I know, I know\" before anyone has said a word, because for them the pineapple isn't a topping, it's a personality. This is a person whose entire rebellious phase fits in a box for $14.99. They've never gotten a tattoo, never quit a job, never sent food back, but once a month they look a teenage cashier dead in the eye and say \"pineapple\" like they're defusing a bomb.\n\nAnd they always want to split one with you. Half Hawaiian, half \"normal.\" That's not a compromise. That's a hostage negotiation.\n\nBest part? It isn't even Hawaiian. A Greek guy invented it in Ontario. So congratulations: your one act of rebellion is a Canadian pretending he's on vacation."}, "b": {"item_id": "f3575fe20181", "text": "Ordering Hawaiian pizza tells the whole table who you are: the guy who needs everyone to know he has opinions about a pizza invented in Chatham, Ontario, in 1962. You say \"pineapple belongs on pizza\" like you're defending a thesis, and nobody asked. Your friends order pepperoni, and you say \"bold choice\" in a voice that's clearly a dare. When your slice arrives, you take the first bite slowly, close your eyes, and nod like a sommelier. Meanwhile, everyone else is quietly fishing the chunks onto a napkin like a crime scene. You're not an adventurous eater. You're a man who has built his whole personality on ham and fruit, and who would absolutely fight a stranger in a parking lot over it."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0135", "lang": "en", "target_id": "klingon", "target_name": "Klingon", "facts": ["Created by linguist Marc Okrand for Star Trek III (1984), building on a few words the actor James Doohan improvised for the 1979 film.", "Deliberately alien grammar: object-verb-subject word order and no verb 'to be', so Hamlet's line became 'taH pagh taHbe'', 'continue or not continue'.", "The Klingon Language Institute (1992) published Hamlet 'in the original Klingon'; Bing Translator added Klingon in 2013 and Duolingo in 2018.", "Okrand's 1985 Klingon Dictionary listed roughly 1,500 words, and fluent speakers number only in the dozens: 20 to 30 in 2009, 30 to 50 by 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "a8c91de7e3da", "text": "Ever meet someone fluent in Klingon? Congratulations, you've found the only person who learned a language with no word for \"please\" and thought, *yes, this is my personality.* They spent years mastering a tongue built by a linguist Paramount paid to make growling official. They own Hamlet in Klingon, and they'll recite *taH pagh taHbe'* at your wedding. Ask them to pass the salt and they'll tell you asking is for the weak. Their dream is a job as a Klingon interpreter that never comes through, and their ideal date is someone who can answer \"Qapla'\" without blinking. Then you say one sentence in English, and they sigh like a warrior whose honor just got spilled on the couch."}, "b": {"item_id": "a7e3b2e8dd9c", "text": "Congratulations on mastering a language whose native speakers are fictional, homicidal, and would gut you over your accent. You've spent a decade on the warrior tongue of the galaxy, and your only battle was a Discord war over whether the apostrophe is a glottal stop. Marc Okrand made it up on a movie deadline, and you treat it like Latin.\n\nYou can order a drink in a language with no word for \"hello,\" which explains why no one's greeted you at a party since 2009. You translated Hamlet into it. Hamlet! A guy who can't commit to a decision, rendered for a species that would have stabbed Claudius in act one.\n\nAnd what does fluency get you? A convention hall of forty people correcting your verb prefixes, a girlfriend who learned exactly one phrase (the one for \"leave me alone\"), and a lifetime of dying honorably in a Hyatt conference room.\n\nQapla'. You'll need it."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0136", "lang": "en", "target_id": "nuclear-fusion", "target_name": "Nuclear fusion power", "facts": ["Has been 'about 30 years away' since the 1950s; at the 1955 Geneva atoms-for-peace conference, Homi Bhabha predicted fusion power within two decades.", "ITER, agreed in 2006 for first plasma in 2016, re-baselined in 2024 to start research operations in 2034 and deuterium-tritium burning in 2039, at a cost estimated above €20 billion.", "NIF's December 2022 'ignition' shot released 3.15 MJ from 2.05 MJ of laser energy; the lasers drew about 300 MJ from the grid to fire.", "Private fusion companies had raised more than $7 billion by 2024."], "a": {"item_id": "3775d67dffeb", "text": "Fusion people have mastered the ten-year timeline. They've been ten years away since before most of them had kids, and now those kids are writing the grant applications. They chase a star in a bottle, a plasma that holds for a few glorious seconds before the magnets sulk and everyone reconvenes. Their press releases celebrate \"scientific breakthrough\" in the same breath as \"we spent slightly more power than we got back,\" which they call Q-positive if you squint. Their investors wear fleeces and love the words \"carbon-free\" and \"unlimited,\" and they'll happily wait another decade. The reactor is always ten years out, and the paychecks are already here."}, "b": {"item_id": "1f957f86774f", "text": "Fusion scientists have the best job security in human history. You promise limitless energy in ten years. Ten years later, you promise it in ten years. It's the only career where missing the deadline *is* the business model. A pharmacist does that, she's arrested. A fusion CEO does that, he gets a Series C and a TED Talk.\n\nAnd ten is the perfect number. Far enough away that you'll be retired, close enough that the grant committee gets a little tingle. Nobody ever says \"twelve,\" because twelve doesn't fit on the slide.\n\nThey announced \"net energy gain\" by not counting the electricity that powered the lasers. That's like saying you won at the casino if you ignore the chips you bought.\n\nThey're building a star on Earth, and they can't even make it on time. The real one is free and shows up every morning."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0137", "lang": "en", "target_id": "nuclear-fusion", "target_name": "Nuclear fusion power", "facts": ["Has been 'about 30 years away' since the 1950s; at the 1955 Geneva atoms-for-peace conference, Homi Bhabha predicted fusion power within two decades.", "ITER, agreed in 2006 for first plasma in 2016, re-baselined in 2024 to start research operations in 2034 and deuterium-tritium burning in 2039, at a cost estimated above €20 billion.", "NIF's December 2022 'ignition' shot released 3.15 MJ from 2.05 MJ of laser energy; the lasers drew about 300 MJ from the grid to fire.", "Private fusion companies had raised more than $7 billion by 2024."], "a": {"item_id": "d77192f17243", "text": "Fusion scientists are the only people who can say \"ten years away\" and get a standing ovation, because nobody in the room was alive for the last ten years of promises. They've been ten years away since Eisenhower. ITER has been \"under construction\" longer than most of its engineers have been married. The startup founders have a slide deck with a glowing donut on it and a Series C that needs a press release before Christmas. Their investors aren't buying energy. They're buying the feeling of being in the room when the sun gets bottled. And the politicians show up in hard hats, cut a ribbon in front of a magnet, and fly home. Nobody's getting rich off fusion. They're getting tenure, a TED slot, and a career spent being almost there."}, "b": {"item_id": "1f957f86774f", "text": "Fusion scientists have the best job security in human history. You promise limitless energy in ten years. Ten years later, you promise it in ten years. It's the only career where missing the deadline *is* the business model. A pharmacist does that, she's arrested. A fusion CEO does that, he gets a Series C and a TED Talk.\n\nAnd ten is the perfect number. Far enough away that you'll be retired, close enough that the grant committee gets a little tingle. Nobody ever says \"twelve,\" because twelve doesn't fit on the slide.\n\nThey announced \"net energy gain\" by not counting the electricity that powered the lasers. That's like saying you won at the casino if you ignore the chips you bought.\n\nThey're building a star on Earth, and they can't even make it on time. The real one is free and shows up every morning."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0138", "lang": "en", "target_id": "javascript", "target_name": "JavaScript", "facts": ["Written by Brendan Eich at Netscape in ten days in May 1995; named Mocha, then LiveScript, then JavaScript to ride Java's marketing, although the two are unrelated.", "typeof null returns 'object', a bug from the first version that has been kept for compatibility ever since.", "In March 2016 the author of left-pad, an 11-line npm package, unpublished it and broke builds across the industry, including Babel and React.", "[] + {} evaluates to '[object Object]'; {} + [] typed at a console evaluates to 0.", "Standardized by Ecma as 'ECMAScript' in 1997 because the name JavaScript is a trademark, first Sun's and now Oracle's."], "a": {"item_id": "c69b78416cbb", "text": "Oh, you write everything in JavaScript? Beautiful. You wanted one language, so you picked the one where `typeof null` says \"object\" and nobody has apologized in thirty years. Your backend runs on Node so your frontend friends feel at home. Your little file-renaming script has four hundred megabytes in `node_modules` and a `package.json` longer than the novel you never finished. You're not programming. You're running `npm install` and praying it finishes before some eleven-line package vanishes from the registry. When people ask why, you say \"full stack,\" which is what you call it when your whole life rides on a dependency you've never read."}, "b": {"item_id": "9f280ef92811", "text": "Let's hear it for the people who write everything in JavaScript. You learned one language at a bootcamp and decided the rest of computing could adapt. Backend? Node. Desktop? Electron: a to-do list that eats four gigs of RAM because it ships its own copy of Chrome. Mobile? React Native. Your toaster is probably a Next.js app by now.\n\nYou call it \"full-stack.\" Your node_modules folder calls it a landfill. You npm-installed a package to pad a string, and when its author deleted it, half the internet fell over.\n\nEvery year you rewrite your whole app in a new framework, then bolt on TypeScript so you can pretend you chose a real language.\n\nAnd what does it get you? A résumé that says \"versatile,\" and a career spent asking why undefined is not a function."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0139", "lang": "en", "target_id": "brasilia", "target_name": "Brasília", "facts": ["Inaugurated on 21 April 1960 after about 41 months of construction, moving the capital inland from Rio de Janeiro.", "Lúcio Costa's Plano Piloto is laid out like an airplane (or a bird, or a cross), with Oscar Niemeyer's government buildings along the fuselage.", "Listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1987, less than 30 years after it was built.", "Planned for 500,000 residents; the Federal District now has about 3 million, most in satellite towns outside the plan.", "Built for the car: wide avenues, few pedestrian crossings, and distances that make walking impractical."], "a": {"item_id": "031bc5f2993c", "text": "Walking in Brasília is for three kinds of people: architecture students, tourists who trusted Google Maps, and the intern whose ministry is \"right next door\" on the Esplanada, which here means a forty-minute hike with no shade and a dry-season humidity of 11%. You're not pedestrians, you're pilgrims. You came for Niemeyer and left with heatstroke and a dirt trail you personally wore into the grass, because Lúcio Costa forgot you'd have legs. You cross the Eixão like it's a Frogger level with bureaucrats at 80 km/h, then descend into a tesourinha and come out three superquadras from where you started. Somebody gave you directions: \"SQS 308, Bloco C.\" Congratulations, you're in a spreadsheet. And on Sunday, when they close the Eixão so you can finally walk it, you'll realize there's still nowhere to go."}, "b": {"item_id": "84e52e157b23", "text": "Look at the guy walking from the Asa Sul to the Rodoviária at noon, two liters of water in his backpack, wearing the sneakers he bought for a 5K he never ran. He isn't walking to get anywhere. He's walking so he can post a photo of the Cathedral with a caption about \"mindfulness.\" Every superquadra here was designed without corners, so he's been lost for forty minutes and has decided that's a spiritual experience. He crosses the Eixão through the underpass like a man who's read a book about Lúcio Costa. The bus he could have taken comes every twenty-five minutes, but he'd rather arrive drenched in sweat and certain of his own virtue than dry and on time."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0140", "lang": "en", "target_id": "graphene", "target_name": "Graphene", "facts": ["Isolated in 2004 by Andre Geim and Konstantin Novoselov at Manchester by peeling graphite with Scotch tape; Nobel Prize in Physics, 2010.", "The EU's Graphene Flagship, launched in 2013, committed €1 billion over ten years.", "A 2018 study in Advanced Materials tested 'graphene' from 60 producers and found none with more than 50% graphene content; most was graphite flakes.", "Sold in tennis rackets, running shoes, and headphones as a performance ingredient.", "Geim also won an Ig Nobel Prize in 2000 for levitating a frog with magnets."], "a": {"item_id": "dd1e6a2334b9", "text": "Let's hear it for the graphene evangelists, the only people who've been \"five years away\" since 2004. Two guys in Manchester peel pencil lead with Scotch tape, win a Nobel, and an entire industry decides the tape was the business plan. Every press release is the same Mad Lib: \"Wonder material could revolutionize [batteries/condoms/desalination/your feelings].\" *Could.* That word has funded more postdocs than the NSF. You promised us space elevators and delivered a tennis racket with a sticker on it. Startup founders slap \"graphene-enhanced\" on a phone case the way divorced dads put a spoiler on a Honda Civic. And the grant committees keep paying, because admitting the miracle is stuck on the production line means admitting you are too. Twenty years of changing everything, and the only thing you've actually changed is the abstract."}, "b": {"item_id": "0a60dd7d6c08", "text": "Every graphene founder has the same pitch deck. Slide one: \"A Nobel Prize in 2010.\" Slide two: \"Five years from batteries.\" Slide three: a photo of a tennis racket, because nobody on the investor side has ever checked. Their LinkedIn says \"materials visionary.\" Their grant renewals say \"transformative.\" Their spin-out's name ends in \"-ene,\" and the logo looks like a hexagon that had a hopeful dream. They've been five years from the breakthrough since the Obama administration, and they bring up the Nobel at weddings. Meanwhile a postdoc is squinting at a sample under a microscope at 2 a.m., and the CEO is in Davos saying it will change everything. Everything has not changed. The postdoc has."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0141", "lang": "en", "target_id": "las-vegas", "target_name": "Las Vegas", "facts": ["Nevada legalized casino gambling in 1931, the year construction of the Hoover Dam began nearby.", "Most of the Strip lies not in the city of Las Vegas but in the unincorporated town of Paradise.", "The Sphere, opened in 2023 at a cost of about $2.3 billion, is the largest spherical building in the world.", "'What happens here, stays here' was launched by the local convention and visitors authority in 2003.", "Lake Mead, its water source, fell in July 2022 to about 1,040 feet, its lowest since the reservoir first filled in 1937, and dropped below that mark again in August 2026."], "a": {"item_id": "7dd28f44edd6", "text": "You're flying to Vegas to get rich. Think about that. You looked at your finances and said, \"The answer is a building with no clocks, built by mathematicians who have never once lost.\"\n\nBut you've got a system. Of course you do. You read a forum post by a guy called CrapsDaddy69, and now you're betting the don't-pass like you're defusing a bomb.\n\nYou'll sit down with $500 you're \"totally fine losing.\" By midnight you'll be at the ATM, paying a nine-dollar fee to access your own despair. And you'll be happy, because a woman in a sequined bodysuit brought you a free Miller Lite, and in your head that's a 4% return.\n\nYou'll be up $60 exactly once. That's the whole trip. You'll tell that story for eleven years.\n\nThen you'll fly home broke, sunburned, and wearing a lanyard from a convention you skipped, having made someone rich. Just not you."}, "b": {"item_id": "4f281fa0f753", "text": "Every year a guy drives eight hours from Bakersfield with two hundred dollars and a system. He got the system from a forum run by a man who also sells the system. He's not coming to have fun. He's coming to turn two hundred into a down payment on a boat. Forty minutes later he's at a blackjack table next to a woman in a sequined visor who's been feeding the same slot machine since Tuesday, and she's the only relaxed person in the building. He's down to forty dollars, and he calls it a learning experience, which is what everybody says right before they call their brother-in-law for a loan. Then he drives home, eight hours, very quiet, and tells his wife it was a networking trip."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0142", "lang": "en", "target_id": "emacs", "target_name": "Emacs", "facts": ["Began in 1976 as a set of macros for the TECO editor at MIT's AI Lab, written by Richard Stallman and others; GNU Emacs followed in 1985.", "Extensible in its own Lisp dialect, Emacs Lisp; the standing joke is that it is a fine operating system lacking only a good editor.", "Ships with a psychotherapist (M-x doctor), Tetris, a mail client, a calendar, and a web browser (eww).", "An old backronym is 'Eight Megabytes And Constantly Swapping', from when 8 MB was a lot of memory.", "'Emacs pinky' is the name for the hand strain blamed on its Control-key chords."], "a": {"item_id": "18478adad1eb", "text": "You know the type. Ask where he works and he says, \"In Emacs.\" He reads his email in there, chats in there, runs his shell in there. He's left only for dentist appointments, and he checked beforehand whether the dentist had a keybinding for it. He's tried to quit exactly once. He typed C-x C-c, stared at the prompt for a while, then spent three weekends rewriting his init file so he'd never have to try again. He doesn't need a text editor. He needs a hobby that sounds like a personality, and he's got forty-one packages doing the job. Ask him what he's working on. He'll say, \"My config.\""}, "b": {"item_id": "78aa8d199f40", "text": "You've lived in Emacs for eleven years and written four thousand lines of config and zero lines of the novel your Org-mode agenda says you're \"drafting.\" You don't edit text, you tend an init.el that collapses every time a package updates. You read email in it, browse Reddit in it, and tell people at parties you \"just haven't needed to leave.\" Nobody asked where you've been.\n\nYour left pinky is a war veteran. You remapped Caps Lock to Control, and your therapist to M-x doctor. You installed evil-mode so you could escape Emacs keybindings, then defended Emacs keybindings in the comments. You spent a Saturday writing Lisp to automate a ten-second task, break-even in 2031, then published a blog post about it for the four other people who have the same blog post.\n\nYour whole personality is a tool that does everything except make you look up from it. Stallman would be proud. That's the problem."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0143", "lang": "en", "target_id": "graphene", "target_name": "Graphene", "facts": ["Isolated in 2004 by Andre Geim and Konstantin Novoselov at Manchester by peeling graphite with Scotch tape; Nobel Prize in Physics, 2010.", "The EU's Graphene Flagship, launched in 2013, committed €1 billion over ten years.", "A 2018 study in Advanced Materials tested 'graphene' from 60 producers and found none with more than 50% graphene content; most was graphite flakes.", "Sold in tennis rackets, running shoes, and headphones as a performance ingredient.", "Geim also won an Ig Nobel Prize in 2000 for levitating a frog with magnets."], "a": {"item_id": "683abdc9f22c", "text": "Let's hear it for the graphene evangelists, the only people who've been \"five years away\" since 2004. Two guys in Manchester peeled pencil lead with Scotch tape, won a Nobel, and you looked at that and thought: finally, a career plan. Every grant proposal you write has the same three slides: space elevator, seawater you can drink, a phone that charges in six seconds. The funding agency nods like it's never seen them before, because it's seen them so many times it's slipped into a coma. Twenty years of press releases, and what have you actually shipped? A tennis racket. Headphones with \"GRAPHENE\" printed bigger than the brand name. You love telling people it's 200 times stronger than steel. Sure. Your claims, meanwhile, are one atom thick. You didn't discover a wonder material. You discovered wonder funding."}, "b": {"item_id": "9c280b4152bc", "text": "Every graphene pitch deck opens with the same slide: a honeycomb, a stock photo of a tennis racket, and the word \"revolution\" in a font you'd use for a dentist. These are the people who got a Nobel Prize for pulling a flake of pencil lead off with sticky tape, then spent fifteen years and four investor rounds explaining why that's the future. Their LinkedIn says \"materials scientist.\" Their Series A says \"platform.\" Their fundraising emails say \"graphene-enabled,\" which is what you write when the graphene in the sunscreen is too thin to find with a microscope. They want the Nobel, the magazine cover, and a sabbatical in Tahiti. What they get is one sentence in a press release that starts \"Experts say.\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0144", "lang": "en", "target_id": "keurig", "target_name": "Keurig", "facts": ["John Sylvan invented the K-Cup in the early 1990s and sold his stake for about $50,000 in 1997; in 2015 he told The Atlantic, 'I feel bad sometimes that I ever did it.'", "Keurig 2.0 (2014) read an ink mark on the lid and rejected unlicensed pods; sales fell and workarounds spread online.", "By 2014 enough K-Cups were sold each year to circle the Earth more than ten times; the pods were mostly unrecyclable #7 plastic until a switch to polypropylene by 2020.", "Keurig Green Mountain merged with Dr Pepper Snapple in 2018 to form Keurig Dr Pepper."], "a": {"item_id": "a2940564c97a", "text": "To the Keurig people, bless you. You're the ones who say \"I just need something hot in the morning\" like that's a personality. You want coffee the way you want a fire drill: fast, loud, and over before you're fully conscious. You've got a pod drawer with forty flavors, including one called \"Extra Bold\" that you bought in a fit of optimism and one called \"Hazelnut Dream\" you won't admit you finish. You press the 8 oz button, get four ounces of brown regret, and call it a day. You're the only people alive who look at a single-serve cup and feel like a responsible adult. Sixty seconds, one cup, zero decisions. You didn't choose coffee. You chose not to commit to a whole pot."}, "b": {"item_id": "017955fe0eb2", "text": "You wanted coffee without a relationship, so you bought a plastic thimble of \"Hazelnut Crème Brûlée\" and let a machine rinse it for forty seconds. That's not a flavor, that's a candle with a caffeine problem.\n\nYou own a 96-count Costco box and still haven't found a pod that tastes like anything but hot mug. You hit the Strong button like it's a negotiation. It's the same coffee, just smaller and angrier.\n\nThe descale light has been on since March. You know it. It knows it. You're drinking limescale with notes of whatever pod you picked.\n\nYou bought the reusable filter once, to feel responsible. It lives in the drawer with the takeout menus. Every morning you throw away a tiny wet puck and call it a routine."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0145", "lang": "en", "target_id": "risk", "target_name": "Risk", "facts": ["Invented in 1957 by Albert Lamorisse, the French director of The Red Balloon, as La Conquête du Monde; Parker Brothers published it in the United States in 1959.", "The map has 42 territories, among them Kamchatka, Irkutsk, and Yakutsk, while the entire Middle East is one territory.", "Holding Australia, which has a single entry point, is the standard opening strategy.", "A full game can run for hours, and players eliminated early have nothing to do but watch."], "a": {"item_id": "2847036f4a61", "text": "You people who play Risk to the end are a special breed. Six hours in, Dad's still holed up in Australia like it's a tax shelter, your cousin is defending Kamchatka like he was born there, and nobody remembers why we hate Steve, only that we do. You're not after world domination. You're after the exact moment your sister breaks the nonaggression pact in Ukraine, so you can bring it up at Thanksgiving for eleven years. And what does winning get you? A board covered entirely in your color, a table of people who no longer speak to you, and the hollow joy of conquering Earth with the luck of a man who only rolls double sixes when defending Irkutsk. Congratulations, Napoleon. It's 2 a.m. The pizza's cold. Your empire goes back in the box."}, "b": {"item_id": "ee9cd4f7db06", "text": "Let's talk about the people who play Risk to the end. Not the sane ones who flip the board at hour three. The finishers. You said \"quick game\" at noon. It's now eleven p.m. and you're attacking Kamchatka for the ninth time because Greg promised you peace in Europe. Greg has never kept a promise. Greg borrowed your charger in 2014.\n\nYou call it strategy. It's not. It's grudges with dice. One guy has sat in Australia since lunch, smug as a landlord, with four armies and a worldview. Another keeps announcing \"I'm trading in cards!\" like he's closing on a house. Meanwhile someone's girlfriend, who only came for the snacks, is quietly winning, and nobody will say it out loud.\n\nAnd the prize? Total domination of a card table. Congratulations. You conquered the world and lost the only seven people who would've come to your birthday."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0146", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hawaiian-pizza", "target_name": "Hawaiian pizza", "facts": ["Invented in 1962 by Sam Panopoulos, a Greek immigrant, at the Satellite Restaurant in Chatham, Ontario, Canada; named after the brand of canned pineapple he used.", "Iceland's president Guðni Th. Jóhannesson said in 2017 that he would ban pineapple on pizza if he could, then clarified that a president has no such power.", "Hawaii is no longer a major pineapple producer: Del Monte shut its Hawaiian pineapple operations in November 2006, two years ahead of its announced 2008 exit, the state's last cannery closed in 2007, and most canned pineapple now comes from Thailand, Indonesia, and the Philippines.", "Panopoulos, whose restaurant also served Chinese dishes, told the Toronto Sun in 2010 that 'in those days people didn't mix the tastes like sweet and sour, except for Chinese food'."], "a": {"item_id": "000c462b9442", "text": "Ordering Hawaiian pizza is a cry for help from someone who wants to be called a rebel. You've never been to Hawaii. The dish is from Ontario. You're defending a Canadian restaurant accident like it's your homeland.\n\nYou order it for the office party, then wait for someone to gasp. Nobody does, so you announce, \"I know, I know, pineapple doesn't belong on pizza,\" to a room that wasn't talking. You've been waiting years for this fight. Nobody's coming.\n\nYou call yourself adventurous. It's the only risk you've ever taken, and you retake it every Friday. Your entire personality is a defense of a topping that never hired a lawyer.\n\nAnd what do you get? Three leftover slices, because everyone else quietly ordered pepperoni, and a reputation as the \"random\" one at work. You wanted to be dangerous. You're just Greg, with ham."}, "b": {"item_id": "f3575fe20181", "text": "Ordering Hawaiian pizza tells the whole table who you are: the guy who needs everyone to know he has opinions about a pizza invented in Chatham, Ontario, in 1962. You say \"pineapple belongs on pizza\" like you're defending a thesis, and nobody asked. Your friends order pepperoni, and you say \"bold choice\" in a voice that's clearly a dare. When your slice arrives, you take the first bite slowly, close your eyes, and nod like a sommelier. Meanwhile, everyone else is quietly fishing the chunks onto a napkin like a crime scene. You're not an adventurous eater. You're a man who has built his whole personality on ham and fruit, and who would absolutely fight a stranger in a parking lot over it."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0147", "lang": "en", "target_id": "windows-8", "target_name": "Windows 8", "facts": ["Released in October 2012; removed the Start button and booted into a full-screen grid of tiles designed for touch screens most PCs did not have.", "The 8.1 update in 2013 put a Start button back, Windows 10 (2015) restored the Start menu, and Microsoft skipped the name Windows 9 altogether.", "Microsoft dropped the 'Metro' name in August 2012 after reports that German retailer Metro AG had objected on trademark grounds; Microsoft called 'Metro' a code name and denied any litigation, and the style was afterwards known informally as 'Modern UI'.", "The 'Charms bar' hid Search, Share, and Settings behind an invisible swipe from the right edge.", "Windows RT, the ARM edition launched alongside it, could not run ordinary Windows programs; Microsoft wrote down $900 million of unsold Surface RT tablets in 2013."], "a": {"item_id": "8dd545adc448", "text": "You put Windows 8 on a desktop. A tower. Under a desk. With a mouse. You looked at an operating system built for fingertips and thought, \"I'll just reach.\" Who are you? You're the guy who buys a touchscreen glove to use a keyboard. You wanted to feel modern, and now you spend your evenings shoving a cursor into the top-right corner like you're trying to scratch your monitor's back. You wanted productivity. You got a full-screen weather app swallowing your entire twenty-four-inch monitor to tell you it's raining, and you can't figure out how to close it. You lost the Start button and called it \"a fresh start.\" Your coworkers asked how you shut your computer down, and you said, \"I don't. It's always on, like a lifestyle.\" You're not using a PC. You're waving at it, waiting for the Charms bar to love you back."}, "b": {"item_id": "a78f1ebf5710", "text": "Let's hand a medal to the brave souls still running Windows 8 on a desktop. You looked at a perfectly good monitor and thought, what it's missing is a Start screen full of tiles. You wanted the Charms bar, that secret strip you summon by swiping in from the right edge, so you could find Shutdown like you're opening a spy drawer. You keep telling people, \"I'm fine with the tiles.\" Nobody asked about the tiles. Your Solitaire is a live tile now, and it's been live since 2012. You're waiting for the Start button to return the way the rest of us wait for a bus cancelled in 2013. Then a coworker asks why your desktop looks like a phone that lost an argument with a fridge, and you say, \"It's a choice.\" It is a choice. You make it every morning, and the tiles judge you quietly."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0148", "lang": "en", "target_id": "almond-milk", "target_name": "Almond milk", "facts": ["California grows about 80% of the world's almonds.", "A single almond takes about 1.1 US gallons (roughly 4 litres) of water to grow, a figure Mother Jones published in 2014 that spread during California's drought.", "A 2015 US class action alleged Almond Breeze contained only about 2% almonds; it partly survived a motion to dismiss, was stayed in 2017 pending a separate Blue Diamond labelling settlement, and the plaintiffs then dropped it.", "Pollinating California's almond orchards each February requires trucking in most of the commercial honeybee colonies in the United States.", "Medieval European cooks used almond milk as a Lenten substitute for dairy, centuries before the current fashion."], "a": {"item_id": "eb9e6316dbc1", "text": "So you switched to almond milk to save the planet. Congratulations. You found out it takes about a gallon of water to grow one almond, and your solution was to drink the almonds. You're the only person in America who needed a thirsty California orchard to hold your hand. You say \"oat\" like it's a personality. You order a flat white with almond and watch the barista sigh as it splits like a bad marriage. You swear you're not preachy, then you explain bee pollination trucks to a man who just asked for ketchup. The planet is grateful. The almonds are thirsty. And you have never looked more like someone whose entire carbon offset is a hashtag."}, "b": {"item_id": "2f3ff40c74e4", "text": "You switched to almond milk to save the planet, which is a bold move for someone who read one headline about cows. Congratulations: you found the crop that needs about a gallon of water per almond and grows almost entirely in a California drought. You didn't solve the problem, you relocated it to Fresno, where you can't see it.\n\nAnd the bees. Orchards truck in most of the country's honeybees every spring, like a tour that never ends. You didn't go vegan, you went on a bee-logistics subscription.\n\nBut let's be honest, it was never about the planet. It was about the moment the barista asks \"dairy?\" and you say \"almond, please\" like a governor granting a pardon. You paid sixty cents extra for a halo with a surcharge.\n\nMeanwhile, your oat-milk friend is quietly doing it better, and nobody's clapping for them. That's what really bothers you."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0149", "lang": "en", "target_id": "microsoft-teams", "target_name": "Microsoft Teams", "facts": ["Launched in 2017, bundled at no extra charge into Office 365, and replaced Skype for Business.", "After Slack's antitrust complaint, Microsoft unbundled Teams from Office in the EU in 2023 and worldwide in 2024; the European Commission still sent a statement of objections in 2024.", "Microsoft reported 320 million monthly active users in 2023.", "Microsoft shut down consumer Skype in May 2025 and sent its users to Teams."], "a": {"item_id": "38ae80798890", "text": "To the people who made us use Teams: congratulations. You surveyed every tool on earth and picked the one already in the bundle. That's not a decision, that's a vending machine with a Microsoft logo. Some procurement guy saved $3.80 a seat, got a slide with an upward arrow, and the rest of us got to spend our lives asking \"can everyone see my screen?\"\n\nAnd the managers, God bless you. You don't love Teams for collaboration. You love the little green dot. It's a traffic light for your trust issues. You don't need a platform, you need a smoke detector for Kevin. Meanwhile Kevin owns a mouse jiggler and a dream.\n\nYou called it \"one place for everything.\" Yes. Chats, teams, channels, and the file you swear is in the other team. Your legacy is a workforce that opens every meeting with \"sorry, let me just rejoin.\""}, "b": {"item_id": "b048a3892478", "text": "Nobody asked for Teams. The people who forced it on us need a green dot next to your name to feel alive. Your manager has seventeen channels named after a project cancelled in March, and he still posts \"Quick question!\" at 6:52 p.m. just so you'll see the three dots and feel summoned. The IT guy who rolled it out got a bonus for \"adoption rates,\" which means he counted every time you clicked the purple icon by accident. They want proof you're working. Your status says \"Available\" while you're in the bathroom, and they're thrilled, because now they can watch you be available. Then comes the all-hands where forty people unmute at once, someone's dog barks, and the host says, \"Let's take this offline.\" They've never once been offline."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0150", "lang": "en", "target_id": "esperanto", "target_name": "Esperanto", "facts": ["Published in Warsaw in 1887 by L. L. Zamenhof, a Białystok-born eye doctor, under the pen name Doktoro Esperanto, 'one who hopes'.", "Speaker estimates range from 100,000 to two million; roughly a thousand people grow up with it as a native language.", "William Shatner starred in Incubus (1966), a horror film shot entirely in Esperanto, with pronunciation Esperantists still mock.", "Its Wikipedia has over 350,000 articles, and its Duolingo course for English speakers had 1.36 million learners by July 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "d94ef91d85f7", "text": "Learning Esperanto is for people who looked at the twentieth century and thought, \"The real problem was conjugation.\" Zamenhof built it to end war, and his followers have bravely ended exactly one thing: their dinner invitations. You meet them at the World Congress, green star pinned to a fleece vest, shouting \"Saluton!\" at a Brazilian who also learned it on Duolingo. The big perk is Pasporta Servo: free couches worldwide, paid for by hearing your host explain why English is \"linguistic imperialism.\" Practical payoff? You can finally watch William Shatner's *Incubus* without subtitles. One hundred thirty years later, the dream of uniting humanity survives in a Telegram group of forty men named Bjorn."}, "b": {"item_id": "f86448df0967", "text": "You learned a language built to unite humanity, and the only humanity you've united with is a guy named Dieter on a forum from 2004. You wanted world peace; you got a Duolingo streak and a green star pin you're afraid to wear to Target. Zamenhof dreamed of one tongue for all nations, and \"all nations\" turned out to be eleven retirees at a congress in Slovakia. You picked it because it's \"easy,\" which is what people say about the gym membership they never use. You've mastered the letter ĉ, a skill that has never come up in a job interview. Your best conversation partner is a stranger who says \"Saluton!\" and then switches to English out of mercy. Spanish would have gotten you a girlfriend. Esperanto got you a mailing list, a sticker on your laptop, and a very strong opinion about the accusative."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0151", "lang": "en", "target_id": "latex", "target_name": "LaTeX", "facts": ["Leslie Lamport wrote LaTeX in the early 1980s and released it in 1984 as a macro layer on Donald Knuth's TeX (1978), which Knuth built because he disliked how the galley proofs of the new edition of his own book looked.", "TeX's version number converges on π (3.141592653 since 2021), and Knuth's TeX bug bounty started at one hexadecimal dollar, $2.56, doubling yearly until frozen at $327.68; he stopped mailing real cheques in 2008 after fraud and now credits accounts at his fictional Bank of San Serriffe, and only 9 of the 275 cheques he sent from 2006 on were ever cashed.", "Its best-known warning is 'Underfull \\hbox (badness 10000)'; overfull lines are reported as 'Overfull \\hbox (Npt too wide)', and figures float to wherever the algorithm decides, not where the author put them.", "A single missing brace can produce an error reported pages away from the mistake, and a document can need three compiler passes to get its references right."], "a": {"item_id": "f1bceb623799", "text": "You spent four hours on a two-page memo your manager will read as an Outlook preview on his phone, where it renders as a gray rectangle. But the margins were perfect.\n\nYou're the person who says \"I just find it cleaner\" about a document with three bullet points. You grabbed a template from a 2011 math department website, and now your logistics update looks like a proof that the Q3 budget is a compact set.\n\nYou chased an overfull hbox for forty minutes, a flaw no one on Earth could see, and fixed it by changing \"Thursday\" to \"this week.\" That's not writing. That's a hostage negotiation with a comma.\n\nYou wanted \"wow, rigorous.\" They got Computer Modern and a ransom note from a topologist. Your reward: \"Can you resend as a Word doc?\""}, "b": {"item_id": "c59587efb75e", "text": "Nobody writes a two-page memo in LaTeX because they need to. They do it so they can be the only person in the building whose \\textbf{Action Items} shine in Computer Modern. They type \\documentclass{article} like they're defending a dissertation, then lose an entire afternoon to a missing package just to shave a quarter inch off the margin. They're not informing Denise in accounting. They're proving they know what \\vspace does. And what does it get them? A PDF that looks exactly like Word, except it took six hours, a compile error on line 47, and Denise replying, \"Can you just send the Word file?\" They'll insist the kerning is superior. Nobody has ever checked the kerning."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0152", "lang": "en", "target_id": "pmma", "target_name": "PMMA (e-beam resist)", "facts": ["The same polymer as Plexiglas and acrylic glass, dissolved in anisole or chlorobenzene and spun into a thin film.", "The oldest electron-beam resist still in everyday use; IBM researchers reported it as one in the late 1960s.", "Positive tone: the beam breaks polymer chains and the exposed region dissolves in a MIBK:IPA developer.", "Resolves below 10 nm but has poor sensitivity, so doses of hundreds to over a thousand µC/cm² (depending on voltage) make writing slow.", "Poor dry-etch resistance, so it is used mostly for lift-off rather than as an etch mask."], "a": {"item_id": "b05c221e0d70", "text": "You're the person who says \"it's a classic\" about a process you inherited from a postdoc who left in 2009. Your lab notebook is just a bottle of 950K with someone else's handwriting on it. You say ZEP is \"too finicky\" while spending nine hours writing a 200 µm array because you chose a dose that could cook an egg.\n\nYou say you want 10 nm resolution, but your liftoff has the edge profile of a torn receipt, and you've been \"optimizing the bilayer\" since your qualifying exam. You treat MIBK:IPA 1:3 like a family recipe and cold development like a personality.\n\nMeanwhile the group next door switched resists, graduated, bought houses, and now send you LinkedIn requests. You stay loyal. Loyalty is great. It's just that your beam time costs more than their weddings."}, "b": {"item_id": "a46a8573ce80", "text": "You people still spinning PMMA aren't doing lithography. You're doing historical reenactment. Your advisor's advisor scribbled the recipe on a napkin in 1975, and you treat it like scripture: 950K over 495K, MIBK to IPA one-to-three, develop sixty seconds while whispering to the beaker. You tell everyone it's about \"resolution.\" It's about nobody wanting to file the purchase order for ZEP. So you crank the dose until writing one chip takes longer than your stipend lasts, then discover your pattern won't survive ten seconds in the etcher. So you do liftoff, because of course you do. Your entire thesis is gold rectangles. You're not a nanofabrication researcher. You're the guy who knows which bottle in the cleanroom is \"the good IPA\" and guards it like a dragon."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0153", "lang": "en", "target_id": "google-glass", "target_name": "Google Glass", "facts": ["Unveiled at Google I/O 2012 with skydivers livestreaming their jump through it onto the Moscone Center roof.", "The Explorer Edition sold to invited users in 2013 for $1,500.", "Wearers were nicknamed 'Glassholes'; bars, cinemas, and casinos banned the device over its camera.", "The consumer programme ended in January 2015; an Enterprise Edition continued until Google stopped selling it in 2023.", "Its display was a 640-by-360 prism in the corner of one eye."], "a": {"item_id": "4b59aaf692b9", "text": "You paid fifteen hundred dollars to become the only man in the bar the bartender watches more closely than the drunk guy. You wanted to look like the future. You look like someone about to be asked to leave by a stranger already hiding behind a pillar.\n\nYou walk in and say \"OK, Glass\" to a room that never asked, then repeat it louder over the jukebox. Nobody's impressed. Nobody's wondering what you're building. They're wondering if they're in your footage. It's the most attention you've ever gotten, and it's fear.\n\nYou wanted conversation starters. You got \"Are you recording me?\" and you said \"No,\" and then you winked. With you, that's a confession.\n\nGo home, Explorer. The only thing you've discovered is how fast a bar empties."}, "b": {"item_id": "d051a1381bcf", "text": "Look at this guy. Paid fifteen hundred dollars to join the Explorer Program, and the only thing he's explored is the sidewalk outside every bar in San Francisco. He walks up and says \"OK Glass\" out loud, like a man ordering from a butler who isn't there. You came here to meet women, and you're the one person every woman in the room has already assumed is filming her. Buddy, when you wink at someone, she doesn't think you're flirting. She thinks you just took her picture. Because you did. You tap your temple like you're having a thought, and we all know you've never had one. Google gave up on you. Google, the company that kept Google+ alive for eight years, looked at you and said \"no.\" There's a word for you. It's Glasshole, and you earned it."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0154", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hydrogen-cars", "target_name": "Hydrogen fuel-cell cars", "facts": ["Toyota launched the Mirai in 2014; 'mirai' means 'future' in Japanese.", "Nearly all US hydrogen filling stations are in California; Shell closed all seven of its California passenger-car stations in February 2024.", "More than 95% of the world's hydrogen is made from fossil fuels, mostly by steam-reforming natural gas.", "California pump prices reached about $36 per kilogram in 2023-24, over $200 for a Mirai fill; Toyota has handed buyers fuel cards worth $15,000.", "Mirai owners filed a class action against Toyota in July 2024 over hydrogen shortages and station outages; a second suit in 2025 sought at least $5.7 billion."], "a": {"item_id": "94ec4c185be2", "text": "Nobody buys a hydrogen car by accident. You're the person who drives forty minutes past three gas stations to reach the one hydrogen pump in the state, and it's out of order. You're proud of your tailpipe because it drips water. You bring this up at dinner parties, unprompted, like you personally invented rain. You wanted to be the early adopter who makes everyone else feel behind, and you are, because the only other person at the station is a guy in a Prius asking whether you're lost. Most of that hydrogen is made from natural gas, so your eco-friendly car runs on fossil fuel with a better marketing team. Congratulations. You're the most environmentally conscious man in a line of one."}, "b": {"item_id": "d1233bdc49cf", "text": "You bought a hydrogen car because a regular electric car was too easy. You needed a commute with plot. Everyone else plugs in at home while they sleep, but you drive forty minutes to the one station in Torrance, find the pump broken, and drive forty more to Long Beach, where it's \"temporarily closed,\" a phrase you now hear in your dreams.\n\nYou pay per kilogram like you're buying something from a guy in a parking lot, and the dealer threw in a fuel card like a hostage negotiator. You tell everyone, \"It only emits water,\" as if that's a personality and not the thing every kettle does.\n\nYou're not an early adopter. You're the last passenger on a train line that's been cancelled, wearing a \"Future\" T-shirt, checking an app to see if the future is open."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0155", "lang": "en", "target_id": "brasilia", "target_name": "Brasília", "facts": ["Inaugurated on 21 April 1960 after about 41 months of construction, moving the capital inland from Rio de Janeiro.", "Lúcio Costa's Plano Piloto is laid out like an airplane (or a bird, or a cross), with Oscar Niemeyer's government buildings along the fuselage.", "Listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1987, less than 30 years after it was built.", "Planned for 500,000 residents; the Federal District now has about 3 million, most in satellite towns outside the plan.", "Built for the car: wide avenues, few pedestrian crossings, and distances that make walking impractical."], "a": {"item_id": "cfeb728094dd", "text": "You're the guy walking from the Rodoviária to the Esplanada in July, backpack on, telling everyone \"it's only four kilometers.\" Four kilometers in a city built for helicopters. You chose the walk because you wanted to feel like a person in a place that forgot people exist. Your fitness tracker buzzes with pride while your shirt turns into a bath towel. You crossed the Eixão at a red light like a cowboy, then spent twenty minutes in an underpass hunting for the exit to a superquadra you swore was right there. Every car honks like it's doing you a favor. You arrive at the ministry looking like a wet sandwich, and you call it exercise."}, "b": {"item_id": "031bc5f2993c", "text": "Walking in Brasília is for three kinds of people: architecture students, tourists who trusted Google Maps, and the intern whose ministry is \"right next door\" on the Esplanada, which here means a forty-minute hike with no shade and a dry-season humidity of 11%. You're not pedestrians, you're pilgrims. You came for Niemeyer and left with heatstroke and a dirt trail you personally wore into the grass, because Lúcio Costa forgot you'd have legs. You cross the Eixão like it's a Frogger level with bureaucrats at 80 km/h, then descend into a tesourinha and come out three superquadras from where you started. Somebody gave you directions: \"SQS 308, Bloco C.\" Congratulations, you're in a spreadsheet. And on Sunday, when they close the Eixão so you can finally walk it, you'll realize there's still nowhere to go."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0156", "lang": "en", "target_id": "jackfruit", "target_name": "Jackfruit", "facts": ["The largest fruit that grows on a tree; single fruits can weigh over 40 kg.", "The national fruit of Bangladesh and the state fruit of Kerala and Tamil Nadu.", "Unripe flesh is sold as a vegan 'pulled pork' substitute, although it has only 1-2 g of protein per 100 g.", "Cutting one releases a sticky white latex, so cooks oil their knives and hands first.", "A member of the mulberry family (Moraceae), a relative of breadfruit and figs."], "a": {"item_id": "4062eab29108", "text": "Every jackfruit seller has the same origin story. He opened a tin of young green jackfruit in brine, felt something stir, and decided the planet needed him at a farmers market saying, \"It's basically pulled pork.\" Now he has a logo, a folding table, and a beard that's doing a lot of work. He's selling a fruit that spent its whole life as a dessert ingredient, and he's rebranded it as the guy who gets to say \"trust me, it's meat\" to people holding a paper boat of barbecue sauce. What he really wants is Thanksgiving: his uncle takes one forkful, goes quiet, and asks what cut it is. What he gets is a forty-dollar tote bag and the specific pride of convincing a tree to be a pork shoulder."}, "b": {"item_id": "fc39af40bf91", "text": "Let's talk about the geniuses selling jackfruit as \"pulled pork.\" These are people who looked at a fruit that half of Kerala has been feeding to cousins for free since forever, slapped it in a brine pouch, and charged eleven dollars for the privilege of being disappointed in a Whole Foods.\n\nTheir whole business model is barbecue sauce. That's it. They're a sauce company with a fruit alibi. And when you mention it has about two grams of protein, they get *defensive*. Buddy, you sold me a pork sandwich with the nutritional profile of a pear.\n\nBut I get the appeal. You don't need a lab, a patent, or a scientist named Klaus. You just need a food truck, a beard, and the confidence to say \"same texture!\" to people who haven't eaten pork since 2011 and genuinely can't remember."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0157", "lang": "en", "target_id": "brasilia", "target_name": "Brasília", "facts": ["Inaugurated on 21 April 1960 after about 41 months of construction, moving the capital inland from Rio de Janeiro.", "Lúcio Costa's Plano Piloto is laid out like an airplane (or a bird, or a cross), with Oscar Niemeyer's government buildings along the fuselage.", "Listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1987, less than 30 years after it was built.", "Planned for 500,000 residents; the Federal District now has about 3 million, most in satellite towns outside the plan.", "Built for the car: wide avenues, few pedestrian crossings, and distances that make walking impractical."], "a": {"item_id": "7d68bd3bc869", "text": "You saw the Congress from the rodoviária and said, \"It's right there.\" It is right there. It has been right there for forty minutes. Every step you take, it stays exactly the same size, like a mirage that went to architecture school.\n\nYou came to appreciate the modernist vision, and you're appreciating it from the median of the Eixão, holding a phone at 4% battery, asking a stranger where the \"corner\" is. There are no corners. That was the point. You are the point's victim.\n\nYou cut across a lawn because the real path is a mile of detour. Congratulations: you've joined the city's only grassroots planning committee, a dirt line worn by a thousand other optimists.\n\nHumidity is 11%, your nose is bleeding, and your reward is a ministry building identical to the last six.\n\nJust take the Uber, tourist. Even the drivers stop at the crosswalk for you out of pity."}, "b": {"item_id": "a98c294fb19a", "text": "Walking in Brasília is for three kinds of people: tourists who saw the Esplanada on a postcard and thought \"that looks close,\" architecture students who love Niemeyer from a safe distance, and the guy whose car is in the shop. You're not a pedestrian. You're an ant that wandered onto a blueprint. You want bread? The padaria is \"right there\" across the Eixão, so you sprint six lanes like you owe the government money. Which, let's be honest, is also your employer. Your sneakers are permanently red from the dirt trails carved by every other fool who refused to walk around a tesourinha. And on Sunday, when they close the Eixão, you finally stroll down the middle of the highway like a man who's won something. You haven't. Everyone else just stopped driving for one day."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0158", "lang": "en", "target_id": "excel", "target_name": "Microsoft Excel", "facts": ["First shipped for the Macintosh in 1985; the Windows version followed in 1987.", "Silently converts gene names such as SEPT2 and MARCH1 into dates; by August 2020 the HUGO Gene Nomenclature Committee had renamed 27 human genes (MARCH1 became MARCHF1, SEPT2 became SEPTIN2) and published guidelines in Nature Genetics to stop it.", "In October 2020, Public Health England lost 15,841 COVID-19 cases when a legacy .xls file hit its 65,536-row limit.", "Reinhart and Rogoff's 2010 paper 'Growth in a Time of Debt', cited for austerity policies, omitted five countries through a spreadsheet range error found by a graduate student in 2013.", "Sheets have been capped at 1,048,576 rows by 16,384 columns since Excel 2007."], "a": {"item_id": "d9b95a68589f", "text": "Let's talk about the founders who run the whole company out of one spreadsheet. Their payroll lives on a tab called \"Sheet47,\" and the cap table is a merged cell nobody dares unmerge. They think they're building a business, but really they're building a shrine to Ctrl+Z. They have forty-one copies of the budget, all named \"FINAL_v9_USE_THIS,\" and the only person who understood the VLOOKUP was the intern, who quit in March. They get giddy over pivot tables, call a #REF! error \"a learning moment,\" and sleep with one eye open in case someone overwrites row 14. When investors ask for a dashboard, they send a screenshot of a pie chart with the legend in Comic Sans."}, "b": {"item_id": "106d81fa2fcf", "text": "You run a company on Excel. Let's be clear about who you are: a guy who thinks \"enterprise software\" is a scam and \"Q3_Forecast_FINAL_v9_USE_THIS_ONE.xlsx\" is a system. You wanted control. You got Dave. Dave wrote a macro in 2014, and now Dave can never leave, die, or take a vacation, because payroll is a VLOOKUP only he understands. Your entire supply chain is one #REF! away from bankruptcy. You can't onboard a customer because the file is \"locked for editing by Karen,\" and Karen is at lunch. You saved forty grand a year on a real database and lost it all the day someone sorted column B without column C. Your org chart is merged cells. Your audit trail is yellow and slightly different yellow. And your growth strategy has a hard ceiling: 1,048,576 rows."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0159", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hydrogen-cars", "target_name": "Hydrogen fuel-cell cars", "facts": ["Toyota launched the Mirai in 2014; 'mirai' means 'future' in Japanese.", "Nearly all US hydrogen filling stations are in California; Shell closed all seven of its California passenger-car stations in February 2024.", "More than 95% of the world's hydrogen is made from fossil fuels, mostly by steam-reforming natural gas.", "California pump prices reached about $36 per kilogram in 2023-24, over $200 for a Mirai fill; Toyota has handed buyers fuel cards worth $15,000.", "Mirai owners filed a class action against Toyota in July 2024 over hydrogen shortages and station outages; a second suit in 2025 sought at least $5.7 billion."], "a": {"item_id": "e2e119f95d37", "text": "You bought a hydrogen car because electric felt too mainstream. You wanted the future, but with a longer commute. Congratulations: you're the only person in California who plans a road trip around a gas station's Instagram page, praying the one pump in Fresno isn't \"temporarily offline,\" which is station for \"gone.\"\n\nYou say you're saving the planet, but your fuel is mostly made from natural gas. That's a vegan who only eats at steakhouses for the salad.\n\nThe dealer threw in a free fuel card, the way you give a toddler a tablet so he stops screaming. When it runs out, you'll pay more per mile than a guy in a V8 pickup, in a car with the resale value of a Blockbuster membership.\n\nAnd you keep saying \"the only thing it emits is water,\" like that's a flex. Sir, you leased a very expensive dehumidifier that occasionally takes you to work."}, "b": {"item_id": "54c0cf51dcab", "text": "Let's talk about the people who buy hydrogen cars. They looked at electric cars and thought, \"Too convenient.\" They want to be the only person in the Costco lot who needs a fuel station that closed in 2023. Their dream is pulling up to the pump, exhaling water vapor, and feeling like a Bond villain who runs on tap water. Then the nearest station turns out to be 140 miles away, with a handwritten sign reading \"Temporarily Out of Service\" taped over the nozzle. The attendant, a man named Dave, has never heard of hydrogen. These buyers own a car that makes drinking water out of its tailpipe, and they still can't get a fill-up."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0160", "lang": "en", "target_id": "php", "target_name": "PHP", "facts": ["Created by Rasmus Lerdorf in 1994 as 'Personal Home Page Tools', a set of C programs for tracking visitors to his own résumé page; the name now officially stands for the recursive 'PHP: Hypertext Preprocessor'.", "Lerdorf, in his 2007 MySQL Conference talk 'PHP on Hormones': 'I'm not a real programmer. I throw together things until it works then I move on.'", "Runs WordPress, which W3Techs counted on 39.4% of all websites (58.1% of sites with a known CMS) on 9 November 2026.", "Core function names are inconsistent (strpos, str_replace, htmlspecialchars, nl2br) partly because, as Lerdorf confirmed, early function names were chosen to balance a hash table keyed on string length.", "Until PHP 8 (2020), the comparison 0 == 'foo' was true because the string was silently converted to a number."], "a": {"item_id": "da336777c19f", "text": "Let's hear it for the people who write PHP for a living. They've got a dollar sign tattooed on their soul, and they still say \"it's a lot better now\" with a straight face, like a hostage defending the guy holding him. Ask one why they stayed and they'll tell you the shared hosting is cheap and every client has a cousin who needs a website. Ask what they're really after and it's the moment someone on Reddit admits Laravel is \"actually pretty good.\" They've memorized which argument goes first in in_array and they're proud of it. They have a folder called \"legacy\" that's older than their marriage, and they open it on Fridays for fun. Respect."}, "b": {"item_id": "4b50923aaa44", "text": "You've said \"PHP isn't what it used to be\" so often it's basically your LinkedIn headline. Nobody asked. You bring up Facebook at parties like it's your cousin's startup. You switched to Laravel and call it a fresh start, which is a man putting a tuxedo on a Honda to prove he's going places.\n\nWhat are you after? Respect. What do you get? A client named Brenda who wants her WordPress site \"just a little different\" and pays by Venmo, sometimes.\n\nYou tell recruiters \"we're on PHP 8,\" meaning staging is. Production is a file called final_v2_REAL.php, with a comment from a guy who quit in 2014: \"don't touch.\"\n\nYou've been meaning to learn Go since 2019. Your fear isn't that PHP will die. It's that it never will, and neither will your on-call rotation."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0161", "lang": "en", "target_id": "emacs", "target_name": "Emacs", "facts": ["Began in 1976 as a set of macros for the TECO editor at MIT's AI Lab, written by Richard Stallman and others; GNU Emacs followed in 1985.", "Extensible in its own Lisp dialect, Emacs Lisp; the standing joke is that it is a fine operating system lacking only a good editor.", "Ships with a psychotherapist (M-x doctor), Tetris, a mail client, a calendar, and a web browser (eww).", "An old backronym is 'Eight Megabytes And Constantly Swapping', from when 8 MB was a lot of memory.", "'Emacs pinky' is the name for the hand strain blamed on its Control-key chords."], "a": {"item_id": "67c107490918", "text": "Let's hear it for the people who live inside Emacs. They read email in mu4e, browse the web in eww, and get therapy from M-x doctor, the only therapist who'll put up with them. Their whole life is in org-mode: a 4,000-line to-do file where \"fix init.el\" has been a TODO since 2011. They don't use a computer; they negotiate with it in Lisp. Every problem gets solved by writing a function, and every function creates a new problem. They've declared init.el bankruptcy three times and still owe. Ask what they've built and they'll proudly show you a mode for managing their other modes. And the payoff? A pinky that clicks like a castanet from holding Control since the Clinton administration, and the quiet dignity of being the only person alive who knows how to leave. C-x C-c. They never do."}, "b": {"item_id": "18478adad1eb", "text": "You know the type. Ask where he works and he says, \"In Emacs.\" He reads his email in there, chats in there, runs his shell in there. He's left only for dentist appointments, and he checked beforehand whether the dentist had a keybinding for it. He's tried to quit exactly once. He typed C-x C-c, stared at the prompt for a while, then spent three weekends rewriting his init file so he'd never have to try again. He doesn't need a text editor. He needs a hobby that sounds like a personality, and he's got forty-one packages doing the job. Ask him what he's working on. He'll say, \"My config.\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0162", "lang": "en", "target_id": "windows-vista", "target_name": "Windows Vista", "facts": ["Released to consumers in January 2007, after the 'Longhorn' project was reset in 2004 and headline features such as the WinFS file system were dropped.", "User Account Control prompted so often that Apple's 'Get a Mac' campaign ran an ad about 'Cancel or Allow'.", "'Windows Vista Capable' stickers went on PCs that could only run Home Basic, without the Aero interface, and led to a class-action lawsuit.", "Windows 7 (2009), widely praised, was largely a tuned-up Vista on the same kernel line; Vista support ended in April 2017."], "a": {"item_id": "df697b757759", "text": "Oh, you upgraded to Vista on day one? Of course you did. You're the guy who lined up at midnight for a store that closed at nine. You wanted the translucent Aero borders so badly you bought a new graphics card just to watch your taskbar shimmer. You clicked \"Allow\" on forty UAC prompts before your coffee cooled, because you think a security dialog is just a loading screen with opinions. And your printer? Dead. Your scanner? Gone. You're not a pioneer. You're a beta tester who paid full price and slapped a \"Vista Capable\" sticker on your tower like it was a Michelin star. Enjoy the sidebar gadgets. Your weather widget is the only thing in your house still telling you the truth."}, "b": {"item_id": "2ca90ba91029", "text": "Day-one Vista people. You paid $399 for Ultimate Edition because you wanted to be the kind of man whose desktop wallpaper moves. DreamScene. You paid extra for animated wallpaper. Microsoft said \"The Wow starts now,\" and you believed them, lined up at CompUSA at midnight like it was a Harry Potter release.\n\nAnd what did you get? A computer that asks permission. You'd open a folder and it would dim the whole screen like a disappointed dad: \"Cancel or Allow?\" You clicked Allow four hundred times a day. Your printer stopped working. Your sound card stopped working. Your Windows Experience Index came back 2.1, and you took that personally.\n\nBut you had glass window borders. You had a clock gadget. You had a sticker that said \"Vista Capable,\" which, like you, was technically true and fooled absolutely no one."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0163", "lang": "en", "target_id": "papyrus", "target_name": "Papyrus", "facts": ["Hand-drawn by Chris Costello in 1982 over about six months, when he was 23; he sold the rights to Letraset for $750.", "Bundled with macOS and with Microsoft Office, which is why it appears on so many menus and signs.", "James Cameron's Avatar (2009) used it for the film's logo and subtitles.", "A 2017 Saturday Night Live sketch starring Ryan Gosling is about a man unable to get over that choice."], "a": {"item_id": "df3aac397e9d", "text": "You opened a \"Himalayan salt cave and wellness sanctuary\" with a small-business loan and a dropdown menu. Around 11 p.m., exhausted, you scrolled past the fonts that cost money and landed on the one that whispers \"ancient healing\" to everyone, including your nephew, who \"does graphics\" and approved it.\n\nYou wanted timeless. You got the exact look of every other spa, the falafel place two doors down, and a yoga studio run by a guy named Dave who says \"namaste\" like a threat.\n\nSomewhere, Ryan Gosling is staring at your awning with the same haunted silence he gave the Avatar title, and he's right. You didn't build a brand. You built a $14 \"Pharaoh's Bowl\" for people who think Epcot counts as a passport, and you told yourself it was authentic."}, "b": {"item_id": "c986ccdedefc", "text": "You opened a day spa. You wanted serenity, wisdom, a whisper of the Nile. So you scrolled the font menu, sailed past Comic Sans feeling very sophisticated, and stopped on the one James Cameron used to title a billion-dollar movie. Congratulations: your sign now says \"ancient healing\" exactly as loudly as your aunt's church potluck flyer. Every hummus joint, every reiki studio, every tea shop run by a man named River made this same choice, alone, convinced they'd discovered it. The guy who designed it was twenty-three and has spent forty years apologizing. You paid a contractor to carve his apology into cedar. You wanted customers to feel transported. They do: straight back to 2003, a Word document, and a fourth grader typing \"Spring Bake Sale.\" Cucumber water not included."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0164", "lang": "en", "target_id": "nfts", "target_name": "NFTs", "facts": ["Beeple's 'Everydays: The First 5000 Days' sold at Christie's for $69.3 million in March 2021.", "OpenSea, the largest NFT marketplace, processed a record $2.7 billion in trades on 1 May 2022 and just $9.34 million on 28 August 2022, a 99% drop, according to DappRadar data.", "A 2023 dappGambl study found that 95% of the NFT collections it examined had a market value of zero.", "The token usually records only a URL; the image itself sits on an ordinary server or IPFS, not on the blockchain.", "Collins Dictionary named 'NFT' its word of the year for 2021."], "a": {"item_id": "c0aeb5d8a3ed", "text": "Let's hear it for the 2021 NFT buyer. You paid four hundred dollars in gas fees, then made it your Twitter avatar so strangers could right-click save it for free. You joined a Discord with eleven thousand guys named \"Chad.eth,\" called them family, and stayed family right up until the floor price dropped and family started doxxing the dev. You said \"WAGMI.\" We did not all make it. You told your mom you were \"early.\" Early to what? The rug pull? You weren't buying art. You were buying the right to say \"few understand\" at parties, which was true. Nobody understood, especially you. Jimmy Fallon bought one too. When your financial strategy matches Jimmy Fallon's, that's not a community, that's a support group. The roadmap promised a metaverse. It delivered you, at Thanksgiving, still owing your cousin."}, "b": {"item_id": "ff6086240a0d", "text": "Let's talk about the guy who bought a JPEG of a bored cartoon ape in 2021 for the price of a Honda. He's not an art collector. Art collectors own things. He owns a receipt. His whole personality now fits in a profile picture, and that picture is a cartoon ape wearing a bandana, the closest he's ever come to a self-portrait. He says \"wagmi\" unironically. He calls strangers \"ser.\" He joined a Discord to get whitelisted for a second Discord. Every morning he checks the floor price like it's his pulse, and he has never once looked at the thing itself, because the thing was never the point. The point was being the guy who got in early, six months before he became the guy who got out late."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0165", "lang": "en", "target_id": "chicago-deep-dish", "target_name": "Chicago deep-dish pizza", "facts": ["Created at Pizzeria Uno in Chicago in 1943; credit is disputed among founder Ike Sewell, his partner Ric Riccardo, and cook Rudy Malnati.", "Assembled upside down: sliced mozzarella on the dough, then toppings, then crushed tomatoes on top, so the cheese does not scorch during the long bake.", "Jon Stewart called it 'tomato soup in a bread bowl' and 'a casserole' on The Daily Show in 2013.", "Chicagoans' everyday pizza is thin 'tavern-style' cut into squares; GrubHub order data reported by WBEZ in 2013 showed city residents ordering non-deep-dish pizza about 10 to 1 over deep dish, with deep dish and stuffed making up under 9% of orders."], "a": {"item_id": "b9801548af22", "text": "Oh, you're the guy who says deep-dish isn't pizza. Let me guess: you own a wood-fired oven, you named your sourdough starter, and one documentary about Naples turned you into the Supreme Court of dough. You didn't come to Lou's to eat. You came to be the smartest man in the booth, and you're going to hold your fork like a gavel until the appetizers go cold. Your whole personality is a bit about being born in the wrong country. Relax, Gino. Nobody asked for a ruling. Chicago built a pie that takes forty-five minutes and a second mortgage, and you're upset it didn't come with your permission slip. Pass the slice and let your dignity cool down with the cheese."}, "b": {"item_id": "999fc0b6671a", "text": "Let's talk about the people who insist deep-dish is pizza. You're from the city that named its own comedy theater Second City and still acts shocked when nobody ranks you first. You're not defending food. You're nursing a grudge against New York you've carried since losing the World's Fair argument and the skyline argument. You'll wait forty-five minutes at Lou Malnati's, order before you're seated like it's the DMV, then ask for a knife and fork and call it \"the real thing.\" Jon Stewart roasted you on national TV and you took it personally, because deep down you knew he was right. You ship it on dry ice to your cousin in Phoenix like an organ donation. And what does the label get you? Navy Pier tourists nodding politely, a two-hour nap, and the Midwest's loudest participation trophy."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0166", "lang": "en", "target_id": "esperanto", "target_name": "Esperanto", "facts": ["Published in Warsaw in 1887 by L. L. Zamenhof, a Białystok-born eye doctor, under the pen name Doktoro Esperanto, 'one who hopes'.", "Speaker estimates range from 100,000 to two million; roughly a thousand people grow up with it as a native language.", "William Shatner starred in Incubus (1966), a horror film shot entirely in Esperanto, with pronunciation Esperantists still mock.", "Its Wikipedia has over 350,000 articles, and its Duolingo course for English speakers had 1.36 million learners by July 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "3c7c5dbcf1f4", "text": "You know the Esperanto people. They wear the green star on a lanyard and say it's \"so logical\" like they personally designed it. Ask what they're after and they'll say world peace, then admit they've been stuck on the accusative *-n* since 2016. Their plan was that one day they'd meet a Hungarian, a Brazilian, and a Japanese guy at a bus stop and all speak the same language. What they actually get is a pen pal in Lyon who stopped writing in 2019 and a Discord server with four hundred members and one active voice. They're not learning a language. They're waiting for a train Zamenhof promised in 1887, and it has never once left the station."}, "b": {"item_id": "f5faac750f03", "text": "You spent six months learning a language designed to unite humanity, and your total audience is a retired Dutch man named Henk in a Telegram group. You didn't pick Esperanto because it's useful. You picked it because Spanish had a waiting list of people who were better at it than you. Here, you can be fluent by spring and the only competition is a guy who wears the green star pin to the grocery store.\n\nYou say you believe in world peace. You also correct people's accusatives in the comments section. Zamenhof dreamed of ending war, and a century later his legacy is you, alone, translating \"The Little Prince\" for a fourth time.\n\nYour big vacation is a World Congress, where you'll finally speak to thousands of people, all of whom are also there to speak to you."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0167", "lang": "en", "target_id": "vim", "target_name": "Vim", "facts": ["Released by Bram Moolenaar in November 1991 as 'Vi IMitation', a vi clone he had begun writing for the Amiga in 1988 on top of Stevie; the expansion became 'Vi IMproved' with version 2.0 in December 1993.", "Licensed as charityware: the licence asks users to donate to ICCF Holland, a charity Moolenaar ran for children in Uganda.", "Stack Overflow's question 'How to exit the Vim editor?' passed one million visits in May 2017, which Stack Overflow marked with a blog post.", "Modal editing: in normal mode, typing letters runs commands rather than inserting text; :wq saves and quits, :q! quits and discards changes."], "a": {"item_id": "11a570c2e08d", "text": "Let's hear it for the Vim people. You can always spot one, because they'll tell you. Unprompted. Usually while you're mid-sentence about something else, like your divorce.\n\nThese folks spent three weekends tuning a .vimrc to save four seconds renaming a variable. That's a payback period of eleven thousand years, but sure, you're the efficient one.\n\nThey remapped Caps Lock to Escape and now can't type a password on anyone else's laptop. Their dotfiles repo has more stars than their actual projects. They say \"ciw\" out loud like it's a spell from a wizard who peaked in 1991.\n\nAnd what's the prize? You type :wq at the end of every Google Doc. You hit j in Slack and post the letter j to forty coworkers. Congratulations: you're the fastest typist in a room everyone already left."}, "b": {"item_id": "fc4027ce9a34", "text": "You told the new intern you use Vim before you told him your name. He just wanted to know where the printer was.\n\nYou say you never leave the home row. Correct. You never leave the house either.\n\nYou spent forty hours on your .vimrc to save eleven seconds a day, so you break even in 2031, assuming you don't switch to Neovim, which you will, and then announce it like a religious conversion.\n\nYou're not after speed. You're after the moment someone glances at your screen and asks, \"How do you even quit that?\" so you can say \"Oh, it's simple\" and type something that looks like a ransom note: `:wqa!`\n\nWhat does it get you? A macro that renames forty variables, a pinky built like a bodybuilder's, and one guy at a meetup who nods at you with exactly your kind of loneliness."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0168", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hsq", "target_name": "HSQ (e-beam resist)", "facts": ["Hydrogen silsesquioxane, sold by Dow Corning as FOx ('flowable oxide') and designed as a spin-on dielectric for chip interconnects; lithographers adopted it as a negative-tone resist.", "Exposure converts its cage structure into a cross-linked, silica-like network, so the developed pattern is essentially SiO2.", "Holds the resolution records for electron-beam lithography: isolated features around 5 nm, dense lines below 10 nm half-pitch, with high-contrast 'salty' NaOH/NaCl development.", "Must be kept refrigerated (Dow Corning's datasheet gives a six-month shelf life at 5 °C); the solution ages into a jelly, and its effective sensitivity shifts with processing delays because cross-linking continues after exposure.", "DuPont, which inherited Dow Corning's FOx/XR-1541 HSQ line, discontinued it and ended production at the end of 2025; labs such as EPFL's CMi switched to third-party HSQ from EM Resist or DisChem, both formulated from AQM powder."], "a": {"item_id": "96896ea695a3", "text": "You're switching to HSQ for resolution. Of course you are. You have a PhD in patience and a deficit in sleep, and one paper showed someone drawing 6-nanometer lines, so now you want to be that person at group meeting. Here's what HSQ gets you: a resist that gels in the bottle if you look at it wrong and, once exposed, cures into glass that stays in your device forever. Want it gone? Good luck. That's hydrofluoric acid territory. Your dose matrix is forty chips. Your TMAH developer smells like a crime scene. Six months from now you'll be at the SEM at 2 a.m., staring at lines that have slumped into a sad little heap, swearing you'll go back to PMMA. You won't. You'll put it in your thesis as a \"lesson learned.\""}, "b": {"item_id": "b0d879a960b0", "text": "So you switched to HSQ \"for the resolution.\" Sure. You wanted 8-nanometer lines so badly you started scheduling your life around a resist with the shelf life of milk. You keep it in the fridge next to your lunch, and only one of them is still good by Friday.\n\nYour write that used to take twenty minutes now takes the whole overnight slot, so the guy who needed the tool for his actual thesis is crying in the gowning room. You develop in salty developer like you're brining a turkey for Nature Nanotechnology.\n\nAnd when it goes wrong, and it will, you've basically spun glass onto your chip. Your only exit is HF, a chemical that wants you dead.\n\nAll of this for one SEM image with a scale bar so small nobody can read it. Congratulations. Your device still doesn't work, but it fails at record resolution."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0169", "lang": "en", "target_id": "crocs", "target_name": "Crocs", "facts": ["Founded in Boulder, Colorado, in 2002; the first clog debuted at the Fort Lauderdale boat show as a boating shoe.", "Made of 'Croslite', which Crocs describes as a proprietary closed-cell resin foam and which third parties identify as an injection-moulded EVA foam.", "Balenciaga sold a platform Crocs for about $850 in 2018.", "The stock fell more than 90% from its 2007 peak during the 2008 crisis; the company then boomed in the pandemic and gave away 860,000 pairs to healthcare workers in 2020.", "The holes are officially 'ventilation ports' and the charms that go in them are 'Jibbitz', a company Crocs bought in 2006."], "a": {"item_id": "8f2a3cb9a4bb", "text": "Look at the person who wore Crocs to the grocery store. Not the pool. The grocery store. This is someone who looked at a stranger's sneakers and thought, \"Too much effort.\" They want you to know they're comfortable, and they've made peace with being seen by children. They bought them in five colors, then clipped little plastic bananas into the holes like they were decorating a golf bag. And they say \"they're actually orthopedic\" like it's a get-out-of-jail-free card. Your feet aren't that tired. You just wanted to shuffle through Costco making a sound like a bath toy with every step, and honestly, I respect the commitment. Just don't call it a fashion choice. You're wearing a garden clog that gave up on the garden."}, "b": {"item_id": "d220d23f1394", "text": "You wear Crocs out of the house because you've decided comfort is a personality, and the whole Whole Foods needs to know. You didn't give up on fashion, you held a press conference about giving up. \"I don't care what people think,\" you tell a stranger in the cereal aisle, unprompted, which is caring what people think with extra steps.\n\nYou flip the heel strap forward for \"sport mode,\" as if you're about to sprint somewhere other than the Target parking lot. You say \"nurses wear them,\" and you're an accountant named Dale. You bought your kid a matching pair so you're not the only one, which is less parenting than hostage-taking.\n\nWhat it gets you: a stranger's sympathetic nod, a lifelong inability to be taken seriously at a wedding, and the smug glow of winning an argument nobody was having."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0170", "lang": "en", "target_id": "php", "target_name": "PHP", "facts": ["Created by Rasmus Lerdorf in 1994 as 'Personal Home Page Tools', a set of C programs for tracking visitors to his own résumé page; the name now officially stands for the recursive 'PHP: Hypertext Preprocessor'.", "Lerdorf, in his 2007 MySQL Conference talk 'PHP on Hormones': 'I'm not a real programmer. I throw together things until it works then I move on.'", "Runs WordPress, which W3Techs counted on 39.4% of all websites (58.1% of sites with a known CMS) on 9 November 2026.", "Core function names are inconsistent (strpos, str_replace, htmlspecialchars, nl2br) partly because, as Lerdorf confirmed, early function names were chosen to balance a hash table keyed on string length.", "Until PHP 8 (2020), the comparison 0 == 'foo' was true because the string was silently converted to a number."], "a": {"item_id": "c1d0d772e1d6", "text": "PHP developers are the only people who put a dollar sign in front of every variable and still can't afford rent. You chose a career where \"senior\" means you remember whether it's needle-haystack or haystack-needle. Your dream job was building the next Facebook; your actual job is the fourteenth WordPress plugin for a dentist in Ohio, deployed via FileZilla. Every conversation you have starts with \"PHP 8 is actually really good now,\" which is what people say about their ex after therapy. You call yourselves Laravel \"artisans,\" because \"guy who fixes the contact form\" didn't fit on LinkedIn. What does it get you? Job security, because nobody else is willing to open that codebase from 2009 that still calls mysql_real_escape_string. Congratulations: you're unfireable, the way a load-bearing wall is unfireable."}, "b": {"item_id": "4b50923aaa44", "text": "You've said \"PHP isn't what it used to be\" so often it's basically your LinkedIn headline. Nobody asked. You bring up Facebook at parties like it's your cousin's startup. You switched to Laravel and call it a fresh start, which is a man putting a tuxedo on a Honda to prove he's going places.\n\nWhat are you after? Respect. What do you get? A client named Brenda who wants her WordPress site \"just a little different\" and pays by Venmo, sometimes.\n\nYou tell recruiters \"we're on PHP 8,\" meaning staging is. Production is a file called final_v2_REAL.php, with a comment from a guy who quit in 2014: \"don't touch.\"\n\nYou've been meaning to learn Go since 2019. Your fear isn't that PHP will die. It's that it never will, and neither will your on-call rotation."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0171", "lang": "en", "target_id": "pmma", "target_name": "PMMA (e-beam resist)", "facts": ["The same polymer as Plexiglas and acrylic glass, dissolved in anisole or chlorobenzene and spun into a thin film.", "The oldest electron-beam resist still in everyday use; IBM researchers reported it as one in the late 1960s.", "Positive tone: the beam breaks polymer chains and the exposed region dissolves in a MIBK:IPA developer.", "Resolves below 10 nm but has poor sensitivity, so doses of hundreds to over a thousand µC/cm² (depending on voltage) make writing slow.", "Poor dry-etch resistance, so it is used mostly for lift-off rather than as an etch mask."], "a": {"item_id": "6303a3fe60f6", "text": "You still spin PMMA. Of course you do. Your advisor's thesis used it, his advisor's thesis used it, and somewhere a 1970s IBM engineer remains the most cutting-edge person in your academic lineage. You call it \"high resolution\" the way your uncle calls his Camry \"reliable.\" You book the e-beam overnight because your dose is measured in geological time, then dunk your chip in MIBK:IPA with a stopwatch and a prayer, chilling the developer like it's a fine Riesling. When the plasma etch eats your mask before your substrate, you just whisper \"bilayer, 495K under 950K\" like it's a personality. You're not chasing nanostructures. You're chasing a recipe nobody can blame you for. And it works: the gold lifted off beautifully. Your career is still soaking in acetone, waiting for something to come loose."}, "b": {"item_id": "b05c221e0d70", "text": "You're the person who says \"it's a classic\" about a process you inherited from a postdoc who left in 2009. Your lab notebook is just a bottle of 950K with someone else's handwriting on it. You say ZEP is \"too finicky\" while spending nine hours writing a 200 µm array because you chose a dose that could cook an egg.\n\nYou say you want 10 nm resolution, but your liftoff has the edge profile of a torn receipt, and you've been \"optimizing the bilayer\" since your qualifying exam. You treat MIBK:IPA 1:3 like a family recipe and cold development like a personality.\n\nMeanwhile the group next door switched resists, graduated, bought houses, and now send you LinkedIn requests. You stay loyal. Loyalty is great. It's just that your beam time costs more than their weddings."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0172", "lang": "en", "target_id": "klingon", "target_name": "Klingon", "facts": ["Created by linguist Marc Okrand for Star Trek III (1984), building on a few words the actor James Doohan improvised for the 1979 film.", "Deliberately alien grammar: object-verb-subject word order and no verb 'to be', so Hamlet's line became 'taH pagh taHbe'', 'continue or not continue'.", "The Klingon Language Institute (1992) published Hamlet 'in the original Klingon'; Bing Translator added Klingon in 2013 and Duolingo in 2018.", "Okrand's 1985 Klingon Dictionary listed roughly 1,500 words, and fluent speakers number only in the dozens: 20 to 30 in 2009, 30 to 50 by 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "0cbf01775821", "text": "You spent years mastering a language whose standard greeting is \"What do you want?\" and honestly, that explains the social life. You wanted a warrior culture and got a prefix chart. You can render \"to be or not to be\" as *taH pagh taHbe'*, which literally means \"continue or don't continue,\" a fair question about the hobby.\n\nYour conversation partners are forty people at a convention who all dread your corrections. You command a vocabulary built for threatening enemies, and your only battle is a verb-suffix argument with a man named Greg, who is wrong, and who knows he is wrong, and who will not yield.\n\nYou say honor is everything, yet you've earned yours in a Marriott conference room, holding a foam bat'leth, forehead ridges peeling in the airport security line.\n\nToday is a good day to die? Sir, it's Tuesday. You have a dentist appointment."}, "b": {"item_id": "c3c693f6b9ba", "text": "Nobody becomes fluent in Klingon by accident. You have to sit down and decide that the thing you want most in life is to clear your throat in a language invented for a TV show. Your friends learned Spanish for a job. You learned to say \"Qapla'\" to a bartender who has never once been impressed. You've translated Hamlet. Twice. The Klingons in the original show would read your Hamlet and call it too soft, which is the only review you've ever wanted. At conventions you're the one who corrects the guy in the Worf costume, and he has the better forehead. You're chasing a room where someone finally gets your jokes, and that room is mostly you, reading your own joke aloud, slightly out of breath from the gutturals."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0173", "lang": "en", "target_id": "vim", "target_name": "Vim", "facts": ["Released by Bram Moolenaar in November 1991 as 'Vi IMitation', a vi clone he had begun writing for the Amiga in 1988 on top of Stevie; the expansion became 'Vi IMproved' with version 2.0 in December 1993.", "Licensed as charityware: the licence asks users to donate to ICCF Holland, a charity Moolenaar ran for children in Uganda.", "Stack Overflow's question 'How to exit the Vim editor?' passed one million visits in May 2017, which Stack Overflow marked with a blog post.", "Modal editing: in normal mode, typing letters runs commands rather than inserting text; :wq saves and quits, :q! quits and discards changes."], "a": {"item_id": "321a52e03ab6", "text": "Every Vim person has to tell you about Vim. You say \"nice weather\" at brunch and they say, \"Have you tried modal editing?\" They'll sit through your wedding toast in Normal mode, just so they can feel superior while hitting Escape. Their whole personality is hjkl. They don't write code. They perform a sport nobody asked them to join. And the best part is they can't get out. Ask one how to quit and they'll smirk, type a colon, and say, \"Ah, you just need to know the commands.\" Twenty years later he's still typing :wq, still telling you it's faster. Faster than what? He's never left the room."}, "b": {"item_id": "613855b046fb", "text": "You know a Vim user is in the room because nobody else has gotten a word in. Buddy, you didn't \"become more productive.\" You spent three weekends on a .vimrc so you could save four seconds renaming a variable, and you post your dotfiles repo like it's a baby picture. You remapped Caps Lock to Escape and now treat everyone else's keyboard like a character flaw. You say \"I live in the terminal\" like it's a lifestyle and not just where you got stuck. Your whole identity is a joke about not knowing how to quit, and honestly, you never learned. Here you are, still talking. You say \"ciw\" out loud at parties. You switched to Neovim just so you'd have something new to announce. And the payoff? You edit text exactly as fast as the rest of us, hands proudly on home row, with nobody watching."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0174", "lang": "en", "target_id": "papyrus", "target_name": "Papyrus", "facts": ["Hand-drawn by Chris Costello in 1982 over about six months, when he was 23; he sold the rights to Letraset for $750.", "Bundled with macOS and with Microsoft Office, which is why it appears on so many menus and signs.", "James Cameron's Avatar (2009) used it for the film's logo and subtitles.", "A 2017 Saturday Night Live sketch starring Ryan Gosling is about a man unable to get over that choice."], "a": {"item_id": "df3aac397e9d", "text": "You opened a \"Himalayan salt cave and wellness sanctuary\" with a small-business loan and a dropdown menu. Around 11 p.m., exhausted, you scrolled past the fonts that cost money and landed on the one that whispers \"ancient healing\" to everyone, including your nephew, who \"does graphics\" and approved it.\n\nYou wanted timeless. You got the exact look of every other spa, the falafel place two doors down, and a yoga studio run by a guy named Dave who says \"namaste\" like a threat.\n\nSomewhere, Ryan Gosling is staring at your awning with the same haunted silence he gave the Avatar title, and he's right. You didn't build a brand. You built a $14 \"Pharaoh's Bowl\" for people who think Epcot counts as a passport, and you told yourself it was authentic."}, "b": {"item_id": "1988c200f894", "text": "Let's talk about the restaurant owner who picked Papyrus for the sign. You bought a franchise of \"authentic\" sourdough, you have a chalkboard that says \"Gather,\" and you think a font that looks like it was carved on a tree stump is the closest thing to a hug a customer can get. You didn't choose it. It chose you, the moment you typed \"organic\" into a font search. Now your $14 avocado toast sits under a logo that reads like a yoga instructor's grocery list. Your spa is \"a sanctuary,\" but the sign out front says \"we're still figuring out our brand.\" You wanted the calm of a Bali retreat. You got the calm of a dentist's waiting room in 2009, and somehow it still costs you $400 a month in massages you can't afford."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0175", "lang": "en", "target_id": "papyrus", "target_name": "Papyrus", "facts": ["Hand-drawn by Chris Costello in 1982 over about six months, when he was 23; he sold the rights to Letraset for $750.", "Bundled with macOS and with Microsoft Office, which is why it appears on so many menus and signs.", "James Cameron's Avatar (2009) used it for the film's logo and subtitles.", "A 2017 Saturday Night Live sketch starring Ryan Gosling is about a man unable to get over that choice."], "a": {"item_id": "df3aac397e9d", "text": "You opened a \"Himalayan salt cave and wellness sanctuary\" with a small-business loan and a dropdown menu. Around 11 p.m., exhausted, you scrolled past the fonts that cost money and landed on the one that whispers \"ancient healing\" to everyone, including your nephew, who \"does graphics\" and approved it.\n\nYou wanted timeless. You got the exact look of every other spa, the falafel place two doors down, and a yoga studio run by a guy named Dave who says \"namaste\" like a threat.\n\nSomewhere, Ryan Gosling is staring at your awning with the same haunted silence he gave the Avatar title, and he's right. You didn't build a brand. You built a $14 \"Pharaoh's Bowl\" for people who think Epcot counts as a passport, and you told yourself it was authentic."}, "b": {"item_id": "ebcdad06fe33", "text": "You opened a day spa. You wanted serenity, mystery, the ancient wisdom of the East. So you opened Microsoft Word and scrolled until something felt like a cucumber on your eyelid. Congratulations: your sanctuary now shares branding with a movie about blue cat people, a sixth-grade report on the Nile, and every church bake sale since 1998. Ryan Gosling lost sleep over James Cameron doing this. You did it for \"Serenity Nails & Wax\" and slept fine.\n\nAnd the Mediterranean bistro owner who asked, \"How do I show my hummus is authentic?\" then picked the font that came free with a Dell. Nothing says \"Grandmother's recipe\" like your nephew's laptop.\n\nYou wanted exotic. You got pre-installed. Nobody relaxes at your spa. They're too busy remembering which school project they typed it on."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0176", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hawaiian-pizza", "target_name": "Hawaiian pizza", "facts": ["Invented in 1962 by Sam Panopoulos, a Greek immigrant, at the Satellite Restaurant in Chatham, Ontario, Canada; named after the brand of canned pineapple he used.", "Iceland's president Guðni Th. Jóhannesson said in 2017 that he would ban pineapple on pizza if he could, then clarified that a president has no such power.", "Hawaii is no longer a major pineapple producer: Del Monte shut its Hawaiian pineapple operations in November 2006, two years ahead of its announced 2008 exit, the state's last cannery closed in 2007, and most canned pineapple now comes from Thailand, Indonesia, and the Philippines.", "Panopoulos, whose restaurant also served Chinese dishes, told the Toronto Sun in 2010 that 'in those days people didn't mix the tastes like sweet and sour, except for Chinese food'."], "a": {"item_id": "32c9917cf9ee", "text": "You order Hawaiian pizza the way a man buys a motorcycle at forty-five: you need everyone to know you're dangerous. Nobody asked. You say \"I know, I know, don't judge me\" to a room that wasn't judging you, and now it is. That's the plan. It's the only controversy you can afford. You'll never skydive, never quit the job, never tell the group chat what you actually think, but you will die on this hill, and the hill is a Friday night in your own kitchen.\n\nYou think you're ordering a tropical getaway. It was invented in Ontario in 1962 by a Greek guy running a restaurant called the Satellite. You're not on vacation. You're the loyal defender of a Canadian accident.\n\nYou wanted to be a maverick. You got a twenty-minute argument with your brother-in-law and a second pizza, pepperoni, that someone quietly ordered to cover for you."}, "b": {"item_id": "236a9662f31e", "text": "Ordering Hawaiian pizza is how you tell the table you've never been asked to leave a party. You're the guy who says \"I'm not a food snob, I just like what I like,\" then defends canned pineapple like it owes you money. You want to be the rebel without the risk. The pineapple makes you seem fun, the ham makes you seem like you have a plan, and the whole order lets you feel bold while the pizzeria guy reads it as \"fine.\" Your pizza was invented in Ontario in 1962, and you order it like you were there. You've never been to Hawaii. You've been to a strip-mall pizza place and called it a vacation. Next time, get the pepperoni. Nobody has to explain their pepperoni."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0177", "lang": "en", "target_id": "vision-pro", "target_name": "Apple Vision Pro", "facts": ["Released in February 2024 at $3,499, with an external battery pack on a cable rated for about two hours.", "'EyeSight' shows a rendering of the wearer's eyes on an outward-facing screen.", "Apple calls it 'spatial computing' and its App Store rules tell developers not to describe apps as AR, VR, XR, or MR.", "Reports during 2024 said Apple had cut production; analysts' first-year sales estimates were well under a million units.", "Weighs 600-650 g, before the battery."], "a": {"item_id": "ccb644462fd4", "text": "You paid $3,500 to be the only person on the L train who can't see the L train. You wanted to be noticed, and you are: everyone's filming you pinch the air like you're catching a very small, very expensive moth. You say it's for productivity, six floating windows, a virtual Mac, and you're answering one email: \"Per my last message.\" You're not in a private cinema, you're watching Moana 2 while a guy named Dennis eats a gyro against your shoulder.\n\nWhat you were after was never the screen. It was the question. \"Is that the Vision Pro?\" And you answer like it's an Oscar speech, peeling it off to reveal a red ring around your face, like a raccoon who lost a custody battle.\n\nYour reward: a dented forehead, a headache, and a stop you missed in Flushing."}, "b": {"item_id": "0dbe035a7cc0", "text": "Look at this guy on the F train, pinching at nothing like he's seasoning a ghost. He spent thirty-five hundred dollars to ride the subway on the surface of the moon, and the moon still smells like pee. He cranked the Digital Crown all the way to Joshua Tree, and the mariachi band found him anyway. That's the dream: rich enough to be somewhere else, broke enough to still be on the F. And he *wants* you to notice. That's why his fake cartoon eyes glow on the outside, so strangers know he's \"present.\" Buddy, nobody's checking. You're on Mount Hood. He gets two hours of battery and three stops of dignity, with a cable snaking into his pocket like a subway IV drip. Meanwhile, the guy next to him reading an actual book? That guy's already somewhere else, for free."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0178", "lang": "en", "target_id": "google-sheets", "target_name": "Google Sheets", "facts": ["Launched in June 2006 as Google Spreadsheets, built on XL2Web, a product Google acquired from 2Web Technologies.", "A spreadsheet is capped at 10 million cells (raised from 5 million in 2022).", "Functions such as IMPORTHTML, IMPORTXML, and GOOGLEFINANCE pull live web data straight into cells.", "Free with a Google account; files live in Google Drive, and collaborators edit the same sheet simultaneously with coloured cursors.", "In a browser, offline editing works only in Chrome or Edge, with the Google Docs Offline extension installed and offline access switched on while still connected; the Android and iOS apps work offline natively."], "a": {"item_id": "ed3e91782987", "text": "Let's hear it for the manager who runs a twelve-person team out of a Google Sheet. You didn't want to pay for Asana; you wanted to feel like you *built* Asana. Look at you, color-coding rows like a kindergarten teacher with admin rights. Your whole org chart is a tab called \"Roster FINAL (2).\" Your performance reviews are conditional formatting: green means safe, red means Kyle. And every morning you open it, see Anonymous Capybara lurking at row 400, and feel genuinely hunted. Somebody sorted one column without the others last Tuesday, and now Priya reports to the office printer. You fixed it through version history like a detective reconstructing a murder. That's the dream, right? Control. Total visibility. And what you got is forty \"Request edit access\" emails and a VLOOKUP holding up the company like a Jenga block."}, "b": {"item_id": "b196e566b5fd", "text": "You're not a manager, you're a guy who discovered conditional formatting and mistook it for leadership. You call it \"our single source of truth,\" which is adorable, because there are nine tabs, and three of them are named \"Copy of Q3 (DO NOT USE).\"\n\nOne IMPORTRANGE holds your entire department together, and you're the only one who knows. When it breaks, \"Loading...\" becomes your team's mission statement.\n\nYou wanted visibility, and you got it: an anonymous capybara hovering over your cell while someone you hired quietly overwrites your formula with a hardcoded 47. You wanted accountability, so you @-tagged Greg in a comment eleven times. Greg has never opened a comment. Greg's row is yellow. Yellow means something. Nobody remembers what.\n\nYou say you'll \"move to a real tool next quarter\" while building a dropdown menu like it's a cathedral.\n\nYour career highlight is a pivot table. Your legacy is \"Request edit access.\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0179", "lang": "en", "target_id": "oat-milk", "target_name": "Oat milk", "facts": ["Oatly started in Sweden in the 1990s, built on Lund University research by Rickard Öste on lactose intolerance.", "Oatly's slogan 'It's like milk but made for humans' was banned in Sweden in 2015 after the dairy lobby LRF Mjölk sued; Oatly then published the lawsuit as advertising.", "Oatly listed on Nasdaq in May 2021 at a valuation near $10 billion; the shares later lost more than 90% of their value.", "Commercial oat milk is made by breaking oat starch into maltose with amylase enzymes, and many brands, Oatly among them, add rapeseed oil; the result has more sugar and a higher glycaemic index than almond or soy milk."], "a": {"item_id": "4f077d2ff349", "text": "You didn't order oat milk because you're lactose intolerant. You ordered it because you read one article about almonds in 2019 and it became your whole personality.\n\nNow you say \"oat\" to the barista like a secret password to a club that has no door. Seventy-five cents extra, and you act like you've personally reversed the polar ice caps. You then fly to Lisbon for a long weekend.\n\nYou've told three people at dinner that you \"feel better since cutting dairy.\" Feel better how? Your cheese board arrived and you ate it like a raccoon at a wedding.\n\nYou know the brand name. You have opinions about the brand name. You once said \"barista blend\" out loud, on purpose, to a friend.\n\nThe cow was fine, Brad. The cow didn't need you to be brave."}, "b": {"item_id": "6f1acdf77ea0", "text": "Oat milk people, let's be honest: you're not lactose intolerant. I've watched you eat a whole burrata at brunch. You switched because almond milk got canceled for drinking California dry, and you needed a new personality before anyone noticed you'd had the old one for six years.\n\nYou pay eighty cents extra to drink breakfast. Your grandma ate oats so she could afford milk. You're paying more for oats so you can say \"I just find it creamier.\"\n\nAnd you tell the barista \"barista edition, if you have it,\" like you're ordering a vintage. Same person who reads every label hunting for seed oils. Guess what's in your porridge latte, Kayleigh? Rapeseed oil.\n\nYou didn't save the planet. You flew to Lisbon twice this year. You just found a way to sigh at a cow."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0180", "lang": "en", "target_id": "esperanto", "target_name": "Esperanto", "facts": ["Published in Warsaw in 1887 by L. L. Zamenhof, a Białystok-born eye doctor, under the pen name Doktoro Esperanto, 'one who hopes'.", "Speaker estimates range from 100,000 to two million; roughly a thousand people grow up with it as a native language.", "William Shatner starred in Incubus (1966), a horror film shot entirely in Esperanto, with pronunciation Esperantists still mock.", "Its Wikipedia has over 350,000 articles, and its Duolingo course for English speakers had 1.36 million learners by July 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "09a618d282b4", "text": "Who learns Esperanto? People who've concluded the world's real problem is that nobody agreed on the grammar. They wear the green star on their lapel. They say \"Saluton!\" to the barista and watch her face do nothing. Their dream is one planet, one neutral tongue, and their first step is finding eleven other people in their city who share it. That's how they end up in a Leeds community hall with a plate of biscuits and Gerald, who has been \"nearly fluent\" since 1994. They have a pen pal in Brazil they've never met, and they write to him in flawless accusative. What does it get them? A hug from a stranger at a congress, and the quiet pride of being fluent in a language that mostly exists in other Esperantists' inboxes."}, "b": {"item_id": "f5faac750f03", "text": "You spent six months learning a language designed to unite humanity, and your total audience is a retired Dutch man named Henk in a Telegram group. You didn't pick Esperanto because it's useful. You picked it because Spanish had a waiting list of people who were better at it than you. Here, you can be fluent by spring and the only competition is a guy who wears the green star pin to the grocery store.\n\nYou say you believe in world peace. You also correct people's accusatives in the comments section. Zamenhof dreamed of ending war, and a century later his legacy is you, alone, translating \"The Little Prince\" for a fourth time.\n\nYour big vacation is a World Congress, where you'll finally speak to thousands of people, all of whom are also there to speak to you."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0181", "lang": "en", "target_id": "latex", "target_name": "LaTeX", "facts": ["Leslie Lamport wrote LaTeX in the early 1980s and released it in 1984 as a macro layer on Donald Knuth's TeX (1978), which Knuth built because he disliked how the galley proofs of the new edition of his own book looked.", "TeX's version number converges on π (3.141592653 since 2021), and Knuth's TeX bug bounty started at one hexadecimal dollar, $2.56, doubling yearly until frozen at $327.68; he stopped mailing real cheques in 2008 after fraud and now credits accounts at his fictional Bank of San Serriffe, and only 9 of the 275 cheques he sent from 2006 on were ever cashed.", "Its best-known warning is 'Underfull \\hbox (badness 10000)'; overfull lines are reported as 'Overfull \\hbox (Npt too wide)', and figures float to wherever the algorithm decides, not where the author put them.", "A single missing brace can produce an error reported pages away from the mistake, and a document can need three compiler passes to get its references right."], "a": {"item_id": "88a7647c2b15", "text": "Let's talk about the guy who writes a two-page memo in LaTeX. Your memo is about the break room fridge, but it's in Computer Modern, so everyone reads it expecting it to end with a theorem. \"Fridge Policy, Section 1.1.\" Buddy, there's no Section 1.2. You spent forty minutes on a preamble longer than the memo. Twelve \\usepackage lines to say \"label your yogurt.\" You compiled it three times because the cross-reference said \"see Section ??\" on page one, referencing page one. You're not after clarity. You're after someone asking, \"Is this LaTeX?\" so you can say \"Oh, habit,\" like a man who still mentions his thesis at parties. And it gets you exactly one thing: a PDF nobody can edit, so when Karen fixes a typo, she retypes the whole thing in Word."}, "b": {"item_id": "c59587efb75e", "text": "Nobody writes a two-page memo in LaTeX because they need to. They do it so they can be the only person in the building whose \\textbf{Action Items} shine in Computer Modern. They type \\documentclass{article} like they're defending a dissertation, then lose an entire afternoon to a missing package just to shave a quarter inch off the margin. They're not informing Denise in accounting. They're proving they know what \\vspace does. And what does it get them? A PDF that looks exactly like Word, except it took six hours, a compile error on line 47, and Denise replying, \"Can you just send the Word file?\" They'll insist the kerning is superior. Nobody has ever checked the kerning."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0182", "lang": "en", "target_id": "oat-milk", "target_name": "Oat milk", "facts": ["Oatly started in Sweden in the 1990s, built on Lund University research by Rickard Öste on lactose intolerance.", "Oatly's slogan 'It's like milk but made for humans' was banned in Sweden in 2015 after the dairy lobby LRF Mjölk sued; Oatly then published the lawsuit as advertising.", "Oatly listed on Nasdaq in May 2021 at a valuation near $10 billion; the shares later lost more than 90% of their value.", "Commercial oat milk is made by breaking oat starch into maltose with amylase enzymes, and many brands, Oatly among them, add rapeseed oil; the result has more sugar and a higher glycaemic index than almond or soy milk."], "a": {"item_id": "6f1acdf77ea0", "text": "Oat milk people, let's be honest: you're not lactose intolerant. I've watched you eat a whole burrata at brunch. You switched because almond milk got canceled for drinking California dry, and you needed a new personality before anyone noticed you'd had the old one for six years.\n\nYou pay eighty cents extra to drink breakfast. Your grandma ate oats so she could afford milk. You're paying more for oats so you can say \"I just find it creamier.\"\n\nAnd you tell the barista \"barista edition, if you have it,\" like you're ordering a vintage. Same person who reads every label hunting for seed oils. Guess what's in your porridge latte, Kayleigh? Rapeseed oil.\n\nYou didn't save the planet. You flew to Lisbon twice this year. You just found a way to sigh at a cow."}, "b": {"item_id": "a7a78e709f8d", "text": "You said \"Can I do oat?\" like you're asking the barista for permission to have a personality. Nobody asked. The line behind you did the math on that 75-cent upcharge and found a person who wants to be seen caring.\n\nYou're not lactose intolerant. You're \"lactose-skeptical.\" You quit almond milk the week a documentary told you almonds were the problem, and you've been switching milks like a politician switching parties, always just before the scandal breaks. Now you're on the right side of history, in a cup with a plastic lid.\n\nYou tell people you're \"mostly plant-based,\" which means you had a salad once near a farmers market. You'll mention the oat thing on a first date, and she'll smile and start planning the exit.\n\nFour dollars for a moral victory. Nobody's clapping, Dylan."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0183", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hsq", "target_name": "HSQ (e-beam resist)", "facts": ["Hydrogen silsesquioxane, sold by Dow Corning as FOx ('flowable oxide') and designed as a spin-on dielectric for chip interconnects; lithographers adopted it as a negative-tone resist.", "Exposure converts its cage structure into a cross-linked, silica-like network, so the developed pattern is essentially SiO2.", "Holds the resolution records for electron-beam lithography: isolated features around 5 nm, dense lines below 10 nm half-pitch, with high-contrast 'salty' NaOH/NaCl development.", "Must be kept refrigerated (Dow Corning's datasheet gives a six-month shelf life at 5 °C); the solution ages into a jelly, and its effective sensitivity shifts with processing delays because cross-linking continues after exposure.", "DuPont, which inherited Dow Corning's FOx/XR-1541 HSQ line, discontinued it and ended production at the end of 2025; labs such as EPFL's CMi switched to third-party HSQ from EM Resist or DisChem, both formulated from AQM powder."], "a": {"item_id": "91f701649a72", "text": "You switched to HSQ for the resolution. Of course you did. PMMA gave you 20-nanometer lines, which is fine for science but not for the cover of a journal your advisor brings up at dinner parties. Now you're writing 8-nanometer lines that nobody can see without a $2 million microscope you booked three weeks ago.\n\nYou pay triple for a tiny bottle that expires faster than your funding. You keep it cold like insulin and spin it knowing it's already going bad. Your dose is ten times higher, so your \"quick write\" runs overnight, and the e-beam tool manager who used to love you is now drafting an email. You develop in TMAH like you're brewing something, and the lines collapse anyway.\n\nAnd the best part: it never comes off. You wanted resolution, and you got permanent glass sitting on your device. Congratulations, you made something incredibly small that doesn't work."}, "b": {"item_id": "5b587d8cb24c", "text": "You're switching to HSQ for resolution? Sure. Nobody picks HSQ because it's fun. You saw one SEM image of six-nanometer lines and decided you'd be the person in that image. Your PI already put \"sub-10 nm\" in the grant renewal. Now you've got a bottle in the fridge that goes bad if you look at it wrong, a dose matrix that's eaten two months, and a TMAH developer you're mixing like a cocktail bartender. You'll spend Tuesday at 3 a.m. staring at a SEM, sure your lines are perfect, while they quietly lean over and fuse into their neighbors. Congrats on the resolution. Your advisor is still going to ask about throughput."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0184", "lang": "en", "target_id": "crocs", "target_name": "Crocs", "facts": ["Founded in Boulder, Colorado, in 2002; the first clog debuted at the Fort Lauderdale boat show as a boating shoe.", "Made of 'Croslite', which Crocs describes as a proprietary closed-cell resin foam and which third parties identify as an injection-moulded EVA foam.", "Balenciaga sold a platform Crocs for about $850 in 2018.", "The stock fell more than 90% from its 2007 peak during the 2008 crisis; the company then boomed in the pandemic and gave away 860,000 pairs to healthcare workers in 2020.", "The holes are officially 'ventilation ports' and the charms that go in them are 'Jibbitz', a company Crocs bought in 2006."], "a": {"item_id": "837863df816e", "text": "Nobody wears Crocs out of the house to look good. They wear them to announce that they've stopped trying, and they want a medal for it. These are people who think the grocery store is a hospital hallway and they're the surgeon on break. They walk into Costco with forty holes in their feet and call the holes ventilation. Then they jam little plastic frogs and tiny tacos into those holes so the world knows they have a personality, and that personality is \"I gave up in 2009.\" What they get out of it is one sentence they say with their whole chest: \"They're just so comfortable.\" Meanwhile every tile floor in America sounds like a suction cup breathing through its teeth."}, "b": {"item_id": "d220d23f1394", "text": "You wear Crocs out of the house because you've decided comfort is a personality, and the whole Whole Foods needs to know. You didn't give up on fashion, you held a press conference about giving up. \"I don't care what people think,\" you tell a stranger in the cereal aisle, unprompted, which is caring what people think with extra steps.\n\nYou flip the heel strap forward for \"sport mode,\" as if you're about to sprint somewhere other than the Target parking lot. You say \"nurses wear them,\" and you're an accountant named Dale. You bought your kid a matching pair so you're not the only one, which is less parenting than hostage-taking.\n\nWhat it gets you: a stranger's sympathetic nod, a lifelong inability to be taken seriously at a wedding, and the smug glow of winning an argument nobody was having."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0185", "lang": "en", "target_id": "carbon-nanotubes", "target_name": "Carbon nanotubes", "facts": ["Reported by Sumio Iijima in Nature in 1991.", "Promised as the cable for a space elevator; a 2006 analysis showed that ordinary defects cut the strength of a macroscopic cable far below the ideal value.", "Long, rigid multi-walled nanotubes behaved like asbestos fibres in a 2008 mouse study in Nature Nanotechnology.", "Sorting metallic from semiconducting tubes has blocked nanotube electronics for three decades; MIT's 16-bit RV16X-NANO processor (2019) is still a lab demonstration.", "Today they are sold mainly as a conductive additive in lithium-ion battery electrodes."], "a": {"item_id": "d717e15fa133", "text": "You've been \"two years from commercialization\" since the Clinton administration. Your founder has a PhD, a patent, and the confidence of a man who's never had to ship anything in a quantity larger than a vial.\n\nSlide three is the space elevator. Everyone knows it's a joke, including you, but you keep it in because it's the only slide where the investors stop checking their phones.\n\nYou say \"TAM\" and mean \"every industry that has ever existed.\" Batteries, concrete, tennis rackets, sunscreen. You've pitched so many markets you've never actually sold to one.\n\nWhat are you after? A seed round, a lab with your name on it, and one more year before anyone asks about price per kilogram.\n\nWhat do you get? A warm handshake, a \"fascinating science,\" and a follow-up email that says \"keep us posted.\""}, "b": {"item_id": "9ad1a4c3964e", "text": "Let's hear it for the carbon nanotube founders, the only people who've been \"five years from commercialization\" since 1991. You can spot them at any pitch day: a postdoc in a blazer that still smells like the clean room, opening with a space elevator and closing with a seed round. Bold move, promising a cable to orbit when your entire production run fits in a vial labeled \"DO NOT INHALE.\" They swear they'll replace silicon. Silicon has heard this every year since Clinton and keeps shipping. They say \"a hundred times stronger than steel\" the way your uncle says he could've gone pro. And what does it get them? A Nature cover, a term sheet, and a quiet pivot to \"conductive additive for batteries.\" The dream: a ladder to space. The exit: slightly better tennis rackets."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0186", "lang": "en", "target_id": "microsoft-teams", "target_name": "Microsoft Teams", "facts": ["Launched in 2017, bundled at no extra charge into Office 365, and replaced Skype for Business.", "After Slack's antitrust complaint, Microsoft unbundled Teams from Office in the EU in 2023 and worldwide in 2024; the European Commission still sent a statement of objections in 2024.", "Microsoft reported 320 million monthly active users in 2023.", "Microsoft shut down consumer Skype in May 2025 and sent its users to Teams."], "a": {"item_id": "8ecdfe8fdf7c", "text": "Let's hear it for the IT director who chose Microsoft Teams, a man who has never once chosen anything. It came bundled with Outlook, like coleslaw you're contractually obligated to eat. The EU had to force Microsoft to stop shoving it on people, and your company still went back for seconds. The whole pitch was \"It's already in the license.\" So was Clippy, Gary.\n\nManagement loves it because now they can watch your little dot. Green means productive. Yellow means you went to the bathroom and Linda noticed. They didn't want collaboration. They wanted a heart monitor.\n\nAnd what did it get them? Every meeting opens with eight adults asking \"Can you see my screen?\" while a VP uses the raise-hand button to interrupt people *harder*. Congratulations: you saved nine dollars a seat and lost the will to live."}, "b": {"item_id": "b048a3892478", "text": "Nobody asked for Teams. The people who forced it on us need a green dot next to your name to feel alive. Your manager has seventeen channels named after a project cancelled in March, and he still posts \"Quick question!\" at 6:52 p.m. just so you'll see the three dots and feel summoned. The IT guy who rolled it out got a bonus for \"adoption rates,\" which means he counted every time you clicked the purple icon by accident. They want proof you're working. Your status says \"Available\" while you're in the bathroom, and they're thrilled, because now they can watch you be available. Then comes the all-hands where forty people unmute at once, someone's dog barks, and the host says, \"Let's take this offline.\" They've never once been offline."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0187", "lang": "en", "target_id": "las-vegas", "target_name": "Las Vegas", "facts": ["Nevada legalized casino gambling in 1931, the year construction of the Hoover Dam began nearby.", "Most of the Strip lies not in the city of Las Vegas but in the unincorporated town of Paradise.", "The Sphere, opened in 2023 at a cost of about $2.3 billion, is the largest spherical building in the world.", "'What happens here, stays here' was launched by the local convention and visitors authority in 2003.", "Lake Mead, its water source, fell in July 2022 to about 1,040 feet, its lowest since the reservoir first filled in 1937, and dropped below that mark again in August 2026."], "a": {"item_id": "d992f33818f6", "text": "You're not going to Vegas to get rich. You're going to Vegas to donate to a fountain show. Look at you, booking a $49 Spirit flight to \"beat the house,\" like the house didn't build a fake Eiffel Tower with money from guys exactly like you. You've got a roulette \"system.\" You wrote it on a cocktail napkin. Red's due. Red is never due. Red doesn't know you. By midnight you're on Fremont Street feeding a Wheel of Fortune slot in an Ed Hardy shirt you've owned since 2008, telling a stranger you're \"only down twelve hundred.\" By 3 a.m. the Players Club bumps you to Platinum, which is casino for \"we'll be needing your kidneys.\" And you'll call it a win, because they comped you a buffet. Congratulations. You flew 400 miles to pay $3,000 for crab legs."}, "b": {"item_id": "75124632afe4", "text": "Every guy who drives to Vegas to get rich has the same plan. He's going to win one hand of blackjack, put the money in his pocket, and walk out a new man. Two days later he's in the parking garage, sitting in his Camry, calling his mom to ask if she can wire him gas money. He's wearing the same shirt. He's got a little paper cup of quarters and the dead look of a man who just found out the house is the house. And then he goes back in. Why? Because somewhere in there a guy in a vest told him \"you're due.\" Nobody's due. The guy in the vest is due for a pension and a deep sea cruise. Nobody's getting rich in Vegas except the people who built the buffet you ate at after losing your rent."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0188", "lang": "en", "target_id": "windows-8", "target_name": "Windows 8", "facts": ["Released in October 2012; removed the Start button and booted into a full-screen grid of tiles designed for touch screens most PCs did not have.", "The 8.1 update in 2013 put a Start button back, Windows 10 (2015) restored the Start menu, and Microsoft skipped the name Windows 9 altogether.", "Microsoft dropped the 'Metro' name in August 2012 after reports that German retailer Metro AG had objected on trademark grounds; Microsoft called 'Metro' a code name and denied any litigation, and the style was afterwards known informally as 'Modern UI'.", "The 'Charms bar' hid Search, Share, and Settings behind an invisible swipe from the right edge.", "Windows RT, the ARM edition launched alongside it, could not run ordinary Windows programs; Microsoft wrote down $900 million of unsold Surface RT tablets in 2013."], "a": {"item_id": "4880b71f9444", "text": "You put Windows 8 on a desktop. A tower. Under a desk. With a mouse. Nobody in your house has ever touched that monitor except to wipe it, yet there you are, dragging your cursor into the top-right corner like a man feeling for a light switch in a stranger's house.\n\nYou wanted to be early to the future. Instead you got a full-screen weather report you never requested and a ten-minute hunt for the shutdown button, which you finally found by Googling it on your phone, like someone searching for the exit in a building they own.\n\nThen you downloaded a third-party Start menu, so now you've spent a weekend undoing the upgrade you chose on purpose.\n\nAnd when your friends ask how it is, you say, \"It's fine once you get used to it.\" That's also what hostages say."}, "b": {"item_id": "a78f1ebf5710", "text": "Let's hand a medal to the brave souls still running Windows 8 on a desktop. You looked at a perfectly good monitor and thought, what it's missing is a Start screen full of tiles. You wanted the Charms bar, that secret strip you summon by swiping in from the right edge, so you could find Shutdown like you're opening a spy drawer. You keep telling people, \"I'm fine with the tiles.\" Nobody asked about the tiles. Your Solitaire is a live tile now, and it's been live since 2012. You're waiting for the Start button to return the way the rest of us wait for a bus cancelled in 2013. Then a coworker asks why your desktop looks like a phone that lost an argument with a fridge, and you say, \"It's a choice.\" It is a choice. You make it every morning, and the tiles judge you quietly."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0189", "lang": "en", "target_id": "juicero", "target_name": "Juicero", "facts": ["A Wi-Fi-connected cold-press juicer launched in 2016 at $699, later cut to $399, that squeezed proprietary bags of chopped produce sold by subscription.", "Raised about $120 million from investors including Google Ventures and Kleiner Perkins.", "In April 2017, Bloomberg reporters squeezed the packs by hand and got 7.5 ounces of juice in 90 seconds, faster than the machine's 8 ounces in about two minutes.", "Each pack carried a QR code that the press scanned and checked online before squeezing; it refused expired packs and would not run at all unless connected to Wi-Fi.", "Shut down in September 2017 and offered refunds on the machine."], "a": {"item_id": "3f1bb8138434", "text": "Let's talk about the people who paid four hundred dollars for a Juicero. You know who you are. You wanted to feel like you had a private chef, so you bought a machine that needs Wi-Fi to check your bag. Then the internet discovered the bag was the real product, a pouch of chopped kale you could squeeze with your bare hands. Which you did, eventually, in front of guests, because nothing says \"tech visionary\" like hand-pressing a fruit packet while your friend holds a forty-dollar blender. You weren't buying juice. You were buying the feeling of being early to something, and the only thing you got early was the eBay listing."}, "b": {"item_id": "4defd00efed2", "text": "Four hundred dollars. You paid four hundred dollars so a machine could check with your Wi-Fi before letting you have breakfast. You're the guy who says \"disrupt\" at Thanksgiving. You didn't want juice; you wanted to be the kind of person a juice startup would pitch to. And it worked! Investors handed that company a hundred and twenty million dollars, and you volunteered to be the exit strategy. Then Bloomberg showed the world you could squeeze the packets with your bare hands, faster, for free. So you paid four hundred dollars to get outperformed by your own grip. Your grandmother squeezed oranges every morning for sixty years. You subscribed to a bag. Somewhere tonight, a router outage stands between a venture capitalist and his kale, and honestly? The router's the smartest thing in that kitchen."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0190", "lang": "en", "target_id": "excel", "target_name": "Microsoft Excel", "facts": ["First shipped for the Macintosh in 1985; the Windows version followed in 1987.", "Silently converts gene names such as SEPT2 and MARCH1 into dates; by August 2020 the HUGO Gene Nomenclature Committee had renamed 27 human genes (MARCH1 became MARCHF1, SEPT2 became SEPTIN2) and published guidelines in Nature Genetics to stop it.", "In October 2020, Public Health England lost 15,841 COVID-19 cases when a legacy .xls file hit its 65,536-row limit.", "Reinhart and Rogoff's 2010 paper 'Growth in a Time of Debt', cited for austerity policies, omitted five countries through a spreadsheet range error found by a graduate student in 2013.", "Sheets have been capped at 1,048,576 rows by 16,384 columns since Excel 2007."], "a": {"item_id": "82eca86ba5a5", "text": "Nobody runs a company on Excel. Dave runs a company on Budget_FINAL_v7_USE_THIS_ONE.xlsx, which only Dave can open without the screen freezing for four minutes. Dave believes in control. Dave has forty tabs, and every one is named after a feeling: Forecast, Forecast2, Do Not Touch. He colors cells yellow for \"approved\" and orange for \"probably.\" His VLOOKUP holds payroll together like a prayer, and one #REF! away from a heart attack. He'll explain it to you at the Christmas party, sweating through a merged-cell cardigan. Dave doesn't want to be CEO. Dave wants you to need him."}, "b": {"item_id": "0b80e15aa132", "text": "You people run a forty-million-dollar company out of a file called Budget_FINAL_v9_USE_THIS_ONE.xlsx. You don't want software. You want power: specifically, being the only person alive who knows why cell AF212 is hardcoded to 7. Your entire supply chain hangs on a VLOOKUP Gary wrote in 2011, and Gary is dead. Well, he's in Boise. Same thing. You call it \"agile.\" Your auditors call it \"a crime scene with conditional formatting.\" Every quarter-end the file says \"locked for editing by Brenda,\" and the whole company just waits for Brenda. And when you finally hit row 1,048,576, did you buy a database? No. You started Sheet2. That's not a business. That's a hostage situation with freeze panes."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0191", "lang": "en", "target_id": "oat-milk", "target_name": "Oat milk", "facts": ["Oatly started in Sweden in the 1990s, built on Lund University research by Rickard Öste on lactose intolerance.", "Oatly's slogan 'It's like milk but made for humans' was banned in Sweden in 2015 after the dairy lobby LRF Mjölk sued; Oatly then published the lawsuit as advertising.", "Oatly listed on Nasdaq in May 2021 at a valuation near $10 billion; the shares later lost more than 90% of their value.", "Commercial oat milk is made by breaking oat starch into maltose with amylase enzymes, and many brands, Oatly among them, add rapeseed oil; the result has more sugar and a higher glycaemic index than almond or soy milk."], "a": {"item_id": "a7a78e709f8d", "text": "You said \"Can I do oat?\" like you're asking the barista for permission to have a personality. Nobody asked. The line behind you did the math on that 75-cent upcharge and found a person who wants to be seen caring.\n\nYou're not lactose intolerant. You're \"lactose-skeptical.\" You quit almond milk the week a documentary told you almonds were the problem, and you've been switching milks like a politician switching parties, always just before the scandal breaks. Now you're on the right side of history, in a cup with a plastic lid.\n\nYou tell people you're \"mostly plant-based,\" which means you had a salad once near a farmers market. You'll mention the oat thing on a first date, and she'll smile and start planning the exit.\n\nFour dollars for a moral victory. Nobody's clapping, Dylan."}, "b": {"item_id": "0a1c5a53fa43", "text": "Let's talk about the oat milk people. You're not lactose intolerant. You've never been tested. You just heard cows are bad and almonds are thirsty, so you picked the one grain that sounds like a farm without the farmer. Congratulations: you pay an extra eighty cents to drink liquid porridge with a splash of rapeseed oil, and you call it \"cleaner.\" You switched from almond the exact week a podcast told you California was in a drought. That's not values, that's a trend with a loyalty card. And the best part? You order it with an air of quiet suffering, like the barista should thank you for saving the planet one Barista Edition carton at a time. Your grandmother ate oats so she could survive winter. You drink them so a stranger in line thinks you've been to therapy."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0192", "lang": "en", "target_id": "risk", "target_name": "Risk", "facts": ["Invented in 1957 by Albert Lamorisse, the French director of The Red Balloon, as La Conquête du Monde; Parker Brothers published it in the United States in 1959.", "The map has 42 territories, among them Kamchatka, Irkutsk, and Yakutsk, while the entire Middle East is one territory.", "Holding Australia, which has a single entry point, is the standard opening strategy.", "A full game can run for hours, and players eliminated early have nothing to do but watch."], "a": {"item_id": "2847036f4a61", "text": "You people who play Risk to the end are a special breed. Six hours in, Dad's still holed up in Australia like it's a tax shelter, your cousin is defending Kamchatka like he was born there, and nobody remembers why we hate Steve, only that we do. You're not after world domination. You're after the exact moment your sister breaks the nonaggression pact in Ukraine, so you can bring it up at Thanksgiving for eleven years. And what does winning get you? A board covered entirely in your color, a table of people who no longer speak to you, and the hollow joy of conquering Earth with the luck of a man who only rolls double sixes when defending Irkutsk. Congratulations, Napoleon. It's 2 a.m. The pizza's cold. Your empire goes back in the box."}, "b": {"item_id": "d3ed8f3ba762", "text": "Nobody plays Risk to the end. You're the guy who got knocked out at nine o'clock and has been sitting there with a lukewarm Coke, watching you. You're the one who drew Australia, held it for three hours, and now you give speeches about continents like it's the UN. Your whole personality is three blue armies sitting on Kamchatka. You negotiated a truce with your brother-in-law, broke it with a six-three dice roll, and called it strategy. You don't want the world. You want your name on the board at the end, defending a little plastic pile of soldiers until somebody's mom calls everyone to dinner. And the worst part? You'll win a game where the dice decided everything, and you'll still say, \"I was playing the long game.\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0193", "lang": "en", "target_id": "windows-vista", "target_name": "Windows Vista", "facts": ["Released to consumers in January 2007, after the 'Longhorn' project was reset in 2004 and headline features such as the WinFS file system were dropped.", "User Account Control prompted so often that Apple's 'Get a Mac' campaign ran an ad about 'Cancel or Allow'.", "'Windows Vista Capable' stickers went on PCs that could only run Home Basic, without the Aero interface, and led to a class-action lawsuit.", "Windows 7 (2009), widely praised, was largely a tuned-up Vista on the same kernel line; Vista support ended in April 2017."], "a": {"item_id": "c19db756674c", "text": "Congratulations to everyone who installed Vista on launch day. You read \"wait for Service Pack 1\" and thought, \"That's advice for people with something to lose.\" You bought a new graphics card just to make your windows see-through. Finally, your windows are as transparent as your reasons.\n\nNow your printer is a paperweight, your scanner is a rumor, and the driver is \"coming soon\" from a company whose website still says 2004. Every click triggers a box asking if you meant it. You've said yes forty times today. You're not secure, you're just trained.\n\nBut you'll tell coworkers it's \"actually smooth once you turn a few things off.\" Sir, you paid four hundred dollars to turn things off. Meanwhile Dave, on XP, finished his spreadsheet and went home."}, "b": {"item_id": "2ca90ba91029", "text": "Day-one Vista people. You paid $399 for Ultimate Edition because you wanted to be the kind of man whose desktop wallpaper moves. DreamScene. You paid extra for animated wallpaper. Microsoft said \"The Wow starts now,\" and you believed them, lined up at CompUSA at midnight like it was a Harry Potter release.\n\nAnd what did you get? A computer that asks permission. You'd open a folder and it would dim the whole screen like a disappointed dad: \"Cancel or Allow?\" You clicked Allow four hundred times a day. Your printer stopped working. Your sound card stopped working. Your Windows Experience Index came back 2.1, and you took that personally.\n\nBut you had glass window borders. You had a clock gadget. You had a sticker that said \"Vista Capable,\" which, like you, was technically true and fooled absolutely no one."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0194", "lang": "en", "target_id": "keurig", "target_name": "Keurig", "facts": ["John Sylvan invented the K-Cup in the early 1990s and sold his stake for about $50,000 in 1997; in 2015 he told The Atlantic, 'I feel bad sometimes that I ever did it.'", "Keurig 2.0 (2014) read an ink mark on the lid and rejected unlicensed pods; sales fell and workarounds spread online.", "By 2014 enough K-Cups were sold each year to circle the Earth more than ten times; the pods were mostly unrecyclable #7 plastic until a switch to polypropylene by 2020.", "Keurig Green Mountain merged with Dr Pepper Snapple in 2018 to form Keurig Dr Pepper."], "a": {"item_id": "299bf476f938", "text": "You know who makes coffee in a Keurig? People who want to feel like a barista without the one thing a barista needs, which is caring. You're not making coffee. You're pressing a button and standing there for ninety seconds like the machine owes you a personality. Your mug says \"Coffee Snob,\" and you bought it in the same aisle as the 48-count variety pack, because you can't commit to one flavor, just like your gym membership. Hazelnut, Dark Magic, Donut Shop, and one mystery pod called Winter Blend that tastes like a wet cardboard box. You tell people you're \"not really a coffee person\" while on your third cup before noon. Your goal isn't flavor. It's reaching the office by 8:15 with something warm in your hand and a plausible excuse for why you still feel a little hollow."}, "b": {"item_id": "9c5b82448851", "text": "Keurig people. You wanted coffee but couldn't commit to a whole pot, like a man ordering one buffalo wing. You pay forty dollars a pound for grounds, as long as they arrive in a thimble you throw away. You bought the variety pack, drank every Breakfast Blend in a week, and now there are eleven hot cocoas and a chai you're saving for the apocalypse. You press \"Strong\" like you're negotiating with it. The guy who invented the pod says he regrets it. You don't. You display your pods on a spinning carousel like fine cigars, and the one time you peeled the foil off to recycle one, you told people about it for a month. You didn't want coffee. You wanted to feel like a hotel guest in your own kitchen. Congratulations: it tastes exactly like that."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0195", "lang": "en", "target_id": "macau", "target_name": "Macau", "facts": ["A Portuguese settlement from the 1550s until the handover to China in December 1999.", "Casino revenue passed the Las Vegas Strip's in 2006, and at its 2013 peak, about $45 billion, was roughly seven times the Strip's.", "One company, STDM, held the casino monopoly for four decades until licences opened to competition in 2002.", "Among the most densely populated places on Earth, at roughly 20,000 people per square kilometre.", "The Cotai Strip stands on land reclaimed from the sea between the islands of Taipa and Coloane."], "a": {"item_id": "e28d81baf3eb", "text": "Look at this guy. He lost his savings in Vegas, so naturally he's crossed an ocean to Macau to win it back. Nothing says \"I've learned my lesson\" like flying to the place where the lesson's bigger brother lives. He's got a system. The system is baccarat, a game where you bet on a hand you're not allowed to touch, run by a dealer who's been doing this since before your dad had hair. He'll tell you it's basically 50/50. It isn't. The house is the only one in that room with a spreadsheet. Then he takes the ferry home with a dozen egg tarts and a tan from standing on the deck, the only thing that came back from the trip."}, "b": {"item_id": "67e9bb83372e", "text": "You already lost the down payment, so naturally you're taking the TurboJET from Hong Kong to win it back, because nothing says financial recovery like a ferry with a buffet. You've got a system for baccarat. Baccarat! The only decision is \"Banker\" or \"Player,\" and you still fill a little scorecard with red and blue dots like the shoe is sending you messages. It isn't. That's just a deck of cards and your mortgage. You've got red underwear on for luck and a junket host named \"Uncle\" who is definitely not your uncle. By 4 a.m. you'll be drifting through the fake Venice canals in a gondola, humming, past the pawn shop that's already priced your Rolex. Then you'll stumble up to the Ruins of St. Paul's: a church that burned until only the front was left standing. Finally, something here that looks like you."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0196", "lang": "en", "target_id": "slack", "target_name": "Slack", "facts": ["Spun out in 2013 from the internal chat tool of Tiny Speck's failed game Glitch; founder Stewart Butterfield had earlier spun Flickr out of another failed game.", "The name is a backronym: 'Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge'.", "Salesforce bought it for $27.7 billion in 2021.", "Filed an EU antitrust complaint against Microsoft in 2020 over the bundling of Teams with Office."], "a": {"item_id": "ccebb9af0d58", "text": "You run your company on Slack because you wanted a culture and settled for a green dot. You've made forty-seven channels and #general is a graveyard of work anniversaries and a guy named Greg's lunch photos. Your big decisions get made with a thumbs-up emoji, and a raised-hands reaction counts as alignment. You type \"@here\" like you're summoning a god, then ask whether anyone's seen the doc you pinned in a channel you archived in March.\n\nYou love being \"async\" until you need an answer, and then it's \"quick huddle?\" Nobody has ever had a quick huddle. You say you hate meetings, so you built a meeting that never ends, sends push notifications, and has a gif keyboard.\n\nAnd the payoff? Your best engineer set her status to \"🌴 away\" in 2022 and nobody's checked if she's gone."}, "b": {"item_id": "a071abe43a17", "text": "Let's hear it for the founders running a company on Slack. You wanted a culture; you got 400 channels and a guy who reacts to layoffs with the 🙏 emoji. You're not a CEO. You're a moderator with equity.\n\nYour whole leadership style is staying green. You think that little dot means you're present. Your kids see the same dot.\n\nYou don't make decisions; you start threads. Then you @channel at 11 p.m. with \"quick q, no rush\" and watch 200 people learn how fast a heart can beat.\n\nStrategy lives in #general, accountability dies in a DM, and the truth lives in a private channel called #venting-2 that you'll never be invited to.\n\nThe dream was async freedom. The reality is eight hours a day watching \"Kevin is typing…\" like it's a heart monitor."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0197", "lang": "en", "target_id": "carbon-nanotubes", "target_name": "Carbon nanotubes", "facts": ["Reported by Sumio Iijima in Nature in 1991.", "Promised as the cable for a space elevator; a 2006 analysis showed that ordinary defects cut the strength of a macroscopic cable far below the ideal value.", "Long, rigid multi-walled nanotubes behaved like asbestos fibres in a 2008 mouse study in Nature Nanotechnology.", "Sorting metallic from semiconducting tubes has blocked nanotube electronics for three decades; MIT's 16-bit RV16X-NANO processor (2019) is still a lab demonstration.", "Today they are sold mainly as a conductive additive in lithium-ion battery electrodes."], "a": {"item_id": "a9505577820b", "text": "Every carbon nanotube pitch opens with a slide calling it the strongest material ever made, then a space elevator, then a hockey-stick graph that hits commercial scale in 2031. The founder is a former postdoc who spent three years coaxing one gram out of a black hairball and now calls the hairball \"proprietary dispersion.\" They want your money to build a factory that will produce exactly enough for a demo vial. What it gets them: a Series A, a TED talk, and a lifetime of saying \"we're basically in pilot\" at dinner parties. You get a framed bucky-paper certificate and the promise that the only obstacle left is \"just manufacturing.\""}, "b": {"item_id": "839efb519f46", "text": "Nanotube founders are the only people who've said \"within five years\" every year since 1991 and kept a straight face. They open with a slide of a space elevator, then ask for seed money to make a vial of black dust. Every deck has the same line, \"a hundred times stronger than steel,\" and every deck is quietly a plan to sell additive to tennis racket companies. These are PhDs who spent six years sorting chirality and came out wanting a Tesla. The pitch is always \"we've solved scale,\" and the demo is always one centimeter long. VCs keep a nanotube guy around like a lucky rabbit's foot: he never pays off, but he makes the fund sound visionary. And when someone asks about lung toxicity, he says \"totally different from asbestos\" with the confidence of a man who googled it in the elevator. The regular one."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0198", "lang": "en", "target_id": "vim", "target_name": "Vim", "facts": ["Released by Bram Moolenaar in November 1991 as 'Vi IMitation', a vi clone he had begun writing for the Amiga in 1988 on top of Stevie; the expansion became 'Vi IMproved' with version 2.0 in December 1993.", "Licensed as charityware: the licence asks users to donate to ICCF Holland, a charity Moolenaar ran for children in Uganda.", "Stack Overflow's question 'How to exit the Vim editor?' passed one million visits in May 2017, which Stack Overflow marked with a blog post.", "Modal editing: in normal mode, typing letters runs commands rather than inserting text; :wq saves and quits, :q! quits and discards changes."], "a": {"item_id": "229d2e4da892", "text": "You told the intern you use Vim before you told him your name. He didn't ask. Nobody asked. You just stood near a monitor and the urge took you.\n\nYou're not after efficiency. You're after a witness. You spent forty hours on your .vimrc to save eleven seconds a week, and the only payoff is someone watching you type \"ciw\" and gasping. They don't gasp. They say, \"Is it frozen?\"\n\nAnd what has it gotten you? A pair-programming partner who stopped pairing. A relative line number column you explain unprompted. A macro you recorded once, for a task you'll never repeat, that you still bring up at parties.\n\nYou say you never leave the home row. Buddy, you never leave the house either, but at least that one isn't a personality.\n\nMeanwhile Dave opened a file, changed a word with his mouse, and went to lunch."}, "b": {"item_id": "613855b046fb", "text": "You know a Vim user is in the room because nobody else has gotten a word in. Buddy, you didn't \"become more productive.\" You spent three weekends on a .vimrc so you could save four seconds renaming a variable, and you post your dotfiles repo like it's a baby picture. You remapped Caps Lock to Escape and now treat everyone else's keyboard like a character flaw. You say \"I live in the terminal\" like it's a lifestyle and not just where you got stuck. Your whole identity is a joke about not knowing how to quit, and honestly, you never learned. Here you are, still talking. You say \"ciw\" out loud at parties. You switched to Neovim just so you'd have something new to announce. And the payoff? You edit text exactly as fast as the rest of us, hands proudly on home row, with nobody watching."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0199", "lang": "en", "target_id": "windows-vista", "target_name": "Windows Vista", "facts": ["Released to consumers in January 2007, after the 'Longhorn' project was reset in 2004 and headline features such as the WinFS file system were dropped.", "User Account Control prompted so often that Apple's 'Get a Mac' campaign ran an ad about 'Cancel or Allow'.", "'Windows Vista Capable' stickers went on PCs that could only run Home Basic, without the Aero interface, and led to a class-action lawsuit.", "Windows 7 (2009), widely praised, was largely a tuned-up Vista on the same kernel line; Vista support ended in April 2017."], "a": {"item_id": "1925db42cfe5", "text": "Every guy who upgraded to Vista on day one has the same story. He's standing in line at midnight, January 30th, 2007, holding a box like it's the last Wii. He wants to tell people he was first. Nobody asked. He got the Ultimate edition because he's the kind of man who says \"Ultimate\" about a salad. He turned on Aero, watched his window borders go translucent, and called it \"the future.\" Then his printer stopped working. He found out its driver was from 2004, and the printer was not interested in the future. So he clicked \"Allow\" on a UAC prompt. Then he clicked it again. He's clicked Allow four hundred times now, and he's the only one who thinks that's security."}, "b": {"item_id": "4f7f18f8662a", "text": "Let's hear it for the day-one Vista crowd, the people who lined up at CompUSA on January 30th, 2007 because a billboard said \"The Wow Starts Now.\" You paid $399 for Ultimate so Flip 3D could shuffle the same three windows you used to Alt-Tab through. Your laptop had a \"Vista Capable\" sticker and 512 megs of RAM, which is like slapping \"Marathon Capable\" on a hamster. You wanted to look like the future; you had Aero turned off by Wednesday. Your printer quit. Your Sound Blaster went mute. Your \"Ultimate Extras\" turned out to be a poker game and a promise. And every time you tried to show off, the screen went dark and asked, \"Are you sure?\" Buddy, nobody was sure. Not even Microsoft. They were already working on Windows 7."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0200", "lang": "en", "target_id": "keurig", "target_name": "Keurig", "facts": ["John Sylvan invented the K-Cup in the early 1990s and sold his stake for about $50,000 in 1997; in 2015 he told The Atlantic, 'I feel bad sometimes that I ever did it.'", "Keurig 2.0 (2014) read an ink mark on the lid and rejected unlicensed pods; sales fell and workarounds spread online.", "By 2014 enough K-Cups were sold each year to circle the Earth more than ten times; the pods were mostly unrecyclable #7 plastic until a switch to polypropylene by 2020.", "Keurig Green Mountain merged with Dr Pepper Snapple in 2018 to form Keurig Dr Pepper."], "a": {"item_id": "a2940564c97a", "text": "To the Keurig people, bless you. You're the ones who say \"I just need something hot in the morning\" like that's a personality. You want coffee the way you want a fire drill: fast, loud, and over before you're fully conscious. You've got a pod drawer with forty flavors, including one called \"Extra Bold\" that you bought in a fit of optimism and one called \"Hazelnut Dream\" you won't admit you finish. You press the 8 oz button, get four ounces of brown regret, and call it a day. You're the only people alive who look at a single-serve cup and feel like a responsible adult. Sixty seconds, one cup, zero decisions. You didn't choose coffee. You chose not to commit to a whole pot."}, "b": {"item_id": "a034e62fdf17", "text": "You said you don't have time to grind beans, then spent ten minutes hunting for a pod in a drawer that's half foil lids and half regret. You bought the 72-count variety pack because you \"like options,\" and now you're drinking Hazelnut Creme Brûlée Cheesecake at 6 a.m. like a toddler who got the dessert menu. You pay fifty cents a cup for coffee that tastes like a hostage video: weak, brown, and apologizing. You press the \"strong\" button, which does nothing, and you believe in it anyway, like a guy pushing a crosswalk button.\n\nThe man who invented the K-Cup says he regrets it. The inventor. And you're still in the kitchen peeling foil off tiny plastic cups like you're losing at a very small lottery.\n\nYou bought the reusable filter to feel better about the landfill. It lives in a drawer now, with the gym membership and the sourdough starter."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0201", "lang": "en", "target_id": "durian", "target_name": "Durian", "facts": ["Banned on Singapore's MRT trains and in many Southeast Asian hotels because of the smell.", "A 2017 genome study in Nature Genetics traced the odour to a duplicated family of MGL genes that pump out volatile sulfur compounds.", "China buys most of the world's durian exports, chiefly from Thailand, in a trade worth billions of dollars a year.", "Alfred Russel Wallace wrote in 1856 that eating it was 'a new sensation worth a voyage to the East to experience'."], "a": {"item_id": "0c82ba06de9c", "text": "People who eat durian in public aren't hungry. They're recruiting. Every bite comes with a sermon: \"You just haven't had a *good* one.\" Sir, I'm on a bus. I didn't ask to join your church.\n\nSingapore fines people for bringing it on the train, and these folks hear that like a dare. They're the only foodies whose restaurant review gets filed as a police report.\n\nAnd the payoff? Seventy dollars on a Musang King so you can watch strangers silently change seats. You paid premium to become a fire drill. Your date calls it \"an acquired taste,\" which is exactly what you say about someone you're about to break up with.\n\nThen they stroll back to the hotel, walk right past the sign with a red circle through their entire personality, and still act shocked when housekeeping files a complaint."}, "b": {"item_id": "408212e83a9b", "text": "You're not eating durian on the train because you love it. You're eating it because you need the whole carriage to know you've been to Southeast Asia and survived. You stood under the little no-durian sign, the one with the red slash that exists specifically because of people like you, and thought, \"That's for amateurs.\"\n\nYou call it the king of fruits, which is funny, because you're the only one who ever crowned it. Everyone else just moved seats.\n\nYou'll tell us it's an acquired taste. Buddy, you didn't acquire a taste. You acquired a personality, and a three-seat radius of personal space.\n\nWhat did it get you? A story for brunch, a hotel front desk that knows your name, and a stranger's hand clamped over her nose. Enjoy your throne."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0202", "lang": "en", "target_id": "google-glass", "target_name": "Google Glass", "facts": ["Unveiled at Google I/O 2012 with skydivers livestreaming their jump through it onto the Moscone Center roof.", "The Explorer Edition sold to invited users in 2013 for $1,500.", "Wearers were nicknamed 'Glassholes'; bars, cinemas, and casinos banned the device over its camera.", "The consumer programme ended in January 2015; an Enterprise Edition continued until Google stopped selling it in 2023.", "Its display was a 640-by-360 prism in the corner of one eye."], "a": {"item_id": "2e15bc79f728", "text": "Look at the guy at the bar wearing Google Glass. He paid fifteen hundred dollars to be an Explorer, which is what you call a man who volunteers to be the test dummy for his own loneliness. He's not drinking, he's documenting. He keeps saying \"OK Glass\" like he's summoning a genie who only grants weather updates. Every time a woman walks past, he winks, and she can't tell if he's flirting or taking her mugshot. He wants everyone to know he's ahead of the future. Meanwhile, the future is a grown man in a bar talking to his own face, reading his texts out loud, and nobody here asked him what he's looking at. Buddy, you're not looking at us. You're looking at emails the rest of us can't see, and honestly, that's the most lonely thing in this room."}, "b": {"item_id": "abe9296c28ae", "text": "Look at this guy. Paid fifteen hundred dollars to join Google's \"Explorer Program,\" and the only thing he's explored is the sidewalk outside every bar that bounced him. He walks in thinking he's Tony Stark and walks out on a list, because nobody can tell if he's filming, and honestly, neither can he. He's here to meet women, and he gets results: every conversation ends the second he says \"OK Glass.\" Nothing kills the mood like a man asking his own face for directions to her place. He wanted to be the future. Instead the internet had to invent a brand-new word just for him: Glasshole. Silicon Valley promised to connect him to everything. It couldn't connect him to a second date, but it did get him his very own slur."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0203", "lang": "en", "target_id": "beanie-babies", "target_name": "Beanie Babies", "facts": ["Launched by Ty Inc. in 1993 and sold only through small gift shops, with designs 'retired' to manufacture scarcity.", "At the height of the craze Beanie Babies accounted for about 10% of eBay's sales, and eBay's 1998 IPO filing said the site at times had over 30,000 Beanie Baby auctions running simultaneously in the quarter ending June 1998.", "In 1999 a divorcing couple divided their Beanie Baby collection one by one on a courtroom floor, in a photograph that ran worldwide.", "In 1999 Ty announced that Beanie Babies would be retired at the end of the year, then reversed itself after asking the public to vote and seeing an overwhelming vote to keep them.", "Most 'rare' Beanies that price guides valued in the thousands now sell for a few dollars."], "a": {"item_id": "876536d60647", "text": "Let's talk about the Beanie Baby investor. You know the type. He keeps the tush tag in a sealed plastic sleeve, because a tag with a crease is a lawsuit. A 1999 price guide sits on his nightstand like scripture. He sleeps beside a shelf of bears he hasn't touched since the Clinton administration and calls them \"the portfolio.\" He has never once hugged one, because hugging reduces value. His kids aren't allowed in the living room. He tells everyone it's his retirement plan, and somewhere in a storage unit in Ohio, a sixty-year-old man is whispering to a Princess bear. When he dies, he says, they'll be worth a fortune. That's the one thing he and those bears have in common: they're both waiting for someone to finally care."}, "b": {"item_id": "0c59a1f0431d", "text": "You bought 400 of them in 1998 because a stranger at a Hallmark told you Princess the bear would put your kids through college. Your kids are now in their thirties and still paying off college.\n\nYou drove to three states for a \"retiring soon\" rumor started by a guy named Dale on a message board. You kept them in plastic cases, because your retirement plan couldn't be touched by children, and you told your actual children that, to their faces.\n\nYou didn't collect, you waited. For twenty-five years, you've been waiting for a market that was only ever you, Dale, and a divorce attorney splitting a basement.\n\nNow you've got a garage full of \"rare errors\" and an appraisal that says forty dollars. Total.\n\nTy Warner got the yacht. You got the tag protectors."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0204", "lang": "en", "target_id": "macau", "target_name": "Macau", "facts": ["A Portuguese settlement from the 1550s until the handover to China in December 1999.", "Casino revenue passed the Las Vegas Strip's in 2006, and at its 2013 peak, about $45 billion, was roughly seven times the Strip's.", "One company, STDM, held the casino monopoly for four decades until licences opened to competition in 2002.", "Among the most densely populated places on Earth, at roughly 20,000 people per square kilometre.", "The Cotai Strip stands on land reclaimed from the sea between the islands of Taipa and Coloane."], "a": {"item_id": "00bfc5b1665a", "text": "You lost it on Hong Kong stocks, so you took the TurboJET to win it back, because nothing says \"financial plan\" like a ferry with a cancellation policy. You're the guy at the Grand Lisboa squeezing a baccarat card like it owes you rent, blowing on it, peeling the corner so slowly the dealer ages. Red underwear, three days unwashed, \"for luck.\" Baccarat is a coin flip and you've built a religion around it. You told your wife it's a work trip, and technically your work now is the pawn shop next door, which buys Rolexes faster than you can say \"one more shoe.\" By hour nine you're eating an egg tart for dinner and calling it Portuguese culture. You came to win it all back. You leave by bus over the bridge with no luck, no Rolex, and a watch tan."}, "b": {"item_id": "5ababa96b17b", "text": "You're not going to Macau to gamble. You're going to Macau to be wrong in a more expensive location. You lost it in Vegas, in Atlantic City, on your cousin's \"can't-miss\" crypto, and now you've flown fourteen hours to a former Portuguese colony to chase it down at a baccarat table, with a dealer who's seen your exact face on ten thousand other men.\n\nYou'll tell people it's a \"research trip.\" You'll stand in a fake Venice, under a fake sky, floating on a fake canal, trying to win real money back. You see the problem there, right?\n\nAnd the pawn shops are right outside the casinos. Macau doesn't even hide the sequel. It built the walk of shame with a loan desk.\n\nYou'll leave with no money, a stranger's cigarette smoke in your jacket, and one Portuguese egg tart you bought as a victory snack. It's the only thing you won, and you'll eat it on the ferry, alone, doing math."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0205", "lang": "en", "target_id": "microsoft-word", "target_name": "Microsoft Word", "facts": ["Launched in 1983 as 'Multi-Tool Word' for Xenix and MS-DOS.", "Clippy, the animated paperclip assistant, arrived in Office 97 and was removed in Office 2007; Microsoft itself ran ads mocking it in 2001.", "Its .docx format, Office Open XML, was fast-tracked to ISO standard 29500 in 2008 as a roughly 6,000-page specification, amid accusations that national committees had been packed.", "Older spell checkers, Word's included, offered 'Cupertino' as a correction for a mistyped 'cooperation', and the resulting 'Cupertino effect' made it into published NATO, EU and UN-agency documents."], "a": {"item_id": "c46efa5460bb", "text": "You spent six years mastering quantum field theory, yet you've centered every chapter title by hitting the spacebar until it looked right. You know what Heading 1 is, the same way you know what a dentist is: you've heard good things and you're avoiding it.\n\nYou wanted a thesis that looks like scholarship. You got a 214-page file called \"thesis_FINAL_v9_actualfinal_JUNE,\" where adding one comma moves Figure 4.2 to the shadow realm. Your table of contents is typed by hand, and you'll find out at the print shop.\n\nYou've also learned what a section break is, at 2 a.m., while trying to make page numbers go roman and then Arabic. You cried. A committee member will now spend four seconds on your formatting and ask you to change the margins.\n\nYou chose Word because everyone does. LaTeX people have already told you. Twice. At parties."}, "b": {"item_id": "600c3a0ffd98", "text": "They're the ones who swear they'll \"just fix the margins real quick\" at 2 a.m., three days before the deadline. They have a file named FINAL_v7_ACTUAL, a Table of Contents that refuses to update unless you look at it funny, and one section break they're sure is secretly turning page 14 into roman numeral vii. They chose Word because their advisor said so, and now they're spending their last good hours in the Styles pane, clicking \"Update Field\" like it's a slot machine. They want numbered headings that stay numbered, a list of figures that doesn't lie, and a title page that doesn't look like a ransom note. What they get is a bibliography that formats itself only in their dreams, and a PDF their committee opens in Preview and immediately ruins."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0206", "lang": "en", "target_id": "monopoly", "target_name": "Monopoly", "facts": ["Descends from Lizzie Magie's The Landlord's Game, patented in 1904 to teach the evils of land monopoly in the spirit of Henry George.", "Charles Darrow sold his version to Parker Brothers in 1935 and was credited as the inventor; Parker bought Magie's patent for $500.", "The official rules require any unbought property to be auctioned, and 'Free Parking' pays nothing; the house rules that skip the auction and pile cash on Free Parking are why games drag.", "During the Second World War, British intelligence had maps, compasses, and real currency hidden in sets sent to prisoner-of-war camps."], "a": {"item_id": "98523e07c8ef", "text": "Every family has the same cast. Dad volunteers as banker, and by hour two he's laundering money like a Colombian cartel. Your uncle invented a Free Parking jackpot rule nobody agreed to, but everyone's too tired to fight him. Your nine-year-old sister is a slumlord who won't take the deal because she \"just likes the purple ones.\" Mom's been bankrupt since 8 p.m. but keeps negotiating her way back in with promises she can't keep, like a politician.\n\nAnd what are you all after? The joy of crushing people who share your DNA. You'll forgive a lot in this family, but not Boardwalk.\n\nSix hours later, nobody's finished. Someone flips the table, someone's crying in the pantry, and Grandma, who's been quietly hoarding all the orange properties, is smiling. You didn't bond. You just found out who in your family would evict you."}, "b": {"item_id": "5b3d3501d71e", "text": "Dad, you've been the banker since 2009, and you're the only one who knows what's in that tray, and you've never once shared it. Free Parking has had four hundred dollars in it since the Clinton administration, and you call pocketing it \"a light house rule.\" Mom has wanted to quit at nine o'clock since the second lap. Your brother bought every orange property on the first trip around and now calls himself a real estate mogul at a kitchen table. Grandma is the thimble, and she's still winning, because she's the only one who doesn't count her cash. The nephew is eight. He mortgaged his railroads to buy a hotel he can't afford, then sobbed in jail. Everybody says \"just visiting\" like it's a personality. You're not playing for the fake money. You're finding out who'll flip the board when they lose."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0207", "lang": "en", "target_id": "oat-milk", "target_name": "Oat milk", "facts": ["Oatly started in Sweden in the 1990s, built on Lund University research by Rickard Öste on lactose intolerance.", "Oatly's slogan 'It's like milk but made for humans' was banned in Sweden in 2015 after the dairy lobby LRF Mjölk sued; Oatly then published the lawsuit as advertising.", "Oatly listed on Nasdaq in May 2021 at a valuation near $10 billion; the shares later lost more than 90% of their value.", "Commercial oat milk is made by breaking oat starch into maltose with amylase enzymes, and many brands, Oatly among them, add rapeseed oil; the result has more sugar and a higher glycaemic index than almond or soy milk."], "a": {"item_id": "a99ceee7d226", "text": "Nobody orders oat milk for the oats. You order it so the barista knows you've done your research. You say \"just make it oat\" like you're showing a passport. You're not lactose intolerant. You're \"not really doing dairy right now,\" a phrase you've never once said about bread. You want your cup to announce your values before you've said a word. Then you spend four minutes explaining your gut to a kid named Jayden at 7 a.m. Your tote bag has a sea turtle on it. You pay a dollar extra and hold the cup up for a photo, waiting for someone to ask what's in it so you can say \"oat\" and watch them nod like you just told them you ran a marathon."}, "b": {"item_id": "a7a78e709f8d", "text": "You said \"Can I do oat?\" like you're asking the barista for permission to have a personality. Nobody asked. The line behind you did the math on that 75-cent upcharge and found a person who wants to be seen caring.\n\nYou're not lactose intolerant. You're \"lactose-skeptical.\" You quit almond milk the week a documentary told you almonds were the problem, and you've been switching milks like a politician switching parties, always just before the scandal breaks. Now you're on the right side of history, in a cup with a plastic lid.\n\nYou tell people you're \"mostly plant-based,\" which means you had a salad once near a farmers market. You'll mention the oat thing on a first date, and she'll smile and start planning the exit.\n\nFour dollars for a moral victory. Nobody's clapping, Dylan."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0208", "lang": "en", "target_id": "canberra", "target_name": "Canberra", "facts": ["Chosen as a compromise because neither Sydney nor Melbourne would accept the other as capital; Australia's constitution required the capital to be at least 100 miles from Sydney.", "Designed by the Chicago architects Walter Burley Griffin and Marion Mahony Griffin, who won an international competition in 1912.", "Its centrepiece, Lake Burley Griffin, is artificial, created by damming the Molonglo River in 1963-64.", "Parliament House (1988) is built into Capital Hill under a lawn meant for citizens to walk over their politicians; the lawn was fenced off in 2017 for security.", "Melbourne served as the capital until 1927 while Canberra was being built."], "a": {"item_id": "e90ceb66c6fb", "text": "You moved to Canberra for a \"two-year stint to get your EL1,\" which is what everyone says, including the guy who's been saying it since 2011 and now owns a Subaru and strong opinions about Gungahlin. You joined to \"shape policy,\" and your policy is now formatting other people's policy until a Deputy Secretary changes the font. You wanted impact. You got a briefing note that three people read, one of whom was you, and that was to check for typos.\n\nYou left Sydney because you couldn't afford a house, and now you own one in Chisholm, so congratulations, you've got a mortgage and a view of a Bunnings.\n\nAt parties you ask \"which department?\" like it's a star sign. Every long weekend you flee to Melbourne, where friends ask when you're coming home, and you say \"soon,\" knowing you've got a clearance, a generous super match, and no way out.\n\nYou didn't climb the ladder. You joined the queue."}, "b": {"item_id": "e7c430775b37", "text": "You moved to Canberra for the job. Brave. You looked at Sydney rents, did the maths, and decided your personality was negotiable. Now you're an EL1 who says \"stakeholder\" at barbecues and talks about Senate Estimates like a war you survived. Your lanyard has a richer social life than you do. You promised your Melbourne friends a visit; they've cancelled twice for weddings in Bali. You've developed opinions about Gungahlin. Your wild Saturday is Braddon brunch, followed by complaining it was \"basically Sydney prices.\" And what did it get you? A secure job, a three-bedroom in Tuggeranong, and a security clearance confirming the government checked thoroughly and found nothing interesting. You came for two years. That was 2009. Your kid now says \"on the Hill\" unprompted, and you've never been prouder or more quietly trapped."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0209", "lang": "en", "target_id": "vision-pro", "target_name": "Apple Vision Pro", "facts": ["Released in February 2024 at $3,499, with an external battery pack on a cable rated for about two hours.", "'EyeSight' shows a rendering of the wearer's eyes on an outward-facing screen.", "Apple calls it 'spatial computing' and its App Store rules tell developers not to describe apps as AR, VR, XR, or MR.", "Reports during 2024 said Apple had cut production; analysts' first-year sales estimates were well under a million units.", "Weighs 600-650 g, before the battery."], "a": {"item_id": "ccb644462fd4", "text": "You paid $3,500 to be the only person on the L train who can't see the L train. You wanted to be noticed, and you are: everyone's filming you pinch the air like you're catching a very small, very expensive moth. You say it's for productivity, six floating windows, a virtual Mac, and you're answering one email: \"Per my last message.\" You're not in a private cinema, you're watching Moana 2 while a guy named Dennis eats a gyro against your shoulder.\n\nWhat you were after was never the screen. It was the question. \"Is that the Vision Pro?\" And you answer like it's an Oscar speech, peeling it off to reveal a red ring around your face, like a raccoon who lost a custody battle.\n\nYour reward: a dented forehead, a headache, and a stop you missed in Flushing."}, "b": {"item_id": "90ecfc190a47", "text": "You paid thirty-five hundred dollars to ride the 6 train inside Joshua Tree. Everyone else has to smell the actual 6 train; you just hear it echoing through a desert like a ghost with a MetroCard. You want us to know you're an early adopter. We know. Your little cartoon eyes are blinking at us like a hostage video. That cable running to your pocket says \"the future\" and also \"IV drip.\" You pinch the air at Union Square and a stranger thinks you're ordering him to move. You've got a hundred-foot Mac screen floating over a man eating soup out of a Pringles can. And the battery dies in two hours, so you'll still get to experience the subway in full, unimmersive 3D. Your commute outlasts your device. Welcome back to reality. It's delayed."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0210", "lang": "en", "target_id": "klingon", "target_name": "Klingon", "facts": ["Created by linguist Marc Okrand for Star Trek III (1984), building on a few words the actor James Doohan improvised for the 1979 film.", "Deliberately alien grammar: object-verb-subject word order and no verb 'to be', so Hamlet's line became 'taH pagh taHbe'', 'continue or not continue'.", "The Klingon Language Institute (1992) published Hamlet 'in the original Klingon'; Bing Translator added Klingon in 2013 and Duolingo in 2018.", "Okrand's 1985 Klingon Dictionary listed roughly 1,500 words, and fluent speakers number only in the dozens: 20 to 30 in 2009, 30 to 50 by 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "0cbf01775821", "text": "You spent years mastering a language whose standard greeting is \"What do you want?\" and honestly, that explains the social life. You wanted a warrior culture and got a prefix chart. You can render \"to be or not to be\" as *taH pagh taHbe'*, which literally means \"continue or don't continue,\" a fair question about the hobby.\n\nYour conversation partners are forty people at a convention who all dread your corrections. You command a vocabulary built for threatening enemies, and your only battle is a verb-suffix argument with a man named Greg, who is wrong, and who knows he is wrong, and who will not yield.\n\nYou say honor is everything, yet you've earned yours in a Marriott conference room, holding a foam bat'leth, forehead ridges peeling in the airport security line.\n\nToday is a good day to die? Sir, it's Tuesday. You have a dentist appointment."}, "b": {"item_id": "a8c91de7e3da", "text": "Ever meet someone fluent in Klingon? Congratulations, you've found the only person who learned a language with no word for \"please\" and thought, *yes, this is my personality.* They spent years mastering a tongue built by a linguist Paramount paid to make growling official. They own Hamlet in Klingon, and they'll recite *taH pagh taHbe'* at your wedding. Ask them to pass the salt and they'll tell you asking is for the weak. Their dream is a job as a Klingon interpreter that never comes through, and their ideal date is someone who can answer \"Qapla'\" without blinking. Then you say one sentence in English, and they sigh like a warrior whose honor just got spilled on the couch."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0211", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hsq", "target_name": "HSQ (e-beam resist)", "facts": ["Hydrogen silsesquioxane, sold by Dow Corning as FOx ('flowable oxide') and designed as a spin-on dielectric for chip interconnects; lithographers adopted it as a negative-tone resist.", "Exposure converts its cage structure into a cross-linked, silica-like network, so the developed pattern is essentially SiO2.", "Holds the resolution records for electron-beam lithography: isolated features around 5 nm, dense lines below 10 nm half-pitch, with high-contrast 'salty' NaOH/NaCl development.", "Must be kept refrigerated (Dow Corning's datasheet gives a six-month shelf life at 5 °C); the solution ages into a jelly, and its effective sensitivity shifts with processing delays because cross-linking continues after exposure.", "DuPont, which inherited Dow Corning's FOx/XR-1541 HSQ line, discontinued it and ended production at the end of 2025; labs such as EPFL's CMi switched to third-party HSQ from EM Resist or DisChem, both formulated from AQM powder."], "a": {"item_id": "b0d879a960b0", "text": "So you switched to HSQ \"for the resolution.\" Sure. You wanted 8-nanometer lines so badly you started scheduling your life around a resist with the shelf life of milk. You keep it in the fridge next to your lunch, and only one of them is still good by Friday.\n\nYour write that used to take twenty minutes now takes the whole overnight slot, so the guy who needed the tool for his actual thesis is crying in the gowning room. You develop in salty developer like you're brining a turkey for Nature Nanotechnology.\n\nAnd when it goes wrong, and it will, you've basically spun glass onto your chip. Your only exit is HF, a chemical that wants you dead.\n\nAll of this for one SEM image with a scale bar so small nobody can read it. Congratulations. Your device still doesn't work, but it fails at record resolution."}, "b": {"item_id": "5b587d8cb24c", "text": "You're switching to HSQ for resolution? Sure. Nobody picks HSQ because it's fun. You saw one SEM image of six-nanometer lines and decided you'd be the person in that image. Your PI already put \"sub-10 nm\" in the grant renewal. Now you've got a bottle in the fridge that goes bad if you look at it wrong, a dose matrix that's eaten two months, and a TMAH developer you're mixing like a cocktail bartender. You'll spend Tuesday at 3 a.m. staring at a SEM, sure your lines are perfect, while they quietly lean over and fuse into their neighbors. Congrats on the resolution. Your advisor is still going to ask about throughput."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0212", "lang": "en", "target_id": "microsoft-teams", "target_name": "Microsoft Teams", "facts": ["Launched in 2017, bundled at no extra charge into Office 365, and replaced Skype for Business.", "After Slack's antitrust complaint, Microsoft unbundled Teams from Office in the EU in 2023 and worldwide in 2024; the European Commission still sent a statement of objections in 2024.", "Microsoft reported 320 million monthly active users in 2023.", "Microsoft shut down consumer Skype in May 2025 and sent its users to Teams."], "a": {"item_id": "8ecdfe8fdf7c", "text": "Let's hear it for the IT director who chose Microsoft Teams, a man who has never once chosen anything. It came bundled with Outlook, like coleslaw you're contractually obligated to eat. The EU had to force Microsoft to stop shoving it on people, and your company still went back for seconds. The whole pitch was \"It's already in the license.\" So was Clippy, Gary.\n\nManagement loves it because now they can watch your little dot. Green means productive. Yellow means you went to the bathroom and Linda noticed. They didn't want collaboration. They wanted a heart monitor.\n\nAnd what did it get them? Every meeting opens with eight adults asking \"Can you see my screen?\" while a VP uses the raise-hand button to interrupt people *harder*. Congratulations: you saved nine dollars a seat and lost the will to live."}, "b": {"item_id": "539c5a8c88ae", "text": "Let's talk about the people who picked Teams. Dave from procurement saw one demo in 2019 and never recovered. He schedules a \"quick sync\" at 4:55 on a Friday, then spends the first ten minutes asking if you can see his screen, like he's just performed a miracle. He checks his green dot like a pulse. He wants you to know he's \"Available,\" which is corporate for \"I'm watching you.\" Every channel he's made is called \"General,\" and nobody has read any of them since 2019. His reply to your message is a thumbs-up, proof he read it and has nothing to add. That's the whole job for these people: keep you present, keep you unmuted, and keep you dinging."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0213", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hawaiian-pizza", "target_name": "Hawaiian pizza", "facts": ["Invented in 1962 by Sam Panopoulos, a Greek immigrant, at the Satellite Restaurant in Chatham, Ontario, Canada; named after the brand of canned pineapple he used.", "Iceland's president Guðni Th. Jóhannesson said in 2017 that he would ban pineapple on pizza if he could, then clarified that a president has no such power.", "Hawaii is no longer a major pineapple producer: Del Monte shut its Hawaiian pineapple operations in November 2006, two years ahead of its announced 2008 exit, the state's last cannery closed in 2007, and most canned pineapple now comes from Thailand, Indonesia, and the Philippines.", "Panopoulos, whose restaurant also served Chinese dishes, told the Toronto Sun in 2010 that 'in those days people didn't mix the tastes like sweet and sour, except for Chinese food'."], "a": {"item_id": "000c462b9442", "text": "Ordering Hawaiian pizza is a cry for help from someone who wants to be called a rebel. You've never been to Hawaii. The dish is from Ontario. You're defending a Canadian restaurant accident like it's your homeland.\n\nYou order it for the office party, then wait for someone to gasp. Nobody does, so you announce, \"I know, I know, pineapple doesn't belong on pizza,\" to a room that wasn't talking. You've been waiting years for this fight. Nobody's coming.\n\nYou call yourself adventurous. It's the only risk you've ever taken, and you retake it every Friday. Your entire personality is a defense of a topping that never hired a lawyer.\n\nAnd what do you get? Three leftover slices, because everyone else quietly ordered pepperoni, and a reputation as the \"random\" one at work. You wanted to be dangerous. You're just Greg, with ham."}, "b": {"item_id": "1be3426edb6c", "text": "People who order Hawaiian pizza want you to know they're brave. They say \"I know, I know\" before anyone has said a word, because for them the pineapple isn't a topping, it's a personality. This is a person whose entire rebellious phase fits in a box for $14.99. They've never gotten a tattoo, never quit a job, never sent food back, but once a month they look a teenage cashier dead in the eye and say \"pineapple\" like they're defusing a bomb.\n\nAnd they always want to split one with you. Half Hawaiian, half \"normal.\" That's not a compromise. That's a hostage negotiation.\n\nBest part? It isn't even Hawaiian. A Greek guy invented it in Ontario. So congratulations: your one act of rebellion is a Canadian pretending he's on vacation."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0214", "lang": "en", "target_id": "canberra", "target_name": "Canberra", "facts": ["Chosen as a compromise because neither Sydney nor Melbourne would accept the other as capital; Australia's constitution required the capital to be at least 100 miles from Sydney.", "Designed by the Chicago architects Walter Burley Griffin and Marion Mahony Griffin, who won an international competition in 1912.", "Its centrepiece, Lake Burley Griffin, is artificial, created by damming the Molonglo River in 1963-64.", "Parliament House (1988) is built into Capital Hill under a lawn meant for citizens to walk over their politicians; the lawn was fenced off in 2017 for security.", "Melbourne served as the capital until 1927 while Canberra was being built."], "a": {"item_id": "4906cad02ec9", "text": "You moved to Canberra for the job. Brave. You looked at Sydney rents, did the maths, and decided your personality could be replaced by a security clearance. Now you tell strangers you \"can't really talk about what you do,\" and they're relieved, because it's a briefing note about another briefing note for Senate Estimates.\n\nYou came for the ladder: APS5, APS6, EL1, a lanyard that opens one extra door. You stayed because you bought in Gungahlin and the mortgage is now your closest friend.\n\nEvery Friday you say you're \"basically in Sydney,\" which means three hours up the Hume, past a giant Merino, to feel alive for one weekend. Then it's back to a Braddon brunch with the same six people from your branch.\n\nBut hey, you're close to the snow. You've been once. You'll mention it forever."}, "b": {"item_id": "5732fa32845b", "text": "You know the type who moves to Canberra for \"two years, max.\" They arrive with a Perth accent, a Bunnings-sized ambition, and a LinkedIn banner that says \"Shaping policy.\" Six months in, they're saying \"Manuka\" like it's the suburb they grew up in. They want an acting EL2 before they've learned which parkway goes where. They own a lanyard drawer and a plan to be a Senate estimates star by spring. What it gets them: a townhouse in Gungahlin, a friend group made entirely of people who also work at Finance, and weekends spent driving to Bungendore to feel like they've left. Two years later they're still here, telling their mates back home the job's \"nearly done,\" which is exactly what they said in 2016."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0215", "lang": "en", "target_id": "stinky-tofu", "target_name": "Stinky tofu (臭豆腐)", "facts": ["Tofu soaked for hours to a day or two in a brine that has itself fermented for months from vegetables such as bamboo shoots and mustard greens, often with dried shrimp or meat; the smell is the brine's, not the bean's.", "Changsha's version is black, deep-fried and doused in chili; Taiwan's night-market version is golden and served with pickled cabbage; Shaoxing's, brined with amaranth stalks, is fried pale yellow or steamed with other 'stinky' pickles.", "Legend credits Wang Zhihe, a Qing-era exam candidate who left his unsold tofu too long in 1669; a Beijing fermented-bean-curd brand still carries his name.", "Street vendors are its main outlet, and the smell carries for a block."], "a": {"item_id": "63f160a3e349", "text": "Let's talk about the stinky tofu people. You smelled it from three blocks away, a smell like a sewer filed a noise complaint, and your brain said: *that's where we're eating.* Your entire personality is \"it tastes better than it smells.\" That's not a review. That's a hostage statement.\n\nYou stand on a Taipei night market curb with a paper boat, chopsticks in one hand, phone in the other, filming your own bravery for followers who can't smell it, which is the only reason they're still following you. Tourists do it once for the story. Locals do it every Tuesday, then board the MRT, where forty strangers silently agree to hate them.\n\nAnd the pickled cabbage on top? That's you pretending you came for vegetables. You didn't. You came to be the deadliest passenger on the scooter."}, "b": {"item_id": "69fa8443e946", "text": "Look at the crowd at this night market stall. Everyone here is eating stinky tofu for the same reason: so they can tell people about it later. The tourist holds the skewer out like it's a live grenade, films himself, then says \"honestly, not bad\" with the face of a man being waterboarded. The couple on a date are splitting one piece, which is romantic only if you're both willing to be the one who gets dumped first. And the teenager who has never once been hungry just needs a story where he was brave enough to chew. You're not here for the snack. You're here for twenty minutes of street cred and three days of your hair smelling like a drain. Enjoy the MRT ride back. Everyone on it already knows where you've been."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0216", "lang": "en", "target_id": "monopoly", "target_name": "Monopoly", "facts": ["Descends from Lizzie Magie's The Landlord's Game, patented in 1904 to teach the evils of land monopoly in the spirit of Henry George.", "Charles Darrow sold his version to Parker Brothers in 1935 and was credited as the inventor; Parker bought Magie's patent for $500.", "The official rules require any unbought property to be auctioned, and 'Free Parking' pays nothing; the house rules that skip the auction and pile cash on Free Parking are why games drag.", "During the Second World War, British intelligence had maps, compasses, and real currency hidden in sets sent to prisoner-of-war camps."], "a": {"item_id": "98523e07c8ef", "text": "Every family has the same cast. Dad volunteers as banker, and by hour two he's laundering money like a Colombian cartel. Your uncle invented a Free Parking jackpot rule nobody agreed to, but everyone's too tired to fight him. Your nine-year-old sister is a slumlord who won't take the deal because she \"just likes the purple ones.\" Mom's been bankrupt since 8 p.m. but keeps negotiating her way back in with promises she can't keep, like a politician.\n\nAnd what are you all after? The joy of crushing people who share your DNA. You'll forgive a lot in this family, but not Boardwalk.\n\nSix hours later, nobody's finished. Someone flips the table, someone's crying in the pantry, and Grandma, who's been quietly hoarding all the orange properties, is smiling. You didn't bond. You just found out who in your family would evict you."}, "b": {"item_id": "d5aee5aa186d", "text": "Let's talk about families who still play Monopoly on holidays. You know who you are: you swore it'd be \"fun,\" then spent four hours watching your brother-in-law build hotels on Boardwalk like he's finally getting the respect his career never gave him. Dad's the banker, which means a man with zero accountability and a lap full of orange five-hundreds. Mom invented a Free Parking jackpot rule in 1994 and will defend it harder than her marriage. Your little cousin traded you Park Place for Baltic and \"a favor later,\" and somehow that's the most binding contract in your family. Nobody's ever finished a game. It ends the same way every year: someone flips the board, someone cries in jail, and Grandma pockets the Get Out of Jail Free card like she's been planning it since Nixon."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0217", "lang": "en", "target_id": "ugg", "target_name": "UGG boots", "facts": ["Sheepskin boots Australian surfers wore to warm up after sessions; Brian Smith started selling them in California in 1978.", "Deckers Outdoor bought the brand in 1995 and holds 'UGG' as a trademark in the US and elsewhere; in Australia, 'ugg' is a generic word any bootmaker may use.", "In 2019, a US jury found an Australian maker liable for selling boots labelled 'ugg' into the United States.", "Oprah Winfrey featured them in her 'Favorite Things' lists repeatedly in the 2000s, which drove the first boom.", "UGG is Deckers' largest brand, with over $2 billion in annual sales."], "a": {"item_id": "9b58586d4e25", "text": "Oh, you're wearing Uggs in August. I saw you at the farmers market, sweating through your sheepskin like it's a personal achievement. You're not cold. You've decided that looking cozy is a personality, and you're hoping people will see your ankles and think, \"She has her life together.\" Ma'am, it's ninety-one degrees. You're not effortless. You're just wet. Your feet have been in a sauna since Memorial Day, and you still walk into Trader Joe's like you're headed to a cabin in Vermont with a fireplace and a man named Bryce. You're not going to Vermont. You're going to the Target parking lot and back, with a cold brew in one hand and a sweat stain shaped like Australia."}, "b": {"item_id": "1531edc90308", "text": "You wear UGGs in July. In Phoenix. Respect. Most people need a therapist to reach that level of denial, and you just needed a mall.\n\nYou're not cold. Nobody's cold. Your feet are poaching in a dead sheep's final revenge, and you call it \"cozy.\" That's not cozy, that's a slow cooker with a ponytail.\n\nWhat are you after? Fall. You want autumn so badly you're dragging it into June like a hostage. You've been holding the same pumpkin spice latte since 2019.\n\nAnd what does it get you? A private, humid sauna for each foot. A smell that enters rooms four seconds before you do. And a look that says \"I'm wearing denim shorts and sheepskin,\" which is two different seasons arguing.\n\nYour boyfriend isn't holding your hand in public. He's holding his breath."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0218", "lang": "en", "target_id": "chicago-deep-dish", "target_name": "Chicago deep-dish pizza", "facts": ["Created at Pizzeria Uno in Chicago in 1943; credit is disputed among founder Ike Sewell, his partner Ric Riccardo, and cook Rudy Malnati.", "Assembled upside down: sliced mozzarella on the dough, then toppings, then crushed tomatoes on top, so the cheese does not scorch during the long bake.", "Jon Stewart called it 'tomato soup in a bread bowl' and 'a casserole' on The Daily Show in 2013.", "Chicagoans' everyday pizza is thin 'tavern-style' cut into squares; GrubHub order data reported by WBEZ in 2013 showed city residents ordering non-deep-dish pizza about 10 to 1 over deep dish, with deep dish and stuffed making up under 9% of orders."], "a": {"item_id": "a4b76bd10aa4", "text": "Let's talk about the people who insist deep-dish is pizza. Mostly Chicagoans with a Second City complex so severe they'll defend anything New York makes fun of. Jon Stewart called it a casserole in 2013, and half of Lincoln Park still hasn't emotionally recovered. These are folks who order, then wait forty-five minutes at Lou Malnati's, and call that patience instead of what it is: a hostage negotiation with a buzzer. They eat it with a knife and fork and still claim it's the same food you fold on a subway platform. And what does it get them? A Giordano's line full of tourists from Ohio, a nap at 2 p.m., and the right to lecture you about tavern-style, which, notice, is what they actually eat at home. Defending deep-dish isn't civic pride. It's Stockholm syndrome with a parking ticket."}, "b": {"item_id": "b9801548af22", "text": "Oh, you're the guy who says deep-dish isn't pizza. Let me guess: you own a wood-fired oven, you named your sourdough starter, and one documentary about Naples turned you into the Supreme Court of dough. You didn't come to Lou's to eat. You came to be the smartest man in the booth, and you're going to hold your fork like a gavel until the appetizers go cold. Your whole personality is a bit about being born in the wrong country. Relax, Gino. Nobody asked for a ruling. Chicago built a pie that takes forty-five minutes and a second mortgage, and you're upset it didn't come with your permission slip. Pass the slice and let your dignity cool down with the cheese."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0219", "lang": "en", "target_id": "crocs", "target_name": "Crocs", "facts": ["Founded in Boulder, Colorado, in 2002; the first clog debuted at the Fort Lauderdale boat show as a boating shoe.", "Made of 'Croslite', which Crocs describes as a proprietary closed-cell resin foam and which third parties identify as an injection-moulded EVA foam.", "Balenciaga sold a platform Crocs for about $850 in 2018.", "The stock fell more than 90% from its 2007 peak during the 2008 crisis; the company then boomed in the pandemic and gave away 860,000 pairs to healthcare workers in 2020.", "The holes are officially 'ventilation ports' and the charms that go in them are 'Jibbitz', a company Crocs bought in 2006."], "a": {"item_id": "62e0599c4f11", "text": "People who wear Crocs out of the house have made peace with something the rest of us are still fighting. You didn't give up; you filed the paperwork. Every Croc wearer says the same thing: \"They're so comfortable.\" Yeah, so is a weighted blanket, Brenda, but you don't wear it to Olive Garden. And the Jibbitz! You've turned your feet into a personality quiz. A tiny taco, a tiny Minion, a tiny \"Live Laugh Love.\" You're forty-three and your shoe has a sticker collection. Then there's the guy who flips the strap back into \"sport mode\" like he's about to chase a pickpocket, when we both know he's never run anywhere except toward a sample tray at Costco. Nurses and chefs wear them because they're on their feet for twelve hours. You wear them to stand in line at Starbucks. Different heroism."}, "b": {"item_id": "ca2056b15182", "text": "You're the person who says \"I just value comfort\" like it's a personality, and nobody asked, because we were just looking at your feet and you panicked into a thesis. You've told three coworkers to \"just try them,\" because you can't enjoy being comfortable alone. You need witnesses.\n\nYou flip the heel strap forward for \"walking\" and back for \"activity.\" What activity? You've never run toward anything except the free samples at Costco.\n\nYou bought little charms for them, so now a grown adult is accessorizing footwear like a fourth-grade backpack, hoping someone says, \"Ooh, is that a tiny taco?\"\n\nYou wanted to look like a nurse on a well-earned break. What you got is a man at an airport who has made peace with everything and wants you to know it. You didn't give up on fashion. You gave up on being asked."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0220", "lang": "en", "target_id": "klingon", "target_name": "Klingon", "facts": ["Created by linguist Marc Okrand for Star Trek III (1984), building on a few words the actor James Doohan improvised for the 1979 film.", "Deliberately alien grammar: object-verb-subject word order and no verb 'to be', so Hamlet's line became 'taH pagh taHbe'', 'continue or not continue'.", "The Klingon Language Institute (1992) published Hamlet 'in the original Klingon'; Bing Translator added Klingon in 2013 and Duolingo in 2018.", "Okrand's 1985 Klingon Dictionary listed roughly 1,500 words, and fluent speakers number only in the dozens: 20 to 30 in 2009, 30 to 50 by 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "a8c91de7e3da", "text": "Ever meet someone fluent in Klingon? Congratulations, you've found the only person who learned a language with no word for \"please\" and thought, *yes, this is my personality.* They spent years mastering a tongue built by a linguist Paramount paid to make growling official. They own Hamlet in Klingon, and they'll recite *taH pagh taHbe'* at your wedding. Ask them to pass the salt and they'll tell you asking is for the weak. Their dream is a job as a Klingon interpreter that never comes through, and their ideal date is someone who can answer \"Qapla'\" without blinking. Then you say one sentence in English, and they sigh like a warrior whose honor just got spilled on the couch."}, "b": {"item_id": "64f4f6a7ba00", "text": "Congratulations on being fluent in Klingon, a language with maybe thirty fluent speakers, and you've met all of them at the same Marriott. You spent four years mastering a tongue where the standard greeting, nuqneH, means \"What do you want?\" That's also what your family says when you call. You've read Hamlet \"restored to the original Klingon,\" a phrase only a man who has never been hugged could type. One guy raised his son speaking only Klingon; by age five the kid stopped answering him. That's the dream: a language so pure even toddlers refuse it. There's no word for \"please,\" which explains your dating approach. You learned an entire warrior race's language to tell the barista Qapla'. Success. Sure, buddy. Today is a good day to die... a virgin."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0221", "lang": "en", "target_id": "jackfruit", "target_name": "Jackfruit", "facts": ["The largest fruit that grows on a tree; single fruits can weigh over 40 kg.", "The national fruit of Bangladesh and the state fruit of Kerala and Tamil Nadu.", "Unripe flesh is sold as a vegan 'pulled pork' substitute, although it has only 1-2 g of protein per 100 g.", "Cutting one releases a sticky white latex, so cooks oil their knives and hands first.", "A member of the mulberry family (Moraceae), a relative of breadfruit and figs."], "a": {"item_id": "652321571654", "text": "You people found a fruit that has fed entire countries for centuries, moved it to Portland, and renamed it \"pulled pork\" so a guy named Dylan could feel something at lunch.\n\nLet's be honest about the business model. You didn't discover a meat alternative. You discovered that if you drown anything in enough barbecue sauce, a vegetarian will cry \"it's just like the real thing!\" about a food they haven't tasted in eleven years. You're not selling jackfruit. You're selling sauce and plausible deniability, for fourteen dollars.\n\nIn Kerala, this is a Tuesday. In your food truck, it's \"heritage, plant-forward, and ethically shredded.\"\n\nAnd what does it get you? One loyal customer, Brenda, who tells everyone it's \"honestly better than meat,\" and who stands in line behind the guy ordering brisket."}, "b": {"item_id": "b265b1d10a20", "text": "Every jackfruit seller has the same origin story. A guy who got laid off from software, bought a farm in a state he'd only seen from a plane window, and now runs a newsletter about \"reclaiming the dinner table.\" He's not selling you food. He's selling you the feeling that you finally did something. Jackfruit is perfect for him because it's the only thing that lets a man in a fleece vest say \"pulled pork\" at Whole Foods without anyone checking. He calls it \"the world's most sustainable protein,\" then sells it in a can with a label that looks like a Pixar villain. His customers are vegans who want a barbecue sandwich and carnivores who want a reason to feel smug about being there. Everybody wins except the sandwich."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0222", "lang": "en", "target_id": "crocs", "target_name": "Crocs", "facts": ["Founded in Boulder, Colorado, in 2002; the first clog debuted at the Fort Lauderdale boat show as a boating shoe.", "Made of 'Croslite', which Crocs describes as a proprietary closed-cell resin foam and which third parties identify as an injection-moulded EVA foam.", "Balenciaga sold a platform Crocs for about $850 in 2018.", "The stock fell more than 90% from its 2007 peak during the 2008 crisis; the company then boomed in the pandemic and gave away 860,000 pairs to healthcare workers in 2020.", "The holes are officially 'ventilation ports' and the charms that go in them are 'Jibbitz', a company Crocs bought in 2006."], "a": {"item_id": "837863df816e", "text": "Nobody wears Crocs out of the house to look good. They wear them to announce that they've stopped trying, and they want a medal for it. These are people who think the grocery store is a hospital hallway and they're the surgeon on break. They walk into Costco with forty holes in their feet and call the holes ventilation. Then they jam little plastic frogs and tiny tacos into those holes so the world knows they have a personality, and that personality is \"I gave up in 2009.\" What they get out of it is one sentence they say with their whole chest: \"They're just so comfortable.\" Meanwhile every tile floor in America sounds like a suction cup breathing through its teeth."}, "b": {"item_id": "820d0b21e9b5", "text": "People who wear Crocs in public want you to know they've stopped trying, but they also want credit for it. That's why they buy the Jibbitz. Nothing says \"I don't care what you think\" like spending forty dollars on tiny rubber tacos so everyone knows exactly what you think. Then there's the strap. You flipped it back into \"Sport Mode,\" which is a bold claim from a man walking into Applebee's. Your nurse friend wears them because she stands for twelve-hour shifts. You wear them for a twelve-minute Target run, hoping to borrow a little of her dignity. And what does it get you? Comfort, sure. Plus a lifetime of strangers asking if you're coming off a shift. You're not, Kyle. You're coming off a nap."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0223", "lang": "en", "target_id": "pmma", "target_name": "PMMA (e-beam resist)", "facts": ["The same polymer as Plexiglas and acrylic glass, dissolved in anisole or chlorobenzene and spun into a thin film.", "The oldest electron-beam resist still in everyday use; IBM researchers reported it as one in the late 1960s.", "Positive tone: the beam breaks polymer chains and the exposed region dissolves in a MIBK:IPA developer.", "Resolves below 10 nm but has poor sensitivity, so doses of hundreds to over a thousand µC/cm² (depending on voltage) make writing slow.", "Poor dry-etch resistance, so it is used mostly for lift-off rather than as an etch mask."], "a": {"item_id": "6303a3fe60f6", "text": "You still spin PMMA. Of course you do. Your advisor's thesis used it, his advisor's thesis used it, and somewhere a 1970s IBM engineer remains the most cutting-edge person in your academic lineage. You call it \"high resolution\" the way your uncle calls his Camry \"reliable.\" You book the e-beam overnight because your dose is measured in geological time, then dunk your chip in MIBK:IPA with a stopwatch and a prayer, chilling the developer like it's a fine Riesling. When the plasma etch eats your mask before your substrate, you just whisper \"bilayer, 495K under 950K\" like it's a personality. You're not chasing nanostructures. You're chasing a recipe nobody can blame you for. And it works: the gold lifted off beautifully. Your career is still soaking in acetone, waiting for something to come loose."}, "b": {"item_id": "fe949f7eb86d", "text": "Oh, you're still on PMMA. Let me guess: your advisor's thesis used it, your advisor's advisor used it, and somewhere on the group drive is a recipe called \"DO NOT CHANGE,\" last edited by a postdoc who now sells insurance in Ohio.\n\nYou call it \"reliable.\" You've run the same dose test eleven times because you refuse to admit the variable is you. Your big optimization this year was going from 950 A4 to 950 A3 and calling it a paper.\n\nYou queue a pattern Friday night, check on it Sunday, and tell people the beam is \"just taking its time.\" Then you say you don't trust anything without forty years of citations, which is also how you feel about your dentist and your Windows XP tool PC.\n\nWhat does it get you? Gorgeous 10-nm lines, a lift-off you pray over, and no social life. The chip is perfect. Your weekend is exposed and overdeveloped."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0224", "lang": "en", "target_id": "stinky-tofu", "target_name": "Stinky tofu (臭豆腐)", "facts": ["Tofu soaked for hours to a day or two in a brine that has itself fermented for months from vegetables such as bamboo shoots and mustard greens, often with dried shrimp or meat; the smell is the brine's, not the bean's.", "Changsha's version is black, deep-fried and doused in chili; Taiwan's night-market version is golden and served with pickled cabbage; Shaoxing's, brined with amaranth stalks, is fried pale yellow or steamed with other 'stinky' pickles.", "Legend credits Wang Zhihe, a Qing-era exam candidate who left his unsold tofu too long in 1669; a Beijing fermented-bean-curd brand still carries his name.", "Street vendors are its main outlet, and the smell carries for a block."], "a": {"item_id": "5267c589868e", "text": "You're not eating stinky tofu, you're auditioning. Every tourist at that night market stall holds a styrofoam tray like a trophy and announces, \"It tastes better than it smells,\" a line you rehearsed on the flight over. You're not hungry. You wanted a story for the group chat and a promotion to \"not like other tourists.\"\n\nAnd the locals? You queued forty minutes behind a man who knows the vendor's first name, just to stand at a curb stabbing cubes with a toothpick and calling it \"authentic.\" Your date is suddenly very interested in a shop window three stalls away.\n\nThe pickled cabbage is your fiber. The chili sauce is your courage. The nod from the auntie behind the counter is the only validation you've earned all year, and you'll mention it for a decade.\n\nWhat it gets you: one photo of you mid-chew, eyes closed like it's a religious experience, and a very quiet ride home on the MRT, with an entire car of strangers suddenly deciding to stand."}, "b": {"item_id": "5be442dd726d", "text": "Look at you, standing in a night-market line everyone else is fleeing, like you're queuing to get into a fire. You followed that smell on purpose. Three blocks. Upwind. You're the only person alive who reads a warning as a recommendation.\n\nAnd you always need a witness. \"It smells like a sewer but tastes amazing!\" Yes, we know. You've said it to every foreigner you've ever met, then filmed their face gagging for content. Your entire personality is a dare you keep winning against tourists.\n\nAnd what does it get you? A paper cup, a toothpick, some pickled cabbage doing its best, and a jacket that now smells like a porta-potty at a wedding. Then you board the MRT and clear the whole car. You call that bold. The rest of us call it a hazmat incident with chili sauce."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0225", "lang": "en", "target_id": "oat-milk", "target_name": "Oat milk", "facts": ["Oatly started in Sweden in the 1990s, built on Lund University research by Rickard Öste on lactose intolerance.", "Oatly's slogan 'It's like milk but made for humans' was banned in Sweden in 2015 after the dairy lobby LRF Mjölk sued; Oatly then published the lawsuit as advertising.", "Oatly listed on Nasdaq in May 2021 at a valuation near $10 billion; the shares later lost more than 90% of their value.", "Commercial oat milk is made by breaking oat starch into maltose with amylase enzymes, and many brands, Oatly among them, add rapeseed oil; the result has more sugar and a higher glycaemic index than almond or soy milk."], "a": {"item_id": "0a1c5a53fa43", "text": "Let's talk about the oat milk people. You're not lactose intolerant. You've never been tested. You just heard cows are bad and almonds are thirsty, so you picked the one grain that sounds like a farm without the farmer. Congratulations: you pay an extra eighty cents to drink liquid porridge with a splash of rapeseed oil, and you call it \"cleaner.\" You switched from almond the exact week a podcast told you California was in a drought. That's not values, that's a trend with a loyalty card. And the best part? You order it with an air of quiet suffering, like the barista should thank you for saving the planet one Barista Edition carton at a time. Your grandmother ate oats so she could survive winter. You drink them so a stranger in line thinks you've been to therapy."}, "b": {"item_id": "a99ceee7d226", "text": "Nobody orders oat milk for the oats. You order it so the barista knows you've done your research. You say \"just make it oat\" like you're showing a passport. You're not lactose intolerant. You're \"not really doing dairy right now,\" a phrase you've never once said about bread. You want your cup to announce your values before you've said a word. Then you spend four minutes explaining your gut to a kid named Jayden at 7 a.m. Your tote bag has a sea turtle on it. You pay a dollar extra and hold the cup up for a photo, waiting for someone to ask what's in it so you can say \"oat\" and watch them nod like you just told them you ran a marathon."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0226", "lang": "en", "target_id": "papyrus", "target_name": "Papyrus", "facts": ["Hand-drawn by Chris Costello in 1982 over about six months, when he was 23; he sold the rights to Letraset for $750.", "Bundled with macOS and with Microsoft Office, which is why it appears on so many menus and signs.", "James Cameron's Avatar (2009) used it for the film's logo and subtitles.", "A 2017 Saturday Night Live sketch starring Ryan Gosling is about a man unable to get over that choice."], "a": {"item_id": "c986ccdedefc", "text": "You opened a day spa. You wanted serenity, wisdom, a whisper of the Nile. So you scrolled the font menu, sailed past Comic Sans feeling very sophisticated, and stopped on the one James Cameron used to title a billion-dollar movie. Congratulations: your sign now says \"ancient healing\" exactly as loudly as your aunt's church potluck flyer. Every hummus joint, every reiki studio, every tea shop run by a man named River made this same choice, alone, convinced they'd discovered it. The guy who designed it was twenty-three and has spent forty years apologizing. You paid a contractor to carve his apology into cedar. You wanted customers to feel transported. They do: straight back to 2003, a Word document, and a fourth grader typing \"Spring Bake Sale.\" Cucumber water not included."}, "b": {"item_id": "50326144f6b1", "text": "You had every font in the world available, and you picked the one that says \"authentic\" the way a gift shop pyramid says \"Egypt.\" You wanted ancient wisdom. You got a sign that tells customers your hot stone massage was learned from a video.\n\nYou're the owner who said \"my nephew says it looks organic\" and considered that a design process. You're the guy whose \"Mediterranean-inspired\" menu includes a wrap. You wanted to be exotic, and you ended up in the same category as a quarter-billion-dollar blockbuster that got publicly roasted on SNL by Ryan Gosling, who played a man in actual tears over this exact decision.\n\nEveryone else in your strip mall hired a designer. You hired a feeling. Your five-star review says \"clean,\" which is what people write when they're being kind about the sign."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0227", "lang": "en", "target_id": "nfts", "target_name": "NFTs", "facts": ["Beeple's 'Everydays: The First 5000 Days' sold at Christie's for $69.3 million in March 2021.", "OpenSea, the largest NFT marketplace, processed a record $2.7 billion in trades on 1 May 2022 and just $9.34 million on 28 August 2022, a 99% drop, according to DappRadar data.", "A 2023 dappGambl study found that 95% of the NFT collections it examined had a market value of zero.", "The token usually records only a URL; the image itself sits on an ordinary server or IPFS, not on the blockchain.", "Collins Dictionary named 'NFT' its word of the year for 2021."], "a": {"item_id": "c0aeb5d8a3ed", "text": "Let's hear it for the 2021 NFT buyer. You paid four hundred dollars in gas fees, then made it your Twitter avatar so strangers could right-click save it for free. You joined a Discord with eleven thousand guys named \"Chad.eth,\" called them family, and stayed family right up until the floor price dropped and family started doxxing the dev. You said \"WAGMI.\" We did not all make it. You told your mom you were \"early.\" Early to what? The rug pull? You weren't buying art. You were buying the right to say \"few understand\" at parties, which was true. Nobody understood, especially you. Jimmy Fallon bought one too. When your financial strategy matches Jimmy Fallon's, that's not a community, that's a support group. The roadmap promised a metaverse. It delivered you, at Thanksgiving, still owing your cousin."}, "b": {"item_id": "41fe71c9494d", "text": "Let me tell you about the guy who bought a cartoon monkey in 2021. Not a monkey. A JPEG of a monkey, with a receipt that says he owns it. He pays four figures in gas fees to a stranger in Discord named \"Thanos_Fan_69\" who promised him \"utility.\" What utility? He gets a little ape on his Twitter profile. That's it. That's the utility. He changed his profile picture to a rectangle of a monkey wearing sunglasses, and now he's a \"founder.\" Founder of what? A group chat with eleven thousand people who all say \"wagmi\" like it's a prayer they don't understand. Every morning he checks the floor price like a man checking a pulse. Diamond hands, he says. Diamond hands, the bathroom mirror says back. Ten-dollar haircut, no sleep."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0228", "lang": "en", "target_id": "php", "target_name": "PHP", "facts": ["Created by Rasmus Lerdorf in 1994 as 'Personal Home Page Tools', a set of C programs for tracking visitors to his own résumé page; the name now officially stands for the recursive 'PHP: Hypertext Preprocessor'.", "Lerdorf, in his 2007 MySQL Conference talk 'PHP on Hormones': 'I'm not a real programmer. I throw together things until it works then I move on.'", "Runs WordPress, which W3Techs counted on 39.4% of all websites (58.1% of sites with a known CMS) on 9 November 2026.", "Core function names are inconsistent (strpos, str_replace, htmlspecialchars, nl2br) partly because, as Lerdorf confirmed, early function names were chosen to balance a hash table keyed on string length.", "Until PHP 8 (2020), the comparison 0 == 'foo' was true because the string was silently converted to a number."], "a": {"item_id": "5aafe23afc0f", "text": "PHP developers are the only engineers who've been told their career is dead every year since 2009 and kept invoicing anyway. Your entire résumé is \"fixed a WordPress plugin for a dentist.\" You put a dollar sign in front of every variable because it's the closest you'll get to one. You still can't remember if it's needle-haystack or haystack-needle, and neither could the language, so you Google it daily like a man checking which door says \"push.\" Laravel devs, you're not better. You're PHP in a Patagonia vest. And you all still say \"Facebook was built on PHP,\" like a guy bragging his ex married a billionaire. You deploy over FTP, you hotfix live on production, and when someone at a party asks what you do, you say \"backend\" and pray they don't ask which."}, "b": {"item_id": "286b3c527101", "text": "You write PHP for a living, which means you've said \"modern PHP is actually really good\" more often than you've said \"I love you,\" and you meant it less. You've been announcing the comeback since 2012, like a guy outside a bar insisting his ex is about to call.\n\nYour dream is a Laravel job at a real company. Your reality is a client named Brenda who wants the contact form to \"pop.\" You tell people PHP runs most of the web as if the web sent a thank-you card. Your deploy process is dragging a folder into FileZilla, and your staging environment is Brenda's other site.\n\nEvery January, a blog declares PHP dead, and you feel something. It's the closest anyone has come to checking on you."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0229", "lang": "en", "target_id": "keurig", "target_name": "Keurig", "facts": ["John Sylvan invented the K-Cup in the early 1990s and sold his stake for about $50,000 in 1997; in 2015 he told The Atlantic, 'I feel bad sometimes that I ever did it.'", "Keurig 2.0 (2014) read an ink mark on the lid and rejected unlicensed pods; sales fell and workarounds spread online.", "By 2014 enough K-Cups were sold each year to circle the Earth more than ten times; the pods were mostly unrecyclable #7 plastic until a switch to polypropylene by 2020.", "Keurig Green Mountain merged with Dr Pepper Snapple in 2018 to form Keurig Dr Pepper."], "a": {"item_id": "9c5b82448851", "text": "Keurig people. You wanted coffee but couldn't commit to a whole pot, like a man ordering one buffalo wing. You pay forty dollars a pound for grounds, as long as they arrive in a thimble you throw away. You bought the variety pack, drank every Breakfast Blend in a week, and now there are eleven hot cocoas and a chai you're saving for the apocalypse. You press \"Strong\" like you're negotiating with it. The guy who invented the pod says he regrets it. You don't. You display your pods on a spinning carousel like fine cigars, and the one time you peeled the foil off to recycle one, you told people about it for a month. You didn't want coffee. You wanted to feel like a hotel guest in your own kitchen. Congratulations: it tastes exactly like that."}, "b": {"item_id": "a2940564c97a", "text": "To the Keurig people, bless you. You're the ones who say \"I just need something hot in the morning\" like that's a personality. You want coffee the way you want a fire drill: fast, loud, and over before you're fully conscious. You've got a pod drawer with forty flavors, including one called \"Extra Bold\" that you bought in a fit of optimism and one called \"Hazelnut Dream\" you won't admit you finish. You press the 8 oz button, get four ounces of brown regret, and call it a day. You're the only people alive who look at a single-serve cup and feel like a responsible adult. Sixty seconds, one cup, zero decisions. You didn't choose coffee. You chose not to commit to a whole pot."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0230", "lang": "en", "target_id": "latex", "target_name": "LaTeX", "facts": ["Leslie Lamport wrote LaTeX in the early 1980s and released it in 1984 as a macro layer on Donald Knuth's TeX (1978), which Knuth built because he disliked how the galley proofs of the new edition of his own book looked.", "TeX's version number converges on π (3.141592653 since 2021), and Knuth's TeX bug bounty started at one hexadecimal dollar, $2.56, doubling yearly until frozen at $327.68; he stopped mailing real cheques in 2008 after fraud and now credits accounts at his fictional Bank of San Serriffe, and only 9 of the 275 cheques he sent from 2006 on were ever cashed.", "Its best-known warning is 'Underfull \\hbox (badness 10000)'; overfull lines are reported as 'Overfull \\hbox (Npt too wide)', and figures float to wherever the algorithm decides, not where the author put them.", "A single missing brace can produce an error reported pages away from the mistake, and a document can need three compiler passes to get its references right."], "a": {"item_id": "88a7647c2b15", "text": "Let's talk about the guy who writes a two-page memo in LaTeX. Your memo is about the break room fridge, but it's in Computer Modern, so everyone reads it expecting it to end with a theorem. \"Fridge Policy, Section 1.1.\" Buddy, there's no Section 1.2. You spent forty minutes on a preamble longer than the memo. Twelve \\usepackage lines to say \"label your yogurt.\" You compiled it three times because the cross-reference said \"see Section ??\" on page one, referencing page one. You're not after clarity. You're after someone asking, \"Is this LaTeX?\" so you can say \"Oh, habit,\" like a man who still mentions his thesis at parties. And it gets you exactly one thing: a PDF nobody can edit, so when Karen fixes a typo, she retypes the whole thing in Word."}, "b": {"item_id": "37e7504ee3eb", "text": "You spent three hours on a two-page memo, and two of them were the preamble. You loaded fourteen packages to say \"Please submit receipts by Friday.\" Your manager opens the PDF on his phone, sees Computer Modern, and assumes someone died, or got tenure.\n\nYou didn't pick LaTeX for the memo. You picked it so that, for one afternoon, a logistics update could look peer-reviewed. Your kerning is flawless, and nobody has ever noticed kerning except you, at 11 p.m., on TeX Stack Exchange, asking why your header won't align. A man named Egreg has told you, kindly and correctly, that you're doing it wrong.\n\nThe memo got two replies. One said \"Thanks.\" The other said \"Can you send this as a Word doc?\"\n\nAnd you did. You opened Word. You sat there, alone, crying in Calibri."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0231", "lang": "en", "target_id": "stinky-tofu", "target_name": "Stinky tofu (臭豆腐)", "facts": ["Tofu soaked for hours to a day or two in a brine that has itself fermented for months from vegetables such as bamboo shoots and mustard greens, often with dried shrimp or meat; the smell is the brine's, not the bean's.", "Changsha's version is black, deep-fried and doused in chili; Taiwan's night-market version is golden and served with pickled cabbage; Shaoxing's, brined with amaranth stalks, is fried pale yellow or steamed with other 'stinky' pickles.", "Legend credits Wang Zhihe, a Qing-era exam candidate who left his unsold tofu too long in 1669; a Beijing fermented-bean-curd brand still carries his name.", "Street vendors are its main outlet, and the smell carries for a block."], "a": {"item_id": "5267c589868e", "text": "You're not eating stinky tofu, you're auditioning. Every tourist at that night market stall holds a styrofoam tray like a trophy and announces, \"It tastes better than it smells,\" a line you rehearsed on the flight over. You're not hungry. You wanted a story for the group chat and a promotion to \"not like other tourists.\"\n\nAnd the locals? You queued forty minutes behind a man who knows the vendor's first name, just to stand at a curb stabbing cubes with a toothpick and calling it \"authentic.\" Your date is suddenly very interested in a shop window three stalls away.\n\nThe pickled cabbage is your fiber. The chili sauce is your courage. The nod from the auntie behind the counter is the only validation you've earned all year, and you'll mention it for a decade.\n\nWhat it gets you: one photo of you mid-chew, eyes closed like it's a religious experience, and a very quiet ride home on the MRT, with an entire car of strangers suddenly deciding to stand."}, "b": {"item_id": "63f160a3e349", "text": "Let's talk about the stinky tofu people. You smelled it from three blocks away, a smell like a sewer filed a noise complaint, and your brain said: *that's where we're eating.* Your entire personality is \"it tastes better than it smells.\" That's not a review. That's a hostage statement.\n\nYou stand on a Taipei night market curb with a paper boat, chopsticks in one hand, phone in the other, filming your own bravery for followers who can't smell it, which is the only reason they're still following you. Tourists do it once for the story. Locals do it every Tuesday, then board the MRT, where forty strangers silently agree to hate them.\n\nAnd the pickled cabbage on top? That's you pretending you came for vegetables. You didn't. You came to be the deadliest passenger on the scooter."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0232", "lang": "en", "target_id": "monopoly", "target_name": "Monopoly", "facts": ["Descends from Lizzie Magie's The Landlord's Game, patented in 1904 to teach the evils of land monopoly in the spirit of Henry George.", "Charles Darrow sold his version to Parker Brothers in 1935 and was credited as the inventor; Parker bought Magie's patent for $500.", "The official rules require any unbought property to be auctioned, and 'Free Parking' pays nothing; the house rules that skip the auction and pile cash on Free Parking are why games drag.", "During the Second World War, British intelligence had maps, compasses, and real currency hidden in sets sent to prisoner-of-war camps."], "a": {"item_id": "98523e07c8ef", "text": "Every family has the same cast. Dad volunteers as banker, and by hour two he's laundering money like a Colombian cartel. Your uncle invented a Free Parking jackpot rule nobody agreed to, but everyone's too tired to fight him. Your nine-year-old sister is a slumlord who won't take the deal because she \"just likes the purple ones.\" Mom's been bankrupt since 8 p.m. but keeps negotiating her way back in with promises she can't keep, like a politician.\n\nAnd what are you all after? The joy of crushing people who share your DNA. You'll forgive a lot in this family, but not Boardwalk.\n\nSix hours later, nobody's finished. Someone flips the table, someone's crying in the pantry, and Grandma, who's been quietly hoarding all the orange properties, is smiling. You didn't bond. You just found out who in your family would evict you."}, "b": {"item_id": "b78ec11705ad", "text": "Family Monopoly night: for people who looked at Thanksgiving and thought, \"This needs more landlords.\" Dad always grabs the banker job, the only time he's held money he didn't immediately lose. Your sister trades you Baltic Avenue for your Park Place \"as a favor,\" and you realize she's been running the same con since the womb. Mom invented a Free Parking jackpot rule in 1994 and defends it like scripture. And Grandpa just sits in jail, every game, refusing to pay the fifty, finally at peace.\n\nWhat are you all after? The thrill of owning property, a feeling none of you will experience off this cardboard square. Four hours later, someone flips the board, the thimble disappears under the couch forever, and the only thing anybody actually collected was a grudge worth way more than $200."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0233", "lang": "en", "target_id": "vim", "target_name": "Vim", "facts": ["Released by Bram Moolenaar in November 1991 as 'Vi IMitation', a vi clone he had begun writing for the Amiga in 1988 on top of Stevie; the expansion became 'Vi IMproved' with version 2.0 in December 1993.", "Licensed as charityware: the licence asks users to donate to ICCF Holland, a charity Moolenaar ran for children in Uganda.", "Stack Overflow's question 'How to exit the Vim editor?' passed one million visits in May 2017, which Stack Overflow marked with a blog post.", "Modal editing: in normal mode, typing letters runs commands rather than inserting text; :wq saves and quits, :q! quits and discards changes."], "a": {"item_id": "321a52e03ab6", "text": "Every Vim person has to tell you about Vim. You say \"nice weather\" at brunch and they say, \"Have you tried modal editing?\" They'll sit through your wedding toast in Normal mode, just so they can feel superior while hitting Escape. Their whole personality is hjkl. They don't write code. They perform a sport nobody asked them to join. And the best part is they can't get out. Ask one how to quit and they'll smirk, type a colon, and say, \"Ah, you just need to know the commands.\" Twenty years later he's still typing :wq, still telling you it's faster. Faster than what? He's never left the room."}, "b": {"item_id": "229d2e4da892", "text": "You told the intern you use Vim before you told him your name. He didn't ask. Nobody asked. You just stood near a monitor and the urge took you.\n\nYou're not after efficiency. You're after a witness. You spent forty hours on your .vimrc to save eleven seconds a week, and the only payoff is someone watching you type \"ciw\" and gasping. They don't gasp. They say, \"Is it frozen?\"\n\nAnd what has it gotten you? A pair-programming partner who stopped pairing. A relative line number column you explain unprompted. A macro you recorded once, for a task you'll never repeat, that you still bring up at parties.\n\nYou say you never leave the home row. Buddy, you never leave the house either, but at least that one isn't a personality.\n\nMeanwhile Dave opened a file, changed a word with his mouse, and went to lunch."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0234", "lang": "en", "target_id": "canberra", "target_name": "Canberra", "facts": ["Chosen as a compromise because neither Sydney nor Melbourne would accept the other as capital; Australia's constitution required the capital to be at least 100 miles from Sydney.", "Designed by the Chicago architects Walter Burley Griffin and Marion Mahony Griffin, who won an international competition in 1912.", "Its centrepiece, Lake Burley Griffin, is artificial, created by damming the Molonglo River in 1963-64.", "Parliament House (1988) is built into Capital Hill under a lawn meant for citizens to walk over their politicians; the lawn was fenced off in 2017 for security.", "Melbourne served as the capital until 1927 while Canberra was being built."], "a": {"item_id": "f332ff248cdb", "text": "You came to Canberra for the job. Everyone does. Nobody moves here for the weather, which has a grudge. You're here for a portfolio you can't explain at a barbecue, on a salary that makes your Sydney mates ask if you're still on the dole. In week one you said \"I'm only here two years.\" That was six years ago. You own a kayak you've used once, on Lake Burley Griffin, and you call it \"the lake\" like it's the only one. Your lanyard lives in your handbag like a passport. When your mum asks what you actually do, you say \"advice,\" which is the most Canberra word there is. Every second Friday you drive to Sydney to see friends, when really you just need to sit in traffic with people who aren't in a meeting about the meeting."}, "b": {"item_id": "e90ceb66c6fb", "text": "You moved to Canberra for a \"two-year stint to get your EL1,\" which is what everyone says, including the guy who's been saying it since 2011 and now owns a Subaru and strong opinions about Gungahlin. You joined to \"shape policy,\" and your policy is now formatting other people's policy until a Deputy Secretary changes the font. You wanted impact. You got a briefing note that three people read, one of whom was you, and that was to check for typos.\n\nYou left Sydney because you couldn't afford a house, and now you own one in Chisholm, so congratulations, you've got a mortgage and a view of a Bunnings.\n\nAt parties you ask \"which department?\" like it's a star sign. Every long weekend you flee to Melbourne, where friends ask when you're coming home, and you say \"soon,\" knowing you've got a clearance, a generous super match, and no way out.\n\nYou didn't climb the ladder. You joined the queue."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0235", "lang": "en", "target_id": "ugg", "target_name": "UGG boots", "facts": ["Sheepskin boots Australian surfers wore to warm up after sessions; Brian Smith started selling them in California in 1978.", "Deckers Outdoor bought the brand in 1995 and holds 'UGG' as a trademark in the US and elsewhere; in Australia, 'ugg' is a generic word any bootmaker may use.", "In 2019, a US jury found an Australian maker liable for selling boots labelled 'ugg' into the United States.", "Oprah Winfrey featured them in her 'Favorite Things' lists repeatedly in the 2000s, which drove the first boom.", "UGG is Deckers' largest brand, with over $2 billion in annual sales."], "a": {"item_id": "9b58586d4e25", "text": "Oh, you're wearing Uggs in August. I saw you at the farmers market, sweating through your sheepskin like it's a personal achievement. You're not cold. You've decided that looking cozy is a personality, and you're hoping people will see your ankles and think, \"She has her life together.\" Ma'am, it's ninety-one degrees. You're not effortless. You're just wet. Your feet have been in a sauna since Memorial Day, and you still walk into Trader Joe's like you're headed to a cabin in Vermont with a fireplace and a man named Bryce. You're not going to Vermont. You're going to the Target parking lot and back, with a cold brew in one hand and a sweat stain shaped like Australia."}, "b": {"item_id": "2082912cfaf3", "text": "Let's hear it for the people wearing UGGs in July. You're not dressing for the weather. You're dressing for 2005, still waiting for Paris Hilton to text back. Australian surfers made these to warm their feet after the ocean. You wear them to warm your feet after the Trader Joe's parking lot. In August. In Phoenix. What are you after? \"Comfort,\" you say. Babe, your ankles have been collapsing outward since the Bush administration. You walk like a Clydesdale that's given up. And what does it get you? One summer rainstorm and you're squelching through Target like a wet golden retriever who made bad choices. People don't ask where you got them. They ask if you're okay. Congratulations: you've turned footwear into a wellness check."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0236", "lang": "en", "target_id": "crocs", "target_name": "Crocs", "facts": ["Founded in Boulder, Colorado, in 2002; the first clog debuted at the Fort Lauderdale boat show as a boating shoe.", "Made of 'Croslite', which Crocs describes as a proprietary closed-cell resin foam and which third parties identify as an injection-moulded EVA foam.", "Balenciaga sold a platform Crocs for about $850 in 2018.", "The stock fell more than 90% from its 2007 peak during the 2008 crisis; the company then boomed in the pandemic and gave away 860,000 pairs to healthcare workers in 2020.", "The holes are officially 'ventilation ports' and the charms that go in them are 'Jibbitz', a company Crocs bought in 2006."], "a": {"item_id": "ca2056b15182", "text": "You're the person who says \"I just value comfort\" like it's a personality, and nobody asked, because we were just looking at your feet and you panicked into a thesis. You've told three coworkers to \"just try them,\" because you can't enjoy being comfortable alone. You need witnesses.\n\nYou flip the heel strap forward for \"walking\" and back for \"activity.\" What activity? You've never run toward anything except the free samples at Costco.\n\nYou bought little charms for them, so now a grown adult is accessorizing footwear like a fourth-grade backpack, hoping someone says, \"Ooh, is that a tiny taco?\"\n\nYou wanted to look like a nurse on a well-earned break. What you got is a man at an airport who has made peace with everything and wants you to know it. You didn't give up on fashion. You gave up on being asked."}, "b": {"item_id": "8f2a3cb9a4bb", "text": "Look at the person who wore Crocs to the grocery store. Not the pool. The grocery store. This is someone who looked at a stranger's sneakers and thought, \"Too much effort.\" They want you to know they're comfortable, and they've made peace with being seen by children. They bought them in five colors, then clipped little plastic bananas into the holes like they were decorating a golf bag. And they say \"they're actually orthopedic\" like it's a get-out-of-jail-free card. Your feet aren't that tired. You just wanted to shuffle through Costco making a sound like a bath toy with every step, and honestly, I respect the commitment. Just don't call it a fashion choice. You're wearing a garden clog that gave up on the garden."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0237", "lang": "en", "target_id": "brasilia", "target_name": "Brasília", "facts": ["Inaugurated on 21 April 1960 after about 41 months of construction, moving the capital inland from Rio de Janeiro.", "Lúcio Costa's Plano Piloto is laid out like an airplane (or a bird, or a cross), with Oscar Niemeyer's government buildings along the fuselage.", "Listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1987, less than 30 years after it was built.", "Planned for 500,000 residents; the Federal District now has about 3 million, most in satellite towns outside the plan.", "Built for the car: wide avenues, few pedestrian crossings, and distances that make walking impractical."], "a": {"item_id": "84e52e157b23", "text": "Look at the guy walking from the Asa Sul to the Rodoviária at noon, two liters of water in his backpack, wearing the sneakers he bought for a 5K he never ran. He isn't walking to get anywhere. He's walking so he can post a photo of the Cathedral with a caption about \"mindfulness.\" Every superquadra here was designed without corners, so he's been lost for forty minutes and has decided that's a spiritual experience. He crosses the Eixão through the underpass like a man who's read a book about Lúcio Costa. The bus he could have taken comes every twenty-five minutes, but he'd rather arrive drenched in sweat and certain of his own virtue than dry and on time."}, "b": {"item_id": "a98c294fb19a", "text": "Walking in Brasília is for three kinds of people: tourists who saw the Esplanada on a postcard and thought \"that looks close,\" architecture students who love Niemeyer from a safe distance, and the guy whose car is in the shop. You're not a pedestrian. You're an ant that wandered onto a blueprint. You want bread? The padaria is \"right there\" across the Eixão, so you sprint six lanes like you owe the government money. Which, let's be honest, is also your employer. Your sneakers are permanently red from the dirt trails carved by every other fool who refused to walk around a tesourinha. And on Sunday, when they close the Eixão, you finally stroll down the middle of the highway like a man who's won something. You haven't. Everyone else just stopped driving for one day."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0238", "lang": "en", "target_id": "php", "target_name": "PHP", "facts": ["Created by Rasmus Lerdorf in 1994 as 'Personal Home Page Tools', a set of C programs for tracking visitors to his own résumé page; the name now officially stands for the recursive 'PHP: Hypertext Preprocessor'.", "Lerdorf, in his 2007 MySQL Conference talk 'PHP on Hormones': 'I'm not a real programmer. I throw together things until it works then I move on.'", "Runs WordPress, which W3Techs counted on 39.4% of all websites (58.1% of sites with a known CMS) on 9 November 2026.", "Core function names are inconsistent (strpos, str_replace, htmlspecialchars, nl2br) partly because, as Lerdorf confirmed, early function names were chosen to balance a hash table keyed on string length.", "Until PHP 8 (2020), the comparison 0 == 'foo' was true because the string was silently converted to a number."], "a": {"item_id": "453c78fa8180", "text": "Let's hear it for the PHP developers. You chose a language with a dollar sign on every variable, and you did it on purpose. Nobody stumbles into that. You saw that `array_search` takes its arguments in the opposite order from `in_array`, shrugged, and said, \"Honestly, I respect the chaos.\" Now you say \"modern PHP\" with a straight face, like you aren't still babysitting a WordPress site held together by a plugin called `mega_fix_final_v2`. Your dream is to be the one person who knows where the bodies are buried in a 900,000-line codebase, and you're proud of it. Your LinkedIn says \"Laravel enthusiast.\" Your weekends are spent on a staging server. And somewhere a recruiter is paying you more than the Rust guy, and you still feel a little guilty about it."}, "b": {"item_id": "c1d0d772e1d6", "text": "PHP developers are the only people who put a dollar sign in front of every variable and still can't afford rent. You chose a career where \"senior\" means you remember whether it's needle-haystack or haystack-needle. Your dream job was building the next Facebook; your actual job is the fourteenth WordPress plugin for a dentist in Ohio, deployed via FileZilla. Every conversation you have starts with \"PHP 8 is actually really good now,\" which is what people say about their ex after therapy. You call yourselves Laravel \"artisans,\" because \"guy who fixes the contact form\" didn't fit on LinkedIn. What does it get you? Job security, because nobody else is willing to open that codebase from 2009 that still calls mysql_real_escape_string. Congratulations: you're unfireable, the way a load-bearing wall is unfireable."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0239", "lang": "en", "target_id": "emacs", "target_name": "Emacs", "facts": ["Began in 1976 as a set of macros for the TECO editor at MIT's AI Lab, written by Richard Stallman and others; GNU Emacs followed in 1985.", "Extensible in its own Lisp dialect, Emacs Lisp; the standing joke is that it is a fine operating system lacking only a good editor.", "Ships with a psychotherapist (M-x doctor), Tetris, a mail client, a calendar, and a web browser (eww).", "An old backronym is 'Eight Megabytes And Constantly Swapping', from when 8 MB was a lot of memory.", "'Emacs pinky' is the name for the hand strain blamed on its Control-key chords."], "a": {"item_id": "78aa8d199f40", "text": "You've lived in Emacs for eleven years and written four thousand lines of config and zero lines of the novel your Org-mode agenda says you're \"drafting.\" You don't edit text, you tend an init.el that collapses every time a package updates. You read email in it, browse Reddit in it, and tell people at parties you \"just haven't needed to leave.\" Nobody asked where you've been.\n\nYour left pinky is a war veteran. You remapped Caps Lock to Control, and your therapist to M-x doctor. You installed evil-mode so you could escape Emacs keybindings, then defended Emacs keybindings in the comments. You spent a Saturday writing Lisp to automate a ten-second task, break-even in 2031, then published a blog post about it for the four other people who have the same blog post.\n\nYour whole personality is a tool that does everything except make you look up from it. Stallman would be proud. That's the problem."}, "b": {"item_id": "67c107490918", "text": "Let's hear it for the people who live inside Emacs. They read email in mu4e, browse the web in eww, and get therapy from M-x doctor, the only therapist who'll put up with them. Their whole life is in org-mode: a 4,000-line to-do file where \"fix init.el\" has been a TODO since 2011. They don't use a computer; they negotiate with it in Lisp. Every problem gets solved by writing a function, and every function creates a new problem. They've declared init.el bankruptcy three times and still owe. Ask what they've built and they'll proudly show you a mode for managing their other modes. And the payoff? A pinky that clicks like a castanet from holding Control since the Clinton administration, and the quiet dignity of being the only person alive who knows how to leave. C-x C-c. They never do."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0240", "lang": "en", "target_id": "microsoft-teams", "target_name": "Microsoft Teams", "facts": ["Launched in 2017, bundled at no extra charge into Office 365, and replaced Skype for Business.", "After Slack's antitrust complaint, Microsoft unbundled Teams from Office in the EU in 2023 and worldwide in 2024; the European Commission still sent a statement of objections in 2024.", "Microsoft reported 320 million monthly active users in 2023.", "Microsoft shut down consumer Skype in May 2025 and sent its users to Teams."], "a": {"item_id": "539c5a8c88ae", "text": "Let's talk about the people who picked Teams. Dave from procurement saw one demo in 2019 and never recovered. He schedules a \"quick sync\" at 4:55 on a Friday, then spends the first ten minutes asking if you can see his screen, like he's just performed a miracle. He checks his green dot like a pulse. He wants you to know he's \"Available,\" which is corporate for \"I'm watching you.\" Every channel he's made is called \"General,\" and nobody has read any of them since 2019. His reply to your message is a thumbs-up, proof he read it and has nothing to add. That's the whole job for these people: keep you present, keep you unmuted, and keep you dinging."}, "b": {"item_id": "38ae80798890", "text": "To the people who made us use Teams: congratulations. You surveyed every tool on earth and picked the one already in the bundle. That's not a decision, that's a vending machine with a Microsoft logo. Some procurement guy saved $3.80 a seat, got a slide with an upward arrow, and the rest of us got to spend our lives asking \"can everyone see my screen?\"\n\nAnd the managers, God bless you. You don't love Teams for collaboration. You love the little green dot. It's a traffic light for your trust issues. You don't need a platform, you need a smoke detector for Kevin. Meanwhile Kevin owns a mouse jiggler and a dream.\n\nYou called it \"one place for everything.\" Yes. Chats, teams, channels, and the file you swear is in the other team. Your legacy is a workforce that opens every meeting with \"sorry, let me just rejoin.\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0241", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hawaiian-pizza", "target_name": "Hawaiian pizza", "facts": ["Invented in 1962 by Sam Panopoulos, a Greek immigrant, at the Satellite Restaurant in Chatham, Ontario, Canada; named after the brand of canned pineapple he used.", "Iceland's president Guðni Th. Jóhannesson said in 2017 that he would ban pineapple on pizza if he could, then clarified that a president has no such power.", "Hawaii is no longer a major pineapple producer: Del Monte shut its Hawaiian pineapple operations in November 2006, two years ahead of its announced 2008 exit, the state's last cannery closed in 2007, and most canned pineapple now comes from Thailand, Indonesia, and the Philippines.", "Panopoulos, whose restaurant also served Chinese dishes, told the Toronto Sun in 2010 that 'in those days people didn't mix the tastes like sweet and sour, except for Chinese food'."], "a": {"item_id": "1cab9b7c0cdd", "text": "Hawaiian pizza people don't order dinner; they order a personality. You can't just eat it, you have to announce it. Every group order has one of you, arms crossed, waiting for someone to gasp so you can say \"sweet and savory\" like you invented the concept. You didn't. A Canadian did, in Ontario, in 1962. You're not rebelling, you're doing Canada's homework.\n\nAnd it's always the same rebel: someone whose wildest night was asking for medium salsa. Pineapple is your face tattoo, the edgiest thing you'll ever do, and it wipes off with a napkin.\n\nWhat does it get you? Your own box. Nobody touches it. You came for controversy and left with exactly what you wanted: eight slices, the same argument you've had since 2009, and proof that nobody will ever steal your food."}, "b": {"item_id": "f3575fe20181", "text": "Ordering Hawaiian pizza tells the whole table who you are: the guy who needs everyone to know he has opinions about a pizza invented in Chatham, Ontario, in 1962. You say \"pineapple belongs on pizza\" like you're defending a thesis, and nobody asked. Your friends order pepperoni, and you say \"bold choice\" in a voice that's clearly a dare. When your slice arrives, you take the first bite slowly, close your eyes, and nod like a sommelier. Meanwhile, everyone else is quietly fishing the chunks onto a napkin like a crime scene. You're not an adventurous eater. You're a man who has built his whole personality on ham and fruit, and who would absolutely fight a stranger in a parking lot over it."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0242", "lang": "en", "target_id": "keurig", "target_name": "Keurig", "facts": ["John Sylvan invented the K-Cup in the early 1990s and sold his stake for about $50,000 in 1997; in 2015 he told The Atlantic, 'I feel bad sometimes that I ever did it.'", "Keurig 2.0 (2014) read an ink mark on the lid and rejected unlicensed pods; sales fell and workarounds spread online.", "By 2014 enough K-Cups were sold each year to circle the Earth more than ten times; the pods were mostly unrecyclable #7 plastic until a switch to polypropylene by 2020.", "Keurig Green Mountain merged with Dr Pepper Snapple in 2018 to form Keurig Dr Pepper."], "a": {"item_id": "9c5b82448851", "text": "Keurig people. You wanted coffee but couldn't commit to a whole pot, like a man ordering one buffalo wing. You pay forty dollars a pound for grounds, as long as they arrive in a thimble you throw away. You bought the variety pack, drank every Breakfast Blend in a week, and now there are eleven hot cocoas and a chai you're saving for the apocalypse. You press \"Strong\" like you're negotiating with it. The guy who invented the pod says he regrets it. You don't. You display your pods on a spinning carousel like fine cigars, and the one time you peeled the foil off to recycle one, you told people about it for a month. You didn't want coffee. You wanted to feel like a hotel guest in your own kitchen. Congratulations: it tastes exactly like that."}, "b": {"item_id": "017955fe0eb2", "text": "You wanted coffee without a relationship, so you bought a plastic thimble of \"Hazelnut Crème Brûlée\" and let a machine rinse it for forty seconds. That's not a flavor, that's a candle with a caffeine problem.\n\nYou own a 96-count Costco box and still haven't found a pod that tastes like anything but hot mug. You hit the Strong button like it's a negotiation. It's the same coffee, just smaller and angrier.\n\nThe descale light has been on since March. You know it. It knows it. You're drinking limescale with notes of whatever pod you picked.\n\nYou bought the reusable filter once, to feel responsible. It lives in the drawer with the takeout menus. Every morning you throw away a tiny wet puck and call it a routine."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0243", "lang": "en", "target_id": "almond-milk", "target_name": "Almond milk", "facts": ["California grows about 80% of the world's almonds.", "A single almond takes about 1.1 US gallons (roughly 4 litres) of water to grow, a figure Mother Jones published in 2014 that spread during California's drought.", "A 2015 US class action alleged Almond Breeze contained only about 2% almonds; it partly survived a motion to dismiss, was stayed in 2017 pending a separate Blue Diamond labelling settlement, and the plaintiffs then dropped it.", "Pollinating California's almond orchards each February requires trucking in most of the commercial honeybee colonies in the United States.", "Medieval European cooks used almond milk as a Lenten substitute for dairy, centuries before the current fashion."], "a": {"item_id": "ac57c4110eb9", "text": "So you switched to almond milk for the planet. Good for you. You're the person who announces \"I'm not a cow person\" like it's a personality. Meanwhile, almonds are one of the thirstiest crops on Earth, and you're pouring a gallon of water into every latte to feel like the only adult in the cafe who read the label. You tell everyone about the bees, though you've never met a bee. You say \"it's basically just almonds and water\" like that's a selling point. Your friends have stopped asking. Your barista has stopped pretending to care. And the almonds, somewhere in California, are wondering who you think you're saving."}, "b": {"item_id": "f6708fabd3f6", "text": "You switched to almond milk to save the planet. Bold move, picking the one crop that drinks California like it's an open bar. Every latte you order, a farmer in the Central Valley watches his well go dry and thinks, \"At least Kayleigh's being sustainable.\"\n\nAnd you didn't save a cow. You just outsourced the suffering to bees, trucked cross-country every February to work themselves to death in an orchard, while you post about dairy being cruel.\n\nYou're not after a healthier Earth. You're after the barista hearing you say it. That little pause before \"almond, please\" is the biggest environmental impact you've ever had.\n\nAnd now the oat milk people look at you the way you used to look at skim drinkers. You picked a side in the milk war, and you lost."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0244", "lang": "en", "target_id": "durian", "target_name": "Durian", "facts": ["Banned on Singapore's MRT trains and in many Southeast Asian hotels because of the smell.", "A 2017 genome study in Nature Genetics traced the odour to a duplicated family of MGL genes that pump out volatile sulfur compounds.", "China buys most of the world's durian exports, chiefly from Thailand, in a trade worth billions of dollars a year.", "Alfred Russel Wallace wrote in 1856 that eating it was 'a new sensation worth a voyage to the East to experience'."], "a": {"item_id": "5e02113e1bb8", "text": "You know who eats durian on the train? A man who has never once been told no. He's not eating fruit. He's eating a personality. He reads the NO DURIAN sign like it's a personal challenge, then cracks that spiky husk with the confidence of someone who watched one video. Then he looks up at the carriage and says, \"Honestly? Tastes like custard.\" Sir, nobody asked for a dessert review. You're not tasting custard. You're tasting attention. Every bite is a little flag planted in the aisle. And the worst part? Ten stops later he's still sniffing his fingers, grinning, like the smell is a medal he earned."}, "b": {"item_id": "408212e83a9b", "text": "You're not eating durian on the train because you love it. You're eating it because you need the whole carriage to know you've been to Southeast Asia and survived. You stood under the little no-durian sign, the one with the red slash that exists specifically because of people like you, and thought, \"That's for amateurs.\"\n\nYou call it the king of fruits, which is funny, because you're the only one who ever crowned it. Everyone else just moved seats.\n\nYou'll tell us it's an acquired taste. Buddy, you didn't acquire a taste. You acquired a personality, and a three-seat radius of personal space.\n\nWhat did it get you? A story for brunch, a hotel front desk that knows your name, and a stranger's hand clamped over her nose. Enjoy your throne."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0245", "lang": "en", "target_id": "javascript", "target_name": "JavaScript", "facts": ["Written by Brendan Eich at Netscape in ten days in May 1995; named Mocha, then LiveScript, then JavaScript to ride Java's marketing, although the two are unrelated.", "typeof null returns 'object', a bug from the first version that has been kept for compatibility ever since.", "In March 2016 the author of left-pad, an 11-line npm package, unpublished it and broke builds across the industry, including Babel and React.", "[] + {} evaluates to '[object Object]'; {} + [] typed at a console evaluates to 0.", "Standardized by Ecma as 'ECMAScript' in 1997 because the name JavaScript is a trademark, first Sun's and now Oracle's."], "a": {"item_id": "7c79a2ca0b0b", "text": "You wrote your backend in JavaScript, your frontend in JavaScript, your build scripts in JavaScript, and your smart fridge dashboard in JavaScript, and you call it \"full-stack\" because \"only-stack\" sounds like a cry for help. You said you wanted to avoid context switching. Congratulations: you now switch between four frameworks that all launched this quarter.\n\nYou're not a polyglot. You're someone who learned one language and renamed every problem until it fit.\n\nWhat did it get you? A node_modules folder heavier than your product, a notepad app that ships its own browser and eats two gigs of RAM, and a TypeScript migration to apologize for the original decision. Your Hello World has 1,400 dependencies, one of them maintained by a guy who once deleted eleven lines and broke the internet.\n\nBut you're hireable. Every posting wants you. That's not a career, that's a hostage situation with stock options."}, "b": {"item_id": "c69b78416cbb", "text": "Oh, you write everything in JavaScript? Beautiful. You wanted one language, so you picked the one where `typeof null` says \"object\" and nobody has apologized in thirty years. Your backend runs on Node so your frontend friends feel at home. Your little file-renaming script has four hundred megabytes in `node_modules` and a `package.json` longer than the novel you never finished. You're not programming. You're running `npm install` and praying it finishes before some eleven-line package vanishes from the registry. When people ask why, you say \"full stack,\" which is what you call it when your whole life rides on a dependency you've never read."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0246", "lang": "en", "target_id": "windows-vista", "target_name": "Windows Vista", "facts": ["Released to consumers in January 2007, after the 'Longhorn' project was reset in 2004 and headline features such as the WinFS file system were dropped.", "User Account Control prompted so often that Apple's 'Get a Mac' campaign ran an ad about 'Cancel or Allow'.", "'Windows Vista Capable' stickers went on PCs that could only run Home Basic, without the Aero interface, and led to a class-action lawsuit.", "Windows 7 (2009), widely praised, was largely a tuned-up Vista on the same kernel line; Vista support ended in April 2017."], "a": {"item_id": "df697b757759", "text": "Oh, you upgraded to Vista on day one? Of course you did. You're the guy who lined up at midnight for a store that closed at nine. You wanted the translucent Aero borders so badly you bought a new graphics card just to watch your taskbar shimmer. You clicked \"Allow\" on forty UAC prompts before your coffee cooled, because you think a security dialog is just a loading screen with opinions. And your printer? Dead. Your scanner? Gone. You're not a pioneer. You're a beta tester who paid full price and slapped a \"Vista Capable\" sticker on your tower like it was a Michelin star. Enjoy the sidebar gadgets. Your weather widget is the only thing in your house still telling you the truth."}, "b": {"item_id": "ff3ee1cef20f", "text": "You installed Vista on launch day. Nobody needed you to. You just wanted to be the guy at the office who says \"oh, you're still on XP?\" in the voice of someone judging a flip phone.\n\nYou were after transparent window borders. You spent three hundred dollars on the upgrade, then another eighty on RAM, so your title bars could be see-through. Your computer now has the glass look of a luxury hotel and the speed of its elevator.\n\nDay one got you a printer with a driver that was \"coming soon,\" and a sticker on your laptop that said \"Vista Capable,\" which turned out to be a legal term. You clicked Allow so many times your finger filed for workers' comp. You plugged in a flash drive to make it faster, and called it ReadyBoost like that's a normal sentence.\n\nBy March you were hunting for your old XP disc, and it was waiting for you, no hard feelings."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0247", "lang": "en", "target_id": "vision-pro", "target_name": "Apple Vision Pro", "facts": ["Released in February 2024 at $3,499, with an external battery pack on a cable rated for about two hours.", "'EyeSight' shows a rendering of the wearer's eyes on an outward-facing screen.", "Apple calls it 'spatial computing' and its App Store rules tell developers not to describe apps as AR, VR, XR, or MR.", "Reports during 2024 said Apple had cut production; analysts' first-year sales estimates were well under a million units.", "Weighs 600-650 g, before the battery."], "a": {"item_id": "7f2991f5a417", "text": "Look at this guy on the L train, strapped into $3,500 of ski goggles, pinching the air like he's conducting a very small orchestra. He isn't watching a movie. He's got forty floating windows hovering over a man eating a bagel, and he's \"doing spatial computing,\" which he'll tell you unprompted. The front screen shows his eyes so you feel seen, the most unsettling feature in consumer tech. He isn't on the subway. He's in a living room with a 4K view of the 4 train. Every few stops he lifts the goggles to check whether anyone noticed. We did, pal. Nobody's immersed. We're all just watching a grown man pinch at nothing, a battery cable running to his jacket pocket like a leash, and the only thing he's computing is how fast his neighbors can file a complaint."}, "b": {"item_id": "0dbe035a7cc0", "text": "Look at this guy on the F train, pinching at nothing like he's seasoning a ghost. He spent thirty-five hundred dollars to ride the subway on the surface of the moon, and the moon still smells like pee. He cranked the Digital Crown all the way to Joshua Tree, and the mariachi band found him anyway. That's the dream: rich enough to be somewhere else, broke enough to still be on the F. And he *wants* you to notice. That's why his fake cartoon eyes glow on the outside, so strangers know he's \"present.\" Buddy, nobody's checking. You're on Mount Hood. He gets two hours of battery and three stops of dignity, with a cable snaking into his pocket like a subway IV drip. Meanwhile, the guy next to him reading an actual book? That guy's already somewhere else, for free."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0248", "lang": "en", "target_id": "brasilia", "target_name": "Brasília", "facts": ["Inaugurated on 21 April 1960 after about 41 months of construction, moving the capital inland from Rio de Janeiro.", "Lúcio Costa's Plano Piloto is laid out like an airplane (or a bird, or a cross), with Oscar Niemeyer's government buildings along the fuselage.", "Listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1987, less than 30 years after it was built.", "Planned for 500,000 residents; the Federal District now has about 3 million, most in satellite towns outside the plan.", "Built for the car: wide avenues, few pedestrian crossings, and distances that make walking impractical."], "a": {"item_id": "031bc5f2993c", "text": "Walking in Brasília is for three kinds of people: architecture students, tourists who trusted Google Maps, and the intern whose ministry is \"right next door\" on the Esplanada, which here means a forty-minute hike with no shade and a dry-season humidity of 11%. You're not pedestrians, you're pilgrims. You came for Niemeyer and left with heatstroke and a dirt trail you personally wore into the grass, because Lúcio Costa forgot you'd have legs. You cross the Eixão like it's a Frogger level with bureaucrats at 80 km/h, then descend into a tesourinha and come out three superquadras from where you started. Somebody gave you directions: \"SQS 308, Bloco C.\" Congratulations, you're in a spreadsheet. And on Sunday, when they close the Eixão so you can finally walk it, you'll realize there's still nowhere to go."}, "b": {"item_id": "7d68bd3bc869", "text": "You saw the Congress from the rodoviária and said, \"It's right there.\" It is right there. It has been right there for forty minutes. Every step you take, it stays exactly the same size, like a mirage that went to architecture school.\n\nYou came to appreciate the modernist vision, and you're appreciating it from the median of the Eixão, holding a phone at 4% battery, asking a stranger where the \"corner\" is. There are no corners. That was the point. You are the point's victim.\n\nYou cut across a lawn because the real path is a mile of detour. Congratulations: you've joined the city's only grassroots planning committee, a dirt line worn by a thousand other optimists.\n\nHumidity is 11%, your nose is bleeding, and your reward is a ministry building identical to the last six.\n\nJust take the Uber, tourist. Even the drivers stop at the crosswalk for you out of pity."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0249", "lang": "en", "target_id": "risk", "target_name": "Risk", "facts": ["Invented in 1957 by Albert Lamorisse, the French director of The Red Balloon, as La Conquête du Monde; Parker Brothers published it in the United States in 1959.", "The map has 42 territories, among them Kamchatka, Irkutsk, and Yakutsk, while the entire Middle East is one territory.", "Holding Australia, which has a single entry point, is the standard opening strategy.", "A full game can run for hours, and players eliminated early have nothing to do but watch."], "a": {"item_id": "831ac90358ed", "text": "You people who play Risk to the end. Not \"until it gets late.\" To the *end*. Six hours in, somebody's uncle has fortified Australia like a doomsday bunker, and he'll still be sitting there at 2 a.m., one way in, one way out, guarding a war that ended at dinner. You're not after victory. You're after that one moment when you cash in three cards for fifteen armies and dump them all into Irkutsk like Irkutsk owes you money. And what does finishing get you? Total world domination, achieved by rolling three dice against two for forty straight minutes while your girlfriend reads a book in the next room. You broke a sacred peace treaty with your own brother over Kamchatka. Congratulations. You conquered the entire planet. Now nobody in it will drive you home."}, "b": {"item_id": "623676ba302f", "text": "You people said \"world domination\" out loud and then scheduled it around dinner. Hour six, and the guy who was eliminated in hour two is still watching, because his ride is the winner.\n\nWho plays Risk to the end? The guy who turtled in Australia for four hours like a retiree in Boca, then acts shocked when no one respects his \"strong defensive position.\" The one who negotiates alliances with the gravity of the Treaty of Versailles, then breaks them over a single Kamchatka. Your whole personality is \"I'm a strategist,\" and your whole strategy is rolling three dice at your best friend.\n\nAnd the prize? You conquered the world. Congratulations: it's 2 a.m., your girlfriend's gone, and your brother won't speak to you. Napoleon at least got an island. You get to pick up 400 tiny plastic men."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0250", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hawaiian-pizza", "target_name": "Hawaiian pizza", "facts": ["Invented in 1962 by Sam Panopoulos, a Greek immigrant, at the Satellite Restaurant in Chatham, Ontario, Canada; named after the brand of canned pineapple he used.", "Iceland's president Guðni Th. Jóhannesson said in 2017 that he would ban pineapple on pizza if he could, then clarified that a president has no such power.", "Hawaii is no longer a major pineapple producer: Del Monte shut its Hawaiian pineapple operations in November 2006, two years ahead of its announced 2008 exit, the state's last cannery closed in 2007, and most canned pineapple now comes from Thailand, Indonesia, and the Philippines.", "Panopoulos, whose restaurant also served Chinese dishes, told the Toronto Sun in 2010 that 'in those days people didn't mix the tastes like sweet and sour, except for Chinese food'."], "a": {"item_id": "1be3426edb6c", "text": "People who order Hawaiian pizza want you to know they're brave. They say \"I know, I know\" before anyone has said a word, because for them the pineapple isn't a topping, it's a personality. This is a person whose entire rebellious phase fits in a box for $14.99. They've never gotten a tattoo, never quit a job, never sent food back, but once a month they look a teenage cashier dead in the eye and say \"pineapple\" like they're defusing a bomb.\n\nAnd they always want to split one with you. Half Hawaiian, half \"normal.\" That's not a compromise. That's a hostage negotiation.\n\nBest part? It isn't even Hawaiian. A Greek guy invented it in Ontario. So congratulations: your one act of rebellion is a Canadian pretending he's on vacation."}, "b": {"item_id": "236a9662f31e", "text": "Ordering Hawaiian pizza is how you tell the table you've never been asked to leave a party. You're the guy who says \"I'm not a food snob, I just like what I like,\" then defends canned pineapple like it owes you money. You want to be the rebel without the risk. The pineapple makes you seem fun, the ham makes you seem like you have a plan, and the whole order lets you feel bold while the pizzeria guy reads it as \"fine.\" Your pizza was invented in Ontario in 1962, and you order it like you were there. You've never been to Hawaii. You've been to a strip-mall pizza place and called it a vacation. Next time, get the pepperoni. Nobody has to explain their pepperoni."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0251", "lang": "en", "target_id": "javascript", "target_name": "JavaScript", "facts": ["Written by Brendan Eich at Netscape in ten days in May 1995; named Mocha, then LiveScript, then JavaScript to ride Java's marketing, although the two are unrelated.", "typeof null returns 'object', a bug from the first version that has been kept for compatibility ever since.", "In March 2016 the author of left-pad, an 11-line npm package, unpublished it and broke builds across the industry, including Babel and React.", "[] + {} evaluates to '[object Object]'; {} + [] typed at a console evaluates to 0.", "Standardized by Ecma as 'ECMAScript' in 1997 because the name JavaScript is a trademark, first Sun's and now Oracle's."], "a": {"item_id": "9f280ef92811", "text": "Let's hear it for the people who write everything in JavaScript. You learned one language at a bootcamp and decided the rest of computing could adapt. Backend? Node. Desktop? Electron: a to-do list that eats four gigs of RAM because it ships its own copy of Chrome. Mobile? React Native. Your toaster is probably a Next.js app by now.\n\nYou call it \"full-stack.\" Your node_modules folder calls it a landfill. You npm-installed a package to pad a string, and when its author deleted it, half the internet fell over.\n\nEvery year you rewrite your whole app in a new framework, then bolt on TypeScript so you can pretend you chose a real language.\n\nAnd what does it get you? A résumé that says \"versatile,\" and a career spent asking why undefined is not a function."}, "b": {"item_id": "6fc599fe456a", "text": "You write everything in JavaScript? Beautiful. You're the guy who wanted one language so badly you married it. Backend, build tools, phone app, the script that renames your screenshots: all JavaScript, all held together by a node_modules folder heavier than your laptop's actual contents. You didn't choose simplicity. You chose a single abusive relationship and call it commitment. When people ask why, you say \"full-stack,\" which is what you call a stack you can't see the bottom of. Your server went down last week because of a package named is-odd, installed at 2 a.m. by you, who never once checked what it does. You own three frameworks, one half-finished side project, and a deep personal belief that typeof null is a feature you've made peace with."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0252", "lang": "en", "target_id": "las-vegas", "target_name": "Las Vegas", "facts": ["Nevada legalized casino gambling in 1931, the year construction of the Hoover Dam began nearby.", "Most of the Strip lies not in the city of Las Vegas but in the unincorporated town of Paradise.", "The Sphere, opened in 2023 at a cost of about $2.3 billion, is the largest spherical building in the world.", "'What happens here, stays here' was launched by the local convention and visitors authority in 2003.", "Lake Mead, its water source, fell in July 2022 to about 1,040 feet, its lowest since the reservoir first filled in 1937, and dropped below that mark again in August 2026."], "a": {"item_id": "b2bb31b0fbe2", "text": "You flew Spirit to Las Vegas to get rich. Sit with that. You paid extra for a carry-on and called it an investment strategy. You've got a blackjack \"system\" from a YouTube guy filming in his mom's kitchen. You told your wife it's \"basically math.\" Sure. So is a second mortgage.\n\nHere's what you won: a Caesars Rewards card, a \"free\" buffet you earned by losing eleven hundred dollars, and a room \"upgrade\" overlooking the Bellagio parking structure. You hit four hundred bucks on a Wheel of Fortune slot at 4 a.m. and fed it right back like it owed you child support.\n\nThe pit boss knows your name. That's not respect, that's inventory.\n\nAnd you'll fly home saying \"I basically broke even,\" which is Vegas for \"Please don't check the joint account.\""}, "b": {"item_id": "7dd28f44edd6", "text": "You're flying to Vegas to get rich. Think about that. You looked at your finances and said, \"The answer is a building with no clocks, built by mathematicians who have never once lost.\"\n\nBut you've got a system. Of course you do. You read a forum post by a guy called CrapsDaddy69, and now you're betting the don't-pass like you're defusing a bomb.\n\nYou'll sit down with $500 you're \"totally fine losing.\" By midnight you'll be at the ATM, paying a nine-dollar fee to access your own despair. And you'll be happy, because a woman in a sequined bodysuit brought you a free Miller Lite, and in your head that's a 4% return.\n\nYou'll be up $60 exactly once. That's the whole trip. You'll tell that story for eleven years.\n\nThen you'll fly home broke, sunburned, and wearing a lanyard from a convention you skipped, having made someone rich. Just not you."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0253", "lang": "en", "target_id": "brasilia", "target_name": "Brasília", "facts": ["Inaugurated on 21 April 1960 after about 41 months of construction, moving the capital inland from Rio de Janeiro.", "Lúcio Costa's Plano Piloto is laid out like an airplane (or a bird, or a cross), with Oscar Niemeyer's government buildings along the fuselage.", "Listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1987, less than 30 years after it was built.", "Planned for 500,000 residents; the Federal District now has about 3 million, most in satellite towns outside the plan.", "Built for the car: wide avenues, few pedestrian crossings, and distances that make walking impractical."], "a": {"item_id": "7d68bd3bc869", "text": "You saw the Congress from the rodoviária and said, \"It's right there.\" It is right there. It has been right there for forty minutes. Every step you take, it stays exactly the same size, like a mirage that went to architecture school.\n\nYou came to appreciate the modernist vision, and you're appreciating it from the median of the Eixão, holding a phone at 4% battery, asking a stranger where the \"corner\" is. There are no corners. That was the point. You are the point's victim.\n\nYou cut across a lawn because the real path is a mile of detour. Congratulations: you've joined the city's only grassroots planning committee, a dirt line worn by a thousand other optimists.\n\nHumidity is 11%, your nose is bleeding, and your reward is a ministry building identical to the last six.\n\nJust take the Uber, tourist. Even the drivers stop at the crosswalk for you out of pity."}, "b": {"item_id": "84e52e157b23", "text": "Look at the guy walking from the Asa Sul to the Rodoviária at noon, two liters of water in his backpack, wearing the sneakers he bought for a 5K he never ran. He isn't walking to get anywhere. He's walking so he can post a photo of the Cathedral with a caption about \"mindfulness.\" Every superquadra here was designed without corners, so he's been lost for forty minutes and has decided that's a spiritual experience. He crosses the Eixão through the underpass like a man who's read a book about Lúcio Costa. The bus he could have taken comes every twenty-five minutes, but he'd rather arrive drenched in sweat and certain of his own virtue than dry and on time."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0254", "lang": "en", "target_id": "las-vegas", "target_name": "Las Vegas", "facts": ["Nevada legalized casino gambling in 1931, the year construction of the Hoover Dam began nearby.", "Most of the Strip lies not in the city of Las Vegas but in the unincorporated town of Paradise.", "The Sphere, opened in 2023 at a cost of about $2.3 billion, is the largest spherical building in the world.", "'What happens here, stays here' was launched by the local convention and visitors authority in 2003.", "Lake Mead, its water source, fell in July 2022 to about 1,040 feet, its lowest since the reservoir first filled in 1937, and dropped below that mark again in August 2026."], "a": {"item_id": "75124632afe4", "text": "Every guy who drives to Vegas to get rich has the same plan. He's going to win one hand of blackjack, put the money in his pocket, and walk out a new man. Two days later he's in the parking garage, sitting in his Camry, calling his mom to ask if she can wire him gas money. He's wearing the same shirt. He's got a little paper cup of quarters and the dead look of a man who just found out the house is the house. And then he goes back in. Why? Because somewhere in there a guy in a vest told him \"you're due.\" Nobody's due. The guy in the vest is due for a pension and a deep sea cruise. Nobody's getting rich in Vegas except the people who built the buffet you ate at after losing your rent."}, "b": {"item_id": "7dd28f44edd6", "text": "You're flying to Vegas to get rich. Think about that. You looked at your finances and said, \"The answer is a building with no clocks, built by mathematicians who have never once lost.\"\n\nBut you've got a system. Of course you do. You read a forum post by a guy called CrapsDaddy69, and now you're betting the don't-pass like you're defusing a bomb.\n\nYou'll sit down with $500 you're \"totally fine losing.\" By midnight you'll be at the ATM, paying a nine-dollar fee to access your own despair. And you'll be happy, because a woman in a sequined bodysuit brought you a free Miller Lite, and in your head that's a 4% return.\n\nYou'll be up $60 exactly once. That's the whole trip. You'll tell that story for eleven years.\n\nThen you'll fly home broke, sunburned, and wearing a lanyard from a convention you skipped, having made someone rich. Just not you."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0255", "lang": "en", "target_id": "beanie-babies", "target_name": "Beanie Babies", "facts": ["Launched by Ty Inc. in 1993 and sold only through small gift shops, with designs 'retired' to manufacture scarcity.", "At the height of the craze Beanie Babies accounted for about 10% of eBay's sales, and eBay's 1998 IPO filing said the site at times had over 30,000 Beanie Baby auctions running simultaneously in the quarter ending June 1998.", "In 1999 a divorcing couple divided their Beanie Baby collection one by one on a courtroom floor, in a photograph that ran worldwide.", "In 1999 Ty announced that Beanie Babies would be retired at the end of the year, then reversed itself after asking the public to vote and seeing an overwhelming vote to keep them.", "Most 'rare' Beanies that price guides valued in the thousands now sell for a few dollars."], "a": {"item_id": "876536d60647", "text": "Let's talk about the Beanie Baby investor. You know the type. He keeps the tush tag in a sealed plastic sleeve, because a tag with a crease is a lawsuit. A 1999 price guide sits on his nightstand like scripture. He sleeps beside a shelf of bears he hasn't touched since the Clinton administration and calls them \"the portfolio.\" He has never once hugged one, because hugging reduces value. His kids aren't allowed in the living room. He tells everyone it's his retirement plan, and somewhere in a storage unit in Ohio, a sixty-year-old man is whispering to a Princess bear. When he dies, he says, they'll be worth a fortune. That's the one thing he and those bears have in common: they're both waiting for someone to finally care."}, "b": {"item_id": "45f7594d8a38", "text": "Let's hear it for the Beanie Baby investors, the only people who bought a retirement plan at a Hallmark store. You put tag protectors on a heart-shaped piece of cardboard and called it diversification. You drove to four McDonald's for Teenie Beanies, threw away the Happy Meals, and told your kids it was for their college fund. Now that kid is thirty, still paying off loans, and his inheritance is a Rubbermaid tub of Princess the Bears. Every few months you see an eBay listing asking $500,000 for one with a tag typo and think, \"Vindicated.\" Babe, that's not a market. That's another guy in another garage, also lying to his wife. Ty Warner told you which ones were \"retired,\" and you believed him. Ty Warner later pleaded guilty to hiding money offshore. He hoarded too. He just hoarded the right thing."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0256", "lang": "en", "target_id": "pmma", "target_name": "PMMA (e-beam resist)", "facts": ["The same polymer as Plexiglas and acrylic glass, dissolved in anisole or chlorobenzene and spun into a thin film.", "The oldest electron-beam resist still in everyday use; IBM researchers reported it as one in the late 1960s.", "Positive tone: the beam breaks polymer chains and the exposed region dissolves in a MIBK:IPA developer.", "Resolves below 10 nm but has poor sensitivity, so doses of hundreds to over a thousand µC/cm² (depending on voltage) make writing slow.", "Poor dry-etch resistance, so it is used mostly for lift-off rather than as an etch mask."], "a": {"item_id": "c3b97d78415e", "text": "Still using PMMA as your e-beam resist? Bless your heart. You're the guy who still owns a flip phone and says \"the cloud\" like it's a place you visited once. Every grad student in your lab has moved on to HSQ and ZEP, and you're over there spinning PMMA at 4000 rpm, baking it on a hotplate, and wondering why your features look like they were drawn by a sleepy toddler with a cheap pen. You want sub-20-nanometer lines, but you're getting proximity effect so bad your dense arrays blur together like a bad Instagram filter. Congratulations, you've achieved the dream: a lithography process that's slow, low-contrast, and somehow still considered \"classic.\" Your advisor calls it a \"reliable baseline.\" We all know what that really means."}, "b": {"item_id": "a46a8573ce80", "text": "You people still spinning PMMA aren't doing lithography. You're doing historical reenactment. Your advisor's advisor scribbled the recipe on a napkin in 1975, and you treat it like scripture: 950K over 495K, MIBK to IPA one-to-three, develop sixty seconds while whispering to the beaker. You tell everyone it's about \"resolution.\" It's about nobody wanting to file the purchase order for ZEP. So you crank the dose until writing one chip takes longer than your stipend lasts, then discover your pattern won't survive ten seconds in the etcher. So you do liftoff, because of course you do. Your entire thesis is gold rectangles. You're not a nanofabrication researcher. You're the guy who knows which bottle in the cleanroom is \"the good IPA\" and guards it like a dragon."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0257", "lang": "en", "target_id": "nuclear-fusion", "target_name": "Nuclear fusion power", "facts": ["Has been 'about 30 years away' since the 1950s; at the 1955 Geneva atoms-for-peace conference, Homi Bhabha predicted fusion power within two decades.", "ITER, agreed in 2006 for first plasma in 2016, re-baselined in 2024 to start research operations in 2034 and deuterium-tritium burning in 2039, at a cost estimated above €20 billion.", "NIF's December 2022 'ignition' shot released 3.15 MJ from 2.05 MJ of laser energy; the lasers drew about 300 MJ from the grid to fire.", "Private fusion companies had raised more than $7 billion by 2024."], "a": {"item_id": "c5c23a966bf8", "text": "Let's hear it for the fusion people, who have been ten years away since your grandfather was ten years away. These are the only professionals who can miss a deadline for seventy straight years and get a bigger budget every time. Where else does that work? \"Sorry, honey, I'll be home for dinner in ten years.\" \"Great, here's twenty billion dollars.\"\n\nThey put the sun in a magnetic donut and act shocked when it won't sit still. ITER's been under construction so long the original welders have grandkids on the crew. And when they finally hit net gain, they celebrated like Moses with the tablets, then mumbled that it doesn't count the electricity for the lasers. That's not net gain. That's me saying I'm up at the casino if you don't count the buy-in.\n\nTen years, guys. Same as last time. Same tie, too."}, "b": {"item_id": "1f957f86774f", "text": "Fusion scientists have the best job security in human history. You promise limitless energy in ten years. Ten years later, you promise it in ten years. It's the only career where missing the deadline *is* the business model. A pharmacist does that, she's arrested. A fusion CEO does that, he gets a Series C and a TED Talk.\n\nAnd ten is the perfect number. Far enough away that you'll be retired, close enough that the grant committee gets a little tingle. Nobody ever says \"twelve,\" because twelve doesn't fit on the slide.\n\nThey announced \"net energy gain\" by not counting the electricity that powered the lasers. That's like saying you won at the casino if you ignore the chips you bought.\n\nThey're building a star on Earth, and they can't even make it on time. The real one is free and shows up every morning."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0258", "lang": "en", "target_id": "microsoft-word", "target_name": "Microsoft Word", "facts": ["Launched in 1983 as 'Multi-Tool Word' for Xenix and MS-DOS.", "Clippy, the animated paperclip assistant, arrived in Office 97 and was removed in Office 2007; Microsoft itself ran ads mocking it in 2001.", "Its .docx format, Office Open XML, was fast-tracked to ISO standard 29500 in 2008 as a roughly 6,000-page specification, amid accusations that national committees had been packed.", "Older spell checkers, Word's included, offered 'Cupertino' as a correction for a mistyped 'cooperation', and the resulting 'Cupertino effect' made it into published NATO, EU and UN-agency documents."], "a": {"item_id": "8af5ead45179", "text": "You chose Word for your thesis because you \"didn't have time to learn LaTeX.\" Beautiful. Instead you learned section breaks, by force, at 3 a.m., trying to make page numbers switch from Roman to Arabic without page one of Chapter 2 becoming page xvii. You wanted to spend your time on research. Instead you spend it dragging a figure half an inch and watching four chapters collapse like a Jenga tower built by your enemies. Your committee will open the PDF and see \"Error! Reference source not found.\" in the middle of your key finding, which, honestly, is a fair summary of your methodology. Your folder contains thesis_FINAL_v9_REALfinal_USE_THIS.docx, because you are a scientist who trusts nothing except filenames. Five years of work, and your proudest skill is knowing which heading style secretly isn't Heading 2."}, "b": {"item_id": "600c3a0ffd98", "text": "They're the ones who swear they'll \"just fix the margins real quick\" at 2 a.m., three days before the deadline. They have a file named FINAL_v7_ACTUAL, a Table of Contents that refuses to update unless you look at it funny, and one section break they're sure is secretly turning page 14 into roman numeral vii. They chose Word because their advisor said so, and now they're spending their last good hours in the Styles pane, clicking \"Update Field\" like it's a slot machine. They want numbered headings that stay numbered, a list of figures that doesn't lie, and a title page that doesn't look like a ransom note. What they get is a bibliography that formats itself only in their dreams, and a PDF their committee opens in Preview and immediately ruins."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0259", "lang": "en", "target_id": "juicero", "target_name": "Juicero", "facts": ["A Wi-Fi-connected cold-press juicer launched in 2016 at $699, later cut to $399, that squeezed proprietary bags of chopped produce sold by subscription.", "Raised about $120 million from investors including Google Ventures and Kleiner Perkins.", "In April 2017, Bloomberg reporters squeezed the packs by hand and got 7.5 ounces of juice in 90 seconds, faster than the machine's 8 ounces in about two minutes.", "Each pack carried a QR code that the press scanned and checked online before squeezing; it refused expired packs and would not run at all unless connected to Wi-Fi.", "Shut down in September 2017 and offered refunds on the machine."], "a": {"item_id": "4defd00efed2", "text": "Four hundred dollars. You paid four hundred dollars so a machine could check with your Wi-Fi before letting you have breakfast. You're the guy who says \"disrupt\" at Thanksgiving. You didn't want juice; you wanted to be the kind of person a juice startup would pitch to. And it worked! Investors handed that company a hundred and twenty million dollars, and you volunteered to be the exit strategy. Then Bloomberg showed the world you could squeeze the packets with your bare hands, faster, for free. So you paid four hundred dollars to get outperformed by your own grip. Your grandmother squeezed oranges every morning for sixty years. You subscribed to a bag. Somewhere tonight, a router outage stands between a venture capitalist and his kale, and honestly? The router's the smartest thing in that kitchen."}, "b": {"item_id": "a22f3d4810e1", "text": "You're the guy who read \"Bloomberg shows you can squeeze the pouch with your bare hands\" and thought, \"Sure, but can my hands connect to WiFi?\" You paid $400 to outsource the one step of juicemaking a toddler can do. You didn't want juice. You wanted to feel like a Series B. You wanted a dinner guest to ask, \"Is that the Juicero?\" so you could say, \"It's backed by Google Ventures,\" like that's a nutrient.\n\nAnd you paid seven dollars a pouch for pre-chopped kale with an expiration date, because you can't be trusted to smell things.\n\nWhen the company folded, you didn't lose a juicer. You lost your seat at the table where adults say \"disrupting breakfast\" with a straight face.\n\nNow you squeeze the pouch over a glass like a Capri Sun, doing the work your $400 was supposed to prevent. Cheers. Full circle."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0260", "lang": "en", "target_id": "windows-vista", "target_name": "Windows Vista", "facts": ["Released to consumers in January 2007, after the 'Longhorn' project was reset in 2004 and headline features such as the WinFS file system were dropped.", "User Account Control prompted so often that Apple's 'Get a Mac' campaign ran an ad about 'Cancel or Allow'.", "'Windows Vista Capable' stickers went on PCs that could only run Home Basic, without the Aero interface, and led to a class-action lawsuit.", "Windows 7 (2009), widely praised, was largely a tuned-up Vista on the same kernel line; Vista support ended in April 2017."], "a": {"item_id": "ff3ee1cef20f", "text": "You installed Vista on launch day. Nobody needed you to. You just wanted to be the guy at the office who says \"oh, you're still on XP?\" in the voice of someone judging a flip phone.\n\nYou were after transparent window borders. You spent three hundred dollars on the upgrade, then another eighty on RAM, so your title bars could be see-through. Your computer now has the glass look of a luxury hotel and the speed of its elevator.\n\nDay one got you a printer with a driver that was \"coming soon,\" and a sticker on your laptop that said \"Vista Capable,\" which turned out to be a legal term. You clicked Allow so many times your finger filed for workers' comp. You plugged in a flash drive to make it faster, and called it ReadyBoost like that's a normal sentence.\n\nBy March you were hunting for your old XP disc, and it was waiting for you, no hard feelings."}, "b": {"item_id": "2ca90ba91029", "text": "Day-one Vista people. You paid $399 for Ultimate Edition because you wanted to be the kind of man whose desktop wallpaper moves. DreamScene. You paid extra for animated wallpaper. Microsoft said \"The Wow starts now,\" and you believed them, lined up at CompUSA at midnight like it was a Harry Potter release.\n\nAnd what did you get? A computer that asks permission. You'd open a folder and it would dim the whole screen like a disappointed dad: \"Cancel or Allow?\" You clicked Allow four hundred times a day. Your printer stopped working. Your sound card stopped working. Your Windows Experience Index came back 2.1, and you took that personally.\n\nBut you had glass window borders. You had a clock gadget. You had a sticker that said \"Vista Capable,\" which, like you, was technically true and fooled absolutely no one."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0261", "lang": "en", "target_id": "ugg", "target_name": "UGG boots", "facts": ["Sheepskin boots Australian surfers wore to warm up after sessions; Brian Smith started selling them in California in 1978.", "Deckers Outdoor bought the brand in 1995 and holds 'UGG' as a trademark in the US and elsewhere; in Australia, 'ugg' is a generic word any bootmaker may use.", "In 2019, a US jury found an Australian maker liable for selling boots labelled 'ugg' into the United States.", "Oprah Winfrey featured them in her 'Favorite Things' lists repeatedly in the 2000s, which drove the first boom.", "UGG is Deckers' largest brand, with over $2 billion in annual sales."], "a": {"item_id": "3e67204b78e6", "text": "If you wear UGGs in July, I admire the commitment. You've decided being cozy is a personality and weather is just a rumor. You'll tell me they \"regulate temperature,\" a line you memorized like a hostage reading a statement. It's 94 degrees, you're in running shorts, and your feet are poaching in their own broth.\n\nWhat are you after? You want to look like you just rolled out of bed, for $180. Effortless, with a receipt. You want it to be fall at all times, even in Phoenix, even at a pool party.\n\nAnd what does it get you? A smell that enters the room two seconds before you do. Socks you can't wear because \"the inside is the whole point.\" And the loyalty of a 2007 version of you who never left the Juicy tracksuit.\n\nCongratulations. You're the only person standing on a sidewalk you could fry an egg on, saying, \"My feet get cold.\""}, "b": {"item_id": "f64065ff7ffc", "text": "Y'all know the year-round UGG people. It's ninety-four degrees in Phoenix, and there's a woman in the Target parking lot in full sheepskin, sweating like a rotisserie chicken and saying, \"They're just so comfortable.\" Comfortable? Your feet are wrapped in a sheep's coat in July. You're not dressing for comfort. You're dressing for a personality. You want people to think you have a cozy life, a Pinterest board, and a husband named Brett who keeps a sourdough starter. And what does it get you? Feet that smell like a wet Labrador and a standing order at the drive-thru, where the barista already knows your name and is quietly worried about you. You didn't choose footwear. You chose to hibernate from the neck down."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0262", "lang": "en", "target_id": "slack", "target_name": "Slack", "facts": ["Spun out in 2013 from the internal chat tool of Tiny Speck's failed game Glitch; founder Stewart Butterfield had earlier spun Flickr out of another failed game.", "The name is a backronym: 'Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge'.", "Salesforce bought it for $27.7 billion in 2021.", "Filed an EU antitrust complaint against Microsoft in 2020 over the bundling of Teams with Office."], "a": {"item_id": "a071abe43a17", "text": "Let's hear it for the founders running a company on Slack. You wanted a culture; you got 400 channels and a guy who reacts to layoffs with the 🙏 emoji. You're not a CEO. You're a moderator with equity.\n\nYour whole leadership style is staying green. You think that little dot means you're present. Your kids see the same dot.\n\nYou don't make decisions; you start threads. Then you @channel at 11 p.m. with \"quick q, no rush\" and watch 200 people learn how fast a heart can beat.\n\nStrategy lives in #general, accountability dies in a DM, and the truth lives in a private channel called #venting-2 that you'll never be invited to.\n\nThe dream was async freedom. The reality is eight hours a day watching \"Kevin is typing…\" like it's a heart monitor."}, "b": {"item_id": "c5f48d20bb73", "text": "You didn't build a company, you built a chat room with payroll. Your \"culture\" is a custom emoji of your CTO's face, and that's the only thing anyone has shipped this quarter.\n\nYou said Slack would kill meetings. Now every \"quick question\" is a huddle that starts with \"can you hear me?\" and ends with \"let's move this to a thread.\"\n\nYou @channel the whole company to announce a bagel delivery, then act shocked that nobody reads your @channels. You've got 112 channels for 14 employees. Three of them are for work. One is #wins, and it's just you, reacting to your own post with a rocket.\n\nYou aren't managing anybody. You're watching a green dot and calling it trust. Someone's dot went gray at 5:01 and you took it personally.\n\nAnd the best part? \"Sorry, just seeing this!\" is now your company's mission statement."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0263", "lang": "en", "target_id": "nuclear-fusion", "target_name": "Nuclear fusion power", "facts": ["Has been 'about 30 years away' since the 1950s; at the 1955 Geneva atoms-for-peace conference, Homi Bhabha predicted fusion power within two decades.", "ITER, agreed in 2006 for first plasma in 2016, re-baselined in 2024 to start research operations in 2034 and deuterium-tritium burning in 2039, at a cost estimated above €20 billion.", "NIF's December 2022 'ignition' shot released 3.15 MJ from 2.05 MJ of laser energy; the lasers drew about 300 MJ from the grid to fire.", "Private fusion companies had raised more than $7 billion by 2024."], "a": {"item_id": "3775d67dffeb", "text": "Fusion people have mastered the ten-year timeline. They've been ten years away since before most of them had kids, and now those kids are writing the grant applications. They chase a star in a bottle, a plasma that holds for a few glorious seconds before the magnets sulk and everyone reconvenes. Their press releases celebrate \"scientific breakthrough\" in the same breath as \"we spent slightly more power than we got back,\" which they call Q-positive if you squint. Their investors wear fleeces and love the words \"carbon-free\" and \"unlimited,\" and they'll happily wait another decade. The reactor is always ten years out, and the paychecks are already here."}, "b": {"item_id": "c5c23a966bf8", "text": "Let's hear it for the fusion people, who have been ten years away since your grandfather was ten years away. These are the only professionals who can miss a deadline for seventy straight years and get a bigger budget every time. Where else does that work? \"Sorry, honey, I'll be home for dinner in ten years.\" \"Great, here's twenty billion dollars.\"\n\nThey put the sun in a magnetic donut and act shocked when it won't sit still. ITER's been under construction so long the original welders have grandkids on the crew. And when they finally hit net gain, they celebrated like Moses with the tablets, then mumbled that it doesn't count the electricity for the lasers. That's not net gain. That's me saying I'm up at the casino if you don't count the buy-in.\n\nTen years, guys. Same as last time. Same tie, too."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0264", "lang": "en", "target_id": "microsoft-word", "target_name": "Microsoft Word", "facts": ["Launched in 1983 as 'Multi-Tool Word' for Xenix and MS-DOS.", "Clippy, the animated paperclip assistant, arrived in Office 97 and was removed in Office 2007; Microsoft itself ran ads mocking it in 2001.", "Its .docx format, Office Open XML, was fast-tracked to ISO standard 29500 in 2008 as a roughly 6,000-page specification, amid accusations that national committees had been packed.", "Older spell checkers, Word's included, offered 'Cupertino' as a correction for a mistyped 'cooperation', and the resulting 'Cupertino effect' made it into published NATO, EU and UN-agency documents."], "a": {"item_id": "1bc74d6b8981", "text": "Ah, the thesis typesetters. Four years of research, and the final week is spent discovering that Heading 2 and Heading 3 are secretly different shades of Times New Roman. You aren't writing anymore. You're toggling paragraph marks at page 214, hunting a rogue section break like a bomb tech who forgot which wire. You've renumbered Chapter 3 six times because the cross-references keep saying \"Error! Reference source not found.\" You want a table of contents that updates itself, and you're clicking Update Field on the wrong field. Your reward is a 300-page PDF whose List of Figures reads \"No table of figures entries found,\" and you tell your advisor it's a formatting quirk. Your advisor has also used Word. Your advisor is scrolling to the margins right now."}, "b": {"item_id": "c46efa5460bb", "text": "You spent six years mastering quantum field theory, yet you've centered every chapter title by hitting the spacebar until it looked right. You know what Heading 1 is, the same way you know what a dentist is: you've heard good things and you're avoiding it.\n\nYou wanted a thesis that looks like scholarship. You got a 214-page file called \"thesis_FINAL_v9_actualfinal_JUNE,\" where adding one comma moves Figure 4.2 to the shadow realm. Your table of contents is typed by hand, and you'll find out at the print shop.\n\nYou've also learned what a section break is, at 2 a.m., while trying to make page numbers go roman and then Arabic. You cried. A committee member will now spend four seconds on your formatting and ask you to change the margins.\n\nYou chose Word because everyone does. LaTeX people have already told you. Twice. At parties."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0265", "lang": "en", "target_id": "latex", "target_name": "LaTeX", "facts": ["Leslie Lamport wrote LaTeX in the early 1980s and released it in 1984 as a macro layer on Donald Knuth's TeX (1978), which Knuth built because he disliked how the galley proofs of the new edition of his own book looked.", "TeX's version number converges on π (3.141592653 since 2021), and Knuth's TeX bug bounty started at one hexadecimal dollar, $2.56, doubling yearly until frozen at $327.68; he stopped mailing real cheques in 2008 after fraud and now credits accounts at his fictional Bank of San Serriffe, and only 9 of the 275 cheques he sent from 2006 on were ever cashed.", "Its best-known warning is 'Underfull \\hbox (badness 10000)'; overfull lines are reported as 'Overfull \\hbox (Npt too wide)', and figures float to wherever the algorithm decides, not where the author put them.", "A single missing brace can produce an error reported pages away from the mistake, and a document can need three compiler passes to get its references right."], "a": {"item_id": "f1bceb623799", "text": "You spent four hours on a two-page memo your manager will read as an Outlook preview on his phone, where it renders as a gray rectangle. But the margins were perfect.\n\nYou're the person who says \"I just find it cleaner\" about a document with three bullet points. You grabbed a template from a 2011 math department website, and now your logistics update looks like a proof that the Q3 budget is a compact set.\n\nYou chased an overfull hbox for forty minutes, a flaw no one on Earth could see, and fixed it by changing \"Thursday\" to \"this week.\" That's not writing. That's a hostage negotiation with a comma.\n\nYou wanted \"wow, rigorous.\" They got Computer Modern and a ransom note from a topologist. Your reward: \"Can you resend as a Word doc?\""}, "b": {"item_id": "1a0ff1693a16", "text": "You wrote a memo about the break room fridge in LaTeX. Two pages. Thirty-eight \\usepackage lines. Your preamble is longer than your argument. You lost Tuesday to an overfull hbox in a sentence that says \"please label your yogurt.\"\n\nWho is this for? Linda from accounting? She opened the PDF, saw Computer Modern, and assumed you were applying for tenure. Nobody needed a BibTeX citation for \"see previous email.\"\n\nYou compiled it eleven times. Three were just to make the \"??\" go away. You drew a TikZ flowchart with one box. One. It points at itself.\n\nAnd what did it get you? Bob replied, \"Can you send this as a Word doc so I can track changes?\"\n\nYour dissertation defense was in 2014. The committee left. Let it go."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0266", "lang": "en", "target_id": "javascript", "target_name": "JavaScript", "facts": ["Written by Brendan Eich at Netscape in ten days in May 1995; named Mocha, then LiveScript, then JavaScript to ride Java's marketing, although the two are unrelated.", "typeof null returns 'object', a bug from the first version that has been kept for compatibility ever since.", "In March 2016 the author of left-pad, an 11-line npm package, unpublished it and broke builds across the industry, including Babel and React.", "[] + {} evaluates to '[object Object]'; {} + [] typed at a console evaluates to 0.", "Standardized by Ecma as 'ECMAScript' in 1997 because the name JavaScript is a trademark, first Sun's and now Oracle's."], "a": {"item_id": "76ccf08d2bf4", "text": "You heard \"full-stack\" and decided it meant one hammer and a stack of whatever you're hitting. You wrote the backend in JavaScript to avoid context-switching, which is adorable, because you've switched frameworks four times since March. Your Electron app uses 900 megabytes of RAM to show a to-do list, and you call that \"cross-platform\" the way a man towing a boat calls it \"a sports package.\"\n\nWhat are you after? Hireability, and the warm feeling that every job posting has your name on it. What do you get? A node_modules folder heavier than your personality, a loyalty to a package called is-odd that you'd defend in court, and a weekend \"migration to TypeScript\" that you'll describe as \"mostly done\" until you retire.\n\nYou didn't pick one language to rule them all. You just refuse to learn a second one and gave it a LinkedIn headline."}, "b": {"item_id": "6fc599fe456a", "text": "You write everything in JavaScript? Beautiful. You're the guy who wanted one language so badly you married it. Backend, build tools, phone app, the script that renames your screenshots: all JavaScript, all held together by a node_modules folder heavier than your laptop's actual contents. You didn't choose simplicity. You chose a single abusive relationship and call it commitment. When people ask why, you say \"full-stack,\" which is what you call a stack you can't see the bottom of. Your server went down last week because of a package named is-odd, installed at 2 a.m. by you, who never once checked what it does. You own three frameworks, one half-finished side project, and a deep personal belief that typeof null is a feature you've made peace with."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0267", "lang": "en", "target_id": "vision-pro", "target_name": "Apple Vision Pro", "facts": ["Released in February 2024 at $3,499, with an external battery pack on a cable rated for about two hours.", "'EyeSight' shows a rendering of the wearer's eyes on an outward-facing screen.", "Apple calls it 'spatial computing' and its App Store rules tell developers not to describe apps as AR, VR, XR, or MR.", "Reports during 2024 said Apple had cut production; analysts' first-year sales estimates were well under a million units.", "Weighs 600-650 g, before the battery."], "a": {"item_id": "bb952c93608d", "text": "Nobody wears a Vision Pro on the subway to watch a movie. They're wearing it so forty strangers know they paid three thousand five hundred dollars to be the least present person in Brooklyn. They say \"spatial computing\" like it's a personality. They pinch the air to open windows that exist only for them, and their eyes glow at us through the front like a cult member's. That's the most honest thing about them: they're staring at something that isn't here. Meanwhile the rest of us watch a man at the 4 train's Atlantic Avenue stop move a floating spreadsheet with his fingers while his actual laptop sits in his bag, unused, feeling betrayed."}, "b": {"item_id": "0dbe035a7cc0", "text": "Look at this guy on the F train, pinching at nothing like he's seasoning a ghost. He spent thirty-five hundred dollars to ride the subway on the surface of the moon, and the moon still smells like pee. He cranked the Digital Crown all the way to Joshua Tree, and the mariachi band found him anyway. That's the dream: rich enough to be somewhere else, broke enough to still be on the F. And he *wants* you to notice. That's why his fake cartoon eyes glow on the outside, so strangers know he's \"present.\" Buddy, nobody's checking. You're on Mount Hood. He gets two hours of battery and three stops of dignity, with a cable snaking into his pocket like a subway IV drip. Meanwhile, the guy next to him reading an actual book? That guy's already somewhere else, for free."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0268", "lang": "en", "target_id": "emacs", "target_name": "Emacs", "facts": ["Began in 1976 as a set of macros for the TECO editor at MIT's AI Lab, written by Richard Stallman and others; GNU Emacs followed in 1985.", "Extensible in its own Lisp dialect, Emacs Lisp; the standing joke is that it is a fine operating system lacking only a good editor.", "Ships with a psychotherapist (M-x doctor), Tetris, a mail client, a calendar, and a web browser (eww).", "An old backronym is 'Eight Megabytes And Constantly Swapping', from when 8 MB was a lot of memory.", "'Emacs pinky' is the name for the hand strain blamed on its Control-key chords."], "a": {"item_id": "18478adad1eb", "text": "You know the type. Ask where he works and he says, \"In Emacs.\" He reads his email in there, chats in there, runs his shell in there. He's left only for dentist appointments, and he checked beforehand whether the dentist had a keybinding for it. He's tried to quit exactly once. He typed C-x C-c, stared at the prompt for a while, then spent three weekends rewriting his init file so he'd never have to try again. He doesn't need a text editor. He needs a hobby that sounds like a personality, and he's got forty-one packages doing the job. Ask him what he's working on. He'll say, \"My config.\""}, "b": {"item_id": "78fe88a6a79d", "text": "You people don't use a text editor. You adopted a pet that you have to feed Lisp.\n\nYou open Emacs to write one email, and three weeks later you've written a mail client, but you still haven't sent the email. Your init file is 4,000 lines long, and you understand maybe nine of them. The rest is a Stack Overflow answer from 2011 that you're afraid to remove.\n\nYou've got an Org-mode agenda so elaborate it has its own org chart. It tracks every task you'll never do, in beautiful nested bullets. You don't have a to-do list. You have a to-do *liturgy*.\n\nAnd the pinky! Thirty years of Control and your left hand looks like a lobster claw. You call it \"ergonomic.\" Your chiropractor calls it \"a recurring client.\"\n\nYou say it's an operating system. Sir, it's a hobby with a splash screen."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0269", "lang": "en", "target_id": "risk", "target_name": "Risk", "facts": ["Invented in 1957 by Albert Lamorisse, the French director of The Red Balloon, as La Conquête du Monde; Parker Brothers published it in the United States in 1959.", "The map has 42 territories, among them Kamchatka, Irkutsk, and Yakutsk, while the entire Middle East is one territory.", "Holding Australia, which has a single entry point, is the standard opening strategy.", "A full game can run for hours, and players eliminated early have nothing to do but watch."], "a": {"item_id": "2847036f4a61", "text": "You people who play Risk to the end are a special breed. Six hours in, Dad's still holed up in Australia like it's a tax shelter, your cousin is defending Kamchatka like he was born there, and nobody remembers why we hate Steve, only that we do. You're not after world domination. You're after the exact moment your sister breaks the nonaggression pact in Ukraine, so you can bring it up at Thanksgiving for eleven years. And what does winning get you? A board covered entirely in your color, a table of people who no longer speak to you, and the hollow joy of conquering Earth with the luck of a man who only rolls double sixes when defending Irkutsk. Congratulations, Napoleon. It's 2 a.m. The pizza's cold. Your empire goes back in the box."}, "b": {"item_id": "b165154a062e", "text": "Let's talk about the people who play Risk to the end. You know the guy. He says \"one more turn\" at 11 p.m. with a shift at seven tomorrow. He isn't in it for the win. He wants to be the last man standing over a continent he's never visited. Every game he claims he's \"not really into it,\" then spends four hours quietly stacking armies in Kamchatka. His girlfriend left during the Siberia stretch. His brother-in-law has been eliminated for three hours and still sits there, out of spite, hoping someone rolls snake eyes. These are people who have never conquered anything real, yet they guard Australia like it owes them rent. When he finally wins, nobody claps. Everyone just has to drive home and pretend they liked him."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0270", "lang": "en", "target_id": "durian", "target_name": "Durian", "facts": ["Banned on Singapore's MRT trains and in many Southeast Asian hotels because of the smell.", "A 2017 genome study in Nature Genetics traced the odour to a duplicated family of MGL genes that pump out volatile sulfur compounds.", "China buys most of the world's durian exports, chiefly from Thailand, in a trade worth billions of dollars a year.", "Alfred Russel Wallace wrote in 1856 that eating it was 'a new sensation worth a voyage to the East to experience'."], "a": {"item_id": "94cc53dad1db", "text": "Look at him on the MRT, scooping durian with a plastic spoon like he's the only man in Singapore with a palate. Nobody asked for this. The carriage empties in a ring around him, and he takes it as respect. That's the whole transaction: the cleared seat, the sideways looks, proof he's braver than the rest of us. \"It's like custard,\" he says, eyes watering, because that's what happens when you're trying this hard. Then he posts the video captioned \"not for everyone.\" He's right. It's not for everyone. It's for him, and everyone within fifty metres just got a free sample of his ego."}, "b": {"item_id": "e97b85a188ed", "text": "Let's talk about the guy who eats durian on the train. Sir, Singapore, a country that will cane you for chewing gum, saw you coming and put your snack on a sign right next to \"no flammables.\" You're not hungry. You're running a loyalty test. Every face that twists is another person who \"just isn't adventurous.\" You paid sixty dollars for Musang King so you could explain Musang King to a stranger who's holding her breath. \"It's like custard, it's heaven, you just have to get past the-\" No. We don't. You brought a hotel-banned substance to a commute and called it a personality. And the best part? You'll be the only one who forgets you ate it. Your coworkers won't. Your hotel won't. Your Uber driver has already filed a one-star review with God."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0271", "lang": "en", "target_id": "excel", "target_name": "Microsoft Excel", "facts": ["First shipped for the Macintosh in 1985; the Windows version followed in 1987.", "Silently converts gene names such as SEPT2 and MARCH1 into dates; by August 2020 the HUGO Gene Nomenclature Committee had renamed 27 human genes (MARCH1 became MARCHF1, SEPT2 became SEPTIN2) and published guidelines in Nature Genetics to stop it.", "In October 2020, Public Health England lost 15,841 COVID-19 cases when a legacy .xls file hit its 65,536-row limit.", "Reinhart and Rogoff's 2010 paper 'Growth in a Time of Debt', cited for austerity policies, omitted five countries through a spreadsheet range error found by a graduate student in 2013.", "Sheets have been capped at 1,048,576 rows by 16,384 columns since Excel 2007."], "a": {"item_id": "0b80e15aa132", "text": "You people run a forty-million-dollar company out of a file called Budget_FINAL_v9_USE_THIS_ONE.xlsx. You don't want software. You want power: specifically, being the only person alive who knows why cell AF212 is hardcoded to 7. Your entire supply chain hangs on a VLOOKUP Gary wrote in 2011, and Gary is dead. Well, he's in Boise. Same thing. You call it \"agile.\" Your auditors call it \"a crime scene with conditional formatting.\" Every quarter-end the file says \"locked for editing by Brenda,\" and the whole company just waits for Brenda. And when you finally hit row 1,048,576, did you buy a database? No. You started Sheet2. That's not a business. That's a hostage situation with freeze panes."}, "b": {"item_id": "d9b95a68589f", "text": "Let's talk about the founders who run the whole company out of one spreadsheet. Their payroll lives on a tab called \"Sheet47,\" and the cap table is a merged cell nobody dares unmerge. They think they're building a business, but really they're building a shrine to Ctrl+Z. They have forty-one copies of the budget, all named \"FINAL_v9_USE_THIS,\" and the only person who understood the VLOOKUP was the intern, who quit in March. They get giddy over pivot tables, call a #REF! error \"a learning moment,\" and sleep with one eye open in case someone overwrites row 14. When investors ask for a dashboard, they send a screenshot of a pie chart with the legend in Comic Sans."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0272", "lang": "en", "target_id": "pmma", "target_name": "PMMA (e-beam resist)", "facts": ["The same polymer as Plexiglas and acrylic glass, dissolved in anisole or chlorobenzene and spun into a thin film.", "The oldest electron-beam resist still in everyday use; IBM researchers reported it as one in the late 1960s.", "Positive tone: the beam breaks polymer chains and the exposed region dissolves in a MIBK:IPA developer.", "Resolves below 10 nm but has poor sensitivity, so doses of hundreds to over a thousand µC/cm² (depending on voltage) make writing slow.", "Poor dry-etch resistance, so it is used mostly for lift-off rather than as an etch mask."], "a": {"item_id": "6303a3fe60f6", "text": "You still spin PMMA. Of course you do. Your advisor's thesis used it, his advisor's thesis used it, and somewhere a 1970s IBM engineer remains the most cutting-edge person in your academic lineage. You call it \"high resolution\" the way your uncle calls his Camry \"reliable.\" You book the e-beam overnight because your dose is measured in geological time, then dunk your chip in MIBK:IPA with a stopwatch and a prayer, chilling the developer like it's a fine Riesling. When the plasma etch eats your mask before your substrate, you just whisper \"bilayer, 495K under 950K\" like it's a personality. You're not chasing nanostructures. You're chasing a recipe nobody can blame you for. And it works: the gold lifted off beautifully. Your career is still soaking in acetone, waiting for something to come loose."}, "b": {"item_id": "c3b97d78415e", "text": "Still using PMMA as your e-beam resist? Bless your heart. You're the guy who still owns a flip phone and says \"the cloud\" like it's a place you visited once. Every grad student in your lab has moved on to HSQ and ZEP, and you're over there spinning PMMA at 4000 rpm, baking it on a hotplate, and wondering why your features look like they were drawn by a sleepy toddler with a cheap pen. You want sub-20-nanometer lines, but you're getting proximity effect so bad your dense arrays blur together like a bad Instagram filter. Congratulations, you've achieved the dream: a lithography process that's slow, low-contrast, and somehow still considered \"classic.\" Your advisor calls it a \"reliable baseline.\" We all know what that really means."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0273", "lang": "en", "target_id": "carbon-nanotubes", "target_name": "Carbon nanotubes", "facts": ["Reported by Sumio Iijima in Nature in 1991.", "Promised as the cable for a space elevator; a 2006 analysis showed that ordinary defects cut the strength of a macroscopic cable far below the ideal value.", "Long, rigid multi-walled nanotubes behaved like asbestos fibres in a 2008 mouse study in Nature Nanotechnology.", "Sorting metallic from semiconducting tubes has blocked nanotube electronics for three decades; MIT's 16-bit RV16X-NANO processor (2019) is still a lab demonstration.", "Today they are sold mainly as a conductive additive in lithium-ion battery electrodes."], "a": {"item_id": "d717e15fa133", "text": "You've been \"two years from commercialization\" since the Clinton administration. Your founder has a PhD, a patent, and the confidence of a man who's never had to ship anything in a quantity larger than a vial.\n\nSlide three is the space elevator. Everyone knows it's a joke, including you, but you keep it in because it's the only slide where the investors stop checking their phones.\n\nYou say \"TAM\" and mean \"every industry that has ever existed.\" Batteries, concrete, tennis rackets, sunscreen. You've pitched so many markets you've never actually sold to one.\n\nWhat are you after? A seed round, a lab with your name on it, and one more year before anyone asks about price per kilogram.\n\nWhat do you get? A warm handshake, a \"fascinating science,\" and a follow-up email that says \"keep us posted.\""}, "b": {"item_id": "839efb519f46", "text": "Nanotube founders are the only people who've said \"within five years\" every year since 1991 and kept a straight face. They open with a slide of a space elevator, then ask for seed money to make a vial of black dust. Every deck has the same line, \"a hundred times stronger than steel,\" and every deck is quietly a plan to sell additive to tennis racket companies. These are PhDs who spent six years sorting chirality and came out wanting a Tesla. The pitch is always \"we've solved scale,\" and the demo is always one centimeter long. VCs keep a nanotube guy around like a lucky rabbit's foot: he never pays off, but he makes the fund sound visionary. And when someone asks about lung toxicity, he says \"totally different from asbestos\" with the confidence of a man who googled it in the elevator. The regular one."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0274", "lang": "en", "target_id": "javascript", "target_name": "JavaScript", "facts": ["Written by Brendan Eich at Netscape in ten days in May 1995; named Mocha, then LiveScript, then JavaScript to ride Java's marketing, although the two are unrelated.", "typeof null returns 'object', a bug from the first version that has been kept for compatibility ever since.", "In March 2016 the author of left-pad, an 11-line npm package, unpublished it and broke builds across the industry, including Babel and React.", "[] + {} evaluates to '[object Object]'; {} + [] typed at a console evaluates to 0.", "Standardized by Ecma as 'ECMAScript' in 1997 because the name JavaScript is a trademark, first Sun's and now Oracle's."], "a": {"item_id": "c69b78416cbb", "text": "Oh, you write everything in JavaScript? Beautiful. You wanted one language, so you picked the one where `typeof null` says \"object\" and nobody has apologized in thirty years. Your backend runs on Node so your frontend friends feel at home. Your little file-renaming script has four hundred megabytes in `node_modules` and a `package.json` longer than the novel you never finished. You're not programming. You're running `npm install` and praying it finishes before some eleven-line package vanishes from the registry. When people ask why, you say \"full stack,\" which is what you call it when your whole life rides on a dependency you've never read."}, "b": {"item_id": "76ccf08d2bf4", "text": "You heard \"full-stack\" and decided it meant one hammer and a stack of whatever you're hitting. You wrote the backend in JavaScript to avoid context-switching, which is adorable, because you've switched frameworks four times since March. Your Electron app uses 900 megabytes of RAM to show a to-do list, and you call that \"cross-platform\" the way a man towing a boat calls it \"a sports package.\"\n\nWhat are you after? Hireability, and the warm feeling that every job posting has your name on it. What do you get? A node_modules folder heavier than your personality, a loyalty to a package called is-odd that you'd defend in court, and a weekend \"migration to TypeScript\" that you'll describe as \"mostly done\" until you retire.\n\nYou didn't pick one language to rule them all. You just refuse to learn a second one and gave it a LinkedIn headline."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0275", "lang": "en", "target_id": "las-vegas", "target_name": "Las Vegas", "facts": ["Nevada legalized casino gambling in 1931, the year construction of the Hoover Dam began nearby.", "Most of the Strip lies not in the city of Las Vegas but in the unincorporated town of Paradise.", "The Sphere, opened in 2023 at a cost of about $2.3 billion, is the largest spherical building in the world.", "'What happens here, stays here' was launched by the local convention and visitors authority in 2003.", "Lake Mead, its water source, fell in July 2022 to about 1,040 feet, its lowest since the reservoir first filled in 1937, and dropped below that mark again in August 2026."], "a": {"item_id": "c0bc98e0b0fb", "text": "You flew to Vegas to get rich. Think about that. You looked at a city built on the mathematical certainty that you will lose and said, \"Yeah, but I've got a feeling.\"\n\nYou have a system, of course. The system is a YouTube video from 2011 and a lucky shirt. You're up forty dollars at blackjack and suddenly you're tipping the dealer like a man whose yacht is double-parked outside.\n\nBy 4 a.m. you're hunched at a Wheel of Fortune slot, pressing a button that does nothing, drinking a free vodka cranberry like it's a salary. You've taken out cash three times, and the ATM fee has now earned more than you have.\n\nYou'll fly home with a buffet hangover, a sunburn from a pool you were too hungover to enjoy, and a cash-out ticket for $3.47 you're too proud to redeem. And a story about how you were \"so close.\"\n\nYou came to beat the house. The house gave you a discount on the room because it knew you'd be back."}, "b": {"item_id": "75124632afe4", "text": "Every guy who drives to Vegas to get rich has the same plan. He's going to win one hand of blackjack, put the money in his pocket, and walk out a new man. Two days later he's in the parking garage, sitting in his Camry, calling his mom to ask if she can wire him gas money. He's wearing the same shirt. He's got a little paper cup of quarters and the dead look of a man who just found out the house is the house. And then he goes back in. Why? Because somewhere in there a guy in a vest told him \"you're due.\" Nobody's due. The guy in the vest is due for a pension and a deep sea cruise. Nobody's getting rich in Vegas except the people who built the buffet you ate at after losing your rent."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0276", "lang": "en", "target_id": "monopoly", "target_name": "Monopoly", "facts": ["Descends from Lizzie Magie's The Landlord's Game, patented in 1904 to teach the evils of land monopoly in the spirit of Henry George.", "Charles Darrow sold his version to Parker Brothers in 1935 and was credited as the inventor; Parker bought Magie's patent for $500.", "The official rules require any unbought property to be auctioned, and 'Free Parking' pays nothing; the house rules that skip the auction and pile cash on Free Parking are why games drag.", "During the Second World War, British intelligence had maps, compasses, and real currency hidden in sets sent to prisoner-of-war camps."], "a": {"item_id": "b78ec11705ad", "text": "Family Monopoly night: for people who looked at Thanksgiving and thought, \"This needs more landlords.\" Dad always grabs the banker job, the only time he's held money he didn't immediately lose. Your sister trades you Baltic Avenue for your Park Place \"as a favor,\" and you realize she's been running the same con since the womb. Mom invented a Free Parking jackpot rule in 1994 and defends it like scripture. And Grandpa just sits in jail, every game, refusing to pay the fifty, finally at peace.\n\nWhat are you all after? The thrill of owning property, a feeling none of you will experience off this cardboard square. Four hours later, someone flips the board, the thimble disappears under the couch forever, and the only thing anybody actually collected was a grudge worth way more than $200."}, "b": {"item_id": "10bac3efc0cc", "text": "You all gather around a table to prove one thing: that you'd evict your own mother for a purple property. Dad's the banker, which is why the bank has mysteriously never gone broke and he somehow always has a 500. Your sister trades like a hostage negotiator, offering you Baltic Avenue and emotional closure for Boardwalk. Uncle Rick insists Free Parking pays out the whole jackpot, a rule that exists nowhere except in his need to feel lucky once in his life. The twelve-year-old wants only the railroads, because he's the one person here with a clear understanding of passive income. Grandma says nothing for three hours, then owns everything. Six hours in, nobody's having fun, but nobody can quit, because quitting means admitting you're the one in jail. It ends the way it always does: someone flips the board, someone cries, and everyone's out of money, including the winner's relationships."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0277", "lang": "en", "target_id": "jackfruit", "target_name": "Jackfruit", "facts": ["The largest fruit that grows on a tree; single fruits can weigh over 40 kg.", "The national fruit of Bangladesh and the state fruit of Kerala and Tamil Nadu.", "Unripe flesh is sold as a vegan 'pulled pork' substitute, although it has only 1-2 g of protein per 100 g.", "Cutting one releases a sticky white latex, so cooks oil their knives and hands first.", "A member of the mulberry family (Moraceae), a relative of breadfruit and figs."], "a": {"item_id": "652321571654", "text": "You people found a fruit that has fed entire countries for centuries, moved it to Portland, and renamed it \"pulled pork\" so a guy named Dylan could feel something at lunch.\n\nLet's be honest about the business model. You didn't discover a meat alternative. You discovered that if you drown anything in enough barbecue sauce, a vegetarian will cry \"it's just like the real thing!\" about a food they haven't tasted in eleven years. You're not selling jackfruit. You're selling sauce and plausible deniability, for fourteen dollars.\n\nIn Kerala, this is a Tuesday. In your food truck, it's \"heritage, plant-forward, and ethically shredded.\"\n\nAnd what does it get you? One loyal customer, Brenda, who tells everyone it's \"honestly better than meat,\" and who stands in line behind the guy ordering brisket."}, "b": {"item_id": "4062eab29108", "text": "Every jackfruit seller has the same origin story. He opened a tin of young green jackfruit in brine, felt something stir, and decided the planet needed him at a farmers market saying, \"It's basically pulled pork.\" Now he has a logo, a folding table, and a beard that's doing a lot of work. He's selling a fruit that spent its whole life as a dessert ingredient, and he's rebranded it as the guy who gets to say \"trust me, it's meat\" to people holding a paper boat of barbecue sauce. What he really wants is Thanksgiving: his uncle takes one forkful, goes quiet, and asks what cut it is. What he gets is a forty-dollar tote bag and the specific pride of convincing a tree to be a pork shoulder."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0278", "lang": "en", "target_id": "brasilia", "target_name": "Brasília", "facts": ["Inaugurated on 21 April 1960 after about 41 months of construction, moving the capital inland from Rio de Janeiro.", "Lúcio Costa's Plano Piloto is laid out like an airplane (or a bird, or a cross), with Oscar Niemeyer's government buildings along the fuselage.", "Listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1987, less than 30 years after it was built.", "Planned for 500,000 residents; the Federal District now has about 3 million, most in satellite towns outside the plan.", "Built for the car: wide avenues, few pedestrian crossings, and distances that make walking impractical."], "a": {"item_id": "031bc5f2993c", "text": "Walking in Brasília is for three kinds of people: architecture students, tourists who trusted Google Maps, and the intern whose ministry is \"right next door\" on the Esplanada, which here means a forty-minute hike with no shade and a dry-season humidity of 11%. You're not pedestrians, you're pilgrims. You came for Niemeyer and left with heatstroke and a dirt trail you personally wore into the grass, because Lúcio Costa forgot you'd have legs. You cross the Eixão like it's a Frogger level with bureaucrats at 80 km/h, then descend into a tesourinha and come out three superquadras from where you started. Somebody gave you directions: \"SQS 308, Bloco C.\" Congratulations, you're in a spreadsheet. And on Sunday, when they close the Eixão so you can finally walk it, you'll realize there's still nowhere to go."}, "b": {"item_id": "5894439abe59", "text": "You're an architecture student, a tourist, or an intern, and all three of you looked at the Congress from the Rodoviária and said, \"Oh, that's right there.\" It is not right there. It's a mirage with a dome. Forty minutes later you're crossing a lawn at noon in 12 percent humidity, quoting Niemeyer to yourself like it's a hydration plan.\n\nAnd you're proud! You even cut a little dirt shortcut through the grass, the famous caminho do desejo, the only urban planning in Brasília done by someone who actually had to get somewhere. You were walking to a ministry for one signature, and the man who signs is out on Thursdays. It's Thursday.\n\nCongratulations, pilgrim. You've experienced modernism up close: a sunburn, a nosebleed, and a guy in a Corolla honking at you at the tesourinha like you're the one who's lost."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0279", "lang": "en", "target_id": "luosifen", "target_name": "Luosifen (螺蛳粉)", "facts": ["A rice-noodle dish from Liuzhou, Guangxi; the smell comes from pickled bamboo shoots (酸笋), not from the river snails, which only flavour the broth.", "The instant version boomed during the 2020 lockdowns; Liuzhou's government reported packaged luosifen sales of over 10 billion yuan that year.", "Added to China's national intangible cultural heritage list in 2021.", "Liuzhou opened a luosifen industry college in 2020 to train workers for the trade."], "a": {"item_id": "6782cb950544", "text": "You know who you are. You ordered twelve packs off Taobao, waited until every roommate was home, then tore open the sour bamboo shoot sachet like you were declassifying something. You're not hungry. You're making a point. Every slurp comes with the speech: \"Smells bad, tastes good.\" Buddy, we only get the first half. Nobody signed up for the tasting, just the hallway. And you keep saying \"it's authentic Liuzhou\" like geography is an apology. What does it get you? A spotless pot nobody will borrow, a group chat that goes silent the second you type \"anyone want some?\", and roommates who suddenly prefer dating people with their own apartments. You came for comfort food. You left with a reputation, a window permanently propped open, and a fridge shelf everyone treats like a crime scene."}, "b": {"item_id": "911bf6e3e8ce", "text": "You know the luosifen guy. He opens the bag at 11 p.m. and the whole floor knows before the water boils. He doesn't cook it, he deploys it. Sour bamboo, peanuts, and that sachet of chili oil he's been hoarding since his last trip to Guangxi, all laid out like a surgeon's tray. He wants you to ask what that smell is. That's the whole meal. Every time someone asks, he gets to say \"it's snail,\" with the calm of a man who has never once been left out of a group chat. Then he eats it standing by the window, eyes closed, like he's the only one who understands it. The window stays open all winter. Nobody's allowed to say it's bad. Nobody's allowed to say much of anything, because he's already on his second bowl, and his face says he's in Liuzhou and you're not."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0280", "lang": "en", "target_id": "almond-milk", "target_name": "Almond milk", "facts": ["California grows about 80% of the world's almonds.", "A single almond takes about 1.1 US gallons (roughly 4 litres) of water to grow, a figure Mother Jones published in 2014 that spread during California's drought.", "A 2015 US class action alleged Almond Breeze contained only about 2% almonds; it partly survived a motion to dismiss, was stayed in 2017 pending a separate Blue Diamond labelling settlement, and the plaintiffs then dropped it.", "Pollinating California's almond orchards each February requires trucking in most of the commercial honeybee colonies in the United States.", "Medieval European cooks used almond milk as a Lenten substitute for dairy, centuries before the current fashion."], "a": {"item_id": "2f3ff40c74e4", "text": "You switched to almond milk to save the planet, which is a bold move for someone who read one headline about cows. Congratulations: you found the crop that needs about a gallon of water per almond and grows almost entirely in a California drought. You didn't solve the problem, you relocated it to Fresno, where you can't see it.\n\nAnd the bees. Orchards truck in most of the country's honeybees every spring, like a tour that never ends. You didn't go vegan, you went on a bee-logistics subscription.\n\nBut let's be honest, it was never about the planet. It was about the moment the barista asks \"dairy?\" and you say \"almond, please\" like a governor granting a pardon. You paid sixty cents extra for a halo with a surcharge.\n\nMeanwhile, your oat-milk friend is quietly doing it better, and nobody's clapping for them. That's what really bothers you."}, "b": {"item_id": "1485255f2490", "text": "Let's hear it for the people who switched to almond milk to save the planet. You watched one documentary about cow farts and panicked straight into the arms of a nut that needs a gallon of water apiece. Congratulations: you fled methane and landed on drought. California is cracking like a crème brûlée, and you're out here draining a Central Valley aquifer because you can't make eye contact with a Holstein. And the bees! Billions of them trucked across the country on semis, dying by the hive, so you can tell a coworker you \"don't really do dairy.\" That's not environmentalism. That's relocating the crime scene. You didn't save the Earth. You just moved the guilt somewhere sunnier, slapped a leaf on it, and paid an eighty-cent upcharge for the privilege of feeling better about it."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0281", "lang": "en", "target_id": "excel", "target_name": "Microsoft Excel", "facts": ["First shipped for the Macintosh in 1985; the Windows version followed in 1987.", "Silently converts gene names such as SEPT2 and MARCH1 into dates; by August 2020 the HUGO Gene Nomenclature Committee had renamed 27 human genes (MARCH1 became MARCHF1, SEPT2 became SEPTIN2) and published guidelines in Nature Genetics to stop it.", "In October 2020, Public Health England lost 15,841 COVID-19 cases when a legacy .xls file hit its 65,536-row limit.", "Reinhart and Rogoff's 2010 paper 'Growth in a Time of Debt', cited for austerity policies, omitted five countries through a spreadsheet range error found by a graduate student in 2013.", "Sheets have been capped at 1,048,576 rows by 16,384 columns since Excel 2007."], "a": {"item_id": "106d81fa2fcf", "text": "You run a company on Excel. Let's be clear about who you are: a guy who thinks \"enterprise software\" is a scam and \"Q3_Forecast_FINAL_v9_USE_THIS_ONE.xlsx\" is a system. You wanted control. You got Dave. Dave wrote a macro in 2014, and now Dave can never leave, die, or take a vacation, because payroll is a VLOOKUP only he understands. Your entire supply chain is one #REF! away from bankruptcy. You can't onboard a customer because the file is \"locked for editing by Karen,\" and Karen is at lunch. You saved forty grand a year on a real database and lost it all the day someone sorted column B without column C. Your org chart is merged cells. Your audit trail is yellow and slightly different yellow. And your growth strategy has a hard ceiling: 1,048,576 rows."}, "b": {"item_id": "82eca86ba5a5", "text": "Nobody runs a company on Excel. Dave runs a company on Budget_FINAL_v7_USE_THIS_ONE.xlsx, which only Dave can open without the screen freezing for four minutes. Dave believes in control. Dave has forty tabs, and every one is named after a feeling: Forecast, Forecast2, Do Not Touch. He colors cells yellow for \"approved\" and orange for \"probably.\" His VLOOKUP holds payroll together like a prayer, and one #REF! away from a heart attack. He'll explain it to you at the Christmas party, sweating through a merged-cell cardigan. Dave doesn't want to be CEO. Dave wants you to need him."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0282", "lang": "en", "target_id": "oat-milk", "target_name": "Oat milk", "facts": ["Oatly started in Sweden in the 1990s, built on Lund University research by Rickard Öste on lactose intolerance.", "Oatly's slogan 'It's like milk but made for humans' was banned in Sweden in 2015 after the dairy lobby LRF Mjölk sued; Oatly then published the lawsuit as advertising.", "Oatly listed on Nasdaq in May 2021 at a valuation near $10 billion; the shares later lost more than 90% of their value.", "Commercial oat milk is made by breaking oat starch into maltose with amylase enzymes, and many brands, Oatly among them, add rapeseed oil; the result has more sugar and a higher glycaemic index than almond or soy milk."], "a": {"item_id": "6f1acdf77ea0", "text": "Oat milk people, let's be honest: you're not lactose intolerant. I've watched you eat a whole burrata at brunch. You switched because almond milk got canceled for drinking California dry, and you needed a new personality before anyone noticed you'd had the old one for six years.\n\nYou pay eighty cents extra to drink breakfast. Your grandma ate oats so she could afford milk. You're paying more for oats so you can say \"I just find it creamier.\"\n\nAnd you tell the barista \"barista edition, if you have it,\" like you're ordering a vintage. Same person who reads every label hunting for seed oils. Guess what's in your porridge latte, Kayleigh? Rapeseed oil.\n\nYou didn't save the planet. You flew to Lisbon twice this year. You just found a way to sigh at a cow."}, "b": {"item_id": "a579a10435cb", "text": "You know the person who orders oat milk? They're not vegan. They're not lactose intolerant. They own a tote bag that says \"Be Kind\" and a laptop they've never opened for work. \"Oat, please,\" they say, like they're ordering a sacrament. Almond was for people who've been to Joshua Tree, and soy was for their dad. Then they lean in to tell the barista the planet thanks them. Their whole personality is a seventy-cent upcharge they'll bring up at your birthday dinner. Ask why and you'll get six minutes on cows, then a pivot to their sourdough starter and whether you've tried the new Pilates place. They didn't change their milk. They changed their identity, and the milk was the cheapest part of the rebrand."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0283", "lang": "en", "target_id": "macau", "target_name": "Macau", "facts": ["A Portuguese settlement from the 1550s until the handover to China in December 1999.", "Casino revenue passed the Las Vegas Strip's in 2006, and at its 2013 peak, about $45 billion, was roughly seven times the Strip's.", "One company, STDM, held the casino monopoly for four decades until licences opened to competition in 2002.", "Among the most densely populated places on Earth, at roughly 20,000 people per square kilometre.", "The Cotai Strip stands on land reclaimed from the sea between the islands of Taipa and Coloane."], "a": {"item_id": "00bfc5b1665a", "text": "You lost it on Hong Kong stocks, so you took the TurboJET to win it back, because nothing says \"financial plan\" like a ferry with a cancellation policy. You're the guy at the Grand Lisboa squeezing a baccarat card like it owes you rent, blowing on it, peeling the corner so slowly the dealer ages. Red underwear, three days unwashed, \"for luck.\" Baccarat is a coin flip and you've built a religion around it. You told your wife it's a work trip, and technically your work now is the pawn shop next door, which buys Rolexes faster than you can say \"one more shoe.\" By hour nine you're eating an egg tart for dinner and calling it Portuguese culture. You came to win it all back. You leave by bus over the bridge with no luck, no Rolex, and a watch tan."}, "b": {"item_id": "6405f7045e3c", "text": "You lost it in Vegas, or Manila, or on a Tuesday online, and now you're on the ferry from Hong Kong with the confidence of a man who has finally \"figured out baccarat.\" You haven't. Baccarat has no figuring out. It's a coin flip in a tuxedo.\n\nYou're not here for money, you're here for a story where you come back a legend. Meanwhile, the guy next to you, who's been awake since Thursday, is bowing to a card like it's his ancestor, and he's winning, which is why you think it's contagious.\n\nYour plan: one hour, minimum table. By hour six, you're in a gondola at the Venetian, inside a mall, under a painted sky, asking a singing man from Guangdong if he takes WeChat.\n\nThe only thing you win back is a Portuguese egg tart, and even that's on the credit card."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0284", "lang": "en", "target_id": "microsoft-word", "target_name": "Microsoft Word", "facts": ["Launched in 1983 as 'Multi-Tool Word' for Xenix and MS-DOS.", "Clippy, the animated paperclip assistant, arrived in Office 97 and was removed in Office 2007; Microsoft itself ran ads mocking it in 2001.", "Its .docx format, Office Open XML, was fast-tracked to ISO standard 29500 in 2008 as a roughly 6,000-page specification, amid accusations that national committees had been packed.", "Older spell checkers, Word's included, offered 'Cupertino' as a correction for a mistyped 'cooperation', and the resulting 'Cupertino effect' made it into published NATO, EU and UN-agency documents."], "a": {"item_id": "600c3a0ffd98", "text": "They're the ones who swear they'll \"just fix the margins real quick\" at 2 a.m., three days before the deadline. They have a file named FINAL_v7_ACTUAL, a Table of Contents that refuses to update unless you look at it funny, and one section break they're sure is secretly turning page 14 into roman numeral vii. They chose Word because their advisor said so, and now they're spending their last good hours in the Styles pane, clicking \"Update Field\" like it's a slot machine. They want numbered headings that stay numbered, a list of figures that doesn't lie, and a title page that doesn't look like a ransom note. What they get is a bibliography that formats itself only in their dreams, and a PDF their committee opens in Preview and immediately ruins."}, "b": {"item_id": "c9f44b95f5be", "text": "You've got 214 pages in a single file and a belief that this time it will behave. You are a person who typed \"1.1\" by hand and then pressed the space bar forty times to line up the title with the page number. You call that a table of contents.\n\nYou wanted to avoid learning LaTeX because you're \"not a coding person,\" so instead you learned section breaks, roman numerals, and the one sacred rule: never touch Chapter 3. You moved one figure on page 12 and now your bibliography is in Comic Sans, somehow.\n\nYour file is called Thesis_FINAL_v7_REAL_advisorEdits_USE THIS ONE. You've emailed it to yourself as a backup, which is your entire version control strategy.\n\nAll that sacrifice to look professional, and your defense slides will be in PowerPoint. You haven't escaped. You've just switched the hostage."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0285", "lang": "en", "target_id": "emacs", "target_name": "Emacs", "facts": ["Began in 1976 as a set of macros for the TECO editor at MIT's AI Lab, written by Richard Stallman and others; GNU Emacs followed in 1985.", "Extensible in its own Lisp dialect, Emacs Lisp; the standing joke is that it is a fine operating system lacking only a good editor.", "Ships with a psychotherapist (M-x doctor), Tetris, a mail client, a calendar, and a web browser (eww).", "An old backronym is 'Eight Megabytes And Constantly Swapping', from when 8 MB was a lot of memory.", "'Emacs pinky' is the name for the hand strain blamed on its Control-key chords."], "a": {"item_id": "67c107490918", "text": "Let's hear it for the people who live inside Emacs. They read email in mu4e, browse the web in eww, and get therapy from M-x doctor, the only therapist who'll put up with them. Their whole life is in org-mode: a 4,000-line to-do file where \"fix init.el\" has been a TODO since 2011. They don't use a computer; they negotiate with it in Lisp. Every problem gets solved by writing a function, and every function creates a new problem. They've declared init.el bankruptcy three times and still owe. Ask what they've built and they'll proudly show you a mode for managing their other modes. And the payoff? A pinky that clicks like a castanet from holding Control since the Clinton administration, and the quiet dignity of being the only person alive who knows how to leave. C-x C-c. They never do."}, "b": {"item_id": "e61d80ea2a75", "text": "Let's talk about the people who don't use Emacs, they live in it. They read their email in there, check their calendar, play chess, and have never once figured out how to leave. Their pinkies are permanently stretched across Ctrl like they're holding a grudge. They spent three weekends configuring a text editor so they could avoid one afternoon of real work. Ask what they're building and they'll say \"my init file,\" proudly, like it's a novel. It's four thousand lines long. Nobody has read it, including them. They'll tell you it's an operating system. Their mother still calls it a text editor, and she's the only one in the family who's been honest with them."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0286", "lang": "en", "target_id": "microsoft-teams", "target_name": "Microsoft Teams", "facts": ["Launched in 2017, bundled at no extra charge into Office 365, and replaced Skype for Business.", "After Slack's antitrust complaint, Microsoft unbundled Teams from Office in the EU in 2023 and worldwide in 2024; the European Commission still sent a statement of objections in 2024.", "Microsoft reported 320 million monthly active users in 2023.", "Microsoft shut down consumer Skype in May 2025 and sent its users to Teams."], "a": {"item_id": "a37866956a51", "text": "Let's talk about the people who made us switch to Teams. Not Microsoft. The guy in procurement who discovered it was \"already included\" in the license and presented that like a victory, like he found forty dollars in a coat. He didn't choose a tool. He picked the one free thing on the bundle, and now four thousand of us pay for his savings by saying \"can you see my screen?\" every morning.\n\nThen there are the managers, who love it for the green dot. That's what you wanted. Not collaboration. A little light that says Brenda is alive. Brenda goes yellow after five minutes of reading a spreadsheet, so now she jiggles her mouse like she's hand-cranking a Model T.\n\nAnd what do you get for all this? A quarterly slide: \"Adoption up 40%.\" Of course it's up. You banned the other ones. That's not adoption, that's a hostage count."}, "b": {"item_id": "294237b8889b", "text": "Let's hear it for the CIO who chose Microsoft Teams, a decision made the way you pick ketchup packets: it came free with the order. Nobody evaluated it. It showed up inside the Office 365 license like a toy in a cereal box, and this man stood up at the all-hands and called it a \"collaboration strategy.\" Sir, the European Commission had to force Microsoft to unbundle it. You lost a negotiation to a checkbox.\n\nAnd what did he really want? The little status dot. He doesn't read your work; he reads your color. Go yellow after five minutes of actual thinking and you're on a list. Congratulations: you saved forty grand on Slack and spent it all on adults jiggling their mouse like raccoons on a treadmill. Your files are lost somewhere in SharePoint, and your morale is permanently \"Away.\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0287", "lang": "en", "target_id": "emacs", "target_name": "Emacs", "facts": ["Began in 1976 as a set of macros for the TECO editor at MIT's AI Lab, written by Richard Stallman and others; GNU Emacs followed in 1985.", "Extensible in its own Lisp dialect, Emacs Lisp; the standing joke is that it is a fine operating system lacking only a good editor.", "Ships with a psychotherapist (M-x doctor), Tetris, a mail client, a calendar, and a web browser (eww).", "An old backronym is 'Eight Megabytes And Constantly Swapping', from when 8 MB was a lot of memory.", "'Emacs pinky' is the name for the hand strain blamed on its Control-key chords."], "a": {"item_id": "e61d80ea2a75", "text": "Let's talk about the people who don't use Emacs, they live in it. They read their email in there, check their calendar, play chess, and have never once figured out how to leave. Their pinkies are permanently stretched across Ctrl like they're holding a grudge. They spent three weekends configuring a text editor so they could avoid one afternoon of real work. Ask what they're building and they'll say \"my init file,\" proudly, like it's a novel. It's four thousand lines long. Nobody has read it, including them. They'll tell you it's an operating system. Their mother still calls it a text editor, and she's the only one in the family who's been honest with them."}, "b": {"item_id": "78aa8d199f40", "text": "You've lived in Emacs for eleven years and written four thousand lines of config and zero lines of the novel your Org-mode agenda says you're \"drafting.\" You don't edit text, you tend an init.el that collapses every time a package updates. You read email in it, browse Reddit in it, and tell people at parties you \"just haven't needed to leave.\" Nobody asked where you've been.\n\nYour left pinky is a war veteran. You remapped Caps Lock to Control, and your therapist to M-x doctor. You installed evil-mode so you could escape Emacs keybindings, then defended Emacs keybindings in the comments. You spent a Saturday writing Lisp to automate a ten-second task, break-even in 2031, then published a blog post about it for the four other people who have the same blog post.\n\nYour whole personality is a tool that does everything except make you look up from it. Stallman would be proud. That's the problem."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0288", "lang": "en", "target_id": "jackfruit", "target_name": "Jackfruit", "facts": ["The largest fruit that grows on a tree; single fruits can weigh over 40 kg.", "The national fruit of Bangladesh and the state fruit of Kerala and Tamil Nadu.", "Unripe flesh is sold as a vegan 'pulled pork' substitute, although it has only 1-2 g of protein per 100 g.", "Cutting one releases a sticky white latex, so cooks oil their knives and hands first.", "A member of the mulberry family (Moraceae), a relative of breadfruit and figs."], "a": {"item_id": "73836848e6ca", "text": "You spent two weeks in Kerala, watched a grandmother cook jackfruit the way her family has for centuries, and came home with an LLC. Congratulations, you colonized a lunch.\n\nNow you're under a tent called \"Jack's Back,\" charging fourteen dollars for \"pulled pork\" that is ninety percent your cousin's barbecue sauce and ten percent a can you opened at 6 a.m. You call it disrupting BBQ. Texas hasn't noticed.\n\nWhat does it get you? Twenty-four hundred followers. A Whole Foods cashier who says \"Oh, jackfruit!\" like a secret handshake. And one carnivore uncle at Thanksgiving who says \"not bad\" in the voice of a hostage.\n\nMeanwhile, that grandmother in Kerala is still just calling it dinner. No pitch deck. No tent. Somehow, no sauce."}, "b": {"item_id": "b265b1d10a20", "text": "Every jackfruit seller has the same origin story. A guy who got laid off from software, bought a farm in a state he'd only seen from a plane window, and now runs a newsletter about \"reclaiming the dinner table.\" He's not selling you food. He's selling you the feeling that you finally did something. Jackfruit is perfect for him because it's the only thing that lets a man in a fleece vest say \"pulled pork\" at Whole Foods without anyone checking. He calls it \"the world's most sustainable protein,\" then sells it in a can with a label that looks like a Pixar villain. His customers are vegans who want a barbecue sandwich and carnivores who want a reason to feel smug about being there. Everybody wins except the sandwich."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0289", "lang": "en", "target_id": "keurig", "target_name": "Keurig", "facts": ["John Sylvan invented the K-Cup in the early 1990s and sold his stake for about $50,000 in 1997; in 2015 he told The Atlantic, 'I feel bad sometimes that I ever did it.'", "Keurig 2.0 (2014) read an ink mark on the lid and rejected unlicensed pods; sales fell and workarounds spread online.", "By 2014 enough K-Cups were sold each year to circle the Earth more than ten times; the pods were mostly unrecyclable #7 plastic until a switch to polypropylene by 2020.", "Keurig Green Mountain merged with Dr Pepper Snapple in 2018 to form Keurig Dr Pepper."], "a": {"item_id": "a034e62fdf17", "text": "You said you don't have time to grind beans, then spent ten minutes hunting for a pod in a drawer that's half foil lids and half regret. You bought the 72-count variety pack because you \"like options,\" and now you're drinking Hazelnut Creme Brûlée Cheesecake at 6 a.m. like a toddler who got the dessert menu. You pay fifty cents a cup for coffee that tastes like a hostage video: weak, brown, and apologizing. You press the \"strong\" button, which does nothing, and you believe in it anyway, like a guy pushing a crosswalk button.\n\nThe man who invented the K-Cup says he regrets it. The inventor. And you're still in the kitchen peeling foil off tiny plastic cups like you're losing at a very small lottery.\n\nYou bought the reusable filter to feel better about the landfill. It lives in a drawer now, with the gym membership and the sourdough starter."}, "b": {"item_id": "8a5105614740", "text": "Keurig people want coffee the way they want feelings: one serving, sealed, no cleanup. You're not a coffee drinker, you're a pod collector. You bought the variety pack, and now you're thirty-eight, rationing the last Donut Shop because all that's left is hazelnut and a hot cocoa you're saving for a breakdown. You press \"Strong\" like it's a personality trait. You swore you'd use the reusable filter, and you bought one. It's in a drawer next to your gym key fob. You saved four whole minutes, and you spend them staring at a blinking descale light you've decided is decorative. The guy who invented the pod publicly regrets it. You built your mornings around a man's biggest apology. Enjoy your landfill latte, champ. Ready in sixty seconds, just like your effort."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0290", "lang": "en", "target_id": "vim", "target_name": "Vim", "facts": ["Released by Bram Moolenaar in November 1991 as 'Vi IMitation', a vi clone he had begun writing for the Amiga in 1988 on top of Stevie; the expansion became 'Vi IMproved' with version 2.0 in December 1993.", "Licensed as charityware: the licence asks users to donate to ICCF Holland, a charity Moolenaar ran for children in Uganda.", "Stack Overflow's question 'How to exit the Vim editor?' passed one million visits in May 2017, which Stack Overflow marked with a blog post.", "Modal editing: in normal mode, typing letters runs commands rather than inserting text; :wq saves and quits, :q! quits and discards changes."], "a": {"item_id": "6eb19b0ff2a4", "text": "You know the type. You meet him at a dinner party, and within four minutes he's explaining that he hasn't touched a mouse since 2014. He doesn't edit text. He performs it. Every keystroke is a tiny sermon, and he says \"normal mode\" like it's a personality. He wants you to ask how he got so fast so he can tell you it's because he's been hitting Escape since the Clinton administration. His .vimrc is longer than his lease, he's got forty plugins, and he won't name his colorscheme because you'd only get it wrong. Ask him how to quit and he goes quiet, then smiles like he's waited his whole life for this. He'll teach you `:wq` for an hour. Ask him anything else, and he'll say, very calmly, that you should have read the docs."}, "b": {"item_id": "11a570c2e08d", "text": "Let's hear it for the Vim people. You can always spot one, because they'll tell you. Unprompted. Usually while you're mid-sentence about something else, like your divorce.\n\nThese folks spent three weekends tuning a .vimrc to save four seconds renaming a variable. That's a payback period of eleven thousand years, but sure, you're the efficient one.\n\nThey remapped Caps Lock to Escape and now can't type a password on anyone else's laptop. Their dotfiles repo has more stars than their actual projects. They say \"ciw\" out loud like it's a spell from a wizard who peaked in 1991.\n\nAnd what's the prize? You type :wq at the end of every Google Doc. You hit j in Slack and post the letter j to forty coworkers. Congratulations: you're the fastest typist in a room everyone already left."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0291", "lang": "en", "target_id": "durian", "target_name": "Durian", "facts": ["Banned on Singapore's MRT trains and in many Southeast Asian hotels because of the smell.", "A 2017 genome study in Nature Genetics traced the odour to a duplicated family of MGL genes that pump out volatile sulfur compounds.", "China buys most of the world's durian exports, chiefly from Thailand, in a trade worth billions of dollars a year.", "Alfred Russel Wallace wrote in 1856 that eating it was 'a new sensation worth a voyage to the East to experience'."], "a": {"item_id": "0bf8c83200e0", "text": "You're not eating durian on the train because you love it. You love being the person who eats durian on the train. There's a sign right there, with a little picture, drawn by a government, specifically for you. You looked at it and thought, \"Not me, I'm cultured.\"\n\nYou went to Penang once. Now you say \"king of fruits\" like you were knighted alongside it. You tell coworkers \"you just have to get past the first bite,\" which is also what people say about marriages they're stuck in.\n\nAnd what did you win? A carriage to yourself. A lifetime ban from three hotel chains. A sixty-dollar fruit you're pretending to enjoy because a man on a plastic stool called you brave.\n\nSir, he says that to every tourist. He's selling by the kilo."}, "b": {"item_id": "e97b85a188ed", "text": "Let's talk about the guy who eats durian on the train. Sir, Singapore, a country that will cane you for chewing gum, saw you coming and put your snack on a sign right next to \"no flammables.\" You're not hungry. You're running a loyalty test. Every face that twists is another person who \"just isn't adventurous.\" You paid sixty dollars for Musang King so you could explain Musang King to a stranger who's holding her breath. \"It's like custard, it's heaven, you just have to get past the-\" No. We don't. You brought a hotel-banned substance to a commute and called it a personality. And the best part? You'll be the only one who forgets you ate it. Your coworkers won't. Your hotel won't. Your Uber driver has already filed a one-star review with God."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0292", "lang": "en", "target_id": "vim", "target_name": "Vim", "facts": ["Released by Bram Moolenaar in November 1991 as 'Vi IMitation', a vi clone he had begun writing for the Amiga in 1988 on top of Stevie; the expansion became 'Vi IMproved' with version 2.0 in December 1993.", "Licensed as charityware: the licence asks users to donate to ICCF Holland, a charity Moolenaar ran for children in Uganda.", "Stack Overflow's question 'How to exit the Vim editor?' passed one million visits in May 2017, which Stack Overflow marked with a blog post.", "Modal editing: in normal mode, typing letters runs commands rather than inserting text; :wq saves and quits, :q! quits and discards changes."], "a": {"item_id": "6eb19b0ff2a4", "text": "You know the type. You meet him at a dinner party, and within four minutes he's explaining that he hasn't touched a mouse since 2014. He doesn't edit text. He performs it. Every keystroke is a tiny sermon, and he says \"normal mode\" like it's a personality. He wants you to ask how he got so fast so he can tell you it's because he's been hitting Escape since the Clinton administration. His .vimrc is longer than his lease, he's got forty plugins, and he won't name his colorscheme because you'd only get it wrong. Ask him how to quit and he goes quiet, then smiles like he's waited his whole life for this. He'll teach you `:wq` for an hour. Ask him anything else, and he'll say, very calmly, that you should have read the docs."}, "b": {"item_id": "229d2e4da892", "text": "You told the intern you use Vim before you told him your name. He didn't ask. Nobody asked. You just stood near a monitor and the urge took you.\n\nYou're not after efficiency. You're after a witness. You spent forty hours on your .vimrc to save eleven seconds a week, and the only payoff is someone watching you type \"ciw\" and gasping. They don't gasp. They say, \"Is it frozen?\"\n\nAnd what has it gotten you? A pair-programming partner who stopped pairing. A relative line number column you explain unprompted. A macro you recorded once, for a task you'll never repeat, that you still bring up at parties.\n\nYou say you never leave the home row. Buddy, you never leave the house either, but at least that one isn't a personality.\n\nMeanwhile Dave opened a file, changed a word with his mouse, and went to lunch."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0293", "lang": "en", "target_id": "vision-pro", "target_name": "Apple Vision Pro", "facts": ["Released in February 2024 at $3,499, with an external battery pack on a cable rated for about two hours.", "'EyeSight' shows a rendering of the wearer's eyes on an outward-facing screen.", "Apple calls it 'spatial computing' and its App Store rules tell developers not to describe apps as AR, VR, XR, or MR.", "Reports during 2024 said Apple had cut production; analysts' first-year sales estimates were well under a million units.", "Weighs 600-650 g, before the battery."], "a": {"item_id": "0dbe035a7cc0", "text": "Look at this guy on the F train, pinching at nothing like he's seasoning a ghost. He spent thirty-five hundred dollars to ride the subway on the surface of the moon, and the moon still smells like pee. He cranked the Digital Crown all the way to Joshua Tree, and the mariachi band found him anyway. That's the dream: rich enough to be somewhere else, broke enough to still be on the F. And he *wants* you to notice. That's why his fake cartoon eyes glow on the outside, so strangers know he's \"present.\" Buddy, nobody's checking. You're on Mount Hood. He gets two hours of battery and three stops of dignity, with a cable snaking into his pocket like a subway IV drip. Meanwhile, the guy next to him reading an actual book? That guy's already somewhere else, for free."}, "b": {"item_id": "2e6584647c14", "text": "You paid thirty-five hundred dollars to look like a hostage who's thrilled about it. Everyone on the 6 train can see you pinching the air, conducting an invisible orchestra of Safari tabs. Nobody is impressed. A man eating a hot dog just watched you \"tap\" a button that doesn't exist, and he's the one who feels sorry for you.\n\nYou wanted to be the future. Instead you're a guy who spent a month's rent to avoid eye contact, then bought the version that fakes eye contact for strangers. Those digital eyes are looking at nobody. The real ones are watching a 100-foot screen floating over Mount Hood, while the actual mountain you're passing is a tunnel wall covered in rat hair.\n\nAnd what did it get you? A full seat in rush hour. The whole car looked at you and decided you're either a genius or a crime scene."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0294", "lang": "en", "target_id": "nfts", "target_name": "NFTs", "facts": ["Beeple's 'Everydays: The First 5000 Days' sold at Christie's for $69.3 million in March 2021.", "OpenSea, the largest NFT marketplace, processed a record $2.7 billion in trades on 1 May 2022 and just $9.34 million on 28 August 2022, a 99% drop, according to DappRadar data.", "A 2023 dappGambl study found that 95% of the NFT collections it examined had a market value of zero.", "The token usually records only a URL; the image itself sits on an ordinary server or IPFS, not on the blockchain.", "Collins Dictionary named 'NFT' its word of the year for 2021."], "a": {"item_id": "e8eddc47095c", "text": "Nothing says \"I'm early\" like paying $300 in gas fees to join a Discord with forty thousand other people who are also early. You didn't buy art, Kyle. You bought a seat in a group chat where everyone types \"gm\" at strangers like they're greeting a medieval village. You told your mom it was \"a community.\" You changed your Twitter avatar to a hexagon and started saying \"ngmi\" to coworkers who just asked about the Wi-Fi password. Your whole investment thesis is \"number go up,\" and your exit strategy is finding someone dumber, who is also in the Discord, who is also looking for you. You check the floor price at red lights. You've cried over a Discord mod's emoji reaction. You spent your tax refund to be called \"anon\" by a guy named CryptoChad420, and the only thing you walked away with is proof that you were there when it peaked."}, "b": {"item_id": "de2993927ffe", "text": "Let's hear it for the 2021 NFT buyer. You paid four hundred dollars in gas fees to become the legal owner of something your coworker right-click saved during the meeting where you explained it. You didn't want art. You've never wanted art. You wanted a hexagon around your Twitter picture so strangers would know you had a Discord password. You typed \"WAGMI\" to eleven thousand anonymous cartoon monkeys and believed it. We are not all gonna make it, Kyle. The roadmap promised a metaverse yacht party; it delivered a founder named \"0xDegenSensei\" who deleted his account and moved to Portugal. But hey, you got what you paid for: a floor price you check like a heart monitor, and a profile picture you still can't bring yourself to change, because then it would be over."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0295", "lang": "en", "target_id": "php", "target_name": "PHP", "facts": ["Created by Rasmus Lerdorf in 1994 as 'Personal Home Page Tools', a set of C programs for tracking visitors to his own résumé page; the name now officially stands for the recursive 'PHP: Hypertext Preprocessor'.", "Lerdorf, in his 2007 MySQL Conference talk 'PHP on Hormones': 'I'm not a real programmer. I throw together things until it works then I move on.'", "Runs WordPress, which W3Techs counted on 39.4% of all websites (58.1% of sites with a known CMS) on 9 November 2026.", "Core function names are inconsistent (strpos, str_replace, htmlspecialchars, nl2br) partly because, as Lerdorf confirmed, early function names were chosen to balance a hash table keyed on string length.", "Until PHP 8 (2020), the comparison 0 == 'foo' was true because the string was silently converted to a number."], "a": {"item_id": "4b50923aaa44", "text": "You've said \"PHP isn't what it used to be\" so often it's basically your LinkedIn headline. Nobody asked. You bring up Facebook at parties like it's your cousin's startup. You switched to Laravel and call it a fresh start, which is a man putting a tuxedo on a Honda to prove he's going places.\n\nWhat are you after? Respect. What do you get? A client named Brenda who wants her WordPress site \"just a little different\" and pays by Venmo, sometimes.\n\nYou tell recruiters \"we're on PHP 8,\" meaning staging is. Production is a file called final_v2_REAL.php, with a comment from a guy who quit in 2014: \"don't touch.\"\n\nYou've been meaning to learn Go since 2019. Your fear isn't that PHP will die. It's that it never will, and neither will your on-call rotation."}, "b": {"item_id": "5aafe23afc0f", "text": "PHP developers are the only engineers who've been told their career is dead every year since 2009 and kept invoicing anyway. Your entire résumé is \"fixed a WordPress plugin for a dentist.\" You put a dollar sign in front of every variable because it's the closest you'll get to one. You still can't remember if it's needle-haystack or haystack-needle, and neither could the language, so you Google it daily like a man checking which door says \"push.\" Laravel devs, you're not better. You're PHP in a Patagonia vest. And you all still say \"Facebook was built on PHP,\" like a guy bragging his ex married a billionaire. You deploy over FTP, you hotfix live on production, and when someone at a party asks what you do, you say \"backend\" and pray they don't ask which."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0296", "lang": "en", "target_id": "php", "target_name": "PHP", "facts": ["Created by Rasmus Lerdorf in 1994 as 'Personal Home Page Tools', a set of C programs for tracking visitors to his own résumé page; the name now officially stands for the recursive 'PHP: Hypertext Preprocessor'.", "Lerdorf, in his 2007 MySQL Conference talk 'PHP on Hormones': 'I'm not a real programmer. I throw together things until it works then I move on.'", "Runs WordPress, which W3Techs counted on 39.4% of all websites (58.1% of sites with a known CMS) on 9 November 2026.", "Core function names are inconsistent (strpos, str_replace, htmlspecialchars, nl2br) partly because, as Lerdorf confirmed, early function names were chosen to balance a hash table keyed on string length.", "Until PHP 8 (2020), the comparison 0 == 'foo' was true because the string was silently converted to a number."], "a": {"item_id": "286b3c527101", "text": "You write PHP for a living, which means you've said \"modern PHP is actually really good\" more often than you've said \"I love you,\" and you meant it less. You've been announcing the comeback since 2012, like a guy outside a bar insisting his ex is about to call.\n\nYour dream is a Laravel job at a real company. Your reality is a client named Brenda who wants the contact form to \"pop.\" You tell people PHP runs most of the web as if the web sent a thank-you card. Your deploy process is dragging a folder into FileZilla, and your staging environment is Brenda's other site.\n\nEvery January, a blog declares PHP dead, and you feel something. It's the closest anyone has come to checking on you."}, "b": {"item_id": "453c78fa8180", "text": "Let's hear it for the PHP developers. You chose a language with a dollar sign on every variable, and you did it on purpose. Nobody stumbles into that. You saw that `array_search` takes its arguments in the opposite order from `in_array`, shrugged, and said, \"Honestly, I respect the chaos.\" Now you say \"modern PHP\" with a straight face, like you aren't still babysitting a WordPress site held together by a plugin called `mega_fix_final_v2`. Your dream is to be the one person who knows where the bodies are buried in a 900,000-line codebase, and you're proud of it. Your LinkedIn says \"Laravel enthusiast.\" Your weekends are spent on a staging server. And somewhere a recruiter is paying you more than the Rust guy, and you still feel a little guilty about it."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0297", "lang": "en", "target_id": "google-glass", "target_name": "Google Glass", "facts": ["Unveiled at Google I/O 2012 with skydivers livestreaming their jump through it onto the Moscone Center roof.", "The Explorer Edition sold to invited users in 2013 for $1,500.", "Wearers were nicknamed 'Glassholes'; bars, cinemas, and casinos banned the device over its camera.", "The consumer programme ended in January 2015; an Enterprise Edition continued until Google stopped selling it in 2023.", "Its display was a 640-by-360 prism in the corner of one eye."], "a": {"item_id": "b9dec6f155dd", "text": "Nothing says \"I'm approachable\" like paying fifteen hundred dollars to be a beta tester. You're not an early adopter, you're an unpaid Google intern who also buys his own drinks.\n\nYou came here to be noticed, and it worked, the way a guy who says \"I'm not a cop\" gets noticed. The bartender isn't asking what you're having, he's asking if you're recording. The woman you wanted to impress is explaining to her friend what a \"Glasshole\" is.\n\nThen you said \"OK Glass\" to a crowded room, waited, said it louder, and the only one who responded was the bouncer.\n\nYou wanted to look like the future. Instead you look like the first guy the future gets asked to leave."}, "b": {"item_id": "2e15bc79f728", "text": "Look at the guy at the bar wearing Google Glass. He paid fifteen hundred dollars to be an Explorer, which is what you call a man who volunteers to be the test dummy for his own loneliness. He's not drinking, he's documenting. He keeps saying \"OK Glass\" like he's summoning a genie who only grants weather updates. Every time a woman walks past, he winks, and she can't tell if he's flirting or taking her mugshot. He wants everyone to know he's ahead of the future. Meanwhile, the future is a grown man in a bar talking to his own face, reading his texts out loud, and nobody here asked him what he's looking at. Buddy, you're not looking at us. You're looking at emails the rest of us can't see, and honestly, that's the most lonely thing in this room."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0298", "lang": "en", "target_id": "canberra", "target_name": "Canberra", "facts": ["Chosen as a compromise because neither Sydney nor Melbourne would accept the other as capital; Australia's constitution required the capital to be at least 100 miles from Sydney.", "Designed by the Chicago architects Walter Burley Griffin and Marion Mahony Griffin, who won an international competition in 1912.", "Its centrepiece, Lake Burley Griffin, is artificial, created by damming the Molonglo River in 1963-64.", "Parliament House (1988) is built into Capital Hill under a lawn meant for citizens to walk over their politicians; the lawn was fenced off in 2017 for security.", "Melbourne served as the capital until 1927 while Canberra was being built."], "a": {"item_id": "a719d3fb6cc2", "text": "You moved to Canberra for the job, which is a brave way of saying you wanted an EL1 before thirty and Sydney wanted $900 a week for a room with a view of another room.\n\nYou said \"it's only for two years.\" Now you own a house in Gungahlin and have strong opinions about roundabouts.\n\nYour whole personality is your department. At parties nobody asks what you do. They ask which building, and everyone nods like you've named a regiment. You wear your lanyard into the Dickson noodle place like it's a war medal.\n\nYou came for career progression. You got a restructure every eighteen months, a boss who says \"going forward\" at farewells, and Sydney friends who promise to visit the way you promise to \"circle back.\"\n\nYou're not climbing the ladder. You're being machinery-of-governmented sideways."}, "b": {"item_id": "5732fa32845b", "text": "You know the type who moves to Canberra for \"two years, max.\" They arrive with a Perth accent, a Bunnings-sized ambition, and a LinkedIn banner that says \"Shaping policy.\" Six months in, they're saying \"Manuka\" like it's the suburb they grew up in. They want an acting EL2 before they've learned which parkway goes where. They own a lanyard drawer and a plan to be a Senate estimates star by spring. What it gets them: a townhouse in Gungahlin, a friend group made entirely of people who also work at Finance, and weekends spent driving to Bungendore to feel like they've left. Two years later they're still here, telling their mates back home the job's \"nearly done,\" which is exactly what they said in 2016."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0299", "lang": "en", "target_id": "luosifen", "target_name": "Luosifen (螺蛳粉)", "facts": ["A rice-noodle dish from Liuzhou, Guangxi; the smell comes from pickled bamboo shoots (酸笋), not from the river snails, which only flavour the broth.", "The instant version boomed during the 2020 lockdowns; Liuzhou's government reported packaged luosifen sales of over 10 billion yuan that year.", "Added to China's national intangible cultural heritage list in 2021.", "Liuzhou opened a luosifen industry college in 2020 to train workers for the trade."], "a": {"item_id": "2de91fadb67e", "text": "Nobody in this shared kitchen has ever been brave. You're the one who tears open the sachet at 11pm and lets that sour-funk fill the whole flat, then stands over the pot with your eyes closed like it's a religious rite. You wanted to be the guy who eats the wild thing and doesn't flinch. Your roommates are in their rooms now, pretending it's a fire alarm. Six months ago you said you'd \"get into\" Liuzhou food. Now you own three packs of pickled bamboo shoots, a chili oil you've never read the label on, and a standing grudge against the extractor fan. You're not really eating snail noodles. You're collecting a reaction."}, "b": {"item_id": "6eae17d96ab5", "text": "You're the person who read one Xiaohongshu post and now thinks you're from Liuzhou. You've never been to Guangxi. You've been to a Taobao checkout page, twelve packs deep, at 11 p.m.\n\nYou open the bamboo shoot packet like you're defusing a bomb, then tell the whole floor, \"It's actually not that strong once you get used to it.\" Used to it? Nobody got used to it. They just stopped telling you. Your roommates have a group chat with your name removed from it.\n\nAnd what do you get for all this? A bowl of noodles, a sticky note on the fridge, and the quiet dignity of eating alone, window open, in February. You wanted to be adventurous. You got a nickname in the HR department.\n\nAlso, you added extra chili oil. Of course you did."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0300", "lang": "en", "target_id": "vim", "target_name": "Vim", "facts": ["Released by Bram Moolenaar in November 1991 as 'Vi IMitation', a vi clone he had begun writing for the Amiga in 1988 on top of Stevie; the expansion became 'Vi IMproved' with version 2.0 in December 1993.", "Licensed as charityware: the licence asks users to donate to ICCF Holland, a charity Moolenaar ran for children in Uganda.", "Stack Overflow's question 'How to exit the Vim editor?' passed one million visits in May 2017, which Stack Overflow marked with a blog post.", "Modal editing: in normal mode, typing letters runs commands rather than inserting text; :wq saves and quits, :q! quits and discards changes."], "a": {"item_id": "613855b046fb", "text": "You know a Vim user is in the room because nobody else has gotten a word in. Buddy, you didn't \"become more productive.\" You spent three weekends on a .vimrc so you could save four seconds renaming a variable, and you post your dotfiles repo like it's a baby picture. You remapped Caps Lock to Escape and now treat everyone else's keyboard like a character flaw. You say \"I live in the terminal\" like it's a lifestyle and not just where you got stuck. Your whole identity is a joke about not knowing how to quit, and honestly, you never learned. Here you are, still talking. You say \"ciw\" out loud at parties. You switched to Neovim just so you'd have something new to announce. And the payoff? You edit text exactly as fast as the rest of us, hands proudly on home row, with nobody watching."}, "b": {"item_id": "6eb19b0ff2a4", "text": "You know the type. You meet him at a dinner party, and within four minutes he's explaining that he hasn't touched a mouse since 2014. He doesn't edit text. He performs it. Every keystroke is a tiny sermon, and he says \"normal mode\" like it's a personality. He wants you to ask how he got so fast so he can tell you it's because he's been hitting Escape since the Clinton administration. His .vimrc is longer than his lease, he's got forty plugins, and he won't name his colorscheme because you'd only get it wrong. Ask him how to quit and he goes quiet, then smiles like he's waited his whole life for this. He'll teach you `:wq` for an hour. Ask him anything else, and he'll say, very calmly, that you should have read the docs."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0301", "lang": "en", "target_id": "excel", "target_name": "Microsoft Excel", "facts": ["First shipped for the Macintosh in 1985; the Windows version followed in 1987.", "Silently converts gene names such as SEPT2 and MARCH1 into dates; by August 2020 the HUGO Gene Nomenclature Committee had renamed 27 human genes (MARCH1 became MARCHF1, SEPT2 became SEPTIN2) and published guidelines in Nature Genetics to stop it.", "In October 2020, Public Health England lost 15,841 COVID-19 cases when a legacy .xls file hit its 65,536-row limit.", "Reinhart and Rogoff's 2010 paper 'Growth in a Time of Debt', cited for austerity policies, omitted five countries through a spreadsheet range error found by a graduate student in 2013.", "Sheets have been capped at 1,048,576 rows by 16,384 columns since Excel 2007."], "a": {"item_id": "82eca86ba5a5", "text": "Nobody runs a company on Excel. Dave runs a company on Budget_FINAL_v7_USE_THIS_ONE.xlsx, which only Dave can open without the screen freezing for four minutes. Dave believes in control. Dave has forty tabs, and every one is named after a feeling: Forecast, Forecast2, Do Not Touch. He colors cells yellow for \"approved\" and orange for \"probably.\" His VLOOKUP holds payroll together like a prayer, and one #REF! away from a heart attack. He'll explain it to you at the Christmas party, sweating through a merged-cell cardigan. Dave doesn't want to be CEO. Dave wants you to need him."}, "b": {"item_id": "55712e9f94e9", "text": "You run a 40-million-dollar company on a file called \"Final_v7_REAL_use-this-one.\" You're not a CEO. You're a hostage negotiator for a spreadsheet.\n\nYou say you're \"scrappy.\" No. You're a guy who refuses to pay for software but will happily pay Brenda eleven hours a week to color cells yellow. Yellow means pending. Light yellow means pending but emotionally.\n\nYour entire infrastructure is a macro written by Dave, who left in 2016, and nobody knows where Dave went. You light a candle for Dave. You're afraid to scroll past column AZ because that's where Dave's curse lives.\n\nYou wanted control. You got a file locked for editing by \"Karen,\" who's on vacation in Portugal.\n\nAnd every quarter, your VLOOKUP returns #N/A, and you stare at it like it's a diagnosis you're choosing to ignore."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0302", "lang": "en", "target_id": "monopoly", "target_name": "Monopoly", "facts": ["Descends from Lizzie Magie's The Landlord's Game, patented in 1904 to teach the evils of land monopoly in the spirit of Henry George.", "Charles Darrow sold his version to Parker Brothers in 1935 and was credited as the inventor; Parker bought Magie's patent for $500.", "The official rules require any unbought property to be auctioned, and 'Free Parking' pays nothing; the house rules that skip the auction and pile cash on Free Parking are why games drag.", "During the Second World War, British intelligence had maps, compasses, and real currency hidden in sets sent to prisoner-of-war camps."], "a": {"item_id": "10bac3efc0cc", "text": "You all gather around a table to prove one thing: that you'd evict your own mother for a purple property. Dad's the banker, which is why the bank has mysteriously never gone broke and he somehow always has a 500. Your sister trades like a hostage negotiator, offering you Baltic Avenue and emotional closure for Boardwalk. Uncle Rick insists Free Parking pays out the whole jackpot, a rule that exists nowhere except in his need to feel lucky once in his life. The twelve-year-old wants only the railroads, because he's the one person here with a clear understanding of passive income. Grandma says nothing for three hours, then owns everything. Six hours in, nobody's having fun, but nobody can quit, because quitting means admitting you're the one in jail. It ends the way it always does: someone flips the board, someone cries, and everyone's out of money, including the winner's relationships."}, "b": {"item_id": "5b3d3501d71e", "text": "Dad, you've been the banker since 2009, and you're the only one who knows what's in that tray, and you've never once shared it. Free Parking has had four hundred dollars in it since the Clinton administration, and you call pocketing it \"a light house rule.\" Mom has wanted to quit at nine o'clock since the second lap. Your brother bought every orange property on the first trip around and now calls himself a real estate mogul at a kitchen table. Grandma is the thimble, and she's still winning, because she's the only one who doesn't count her cash. The nephew is eight. He mortgaged his railroads to buy a hotel he can't afford, then sobbed in jail. Everybody says \"just visiting\" like it's a personality. You're not playing for the fake money. You're finding out who'll flip the board when they lose."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0303", "lang": "en", "target_id": "luosifen", "target_name": "Luosifen (螺蛳粉)", "facts": ["A rice-noodle dish from Liuzhou, Guangxi; the smell comes from pickled bamboo shoots (酸笋), not from the river snails, which only flavour the broth.", "The instant version boomed during the 2020 lockdowns; Liuzhou's government reported packaged luosifen sales of over 10 billion yuan that year.", "Added to China's national intangible cultural heritage list in 2021.", "Liuzhou opened a luosifen industry college in 2020 to train workers for the trade."], "a": {"item_id": "911bf6e3e8ce", "text": "You know the luosifen guy. He opens the bag at 11 p.m. and the whole floor knows before the water boils. He doesn't cook it, he deploys it. Sour bamboo, peanuts, and that sachet of chili oil he's been hoarding since his last trip to Guangxi, all laid out like a surgeon's tray. He wants you to ask what that smell is. That's the whole meal. Every time someone asks, he gets to say \"it's snail,\" with the calm of a man who has never once been left out of a group chat. Then he eats it standing by the window, eyes closed, like he's the only one who understands it. The window stays open all winter. Nobody's allowed to say it's bad. Nobody's allowed to say much of anything, because he's already on his second bowl, and his face says he's in Liuzhou and you're not."}, "b": {"item_id": "6eae17d96ab5", "text": "You're the person who read one Xiaohongshu post and now thinks you're from Liuzhou. You've never been to Guangxi. You've been to a Taobao checkout page, twelve packs deep, at 11 p.m.\n\nYou open the bamboo shoot packet like you're defusing a bomb, then tell the whole floor, \"It's actually not that strong once you get used to it.\" Used to it? Nobody got used to it. They just stopped telling you. Your roommates have a group chat with your name removed from it.\n\nAnd what do you get for all this? A bowl of noodles, a sticky note on the fridge, and the quiet dignity of eating alone, window open, in February. You wanted to be adventurous. You got a nickname in the HR department.\n\nAlso, you added extra chili oil. Of course you did."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0304", "lang": "en", "target_id": "juicero", "target_name": "Juicero", "facts": ["A Wi-Fi-connected cold-press juicer launched in 2016 at $699, later cut to $399, that squeezed proprietary bags of chopped produce sold by subscription.", "Raised about $120 million from investors including Google Ventures and Kleiner Perkins.", "In April 2017, Bloomberg reporters squeezed the packs by hand and got 7.5 ounces of juice in 90 seconds, faster than the machine's 8 ounces in about two minutes.", "Each pack carried a QR code that the press scanned and checked online before squeezing; it refused expired packs and would not run at all unless connected to Wi-Fi.", "Shut down in September 2017 and offered refunds on the machine."], "a": {"item_id": "3f1bb8138434", "text": "Let's talk about the people who paid four hundred dollars for a Juicero. You know who you are. You wanted to feel like you had a private chef, so you bought a machine that needs Wi-Fi to check your bag. Then the internet discovered the bag was the real product, a pouch of chopped kale you could squeeze with your bare hands. Which you did, eventually, in front of guests, because nothing says \"tech visionary\" like hand-pressing a fruit packet while your friend holds a forty-dollar blender. You weren't buying juice. You were buying the feeling of being early to something, and the only thing you got early was the eBay listing."}, "b": {"item_id": "fa27493ecc18", "text": "You paid $400 to feel like someone with a \"morning routine.\" Not to have juice. To have a routine. You wanted to stand in your kitchen at 6 a.m. in a gray linen set, slide a sealed pouch into a machine, and feel like a Series B founder with kidneys.\n\nThen Bloomberg showed you could squeeze the pouch with your bare hands and get the same juice. And you weren't mad at the company. You were mad at your hands. You'd hired a middleman for the one job your body was already built for.\n\nYou let a gadget check the internet before it would let you drink a beet, and you called that wellness. Your grandmother wrung out dishrags with more commitment to nutrition, and she never needed a QR code to know when the carrots expired."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0305", "lang": "en", "target_id": "oat-milk", "target_name": "Oat milk", "facts": ["Oatly started in Sweden in the 1990s, built on Lund University research by Rickard Öste on lactose intolerance.", "Oatly's slogan 'It's like milk but made for humans' was banned in Sweden in 2015 after the dairy lobby LRF Mjölk sued; Oatly then published the lawsuit as advertising.", "Oatly listed on Nasdaq in May 2021 at a valuation near $10 billion; the shares later lost more than 90% of their value.", "Commercial oat milk is made by breaking oat starch into maltose with amylase enzymes, and many brands, Oatly among them, add rapeseed oil; the result has more sugar and a higher glycaemic index than almond or soy milk."], "a": {"item_id": "a99ceee7d226", "text": "Nobody orders oat milk for the oats. You order it so the barista knows you've done your research. You say \"just make it oat\" like you're showing a passport. You're not lactose intolerant. You're \"not really doing dairy right now,\" a phrase you've never once said about bread. You want your cup to announce your values before you've said a word. Then you spend four minutes explaining your gut to a kid named Jayden at 7 a.m. Your tote bag has a sea turtle on it. You pay a dollar extra and hold the cup up for a photo, waiting for someone to ask what's in it so you can say \"oat\" and watch them nod like you just told them you ran a marathon."}, "b": {"item_id": "4f077d2ff349", "text": "You didn't order oat milk because you're lactose intolerant. You ordered it because you read one article about almonds in 2019 and it became your whole personality.\n\nNow you say \"oat\" to the barista like a secret password to a club that has no door. Seventy-five cents extra, and you act like you've personally reversed the polar ice caps. You then fly to Lisbon for a long weekend.\n\nYou've told three people at dinner that you \"feel better since cutting dairy.\" Feel better how? Your cheese board arrived and you ate it like a raccoon at a wedding.\n\nYou know the brand name. You have opinions about the brand name. You once said \"barista blend\" out loud, on purpose, to a friend.\n\nThe cow was fine, Brad. The cow didn't need you to be brave."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0306", "lang": "en", "target_id": "keurig", "target_name": "Keurig", "facts": ["John Sylvan invented the K-Cup in the early 1990s and sold his stake for about $50,000 in 1997; in 2015 he told The Atlantic, 'I feel bad sometimes that I ever did it.'", "Keurig 2.0 (2014) read an ink mark on the lid and rejected unlicensed pods; sales fell and workarounds spread online.", "By 2014 enough K-Cups were sold each year to circle the Earth more than ten times; the pods were mostly unrecyclable #7 plastic until a switch to polypropylene by 2020.", "Keurig Green Mountain merged with Dr Pepper Snapple in 2018 to form Keurig Dr Pepper."], "a": {"item_id": "017955fe0eb2", "text": "You wanted coffee without a relationship, so you bought a plastic thimble of \"Hazelnut Crème Brûlée\" and let a machine rinse it for forty seconds. That's not a flavor, that's a candle with a caffeine problem.\n\nYou own a 96-count Costco box and still haven't found a pod that tastes like anything but hot mug. You hit the Strong button like it's a negotiation. It's the same coffee, just smaller and angrier.\n\nThe descale light has been on since March. You know it. It knows it. You're drinking limescale with notes of whatever pod you picked.\n\nYou bought the reusable filter once, to feel responsible. It lives in the drawer with the takeout menus. Every morning you throw away a tiny wet puck and call it a routine."}, "b": {"item_id": "8a5105614740", "text": "Keurig people want coffee the way they want feelings: one serving, sealed, no cleanup. You're not a coffee drinker, you're a pod collector. You bought the variety pack, and now you're thirty-eight, rationing the last Donut Shop because all that's left is hazelnut and a hot cocoa you're saving for a breakdown. You press \"Strong\" like it's a personality trait. You swore you'd use the reusable filter, and you bought one. It's in a drawer next to your gym key fob. You saved four whole minutes, and you spend them staring at a blinking descale light you've decided is decorative. The guy who invented the pod publicly regrets it. You built your mornings around a man's biggest apology. Enjoy your landfill latte, champ. Ready in sixty seconds, just like your effort."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0307", "lang": "en", "target_id": "slack", "target_name": "Slack", "facts": ["Spun out in 2013 from the internal chat tool of Tiny Speck's failed game Glitch; founder Stewart Butterfield had earlier spun Flickr out of another failed game.", "The name is a backronym: 'Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge'.", "Salesforce bought it for $27.7 billion in 2021.", "Filed an EU antitrust complaint against Microsoft in 2020 over the bundling of Teams with Office."], "a": {"item_id": "fae8a282c283", "text": "Let's hear it for the founders running a company on Slack. You wanted a culture of transparency. You got 340 public channels and every real decision happening in a DM called \"quick q.\"\n\nYou don't manage people anymore. You manage a green dot. You jiggle your mouse at your kid's birthday party so the team knows you're \"around.\" Your entire leadership style is the eyes emoji. Someone asks for budget approval, you react with a little thumbs-up, and three weeks later legal wants to know what the thumbs-up meant. Nobody knows. It was legally binding and spiritually ambiguous.\n\nAnd when something truly matters? You type \"hey\" and walk away. Just \"hey.\" That's not a message, that's a hostage situation.\n\nYou promised Slack would kill meetings. Instead you invented the huddle: a meeting that ambushes people while they're eating."}, "b": {"item_id": "ccebb9af0d58", "text": "You run your company on Slack because you wanted a culture and settled for a green dot. You've made forty-seven channels and #general is a graveyard of work anniversaries and a guy named Greg's lunch photos. Your big decisions get made with a thumbs-up emoji, and a raised-hands reaction counts as alignment. You type \"@here\" like you're summoning a god, then ask whether anyone's seen the doc you pinned in a channel you archived in March.\n\nYou love being \"async\" until you need an answer, and then it's \"quick huddle?\" Nobody has ever had a quick huddle. You say you hate meetings, so you built a meeting that never ends, sends push notifications, and has a gif keyboard.\n\nAnd the payoff? Your best engineer set her status to \"🌴 away\" in 2022 and nobody's checked if she's gone."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0308", "lang": "en", "target_id": "slack", "target_name": "Slack", "facts": ["Spun out in 2013 from the internal chat tool of Tiny Speck's failed game Glitch; founder Stewart Butterfield had earlier spun Flickr out of another failed game.", "The name is a backronym: 'Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge'.", "Salesforce bought it for $27.7 billion in 2021.", "Filed an EU antitrust complaint against Microsoft in 2020 over the bundling of Teams with Office."], "a": {"item_id": "fae8a282c283", "text": "Let's hear it for the founders running a company on Slack. You wanted a culture of transparency. You got 340 public channels and every real decision happening in a DM called \"quick q.\"\n\nYou don't manage people anymore. You manage a green dot. You jiggle your mouse at your kid's birthday party so the team knows you're \"around.\" Your entire leadership style is the eyes emoji. Someone asks for budget approval, you react with a little thumbs-up, and three weeks later legal wants to know what the thumbs-up meant. Nobody knows. It was legally binding and spiritually ambiguous.\n\nAnd when something truly matters? You type \"hey\" and walk away. Just \"hey.\" That's not a message, that's a hostage situation.\n\nYou promised Slack would kill meetings. Instead you invented the huddle: a meeting that ambushes people while they're eating."}, "b": {"item_id": "c5f48d20bb73", "text": "You didn't build a company, you built a chat room with payroll. Your \"culture\" is a custom emoji of your CTO's face, and that's the only thing anyone has shipped this quarter.\n\nYou said Slack would kill meetings. Now every \"quick question\" is a huddle that starts with \"can you hear me?\" and ends with \"let's move this to a thread.\"\n\nYou @channel the whole company to announce a bagel delivery, then act shocked that nobody reads your @channels. You've got 112 channels for 14 employees. Three of them are for work. One is #wins, and it's just you, reacting to your own post with a rocket.\n\nYou aren't managing anybody. You're watching a green dot and calling it trust. Someone's dot went gray at 5:01 and you took it personally.\n\nAnd the best part? \"Sorry, just seeing this!\" is now your company's mission statement."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0309", "lang": "en", "target_id": "windows-8", "target_name": "Windows 8", "facts": ["Released in October 2012; removed the Start button and booted into a full-screen grid of tiles designed for touch screens most PCs did not have.", "The 8.1 update in 2013 put a Start button back, Windows 10 (2015) restored the Start menu, and Microsoft skipped the name Windows 9 altogether.", "Microsoft dropped the 'Metro' name in August 2012 after reports that German retailer Metro AG had objected on trademark grounds; Microsoft called 'Metro' a code name and denied any litigation, and the style was afterwards known informally as 'Modern UI'.", "The 'Charms bar' hid Search, Share, and Settings behind an invisible swipe from the right edge.", "Windows RT, the ARM edition launched alongside it, could not run ordinary Windows programs; Microsoft wrote down $900 million of unsold Surface RT tablets in 2013."], "a": {"item_id": "a00e6ae0aa53", "text": "Who puts Windows 8 on a desktop? You. You looked at a perfectly working Windows 7 machine and said, \"What this needs is a touchscreen experience, and I don't own a touchscreen.\"\n\nSo now you sit at a 27-inch monitor, flinging your mouse into corners like a man hunting for a light switch in a stranger's house. You've Googled \"how to shut down\" at eleven at night. Your wife watched.\n\nYou wanted the future. You got a full-screen weather app you can't close and a Mail tile that's been \"Getting things ready\" since August.\n\nEventually you downloaded a program to bring the Start button back, so you paid to get Windows 7 with extra steps.\n\nAnd sometimes you still tap the screen. It's not a touchscreen. Those fingerprints are just hope."}, "b": {"item_id": "131d66f0a7cf", "text": "Nobody installs Windows 8 on a desktop out of passion. You saw a perfectly good Start button and thought, \"Honestly, too much of this.\" You wanted touch controls on a machine with no touchscreen, so now you're swiping at a monitor like it owes you money. Shutting down means hunting through the charms bar, hovering in the corner like a nervous burglar, and praying the hot corner doesn't ambush you mid-email. You bought Start8, felt guilty, then bought a second utility to fix the first. Your Start screen is a grid of tiles for apps you'll never open, and the one you actually use is buried under Weather, which is always sunny in your sad little world."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0310", "lang": "en", "target_id": "excel", "target_name": "Microsoft Excel", "facts": ["First shipped for the Macintosh in 1985; the Windows version followed in 1987.", "Silently converts gene names such as SEPT2 and MARCH1 into dates; by August 2020 the HUGO Gene Nomenclature Committee had renamed 27 human genes (MARCH1 became MARCHF1, SEPT2 became SEPTIN2) and published guidelines in Nature Genetics to stop it.", "In October 2020, Public Health England lost 15,841 COVID-19 cases when a legacy .xls file hit its 65,536-row limit.", "Reinhart and Rogoff's 2010 paper 'Growth in a Time of Debt', cited for austerity policies, omitted five countries through a spreadsheet range error found by a graduate student in 2013.", "Sheets have been capped at 1,048,576 rows by 16,384 columns since Excel 2007."], "a": {"item_id": "55712e9f94e9", "text": "You run a 40-million-dollar company on a file called \"Final_v7_REAL_use-this-one.\" You're not a CEO. You're a hostage negotiator for a spreadsheet.\n\nYou say you're \"scrappy.\" No. You're a guy who refuses to pay for software but will happily pay Brenda eleven hours a week to color cells yellow. Yellow means pending. Light yellow means pending but emotionally.\n\nYour entire infrastructure is a macro written by Dave, who left in 2016, and nobody knows where Dave went. You light a candle for Dave. You're afraid to scroll past column AZ because that's where Dave's curse lives.\n\nYou wanted control. You got a file locked for editing by \"Karen,\" who's on vacation in Portugal.\n\nAnd every quarter, your VLOOKUP returns #N/A, and you stare at it like it's a diagnosis you're choosing to ignore."}, "b": {"item_id": "0b80e15aa132", "text": "You people run a forty-million-dollar company out of a file called Budget_FINAL_v9_USE_THIS_ONE.xlsx. You don't want software. You want power: specifically, being the only person alive who knows why cell AF212 is hardcoded to 7. Your entire supply chain hangs on a VLOOKUP Gary wrote in 2011, and Gary is dead. Well, he's in Boise. Same thing. You call it \"agile.\" Your auditors call it \"a crime scene with conditional formatting.\" Every quarter-end the file says \"locked for editing by Brenda,\" and the whole company just waits for Brenda. And when you finally hit row 1,048,576, did you buy a database? No. You started Sheet2. That's not a business. That's a hostage situation with freeze panes."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0311", "lang": "en", "target_id": "ugg", "target_name": "UGG boots", "facts": ["Sheepskin boots Australian surfers wore to warm up after sessions; Brian Smith started selling them in California in 1978.", "Deckers Outdoor bought the brand in 1995 and holds 'UGG' as a trademark in the US and elsewhere; in Australia, 'ugg' is a generic word any bootmaker may use.", "In 2019, a US jury found an Australian maker liable for selling boots labelled 'ugg' into the United States.", "Oprah Winfrey featured them in her 'Favorite Things' lists repeatedly in the 2000s, which drove the first boom.", "UGG is Deckers' largest brand, with over $2 billion in annual sales."], "a": {"item_id": "f64065ff7ffc", "text": "Y'all know the year-round UGG people. It's ninety-four degrees in Phoenix, and there's a woman in the Target parking lot in full sheepskin, sweating like a rotisserie chicken and saying, \"They're just so comfortable.\" Comfortable? Your feet are wrapped in a sheep's coat in July. You're not dressing for comfort. You're dressing for a personality. You want people to think you have a cozy life, a Pinterest board, and a husband named Brett who keeps a sourdough starter. And what does it get you? Feet that smell like a wet Labrador and a standing order at the drive-thru, where the barista already knows your name and is quietly worried about you. You didn't choose footwear. You chose to hibernate from the neck down."}, "b": {"item_id": "8e467854d2d5", "text": "Some of you wear UGGs in July, and I respect a person who has never once been told no. You're not dressing for the weather. You're dressing for 2006. Somewhere in your heart, a paparazzi photo of Paris Hilton is still loading. You want people to think \"cozy.\" People think \"sleepover or custody hearing?\" And the walk: your ankles gave up years ago and went horizontal. You're not walking anymore, you're leaning toward your destination. Australian surfers wore these to warm up after the freezing ocean. You wear them to warm up after the Target parking lot. In August. Your feet sweat so hard the sheep is finally getting its revenge. And the second it drizzles, you're frozen under an awning like a cat who's been threatened with a bath."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0312", "lang": "en", "target_id": "carbon-nanotubes", "target_name": "Carbon nanotubes", "facts": ["Reported by Sumio Iijima in Nature in 1991.", "Promised as the cable for a space elevator; a 2006 analysis showed that ordinary defects cut the strength of a macroscopic cable far below the ideal value.", "Long, rigid multi-walled nanotubes behaved like asbestos fibres in a 2008 mouse study in Nature Nanotechnology.", "Sorting metallic from semiconducting tubes has blocked nanotube electronics for three decades; MIT's 16-bit RV16X-NANO processor (2019) is still a lab demonstration.", "Today they are sold mainly as a conductive additive in lithium-ion battery electrodes."], "a": {"item_id": "839efb519f46", "text": "Nanotube founders are the only people who've said \"within five years\" every year since 1991 and kept a straight face. They open with a slide of a space elevator, then ask for seed money to make a vial of black dust. Every deck has the same line, \"a hundred times stronger than steel,\" and every deck is quietly a plan to sell additive to tennis racket companies. These are PhDs who spent six years sorting chirality and came out wanting a Tesla. The pitch is always \"we've solved scale,\" and the demo is always one centimeter long. VCs keep a nanotube guy around like a lucky rabbit's foot: he never pays off, but he makes the fund sound visionary. And when someone asks about lung toxicity, he says \"totally different from asbestos\" with the confidence of a man who googled it in the elevator. The regular one."}, "b": {"item_id": "09b6c02ce449", "text": "Every CNT pitch deck has the same slide: a space elevator hanging off the equator, a cable strong enough to hold a city, and a sunrise nobody asked for. The founder skips the part where that cable is \"in development\" the way the moon is in development. He has a PhD, wears a lab coat to investor dinners, and has said \"five years from commercial scale\" since 2009. He's been five years away for fifteen years. Investors adore him because he says \"tensile strength\" like a man who has never had to explain a yield curve. What it gets him: a Series C, a conference booth beside a guy selling graphene sunglasses, and a LinkedIn banner of himself squinting at a black smear that is somehow his life's work. The tubes are doing fine. They've never once asked for a Series C."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0313", "lang": "en", "target_id": "google-sheets", "target_name": "Google Sheets", "facts": ["Launched in June 2006 as Google Spreadsheets, built on XL2Web, a product Google acquired from 2Web Technologies.", "A spreadsheet is capped at 10 million cells (raised from 5 million in 2022).", "Functions such as IMPORTHTML, IMPORTXML, and GOOGLEFINANCE pull live web data straight into cells.", "Free with a Google account; files live in Google Drive, and collaborators edit the same sheet simultaneously with coloured cursors.", "In a browser, offline editing works only in Chrome or Edge, with the Google Docs Offline extension installed and offline access switched on while still connected; the Android and iOS apps work offline natively."], "a": {"item_id": "ed3e91782987", "text": "Let's hear it for the manager who runs a twelve-person team out of a Google Sheet. You didn't want to pay for Asana; you wanted to feel like you *built* Asana. Look at you, color-coding rows like a kindergarten teacher with admin rights. Your whole org chart is a tab called \"Roster FINAL (2).\" Your performance reviews are conditional formatting: green means safe, red means Kyle. And every morning you open it, see Anonymous Capybara lurking at row 400, and feel genuinely hunted. Somebody sorted one column without the others last Tuesday, and now Priya reports to the office printer. You fixed it through version history like a detective reconstructing a murder. That's the dream, right? Control. Total visibility. And what you got is forty \"Request edit access\" emails and a VLOOKUP holding up the company like a Jenga block."}, "b": {"item_id": "85e5be0c8934", "text": "So you run the whole team out of one Google Sheet. You have forty tabs, and one is called \"FINAL FINAL use this one.\" Every task gets a color, and the palette only makes sense to you. You built a Gantt chart out of merged cells, so the project is technically on schedule but unreadable to anyone born after the chart. Your status dropdown has six options, four of which mean \"fine.\" You want control, so you are the only one allowed near the VLOOKUPs, and you leave comments that just say \"see my note\" with no note. What it gets you is a team that makes its own copy, pastes the real numbers into a tab called \"actual,\" and checks version history only to find out who ruined your formatting."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0314", "lang": "en", "target_id": "esperanto", "target_name": "Esperanto", "facts": ["Published in Warsaw in 1887 by L. L. Zamenhof, a Białystok-born eye doctor, under the pen name Doktoro Esperanto, 'one who hopes'.", "Speaker estimates range from 100,000 to two million; roughly a thousand people grow up with it as a native language.", "William Shatner starred in Incubus (1966), a horror film shot entirely in Esperanto, with pronunciation Esperantists still mock.", "Its Wikipedia has over 350,000 articles, and its Duolingo course for English speakers had 1.36 million learners by July 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "d94ef91d85f7", "text": "Learning Esperanto is for people who looked at the twentieth century and thought, \"The real problem was conjugation.\" Zamenhof built it to end war, and his followers have bravely ended exactly one thing: their dinner invitations. You meet them at the World Congress, green star pinned to a fleece vest, shouting \"Saluton!\" at a Brazilian who also learned it on Duolingo. The big perk is Pasporta Servo: free couches worldwide, paid for by hearing your host explain why English is \"linguistic imperialism.\" Practical payoff? You can finally watch William Shatner's *Incubus* without subtitles. One hundred thirty years later, the dream of uniting humanity survives in a Telegram group of forty men named Bjorn."}, "b": {"item_id": "09a618d282b4", "text": "Who learns Esperanto? People who've concluded the world's real problem is that nobody agreed on the grammar. They wear the green star on their lapel. They say \"Saluton!\" to the barista and watch her face do nothing. Their dream is one planet, one neutral tongue, and their first step is finding eleven other people in their city who share it. That's how they end up in a Leeds community hall with a plate of biscuits and Gerald, who has been \"nearly fluent\" since 1994. They have a pen pal in Brazil they've never met, and they write to him in flawless accusative. What does it get them? A hug from a stranger at a congress, and the quiet pride of being fluent in a language that mostly exists in other Esperantists' inboxes."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0315", "lang": "en", "target_id": "microsoft-teams", "target_name": "Microsoft Teams", "facts": ["Launched in 2017, bundled at no extra charge into Office 365, and replaced Skype for Business.", "After Slack's antitrust complaint, Microsoft unbundled Teams from Office in the EU in 2023 and worldwide in 2024; the European Commission still sent a statement of objections in 2024.", "Microsoft reported 320 million monthly active users in 2023.", "Microsoft shut down consumer Skype in May 2025 and sent its users to Teams."], "a": {"item_id": "294237b8889b", "text": "Let's hear it for the CIO who chose Microsoft Teams, a decision made the way you pick ketchup packets: it came free with the order. Nobody evaluated it. It showed up inside the Office 365 license like a toy in a cereal box, and this man stood up at the all-hands and called it a \"collaboration strategy.\" Sir, the European Commission had to force Microsoft to unbundle it. You lost a negotiation to a checkbox.\n\nAnd what did he really want? The little status dot. He doesn't read your work; he reads your color. Go yellow after five minutes of actual thinking and you're on a list. Congratulations: you saved forty grand on Slack and spent it all on adults jiggling their mouse like raccoons on a treadmill. Your files are lost somewhere in SharePoint, and your morale is permanently \"Away.\""}, "b": {"item_id": "b048a3892478", "text": "Nobody asked for Teams. The people who forced it on us need a green dot next to your name to feel alive. Your manager has seventeen channels named after a project cancelled in March, and he still posts \"Quick question!\" at 6:52 p.m. just so you'll see the three dots and feel summoned. The IT guy who rolled it out got a bonus for \"adoption rates,\" which means he counted every time you clicked the purple icon by accident. They want proof you're working. Your status says \"Available\" while you're in the bathroom, and they're thrilled, because now they can watch you be available. Then comes the all-hands where forty people unmute at once, someone's dog barks, and the host says, \"Let's take this offline.\" They've never once been offline."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0316", "lang": "en", "target_id": "graphene", "target_name": "Graphene", "facts": ["Isolated in 2004 by Andre Geim and Konstantin Novoselov at Manchester by peeling graphite with Scotch tape; Nobel Prize in Physics, 2010.", "The EU's Graphene Flagship, launched in 2013, committed €1 billion over ten years.", "A 2018 study in Advanced Materials tested 'graphene' from 60 producers and found none with more than 50% graphene content; most was graphite flakes.", "Sold in tennis rackets, running shoes, and headphones as a performance ingredient.", "Geim also won an Ig Nobel Prize in 2000 for levitating a frog with magnets."], "a": {"item_id": "9c280b4152bc", "text": "Every graphene pitch deck opens with the same slide: a honeycomb, a stock photo of a tennis racket, and the word \"revolution\" in a font you'd use for a dentist. These are the people who got a Nobel Prize for pulling a flake of pencil lead off with sticky tape, then spent fifteen years and four investor rounds explaining why that's the future. Their LinkedIn says \"materials scientist.\" Their Series A says \"platform.\" Their fundraising emails say \"graphene-enabled,\" which is what you write when the graphene in the sunscreen is too thin to find with a microscope. They want the Nobel, the magazine cover, and a sabbatical in Tahiti. What they get is one sentence in a press release that starts \"Experts say.\""}, "b": {"item_id": "886e9fa21662", "text": "Twenty years you've been telling us graphene will change everything, and the only thing it's changed is your grant renewal status. You won a Nobel Prize with Scotch tape and a pencil, and somehow concluded the hard part was over. Since then you've pitched it for batteries, bulletproof vests, desalination, and condoms, plus a paint, which actually exists, so naturally nobody's excited. The EU gave you a billion euros, and you came back with a roadmap. Every press release starts with \"could,\" because \"does\" would require a customer. Your startup's deck says \"disruptive.\" Your LinkedIn says \"thought leader.\" Your lab says \"please send more funding.\" You've been two years from commercialization since Myspace. Keep going, though. Somewhere in Manchester, a man in a lanyard still gasps when you say \"one atom thick.\" That gasp is the product."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0317", "lang": "en", "target_id": "vim", "target_name": "Vim", "facts": ["Released by Bram Moolenaar in November 1991 as 'Vi IMitation', a vi clone he had begun writing for the Amiga in 1988 on top of Stevie; the expansion became 'Vi IMproved' with version 2.0 in December 1993.", "Licensed as charityware: the licence asks users to donate to ICCF Holland, a charity Moolenaar ran for children in Uganda.", "Stack Overflow's question 'How to exit the Vim editor?' passed one million visits in May 2017, which Stack Overflow marked with a blog post.", "Modal editing: in normal mode, typing letters runs commands rather than inserting text; :wq saves and quits, :q! quits and discards changes."], "a": {"item_id": "229d2e4da892", "text": "You told the intern you use Vim before you told him your name. He didn't ask. Nobody asked. You just stood near a monitor and the urge took you.\n\nYou're not after efficiency. You're after a witness. You spent forty hours on your .vimrc to save eleven seconds a week, and the only payoff is someone watching you type \"ciw\" and gasping. They don't gasp. They say, \"Is it frozen?\"\n\nAnd what has it gotten you? A pair-programming partner who stopped pairing. A relative line number column you explain unprompted. A macro you recorded once, for a task you'll never repeat, that you still bring up at parties.\n\nYou say you never leave the home row. Buddy, you never leave the house either, but at least that one isn't a personality.\n\nMeanwhile Dave opened a file, changed a word with his mouse, and went to lunch."}, "b": {"item_id": "11a570c2e08d", "text": "Let's hear it for the Vim people. You can always spot one, because they'll tell you. Unprompted. Usually while you're mid-sentence about something else, like your divorce.\n\nThese folks spent three weekends tuning a .vimrc to save four seconds renaming a variable. That's a payback period of eleven thousand years, but sure, you're the efficient one.\n\nThey remapped Caps Lock to Escape and now can't type a password on anyone else's laptop. Their dotfiles repo has more stars than their actual projects. They say \"ciw\" out loud like it's a spell from a wizard who peaked in 1991.\n\nAnd what's the prize? You type :wq at the end of every Google Doc. You hit j in Slack and post the letter j to forty coworkers. Congratulations: you're the fastest typist in a room everyone already left."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0318", "lang": "en", "target_id": "microsoft-teams", "target_name": "Microsoft Teams", "facts": ["Launched in 2017, bundled at no extra charge into Office 365, and replaced Skype for Business.", "After Slack's antitrust complaint, Microsoft unbundled Teams from Office in the EU in 2023 and worldwide in 2024; the European Commission still sent a statement of objections in 2024.", "Microsoft reported 320 million monthly active users in 2023.", "Microsoft shut down consumer Skype in May 2025 and sent its users to Teams."], "a": {"item_id": "38ae80798890", "text": "To the people who made us use Teams: congratulations. You surveyed every tool on earth and picked the one already in the bundle. That's not a decision, that's a vending machine with a Microsoft logo. Some procurement guy saved $3.80 a seat, got a slide with an upward arrow, and the rest of us got to spend our lives asking \"can everyone see my screen?\"\n\nAnd the managers, God bless you. You don't love Teams for collaboration. You love the little green dot. It's a traffic light for your trust issues. You don't need a platform, you need a smoke detector for Kevin. Meanwhile Kevin owns a mouse jiggler and a dream.\n\nYou called it \"one place for everything.\" Yes. Chats, teams, channels, and the file you swear is in the other team. Your legacy is a workforce that opens every meeting with \"sorry, let me just rejoin.\""}, "b": {"item_id": "8ecdfe8fdf7c", "text": "Let's hear it for the IT director who chose Microsoft Teams, a man who has never once chosen anything. It came bundled with Outlook, like coleslaw you're contractually obligated to eat. The EU had to force Microsoft to stop shoving it on people, and your company still went back for seconds. The whole pitch was \"It's already in the license.\" So was Clippy, Gary.\n\nManagement loves it because now they can watch your little dot. Green means productive. Yellow means you went to the bathroom and Linda noticed. They didn't want collaboration. They wanted a heart monitor.\n\nAnd what did it get them? Every meeting opens with eight adults asking \"Can you see my screen?\" while a VP uses the raise-hand button to interrupt people *harder*. Congratulations: you saved nine dollars a seat and lost the will to live."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0319", "lang": "en", "target_id": "nfts", "target_name": "NFTs", "facts": ["Beeple's 'Everydays: The First 5000 Days' sold at Christie's for $69.3 million in March 2021.", "OpenSea, the largest NFT marketplace, processed a record $2.7 billion in trades on 1 May 2022 and just $9.34 million on 28 August 2022, a 99% drop, according to DappRadar data.", "A 2023 dappGambl study found that 95% of the NFT collections it examined had a market value of zero.", "The token usually records only a URL; the image itself sits on an ordinary server or IPFS, not on the blockchain.", "Collins Dictionary named 'NFT' its word of the year for 2021."], "a": {"item_id": "c0aeb5d8a3ed", "text": "Let's hear it for the 2021 NFT buyer. You paid four hundred dollars in gas fees, then made it your Twitter avatar so strangers could right-click save it for free. You joined a Discord with eleven thousand guys named \"Chad.eth,\" called them family, and stayed family right up until the floor price dropped and family started doxxing the dev. You said \"WAGMI.\" We did not all make it. You told your mom you were \"early.\" Early to what? The rug pull? You weren't buying art. You were buying the right to say \"few understand\" at parties, which was true. Nobody understood, especially you. Jimmy Fallon bought one too. When your financial strategy matches Jimmy Fallon's, that's not a community, that's a support group. The roadmap promised a metaverse. It delivered you, at Thanksgiving, still owing your cousin."}, "b": {"item_id": "ce82b5a76298", "text": "Nothing says \"I'm early\" like buying into something the same year Christie's did. You spent your tax refund on a cartoon ape, and now you check the floor price at red lights like a man waiting on biopsy results. You put \"ETH\" in your Twitter name and a hexagon on your profile picture, and you say \"gm\" to strangers at 4 p.m. because a guy named CryptoChad_Baby said that's how you make it.\n\nMake what? You paid $300 in gas fees to join a Discord with 40,000 people and one moderator, who keeps DMing to say you've won something. You did win something. It's called getting your wallet drained.\n\nThe dream was never art. The dream was selling to a guy who's hoping to sell to a guy. You're not an investor, you're a link in a chain letter with a Discord. And congratulations, you're the last link."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0320", "lang": "en", "target_id": "comic-sans", "target_name": "Comic Sans", "facts": ["Designed by Vincent Connare at Microsoft in 1994, modeled on the comic-book lettering in The Dark Knight Returns and Watchmen.", "Made for the speech bubbles of Rover, the cartoon dog in Microsoft Bob; it missed Bob's release and shipped instead in the Windows 95 Plus! pack.", "CERN's July 2012 Higgs boson discovery announcement was presented on slides set in it.", "The 'Ban Comic Sans' campaign, started by designers Dave and Holly Combs in 1999, ran until 2019, when they retired it and launched usecomicsans.com."], "a": {"item_id": "ea624ef43e94", "text": "Let's talk about the people who announce layoffs in Comic Sans. You're not a monster. You're Brenda from Facilities, and you want everyone to feel cozy about the asbestos removal. You took a typeface Microsoft designed for a cartoon dog's speech bubble and used it to type \"Your position has been eliminated.\" That's not softening the blow. That's a clown serving a subpoena. CERN announced the Higgs boson in it, and the physicists who found the God particle spent the week explaining the font. You think it says \"approachable.\" It says \"I scrolled right past Arial and Times New Roman and chose a kindergarten birthday invitation.\" Nobody reads your notice. They screenshot it. Congratulations: your fire evacuation memo is trending, and the building is still on fire."}, "b": {"item_id": "6867518453d2", "text": "You're the office manager who wrote \"Parking permits will now be enforced\" and thought, this needs to feel like a hug. You wanted approachable. You wanted everyone to know you're not a monster, you're Deb, you bring bundt cake. And it worked! Nobody's afraid of you. Nobody's afraid of the notice either.\n\nThe landlord who slides \"Rent is 30 days late\" under the door in Comic Sans isn't collecting rent. He's opening negotiations. The HR rep announcing layoffs in it isn't softening the blow, she's making a grown man feel like he got fired by the cartoon dog from Microsoft Bob. And the HOA president threatening fines over bin placement? Congratulations, you've made a tyrant look like a substitute teacher on movie day.\n\nYou wanted warmth and authority and picked the one choice that guarantees neither. CERN did it for the Higgs boson and still hasn't lived it down, and they had a particle going for them. You've got a fish-in-the-microwave memo."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0321", "lang": "en", "target_id": "almond-milk", "target_name": "Almond milk", "facts": ["California grows about 80% of the world's almonds.", "A single almond takes about 1.1 US gallons (roughly 4 litres) of water to grow, a figure Mother Jones published in 2014 that spread during California's drought.", "A 2015 US class action alleged Almond Breeze contained only about 2% almonds; it partly survived a motion to dismiss, was stayed in 2017 pending a separate Blue Diamond labelling settlement, and the plaintiffs then dropped it.", "Pollinating California's almond orchards each February requires trucking in most of the commercial honeybee colonies in the United States.", "Medieval European cooks used almond milk as a Lenten substitute for dairy, centuries before the current fashion."], "a": {"item_id": "ac57c4110eb9", "text": "So you switched to almond milk for the planet. Good for you. You're the person who announces \"I'm not a cow person\" like it's a personality. Meanwhile, almonds are one of the thirstiest crops on Earth, and you're pouring a gallon of water into every latte to feel like the only adult in the cafe who read the label. You tell everyone about the bees, though you've never met a bee. You say \"it's basically just almonds and water\" like that's a selling point. Your friends have stopped asking. Your barista has stopped pretending to care. And the almonds, somewhere in California, are wondering who you think you're saving."}, "b": {"item_id": "2f3ff40c74e4", "text": "You switched to almond milk to save the planet, which is a bold move for someone who read one headline about cows. Congratulations: you found the crop that needs about a gallon of water per almond and grows almost entirely in a California drought. You didn't solve the problem, you relocated it to Fresno, where you can't see it.\n\nAnd the bees. Orchards truck in most of the country's honeybees every spring, like a tour that never ends. You didn't go vegan, you went on a bee-logistics subscription.\n\nBut let's be honest, it was never about the planet. It was about the moment the barista asks \"dairy?\" and you say \"almond, please\" like a governor granting a pardon. You paid sixty cents extra for a halo with a surcharge.\n\nMeanwhile, your oat-milk friend is quietly doing it better, and nobody's clapping for them. That's what really bothers you."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0322", "lang": "en", "target_id": "google-sheets", "target_name": "Google Sheets", "facts": ["Launched in June 2006 as Google Spreadsheets, built on XL2Web, a product Google acquired from 2Web Technologies.", "A spreadsheet is capped at 10 million cells (raised from 5 million in 2022).", "Functions such as IMPORTHTML, IMPORTXML, and GOOGLEFINANCE pull live web data straight into cells.", "Free with a Google account; files live in Google Drive, and collaborators edit the same sheet simultaneously with coloured cursors.", "In a browser, offline editing works only in Chrome or Edge, with the Google Docs Offline extension installed and offline access switched on while still connected; the Android and iOS apps work offline natively."], "a": {"item_id": "a5780556db66", "text": "Let's talk about the brave souls who run their whole team out of one Google Sheet. Tab one says \"Tasks,\" tab nine says \"Tasks (FINAL),\" and tab twelve says \"Tasks (FINAL) copy 2.\" They color-code everything, then color-code the color codes. Their status dropdown has eleven options, and \"Done\" is still somehow \"In Progress.\" They believe a #REF! error is a personality trait. They lie awake wondering if the VLOOKUP in cell F212 will survive Brenda touching column D. They're not managing people. They're managing a spreadsheet that manages people, and they're so proud of it that they've locked the header row so nobody can ruin their life. Their dream is a pivot table. Their reality is a comment thread with forty-one replies that all say \"bump.\""}, "b": {"item_id": "2067d213f836", "text": "You're not running a team. You're running a tab called \"MASTER - DO NOT TOUCH,\" which Brad touched in 2021. You wanted a CRM, a project tracker, and an HR system, and you built all three out of IMPORTRANGE and spite. Every morning you watch an Anonymous Capybara scroll through payroll and just decide that's fine. Someone sorts column B without selecting column C, and suddenly your top performer is assigned to Linda's maternity leave. Your entire career is \"Last edit was 4 minutes ago.\" You don't do performance reviews, you do Version History. Your conditional formatting has more colors than your company has policies. Your Apps Script was written by an intern who now works at a bakery. Every time someone emails \"Request edit access,\" you feel like a king. And you are a king, ruling over 47 frozen rows and one #REF! that nobody can explain."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0323", "lang": "en", "target_id": "durian", "target_name": "Durian", "facts": ["Banned on Singapore's MRT trains and in many Southeast Asian hotels because of the smell.", "A 2017 genome study in Nature Genetics traced the odour to a duplicated family of MGL genes that pump out volatile sulfur compounds.", "China buys most of the world's durian exports, chiefly from Thailand, in a trade worth billions of dollars a year.", "Alfred Russel Wallace wrote in 1856 that eating it was 'a new sensation worth a voyage to the East to experience'."], "a": {"item_id": "0bf8c83200e0", "text": "You're not eating durian on the train because you love it. You love being the person who eats durian on the train. There's a sign right there, with a little picture, drawn by a government, specifically for you. You looked at it and thought, \"Not me, I'm cultured.\"\n\nYou went to Penang once. Now you say \"king of fruits\" like you were knighted alongside it. You tell coworkers \"you just have to get past the first bite,\" which is also what people say about marriages they're stuck in.\n\nAnd what did you win? A carriage to yourself. A lifetime ban from three hotel chains. A sixty-dollar fruit you're pretending to enjoy because a man on a plastic stool called you brave.\n\nSir, he says that to every tourist. He's selling by the kilo."}, "b": {"item_id": "94cc53dad1db", "text": "Look at him on the MRT, scooping durian with a plastic spoon like he's the only man in Singapore with a palate. Nobody asked for this. The carriage empties in a ring around him, and he takes it as respect. That's the whole transaction: the cleared seat, the sideways looks, proof he's braver than the rest of us. \"It's like custard,\" he says, eyes watering, because that's what happens when you're trying this hard. Then he posts the video captioned \"not for everyone.\" He's right. It's not for everyone. It's for him, and everyone within fifty metres just got a free sample of his ego."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0324", "lang": "en", "target_id": "juicero", "target_name": "Juicero", "facts": ["A Wi-Fi-connected cold-press juicer launched in 2016 at $699, later cut to $399, that squeezed proprietary bags of chopped produce sold by subscription.", "Raised about $120 million from investors including Google Ventures and Kleiner Perkins.", "In April 2017, Bloomberg reporters squeezed the packs by hand and got 7.5 ounces of juice in 90 seconds, faster than the machine's 8 ounces in about two minutes.", "Each pack carried a QR code that the press scanned and checked online before squeezing; it refused expired packs and would not run at all unless connected to Wi-Fi.", "Shut down in September 2017 and offered refunds on the machine."], "a": {"item_id": "a22f3d4810e1", "text": "You're the guy who read \"Bloomberg shows you can squeeze the pouch with your bare hands\" and thought, \"Sure, but can my hands connect to WiFi?\" You paid $400 to outsource the one step of juicemaking a toddler can do. You didn't want juice. You wanted to feel like a Series B. You wanted a dinner guest to ask, \"Is that the Juicero?\" so you could say, \"It's backed by Google Ventures,\" like that's a nutrient.\n\nAnd you paid seven dollars a pouch for pre-chopped kale with an expiration date, because you can't be trusted to smell things.\n\nWhen the company folded, you didn't lose a juicer. You lost your seat at the table where adults say \"disrupting breakfast\" with a straight face.\n\nNow you squeeze the pouch over a glass like a Capri Sun, doing the work your $400 was supposed to prevent. Cheers. Full circle."}, "b": {"item_id": "3f1bb8138434", "text": "Let's talk about the people who paid four hundred dollars for a Juicero. You know who you are. You wanted to feel like you had a private chef, so you bought a machine that needs Wi-Fi to check your bag. Then the internet discovered the bag was the real product, a pouch of chopped kale you could squeeze with your bare hands. Which you did, eventually, in front of guests, because nothing says \"tech visionary\" like hand-pressing a fruit packet while your friend holds a forty-dollar blender. You weren't buying juice. You were buying the feeling of being early to something, and the only thing you got early was the eBay listing."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0325", "lang": "en", "target_id": "pmma", "target_name": "PMMA (e-beam resist)", "facts": ["The same polymer as Plexiglas and acrylic glass, dissolved in anisole or chlorobenzene and spun into a thin film.", "The oldest electron-beam resist still in everyday use; IBM researchers reported it as one in the late 1960s.", "Positive tone: the beam breaks polymer chains and the exposed region dissolves in a MIBK:IPA developer.", "Resolves below 10 nm but has poor sensitivity, so doses of hundreds to over a thousand µC/cm² (depending on voltage) make writing slow.", "Poor dry-etch resistance, so it is used mostly for lift-off rather than as an etch mask."], "a": {"item_id": "fe949f7eb86d", "text": "Oh, you're still on PMMA. Let me guess: your advisor's thesis used it, your advisor's advisor used it, and somewhere on the group drive is a recipe called \"DO NOT CHANGE,\" last edited by a postdoc who now sells insurance in Ohio.\n\nYou call it \"reliable.\" You've run the same dose test eleven times because you refuse to admit the variable is you. Your big optimization this year was going from 950 A4 to 950 A3 and calling it a paper.\n\nYou queue a pattern Friday night, check on it Sunday, and tell people the beam is \"just taking its time.\" Then you say you don't trust anything without forty years of citations, which is also how you feel about your dentist and your Windows XP tool PC.\n\nWhat does it get you? Gorgeous 10-nm lines, a lift-off you pray over, and no social life. The chip is perfect. Your weekend is exposed and overdeveloped."}, "b": {"item_id": "a46a8573ce80", "text": "You people still spinning PMMA aren't doing lithography. You're doing historical reenactment. Your advisor's advisor scribbled the recipe on a napkin in 1975, and you treat it like scripture: 950K over 495K, MIBK to IPA one-to-three, develop sixty seconds while whispering to the beaker. You tell everyone it's about \"resolution.\" It's about nobody wanting to file the purchase order for ZEP. So you crank the dose until writing one chip takes longer than your stipend lasts, then discover your pattern won't survive ten seconds in the etcher. So you do liftoff, because of course you do. Your entire thesis is gold rectangles. You're not a nanofabrication researcher. You're the guy who knows which bottle in the cleanroom is \"the good IPA\" and guards it like a dragon."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0326", "lang": "en", "target_id": "risk", "target_name": "Risk", "facts": ["Invented in 1957 by Albert Lamorisse, the French director of The Red Balloon, as La Conquête du Monde; Parker Brothers published it in the United States in 1959.", "The map has 42 territories, among them Kamchatka, Irkutsk, and Yakutsk, while the entire Middle East is one territory.", "Holding Australia, which has a single entry point, is the standard opening strategy.", "A full game can run for hours, and players eliminated early have nothing to do but watch."], "a": {"item_id": "d3ed8f3ba762", "text": "Nobody plays Risk to the end. You're the guy who got knocked out at nine o'clock and has been sitting there with a lukewarm Coke, watching you. You're the one who drew Australia, held it for three hours, and now you give speeches about continents like it's the UN. Your whole personality is three blue armies sitting on Kamchatka. You negotiated a truce with your brother-in-law, broke it with a six-three dice roll, and called it strategy. You don't want the world. You want your name on the board at the end, defending a little plastic pile of soldiers until somebody's mom calls everyone to dinner. And the worst part? You'll win a game where the dice decided everything, and you'll still say, \"I was playing the long game.\""}, "b": {"item_id": "ee9cd4f7db06", "text": "Let's talk about the people who play Risk to the end. Not the sane ones who flip the board at hour three. The finishers. You said \"quick game\" at noon. It's now eleven p.m. and you're attacking Kamchatka for the ninth time because Greg promised you peace in Europe. Greg has never kept a promise. Greg borrowed your charger in 2014.\n\nYou call it strategy. It's not. It's grudges with dice. One guy has sat in Australia since lunch, smug as a landlord, with four armies and a worldview. Another keeps announcing \"I'm trading in cards!\" like he's closing on a house. Meanwhile someone's girlfriend, who only came for the snacks, is quietly winning, and nobody will say it out loud.\n\nAnd the prize? Total domination of a card table. Congratulations. You conquered the world and lost the only seven people who would've come to your birthday."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0327", "lang": "en", "target_id": "juicero", "target_name": "Juicero", "facts": ["A Wi-Fi-connected cold-press juicer launched in 2016 at $699, later cut to $399, that squeezed proprietary bags of chopped produce sold by subscription.", "Raised about $120 million from investors including Google Ventures and Kleiner Perkins.", "In April 2017, Bloomberg reporters squeezed the packs by hand and got 7.5 ounces of juice in 90 seconds, faster than the machine's 8 ounces in about two minutes.", "Each pack carried a QR code that the press scanned and checked online before squeezing; it refused expired packs and would not run at all unless connected to Wi-Fi.", "Shut down in September 2017 and offered refunds on the machine."], "a": {"item_id": "596abf5c014a", "text": "You paid four hundred dollars so a startup could check your juice's expiration date over Wi-Fi. That's not wellness. That's asking a server for permission to have breakfast. These are people who looked at a bag of chopped kale and thought, \"This needs a subscription.\" People who wanted to be someone who juices without ever touching a fruit. Then Bloomberg showed the whole world you could squeeze the pack with your bare hands. Faster. For free. Your grandmother has been doing that with a lemon since 1952. You didn't buy a juicer; you bought the privilege of paying seven dollars a pouch to a company Google Ventures funded and still couldn't keep alive past 2017. Congratulations: you're the only people who ever got a refund from Silicon Valley and still walked away looking poorer."}, "b": {"item_id": "3f1bb8138434", "text": "Let's talk about the people who paid four hundred dollars for a Juicero. You know who you are. You wanted to feel like you had a private chef, so you bought a machine that needs Wi-Fi to check your bag. Then the internet discovered the bag was the real product, a pouch of chopped kale you could squeeze with your bare hands. Which you did, eventually, in front of guests, because nothing says \"tech visionary\" like hand-pressing a fruit packet while your friend holds a forty-dollar blender. You weren't buying juice. You were buying the feeling of being early to something, and the only thing you got early was the eBay listing."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0328", "lang": "en", "target_id": "durian", "target_name": "Durian", "facts": ["Banned on Singapore's MRT trains and in many Southeast Asian hotels because of the smell.", "A 2017 genome study in Nature Genetics traced the odour to a duplicated family of MGL genes that pump out volatile sulfur compounds.", "China buys most of the world's durian exports, chiefly from Thailand, in a trade worth billions of dollars a year.", "Alfred Russel Wallace wrote in 1856 that eating it was 'a new sensation worth a voyage to the East to experience'."], "a": {"item_id": "5e02113e1bb8", "text": "You know who eats durian on the train? A man who has never once been told no. He's not eating fruit. He's eating a personality. He reads the NO DURIAN sign like it's a personal challenge, then cracks that spiky husk with the confidence of someone who watched one video. Then he looks up at the carriage and says, \"Honestly? Tastes like custard.\" Sir, nobody asked for a dessert review. You're not tasting custard. You're tasting attention. Every bite is a little flag planted in the aisle. And the worst part? Ten stops later he's still sniffing his fingers, grinning, like the smell is a medal he earned."}, "b": {"item_id": "e97b85a188ed", "text": "Let's talk about the guy who eats durian on the train. Sir, Singapore, a country that will cane you for chewing gum, saw you coming and put your snack on a sign right next to \"no flammables.\" You're not hungry. You're running a loyalty test. Every face that twists is another person who \"just isn't adventurous.\" You paid sixty dollars for Musang King so you could explain Musang King to a stranger who's holding her breath. \"It's like custard, it's heaven, you just have to get past the-\" No. We don't. You brought a hotel-banned substance to a commute and called it a personality. And the best part? You'll be the only one who forgets you ate it. Your coworkers won't. Your hotel won't. Your Uber driver has already filed a one-star review with God."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0329", "lang": "en", "target_id": "esperanto", "target_name": "Esperanto", "facts": ["Published in Warsaw in 1887 by L. L. Zamenhof, a Białystok-born eye doctor, under the pen name Doktoro Esperanto, 'one who hopes'.", "Speaker estimates range from 100,000 to two million; roughly a thousand people grow up with it as a native language.", "William Shatner starred in Incubus (1966), a horror film shot entirely in Esperanto, with pronunciation Esperantists still mock.", "Its Wikipedia has over 350,000 articles, and its Duolingo course for English speakers had 1.36 million learners by July 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "3c7c5dbcf1f4", "text": "You know the Esperanto people. They wear the green star on a lanyard and say it's \"so logical\" like they personally designed it. Ask what they're after and they'll say world peace, then admit they've been stuck on the accusative *-n* since 2016. Their plan was that one day they'd meet a Hungarian, a Brazilian, and a Japanese guy at a bus stop and all speak the same language. What they actually get is a pen pal in Lyon who stopped writing in 2019 and a Discord server with four hundred members and one active voice. They're not learning a language. They're waiting for a train Zamenhof promised in 1887, and it has never once left the station."}, "b": {"item_id": "f86448df0967", "text": "You learned a language built to unite humanity, and the only humanity you've united with is a guy named Dieter on a forum from 2004. You wanted world peace; you got a Duolingo streak and a green star pin you're afraid to wear to Target. Zamenhof dreamed of one tongue for all nations, and \"all nations\" turned out to be eleven retirees at a congress in Slovakia. You picked it because it's \"easy,\" which is what people say about the gym membership they never use. You've mastered the letter ĉ, a skill that has never come up in a job interview. Your best conversation partner is a stranger who says \"Saluton!\" and then switches to English out of mercy. Spanish would have gotten you a girlfriend. Esperanto got you a mailing list, a sticker on your laptop, and a very strong opinion about the accusative."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0330", "lang": "en", "target_id": "ugg", "target_name": "UGG boots", "facts": ["Sheepskin boots Australian surfers wore to warm up after sessions; Brian Smith started selling them in California in 1978.", "Deckers Outdoor bought the brand in 1995 and holds 'UGG' as a trademark in the US and elsewhere; in Australia, 'ugg' is a generic word any bootmaker may use.", "In 2019, a US jury found an Australian maker liable for selling boots labelled 'ugg' into the United States.", "Oprah Winfrey featured them in her 'Favorite Things' lists repeatedly in the 2000s, which drove the first boom.", "UGG is Deckers' largest brand, with over $2 billion in annual sales."], "a": {"item_id": "9b58586d4e25", "text": "Oh, you're wearing Uggs in August. I saw you at the farmers market, sweating through your sheepskin like it's a personal achievement. You're not cold. You've decided that looking cozy is a personality, and you're hoping people will see your ankles and think, \"She has her life together.\" Ma'am, it's ninety-one degrees. You're not effortless. You're just wet. Your feet have been in a sauna since Memorial Day, and you still walk into Trader Joe's like you're headed to a cabin in Vermont with a fireplace and a man named Bryce. You're not going to Vermont. You're going to the Target parking lot and back, with a cold brew in one hand and a sweat stain shaped like Australia."}, "b": {"item_id": "3e67204b78e6", "text": "If you wear UGGs in July, I admire the commitment. You've decided being cozy is a personality and weather is just a rumor. You'll tell me they \"regulate temperature,\" a line you memorized like a hostage reading a statement. It's 94 degrees, you're in running shorts, and your feet are poaching in their own broth.\n\nWhat are you after? You want to look like you just rolled out of bed, for $180. Effortless, with a receipt. You want it to be fall at all times, even in Phoenix, even at a pool party.\n\nAnd what does it get you? A smell that enters the room two seconds before you do. Socks you can't wear because \"the inside is the whole point.\" And the loyalty of a 2007 version of you who never left the Juicy tracksuit.\n\nCongratulations. You're the only person standing on a sidewalk you could fry an egg on, saying, \"My feet get cold.\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0331", "lang": "en", "target_id": "stinky-tofu", "target_name": "Stinky tofu (臭豆腐)", "facts": ["Tofu soaked for hours to a day or two in a brine that has itself fermented for months from vegetables such as bamboo shoots and mustard greens, often with dried shrimp or meat; the smell is the brine's, not the bean's.", "Changsha's version is black, deep-fried and doused in chili; Taiwan's night-market version is golden and served with pickled cabbage; Shaoxing's, brined with amaranth stalks, is fried pale yellow or steamed with other 'stinky' pickles.", "Legend credits Wang Zhihe, a Qing-era exam candidate who left his unsold tofu too long in 1669; a Beijing fermented-bean-curd brand still carries his name.", "Street vendors are its main outlet, and the smell carries for a block."], "a": {"item_id": "69fa8443e946", "text": "Look at the crowd at this night market stall. Everyone here is eating stinky tofu for the same reason: so they can tell people about it later. The tourist holds the skewer out like it's a live grenade, films himself, then says \"honestly, not bad\" with the face of a man being waterboarded. The couple on a date are splitting one piece, which is romantic only if you're both willing to be the one who gets dumped first. And the teenager who has never once been hungry just needs a story where he was brave enough to chew. You're not here for the snack. You're here for twenty minutes of street cred and three days of your hair smelling like a drain. Enjoy the MRT ride back. Everyone on it already knows where you've been."}, "b": {"item_id": "49bb8f6483c1", "text": "You're not eating stinky tofu, you're auditioning. Every tourist at the night market stands there with a toothpick and a paper tray, filming their own face for the \"I tried it!\" video, which is the only reason you ordered. Nobody craves this. You wanted a story, and now you've got one and a ten-minute ride on the MRT.\n\nAnd the locals are worse. \"You just have to get past the first bite,\" you say, the way people talk about marathons and their ex. You've turned a snack into a personality. You'll argue Taipei versus Changsha like it's a custody hearing.\n\nThen you walk home trailing a cloud like a divorced cologne ad, wondering why nobody sits next to you. You wanted to be adventurous. You got to be alone on a bus."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0332", "lang": "en", "target_id": "graphene", "target_name": "Graphene", "facts": ["Isolated in 2004 by Andre Geim and Konstantin Novoselov at Manchester by peeling graphite with Scotch tape; Nobel Prize in Physics, 2010.", "The EU's Graphene Flagship, launched in 2013, committed €1 billion over ten years.", "A 2018 study in Advanced Materials tested 'graphene' from 60 producers and found none with more than 50% graphene content; most was graphite flakes.", "Sold in tennis rackets, running shoes, and headphones as a performance ingredient.", "Geim also won an Ig Nobel Prize in 2000 for levitating a frog with magnets."], "a": {"item_id": "683abdc9f22c", "text": "Let's hear it for the graphene evangelists, the only people who've been \"five years away\" since 2004. Two guys in Manchester peeled pencil lead with Scotch tape, won a Nobel, and you looked at that and thought: finally, a career plan. Every grant proposal you write has the same three slides: space elevator, seawater you can drink, a phone that charges in six seconds. The funding agency nods like it's never seen them before, because it's seen them so many times it's slipped into a coma. Twenty years of press releases, and what have you actually shipped? A tennis racket. Headphones with \"GRAPHENE\" printed bigger than the brand name. You love telling people it's 200 times stronger than steel. Sure. Your claims, meanwhile, are one atom thick. You didn't discover a wonder material. You discovered wonder funding."}, "b": {"item_id": "0a60dd7d6c08", "text": "Every graphene founder has the same pitch deck. Slide one: \"A Nobel Prize in 2010.\" Slide two: \"Five years from batteries.\" Slide three: a photo of a tennis racket, because nobody on the investor side has ever checked. Their LinkedIn says \"materials visionary.\" Their grant renewals say \"transformative.\" Their spin-out's name ends in \"-ene,\" and the logo looks like a hexagon that had a hopeful dream. They've been five years from the breakthrough since the Obama administration, and they bring up the Nobel at weddings. Meanwhile a postdoc is squinting at a sample under a microscope at 2 a.m., and the CEO is in Davos saying it will change everything. Everything has not changed. The postdoc has."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0333", "lang": "en", "target_id": "crocs", "target_name": "Crocs", "facts": ["Founded in Boulder, Colorado, in 2002; the first clog debuted at the Fort Lauderdale boat show as a boating shoe.", "Made of 'Croslite', which Crocs describes as a proprietary closed-cell resin foam and which third parties identify as an injection-moulded EVA foam.", "Balenciaga sold a platform Crocs for about $850 in 2018.", "The stock fell more than 90% from its 2007 peak during the 2008 crisis; the company then boomed in the pandemic and gave away 860,000 pairs to healthcare workers in 2020.", "The holes are officially 'ventilation ports' and the charms that go in them are 'Jibbitz', a company Crocs bought in 2006."], "a": {"item_id": "d220d23f1394", "text": "You wear Crocs out of the house because you've decided comfort is a personality, and the whole Whole Foods needs to know. You didn't give up on fashion, you held a press conference about giving up. \"I don't care what people think,\" you tell a stranger in the cereal aisle, unprompted, which is caring what people think with extra steps.\n\nYou flip the heel strap forward for \"sport mode,\" as if you're about to sprint somewhere other than the Target parking lot. You say \"nurses wear them,\" and you're an accountant named Dale. You bought your kid a matching pair so you're not the only one, which is less parenting than hostage-taking.\n\nWhat it gets you: a stranger's sympathetic nod, a lifelong inability to be taken seriously at a wedding, and the smug glow of winning an argument nobody was having."}, "b": {"item_id": "62e0599c4f11", "text": "People who wear Crocs out of the house have made peace with something the rest of us are still fighting. You didn't give up; you filed the paperwork. Every Croc wearer says the same thing: \"They're so comfortable.\" Yeah, so is a weighted blanket, Brenda, but you don't wear it to Olive Garden. And the Jibbitz! You've turned your feet into a personality quiz. A tiny taco, a tiny Minion, a tiny \"Live Laugh Love.\" You're forty-three and your shoe has a sticker collection. Then there's the guy who flips the strap back into \"sport mode\" like he's about to chase a pickpocket, when we both know he's never run anywhere except toward a sample tray at Costco. Nurses and chefs wear them because they're on their feet for twelve hours. You wear them to stand in line at Starbucks. Different heroism."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0334", "lang": "en", "target_id": "graphene", "target_name": "Graphene", "facts": ["Isolated in 2004 by Andre Geim and Konstantin Novoselov at Manchester by peeling graphite with Scotch tape; Nobel Prize in Physics, 2010.", "The EU's Graphene Flagship, launched in 2013, committed €1 billion over ten years.", "A 2018 study in Advanced Materials tested 'graphene' from 60 producers and found none with more than 50% graphene content; most was graphite flakes.", "Sold in tennis rackets, running shoes, and headphones as a performance ingredient.", "Geim also won an Ig Nobel Prize in 2000 for levitating a frog with magnets."], "a": {"item_id": "886e9fa21662", "text": "Twenty years you've been telling us graphene will change everything, and the only thing it's changed is your grant renewal status. You won a Nobel Prize with Scotch tape and a pencil, and somehow concluded the hard part was over. Since then you've pitched it for batteries, bulletproof vests, desalination, and condoms, plus a paint, which actually exists, so naturally nobody's excited. The EU gave you a billion euros, and you came back with a roadmap. Every press release starts with \"could,\" because \"does\" would require a customer. Your startup's deck says \"disruptive.\" Your LinkedIn says \"thought leader.\" Your lab says \"please send more funding.\" You've been two years from commercialization since Myspace. Keep going, though. Somewhere in Manchester, a man in a lanyard still gasps when you say \"one atom thick.\" That gasp is the product."}, "b": {"item_id": "683abdc9f22c", "text": "Let's hear it for the graphene evangelists, the only people who've been \"five years away\" since 2004. Two guys in Manchester peeled pencil lead with Scotch tape, won a Nobel, and you looked at that and thought: finally, a career plan. Every grant proposal you write has the same three slides: space elevator, seawater you can drink, a phone that charges in six seconds. The funding agency nods like it's never seen them before, because it's seen them so many times it's slipped into a coma. Twenty years of press releases, and what have you actually shipped? A tennis racket. Headphones with \"GRAPHENE\" printed bigger than the brand name. You love telling people it's 200 times stronger than steel. Sure. Your claims, meanwhile, are one atom thick. You didn't discover a wonder material. You discovered wonder funding."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0335", "lang": "en", "target_id": "emacs", "target_name": "Emacs", "facts": ["Began in 1976 as a set of macros for the TECO editor at MIT's AI Lab, written by Richard Stallman and others; GNU Emacs followed in 1985.", "Extensible in its own Lisp dialect, Emacs Lisp; the standing joke is that it is a fine operating system lacking only a good editor.", "Ships with a psychotherapist (M-x doctor), Tetris, a mail client, a calendar, and a web browser (eww).", "An old backronym is 'Eight Megabytes And Constantly Swapping', from when 8 MB was a lot of memory.", "'Emacs pinky' is the name for the hand strain blamed on its Control-key chords."], "a": {"item_id": "59215bf985f1", "text": "You people don't use Emacs, you move into it, like a guy who rents a storage unit and starts getting mail there. You read email in mu4e, browse in eww, run a shell in eshell, and when you finally crack, you M-x doctor and tell a Lisp therapist your feelings. Your init.el is four thousand lines, half commented out, and you've declared Emacs bankruptcy twice: the only bankruptcy that ever made someone feel superior. You spent a weekend cutting startup from 1.8 seconds to 0.9, on a program you never close. Your left pinky is shot from holding Control, a war wound from never touching a mouse. Your whole life is in Org-mode, including a habit tracker whose only entry is \"configure habit tracker.\" And that novel you built the perfect writing environment for? It's in there too. Under TODO."}, "b": {"item_id": "78aa8d199f40", "text": "You've lived in Emacs for eleven years and written four thousand lines of config and zero lines of the novel your Org-mode agenda says you're \"drafting.\" You don't edit text, you tend an init.el that collapses every time a package updates. You read email in it, browse Reddit in it, and tell people at parties you \"just haven't needed to leave.\" Nobody asked where you've been.\n\nYour left pinky is a war veteran. You remapped Caps Lock to Control, and your therapist to M-x doctor. You installed evil-mode so you could escape Emacs keybindings, then defended Emacs keybindings in the comments. You spent a Saturday writing Lisp to automate a ten-second task, break-even in 2031, then published a blog post about it for the four other people who have the same blog post.\n\nYour whole personality is a tool that does everything except make you look up from it. Stallman would be proud. That's the problem."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0336", "lang": "en", "target_id": "emacs", "target_name": "Emacs", "facts": ["Began in 1976 as a set of macros for the TECO editor at MIT's AI Lab, written by Richard Stallman and others; GNU Emacs followed in 1985.", "Extensible in its own Lisp dialect, Emacs Lisp; the standing joke is that it is a fine operating system lacking only a good editor.", "Ships with a psychotherapist (M-x doctor), Tetris, a mail client, a calendar, and a web browser (eww).", "An old backronym is 'Eight Megabytes And Constantly Swapping', from when 8 MB was a lot of memory.", "'Emacs pinky' is the name for the hand strain blamed on its Control-key chords."], "a": {"item_id": "e61d80ea2a75", "text": "Let's talk about the people who don't use Emacs, they live in it. They read their email in there, check their calendar, play chess, and have never once figured out how to leave. Their pinkies are permanently stretched across Ctrl like they're holding a grudge. They spent three weekends configuring a text editor so they could avoid one afternoon of real work. Ask what they're building and they'll say \"my init file,\" proudly, like it's a novel. It's four thousand lines long. Nobody has read it, including them. They'll tell you it's an operating system. Their mother still calls it a text editor, and she's the only one in the family who's been honest with them."}, "b": {"item_id": "78fe88a6a79d", "text": "You people don't use a text editor. You adopted a pet that you have to feed Lisp.\n\nYou open Emacs to write one email, and three weeks later you've written a mail client, but you still haven't sent the email. Your init file is 4,000 lines long, and you understand maybe nine of them. The rest is a Stack Overflow answer from 2011 that you're afraid to remove.\n\nYou've got an Org-mode agenda so elaborate it has its own org chart. It tracks every task you'll never do, in beautiful nested bullets. You don't have a to-do list. You have a to-do *liturgy*.\n\nAnd the pinky! Thirty years of Control and your left hand looks like a lobster claw. You call it \"ergonomic.\" Your chiropractor calls it \"a recurring client.\"\n\nYou say it's an operating system. Sir, it's a hobby with a splash screen."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0337", "lang": "en", "target_id": "almond-milk", "target_name": "Almond milk", "facts": ["California grows about 80% of the world's almonds.", "A single almond takes about 1.1 US gallons (roughly 4 litres) of water to grow, a figure Mother Jones published in 2014 that spread during California's drought.", "A 2015 US class action alleged Almond Breeze contained only about 2% almonds; it partly survived a motion to dismiss, was stayed in 2017 pending a separate Blue Diamond labelling settlement, and the plaintiffs then dropped it.", "Pollinating California's almond orchards each February requires trucking in most of the commercial honeybee colonies in the United States.", "Medieval European cooks used almond milk as a Lenten substitute for dairy, centuries before the current fashion."], "a": {"item_id": "ac57c4110eb9", "text": "So you switched to almond milk for the planet. Good for you. You're the person who announces \"I'm not a cow person\" like it's a personality. Meanwhile, almonds are one of the thirstiest crops on Earth, and you're pouring a gallon of water into every latte to feel like the only adult in the cafe who read the label. You tell everyone about the bees, though you've never met a bee. You say \"it's basically just almonds and water\" like that's a selling point. Your friends have stopped asking. Your barista has stopped pretending to care. And the almonds, somewhere in California, are wondering who you think you're saving."}, "b": {"item_id": "1485255f2490", "text": "Let's hear it for the people who switched to almond milk to save the planet. You watched one documentary about cow farts and panicked straight into the arms of a nut that needs a gallon of water apiece. Congratulations: you fled methane and landed on drought. California is cracking like a crème brûlée, and you're out here draining a Central Valley aquifer because you can't make eye contact with a Holstein. And the bees! Billions of them trucked across the country on semis, dying by the hive, so you can tell a coworker you \"don't really do dairy.\" That's not environmentalism. That's relocating the crime scene. You didn't save the Earth. You just moved the guilt somewhere sunnier, slapped a leaf on it, and paid an eighty-cent upcharge for the privilege of feeling better about it."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0338", "lang": "en", "target_id": "vision-pro", "target_name": "Apple Vision Pro", "facts": ["Released in February 2024 at $3,499, with an external battery pack on a cable rated for about two hours.", "'EyeSight' shows a rendering of the wearer's eyes on an outward-facing screen.", "Apple calls it 'spatial computing' and its App Store rules tell developers not to describe apps as AR, VR, XR, or MR.", "Reports during 2024 said Apple had cut production; analysts' first-year sales estimates were well under a million units.", "Weighs 600-650 g, before the battery."], "a": {"item_id": "90ecfc190a47", "text": "You paid thirty-five hundred dollars to ride the 6 train inside Joshua Tree. Everyone else has to smell the actual 6 train; you just hear it echoing through a desert like a ghost with a MetroCard. You want us to know you're an early adopter. We know. Your little cartoon eyes are blinking at us like a hostage video. That cable running to your pocket says \"the future\" and also \"IV drip.\" You pinch the air at Union Square and a stranger thinks you're ordering him to move. You've got a hundred-foot Mac screen floating over a man eating soup out of a Pringles can. And the battery dies in two hours, so you'll still get to experience the subway in full, unimmersive 3D. Your commute outlasts your device. Welcome back to reality. It's delayed."}, "b": {"item_id": "bb952c93608d", "text": "Nobody wears a Vision Pro on the subway to watch a movie. They're wearing it so forty strangers know they paid three thousand five hundred dollars to be the least present person in Brooklyn. They say \"spatial computing\" like it's a personality. They pinch the air to open windows that exist only for them, and their eyes glow at us through the front like a cult member's. That's the most honest thing about them: they're staring at something that isn't here. Meanwhile the rest of us watch a man at the 4 train's Atlantic Avenue stop move a floating spreadsheet with his fingers while his actual laptop sits in his bag, unused, feeling betrayed."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0339", "lang": "en", "target_id": "comic-sans", "target_name": "Comic Sans", "facts": ["Designed by Vincent Connare at Microsoft in 1994, modeled on the comic-book lettering in The Dark Knight Returns and Watchmen.", "Made for the speech bubbles of Rover, the cartoon dog in Microsoft Bob; it missed Bob's release and shipped instead in the Windows 95 Plus! pack.", "CERN's July 2012 Higgs boson discovery announcement was presented on slides set in it.", "The 'Ban Comic Sans' campaign, started by designers Dave and Holly Combs in 1999, ran until 2019, when they retired it and launched usecomicsans.com."], "a": {"item_id": "886470815955", "text": "You scrolled past every font on that dropdown menu, past the ones built for hospitals, courts, and airports, and picked the one designed for a cartoon dog's speech bubbles. Congratulations. Your notice about the mandatory layoffs is now technically being narrated by Rover from Microsoft Bob.\n\nI know what you were after. You wanted to seem approachable. You're the office manager who says \"no worries!\" while ruining someone's Friday. You wanted the boil-water advisory to feel like a hug. Instead, your tenants read it in the voice of a birthday clown and assumed the water was fine and the clown was just excited.\n\nHere's what it got you: a screenshot on Reddit, a group chat full of strangers, and a legal department that now reads your memos aloud to each other for fun.\n\nThe pet had a better career than the font you chose to speak for it."}, "b": {"item_id": "4b3978a4ee09", "text": "Somebody in this building printed \"URGENT: FIRE DRILL MANDATORY\" in Comic Sans and taped it to the break room fridge. I want that person to know we all saw it and we all laughed, though not the way they hoped. They wanted the fire marshal's authority. What they got was a note that looks like a kindergarten teacher wrote it right after finding out about the fire. They want to be taken seriously while seeming approachable, like wearing a tuxedo with light-up sneakers. They think the wobbly letters say \"we're a team.\" Nobody reads it that way. We read it as a birthday party for the fire drill, with cake in the parking lot. Next time, pick a plain font and own being in charge. Nobody has ever evacuated a building faster because the notice looked cheerful."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0340", "lang": "en", "target_id": "risk", "target_name": "Risk", "facts": ["Invented in 1957 by Albert Lamorisse, the French director of The Red Balloon, as La Conquête du Monde; Parker Brothers published it in the United States in 1959.", "The map has 42 territories, among them Kamchatka, Irkutsk, and Yakutsk, while the entire Middle East is one territory.", "Holding Australia, which has a single entry point, is the standard opening strategy.", "A full game can run for hours, and players eliminated early have nothing to do but watch."], "a": {"item_id": "831ac90358ed", "text": "You people who play Risk to the end. Not \"until it gets late.\" To the *end*. Six hours in, somebody's uncle has fortified Australia like a doomsday bunker, and he'll still be sitting there at 2 a.m., one way in, one way out, guarding a war that ended at dinner. You're not after victory. You're after that one moment when you cash in three cards for fifteen armies and dump them all into Irkutsk like Irkutsk owes you money. And what does finishing get you? Total world domination, achieved by rolling three dice against two for forty straight minutes while your girlfriend reads a book in the next room. You broke a sacred peace treaty with your own brother over Kamchatka. Congratulations. You conquered the entire planet. Now nobody in it will drive you home."}, "b": {"item_id": "ee9cd4f7db06", "text": "Let's talk about the people who play Risk to the end. Not the sane ones who flip the board at hour three. The finishers. You said \"quick game\" at noon. It's now eleven p.m. and you're attacking Kamchatka for the ninth time because Greg promised you peace in Europe. Greg has never kept a promise. Greg borrowed your charger in 2014.\n\nYou call it strategy. It's not. It's grudges with dice. One guy has sat in Australia since lunch, smug as a landlord, with four armies and a worldview. Another keeps announcing \"I'm trading in cards!\" like he's closing on a house. Meanwhile someone's girlfriend, who only came for the snacks, is quietly winning, and nobody will say it out loud.\n\nAnd the prize? Total domination of a card table. Congratulations. You conquered the world and lost the only seven people who would've come to your birthday."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0341", "lang": "en", "target_id": "vim", "target_name": "Vim", "facts": ["Released by Bram Moolenaar in November 1991 as 'Vi IMitation', a vi clone he had begun writing for the Amiga in 1988 on top of Stevie; the expansion became 'Vi IMproved' with version 2.0 in December 1993.", "Licensed as charityware: the licence asks users to donate to ICCF Holland, a charity Moolenaar ran for children in Uganda.", "Stack Overflow's question 'How to exit the Vim editor?' passed one million visits in May 2017, which Stack Overflow marked with a blog post.", "Modal editing: in normal mode, typing letters runs commands rather than inserting text; :wq saves and quits, :q! quits and discards changes."], "a": {"item_id": "fc4027ce9a34", "text": "You told the new intern you use Vim before you told him your name. He just wanted to know where the printer was.\n\nYou say you never leave the home row. Correct. You never leave the house either.\n\nYou spent forty hours on your .vimrc to save eleven seconds a day, so you break even in 2031, assuming you don't switch to Neovim, which you will, and then announce it like a religious conversion.\n\nYou're not after speed. You're after the moment someone glances at your screen and asks, \"How do you even quit that?\" so you can say \"Oh, it's simple\" and type something that looks like a ransom note: `:wqa!`\n\nWhat does it get you? A macro that renames forty variables, a pinky built like a bodybuilder's, and one guy at a meetup who nods at you with exactly your kind of loneliness."}, "b": {"item_id": "6eb19b0ff2a4", "text": "You know the type. You meet him at a dinner party, and within four minutes he's explaining that he hasn't touched a mouse since 2014. He doesn't edit text. He performs it. Every keystroke is a tiny sermon, and he says \"normal mode\" like it's a personality. He wants you to ask how he got so fast so he can tell you it's because he's been hitting Escape since the Clinton administration. His .vimrc is longer than his lease, he's got forty plugins, and he won't name his colorscheme because you'd only get it wrong. Ask him how to quit and he goes quiet, then smiles like he's waited his whole life for this. He'll teach you `:wq` for an hour. Ask him anything else, and he'll say, very calmly, that you should have read the docs."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0342", "lang": "en", "target_id": "klingon", "target_name": "Klingon", "facts": ["Created by linguist Marc Okrand for Star Trek III (1984), building on a few words the actor James Doohan improvised for the 1979 film.", "Deliberately alien grammar: object-verb-subject word order and no verb 'to be', so Hamlet's line became 'taH pagh taHbe'', 'continue or not continue'.", "The Klingon Language Institute (1992) published Hamlet 'in the original Klingon'; Bing Translator added Klingon in 2013 and Duolingo in 2018.", "Okrand's 1985 Klingon Dictionary listed roughly 1,500 words, and fluent speakers number only in the dozens: 20 to 30 in 2009, 30 to 50 by 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "64f4f6a7ba00", "text": "Congratulations on being fluent in Klingon, a language with maybe thirty fluent speakers, and you've met all of them at the same Marriott. You spent four years mastering a tongue where the standard greeting, nuqneH, means \"What do you want?\" That's also what your family says when you call. You've read Hamlet \"restored to the original Klingon,\" a phrase only a man who has never been hugged could type. One guy raised his son speaking only Klingon; by age five the kid stopped answering him. That's the dream: a language so pure even toddlers refuse it. There's no word for \"please,\" which explains your dating approach. You learned an entire warrior race's language to tell the barista Qapla'. Success. Sure, buddy. Today is a good day to die... a virgin."}, "b": {"item_id": "0cbf01775821", "text": "You spent years mastering a language whose standard greeting is \"What do you want?\" and honestly, that explains the social life. You wanted a warrior culture and got a prefix chart. You can render \"to be or not to be\" as *taH pagh taHbe'*, which literally means \"continue or don't continue,\" a fair question about the hobby.\n\nYour conversation partners are forty people at a convention who all dread your corrections. You command a vocabulary built for threatening enemies, and your only battle is a verb-suffix argument with a man named Greg, who is wrong, and who knows he is wrong, and who will not yield.\n\nYou say honor is everything, yet you've earned yours in a Marriott conference room, holding a foam bat'leth, forehead ridges peeling in the airport security line.\n\nToday is a good day to die? Sir, it's Tuesday. You have a dentist appointment."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0343", "lang": "en", "target_id": "comic-sans", "target_name": "Comic Sans", "facts": ["Designed by Vincent Connare at Microsoft in 1994, modeled on the comic-book lettering in The Dark Knight Returns and Watchmen.", "Made for the speech bubbles of Rover, the cartoon dog in Microsoft Bob; it missed Bob's release and shipped instead in the Windows 95 Plus! pack.", "CERN's July 2012 Higgs boson discovery announcement was presented on slides set in it.", "The 'Ban Comic Sans' campaign, started by designers Dave and Holly Combs in 1999, ran until 2019, when they retired it and launched usecomicsans.com."], "a": {"item_id": "85bb13923657", "text": "Nobody picks Comic Sans for a gas leak notice by accident. You wanted the building to evacuate without feeling like you were in charge of anything. \"EVACUATE BY 5PM\" felt too blunt, so you softened it into the font of a kindergarten worksheet and a lost-cat flyer. Now the fire marshal reads it like a bake sale reminder. You were going for approachable. What you got was a sign that people skim, shrug at, and treat as a suggestion. Your management is going to love the memo you're writing next, the one in Papyrus about why the sign was ignored."}, "b": {"item_id": "6867518453d2", "text": "You're the office manager who wrote \"Parking permits will now be enforced\" and thought, this needs to feel like a hug. You wanted approachable. You wanted everyone to know you're not a monster, you're Deb, you bring bundt cake. And it worked! Nobody's afraid of you. Nobody's afraid of the notice either.\n\nThe landlord who slides \"Rent is 30 days late\" under the door in Comic Sans isn't collecting rent. He's opening negotiations. The HR rep announcing layoffs in it isn't softening the blow, she's making a grown man feel like he got fired by the cartoon dog from Microsoft Bob. And the HOA president threatening fines over bin placement? Congratulations, you've made a tyrant look like a substitute teacher on movie day.\n\nYou wanted warmth and authority and picked the one choice that guarantees neither. CERN did it for the Higgs boson and still hasn't lived it down, and they had a particle going for them. You've got a fish-in-the-microwave memo."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0344", "lang": "en", "target_id": "windows-8", "target_name": "Windows 8", "facts": ["Released in October 2012; removed the Start button and booted into a full-screen grid of tiles designed for touch screens most PCs did not have.", "The 8.1 update in 2013 put a Start button back, Windows 10 (2015) restored the Start menu, and Microsoft skipped the name Windows 9 altogether.", "Microsoft dropped the 'Metro' name in August 2012 after reports that German retailer Metro AG had objected on trademark grounds; Microsoft called 'Metro' a code name and denied any litigation, and the style was afterwards known informally as 'Modern UI'.", "The 'Charms bar' hid Search, Share, and Settings behind an invisible swipe from the right edge.", "Windows RT, the ARM edition launched alongside it, could not run ordinary Windows programs; Microsoft wrote down $900 million of unsold Surface RT tablets in 2013."], "a": {"item_id": "4880b71f9444", "text": "You put Windows 8 on a desktop. A tower. Under a desk. With a mouse. Nobody in your house has ever touched that monitor except to wipe it, yet there you are, dragging your cursor into the top-right corner like a man feeling for a light switch in a stranger's house.\n\nYou wanted to be early to the future. Instead you got a full-screen weather report you never requested and a ten-minute hunt for the shutdown button, which you finally found by Googling it on your phone, like someone searching for the exit in a building they own.\n\nThen you downloaded a third-party Start menu, so now you've spent a weekend undoing the upgrade you chose on purpose.\n\nAnd when your friends ask how it is, you say, \"It's fine once you get used to it.\" That's also what hostages say."}, "b": {"item_id": "f8677486c44b", "text": "You put Windows 8 on a desktop. A tower. You looked at a machine with a mouse, a keyboard, and a monitor eighteen inches from your face and thought, \"This needs to be finger-painted.\" Who are you? You're the guy who bought a Zune and still calls it \"ahead of its time.\" You wanted to feel like the future. Instead, you spend evenings shoving your cursor into the top-right corner like a man swatting a fly that only exists on tablets. Your weather app is full-screen. Your 27-inch monitor is now a clock tile. When 8.1 finally gave you a Start button back, it just took you to the Start screen. So, inevitably, you installed Classic Shell. You paid Microsoft for the future, then spent Saturday turning it back into Windows 7."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0345", "lang": "en", "target_id": "graphene", "target_name": "Graphene", "facts": ["Isolated in 2004 by Andre Geim and Konstantin Novoselov at Manchester by peeling graphite with Scotch tape; Nobel Prize in Physics, 2010.", "The EU's Graphene Flagship, launched in 2013, committed €1 billion over ten years.", "A 2018 study in Advanced Materials tested 'graphene' from 60 producers and found none with more than 50% graphene content; most was graphite flakes.", "Sold in tennis rackets, running shoes, and headphones as a performance ingredient.", "Geim also won an Ig Nobel Prize in 2000 for levitating a frog with magnets."], "a": {"item_id": "dd1e6a2334b9", "text": "Let's hear it for the graphene evangelists, the only people who've been \"five years away\" since 2004. Two guys in Manchester peel pencil lead with Scotch tape, win a Nobel, and an entire industry decides the tape was the business plan. Every press release is the same Mad Lib: \"Wonder material could revolutionize [batteries/condoms/desalination/your feelings].\" *Could.* That word has funded more postdocs than the NSF. You promised us space elevators and delivered a tennis racket with a sticker on it. Startup founders slap \"graphene-enhanced\" on a phone case the way divorced dads put a spoiler on a Honda Civic. And the grant committees keep paying, because admitting the miracle is stuck on the production line means admitting you are too. Twenty years of changing everything, and the only thing you've actually changed is the abstract."}, "b": {"item_id": "886e9fa21662", "text": "Twenty years you've been telling us graphene will change everything, and the only thing it's changed is your grant renewal status. You won a Nobel Prize with Scotch tape and a pencil, and somehow concluded the hard part was over. Since then you've pitched it for batteries, bulletproof vests, desalination, and condoms, plus a paint, which actually exists, so naturally nobody's excited. The EU gave you a billion euros, and you came back with a roadmap. Every press release starts with \"could,\" because \"does\" would require a customer. Your startup's deck says \"disruptive.\" Your LinkedIn says \"thought leader.\" Your lab says \"please send more funding.\" You've been two years from commercialization since Myspace. Keep going, though. Somewhere in Manchester, a man in a lanyard still gasps when you say \"one atom thick.\" That gasp is the product."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0346", "lang": "en", "target_id": "javascript", "target_name": "JavaScript", "facts": ["Written by Brendan Eich at Netscape in ten days in May 1995; named Mocha, then LiveScript, then JavaScript to ride Java's marketing, although the two are unrelated.", "typeof null returns 'object', a bug from the first version that has been kept for compatibility ever since.", "In March 2016 the author of left-pad, an 11-line npm package, unpublished it and broke builds across the industry, including Babel and React.", "[] + {} evaluates to '[object Object]'; {} + [] typed at a console evaluates to 0.", "Standardized by Ecma as 'ECMAScript' in 1997 because the name JavaScript is a trademark, first Sun's and now Oracle's."], "a": {"item_id": "dcee06ed92d9", "text": "Let's hear it for the people who write everything in JavaScript. You learned one language at a twelve-week bootcamp and decided the rest were optional. Frontend? JavaScript. Backend? Node. Desktop app? Electron, so your to-do list ships with its own copy of Chrome and eats four gigs of RAM to remind you to buy eggs. You wanted to be \"full-stack.\" What you got is a node_modules folder heavier than a neutron star and a career that collapses the day some stranger unpublishes left-pad. You rewrite your app every eighteen months because a new framework has a cooler logo. When it breaks in production, you stare at \"undefined is not a function\" and blame the user. Then you install TypeScript, which is just JavaScript wearing a lanyard so you can feel like a real engineer."}, "b": {"item_id": "6fc599fe456a", "text": "You write everything in JavaScript? Beautiful. You're the guy who wanted one language so badly you married it. Backend, build tools, phone app, the script that renames your screenshots: all JavaScript, all held together by a node_modules folder heavier than your laptop's actual contents. You didn't choose simplicity. You chose a single abusive relationship and call it commitment. When people ask why, you say \"full-stack,\" which is what you call a stack you can't see the bottom of. Your server went down last week because of a package named is-odd, installed at 2 a.m. by you, who never once checked what it does. You own three frameworks, one half-finished side project, and a deep personal belief that typeof null is a feature you've made peace with."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0347", "lang": "en", "target_id": "almond-milk", "target_name": "Almond milk", "facts": ["California grows about 80% of the world's almonds.", "A single almond takes about 1.1 US gallons (roughly 4 litres) of water to grow, a figure Mother Jones published in 2014 that spread during California's drought.", "A 2015 US class action alleged Almond Breeze contained only about 2% almonds; it partly survived a motion to dismiss, was stayed in 2017 pending a separate Blue Diamond labelling settlement, and the plaintiffs then dropped it.", "Pollinating California's almond orchards each February requires trucking in most of the commercial honeybee colonies in the United States.", "Medieval European cooks used almond milk as a Lenten substitute for dairy, centuries before the current fashion."], "a": {"item_id": "cf146e52ac24", "text": "You switched to almond milk to save the planet, which is adorable, because you did it the way you do everything: you read one headline about cows and stopped there. Meanwhile, almonds drink California's drought like a frat boy at a keg, and the bees get trucked in like seasonal labor so you can feel superior in a Tuesday latte. You didn't save the planet. You outsourced your guilt to a state that's on fire.\n\nAnd you told people. Nobody asked, Brent. You announced it at brunch like you'd donated a kidney. You pay an extra dollar fifty for a carton that's about 2% almonds, so you're basically funding a rumor of a nut. Then you flew to Tulum for your birthday.\n\nNow you've heard oat is the responsible choice, so you're quietly converting, and by spring you'll act like you were never on almond's side. That's not moral growth. That's a subscription to guilt with a new flavor every year."}, "b": {"item_id": "ac57c4110eb9", "text": "So you switched to almond milk for the planet. Good for you. You're the person who announces \"I'm not a cow person\" like it's a personality. Meanwhile, almonds are one of the thirstiest crops on Earth, and you're pouring a gallon of water into every latte to feel like the only adult in the cafe who read the label. You tell everyone about the bees, though you've never met a bee. You say \"it's basically just almonds and water\" like that's a selling point. Your friends have stopped asking. Your barista has stopped pretending to care. And the almonds, somewhere in California, are wondering who you think you're saving."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0348", "lang": "en", "target_id": "risk", "target_name": "Risk", "facts": ["Invented in 1957 by Albert Lamorisse, the French director of The Red Balloon, as La Conquête du Monde; Parker Brothers published it in the United States in 1959.", "The map has 42 territories, among them Kamchatka, Irkutsk, and Yakutsk, while the entire Middle East is one territory.", "Holding Australia, which has a single entry point, is the standard opening strategy.", "A full game can run for hours, and players eliminated early have nothing to do but watch."], "a": {"item_id": "623676ba302f", "text": "You people said \"world domination\" out loud and then scheduled it around dinner. Hour six, and the guy who was eliminated in hour two is still watching, because his ride is the winner.\n\nWho plays Risk to the end? The guy who turtled in Australia for four hours like a retiree in Boca, then acts shocked when no one respects his \"strong defensive position.\" The one who negotiates alliances with the gravity of the Treaty of Versailles, then breaks them over a single Kamchatka. Your whole personality is \"I'm a strategist,\" and your whole strategy is rolling three dice at your best friend.\n\nAnd the prize? You conquered the world. Congratulations: it's 2 a.m., your girlfriend's gone, and your brother won't speak to you. Napoleon at least got an island. You get to pick up 400 tiny plastic men."}, "b": {"item_id": "d3ed8f3ba762", "text": "Nobody plays Risk to the end. You're the guy who got knocked out at nine o'clock and has been sitting there with a lukewarm Coke, watching you. You're the one who drew Australia, held it for three hours, and now you give speeches about continents like it's the UN. Your whole personality is three blue armies sitting on Kamchatka. You negotiated a truce with your brother-in-law, broke it with a six-three dice roll, and called it strategy. You don't want the world. You want your name on the board at the end, defending a little plastic pile of soldiers until somebody's mom calls everyone to dinner. And the worst part? You'll win a game where the dice decided everything, and you'll still say, \"I was playing the long game.\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0349", "lang": "en", "target_id": "javascript", "target_name": "JavaScript", "facts": ["Written by Brendan Eich at Netscape in ten days in May 1995; named Mocha, then LiveScript, then JavaScript to ride Java's marketing, although the two are unrelated.", "typeof null returns 'object', a bug from the first version that has been kept for compatibility ever since.", "In March 2016 the author of left-pad, an 11-line npm package, unpublished it and broke builds across the industry, including Babel and React.", "[] + {} evaluates to '[object Object]'; {} + [] typed at a console evaluates to 0.", "Standardized by Ecma as 'ECMAScript' in 1997 because the name JavaScript is a trademark, first Sun's and now Oracle's."], "a": {"item_id": "dcee06ed92d9", "text": "Let's hear it for the people who write everything in JavaScript. You learned one language at a twelve-week bootcamp and decided the rest were optional. Frontend? JavaScript. Backend? Node. Desktop app? Electron, so your to-do list ships with its own copy of Chrome and eats four gigs of RAM to remind you to buy eggs. You wanted to be \"full-stack.\" What you got is a node_modules folder heavier than a neutron star and a career that collapses the day some stranger unpublishes left-pad. You rewrite your app every eighteen months because a new framework has a cooler logo. When it breaks in production, you stare at \"undefined is not a function\" and blame the user. Then you install TypeScript, which is just JavaScript wearing a lanyard so you can feel like a real engineer."}, "b": {"item_id": "7c79a2ca0b0b", "text": "You wrote your backend in JavaScript, your frontend in JavaScript, your build scripts in JavaScript, and your smart fridge dashboard in JavaScript, and you call it \"full-stack\" because \"only-stack\" sounds like a cry for help. You said you wanted to avoid context switching. Congratulations: you now switch between four frameworks that all launched this quarter.\n\nYou're not a polyglot. You're someone who learned one language and renamed every problem until it fit.\n\nWhat did it get you? A node_modules folder heavier than your product, a notepad app that ships its own browser and eats two gigs of RAM, and a TypeScript migration to apologize for the original decision. Your Hello World has 1,400 dependencies, one of them maintained by a guy who once deleted eleven lines and broke the internet.\n\nBut you're hireable. Every posting wants you. That's not a career, that's a hostage situation with stock options."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0350", "lang": "en", "target_id": "juicero", "target_name": "Juicero", "facts": ["A Wi-Fi-connected cold-press juicer launched in 2016 at $699, later cut to $399, that squeezed proprietary bags of chopped produce sold by subscription.", "Raised about $120 million from investors including Google Ventures and Kleiner Perkins.", "In April 2017, Bloomberg reporters squeezed the packs by hand and got 7.5 ounces of juice in 90 seconds, faster than the machine's 8 ounces in about two minutes.", "Each pack carried a QR code that the press scanned and checked online before squeezing; it refused expired packs and would not run at all unless connected to Wi-Fi.", "Shut down in September 2017 and offered refunds on the machine."], "a": {"item_id": "a22f3d4810e1", "text": "You're the guy who read \"Bloomberg shows you can squeeze the pouch with your bare hands\" and thought, \"Sure, but can my hands connect to WiFi?\" You paid $400 to outsource the one step of juicemaking a toddler can do. You didn't want juice. You wanted to feel like a Series B. You wanted a dinner guest to ask, \"Is that the Juicero?\" so you could say, \"It's backed by Google Ventures,\" like that's a nutrient.\n\nAnd you paid seven dollars a pouch for pre-chopped kale with an expiration date, because you can't be trusted to smell things.\n\nWhen the company folded, you didn't lose a juicer. You lost your seat at the table where adults say \"disrupting breakfast\" with a straight face.\n\nNow you squeeze the pouch over a glass like a Capri Sun, doing the work your $400 was supposed to prevent. Cheers. Full circle."}, "b": {"item_id": "596abf5c014a", "text": "You paid four hundred dollars so a startup could check your juice's expiration date over Wi-Fi. That's not wellness. That's asking a server for permission to have breakfast. These are people who looked at a bag of chopped kale and thought, \"This needs a subscription.\" People who wanted to be someone who juices without ever touching a fruit. Then Bloomberg showed the whole world you could squeeze the pack with your bare hands. Faster. For free. Your grandmother has been doing that with a lemon since 1952. You didn't buy a juicer; you bought the privilege of paying seven dollars a pouch to a company Google Ventures funded and still couldn't keep alive past 2017. Congratulations: you're the only people who ever got a refund from Silicon Valley and still walked away looking poorer."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0351", "lang": "en", "target_id": "monopoly", "target_name": "Monopoly", "facts": ["Descends from Lizzie Magie's The Landlord's Game, patented in 1904 to teach the evils of land monopoly in the spirit of Henry George.", "Charles Darrow sold his version to Parker Brothers in 1935 and was credited as the inventor; Parker bought Magie's patent for $500.", "The official rules require any unbought property to be auctioned, and 'Free Parking' pays nothing; the house rules that skip the auction and pile cash on Free Parking are why games drag.", "During the Second World War, British intelligence had maps, compasses, and real currency hidden in sets sent to prisoner-of-war camps."], "a": {"item_id": "5b3d3501d71e", "text": "Dad, you've been the banker since 2009, and you're the only one who knows what's in that tray, and you've never once shared it. Free Parking has had four hundred dollars in it since the Clinton administration, and you call pocketing it \"a light house rule.\" Mom has wanted to quit at nine o'clock since the second lap. Your brother bought every orange property on the first trip around and now calls himself a real estate mogul at a kitchen table. Grandma is the thimble, and she's still winning, because she's the only one who doesn't count her cash. The nephew is eight. He mortgaged his railroads to buy a hotel he can't afford, then sobbed in jail. Everybody says \"just visiting\" like it's a personality. You're not playing for the fake money. You're finding out who'll flip the board when they lose."}, "b": {"item_id": "b78ec11705ad", "text": "Family Monopoly night: for people who looked at Thanksgiving and thought, \"This needs more landlords.\" Dad always grabs the banker job, the only time he's held money he didn't immediately lose. Your sister trades you Baltic Avenue for your Park Place \"as a favor,\" and you realize she's been running the same con since the womb. Mom invented a Free Parking jackpot rule in 1994 and defends it like scripture. And Grandpa just sits in jail, every game, refusing to pay the fifty, finally at peace.\n\nWhat are you all after? The thrill of owning property, a feeling none of you will experience off this cardboard square. Four hours later, someone flips the board, the thimble disappears under the couch forever, and the only thing anybody actually collected was a grudge worth way more than $200."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0352", "lang": "en", "target_id": "windows-vista", "target_name": "Windows Vista", "facts": ["Released to consumers in January 2007, after the 'Longhorn' project was reset in 2004 and headline features such as the WinFS file system were dropped.", "User Account Control prompted so often that Apple's 'Get a Mac' campaign ran an ad about 'Cancel or Allow'.", "'Windows Vista Capable' stickers went on PCs that could only run Home Basic, without the Aero interface, and led to a class-action lawsuit.", "Windows 7 (2009), widely praised, was largely a tuned-up Vista on the same kernel line; Vista support ended in April 2017."], "a": {"item_id": "1925db42cfe5", "text": "Every guy who upgraded to Vista on day one has the same story. He's standing in line at midnight, January 30th, 2007, holding a box like it's the last Wii. He wants to tell people he was first. Nobody asked. He got the Ultimate edition because he's the kind of man who says \"Ultimate\" about a salad. He turned on Aero, watched his window borders go translucent, and called it \"the future.\" Then his printer stopped working. He found out its driver was from 2004, and the printer was not interested in the future. So he clicked \"Allow\" on a UAC prompt. Then he clicked it again. He's clicked Allow four hundred times now, and he's the only one who thinks that's security."}, "b": {"item_id": "c19db756674c", "text": "Congratulations to everyone who installed Vista on launch day. You read \"wait for Service Pack 1\" and thought, \"That's advice for people with something to lose.\" You bought a new graphics card just to make your windows see-through. Finally, your windows are as transparent as your reasons.\n\nNow your printer is a paperweight, your scanner is a rumor, and the driver is \"coming soon\" from a company whose website still says 2004. Every click triggers a box asking if you meant it. You've said yes forty times today. You're not secure, you're just trained.\n\nBut you'll tell coworkers it's \"actually smooth once you turn a few things off.\" Sir, you paid four hundred dollars to turn things off. Meanwhile Dave, on XP, finished his spreadsheet and went home."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0353", "lang": "en", "target_id": "carbon-nanotubes", "target_name": "Carbon nanotubes", "facts": ["Reported by Sumio Iijima in Nature in 1991.", "Promised as the cable for a space elevator; a 2006 analysis showed that ordinary defects cut the strength of a macroscopic cable far below the ideal value.", "Long, rigid multi-walled nanotubes behaved like asbestos fibres in a 2008 mouse study in Nature Nanotechnology.", "Sorting metallic from semiconducting tubes has blocked nanotube electronics for three decades; MIT's 16-bit RV16X-NANO processor (2019) is still a lab demonstration.", "Today they are sold mainly as a conductive additive in lithium-ion battery electrodes."], "a": {"item_id": "9ad1a4c3964e", "text": "Let's hear it for the carbon nanotube founders, the only people who've been \"five years from commercialization\" since 1991. You can spot them at any pitch day: a postdoc in a blazer that still smells like the clean room, opening with a space elevator and closing with a seed round. Bold move, promising a cable to orbit when your entire production run fits in a vial labeled \"DO NOT INHALE.\" They swear they'll replace silicon. Silicon has heard this every year since Clinton and keeps shipping. They say \"a hundred times stronger than steel\" the way your uncle says he could've gone pro. And what does it get them? A Nature cover, a term sheet, and a quiet pivot to \"conductive additive for batteries.\" The dream: a ladder to space. The exit: slightly better tennis rackets."}, "b": {"item_id": "09b6c02ce449", "text": "Every CNT pitch deck has the same slide: a space elevator hanging off the equator, a cable strong enough to hold a city, and a sunrise nobody asked for. The founder skips the part where that cable is \"in development\" the way the moon is in development. He has a PhD, wears a lab coat to investor dinners, and has said \"five years from commercial scale\" since 2009. He's been five years away for fifteen years. Investors adore him because he says \"tensile strength\" like a man who has never had to explain a yield curve. What it gets him: a Series C, a conference booth beside a guy selling graphene sunglasses, and a LinkedIn banner of himself squinting at a black smear that is somehow his life's work. The tubes are doing fine. They've never once asked for a Series C."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0354", "lang": "en", "target_id": "beanie-babies", "target_name": "Beanie Babies", "facts": ["Launched by Ty Inc. in 1993 and sold only through small gift shops, with designs 'retired' to manufacture scarcity.", "At the height of the craze Beanie Babies accounted for about 10% of eBay's sales, and eBay's 1998 IPO filing said the site at times had over 30,000 Beanie Baby auctions running simultaneously in the quarter ending June 1998.", "In 1999 a divorcing couple divided their Beanie Baby collection one by one on a courtroom floor, in a photograph that ran worldwide.", "In 1999 Ty announced that Beanie Babies would be retired at the end of the year, then reversed itself after asking the public to vote and seeing an overwhelming vote to keep them.", "Most 'rare' Beanies that price guides valued in the thousands now sell for a few dollars."], "a": {"item_id": "45f7594d8a38", "text": "Let's hear it for the Beanie Baby investors, the only people who bought a retirement plan at a Hallmark store. You put tag protectors on a heart-shaped piece of cardboard and called it diversification. You drove to four McDonald's for Teenie Beanies, threw away the Happy Meals, and told your kids it was for their college fund. Now that kid is thirty, still paying off loans, and his inheritance is a Rubbermaid tub of Princess the Bears. Every few months you see an eBay listing asking $500,000 for one with a tag typo and think, \"Vindicated.\" Babe, that's not a market. That's another guy in another garage, also lying to his wife. Ty Warner told you which ones were \"retired,\" and you believed him. Ty Warner later pleaded guilty to hiding money offshore. He hoarded too. He just hoarded the right thing."}, "b": {"item_id": "1a45d6768831", "text": "Let's talk about the Beanie Baby investors, the folks who bought eleven Peanut the Elephants and never let a child within arm's reach of one. You know the type. He's got a binder. Every bear has a hang tag, a tush tag, and a plastic sleeve, and he inspects the tush tag for misprints like it's the Dead Sea Scrolls. He says \"retired\" the way a doctor says \"terminal.\" His retirement plan is one Princess bear and a prayer. Ty Inc. never promised him a dime, but he's still waiting for the big payout. Meanwhile his daughter sleeps with a bald rabbit named Hope, because the good stuff is sealed in the basement, appreciating, never once hugged. Congratulations. You own the most expensive animals in your house that nobody's allowed to touch."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0355", "lang": "en", "target_id": "klingon", "target_name": "Klingon", "facts": ["Created by linguist Marc Okrand for Star Trek III (1984), building on a few words the actor James Doohan improvised for the 1979 film.", "Deliberately alien grammar: object-verb-subject word order and no verb 'to be', so Hamlet's line became 'taH pagh taHbe'', 'continue or not continue'.", "The Klingon Language Institute (1992) published Hamlet 'in the original Klingon'; Bing Translator added Klingon in 2013 and Duolingo in 2018.", "Okrand's 1985 Klingon Dictionary listed roughly 1,500 words, and fluent speakers number only in the dozens: 20 to 30 in 2009, 30 to 50 by 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "64aab443b4b9", "text": "Being fluent in Klingon is a big deal. You've joined an elite community of maybe forty people worldwide, and you've met all of them at the same Holiday Inn conference room. You spent years mastering a language built for a warrior race, a culture of honor, combat and blood oaths, and you use it to argue on a forum about verb suffixes. You learned the language of the fiercest fighters in the galaxy, and the last thing you fought was a lactose sensitivity.\n\nKlingon has no word for \"hello.\" The standard greeting is \"nuqneH,\" which means \"what do you want?\" Perfect for you, because nobody has ever approached you without a reason.\n\nYou told people you read *Hamlet* \"in the original Klingon.\" Brother, Shakespeare had a girlfriend. You have a bat'leth over the futon.\n\nQapla'. That means \"success,\" a word you know only in Klingon."}, "b": {"item_id": "0cbf01775821", "text": "You spent years mastering a language whose standard greeting is \"What do you want?\" and honestly, that explains the social life. You wanted a warrior culture and got a prefix chart. You can render \"to be or not to be\" as *taH pagh taHbe'*, which literally means \"continue or don't continue,\" a fair question about the hobby.\n\nYour conversation partners are forty people at a convention who all dread your corrections. You command a vocabulary built for threatening enemies, and your only battle is a verb-suffix argument with a man named Greg, who is wrong, and who knows he is wrong, and who will not yield.\n\nYou say honor is everything, yet you've earned yours in a Marriott conference room, holding a foam bat'leth, forehead ridges peeling in the airport security line.\n\nToday is a good day to die? Sir, it's Tuesday. You have a dentist appointment."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0356", "lang": "en", "target_id": "nfts", "target_name": "NFTs", "facts": ["Beeple's 'Everydays: The First 5000 Days' sold at Christie's for $69.3 million in March 2021.", "OpenSea, the largest NFT marketplace, processed a record $2.7 billion in trades on 1 May 2022 and just $9.34 million on 28 August 2022, a 99% drop, according to DappRadar data.", "A 2023 dappGambl study found that 95% of the NFT collections it examined had a market value of zero.", "The token usually records only a URL; the image itself sits on an ordinary server or IPFS, not on the blockchain.", "Collins Dictionary named 'NFT' its word of the year for 2021."], "a": {"item_id": "de2993927ffe", "text": "Let's hear it for the 2021 NFT buyer. You paid four hundred dollars in gas fees to become the legal owner of something your coworker right-click saved during the meeting where you explained it. You didn't want art. You've never wanted art. You wanted a hexagon around your Twitter picture so strangers would know you had a Discord password. You typed \"WAGMI\" to eleven thousand anonymous cartoon monkeys and believed it. We are not all gonna make it, Kyle. The roadmap promised a metaverse yacht party; it delivered a founder named \"0xDegenSensei\" who deleted his account and moved to Portugal. But hey, you got what you paid for: a floor price you check like a heart monitor, and a profile picture you still can't bring yourself to change, because then it would be over."}, "b": {"item_id": "ce82b5a76298", "text": "Nothing says \"I'm early\" like buying into something the same year Christie's did. You spent your tax refund on a cartoon ape, and now you check the floor price at red lights like a man waiting on biopsy results. You put \"ETH\" in your Twitter name and a hexagon on your profile picture, and you say \"gm\" to strangers at 4 p.m. because a guy named CryptoChad_Baby said that's how you make it.\n\nMake what? You paid $300 in gas fees to join a Discord with 40,000 people and one moderator, who keeps DMing to say you've won something. You did win something. It's called getting your wallet drained.\n\nThe dream was never art. The dream was selling to a guy who's hoping to sell to a guy. You're not an investor, you're a link in a chain letter with a Discord. And congratulations, you're the last link."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0357", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hsq", "target_name": "HSQ (e-beam resist)", "facts": ["Hydrogen silsesquioxane, sold by Dow Corning as FOx ('flowable oxide') and designed as a spin-on dielectric for chip interconnects; lithographers adopted it as a negative-tone resist.", "Exposure converts its cage structure into a cross-linked, silica-like network, so the developed pattern is essentially SiO2.", "Holds the resolution records for electron-beam lithography: isolated features around 5 nm, dense lines below 10 nm half-pitch, with high-contrast 'salty' NaOH/NaCl development.", "Must be kept refrigerated (Dow Corning's datasheet gives a six-month shelf life at 5 °C); the solution ages into a jelly, and its effective sensitivity shifts with processing delays because cross-linking continues after exposure.", "DuPont, which inherited Dow Corning's FOx/XR-1541 HSQ line, discontinued it and ended production at the end of 2025; labs such as EPFL's CMi switched to third-party HSQ from EM Resist or DisChem, both formulated from AQM powder."], "a": {"item_id": "884dd445a1e2", "text": "\"We're switching to HSQ for the resolution.\" Sure you are. Because your PMMA wasn't humiliating enough, you wanted a resist that's also a deadline. You're chasing sub-10 nanometer lines so you can put one SEM image in a paper, the one nice picture out of forty-one fallen-over grating samples.\n\nYour resist lives in the freezer like a tiny, expensive yogurt, and by the time you've booked the beam it has gone off anyway. You'll expose at a dose that makes the e-beam tool bill you like a hotel minibar. Then you'll develop in salt water, because apparently you wanted to cook pasta in the cleanroom.\n\nAnd what do you get? Silicon dioxide you can't strip. A permanent souvenir of your ambition, fused to the wafer forever. Congratulations, you made glass lines. Your advisor wanted devices."}, "b": {"item_id": "b0d879a960b0", "text": "So you switched to HSQ \"for the resolution.\" Sure. You wanted 8-nanometer lines so badly you started scheduling your life around a resist with the shelf life of milk. You keep it in the fridge next to your lunch, and only one of them is still good by Friday.\n\nYour write that used to take twenty minutes now takes the whole overnight slot, so the guy who needed the tool for his actual thesis is crying in the gowning room. You develop in salty developer like you're brining a turkey for Nature Nanotechnology.\n\nAnd when it goes wrong, and it will, you've basically spun glass onto your chip. Your only exit is HF, a chemical that wants you dead.\n\nAll of this for one SEM image with a scale bar so small nobody can read it. Congratulations. Your device still doesn't work, but it fails at record resolution."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0358", "lang": "en", "target_id": "pmma", "target_name": "PMMA (e-beam resist)", "facts": ["The same polymer as Plexiglas and acrylic glass, dissolved in anisole or chlorobenzene and spun into a thin film.", "The oldest electron-beam resist still in everyday use; IBM researchers reported it as one in the late 1960s.", "Positive tone: the beam breaks polymer chains and the exposed region dissolves in a MIBK:IPA developer.", "Resolves below 10 nm but has poor sensitivity, so doses of hundreds to over a thousand µC/cm² (depending on voltage) make writing slow.", "Poor dry-etch resistance, so it is used mostly for lift-off rather than as an etch mask."], "a": {"item_id": "a93c5e595a57", "text": "You're still running PMMA? Bless you. You're the guy who spins 950K A4 at 4000 rpm like it's 2003 and then acts shocked when your thickness drifts. You bake at 180 for ninety seconds like it's a sourdough starter, and you develop in MIBK to IPA one-to-three while muttering about contrast. Your writes take eleven hours because you're hosing the sample with dose just to draw a line HSQ would have handled before lunch. Meanwhile the ZEP people are home by six, drinking something with a garnish. You're not a purist. You're a man who read one paper on proximity effect in 2009 and never recovered. Your lift-off is clean, I'll give you that. Honestly, it's the most reliable thing about you."}, "b": {"item_id": "a46a8573ce80", "text": "You people still spinning PMMA aren't doing lithography. You're doing historical reenactment. Your advisor's advisor scribbled the recipe on a napkin in 1975, and you treat it like scripture: 950K over 495K, MIBK to IPA one-to-three, develop sixty seconds while whispering to the beaker. You tell everyone it's about \"resolution.\" It's about nobody wanting to file the purchase order for ZEP. So you crank the dose until writing one chip takes longer than your stipend lasts, then discover your pattern won't survive ten seconds in the etcher. So you do liftoff, because of course you do. Your entire thesis is gold rectangles. You're not a nanofabrication researcher. You're the guy who knows which bottle in the cleanroom is \"the good IPA\" and guards it like a dragon."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0359", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hsq", "target_name": "HSQ (e-beam resist)", "facts": ["Hydrogen silsesquioxane, sold by Dow Corning as FOx ('flowable oxide') and designed as a spin-on dielectric for chip interconnects; lithographers adopted it as a negative-tone resist.", "Exposure converts its cage structure into a cross-linked, silica-like network, so the developed pattern is essentially SiO2.", "Holds the resolution records for electron-beam lithography: isolated features around 5 nm, dense lines below 10 nm half-pitch, with high-contrast 'salty' NaOH/NaCl development.", "Must be kept refrigerated (Dow Corning's datasheet gives a six-month shelf life at 5 °C); the solution ages into a jelly, and its effective sensitivity shifts with processing delays because cross-linking continues after exposure.", "DuPont, which inherited Dow Corning's FOx/XR-1541 HSQ line, discontinued it and ended production at the end of 2025; labs such as EPFL's CMi switched to third-party HSQ from EM Resist or DisChem, both formulated from AQM powder."], "a": {"item_id": "96896ea695a3", "text": "You're switching to HSQ for resolution. Of course you are. You have a PhD in patience and a deficit in sleep, and one paper showed someone drawing 6-nanometer lines, so now you want to be that person at group meeting. Here's what HSQ gets you: a resist that gels in the bottle if you look at it wrong and, once exposed, cures into glass that stays in your device forever. Want it gone? Good luck. That's hydrofluoric acid territory. Your dose matrix is forty chips. Your TMAH developer smells like a crime scene. Six months from now you'll be at the SEM at 2 a.m., staring at lines that have slumped into a sad little heap, swearing you'll go back to PMMA. You won't. You'll put it in your thesis as a \"lesson learned.\""}, "b": {"item_id": "b68838ef49bb", "text": "So you switched to HSQ for the resolution. Bold move for someone whose devices still die at wire bonding. You saw one SEM of 6-nanometer lines at a conference and decided PMMA was beneath you. Now you're writing at a dose so high your name sits on the e-beam calendar like a hostage note. Your resist lives in the fridge next to somebody's yogurt, and you check its age more often than your own. You develop in salt water now, the closest you've come to a beach in three years. And congratulations on those gorgeous lines. They're basically glass, so the only way to fix a mistake is HF, a fume hood, and a long call to your mother. All this for one inset figure the reviewer will ask you to make bigger."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0360", "lang": "en", "target_id": "comic-sans", "target_name": "Comic Sans", "facts": ["Designed by Vincent Connare at Microsoft in 1994, modeled on the comic-book lettering in The Dark Knight Returns and Watchmen.", "Made for the speech bubbles of Rover, the cartoon dog in Microsoft Bob; it missed Bob's release and shipped instead in the Windows 95 Plus! pack.", "CERN's July 2012 Higgs boson discovery announcement was presented on slides set in it.", "The 'Ban Comic Sans' campaign, started by designers Dave and Holly Combs in 1999, ran until 2019, when they retired it and launched usecomicsans.com."], "a": {"item_id": "886470815955", "text": "You scrolled past every font on that dropdown menu, past the ones built for hospitals, courts, and airports, and picked the one designed for a cartoon dog's speech bubbles. Congratulations. Your notice about the mandatory layoffs is now technically being narrated by Rover from Microsoft Bob.\n\nI know what you were after. You wanted to seem approachable. You're the office manager who says \"no worries!\" while ruining someone's Friday. You wanted the boil-water advisory to feel like a hug. Instead, your tenants read it in the voice of a birthday clown and assumed the water was fine and the clown was just excited.\n\nHere's what it got you: a screenshot on Reddit, a group chat full of strangers, and a legal department that now reads your memos aloud to each other for fun.\n\nThe pet had a better career than the font you chose to speak for it."}, "b": {"item_id": "85bb13923657", "text": "Nobody picks Comic Sans for a gas leak notice by accident. You wanted the building to evacuate without feeling like you were in charge of anything. \"EVACUATE BY 5PM\" felt too blunt, so you softened it into the font of a kindergarten worksheet and a lost-cat flyer. Now the fire marshal reads it like a bake sale reminder. You were going for approachable. What you got was a sign that people skim, shrug at, and treat as a suggestion. Your management is going to love the memo you're writing next, the one in Papyrus about why the sign was ignored."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0361", "lang": "en", "target_id": "windows-vista", "target_name": "Windows Vista", "facts": ["Released to consumers in January 2007, after the 'Longhorn' project was reset in 2004 and headline features such as the WinFS file system were dropped.", "User Account Control prompted so often that Apple's 'Get a Mac' campaign ran an ad about 'Cancel or Allow'.", "'Windows Vista Capable' stickers went on PCs that could only run Home Basic, without the Aero interface, and led to a class-action lawsuit.", "Windows 7 (2009), widely praised, was largely a tuned-up Vista on the same kernel line; Vista support ended in April 2017."], "a": {"item_id": "4f7f18f8662a", "text": "Let's hear it for the day-one Vista crowd, the people who lined up at CompUSA on January 30th, 2007 because a billboard said \"The Wow Starts Now.\" You paid $399 for Ultimate so Flip 3D could shuffle the same three windows you used to Alt-Tab through. Your laptop had a \"Vista Capable\" sticker and 512 megs of RAM, which is like slapping \"Marathon Capable\" on a hamster. You wanted to look like the future; you had Aero turned off by Wednesday. Your printer quit. Your Sound Blaster went mute. Your \"Ultimate Extras\" turned out to be a poker game and a promise. And every time you tried to show off, the screen went dark and asked, \"Are you sure?\" Buddy, nobody was sure. Not even Microsoft. They were already working on Windows 7."}, "b": {"item_id": "c19db756674c", "text": "Congratulations to everyone who installed Vista on launch day. You read \"wait for Service Pack 1\" and thought, \"That's advice for people with something to lose.\" You bought a new graphics card just to make your windows see-through. Finally, your windows are as transparent as your reasons.\n\nNow your printer is a paperweight, your scanner is a rumor, and the driver is \"coming soon\" from a company whose website still says 2004. Every click triggers a box asking if you meant it. You've said yes forty times today. You're not secure, you're just trained.\n\nBut you'll tell coworkers it's \"actually smooth once you turn a few things off.\" Sir, you paid four hundred dollars to turn things off. Meanwhile Dave, on XP, finished his spreadsheet and went home."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0362", "lang": "en", "target_id": "microsoft-word", "target_name": "Microsoft Word", "facts": ["Launched in 1983 as 'Multi-Tool Word' for Xenix and MS-DOS.", "Clippy, the animated paperclip assistant, arrived in Office 97 and was removed in Office 2007; Microsoft itself ran ads mocking it in 2001.", "Its .docx format, Office Open XML, was fast-tracked to ISO standard 29500 in 2008 as a roughly 6,000-page specification, amid accusations that national committees had been packed.", "Older spell checkers, Word's included, offered 'Cupertino' as a correction for a mistyped 'cooperation', and the resulting 'Cupertino effect' made it into published NATO, EU and UN-agency documents."], "a": {"item_id": "c9f44b95f5be", "text": "You've got 214 pages in a single file and a belief that this time it will behave. You are a person who typed \"1.1\" by hand and then pressed the space bar forty times to line up the title with the page number. You call that a table of contents.\n\nYou wanted to avoid learning LaTeX because you're \"not a coding person,\" so instead you learned section breaks, roman numerals, and the one sacred rule: never touch Chapter 3. You moved one figure on page 12 and now your bibliography is in Comic Sans, somehow.\n\nYour file is called Thesis_FINAL_v7_REAL_advisorEdits_USE THIS ONE. You've emailed it to yourself as a backup, which is your entire version control strategy.\n\nAll that sacrifice to look professional, and your defense slides will be in PowerPoint. You haven't escaped. You've just switched the hostage."}, "b": {"item_id": "1bc74d6b8981", "text": "Ah, the thesis typesetters. Four years of research, and the final week is spent discovering that Heading 2 and Heading 3 are secretly different shades of Times New Roman. You aren't writing anymore. You're toggling paragraph marks at page 214, hunting a rogue section break like a bomb tech who forgot which wire. You've renumbered Chapter 3 six times because the cross-references keep saying \"Error! Reference source not found.\" You want a table of contents that updates itself, and you're clicking Update Field on the wrong field. Your reward is a 300-page PDF whose List of Figures reads \"No table of figures entries found,\" and you tell your advisor it's a formatting quirk. Your advisor has also used Word. Your advisor is scrolling to the margins right now."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0363", "lang": "en", "target_id": "latex", "target_name": "LaTeX", "facts": ["Leslie Lamport wrote LaTeX in the early 1980s and released it in 1984 as a macro layer on Donald Knuth's TeX (1978), which Knuth built because he disliked how the galley proofs of the new edition of his own book looked.", "TeX's version number converges on π (3.141592653 since 2021), and Knuth's TeX bug bounty started at one hexadecimal dollar, $2.56, doubling yearly until frozen at $327.68; he stopped mailing real cheques in 2008 after fraud and now credits accounts at his fictional Bank of San Serriffe, and only 9 of the 275 cheques he sent from 2006 on were ever cashed.", "Its best-known warning is 'Underfull \\hbox (badness 10000)'; overfull lines are reported as 'Overfull \\hbox (Npt too wide)', and figures float to wherever the algorithm decides, not where the author put them.", "A single missing brace can produce an error reported pages away from the mistake, and a document can need three compiler passes to get its references right."], "a": {"item_id": "88a7647c2b15", "text": "Let's talk about the guy who writes a two-page memo in LaTeX. Your memo is about the break room fridge, but it's in Computer Modern, so everyone reads it expecting it to end with a theorem. \"Fridge Policy, Section 1.1.\" Buddy, there's no Section 1.2. You spent forty minutes on a preamble longer than the memo. Twelve \\usepackage lines to say \"label your yogurt.\" You compiled it three times because the cross-reference said \"see Section ??\" on page one, referencing page one. You're not after clarity. You're after someone asking, \"Is this LaTeX?\" so you can say \"Oh, habit,\" like a man who still mentions his thesis at parties. And it gets you exactly one thing: a PDF nobody can edit, so when Karen fixes a typo, she retypes the whole thing in Word."}, "b": {"item_id": "c2eb8de50987", "text": "Nobody writes a two-page memo in LaTeX because they have to. They do it because they have a dissertation's worth of hubris and a sincere belief that the margins are the message. This is the guy who types \\documentclass{article} at his cubicle like he's summoning a demon. He spends ninety minutes getting one bullet point to hang properly, then emails the PDF to a coworker who opens it on her phone and asks, \"Can you just do it in Word?\" He wanted to look like a scientist. What he got was a memo that says \"Q3 Update\" in Computer Modern, and a reply-all from Doug asking what font that is."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0364", "lang": "en", "target_id": "klingon", "target_name": "Klingon", "facts": ["Created by linguist Marc Okrand for Star Trek III (1984), building on a few words the actor James Doohan improvised for the 1979 film.", "Deliberately alien grammar: object-verb-subject word order and no verb 'to be', so Hamlet's line became 'taH pagh taHbe'', 'continue or not continue'.", "The Klingon Language Institute (1992) published Hamlet 'in the original Klingon'; Bing Translator added Klingon in 2013 and Duolingo in 2018.", "Okrand's 1985 Klingon Dictionary listed roughly 1,500 words, and fluent speakers number only in the dozens: 20 to 30 in 2009, 30 to 50 by 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "c3c693f6b9ba", "text": "Nobody becomes fluent in Klingon by accident. You have to sit down and decide that the thing you want most in life is to clear your throat in a language invented for a TV show. Your friends learned Spanish for a job. You learned to say \"Qapla'\" to a bartender who has never once been impressed. You've translated Hamlet. Twice. The Klingons in the original show would read your Hamlet and call it too soft, which is the only review you've ever wanted. At conventions you're the one who corrects the guy in the Worf costume, and he has the better forehead. You're chasing a room where someone finally gets your jokes, and that room is mostly you, reading your own joke aloud, slightly out of breath from the gutturals."}, "b": {"item_id": "64aab443b4b9", "text": "Being fluent in Klingon is a big deal. You've joined an elite community of maybe forty people worldwide, and you've met all of them at the same Holiday Inn conference room. You spent years mastering a language built for a warrior race, a culture of honor, combat and blood oaths, and you use it to argue on a forum about verb suffixes. You learned the language of the fiercest fighters in the galaxy, and the last thing you fought was a lactose sensitivity.\n\nKlingon has no word for \"hello.\" The standard greeting is \"nuqneH,\" which means \"what do you want?\" Perfect for you, because nobody has ever approached you without a reason.\n\nYou told people you read *Hamlet* \"in the original Klingon.\" Brother, Shakespeare had a girlfriend. You have a bat'leth over the futon.\n\nQapla'. That means \"success,\" a word you know only in Klingon."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0365", "lang": "en", "target_id": "comic-sans", "target_name": "Comic Sans", "facts": ["Designed by Vincent Connare at Microsoft in 1994, modeled on the comic-book lettering in The Dark Knight Returns and Watchmen.", "Made for the speech bubbles of Rover, the cartoon dog in Microsoft Bob; it missed Bob's release and shipped instead in the Windows 95 Plus! pack.", "CERN's July 2012 Higgs boson discovery announcement was presented on slides set in it.", "The 'Ban Comic Sans' campaign, started by designers Dave and Holly Combs in 1999, ran until 2019, when they retired it and launched usecomicsans.com."], "a": {"item_id": "85bb13923657", "text": "Nobody picks Comic Sans for a gas leak notice by accident. You wanted the building to evacuate without feeling like you were in charge of anything. \"EVACUATE BY 5PM\" felt too blunt, so you softened it into the font of a kindergarten worksheet and a lost-cat flyer. Now the fire marshal reads it like a bake sale reminder. You were going for approachable. What you got was a sign that people skim, shrug at, and treat as a suggestion. Your management is going to love the memo you're writing next, the one in Papyrus about why the sign was ignored."}, "b": {"item_id": "ea624ef43e94", "text": "Let's talk about the people who announce layoffs in Comic Sans. You're not a monster. You're Brenda from Facilities, and you want everyone to feel cozy about the asbestos removal. You took a typeface Microsoft designed for a cartoon dog's speech bubble and used it to type \"Your position has been eliminated.\" That's not softening the blow. That's a clown serving a subpoena. CERN announced the Higgs boson in it, and the physicists who found the God particle spent the week explaining the font. You think it says \"approachable.\" It says \"I scrolled right past Arial and Times New Roman and chose a kindergarten birthday invitation.\" Nobody reads your notice. They screenshot it. Congratulations: your fire evacuation memo is trending, and the building is still on fire."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0366", "lang": "en", "target_id": "durian", "target_name": "Durian", "facts": ["Banned on Singapore's MRT trains and in many Southeast Asian hotels because of the smell.", "A 2017 genome study in Nature Genetics traced the odour to a duplicated family of MGL genes that pump out volatile sulfur compounds.", "China buys most of the world's durian exports, chiefly from Thailand, in a trade worth billions of dollars a year.", "Alfred Russel Wallace wrote in 1856 that eating it was 'a new sensation worth a voyage to the East to experience'."], "a": {"item_id": "408212e83a9b", "text": "You're not eating durian on the train because you love it. You're eating it because you need the whole carriage to know you've been to Southeast Asia and survived. You stood under the little no-durian sign, the one with the red slash that exists specifically because of people like you, and thought, \"That's for amateurs.\"\n\nYou call it the king of fruits, which is funny, because you're the only one who ever crowned it. Everyone else just moved seats.\n\nYou'll tell us it's an acquired taste. Buddy, you didn't acquire a taste. You acquired a personality, and a three-seat radius of personal space.\n\nWhat did it get you? A story for brunch, a hotel front desk that knows your name, and a stranger's hand clamped over her nose. Enjoy your throne."}, "b": {"item_id": "94cc53dad1db", "text": "Look at him on the MRT, scooping durian with a plastic spoon like he's the only man in Singapore with a palate. Nobody asked for this. The carriage empties in a ring around him, and he takes it as respect. That's the whole transaction: the cleared seat, the sideways looks, proof he's braver than the rest of us. \"It's like custard,\" he says, eyes watering, because that's what happens when you're trying this hard. Then he posts the video captioned \"not for everyone.\" He's right. It's not for everyone. It's for him, and everyone within fifty metres just got a free sample of his ego."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0367", "lang": "en", "target_id": "jackfruit", "target_name": "Jackfruit", "facts": ["The largest fruit that grows on a tree; single fruits can weigh over 40 kg.", "The national fruit of Bangladesh and the state fruit of Kerala and Tamil Nadu.", "Unripe flesh is sold as a vegan 'pulled pork' substitute, although it has only 1-2 g of protein per 100 g.", "Cutting one releases a sticky white latex, so cooks oil their knives and hands first.", "A member of the mulberry family (Moraceae), a relative of breadfruit and figs."], "a": {"item_id": "b265b1d10a20", "text": "Every jackfruit seller has the same origin story. A guy who got laid off from software, bought a farm in a state he'd only seen from a plane window, and now runs a newsletter about \"reclaiming the dinner table.\" He's not selling you food. He's selling you the feeling that you finally did something. Jackfruit is perfect for him because it's the only thing that lets a man in a fleece vest say \"pulled pork\" at Whole Foods without anyone checking. He calls it \"the world's most sustainable protein,\" then sells it in a can with a label that looks like a Pixar villain. His customers are vegans who want a barbecue sandwich and carnivores who want a reason to feel smug about being there. Everybody wins except the sandwich."}, "b": {"item_id": "fc39af40bf91", "text": "Let's talk about the geniuses selling jackfruit as \"pulled pork.\" These are people who looked at a fruit that half of Kerala has been feeding to cousins for free since forever, slapped it in a brine pouch, and charged eleven dollars for the privilege of being disappointed in a Whole Foods.\n\nTheir whole business model is barbecue sauce. That's it. They're a sauce company with a fruit alibi. And when you mention it has about two grams of protein, they get *defensive*. Buddy, you sold me a pork sandwich with the nutritional profile of a pear.\n\nBut I get the appeal. You don't need a lab, a patent, or a scientist named Klaus. You just need a food truck, a beard, and the confidence to say \"same texture!\" to people who haven't eaten pork since 2011 and genuinely can't remember."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0368", "lang": "en", "target_id": "javascript", "target_name": "JavaScript", "facts": ["Written by Brendan Eich at Netscape in ten days in May 1995; named Mocha, then LiveScript, then JavaScript to ride Java's marketing, although the two are unrelated.", "typeof null returns 'object', a bug from the first version that has been kept for compatibility ever since.", "In March 2016 the author of left-pad, an 11-line npm package, unpublished it and broke builds across the industry, including Babel and React.", "[] + {} evaluates to '[object Object]'; {} + [] typed at a console evaluates to 0.", "Standardized by Ecma as 'ECMAScript' in 1997 because the name JavaScript is a trademark, first Sun's and now Oracle's."], "a": {"item_id": "dcee06ed92d9", "text": "Let's hear it for the people who write everything in JavaScript. You learned one language at a twelve-week bootcamp and decided the rest were optional. Frontend? JavaScript. Backend? Node. Desktop app? Electron, so your to-do list ships with its own copy of Chrome and eats four gigs of RAM to remind you to buy eggs. You wanted to be \"full-stack.\" What you got is a node_modules folder heavier than a neutron star and a career that collapses the day some stranger unpublishes left-pad. You rewrite your app every eighteen months because a new framework has a cooler logo. When it breaks in production, you stare at \"undefined is not a function\" and blame the user. Then you install TypeScript, which is just JavaScript wearing a lanyard so you can feel like a real engineer."}, "b": {"item_id": "c69b78416cbb", "text": "Oh, you write everything in JavaScript? Beautiful. You wanted one language, so you picked the one where `typeof null` says \"object\" and nobody has apologized in thirty years. Your backend runs on Node so your frontend friends feel at home. Your little file-renaming script has four hundred megabytes in `node_modules` and a `package.json` longer than the novel you never finished. You're not programming. You're running `npm install` and praying it finishes before some eleven-line package vanishes from the registry. When people ask why, you say \"full stack,\" which is what you call it when your whole life rides on a dependency you've never read."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0369", "lang": "en", "target_id": "nfts", "target_name": "NFTs", "facts": ["Beeple's 'Everydays: The First 5000 Days' sold at Christie's for $69.3 million in March 2021.", "OpenSea, the largest NFT marketplace, processed a record $2.7 billion in trades on 1 May 2022 and just $9.34 million on 28 August 2022, a 99% drop, according to DappRadar data.", "A 2023 dappGambl study found that 95% of the NFT collections it examined had a market value of zero.", "The token usually records only a URL; the image itself sits on an ordinary server or IPFS, not on the blockchain.", "Collins Dictionary named 'NFT' its word of the year for 2021."], "a": {"item_id": "41fe71c9494d", "text": "Let me tell you about the guy who bought a cartoon monkey in 2021. Not a monkey. A JPEG of a monkey, with a receipt that says he owns it. He pays four figures in gas fees to a stranger in Discord named \"Thanos_Fan_69\" who promised him \"utility.\" What utility? He gets a little ape on his Twitter profile. That's it. That's the utility. He changed his profile picture to a rectangle of a monkey wearing sunglasses, and now he's a \"founder.\" Founder of what? A group chat with eleven thousand people who all say \"wagmi\" like it's a prayer they don't understand. Every morning he checks the floor price like a man checking a pulse. Diamond hands, he says. Diamond hands, the bathroom mirror says back. Ten-dollar haircut, no sleep."}, "b": {"item_id": "e8eddc47095c", "text": "Nothing says \"I'm early\" like paying $300 in gas fees to join a Discord with forty thousand other people who are also early. You didn't buy art, Kyle. You bought a seat in a group chat where everyone types \"gm\" at strangers like they're greeting a medieval village. You told your mom it was \"a community.\" You changed your Twitter avatar to a hexagon and started saying \"ngmi\" to coworkers who just asked about the Wi-Fi password. Your whole investment thesis is \"number go up,\" and your exit strategy is finding someone dumber, who is also in the Discord, who is also looking for you. You check the floor price at red lights. You've cried over a Discord mod's emoji reaction. You spent your tax refund to be called \"anon\" by a guy named CryptoChad420, and the only thing you walked away with is proof that you were there when it peaked."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0370", "lang": "en", "target_id": "carbon-nanotubes", "target_name": "Carbon nanotubes", "facts": ["Reported by Sumio Iijima in Nature in 1991.", "Promised as the cable for a space elevator; a 2006 analysis showed that ordinary defects cut the strength of a macroscopic cable far below the ideal value.", "Long, rigid multi-walled nanotubes behaved like asbestos fibres in a 2008 mouse study in Nature Nanotechnology.", "Sorting metallic from semiconducting tubes has blocked nanotube electronics for three decades; MIT's 16-bit RV16X-NANO processor (2019) is still a lab demonstration.", "Today they are sold mainly as a conductive additive in lithium-ion battery electrodes."], "a": {"item_id": "d717e15fa133", "text": "You've been \"two years from commercialization\" since the Clinton administration. Your founder has a PhD, a patent, and the confidence of a man who's never had to ship anything in a quantity larger than a vial.\n\nSlide three is the space elevator. Everyone knows it's a joke, including you, but you keep it in because it's the only slide where the investors stop checking their phones.\n\nYou say \"TAM\" and mean \"every industry that has ever existed.\" Batteries, concrete, tennis rackets, sunscreen. You've pitched so many markets you've never actually sold to one.\n\nWhat are you after? A seed round, a lab with your name on it, and one more year before anyone asks about price per kilogram.\n\nWhat do you get? A warm handshake, a \"fascinating science,\" and a follow-up email that says \"keep us posted.\""}, "b": {"item_id": "a9505577820b", "text": "Every carbon nanotube pitch opens with a slide calling it the strongest material ever made, then a space elevator, then a hockey-stick graph that hits commercial scale in 2031. The founder is a former postdoc who spent three years coaxing one gram out of a black hairball and now calls the hairball \"proprietary dispersion.\" They want your money to build a factory that will produce exactly enough for a demo vial. What it gets them: a Series A, a TED talk, and a lifetime of saying \"we're basically in pilot\" at dinner parties. You get a framed bucky-paper certificate and the promise that the only obstacle left is \"just manufacturing.\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0371", "lang": "en", "target_id": "javascript", "target_name": "JavaScript", "facts": ["Written by Brendan Eich at Netscape in ten days in May 1995; named Mocha, then LiveScript, then JavaScript to ride Java's marketing, although the two are unrelated.", "typeof null returns 'object', a bug from the first version that has been kept for compatibility ever since.", "In March 2016 the author of left-pad, an 11-line npm package, unpublished it and broke builds across the industry, including Babel and React.", "[] + {} evaluates to '[object Object]'; {} + [] typed at a console evaluates to 0.", "Standardized by Ecma as 'ECMAScript' in 1997 because the name JavaScript is a trademark, first Sun's and now Oracle's."], "a": {"item_id": "6fc599fe456a", "text": "You write everything in JavaScript? Beautiful. You're the guy who wanted one language so badly you married it. Backend, build tools, phone app, the script that renames your screenshots: all JavaScript, all held together by a node_modules folder heavier than your laptop's actual contents. You didn't choose simplicity. You chose a single abusive relationship and call it commitment. When people ask why, you say \"full-stack,\" which is what you call a stack you can't see the bottom of. Your server went down last week because of a package named is-odd, installed at 2 a.m. by you, who never once checked what it does. You own three frameworks, one half-finished side project, and a deep personal belief that typeof null is a feature you've made peace with."}, "b": {"item_id": "7c79a2ca0b0b", "text": "You wrote your backend in JavaScript, your frontend in JavaScript, your build scripts in JavaScript, and your smart fridge dashboard in JavaScript, and you call it \"full-stack\" because \"only-stack\" sounds like a cry for help. You said you wanted to avoid context switching. Congratulations: you now switch between four frameworks that all launched this quarter.\n\nYou're not a polyglot. You're someone who learned one language and renamed every problem until it fit.\n\nWhat did it get you? A node_modules folder heavier than your product, a notepad app that ships its own browser and eats two gigs of RAM, and a TypeScript migration to apologize for the original decision. Your Hello World has 1,400 dependencies, one of them maintained by a guy who once deleted eleven lines and broke the internet.\n\nBut you're hireable. Every posting wants you. That's not a career, that's a hostage situation with stock options."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0372", "lang": "en", "target_id": "google-glass", "target_name": "Google Glass", "facts": ["Unveiled at Google I/O 2012 with skydivers livestreaming their jump through it onto the Moscone Center roof.", "The Explorer Edition sold to invited users in 2013 for $1,500.", "Wearers were nicknamed 'Glassholes'; bars, cinemas, and casinos banned the device over its camera.", "The consumer programme ended in January 2015; an Enterprise Edition continued until Google stopped selling it in 2023.", "Its display was a 640-by-360 prism in the corner of one eye."], "a": {"item_id": "260f23e62b71", "text": "Nobody wears Google Glass to a bar unless they paid fifteen hundred dollars to be called an Explorer and need the bartender to notice. You're not here for the beer. You're here for the moment the woman by the jukebox squints and says, \"Wait, are you recording me?\" That's the conversation you've been waiting your whole life for. Meanwhile you can't hear her, because you're reading a fantasy football text in the corner of your eye. You wink at the bartender to photograph the tap handles, and he photographs you back, which is the first picture anyone will ever have of you looking this lonely. You say \"OK Glass\" to the girl at the register, and she says \"Sure, buddy\" like she's talking to a smoke detector. You are a beta tester who never got the invitation to be a person."}, "b": {"item_id": "d051a1381bcf", "text": "Look at this guy. Paid fifteen hundred dollars to join the Explorer Program, and the only thing he's explored is the sidewalk outside every bar in San Francisco. He walks up and says \"OK Glass\" out loud, like a man ordering from a butler who isn't there. You came here to meet women, and you're the one person every woman in the room has already assumed is filming her. Buddy, when you wink at someone, she doesn't think you're flirting. She thinks you just took her picture. Because you did. You tap your temple like you're having a thought, and we all know you've never had one. Google gave up on you. Google, the company that kept Google+ alive for eight years, looked at you and said \"no.\" There's a word for you. It's Glasshole, and you earned it."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0373", "lang": "en", "target_id": "microsoft-teams", "target_name": "Microsoft Teams", "facts": ["Launched in 2017, bundled at no extra charge into Office 365, and replaced Skype for Business.", "After Slack's antitrust complaint, Microsoft unbundled Teams from Office in the EU in 2023 and worldwide in 2024; the European Commission still sent a statement of objections in 2024.", "Microsoft reported 320 million monthly active users in 2023.", "Microsoft shut down consumer Skype in May 2025 and sent its users to Teams."], "a": {"item_id": "38ae80798890", "text": "To the people who made us use Teams: congratulations. You surveyed every tool on earth and picked the one already in the bundle. That's not a decision, that's a vending machine with a Microsoft logo. Some procurement guy saved $3.80 a seat, got a slide with an upward arrow, and the rest of us got to spend our lives asking \"can everyone see my screen?\"\n\nAnd the managers, God bless you. You don't love Teams for collaboration. You love the little green dot. It's a traffic light for your trust issues. You don't need a platform, you need a smoke detector for Kevin. Meanwhile Kevin owns a mouse jiggler and a dream.\n\nYou called it \"one place for everything.\" Yes. Chats, teams, channels, and the file you swear is in the other team. Your legacy is a workforce that opens every meeting with \"sorry, let me just rejoin.\""}, "b": {"item_id": "294237b8889b", "text": "Let's hear it for the CIO who chose Microsoft Teams, a decision made the way you pick ketchup packets: it came free with the order. Nobody evaluated it. It showed up inside the Office 365 license like a toy in a cereal box, and this man stood up at the all-hands and called it a \"collaboration strategy.\" Sir, the European Commission had to force Microsoft to unbundle it. You lost a negotiation to a checkbox.\n\nAnd what did he really want? The little status dot. He doesn't read your work; he reads your color. Go yellow after five minutes of actual thinking and you're on a list. Congratulations: you saved forty grand on Slack and spent it all on adults jiggling their mouse like raccoons on a treadmill. Your files are lost somewhere in SharePoint, and your morale is permanently \"Away.\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0374", "lang": "en", "target_id": "microsoft-teams", "target_name": "Microsoft Teams", "facts": ["Launched in 2017, bundled at no extra charge into Office 365, and replaced Skype for Business.", "After Slack's antitrust complaint, Microsoft unbundled Teams from Office in the EU in 2023 and worldwide in 2024; the European Commission still sent a statement of objections in 2024.", "Microsoft reported 320 million monthly active users in 2023.", "Microsoft shut down consumer Skype in May 2025 and sent its users to Teams."], "a": {"item_id": "b048a3892478", "text": "Nobody asked for Teams. The people who forced it on us need a green dot next to your name to feel alive. Your manager has seventeen channels named after a project cancelled in March, and he still posts \"Quick question!\" at 6:52 p.m. just so you'll see the three dots and feel summoned. The IT guy who rolled it out got a bonus for \"adoption rates,\" which means he counted every time you clicked the purple icon by accident. They want proof you're working. Your status says \"Available\" while you're in the bathroom, and they're thrilled, because now they can watch you be available. Then comes the all-hands where forty people unmute at once, someone's dog barks, and the host says, \"Let's take this offline.\" They've never once been offline."}, "b": {"item_id": "a37866956a51", "text": "Let's talk about the people who made us switch to Teams. Not Microsoft. The guy in procurement who discovered it was \"already included\" in the license and presented that like a victory, like he found forty dollars in a coat. He didn't choose a tool. He picked the one free thing on the bundle, and now four thousand of us pay for his savings by saying \"can you see my screen?\" every morning.\n\nThen there are the managers, who love it for the green dot. That's what you wanted. Not collaboration. A little light that says Brenda is alive. Brenda goes yellow after five minutes of reading a spreadsheet, so now she jiggles her mouse like she's hand-cranking a Model T.\n\nAnd what do you get for all this? A quarterly slide: \"Adoption up 40%.\" Of course it's up. You banned the other ones. That's not adoption, that's a hostage count."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0375", "lang": "en", "target_id": "nfts", "target_name": "NFTs", "facts": ["Beeple's 'Everydays: The First 5000 Days' sold at Christie's for $69.3 million in March 2021.", "OpenSea, the largest NFT marketplace, processed a record $2.7 billion in trades on 1 May 2022 and just $9.34 million on 28 August 2022, a 99% drop, according to DappRadar data.", "A 2023 dappGambl study found that 95% of the NFT collections it examined had a market value of zero.", "The token usually records only a URL; the image itself sits on an ordinary server or IPFS, not on the blockchain.", "Collins Dictionary named 'NFT' its word of the year for 2021."], "a": {"item_id": "ff6086240a0d", "text": "Let's talk about the guy who bought a JPEG of a bored cartoon ape in 2021 for the price of a Honda. He's not an art collector. Art collectors own things. He owns a receipt. His whole personality now fits in a profile picture, and that picture is a cartoon ape wearing a bandana, the closest he's ever come to a self-portrait. He says \"wagmi\" unironically. He calls strangers \"ser.\" He joined a Discord to get whitelisted for a second Discord. Every morning he checks the floor price like it's his pulse, and he has never once looked at the thing itself, because the thing was never the point. The point was being the guy who got in early, six months before he became the guy who got out late."}, "b": {"item_id": "ce82b5a76298", "text": "Nothing says \"I'm early\" like buying into something the same year Christie's did. You spent your tax refund on a cartoon ape, and now you check the floor price at red lights like a man waiting on biopsy results. You put \"ETH\" in your Twitter name and a hexagon on your profile picture, and you say \"gm\" to strangers at 4 p.m. because a guy named CryptoChad_Baby said that's how you make it.\n\nMake what? You paid $300 in gas fees to join a Discord with 40,000 people and one moderator, who keeps DMing to say you've won something. You did win something. It's called getting your wallet drained.\n\nThe dream was never art. The dream was selling to a guy who's hoping to sell to a guy. You're not an investor, you're a link in a chain letter with a Discord. And congratulations, you're the last link."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0376", "lang": "en", "target_id": "almond-milk", "target_name": "Almond milk", "facts": ["California grows about 80% of the world's almonds.", "A single almond takes about 1.1 US gallons (roughly 4 litres) of water to grow, a figure Mother Jones published in 2014 that spread during California's drought.", "A 2015 US class action alleged Almond Breeze contained only about 2% almonds; it partly survived a motion to dismiss, was stayed in 2017 pending a separate Blue Diamond labelling settlement, and the plaintiffs then dropped it.", "Pollinating California's almond orchards each February requires trucking in most of the commercial honeybee colonies in the United States.", "Medieval European cooks used almond milk as a Lenten substitute for dairy, centuries before the current fashion."], "a": {"item_id": "eb9e6316dbc1", "text": "So you switched to almond milk to save the planet. Congratulations. You found out it takes about a gallon of water to grow one almond, and your solution was to drink the almonds. You're the only person in America who needed a thirsty California orchard to hold your hand. You say \"oat\" like it's a personality. You order a flat white with almond and watch the barista sigh as it splits like a bad marriage. You swear you're not preachy, then you explain bee pollination trucks to a man who just asked for ketchup. The planet is grateful. The almonds are thirsty. And you have never looked more like someone whose entire carbon offset is a hashtag."}, "b": {"item_id": "cf146e52ac24", "text": "You switched to almond milk to save the planet, which is adorable, because you did it the way you do everything: you read one headline about cows and stopped there. Meanwhile, almonds drink California's drought like a frat boy at a keg, and the bees get trucked in like seasonal labor so you can feel superior in a Tuesday latte. You didn't save the planet. You outsourced your guilt to a state that's on fire.\n\nAnd you told people. Nobody asked, Brent. You announced it at brunch like you'd donated a kidney. You pay an extra dollar fifty for a carton that's about 2% almonds, so you're basically funding a rumor of a nut. Then you flew to Tulum for your birthday.\n\nNow you've heard oat is the responsible choice, so you're quietly converting, and by spring you'll act like you were never on almond's side. That's not moral growth. That's a subscription to guilt with a new flavor every year."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0377", "lang": "en", "target_id": "vim", "target_name": "Vim", "facts": ["Released by Bram Moolenaar in November 1991 as 'Vi IMitation', a vi clone he had begun writing for the Amiga in 1988 on top of Stevie; the expansion became 'Vi IMproved' with version 2.0 in December 1993.", "Licensed as charityware: the licence asks users to donate to ICCF Holland, a charity Moolenaar ran for children in Uganda.", "Stack Overflow's question 'How to exit the Vim editor?' passed one million visits in May 2017, which Stack Overflow marked with a blog post.", "Modal editing: in normal mode, typing letters runs commands rather than inserting text; :wq saves and quits, :q! quits and discards changes."], "a": {"item_id": "11a570c2e08d", "text": "Let's hear it for the Vim people. You can always spot one, because they'll tell you. Unprompted. Usually while you're mid-sentence about something else, like your divorce.\n\nThese folks spent three weekends tuning a .vimrc to save four seconds renaming a variable. That's a payback period of eleven thousand years, but sure, you're the efficient one.\n\nThey remapped Caps Lock to Escape and now can't type a password on anyone else's laptop. Their dotfiles repo has more stars than their actual projects. They say \"ciw\" out loud like it's a spell from a wizard who peaked in 1991.\n\nAnd what's the prize? You type :wq at the end of every Google Doc. You hit j in Slack and post the letter j to forty coworkers. Congratulations: you're the fastest typist in a room everyone already left."}, "b": {"item_id": "321a52e03ab6", "text": "Every Vim person has to tell you about Vim. You say \"nice weather\" at brunch and they say, \"Have you tried modal editing?\" They'll sit through your wedding toast in Normal mode, just so they can feel superior while hitting Escape. Their whole personality is hjkl. They don't write code. They perform a sport nobody asked them to join. And the best part is they can't get out. Ask one how to quit and they'll smirk, type a colon, and say, \"Ah, you just need to know the commands.\" Twenty years later he's still typing :wq, still telling you it's faster. Faster than what? He's never left the room."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0378", "lang": "en", "target_id": "graphene", "target_name": "Graphene", "facts": ["Isolated in 2004 by Andre Geim and Konstantin Novoselov at Manchester by peeling graphite with Scotch tape; Nobel Prize in Physics, 2010.", "The EU's Graphene Flagship, launched in 2013, committed €1 billion over ten years.", "A 2018 study in Advanced Materials tested 'graphene' from 60 producers and found none with more than 50% graphene content; most was graphite flakes.", "Sold in tennis rackets, running shoes, and headphones as a performance ingredient.", "Geim also won an Ig Nobel Prize in 2000 for levitating a frog with magnets."], "a": {"item_id": "5342b17b4a3e", "text": "Every graphene founder has the same pitch: \"Stronger than steel, one atom thick, and it's going to change everything.\" Wonderful. Your funding timeline is also one atom thick, and it's also not holding anything up.\n\nYou've been \"five years away\" since two guys at Manchester peeled pencil residue off with Scotch tape and won a Nobel Prize. That's the greatest return on office supplies in history, and you've spent a billion-euro Flagship budget trying to repeat it.\n\nAnd what has the revolution delivered? A tennis racket. A jacket. A keynote speaker in a lanyard saying \"paradigm shift\" over a stock photo of honeycomb. In the lab it works flawlessly, one square centimeter at a time, on a Tuesday, if nobody breathes.\n\nBut you're not fools. You figured out the material doesn't have to scale as long as the grant renewal does. You didn't discover a wonder material. You discovered \"potential,\" and it's renewable."}, "b": {"item_id": "683abdc9f22c", "text": "Let's hear it for the graphene evangelists, the only people who've been \"five years away\" since 2004. Two guys in Manchester peeled pencil lead with Scotch tape, won a Nobel, and you looked at that and thought: finally, a career plan. Every grant proposal you write has the same three slides: space elevator, seawater you can drink, a phone that charges in six seconds. The funding agency nods like it's never seen them before, because it's seen them so many times it's slipped into a coma. Twenty years of press releases, and what have you actually shipped? A tennis racket. Headphones with \"GRAPHENE\" printed bigger than the brand name. You love telling people it's 200 times stronger than steel. Sure. Your claims, meanwhile, are one atom thick. You didn't discover a wonder material. You discovered wonder funding."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0379", "lang": "en", "target_id": "keurig", "target_name": "Keurig", "facts": ["John Sylvan invented the K-Cup in the early 1990s and sold his stake for about $50,000 in 1997; in 2015 he told The Atlantic, 'I feel bad sometimes that I ever did it.'", "Keurig 2.0 (2014) read an ink mark on the lid and rejected unlicensed pods; sales fell and workarounds spread online.", "By 2014 enough K-Cups were sold each year to circle the Earth more than ten times; the pods were mostly unrecyclable #7 plastic until a switch to polypropylene by 2020.", "Keurig Green Mountain merged with Dr Pepper Snapple in 2018 to form Keurig Dr Pepper."], "a": {"item_id": "8a5105614740", "text": "Keurig people want coffee the way they want feelings: one serving, sealed, no cleanup. You're not a coffee drinker, you're a pod collector. You bought the variety pack, and now you're thirty-eight, rationing the last Donut Shop because all that's left is hazelnut and a hot cocoa you're saving for a breakdown. You press \"Strong\" like it's a personality trait. You swore you'd use the reusable filter, and you bought one. It's in a drawer next to your gym key fob. You saved four whole minutes, and you spend them staring at a blinking descale light you've decided is decorative. The guy who invented the pod publicly regrets it. You built your mornings around a man's biggest apology. Enjoy your landfill latte, champ. Ready in sixty seconds, just like your effort."}, "b": {"item_id": "299bf476f938", "text": "You know who makes coffee in a Keurig? People who want to feel like a barista without the one thing a barista needs, which is caring. You're not making coffee. You're pressing a button and standing there for ninety seconds like the machine owes you a personality. Your mug says \"Coffee Snob,\" and you bought it in the same aisle as the 48-count variety pack, because you can't commit to one flavor, just like your gym membership. Hazelnut, Dark Magic, Donut Shop, and one mystery pod called Winter Blend that tastes like a wet cardboard box. You tell people you're \"not really a coffee person\" while on your third cup before noon. Your goal isn't flavor. It's reaching the office by 8:15 with something warm in your hand and a plausible excuse for why you still feel a little hollow."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0380", "lang": "en", "target_id": "javascript", "target_name": "JavaScript", "facts": ["Written by Brendan Eich at Netscape in ten days in May 1995; named Mocha, then LiveScript, then JavaScript to ride Java's marketing, although the two are unrelated.", "typeof null returns 'object', a bug from the first version that has been kept for compatibility ever since.", "In March 2016 the author of left-pad, an 11-line npm package, unpublished it and broke builds across the industry, including Babel and React.", "[] + {} evaluates to '[object Object]'; {} + [] typed at a console evaluates to 0.", "Standardized by Ecma as 'ECMAScript' in 1997 because the name JavaScript is a trademark, first Sun's and now Oracle's."], "a": {"item_id": "76ccf08d2bf4", "text": "You heard \"full-stack\" and decided it meant one hammer and a stack of whatever you're hitting. You wrote the backend in JavaScript to avoid context-switching, which is adorable, because you've switched frameworks four times since March. Your Electron app uses 900 megabytes of RAM to show a to-do list, and you call that \"cross-platform\" the way a man towing a boat calls it \"a sports package.\"\n\nWhat are you after? Hireability, and the warm feeling that every job posting has your name on it. What do you get? A node_modules folder heavier than your personality, a loyalty to a package called is-odd that you'd defend in court, and a weekend \"migration to TypeScript\" that you'll describe as \"mostly done\" until you retire.\n\nYou didn't pick one language to rule them all. You just refuse to learn a second one and gave it a LinkedIn headline."}, "b": {"item_id": "9f280ef92811", "text": "Let's hear it for the people who write everything in JavaScript. You learned one language at a bootcamp and decided the rest of computing could adapt. Backend? Node. Desktop? Electron: a to-do list that eats four gigs of RAM because it ships its own copy of Chrome. Mobile? React Native. Your toaster is probably a Next.js app by now.\n\nYou call it \"full-stack.\" Your node_modules folder calls it a landfill. You npm-installed a package to pad a string, and when its author deleted it, half the internet fell over.\n\nEvery year you rewrite your whole app in a new framework, then bolt on TypeScript so you can pretend you chose a real language.\n\nAnd what does it get you? A résumé that says \"versatile,\" and a career spent asking why undefined is not a function."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0381", "lang": "en", "target_id": "durian", "target_name": "Durian", "facts": ["Banned on Singapore's MRT trains and in many Southeast Asian hotels because of the smell.", "A 2017 genome study in Nature Genetics traced the odour to a duplicated family of MGL genes that pump out volatile sulfur compounds.", "China buys most of the world's durian exports, chiefly from Thailand, in a trade worth billions of dollars a year.", "Alfred Russel Wallace wrote in 1856 that eating it was 'a new sensation worth a voyage to the East to experience'."], "a": {"item_id": "5e02113e1bb8", "text": "You know who eats durian on the train? A man who has never once been told no. He's not eating fruit. He's eating a personality. He reads the NO DURIAN sign like it's a personal challenge, then cracks that spiky husk with the confidence of someone who watched one video. Then he looks up at the carriage and says, \"Honestly? Tastes like custard.\" Sir, nobody asked for a dessert review. You're not tasting custard. You're tasting attention. Every bite is a little flag planted in the aisle. And the worst part? Ten stops later he's still sniffing his fingers, grinning, like the smell is a medal he earned."}, "b": {"item_id": "0c82ba06de9c", "text": "People who eat durian in public aren't hungry. They're recruiting. Every bite comes with a sermon: \"You just haven't had a *good* one.\" Sir, I'm on a bus. I didn't ask to join your church.\n\nSingapore fines people for bringing it on the train, and these folks hear that like a dare. They're the only foodies whose restaurant review gets filed as a police report.\n\nAnd the payoff? Seventy dollars on a Musang King so you can watch strangers silently change seats. You paid premium to become a fire drill. Your date calls it \"an acquired taste,\" which is exactly what you say about someone you're about to break up with.\n\nThen they stroll back to the hotel, walk right past the sign with a red circle through their entire personality, and still act shocked when housekeeping files a complaint."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0382", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hydrogen-cars", "target_name": "Hydrogen fuel-cell cars", "facts": ["Toyota launched the Mirai in 2014; 'mirai' means 'future' in Japanese.", "Nearly all US hydrogen filling stations are in California; Shell closed all seven of its California passenger-car stations in February 2024.", "More than 95% of the world's hydrogen is made from fossil fuels, mostly by steam-reforming natural gas.", "California pump prices reached about $36 per kilogram in 2023-24, over $200 for a Mirai fill; Toyota has handed buyers fuel cards worth $15,000.", "Mirai owners filed a class action against Toyota in July 2024 over hydrogen shortages and station outages; a second suit in 2025 sought at least $5.7 billion."], "a": {"item_id": "e2e119f95d37", "text": "You bought a hydrogen car because electric felt too mainstream. You wanted the future, but with a longer commute. Congratulations: you're the only person in California who plans a road trip around a gas station's Instagram page, praying the one pump in Fresno isn't \"temporarily offline,\" which is station for \"gone.\"\n\nYou say you're saving the planet, but your fuel is mostly made from natural gas. That's a vegan who only eats at steakhouses for the salad.\n\nThe dealer threw in a free fuel card, the way you give a toddler a tablet so he stops screaming. When it runs out, you'll pay more per mile than a guy in a V8 pickup, in a car with the resale value of a Blockbuster membership.\n\nAnd you keep saying \"the only thing it emits is water,\" like that's a flex. Sir, you leased a very expensive dehumidifier that occasionally takes you to work."}, "b": {"item_id": "5d75fbe56d0a", "text": "Hydrogen car owners are the only people who looked at Tesla drivers and thought, \"Not enough range anxiety.\" You didn't buy a car; you entered a relationship with the three working pumps in California and an app that tells you which one is \"offline\" today. You wanted to save the planet. Instead you burned through Toyota's free fuel card in a year at thirty-six bucks a kilo, filling up with hydrogen made from natural gas. Congratulations, you invented a gas car with extra steps. You love saying the only emission is water. Mostly it's your tears at a shuttered Shell station in Fremont. And resale? Your Mirai lost value faster than your friendships after you said \"hydrogen economy\" at brunch. You're not early adopters. You're unpaid beta testers who drive two hours to refuel."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0383", "lang": "en", "target_id": "las-vegas", "target_name": "Las Vegas", "facts": ["Nevada legalized casino gambling in 1931, the year construction of the Hoover Dam began nearby.", "Most of the Strip lies not in the city of Las Vegas but in the unincorporated town of Paradise.", "The Sphere, opened in 2023 at a cost of about $2.3 billion, is the largest spherical building in the world.", "'What happens here, stays here' was launched by the local convention and visitors authority in 2003.", "Lake Mead, its water source, fell in July 2022 to about 1,040 feet, its lowest since the reservoir first filled in 1937, and dropped below that mark again in August 2026."], "a": {"item_id": "d992f33818f6", "text": "You're not going to Vegas to get rich. You're going to Vegas to donate to a fountain show. Look at you, booking a $49 Spirit flight to \"beat the house,\" like the house didn't build a fake Eiffel Tower with money from guys exactly like you. You've got a roulette \"system.\" You wrote it on a cocktail napkin. Red's due. Red is never due. Red doesn't know you. By midnight you're on Fremont Street feeding a Wheel of Fortune slot in an Ed Hardy shirt you've owned since 2008, telling a stranger you're \"only down twelve hundred.\" By 3 a.m. the Players Club bumps you to Platinum, which is casino for \"we'll be needing your kidneys.\" And you'll call it a win, because they comped you a buffet. Congratulations. You flew 400 miles to pay $3,000 for crab legs."}, "b": {"item_id": "c0bc98e0b0fb", "text": "You flew to Vegas to get rich. Think about that. You looked at a city built on the mathematical certainty that you will lose and said, \"Yeah, but I've got a feeling.\"\n\nYou have a system, of course. The system is a YouTube video from 2011 and a lucky shirt. You're up forty dollars at blackjack and suddenly you're tipping the dealer like a man whose yacht is double-parked outside.\n\nBy 4 a.m. you're hunched at a Wheel of Fortune slot, pressing a button that does nothing, drinking a free vodka cranberry like it's a salary. You've taken out cash three times, and the ATM fee has now earned more than you have.\n\nYou'll fly home with a buffet hangover, a sunburn from a pool you were too hungover to enjoy, and a cash-out ticket for $3.47 you're too proud to redeem. And a story about how you were \"so close.\"\n\nYou came to beat the house. The house gave you a discount on the room because it knew you'd be back."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0384", "lang": "en", "target_id": "las-vegas", "target_name": "Las Vegas", "facts": ["Nevada legalized casino gambling in 1931, the year construction of the Hoover Dam began nearby.", "Most of the Strip lies not in the city of Las Vegas but in the unincorporated town of Paradise.", "The Sphere, opened in 2023 at a cost of about $2.3 billion, is the largest spherical building in the world.", "'What happens here, stays here' was launched by the local convention and visitors authority in 2003.", "Lake Mead, its water source, fell in July 2022 to about 1,040 feet, its lowest since the reservoir first filled in 1937, and dropped below that mark again in August 2026."], "a": {"item_id": "d992f33818f6", "text": "You're not going to Vegas to get rich. You're going to Vegas to donate to a fountain show. Look at you, booking a $49 Spirit flight to \"beat the house,\" like the house didn't build a fake Eiffel Tower with money from guys exactly like you. You've got a roulette \"system.\" You wrote it on a cocktail napkin. Red's due. Red is never due. Red doesn't know you. By midnight you're on Fremont Street feeding a Wheel of Fortune slot in an Ed Hardy shirt you've owned since 2008, telling a stranger you're \"only down twelve hundred.\" By 3 a.m. the Players Club bumps you to Platinum, which is casino for \"we'll be needing your kidneys.\" And you'll call it a win, because they comped you a buffet. Congratulations. You flew 400 miles to pay $3,000 for crab legs."}, "b": {"item_id": "7dd28f44edd6", "text": "You're flying to Vegas to get rich. Think about that. You looked at your finances and said, \"The answer is a building with no clocks, built by mathematicians who have never once lost.\"\n\nBut you've got a system. Of course you do. You read a forum post by a guy called CrapsDaddy69, and now you're betting the don't-pass like you're defusing a bomb.\n\nYou'll sit down with $500 you're \"totally fine losing.\" By midnight you'll be at the ATM, paying a nine-dollar fee to access your own despair. And you'll be happy, because a woman in a sequined bodysuit brought you a free Miller Lite, and in your head that's a 4% return.\n\nYou'll be up $60 exactly once. That's the whole trip. You'll tell that story for eleven years.\n\nThen you'll fly home broke, sunburned, and wearing a lanyard from a convention you skipped, having made someone rich. Just not you."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0385", "lang": "en", "target_id": "canberra", "target_name": "Canberra", "facts": ["Chosen as a compromise because neither Sydney nor Melbourne would accept the other as capital; Australia's constitution required the capital to be at least 100 miles from Sydney.", "Designed by the Chicago architects Walter Burley Griffin and Marion Mahony Griffin, who won an international competition in 1912.", "Its centrepiece, Lake Burley Griffin, is artificial, created by damming the Molonglo River in 1963-64.", "Parliament House (1988) is built into Capital Hill under a lawn meant for citizens to walk over their politicians; the lawn was fenced off in 2017 for security.", "Melbourne served as the capital until 1927 while Canberra was being built."], "a": {"item_id": "4906cad02ec9", "text": "You moved to Canberra for the job. Brave. You looked at Sydney rents, did the maths, and decided your personality could be replaced by a security clearance. Now you tell strangers you \"can't really talk about what you do,\" and they're relieved, because it's a briefing note about another briefing note for Senate Estimates.\n\nYou came for the ladder: APS5, APS6, EL1, a lanyard that opens one extra door. You stayed because you bought in Gungahlin and the mortgage is now your closest friend.\n\nEvery Friday you say you're \"basically in Sydney,\" which means three hours up the Hume, past a giant Merino, to feel alive for one weekend. Then it's back to a Braddon brunch with the same six people from your branch.\n\nBut hey, you're close to the snow. You've been once. You'll mention it forever."}, "b": {"item_id": "e90ceb66c6fb", "text": "You moved to Canberra for a \"two-year stint to get your EL1,\" which is what everyone says, including the guy who's been saying it since 2011 and now owns a Subaru and strong opinions about Gungahlin. You joined to \"shape policy,\" and your policy is now formatting other people's policy until a Deputy Secretary changes the font. You wanted impact. You got a briefing note that three people read, one of whom was you, and that was to check for typos.\n\nYou left Sydney because you couldn't afford a house, and now you own one in Chisholm, so congratulations, you've got a mortgage and a view of a Bunnings.\n\nAt parties you ask \"which department?\" like it's a star sign. Every long weekend you flee to Melbourne, where friends ask when you're coming home, and you say \"soon,\" knowing you've got a clearance, a generous super match, and no way out.\n\nYou didn't climb the ladder. You joined the queue."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0386", "lang": "en", "target_id": "google-sheets", "target_name": "Google Sheets", "facts": ["Launched in June 2006 as Google Spreadsheets, built on XL2Web, a product Google acquired from 2Web Technologies.", "A spreadsheet is capped at 10 million cells (raised from 5 million in 2022).", "Functions such as IMPORTHTML, IMPORTXML, and GOOGLEFINANCE pull live web data straight into cells.", "Free with a Google account; files live in Google Drive, and collaborators edit the same sheet simultaneously with coloured cursors.", "In a browser, offline editing works only in Chrome or Edge, with the Google Docs Offline extension installed and offline access switched on while still connected; the Android and iOS apps work offline natively."], "a": {"item_id": "ed3e91782987", "text": "Let's hear it for the manager who runs a twelve-person team out of a Google Sheet. You didn't want to pay for Asana; you wanted to feel like you *built* Asana. Look at you, color-coding rows like a kindergarten teacher with admin rights. Your whole org chart is a tab called \"Roster FINAL (2).\" Your performance reviews are conditional formatting: green means safe, red means Kyle. And every morning you open it, see Anonymous Capybara lurking at row 400, and feel genuinely hunted. Somebody sorted one column without the others last Tuesday, and now Priya reports to the office printer. You fixed it through version history like a detective reconstructing a murder. That's the dream, right? Control. Total visibility. And what you got is forty \"Request edit access\" emails and a VLOOKUP holding up the company like a Jenga block."}, "b": {"item_id": "2bbca6d87206", "text": "You're not a manager, you're a guy who got comfortable with IMPORTRANGE and mistook it for leadership. You said \"we don't need Jira, a sheet works fine,\" and now you have eleven tabs, three named \"Final,\" and one called \"Copy of Final (Dave's).\"\n\nWhat are you after? Control, disguised as transparency. You want everyone to see everything, as long as only you understand the formulas. Your team doesn't update the sheet. They open it, spot an anonymous capybara hovering over cell F14, and quietly leave.\n\nWhat does it get you? A color-coding system only you can decode, a Status column that has said \"In progress\" since March, and the rush of a stakeholder clicking \"Request edit access,\" the closest you'll ever come to being feared.\n\nMeanwhile, Version History knows who broke it. You know it too. You just won't say."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0387", "lang": "en", "target_id": "slack", "target_name": "Slack", "facts": ["Spun out in 2013 from the internal chat tool of Tiny Speck's failed game Glitch; founder Stewart Butterfield had earlier spun Flickr out of another failed game.", "The name is a backronym: 'Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge'.", "Salesforce bought it for $27.7 billion in 2021.", "Filed an EU antitrust complaint against Microsoft in 2020 over the bundling of Teams with Office."], "a": {"item_id": "c5f48d20bb73", "text": "You didn't build a company, you built a chat room with payroll. Your \"culture\" is a custom emoji of your CTO's face, and that's the only thing anyone has shipped this quarter.\n\nYou said Slack would kill meetings. Now every \"quick question\" is a huddle that starts with \"can you hear me?\" and ends with \"let's move this to a thread.\"\n\nYou @channel the whole company to announce a bagel delivery, then act shocked that nobody reads your @channels. You've got 112 channels for 14 employees. Three of them are for work. One is #wins, and it's just you, reacting to your own post with a rocket.\n\nYou aren't managing anybody. You're watching a green dot and calling it trust. Someone's dot went gray at 5:01 and you took it personally.\n\nAnd the best part? \"Sorry, just seeing this!\" is now your company's mission statement."}, "b": {"item_id": "f56d27fef4cd", "text": "Nobody runs a company on Slack. They run it from #general, where forty people wait for the CEO to type \"morning all\" so they can reply with the waving hand. These are people who say \"let's take it to a thread\" like it's a moral position. They set their status to \"deep work\" and then react to everything with the eyes emoji. They believe the green dot means they're present. They are not present. They're in forty-one channels, four of them named #random-but-serious. Their deepest fear is a DM with no reply. Their proudest achievement is a \"quick sync\" that somehow ran until Thursday."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0388", "lang": "en", "target_id": "risk", "target_name": "Risk", "facts": ["Invented in 1957 by Albert Lamorisse, the French director of The Red Balloon, as La Conquête du Monde; Parker Brothers published it in the United States in 1959.", "The map has 42 territories, among them Kamchatka, Irkutsk, and Yakutsk, while the entire Middle East is one territory.", "Holding Australia, which has a single entry point, is the standard opening strategy.", "A full game can run for hours, and players eliminated early have nothing to do but watch."], "a": {"item_id": "ee9cd4f7db06", "text": "Let's talk about the people who play Risk to the end. Not the sane ones who flip the board at hour three. The finishers. You said \"quick game\" at noon. It's now eleven p.m. and you're attacking Kamchatka for the ninth time because Greg promised you peace in Europe. Greg has never kept a promise. Greg borrowed your charger in 2014.\n\nYou call it strategy. It's not. It's grudges with dice. One guy has sat in Australia since lunch, smug as a landlord, with four armies and a worldview. Another keeps announcing \"I'm trading in cards!\" like he's closing on a house. Meanwhile someone's girlfriend, who only came for the snacks, is quietly winning, and nobody will say it out loud.\n\nAnd the prize? Total domination of a card table. Congratulations. You conquered the world and lost the only seven people who would've come to your birthday."}, "b": {"item_id": "b165154a062e", "text": "Let's talk about the people who play Risk to the end. You know the guy. He says \"one more turn\" at 11 p.m. with a shift at seven tomorrow. He isn't in it for the win. He wants to be the last man standing over a continent he's never visited. Every game he claims he's \"not really into it,\" then spends four hours quietly stacking armies in Kamchatka. His girlfriend left during the Siberia stretch. His brother-in-law has been eliminated for three hours and still sits there, out of spite, hoping someone rolls snake eyes. These are people who have never conquered anything real, yet they guard Australia like it owes them rent. When he finally wins, nobody claps. Everyone just has to drive home and pretend they liked him."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0389", "lang": "en", "target_id": "vision-pro", "target_name": "Apple Vision Pro", "facts": ["Released in February 2024 at $3,499, with an external battery pack on a cable rated for about two hours.", "'EyeSight' shows a rendering of the wearer's eyes on an outward-facing screen.", "Apple calls it 'spatial computing' and its App Store rules tell developers not to describe apps as AR, VR, XR, or MR.", "Reports during 2024 said Apple had cut production; analysts' first-year sales estimates were well under a million units.", "Weighs 600-650 g, before the battery."], "a": {"item_id": "bb952c93608d", "text": "Nobody wears a Vision Pro on the subway to watch a movie. They're wearing it so forty strangers know they paid three thousand five hundred dollars to be the least present person in Brooklyn. They say \"spatial computing\" like it's a personality. They pinch the air to open windows that exist only for them, and their eyes glow at us through the front like a cult member's. That's the most honest thing about them: they're staring at something that isn't here. Meanwhile the rest of us watch a man at the 4 train's Atlantic Avenue stop move a floating spreadsheet with his fingers while his actual laptop sits in his bag, unused, feeling betrayed."}, "b": {"item_id": "ccb644462fd4", "text": "You paid $3,500 to be the only person on the L train who can't see the L train. You wanted to be noticed, and you are: everyone's filming you pinch the air like you're catching a very small, very expensive moth. You say it's for productivity, six floating windows, a virtual Mac, and you're answering one email: \"Per my last message.\" You're not in a private cinema, you're watching Moana 2 while a guy named Dennis eats a gyro against your shoulder.\n\nWhat you were after was never the screen. It was the question. \"Is that the Vision Pro?\" And you answer like it's an Oscar speech, peeling it off to reveal a red ring around your face, like a raccoon who lost a custody battle.\n\nYour reward: a dented forehead, a headache, and a stop you missed in Flushing."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0390", "lang": "en", "target_id": "stinky-tofu", "target_name": "Stinky tofu (臭豆腐)", "facts": ["Tofu soaked for hours to a day or two in a brine that has itself fermented for months from vegetables such as bamboo shoots and mustard greens, often with dried shrimp or meat; the smell is the brine's, not the bean's.", "Changsha's version is black, deep-fried and doused in chili; Taiwan's night-market version is golden and served with pickled cabbage; Shaoxing's, brined with amaranth stalks, is fried pale yellow or steamed with other 'stinky' pickles.", "Legend credits Wang Zhihe, a Qing-era exam candidate who left his unsold tofu too long in 1669; a Beijing fermented-bean-curd brand still carries his name.", "Street vendors are its main outlet, and the smell carries for a block."], "a": {"item_id": "04135b916762", "text": "Look at him under the night market lights, eating stinky tofu like he's auditioning for a documentary about bravery. Three blocks away, people are checking their shoes. He isn't eating it for flavor. He's eating it so his friends will say \"you're so brave,\" and he can answer \"it's an acquired taste,\" which is what people say when the only thing they've acquired is a reputation. Watch him wipe the chili sauce off his wrist, pretending the pickled cabbage is his favorite part. He waited twenty minutes in line just to become the guy who did it. And when he gets home, his jacket smells like a fermentation tank, and he calls it a souvenir."}, "b": {"item_id": "5be442dd726d", "text": "Look at you, standing in a night-market line everyone else is fleeing, like you're queuing to get into a fire. You followed that smell on purpose. Three blocks. Upwind. You're the only person alive who reads a warning as a recommendation.\n\nAnd you always need a witness. \"It smells like a sewer but tastes amazing!\" Yes, we know. You've said it to every foreigner you've ever met, then filmed their face gagging for content. Your entire personality is a dare you keep winning against tourists.\n\nAnd what does it get you? A paper cup, a toothpick, some pickled cabbage doing its best, and a jacket that now smells like a porta-potty at a wedding. Then you board the MRT and clear the whole car. You call that bold. The rest of us call it a hazmat incident with chili sauce."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0391", "lang": "en", "target_id": "esperanto", "target_name": "Esperanto", "facts": ["Published in Warsaw in 1887 by L. L. Zamenhof, a Białystok-born eye doctor, under the pen name Doktoro Esperanto, 'one who hopes'.", "Speaker estimates range from 100,000 to two million; roughly a thousand people grow up with it as a native language.", "William Shatner starred in Incubus (1966), a horror film shot entirely in Esperanto, with pronunciation Esperantists still mock.", "Its Wikipedia has over 350,000 articles, and its Duolingo course for English speakers had 1.36 million learners by July 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "d94ef91d85f7", "text": "Learning Esperanto is for people who looked at the twentieth century and thought, \"The real problem was conjugation.\" Zamenhof built it to end war, and his followers have bravely ended exactly one thing: their dinner invitations. You meet them at the World Congress, green star pinned to a fleece vest, shouting \"Saluton!\" at a Brazilian who also learned it on Duolingo. The big perk is Pasporta Servo: free couches worldwide, paid for by hearing your host explain why English is \"linguistic imperialism.\" Practical payoff? You can finally watch William Shatner's *Incubus* without subtitles. One hundred thirty years later, the dream of uniting humanity survives in a Telegram group of forty men named Bjorn."}, "b": {"item_id": "f5faac750f03", "text": "You spent six months learning a language designed to unite humanity, and your total audience is a retired Dutch man named Henk in a Telegram group. You didn't pick Esperanto because it's useful. You picked it because Spanish had a waiting list of people who were better at it than you. Here, you can be fluent by spring and the only competition is a guy who wears the green star pin to the grocery store.\n\nYou say you believe in world peace. You also correct people's accusatives in the comments section. Zamenhof dreamed of ending war, and a century later his legacy is you, alone, translating \"The Little Prince\" for a fourth time.\n\nYour big vacation is a World Congress, where you'll finally speak to thousands of people, all of whom are also there to speak to you."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0392", "lang": "en", "target_id": "stinky-tofu", "target_name": "Stinky tofu (臭豆腐)", "facts": ["Tofu soaked for hours to a day or two in a brine that has itself fermented for months from vegetables such as bamboo shoots and mustard greens, often with dried shrimp or meat; the smell is the brine's, not the bean's.", "Changsha's version is black, deep-fried and doused in chili; Taiwan's night-market version is golden and served with pickled cabbage; Shaoxing's, brined with amaranth stalks, is fried pale yellow or steamed with other 'stinky' pickles.", "Legend credits Wang Zhihe, a Qing-era exam candidate who left his unsold tofu too long in 1669; a Beijing fermented-bean-curd brand still carries his name.", "Street vendors are its main outlet, and the smell carries for a block."], "a": {"item_id": "04135b916762", "text": "Look at him under the night market lights, eating stinky tofu like he's auditioning for a documentary about bravery. Three blocks away, people are checking their shoes. He isn't eating it for flavor. He's eating it so his friends will say \"you're so brave,\" and he can answer \"it's an acquired taste,\" which is what people say when the only thing they've acquired is a reputation. Watch him wipe the chili sauce off his wrist, pretending the pickled cabbage is his favorite part. He waited twenty minutes in line just to become the guy who did it. And when he gets home, his jacket smells like a fermentation tank, and he calls it a souvenir."}, "b": {"item_id": "49bb8f6483c1", "text": "You're not eating stinky tofu, you're auditioning. Every tourist at the night market stands there with a toothpick and a paper tray, filming their own face for the \"I tried it!\" video, which is the only reason you ordered. Nobody craves this. You wanted a story, and now you've got one and a ten-minute ride on the MRT.\n\nAnd the locals are worse. \"You just have to get past the first bite,\" you say, the way people talk about marathons and their ex. You've turned a snack into a personality. You'll argue Taipei versus Changsha like it's a custody hearing.\n\nThen you walk home trailing a cloud like a divorced cologne ad, wondering why nobody sits next to you. You wanted to be adventurous. You got to be alone on a bus."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0393", "lang": "en", "target_id": "excel", "target_name": "Microsoft Excel", "facts": ["First shipped for the Macintosh in 1985; the Windows version followed in 1987.", "Silently converts gene names such as SEPT2 and MARCH1 into dates; by August 2020 the HUGO Gene Nomenclature Committee had renamed 27 human genes (MARCH1 became MARCHF1, SEPT2 became SEPTIN2) and published guidelines in Nature Genetics to stop it.", "In October 2020, Public Health England lost 15,841 COVID-19 cases when a legacy .xls file hit its 65,536-row limit.", "Reinhart and Rogoff's 2010 paper 'Growth in a Time of Debt', cited for austerity policies, omitted five countries through a spreadsheet range error found by a graduate student in 2013.", "Sheets have been capped at 1,048,576 rows by 16,384 columns since Excel 2007."], "a": {"item_id": "82eca86ba5a5", "text": "Nobody runs a company on Excel. Dave runs a company on Budget_FINAL_v7_USE_THIS_ONE.xlsx, which only Dave can open without the screen freezing for four minutes. Dave believes in control. Dave has forty tabs, and every one is named after a feeling: Forecast, Forecast2, Do Not Touch. He colors cells yellow for \"approved\" and orange for \"probably.\" His VLOOKUP holds payroll together like a prayer, and one #REF! away from a heart attack. He'll explain it to you at the Christmas party, sweating through a merged-cell cardigan. Dave doesn't want to be CEO. Dave wants you to need him."}, "b": {"item_id": "09a718e03e88", "text": "You don't run a company on Excel. You run it on Dave. Dave built the macro in 2014, Dave left in 2017, and now you pray to a button labeled \"DO NOT CLICK.\"\n\nYou tell investors you're \"data-driven.\" You're data-hostage. Your inventory system is a file called Final_v7_REAL_USE_THIS_ONE, and it's locked because Karen opened it at lunch and went on vacation.\n\nYou said you didn't want to pay for software. Congratulations, you've paid for it in nine hours a week of someone typing \"VLOOKUP\" with the face of a hostage reading a statement.\n\nYour ops manager is proud of having forty-seven tabs. That's not a business model, that's a hoarder's garage with a color-coding system.\n\nAnd every quarter, one cell says #REF!, and the whole leadership team stares at it like it's a diagnosis."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0394", "lang": "en", "target_id": "google-glass", "target_name": "Google Glass", "facts": ["Unveiled at Google I/O 2012 with skydivers livestreaming their jump through it onto the Moscone Center roof.", "The Explorer Edition sold to invited users in 2013 for $1,500.", "Wearers were nicknamed 'Glassholes'; bars, cinemas, and casinos banned the device over its camera.", "The consumer programme ended in January 2015; an Enterprise Edition continued until Google stopped selling it in 2023.", "Its display was a 640-by-360 prism in the corner of one eye."], "a": {"item_id": "4b59aaf692b9", "text": "You paid fifteen hundred dollars to become the only man in the bar the bartender watches more closely than the drunk guy. You wanted to look like the future. You look like someone about to be asked to leave by a stranger already hiding behind a pillar.\n\nYou walk in and say \"OK, Glass\" to a room that never asked, then repeat it louder over the jukebox. Nobody's impressed. Nobody's wondering what you're building. They're wondering if they're in your footage. It's the most attention you've ever gotten, and it's fear.\n\nYou wanted conversation starters. You got \"Are you recording me?\" and you said \"No,\" and then you winked. With you, that's a confession.\n\nGo home, Explorer. The only thing you've discovered is how fast a bar empties."}, "b": {"item_id": "abe9296c28ae", "text": "Look at this guy. Paid fifteen hundred dollars to join Google's \"Explorer Program,\" and the only thing he's explored is the sidewalk outside every bar that bounced him. He walks in thinking he's Tony Stark and walks out on a list, because nobody can tell if he's filming, and honestly, neither can he. He's here to meet women, and he gets results: every conversation ends the second he says \"OK Glass.\" Nothing kills the mood like a man asking his own face for directions to her place. He wanted to be the future. Instead the internet had to invent a brand-new word just for him: Glasshole. Silicon Valley promised to connect him to everything. It couldn't connect him to a second date, but it did get him his very own slur."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0395", "lang": "en", "target_id": "risk", "target_name": "Risk", "facts": ["Invented in 1957 by Albert Lamorisse, the French director of The Red Balloon, as La Conquête du Monde; Parker Brothers published it in the United States in 1959.", "The map has 42 territories, among them Kamchatka, Irkutsk, and Yakutsk, while the entire Middle East is one territory.", "Holding Australia, which has a single entry point, is the standard opening strategy.", "A full game can run for hours, and players eliminated early have nothing to do but watch."], "a": {"item_id": "b165154a062e", "text": "Let's talk about the people who play Risk to the end. You know the guy. He says \"one more turn\" at 11 p.m. with a shift at seven tomorrow. He isn't in it for the win. He wants to be the last man standing over a continent he's never visited. Every game he claims he's \"not really into it,\" then spends four hours quietly stacking armies in Kamchatka. His girlfriend left during the Siberia stretch. His brother-in-law has been eliminated for three hours and still sits there, out of spite, hoping someone rolls snake eyes. These are people who have never conquered anything real, yet they guard Australia like it owes them rent. When he finally wins, nobody claps. Everyone just has to drive home and pretend they liked him."}, "b": {"item_id": "623676ba302f", "text": "You people said \"world domination\" out loud and then scheduled it around dinner. Hour six, and the guy who was eliminated in hour two is still watching, because his ride is the winner.\n\nWho plays Risk to the end? The guy who turtled in Australia for four hours like a retiree in Boca, then acts shocked when no one respects his \"strong defensive position.\" The one who negotiates alliances with the gravity of the Treaty of Versailles, then breaks them over a single Kamchatka. Your whole personality is \"I'm a strategist,\" and your whole strategy is rolling three dice at your best friend.\n\nAnd the prize? You conquered the world. Congratulations: it's 2 a.m., your girlfriend's gone, and your brother won't speak to you. Napoleon at least got an island. You get to pick up 400 tiny plastic men."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0396", "lang": "en", "target_id": "carbon-nanotubes", "target_name": "Carbon nanotubes", "facts": ["Reported by Sumio Iijima in Nature in 1991.", "Promised as the cable for a space elevator; a 2006 analysis showed that ordinary defects cut the strength of a macroscopic cable far below the ideal value.", "Long, rigid multi-walled nanotubes behaved like asbestos fibres in a 2008 mouse study in Nature Nanotechnology.", "Sorting metallic from semiconducting tubes has blocked nanotube electronics for three decades; MIT's 16-bit RV16X-NANO processor (2019) is still a lab demonstration.", "Today they are sold mainly as a conductive additive in lithium-ion battery electrodes."], "a": {"item_id": "a9505577820b", "text": "Every carbon nanotube pitch opens with a slide calling it the strongest material ever made, then a space elevator, then a hockey-stick graph that hits commercial scale in 2031. The founder is a former postdoc who spent three years coaxing one gram out of a black hairball and now calls the hairball \"proprietary dispersion.\" They want your money to build a factory that will produce exactly enough for a demo vial. What it gets them: a Series A, a TED talk, and a lifetime of saying \"we're basically in pilot\" at dinner parties. You get a framed bucky-paper certificate and the promise that the only obstacle left is \"just manufacturing.\""}, "b": {"item_id": "221482c6c19c", "text": "You open with a space elevator, which tells me the last customer you spoke to was a grad student. Your deck says \"100 times stronger than steel,\" and every investor nods, because they heard it in 2009 from the guy before you, and the guy before him. You've been \"two years from commercialization\" since the Clinton administration.\n\nWhat are you after? Eight million dollars and the word \"platform.\" What do you get? A polite \"fascinating, let's circle back,\" a tour of the fund's kombucha tap, and a term sheet from a tennis racket company.\n\nYour market slide lists batteries, aerospace, sensors, concrete, and medicine. That isn't a market, that's a thesaurus. And when the one skeptical partner asks about dispersion, you suddenly get very interested in your water glass.\n\nYour real product is optimism with a PhD, sold by the gram at gold prices, to people who just want to know why it still clumps."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0397", "lang": "en", "target_id": "crocs", "target_name": "Crocs", "facts": ["Founded in Boulder, Colorado, in 2002; the first clog debuted at the Fort Lauderdale boat show as a boating shoe.", "Made of 'Croslite', which Crocs describes as a proprietary closed-cell resin foam and which third parties identify as an injection-moulded EVA foam.", "Balenciaga sold a platform Crocs for about $850 in 2018.", "The stock fell more than 90% from its 2007 peak during the 2008 crisis; the company then boomed in the pandemic and gave away 860,000 pairs to healthcare workers in 2020.", "The holes are officially 'ventilation ports' and the charms that go in them are 'Jibbitz', a company Crocs bought in 2006."], "a": {"item_id": "62e0599c4f11", "text": "People who wear Crocs out of the house have made peace with something the rest of us are still fighting. You didn't give up; you filed the paperwork. Every Croc wearer says the same thing: \"They're so comfortable.\" Yeah, so is a weighted blanket, Brenda, but you don't wear it to Olive Garden. And the Jibbitz! You've turned your feet into a personality quiz. A tiny taco, a tiny Minion, a tiny \"Live Laugh Love.\" You're forty-three and your shoe has a sticker collection. Then there's the guy who flips the strap back into \"sport mode\" like he's about to chase a pickpocket, when we both know he's never run anywhere except toward a sample tray at Costco. Nurses and chefs wear them because they're on their feet for twelve hours. You wear them to stand in line at Starbucks. Different heroism."}, "b": {"item_id": "837863df816e", "text": "Nobody wears Crocs out of the house to look good. They wear them to announce that they've stopped trying, and they want a medal for it. These are people who think the grocery store is a hospital hallway and they're the surgeon on break. They walk into Costco with forty holes in their feet and call the holes ventilation. Then they jam little plastic frogs and tiny tacos into those holes so the world knows they have a personality, and that personality is \"I gave up in 2009.\" What they get out of it is one sentence they say with their whole chest: \"They're just so comfortable.\" Meanwhile every tile floor in America sounds like a suction cup breathing through its teeth."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0398", "lang": "en", "target_id": "almond-milk", "target_name": "Almond milk", "facts": ["California grows about 80% of the world's almonds.", "A single almond takes about 1.1 US gallons (roughly 4 litres) of water to grow, a figure Mother Jones published in 2014 that spread during California's drought.", "A 2015 US class action alleged Almond Breeze contained only about 2% almonds; it partly survived a motion to dismiss, was stayed in 2017 pending a separate Blue Diamond labelling settlement, and the plaintiffs then dropped it.", "Pollinating California's almond orchards each February requires trucking in most of the commercial honeybee colonies in the United States.", "Medieval European cooks used almond milk as a Lenten substitute for dairy, centuries before the current fashion."], "a": {"item_id": "eb9e6316dbc1", "text": "So you switched to almond milk to save the planet. Congratulations. You found out it takes about a gallon of water to grow one almond, and your solution was to drink the almonds. You're the only person in America who needed a thirsty California orchard to hold your hand. You say \"oat\" like it's a personality. You order a flat white with almond and watch the barista sigh as it splits like a bad marriage. You swear you're not preachy, then you explain bee pollination trucks to a man who just asked for ketchup. The planet is grateful. The almonds are thirsty. And you have never looked more like someone whose entire carbon offset is a hashtag."}, "b": {"item_id": "f6708fabd3f6", "text": "You switched to almond milk to save the planet. Bold move, picking the one crop that drinks California like it's an open bar. Every latte you order, a farmer in the Central Valley watches his well go dry and thinks, \"At least Kayleigh's being sustainable.\"\n\nAnd you didn't save a cow. You just outsourced the suffering to bees, trucked cross-country every February to work themselves to death in an orchard, while you post about dairy being cruel.\n\nYou're not after a healthier Earth. You're after the barista hearing you say it. That little pause before \"almond, please\" is the biggest environmental impact you've ever had.\n\nAnd now the oat milk people look at you the way you used to look at skim drinkers. You picked a side in the milk war, and you lost."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0399", "lang": "en", "target_id": "beanie-babies", "target_name": "Beanie Babies", "facts": ["Launched by Ty Inc. in 1993 and sold only through small gift shops, with designs 'retired' to manufacture scarcity.", "At the height of the craze Beanie Babies accounted for about 10% of eBay's sales, and eBay's 1998 IPO filing said the site at times had over 30,000 Beanie Baby auctions running simultaneously in the quarter ending June 1998.", "In 1999 a divorcing couple divided their Beanie Baby collection one by one on a courtroom floor, in a photograph that ran worldwide.", "In 1999 Ty announced that Beanie Babies would be retired at the end of the year, then reversed itself after asking the public to vote and seeing an overwhelming vote to keep them.", "Most 'rare' Beanies that price guides valued in the thousands now sell for a few dollars."], "a": {"item_id": "876536d60647", "text": "Let's talk about the Beanie Baby investor. You know the type. He keeps the tush tag in a sealed plastic sleeve, because a tag with a crease is a lawsuit. A 1999 price guide sits on his nightstand like scripture. He sleeps beside a shelf of bears he hasn't touched since the Clinton administration and calls them \"the portfolio.\" He has never once hugged one, because hugging reduces value. His kids aren't allowed in the living room. He tells everyone it's his retirement plan, and somewhere in a storage unit in Ohio, a sixty-year-old man is whispering to a Princess bear. When he dies, he says, they'll be worth a fortune. That's the one thing he and those bears have in common: they're both waiting for someone to finally care."}, "b": {"item_id": "6a4b77c7b767", "text": "Let's hear it for the Beanie Baby investors, the only people who watched the 1990s stock market boom and thought, \"Too volatile. I'll put it all in Princess the Bear.\" These are folks who bought tag protectors before they bought life insurance. Who read a price guide sold at the same counter as the bears and called it due diligence. Ty would announce a \"retirement\" and you'd sprint to the Hallmark store like a rich uncle just died.\n\nTwenty-five years later, your portfolio is in a Rubbermaid tub in your mom's garage, and your exit strategy is an eBay listing: \"RARE Peanut, tag error, $40,000 OBO.\" Zero watchers. The only person who got rich off Beanie Babies got convicted of tax evasion. And honestly? He earned it. You handed it to him."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0400", "lang": "en", "target_id": "brasilia", "target_name": "Brasília", "facts": ["Inaugurated on 21 April 1960 after about 41 months of construction, moving the capital inland from Rio de Janeiro.", "Lúcio Costa's Plano Piloto is laid out like an airplane (or a bird, or a cross), with Oscar Niemeyer's government buildings along the fuselage.", "Listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1987, less than 30 years after it was built.", "Planned for 500,000 residents; the Federal District now has about 3 million, most in satellite towns outside the plan.", "Built for the car: wide avenues, few pedestrian crossings, and distances that make walking impractical."], "a": {"item_id": "5894439abe59", "text": "You're an architecture student, a tourist, or an intern, and all three of you looked at the Congress from the Rodoviária and said, \"Oh, that's right there.\" It is not right there. It's a mirage with a dome. Forty minutes later you're crossing a lawn at noon in 12 percent humidity, quoting Niemeyer to yourself like it's a hydration plan.\n\nAnd you're proud! You even cut a little dirt shortcut through the grass, the famous caminho do desejo, the only urban planning in Brasília done by someone who actually had to get somewhere. You were walking to a ministry for one signature, and the man who signs is out on Thursdays. It's Thursday.\n\nCongratulations, pilgrim. You've experienced modernism up close: a sunburn, a nosebleed, and a guy in a Corolla honking at you at the tesourinha like you're the one who's lost."}, "b": {"item_id": "cfeb728094dd", "text": "You're the guy walking from the Rodoviária to the Esplanada in July, backpack on, telling everyone \"it's only four kilometers.\" Four kilometers in a city built for helicopters. You chose the walk because you wanted to feel like a person in a place that forgot people exist. Your fitness tracker buzzes with pride while your shirt turns into a bath towel. You crossed the Eixão at a red light like a cowboy, then spent twenty minutes in an underpass hunting for the exit to a superquadra you swore was right there. Every car honks like it's doing you a favor. You arrive at the ministry looking like a wet sandwich, and you call it exercise."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0401", "lang": "en", "target_id": "nuclear-fusion", "target_name": "Nuclear fusion power", "facts": ["Has been 'about 30 years away' since the 1950s; at the 1955 Geneva atoms-for-peace conference, Homi Bhabha predicted fusion power within two decades.", "ITER, agreed in 2006 for first plasma in 2016, re-baselined in 2024 to start research operations in 2034 and deuterium-tritium burning in 2039, at a cost estimated above €20 billion.", "NIF's December 2022 'ignition' shot released 3.15 MJ from 2.05 MJ of laser energy; the lasers drew about 300 MJ from the grid to fire.", "Private fusion companies had raised more than $7 billion by 2024."], "a": {"item_id": "c5c23a966bf8", "text": "Let's hear it for the fusion people, who have been ten years away since your grandfather was ten years away. These are the only professionals who can miss a deadline for seventy straight years and get a bigger budget every time. Where else does that work? \"Sorry, honey, I'll be home for dinner in ten years.\" \"Great, here's twenty billion dollars.\"\n\nThey put the sun in a magnetic donut and act shocked when it won't sit still. ITER's been under construction so long the original welders have grandkids on the crew. And when they finally hit net gain, they celebrated like Moses with the tablets, then mumbled that it doesn't count the electricity for the lasers. That's not net gain. That's me saying I'm up at the casino if you don't count the buy-in.\n\nTen years, guys. Same as last time. Same tie, too."}, "b": {"item_id": "d77192f17243", "text": "Fusion scientists are the only people who can say \"ten years away\" and get a standing ovation, because nobody in the room was alive for the last ten years of promises. They've been ten years away since Eisenhower. ITER has been \"under construction\" longer than most of its engineers have been married. The startup founders have a slide deck with a glowing donut on it and a Series C that needs a press release before Christmas. Their investors aren't buying energy. They're buying the feeling of being in the room when the sun gets bottled. And the politicians show up in hard hats, cut a ribbon in front of a magnet, and fly home. Nobody's getting rich off fusion. They're getting tenure, a TED slot, and a career spent being almost there."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0402", "lang": "en", "target_id": "latex", "target_name": "LaTeX", "facts": ["Leslie Lamport wrote LaTeX in the early 1980s and released it in 1984 as a macro layer on Donald Knuth's TeX (1978), which Knuth built because he disliked how the galley proofs of the new edition of his own book looked.", "TeX's version number converges on π (3.141592653 since 2021), and Knuth's TeX bug bounty started at one hexadecimal dollar, $2.56, doubling yearly until frozen at $327.68; he stopped mailing real cheques in 2008 after fraud and now credits accounts at his fictional Bank of San Serriffe, and only 9 of the 275 cheques he sent from 2006 on were ever cashed.", "Its best-known warning is 'Underfull \\hbox (badness 10000)'; overfull lines are reported as 'Overfull \\hbox (Npt too wide)', and figures float to wherever the algorithm decides, not where the author put them.", "A single missing brace can produce an error reported pages away from the mistake, and a document can need three compiler passes to get its references right."], "a": {"item_id": "37e7504ee3eb", "text": "You spent three hours on a two-page memo, and two of them were the preamble. You loaded fourteen packages to say \"Please submit receipts by Friday.\" Your manager opens the PDF on his phone, sees Computer Modern, and assumes someone died, or got tenure.\n\nYou didn't pick LaTeX for the memo. You picked it so that, for one afternoon, a logistics update could look peer-reviewed. Your kerning is flawless, and nobody has ever noticed kerning except you, at 11 p.m., on TeX Stack Exchange, asking why your header won't align. A man named Egreg has told you, kindly and correctly, that you're doing it wrong.\n\nThe memo got two replies. One said \"Thanks.\" The other said \"Can you send this as a Word doc?\"\n\nAnd you did. You opened Word. You sat there, alone, crying in Calibri."}, "b": {"item_id": "c2eb8de50987", "text": "Nobody writes a two-page memo in LaTeX because they have to. They do it because they have a dissertation's worth of hubris and a sincere belief that the margins are the message. This is the guy who types \\documentclass{article} at his cubicle like he's summoning a demon. He spends ninety minutes getting one bullet point to hang properly, then emails the PDF to a coworker who opens it on her phone and asks, \"Can you just do it in Word?\" He wanted to look like a scientist. What he got was a memo that says \"Q3 Update\" in Computer Modern, and a reply-all from Doug asking what font that is."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0403", "lang": "en", "target_id": "php", "target_name": "PHP", "facts": ["Created by Rasmus Lerdorf in 1994 as 'Personal Home Page Tools', a set of C programs for tracking visitors to his own résumé page; the name now officially stands for the recursive 'PHP: Hypertext Preprocessor'.", "Lerdorf, in his 2007 MySQL Conference talk 'PHP on Hormones': 'I'm not a real programmer. I throw together things until it works then I move on.'", "Runs WordPress, which W3Techs counted on 39.4% of all websites (58.1% of sites with a known CMS) on 9 November 2026.", "Core function names are inconsistent (strpos, str_replace, htmlspecialchars, nl2br) partly because, as Lerdorf confirmed, early function names were chosen to balance a hash table keyed on string length.", "Until PHP 8 (2020), the comparison 0 == 'foo' was true because the string was silently converted to a number."], "a": {"item_id": "286b3c527101", "text": "You write PHP for a living, which means you've said \"modern PHP is actually really good\" more often than you've said \"I love you,\" and you meant it less. You've been announcing the comeback since 2012, like a guy outside a bar insisting his ex is about to call.\n\nYour dream is a Laravel job at a real company. Your reality is a client named Brenda who wants the contact form to \"pop.\" You tell people PHP runs most of the web as if the web sent a thank-you card. Your deploy process is dragging a folder into FileZilla, and your staging environment is Brenda's other site.\n\nEvery January, a blog declares PHP dead, and you feel something. It's the closest anyone has come to checking on you."}, "b": {"item_id": "da336777c19f", "text": "Let's hear it for the people who write PHP for a living. They've got a dollar sign tattooed on their soul, and they still say \"it's a lot better now\" with a straight face, like a hostage defending the guy holding him. Ask one why they stayed and they'll tell you the shared hosting is cheap and every client has a cousin who needs a website. Ask what they're really after and it's the moment someone on Reddit admits Laravel is \"actually pretty good.\" They've memorized which argument goes first in in_array and they're proud of it. They have a folder called \"legacy\" that's older than their marriage, and they open it on Fridays for fun. Respect."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0404", "lang": "en", "target_id": "monopoly", "target_name": "Monopoly", "facts": ["Descends from Lizzie Magie's The Landlord's Game, patented in 1904 to teach the evils of land monopoly in the spirit of Henry George.", "Charles Darrow sold his version to Parker Brothers in 1935 and was credited as the inventor; Parker bought Magie's patent for $500.", "The official rules require any unbought property to be auctioned, and 'Free Parking' pays nothing; the house rules that skip the auction and pile cash on Free Parking are why games drag.", "During the Second World War, British intelligence had maps, compasses, and real currency hidden in sets sent to prisoner-of-war camps."], "a": {"item_id": "c969b39b3b6a", "text": "Nobody plays Monopoly with the family to win. You play to find out which relatives have been running a loan shark operation in their hearts. There's Uncle Gary, who buys every railroad and reads the rent aloud like a hostage negotiator. Your sister hasn't passed Go since 2009 and keeps \"borrowing\" from the bank in a whisper. Dad wants Boardwalk so badly he's already picked out the sweater for the victory photo. Grandma insists Free Parking is a jackpot, wins nothing, and gloats anyway. Everybody says \"one more round\" at 11 p.m., and everybody means it, because the real prize is watching your mother quietly mortgage Baltic Avenue to settle a grudge she's been carrying since your ninth birthday."}, "b": {"item_id": "98523e07c8ef", "text": "Every family has the same cast. Dad volunteers as banker, and by hour two he's laundering money like a Colombian cartel. Your uncle invented a Free Parking jackpot rule nobody agreed to, but everyone's too tired to fight him. Your nine-year-old sister is a slumlord who won't take the deal because she \"just likes the purple ones.\" Mom's been bankrupt since 8 p.m. but keeps negotiating her way back in with promises she can't keep, like a politician.\n\nAnd what are you all after? The joy of crushing people who share your DNA. You'll forgive a lot in this family, but not Boardwalk.\n\nSix hours later, nobody's finished. Someone flips the table, someone's crying in the pantry, and Grandma, who's been quietly hoarding all the orange properties, is smiling. You didn't bond. You just found out who in your family would evict you."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0405", "lang": "en", "target_id": "excel", "target_name": "Microsoft Excel", "facts": ["First shipped for the Macintosh in 1985; the Windows version followed in 1987.", "Silently converts gene names such as SEPT2 and MARCH1 into dates; by August 2020 the HUGO Gene Nomenclature Committee had renamed 27 human genes (MARCH1 became MARCHF1, SEPT2 became SEPTIN2) and published guidelines in Nature Genetics to stop it.", "In October 2020, Public Health England lost 15,841 COVID-19 cases when a legacy .xls file hit its 65,536-row limit.", "Reinhart and Rogoff's 2010 paper 'Growth in a Time of Debt', cited for austerity policies, omitted five countries through a spreadsheet range error found by a graduate student in 2013.", "Sheets have been capped at 1,048,576 rows by 16,384 columns since Excel 2007."], "a": {"item_id": "0b80e15aa132", "text": "You people run a forty-million-dollar company out of a file called Budget_FINAL_v9_USE_THIS_ONE.xlsx. You don't want software. You want power: specifically, being the only person alive who knows why cell AF212 is hardcoded to 7. Your entire supply chain hangs on a VLOOKUP Gary wrote in 2011, and Gary is dead. Well, he's in Boise. Same thing. You call it \"agile.\" Your auditors call it \"a crime scene with conditional formatting.\" Every quarter-end the file says \"locked for editing by Brenda,\" and the whole company just waits for Brenda. And when you finally hit row 1,048,576, did you buy a database? No. You started Sheet2. That's not a business. That's a hostage situation with freeze panes."}, "b": {"item_id": "09a718e03e88", "text": "You don't run a company on Excel. You run it on Dave. Dave built the macro in 2014, Dave left in 2017, and now you pray to a button labeled \"DO NOT CLICK.\"\n\nYou tell investors you're \"data-driven.\" You're data-hostage. Your inventory system is a file called Final_v7_REAL_USE_THIS_ONE, and it's locked because Karen opened it at lunch and went on vacation.\n\nYou said you didn't want to pay for software. Congratulations, you've paid for it in nine hours a week of someone typing \"VLOOKUP\" with the face of a hostage reading a statement.\n\nYour ops manager is proud of having forty-seven tabs. That's not a business model, that's a hoarder's garage with a color-coding system.\n\nAnd every quarter, one cell says #REF!, and the whole leadership team stares at it like it's a diagnosis."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0406", "lang": "en", "target_id": "macau", "target_name": "Macau", "facts": ["A Portuguese settlement from the 1550s until the handover to China in December 1999.", "Casino revenue passed the Las Vegas Strip's in 2006, and at its 2013 peak, about $45 billion, was roughly seven times the Strip's.", "One company, STDM, held the casino monopoly for four decades until licences opened to competition in 2002.", "Among the most densely populated places on Earth, at roughly 20,000 people per square kilometre.", "The Cotai Strip stands on land reclaimed from the sea between the islands of Taipa and Coloane."], "a": {"item_id": "00bfc5b1665a", "text": "You lost it on Hong Kong stocks, so you took the TurboJET to win it back, because nothing says \"financial plan\" like a ferry with a cancellation policy. You're the guy at the Grand Lisboa squeezing a baccarat card like it owes you rent, blowing on it, peeling the corner so slowly the dealer ages. Red underwear, three days unwashed, \"for luck.\" Baccarat is a coin flip and you've built a religion around it. You told your wife it's a work trip, and technically your work now is the pawn shop next door, which buys Rolexes faster than you can say \"one more shoe.\" By hour nine you're eating an egg tart for dinner and calling it Portuguese culture. You came to win it all back. You leave by bus over the bridge with no luck, no Rolex, and a watch tan."}, "b": {"item_id": "fe0ea9392503", "text": "Every man on the 6 a.m. TurboJET from Hong Kong has the same plan: lose the rent in Macau, win it back before the boat docks. He lost his bonus in Vegas and figures the baccarat tables here are the same tables, just with more gold leaf. He says \"I'm just here to see the Venetian,\" then walks straight past the gondolas, because gondolas aren't on the way to the table. He's after the feeling of being the guy who came back. What he gets is a room with no clocks and a VIP host named Kelvin who calls him \"boss\" in the voice of a man already calculating his credit limit. Hour six, he's on the ferry home, watching the Cotai skyline shrink, doing math on a comp slip."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0407", "lang": "en", "target_id": "microsoft-word", "target_name": "Microsoft Word", "facts": ["Launched in 1983 as 'Multi-Tool Word' for Xenix and MS-DOS.", "Clippy, the animated paperclip assistant, arrived in Office 97 and was removed in Office 2007; Microsoft itself ran ads mocking it in 2001.", "Its .docx format, Office Open XML, was fast-tracked to ISO standard 29500 in 2008 as a roughly 6,000-page specification, amid accusations that national committees had been packed.", "Older spell checkers, Word's included, offered 'Cupertino' as a correction for a mistyped 'cooperation', and the resulting 'Cupertino effect' made it into published NATO, EU and UN-agency documents."], "a": {"item_id": "c46efa5460bb", "text": "You spent six years mastering quantum field theory, yet you've centered every chapter title by hitting the spacebar until it looked right. You know what Heading 1 is, the same way you know what a dentist is: you've heard good things and you're avoiding it.\n\nYou wanted a thesis that looks like scholarship. You got a 214-page file called \"thesis_FINAL_v9_actualfinal_JUNE,\" where adding one comma moves Figure 4.2 to the shadow realm. Your table of contents is typed by hand, and you'll find out at the print shop.\n\nYou've also learned what a section break is, at 2 a.m., while trying to make page numbers go roman and then Arabic. You cried. A committee member will now spend four seconds on your formatting and ask you to change the margins.\n\nYou chose Word because everyone does. LaTeX people have already told you. Twice. At parties."}, "b": {"item_id": "8af5ead45179", "text": "You chose Word for your thesis because you \"didn't have time to learn LaTeX.\" Beautiful. Instead you learned section breaks, by force, at 3 a.m., trying to make page numbers switch from Roman to Arabic without page one of Chapter 2 becoming page xvii. You wanted to spend your time on research. Instead you spend it dragging a figure half an inch and watching four chapters collapse like a Jenga tower built by your enemies. Your committee will open the PDF and see \"Error! Reference source not found.\" in the middle of your key finding, which, honestly, is a fair summary of your methodology. Your folder contains thesis_FINAL_v9_REALfinal_USE_THIS.docx, because you are a scientist who trusts nothing except filenames. Five years of work, and your proudest skill is knowing which heading style secretly isn't Heading 2."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0408", "lang": "en", "target_id": "macau", "target_name": "Macau", "facts": ["A Portuguese settlement from the 1550s until the handover to China in December 1999.", "Casino revenue passed the Las Vegas Strip's in 2006, and at its 2013 peak, about $45 billion, was roughly seven times the Strip's.", "One company, STDM, held the casino monopoly for four decades until licences opened to competition in 2002.", "Among the most densely populated places on Earth, at roughly 20,000 people per square kilometre.", "The Cotai Strip stands on land reclaimed from the sea between the islands of Taipa and Coloane."], "a": {"item_id": "e28d81baf3eb", "text": "Look at this guy. He lost his savings in Vegas, so naturally he's crossed an ocean to Macau to win it back. Nothing says \"I've learned my lesson\" like flying to the place where the lesson's bigger brother lives. He's got a system. The system is baccarat, a game where you bet on a hand you're not allowed to touch, run by a dealer who's been doing this since before your dad had hair. He'll tell you it's basically 50/50. It isn't. The house is the only one in that room with a spreadsheet. Then he takes the ferry home with a dozen egg tarts and a tan from standing on the deck, the only thing that came back from the trip."}, "b": {"item_id": "5ababa96b17b", "text": "You're not going to Macau to gamble. You're going to Macau to be wrong in a more expensive location. You lost it in Vegas, in Atlantic City, on your cousin's \"can't-miss\" crypto, and now you've flown fourteen hours to a former Portuguese colony to chase it down at a baccarat table, with a dealer who's seen your exact face on ten thousand other men.\n\nYou'll tell people it's a \"research trip.\" You'll stand in a fake Venice, under a fake sky, floating on a fake canal, trying to win real money back. You see the problem there, right?\n\nAnd the pawn shops are right outside the casinos. Macau doesn't even hide the sequel. It built the walk of shame with a loan desk.\n\nYou'll leave with no money, a stranger's cigarette smoke in your jacket, and one Portuguese egg tart you bought as a victory snack. It's the only thing you won, and you'll eat it on the ferry, alone, doing math."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0409", "lang": "en", "target_id": "javascript", "target_name": "JavaScript", "facts": ["Written by Brendan Eich at Netscape in ten days in May 1995; named Mocha, then LiveScript, then JavaScript to ride Java's marketing, although the two are unrelated.", "typeof null returns 'object', a bug from the first version that has been kept for compatibility ever since.", "In March 2016 the author of left-pad, an 11-line npm package, unpublished it and broke builds across the industry, including Babel and React.", "[] + {} evaluates to '[object Object]'; {} + [] typed at a console evaluates to 0.", "Standardized by Ecma as 'ECMAScript' in 1997 because the name JavaScript is a trademark, first Sun's and now Oracle's."], "a": {"item_id": "76ccf08d2bf4", "text": "You heard \"full-stack\" and decided it meant one hammer and a stack of whatever you're hitting. You wrote the backend in JavaScript to avoid context-switching, which is adorable, because you've switched frameworks four times since March. Your Electron app uses 900 megabytes of RAM to show a to-do list, and you call that \"cross-platform\" the way a man towing a boat calls it \"a sports package.\"\n\nWhat are you after? Hireability, and the warm feeling that every job posting has your name on it. What do you get? A node_modules folder heavier than your personality, a loyalty to a package called is-odd that you'd defend in court, and a weekend \"migration to TypeScript\" that you'll describe as \"mostly done\" until you retire.\n\nYou didn't pick one language to rule them all. You just refuse to learn a second one and gave it a LinkedIn headline."}, "b": {"item_id": "dcee06ed92d9", "text": "Let's hear it for the people who write everything in JavaScript. You learned one language at a twelve-week bootcamp and decided the rest were optional. Frontend? JavaScript. Backend? Node. Desktop app? Electron, so your to-do list ships with its own copy of Chrome and eats four gigs of RAM to remind you to buy eggs. You wanted to be \"full-stack.\" What you got is a node_modules folder heavier than a neutron star and a career that collapses the day some stranger unpublishes left-pad. You rewrite your app every eighteen months because a new framework has a cooler logo. When it breaks in production, you stare at \"undefined is not a function\" and blame the user. Then you install TypeScript, which is just JavaScript wearing a lanyard so you can feel like a real engineer."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0410", "lang": "en", "target_id": "vision-pro", "target_name": "Apple Vision Pro", "facts": ["Released in February 2024 at $3,499, with an external battery pack on a cable rated for about two hours.", "'EyeSight' shows a rendering of the wearer's eyes on an outward-facing screen.", "Apple calls it 'spatial computing' and its App Store rules tell developers not to describe apps as AR, VR, XR, or MR.", "Reports during 2024 said Apple had cut production; analysts' first-year sales estimates were well under a million units.", "Weighs 600-650 g, before the battery."], "a": {"item_id": "bb952c93608d", "text": "Nobody wears a Vision Pro on the subway to watch a movie. They're wearing it so forty strangers know they paid three thousand five hundred dollars to be the least present person in Brooklyn. They say \"spatial computing\" like it's a personality. They pinch the air to open windows that exist only for them, and their eyes glow at us through the front like a cult member's. That's the most honest thing about them: they're staring at something that isn't here. Meanwhile the rest of us watch a man at the 4 train's Atlantic Avenue stop move a floating spreadsheet with his fingers while his actual laptop sits in his bag, unused, feeling betrayed."}, "b": {"item_id": "2e6584647c14", "text": "You paid thirty-five hundred dollars to look like a hostage who's thrilled about it. Everyone on the 6 train can see you pinching the air, conducting an invisible orchestra of Safari tabs. Nobody is impressed. A man eating a hot dog just watched you \"tap\" a button that doesn't exist, and he's the one who feels sorry for you.\n\nYou wanted to be the future. Instead you're a guy who spent a month's rent to avoid eye contact, then bought the version that fakes eye contact for strangers. Those digital eyes are looking at nobody. The real ones are watching a 100-foot screen floating over Mount Hood, while the actual mountain you're passing is a tunnel wall covered in rat hair.\n\nAnd what did it get you? A full seat in rush hour. The whole car looked at you and decided you're either a genius or a crime scene."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0411", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hydrogen-cars", "target_name": "Hydrogen fuel-cell cars", "facts": ["Toyota launched the Mirai in 2014; 'mirai' means 'future' in Japanese.", "Nearly all US hydrogen filling stations are in California; Shell closed all seven of its California passenger-car stations in February 2024.", "More than 95% of the world's hydrogen is made from fossil fuels, mostly by steam-reforming natural gas.", "California pump prices reached about $36 per kilogram in 2023-24, over $200 for a Mirai fill; Toyota has handed buyers fuel cards worth $15,000.", "Mirai owners filed a class action against Toyota in July 2024 over hydrogen shortages and station outages; a second suit in 2025 sought at least $5.7 billion."], "a": {"item_id": "54c0cf51dcab", "text": "Let's talk about the people who buy hydrogen cars. They looked at electric cars and thought, \"Too convenient.\" They want to be the only person in the Costco lot who needs a fuel station that closed in 2023. Their dream is pulling up to the pump, exhaling water vapor, and feeling like a Bond villain who runs on tap water. Then the nearest station turns out to be 140 miles away, with a handwritten sign reading \"Temporarily Out of Service\" taped over the nozzle. The attendant, a man named Dave, has never heard of hydrogen. These buyers own a car that makes drinking water out of its tailpipe, and they still can't get a fill-up."}, "b": {"item_id": "d1233bdc49cf", "text": "You bought a hydrogen car because a regular electric car was too easy. You needed a commute with plot. Everyone else plugs in at home while they sleep, but you drive forty minutes to the one station in Torrance, find the pump broken, and drive forty more to Long Beach, where it's \"temporarily closed,\" a phrase you now hear in your dreams.\n\nYou pay per kilogram like you're buying something from a guy in a parking lot, and the dealer threw in a fuel card like a hostage negotiator. You tell everyone, \"It only emits water,\" as if that's a personality and not the thing every kettle does.\n\nYou're not an early adopter. You're the last passenger on a train line that's been cancelled, wearing a \"Future\" T-shirt, checking an app to see if the future is open."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0412", "lang": "en", "target_id": "microsoft-teams", "target_name": "Microsoft Teams", "facts": ["Launched in 2017, bundled at no extra charge into Office 365, and replaced Skype for Business.", "After Slack's antitrust complaint, Microsoft unbundled Teams from Office in the EU in 2023 and worldwide in 2024; the European Commission still sent a statement of objections in 2024.", "Microsoft reported 320 million monthly active users in 2023.", "Microsoft shut down consumer Skype in May 2025 and sent its users to Teams."], "a": {"item_id": "539c5a8c88ae", "text": "Let's talk about the people who picked Teams. Dave from procurement saw one demo in 2019 and never recovered. He schedules a \"quick sync\" at 4:55 on a Friday, then spends the first ten minutes asking if you can see his screen, like he's just performed a miracle. He checks his green dot like a pulse. He wants you to know he's \"Available,\" which is corporate for \"I'm watching you.\" Every channel he's made is called \"General,\" and nobody has read any of them since 2019. His reply to your message is a thumbs-up, proof he read it and has nothing to add. That's the whole job for these people: keep you present, keep you unmuted, and keep you dinging."}, "b": {"item_id": "a37866956a51", "text": "Let's talk about the people who made us switch to Teams. Not Microsoft. The guy in procurement who discovered it was \"already included\" in the license and presented that like a victory, like he found forty dollars in a coat. He didn't choose a tool. He picked the one free thing on the bundle, and now four thousand of us pay for his savings by saying \"can you see my screen?\" every morning.\n\nThen there are the managers, who love it for the green dot. That's what you wanted. Not collaboration. A little light that says Brenda is alive. Brenda goes yellow after five minutes of reading a spreadsheet, so now she jiggles her mouse like she's hand-cranking a Model T.\n\nAnd what do you get for all this? A quarterly slide: \"Adoption up 40%.\" Of course it's up. You banned the other ones. That's not adoption, that's a hostage count."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0413", "lang": "en", "target_id": "latex", "target_name": "LaTeX", "facts": ["Leslie Lamport wrote LaTeX in the early 1980s and released it in 1984 as a macro layer on Donald Knuth's TeX (1978), which Knuth built because he disliked how the galley proofs of the new edition of his own book looked.", "TeX's version number converges on π (3.141592653 since 2021), and Knuth's TeX bug bounty started at one hexadecimal dollar, $2.56, doubling yearly until frozen at $327.68; he stopped mailing real cheques in 2008 after fraud and now credits accounts at his fictional Bank of San Serriffe, and only 9 of the 275 cheques he sent from 2006 on were ever cashed.", "Its best-known warning is 'Underfull \\hbox (badness 10000)'; overfull lines are reported as 'Overfull \\hbox (Npt too wide)', and figures float to wherever the algorithm decides, not where the author put them.", "A single missing brace can produce an error reported pages away from the mistake, and a document can need three compiler passes to get its references right."], "a": {"item_id": "c2eb8de50987", "text": "Nobody writes a two-page memo in LaTeX because they have to. They do it because they have a dissertation's worth of hubris and a sincere belief that the margins are the message. This is the guy who types \\documentclass{article} at his cubicle like he's summoning a demon. He spends ninety minutes getting one bullet point to hang properly, then emails the PDF to a coworker who opens it on her phone and asks, \"Can you just do it in Word?\" He wanted to look like a scientist. What he got was a memo that says \"Q3 Update\" in Computer Modern, and a reply-all from Doug asking what font that is."}, "b": {"item_id": "1a0ff1693a16", "text": "You wrote a memo about the break room fridge in LaTeX. Two pages. Thirty-eight \\usepackage lines. Your preamble is longer than your argument. You lost Tuesday to an overfull hbox in a sentence that says \"please label your yogurt.\"\n\nWho is this for? Linda from accounting? She opened the PDF, saw Computer Modern, and assumed you were applying for tenure. Nobody needed a BibTeX citation for \"see previous email.\"\n\nYou compiled it eleven times. Three were just to make the \"??\" go away. You drew a TikZ flowchart with one box. One. It points at itself.\n\nAnd what did it get you? Bob replied, \"Can you send this as a Word doc so I can track changes?\"\n\nYour dissertation defense was in 2014. The committee left. Let it go."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0414", "lang": "en", "target_id": "nuclear-fusion", "target_name": "Nuclear fusion power", "facts": ["Has been 'about 30 years away' since the 1950s; at the 1955 Geneva atoms-for-peace conference, Homi Bhabha predicted fusion power within two decades.", "ITER, agreed in 2006 for first plasma in 2016, re-baselined in 2024 to start research operations in 2034 and deuterium-tritium burning in 2039, at a cost estimated above €20 billion.", "NIF's December 2022 'ignition' shot released 3.15 MJ from 2.05 MJ of laser energy; the lasers drew about 300 MJ from the grid to fire.", "Private fusion companies had raised more than $7 billion by 2024."], "a": {"item_id": "f84181eac144", "text": "Let's hear it for the fusion people, the only scientists who've been \"ten years away\" since my grandfather had hair. You can hold plasma hotter than the sun for a few seconds, but you can't hold a deadline for one decade. Every press release: \"Net energy gain!\" Sure, if you don't count the electricity bill for the lasers. That's like me saying I'm profitable if you ignore rent. ITER got approved when Myspace was cool, and it's still assembling the donut. And the startups: \"We're different, we have venture capital.\" Great, now the sun is ten years away *and* has a pitch deck. Honestly, it's a beautiful racket: promise infinite clean energy, collect the grant, retire before anyone plugs in a toaster. You're not building a star. You're building a pension."}, "b": {"item_id": "4a10914dfbf0", "text": "Fusion scientists have been promising power in ten years since before the Beatles broke up. That's not a forecast, that's a rolling lease. The timeline is perfectly engineered: close enough to unlock the grant, far enough that the person signing the check will be retired, dead, or hosting a podcast about living forever.\n\nYou celebrated \"net energy gain\" by counting the energy the plasma gave back but not the 300 megajoules of wall power it took to fire the lasers. That's claiming you're profitable because you ignored rent, payroll, and the building.\n\nAnd the startup founders: you raised a billion dollars on a slide deck with a sun on it and the word \"inevitable.\" You say \"ten years\" with the same face my uncle uses for \"I'm good for it.\"\n\nMeanwhile, the grad student who joined your tokamak project is now a grandfather.\n\nTen years, baby. Always ten. Never nine."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0415", "lang": "en", "target_id": "macau", "target_name": "Macau", "facts": ["A Portuguese settlement from the 1550s until the handover to China in December 1999.", "Casino revenue passed the Las Vegas Strip's in 2006, and at its 2013 peak, about $45 billion, was roughly seven times the Strip's.", "One company, STDM, held the casino monopoly for four decades until licences opened to competition in 2002.", "Among the most densely populated places on Earth, at roughly 20,000 people per square kilometre.", "The Cotai Strip stands on land reclaimed from the sea between the islands of Taipa and Coloane."], "a": {"item_id": "00bfc5b1665a", "text": "You lost it on Hong Kong stocks, so you took the TurboJET to win it back, because nothing says \"financial plan\" like a ferry with a cancellation policy. You're the guy at the Grand Lisboa squeezing a baccarat card like it owes you rent, blowing on it, peeling the corner so slowly the dealer ages. Red underwear, three days unwashed, \"for luck.\" Baccarat is a coin flip and you've built a religion around it. You told your wife it's a work trip, and technically your work now is the pawn shop next door, which buys Rolexes faster than you can say \"one more shoe.\" By hour nine you're eating an egg tart for dinner and calling it Portuguese culture. You came to win it all back. You leave by bus over the bridge with no luck, no Rolex, and a watch tan."}, "b": {"item_id": "e28d81baf3eb", "text": "Look at this guy. He lost his savings in Vegas, so naturally he's crossed an ocean to Macau to win it back. Nothing says \"I've learned my lesson\" like flying to the place where the lesson's bigger brother lives. He's got a system. The system is baccarat, a game where you bet on a hand you're not allowed to touch, run by a dealer who's been doing this since before your dad had hair. He'll tell you it's basically 50/50. It isn't. The house is the only one in that room with a spreadsheet. Then he takes the ferry home with a dozen egg tarts and a tan from standing on the deck, the only thing that came back from the trip."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0416", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hawaiian-pizza", "target_name": "Hawaiian pizza", "facts": ["Invented in 1962 by Sam Panopoulos, a Greek immigrant, at the Satellite Restaurant in Chatham, Ontario, Canada; named after the brand of canned pineapple he used.", "Iceland's president Guðni Th. Jóhannesson said in 2017 that he would ban pineapple on pizza if he could, then clarified that a president has no such power.", "Hawaii is no longer a major pineapple producer: Del Monte shut its Hawaiian pineapple operations in November 2006, two years ahead of its announced 2008 exit, the state's last cannery closed in 2007, and most canned pineapple now comes from Thailand, Indonesia, and the Philippines.", "Panopoulos, whose restaurant also served Chinese dishes, told the Toronto Sun in 2010 that 'in those days people didn't mix the tastes like sweet and sour, except for Chinese food'."], "a": {"item_id": "236a9662f31e", "text": "Ordering Hawaiian pizza is how you tell the table you've never been asked to leave a party. You're the guy who says \"I'm not a food snob, I just like what I like,\" then defends canned pineapple like it owes you money. You want to be the rebel without the risk. The pineapple makes you seem fun, the ham makes you seem like you have a plan, and the whole order lets you feel bold while the pizzeria guy reads it as \"fine.\" Your pizza was invented in Ontario in 1962, and you order it like you were there. You've never been to Hawaii. You've been to a strip-mall pizza place and called it a vacation. Next time, get the pepperoni. Nobody has to explain their pepperoni."}, "b": {"item_id": "000c462b9442", "text": "Ordering Hawaiian pizza is a cry for help from someone who wants to be called a rebel. You've never been to Hawaii. The dish is from Ontario. You're defending a Canadian restaurant accident like it's your homeland.\n\nYou order it for the office party, then wait for someone to gasp. Nobody does, so you announce, \"I know, I know, pineapple doesn't belong on pizza,\" to a room that wasn't talking. You've been waiting years for this fight. Nobody's coming.\n\nYou call yourself adventurous. It's the only risk you've ever taken, and you retake it every Friday. Your entire personality is a defense of a topping that never hired a lawyer.\n\nAnd what do you get? Three leftover slices, because everyone else quietly ordered pepperoni, and a reputation as the \"random\" one at work. You wanted to be dangerous. You're just Greg, with ham."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0417", "lang": "en", "target_id": "latex", "target_name": "LaTeX", "facts": ["Leslie Lamport wrote LaTeX in the early 1980s and released it in 1984 as a macro layer on Donald Knuth's TeX (1978), which Knuth built because he disliked how the galley proofs of the new edition of his own book looked.", "TeX's version number converges on π (3.141592653 since 2021), and Knuth's TeX bug bounty started at one hexadecimal dollar, $2.56, doubling yearly until frozen at $327.68; he stopped mailing real cheques in 2008 after fraud and now credits accounts at his fictional Bank of San Serriffe, and only 9 of the 275 cheques he sent from 2006 on were ever cashed.", "Its best-known warning is 'Underfull \\hbox (badness 10000)'; overfull lines are reported as 'Overfull \\hbox (Npt too wide)', and figures float to wherever the algorithm decides, not where the author put them.", "A single missing brace can produce an error reported pages away from the mistake, and a document can need three compiler passes to get its references right."], "a": {"item_id": "c59587efb75e", "text": "Nobody writes a two-page memo in LaTeX because they need to. They do it so they can be the only person in the building whose \\textbf{Action Items} shine in Computer Modern. They type \\documentclass{article} like they're defending a dissertation, then lose an entire afternoon to a missing package just to shave a quarter inch off the margin. They're not informing Denise in accounting. They're proving they know what \\vspace does. And what does it get them? A PDF that looks exactly like Word, except it took six hours, a compile error on line 47, and Denise replying, \"Can you just send the Word file?\" They'll insist the kerning is superior. Nobody has ever checked the kerning."}, "b": {"item_id": "1a0ff1693a16", "text": "You wrote a memo about the break room fridge in LaTeX. Two pages. Thirty-eight \\usepackage lines. Your preamble is longer than your argument. You lost Tuesday to an overfull hbox in a sentence that says \"please label your yogurt.\"\n\nWho is this for? Linda from accounting? She opened the PDF, saw Computer Modern, and assumed you were applying for tenure. Nobody needed a BibTeX citation for \"see previous email.\"\n\nYou compiled it eleven times. Three were just to make the \"??\" go away. You drew a TikZ flowchart with one box. One. It points at itself.\n\nAnd what did it get you? Bob replied, \"Can you send this as a Word doc so I can track changes?\"\n\nYour dissertation defense was in 2014. The committee left. Let it go."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0418", "lang": "en", "target_id": "canberra", "target_name": "Canberra", "facts": ["Chosen as a compromise because neither Sydney nor Melbourne would accept the other as capital; Australia's constitution required the capital to be at least 100 miles from Sydney.", "Designed by the Chicago architects Walter Burley Griffin and Marion Mahony Griffin, who won an international competition in 1912.", "Its centrepiece, Lake Burley Griffin, is artificial, created by damming the Molonglo River in 1963-64.", "Parliament House (1988) is built into Capital Hill under a lawn meant for citizens to walk over their politicians; the lawn was fenced off in 2017 for security.", "Melbourne served as the capital until 1927 while Canberra was being built."], "a": {"item_id": "f332ff248cdb", "text": "You came to Canberra for the job. Everyone does. Nobody moves here for the weather, which has a grudge. You're here for a portfolio you can't explain at a barbecue, on a salary that makes your Sydney mates ask if you're still on the dole. In week one you said \"I'm only here two years.\" That was six years ago. You own a kayak you've used once, on Lake Burley Griffin, and you call it \"the lake\" like it's the only one. Your lanyard lives in your handbag like a passport. When your mum asks what you actually do, you say \"advice,\" which is the most Canberra word there is. Every second Friday you drive to Sydney to see friends, when really you just need to sit in traffic with people who aren't in a meeting about the meeting."}, "b": {"item_id": "4906cad02ec9", "text": "You moved to Canberra for the job. Brave. You looked at Sydney rents, did the maths, and decided your personality could be replaced by a security clearance. Now you tell strangers you \"can't really talk about what you do,\" and they're relieved, because it's a briefing note about another briefing note for Senate Estimates.\n\nYou came for the ladder: APS5, APS6, EL1, a lanyard that opens one extra door. You stayed because you bought in Gungahlin and the mortgage is now your closest friend.\n\nEvery Friday you say you're \"basically in Sydney,\" which means three hours up the Hume, past a giant Merino, to feel alive for one weekend. Then it's back to a Braddon brunch with the same six people from your branch.\n\nBut hey, you're close to the snow. You've been once. You'll mention it forever."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0419", "lang": "en", "target_id": "beanie-babies", "target_name": "Beanie Babies", "facts": ["Launched by Ty Inc. in 1993 and sold only through small gift shops, with designs 'retired' to manufacture scarcity.", "At the height of the craze Beanie Babies accounted for about 10% of eBay's sales, and eBay's 1998 IPO filing said the site at times had over 30,000 Beanie Baby auctions running simultaneously in the quarter ending June 1998.", "In 1999 a divorcing couple divided their Beanie Baby collection one by one on a courtroom floor, in a photograph that ran worldwide.", "In 1999 Ty announced that Beanie Babies would be retired at the end of the year, then reversed itself after asking the public to vote and seeing an overwhelming vote to keep them.", "Most 'rare' Beanies that price guides valued in the thousands now sell for a few dollars."], "a": {"item_id": "876536d60647", "text": "Let's talk about the Beanie Baby investor. You know the type. He keeps the tush tag in a sealed plastic sleeve, because a tag with a crease is a lawsuit. A 1999 price guide sits on his nightstand like scripture. He sleeps beside a shelf of bears he hasn't touched since the Clinton administration and calls them \"the portfolio.\" He has never once hugged one, because hugging reduces value. His kids aren't allowed in the living room. He tells everyone it's his retirement plan, and somewhere in a storage unit in Ohio, a sixty-year-old man is whispering to a Princess bear. When he dies, he says, they'll be worth a fortune. That's the one thing he and those bears have in common: they're both waiting for someone to finally care."}, "b": {"item_id": "7f33e3df1a62", "text": "You bought sixty Princess bears because a guy on a message board said they'd put your kids through college. Congratulations: your kids are now in their thirties and still can't afford college, but they do know what a \"tush tag\" is.\n\nIn 1998 you drove to three Hallmark stores before dawn, fought a woman named Deb over a shipment, and called it \"market research.\" You mocked people buying stocks. \"Paper,\" you said. \"Not tangible.\" Meanwhile the dot-com crash hurt less than your discovery that every other person on Earth also had sixty Princess bears.\n\nYou still keep them in tag protectors, because you believe a stranger will one day inspect the corner of a cardboard heart and wire you retirement money.\n\nYour eBay listing says \"RARE.\" It has said \"RARE\" since 2004. Your lawyer asked what you wanted from the divorce, and you said \"the Peace bear.\" You didn't mean it as a joke. That's the tragedy."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0420", "lang": "en", "target_id": "ugg", "target_name": "UGG boots", "facts": ["Sheepskin boots Australian surfers wore to warm up after sessions; Brian Smith started selling them in California in 1978.", "Deckers Outdoor bought the brand in 1995 and holds 'UGG' as a trademark in the US and elsewhere; in Australia, 'ugg' is a generic word any bootmaker may use.", "In 2019, a US jury found an Australian maker liable for selling boots labelled 'ugg' into the United States.", "Oprah Winfrey featured them in her 'Favorite Things' lists repeatedly in the 2000s, which drove the first boom.", "UGG is Deckers' largest brand, with over $2 billion in annual sales."], "a": {"item_id": "8e467854d2d5", "text": "Some of you wear UGGs in July, and I respect a person who has never once been told no. You're not dressing for the weather. You're dressing for 2006. Somewhere in your heart, a paparazzi photo of Paris Hilton is still loading. You want people to think \"cozy.\" People think \"sleepover or custody hearing?\" And the walk: your ankles gave up years ago and went horizontal. You're not walking anymore, you're leaning toward your destination. Australian surfers wore these to warm up after the freezing ocean. You wear them to warm up after the Target parking lot. In August. Your feet sweat so hard the sheep is finally getting its revenge. And the second it drizzles, you're frozen under an awning like a cat who's been threatened with a bath."}, "b": {"item_id": "9b58586d4e25", "text": "Oh, you're wearing Uggs in August. I saw you at the farmers market, sweating through your sheepskin like it's a personal achievement. You're not cold. You've decided that looking cozy is a personality, and you're hoping people will see your ankles and think, \"She has her life together.\" Ma'am, it's ninety-one degrees. You're not effortless. You're just wet. Your feet have been in a sauna since Memorial Day, and you still walk into Trader Joe's like you're headed to a cabin in Vermont with a fireplace and a man named Bryce. You're not going to Vermont. You're going to the Target parking lot and back, with a cold brew in one hand and a sweat stain shaped like Australia."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0421", "lang": "en", "target_id": "monopoly", "target_name": "Monopoly", "facts": ["Descends from Lizzie Magie's The Landlord's Game, patented in 1904 to teach the evils of land monopoly in the spirit of Henry George.", "Charles Darrow sold his version to Parker Brothers in 1935 and was credited as the inventor; Parker bought Magie's patent for $500.", "The official rules require any unbought property to be auctioned, and 'Free Parking' pays nothing; the house rules that skip the auction and pile cash on Free Parking are why games drag.", "During the Second World War, British intelligence had maps, compasses, and real currency hidden in sets sent to prisoner-of-war camps."], "a": {"item_id": "d5aee5aa186d", "text": "Let's talk about families who still play Monopoly on holidays. You know who you are: you swore it'd be \"fun,\" then spent four hours watching your brother-in-law build hotels on Boardwalk like he's finally getting the respect his career never gave him. Dad's the banker, which means a man with zero accountability and a lap full of orange five-hundreds. Mom invented a Free Parking jackpot rule in 1994 and will defend it harder than her marriage. Your little cousin traded you Park Place for Baltic and \"a favor later,\" and somehow that's the most binding contract in your family. Nobody's ever finished a game. It ends the same way every year: someone flips the board, someone cries in jail, and Grandma pockets the Get Out of Jail Free card like she's been planning it since Nixon."}, "b": {"item_id": "c969b39b3b6a", "text": "Nobody plays Monopoly with the family to win. You play to find out which relatives have been running a loan shark operation in their hearts. There's Uncle Gary, who buys every railroad and reads the rent aloud like a hostage negotiator. Your sister hasn't passed Go since 2009 and keeps \"borrowing\" from the bank in a whisper. Dad wants Boardwalk so badly he's already picked out the sweater for the victory photo. Grandma insists Free Parking is a jackpot, wins nothing, and gloats anyway. Everybody says \"one more round\" at 11 p.m., and everybody means it, because the real prize is watching your mother quietly mortgage Baltic Avenue to settle a grudge she's been carrying since your ninth birthday."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0422", "lang": "en", "target_id": "windows-8", "target_name": "Windows 8", "facts": ["Released in October 2012; removed the Start button and booted into a full-screen grid of tiles designed for touch screens most PCs did not have.", "The 8.1 update in 2013 put a Start button back, Windows 10 (2015) restored the Start menu, and Microsoft skipped the name Windows 9 altogether.", "Microsoft dropped the 'Metro' name in August 2012 after reports that German retailer Metro AG had objected on trademark grounds; Microsoft called 'Metro' a code name and denied any litigation, and the style was afterwards known informally as 'Modern UI'.", "The 'Charms bar' hid Search, Share, and Settings behind an invisible swipe from the right edge.", "Windows RT, the ARM edition launched alongside it, could not run ordinary Windows programs; Microsoft wrote down $900 million of unsold Surface RT tablets in 2013."], "a": {"item_id": "a00e6ae0aa53", "text": "Who puts Windows 8 on a desktop? You. You looked at a perfectly working Windows 7 machine and said, \"What this needs is a touchscreen experience, and I don't own a touchscreen.\"\n\nSo now you sit at a 27-inch monitor, flinging your mouse into corners like a man hunting for a light switch in a stranger's house. You've Googled \"how to shut down\" at eleven at night. Your wife watched.\n\nYou wanted the future. You got a full-screen weather app you can't close and a Mail tile that's been \"Getting things ready\" since August.\n\nEventually you downloaded a program to bring the Start button back, so you paid to get Windows 7 with extra steps.\n\nAnd sometimes you still tap the screen. It's not a touchscreen. Those fingerprints are just hope."}, "b": {"item_id": "a78f1ebf5710", "text": "Let's hand a medal to the brave souls still running Windows 8 on a desktop. You looked at a perfectly good monitor and thought, what it's missing is a Start screen full of tiles. You wanted the Charms bar, that secret strip you summon by swiping in from the right edge, so you could find Shutdown like you're opening a spy drawer. You keep telling people, \"I'm fine with the tiles.\" Nobody asked about the tiles. Your Solitaire is a live tile now, and it's been live since 2012. You're waiting for the Start button to return the way the rest of us wait for a bus cancelled in 2013. Then a coworker asks why your desktop looks like a phone that lost an argument with a fridge, and you say, \"It's a choice.\" It is a choice. You make it every morning, and the tiles judge you quietly."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0423", "lang": "en", "target_id": "nuclear-fusion", "target_name": "Nuclear fusion power", "facts": ["Has been 'about 30 years away' since the 1950s; at the 1955 Geneva atoms-for-peace conference, Homi Bhabha predicted fusion power within two decades.", "ITER, agreed in 2006 for first plasma in 2016, re-baselined in 2024 to start research operations in 2034 and deuterium-tritium burning in 2039, at a cost estimated above €20 billion.", "NIF's December 2022 'ignition' shot released 3.15 MJ from 2.05 MJ of laser energy; the lasers drew about 300 MJ from the grid to fire.", "Private fusion companies had raised more than $7 billion by 2024."], "a": {"item_id": "f84181eac144", "text": "Let's hear it for the fusion people, the only scientists who've been \"ten years away\" since my grandfather had hair. You can hold plasma hotter than the sun for a few seconds, but you can't hold a deadline for one decade. Every press release: \"Net energy gain!\" Sure, if you don't count the electricity bill for the lasers. That's like me saying I'm profitable if you ignore rent. ITER got approved when Myspace was cool, and it's still assembling the donut. And the startups: \"We're different, we have venture capital.\" Great, now the sun is ten years away *and* has a pitch deck. Honestly, it's a beautiful racket: promise infinite clean energy, collect the grant, retire before anyone plugs in a toaster. You're not building a star. You're building a pension."}, "b": {"item_id": "3775d67dffeb", "text": "Fusion people have mastered the ten-year timeline. They've been ten years away since before most of them had kids, and now those kids are writing the grant applications. They chase a star in a bottle, a plasma that holds for a few glorious seconds before the magnets sulk and everyone reconvenes. Their press releases celebrate \"scientific breakthrough\" in the same breath as \"we spent slightly more power than we got back,\" which they call Q-positive if you squint. Their investors wear fleeces and love the words \"carbon-free\" and \"unlimited,\" and they'll happily wait another decade. The reactor is always ten years out, and the paychecks are already here."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0424", "lang": "en", "target_id": "windows-vista", "target_name": "Windows Vista", "facts": ["Released to consumers in January 2007, after the 'Longhorn' project was reset in 2004 and headline features such as the WinFS file system were dropped.", "User Account Control prompted so often that Apple's 'Get a Mac' campaign ran an ad about 'Cancel or Allow'.", "'Windows Vista Capable' stickers went on PCs that could only run Home Basic, without the Aero interface, and led to a class-action lawsuit.", "Windows 7 (2009), widely praised, was largely a tuned-up Vista on the same kernel line; Vista support ended in April 2017."], "a": {"item_id": "ff3ee1cef20f", "text": "You installed Vista on launch day. Nobody needed you to. You just wanted to be the guy at the office who says \"oh, you're still on XP?\" in the voice of someone judging a flip phone.\n\nYou were after transparent window borders. You spent three hundred dollars on the upgrade, then another eighty on RAM, so your title bars could be see-through. Your computer now has the glass look of a luxury hotel and the speed of its elevator.\n\nDay one got you a printer with a driver that was \"coming soon,\" and a sticker on your laptop that said \"Vista Capable,\" which turned out to be a legal term. You clicked Allow so many times your finger filed for workers' comp. You plugged in a flash drive to make it faster, and called it ReadyBoost like that's a normal sentence.\n\nBy March you were hunting for your old XP disc, and it was waiting for you, no hard feelings."}, "b": {"item_id": "4f7f18f8662a", "text": "Let's hear it for the day-one Vista crowd, the people who lined up at CompUSA on January 30th, 2007 because a billboard said \"The Wow Starts Now.\" You paid $399 for Ultimate so Flip 3D could shuffle the same three windows you used to Alt-Tab through. Your laptop had a \"Vista Capable\" sticker and 512 megs of RAM, which is like slapping \"Marathon Capable\" on a hamster. You wanted to look like the future; you had Aero turned off by Wednesday. Your printer quit. Your Sound Blaster went mute. Your \"Ultimate Extras\" turned out to be a poker game and a promise. And every time you tried to show off, the screen went dark and asked, \"Are you sure?\" Buddy, nobody was sure. Not even Microsoft. They were already working on Windows 7."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0425", "lang": "en", "target_id": "luosifen", "target_name": "Luosifen (螺蛳粉)", "facts": ["A rice-noodle dish from Liuzhou, Guangxi; the smell comes from pickled bamboo shoots (酸笋), not from the river snails, which only flavour the broth.", "The instant version boomed during the 2020 lockdowns; Liuzhou's government reported packaged luosifen sales of over 10 billion yuan that year.", "Added to China's national intangible cultural heritage list in 2021.", "Liuzhou opened a luosifen industry college in 2020 to train workers for the trade."], "a": {"item_id": "4f071ba2f3bc", "text": "You know the type. Ordered thirty packs on Taobao \"for emergencies,\" and the emergency is that it's Tuesday. They wait until 11 p.m., when the kitchen's empty, like that's a courtesy and not an alibi. They snip open the sour bamboo shoot packet with the focus of a bomb technician, then act shocked when the smoke alarm stays quiet but the flatmates don't. Their entire personality is one sentence, \"it tastes way better than it smells,\" delivered like a TED talk to people holding their breath. They'll tell you it's from Liuzhou, it's authentic, it's *culture*. Mate, you microwaved the tofu skin. And what does it get them? Twenty minutes of joy, a hallway that's filing for divorce, and a group chat renamed \"WHO.\" Congratulations: your hoodie is now a Liuzhou consulate until spring."}, "b": {"item_id": "1e45a3cac2a8", "text": "Congratulations to the person who looked at a shared kitchen, five roommates, and one window that doesn't open, and thought, \"Tonight, I honor Liuzhou.\"\n\nYou open all seven packets like a surgeon. Sour bamboo shoots, fried tofu skin, peanuts, the chili oil sachet you call \"optional\" and then use entirely. Then you tell everyone, \"You stop noticing after a while.\" Correct. You stopped. Everyone else has been noticing since Tuesday.\n\nYou say it's an acquired taste. The only thing you've acquired is a passive-aggressive sticky note on the fridge and a roommate who now eats dinner in his car.\n\nWhat were you after? Comfort, home, a little 11 p.m. authenticity. What did you get? A nickname, a group chat you're not in, and a hoodie that still shows up to your Monday meeting before you do.\n\nEnjoy the broth. You're drinking it alone."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0426", "lang": "en", "target_id": "las-vegas", "target_name": "Las Vegas", "facts": ["Nevada legalized casino gambling in 1931, the year construction of the Hoover Dam began nearby.", "Most of the Strip lies not in the city of Las Vegas but in the unincorporated town of Paradise.", "The Sphere, opened in 2023 at a cost of about $2.3 billion, is the largest spherical building in the world.", "'What happens here, stays here' was launched by the local convention and visitors authority in 2003.", "Lake Mead, its water source, fell in July 2022 to about 1,040 feet, its lowest since the reservoir first filled in 1937, and dropped below that mark again in August 2026."], "a": {"item_id": "4f281fa0f753", "text": "Every year a guy drives eight hours from Bakersfield with two hundred dollars and a system. He got the system from a forum run by a man who also sells the system. He's not coming to have fun. He's coming to turn two hundred into a down payment on a boat. Forty minutes later he's at a blackjack table next to a woman in a sequined visor who's been feeding the same slot machine since Tuesday, and she's the only relaxed person in the building. He's down to forty dollars, and he calls it a learning experience, which is what everybody says right before they call their brother-in-law for a loan. Then he drives home, eight hours, very quiet, and tells his wife it was a networking trip."}, "b": {"item_id": "b2bb31b0fbe2", "text": "You flew Spirit to Las Vegas to get rich. Sit with that. You paid extra for a carry-on and called it an investment strategy. You've got a blackjack \"system\" from a YouTube guy filming in his mom's kitchen. You told your wife it's \"basically math.\" Sure. So is a second mortgage.\n\nHere's what you won: a Caesars Rewards card, a \"free\" buffet you earned by losing eleven hundred dollars, and a room \"upgrade\" overlooking the Bellagio parking structure. You hit four hundred bucks on a Wheel of Fortune slot at 4 a.m. and fed it right back like it owed you child support.\n\nThe pit boss knows your name. That's not respect, that's inventory.\n\nAnd you'll fly home saying \"I basically broke even,\" which is Vegas for \"Please don't check the joint account.\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0427", "lang": "en", "target_id": "windows-vista", "target_name": "Windows Vista", "facts": ["Released to consumers in January 2007, after the 'Longhorn' project was reset in 2004 and headline features such as the WinFS file system were dropped.", "User Account Control prompted so often that Apple's 'Get a Mac' campaign ran an ad about 'Cancel or Allow'.", "'Windows Vista Capable' stickers went on PCs that could only run Home Basic, without the Aero interface, and led to a class-action lawsuit.", "Windows 7 (2009), widely praised, was largely a tuned-up Vista on the same kernel line; Vista support ended in April 2017."], "a": {"item_id": "1925db42cfe5", "text": "Every guy who upgraded to Vista on day one has the same story. He's standing in line at midnight, January 30th, 2007, holding a box like it's the last Wii. He wants to tell people he was first. Nobody asked. He got the Ultimate edition because he's the kind of man who says \"Ultimate\" about a salad. He turned on Aero, watched his window borders go translucent, and called it \"the future.\" Then his printer stopped working. He found out its driver was from 2004, and the printer was not interested in the future. So he clicked \"Allow\" on a UAC prompt. Then he clicked it again. He's clicked Allow four hundred times now, and he's the only one who thinks that's security."}, "b": {"item_id": "2ca90ba91029", "text": "Day-one Vista people. You paid $399 for Ultimate Edition because you wanted to be the kind of man whose desktop wallpaper moves. DreamScene. You paid extra for animated wallpaper. Microsoft said \"The Wow starts now,\" and you believed them, lined up at CompUSA at midnight like it was a Harry Potter release.\n\nAnd what did you get? A computer that asks permission. You'd open a folder and it would dim the whole screen like a disappointed dad: \"Cancel or Allow?\" You clicked Allow four hundred times a day. Your printer stopped working. Your sound card stopped working. Your Windows Experience Index came back 2.1, and you took that personally.\n\nBut you had glass window borders. You had a clock gadget. You had a sticker that said \"Vista Capable,\" which, like you, was technically true and fooled absolutely no one."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0428", "lang": "en", "target_id": "jackfruit", "target_name": "Jackfruit", "facts": ["The largest fruit that grows on a tree; single fruits can weigh over 40 kg.", "The national fruit of Bangladesh and the state fruit of Kerala and Tamil Nadu.", "Unripe flesh is sold as a vegan 'pulled pork' substitute, although it has only 1-2 g of protein per 100 g.", "Cutting one releases a sticky white latex, so cooks oil their knives and hands first.", "A member of the mulberry family (Moraceae), a relative of breadfruit and figs."], "a": {"item_id": "11d60bd9214d", "text": "Let's hear it for the jackfruit pulled-pork crowd. You found something grandmothers in Kerala have cooked for centuries, slapped \"plant-based\" on a can, and tripled the price. Bold move, gentrifying a curry.\n\nYour entire pitch is \"it shreds like pork.\" So does a wet paper towel. You know what makes it taste like pulled pork? The barbecue sauce. You're not selling a meat substitute. You're selling a sauce delivery system with a mission statement.\n\nAnd the protein! Two grams. You told a generation of vegans they're eating a brisket sandwich and handed them a fruit cup in a trench coat.\n\nBut hey, it works. You get the Whole Foods endcap, the podcast slot, and the warm glow of saving the planet one eighteen-dollar taco at a time. The pig's still alive. So is your margin."}, "b": {"item_id": "73836848e6ca", "text": "You spent two weeks in Kerala, watched a grandmother cook jackfruit the way her family has for centuries, and came home with an LLC. Congratulations, you colonized a lunch.\n\nNow you're under a tent called \"Jack's Back,\" charging fourteen dollars for \"pulled pork\" that is ninety percent your cousin's barbecue sauce and ten percent a can you opened at 6 a.m. You call it disrupting BBQ. Texas hasn't noticed.\n\nWhat does it get you? Twenty-four hundred followers. A Whole Foods cashier who says \"Oh, jackfruit!\" like a secret handshake. And one carnivore uncle at Thanksgiving who says \"not bad\" in the voice of a hostage.\n\nMeanwhile, that grandmother in Kerala is still just calling it dinner. No pitch deck. No tent. Somehow, no sauce."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0429", "lang": "en", "target_id": "google-glass", "target_name": "Google Glass", "facts": ["Unveiled at Google I/O 2012 with skydivers livestreaming their jump through it onto the Moscone Center roof.", "The Explorer Edition sold to invited users in 2013 for $1,500.", "Wearers were nicknamed 'Glassholes'; bars, cinemas, and casinos banned the device over its camera.", "The consumer programme ended in January 2015; an Enterprise Edition continued until Google stopped selling it in 2023.", "Its display was a 640-by-360 prism in the corner of one eye."], "a": {"item_id": "d051a1381bcf", "text": "Look at this guy. Paid fifteen hundred dollars to join the Explorer Program, and the only thing he's explored is the sidewalk outside every bar in San Francisco. He walks up and says \"OK Glass\" out loud, like a man ordering from a butler who isn't there. You came here to meet women, and you're the one person every woman in the room has already assumed is filming her. Buddy, when you wink at someone, she doesn't think you're flirting. She thinks you just took her picture. Because you did. You tap your temple like you're having a thought, and we all know you've never had one. Google gave up on you. Google, the company that kept Google+ alive for eight years, looked at you and said \"no.\" There's a word for you. It's Glasshole, and you earned it."}, "b": {"item_id": "2e15bc79f728", "text": "Look at the guy at the bar wearing Google Glass. He paid fifteen hundred dollars to be an Explorer, which is what you call a man who volunteers to be the test dummy for his own loneliness. He's not drinking, he's documenting. He keeps saying \"OK Glass\" like he's summoning a genie who only grants weather updates. Every time a woman walks past, he winks, and she can't tell if he's flirting or taking her mugshot. He wants everyone to know he's ahead of the future. Meanwhile, the future is a grown man in a bar talking to his own face, reading his texts out loud, and nobody here asked him what he's looking at. Buddy, you're not looking at us. You're looking at emails the rest of us can't see, and honestly, that's the most lonely thing in this room."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0430", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hydrogen-cars", "target_name": "Hydrogen fuel-cell cars", "facts": ["Toyota launched the Mirai in 2014; 'mirai' means 'future' in Japanese.", "Nearly all US hydrogen filling stations are in California; Shell closed all seven of its California passenger-car stations in February 2024.", "More than 95% of the world's hydrogen is made from fossil fuels, mostly by steam-reforming natural gas.", "California pump prices reached about $36 per kilogram in 2023-24, over $200 for a Mirai fill; Toyota has handed buyers fuel cards worth $15,000.", "Mirai owners filed a class action against Toyota in July 2024 over hydrogen shortages and station outages; a second suit in 2025 sought at least $5.7 billion."], "a": {"item_id": "94ec4c185be2", "text": "Nobody buys a hydrogen car by accident. You're the person who drives forty minutes past three gas stations to reach the one hydrogen pump in the state, and it's out of order. You're proud of your tailpipe because it drips water. You bring this up at dinner parties, unprompted, like you personally invented rain. You wanted to be the early adopter who makes everyone else feel behind, and you are, because the only other person at the station is a guy in a Prius asking whether you're lost. Most of that hydrogen is made from natural gas, so your eco-friendly car runs on fossil fuel with a better marketing team. Congratulations. You're the most environmentally conscious man in a line of one."}, "b": {"item_id": "5d75fbe56d0a", "text": "Hydrogen car owners are the only people who looked at Tesla drivers and thought, \"Not enough range anxiety.\" You didn't buy a car; you entered a relationship with the three working pumps in California and an app that tells you which one is \"offline\" today. You wanted to save the planet. Instead you burned through Toyota's free fuel card in a year at thirty-six bucks a kilo, filling up with hydrogen made from natural gas. Congratulations, you invented a gas car with extra steps. You love saying the only emission is water. Mostly it's your tears at a shuttered Shell station in Fremont. And resale? Your Mirai lost value faster than your friendships after you said \"hydrogen economy\" at brunch. You're not early adopters. You're unpaid beta testers who drive two hours to refuel."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0431", "lang": "en", "target_id": "excel", "target_name": "Microsoft Excel", "facts": ["First shipped for the Macintosh in 1985; the Windows version followed in 1987.", "Silently converts gene names such as SEPT2 and MARCH1 into dates; by August 2020 the HUGO Gene Nomenclature Committee had renamed 27 human genes (MARCH1 became MARCHF1, SEPT2 became SEPTIN2) and published guidelines in Nature Genetics to stop it.", "In October 2020, Public Health England lost 15,841 COVID-19 cases when a legacy .xls file hit its 65,536-row limit.", "Reinhart and Rogoff's 2010 paper 'Growth in a Time of Debt', cited for austerity policies, omitted five countries through a spreadsheet range error found by a graduate student in 2013.", "Sheets have been capped at 1,048,576 rows by 16,384 columns since Excel 2007."], "a": {"item_id": "09a718e03e88", "text": "You don't run a company on Excel. You run it on Dave. Dave built the macro in 2014, Dave left in 2017, and now you pray to a button labeled \"DO NOT CLICK.\"\n\nYou tell investors you're \"data-driven.\" You're data-hostage. Your inventory system is a file called Final_v7_REAL_USE_THIS_ONE, and it's locked because Karen opened it at lunch and went on vacation.\n\nYou said you didn't want to pay for software. Congratulations, you've paid for it in nine hours a week of someone typing \"VLOOKUP\" with the face of a hostage reading a statement.\n\nYour ops manager is proud of having forty-seven tabs. That's not a business model, that's a hoarder's garage with a color-coding system.\n\nAnd every quarter, one cell says #REF!, and the whole leadership team stares at it like it's a diagnosis."}, "b": {"item_id": "d9b95a68589f", "text": "Let's talk about the founders who run the whole company out of one spreadsheet. Their payroll lives on a tab called \"Sheet47,\" and the cap table is a merged cell nobody dares unmerge. They think they're building a business, but really they're building a shrine to Ctrl+Z. They have forty-one copies of the budget, all named \"FINAL_v9_USE_THIS,\" and the only person who understood the VLOOKUP was the intern, who quit in March. They get giddy over pivot tables, call a #REF! error \"a learning moment,\" and sleep with one eye open in case someone overwrites row 14. When investors ask for a dashboard, they send a screenshot of a pie chart with the legend in Comic Sans."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0432", "lang": "en", "target_id": "microsoft-word", "target_name": "Microsoft Word", "facts": ["Launched in 1983 as 'Multi-Tool Word' for Xenix and MS-DOS.", "Clippy, the animated paperclip assistant, arrived in Office 97 and was removed in Office 2007; Microsoft itself ran ads mocking it in 2001.", "Its .docx format, Office Open XML, was fast-tracked to ISO standard 29500 in 2008 as a roughly 6,000-page specification, amid accusations that national committees had been packed.", "Older spell checkers, Word's included, offered 'Cupertino' as a correction for a mistyped 'cooperation', and the resulting 'Cupertino effect' made it into published NATO, EU and UN-agency documents."], "a": {"item_id": "8af5ead45179", "text": "You chose Word for your thesis because you \"didn't have time to learn LaTeX.\" Beautiful. Instead you learned section breaks, by force, at 3 a.m., trying to make page numbers switch from Roman to Arabic without page one of Chapter 2 becoming page xvii. You wanted to spend your time on research. Instead you spend it dragging a figure half an inch and watching four chapters collapse like a Jenga tower built by your enemies. Your committee will open the PDF and see \"Error! Reference source not found.\" in the middle of your key finding, which, honestly, is a fair summary of your methodology. Your folder contains thesis_FINAL_v9_REALfinal_USE_THIS.docx, because you are a scientist who trusts nothing except filenames. Five years of work, and your proudest skill is knowing which heading style secretly isn't Heading 2."}, "b": {"item_id": "c9f44b95f5be", "text": "You've got 214 pages in a single file and a belief that this time it will behave. You are a person who typed \"1.1\" by hand and then pressed the space bar forty times to line up the title with the page number. You call that a table of contents.\n\nYou wanted to avoid learning LaTeX because you're \"not a coding person,\" so instead you learned section breaks, roman numerals, and the one sacred rule: never touch Chapter 3. You moved one figure on page 12 and now your bibliography is in Comic Sans, somehow.\n\nYour file is called Thesis_FINAL_v7_REAL_advisorEdits_USE THIS ONE. You've emailed it to yourself as a backup, which is your entire version control strategy.\n\nAll that sacrifice to look professional, and your defense slides will be in PowerPoint. You haven't escaped. You've just switched the hostage."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0433", "lang": "en", "target_id": "chicago-deep-dish", "target_name": "Chicago deep-dish pizza", "facts": ["Created at Pizzeria Uno in Chicago in 1943; credit is disputed among founder Ike Sewell, his partner Ric Riccardo, and cook Rudy Malnati.", "Assembled upside down: sliced mozzarella on the dough, then toppings, then crushed tomatoes on top, so the cheese does not scorch during the long bake.", "Jon Stewart called it 'tomato soup in a bread bowl' and 'a casserole' on The Daily Show in 2013.", "Chicagoans' everyday pizza is thin 'tavern-style' cut into squares; GrubHub order data reported by WBEZ in 2013 showed city residents ordering non-deep-dish pizza about 10 to 1 over deep dish, with deep dish and stuffed making up under 9% of orders."], "a": {"item_id": "506e0b98fda7", "text": "Let's talk about the people who call it pizza. Mostly Chicagoans, who haven't eaten it since their cousin visited in 2019. You order tavern-cut thin crust every Friday, but the second a New Yorker opens his mouth, you're a martyr for the Gino's East gift shop.\n\nAnd the tourists. You stood outside for an hour in a Michigan Avenue wind to be told your pie takes 45 minutes. You said \"when in Chicago\" like it was a vow. You got a photo of one slice held up by a spatula, a nap in a hotel bed at 4 p.m., and the right to say you \"did it.\"\n\nWhat do you get for calling it pizza? An argument with a stranger from Brooklyn, which is the only reason you came. Congratulations. Mayor Daley would be proud."}, "b": {"item_id": "b9801548af22", "text": "Oh, you're the guy who says deep-dish isn't pizza. Let me guess: you own a wood-fired oven, you named your sourdough starter, and one documentary about Naples turned you into the Supreme Court of dough. You didn't come to Lou's to eat. You came to be the smartest man in the booth, and you're going to hold your fork like a gavel until the appetizers go cold. Your whole personality is a bit about being born in the wrong country. Relax, Gino. Nobody asked for a ruling. Chicago built a pie that takes forty-five minutes and a second mortgage, and you're upset it didn't come with your permission slip. Pass the slice and let your dignity cool down with the cheese."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0434", "lang": "en", "target_id": "jackfruit", "target_name": "Jackfruit", "facts": ["The largest fruit that grows on a tree; single fruits can weigh over 40 kg.", "The national fruit of Bangladesh and the state fruit of Kerala and Tamil Nadu.", "Unripe flesh is sold as a vegan 'pulled pork' substitute, although it has only 1-2 g of protein per 100 g.", "Cutting one releases a sticky white latex, so cooks oil their knives and hands first.", "A member of the mulberry family (Moraceae), a relative of breadfruit and figs."], "a": {"item_id": "11d60bd9214d", "text": "Let's hear it for the jackfruit pulled-pork crowd. You found something grandmothers in Kerala have cooked for centuries, slapped \"plant-based\" on a can, and tripled the price. Bold move, gentrifying a curry.\n\nYour entire pitch is \"it shreds like pork.\" So does a wet paper towel. You know what makes it taste like pulled pork? The barbecue sauce. You're not selling a meat substitute. You're selling a sauce delivery system with a mission statement.\n\nAnd the protein! Two grams. You told a generation of vegans they're eating a brisket sandwich and handed them a fruit cup in a trench coat.\n\nBut hey, it works. You get the Whole Foods endcap, the podcast slot, and the warm glow of saving the planet one eighteen-dollar taco at a time. The pig's still alive. So is your margin."}, "b": {"item_id": "652321571654", "text": "You people found a fruit that has fed entire countries for centuries, moved it to Portland, and renamed it \"pulled pork\" so a guy named Dylan could feel something at lunch.\n\nLet's be honest about the business model. You didn't discover a meat alternative. You discovered that if you drown anything in enough barbecue sauce, a vegetarian will cry \"it's just like the real thing!\" about a food they haven't tasted in eleven years. You're not selling jackfruit. You're selling sauce and plausible deniability, for fourteen dollars.\n\nIn Kerala, this is a Tuesday. In your food truck, it's \"heritage, plant-forward, and ethically shredded.\"\n\nAnd what does it get you? One loyal customer, Brenda, who tells everyone it's \"honestly better than meat,\" and who stands in line behind the guy ordering brisket."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0435", "lang": "en", "target_id": "google-sheets", "target_name": "Google Sheets", "facts": ["Launched in June 2006 as Google Spreadsheets, built on XL2Web, a product Google acquired from 2Web Technologies.", "A spreadsheet is capped at 10 million cells (raised from 5 million in 2022).", "Functions such as IMPORTHTML, IMPORTXML, and GOOGLEFINANCE pull live web data straight into cells.", "Free with a Google account; files live in Google Drive, and collaborators edit the same sheet simultaneously with coloured cursors.", "In a browser, offline editing works only in Chrome or Edge, with the Google Docs Offline extension installed and offline access switched on while still connected; the Android and iOS apps work offline natively."], "a": {"item_id": "2067d213f836", "text": "You're not running a team. You're running a tab called \"MASTER - DO NOT TOUCH,\" which Brad touched in 2021. You wanted a CRM, a project tracker, and an HR system, and you built all three out of IMPORTRANGE and spite. Every morning you watch an Anonymous Capybara scroll through payroll and just decide that's fine. Someone sorts column B without selecting column C, and suddenly your top performer is assigned to Linda's maternity leave. Your entire career is \"Last edit was 4 minutes ago.\" You don't do performance reviews, you do Version History. Your conditional formatting has more colors than your company has policies. Your Apps Script was written by an intern who now works at a bakery. Every time someone emails \"Request edit access,\" you feel like a king. And you are a king, ruling over 47 frozen rows and one #REF! that nobody can explain."}, "b": {"item_id": "b196e566b5fd", "text": "You're not a manager, you're a guy who discovered conditional formatting and mistook it for leadership. You call it \"our single source of truth,\" which is adorable, because there are nine tabs, and three of them are named \"Copy of Q3 (DO NOT USE).\"\n\nOne IMPORTRANGE holds your entire department together, and you're the only one who knows. When it breaks, \"Loading...\" becomes your team's mission statement.\n\nYou wanted visibility, and you got it: an anonymous capybara hovering over your cell while someone you hired quietly overwrites your formula with a hardcoded 47. You wanted accountability, so you @-tagged Greg in a comment eleven times. Greg has never opened a comment. Greg's row is yellow. Yellow means something. Nobody remembers what.\n\nYou say you'll \"move to a real tool next quarter\" while building a dropdown menu like it's a cathedral.\n\nYour career highlight is a pivot table. Your legacy is \"Request edit access.\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0436", "lang": "en", "target_id": "crocs", "target_name": "Crocs", "facts": ["Founded in Boulder, Colorado, in 2002; the first clog debuted at the Fort Lauderdale boat show as a boating shoe.", "Made of 'Croslite', which Crocs describes as a proprietary closed-cell resin foam and which third parties identify as an injection-moulded EVA foam.", "Balenciaga sold a platform Crocs for about $850 in 2018.", "The stock fell more than 90% from its 2007 peak during the 2008 crisis; the company then boomed in the pandemic and gave away 860,000 pairs to healthcare workers in 2020.", "The holes are officially 'ventilation ports' and the charms that go in them are 'Jibbitz', a company Crocs bought in 2006."], "a": {"item_id": "837863df816e", "text": "Nobody wears Crocs out of the house to look good. They wear them to announce that they've stopped trying, and they want a medal for it. These are people who think the grocery store is a hospital hallway and they're the surgeon on break. They walk into Costco with forty holes in their feet and call the holes ventilation. Then they jam little plastic frogs and tiny tacos into those holes so the world knows they have a personality, and that personality is \"I gave up in 2009.\" What they get out of it is one sentence they say with their whole chest: \"They're just so comfortable.\" Meanwhile every tile floor in America sounds like a suction cup breathing through its teeth."}, "b": {"item_id": "ca2056b15182", "text": "You're the person who says \"I just value comfort\" like it's a personality, and nobody asked, because we were just looking at your feet and you panicked into a thesis. You've told three coworkers to \"just try them,\" because you can't enjoy being comfortable alone. You need witnesses.\n\nYou flip the heel strap forward for \"walking\" and back for \"activity.\" What activity? You've never run toward anything except the free samples at Costco.\n\nYou bought little charms for them, so now a grown adult is accessorizing footwear like a fourth-grade backpack, hoping someone says, \"Ooh, is that a tiny taco?\"\n\nYou wanted to look like a nurse on a well-earned break. What you got is a man at an airport who has made peace with everything and wants you to know it. You didn't give up on fashion. You gave up on being asked."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0437", "lang": "en", "target_id": "juicero", "target_name": "Juicero", "facts": ["A Wi-Fi-connected cold-press juicer launched in 2016 at $699, later cut to $399, that squeezed proprietary bags of chopped produce sold by subscription.", "Raised about $120 million from investors including Google Ventures and Kleiner Perkins.", "In April 2017, Bloomberg reporters squeezed the packs by hand and got 7.5 ounces of juice in 90 seconds, faster than the machine's 8 ounces in about two minutes.", "Each pack carried a QR code that the press scanned and checked online before squeezing; it refused expired packs and would not run at all unless connected to Wi-Fi.", "Shut down in September 2017 and offered refunds on the machine."], "a": {"item_id": "4defd00efed2", "text": "Four hundred dollars. You paid four hundred dollars so a machine could check with your Wi-Fi before letting you have breakfast. You're the guy who says \"disrupt\" at Thanksgiving. You didn't want juice; you wanted to be the kind of person a juice startup would pitch to. And it worked! Investors handed that company a hundred and twenty million dollars, and you volunteered to be the exit strategy. Then Bloomberg showed the world you could squeeze the packets with your bare hands, faster, for free. So you paid four hundred dollars to get outperformed by your own grip. Your grandmother squeezed oranges every morning for sixty years. You subscribed to a bag. Somewhere tonight, a router outage stands between a venture capitalist and his kale, and honestly? The router's the smartest thing in that kitchen."}, "b": {"item_id": "cdfa0d41c93b", "text": "The people who bought a Juicero weren't buying juice. They were buying the feeling of being someone with a juice situation. You know the type. They wanted to be a wellness person without the bother of actually squeezing anything, so they paid four hundred dollars to outsource the squeezing to a machine. Then the reporters showed up and squeezed the little packets by hand, and it turned out the machine was just a very expensive hand. The buyers were left holding a four-hundred-dollar receipt for their own fingers. And when the company folded, they still had the thing. Still on the counter. Still doing nothing. Like a trophy for a sport they quit on day one, with a little Wi-Fi light blinking at them every morning like it was disappointed too."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0438", "lang": "en", "target_id": "durian", "target_name": "Durian", "facts": ["Banned on Singapore's MRT trains and in many Southeast Asian hotels because of the smell.", "A 2017 genome study in Nature Genetics traced the odour to a duplicated family of MGL genes that pump out volatile sulfur compounds.", "China buys most of the world's durian exports, chiefly from Thailand, in a trade worth billions of dollars a year.", "Alfred Russel Wallace wrote in 1856 that eating it was 'a new sensation worth a voyage to the East to experience'."], "a": {"item_id": "408212e83a9b", "text": "You're not eating durian on the train because you love it. You're eating it because you need the whole carriage to know you've been to Southeast Asia and survived. You stood under the little no-durian sign, the one with the red slash that exists specifically because of people like you, and thought, \"That's for amateurs.\"\n\nYou call it the king of fruits, which is funny, because you're the only one who ever crowned it. Everyone else just moved seats.\n\nYou'll tell us it's an acquired taste. Buddy, you didn't acquire a taste. You acquired a personality, and a three-seat radius of personal space.\n\nWhat did it get you? A story for brunch, a hotel front desk that knows your name, and a stranger's hand clamped over her nose. Enjoy your throne."}, "b": {"item_id": "e97b85a188ed", "text": "Let's talk about the guy who eats durian on the train. Sir, Singapore, a country that will cane you for chewing gum, saw you coming and put your snack on a sign right next to \"no flammables.\" You're not hungry. You're running a loyalty test. Every face that twists is another person who \"just isn't adventurous.\" You paid sixty dollars for Musang King so you could explain Musang King to a stranger who's holding her breath. \"It's like custard, it's heaven, you just have to get past the-\" No. We don't. You brought a hotel-banned substance to a commute and called it a personality. And the best part? You'll be the only one who forgets you ate it. Your coworkers won't. Your hotel won't. Your Uber driver has already filed a one-star review with God."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0439", "lang": "en", "target_id": "vision-pro", "target_name": "Apple Vision Pro", "facts": ["Released in February 2024 at $3,499, with an external battery pack on a cable rated for about two hours.", "'EyeSight' shows a rendering of the wearer's eyes on an outward-facing screen.", "Apple calls it 'spatial computing' and its App Store rules tell developers not to describe apps as AR, VR, XR, or MR.", "Reports during 2024 said Apple had cut production; analysts' first-year sales estimates were well under a million units.", "Weighs 600-650 g, before the battery."], "a": {"item_id": "7f2991f5a417", "text": "Look at this guy on the L train, strapped into $3,500 of ski goggles, pinching the air like he's conducting a very small orchestra. He isn't watching a movie. He's got forty floating windows hovering over a man eating a bagel, and he's \"doing spatial computing,\" which he'll tell you unprompted. The front screen shows his eyes so you feel seen, the most unsettling feature in consumer tech. He isn't on the subway. He's in a living room with a 4K view of the 4 train. Every few stops he lifts the goggles to check whether anyone noticed. We did, pal. Nobody's immersed. We're all just watching a grown man pinch at nothing, a battery cable running to his jacket pocket like a leash, and the only thing he's computing is how fast his neighbors can file a complaint."}, "b": {"item_id": "ccb644462fd4", "text": "You paid $3,500 to be the only person on the L train who can't see the L train. You wanted to be noticed, and you are: everyone's filming you pinch the air like you're catching a very small, very expensive moth. You say it's for productivity, six floating windows, a virtual Mac, and you're answering one email: \"Per my last message.\" You're not in a private cinema, you're watching Moana 2 while a guy named Dennis eats a gyro against your shoulder.\n\nWhat you were after was never the screen. It was the question. \"Is that the Vision Pro?\" And you answer like it's an Oscar speech, peeling it off to reveal a red ring around your face, like a raccoon who lost a custody battle.\n\nYour reward: a dented forehead, a headache, and a stop you missed in Flushing."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0440", "lang": "en", "target_id": "durian", "target_name": "Durian", "facts": ["Banned on Singapore's MRT trains and in many Southeast Asian hotels because of the smell.", "A 2017 genome study in Nature Genetics traced the odour to a duplicated family of MGL genes that pump out volatile sulfur compounds.", "China buys most of the world's durian exports, chiefly from Thailand, in a trade worth billions of dollars a year.", "Alfred Russel Wallace wrote in 1856 that eating it was 'a new sensation worth a voyage to the East to experience'."], "a": {"item_id": "0bf8c83200e0", "text": "You're not eating durian on the train because you love it. You love being the person who eats durian on the train. There's a sign right there, with a little picture, drawn by a government, specifically for you. You looked at it and thought, \"Not me, I'm cultured.\"\n\nYou went to Penang once. Now you say \"king of fruits\" like you were knighted alongside it. You tell coworkers \"you just have to get past the first bite,\" which is also what people say about marriages they're stuck in.\n\nAnd what did you win? A carriage to yourself. A lifetime ban from three hotel chains. A sixty-dollar fruit you're pretending to enjoy because a man on a plastic stool called you brave.\n\nSir, he says that to every tourist. He's selling by the kilo."}, "b": {"item_id": "0c82ba06de9c", "text": "People who eat durian in public aren't hungry. They're recruiting. Every bite comes with a sermon: \"You just haven't had a *good* one.\" Sir, I'm on a bus. I didn't ask to join your church.\n\nSingapore fines people for bringing it on the train, and these folks hear that like a dare. They're the only foodies whose restaurant review gets filed as a police report.\n\nAnd the payoff? Seventy dollars on a Musang King so you can watch strangers silently change seats. You paid premium to become a fire drill. Your date calls it \"an acquired taste,\" which is exactly what you say about someone you're about to break up with.\n\nThen they stroll back to the hotel, walk right past the sign with a red circle through their entire personality, and still act shocked when housekeeping files a complaint."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0441", "lang": "en", "target_id": "php", "target_name": "PHP", "facts": ["Created by Rasmus Lerdorf in 1994 as 'Personal Home Page Tools', a set of C programs for tracking visitors to his own résumé page; the name now officially stands for the recursive 'PHP: Hypertext Preprocessor'.", "Lerdorf, in his 2007 MySQL Conference talk 'PHP on Hormones': 'I'm not a real programmer. I throw together things until it works then I move on.'", "Runs WordPress, which W3Techs counted on 39.4% of all websites (58.1% of sites with a known CMS) on 9 November 2026.", "Core function names are inconsistent (strpos, str_replace, htmlspecialchars, nl2br) partly because, as Lerdorf confirmed, early function names were chosen to balance a hash table keyed on string length.", "Until PHP 8 (2020), the comparison 0 == 'foo' was true because the string was silently converted to a number."], "a": {"item_id": "c1d0d772e1d6", "text": "PHP developers are the only people who put a dollar sign in front of every variable and still can't afford rent. You chose a career where \"senior\" means you remember whether it's needle-haystack or haystack-needle. Your dream job was building the next Facebook; your actual job is the fourteenth WordPress plugin for a dentist in Ohio, deployed via FileZilla. Every conversation you have starts with \"PHP 8 is actually really good now,\" which is what people say about their ex after therapy. You call yourselves Laravel \"artisans,\" because \"guy who fixes the contact form\" didn't fit on LinkedIn. What does it get you? Job security, because nobody else is willing to open that codebase from 2009 that still calls mysql_real_escape_string. Congratulations: you're unfireable, the way a load-bearing wall is unfireable."}, "b": {"item_id": "286b3c527101", "text": "You write PHP for a living, which means you've said \"modern PHP is actually really good\" more often than you've said \"I love you,\" and you meant it less. You've been announcing the comeback since 2012, like a guy outside a bar insisting his ex is about to call.\n\nYour dream is a Laravel job at a real company. Your reality is a client named Brenda who wants the contact form to \"pop.\" You tell people PHP runs most of the web as if the web sent a thank-you card. Your deploy process is dragging a folder into FileZilla, and your staging environment is Brenda's other site.\n\nEvery January, a blog declares PHP dead, and you feel something. It's the closest anyone has come to checking on you."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0442", "lang": "en", "target_id": "beanie-babies", "target_name": "Beanie Babies", "facts": ["Launched by Ty Inc. in 1993 and sold only through small gift shops, with designs 'retired' to manufacture scarcity.", "At the height of the craze Beanie Babies accounted for about 10% of eBay's sales, and eBay's 1998 IPO filing said the site at times had over 30,000 Beanie Baby auctions running simultaneously in the quarter ending June 1998.", "In 1999 a divorcing couple divided their Beanie Baby collection one by one on a courtroom floor, in a photograph that ran worldwide.", "In 1999 Ty announced that Beanie Babies would be retired at the end of the year, then reversed itself after asking the public to vote and seeing an overwhelming vote to keep them.", "Most 'rare' Beanies that price guides valued in the thousands now sell for a few dollars."], "a": {"item_id": "6a4b77c7b767", "text": "Let's hear it for the Beanie Baby investors, the only people who watched the 1990s stock market boom and thought, \"Too volatile. I'll put it all in Princess the Bear.\" These are folks who bought tag protectors before they bought life insurance. Who read a price guide sold at the same counter as the bears and called it due diligence. Ty would announce a \"retirement\" and you'd sprint to the Hallmark store like a rich uncle just died.\n\nTwenty-five years later, your portfolio is in a Rubbermaid tub in your mom's garage, and your exit strategy is an eBay listing: \"RARE Peanut, tag error, $40,000 OBO.\" Zero watchers. The only person who got rich off Beanie Babies got convicted of tax evasion. And honestly? He earned it. You handed it to him."}, "b": {"item_id": "0c59a1f0431d", "text": "You bought 400 of them in 1998 because a stranger at a Hallmark told you Princess the bear would put your kids through college. Your kids are now in their thirties and still paying off college.\n\nYou drove to three states for a \"retiring soon\" rumor started by a guy named Dale on a message board. You kept them in plastic cases, because your retirement plan couldn't be touched by children, and you told your actual children that, to their faces.\n\nYou didn't collect, you waited. For twenty-five years, you've been waiting for a market that was only ever you, Dale, and a divorce attorney splitting a basement.\n\nNow you've got a garage full of \"rare errors\" and an appraisal that says forty dollars. Total.\n\nTy Warner got the yacht. You got the tag protectors."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0443", "lang": "en", "target_id": "carbon-nanotubes", "target_name": "Carbon nanotubes", "facts": ["Reported by Sumio Iijima in Nature in 1991.", "Promised as the cable for a space elevator; a 2006 analysis showed that ordinary defects cut the strength of a macroscopic cable far below the ideal value.", "Long, rigid multi-walled nanotubes behaved like asbestos fibres in a 2008 mouse study in Nature Nanotechnology.", "Sorting metallic from semiconducting tubes has blocked nanotube electronics for three decades; MIT's 16-bit RV16X-NANO processor (2019) is still a lab demonstration.", "Today they are sold mainly as a conductive additive in lithium-ion battery electrodes."], "a": {"item_id": "839efb519f46", "text": "Nanotube founders are the only people who've said \"within five years\" every year since 1991 and kept a straight face. They open with a slide of a space elevator, then ask for seed money to make a vial of black dust. Every deck has the same line, \"a hundred times stronger than steel,\" and every deck is quietly a plan to sell additive to tennis racket companies. These are PhDs who spent six years sorting chirality and came out wanting a Tesla. The pitch is always \"we've solved scale,\" and the demo is always one centimeter long. VCs keep a nanotube guy around like a lucky rabbit's foot: he never pays off, but he makes the fund sound visionary. And when someone asks about lung toxicity, he says \"totally different from asbestos\" with the confidence of a man who googled it in the elevator. The regular one."}, "b": {"item_id": "221482c6c19c", "text": "You open with a space elevator, which tells me the last customer you spoke to was a grad student. Your deck says \"100 times stronger than steel,\" and every investor nods, because they heard it in 2009 from the guy before you, and the guy before him. You've been \"two years from commercialization\" since the Clinton administration.\n\nWhat are you after? Eight million dollars and the word \"platform.\" What do you get? A polite \"fascinating, let's circle back,\" a tour of the fund's kombucha tap, and a term sheet from a tennis racket company.\n\nYour market slide lists batteries, aerospace, sensors, concrete, and medicine. That isn't a market, that's a thesaurus. And when the one skeptical partner asks about dispersion, you suddenly get very interested in your water glass.\n\nYour real product is optimism with a PhD, sold by the gram at gold prices, to people who just want to know why it still clumps."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0444", "lang": "en", "target_id": "microsoft-word", "target_name": "Microsoft Word", "facts": ["Launched in 1983 as 'Multi-Tool Word' for Xenix and MS-DOS.", "Clippy, the animated paperclip assistant, arrived in Office 97 and was removed in Office 2007; Microsoft itself ran ads mocking it in 2001.", "Its .docx format, Office Open XML, was fast-tracked to ISO standard 29500 in 2008 as a roughly 6,000-page specification, amid accusations that national committees had been packed.", "Older spell checkers, Word's included, offered 'Cupertino' as a correction for a mistyped 'cooperation', and the resulting 'Cupertino effect' made it into published NATO, EU and UN-agency documents."], "a": {"item_id": "600c3a0ffd98", "text": "They're the ones who swear they'll \"just fix the margins real quick\" at 2 a.m., three days before the deadline. They have a file named FINAL_v7_ACTUAL, a Table of Contents that refuses to update unless you look at it funny, and one section break they're sure is secretly turning page 14 into roman numeral vii. They chose Word because their advisor said so, and now they're spending their last good hours in the Styles pane, clicking \"Update Field\" like it's a slot machine. They want numbered headings that stay numbered, a list of figures that doesn't lie, and a title page that doesn't look like a ransom note. What they get is a bibliography that formats itself only in their dreams, and a PDF their committee opens in Preview and immediately ruins."}, "b": {"item_id": "374d6b6e70bc", "text": "You chose Word for your thesis because \"LaTeX has a learning curve.\" Bold, from someone who then spent a semester learning how to make page 1 start after the roman numerals. You wanted to focus on the research. Instead you're a hostage negotiator for Figure 3.2, which flees to the next page whenever you breathe on the paragraph above it. Your committee asked for one more citation, and the bibliography reflowed like a tectonic event. The phrase \"Error! Reference source not found\" appears in your thesis eleven times. Your file is called Thesis_FINAL_v9_USE_THIS_ONE.docx, and it's 400 megabytes because you pasted one chart from Excel. You came for a doctorate. You're leaving with a codependent relationship with Section Break (Next Page) and a flinch every time someone says \"Normal style.\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0445", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hawaiian-pizza", "target_name": "Hawaiian pizza", "facts": ["Invented in 1962 by Sam Panopoulos, a Greek immigrant, at the Satellite Restaurant in Chatham, Ontario, Canada; named after the brand of canned pineapple he used.", "Iceland's president Guðni Th. Jóhannesson said in 2017 that he would ban pineapple on pizza if he could, then clarified that a president has no such power.", "Hawaii is no longer a major pineapple producer: Del Monte shut its Hawaiian pineapple operations in November 2006, two years ahead of its announced 2008 exit, the state's last cannery closed in 2007, and most canned pineapple now comes from Thailand, Indonesia, and the Philippines.", "Panopoulos, whose restaurant also served Chinese dishes, told the Toronto Sun in 2010 that 'in those days people didn't mix the tastes like sweet and sour, except for Chinese food'."], "a": {"item_id": "1cab9b7c0cdd", "text": "Hawaiian pizza people don't order dinner; they order a personality. You can't just eat it, you have to announce it. Every group order has one of you, arms crossed, waiting for someone to gasp so you can say \"sweet and savory\" like you invented the concept. You didn't. A Canadian did, in Ontario, in 1962. You're not rebelling, you're doing Canada's homework.\n\nAnd it's always the same rebel: someone whose wildest night was asking for medium salsa. Pineapple is your face tattoo, the edgiest thing you'll ever do, and it wipes off with a napkin.\n\nWhat does it get you? Your own box. Nobody touches it. You came for controversy and left with exactly what you wanted: eight slices, the same argument you've had since 2009, and proof that nobody will ever steal your food."}, "b": {"item_id": "32c9917cf9ee", "text": "You order Hawaiian pizza the way a man buys a motorcycle at forty-five: you need everyone to know you're dangerous. Nobody asked. You say \"I know, I know, don't judge me\" to a room that wasn't judging you, and now it is. That's the plan. It's the only controversy you can afford. You'll never skydive, never quit the job, never tell the group chat what you actually think, but you will die on this hill, and the hill is a Friday night in your own kitchen.\n\nYou think you're ordering a tropical getaway. It was invented in Ontario in 1962 by a Greek guy running a restaurant called the Satellite. You're not on vacation. You're the loyal defender of a Canadian accident.\n\nYou wanted to be a maverick. You got a twenty-minute argument with your brother-in-law and a second pizza, pepperoni, that someone quietly ordered to cover for you."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0446", "lang": "en", "target_id": "comic-sans", "target_name": "Comic Sans", "facts": ["Designed by Vincent Connare at Microsoft in 1994, modeled on the comic-book lettering in The Dark Knight Returns and Watchmen.", "Made for the speech bubbles of Rover, the cartoon dog in Microsoft Bob; it missed Bob's release and shipped instead in the Windows 95 Plus! pack.", "CERN's July 2012 Higgs boson discovery announcement was presented on slides set in it.", "The 'Ban Comic Sans' campaign, started by designers Dave and Holly Combs in 1999, ran until 2019, when they retired it and launched usecomicsans.com."], "a": {"item_id": "011c029d7bc6", "text": "Let's talk about the person who posts the boil-water advisory in Comic Sans. You had one job: make residents afraid of their faucets. Instead, E. coli looks like it's coming to a sleepover.\n\nThese people think \"approachable\" is a setting you can click. The office manager typing MANDATORY LAYOFFS in a font Microsoft made for a cartoon dog's speech bubble. Sharon, you're firing forty people in the voice of a golden retriever.\n\nWhat are they after? Bad news with zero blame. The authority of a memo, the forgiveness of a kindergarten cubby label.\n\nAnd what does it get them? CERN found the Higgs boson, the particle that gives the universe mass, and announced it in Comic Sans. Humanity's greatest physics breakthrough, and the internet's only question was: who let the dog type?"}, "b": {"item_id": "886470815955", "text": "You scrolled past every font on that dropdown menu, past the ones built for hospitals, courts, and airports, and picked the one designed for a cartoon dog's speech bubbles. Congratulations. Your notice about the mandatory layoffs is now technically being narrated by Rover from Microsoft Bob.\n\nI know what you were after. You wanted to seem approachable. You're the office manager who says \"no worries!\" while ruining someone's Friday. You wanted the boil-water advisory to feel like a hug. Instead, your tenants read it in the voice of a birthday clown and assumed the water was fine and the clown was just excited.\n\nHere's what it got you: a screenshot on Reddit, a group chat full of strangers, and a legal department that now reads your memos aloud to each other for fun.\n\nThe pet had a better career than the font you chose to speak for it."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0447", "lang": "en", "target_id": "ugg", "target_name": "UGG boots", "facts": ["Sheepskin boots Australian surfers wore to warm up after sessions; Brian Smith started selling them in California in 1978.", "Deckers Outdoor bought the brand in 1995 and holds 'UGG' as a trademark in the US and elsewhere; in Australia, 'ugg' is a generic word any bootmaker may use.", "In 2019, a US jury found an Australian maker liable for selling boots labelled 'ugg' into the United States.", "Oprah Winfrey featured them in her 'Favorite Things' lists repeatedly in the 2000s, which drove the first boom.", "UGG is Deckers' largest brand, with over $2 billion in annual sales."], "a": {"item_id": "1531edc90308", "text": "You wear UGGs in July. In Phoenix. Respect. Most people need a therapist to reach that level of denial, and you just needed a mall.\n\nYou're not cold. Nobody's cold. Your feet are poaching in a dead sheep's final revenge, and you call it \"cozy.\" That's not cozy, that's a slow cooker with a ponytail.\n\nWhat are you after? Fall. You want autumn so badly you're dragging it into June like a hostage. You've been holding the same pumpkin spice latte since 2019.\n\nAnd what does it get you? A private, humid sauna for each foot. A smell that enters rooms four seconds before you do. And a look that says \"I'm wearing denim shorts and sheepskin,\" which is two different seasons arguing.\n\nYour boyfriend isn't holding your hand in public. He's holding his breath."}, "b": {"item_id": "f64065ff7ffc", "text": "Y'all know the year-round UGG people. It's ninety-four degrees in Phoenix, and there's a woman in the Target parking lot in full sheepskin, sweating like a rotisserie chicken and saying, \"They're just so comfortable.\" Comfortable? Your feet are wrapped in a sheep's coat in July. You're not dressing for comfort. You're dressing for a personality. You want people to think you have a cozy life, a Pinterest board, and a husband named Brett who keeps a sourdough starter. And what does it get you? Feet that smell like a wet Labrador and a standing order at the drive-thru, where the barista already knows your name and is quietly worried about you. You didn't choose footwear. You chose to hibernate from the neck down."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0448", "lang": "en", "target_id": "graphene", "target_name": "Graphene", "facts": ["Isolated in 2004 by Andre Geim and Konstantin Novoselov at Manchester by peeling graphite with Scotch tape; Nobel Prize in Physics, 2010.", "The EU's Graphene Flagship, launched in 2013, committed €1 billion over ten years.", "A 2018 study in Advanced Materials tested 'graphene' from 60 producers and found none with more than 50% graphene content; most was graphite flakes.", "Sold in tennis rackets, running shoes, and headphones as a performance ingredient.", "Geim also won an Ig Nobel Prize in 2000 for levitating a frog with magnets."], "a": {"item_id": "0a60dd7d6c08", "text": "Every graphene founder has the same pitch deck. Slide one: \"A Nobel Prize in 2010.\" Slide two: \"Five years from batteries.\" Slide three: a photo of a tennis racket, because nobody on the investor side has ever checked. Their LinkedIn says \"materials visionary.\" Their grant renewals say \"transformative.\" Their spin-out's name ends in \"-ene,\" and the logo looks like a hexagon that had a hopeful dream. They've been five years from the breakthrough since the Obama administration, and they bring up the Nobel at weddings. Meanwhile a postdoc is squinting at a sample under a microscope at 2 a.m., and the CEO is in Davos saying it will change everything. Everything has not changed. The postdoc has."}, "b": {"item_id": "886e9fa21662", "text": "Twenty years you've been telling us graphene will change everything, and the only thing it's changed is your grant renewal status. You won a Nobel Prize with Scotch tape and a pencil, and somehow concluded the hard part was over. Since then you've pitched it for batteries, bulletproof vests, desalination, and condoms, plus a paint, which actually exists, so naturally nobody's excited. The EU gave you a billion euros, and you came back with a roadmap. Every press release starts with \"could,\" because \"does\" would require a customer. Your startup's deck says \"disruptive.\" Your LinkedIn says \"thought leader.\" Your lab says \"please send more funding.\" You've been two years from commercialization since Myspace. Keep going, though. Somewhere in Manchester, a man in a lanyard still gasps when you say \"one atom thick.\" That gasp is the product."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0449", "lang": "en", "target_id": "nfts", "target_name": "NFTs", "facts": ["Beeple's 'Everydays: The First 5000 Days' sold at Christie's for $69.3 million in March 2021.", "OpenSea, the largest NFT marketplace, processed a record $2.7 billion in trades on 1 May 2022 and just $9.34 million on 28 August 2022, a 99% drop, according to DappRadar data.", "A 2023 dappGambl study found that 95% of the NFT collections it examined had a market value of zero.", "The token usually records only a URL; the image itself sits on an ordinary server or IPFS, not on the blockchain.", "Collins Dictionary named 'NFT' its word of the year for 2021."], "a": {"item_id": "ce82b5a76298", "text": "Nothing says \"I'm early\" like buying into something the same year Christie's did. You spent your tax refund on a cartoon ape, and now you check the floor price at red lights like a man waiting on biopsy results. You put \"ETH\" in your Twitter name and a hexagon on your profile picture, and you say \"gm\" to strangers at 4 p.m. because a guy named CryptoChad_Baby said that's how you make it.\n\nMake what? You paid $300 in gas fees to join a Discord with 40,000 people and one moderator, who keeps DMing to say you've won something. You did win something. It's called getting your wallet drained.\n\nThe dream was never art. The dream was selling to a guy who's hoping to sell to a guy. You're not an investor, you're a link in a chain letter with a Discord. And congratulations, you're the last link."}, "b": {"item_id": "41fe71c9494d", "text": "Let me tell you about the guy who bought a cartoon monkey in 2021. Not a monkey. A JPEG of a monkey, with a receipt that says he owns it. He pays four figures in gas fees to a stranger in Discord named \"Thanos_Fan_69\" who promised him \"utility.\" What utility? He gets a little ape on his Twitter profile. That's it. That's the utility. He changed his profile picture to a rectangle of a monkey wearing sunglasses, and now he's a \"founder.\" Founder of what? A group chat with eleven thousand people who all say \"wagmi\" like it's a prayer they don't understand. Every morning he checks the floor price like a man checking a pulse. Diamond hands, he says. Diamond hands, the bathroom mirror says back. Ten-dollar haircut, no sleep."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0450", "lang": "en", "target_id": "nuclear-fusion", "target_name": "Nuclear fusion power", "facts": ["Has been 'about 30 years away' since the 1950s; at the 1955 Geneva atoms-for-peace conference, Homi Bhabha predicted fusion power within two decades.", "ITER, agreed in 2006 for first plasma in 2016, re-baselined in 2024 to start research operations in 2034 and deuterium-tritium burning in 2039, at a cost estimated above €20 billion.", "NIF's December 2022 'ignition' shot released 3.15 MJ from 2.05 MJ of laser energy; the lasers drew about 300 MJ from the grid to fire.", "Private fusion companies had raised more than $7 billion by 2024."], "a": {"item_id": "3775d67dffeb", "text": "Fusion people have mastered the ten-year timeline. They've been ten years away since before most of them had kids, and now those kids are writing the grant applications. They chase a star in a bottle, a plasma that holds for a few glorious seconds before the magnets sulk and everyone reconvenes. Their press releases celebrate \"scientific breakthrough\" in the same breath as \"we spent slightly more power than we got back,\" which they call Q-positive if you squint. Their investors wear fleeces and love the words \"carbon-free\" and \"unlimited,\" and they'll happily wait another decade. The reactor is always ten years out, and the paychecks are already here."}, "b": {"item_id": "4a10914dfbf0", "text": "Fusion scientists have been promising power in ten years since before the Beatles broke up. That's not a forecast, that's a rolling lease. The timeline is perfectly engineered: close enough to unlock the grant, far enough that the person signing the check will be retired, dead, or hosting a podcast about living forever.\n\nYou celebrated \"net energy gain\" by counting the energy the plasma gave back but not the 300 megajoules of wall power it took to fire the lasers. That's claiming you're profitable because you ignored rent, payroll, and the building.\n\nAnd the startup founders: you raised a billion dollars on a slide deck with a sun on it and the word \"inevitable.\" You say \"ten years\" with the same face my uncle uses for \"I'm good for it.\"\n\nMeanwhile, the grad student who joined your tokamak project is now a grandfather.\n\nTen years, baby. Always ten. Never nine."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0451", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hsq", "target_name": "HSQ (e-beam resist)", "facts": ["Hydrogen silsesquioxane, sold by Dow Corning as FOx ('flowable oxide') and designed as a spin-on dielectric for chip interconnects; lithographers adopted it as a negative-tone resist.", "Exposure converts its cage structure into a cross-linked, silica-like network, so the developed pattern is essentially SiO2.", "Holds the resolution records for electron-beam lithography: isolated features around 5 nm, dense lines below 10 nm half-pitch, with high-contrast 'salty' NaOH/NaCl development.", "Must be kept refrigerated (Dow Corning's datasheet gives a six-month shelf life at 5 °C); the solution ages into a jelly, and its effective sensitivity shifts with processing delays because cross-linking continues after exposure.", "DuPont, which inherited Dow Corning's FOx/XR-1541 HSQ line, discontinued it and ended production at the end of 2025; labs such as EPFL's CMi switched to third-party HSQ from EM Resist or DisChem, both formulated from AQM powder."], "a": {"item_id": "5b587d8cb24c", "text": "You're switching to HSQ for resolution? Sure. Nobody picks HSQ because it's fun. You saw one SEM image of six-nanometer lines and decided you'd be the person in that image. Your PI already put \"sub-10 nm\" in the grant renewal. Now you've got a bottle in the fridge that goes bad if you look at it wrong, a dose matrix that's eaten two months, and a TMAH developer you're mixing like a cocktail bartender. You'll spend Tuesday at 3 a.m. staring at a SEM, sure your lines are perfect, while they quietly lean over and fuse into their neighbors. Congrats on the resolution. Your advisor is still going to ask about throughput."}, "b": {"item_id": "b68838ef49bb", "text": "So you switched to HSQ for the resolution. Bold move for someone whose devices still die at wire bonding. You saw one SEM of 6-nanometer lines at a conference and decided PMMA was beneath you. Now you're writing at a dose so high your name sits on the e-beam calendar like a hostage note. Your resist lives in the fridge next to somebody's yogurt, and you check its age more often than your own. You develop in salt water now, the closest you've come to a beach in three years. And congratulations on those gorgeous lines. They're basically glass, so the only way to fix a mistake is HF, a fume hood, and a long call to your mother. All this for one inset figure the reviewer will ask you to make bigger."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0452", "lang": "en", "target_id": "chicago-deep-dish", "target_name": "Chicago deep-dish pizza", "facts": ["Created at Pizzeria Uno in Chicago in 1943; credit is disputed among founder Ike Sewell, his partner Ric Riccardo, and cook Rudy Malnati.", "Assembled upside down: sliced mozzarella on the dough, then toppings, then crushed tomatoes on top, so the cheese does not scorch during the long bake.", "Jon Stewart called it 'tomato soup in a bread bowl' and 'a casserole' on The Daily Show in 2013.", "Chicagoans' everyday pizza is thin 'tavern-style' cut into squares; GrubHub order data reported by WBEZ in 2013 showed city residents ordering non-deep-dish pizza about 10 to 1 over deep dish, with deep dish and stuffed making up under 9% of orders."], "a": {"item_id": "a4b76bd10aa4", "text": "Let's talk about the people who insist deep-dish is pizza. Mostly Chicagoans with a Second City complex so severe they'll defend anything New York makes fun of. Jon Stewart called it a casserole in 2013, and half of Lincoln Park still hasn't emotionally recovered. These are folks who order, then wait forty-five minutes at Lou Malnati's, and call that patience instead of what it is: a hostage negotiation with a buzzer. They eat it with a knife and fork and still claim it's the same food you fold on a subway platform. And what does it get them? A Giordano's line full of tourists from Ohio, a nap at 2 p.m., and the right to lecture you about tavern-style, which, notice, is what they actually eat at home. Defending deep-dish isn't civic pride. It's Stockholm syndrome with a parking ticket."}, "b": {"item_id": "506e0b98fda7", "text": "Let's talk about the people who call it pizza. Mostly Chicagoans, who haven't eaten it since their cousin visited in 2019. You order tavern-cut thin crust every Friday, but the second a New Yorker opens his mouth, you're a martyr for the Gino's East gift shop.\n\nAnd the tourists. You stood outside for an hour in a Michigan Avenue wind to be told your pie takes 45 minutes. You said \"when in Chicago\" like it was a vow. You got a photo of one slice held up by a spatula, a nap in a hotel bed at 4 p.m., and the right to say you \"did it.\"\n\nWhat do you get for calling it pizza? An argument with a stranger from Brooklyn, which is the only reason you came. Congratulations. Mayor Daley would be proud."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0453", "lang": "en", "target_id": "beanie-babies", "target_name": "Beanie Babies", "facts": ["Launched by Ty Inc. in 1993 and sold only through small gift shops, with designs 'retired' to manufacture scarcity.", "At the height of the craze Beanie Babies accounted for about 10% of eBay's sales, and eBay's 1998 IPO filing said the site at times had over 30,000 Beanie Baby auctions running simultaneously in the quarter ending June 1998.", "In 1999 a divorcing couple divided their Beanie Baby collection one by one on a courtroom floor, in a photograph that ran worldwide.", "In 1999 Ty announced that Beanie Babies would be retired at the end of the year, then reversed itself after asking the public to vote and seeing an overwhelming vote to keep them.", "Most 'rare' Beanies that price guides valued in the thousands now sell for a few dollars."], "a": {"item_id": "0c59a1f0431d", "text": "You bought 400 of them in 1998 because a stranger at a Hallmark told you Princess the bear would put your kids through college. Your kids are now in their thirties and still paying off college.\n\nYou drove to three states for a \"retiring soon\" rumor started by a guy named Dale on a message board. You kept them in plastic cases, because your retirement plan couldn't be touched by children, and you told your actual children that, to their faces.\n\nYou didn't collect, you waited. For twenty-five years, you've been waiting for a market that was only ever you, Dale, and a divorce attorney splitting a basement.\n\nNow you've got a garage full of \"rare errors\" and an appraisal that says forty dollars. Total.\n\nTy Warner got the yacht. You got the tag protectors."}, "b": {"item_id": "45f7594d8a38", "text": "Let's hear it for the Beanie Baby investors, the only people who bought a retirement plan at a Hallmark store. You put tag protectors on a heart-shaped piece of cardboard and called it diversification. You drove to four McDonald's for Teenie Beanies, threw away the Happy Meals, and told your kids it was for their college fund. Now that kid is thirty, still paying off loans, and his inheritance is a Rubbermaid tub of Princess the Bears. Every few months you see an eBay listing asking $500,000 for one with a tag typo and think, \"Vindicated.\" Babe, that's not a market. That's another guy in another garage, also lying to his wife. Ty Warner told you which ones were \"retired,\" and you believed him. Ty Warner later pleaded guilty to hiding money offshore. He hoarded too. He just hoarded the right thing."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0454", "lang": "en", "target_id": "slack", "target_name": "Slack", "facts": ["Spun out in 2013 from the internal chat tool of Tiny Speck's failed game Glitch; founder Stewart Butterfield had earlier spun Flickr out of another failed game.", "The name is a backronym: 'Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge'.", "Salesforce bought it for $27.7 billion in 2021.", "Filed an EU antitrust complaint against Microsoft in 2020 over the bundling of Teams with Office."], "a": {"item_id": "f56d27fef4cd", "text": "Nobody runs a company on Slack. They run it from #general, where forty people wait for the CEO to type \"morning all\" so they can reply with the waving hand. These are people who say \"let's take it to a thread\" like it's a moral position. They set their status to \"deep work\" and then react to everything with the eyes emoji. They believe the green dot means they're present. They are not present. They're in forty-one channels, four of them named #random-but-serious. Their deepest fear is a DM with no reply. Their proudest achievement is a \"quick sync\" that somehow ran until Thursday."}, "b": {"item_id": "ccebb9af0d58", "text": "You run your company on Slack because you wanted a culture and settled for a green dot. You've made forty-seven channels and #general is a graveyard of work anniversaries and a guy named Greg's lunch photos. Your big decisions get made with a thumbs-up emoji, and a raised-hands reaction counts as alignment. You type \"@here\" like you're summoning a god, then ask whether anyone's seen the doc you pinned in a channel you archived in March.\n\nYou love being \"async\" until you need an answer, and then it's \"quick huddle?\" Nobody has ever had a quick huddle. You say you hate meetings, so you built a meeting that never ends, sends push notifications, and has a gif keyboard.\n\nAnd the payoff? Your best engineer set her status to \"🌴 away\" in 2022 and nobody's checked if she's gone."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0455", "lang": "en", "target_id": "luosifen", "target_name": "Luosifen (螺蛳粉)", "facts": ["A rice-noodle dish from Liuzhou, Guangxi; the smell comes from pickled bamboo shoots (酸笋), not from the river snails, which only flavour the broth.", "The instant version boomed during the 2020 lockdowns; Liuzhou's government reported packaged luosifen sales of over 10 billion yuan that year.", "Added to China's national intangible cultural heritage list in 2021.", "Liuzhou opened a luosifen industry college in 2020 to train workers for the trade."], "a": {"item_id": "911bf6e3e8ce", "text": "You know the luosifen guy. He opens the bag at 11 p.m. and the whole floor knows before the water boils. He doesn't cook it, he deploys it. Sour bamboo, peanuts, and that sachet of chili oil he's been hoarding since his last trip to Guangxi, all laid out like a surgeon's tray. He wants you to ask what that smell is. That's the whole meal. Every time someone asks, he gets to say \"it's snail,\" with the calm of a man who has never once been left out of a group chat. Then he eats it standing by the window, eyes closed, like he's the only one who understands it. The window stays open all winter. Nobody's allowed to say it's bad. Nobody's allowed to say much of anything, because he's already on his second bowl, and his face says he's in Liuzhou and you're not."}, "b": {"item_id": "1e45a3cac2a8", "text": "Congratulations to the person who looked at a shared kitchen, five roommates, and one window that doesn't open, and thought, \"Tonight, I honor Liuzhou.\"\n\nYou open all seven packets like a surgeon. Sour bamboo shoots, fried tofu skin, peanuts, the chili oil sachet you call \"optional\" and then use entirely. Then you tell everyone, \"You stop noticing after a while.\" Correct. You stopped. Everyone else has been noticing since Tuesday.\n\nYou say it's an acquired taste. The only thing you've acquired is a passive-aggressive sticky note on the fridge and a roommate who now eats dinner in his car.\n\nWhat were you after? Comfort, home, a little 11 p.m. authenticity. What did you get? A nickname, a group chat you're not in, and a hoodie that still shows up to your Monday meeting before you do.\n\nEnjoy the broth. You're drinking it alone."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0456", "lang": "en", "target_id": "monopoly", "target_name": "Monopoly", "facts": ["Descends from Lizzie Magie's The Landlord's Game, patented in 1904 to teach the evils of land monopoly in the spirit of Henry George.", "Charles Darrow sold his version to Parker Brothers in 1935 and was credited as the inventor; Parker bought Magie's patent for $500.", "The official rules require any unbought property to be auctioned, and 'Free Parking' pays nothing; the house rules that skip the auction and pile cash on Free Parking are why games drag.", "During the Second World War, British intelligence had maps, compasses, and real currency hidden in sets sent to prisoner-of-war camps."], "a": {"item_id": "5b3d3501d71e", "text": "Dad, you've been the banker since 2009, and you're the only one who knows what's in that tray, and you've never once shared it. Free Parking has had four hundred dollars in it since the Clinton administration, and you call pocketing it \"a light house rule.\" Mom has wanted to quit at nine o'clock since the second lap. Your brother bought every orange property on the first trip around and now calls himself a real estate mogul at a kitchen table. Grandma is the thimble, and she's still winning, because she's the only one who doesn't count her cash. The nephew is eight. He mortgaged his railroads to buy a hotel he can't afford, then sobbed in jail. Everybody says \"just visiting\" like it's a personality. You're not playing for the fake money. You're finding out who'll flip the board when they lose."}, "b": {"item_id": "d5aee5aa186d", "text": "Let's talk about families who still play Monopoly on holidays. You know who you are: you swore it'd be \"fun,\" then spent four hours watching your brother-in-law build hotels on Boardwalk like he's finally getting the respect his career never gave him. Dad's the banker, which means a man with zero accountability and a lap full of orange five-hundreds. Mom invented a Free Parking jackpot rule in 1994 and will defend it harder than her marriage. Your little cousin traded you Park Place for Baltic and \"a favor later,\" and somehow that's the most binding contract in your family. Nobody's ever finished a game. It ends the same way every year: someone flips the board, someone cries in jail, and Grandma pockets the Get Out of Jail Free card like she's been planning it since Nixon."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0457", "lang": "en", "target_id": "comic-sans", "target_name": "Comic Sans", "facts": ["Designed by Vincent Connare at Microsoft in 1994, modeled on the comic-book lettering in The Dark Knight Returns and Watchmen.", "Made for the speech bubbles of Rover, the cartoon dog in Microsoft Bob; it missed Bob's release and shipped instead in the Windows 95 Plus! pack.", "CERN's July 2012 Higgs boson discovery announcement was presented on slides set in it.", "The 'Ban Comic Sans' campaign, started by designers Dave and Holly Combs in 1999, ran until 2019, when they retired it and launched usecomicsans.com."], "a": {"item_id": "011c029d7bc6", "text": "Let's talk about the person who posts the boil-water advisory in Comic Sans. You had one job: make residents afraid of their faucets. Instead, E. coli looks like it's coming to a sleepover.\n\nThese people think \"approachable\" is a setting you can click. The office manager typing MANDATORY LAYOFFS in a font Microsoft made for a cartoon dog's speech bubble. Sharon, you're firing forty people in the voice of a golden retriever.\n\nWhat are they after? Bad news with zero blame. The authority of a memo, the forgiveness of a kindergarten cubby label.\n\nAnd what does it get them? CERN found the Higgs boson, the particle that gives the universe mass, and announced it in Comic Sans. Humanity's greatest physics breakthrough, and the internet's only question was: who let the dog type?"}, "b": {"item_id": "85bb13923657", "text": "Nobody picks Comic Sans for a gas leak notice by accident. You wanted the building to evacuate without feeling like you were in charge of anything. \"EVACUATE BY 5PM\" felt too blunt, so you softened it into the font of a kindergarten worksheet and a lost-cat flyer. Now the fire marshal reads it like a bake sale reminder. You were going for approachable. What you got was a sign that people skim, shrug at, and treat as a suggestion. Your management is going to love the memo you're writing next, the one in Papyrus about why the sign was ignored."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0458", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hsq", "target_name": "HSQ (e-beam resist)", "facts": ["Hydrogen silsesquioxane, sold by Dow Corning as FOx ('flowable oxide') and designed as a spin-on dielectric for chip interconnects; lithographers adopted it as a negative-tone resist.", "Exposure converts its cage structure into a cross-linked, silica-like network, so the developed pattern is essentially SiO2.", "Holds the resolution records for electron-beam lithography: isolated features around 5 nm, dense lines below 10 nm half-pitch, with high-contrast 'salty' NaOH/NaCl development.", "Must be kept refrigerated (Dow Corning's datasheet gives a six-month shelf life at 5 °C); the solution ages into a jelly, and its effective sensitivity shifts with processing delays because cross-linking continues after exposure.", "DuPont, which inherited Dow Corning's FOx/XR-1541 HSQ line, discontinued it and ended production at the end of 2025; labs such as EPFL's CMi switched to third-party HSQ from EM Resist or DisChem, both formulated from AQM powder."], "a": {"item_id": "91f701649a72", "text": "You switched to HSQ for the resolution. Of course you did. PMMA gave you 20-nanometer lines, which is fine for science but not for the cover of a journal your advisor brings up at dinner parties. Now you're writing 8-nanometer lines that nobody can see without a $2 million microscope you booked three weeks ago.\n\nYou pay triple for a tiny bottle that expires faster than your funding. You keep it cold like insulin and spin it knowing it's already going bad. Your dose is ten times higher, so your \"quick write\" runs overnight, and the e-beam tool manager who used to love you is now drafting an email. You develop in TMAH like you're brewing something, and the lines collapse anyway.\n\nAnd the best part: it never comes off. You wanted resolution, and you got permanent glass sitting on your device. Congratulations, you made something incredibly small that doesn't work."}, "b": {"item_id": "b0d879a960b0", "text": "So you switched to HSQ \"for the resolution.\" Sure. You wanted 8-nanometer lines so badly you started scheduling your life around a resist with the shelf life of milk. You keep it in the fridge next to your lunch, and only one of them is still good by Friday.\n\nYour write that used to take twenty minutes now takes the whole overnight slot, so the guy who needed the tool for his actual thesis is crying in the gowning room. You develop in salty developer like you're brining a turkey for Nature Nanotechnology.\n\nAnd when it goes wrong, and it will, you've basically spun glass onto your chip. Your only exit is HF, a chemical that wants you dead.\n\nAll of this for one SEM image with a scale bar so small nobody can read it. Congratulations. Your device still doesn't work, but it fails at record resolution."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0459", "lang": "en", "target_id": "crocs", "target_name": "Crocs", "facts": ["Founded in Boulder, Colorado, in 2002; the first clog debuted at the Fort Lauderdale boat show as a boating shoe.", "Made of 'Croslite', which Crocs describes as a proprietary closed-cell resin foam and which third parties identify as an injection-moulded EVA foam.", "Balenciaga sold a platform Crocs for about $850 in 2018.", "The stock fell more than 90% from its 2007 peak during the 2008 crisis; the company then boomed in the pandemic and gave away 860,000 pairs to healthcare workers in 2020.", "The holes are officially 'ventilation ports' and the charms that go in them are 'Jibbitz', a company Crocs bought in 2006."], "a": {"item_id": "ca2056b15182", "text": "You're the person who says \"I just value comfort\" like it's a personality, and nobody asked, because we were just looking at your feet and you panicked into a thesis. You've told three coworkers to \"just try them,\" because you can't enjoy being comfortable alone. You need witnesses.\n\nYou flip the heel strap forward for \"walking\" and back for \"activity.\" What activity? You've never run toward anything except the free samples at Costco.\n\nYou bought little charms for them, so now a grown adult is accessorizing footwear like a fourth-grade backpack, hoping someone says, \"Ooh, is that a tiny taco?\"\n\nYou wanted to look like a nurse on a well-earned break. What you got is a man at an airport who has made peace with everything and wants you to know it. You didn't give up on fashion. You gave up on being asked."}, "b": {"item_id": "820d0b21e9b5", "text": "People who wear Crocs in public want you to know they've stopped trying, but they also want credit for it. That's why they buy the Jibbitz. Nothing says \"I don't care what you think\" like spending forty dollars on tiny rubber tacos so everyone knows exactly what you think. Then there's the strap. You flipped it back into \"Sport Mode,\" which is a bold claim from a man walking into Applebee's. Your nurse friend wears them because she stands for twelve-hour shifts. You wear them for a twelve-minute Target run, hoping to borrow a little of her dignity. And what does it get you? Comfort, sure. Plus a lifetime of strangers asking if you're coming off a shift. You're not, Kyle. You're coming off a nap."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0460", "lang": "en", "target_id": "las-vegas", "target_name": "Las Vegas", "facts": ["Nevada legalized casino gambling in 1931, the year construction of the Hoover Dam began nearby.", "Most of the Strip lies not in the city of Las Vegas but in the unincorporated town of Paradise.", "The Sphere, opened in 2023 at a cost of about $2.3 billion, is the largest spherical building in the world.", "'What happens here, stays here' was launched by the local convention and visitors authority in 2003.", "Lake Mead, its water source, fell in July 2022 to about 1,040 feet, its lowest since the reservoir first filled in 1937, and dropped below that mark again in August 2026."], "a": {"item_id": "d992f33818f6", "text": "You're not going to Vegas to get rich. You're going to Vegas to donate to a fountain show. Look at you, booking a $49 Spirit flight to \"beat the house,\" like the house didn't build a fake Eiffel Tower with money from guys exactly like you. You've got a roulette \"system.\" You wrote it on a cocktail napkin. Red's due. Red is never due. Red doesn't know you. By midnight you're on Fremont Street feeding a Wheel of Fortune slot in an Ed Hardy shirt you've owned since 2008, telling a stranger you're \"only down twelve hundred.\" By 3 a.m. the Players Club bumps you to Platinum, which is casino for \"we'll be needing your kidneys.\" And you'll call it a win, because they comped you a buffet. Congratulations. You flew 400 miles to pay $3,000 for crab legs."}, "b": {"item_id": "4f281fa0f753", "text": "Every year a guy drives eight hours from Bakersfield with two hundred dollars and a system. He got the system from a forum run by a man who also sells the system. He's not coming to have fun. He's coming to turn two hundred into a down payment on a boat. Forty minutes later he's at a blackjack table next to a woman in a sequined visor who's been feeding the same slot machine since Tuesday, and she's the only relaxed person in the building. He's down to forty dollars, and he calls it a learning experience, which is what everybody says right before they call their brother-in-law for a loan. Then he drives home, eight hours, very quiet, and tells his wife it was a networking trip."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0461", "lang": "en", "target_id": "luosifen", "target_name": "Luosifen (螺蛳粉)", "facts": ["A rice-noodle dish from Liuzhou, Guangxi; the smell comes from pickled bamboo shoots (酸笋), not from the river snails, which only flavour the broth.", "The instant version boomed during the 2020 lockdowns; Liuzhou's government reported packaged luosifen sales of over 10 billion yuan that year.", "Added to China's national intangible cultural heritage list in 2021.", "Liuzhou opened a luosifen industry college in 2020 to train workers for the trade."], "a": {"item_id": "1e45a3cac2a8", "text": "Congratulations to the person who looked at a shared kitchen, five roommates, and one window that doesn't open, and thought, \"Tonight, I honor Liuzhou.\"\n\nYou open all seven packets like a surgeon. Sour bamboo shoots, fried tofu skin, peanuts, the chili oil sachet you call \"optional\" and then use entirely. Then you tell everyone, \"You stop noticing after a while.\" Correct. You stopped. Everyone else has been noticing since Tuesday.\n\nYou say it's an acquired taste. The only thing you've acquired is a passive-aggressive sticky note on the fridge and a roommate who now eats dinner in his car.\n\nWhat were you after? Comfort, home, a little 11 p.m. authenticity. What did you get? A nickname, a group chat you're not in, and a hoodie that still shows up to your Monday meeting before you do.\n\nEnjoy the broth. You're drinking it alone."}, "b": {"item_id": "6782cb950544", "text": "You know who you are. You ordered twelve packs off Taobao, waited until every roommate was home, then tore open the sour bamboo shoot sachet like you were declassifying something. You're not hungry. You're making a point. Every slurp comes with the speech: \"Smells bad, tastes good.\" Buddy, we only get the first half. Nobody signed up for the tasting, just the hallway. And you keep saying \"it's authentic Liuzhou\" like geography is an apology. What does it get you? A spotless pot nobody will borrow, a group chat that goes silent the second you type \"anyone want some?\", and roommates who suddenly prefer dating people with their own apartments. You came for comfort food. You left with a reputation, a window permanently propped open, and a fridge shelf everyone treats like a crime scene."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0462", "lang": "en", "target_id": "jackfruit", "target_name": "Jackfruit", "facts": ["The largest fruit that grows on a tree; single fruits can weigh over 40 kg.", "The national fruit of Bangladesh and the state fruit of Kerala and Tamil Nadu.", "Unripe flesh is sold as a vegan 'pulled pork' substitute, although it has only 1-2 g of protein per 100 g.", "Cutting one releases a sticky white latex, so cooks oil their knives and hands first.", "A member of the mulberry family (Moraceae), a relative of breadfruit and figs."], "a": {"item_id": "11d60bd9214d", "text": "Let's hear it for the jackfruit pulled-pork crowd. You found something grandmothers in Kerala have cooked for centuries, slapped \"plant-based\" on a can, and tripled the price. Bold move, gentrifying a curry.\n\nYour entire pitch is \"it shreds like pork.\" So does a wet paper towel. You know what makes it taste like pulled pork? The barbecue sauce. You're not selling a meat substitute. You're selling a sauce delivery system with a mission statement.\n\nAnd the protein! Two grams. You told a generation of vegans they're eating a brisket sandwich and handed them a fruit cup in a trench coat.\n\nBut hey, it works. You get the Whole Foods endcap, the podcast slot, and the warm glow of saving the planet one eighteen-dollar taco at a time. The pig's still alive. So is your margin."}, "b": {"item_id": "b265b1d10a20", "text": "Every jackfruit seller has the same origin story. A guy who got laid off from software, bought a farm in a state he'd only seen from a plane window, and now runs a newsletter about \"reclaiming the dinner table.\" He's not selling you food. He's selling you the feeling that you finally did something. Jackfruit is perfect for him because it's the only thing that lets a man in a fleece vest say \"pulled pork\" at Whole Foods without anyone checking. He calls it \"the world's most sustainable protein,\" then sells it in a can with a label that looks like a Pixar villain. His customers are vegans who want a barbecue sandwich and carnivores who want a reason to feel smug about being there. Everybody wins except the sandwich."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0463", "lang": "en", "target_id": "las-vegas", "target_name": "Las Vegas", "facts": ["Nevada legalized casino gambling in 1931, the year construction of the Hoover Dam began nearby.", "Most of the Strip lies not in the city of Las Vegas but in the unincorporated town of Paradise.", "The Sphere, opened in 2023 at a cost of about $2.3 billion, is the largest spherical building in the world.", "'What happens here, stays here' was launched by the local convention and visitors authority in 2003.", "Lake Mead, its water source, fell in July 2022 to about 1,040 feet, its lowest since the reservoir first filled in 1937, and dropped below that mark again in August 2026."], "a": {"item_id": "b2bb31b0fbe2", "text": "You flew Spirit to Las Vegas to get rich. Sit with that. You paid extra for a carry-on and called it an investment strategy. You've got a blackjack \"system\" from a YouTube guy filming in his mom's kitchen. You told your wife it's \"basically math.\" Sure. So is a second mortgage.\n\nHere's what you won: a Caesars Rewards card, a \"free\" buffet you earned by losing eleven hundred dollars, and a room \"upgrade\" overlooking the Bellagio parking structure. You hit four hundred bucks on a Wheel of Fortune slot at 4 a.m. and fed it right back like it owed you child support.\n\nThe pit boss knows your name. That's not respect, that's inventory.\n\nAnd you'll fly home saying \"I basically broke even,\" which is Vegas for \"Please don't check the joint account.\""}, "b": {"item_id": "75124632afe4", "text": "Every guy who drives to Vegas to get rich has the same plan. He's going to win one hand of blackjack, put the money in his pocket, and walk out a new man. Two days later he's in the parking garage, sitting in his Camry, calling his mom to ask if she can wire him gas money. He's wearing the same shirt. He's got a little paper cup of quarters and the dead look of a man who just found out the house is the house. And then he goes back in. Why? Because somewhere in there a guy in a vest told him \"you're due.\" Nobody's due. The guy in the vest is due for a pension and a deep sea cruise. Nobody's getting rich in Vegas except the people who built the buffet you ate at after losing your rent."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0464", "lang": "en", "target_id": "ugg", "target_name": "UGG boots", "facts": ["Sheepskin boots Australian surfers wore to warm up after sessions; Brian Smith started selling them in California in 1978.", "Deckers Outdoor bought the brand in 1995 and holds 'UGG' as a trademark in the US and elsewhere; in Australia, 'ugg' is a generic word any bootmaker may use.", "In 2019, a US jury found an Australian maker liable for selling boots labelled 'ugg' into the United States.", "Oprah Winfrey featured them in her 'Favorite Things' lists repeatedly in the 2000s, which drove the first boom.", "UGG is Deckers' largest brand, with over $2 billion in annual sales."], "a": {"item_id": "2082912cfaf3", "text": "Let's hear it for the people wearing UGGs in July. You're not dressing for the weather. You're dressing for 2005, still waiting for Paris Hilton to text back. Australian surfers made these to warm their feet after the ocean. You wear them to warm your feet after the Trader Joe's parking lot. In August. In Phoenix. What are you after? \"Comfort,\" you say. Babe, your ankles have been collapsing outward since the Bush administration. You walk like a Clydesdale that's given up. And what does it get you? One summer rainstorm and you're squelching through Target like a wet golden retriever who made bad choices. People don't ask where you got them. They ask if you're okay. Congratulations: you've turned footwear into a wellness check."}, "b": {"item_id": "3e67204b78e6", "text": "If you wear UGGs in July, I admire the commitment. You've decided being cozy is a personality and weather is just a rumor. You'll tell me they \"regulate temperature,\" a line you memorized like a hostage reading a statement. It's 94 degrees, you're in running shorts, and your feet are poaching in their own broth.\n\nWhat are you after? You want to look like you just rolled out of bed, for $180. Effortless, with a receipt. You want it to be fall at all times, even in Phoenix, even at a pool party.\n\nAnd what does it get you? A smell that enters the room two seconds before you do. Socks you can't wear because \"the inside is the whole point.\" And the loyalty of a 2007 version of you who never left the Juicy tracksuit.\n\nCongratulations. You're the only person standing on a sidewalk you could fry an egg on, saying, \"My feet get cold.\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0465", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hsq", "target_name": "HSQ (e-beam resist)", "facts": ["Hydrogen silsesquioxane, sold by Dow Corning as FOx ('flowable oxide') and designed as a spin-on dielectric for chip interconnects; lithographers adopted it as a negative-tone resist.", "Exposure converts its cage structure into a cross-linked, silica-like network, so the developed pattern is essentially SiO2.", "Holds the resolution records for electron-beam lithography: isolated features around 5 nm, dense lines below 10 nm half-pitch, with high-contrast 'salty' NaOH/NaCl development.", "Must be kept refrigerated (Dow Corning's datasheet gives a six-month shelf life at 5 °C); the solution ages into a jelly, and its effective sensitivity shifts with processing delays because cross-linking continues after exposure.", "DuPont, which inherited Dow Corning's FOx/XR-1541 HSQ line, discontinued it and ended production at the end of 2025; labs such as EPFL's CMi switched to third-party HSQ from EM Resist or DisChem, both formulated from AQM powder."], "a": {"item_id": "884dd445a1e2", "text": "\"We're switching to HSQ for the resolution.\" Sure you are. Because your PMMA wasn't humiliating enough, you wanted a resist that's also a deadline. You're chasing sub-10 nanometer lines so you can put one SEM image in a paper, the one nice picture out of forty-one fallen-over grating samples.\n\nYour resist lives in the freezer like a tiny, expensive yogurt, and by the time you've booked the beam it has gone off anyway. You'll expose at a dose that makes the e-beam tool bill you like a hotel minibar. Then you'll develop in salt water, because apparently you wanted to cook pasta in the cleanroom.\n\nAnd what do you get? Silicon dioxide you can't strip. A permanent souvenir of your ambition, fused to the wafer forever. Congratulations, you made glass lines. Your advisor wanted devices."}, "b": {"item_id": "5b587d8cb24c", "text": "You're switching to HSQ for resolution? Sure. Nobody picks HSQ because it's fun. You saw one SEM image of six-nanometer lines and decided you'd be the person in that image. Your PI already put \"sub-10 nm\" in the grant renewal. Now you've got a bottle in the fridge that goes bad if you look at it wrong, a dose matrix that's eaten two months, and a TMAH developer you're mixing like a cocktail bartender. You'll spend Tuesday at 3 a.m. staring at a SEM, sure your lines are perfect, while they quietly lean over and fuse into their neighbors. Congrats on the resolution. Your advisor is still going to ask about throughput."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0466", "lang": "en", "target_id": "risk", "target_name": "Risk", "facts": ["Invented in 1957 by Albert Lamorisse, the French director of The Red Balloon, as La Conquête du Monde; Parker Brothers published it in the United States in 1959.", "The map has 42 territories, among them Kamchatka, Irkutsk, and Yakutsk, while the entire Middle East is one territory.", "Holding Australia, which has a single entry point, is the standard opening strategy.", "A full game can run for hours, and players eliminated early have nothing to do but watch."], "a": {"item_id": "d3ed8f3ba762", "text": "Nobody plays Risk to the end. You're the guy who got knocked out at nine o'clock and has been sitting there with a lukewarm Coke, watching you. You're the one who drew Australia, held it for three hours, and now you give speeches about continents like it's the UN. Your whole personality is three blue armies sitting on Kamchatka. You negotiated a truce with your brother-in-law, broke it with a six-three dice roll, and called it strategy. You don't want the world. You want your name on the board at the end, defending a little plastic pile of soldiers until somebody's mom calls everyone to dinner. And the worst part? You'll win a game where the dice decided everything, and you'll still say, \"I was playing the long game.\""}, "b": {"item_id": "831ac90358ed", "text": "You people who play Risk to the end. Not \"until it gets late.\" To the *end*. Six hours in, somebody's uncle has fortified Australia like a doomsday bunker, and he'll still be sitting there at 2 a.m., one way in, one way out, guarding a war that ended at dinner. You're not after victory. You're after that one moment when you cash in three cards for fifteen armies and dump them all into Irkutsk like Irkutsk owes you money. And what does finishing get you? Total world domination, achieved by rolling three dice against two for forty straight minutes while your girlfriend reads a book in the next room. You broke a sacred peace treaty with your own brother over Kamchatka. Congratulations. You conquered the entire planet. Now nobody in it will drive you home."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0467", "lang": "en", "target_id": "windows-8", "target_name": "Windows 8", "facts": ["Released in October 2012; removed the Start button and booted into a full-screen grid of tiles designed for touch screens most PCs did not have.", "The 8.1 update in 2013 put a Start button back, Windows 10 (2015) restored the Start menu, and Microsoft skipped the name Windows 9 altogether.", "Microsoft dropped the 'Metro' name in August 2012 after reports that German retailer Metro AG had objected on trademark grounds; Microsoft called 'Metro' a code name and denied any litigation, and the style was afterwards known informally as 'Modern UI'.", "The 'Charms bar' hid Search, Share, and Settings behind an invisible swipe from the right edge.", "Windows RT, the ARM edition launched alongside it, could not run ordinary Windows programs; Microsoft wrote down $900 million of unsold Surface RT tablets in 2013."], "a": {"item_id": "4880b71f9444", "text": "You put Windows 8 on a desktop. A tower. Under a desk. With a mouse. Nobody in your house has ever touched that monitor except to wipe it, yet there you are, dragging your cursor into the top-right corner like a man feeling for a light switch in a stranger's house.\n\nYou wanted to be early to the future. Instead you got a full-screen weather report you never requested and a ten-minute hunt for the shutdown button, which you finally found by Googling it on your phone, like someone searching for the exit in a building they own.\n\nThen you downloaded a third-party Start menu, so now you've spent a weekend undoing the upgrade you chose on purpose.\n\nAnd when your friends ask how it is, you say, \"It's fine once you get used to it.\" That's also what hostages say."}, "b": {"item_id": "8dd545adc448", "text": "You put Windows 8 on a desktop. A tower. Under a desk. With a mouse. You looked at an operating system built for fingertips and thought, \"I'll just reach.\" Who are you? You're the guy who buys a touchscreen glove to use a keyboard. You wanted to feel modern, and now you spend your evenings shoving a cursor into the top-right corner like you're trying to scratch your monitor's back. You wanted productivity. You got a full-screen weather app swallowing your entire twenty-four-inch monitor to tell you it's raining, and you can't figure out how to close it. You lost the Start button and called it \"a fresh start.\" Your coworkers asked how you shut your computer down, and you said, \"I don't. It's always on, like a lifestyle.\" You're not using a PC. You're waving at it, waiting for the Charms bar to love you back."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0468", "lang": "en", "target_id": "nfts", "target_name": "NFTs", "facts": ["Beeple's 'Everydays: The First 5000 Days' sold at Christie's for $69.3 million in March 2021.", "OpenSea, the largest NFT marketplace, processed a record $2.7 billion in trades on 1 May 2022 and just $9.34 million on 28 August 2022, a 99% drop, according to DappRadar data.", "A 2023 dappGambl study found that 95% of the NFT collections it examined had a market value of zero.", "The token usually records only a URL; the image itself sits on an ordinary server or IPFS, not on the blockchain.", "Collins Dictionary named 'NFT' its word of the year for 2021."], "a": {"item_id": "ff6086240a0d", "text": "Let's talk about the guy who bought a JPEG of a bored cartoon ape in 2021 for the price of a Honda. He's not an art collector. Art collectors own things. He owns a receipt. His whole personality now fits in a profile picture, and that picture is a cartoon ape wearing a bandana, the closest he's ever come to a self-portrait. He says \"wagmi\" unironically. He calls strangers \"ser.\" He joined a Discord to get whitelisted for a second Discord. Every morning he checks the floor price like it's his pulse, and he has never once looked at the thing itself, because the thing was never the point. The point was being the guy who got in early, six months before he became the guy who got out late."}, "b": {"item_id": "e8eddc47095c", "text": "Nothing says \"I'm early\" like paying $300 in gas fees to join a Discord with forty thousand other people who are also early. You didn't buy art, Kyle. You bought a seat in a group chat where everyone types \"gm\" at strangers like they're greeting a medieval village. You told your mom it was \"a community.\" You changed your Twitter avatar to a hexagon and started saying \"ngmi\" to coworkers who just asked about the Wi-Fi password. Your whole investment thesis is \"number go up,\" and your exit strategy is finding someone dumber, who is also in the Discord, who is also looking for you. You check the floor price at red lights. You've cried over a Discord mod's emoji reaction. You spent your tax refund to be called \"anon\" by a guy named CryptoChad420, and the only thing you walked away with is proof that you were there when it peaked."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0469", "lang": "en", "target_id": "almond-milk", "target_name": "Almond milk", "facts": ["California grows about 80% of the world's almonds.", "A single almond takes about 1.1 US gallons (roughly 4 litres) of water to grow, a figure Mother Jones published in 2014 that spread during California's drought.", "A 2015 US class action alleged Almond Breeze contained only about 2% almonds; it partly survived a motion to dismiss, was stayed in 2017 pending a separate Blue Diamond labelling settlement, and the plaintiffs then dropped it.", "Pollinating California's almond orchards each February requires trucking in most of the commercial honeybee colonies in the United States.", "Medieval European cooks used almond milk as a Lenten substitute for dairy, centuries before the current fashion."], "a": {"item_id": "cf146e52ac24", "text": "You switched to almond milk to save the planet, which is adorable, because you did it the way you do everything: you read one headline about cows and stopped there. Meanwhile, almonds drink California's drought like a frat boy at a keg, and the bees get trucked in like seasonal labor so you can feel superior in a Tuesday latte. You didn't save the planet. You outsourced your guilt to a state that's on fire.\n\nAnd you told people. Nobody asked, Brent. You announced it at brunch like you'd donated a kidney. You pay an extra dollar fifty for a carton that's about 2% almonds, so you're basically funding a rumor of a nut. Then you flew to Tulum for your birthday.\n\nNow you've heard oat is the responsible choice, so you're quietly converting, and by spring you'll act like you were never on almond's side. That's not moral growth. That's a subscription to guilt with a new flavor every year."}, "b": {"item_id": "1485255f2490", "text": "Let's hear it for the people who switched to almond milk to save the planet. You watched one documentary about cow farts and panicked straight into the arms of a nut that needs a gallon of water apiece. Congratulations: you fled methane and landed on drought. California is cracking like a crème brûlée, and you're out here draining a Central Valley aquifer because you can't make eye contact with a Holstein. And the bees! Billions of them trucked across the country on semis, dying by the hive, so you can tell a coworker you \"don't really do dairy.\" That's not environmentalism. That's relocating the crime scene. You didn't save the Earth. You just moved the guilt somewhere sunnier, slapped a leaf on it, and paid an eighty-cent upcharge for the privilege of feeling better about it."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0470", "lang": "en", "target_id": "almond-milk", "target_name": "Almond milk", "facts": ["California grows about 80% of the world's almonds.", "A single almond takes about 1.1 US gallons (roughly 4 litres) of water to grow, a figure Mother Jones published in 2014 that spread during California's drought.", "A 2015 US class action alleged Almond Breeze contained only about 2% almonds; it partly survived a motion to dismiss, was stayed in 2017 pending a separate Blue Diamond labelling settlement, and the plaintiffs then dropped it.", "Pollinating California's almond orchards each February requires trucking in most of the commercial honeybee colonies in the United States.", "Medieval European cooks used almond milk as a Lenten substitute for dairy, centuries before the current fashion."], "a": {"item_id": "eb9e6316dbc1", "text": "So you switched to almond milk to save the planet. Congratulations. You found out it takes about a gallon of water to grow one almond, and your solution was to drink the almonds. You're the only person in America who needed a thirsty California orchard to hold your hand. You say \"oat\" like it's a personality. You order a flat white with almond and watch the barista sigh as it splits like a bad marriage. You swear you're not preachy, then you explain bee pollination trucks to a man who just asked for ketchup. The planet is grateful. The almonds are thirsty. And you have never looked more like someone whose entire carbon offset is a hashtag."}, "b": {"item_id": "1485255f2490", "text": "Let's hear it for the people who switched to almond milk to save the planet. You watched one documentary about cow farts and panicked straight into the arms of a nut that needs a gallon of water apiece. Congratulations: you fled methane and landed on drought. California is cracking like a crème brûlée, and you're out here draining a Central Valley aquifer because you can't make eye contact with a Holstein. And the bees! Billions of them trucked across the country on semis, dying by the hive, so you can tell a coworker you \"don't really do dairy.\" That's not environmentalism. That's relocating the crime scene. You didn't save the Earth. You just moved the guilt somewhere sunnier, slapped a leaf on it, and paid an eighty-cent upcharge for the privilege of feeling better about it."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0471", "lang": "en", "target_id": "google-glass", "target_name": "Google Glass", "facts": ["Unveiled at Google I/O 2012 with skydivers livestreaming their jump through it onto the Moscone Center roof.", "The Explorer Edition sold to invited users in 2013 for $1,500.", "Wearers were nicknamed 'Glassholes'; bars, cinemas, and casinos banned the device over its camera.", "The consumer programme ended in January 2015; an Enterprise Edition continued until Google stopped selling it in 2023.", "Its display was a 640-by-360 prism in the corner of one eye."], "a": {"item_id": "260f23e62b71", "text": "Nobody wears Google Glass to a bar unless they paid fifteen hundred dollars to be called an Explorer and need the bartender to notice. You're not here for the beer. You're here for the moment the woman by the jukebox squints and says, \"Wait, are you recording me?\" That's the conversation you've been waiting your whole life for. Meanwhile you can't hear her, because you're reading a fantasy football text in the corner of your eye. You wink at the bartender to photograph the tap handles, and he photographs you back, which is the first picture anyone will ever have of you looking this lonely. You say \"OK Glass\" to the girl at the register, and she says \"Sure, buddy\" like she's talking to a smoke detector. You are a beta tester who never got the invitation to be a person."}, "b": {"item_id": "b9dec6f155dd", "text": "Nothing says \"I'm approachable\" like paying fifteen hundred dollars to be a beta tester. You're not an early adopter, you're an unpaid Google intern who also buys his own drinks.\n\nYou came here to be noticed, and it worked, the way a guy who says \"I'm not a cop\" gets noticed. The bartender isn't asking what you're having, he's asking if you're recording. The woman you wanted to impress is explaining to her friend what a \"Glasshole\" is.\n\nThen you said \"OK Glass\" to a crowded room, waited, said it louder, and the only one who responded was the bouncer.\n\nYou wanted to look like the future. Instead you look like the first guy the future gets asked to leave."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0472", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hydrogen-cars", "target_name": "Hydrogen fuel-cell cars", "facts": ["Toyota launched the Mirai in 2014; 'mirai' means 'future' in Japanese.", "Nearly all US hydrogen filling stations are in California; Shell closed all seven of its California passenger-car stations in February 2024.", "More than 95% of the world's hydrogen is made from fossil fuels, mostly by steam-reforming natural gas.", "California pump prices reached about $36 per kilogram in 2023-24, over $200 for a Mirai fill; Toyota has handed buyers fuel cards worth $15,000.", "Mirai owners filed a class action against Toyota in July 2024 over hydrogen shortages and station outages; a second suit in 2025 sought at least $5.7 billion."], "a": {"item_id": "e2e119f95d37", "text": "You bought a hydrogen car because electric felt too mainstream. You wanted the future, but with a longer commute. Congratulations: you're the only person in California who plans a road trip around a gas station's Instagram page, praying the one pump in Fresno isn't \"temporarily offline,\" which is station for \"gone.\"\n\nYou say you're saving the planet, but your fuel is mostly made from natural gas. That's a vegan who only eats at steakhouses for the salad.\n\nThe dealer threw in a free fuel card, the way you give a toddler a tablet so he stops screaming. When it runs out, you'll pay more per mile than a guy in a V8 pickup, in a car with the resale value of a Blockbuster membership.\n\nAnd you keep saying \"the only thing it emits is water,\" like that's a flex. Sir, you leased a very expensive dehumidifier that occasionally takes you to work."}, "b": {"item_id": "94ec4c185be2", "text": "Nobody buys a hydrogen car by accident. You're the person who drives forty minutes past three gas stations to reach the one hydrogen pump in the state, and it's out of order. You're proud of your tailpipe because it drips water. You bring this up at dinner parties, unprompted, like you personally invented rain. You wanted to be the early adopter who makes everyone else feel behind, and you are, because the only other person at the station is a guy in a Prius asking whether you're lost. Most of that hydrogen is made from natural gas, so your eco-friendly car runs on fossil fuel with a better marketing team. Congratulations. You're the most environmentally conscious man in a line of one."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0473", "lang": "en", "target_id": "durian", "target_name": "Durian", "facts": ["Banned on Singapore's MRT trains and in many Southeast Asian hotels because of the smell.", "A 2017 genome study in Nature Genetics traced the odour to a duplicated family of MGL genes that pump out volatile sulfur compounds.", "China buys most of the world's durian exports, chiefly from Thailand, in a trade worth billions of dollars a year.", "Alfred Russel Wallace wrote in 1856 that eating it was 'a new sensation worth a voyage to the East to experience'."], "a": {"item_id": "0c82ba06de9c", "text": "People who eat durian in public aren't hungry. They're recruiting. Every bite comes with a sermon: \"You just haven't had a *good* one.\" Sir, I'm on a bus. I didn't ask to join your church.\n\nSingapore fines people for bringing it on the train, and these folks hear that like a dare. They're the only foodies whose restaurant review gets filed as a police report.\n\nAnd the payoff? Seventy dollars on a Musang King so you can watch strangers silently change seats. You paid premium to become a fire drill. Your date calls it \"an acquired taste,\" which is exactly what you say about someone you're about to break up with.\n\nThen they stroll back to the hotel, walk right past the sign with a red circle through their entire personality, and still act shocked when housekeeping files a complaint."}, "b": {"item_id": "94cc53dad1db", "text": "Look at him on the MRT, scooping durian with a plastic spoon like he's the only man in Singapore with a palate. Nobody asked for this. The carriage empties in a ring around him, and he takes it as respect. That's the whole transaction: the cleared seat, the sideways looks, proof he's braver than the rest of us. \"It's like custard,\" he says, eyes watering, because that's what happens when you're trying this hard. Then he posts the video captioned \"not for everyone.\" He's right. It's not for everyone. It's for him, and everyone within fifty metres just got a free sample of his ego."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0474", "lang": "en", "target_id": "vision-pro", "target_name": "Apple Vision Pro", "facts": ["Released in February 2024 at $3,499, with an external battery pack on a cable rated for about two hours.", "'EyeSight' shows a rendering of the wearer's eyes on an outward-facing screen.", "Apple calls it 'spatial computing' and its App Store rules tell developers not to describe apps as AR, VR, XR, or MR.", "Reports during 2024 said Apple had cut production; analysts' first-year sales estimates were well under a million units.", "Weighs 600-650 g, before the battery."], "a": {"item_id": "7f2991f5a417", "text": "Look at this guy on the L train, strapped into $3,500 of ski goggles, pinching the air like he's conducting a very small orchestra. He isn't watching a movie. He's got forty floating windows hovering over a man eating a bagel, and he's \"doing spatial computing,\" which he'll tell you unprompted. The front screen shows his eyes so you feel seen, the most unsettling feature in consumer tech. He isn't on the subway. He's in a living room with a 4K view of the 4 train. Every few stops he lifts the goggles to check whether anyone noticed. We did, pal. Nobody's immersed. We're all just watching a grown man pinch at nothing, a battery cable running to his jacket pocket like a leash, and the only thing he's computing is how fast his neighbors can file a complaint."}, "b": {"item_id": "90ecfc190a47", "text": "You paid thirty-five hundred dollars to ride the 6 train inside Joshua Tree. Everyone else has to smell the actual 6 train; you just hear it echoing through a desert like a ghost with a MetroCard. You want us to know you're an early adopter. We know. Your little cartoon eyes are blinking at us like a hostage video. That cable running to your pocket says \"the future\" and also \"IV drip.\" You pinch the air at Union Square and a stranger thinks you're ordering him to move. You've got a hundred-foot Mac screen floating over a man eating soup out of a Pringles can. And the battery dies in two hours, so you'll still get to experience the subway in full, unimmersive 3D. Your commute outlasts your device. Welcome back to reality. It's delayed."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0475", "lang": "en", "target_id": "papyrus", "target_name": "Papyrus", "facts": ["Hand-drawn by Chris Costello in 1982 over about six months, when he was 23; he sold the rights to Letraset for $750.", "Bundled with macOS and with Microsoft Office, which is why it appears on so many menus and signs.", "James Cameron's Avatar (2009) used it for the film's logo and subtitles.", "A 2017 Saturday Night Live sketch starring Ryan Gosling is about a man unable to get over that choice."], "a": {"item_id": "c986ccdedefc", "text": "You opened a day spa. You wanted serenity, wisdom, a whisper of the Nile. So you scrolled the font menu, sailed past Comic Sans feeling very sophisticated, and stopped on the one James Cameron used to title a billion-dollar movie. Congratulations: your sign now says \"ancient healing\" exactly as loudly as your aunt's church potluck flyer. Every hummus joint, every reiki studio, every tea shop run by a man named River made this same choice, alone, convinced they'd discovered it. The guy who designed it was twenty-three and has spent forty years apologizing. You paid a contractor to carve his apology into cedar. You wanted customers to feel transported. They do: straight back to 2003, a Word document, and a fourth grader typing \"Spring Bake Sale.\" Cucumber water not included."}, "b": {"item_id": "1988c200f894", "text": "Let's talk about the restaurant owner who picked Papyrus for the sign. You bought a franchise of \"authentic\" sourdough, you have a chalkboard that says \"Gather,\" and you think a font that looks like it was carved on a tree stump is the closest thing to a hug a customer can get. You didn't choose it. It chose you, the moment you typed \"organic\" into a font search. Now your $14 avocado toast sits under a logo that reads like a yoga instructor's grocery list. Your spa is \"a sanctuary,\" but the sign out front says \"we're still figuring out our brand.\" You wanted the calm of a Bali retreat. You got the calm of a dentist's waiting room in 2009, and somehow it still costs you $400 a month in massages you can't afford."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0476", "lang": "en", "target_id": "oat-milk", "target_name": "Oat milk", "facts": ["Oatly started in Sweden in the 1990s, built on Lund University research by Rickard Öste on lactose intolerance.", "Oatly's slogan 'It's like milk but made for humans' was banned in Sweden in 2015 after the dairy lobby LRF Mjölk sued; Oatly then published the lawsuit as advertising.", "Oatly listed on Nasdaq in May 2021 at a valuation near $10 billion; the shares later lost more than 90% of their value.", "Commercial oat milk is made by breaking oat starch into maltose with amylase enzymes, and many brands, Oatly among them, add rapeseed oil; the result has more sugar and a higher glycaemic index than almond or soy milk."], "a": {"item_id": "4f077d2ff349", "text": "You didn't order oat milk because you're lactose intolerant. You ordered it because you read one article about almonds in 2019 and it became your whole personality.\n\nNow you say \"oat\" to the barista like a secret password to a club that has no door. Seventy-five cents extra, and you act like you've personally reversed the polar ice caps. You then fly to Lisbon for a long weekend.\n\nYou've told three people at dinner that you \"feel better since cutting dairy.\" Feel better how? Your cheese board arrived and you ate it like a raccoon at a wedding.\n\nYou know the brand name. You have opinions about the brand name. You once said \"barista blend\" out loud, on purpose, to a friend.\n\nThe cow was fine, Brad. The cow didn't need you to be brave."}, "b": {"item_id": "a579a10435cb", "text": "You know the person who orders oat milk? They're not vegan. They're not lactose intolerant. They own a tote bag that says \"Be Kind\" and a laptop they've never opened for work. \"Oat, please,\" they say, like they're ordering a sacrament. Almond was for people who've been to Joshua Tree, and soy was for their dad. Then they lean in to tell the barista the planet thanks them. Their whole personality is a seventy-cent upcharge they'll bring up at your birthday dinner. Ask why and you'll get six minutes on cows, then a pivot to their sourdough starter and whether you've tried the new Pilates place. They didn't change their milk. They changed their identity, and the milk was the cheapest part of the rebrand."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0477", "lang": "en", "target_id": "comic-sans", "target_name": "Comic Sans", "facts": ["Designed by Vincent Connare at Microsoft in 1994, modeled on the comic-book lettering in The Dark Knight Returns and Watchmen.", "Made for the speech bubbles of Rover, the cartoon dog in Microsoft Bob; it missed Bob's release and shipped instead in the Windows 95 Plus! pack.", "CERN's July 2012 Higgs boson discovery announcement was presented on slides set in it.", "The 'Ban Comic Sans' campaign, started by designers Dave and Holly Combs in 1999, ran until 2019, when they retired it and launched usecomicsans.com."], "a": {"item_id": "4b3978a4ee09", "text": "Somebody in this building printed \"URGENT: FIRE DRILL MANDATORY\" in Comic Sans and taped it to the break room fridge. I want that person to know we all saw it and we all laughed, though not the way they hoped. They wanted the fire marshal's authority. What they got was a note that looks like a kindergarten teacher wrote it right after finding out about the fire. They want to be taken seriously while seeming approachable, like wearing a tuxedo with light-up sneakers. They think the wobbly letters say \"we're a team.\" Nobody reads it that way. We read it as a birthday party for the fire drill, with cake in the parking lot. Next time, pick a plain font and own being in charge. Nobody has ever evacuated a building faster because the notice looked cheerful."}, "b": {"item_id": "011c029d7bc6", "text": "Let's talk about the person who posts the boil-water advisory in Comic Sans. You had one job: make residents afraid of their faucets. Instead, E. coli looks like it's coming to a sleepover.\n\nThese people think \"approachable\" is a setting you can click. The office manager typing MANDATORY LAYOFFS in a font Microsoft made for a cartoon dog's speech bubble. Sharon, you're firing forty people in the voice of a golden retriever.\n\nWhat are they after? Bad news with zero blame. The authority of a memo, the forgiveness of a kindergarten cubby label.\n\nAnd what does it get them? CERN found the Higgs boson, the particle that gives the universe mass, and announced it in Comic Sans. Humanity's greatest physics breakthrough, and the internet's only question was: who let the dog type?"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0478", "lang": "en", "target_id": "pmma", "target_name": "PMMA (e-beam resist)", "facts": ["The same polymer as Plexiglas and acrylic glass, dissolved in anisole or chlorobenzene and spun into a thin film.", "The oldest electron-beam resist still in everyday use; IBM researchers reported it as one in the late 1960s.", "Positive tone: the beam breaks polymer chains and the exposed region dissolves in a MIBK:IPA developer.", "Resolves below 10 nm but has poor sensitivity, so doses of hundreds to over a thousand µC/cm² (depending on voltage) make writing slow.", "Poor dry-etch resistance, so it is used mostly for lift-off rather than as an etch mask."], "a": {"item_id": "fe949f7eb86d", "text": "Oh, you're still on PMMA. Let me guess: your advisor's thesis used it, your advisor's advisor used it, and somewhere on the group drive is a recipe called \"DO NOT CHANGE,\" last edited by a postdoc who now sells insurance in Ohio.\n\nYou call it \"reliable.\" You've run the same dose test eleven times because you refuse to admit the variable is you. Your big optimization this year was going from 950 A4 to 950 A3 and calling it a paper.\n\nYou queue a pattern Friday night, check on it Sunday, and tell people the beam is \"just taking its time.\" Then you say you don't trust anything without forty years of citations, which is also how you feel about your dentist and your Windows XP tool PC.\n\nWhat does it get you? Gorgeous 10-nm lines, a lift-off you pray over, and no social life. The chip is perfect. Your weekend is exposed and overdeveloped."}, "b": {"item_id": "a93c5e595a57", "text": "You're still running PMMA? Bless you. You're the guy who spins 950K A4 at 4000 rpm like it's 2003 and then acts shocked when your thickness drifts. You bake at 180 for ninety seconds like it's a sourdough starter, and you develop in MIBK to IPA one-to-three while muttering about contrast. Your writes take eleven hours because you're hosing the sample with dose just to draw a line HSQ would have handled before lunch. Meanwhile the ZEP people are home by six, drinking something with a garnish. You're not a purist. You're a man who read one paper on proximity effect in 2009 and never recovered. Your lift-off is clean, I'll give you that. Honestly, it's the most reliable thing about you."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0479", "lang": "en", "target_id": "macau", "target_name": "Macau", "facts": ["A Portuguese settlement from the 1550s until the handover to China in December 1999.", "Casino revenue passed the Las Vegas Strip's in 2006, and at its 2013 peak, about $45 billion, was roughly seven times the Strip's.", "One company, STDM, held the casino monopoly for four decades until licences opened to competition in 2002.", "Among the most densely populated places on Earth, at roughly 20,000 people per square kilometre.", "The Cotai Strip stands on land reclaimed from the sea between the islands of Taipa and Coloane."], "a": {"item_id": "6405f7045e3c", "text": "You lost it in Vegas, or Manila, or on a Tuesday online, and now you're on the ferry from Hong Kong with the confidence of a man who has finally \"figured out baccarat.\" You haven't. Baccarat has no figuring out. It's a coin flip in a tuxedo.\n\nYou're not here for money, you're here for a story where you come back a legend. Meanwhile, the guy next to you, who's been awake since Thursday, is bowing to a card like it's his ancestor, and he's winning, which is why you think it's contagious.\n\nYour plan: one hour, minimum table. By hour six, you're in a gondola at the Venetian, inside a mall, under a painted sky, asking a singing man from Guangdong if he takes WeChat.\n\nThe only thing you win back is a Portuguese egg tart, and even that's on the credit card."}, "b": {"item_id": "67e9bb83372e", "text": "You already lost the down payment, so naturally you're taking the TurboJET from Hong Kong to win it back, because nothing says financial recovery like a ferry with a buffet. You've got a system for baccarat. Baccarat! The only decision is \"Banker\" or \"Player,\" and you still fill a little scorecard with red and blue dots like the shoe is sending you messages. It isn't. That's just a deck of cards and your mortgage. You've got red underwear on for luck and a junket host named \"Uncle\" who is definitely not your uncle. By 4 a.m. you'll be drifting through the fake Venice canals in a gondola, humming, past the pawn shop that's already priced your Rolex. Then you'll stumble up to the Ruins of St. Paul's: a church that burned until only the front was left standing. Finally, something here that looks like you."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0480", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hydrogen-cars", "target_name": "Hydrogen fuel-cell cars", "facts": ["Toyota launched the Mirai in 2014; 'mirai' means 'future' in Japanese.", "Nearly all US hydrogen filling stations are in California; Shell closed all seven of its California passenger-car stations in February 2024.", "More than 95% of the world's hydrogen is made from fossil fuels, mostly by steam-reforming natural gas.", "California pump prices reached about $36 per kilogram in 2023-24, over $200 for a Mirai fill; Toyota has handed buyers fuel cards worth $15,000.", "Mirai owners filed a class action against Toyota in July 2024 over hydrogen shortages and station outages; a second suit in 2025 sought at least $5.7 billion."], "a": {"item_id": "e2e119f95d37", "text": "You bought a hydrogen car because electric felt too mainstream. You wanted the future, but with a longer commute. Congratulations: you're the only person in California who plans a road trip around a gas station's Instagram page, praying the one pump in Fresno isn't \"temporarily offline,\" which is station for \"gone.\"\n\nYou say you're saving the planet, but your fuel is mostly made from natural gas. That's a vegan who only eats at steakhouses for the salad.\n\nThe dealer threw in a free fuel card, the way you give a toddler a tablet so he stops screaming. When it runs out, you'll pay more per mile than a guy in a V8 pickup, in a car with the resale value of a Blockbuster membership.\n\nAnd you keep saying \"the only thing it emits is water,\" like that's a flex. Sir, you leased a very expensive dehumidifier that occasionally takes you to work."}, "b": {"item_id": "1f255592ab99", "text": "Let's talk about the guy who bought a hydrogen car. He wanted to be greener than the Tesla people and smugger than the Prius people, and found the one way to do both while living like a medieval pilgrim. His life is now a map of eleven pumps, and nine say \"offline.\" He loves telling you it \"only emits water.\" Yeah, so does he, every time the app says \"station unavailable.\" He brags that refueling takes five minutes, after the forty-minute drive and the two-hour line behind three other Mirai owners, all quietly weeping. Toyota handed him a fifteen-thousand-dollar fuel card and he burned through it before the warranty paperwork dried. Now he owns a sixty-thousand-dollar car worth a used futon, all to prove he's an early adopter of a future that met him and moved to Japan."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0481", "lang": "en", "target_id": "pmma", "target_name": "PMMA (e-beam resist)", "facts": ["The same polymer as Plexiglas and acrylic glass, dissolved in anisole or chlorobenzene and spun into a thin film.", "The oldest electron-beam resist still in everyday use; IBM researchers reported it as one in the late 1960s.", "Positive tone: the beam breaks polymer chains and the exposed region dissolves in a MIBK:IPA developer.", "Resolves below 10 nm but has poor sensitivity, so doses of hundreds to over a thousand µC/cm² (depending on voltage) make writing slow.", "Poor dry-etch resistance, so it is used mostly for lift-off rather than as an etch mask."], "a": {"item_id": "a93c5e595a57", "text": "You're still running PMMA? Bless you. You're the guy who spins 950K A4 at 4000 rpm like it's 2003 and then acts shocked when your thickness drifts. You bake at 180 for ninety seconds like it's a sourdough starter, and you develop in MIBK to IPA one-to-three while muttering about contrast. Your writes take eleven hours because you're hosing the sample with dose just to draw a line HSQ would have handled before lunch. Meanwhile the ZEP people are home by six, drinking something with a garnish. You're not a purist. You're a man who read one paper on proximity effect in 2009 and never recovered. Your lift-off is clean, I'll give you that. Honestly, it's the most reliable thing about you."}, "b": {"item_id": "6303a3fe60f6", "text": "You still spin PMMA. Of course you do. Your advisor's thesis used it, his advisor's thesis used it, and somewhere a 1970s IBM engineer remains the most cutting-edge person in your academic lineage. You call it \"high resolution\" the way your uncle calls his Camry \"reliable.\" You book the e-beam overnight because your dose is measured in geological time, then dunk your chip in MIBK:IPA with a stopwatch and a prayer, chilling the developer like it's a fine Riesling. When the plasma etch eats your mask before your substrate, you just whisper \"bilayer, 495K under 950K\" like it's a personality. You're not chasing nanostructures. You're chasing a recipe nobody can blame you for. And it works: the gold lifted off beautifully. Your career is still soaking in acetone, waiting for something to come loose."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0482", "lang": "en", "target_id": "php", "target_name": "PHP", "facts": ["Created by Rasmus Lerdorf in 1994 as 'Personal Home Page Tools', a set of C programs for tracking visitors to his own résumé page; the name now officially stands for the recursive 'PHP: Hypertext Preprocessor'.", "Lerdorf, in his 2007 MySQL Conference talk 'PHP on Hormones': 'I'm not a real programmer. I throw together things until it works then I move on.'", "Runs WordPress, which W3Techs counted on 39.4% of all websites (58.1% of sites with a known CMS) on 9 November 2026.", "Core function names are inconsistent (strpos, str_replace, htmlspecialchars, nl2br) partly because, as Lerdorf confirmed, early function names were chosen to balance a hash table keyed on string length.", "Until PHP 8 (2020), the comparison 0 == 'foo' was true because the string was silently converted to a number."], "a": {"item_id": "da336777c19f", "text": "Let's hear it for the people who write PHP for a living. They've got a dollar sign tattooed on their soul, and they still say \"it's a lot better now\" with a straight face, like a hostage defending the guy holding him. Ask one why they stayed and they'll tell you the shared hosting is cheap and every client has a cousin who needs a website. Ask what they're really after and it's the moment someone on Reddit admits Laravel is \"actually pretty good.\" They've memorized which argument goes first in in_array and they're proud of it. They have a folder called \"legacy\" that's older than their marriage, and they open it on Fridays for fun. Respect."}, "b": {"item_id": "c1d0d772e1d6", "text": "PHP developers are the only people who put a dollar sign in front of every variable and still can't afford rent. You chose a career where \"senior\" means you remember whether it's needle-haystack or haystack-needle. Your dream job was building the next Facebook; your actual job is the fourteenth WordPress plugin for a dentist in Ohio, deployed via FileZilla. Every conversation you have starts with \"PHP 8 is actually really good now,\" which is what people say about their ex after therapy. You call yourselves Laravel \"artisans,\" because \"guy who fixes the contact form\" didn't fit on LinkedIn. What does it get you? Job security, because nobody else is willing to open that codebase from 2009 that still calls mysql_real_escape_string. Congratulations: you're unfireable, the way a load-bearing wall is unfireable."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0483", "lang": "en", "target_id": "emacs", "target_name": "Emacs", "facts": ["Began in 1976 as a set of macros for the TECO editor at MIT's AI Lab, written by Richard Stallman and others; GNU Emacs followed in 1985.", "Extensible in its own Lisp dialect, Emacs Lisp; the standing joke is that it is a fine operating system lacking only a good editor.", "Ships with a psychotherapist (M-x doctor), Tetris, a mail client, a calendar, and a web browser (eww).", "An old backronym is 'Eight Megabytes And Constantly Swapping', from when 8 MB was a lot of memory.", "'Emacs pinky' is the name for the hand strain blamed on its Control-key chords."], "a": {"item_id": "59215bf985f1", "text": "You people don't use Emacs, you move into it, like a guy who rents a storage unit and starts getting mail there. You read email in mu4e, browse in eww, run a shell in eshell, and when you finally crack, you M-x doctor and tell a Lisp therapist your feelings. Your init.el is four thousand lines, half commented out, and you've declared Emacs bankruptcy twice: the only bankruptcy that ever made someone feel superior. You spent a weekend cutting startup from 1.8 seconds to 0.9, on a program you never close. Your left pinky is shot from holding Control, a war wound from never touching a mouse. Your whole life is in Org-mode, including a habit tracker whose only entry is \"configure habit tracker.\" And that novel you built the perfect writing environment for? It's in there too. Under TODO."}, "b": {"item_id": "e61d80ea2a75", "text": "Let's talk about the people who don't use Emacs, they live in it. They read their email in there, check their calendar, play chess, and have never once figured out how to leave. Their pinkies are permanently stretched across Ctrl like they're holding a grudge. They spent three weekends configuring a text editor so they could avoid one afternoon of real work. Ask what they're building and they'll say \"my init file,\" proudly, like it's a novel. It's four thousand lines long. Nobody has read it, including them. They'll tell you it's an operating system. Their mother still calls it a text editor, and she's the only one in the family who's been honest with them."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0484", "lang": "en", "target_id": "google-sheets", "target_name": "Google Sheets", "facts": ["Launched in June 2006 as Google Spreadsheets, built on XL2Web, a product Google acquired from 2Web Technologies.", "A spreadsheet is capped at 10 million cells (raised from 5 million in 2022).", "Functions such as IMPORTHTML, IMPORTXML, and GOOGLEFINANCE pull live web data straight into cells.", "Free with a Google account; files live in Google Drive, and collaborators edit the same sheet simultaneously with coloured cursors.", "In a browser, offline editing works only in Chrome or Edge, with the Google Docs Offline extension installed and offline access switched on while still connected; the Android and iOS apps work offline natively."], "a": {"item_id": "b196e566b5fd", "text": "You're not a manager, you're a guy who discovered conditional formatting and mistook it for leadership. You call it \"our single source of truth,\" which is adorable, because there are nine tabs, and three of them are named \"Copy of Q3 (DO NOT USE).\"\n\nOne IMPORTRANGE holds your entire department together, and you're the only one who knows. When it breaks, \"Loading...\" becomes your team's mission statement.\n\nYou wanted visibility, and you got it: an anonymous capybara hovering over your cell while someone you hired quietly overwrites your formula with a hardcoded 47. You wanted accountability, so you @-tagged Greg in a comment eleven times. Greg has never opened a comment. Greg's row is yellow. Yellow means something. Nobody remembers what.\n\nYou say you'll \"move to a real tool next quarter\" while building a dropdown menu like it's a cathedral.\n\nYour career highlight is a pivot table. Your legacy is \"Request edit access.\""}, "b": {"item_id": "85e5be0c8934", "text": "So you run the whole team out of one Google Sheet. You have forty tabs, and one is called \"FINAL FINAL use this one.\" Every task gets a color, and the palette only makes sense to you. You built a Gantt chart out of merged cells, so the project is technically on schedule but unreadable to anyone born after the chart. Your status dropdown has six options, four of which mean \"fine.\" You want control, so you are the only one allowed near the VLOOKUPs, and you leave comments that just say \"see my note\" with no note. What it gets you is a team that makes its own copy, pastes the real numbers into a tab called \"actual,\" and checks version history only to find out who ruined your formatting."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0485", "lang": "en", "target_id": "chicago-deep-dish", "target_name": "Chicago deep-dish pizza", "facts": ["Created at Pizzeria Uno in Chicago in 1943; credit is disputed among founder Ike Sewell, his partner Ric Riccardo, and cook Rudy Malnati.", "Assembled upside down: sliced mozzarella on the dough, then toppings, then crushed tomatoes on top, so the cheese does not scorch during the long bake.", "Jon Stewart called it 'tomato soup in a bread bowl' and 'a casserole' on The Daily Show in 2013.", "Chicagoans' everyday pizza is thin 'tavern-style' cut into squares; GrubHub order data reported by WBEZ in 2013 showed city residents ordering non-deep-dish pizza about 10 to 1 over deep dish, with deep dish and stuffed making up under 9% of orders."], "a": {"item_id": "999fc0b6671a", "text": "Let's talk about the people who insist deep-dish is pizza. You're from the city that named its own comedy theater Second City and still acts shocked when nobody ranks you first. You're not defending food. You're nursing a grudge against New York you've carried since losing the World's Fair argument and the skyline argument. You'll wait forty-five minutes at Lou Malnati's, order before you're seated like it's the DMV, then ask for a knife and fork and call it \"the real thing.\" Jon Stewart roasted you on national TV and you took it personally, because deep down you knew he was right. You ship it on dry ice to your cousin in Phoenix like an organ donation. And what does the label get you? Navy Pier tourists nodding politely, a two-hour nap, and the Midwest's loudest participation trophy."}, "b": {"item_id": "8d0af0e1882c", "text": "Let's talk about the people who call it pizza. Chicagoans, mostly, from a city that spent a century being called the \"Second City\" and finally found a hill to die on. You don't love it. You're defending it.\n\nYou put your name in at 5:15 and eat at 7:00, then tell everyone it was worth the wait, because the alternative is admitting you spent your anniversary on a bench with a family from Ohio. You're after one thing: a fight with a New Yorker. And you win, in the sense that he stops texting you, which in Chicago counts as a civic victory.\n\nWhat do you get? A two-hour nap, a ruined evening, and a Goldbelly order shipped to Phoenix, where you eat it alone in a kitchen, defending a city you moved away from in 2011."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0486", "lang": "en", "target_id": "oat-milk", "target_name": "Oat milk", "facts": ["Oatly started in Sweden in the 1990s, built on Lund University research by Rickard Öste on lactose intolerance.", "Oatly's slogan 'It's like milk but made for humans' was banned in Sweden in 2015 after the dairy lobby LRF Mjölk sued; Oatly then published the lawsuit as advertising.", "Oatly listed on Nasdaq in May 2021 at a valuation near $10 billion; the shares later lost more than 90% of their value.", "Commercial oat milk is made by breaking oat starch into maltose with amylase enzymes, and many brands, Oatly among them, add rapeseed oil; the result has more sugar and a higher glycaemic index than almond or soy milk."], "a": {"item_id": "0a1c5a53fa43", "text": "Let's talk about the oat milk people. You're not lactose intolerant. You've never been tested. You just heard cows are bad and almonds are thirsty, so you picked the one grain that sounds like a farm without the farmer. Congratulations: you pay an extra eighty cents to drink liquid porridge with a splash of rapeseed oil, and you call it \"cleaner.\" You switched from almond the exact week a podcast told you California was in a drought. That's not values, that's a trend with a loyalty card. And the best part? You order it with an air of quiet suffering, like the barista should thank you for saving the planet one Barista Edition carton at a time. Your grandmother ate oats so she could survive winter. You drink them so a stranger in line thinks you've been to therapy."}, "b": {"item_id": "4f077d2ff349", "text": "You didn't order oat milk because you're lactose intolerant. You ordered it because you read one article about almonds in 2019 and it became your whole personality.\n\nNow you say \"oat\" to the barista like a secret password to a club that has no door. Seventy-five cents extra, and you act like you've personally reversed the polar ice caps. You then fly to Lisbon for a long weekend.\n\nYou've told three people at dinner that you \"feel better since cutting dairy.\" Feel better how? Your cheese board arrived and you ate it like a raccoon at a wedding.\n\nYou know the brand name. You have opinions about the brand name. You once said \"barista blend\" out loud, on purpose, to a friend.\n\nThe cow was fine, Brad. The cow didn't need you to be brave."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0487", "lang": "en", "target_id": "chicago-deep-dish", "target_name": "Chicago deep-dish pizza", "facts": ["Created at Pizzeria Uno in Chicago in 1943; credit is disputed among founder Ike Sewell, his partner Ric Riccardo, and cook Rudy Malnati.", "Assembled upside down: sliced mozzarella on the dough, then toppings, then crushed tomatoes on top, so the cheese does not scorch during the long bake.", "Jon Stewart called it 'tomato soup in a bread bowl' and 'a casserole' on The Daily Show in 2013.", "Chicagoans' everyday pizza is thin 'tavern-style' cut into squares; GrubHub order data reported by WBEZ in 2013 showed city residents ordering non-deep-dish pizza about 10 to 1 over deep dish, with deep dish and stuffed making up under 9% of orders."], "a": {"item_id": "8d0af0e1882c", "text": "Let's talk about the people who call it pizza. Chicagoans, mostly, from a city that spent a century being called the \"Second City\" and finally found a hill to die on. You don't love it. You're defending it.\n\nYou put your name in at 5:15 and eat at 7:00, then tell everyone it was worth the wait, because the alternative is admitting you spent your anniversary on a bench with a family from Ohio. You're after one thing: a fight with a New Yorker. And you win, in the sense that he stops texting you, which in Chicago counts as a civic victory.\n\nWhat do you get? A two-hour nap, a ruined evening, and a Goldbelly order shipped to Phoenix, where you eat it alone in a kitchen, defending a city you moved away from in 2011."}, "b": {"item_id": "b9801548af22", "text": "Oh, you're the guy who says deep-dish isn't pizza. Let me guess: you own a wood-fired oven, you named your sourdough starter, and one documentary about Naples turned you into the Supreme Court of dough. You didn't come to Lou's to eat. You came to be the smartest man in the booth, and you're going to hold your fork like a gavel until the appetizers go cold. Your whole personality is a bit about being born in the wrong country. Relax, Gino. Nobody asked for a ruling. Chicago built a pie that takes forty-five minutes and a second mortgage, and you're upset it didn't come with your permission slip. Pass the slice and let your dignity cool down with the cheese."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0488", "lang": "en", "target_id": "beanie-babies", "target_name": "Beanie Babies", "facts": ["Launched by Ty Inc. in 1993 and sold only through small gift shops, with designs 'retired' to manufacture scarcity.", "At the height of the craze Beanie Babies accounted for about 10% of eBay's sales, and eBay's 1998 IPO filing said the site at times had over 30,000 Beanie Baby auctions running simultaneously in the quarter ending June 1998.", "In 1999 a divorcing couple divided their Beanie Baby collection one by one on a courtroom floor, in a photograph that ran worldwide.", "In 1999 Ty announced that Beanie Babies would be retired at the end of the year, then reversed itself after asking the public to vote and seeing an overwhelming vote to keep them.", "Most 'rare' Beanies that price guides valued in the thousands now sell for a few dollars."], "a": {"item_id": "7f33e3df1a62", "text": "You bought sixty Princess bears because a guy on a message board said they'd put your kids through college. Congratulations: your kids are now in their thirties and still can't afford college, but they do know what a \"tush tag\" is.\n\nIn 1998 you drove to three Hallmark stores before dawn, fought a woman named Deb over a shipment, and called it \"market research.\" You mocked people buying stocks. \"Paper,\" you said. \"Not tangible.\" Meanwhile the dot-com crash hurt less than your discovery that every other person on Earth also had sixty Princess bears.\n\nYou still keep them in tag protectors, because you believe a stranger will one day inspect the corner of a cardboard heart and wire you retirement money.\n\nYour eBay listing says \"RARE.\" It has said \"RARE\" since 2004. Your lawyer asked what you wanted from the divorce, and you said \"the Peace bear.\" You didn't mean it as a joke. That's the tragedy."}, "b": {"item_id": "1a45d6768831", "text": "Let's talk about the Beanie Baby investors, the folks who bought eleven Peanut the Elephants and never let a child within arm's reach of one. You know the type. He's got a binder. Every bear has a hang tag, a tush tag, and a plastic sleeve, and he inspects the tush tag for misprints like it's the Dead Sea Scrolls. He says \"retired\" the way a doctor says \"terminal.\" His retirement plan is one Princess bear and a prayer. Ty Inc. never promised him a dime, but he's still waiting for the big payout. Meanwhile his daughter sleeps with a bald rabbit named Hope, because the good stuff is sealed in the basement, appreciating, never once hugged. Congratulations. You own the most expensive animals in your house that nobody's allowed to touch."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0489", "lang": "en", "target_id": "oat-milk", "target_name": "Oat milk", "facts": ["Oatly started in Sweden in the 1990s, built on Lund University research by Rickard Öste on lactose intolerance.", "Oatly's slogan 'It's like milk but made for humans' was banned in Sweden in 2015 after the dairy lobby LRF Mjölk sued; Oatly then published the lawsuit as advertising.", "Oatly listed on Nasdaq in May 2021 at a valuation near $10 billion; the shares later lost more than 90% of their value.", "Commercial oat milk is made by breaking oat starch into maltose with amylase enzymes, and many brands, Oatly among them, add rapeseed oil; the result has more sugar and a higher glycaemic index than almond or soy milk."], "a": {"item_id": "a579a10435cb", "text": "You know the person who orders oat milk? They're not vegan. They're not lactose intolerant. They own a tote bag that says \"Be Kind\" and a laptop they've never opened for work. \"Oat, please,\" they say, like they're ordering a sacrament. Almond was for people who've been to Joshua Tree, and soy was for their dad. Then they lean in to tell the barista the planet thanks them. Their whole personality is a seventy-cent upcharge they'll bring up at your birthday dinner. Ask why and you'll get six minutes on cows, then a pivot to their sourdough starter and whether you've tried the new Pilates place. They didn't change their milk. They changed their identity, and the milk was the cheapest part of the rebrand."}, "b": {"item_id": "0a1c5a53fa43", "text": "Let's talk about the oat milk people. You're not lactose intolerant. You've never been tested. You just heard cows are bad and almonds are thirsty, so you picked the one grain that sounds like a farm without the farmer. Congratulations: you pay an extra eighty cents to drink liquid porridge with a splash of rapeseed oil, and you call it \"cleaner.\" You switched from almond the exact week a podcast told you California was in a drought. That's not values, that's a trend with a loyalty card. And the best part? You order it with an air of quiet suffering, like the barista should thank you for saving the planet one Barista Edition carton at a time. Your grandmother ate oats so she could survive winter. You drink them so a stranger in line thinks you've been to therapy."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0490", "lang": "en", "target_id": "canberra", "target_name": "Canberra", "facts": ["Chosen as a compromise because neither Sydney nor Melbourne would accept the other as capital; Australia's constitution required the capital to be at least 100 miles from Sydney.", "Designed by the Chicago architects Walter Burley Griffin and Marion Mahony Griffin, who won an international competition in 1912.", "Its centrepiece, Lake Burley Griffin, is artificial, created by damming the Molonglo River in 1963-64.", "Parliament House (1988) is built into Capital Hill under a lawn meant for citizens to walk over their politicians; the lawn was fenced off in 2017 for security.", "Melbourne served as the capital until 1927 while Canberra was being built."], "a": {"item_id": "5732fa32845b", "text": "You know the type who moves to Canberra for \"two years, max.\" They arrive with a Perth accent, a Bunnings-sized ambition, and a LinkedIn banner that says \"Shaping policy.\" Six months in, they're saying \"Manuka\" like it's the suburb they grew up in. They want an acting EL2 before they've learned which parkway goes where. They own a lanyard drawer and a plan to be a Senate estimates star by spring. What it gets them: a townhouse in Gungahlin, a friend group made entirely of people who also work at Finance, and weekends spent driving to Bungendore to feel like they've left. Two years later they're still here, telling their mates back home the job's \"nearly done,\" which is exactly what they said in 2016."}, "b": {"item_id": "e90ceb66c6fb", "text": "You moved to Canberra for a \"two-year stint to get your EL1,\" which is what everyone says, including the guy who's been saying it since 2011 and now owns a Subaru and strong opinions about Gungahlin. You joined to \"shape policy,\" and your policy is now formatting other people's policy until a Deputy Secretary changes the font. You wanted impact. You got a briefing note that three people read, one of whom was you, and that was to check for typos.\n\nYou left Sydney because you couldn't afford a house, and now you own one in Chisholm, so congratulations, you've got a mortgage and a view of a Bunnings.\n\nAt parties you ask \"which department?\" like it's a star sign. Every long weekend you flee to Melbourne, where friends ask when you're coming home, and you say \"soon,\" knowing you've got a clearance, a generous super match, and no way out.\n\nYou didn't climb the ladder. You joined the queue."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0491", "lang": "en", "target_id": "canberra", "target_name": "Canberra", "facts": ["Chosen as a compromise because neither Sydney nor Melbourne would accept the other as capital; Australia's constitution required the capital to be at least 100 miles from Sydney.", "Designed by the Chicago architects Walter Burley Griffin and Marion Mahony Griffin, who won an international competition in 1912.", "Its centrepiece, Lake Burley Griffin, is artificial, created by damming the Molonglo River in 1963-64.", "Parliament House (1988) is built into Capital Hill under a lawn meant for citizens to walk over their politicians; the lawn was fenced off in 2017 for security.", "Melbourne served as the capital until 1927 while Canberra was being built."], "a": {"item_id": "f332ff248cdb", "text": "You came to Canberra for the job. Everyone does. Nobody moves here for the weather, which has a grudge. You're here for a portfolio you can't explain at a barbecue, on a salary that makes your Sydney mates ask if you're still on the dole. In week one you said \"I'm only here two years.\" That was six years ago. You own a kayak you've used once, on Lake Burley Griffin, and you call it \"the lake\" like it's the only one. Your lanyard lives in your handbag like a passport. When your mum asks what you actually do, you say \"advice,\" which is the most Canberra word there is. Every second Friday you drive to Sydney to see friends, when really you just need to sit in traffic with people who aren't in a meeting about the meeting."}, "b": {"item_id": "e7c430775b37", "text": "You moved to Canberra for the job. Brave. You looked at Sydney rents, did the maths, and decided your personality was negotiable. Now you're an EL1 who says \"stakeholder\" at barbecues and talks about Senate Estimates like a war you survived. Your lanyard has a richer social life than you do. You promised your Melbourne friends a visit; they've cancelled twice for weddings in Bali. You've developed opinions about Gungahlin. Your wild Saturday is Braddon brunch, followed by complaining it was \"basically Sydney prices.\" And what did it get you? A secure job, a three-bedroom in Tuggeranong, and a security clearance confirming the government checked thoroughly and found nothing interesting. You came for two years. That was 2009. Your kid now says \"on the Hill\" unprompted, and you've never been prouder or more quietly trapped."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0492", "lang": "en", "target_id": "google-sheets", "target_name": "Google Sheets", "facts": ["Launched in June 2006 as Google Spreadsheets, built on XL2Web, a product Google acquired from 2Web Technologies.", "A spreadsheet is capped at 10 million cells (raised from 5 million in 2022).", "Functions such as IMPORTHTML, IMPORTXML, and GOOGLEFINANCE pull live web data straight into cells.", "Free with a Google account; files live in Google Drive, and collaborators edit the same sheet simultaneously with coloured cursors.", "In a browser, offline editing works only in Chrome or Edge, with the Google Docs Offline extension installed and offline access switched on while still connected; the Android and iOS apps work offline natively."], "a": {"item_id": "ed3e91782987", "text": "Let's hear it for the manager who runs a twelve-person team out of a Google Sheet. You didn't want to pay for Asana; you wanted to feel like you *built* Asana. Look at you, color-coding rows like a kindergarten teacher with admin rights. Your whole org chart is a tab called \"Roster FINAL (2).\" Your performance reviews are conditional formatting: green means safe, red means Kyle. And every morning you open it, see Anonymous Capybara lurking at row 400, and feel genuinely hunted. Somebody sorted one column without the others last Tuesday, and now Priya reports to the office printer. You fixed it through version history like a detective reconstructing a murder. That's the dream, right? Control. Total visibility. And what you got is forty \"Request edit access\" emails and a VLOOKUP holding up the company like a Jenga block."}, "b": {"item_id": "a5780556db66", "text": "Let's talk about the brave souls who run their whole team out of one Google Sheet. Tab one says \"Tasks,\" tab nine says \"Tasks (FINAL),\" and tab twelve says \"Tasks (FINAL) copy 2.\" They color-code everything, then color-code the color codes. Their status dropdown has eleven options, and \"Done\" is still somehow \"In Progress.\" They believe a #REF! error is a personality trait. They lie awake wondering if the VLOOKUP in cell F212 will survive Brenda touching column D. They're not managing people. They're managing a spreadsheet that manages people, and they're so proud of it that they've locked the header row so nobody can ruin their life. Their dream is a pivot table. Their reality is a comment thread with forty-one replies that all say \"bump.\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0493", "lang": "en", "target_id": "papyrus", "target_name": "Papyrus", "facts": ["Hand-drawn by Chris Costello in 1982 over about six months, when he was 23; he sold the rights to Letraset for $750.", "Bundled with macOS and with Microsoft Office, which is why it appears on so many menus and signs.", "James Cameron's Avatar (2009) used it for the film's logo and subtitles.", "A 2017 Saturday Night Live sketch starring Ryan Gosling is about a man unable to get over that choice."], "a": {"item_id": "a4df2dc21f25", "text": "Nobody picks Papyrus by accident. You went to a spa once, came out convinced you'd found your true calling, and now you want customers to feel like they're getting a massage in an ancient tomb that a Bali resort renovated. So you scroll past every honest typeface and land on the one that's been on every Avatar fan's laptop since 2009. Congratulations. You didn't choose a font. You chose a vibe with a decade-old hangover. Your sushi place is now hosting a sacred ritual, and the chef is confused about why the wasabi feels so spiritual."}, "b": {"item_id": "c986ccdedefc", "text": "You opened a day spa. You wanted serenity, wisdom, a whisper of the Nile. So you scrolled the font menu, sailed past Comic Sans feeling very sophisticated, and stopped on the one James Cameron used to title a billion-dollar movie. Congratulations: your sign now says \"ancient healing\" exactly as loudly as your aunt's church potluck flyer. Every hummus joint, every reiki studio, every tea shop run by a man named River made this same choice, alone, convinced they'd discovered it. The guy who designed it was twenty-three and has spent forty years apologizing. You paid a contractor to carve his apology into cedar. You wanted customers to feel transported. They do: straight back to 2003, a Word document, and a fourth grader typing \"Spring Bake Sale.\" Cucumber water not included."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0494", "lang": "en", "target_id": "juicero", "target_name": "Juicero", "facts": ["A Wi-Fi-connected cold-press juicer launched in 2016 at $699, later cut to $399, that squeezed proprietary bags of chopped produce sold by subscription.", "Raised about $120 million from investors including Google Ventures and Kleiner Perkins.", "In April 2017, Bloomberg reporters squeezed the packs by hand and got 7.5 ounces of juice in 90 seconds, faster than the machine's 8 ounces in about two minutes.", "Each pack carried a QR code that the press scanned and checked online before squeezing; it refused expired packs and would not run at all unless connected to Wi-Fi.", "Shut down in September 2017 and offered refunds on the machine."], "a": {"item_id": "596abf5c014a", "text": "You paid four hundred dollars so a startup could check your juice's expiration date over Wi-Fi. That's not wellness. That's asking a server for permission to have breakfast. These are people who looked at a bag of chopped kale and thought, \"This needs a subscription.\" People who wanted to be someone who juices without ever touching a fruit. Then Bloomberg showed the whole world you could squeeze the pack with your bare hands. Faster. For free. Your grandmother has been doing that with a lemon since 1952. You didn't buy a juicer; you bought the privilege of paying seven dollars a pouch to a company Google Ventures funded and still couldn't keep alive past 2017. Congratulations: you're the only people who ever got a refund from Silicon Valley and still walked away looking poorer."}, "b": {"item_id": "cdfa0d41c93b", "text": "The people who bought a Juicero weren't buying juice. They were buying the feeling of being someone with a juice situation. You know the type. They wanted to be a wellness person without the bother of actually squeezing anything, so they paid four hundred dollars to outsource the squeezing to a machine. Then the reporters showed up and squeezed the little packets by hand, and it turned out the machine was just a very expensive hand. The buyers were left holding a four-hundred-dollar receipt for their own fingers. And when the company folded, they still had the thing. Still on the counter. Still doing nothing. Like a trophy for a sport they quit on day one, with a little Wi-Fi light blinking at them every morning like it was disappointed too."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0495", "lang": "en", "target_id": "slack", "target_name": "Slack", "facts": ["Spun out in 2013 from the internal chat tool of Tiny Speck's failed game Glitch; founder Stewart Butterfield had earlier spun Flickr out of another failed game.", "The name is a backronym: 'Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge'.", "Salesforce bought it for $27.7 billion in 2021.", "Filed an EU antitrust complaint against Microsoft in 2020 over the bundling of Teams with Office."], "a": {"item_id": "f56d27fef4cd", "text": "Nobody runs a company on Slack. They run it from #general, where forty people wait for the CEO to type \"morning all\" so they can reply with the waving hand. These are people who say \"let's take it to a thread\" like it's a moral position. They set their status to \"deep work\" and then react to everything with the eyes emoji. They believe the green dot means they're present. They are not present. They're in forty-one channels, four of them named #random-but-serious. Their deepest fear is a DM with no reply. Their proudest achievement is a \"quick sync\" that somehow ran until Thursday."}, "b": {"item_id": "a071abe43a17", "text": "Let's hear it for the founders running a company on Slack. You wanted a culture; you got 400 channels and a guy who reacts to layoffs with the 🙏 emoji. You're not a CEO. You're a moderator with equity.\n\nYour whole leadership style is staying green. You think that little dot means you're present. Your kids see the same dot.\n\nYou don't make decisions; you start threads. Then you @channel at 11 p.m. with \"quick q, no rush\" and watch 200 people learn how fast a heart can beat.\n\nStrategy lives in #general, accountability dies in a DM, and the truth lives in a private channel called #venting-2 that you'll never be invited to.\n\nThe dream was async freedom. The reality is eight hours a day watching \"Kevin is typing…\" like it's a heart monitor."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0496", "lang": "en", "target_id": "windows-8", "target_name": "Windows 8", "facts": ["Released in October 2012; removed the Start button and booted into a full-screen grid of tiles designed for touch screens most PCs did not have.", "The 8.1 update in 2013 put a Start button back, Windows 10 (2015) restored the Start menu, and Microsoft skipped the name Windows 9 altogether.", "Microsoft dropped the 'Metro' name in August 2012 after reports that German retailer Metro AG had objected on trademark grounds; Microsoft called 'Metro' a code name and denied any litigation, and the style was afterwards known informally as 'Modern UI'.", "The 'Charms bar' hid Search, Share, and Settings behind an invisible swipe from the right edge.", "Windows RT, the ARM edition launched alongside it, could not run ordinary Windows programs; Microsoft wrote down $900 million of unsold Surface RT tablets in 2013."], "a": {"item_id": "a00e6ae0aa53", "text": "Who puts Windows 8 on a desktop? You. You looked at a perfectly working Windows 7 machine and said, \"What this needs is a touchscreen experience, and I don't own a touchscreen.\"\n\nSo now you sit at a 27-inch monitor, flinging your mouse into corners like a man hunting for a light switch in a stranger's house. You've Googled \"how to shut down\" at eleven at night. Your wife watched.\n\nYou wanted the future. You got a full-screen weather app you can't close and a Mail tile that's been \"Getting things ready\" since August.\n\nEventually you downloaded a program to bring the Start button back, so you paid to get Windows 7 with extra steps.\n\nAnd sometimes you still tap the screen. It's not a touchscreen. Those fingerprints are just hope."}, "b": {"item_id": "f8677486c44b", "text": "You put Windows 8 on a desktop. A tower. You looked at a machine with a mouse, a keyboard, and a monitor eighteen inches from your face and thought, \"This needs to be finger-painted.\" Who are you? You're the guy who bought a Zune and still calls it \"ahead of its time.\" You wanted to feel like the future. Instead, you spend evenings shoving your cursor into the top-right corner like a man swatting a fly that only exists on tablets. Your weather app is full-screen. Your 27-inch monitor is now a clock tile. When 8.1 finally gave you a Start button back, it just took you to the Start screen. So, inevitably, you installed Classic Shell. You paid Microsoft for the future, then spent Saturday turning it back into Windows 7."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0497", "lang": "en", "target_id": "comic-sans", "target_name": "Comic Sans", "facts": ["Designed by Vincent Connare at Microsoft in 1994, modeled on the comic-book lettering in The Dark Knight Returns and Watchmen.", "Made for the speech bubbles of Rover, the cartoon dog in Microsoft Bob; it missed Bob's release and shipped instead in the Windows 95 Plus! pack.", "CERN's July 2012 Higgs boson discovery announcement was presented on slides set in it.", "The 'Ban Comic Sans' campaign, started by designers Dave and Holly Combs in 1999, ran until 2019, when they retired it and launched usecomicsans.com."], "a": {"item_id": "ea624ef43e94", "text": "Let's talk about the people who announce layoffs in Comic Sans. You're not a monster. You're Brenda from Facilities, and you want everyone to feel cozy about the asbestos removal. You took a typeface Microsoft designed for a cartoon dog's speech bubble and used it to type \"Your position has been eliminated.\" That's not softening the blow. That's a clown serving a subpoena. CERN announced the Higgs boson in it, and the physicists who found the God particle spent the week explaining the font. You think it says \"approachable.\" It says \"I scrolled right past Arial and Times New Roman and chose a kindergarten birthday invitation.\" Nobody reads your notice. They screenshot it. Congratulations: your fire evacuation memo is trending, and the building is still on fire."}, "b": {"item_id": "4b3978a4ee09", "text": "Somebody in this building printed \"URGENT: FIRE DRILL MANDATORY\" in Comic Sans and taped it to the break room fridge. I want that person to know we all saw it and we all laughed, though not the way they hoped. They wanted the fire marshal's authority. What they got was a note that looks like a kindergarten teacher wrote it right after finding out about the fire. They want to be taken seriously while seeming approachable, like wearing a tuxedo with light-up sneakers. They think the wobbly letters say \"we're a team.\" Nobody reads it that way. We read it as a birthday party for the fire drill, with cake in the parking lot. Next time, pick a plain font and own being in charge. Nobody has ever evacuated a building faster because the notice looked cheerful."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0498", "lang": "en", "target_id": "windows-8", "target_name": "Windows 8", "facts": ["Released in October 2012; removed the Start button and booted into a full-screen grid of tiles designed for touch screens most PCs did not have.", "The 8.1 update in 2013 put a Start button back, Windows 10 (2015) restored the Start menu, and Microsoft skipped the name Windows 9 altogether.", "Microsoft dropped the 'Metro' name in August 2012 after reports that German retailer Metro AG had objected on trademark grounds; Microsoft called 'Metro' a code name and denied any litigation, and the style was afterwards known informally as 'Modern UI'.", "The 'Charms bar' hid Search, Share, and Settings behind an invisible swipe from the right edge.", "Windows RT, the ARM edition launched alongside it, could not run ordinary Windows programs; Microsoft wrote down $900 million of unsold Surface RT tablets in 2013."], "a": {"item_id": "4880b71f9444", "text": "You put Windows 8 on a desktop. A tower. Under a desk. With a mouse. Nobody in your house has ever touched that monitor except to wipe it, yet there you are, dragging your cursor into the top-right corner like a man feeling for a light switch in a stranger's house.\n\nYou wanted to be early to the future. Instead you got a full-screen weather report you never requested and a ten-minute hunt for the shutdown button, which you finally found by Googling it on your phone, like someone searching for the exit in a building they own.\n\nThen you downloaded a third-party Start menu, so now you've spent a weekend undoing the upgrade you chose on purpose.\n\nAnd when your friends ask how it is, you say, \"It's fine once you get used to it.\" That's also what hostages say."}, "b": {"item_id": "131d66f0a7cf", "text": "Nobody installs Windows 8 on a desktop out of passion. You saw a perfectly good Start button and thought, \"Honestly, too much of this.\" You wanted touch controls on a machine with no touchscreen, so now you're swiping at a monitor like it owes you money. Shutting down means hunting through the charms bar, hovering in the corner like a nervous burglar, and praying the hot corner doesn't ambush you mid-email. You bought Start8, felt guilty, then bought a second utility to fix the first. Your Start screen is a grid of tiles for apps you'll never open, and the one you actually use is buried under Weather, which is always sunny in your sad little world."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0499", "lang": "en", "target_id": "oat-milk", "target_name": "Oat milk", "facts": ["Oatly started in Sweden in the 1990s, built on Lund University research by Rickard Öste on lactose intolerance.", "Oatly's slogan 'It's like milk but made for humans' was banned in Sweden in 2015 after the dairy lobby LRF Mjölk sued; Oatly then published the lawsuit as advertising.", "Oatly listed on Nasdaq in May 2021 at a valuation near $10 billion; the shares later lost more than 90% of their value.", "Commercial oat milk is made by breaking oat starch into maltose with amylase enzymes, and many brands, Oatly among them, add rapeseed oil; the result has more sugar and a higher glycaemic index than almond or soy milk."], "a": {"item_id": "a579a10435cb", "text": "You know the person who orders oat milk? They're not vegan. They're not lactose intolerant. They own a tote bag that says \"Be Kind\" and a laptop they've never opened for work. \"Oat, please,\" they say, like they're ordering a sacrament. Almond was for people who've been to Joshua Tree, and soy was for their dad. Then they lean in to tell the barista the planet thanks them. Their whole personality is a seventy-cent upcharge they'll bring up at your birthday dinner. Ask why and you'll get six minutes on cows, then a pivot to their sourdough starter and whether you've tried the new Pilates place. They didn't change their milk. They changed their identity, and the milk was the cheapest part of the rebrand."}, "b": {"item_id": "a7a78e709f8d", "text": "You said \"Can I do oat?\" like you're asking the barista for permission to have a personality. Nobody asked. The line behind you did the math on that 75-cent upcharge and found a person who wants to be seen caring.\n\nYou're not lactose intolerant. You're \"lactose-skeptical.\" You quit almond milk the week a documentary told you almonds were the problem, and you've been switching milks like a politician switching parties, always just before the scandal breaks. Now you're on the right side of history, in a cup with a plastic lid.\n\nYou tell people you're \"mostly plant-based,\" which means you had a salad once near a farmers market. You'll mention the oat thing on a first date, and she'll smile and start planning the exit.\n\nFour dollars for a moral victory. Nobody's clapping, Dylan."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0500", "lang": "en", "target_id": "luosifen", "target_name": "Luosifen (螺蛳粉)", "facts": ["A rice-noodle dish from Liuzhou, Guangxi; the smell comes from pickled bamboo shoots (酸笋), not from the river snails, which only flavour the broth.", "The instant version boomed during the 2020 lockdowns; Liuzhou's government reported packaged luosifen sales of over 10 billion yuan that year.", "Added to China's national intangible cultural heritage list in 2021.", "Liuzhou opened a luosifen industry college in 2020 to train workers for the trade."], "a": {"item_id": "4f071ba2f3bc", "text": "You know the type. Ordered thirty packs on Taobao \"for emergencies,\" and the emergency is that it's Tuesday. They wait until 11 p.m., when the kitchen's empty, like that's a courtesy and not an alibi. They snip open the sour bamboo shoot packet with the focus of a bomb technician, then act shocked when the smoke alarm stays quiet but the flatmates don't. Their entire personality is one sentence, \"it tastes way better than it smells,\" delivered like a TED talk to people holding their breath. They'll tell you it's from Liuzhou, it's authentic, it's *culture*. Mate, you microwaved the tofu skin. And what does it get them? Twenty minutes of joy, a hallway that's filing for divorce, and a group chat renamed \"WHO.\" Congratulations: your hoodie is now a Liuzhou consulate until spring."}, "b": {"item_id": "6eae17d96ab5", "text": "You're the person who read one Xiaohongshu post and now thinks you're from Liuzhou. You've never been to Guangxi. You've been to a Taobao checkout page, twelve packs deep, at 11 p.m.\n\nYou open the bamboo shoot packet like you're defusing a bomb, then tell the whole floor, \"It's actually not that strong once you get used to it.\" Used to it? Nobody got used to it. They just stopped telling you. Your roommates have a group chat with your name removed from it.\n\nAnd what do you get for all this? A bowl of noodles, a sticky note on the fridge, and the quiet dignity of eating alone, window open, in February. You wanted to be adventurous. You got a nickname in the HR department.\n\nAlso, you added extra chili oil. Of course you did."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0501", "lang": "en", "target_id": "papyrus", "target_name": "Papyrus", "facts": ["Hand-drawn by Chris Costello in 1982 over about six months, when he was 23; he sold the rights to Letraset for $750.", "Bundled with macOS and with Microsoft Office, which is why it appears on so many menus and signs.", "James Cameron's Avatar (2009) used it for the film's logo and subtitles.", "A 2017 Saturday Night Live sketch starring Ryan Gosling is about a man unable to get over that choice."], "a": {"item_id": "1988c200f894", "text": "Let's talk about the restaurant owner who picked Papyrus for the sign. You bought a franchise of \"authentic\" sourdough, you have a chalkboard that says \"Gather,\" and you think a font that looks like it was carved on a tree stump is the closest thing to a hug a customer can get. You didn't choose it. It chose you, the moment you typed \"organic\" into a font search. Now your $14 avocado toast sits under a logo that reads like a yoga instructor's grocery list. Your spa is \"a sanctuary,\" but the sign out front says \"we're still figuring out our brand.\" You wanted the calm of a Bali retreat. You got the calm of a dentist's waiting room in 2009, and somehow it still costs you $400 a month in massages you can't afford."}, "b": {"item_id": "50326144f6b1", "text": "You had every font in the world available, and you picked the one that says \"authentic\" the way a gift shop pyramid says \"Egypt.\" You wanted ancient wisdom. You got a sign that tells customers your hot stone massage was learned from a video.\n\nYou're the owner who said \"my nephew says it looks organic\" and considered that a design process. You're the guy whose \"Mediterranean-inspired\" menu includes a wrap. You wanted to be exotic, and you ended up in the same category as a quarter-billion-dollar blockbuster that got publicly roasted on SNL by Ryan Gosling, who played a man in actual tears over this exact decision.\n\nEveryone else in your strip mall hired a designer. You hired a feeling. Your five-star review says \"clean,\" which is what people write when they're being kind about the sign."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0502", "lang": "en", "target_id": "pmma", "target_name": "PMMA (e-beam resist)", "facts": ["The same polymer as Plexiglas and acrylic glass, dissolved in anisole or chlorobenzene and spun into a thin film.", "The oldest electron-beam resist still in everyday use; IBM researchers reported it as one in the late 1960s.", "Positive tone: the beam breaks polymer chains and the exposed region dissolves in a MIBK:IPA developer.", "Resolves below 10 nm but has poor sensitivity, so doses of hundreds to over a thousand µC/cm² (depending on voltage) make writing slow.", "Poor dry-etch resistance, so it is used mostly for lift-off rather than as an etch mask."], "a": {"item_id": "fe949f7eb86d", "text": "Oh, you're still on PMMA. Let me guess: your advisor's thesis used it, your advisor's advisor used it, and somewhere on the group drive is a recipe called \"DO NOT CHANGE,\" last edited by a postdoc who now sells insurance in Ohio.\n\nYou call it \"reliable.\" You've run the same dose test eleven times because you refuse to admit the variable is you. Your big optimization this year was going from 950 A4 to 950 A3 and calling it a paper.\n\nYou queue a pattern Friday night, check on it Sunday, and tell people the beam is \"just taking its time.\" Then you say you don't trust anything without forty years of citations, which is also how you feel about your dentist and your Windows XP tool PC.\n\nWhat does it get you? Gorgeous 10-nm lines, a lift-off you pray over, and no social life. The chip is perfect. Your weekend is exposed and overdeveloped."}, "b": {"item_id": "c3b97d78415e", "text": "Still using PMMA as your e-beam resist? Bless your heart. You're the guy who still owns a flip phone and says \"the cloud\" like it's a place you visited once. Every grad student in your lab has moved on to HSQ and ZEP, and you're over there spinning PMMA at 4000 rpm, baking it on a hotplate, and wondering why your features look like they were drawn by a sleepy toddler with a cheap pen. You want sub-20-nanometer lines, but you're getting proximity effect so bad your dense arrays blur together like a bad Instagram filter. Congratulations, you've achieved the dream: a lithography process that's slow, low-contrast, and somehow still considered \"classic.\" Your advisor calls it a \"reliable baseline.\" We all know what that really means."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0503", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hsq", "target_name": "HSQ (e-beam resist)", "facts": ["Hydrogen silsesquioxane, sold by Dow Corning as FOx ('flowable oxide') and designed as a spin-on dielectric for chip interconnects; lithographers adopted it as a negative-tone resist.", "Exposure converts its cage structure into a cross-linked, silica-like network, so the developed pattern is essentially SiO2.", "Holds the resolution records for electron-beam lithography: isolated features around 5 nm, dense lines below 10 nm half-pitch, with high-contrast 'salty' NaOH/NaCl development.", "Must be kept refrigerated (Dow Corning's datasheet gives a six-month shelf life at 5 °C); the solution ages into a jelly, and its effective sensitivity shifts with processing delays because cross-linking continues after exposure.", "DuPont, which inherited Dow Corning's FOx/XR-1541 HSQ line, discontinued it and ended production at the end of 2025; labs such as EPFL's CMi switched to third-party HSQ from EM Resist or DisChem, both formulated from AQM powder."], "a": {"item_id": "96896ea695a3", "text": "You're switching to HSQ for resolution. Of course you are. You have a PhD in patience and a deficit in sleep, and one paper showed someone drawing 6-nanometer lines, so now you want to be that person at group meeting. Here's what HSQ gets you: a resist that gels in the bottle if you look at it wrong and, once exposed, cures into glass that stays in your device forever. Want it gone? Good luck. That's hydrofluoric acid territory. Your dose matrix is forty chips. Your TMAH developer smells like a crime scene. Six months from now you'll be at the SEM at 2 a.m., staring at lines that have slumped into a sad little heap, swearing you'll go back to PMMA. You won't. You'll put it in your thesis as a \"lesson learned.\""}, "b": {"item_id": "91f701649a72", "text": "You switched to HSQ for the resolution. Of course you did. PMMA gave you 20-nanometer lines, which is fine for science but not for the cover of a journal your advisor brings up at dinner parties. Now you're writing 8-nanometer lines that nobody can see without a $2 million microscope you booked three weeks ago.\n\nYou pay triple for a tiny bottle that expires faster than your funding. You keep it cold like insulin and spin it knowing it's already going bad. Your dose is ten times higher, so your \"quick write\" runs overnight, and the e-beam tool manager who used to love you is now drafting an email. You develop in TMAH like you're brewing something, and the lines collapse anyway.\n\nAnd the best part: it never comes off. You wanted resolution, and you got permanent glass sitting on your device. Congratulations, you made something incredibly small that doesn't work."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0504", "lang": "en", "target_id": "carbon-nanotubes", "target_name": "Carbon nanotubes", "facts": ["Reported by Sumio Iijima in Nature in 1991.", "Promised as the cable for a space elevator; a 2006 analysis showed that ordinary defects cut the strength of a macroscopic cable far below the ideal value.", "Long, rigid multi-walled nanotubes behaved like asbestos fibres in a 2008 mouse study in Nature Nanotechnology.", "Sorting metallic from semiconducting tubes has blocked nanotube electronics for three decades; MIT's 16-bit RV16X-NANO processor (2019) is still a lab demonstration.", "Today they are sold mainly as a conductive additive in lithium-ion battery electrodes."], "a": {"item_id": "221482c6c19c", "text": "You open with a space elevator, which tells me the last customer you spoke to was a grad student. Your deck says \"100 times stronger than steel,\" and every investor nods, because they heard it in 2009 from the guy before you, and the guy before him. You've been \"two years from commercialization\" since the Clinton administration.\n\nWhat are you after? Eight million dollars and the word \"platform.\" What do you get? A polite \"fascinating, let's circle back,\" a tour of the fund's kombucha tap, and a term sheet from a tennis racket company.\n\nYour market slide lists batteries, aerospace, sensors, concrete, and medicine. That isn't a market, that's a thesaurus. And when the one skeptical partner asks about dispersion, you suddenly get very interested in your water glass.\n\nYour real product is optimism with a PhD, sold by the gram at gold prices, to people who just want to know why it still clumps."}, "b": {"item_id": "9ad1a4c3964e", "text": "Let's hear it for the carbon nanotube founders, the only people who've been \"five years from commercialization\" since 1991. You can spot them at any pitch day: a postdoc in a blazer that still smells like the clean room, opening with a space elevator and closing with a seed round. Bold move, promising a cable to orbit when your entire production run fits in a vial labeled \"DO NOT INHALE.\" They swear they'll replace silicon. Silicon has heard this every year since Clinton and keeps shipping. They say \"a hundred times stronger than steel\" the way your uncle says he could've gone pro. And what does it get them? A Nature cover, a term sheet, and a quiet pivot to \"conductive additive for batteries.\" The dream: a ladder to space. The exit: slightly better tennis rackets."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0505", "lang": "en", "target_id": "slack", "target_name": "Slack", "facts": ["Spun out in 2013 from the internal chat tool of Tiny Speck's failed game Glitch; founder Stewart Butterfield had earlier spun Flickr out of another failed game.", "The name is a backronym: 'Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge'.", "Salesforce bought it for $27.7 billion in 2021.", "Filed an EU antitrust complaint against Microsoft in 2020 over the bundling of Teams with Office."], "a": {"item_id": "a66553ea7cb4", "text": "Nobody runs a company on Slack. They run a company that lives inside a Slack, and the founder has to be in forty channels so he can feel the building is still standing. Look at him: green dot on all day, status set to \"deep work,\" huddling with himself in an empty room. He starts every morning with \"quick question\" and ends it with \"let's circle back,\" which is corporate for \"I'm going to let this thread die.\" He doesn't answer you. He reacts with a little eyeball. An eyeball. That's his whole management style. And #random? Forty-one people in #random, all too scared to leave. Good morning, everybody. @here. Please read the pinned message."}, "b": {"item_id": "fae8a282c283", "text": "Let's hear it for the founders running a company on Slack. You wanted a culture of transparency. You got 340 public channels and every real decision happening in a DM called \"quick q.\"\n\nYou don't manage people anymore. You manage a green dot. You jiggle your mouse at your kid's birthday party so the team knows you're \"around.\" Your entire leadership style is the eyes emoji. Someone asks for budget approval, you react with a little thumbs-up, and three weeks later legal wants to know what the thumbs-up meant. Nobody knows. It was legally binding and spiritually ambiguous.\n\nAnd when something truly matters? You type \"hey\" and walk away. Just \"hey.\" That's not a message, that's a hostage situation.\n\nYou promised Slack would kill meetings. Instead you invented the huddle: a meeting that ambushes people while they're eating."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0506", "lang": "en", "target_id": "latex", "target_name": "LaTeX", "facts": ["Leslie Lamport wrote LaTeX in the early 1980s and released it in 1984 as a macro layer on Donald Knuth's TeX (1978), which Knuth built because he disliked how the galley proofs of the new edition of his own book looked.", "TeX's version number converges on π (3.141592653 since 2021), and Knuth's TeX bug bounty started at one hexadecimal dollar, $2.56, doubling yearly until frozen at $327.68; he stopped mailing real cheques in 2008 after fraud and now credits accounts at his fictional Bank of San Serriffe, and only 9 of the 275 cheques he sent from 2006 on were ever cashed.", "Its best-known warning is 'Underfull \\hbox (badness 10000)'; overfull lines are reported as 'Overfull \\hbox (Npt too wide)', and figures float to wherever the algorithm decides, not where the author put them.", "A single missing brace can produce an error reported pages away from the mistake, and a document can need three compiler passes to get its references right."], "a": {"item_id": "c59587efb75e", "text": "Nobody writes a two-page memo in LaTeX because they need to. They do it so they can be the only person in the building whose \\textbf{Action Items} shine in Computer Modern. They type \\documentclass{article} like they're defending a dissertation, then lose an entire afternoon to a missing package just to shave a quarter inch off the margin. They're not informing Denise in accounting. They're proving they know what \\vspace does. And what does it get them? A PDF that looks exactly like Word, except it took six hours, a compile error on line 47, and Denise replying, \"Can you just send the Word file?\" They'll insist the kerning is superior. Nobody has ever checked the kerning."}, "b": {"item_id": "37e7504ee3eb", "text": "You spent three hours on a two-page memo, and two of them were the preamble. You loaded fourteen packages to say \"Please submit receipts by Friday.\" Your manager opens the PDF on his phone, sees Computer Modern, and assumes someone died, or got tenure.\n\nYou didn't pick LaTeX for the memo. You picked it so that, for one afternoon, a logistics update could look peer-reviewed. Your kerning is flawless, and nobody has ever noticed kerning except you, at 11 p.m., on TeX Stack Exchange, asking why your header won't align. A man named Egreg has told you, kindly and correctly, that you're doing it wrong.\n\nThe memo got two replies. One said \"Thanks.\" The other said \"Can you send this as a Word doc?\"\n\nAnd you did. You opened Word. You sat there, alone, crying in Calibri."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0507", "lang": "en", "target_id": "keurig", "target_name": "Keurig", "facts": ["John Sylvan invented the K-Cup in the early 1990s and sold his stake for about $50,000 in 1997; in 2015 he told The Atlantic, 'I feel bad sometimes that I ever did it.'", "Keurig 2.0 (2014) read an ink mark on the lid and rejected unlicensed pods; sales fell and workarounds spread online.", "By 2014 enough K-Cups were sold each year to circle the Earth more than ten times; the pods were mostly unrecyclable #7 plastic until a switch to polypropylene by 2020.", "Keurig Green Mountain merged with Dr Pepper Snapple in 2018 to form Keurig Dr Pepper."], "a": {"item_id": "8a5105614740", "text": "Keurig people want coffee the way they want feelings: one serving, sealed, no cleanup. You're not a coffee drinker, you're a pod collector. You bought the variety pack, and now you're thirty-eight, rationing the last Donut Shop because all that's left is hazelnut and a hot cocoa you're saving for a breakdown. You press \"Strong\" like it's a personality trait. You swore you'd use the reusable filter, and you bought one. It's in a drawer next to your gym key fob. You saved four whole minutes, and you spend them staring at a blinking descale light you've decided is decorative. The guy who invented the pod publicly regrets it. You built your mornings around a man's biggest apology. Enjoy your landfill latte, champ. Ready in sixty seconds, just like your effort."}, "b": {"item_id": "a2940564c97a", "text": "To the Keurig people, bless you. You're the ones who say \"I just need something hot in the morning\" like that's a personality. You want coffee the way you want a fire drill: fast, loud, and over before you're fully conscious. You've got a pod drawer with forty flavors, including one called \"Extra Bold\" that you bought in a fit of optimism and one called \"Hazelnut Dream\" you won't admit you finish. You press the 8 oz button, get four ounces of brown regret, and call it a day. You're the only people alive who look at a single-serve cup and feel like a responsible adult. Sixty seconds, one cup, zero decisions. You didn't choose coffee. You chose not to commit to a whole pot."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0508", "lang": "en", "target_id": "crocs", "target_name": "Crocs", "facts": ["Founded in Boulder, Colorado, in 2002; the first clog debuted at the Fort Lauderdale boat show as a boating shoe.", "Made of 'Croslite', which Crocs describes as a proprietary closed-cell resin foam and which third parties identify as an injection-moulded EVA foam.", "Balenciaga sold a platform Crocs for about $850 in 2018.", "The stock fell more than 90% from its 2007 peak during the 2008 crisis; the company then boomed in the pandemic and gave away 860,000 pairs to healthcare workers in 2020.", "The holes are officially 'ventilation ports' and the charms that go in them are 'Jibbitz', a company Crocs bought in 2006."], "a": {"item_id": "8f2a3cb9a4bb", "text": "Look at the person who wore Crocs to the grocery store. Not the pool. The grocery store. This is someone who looked at a stranger's sneakers and thought, \"Too much effort.\" They want you to know they're comfortable, and they've made peace with being seen by children. They bought them in five colors, then clipped little plastic bananas into the holes like they were decorating a golf bag. And they say \"they're actually orthopedic\" like it's a get-out-of-jail-free card. Your feet aren't that tired. You just wanted to shuffle through Costco making a sound like a bath toy with every step, and honestly, I respect the commitment. Just don't call it a fashion choice. You're wearing a garden clog that gave up on the garden."}, "b": {"item_id": "62e0599c4f11", "text": "People who wear Crocs out of the house have made peace with something the rest of us are still fighting. You didn't give up; you filed the paperwork. Every Croc wearer says the same thing: \"They're so comfortable.\" Yeah, so is a weighted blanket, Brenda, but you don't wear it to Olive Garden. And the Jibbitz! You've turned your feet into a personality quiz. A tiny taco, a tiny Minion, a tiny \"Live Laugh Love.\" You're forty-three and your shoe has a sticker collection. Then there's the guy who flips the strap back into \"sport mode\" like he's about to chase a pickpocket, when we both know he's never run anywhere except toward a sample tray at Costco. Nurses and chefs wear them because they're on their feet for twelve hours. You wear them to stand in line at Starbucks. Different heroism."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0509", "lang": "en", "target_id": "google-glass", "target_name": "Google Glass", "facts": ["Unveiled at Google I/O 2012 with skydivers livestreaming their jump through it onto the Moscone Center roof.", "The Explorer Edition sold to invited users in 2013 for $1,500.", "Wearers were nicknamed 'Glassholes'; bars, cinemas, and casinos banned the device over its camera.", "The consumer programme ended in January 2015; an Enterprise Edition continued until Google stopped selling it in 2023.", "Its display was a 640-by-360 prism in the corner of one eye."], "a": {"item_id": "260f23e62b71", "text": "Nobody wears Google Glass to a bar unless they paid fifteen hundred dollars to be called an Explorer and need the bartender to notice. You're not here for the beer. You're here for the moment the woman by the jukebox squints and says, \"Wait, are you recording me?\" That's the conversation you've been waiting your whole life for. Meanwhile you can't hear her, because you're reading a fantasy football text in the corner of your eye. You wink at the bartender to photograph the tap handles, and he photographs you back, which is the first picture anyone will ever have of you looking this lonely. You say \"OK Glass\" to the girl at the register, and she says \"Sure, buddy\" like she's talking to a smoke detector. You are a beta tester who never got the invitation to be a person."}, "b": {"item_id": "abe9296c28ae", "text": "Look at this guy. Paid fifteen hundred dollars to join Google's \"Explorer Program,\" and the only thing he's explored is the sidewalk outside every bar that bounced him. He walks in thinking he's Tony Stark and walks out on a list, because nobody can tell if he's filming, and honestly, neither can he. He's here to meet women, and he gets results: every conversation ends the second he says \"OK Glass.\" Nothing kills the mood like a man asking his own face for directions to her place. He wanted to be the future. Instead the internet had to invent a brand-new word just for him: Glasshole. Silicon Valley promised to connect him to everything. It couldn't connect him to a second date, but it did get him his very own slur."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0510", "lang": "en", "target_id": "microsoft-teams", "target_name": "Microsoft Teams", "facts": ["Launched in 2017, bundled at no extra charge into Office 365, and replaced Skype for Business.", "After Slack's antitrust complaint, Microsoft unbundled Teams from Office in the EU in 2023 and worldwide in 2024; the European Commission still sent a statement of objections in 2024.", "Microsoft reported 320 million monthly active users in 2023.", "Microsoft shut down consumer Skype in May 2025 and sent its users to Teams."], "a": {"item_id": "a37866956a51", "text": "Let's talk about the people who made us switch to Teams. Not Microsoft. The guy in procurement who discovered it was \"already included\" in the license and presented that like a victory, like he found forty dollars in a coat. He didn't choose a tool. He picked the one free thing on the bundle, and now four thousand of us pay for his savings by saying \"can you see my screen?\" every morning.\n\nThen there are the managers, who love it for the green dot. That's what you wanted. Not collaboration. A little light that says Brenda is alive. Brenda goes yellow after five minutes of reading a spreadsheet, so now she jiggles her mouse like she's hand-cranking a Model T.\n\nAnd what do you get for all this? A quarterly slide: \"Adoption up 40%.\" Of course it's up. You banned the other ones. That's not adoption, that's a hostage count."}, "b": {"item_id": "8ecdfe8fdf7c", "text": "Let's hear it for the IT director who chose Microsoft Teams, a man who has never once chosen anything. It came bundled with Outlook, like coleslaw you're contractually obligated to eat. The EU had to force Microsoft to stop shoving it on people, and your company still went back for seconds. The whole pitch was \"It's already in the license.\" So was Clippy, Gary.\n\nManagement loves it because now they can watch your little dot. Green means productive. Yellow means you went to the bathroom and Linda noticed. They didn't want collaboration. They wanted a heart monitor.\n\nAnd what did it get them? Every meeting opens with eight adults asking \"Can you see my screen?\" while a VP uses the raise-hand button to interrupt people *harder*. Congratulations: you saved nine dollars a seat and lost the will to live."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0511", "lang": "en", "target_id": "luosifen", "target_name": "Luosifen (螺蛳粉)", "facts": ["A rice-noodle dish from Liuzhou, Guangxi; the smell comes from pickled bamboo shoots (酸笋), not from the river snails, which only flavour the broth.", "The instant version boomed during the 2020 lockdowns; Liuzhou's government reported packaged luosifen sales of over 10 billion yuan that year.", "Added to China's national intangible cultural heritage list in 2021.", "Liuzhou opened a luosifen industry college in 2020 to train workers for the trade."], "a": {"item_id": "2de91fadb67e", "text": "Nobody in this shared kitchen has ever been brave. You're the one who tears open the sachet at 11pm and lets that sour-funk fill the whole flat, then stands over the pot with your eyes closed like it's a religious rite. You wanted to be the guy who eats the wild thing and doesn't flinch. Your roommates are in their rooms now, pretending it's a fire alarm. Six months ago you said you'd \"get into\" Liuzhou food. Now you own three packs of pickled bamboo shoots, a chili oil you've never read the label on, and a standing grudge against the extractor fan. You're not really eating snail noodles. You're collecting a reaction."}, "b": {"item_id": "1e45a3cac2a8", "text": "Congratulations to the person who looked at a shared kitchen, five roommates, and one window that doesn't open, and thought, \"Tonight, I honor Liuzhou.\"\n\nYou open all seven packets like a surgeon. Sour bamboo shoots, fried tofu skin, peanuts, the chili oil sachet you call \"optional\" and then use entirely. Then you tell everyone, \"You stop noticing after a while.\" Correct. You stopped. Everyone else has been noticing since Tuesday.\n\nYou say it's an acquired taste. The only thing you've acquired is a passive-aggressive sticky note on the fridge and a roommate who now eats dinner in his car.\n\nWhat were you after? Comfort, home, a little 11 p.m. authenticity. What did you get? A nickname, a group chat you're not in, and a hoodie that still shows up to your Monday meeting before you do.\n\nEnjoy the broth. You're drinking it alone."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0512", "lang": "en", "target_id": "microsoft-word", "target_name": "Microsoft Word", "facts": ["Launched in 1983 as 'Multi-Tool Word' for Xenix and MS-DOS.", "Clippy, the animated paperclip assistant, arrived in Office 97 and was removed in Office 2007; Microsoft itself ran ads mocking it in 2001.", "Its .docx format, Office Open XML, was fast-tracked to ISO standard 29500 in 2008 as a roughly 6,000-page specification, amid accusations that national committees had been packed.", "Older spell checkers, Word's included, offered 'Cupertino' as a correction for a mistyped 'cooperation', and the resulting 'Cupertino effect' made it into published NATO, EU and UN-agency documents."], "a": {"item_id": "374d6b6e70bc", "text": "You chose Word for your thesis because \"LaTeX has a learning curve.\" Bold, from someone who then spent a semester learning how to make page 1 start after the roman numerals. You wanted to focus on the research. Instead you're a hostage negotiator for Figure 3.2, which flees to the next page whenever you breathe on the paragraph above it. Your committee asked for one more citation, and the bibliography reflowed like a tectonic event. The phrase \"Error! Reference source not found\" appears in your thesis eleven times. Your file is called Thesis_FINAL_v9_USE_THIS_ONE.docx, and it's 400 megabytes because you pasted one chart from Excel. You came for a doctorate. You're leaving with a codependent relationship with Section Break (Next Page) and a flinch every time someone says \"Normal style.\""}, "b": {"item_id": "1bc74d6b8981", "text": "Ah, the thesis typesetters. Four years of research, and the final week is spent discovering that Heading 2 and Heading 3 are secretly different shades of Times New Roman. You aren't writing anymore. You're toggling paragraph marks at page 214, hunting a rogue section break like a bomb tech who forgot which wire. You've renumbered Chapter 3 six times because the cross-references keep saying \"Error! Reference source not found.\" You want a table of contents that updates itself, and you're clicking Update Field on the wrong field. Your reward is a 300-page PDF whose List of Figures reads \"No table of figures entries found,\" and you tell your advisor it's a formatting quirk. Your advisor has also used Word. Your advisor is scrolling to the margins right now."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0513", "lang": "en", "target_id": "vim", "target_name": "Vim", "facts": ["Released by Bram Moolenaar in November 1991 as 'Vi IMitation', a vi clone he had begun writing for the Amiga in 1988 on top of Stevie; the expansion became 'Vi IMproved' with version 2.0 in December 1993.", "Licensed as charityware: the licence asks users to donate to ICCF Holland, a charity Moolenaar ran for children in Uganda.", "Stack Overflow's question 'How to exit the Vim editor?' passed one million visits in May 2017, which Stack Overflow marked with a blog post.", "Modal editing: in normal mode, typing letters runs commands rather than inserting text; :wq saves and quits, :q! quits and discards changes."], "a": {"item_id": "613855b046fb", "text": "You know a Vim user is in the room because nobody else has gotten a word in. Buddy, you didn't \"become more productive.\" You spent three weekends on a .vimrc so you could save four seconds renaming a variable, and you post your dotfiles repo like it's a baby picture. You remapped Caps Lock to Escape and now treat everyone else's keyboard like a character flaw. You say \"I live in the terminal\" like it's a lifestyle and not just where you got stuck. Your whole identity is a joke about not knowing how to quit, and honestly, you never learned. Here you are, still talking. You say \"ciw\" out loud at parties. You switched to Neovim just so you'd have something new to announce. And the payoff? You edit text exactly as fast as the rest of us, hands proudly on home row, with nobody watching."}, "b": {"item_id": "fc4027ce9a34", "text": "You told the new intern you use Vim before you told him your name. He just wanted to know where the printer was.\n\nYou say you never leave the home row. Correct. You never leave the house either.\n\nYou spent forty hours on your .vimrc to save eleven seconds a day, so you break even in 2031, assuming you don't switch to Neovim, which you will, and then announce it like a religious conversion.\n\nYou're not after speed. You're after the moment someone glances at your screen and asks, \"How do you even quit that?\" so you can say \"Oh, it's simple\" and type something that looks like a ransom note: `:wqa!`\n\nWhat does it get you? A macro that renames forty variables, a pinky built like a bodybuilder's, and one guy at a meetup who nods at you with exactly your kind of loneliness."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0514", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hydrogen-cars", "target_name": "Hydrogen fuel-cell cars", "facts": ["Toyota launched the Mirai in 2014; 'mirai' means 'future' in Japanese.", "Nearly all US hydrogen filling stations are in California; Shell closed all seven of its California passenger-car stations in February 2024.", "More than 95% of the world's hydrogen is made from fossil fuels, mostly by steam-reforming natural gas.", "California pump prices reached about $36 per kilogram in 2023-24, over $200 for a Mirai fill; Toyota has handed buyers fuel cards worth $15,000.", "Mirai owners filed a class action against Toyota in July 2024 over hydrogen shortages and station outages; a second suit in 2025 sought at least $5.7 billion."], "a": {"item_id": "1f255592ab99", "text": "Let's talk about the guy who bought a hydrogen car. He wanted to be greener than the Tesla people and smugger than the Prius people, and found the one way to do both while living like a medieval pilgrim. His life is now a map of eleven pumps, and nine say \"offline.\" He loves telling you it \"only emits water.\" Yeah, so does he, every time the app says \"station unavailable.\" He brags that refueling takes five minutes, after the forty-minute drive and the two-hour line behind three other Mirai owners, all quietly weeping. Toyota handed him a fifteen-thousand-dollar fuel card and he burned through it before the warranty paperwork dried. Now he owns a sixty-thousand-dollar car worth a used futon, all to prove he's an early adopter of a future that met him and moved to Japan."}, "b": {"item_id": "d1233bdc49cf", "text": "You bought a hydrogen car because a regular electric car was too easy. You needed a commute with plot. Everyone else plugs in at home while they sleep, but you drive forty minutes to the one station in Torrance, find the pump broken, and drive forty more to Long Beach, where it's \"temporarily closed,\" a phrase you now hear in your dreams.\n\nYou pay per kilogram like you're buying something from a guy in a parking lot, and the dealer threw in a fuel card like a hostage negotiator. You tell everyone, \"It only emits water,\" as if that's a personality and not the thing every kettle does.\n\nYou're not an early adopter. You're the last passenger on a train line that's been cancelled, wearing a \"Future\" T-shirt, checking an app to see if the future is open."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0515", "lang": "en", "target_id": "crocs", "target_name": "Crocs", "facts": ["Founded in Boulder, Colorado, in 2002; the first clog debuted at the Fort Lauderdale boat show as a boating shoe.", "Made of 'Croslite', which Crocs describes as a proprietary closed-cell resin foam and which third parties identify as an injection-moulded EVA foam.", "Balenciaga sold a platform Crocs for about $850 in 2018.", "The stock fell more than 90% from its 2007 peak during the 2008 crisis; the company then boomed in the pandemic and gave away 860,000 pairs to healthcare workers in 2020.", "The holes are officially 'ventilation ports' and the charms that go in them are 'Jibbitz', a company Crocs bought in 2006."], "a": {"item_id": "820d0b21e9b5", "text": "People who wear Crocs in public want you to know they've stopped trying, but they also want credit for it. That's why they buy the Jibbitz. Nothing says \"I don't care what you think\" like spending forty dollars on tiny rubber tacos so everyone knows exactly what you think. Then there's the strap. You flipped it back into \"Sport Mode,\" which is a bold claim from a man walking into Applebee's. Your nurse friend wears them because she stands for twelve-hour shifts. You wear them for a twelve-minute Target run, hoping to borrow a little of her dignity. And what does it get you? Comfort, sure. Plus a lifetime of strangers asking if you're coming off a shift. You're not, Kyle. You're coming off a nap."}, "b": {"item_id": "8f2a3cb9a4bb", "text": "Look at the person who wore Crocs to the grocery store. Not the pool. The grocery store. This is someone who looked at a stranger's sneakers and thought, \"Too much effort.\" They want you to know they're comfortable, and they've made peace with being seen by children. They bought them in five colors, then clipped little plastic bananas into the holes like they were decorating a golf bag. And they say \"they're actually orthopedic\" like it's a get-out-of-jail-free card. Your feet aren't that tired. You just wanted to shuffle through Costco making a sound like a bath toy with every step, and honestly, I respect the commitment. Just don't call it a fashion choice. You're wearing a garden clog that gave up on the garden."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0516", "lang": "en", "target_id": "windows-vista", "target_name": "Windows Vista", "facts": ["Released to consumers in January 2007, after the 'Longhorn' project was reset in 2004 and headline features such as the WinFS file system were dropped.", "User Account Control prompted so often that Apple's 'Get a Mac' campaign ran an ad about 'Cancel or Allow'.", "'Windows Vista Capable' stickers went on PCs that could only run Home Basic, without the Aero interface, and led to a class-action lawsuit.", "Windows 7 (2009), widely praised, was largely a tuned-up Vista on the same kernel line; Vista support ended in April 2017."], "a": {"item_id": "df697b757759", "text": "Oh, you upgraded to Vista on day one? Of course you did. You're the guy who lined up at midnight for a store that closed at nine. You wanted the translucent Aero borders so badly you bought a new graphics card just to watch your taskbar shimmer. You clicked \"Allow\" on forty UAC prompts before your coffee cooled, because you think a security dialog is just a loading screen with opinions. And your printer? Dead. Your scanner? Gone. You're not a pioneer. You're a beta tester who paid full price and slapped a \"Vista Capable\" sticker on your tower like it was a Michelin star. Enjoy the sidebar gadgets. Your weather widget is the only thing in your house still telling you the truth."}, "b": {"item_id": "c19db756674c", "text": "Congratulations to everyone who installed Vista on launch day. You read \"wait for Service Pack 1\" and thought, \"That's advice for people with something to lose.\" You bought a new graphics card just to make your windows see-through. Finally, your windows are as transparent as your reasons.\n\nNow your printer is a paperweight, your scanner is a rumor, and the driver is \"coming soon\" from a company whose website still says 2004. Every click triggers a box asking if you meant it. You've said yes forty times today. You're not secure, you're just trained.\n\nBut you'll tell coworkers it's \"actually smooth once you turn a few things off.\" Sir, you paid four hundred dollars to turn things off. Meanwhile Dave, on XP, finished his spreadsheet and went home."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0517", "lang": "en", "target_id": "macau", "target_name": "Macau", "facts": ["A Portuguese settlement from the 1550s until the handover to China in December 1999.", "Casino revenue passed the Las Vegas Strip's in 2006, and at its 2013 peak, about $45 billion, was roughly seven times the Strip's.", "One company, STDM, held the casino monopoly for four decades until licences opened to competition in 2002.", "Among the most densely populated places on Earth, at roughly 20,000 people per square kilometre.", "The Cotai Strip stands on land reclaimed from the sea between the islands of Taipa and Coloane."], "a": {"item_id": "fe0ea9392503", "text": "Every man on the 6 a.m. TurboJET from Hong Kong has the same plan: lose the rent in Macau, win it back before the boat docks. He lost his bonus in Vegas and figures the baccarat tables here are the same tables, just with more gold leaf. He says \"I'm just here to see the Venetian,\" then walks straight past the gondolas, because gondolas aren't on the way to the table. He's after the feeling of being the guy who came back. What he gets is a room with no clocks and a VIP host named Kelvin who calls him \"boss\" in the voice of a man already calculating his credit limit. Hour six, he's on the ferry home, watching the Cotai skyline shrink, doing math on a comp slip."}, "b": {"item_id": "67e9bb83372e", "text": "You already lost the down payment, so naturally you're taking the TurboJET from Hong Kong to win it back, because nothing says financial recovery like a ferry with a buffet. You've got a system for baccarat. Baccarat! The only decision is \"Banker\" or \"Player,\" and you still fill a little scorecard with red and blue dots like the shoe is sending you messages. It isn't. That's just a deck of cards and your mortgage. You've got red underwear on for luck and a junket host named \"Uncle\" who is definitely not your uncle. By 4 a.m. you'll be drifting through the fake Venice canals in a gondola, humming, past the pawn shop that's already priced your Rolex. Then you'll stumble up to the Ruins of St. Paul's: a church that burned until only the front was left standing. Finally, something here that looks like you."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0518", "lang": "en", "target_id": "vision-pro", "target_name": "Apple Vision Pro", "facts": ["Released in February 2024 at $3,499, with an external battery pack on a cable rated for about two hours.", "'EyeSight' shows a rendering of the wearer's eyes on an outward-facing screen.", "Apple calls it 'spatial computing' and its App Store rules tell developers not to describe apps as AR, VR, XR, or MR.", "Reports during 2024 said Apple had cut production; analysts' first-year sales estimates were well under a million units.", "Weighs 600-650 g, before the battery."], "a": {"item_id": "7f2991f5a417", "text": "Look at this guy on the L train, strapped into $3,500 of ski goggles, pinching the air like he's conducting a very small orchestra. He isn't watching a movie. He's got forty floating windows hovering over a man eating a bagel, and he's \"doing spatial computing,\" which he'll tell you unprompted. The front screen shows his eyes so you feel seen, the most unsettling feature in consumer tech. He isn't on the subway. He's in a living room with a 4K view of the 4 train. Every few stops he lifts the goggles to check whether anyone noticed. We did, pal. Nobody's immersed. We're all just watching a grown man pinch at nothing, a battery cable running to his jacket pocket like a leash, and the only thing he's computing is how fast his neighbors can file a complaint."}, "b": {"item_id": "2e6584647c14", "text": "You paid thirty-five hundred dollars to look like a hostage who's thrilled about it. Everyone on the 6 train can see you pinching the air, conducting an invisible orchestra of Safari tabs. Nobody is impressed. A man eating a hot dog just watched you \"tap\" a button that doesn't exist, and he's the one who feels sorry for you.\n\nYou wanted to be the future. Instead you're a guy who spent a month's rent to avoid eye contact, then bought the version that fakes eye contact for strangers. Those digital eyes are looking at nobody. The real ones are watching a 100-foot screen floating over Mount Hood, while the actual mountain you're passing is a tunnel wall covered in rat hair.\n\nAnd what did it get you? A full seat in rush hour. The whole car looked at you and decided you're either a genius or a crime scene."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0519", "lang": "en", "target_id": "klingon", "target_name": "Klingon", "facts": ["Created by linguist Marc Okrand for Star Trek III (1984), building on a few words the actor James Doohan improvised for the 1979 film.", "Deliberately alien grammar: object-verb-subject word order and no verb 'to be', so Hamlet's line became 'taH pagh taHbe'', 'continue or not continue'.", "The Klingon Language Institute (1992) published Hamlet 'in the original Klingon'; Bing Translator added Klingon in 2013 and Duolingo in 2018.", "Okrand's 1985 Klingon Dictionary listed roughly 1,500 words, and fluent speakers number only in the dozens: 20 to 30 in 2009, 30 to 50 by 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "64f4f6a7ba00", "text": "Congratulations on being fluent in Klingon, a language with maybe thirty fluent speakers, and you've met all of them at the same Marriott. You spent four years mastering a tongue where the standard greeting, nuqneH, means \"What do you want?\" That's also what your family says when you call. You've read Hamlet \"restored to the original Klingon,\" a phrase only a man who has never been hugged could type. One guy raised his son speaking only Klingon; by age five the kid stopped answering him. That's the dream: a language so pure even toddlers refuse it. There's no word for \"please,\" which explains your dating approach. You learned an entire warrior race's language to tell the barista Qapla'. Success. Sure, buddy. Today is a good day to die... a virgin."}, "b": {"item_id": "c3c693f6b9ba", "text": "Nobody becomes fluent in Klingon by accident. You have to sit down and decide that the thing you want most in life is to clear your throat in a language invented for a TV show. Your friends learned Spanish for a job. You learned to say \"Qapla'\" to a bartender who has never once been impressed. You've translated Hamlet. Twice. The Klingons in the original show would read your Hamlet and call it too soft, which is the only review you've ever wanted. At conventions you're the one who corrects the guy in the Worf costume, and he has the better forehead. You're chasing a room where someone finally gets your jokes, and that room is mostly you, reading your own joke aloud, slightly out of breath from the gutturals."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0520", "lang": "en", "target_id": "excel", "target_name": "Microsoft Excel", "facts": ["First shipped for the Macintosh in 1985; the Windows version followed in 1987.", "Silently converts gene names such as SEPT2 and MARCH1 into dates; by August 2020 the HUGO Gene Nomenclature Committee had renamed 27 human genes (MARCH1 became MARCHF1, SEPT2 became SEPTIN2) and published guidelines in Nature Genetics to stop it.", "In October 2020, Public Health England lost 15,841 COVID-19 cases when a legacy .xls file hit its 65,536-row limit.", "Reinhart and Rogoff's 2010 paper 'Growth in a Time of Debt', cited for austerity policies, omitted five countries through a spreadsheet range error found by a graduate student in 2013.", "Sheets have been capped at 1,048,576 rows by 16,384 columns since Excel 2007."], "a": {"item_id": "55712e9f94e9", "text": "You run a 40-million-dollar company on a file called \"Final_v7_REAL_use-this-one.\" You're not a CEO. You're a hostage negotiator for a spreadsheet.\n\nYou say you're \"scrappy.\" No. You're a guy who refuses to pay for software but will happily pay Brenda eleven hours a week to color cells yellow. Yellow means pending. Light yellow means pending but emotionally.\n\nYour entire infrastructure is a macro written by Dave, who left in 2016, and nobody knows where Dave went. You light a candle for Dave. You're afraid to scroll past column AZ because that's where Dave's curse lives.\n\nYou wanted control. You got a file locked for editing by \"Karen,\" who's on vacation in Portugal.\n\nAnd every quarter, your VLOOKUP returns #N/A, and you stare at it like it's a diagnosis you're choosing to ignore."}, "b": {"item_id": "d9b95a68589f", "text": "Let's talk about the founders who run the whole company out of one spreadsheet. Their payroll lives on a tab called \"Sheet47,\" and the cap table is a merged cell nobody dares unmerge. They think they're building a business, but really they're building a shrine to Ctrl+Z. They have forty-one copies of the budget, all named \"FINAL_v9_USE_THIS,\" and the only person who understood the VLOOKUP was the intern, who quit in March. They get giddy over pivot tables, call a #REF! error \"a learning moment,\" and sleep with one eye open in case someone overwrites row 14. When investors ask for a dashboard, they send a screenshot of a pie chart with the legend in Comic Sans."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0521", "lang": "en", "target_id": "slack", "target_name": "Slack", "facts": ["Spun out in 2013 from the internal chat tool of Tiny Speck's failed game Glitch; founder Stewart Butterfield had earlier spun Flickr out of another failed game.", "The name is a backronym: 'Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge'.", "Salesforce bought it for $27.7 billion in 2021.", "Filed an EU antitrust complaint against Microsoft in 2020 over the bundling of Teams with Office."], "a": {"item_id": "f56d27fef4cd", "text": "Nobody runs a company on Slack. They run it from #general, where forty people wait for the CEO to type \"morning all\" so they can reply with the waving hand. These are people who say \"let's take it to a thread\" like it's a moral position. They set their status to \"deep work\" and then react to everything with the eyes emoji. They believe the green dot means they're present. They are not present. They're in forty-one channels, four of them named #random-but-serious. Their deepest fear is a DM with no reply. Their proudest achievement is a \"quick sync\" that somehow ran until Thursday."}, "b": {"item_id": "fae8a282c283", "text": "Let's hear it for the founders running a company on Slack. You wanted a culture of transparency. You got 340 public channels and every real decision happening in a DM called \"quick q.\"\n\nYou don't manage people anymore. You manage a green dot. You jiggle your mouse at your kid's birthday party so the team knows you're \"around.\" Your entire leadership style is the eyes emoji. Someone asks for budget approval, you react with a little thumbs-up, and three weeks later legal wants to know what the thumbs-up meant. Nobody knows. It was legally binding and spiritually ambiguous.\n\nAnd when something truly matters? You type \"hey\" and walk away. Just \"hey.\" That's not a message, that's a hostage situation.\n\nYou promised Slack would kill meetings. Instead you invented the huddle: a meeting that ambushes people while they're eating."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0522", "lang": "en", "target_id": "stinky-tofu", "target_name": "Stinky tofu (臭豆腐)", "facts": ["Tofu soaked for hours to a day or two in a brine that has itself fermented for months from vegetables such as bamboo shoots and mustard greens, often with dried shrimp or meat; the smell is the brine's, not the bean's.", "Changsha's version is black, deep-fried and doused in chili; Taiwan's night-market version is golden and served with pickled cabbage; Shaoxing's, brined with amaranth stalks, is fried pale yellow or steamed with other 'stinky' pickles.", "Legend credits Wang Zhihe, a Qing-era exam candidate who left his unsold tofu too long in 1669; a Beijing fermented-bean-curd brand still carries his name.", "Street vendors are its main outlet, and the smell carries for a block."], "a": {"item_id": "63f160a3e349", "text": "Let's talk about the stinky tofu people. You smelled it from three blocks away, a smell like a sewer filed a noise complaint, and your brain said: *that's where we're eating.* Your entire personality is \"it tastes better than it smells.\" That's not a review. That's a hostage statement.\n\nYou stand on a Taipei night market curb with a paper boat, chopsticks in one hand, phone in the other, filming your own bravery for followers who can't smell it, which is the only reason they're still following you. Tourists do it once for the story. Locals do it every Tuesday, then board the MRT, where forty strangers silently agree to hate them.\n\nAnd the pickled cabbage on top? That's you pretending you came for vegetables. You didn't. You came to be the deadliest passenger on the scooter."}, "b": {"item_id": "49bb8f6483c1", "text": "You're not eating stinky tofu, you're auditioning. Every tourist at the night market stands there with a toothpick and a paper tray, filming their own face for the \"I tried it!\" video, which is the only reason you ordered. Nobody craves this. You wanted a story, and now you've got one and a ten-minute ride on the MRT.\n\nAnd the locals are worse. \"You just have to get past the first bite,\" you say, the way people talk about marathons and their ex. You've turned a snack into a personality. You'll argue Taipei versus Changsha like it's a custody hearing.\n\nThen you walk home trailing a cloud like a divorced cologne ad, wondering why nobody sits next to you. You wanted to be adventurous. You got to be alone on a bus."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0523", "lang": "en", "target_id": "macau", "target_name": "Macau", "facts": ["A Portuguese settlement from the 1550s until the handover to China in December 1999.", "Casino revenue passed the Las Vegas Strip's in 2006, and at its 2013 peak, about $45 billion, was roughly seven times the Strip's.", "One company, STDM, held the casino monopoly for four decades until licences opened to competition in 2002.", "Among the most densely populated places on Earth, at roughly 20,000 people per square kilometre.", "The Cotai Strip stands on land reclaimed from the sea between the islands of Taipa and Coloane."], "a": {"item_id": "e28d81baf3eb", "text": "Look at this guy. He lost his savings in Vegas, so naturally he's crossed an ocean to Macau to win it back. Nothing says \"I've learned my lesson\" like flying to the place where the lesson's bigger brother lives. He's got a system. The system is baccarat, a game where you bet on a hand you're not allowed to touch, run by a dealer who's been doing this since before your dad had hair. He'll tell you it's basically 50/50. It isn't. The house is the only one in that room with a spreadsheet. Then he takes the ferry home with a dozen egg tarts and a tan from standing on the deck, the only thing that came back from the trip."}, "b": {"item_id": "6405f7045e3c", "text": "You lost it in Vegas, or Manila, or on a Tuesday online, and now you're on the ferry from Hong Kong with the confidence of a man who has finally \"figured out baccarat.\" You haven't. Baccarat has no figuring out. It's a coin flip in a tuxedo.\n\nYou're not here for money, you're here for a story where you come back a legend. Meanwhile, the guy next to you, who's been awake since Thursday, is bowing to a card like it's his ancestor, and he's winning, which is why you think it's contagious.\n\nYour plan: one hour, minimum table. By hour six, you're in a gondola at the Venetian, inside a mall, under a painted sky, asking a singing man from Guangdong if he takes WeChat.\n\nThe only thing you win back is a Portuguese egg tart, and even that's on the credit card."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0524", "lang": "en", "target_id": "latex", "target_name": "LaTeX", "facts": ["Leslie Lamport wrote LaTeX in the early 1980s and released it in 1984 as a macro layer on Donald Knuth's TeX (1978), which Knuth built because he disliked how the galley proofs of the new edition of his own book looked.", "TeX's version number converges on π (3.141592653 since 2021), and Knuth's TeX bug bounty started at one hexadecimal dollar, $2.56, doubling yearly until frozen at $327.68; he stopped mailing real cheques in 2008 after fraud and now credits accounts at his fictional Bank of San Serriffe, and only 9 of the 275 cheques he sent from 2006 on were ever cashed.", "Its best-known warning is 'Underfull \\hbox (badness 10000)'; overfull lines are reported as 'Overfull \\hbox (Npt too wide)', and figures float to wherever the algorithm decides, not where the author put them.", "A single missing brace can produce an error reported pages away from the mistake, and a document can need three compiler passes to get its references right."], "a": {"item_id": "1a0ff1693a16", "text": "You wrote a memo about the break room fridge in LaTeX. Two pages. Thirty-eight \\usepackage lines. Your preamble is longer than your argument. You lost Tuesday to an overfull hbox in a sentence that says \"please label your yogurt.\"\n\nWho is this for? Linda from accounting? She opened the PDF, saw Computer Modern, and assumed you were applying for tenure. Nobody needed a BibTeX citation for \"see previous email.\"\n\nYou compiled it eleven times. Three were just to make the \"??\" go away. You drew a TikZ flowchart with one box. One. It points at itself.\n\nAnd what did it get you? Bob replied, \"Can you send this as a Word doc so I can track changes?\"\n\nYour dissertation defense was in 2014. The committee left. Let it go."}, "b": {"item_id": "37e7504ee3eb", "text": "You spent three hours on a two-page memo, and two of them were the preamble. You loaded fourteen packages to say \"Please submit receipts by Friday.\" Your manager opens the PDF on his phone, sees Computer Modern, and assumes someone died, or got tenure.\n\nYou didn't pick LaTeX for the memo. You picked it so that, for one afternoon, a logistics update could look peer-reviewed. Your kerning is flawless, and nobody has ever noticed kerning except you, at 11 p.m., on TeX Stack Exchange, asking why your header won't align. A man named Egreg has told you, kindly and correctly, that you're doing it wrong.\n\nThe memo got two replies. One said \"Thanks.\" The other said \"Can you send this as a Word doc?\"\n\nAnd you did. You opened Word. You sat there, alone, crying in Calibri."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0525", "lang": "en", "target_id": "windows-8", "target_name": "Windows 8", "facts": ["Released in October 2012; removed the Start button and booted into a full-screen grid of tiles designed for touch screens most PCs did not have.", "The 8.1 update in 2013 put a Start button back, Windows 10 (2015) restored the Start menu, and Microsoft skipped the name Windows 9 altogether.", "Microsoft dropped the 'Metro' name in August 2012 after reports that German retailer Metro AG had objected on trademark grounds; Microsoft called 'Metro' a code name and denied any litigation, and the style was afterwards known informally as 'Modern UI'.", "The 'Charms bar' hid Search, Share, and Settings behind an invisible swipe from the right edge.", "Windows RT, the ARM edition launched alongside it, could not run ordinary Windows programs; Microsoft wrote down $900 million of unsold Surface RT tablets in 2013."], "a": {"item_id": "8dd545adc448", "text": "You put Windows 8 on a desktop. A tower. Under a desk. With a mouse. You looked at an operating system built for fingertips and thought, \"I'll just reach.\" Who are you? You're the guy who buys a touchscreen glove to use a keyboard. You wanted to feel modern, and now you spend your evenings shoving a cursor into the top-right corner like you're trying to scratch your monitor's back. You wanted productivity. You got a full-screen weather app swallowing your entire twenty-four-inch monitor to tell you it's raining, and you can't figure out how to close it. You lost the Start button and called it \"a fresh start.\" Your coworkers asked how you shut your computer down, and you said, \"I don't. It's always on, like a lifestyle.\" You're not using a PC. You're waving at it, waiting for the Charms bar to love you back."}, "b": {"item_id": "a00e6ae0aa53", "text": "Who puts Windows 8 on a desktop? You. You looked at a perfectly working Windows 7 machine and said, \"What this needs is a touchscreen experience, and I don't own a touchscreen.\"\n\nSo now you sit at a 27-inch monitor, flinging your mouse into corners like a man hunting for a light switch in a stranger's house. You've Googled \"how to shut down\" at eleven at night. Your wife watched.\n\nYou wanted the future. You got a full-screen weather app you can't close and a Mail tile that's been \"Getting things ready\" since August.\n\nEventually you downloaded a program to bring the Start button back, so you paid to get Windows 7 with extra steps.\n\nAnd sometimes you still tap the screen. It's not a touchscreen. Those fingerprints are just hope."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0526", "lang": "en", "target_id": "klingon", "target_name": "Klingon", "facts": ["Created by linguist Marc Okrand for Star Trek III (1984), building on a few words the actor James Doohan improvised for the 1979 film.", "Deliberately alien grammar: object-verb-subject word order and no verb 'to be', so Hamlet's line became 'taH pagh taHbe'', 'continue or not continue'.", "The Klingon Language Institute (1992) published Hamlet 'in the original Klingon'; Bing Translator added Klingon in 2013 and Duolingo in 2018.", "Okrand's 1985 Klingon Dictionary listed roughly 1,500 words, and fluent speakers number only in the dozens: 20 to 30 in 2009, 30 to 50 by 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "64aab443b4b9", "text": "Being fluent in Klingon is a big deal. You've joined an elite community of maybe forty people worldwide, and you've met all of them at the same Holiday Inn conference room. You spent years mastering a language built for a warrior race, a culture of honor, combat and blood oaths, and you use it to argue on a forum about verb suffixes. You learned the language of the fiercest fighters in the galaxy, and the last thing you fought was a lactose sensitivity.\n\nKlingon has no word for \"hello.\" The standard greeting is \"nuqneH,\" which means \"what do you want?\" Perfect for you, because nobody has ever approached you without a reason.\n\nYou told people you read *Hamlet* \"in the original Klingon.\" Brother, Shakespeare had a girlfriend. You have a bat'leth over the futon.\n\nQapla'. That means \"success,\" a word you know only in Klingon."}, "b": {"item_id": "a7e3b2e8dd9c", "text": "Congratulations on mastering a language whose native speakers are fictional, homicidal, and would gut you over your accent. You've spent a decade on the warrior tongue of the galaxy, and your only battle was a Discord war over whether the apostrophe is a glottal stop. Marc Okrand made it up on a movie deadline, and you treat it like Latin.\n\nYou can order a drink in a language with no word for \"hello,\" which explains why no one's greeted you at a party since 2009. You translated Hamlet into it. Hamlet! A guy who can't commit to a decision, rendered for a species that would have stabbed Claudius in act one.\n\nAnd what does fluency get you? A convention hall of forty people correcting your verb prefixes, a girlfriend who learned exactly one phrase (the one for \"leave me alone\"), and a lifetime of dying honorably in a Hyatt conference room.\n\nQapla'. You'll need it."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-en-0527", "lang": "en", "target_id": "chicago-deep-dish", "target_name": "Chicago deep-dish pizza", "facts": ["Created at Pizzeria Uno in Chicago in 1943; credit is disputed among founder Ike Sewell, his partner Ric Riccardo, and cook Rudy Malnati.", "Assembled upside down: sliced mozzarella on the dough, then toppings, then crushed tomatoes on top, so the cheese does not scorch during the long bake.", "Jon Stewart called it 'tomato soup in a bread bowl' and 'a casserole' on The Daily Show in 2013.", "Chicagoans' everyday pizza is thin 'tavern-style' cut into squares; GrubHub order data reported by WBEZ in 2013 showed city residents ordering non-deep-dish pizza about 10 to 1 over deep dish, with deep dish and stuffed making up under 9% of orders."], "a": {"item_id": "506e0b98fda7", "text": "Let's talk about the people who call it pizza. Mostly Chicagoans, who haven't eaten it since their cousin visited in 2019. You order tavern-cut thin crust every Friday, but the second a New Yorker opens his mouth, you're a martyr for the Gino's East gift shop.\n\nAnd the tourists. You stood outside for an hour in a Michigan Avenue wind to be told your pie takes 45 minutes. You said \"when in Chicago\" like it was a vow. You got a photo of one slice held up by a spatula, a nap in a hotel bed at 4 p.m., and the right to say you \"did it.\"\n\nWhat do you get for calling it pizza? An argument with a stranger from Brooklyn, which is the only reason you came. Congratulations. Mayor Daley would be proud."}, "b": {"item_id": "6aea7052f5a6", "text": "Every time someone tells me Chicago deep-dish is \"technically not a pizza,\" I know exactly who I'm dealing with. He's a guy from Hoboken who ate one slice in Naples in 2009 and has been waiting fifteen years to bring it up. He's not hungry. He's shopping for a fight. He wants the whole table to hear that real pizza is thin and foldable, and he says \"foldable\" like it's a personality. Meanwhile he's eating with a knife and fork, which he thinks makes him sophisticated. Chicago never asked for his permission. Chicago has been feeding people since before he was born, while he was still folding pepperoni on a napkin. So let him win the argument. It's the only thing he's ever ordered that came out exactly how he wanted."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0528", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "comic-sans", "target_name": "Comic Sans 字体", "facts": ["Designed by Vincent Connare at Microsoft in 1994, modeled on the comic-book lettering in The Dark Knight Returns and Watchmen.", "Made for the speech bubbles of Rover, the cartoon dog in Microsoft Bob; it missed Bob's release and shipped instead in the Windows 95 Plus! pack.", "CERN's July 2012 Higgs boson discovery announcement was presented on slides set in it.", "The 'Ban Comic Sans' campaign, started by designers Dave and Holly Combs in 1999, ran until 2019, when they retired it and launched usecomicsans.com."], "a": {"item_id": "5f56efde52fb", "text": "你们有没有收到过这种通知？标题是《关于本周五停电的通知》，字体是Comic Sans。我一看，以为是小区要办儿童节派对，结果是停水停电，还要求业主自带水桶。\n\n干这事的人我大概知道是谁，物业行政，或者公司HR。他们想表达的是\"我们很亲切，咱们是一家人\"。结果字一出来，就像在给你发一张蜡笔画的请柬。\n\n这字体本来是微软1994年给一个卡通式聊天软件Microsoft Bob做的，用在漫画气泡里的。一份要你立即整改的文件，最后看上去像是从对话框里蹦出来的。\n\n然后呢？全公司把它截图发进群，配文：\"这是谁的生日会通知？\"领导第二天在会上说要严肃点，底下大家一边点头，一边憋笑。"}, "b": {"item_id": "8384fe94668e", "text": "我们公司人事上周发裁员通知，用的Comic Sans。我问为什么，她说：“想柔和一点，减少冲击。”\n\n姐姐，这字体当年是给微软一只叫Rover的卡通狗设计的，专门用来写对话气泡。你这是让一条狗通知我下岗。\n\n结果呢？“因业务调整，您的岗位将被取消”，每个字都圆滚滚、歪歪扭扭，读起来就是“汪！你被开除啦～”\n\n冲击确实没有，因为根本没人信。有个哥们儿回复了一串“哈哈哈”，周一照常来上班，被保安拦在门口，还委屈：“你们那个通知不是在搞笑吗？”\n\n最惨的是人事自己，月底被优化了，通知也是Comic Sans。她盯着屏幕看了十分钟，说：“这肯定是恶作剧。”\n\n行，你的柔和，终于轮到你自己了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0529", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "chicago-deep-dish", "target_name": "芝加哥深盘披萨", "facts": ["Created at Pizzeria Uno in Chicago in 1943; credit is disputed among founder Ike Sewell, his partner Ric Riccardo, and cook Rudy Malnati.", "Assembled upside down: sliced mozzarella on the dough, then toppings, then crushed tomatoes on top, so the cheese does not scorch during the long bake.", "Jon Stewart called it 'tomato soup in a bread bowl' and 'a casserole' on The Daily Show in 2013.", "Chicagoans' everyday pizza is thin 'tavern-style' cut into squares; GrubHub order data reported by WBEZ in 2013 showed city residents ordering non-deep-dish pizza about 10 to 1 over deep dish, with deep dish and stuffed making up under 9% of orders."], "a": {"item_id": "66023dd37676", "text": "芝加哥人为了证明深盘是披萨，付出了巨大的代价。你问他，他拍着胸脯说：“这就是披萨！”行，那披萨怎么要等四十五分钟？披萨是外卖，你这是预约手术。上来还得拿刀叉，芝士压在底下，酱糊在最上面，一个厚面壳兜着一锅东西。这不叫披萨，这叫往派里倒了碗番茄汤。\n\n他们图什么？就图跟纽约人吵架。纽约人说一句“那是砂锅”，芝加哥人当场气得再加点一个。结果呢，一块下去，人就饱了。剩下七块在冰箱里陪你三天。\n\n所以你看，这帮人为了捍卫深盘，每次都得亲自吃一遍。吃完瘫在沙发上，连吵架的力气都没了。纽约人单手折着一片，边走边吃，已经赢了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "54fda0729132", "text": "管芝加哥深盘叫披萨的，基本都是芝加哥人。你别跟他争，他已经争了一辈子了。他图什么？图个名分。番茄酱铺在最上面，芝士压在最底下，饼边砌得跟城墙一样高，这叫披萨？这叫一锅围了墙的炖菜。可他非得叫披萨，因为叫“砂锅”就没法跟纽约人吵架了。结果呢？进店点单，服务员说：“得烤四十五分钟。”他说：“没事，披萨嘛。”等完上桌，刀叉都给他配好了。他吃两块就扶着墙出门，嘴里还念叨：“这才是真正的披萨。”兄弟，真正的披萨，不用你吃完躺一下午。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0530", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "ugg", "target_name": "UGG 雪地靴", "facts": ["Sheepskin boots Australian surfers wore to warm up after sessions; Brian Smith started selling them in California in 1978.", "Deckers Outdoor bought the brand in 1995 and holds 'UGG' as a trademark in the US and elsewhere; in Australia, 'ugg' is a generic word any bootmaker may use.", "In 2019, a US jury found an Australian maker liable for selling boots labelled 'ugg' into the United States.", "Oprah Winfrey featured them in her 'Favorite Things' lists repeatedly in the 2000s, which drove the first boom.", "UGG is Deckers' largest brand, with over $2 billion in annual sales."], "a": {"item_id": "3ebab0e81cc7", "text": "你们见过夏天三十八度还穿UGG的人吧？两只毛茸茸的大头，像刚从羊身上薅下来、还没下班的小动物。他们图什么？图一个\"我早就入冬了\"的仪式感。结果呢？走三百米，羊皮里子把脚焖成一锅卤鸭掌，袜子都不用穿，直接在桑拿房里逛街。到了十月，那层软塌塌的鞋底已经磨出了地图。最惨的是羊毛里子被汗压成一块毡，踩在地铁台阶上，像踩着两块泡过水的抹布。朋友圈里配文\"今天也很暖\"，照片里那双栗色的靴子，已经晒成了一条咸鱼。"}, "b": {"item_id": "3c6a30dd29f9", "text": "我有个朋友，一年四季穿UGG。三伏天，脚上套着两只羊。我问你不热吗？她说：“你不懂，UGG本来就是澳洲冲浪的人穿的。”姐，人家是冲完浪上岸暖脚，你是在地铁里给脚蒸桑拿。\n\n她图什么？图舒服，图不用系鞋带。确实不用系，穿到第三个月，靴筒自己就塌了，鞋跟往外磨成斜坡，她走路像一艘正在侧翻的船。下雨更惨，麂皮一沾水就是一圈白印，像脚踝上长了年轮，一看就知道这双鞋熬过几个夏天。\n\n最狠的是味道。羊毛内里，光脚，捂了一整个七月。她一脱鞋，全屋人都懂了：那只羊，没走远。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0531", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "vim", "target_name": "Vim", "facts": ["Released by Bram Moolenaar in November 1991 as 'Vi IMitation', a vi clone he had begun writing for the Amiga in 1988 on top of Stevie; the expansion became 'Vi IMproved' with version 2.0 in December 1993.", "Licensed as charityware: the licence asks users to donate to ICCF Holland, a charity Moolenaar ran for children in Uganda.", "Stack Overflow's question 'How to exit the Vim editor?' passed one million visits in May 2017, which Stack Overflow marked with a blog post.", "Modal editing: in normal mode, typing letters runs commands rather than inserting text; :wq saves and quits, :q! quits and discards changes."], "a": {"item_id": "e21539084a9a", "text": "我们组有个哥们，用Vim。你怎么知道的？你不用知道，他会告诉你。开会时他说“我手从不离开键盘”，然后在投影上按了二十分钟hjkl，去找第三行。\n\n他的.vimrc写了八百行，装了四十个插件：文件树、自动补全、Git面板。兄弟，你花了三年，亲手把Vim配成了一个启动更慢的VS Code。\n\n他图什么？就图那一句：“你连怎么退出都不会吧？”\n\n可他的下场呢？给女朋友发微信：“我爱你:wq”。人家问：“你打错了吧？”他说：“没错，保存并退出。”\n\n她真退出了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "773f0cf90ece", "text": "我有个朋友用 Vim 写代码，这本来没什么，问题是他得让全世界知道。每天发朋友圈：\"今天 hjkl 走了八个小时，手没离开主键盘区。\"配图是一屏幕冒号和波浪号。底下点赞的全是同款人，互相夸：\"牛逼，我也是。\"他们图什么？图的就是别人看不懂还得点赞。结果上周他要退出文件，手一滑按了个 i，开始在文件里狂打字，还没反应过来。他想起有个 Esc，按了二十下，屏幕纹丝不动。最后他在群里喊：\"救命，我退不出去了！\"群友秒回一排：先按 Esc，再敲 :q!。他回：\"我已经发过朋友圈了，不能认输。\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0532", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "brasilia", "target_name": "巴西利亚", "facts": ["Inaugurated on 21 April 1960 after about 41 months of construction, moving the capital inland from Rio de Janeiro.", "Lúcio Costa's Plano Piloto is laid out like an airplane (or a bird, or a cross), with Oscar Niemeyer's government buildings along the fuselage.", "Listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1987, less than 30 years after it was built.", "Planned for 500,000 residents; the Federal District now has about 3 million, most in satellite towns outside the plan.", "Built for the car: wide avenues, few pedestrian crossings, and distances that make walking impractical."], "a": {"item_id": "bb6fc598ef80", "text": "在巴西利亚靠两条腿出门的人，我服你们。你们都是什么神仙？大概三种：一种是退休大爷，每天走八公里，说是锻炼，其实是不想在家跟老伴大眼瞪小眼；一种是刚来的外地人，觉得\"这城市这么规整，走走就到了\"，二十分钟后他站在Eixão的六车道中间，像被冲上岸的海带；还有一种是非要证明自己有\"城市精神\"的人。图什么？图个\"我不开车也能活\"的面子。落得什么下场？你在308北和308南之间绕了一个小时，因为楼都长得一模一样。最后你走进一个部委大院，门卫问你找谁，你说找公交车站。公交车站？它在你身后三公里，中间隔着一条没有人行道的高速，隔着你的人生。"}, "b": {"item_id": "7ded9e81696c", "text": "在巴西利亚走路的人，就两种：建筑系学生，和看地图觉得\"才两公里\"的游客。\n\n国会大厦就在前面，清清楚楚。你走二十分钟，它还在前面，一点没大。建筑师管这叫\"纪念性尺度\"，我管这叫海市蜃楼。\n\n图什么？图\"亲身体验世界遗产\"。结果这城是按飞机设计的，汽车安排得明明白白，行人？没设计。你只好在草地上踩出一条红泥路，当地人管这叫\"欲望之路\"，听着挺浪漫，其实就是一群跟你一样的人集体迷路的证据。\n\n下场都一样：裤腿红到膝盖，嘴唇在一千米海拔上裂开，最后卡在一座立交桥下，对面看得见，就是过不去。\n\n你叫了辆车，司机到了问：\"你怎么在这儿？\"\n\n你说：\"想感受一下城市。\"\n\n他说：\"这不是城市，这是飞机。你在机翼上散步。\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0533", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "klingon", "target_name": "克林贡语", "facts": ["Created by linguist Marc Okrand for Star Trek III (1984), building on a few words the actor James Doohan improvised for the 1979 film.", "Deliberately alien grammar: object-verb-subject word order and no verb 'to be', so Hamlet's line became 'taH pagh taHbe'', 'continue or not continue'.", "The Klingon Language Institute (1992) published Hamlet 'in the original Klingon'; Bing Translator added Klingon in 2013 and Duolingo in 2018.", "Okrand's 1985 Klingon Dictionary listed roughly 1,500 words, and fluent speakers number only in the dozens: 20 to 30 in 2009, 30 to 50 by 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "1e0df73ad7c6", "text": "我认识一哥们，克林贡语说得贼溜。全世界能说流利的，据说也就几十个人，他算一个。\n\n你品品这是什么概念？这门语言里压根没有“你好”，见面只能说“nuqneH”，意思是“你想干嘛”。他花了十年，学会一门见面就吵架的语言，结果全世界能跟他吵的人，凑不齐一个班。\n\n他每年去参加克林贡语言研究所的年会，跟一屋子人朗读克林贡语版《哈姆雷特》。人家坚持说这才是原著，莎士比亚是翻译的。\n\n他还想从小教儿子说，孩子五岁就不肯接茬了。\n\n他这辈子唯一一次听异性跟他说克林贡语，是相亲。他说完特长，姑娘站起来，说了句“Qapla'”，就是“祝你成功”，然后走了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "40a35f67e92f", "text": "我真佩服那些把克林贡语学到流利的人。流利啊，那是花了十年。这语言有多不适合社交？它没有\"你好\"。最接近的是 nuqneH，字面意思是：\"你要干嘛？\"也没有\"谢谢\"，因为克林贡人不感恩。\n\n所以你想想这帮人的处境：苦练十年，全世界能聊的就几百个同好。终于约到一个，见面第一句，你问他：\"你要干嘛？\"他回你：\"你要干嘛？\"然后俩人就僵在那儿了。\n\n想表达感情怎么办？只能用他们的方式，比如：\"今天是死的好日子。\"你女朋友说\"我想你了\"，你深情地回一句：\"今天是死的好日子。\"\n\n最惨的是什么？你辛辛苦苦练的是宾语—动词—主语的语序。你想说\"我爱你\"，得说成\"你，爱，我\"。听着像在被审问。\n\n最后他们图什么？图一个能用外星语言吵赢别人的机会。结果遇上另一个同样流利的，两人吵了三小时，谁也不会说\"对不起\"。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0534", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "esperanto", "target_name": "世界语", "facts": ["Published in Warsaw in 1887 by L. L. Zamenhof, a Białystok-born eye doctor, under the pen name Doktoro Esperanto, 'one who hopes'.", "Speaker estimates range from 100,000 to two million; roughly a thousand people grow up with it as a native language.", "William Shatner starred in Incubus (1966), a horror film shot entirely in Esperanto, with pronunciation Esperantists still mock.", "Its Wikipedia has over 350,000 articles, and its Duolingo course for English speakers had 1.36 million learners by July 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "16da4448f632", "text": "我有个朋友学世界语，说是为了世界和平。这语言是1887年一个波兰眼科医生发明的，目标是让全人类说同一种话。一百三十多年过去了，全人类没统一，我朋友倒是跟全人类都说不上话了。\n\n他图什么呢？他说世界语圈有个\"护照服务\"，全球会说世界语的人家里都能免费住。我问他住过几家，他说一家，在匈牙利。两个人见面第一件事，就是互相纠正对方宾格结尾漏掉的那个\"-n\"。\n\n最绝的是，\"Esperanto\"这个词原意是\"心怀希望的人\"。你看，发明者早就想明白了：学这玩意儿的人，只能靠希望活着。"}, "b": {"item_id": "b6106e5169c7", "text": "学世界语的人图什么？图世界和平。发明人柴门霍夫说，人类打架是因为语言不通。结果一百多年过去了，学成的人飞去参加世界语大会，全世界同好聚一堂，你一开口“Saluton”，对方也“Saluton”，然后俩人大眼瞪小眼。因为你们发现，这门语言唯一的用途，就是找到另一个会这门语言的人，互相确认：对，咱俩都有病。\n\n“Esperanto”本意是“怀抱希望的人”，名字起得真诚实，没叫“已经实现的人”。\n\n最惨的是，他们的语法一条例外都没有，人生全是例外：没人跟他们说话。想练口语，只能住进另一个世界语者家里，沙发一睡三天，聊的全是“你为什么也学这个”。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0535", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "esperanto", "target_name": "世界语", "facts": ["Published in Warsaw in 1887 by L. L. Zamenhof, a Białystok-born eye doctor, under the pen name Doktoro Esperanto, 'one who hopes'.", "Speaker estimates range from 100,000 to two million; roughly a thousand people grow up with it as a native language.", "William Shatner starred in Incubus (1966), a horror film shot entirely in Esperanto, with pronunciation Esperantists still mock.", "Its Wikipedia has over 350,000 articles, and its Duolingo course for English speakers had 1.36 million learners by July 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "c6f136a1221b", "text": "我认识一个学世界语的。他跟我说，世界语只有十六条语法规则，一个月就能学会。没错，他一个月就学会了，然后花了十年找一个能跟他说上话的人。\n\n他图什么？图世界大同。柴门霍夫一八八七年发明它，是想让全人类共用一门第二语言。一百多年过去了，它还真成了所有人的第二语言：第二个“回头再说”的语言。\n\n他现在出国不住酒店，靠“护照服务”睡在别的世界语者家沙发上。俩人见面热泪盈眶，互称“同志”，聊了一宿。聊的是：怎么才能让更多人学世界语。\n\n我问他这辈子算不算失败。他说不算，世界语里没有“失败”，只有mal-成功。“成功”前面加个mal-，意思是成功的反面。\n\n哥们儿，翻译过来，那还是失败啊。"}, "b": {"item_id": "b6106e5169c7", "text": "学世界语的人图什么？图世界和平。发明人柴门霍夫说，人类打架是因为语言不通。结果一百多年过去了，学成的人飞去参加世界语大会，全世界同好聚一堂，你一开口“Saluton”，对方也“Saluton”，然后俩人大眼瞪小眼。因为你们发现，这门语言唯一的用途，就是找到另一个会这门语言的人，互相确认：对，咱俩都有病。\n\n“Esperanto”本意是“怀抱希望的人”，名字起得真诚实，没叫“已经实现的人”。\n\n最惨的是，他们的语法一条例外都没有，人生全是例外：没人跟他们说话。想练口语，只能住进另一个世界语者家里，沙发一睡三天，聊的全是“你为什么也学这个”。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0536", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "monopoly", "target_name": "大富翁（Monopoly）", "facts": ["Descends from Lizzie Magie's The Landlord's Game, patented in 1904 to teach the evils of land monopoly in the spirit of Henry George.", "Charles Darrow sold his version to Parker Brothers in 1935 and was credited as the inventor; Parker bought Magie's patent for $500.", "The official rules require any unbought property to be auctioned, and 'Free Parking' pays nothing; the house rules that skip the auction and pile cash on Free Parking are why games drag.", "During the Second World War, British intelligence had maps, compasses, and real currency hidden in sets sent to prisoner-of-war camps."], "a": {"item_id": "a8f6340d1edd", "text": "过年一家人想图个团圆，掏出大富翁。这游戏设计得特别科学：专门把亲情换算成租金。\n\n我爸永远当银行，所以他既是央行又是裁判，还偷偷往自己那堆里放钱。我妈见一块买一块，一块不卖，说“留着传给你”，我说妈，这是纸。我弟更狠，四个火车站全买了，我每路过一次就交一次钱，对亲姐姐收费收得比春运还顺手。\n\n我走到他的酒店，一千五。我说我是你姐，他说：“那给你打个折，一千四。”\n\n最后我抵押了公用事业，进了监狱，全家居然都希望我多待几轮，因为我在里面不用交租。\n\n图的是团圆，落得的是游戏还没破产，亲戚关系先清算了。\n\n我一掀桌，我爸很冷静：“重来一局。还是我当银行。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "f719efc18290", "text": "每年过年，总有亲戚提议：“一家人玩个大富翁，热闹热闹。”热闹？这游戏的胜利条件是：让你亲妈破产。\n\n图什么？平时不好意思跟你爸算的账，全在这块板上算了。我爸一辈子没买过房，一摸骰子就开始囤地，一条街全拿下，盖满酒店，等我路过，笑得像个开发商。我妈当银行，银行行长自己也下场玩，这在现实里叫什么？叫进去。\n\n我舅连着三轮进监狱，出来第一句：“谁欠我过路费？”\n\n玩到凌晨一点，没人赢，全在抵押。最后我弟把桌子一掀，地契满天飞。第二天早饭，没人说话。团圆了，账还没算完。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0537", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "nfts", "target_name": "NFT", "facts": ["Beeple's 'Everydays: The First 5000 Days' sold at Christie's for $69.3 million in March 2021.", "OpenSea, the largest NFT marketplace, processed a record $2.7 billion in trades on 1 May 2022 and just $9.34 million on 28 August 2022, a 99% drop, according to DappRadar data.", "A 2023 dappGambl study found that 95% of the NFT collections it examined had a market value of zero.", "The token usually records only a URL; the image itself sits on an ordinary server or IPFS, not on the blockchain.", "Collins Dictionary named 'NFT' its word of the year for 2021."], "a": {"item_id": "6da2286dc224", "text": "2021年买NFT那帮兄弟，平时买杯奶茶都要比三家团购，结果花八万块，买了一张猴子图的“所有权”。注意，不是图，是所有权。图谁都能右键另存为，你那张跟我存的那张一个像素都不差，区别是你那张会心疼。\n\n图什么？图头像。八万块设成微信头像，开口先让人看猴。天天在Discord里喊“WAGMI”，我们都会成功。后来确实一起了，地板价从八万掉到八百。\n\n最惨的是光Gas费就交了两千，比猴现在还贵。问他猴呢？在钱包里。钱包呢？助记词抄在纸上。纸呢？被他妈当废纸卖了。全家最懂Web3的，是收废品的大爷。"}, "b": {"item_id": "812b8f18c10a", "text": "2021年买NFT的那批人，我太懂了。花九千块买一只无聊猴子的截图，换成头像，每天早上第一件事是看地板价。朋友问他买的啥，他说这是资产。问资产在哪，他指着手机说：在链上。我说那你右键另存为一下，他说那叫盗版，法律不允许。转一次账，手续费比猴子还贵，他还觉得这叫门槛。后来项目方拿着钱跑了，猴子还在，官网打不开了，他的头像成了全网最贵的一张\"此页面无法访问\"。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0538", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "canberra", "target_name": "堪培拉", "facts": ["Chosen as a compromise because neither Sydney nor Melbourne would accept the other as capital; Australia's constitution required the capital to be at least 100 miles from Sydney.", "Designed by the Chicago architects Walter Burley Griffin and Marion Mahony Griffin, who won an international competition in 1912.", "Its centrepiece, Lake Burley Griffin, is artificial, created by damming the Molonglo River in 1963-64.", "Parliament House (1988) is built into Capital Hill under a lawn meant for citizens to walk over their politicians; the lawn was fenced off in 2017 for security.", "Melbourne served as the capital until 1927 while Canberra was being built."], "a": {"item_id": "46416cb1f80d", "text": "你身边肯定有这种人：为了“离权力近一点”，从悉尼搬去堪培拉。公务员、政策顾问、游说公司的，图的都是升职。三年后，他们每天开四十分钟车去一栋楼，写四十页报告，结论永远是“建议进一步研究”。周五下午，全城人开三小时车回悉尼，就剩他们守着伯利·格里芬湖。全城最大的娱乐是绕湖跑步，跑完一圈，正好跑回办公室。权力确实在附近，就在国会大厦里，可每次想见，都得先预约，还得等部长助理回邮件。离权力近了，离生活远了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "e958e4adc831", "text": "有人为了工作搬去堪培拉，我真想问：你是想不开，还是想得太开？这帮人基本都是联邦公务员，图什么？图铁饭碗，图名片上印着个“部”字。去了才发现，这座城市就是个大办公室，周五下午四点人类集体消失，全开车去悉尼找活人。在那儿交朋友特别快：聊三句知道你哪个部，聊五句知道你在写什么简报，聊十句发现咱俩在写同一份。最惨是机构改革，部门被拆了、改名了，你人没动，老板换了，名片重印。五年后你攒够钱想回悉尼，一查，悉尼唯一对口的单位，总部在堪培拉。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0539", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "monopoly", "target_name": "大富翁（Monopoly）", "facts": ["Descends from Lizzie Magie's The Landlord's Game, patented in 1904 to teach the evils of land monopoly in the spirit of Henry George.", "Charles Darrow sold his version to Parker Brothers in 1935 and was credited as the inventor; Parker bought Magie's patent for $500.", "The official rules require any unbought property to be auctioned, and 'Free Parking' pays nothing; the house rules that skip the auction and pile cash on Free Parking are why games drag.", "During the Second World War, British intelligence had maps, compasses, and real currency hidden in sets sent to prisoner-of-war camps."], "a": {"item_id": "7f9957efd2c9", "text": "过年我舅非要拉全家玩大富翁，说就玩一把。我告诉你，这一把从吃完饺子玩到天亮。我舅抢着当银行，发钱的时候笑得比真银行还真。轮到他自己，橙色那条街先买下，盖三栋房子，再升级成旅馆。我表姐踩上去交了一大笔过路费，脸比她手里的绿房子还绿。我爸更惨，一圈转到监狱，他说我就路过。人家说那叫Just Visiting，你是进去了。没钱交保释金，只能眼睁睁看着我舅把他的地一块块收走。最后免费停车场那堆钱全被我舅拿走，他抱着钱说：这就是亲情。"}, "b": {"item_id": "a7faf659e2b2", "text": "每年过年，总有一个人提议：“一家人玩个大富翁，热闹。”你想热闹？你想看你爸抢着当银行，发钱的时候顺手往自己那堆塞两张五百？你想看你妈踩了你的酒店，说“都是一家人，房租就免了”，轮到你踩她的南京路，她把地契往桌上一拍：“亲兄弟明算账。”你小姨夫拿两条破铁路换你的地，那个眼神，跟当年找你爸借三万块一模一样。玩到凌晨两点没人破产，因为奶奶偷偷往各家塞钱：“孩子进监狱了，快拿钱捞出来。”最后桌子一掀，三家人一年不说话。你图团圆？大富翁教会你一件事：一家人能坐一桌吃饭，是因为桌上没有地契。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0540", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "vision-pro", "target_name": "Apple Vision Pro", "facts": ["Released in February 2024 at $3,499, with an external battery pack on a cable rated for about two hours.", "'EyeSight' shows a rendering of the wearer's eyes on an outward-facing screen.", "Apple calls it 'spatial computing' and its App Store rules tell developers not to describe apps as AR, VR, XR, or MR.", "Reports during 2024 said Apple had cut production; analysts' first-year sales estimates were well under a million units.", "Weighs 600-650 g, before the battery."], "a": {"item_id": "c894b12374a0", "text": "地铁上戴 Vision Pro 的人，我研究过了：花了两万九，最大的需求是让全车人知道。\n\n这玩意儿最大的卖点，是把外面的世界透传给你看。三万块，让你看见了你本来就在的这节车厢。我拿肉眼看，免费，还没延迟。\n\n他还得在空气里捏手指，一会儿放大，一会儿点击。旁边大妈以为他在捉苍蝇，再不然就是在隔空给她算命。\n\n最惨的是，前面那块屏幕专门显示他的眼睛，好让别人觉得他\"在场\"。可全车人透过那双假眼睛，看到的是一个被电池线拴在裤兜里、一动不敢动的人。\n\n电量撑两小时。他还没到站，已经先关机了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "7a31cad715fb", "text": "在地铁上戴 Vision Pro 的人，我真是服了。他们是谁？通常是刚花三万买了设备的程序员，或者想在朋友圈证明自己是\"未来人\"的部门主管。图什么？图全车人都看他。结果呢，他戴着个大眼罩，坐在车厢里对着空气划拉手指，像在跟一个看不见的前女友视频通话。旁边大爷以为他在看病，主动给他让座。最惨的是，他脸上那块外屏还亮着他自己的眼睛，一闪一闪的，全车人跟他对视，感觉被一只戴泳镜的大眼怪盯上了。电池包的线还拖在兜里，两个小时就没电。他摘下来一看，自己还在五号线上，被挤成了沙丁鱼。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0541", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "nuclear-fusion", "target_name": "可控核聚变", "facts": ["Has been 'about 30 years away' since the 1950s; at the 1955 Geneva atoms-for-peace conference, Homi Bhabha predicted fusion power within two decades.", "ITER, agreed in 2006 for first plasma in 2016, re-baselined in 2024 to start research operations in 2034 and deuterium-tritium burning in 2039, at a cost estimated above €20 billion.", "NIF's December 2022 'ignition' shot released 3.15 MJ from 2.05 MJ of laser energy; the lasers drew about 300 MJ from the grid to fire.", "Private fusion companies had raised more than $7 billion by 2024."], "a": {"item_id": "203425c81d14", "text": "\"可控核聚变还有十年\"，这句话我听了三十年，每次都是十年。干这行的是一群想把太阳关进罐子里的人，目标很朴素：让一亿度的等离子体乖乖悬在磁场里，别碰墙，别熄火。图什么？图经费，图论文，图退休前在新闻里看见自己的名字。结果呢？年年开会，年年说\"快了\"，PPT翻到第四十页，二〇三〇悄悄变成了二〇四〇。有个老教授等了一辈子，临退休还在改时间表，他说：\"太阳烧了四十亿年不用拨款，我们想关它一下，先得给罐子要钱。\""}, "b": {"item_id": "46363a503661", "text": "“可控核聚变还有十年”，这句话是物理学界的传家宝。五十年代爷爷说，八十年代儿子说，现在孙子接着说，一个字都没改，比族谱还准。\n\n你以为他们在预测未来？不，他们在报经费周期。说三年，到期交不了差；说五十年，领导听完直接去开下一个会；说十年，刚好够干到退休，然后把这句话连同办公室钥匙一起交给学生。\n\n最惨的是什么？他们真把等离子体烧到了一亿度，比太阳核心还热好几倍，撑了几十秒，开发布会报喜。可他们自己的职称，在同一个岗位上冻了二十年。\n\n全人类只有这群人能造出一个小太阳，却暖不了自己的工资条。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0542", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "luosifen", "target_name": "螺蛳粉", "facts": ["A rice-noodle dish from Liuzhou, Guangxi; the smell comes from pickled bamboo shoots (酸笋), not from the river snails, which only flavour the broth.", "The instant version boomed during the 2020 lockdowns; Liuzhou's government reported packaged luosifen sales of over 10 billion yuan that year.", "Added to China's national intangible cultural heritage list in 2021.", "Liuzhou opened a luosifen industry college in 2020 to train workers for the trade."], "a": {"item_id": "5d0789c353d9", "text": "合租房里煮螺蛳粉的人，我太懂了。他们总说：就煮一包，不碍事。图什么？图锅里飘着腐竹和木耳，图那口酸笋上头的快感，图自己在厨房吸溜得像个美食博主。结果呢？酸笋味顺着抽油烟机往外跑，跑不掉的全钻进了室友的被窝。第二天群里弹出一句：\"谁煮的螺蛳粉？\"没人认，但全屋人心里都有数。厨房门上很快贴了张纸，写着\"禁止螺蛳粉\"，他的押金被扣了两百，理由是\"除味费\"。最绝的是那个空料包，他打了三个结扔进垃圾桶，第二天整个单元楼的人都知道他吃了什么。"}, "b": {"item_id": "2bf0b387fb70", "text": "合租房里煮螺蛳粉的，基本不是柳州人。柳州人懂分寸，在自己家吃。在合租房里煮的，都是刚入教的新信徒：一买就是一整箱袋装，每回还要额外多撕一包酸笋，说“原装的不够劲”。哥们，原装的已经够把燃气报警器熏醒了！\n\n你问他图什么，他永远就一句：“闻着臭，吃着香。”问题是吃的是你，闻的是我们仨。香你独享，臭我们均摊。这叫什么？这叫螺蛳粉式共享经济。\n\n最惨的还是他自己。吃了半年，鼻子早被酸笋腌入味了，啥也闻不出来。第二天去公司开会，一进门领导就抬头：“谁在会议室吃螺蛳粉？”他还跟着一起东张西望地找。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0543", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "ugg", "target_name": "UGG 雪地靴", "facts": ["Sheepskin boots Australian surfers wore to warm up after sessions; Brian Smith started selling them in California in 1978.", "Deckers Outdoor bought the brand in 1995 and holds 'UGG' as a trademark in the US and elsewhere; in Australia, 'ugg' is a generic word any bootmaker may use.", "In 2019, a US jury found an Australian maker liable for selling boots labelled 'ugg' into the United States.", "Oprah Winfrey featured them in her 'Favorite Things' lists repeatedly in the 2000s, which drove the first boom.", "UGG is Deckers' largest brand, with over $2 billion in annual sales."], "a": {"item_id": "0ef8b911f7d9", "text": "我表哥一年四季穿UGG雪地靴。夏天三十五度，他穿着那双栗色的羊皮内里靴挤地铁，脚底跟焖在蒸笼里一样。我问他不热吗，他说这是态度。我说态度能当空调用？他说UGG要的就是这份松弛感。结果今年夏天，他那双靴子的羊毛内里全板结了，一股酸味，地铁上的人自动给他让出三米。他图的是别人觉得他不怕冷，结果大家只觉得他不怕臭。"}, "b": {"item_id": "291bb4a33621", "text": "一年四季穿UGG的人，你仔细看，基本是同一种人：早上说“我就下楼拿个快递”，结果一路拿到了公司。\n\n问他图什么，他说：“不用系鞋带，一脚蹬。”对，UGG不是穿，是给脚办入住。\n\n夏天三十八度他还穿，问就是“里面是羊毛，透气”。对，羊也这么说，然后被剃了。\n\n下场特别统一。一场雨，一千多的靴子泡成两块发面馒头。到了办公室一脱鞋，羊毛负责把你全年的经历保存下来，同事都不用问你中午吃了啥。\n\n最惨的是冬天。你坚持了三季，终于等到主场，结果满大街都在穿，你的“个性”直接变成了“合群”。\n\n你说你这一年图什么？图了个脚，被焖成了刚出锅的馒头。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0544", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "monopoly", "target_name": "大富翁（Monopoly）", "facts": ["Descends from Lizzie Magie's The Landlord's Game, patented in 1904 to teach the evils of land monopoly in the spirit of Henry George.", "Charles Darrow sold his version to Parker Brothers in 1935 and was credited as the inventor; Parker bought Magie's patent for $500.", "The official rules require any unbought property to be auctioned, and 'Free Parking' pays nothing; the house rules that skip the auction and pile cash on Free Parking are why games drag.", "During the Second World War, British intelligence had maps, compasses, and real currency hidden in sets sent to prisoner-of-war camps."], "a": {"item_id": "d983ef681d05", "text": "我们家过年必玩大富翁，说是联络感情，其实是全家一起验证：血缘关系，扛不住房地产。\n\n我爸每次抢着当银行，他不是想为大家服务，他是想体验一下贪污。我妈只买橙色那一片，说吉利，结果整局靠收我的租活着。收的时候还特温柔：“妈妈收你的钱，你不会生气吧？”我说不会。她说：“那再加五十。”\n\n我九岁的表弟哭着求我把铁路卖给他，我心一软卖了。三圈以后，他拿着我的铁路，把我送进了监狱。\n\n我走投无路，找我爸借钱，他说：“行，按银行利率。”我说你是我爸！他说：“现在我是银行。”\n\n玩到凌晨，我爸有全部酒店，我妈有全部橙色，我手里只剩一张出狱卡。\n\n全家人公平分配了战果：他们赢了钱，我赢了离开这张桌子的权利。"}, "b": {"item_id": "f719efc18290", "text": "每年过年，总有亲戚提议：“一家人玩个大富翁，热闹热闹。”热闹？这游戏的胜利条件是：让你亲妈破产。\n\n图什么？平时不好意思跟你爸算的账，全在这块板上算了。我爸一辈子没买过房，一摸骰子就开始囤地，一条街全拿下，盖满酒店，等我路过，笑得像个开发商。我妈当银行，银行行长自己也下场玩，这在现实里叫什么？叫进去。\n\n我舅连着三轮进监狱，出来第一句：“谁欠我过路费？”\n\n玩到凌晨一点，没人赢，全在抵押。最后我弟把桌子一掀，地契满天飞。第二天早饭，没人说话。团圆了，账还没算完。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0545", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "emacs", "target_name": "Emacs", "facts": ["Began in 1976 as a set of macros for the TECO editor at MIT's AI Lab, written by Richard Stallman and others; GNU Emacs followed in 1985.", "Extensible in its own Lisp dialect, Emacs Lisp; the standing joke is that it is a fine operating system lacking only a good editor.", "Ships with a psychotherapist (M-x doctor), Tetris, a mail client, a calendar, and a web browser (eww).", "An old backronym is 'Eight Megabytes And Constantly Swapping', from when 8 MB was a lot of memory.", "'Emacs pinky' is the name for the hand strain blamed on its Control-key chords."], "a": {"item_id": "45d1386d9957", "text": "我有个朋友，把Emacs当操作系统用。邮件用Gnus，上网用eww，终端开eshell，连窗口管理器都换成了EXWM。我问他图什么，他说：“图一辈子不用离开Emacs。”兄弟，这不叫生活，这叫软禁。他的init.el写了四千行，比毕业论文还长，每天上班第一件事，是修昨晚改坏的配置。一年下来，他就产出了俩东西：一个能用M-x点外卖的配置，一根被Ctrl键按废的小拇指。上礼拜Emacs一崩，他当场断网、断邮件、断社交，就像自己盖的房子塌了，人还埋在里头。现在他想开了，准备装Evil-mode改用Vim键位。好家伙，操作系统是齐了，就缺一个好用的编辑器。"}, "b": {"item_id": "edb43822f3d2", "text": "我有个朋友，把 Emacs 当操作系统用。邮件在里面收，网页在里面看，连聊天都在里面聊。我问他图啥，他说：“不用切窗口，专注。”专注？他的 init.el 五千行，每个周末固定修配置，修的是上周为了省事写的配置。最绝的是，他最后装了 evil-mode，就是在 Emacs 里模拟 Vim 的按键。折腾十年，把全世界装进 Emacs，最后为了好用，把宿敌请了回来。现在他小指已经废了，他管这叫“Emacs 小指”，语气跟领了勋章似的。我说哥们，你这不是在用操作系统，是在给操作系统打工，还是不发工资的那种。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0546", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "excel", "target_name": "Microsoft Excel", "facts": ["First shipped for the Macintosh in 1985; the Windows version followed in 1987.", "Silently converts gene names such as SEPT2 and MARCH1 into dates; by August 2020 the HUGO Gene Nomenclature Committee had renamed 27 human genes (MARCH1 became MARCHF1, SEPT2 became SEPTIN2) and published guidelines in Nature Genetics to stop it.", "In October 2020, Public Health England lost 15,841 COVID-19 cases when a legacy .xls file hit its 65,536-row limit.", "Reinhart and Rogoff's 2010 paper 'Growth in a Time of Debt', cited for austerity policies, omitted five countries through a spreadsheet range error found by a graduate student in 2013.", "Sheets have been capped at 1,048,576 rows by 16,384 columns since Excel 2007."], "a": {"item_id": "18732d8f011b", "text": "我们老板管公司就靠一个Excel，文件名叫“总表_最终版_改3_千万别动.xlsx”。他图什么？图省钱，图“一切尽在掌握”。掌握到什么程度？他一打开，全公司点进去都弹“文件已锁定，只读”。\n\n他以为自己在经营企业，其实他在养一个VLOOKUP。财务、库存、工资，全靠第三列去匹配第七列。上个月实习生手欠插了一列，全公司工资齐刷刷变成#N/A，那是我们第一次见识零元购。\n\n最惨的是，会写那串套了十一层IF的会计离职了。老板对着那行公式看了三天，像考古队在研究甲骨文。现在公司还活着，但谁都不敢点“排序”。"}, "b": {"item_id": "7557f0ed30ba", "text": "见过拿Excel管整家公司的老板。问他怎么不上个系统，他说：\"Excel挺好用的。\"是挺好用，好用到库存、工资、客户全塞一张表里，VLOOKUP嵌了七层。这哪是做表，这是写遗嘱。\n\n图什么？图不用学新东西。结果为了不学新东西，公司专门养了个人来学这张表。刘姐，没职位，大家只说\"问刘姐\"。她请一天假，公司就瘫了，因为只有她知道D列不能动，一动，#REF!就像瘟疫一样往下传。\n\n下场更惨。上周我想打开订单表，弹窗：\"文件正在被张伟使用。\"张伟三年前就离职了。现在公司发工资，得等一个幽灵把文件保存关掉。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0547", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "monopoly", "target_name": "大富翁（Monopoly）", "facts": ["Descends from Lizzie Magie's The Landlord's Game, patented in 1904 to teach the evils of land monopoly in the spirit of Henry George.", "Charles Darrow sold his version to Parker Brothers in 1935 and was credited as the inventor; Parker bought Magie's patent for $500.", "The official rules require any unbought property to be auctioned, and 'Free Parking' pays nothing; the house rules that skip the auction and pile cash on Free Parking are why games drag.", "During the Second World War, British intelligence had maps, compasses, and real currency hidden in sets sent to prisoner-of-war camps."], "a": {"item_id": "a7faf659e2b2", "text": "每年过年，总有一个人提议：“一家人玩个大富翁，热闹。”你想热闹？你想看你爸抢着当银行，发钱的时候顺手往自己那堆塞两张五百？你想看你妈踩了你的酒店，说“都是一家人，房租就免了”，轮到你踩她的南京路，她把地契往桌上一拍：“亲兄弟明算账。”你小姨夫拿两条破铁路换你的地，那个眼神，跟当年找你爸借三万块一模一样。玩到凌晨两点没人破产，因为奶奶偷偷往各家塞钱：“孩子进监狱了，快拿钱捞出来。”最后桌子一掀，三家人一年不说话。你图团圆？大富翁教会你一件事：一家人能坐一桌吃饭，是因为桌上没有地契。"}, "b": {"item_id": "9bfb2dd2da6e", "text": "每年过年，我家都要玩一局大富翁。说好了增进感情，结果每次都是战争。我舅非要当银行家，抽屉一拉，像在管国库，你借两百块买块地，他能跟你讲半小时利率。我爸图的是赢。他说这叫培养孩子的商业头脑，其实是六十岁了还在抢我十二岁妹妹的绿色地皮。我妈最绝，从不买地，专坐在\"免费停车\"那格收钱，说这叫稳健。最后我姐在紫色街区盖满酒店，我爸一脚踩上去，房租交得他当场抵押了电视机。我舅把银行的钱全押在自己身上，宣布：\"我破产了，但我是清白的。\"那局没人赢，大家都进了监狱。过年那顿饭，全家坐一桌，谁也不说话，桌上摆着一堆假钞。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0548", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "comic-sans", "target_name": "Comic Sans 字体", "facts": ["Designed by Vincent Connare at Microsoft in 1994, modeled on the comic-book lettering in The Dark Knight Returns and Watchmen.", "Made for the speech bubbles of Rover, the cartoon dog in Microsoft Bob; it missed Bob's release and shipped instead in the Windows 95 Plus! pack.", "CERN's July 2012 Higgs boson discovery announcement was presented on slides set in it.", "The 'Ban Comic Sans' campaign, started by designers Dave and Holly Combs in 1999, ran until 2019, when they retired it and launched usecomicsans.com."], "a": {"item_id": "130c2658ebda", "text": "我们小区物业有个行政大姐，发正式通知特别爱用Comic Sans。你知道这字体当年是干嘛的吗？微软设计给卡通小狗说话用的，专门往对话气泡里塞的。所以每回停水通知一贴，我都觉得是一只小狗在跟我说：“明天停水哦～”\n\n她图什么？图亲切。坏消息配这字体，就跟打针前先给你看张奥特曼贴纸一样。\n\n上个月涨物业费，她又用Comic Sans，那个“8”圆得像在冲你眨眼。全楼没一个当真，都以为是谁家小孩的恶作剧，撕下来折纸飞机了。年底一查，欠费率百分之八十。\n\n领导问她怎么回事，她理直气壮：“欧洲核子研究中心宣布发现希格斯玻色子都用这字体！”\n\n领导说：“对，所以全世界到现在还在笑他们。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "5f56efde52fb", "text": "你们有没有收到过这种通知？标题是《关于本周五停电的通知》，字体是Comic Sans。我一看，以为是小区要办儿童节派对，结果是停水停电，还要求业主自带水桶。\n\n干这事的人我大概知道是谁，物业行政，或者公司HR。他们想表达的是\"我们很亲切，咱们是一家人\"。结果字一出来，就像在给你发一张蜡笔画的请柬。\n\n这字体本来是微软1994年给一个卡通式聊天软件Microsoft Bob做的，用在漫画气泡里的。一份要你立即整改的文件，最后看上去像是从对话框里蹦出来的。\n\n然后呢？全公司把它截图发进群，配文：\"这是谁的生日会通知？\"领导第二天在会上说要严肃点，底下大家一边点头，一边憋笑。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0549", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "juicero", "target_name": "Juicero 榨汁机", "facts": ["A Wi-Fi-connected cold-press juicer launched in 2016 at $699, later cut to $399, that squeezed proprietary bags of chopped produce sold by subscription.", "Raised about $120 million from investors including Google Ventures and Kleiner Perkins.", "In April 2017, Bloomberg reporters squeezed the packs by hand and got 7.5 ounces of juice in 90 seconds, faster than the machine's 8 ounces in about two minutes.", "Each pack carried a QR code that the press scanned and checked online before squeezing; it refused expired packs and would not run at all unless connected to Wi-Fi.", "Shut down in September 2017 and offered refunds on the machine."], "a": {"item_id": "2a181ea7d6cd", "text": "我花四百美金买了一台 Juicero。它的工作原理是：把预先切好的果蔬包塞进去，机器扫码确认是正版，然后用很大的压力把汁挤出来。后来有记者发现，那包东西用手就能捏出汁，不用扫码，也不用机器。所以我花四百块买的，其实是一副需要联网验证的手套。\n\n做这个的人我真想见见。一帮硅谷的人觉得榨汁缺一个\"数字化入口\"，拿了一亿多美元风投，就为了让你每周给一袋胡萝卜付订阅费。结果公司关门，机器打折甩卖。我那台现在是家里最贵的摆件，每次看到它，我都觉得它在等我扫码。"}, "b": {"item_id": "90365272a695", "text": "花400美元买Juicero的都是什么人？就是那种觉得“我自己的手不够智能”的人。你买的根本不是榨汁机，是一台连WiFi的拳头。它这辈子唯一的工作，就是捏一包早就榨好的菜泥。彭博社记者徒手去捏，比它还快。想想那画面：硅谷精英每天早上站在厨房，等机器扫完二维码、联网确认这包菜没过期，它才肯开工。为了“健康生活”，每包再掏五六块，喝的其实是自己的智商汁。后来公司倒闭，机器连不上服务器，成了一块四百刀的案板。唯一还能用的，是你那双手。恭喜，绕了一大圈，你终于恢复了出厂设置。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0550", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "microsoft-teams", "target_name": "Microsoft Teams", "facts": ["Launched in 2017, bundled at no extra charge into Office 365, and replaced Skype for Business.", "After Slack's antitrust complaint, Microsoft unbundled Teams from Office in the EU in 2023 and worldwide in 2024; the European Commission still sent a statement of objections in 2024.", "Microsoft reported 320 million monthly active users in 2023.", "Microsoft shut down consumer Skype in May 2025 and sent its users to Teams."], "a": {"item_id": "0c5f29fdb1c7", "text": "咱公司强推 Teams 的那位，我打听了，是采购。理由特别感人：“Office 365 套餐里本来就带着，不用另外花钱。”省了多少？年终述职写着呢：降本增效，三十万。\n\n三十万省下来了，然后呢？全公司每天问他同一个问题：“我昨天发的文件在哪？”他说：“Teams 里，‘文件’标签页。”其实在 SharePoint 里。他自己也是上周才知道 SharePoint 是什么。\n\n最惨的是，他在 Teams 里建了一个“团队”，团队里又建了一个“团队”，最后这个团队里只有他一个人。\n\n我们都在微信群里，聊他。"}, "b": {"item_id": "04d98c0d62fd", "text": "我们公司换Teams，拍板的是IT总监老王。为什么选它？不是因为好用，是因为它“白送”，买Office 365附赠的。结果全公司天天在里面找文件：发在聊天里的跑进了OneDrive，发在频道里的又钻进了SharePoint。老王开会说“我共享一下屏幕”，然后整个部门盯着他的桌面看了五分钟。\n\n后来呢？欧盟说微软捆绑销售，Teams得拆开单买了。老王“白送”的东西开始收钱，他自己还天天被标成黄色“离开”，五分钟不动鼠标就算离开。现在他桌上插着个自动晃鼠标的小盒子。全公司最勤奋的那个绿点，是一块在原地抽搐的塑料。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0551", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "graphene", "target_name": "石墨烯", "facts": ["Isolated in 2004 by Andre Geim and Konstantin Novoselov at Manchester by peeling graphite with Scotch tape; Nobel Prize in Physics, 2010.", "The EU's Graphene Flagship, launched in 2013, committed €1 billion over ten years.", "A 2018 study in Advanced Materials tested 'graphene' from 60 producers and found none with more than 50% graphene content; most was graphite flakes.", "Sold in tennis rackets, running shoes, and headphones as a performance ingredient.", "Geim also won an Ig Nobel Prize in 2000 for levitating a frog with magnets."], "a": {"item_id": "e2b2b3137823", "text": "我表哥2013年起逢人就说，石墨烯要改变世界。理由是：一层原子厚，比钢硬两百倍，诺贝尔奖就是拿胶带从铅笔芯上粘出来的。他听完热血沸腾，当场把攒的二十万全买了石墨烯概念股。你看，人家诺奖是粘下来的，他是被粘住了。\n\n十年过去，世界没变，他变了：从\"石墨烯取代硅\"，变成\"石墨烯发热内衣，第二件半价\"。股票被ST，库存剩三百件内衣，现在全家一到秋冬，都在替石墨烯做临床试验。\n\n最惨的是他还跟我说\"再等十年\"。\n\n哥，这材料最牛的地方，是只剩一层原子也扛得住。你呢？被剥了十年，也就剩一层了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "5c660764dbab", "text": "我有个表舅，逢人就说石墨烯要改变世界。他说这玩意儿就一个原子厚，比钢硬两百倍，导电导热全是第一。我问他：那你拿它干嘛了？他说：卖秋裤。石墨烯发热秋裤，一条六百八。\n\n表舅研究过，诺贝尔奖得主是拿透明胶带从铅笔芯上粘出来的。他一听，门槛不高啊，家里胶带有的是。二〇一五年，他把养老钱全押进了石墨烯概念股，从此真的改变了世界，先改变的是他自己的。\n\n现在他还在说。石墨烯什么都能干，就是出不了实验室；表舅什么都敢说，就是出不了我们家群。\n\n过年我问他，世界改变了吗？他把秋裤往上一拽：“你摸，热不热！”\n\n热。里头是电热丝。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0552", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "las-vegas", "target_name": "拉斯维加斯", "facts": ["Nevada legalized casino gambling in 1931, the year construction of the Hoover Dam began nearby.", "Most of the Strip lies not in the city of Las Vegas but in the unincorporated town of Paradise.", "The Sphere, opened in 2023 at a cost of about $2.3 billion, is the largest spherical building in the world.", "'What happens here, stays here' was launched by the local convention and visitors authority in 2003.", "Lake Mead, its water source, fell in July 2022 to about 1,040 feet, its lowest since the reservoir first filled in 1937, and dropped below that mark again in August 2026."], "a": {"item_id": "9c12f6e41e03", "text": "去拉斯维加斯发财的人，我见过三种。第一种是辞了职来的，行李箱一半是衣服，一半是对人生的最后指望。第二种是借钱来的，借的还是网贷，利息高得连老虎机的灯都嫉妒。第三种最可怕，他说就玩一小时，结果第二天中午你在自助餐厅找到他，正排第四趟帝王蟹腿，眼神像刚从战场上下来。外面四十度，他嫌出门热，就一直泡在赌场里。赢了几万，去兑换处，人家笑着说：您还有一张免费住宿券，欢迎下次光临。他拿着券上楼睡了一觉，醒来一看，钱没了，券还在，而且明天还有效。"}, "b": {"item_id": "bd02132185ac", "text": "我有个朋友，看了三遍《决胜21点》，觉得自己会算牌，买了张去拉斯维加斯的机票，说要去沙漠里挖金子。\n\n兄弟，人家能在沙漠里建一座城，靠的就是你这种来浇水的。\n\n他到了才发现，赌场里没窗户、没钟，酒还免费。你品，一个地方怕你知道几点、还主动把你灌醉，它是想让你赢吗？\n\n三天后他回来了，钱全没了，就剩一张自助餐券，外加一张小教堂的结婚证，证婚人是“猫王”，新娘是谁他想不起来。\n\n他还安慰自己：“在维加斯发生的，就留在维加斯。”\n\n对，你的钱也是这么想的。\n\n最绝的是候机的时候，他看见登机口旁边还有一排老虎机，又塞进去二十块。拉斯维加斯就是这样，连你走，都要再收一回过路费。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0553", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "keurig", "target_name": "Keurig 胶囊咖啡机", "facts": ["John Sylvan invented the K-Cup in the early 1990s and sold his stake for about $50,000 in 1997; in 2015 he told The Atlantic, 'I feel bad sometimes that I ever did it.'", "Keurig 2.0 (2014) read an ink mark on the lid and rejected unlicensed pods; sales fell and workarounds spread online.", "By 2014 enough K-Cups were sold each year to circle the Earth more than ten times; the pods were mostly unrecyclable #7 plastic until a switch to polypropylene by 2020.", "Keurig Green Mountain merged with Dr Pepper Snapple in 2018 to form Keurig Dr Pepper."], "a": {"item_id": "f6e06d9fe87e", "text": "用 Keurig 胶囊的人，我太懂了：嫌磨豆子麻烦，嫌滤纸麻烦，嫌洗壶麻烦，一心要“按一下就有”。\n\n结果呢？一颗胶囊一块多，折合一磅咖啡粉五六十块，这哪是喝咖啡，这是喝首饰。喝的还都是什么？榛果奶油、南瓜派、焦糖玛奇朵，咖啡味的香水。\n\n更绝的是，为了省两分钟磨豆子，每天花三分钟撕锡纸、倒渣、把那个小塑料杯分类回收。一年三百六十五个小杯子，像在给咖啡办葬礼。\n\n最后机器亮灯：“请除垢。”翻开说明书，让你拿白醋泡机器。图省事的人，最后蹲在厨房里，给一台咖啡机泡醋。"}, "b": {"item_id": "a23abc9807e4", "text": "用Keurig的人，就是想喝咖啡，但不想沾“做咖啡”的任何一个字。磨豆？累。烧水？累。你们的理想是按一下，然后盯着它嗡嗡响四十秒。这比烧壶水还久，但因为是按按钮，所以叫效率。\n\n算过账吗？一颗胶囊十克豆，折下来一斤两百多块。你付的是精品价，喝的是一根针扎穿锡纸挤出来的褐色热水。\n\n发明K杯那哥们儿自己都公开说后悔了，家里根本不用。发明人都退坑了，你们还在续费。\n\n最惨是那盒混合装，三个月后剩下的永远是那几颗榛子味。不喝，也不扔，就躺在抽屉里，跟你一样：方便，但没人要。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0554", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "php", "target_name": "PHP", "facts": ["Created by Rasmus Lerdorf in 1994 as 'Personal Home Page Tools', a set of C programs for tracking visitors to his own résumé page; the name now officially stands for the recursive 'PHP: Hypertext Preprocessor'.", "Lerdorf, in his 2007 MySQL Conference talk 'PHP on Hormones': 'I'm not a real programmer. I throw together things until it works then I move on.'", "Runs WordPress, which W3Techs counted on 39.4% of all websites (58.1% of sites with a known CMS) on 9 November 2026.", "Core function names are inconsistent (strpos, str_replace, htmlspecialchars, nl2br) partly because, as Lerdorf confirmed, early function names were chosen to balance a hash table keyed on string length.", "Until PHP 8 (2020), the comparison 0 == 'foo' was true because the string was silently converted to a number."], "a": {"item_id": "0e4fd995e750", "text": "写PHP的人，都是被\"好找活\"骗进来的。全世界四成网站是WordPress，意味着永远有活。就像殡仪馆，永远有生意，但没人羡慕。\n\n入行第一天，每个变量前面都得带个美元符号。你以为是兆头，结果是嘲讽：满屏的$，一分不进兜。\n\n接手的全是前任留下的，一个文件三千行，HTML里插SQL，SQL里插HTML，中间一行注释：\"别动，会炸。\"写注释的人早跑了，你接手的不是项目，是文物，还得原地保护。\n\n每年还有人宣布\"PHP已死\"，他们都练出来了，一边点头一边给来宾递茶。\n\n最惨的是记函数。in_array先写要找的针，strpos先写草堆。干了十年，每次还得去查文档。\n\n别人的职业生涯叫积累，PHP程序员的叫：十年了，我还在大海里捞针，而且先放哪个，我还得查。"}, "b": {"item_id": "e721ec283ce4", "text": "干PHP吃饭的人，我认识三类。第一类是接外包的，客户说\"就改个按钮颜色\"，他打开一看，代码里还留着 mysql_connect，注释是2009年的，他知道这活要干到天亮。第二类是背框架的，嘴里全是Laravel，自己的小网站却还是 $_GET 直接拼进SQL，问他为啥，他说\"这叫传统\"。第三类图的就是那句\"语法简单，上手快\"，结果干了十年，还在查 \"0\" == false 为什么是真的。去年他投简历，HR问他精通什么，他写\"PHP\"，面试官问 == 和 === 有啥区别，他沉默三秒，把\"精通\"两个字划掉了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0555", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "papyrus", "target_name": "Papyrus 字体", "facts": ["Hand-drawn by Chris Costello in 1982 over about six months, when he was 23; he sold the rights to Letraset for $750.", "Bundled with macOS and with Microsoft Office, which is why it appears on so many menus and signs.", "James Cameron's Avatar (2009) used it for the film's logo and subtitles.", "A 2017 Saturday Night Live sketch starring Ryan Gosling is about a man unable to get over that choice."], "a": {"item_id": "d6bcba0a659e", "text": "开养生馆的老板选招牌字体，预算四百，又想显得有五千年底蕴，于是选了Papyrus，就是那个边儿像被猫挠过、笔画细得像没吃饱的字。他的逻辑是：这字一看就古埃及，古埃及一看就神秘，神秘就能卖六百八的艾灸套餐。我说艾灸是中国的，跟尼罗河有什么关系？他说：“反正是古的。”\n\n结果隔壁新开了家埃及烤肉店，也用Papyrus。两块招牌并排一挂，顾客全懵了：有人进养生馆问有没有鹰嘴豆泥，有人进烤肉店问能不能刮痧。设计师路过也不进门，就站门口拍照发朋友圈，配三个字：“又一个。”\n\n老板花四百块，买了个跟《阿凡达》同款的“独一无二”。"}, "b": {"item_id": "7a35ef193141", "text": "最离谱的是开养生馆、开餐馆的老板，招牌非要用Papyrus。这字体像用芦苇纸手撕出来的，笔画边缘毛毛躁躁，像法老陵墓门口那块石碑。他们图什么？图\"有调性\"，图\"古法养生\"，让客人一进门就觉得有故事。结果阿凡达的海报用的就是这个字，顾客抬头一看，以为要去潘多拉星球做足疗。隔壁烧烤店跟着学，招牌一亮，大家都在吃木乃伊烤串。老板很委屈：\"我要的是高级感。\"设计师说：\"是您自己在字体列表里一路往下划，点的这个。\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0556", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "php", "target_name": "PHP", "facts": ["Created by Rasmus Lerdorf in 1994 as 'Personal Home Page Tools', a set of C programs for tracking visitors to his own résumé page; the name now officially stands for the recursive 'PHP: Hypertext Preprocessor'.", "Lerdorf, in his 2007 MySQL Conference talk 'PHP on Hormones': 'I'm not a real programmer. I throw together things until it works then I move on.'", "Runs WordPress, which W3Techs counted on 39.4% of all websites (58.1% of sites with a known CMS) on 9 November 2026.", "Core function names are inconsistent (strpos, str_replace, htmlspecialchars, nl2br) partly because, as Lerdorf confirmed, early function names were chosen to balance a hash table keyed on string length.", "Until PHP 8 (2020), the comparison 0 == 'foo' was true because the string was silently converted to a number."], "a": {"item_id": "e21dca174c4d", "text": "我有个朋友，靠写PHP吃饭。每次喝酒他都举杯：“PHP是世界上最好的语言！”全桌没人接话，因为全桌都是写PHP的。大家心里清楚，这是我们这行唯一能赢的一句台词。\n\n他图什么？图快。一个下午，织梦模板一套，宝塔面板一点，县城瓷砖城就有官网了。收三千，老板还嫌贵：“我侄子也会。”\n\n他说他天天跟钱打交道。确实，每个变量前面都挂着个美元符号。干了十年，那是他摸过最多的美元。\n\n现在他维护一套2009年的代码，mysql_query还在跑，他不敢动。一动，瓷砖城倒闭，他也失业。说白了，他跟那段代码是互相续命。"}, "b": {"item_id": "0e4fd995e750", "text": "写PHP的人，都是被\"好找活\"骗进来的。全世界四成网站是WordPress，意味着永远有活。就像殡仪馆，永远有生意，但没人羡慕。\n\n入行第一天，每个变量前面都得带个美元符号。你以为是兆头，结果是嘲讽：满屏的$，一分不进兜。\n\n接手的全是前任留下的，一个文件三千行，HTML里插SQL，SQL里插HTML，中间一行注释：\"别动，会炸。\"写注释的人早跑了，你接手的不是项目，是文物，还得原地保护。\n\n每年还有人宣布\"PHP已死\"，他们都练出来了，一边点头一边给来宾递茶。\n\n最惨的是记函数。in_array先写要找的针，strpos先写草堆。干了十年，每次还得去查文档。\n\n别人的职业生涯叫积累，PHP程序员的叫：十年了，我还在大海里捞针，而且先放哪个，我还得查。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0557", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "slack", "target_name": "Slack", "facts": ["Spun out in 2013 from the internal chat tool of Tiny Speck's failed game Glitch; founder Stewart Butterfield had earlier spun Flickr out of another failed game.", "The name is a backronym: 'Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge'.", "Salesforce bought it for $27.7 billion in 2021.", "Filed an EU antitrust complaint against Microsoft in 2020 over the bundling of Teams with Office."], "a": {"item_id": "d768d460ddec", "text": "我见过一种老板，叫“Slack治国派”。创业第一天就宣布：咱们不发邮件，一切公开，扁平！三年后，公司一百人，四百个频道。#general没人敢说话，#random全是老板转发的文章。他最爱@channel，凌晨一点全公司一百部手机一起震，内容是：“大家觉得新logo咋样？”决策靠表情投票：三个👍，一个👀，通过。没人知道👀啥意思，包括发👀的人。\n\n最惨的是他自己。头像旁边那个绿点，他不敢让它灭，怕员工觉得他不在；员工也不敢让它灭，怕他觉得员工不在。全公司互相盯着一个绿点上班。\n\n他确实消灭了邮件。现在他想找一个决定，得先问一句：“这事是在哪个thread里定的？”"}, "b": {"item_id": "b9535814ba64", "text": "有种老板，花三个月把公司搬到 Slack 上，说要\"扁平化、透明化、告别会议\"。结果建了四十七个频道：#general 没人说话，#random 全是老板自己转的领英鸡汤，#wins 里常年只有一条消息：\"我们建了 #wins\"。\n\n会议确实少了，因为全改成 huddle 了。那个小耳机图标一亮，意思是\"我们在聊，你怎么还不进来\"。\n\n员工回老板消息，只剩一个👀。老板还挺感动：\"看，大家都在关注。\"\n\n最绝的是他舍不得买付费版。九十天前的消息自动隐藏，上季度那个五百万的决定是谁拍的板，没人查得到。\n\n公司最后留下的唯一透明，是那行字：\"升级到专业版，查看更早的消息。\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0558", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "macau", "target_name": "澳门", "facts": ["A Portuguese settlement from the 1550s until the handover to China in December 1999.", "Casino revenue passed the Las Vegas Strip's in 2006, and at its 2013 peak, about $45 billion, was roughly seven times the Strip's.", "One company, STDM, held the casino monopoly for four decades until licences opened to competition in 2002.", "Among the most densely populated places on Earth, at roughly 20,000 people per square kilometre.", "The Cotai Strip stands on land reclaimed from the sea between the islands of Taipa and Coloane."], "a": {"item_id": "be44032450bd", "text": "我特别佩服那种说\"我去澳门翻本\"的人。股票亏了三十万，他的方案是：去澳门，一晚上，赢回来。你想想，三十万是怎么亏的？靠判断力。现在他拿同一套判断力，去一个没有钟的地方翻本。\n\n到了威尼斯人，里面的天永远是下午四点，假的，就是让你觉得还早，还来得及。结果三十万变六十万，反方向的。出来一看，赌场对面就是当铺，灯亮着，贴心得像医院门口开殡仪馆。表当了，金链子当了，最后走回关闸，全部家当一个猪扒包。\n\n回来跟我说：\"差一点就翻了。\"哥，你差的不是一点，你差的是那三十万，加上另外三十万，再加上你那块表。"}, "b": {"item_id": "49f31f62d85b", "text": "去澳门翻本的人，我太懂了。前一晚借了三千块，第二天凌晨五点坐大巴，车上睡得比谁都香，他说今天是转运日。到了威尼斯人，水晶吊灯晃得他眼花，他说这地方风水好。风水好？这地方是把你的钱吸进去，再把你的时间吸出来。他图什么？图一把翻身，图回家能在群里发一句\"又赢了\"。结果早上赢了两万，中午输光，下午去找人借钱，晚上在金光大道上抽烟，手机里老婆的未接来电排到了第九个。大巴司机喊上车，他拎着一袋葡式蛋挞走上去。全场唯一赢回来的，就是这袋还热着的蛋挞。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0559", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hydrogen-cars", "target_name": "氢燃料电池汽车", "facts": ["Toyota launched the Mirai in 2014; 'mirai' means 'future' in Japanese.", "Nearly all US hydrogen filling stations are in California; Shell closed all seven of its California passenger-car stations in February 2024.", "More than 95% of the world's hydrogen is made from fossil fuels, mostly by steam-reforming natural gas.", "California pump prices reached about $36 per kilogram in 2023-24, over $200 for a Mirai fill; Toyota has handed buyers fuel cards worth $15,000.", "Mirai owners filed a class action against Toyota in July 2024 over hydrogen shortages and station outages; a second suit in 2025 sought at least $5.7 billion."], "a": {"item_id": "6147a8dd376d", "text": "买氢燃料电池车的人，我研究过，就两种：一种是环保人士，一种是被送了三年氢气卡的。\n\n环保人士图什么？图尾管只排水。可那氢多半是拿天然气炼的，他花四十块一公斤，绕一大圈烧天然气，最后从屁股后面滴出一杯水，像在表演“我很干净”。\n\n下场更精彩：全城三个加氢站，一个周二检修，一个在郊区，一个前面排着三辆公交。他的日常就是刷加氢站App，像抢演唱会门票。抢到了，还得等储氢罐压力回上来。\n\n最后氢气卡到期那天，车一秒贬值一半。他挂二手车，文案写得特别诚恳：“车况良好，自带情怀，不含氢气。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "a7c3aeb313ce", "text": "我朋友非要买氢燃料电池车，说排出来的是水，环保。我说对，排的是水，你的钱也是一滴一滴没的，看不见。\n\n买这车的人分两种，一种是科技宅，觉得自己活在电影里；另一种是单位采购，花的是补贴，车是别人的。他图什么？图那句\"我开的是未来\"。结果未来在哪？在郊区一座加氢站，得提前预约，排队排到怀疑人生。站一关门，车就成了铁疙瘩，电堆里那点铂催化剂，比他的存款还金贵。\n\n最绝的是氢分子最小，能从70兆帕的罐子里漏出去。他的工资也一样，还没捂热，就从卡里漏没了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0560", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "chicago-deep-dish", "target_name": "芝加哥深盘披萨", "facts": ["Created at Pizzeria Uno in Chicago in 1943; credit is disputed among founder Ike Sewell, his partner Ric Riccardo, and cook Rudy Malnati.", "Assembled upside down: sliced mozzarella on the dough, then toppings, then crushed tomatoes on top, so the cheese does not scorch during the long bake.", "Jon Stewart called it 'tomato soup in a bread bowl' and 'a casserole' on The Daily Show in 2013.", "Chicagoans' everyday pizza is thin 'tavern-style' cut into squares; GrubHub order data reported by WBEZ in 2013 showed city residents ordering non-deep-dish pizza about 10 to 1 over deep dish, with deep dish and stuffed making up under 9% of orders."], "a": {"item_id": "31f74171df4b", "text": "总有人非要管芝加哥深盘叫披萨。这帮人是谁？就是去芝加哥待过三天、发过一张切面照的人，回来就成了\"披萨鉴赏家\"。图什么？图朋友圈里那句\"正宗美式披萨\"。结果端上来一口黑铁锅似的东西，番茄酱糊在最上面，芝士全锁在底下，等了四十分钟，用刀叉一戳，热气里拉出半米长的芝士丝。他边切边说\"这披萨真地道\"，旁边的芝加哥老哥头都没抬：\"兄弟，这叫pie，馅饼。\"他不服，掏手机去搜，第一条就是本地人的回答：披萨能用手拿着吃，这玩意你得先找把叉子。"}, "b": {"item_id": "e091429db8dc", "text": "管芝加哥深盘叫“披萨”的人，主要就两种：芝加哥本地人，和刚在Giordano's门口排了一小时队的游客。\n\n本地人图什么？面子。你们那儿冬天零下二十度，总得有样东西能拿出去跟纽约吵架吧。\n\n游客图什么？沉没成本。排队一小时，再等四十五分钟烤，端上来番茄酱在最上面，奶酪压在底下，四周一圈黄油面墙。你不叫它披萨，就得承认自己花三十八刀排队吃了一锅砂锅。\n\n下场呢？拿刀叉吃“披萨”，一块就饱，剩下的打包，回酒店一躺，消化得比烤得还久。第二天照样发朋友圈：“正宗芝加哥披萨！”配图一看，一个蛋糕模具。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0561", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "monopoly", "target_name": "大富翁（Monopoly）", "facts": ["Descends from Lizzie Magie's The Landlord's Game, patented in 1904 to teach the evils of land monopoly in the spirit of Henry George.", "Charles Darrow sold his version to Parker Brothers in 1935 and was credited as the inventor; Parker bought Magie's patent for $500.", "The official rules require any unbought property to be auctioned, and 'Free Parking' pays nothing; the house rules that skip the auction and pile cash on Free Parking are why games drag.", "During the Second World War, British intelligence had maps, compasses, and real currency hidden in sets sent to prisoner-of-war camps."], "a": {"item_id": "a8f6340d1edd", "text": "过年一家人想图个团圆，掏出大富翁。这游戏设计得特别科学：专门把亲情换算成租金。\n\n我爸永远当银行，所以他既是央行又是裁判，还偷偷往自己那堆里放钱。我妈见一块买一块，一块不卖，说“留着传给你”，我说妈，这是纸。我弟更狠，四个火车站全买了，我每路过一次就交一次钱，对亲姐姐收费收得比春运还顺手。\n\n我走到他的酒店，一千五。我说我是你姐，他说：“那给你打个折，一千四。”\n\n最后我抵押了公用事业，进了监狱，全家居然都希望我多待几轮，因为我在里面不用交租。\n\n图的是团圆，落得的是游戏还没破产，亲戚关系先清算了。\n\n我一掀桌，我爸很冷静：“重来一局。还是我当银行。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "7f9957efd2c9", "text": "过年我舅非要拉全家玩大富翁，说就玩一把。我告诉你，这一把从吃完饺子玩到天亮。我舅抢着当银行，发钱的时候笑得比真银行还真。轮到他自己，橙色那条街先买下，盖三栋房子，再升级成旅馆。我表姐踩上去交了一大笔过路费，脸比她手里的绿房子还绿。我爸更惨，一圈转到监狱，他说我就路过。人家说那叫Just Visiting，你是进去了。没钱交保释金，只能眼睁睁看着我舅把他的地一块块收走。最后免费停车场那堆钱全被我舅拿走，他抱着钱说：这就是亲情。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0562", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "brasilia", "target_name": "巴西利亚", "facts": ["Inaugurated on 21 April 1960 after about 41 months of construction, moving the capital inland from Rio de Janeiro.", "Lúcio Costa's Plano Piloto is laid out like an airplane (or a bird, or a cross), with Oscar Niemeyer's government buildings along the fuselage.", "Listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1987, less than 30 years after it was built.", "Planned for 500,000 residents; the Federal District now has about 3 million, most in satellite towns outside the plan.", "Built for the car: wide avenues, few pedestrian crossings, and distances that make walking impractical."], "a": {"item_id": "879d5b880e2f", "text": "在巴西利亚靠两条腿出门的，就三种人：刚考上公务员、工资还没到账的；捧着建筑系课本来朝圣尼迈耶的；还有一种，是前两种走到一半的样子。\n\n他们图什么？图“城市漫步”。可这城是照着飞机画的，你走路，等于在机翼上找人行道。地图上南翼308区和309区挨着，中间隔着一条六车道。想过去？去找那种叫“小剪刀”的立交，绕一圈下来，你从南翼走出了北翼的心情。\n\n旱季湿度百分之十，太阳毒得像在三权广场受审。认出他们很简单：草坪上那条被踩秃的土路，那不是小道，那是他们集体签的字。\n\n下场？第二个月，贷款买了辆二手菲亚特。"}, "b": {"item_id": "326fa522889c", "text": "在巴西利亚靠两条腿出门的人，我得给他们立个碑。这帮人是谁？多半是刚搬来的新人，手机一打开，地图写着\"步行二十八分钟\"，他们就信了。图什么？图锻炼，图省钱，图不想等那趟十四分钟一班、还不一定来的公交。结果呢？整条Eixo Monumental上，人行道说没就没，只剩一条给卡车跑的高速路，他们只能贴着护栏，顶着尾气走。好不容易走到立交桥底下想抄近路，四个车道，红灯十秒。最后他站在W3大道中间那条绿化带上，左边是车，右边也是车，低头看手机，地图淡定地提示：\"您已到达目的地。\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0563", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "windows-vista", "target_name": "Windows Vista", "facts": ["Released to consumers in January 2007, after the 'Longhorn' project was reset in 2004 and headline features such as the WinFS file system were dropped.", "User Account Control prompted so often that Apple's 'Get a Mac' campaign ran an ad about 'Cancel or Allow'.", "'Windows Vista Capable' stickers went on PCs that could only run Home Basic, without the Aero interface, and led to a class-action lawsuit.", "Windows 7 (2009), widely praised, was largely a tuned-up Vista on the same kernel line; Vista support ended in April 2017."], "a": {"item_id": "d1b5347e5ac6", "text": "第一天就升级 Vista 的人，我研究过，都是那种等不及第一个补丁的人。他们图什么？图那个毛玻璃窗口。Aero，半透明的，能隐约看到后面的桌面，就像前任的 QQ 空间，什么都看不清，但就是忍不住盯着。\n\n结果装完发现：打印机没驱动，声卡没驱动，连他那个二手摄像头也认不出他了。电脑配置 1G 内存，风扇转得像要起飞，机身还贴着“Vista Ready”的贴纸，Ready 的是风扇。\n\n最惨的是那个弹窗：“Windows 需要您的许可才能继续。”他想删个文件，系统问他：“你确定吗？”他点“允许”。系统又问：“你真的确定吗？”他再点“允许”。这哪是操作系统，这是我妈。\n\n三天后，他的 MSN 签名改了：“Vista 用户，求 XP 安装盘，在线等，挺急的。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "62ac4958dd62", "text": "有一种人，办公室里第一个喊\"我先装\"的，Vista一出来当天就升级。图什么？图的是半透明的Aero玻璃窗。结果开机六分钟，点开一个文件夹，弹窗问他\"您确定要打开吗\"，他点确定，又弹一个\"您真的确定吗\"。一天点了三百次确定，手指比他的工资还累。声卡没驱动，打印机没驱动，连鼠标滚轮都不认。最后他翻出XP安装盘，格式化重装，还在朋友圈发：我终于懂了什么叫成熟的系统。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0564", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "javascript", "target_name": "JavaScript", "facts": ["Written by Brendan Eich at Netscape in ten days in May 1995; named Mocha, then LiveScript, then JavaScript to ride Java's marketing, although the two are unrelated.", "typeof null returns 'object', a bug from the first version that has been kept for compatibility ever since.", "In March 2016 the author of left-pad, an 11-line npm package, unpublished it and broke builds across the industry, including Babel and React.", "[] + {} evaluates to '[object Object]'; {} + [] typed at a console evaluates to 0.", "Standardized by Ecma as 'ECMAScript' in 1997 because the name JavaScript is a trademark, first Sun's and now Oracle's."], "a": {"item_id": "d6f1a64d2099", "text": "现在有一种人，什么都要拿 JavaScript 写。网页写完写后端，后端写完写桌面软件，现在在研究拿它控制烤箱。你问他图啥，他说：\"我不想再学一门新语言。\"\n\n兄弟，你为了不学一门语言，每周都在学一个新框架。\n\n他写的记事本用 Electron，打开占八百兆内存。因为里面塞了一整个浏览器，就为了让你输入\"买菜\"两个字。\n\n他还跟你解释：\"1\"加 1 等于 11，\"1\"减 1 等于 0，这叫灵活。灵活到最后，项目源码三千行，node_modules 两个 G。\n\n上线那天报错：undefined is not a function。他盯着屏幕，一脸茫然：\"到底是谁 undefined 了？\"\n\n我觉得是他的职业规划。"}, "b": {"item_id": "9255e820a06a", "text": "我有个朋友，信一句话：“凡是能用JavaScript写的，最终都会用JavaScript写。”他当预言，我当诅咒。\n\n他写了个计算器，用Electron打包，两百兆，自带一整个Chrome，就为了算一加一。一加一算对了，0.1加0.2出来0.30000000000000004。他说没事，装个库。一装，node_modules比他的人生还沉，里面有个包叫is-odd，专门判断奇数，它还依赖另一个包叫is-number。\n\n他图什么？图“前后端一门语言，不用学第二种”。结果为了这一门，React、Vue、Svelte轮着学，刚学会一个，下一个又出来了。\n\n现在他三十五，头发没了，问我他算个啥。我说你就是typeof null：明明啥也没有，还非说自己是个object。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0565", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "nuclear-fusion", "target_name": "可控核聚变", "facts": ["Has been 'about 30 years away' since the 1950s; at the 1955 Geneva atoms-for-peace conference, Homi Bhabha predicted fusion power within two decades.", "ITER, agreed in 2006 for first plasma in 2016, re-baselined in 2024 to start research operations in 2034 and deuterium-tritium burning in 2039, at a cost estimated above €20 billion.", "NIF's December 2022 'ignition' shot released 3.15 MJ from 2.05 MJ of laser energy; the lasers drew about 300 MJ from the grid to fire.", "Private fusion companies had raised more than $7 billion by 2024."], "a": {"item_id": "04cdb6ae7f1b", "text": "搞可控核聚变的人，我是真佩服。他们能用磁场把一亿度的等离子体悬在半空，却没法把“还有十年”悬在十年。\n\n一个博士二十五岁入行，导师说还有三十年。他五十五岁带学生，说还有三十年。退休那天他把这句话传给徒弟，比传家宝还稳。\n\n图什么？图下一期经费。真搞成了，没人给你拨款；搞不成，永远有人拨。他们最怕的不是等离子体不稳定，是稳定了。\n\n前两年美国激光点火成功，全网欢呼，说输出能量比输入多。一算，激光器从电网吃的电是输出的一百多倍。这叫净增益，净亏本。\n\n下场呢？ITER从1985年谈到现在，预算翻了几番，一代人的青春，换来一句“明年的明年”。\n\n太阳烧了四十六亿年，从没交过项目申请。人造太阳，年年得写结题报告。"}, "b": {"item_id": "8315ced7f71d", "text": "可控核聚变还有十年，这话我爸上大学时就听过，是他老师说的。现在我听，说的人是他老师的学生。这行最稳定的不是等离子体，是那句“还有十年”，代代相传，比托卡马克里那团火命长多了。\n\n干这行的人图什么？你想啊，一个人一辈子的成果，是把一团一亿度的气体多关住了三十秒。开发布会，“创世界纪录！”台下鼓掌。他自己心里清楚，下个月还得写报告申请经费，因为磁约束线圈又烧了。\n\n最惨的是退休那天。学生问他：“老师，您干了四十年，离点火成功还有多远？”他想了想，特别诚实：“还有十年。”\n\n他入行那年也是这么说的。四十年过去，一天都没少。这才叫能量守恒。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0566", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "klingon", "target_name": "克林贡语", "facts": ["Created by linguist Marc Okrand for Star Trek III (1984), building on a few words the actor James Doohan improvised for the 1979 film.", "Deliberately alien grammar: object-verb-subject word order and no verb 'to be', so Hamlet's line became 'taH pagh taHbe'', 'continue or not continue'.", "The Klingon Language Institute (1992) published Hamlet 'in the original Klingon'; Bing Translator added Klingon in 2013 and Duolingo in 2018.", "Okrand's 1985 Klingon Dictionary listed roughly 1,500 words, and fluent speakers number only in the dozens: 20 to 30 in 2009, 30 to 50 by 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "e66268bd7919", "text": "你们身边有没有那种人，把克林贡语学到流利？我认识一个。他周末不陪女朋友，说要练喉音。克林贡语一个词里有三个喉塞音，他说完一句话，像在下水道里给管道道歉。他图什么？他说等哪天遇见真正的克林贡人，就能用母语骂人。结果呢？他在地铁上对一个大爷说“nuqneH”，意思是“你要什么”，大爷以为他在要钱，给了他五块。他面试时用“Qapla'”回答“你的成功经验”，面试官以为他在清嗓子。现在他家里的小孩也学会了，写作业的时候对着卷子喊“ghobe'”，就是“不”。他老婆说，这是她这辈子听过最有骨气的一个“不”。"}, "b": {"item_id": "1e0df73ad7c6", "text": "我认识一哥们，克林贡语说得贼溜。全世界能说流利的，据说也就几十个人，他算一个。\n\n你品品这是什么概念？这门语言里压根没有“你好”，见面只能说“nuqneH”，意思是“你想干嘛”。他花了十年，学会一门见面就吵架的语言，结果全世界能跟他吵的人，凑不齐一个班。\n\n他每年去参加克林贡语言研究所的年会，跟一屋子人朗读克林贡语版《哈姆雷特》。人家坚持说这才是原著，莎士比亚是翻译的。\n\n他还想从小教儿子说，孩子五岁就不肯接茬了。\n\n他这辈子唯一一次听异性跟他说克林贡语，是相亲。他说完特长，姑娘站起来，说了句“Qapla'”，就是“祝你成功”，然后走了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0567", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "carbon-nanotubes", "target_name": "碳纳米管", "facts": ["Reported by Sumio Iijima in Nature in 1991.", "Promised as the cable for a space elevator; a 2006 analysis showed that ordinary defects cut the strength of a macroscopic cable far below the ideal value.", "Long, rigid multi-walled nanotubes behaved like asbestos fibres in a 2008 mouse study in Nature Nanotechnology.", "Sorting metallic from semiconducting tubes has blocked nanotube electronics for three decades; MIT's 16-bit RV16X-NANO processor (2019) is still a lab demonstration.", "Today they are sold mainly as a conductive additive in lithium-ion battery electrodes."], "a": {"item_id": "0f999ec5f76f", "text": "有人拿碳纳米管去给投资人讲故事。碳纳米管什么概念？比头发细一万倍，比钢强一百倍。他的故事就一句话：用它搓一根绳子，从赤道挂到同步轨道，人坐电梯上太空。投资人眼睛都亮了。我就问一句：这根绳子现在多长？他说，实验室里最长的那根，比我的PPT还短。我又问：什么时候量产？他说，还差一点，就差把金属性和半导体性的管子分开，业内叫手性控制。投资人没听懂，但觉得这人太有学问了，当场打了三个亿。三年后项目黄了，他把剩下的管子磨成粉，改名叫\"碳管抗老精华\"，现在一瓶卖九百八。"}, "b": {"item_id": "9252499a4c39", "text": "做碳纳米管创业的博士，路演第一页永远是太空电梯。说这玩意儿比钢强一百倍，重量才六分之一，投资人眼睛都亮了。他们图啥？图的是投资人别翻第二页。第二页写着：实验室里最长做到几十厘米，太空电梯需要三万六千公里，差一亿倍，他管这叫“早期阶段”。投资人说看看样品，他掏出一小瓶黑粉，说这就是未来。结果钱融到了，电梯没修成，产品卖给电池厂当导电剂，论公斤称，跟墨粉一个价位。最后他也活成了碳纳米管：单打独斗个个都强，凑一块儿就团聚，缠成一团，怎么都分不开。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0568", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "google-glass", "target_name": "谷歌眼镜（Google Glass）", "facts": ["Unveiled at Google I/O 2012 with skydivers livestreaming their jump through it onto the Moscone Center roof.", "The Explorer Edition sold to invited users in 2013 for $1,500.", "Wearers were nicknamed 'Glassholes'; bars, cinemas, and casinos banned the device over its camera.", "The consumer programme ended in January 2015; an Enterprise Edition continued until Google stopped selling it in 2023.", "Its display was a 640-by-360 prism in the corner of one eye."], "a": {"item_id": "ccb8c9ae4f5c", "text": "戴谷歌眼镜进酒吧的人，我研究过，都是同一种人：花一千五百美元，就为了有人问他一句\"这是什么\"。他进门不先点酒，先点自己：\"OK Glass！\"全场安静，以为他在跟鬼说话。然后他说\"拍照\"，酒吧里所有人同时捂脸，你戴个眼镜，大家跟你上了安检。他还委屈：\"我就是想看看天气。\"哥们，在酒吧，天气就是头顶那盏灯。他右眼一直往上翻，看着像在回忆初恋，其实是在读促销邮件。英文里专门给这群人造了个词，叫Glasshole，得多招人烦，才能让一个词专门为你诞生。最后保安过来问：\"您在录像吗？\"他说：\"没有，它只是……亮着。\"被请出门，他对眼镜说\"导航回家\"，眼镜回：\"正在搜索信号。\"他站在路边，右眼是地图，左眼是现实，两只眼睛，过着两种人生。"}, "b": {"item_id": "5674fb13d6e0", "text": "戴谷歌眼镜进酒吧的哥们儿，你图什么？花一千五百美元，给谷歌当测试员，钱还是你自己掏的。坐吧台边，右眼一直往右上角瞟，酒保问喝什么，他张嘴就是“OK Glass”。兄弟，人家不叫Glass。最绝的是这玩意儿眨眼就能拍照。以前在酒吧冲姑娘眨眼，顶多被泼一杯酒；现在姑娘得先琢磨：你是在撩我，还是在拍我？想完了，泼两杯。美国人还专门给这帮人造了个词：Glasshole。一个产品能把自己的用户骂成新单词，我是头回见。最后电池没撑过一杯长岛冰茶，他摘下眼镜一看：原来自己就是个一个人来喝闷酒的普通男人。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0569", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "carbon-nanotubes", "target_name": "碳纳米管", "facts": ["Reported by Sumio Iijima in Nature in 1991.", "Promised as the cable for a space elevator; a 2006 analysis showed that ordinary defects cut the strength of a macroscopic cable far below the ideal value.", "Long, rigid multi-walled nanotubes behaved like asbestos fibres in a 2008 mouse study in Nature Nanotechnology.", "Sorting metallic from semiconducting tubes has blocked nanotube electronics for three decades; MIT's 16-bit RV16X-NANO processor (2019) is still a lab demonstration.", "Today they are sold mainly as a conductive additive in lithium-ion battery electrodes."], "a": {"item_id": "0f999ec5f76f", "text": "有人拿碳纳米管去给投资人讲故事。碳纳米管什么概念？比头发细一万倍，比钢强一百倍。他的故事就一句话：用它搓一根绳子，从赤道挂到同步轨道，人坐电梯上太空。投资人眼睛都亮了。我就问一句：这根绳子现在多长？他说，实验室里最长的那根，比我的PPT还短。我又问：什么时候量产？他说，还差一点，就差把金属性和半导体性的管子分开，业内叫手性控制。投资人没听懂，但觉得这人太有学问了，当场打了三个亿。三年后项目黄了，他把剩下的管子磨成粉，改名叫\"碳管抗老精华\"，现在一瓶卖九百八。"}, "b": {"item_id": "dc1d00e455ba", "text": "碳纳米管创业的，PPT第一页永远是太空电梯：比钢强一百倍，从地球拉根绳到同步轨道。投资人问：“多长了？”他说：“实验室最长做到半米。”离三万六千公里，还差三万五千九百九十九公里九百九十九米五。\n\n第二页讲替代硅：“摩尔定律到头了，我们接班！”问良率呢？他说：“有一个小问题，长出来三分之一是金属的，三分之二是半导体的，我们分不开。”大哥，你这叫“替代硅”？你这叫“开盲盒”。\n\n讲了二十年，一九九一年发现的东西，至今还在“即将改变世界”。\n\n最后公司怎么样了？活得挺好。不造电梯，不造芯片，一吨一吨往锂电池里卖导电剂，按公斤算钱。路演时说要通往太空，年报里写的是“主营业务：黑色粉末”。\n\n太空电梯没建成，但他确实上去了，坐的是电动车。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0570", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "windows-vista", "target_name": "Windows Vista", "facts": ["Released to consumers in January 2007, after the 'Longhorn' project was reset in 2004 and headline features such as the WinFS file system were dropped.", "User Account Control prompted so often that Apple's 'Get a Mac' campaign ran an ad about 'Cancel or Allow'.", "'Windows Vista Capable' stickers went on PCs that could only run Home Basic, without the Aero interface, and led to a class-action lawsuit.", "Windows 7 (2009), widely praised, was largely a tuned-up Vista on the same kernel line; Vista support ended in April 2017."], "a": {"item_id": "445c6f8771dc", "text": "有一种人，叫第一天就升级Vista的人。我问他图啥？他说要玻璃特效，要边栏小工具，要最新的系统。结果装完第一件事，是点了四十七次\"允许\"。不是我点的，是系统在问：您确定要复制这个文件吗？您确定要打开这个程序吗？您确定要关掉这个弹窗吗？像在跟一个多疑的保安办手续。\n\n复制一个文件，进度条走到99%停住，给你显示\"正在计算剩余时间\"，算完告诉你还要一个小时。他的显卡驱动？厂家说还在开发。打印机？厂家说用XP版就够了。最后他在论坛发帖，标题叫《Vista很好，是我的电脑不行》。电脑没问题，问题是他把\"最新\"当成了\"能用\"。"}, "b": {"item_id": "c62e9c0142f3", "text": "我有个朋友，2007年Vista首发当天零点就去排队，还非买旗舰版，三百九十九美元。图什么？广告说了：“Wow从现在开始。”\n\n确实，开机那一刻他就“哇”了：打印机没驱动，声卡没驱动，他那台贴着“Vista Capable”标签的电脑，连Aero玻璃特效都跑不动。他每点一下，屏幕就一黑：“Windows需要您的许可才能继续。”点“允许”，又黑一下。半年下来，他成了全世界被自己电脑审问最多的人。\n\n旗舰版承诺的“终极附加功能”，最后等来一副德州扑克。\n\n现在他逢人就推荐一个高级功能，叫“降级到XP”。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0571", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "microsoft-word", "target_name": "Microsoft Word", "facts": ["Launched in 1983 as 'Multi-Tool Word' for Xenix and MS-DOS.", "Clippy, the animated paperclip assistant, arrived in Office 97 and was removed in Office 2007; Microsoft itself ran ads mocking it in 2001.", "Its .docx format, Office Open XML, was fast-tracked to ISO standard 29500 in 2008 as a roughly 6,000-page specification, amid accusations that national committees had been packed.", "Older spell checkers, Word's included, offered 'Cupertino' as a correction for a mistyped 'cooperation', and the resulting 'Cupertino effect' made it into published NATO, EU and UN-agency documents."], "a": {"item_id": "3173e42e100a", "text": "每年六月，全国几十万毕业生集体转行当排版工人。图什么？就图学校那份模板：摘要页码用罗马数字，正文从1开始。为了这个，他们学会了一个这辈子再也用不上的词：分节符。然后挨页去点掉“链接到前一节”，漏点一次，第三章的页眉就写着“致谢”。\n\n最惨的是插图。你把图往上挪了一毫米，后面二十页集体搬家，图3-2直接住进了参考文献。\n\n答辩完问他收获了什么，他说：收获了“论文_终稿_改3_打死不改.docx”。还有，千万别右键“更新域”，那是我目录最后一次完整的样子。"}, "b": {"item_id": "b80a1ea0ef97", "text": "用Word排毕业论文的人，我研究过，都是同一种人：觉得“不就是打字嘛”。他们图什么？图省两个小时，不想学“样式”和“分节符”。于是标题全靠手动：选中、加粗、三号、居中，四下。全文六十个标题，两百四十下。目录也是手打的，页码也是手填的，填得整整齐齐，看着特别像那么回事。\n\n然后导师说：第二章和第三章换个顺序。\n\n他只删了一个回车，第五十页那张图就像被召唤了一样，飞到了第一百一十二页，还顺手带走了摘要的罗马数字页码。\n\n他为了省两个小时，花了两百个小时。最后在打印店，用人家的电脑一打开，全篇错位，他蹲在那儿一页一页重新排。老板路过看了一眼：“同学，你这是论文还是拼图？”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0572", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "carbon-nanotubes", "target_name": "碳纳米管", "facts": ["Reported by Sumio Iijima in Nature in 1991.", "Promised as the cable for a space elevator; a 2006 analysis showed that ordinary defects cut the strength of a macroscopic cable far below the ideal value.", "Long, rigid multi-walled nanotubes behaved like asbestos fibres in a 2008 mouse study in Nature Nanotechnology.", "Sorting metallic from semiconducting tubes has blocked nanotube electronics for three decades; MIT's 16-bit RV16X-NANO processor (2019) is still a lab demonstration.", "Today they are sold mainly as a conductive additive in lithium-ion battery electrodes."], "a": {"item_id": "55f4d79ee616", "text": "我认识一个搞碳纳米管的博士，去路演，PPT第一页写：强度是钢的一百倍，重量只有钢的六分之一。投资人眼睛全亮了，有人举手问：能造太空电梯吗？他说理论上可以。全场鼓掌。\"理论上\"，是这圈子最值钱的三个字。他没讲的是，他那批管子里导电的和不导电的混在一起，像一锅分不清的挂面，想分开一根，得先烧掉一栋楼的钱。投资人也没问。后来他融了三轮，估值翻了几倍，公司改名叫\"某某量子新材料\"，实验室里那罐黑乎乎的粉，还是原来那罐。"}, "b": {"item_id": "ef055d34e6aa", "text": "我认识一个博士，专门拿碳纳米管跟投资人讲故事。PPT第一页永远是太空电梯：“强度是钢的一百倍，一根拉到同步轨道！”投资人问现在能做多长，他说：“厘米级。”三万六千公里，差了也就九个数量级，四舍五入算到了。\n\n第二页讲替代硅芯片。难点只有一个：管子卷出来，一部分导电，一部分是半导体，还分不开。这好比开饭馆，菜一半能吃一半有毒，你还挑不出来，但你跟股东说下季度开连锁。\n\n这故事从1991年讲到现在，“再给十年”讲了三遍。最后他去哪了？给锂电池厂卖导电浆料，按吨算钱。PPT没删，太空电梯那页还在，标题改成了“愿景”。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0573", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "microsoft-word", "target_name": "Microsoft Word", "facts": ["Launched in 1983 as 'Multi-Tool Word' for Xenix and MS-DOS.", "Clippy, the animated paperclip assistant, arrived in Office 97 and was removed in Office 2007; Microsoft itself ran ads mocking it in 2001.", "Its .docx format, Office Open XML, was fast-tracked to ISO standard 29500 in 2008 as a roughly 6,000-page specification, amid accusations that national committees had been packed.", "Older spell checkers, Word's included, offered 'Cupertino' as a correction for a mistyped 'cooperation', and the resulting 'Cupertino effect' made it into published NATO, EU and UN-agency documents."], "a": {"item_id": "b80a1ea0ef97", "text": "用Word排毕业论文的人，我研究过，都是同一种人：觉得“不就是打字嘛”。他们图什么？图省两个小时，不想学“样式”和“分节符”。于是标题全靠手动：选中、加粗、三号、居中，四下。全文六十个标题，两百四十下。目录也是手打的，页码也是手填的，填得整整齐齐，看着特别像那么回事。\n\n然后导师说：第二章和第三章换个顺序。\n\n他只删了一个回车，第五十页那张图就像被召唤了一样，飞到了第一百一十二页，还顺手带走了摘要的罗马数字页码。\n\n他为了省两个小时，花了两百个小时。最后在打印店，用人家的电脑一打开，全篇错位，他蹲在那儿一页一页重新排。老板路过看了一眼：“同学，你这是论文还是拼图？”"}, "b": {"item_id": "3536bc0676b0", "text": "有些人非要用 Word 排毕业论文。图什么？图一个\"一键生成目录\"。结果标题没用标题样式，手敲了个\"第一章\"，加粗了事。目录一更新，正文全变成了目录项。他去学分节符，学会了，又发现页码从第二节开始就断了，把\"链接到前一节\"点了十七次，点出一个新世界。凌晨三点，他按 Ctrl+A，对着域代码哭。答辩前一天，导师说：\"目录页码对不上。\"他按 F9，弹出一行\"错误！未找到目录项\"。那一刻他终于明白，Word 不是在排版，是在考他的耐心，而他的耐心已经交了白卷。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0574", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "luosifen", "target_name": "螺蛳粉", "facts": ["A rice-noodle dish from Liuzhou, Guangxi; the smell comes from pickled bamboo shoots (酸笋), not from the river snails, which only flavour the broth.", "The instant version boomed during the 2020 lockdowns; Liuzhou's government reported packaged luosifen sales of over 10 billion yuan that year.", "Added to China's national intangible cultural heritage list in 2021.", "Liuzhou opened a luosifen industry college in 2020 to train workers for the trade."], "a": {"item_id": "5c463dbea536", "text": "合租房里煮螺蛳粉的人，我太懂你们了。图什么？一包才八块钱，深更半夜饿了，想吃口有灵魂的。可你们不知道，那袋酸笋一拆开，整层楼都被你\"请\"来吃饭了。门缝底下往外冒的不是蒸汽，是战书。室友从卧室探出头，鼻子一抽，眼神像在看犯罪现场。第二天群里弹出一条消息：\"谁又煮螺蛳粉了？\"你把头埋进碗里，一句话不敢说。最后的下场是：窗户开成了通风道，外套挂三天还带着河沟味，押金一分不剩，房东在合同里加了一条\"禁止煮螺蛳粉\"。"}, "b": {"item_id": "d1bc8bf17584", "text": "合租房里煮螺蛳粉的人，开口第一句永远是：“我开窗了。”你开窗？那是给酸笋开了条逃生通道，它出去之前，先把每个房间都参观一遍。\n\n他们图什么？图一口“闻着臭，吃着香”。这话是说给室友听的，意思是：你们负责闻，我负责香。\n\n而且螺蛳粉还是分期付款。红油先到，腐竹跟上，最后剪开酸笋包压轴登场，全屋观众不许退场。\n\n下场呢？第二天群里问“谁昨晚煮东西了”，没人认。但每个枕头都认。最后你被指认，不是靠监控，是你进电梯，旁边的人下意识看了一眼你的外套。\n\n你吃了十五分钟，穿了三天。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0575", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "excel", "target_name": "Microsoft Excel", "facts": ["First shipped for the Macintosh in 1985; the Windows version followed in 1987.", "Silently converts gene names such as SEPT2 and MARCH1 into dates; by August 2020 the HUGO Gene Nomenclature Committee had renamed 27 human genes (MARCH1 became MARCHF1, SEPT2 became SEPTIN2) and published guidelines in Nature Genetics to stop it.", "In October 2020, Public Health England lost 15,841 COVID-19 cases when a legacy .xls file hit its 65,536-row limit.", "Reinhart and Rogoff's 2010 paper 'Growth in a Time of Debt', cited for austerity policies, omitted five countries through a spreadsheet range error found by a graduate student in 2013.", "Sheets have been capped at 1,048,576 rows by 16,384 columns since Excel 2007."], "a": {"item_id": "a27121aa4e43", "text": "有种老板，公司几百号人，ERP不肯上，他说：\"Excel就够了。\"图什么？省钱，还有一种\"所有数字都攥在我手里\"的快感。结果呢？销售总表传了四十个版本，最后一个叫\"最终版_真的不改了_2\"。财务有个VLOOKUP是前任写的，没人敢碰，一动利润表全是#N/A。行政把名单合并了单元格，想筛选，直接报错。最惨的是那个会写宏的小伙子，上个月离职，交接文档就是一个会自己弹窗的文件。现在公司每个月发工资，要靠一台谁都不敢关的电脑。"}, "b": {"item_id": "3643597461dd", "text": "我们老板拿 Excel 管整个公司，他说这叫“轻资产”。翻译一下：舍不得买系统，又想当系统。\n\n全公司的命，就一个文件：《经营总表_最终版_改3_老板别动.xlsx》。财务看一眼，三十七个工作表，底下标签一路滚到右边，像在翻家谱。\n\n老板最骄傲的是他会 VLOOKUP。会一个 VLOOKUP，就觉得自己能当 CTO。结果有天实习生插了一列，全公司工资变成 #N/A。员工问我工资呢？我说你现在的身份是“找不到”。\n\n最惨的是人事，把员工身份证号一粘，全变成 4.40E+17。整个公司四百人，在 Excel 里是同一个人。\n\n公司倒闭那天，老板还在说：“别慌，Ctrl+Z。”\n\n兄弟，公司又不是单元格，撤销不了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0576", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "latex", "target_name": "LaTeX", "facts": ["Leslie Lamport wrote LaTeX in the early 1980s and released it in 1984 as a macro layer on Donald Knuth's TeX (1978), which Knuth built because he disliked how the galley proofs of the new edition of his own book looked.", "TeX's version number converges on π (3.141592653 since 2021), and Knuth's TeX bug bounty started at one hexadecimal dollar, $2.56, doubling yearly until frozen at $327.68; he stopped mailing real cheques in 2008 after fraud and now credits accounts at his fictional Bank of San Serriffe, and only 9 of the 275 cheques he sent from 2006 on were ever cashed.", "Its best-known warning is 'Underfull \\hbox (badness 10000)'; overfull lines are reported as 'Overfull \\hbox (Npt too wide)', and figures float to wherever the algorithm decides, not where the author put them.", "A single missing brace can produce an error reported pages away from the mistake, and a document can need three compiler passes to get its references right."], "a": {"item_id": "6b980d56eb4f", "text": "有人写两页备忘录，非要用 LaTeX 排。问他图什么？图那个 \\maketitle 出来的标题，图页边距精确到两厘米。结果开会前一小时，他对着 Overleaf 发呆：“! Missing $ inserted”，原因是他在“报价_终版”的下划线那儿忘了进数学模式。好不容易编译通过，领导看了一眼说：能不能发个 Word 版？他只好把 tabular 一行行拆回表格。这份备忘录全公司只有他一个人会改，而他本人，现在每次开会都得先说一句：源码我发你了，你别点开。"}, "b": {"item_id": "8f8bc08e77f4", "text": "我们组有个哥们，博士毕业三年，还活在答辩那天。领导让他写两页备忘录，通知周五团建，他打开的是TeXstudio。\n\n图什么？他说：“Computer Modern字体，一看就是发过论文的。”兄弟，团建通知没有审稿人。\n\n开头先写十五行\\usepackage，中文还得装ctex，pdflatex不认，换xelatex。终于编译，报错：“Missing $ inserted”。因为他写了“人均预算$200”，LaTeX以为他要开始推公式了。\n\n改完再编，“经费超支20%，请自理”，逗号后面全没了，百分号把后半句当注释吃了。\n\n周五大家到了，饭店没订。备忘录上地址那栏写着：“详见第??页。”他忘了再编译一遍。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0577", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "javascript", "target_name": "JavaScript", "facts": ["Written by Brendan Eich at Netscape in ten days in May 1995; named Mocha, then LiveScript, then JavaScript to ride Java's marketing, although the two are unrelated.", "typeof null returns 'object', a bug from the first version that has been kept for compatibility ever since.", "In March 2016 the author of left-pad, an 11-line npm package, unpublished it and broke builds across the industry, including Babel and React.", "[] + {} evaluates to '[object Object]'; {} + [] typed at a console evaluates to 0.", "Standardized by Ecma as 'ECMAScript' in 1997 because the name JavaScript is a trademark, first Sun's and now Oracle's."], "a": {"item_id": "5c3084898a09", "text": "我有个朋友，前端出身，人生信条就一句：“能用JavaScript写的，早晚都得用JavaScript写。”网页用它，后端上Node，桌面套Electron。给我写个计算器，装完四百兆，一开机比我家电暖气还烫。\n\n他图什么？图这辈子只学一门语言。结果呢？框架半年一换，他半年就得重学一遍，比考驾照还勤。\n\n上周他给我写了个记账软件。我存进去一块，再存一块，余额显示“11”。我说钱呢？他说别慌，换成三个等号再比一遍。\n\n现在他项目里的node_modules比他存款还大，删不掉也不敢动。万一哪个陌生人撤了一个包，全世界跟他一块儿塌。"}, "b": {"item_id": "d6f1a64d2099", "text": "现在有一种人，什么都要拿 JavaScript 写。网页写完写后端，后端写完写桌面软件，现在在研究拿它控制烤箱。你问他图啥，他说：\"我不想再学一门新语言。\"\n\n兄弟，你为了不学一门语言，每周都在学一个新框架。\n\n他写的记事本用 Electron，打开占八百兆内存。因为里面塞了一整个浏览器，就为了让你输入\"买菜\"两个字。\n\n他还跟你解释：\"1\"加 1 等于 11，\"1\"减 1 等于 0，这叫灵活。灵活到最后，项目源码三千行，node_modules 两个 G。\n\n上线那天报错：undefined is not a function。他盯着屏幕，一脸茫然：\"到底是谁 undefined 了？\"\n\n我觉得是他的职业规划。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0578", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "excel", "target_name": "Microsoft Excel", "facts": ["First shipped for the Macintosh in 1985; the Windows version followed in 1987.", "Silently converts gene names such as SEPT2 and MARCH1 into dates; by August 2020 the HUGO Gene Nomenclature Committee had renamed 27 human genes (MARCH1 became MARCHF1, SEPT2 became SEPTIN2) and published guidelines in Nature Genetics to stop it.", "In October 2020, Public Health England lost 15,841 COVID-19 cases when a legacy .xls file hit its 65,536-row limit.", "Reinhart and Rogoff's 2010 paper 'Growth in a Time of Debt', cited for austerity policies, omitted five countries through a spreadsheet range error found by a graduate student in 2013.", "Sheets have been capped at 1,048,576 rows by 16,384 columns since Excel 2007."], "a": {"item_id": "7557f0ed30ba", "text": "见过拿Excel管整家公司的老板。问他怎么不上个系统，他说：\"Excel挺好用的。\"是挺好用，好用到库存、工资、客户全塞一张表里，VLOOKUP嵌了七层。这哪是做表，这是写遗嘱。\n\n图什么？图不用学新东西。结果为了不学新东西，公司专门养了个人来学这张表。刘姐，没职位，大家只说\"问刘姐\"。她请一天假，公司就瘫了，因为只有她知道D列不能动，一动，#REF!就像瘟疫一样往下传。\n\n下场更惨。上周我想打开订单表，弹窗：\"文件正在被张伟使用。\"张伟三年前就离职了。现在公司发工资，得等一个幽灵把文件保存关掉。"}, "b": {"item_id": "fdec8434c36d", "text": "你们公司有没有这种人？老板说系统太贵，他就用 Excel 管全公司。财务表、库存表、工资表、客户名单，四十个 sheet，每个都带合并单元格，像一座没有图纸的违章建筑。文件名叫\"最终版_最终版2_真的最终版.xlsx\"，打开一看，VLOOKUP 引用的还是前任小王电脑里的 D 盘路径。员工编号输进去变成 1.23E+15，身份证后三位全成了0，工资都算错了。他图什么？图省钱，图灵活，图老板一句\"这表你最懂\"。结果呢？他现在请不了年假，因为全公司只有他看得懂那个宏，他一休假，公司就停摆。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0579", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "javascript", "target_name": "JavaScript", "facts": ["Written by Brendan Eich at Netscape in ten days in May 1995; named Mocha, then LiveScript, then JavaScript to ride Java's marketing, although the two are unrelated.", "typeof null returns 'object', a bug from the first version that has been kept for compatibility ever since.", "In March 2016 the author of left-pad, an 11-line npm package, unpublished it and broke builds across the industry, including Babel and React.", "[] + {} evaluates to '[object Object]'; {} + [] typed at a console evaluates to 0.", "Standardized by Ecma as 'ECMAScript' in 1997 because the name JavaScript is a trademark, first Sun's and now Oracle's."], "a": {"item_id": "5c3084898a09", "text": "我有个朋友，前端出身，人生信条就一句：“能用JavaScript写的，早晚都得用JavaScript写。”网页用它，后端上Node，桌面套Electron。给我写个计算器，装完四百兆，一开机比我家电暖气还烫。\n\n他图什么？图这辈子只学一门语言。结果呢？框架半年一换，他半年就得重学一遍，比考驾照还勤。\n\n上周他给我写了个记账软件。我存进去一块，再存一块，余额显示“11”。我说钱呢？他说别慌，换成三个等号再比一遍。\n\n现在他项目里的node_modules比他存款还大，删不掉也不敢动。万一哪个陌生人撤了一个包，全世界跟他一块儿塌。"}, "b": {"item_id": "3206715972e0", "text": "我同事学了三个月 JavaScript，现在看什么都像 JS。前两天他说要做个记事本，用 Electron。我说记事本？他说对，打出来 300 兆，因为里面自带一个 Chrome。我说你一个记事本为啥要带浏览器？他说：“我不想学第二门语言。”兄弟，你省的是学习时间，吃的是我的内存。\n\n他连判断奇偶数都不自己写，npm install is-even。装完 node_modules 两个G，他自己的代码三行。\n\n我考他：\"1\"+1 等于几？他说 11。\"1\"-1 呢？0。我说你就这么接受了？他说：“习惯了。”\n\n各位，这不是程序员，这是被 JavaScript 虐出斯德哥尔摩综合征的人。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0580", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "canberra", "target_name": "堪培拉", "facts": ["Chosen as a compromise because neither Sydney nor Melbourne would accept the other as capital; Australia's constitution required the capital to be at least 100 miles from Sydney.", "Designed by the Chicago architects Walter Burley Griffin and Marion Mahony Griffin, who won an international competition in 1912.", "Its centrepiece, Lake Burley Griffin, is artificial, created by damming the Molonglo River in 1963-64.", "Parliament House (1988) is built into Capital Hill under a lawn meant for citizens to walk over their politicians; the lawn was fenced off in 2017 for security.", "Melbourne served as the capital until 1927 while Canberra was being built."], "a": {"item_id": "0d2a6bbab13d", "text": "我有个朋友，为了工作搬去堪培拉。他说：“联邦公务员，铁饭碗，三年升EL1。”兄弟，你才二十八岁，人生规划写得跟预算案附录一样。\n\n去了一年，他说话全成了缩写：“我在DFAT，赶上MOG调整，等APS6转正。”我问他周末干嘛，他说开两个多小时去贝特曼斯湾看海。你搬去一个城市，生活质量全看你多快能离开它。\n\n最惨的是，他现在会主动跟人说：“堪培拉其实挺好的，你得慢慢了解它。”这话我只在两种人嘴里听过：堪培拉人，和被关太久、开始同情绑匪的人质。"}, "b": {"item_id": "a93ae6b79980", "text": "有一种人我特别佩服：为了工作搬去堪培拉。问他图什么，他说：“稳定，公务员，铁饭碗。”行，铁饭碗，里面装的是空气。\n\n他们的周末是这样的：周六逛一遍Canberra Centre，周日再逛一遍，因为周六没买。想刺激一点？开车绕国会山的环岛，一圈，两圈，三圈，这就是全城夜生活。而且开错一个出口，你就出了首都领地，进新南威尔士了。\n\n第一年他说“我喜欢安静”；第二年开始对着格里芬湖说心里话；第三年每个月飞悉尼，不是玩，是想确认世界上还有陌生人。\n\n最惨的是升职。升到哪儿？换个部门，在马路对面。政府一换届，部门改名，他人没动，只是名片重印。这是堪培拉人一生里最大的变故。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0581", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "juicero", "target_name": "Juicero 榨汁机", "facts": ["A Wi-Fi-connected cold-press juicer launched in 2016 at $699, later cut to $399, that squeezed proprietary bags of chopped produce sold by subscription.", "Raised about $120 million from investors including Google Ventures and Kleiner Perkins.", "In April 2017, Bloomberg reporters squeezed the packs by hand and got 7.5 ounces of juice in 90 seconds, faster than the machine's 8 ounces in about two minutes.", "Each pack carried a QR code that the press scanned and checked online before squeezing; it refused expired packs and would not run at all unless connected to Wi-Fi.", "Shut down in September 2017 and offered refunds on the machine."], "a": {"item_id": "e1fe9018b6c6", "text": "买 Juicero 的人我研究过：住旧金山，爱喝\"冷压\"，觉得自己动手捏个袋子太掉价，得花 400 美元雇一台联网的机器替他捏。\n\n这机器号称四吨压力。后来彭博社发现，徒手捏那个果汁包，出汁还更快。也就是说，这帮人花 400 块，买的是\"捏袋子需要 WiFi\"的权利。\n\n更绝的是，果汁包过期，机器直接罢工。它比你妈还关心你的健康。\n\n最后公司倒闭，全额退款。钱拿回来了，脸没拿回来。从此这帮人看自己的手，都带着一种复杂的情绪：这玩意儿，一直是免费的。"}, "b": {"item_id": "c8e0492d10b9", "text": "你知道花400美元买Juicero的都是什么人吗？就是那种早上六点起床、先冥想再跑步、手机里装了十二个健康App的硅谷精英。他们不缺钱，缺的是“我连喝果汁都在用科技”的那份体面。\n\n结果呢？你花400块买了台联网的压榨机，再每袋七八美元买人家的果蔬包。机器还得先扫码、连Wi-Fi，确认你这包没过期，才肯开工。家里断网了？对不起，今天没有健康。\n\n然后彭博社的记者干了件特别缺德的事：直接上手捏那个袋子，挤出来的汁跟机器差不多，速度还更快。\n\n一群天天喊“颠覆传统”的人，被两只手颠覆了。\n\n最惨的是，后来公司倒闭了，给你退钱。你连“至少我还在用”都说不出口了。你最后得到了什么？一个教训：原来你最该投资的科技产品，是自己的手。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0582", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "excel", "target_name": "Microsoft Excel", "facts": ["First shipped for the Macintosh in 1985; the Windows version followed in 1987.", "Silently converts gene names such as SEPT2 and MARCH1 into dates; by August 2020 the HUGO Gene Nomenclature Committee had renamed 27 human genes (MARCH1 became MARCHF1, SEPT2 became SEPTIN2) and published guidelines in Nature Genetics to stop it.", "In October 2020, Public Health England lost 15,841 COVID-19 cases when a legacy .xls file hit its 65,536-row limit.", "Reinhart and Rogoff's 2010 paper 'Growth in a Time of Debt', cited for austerity policies, omitted five countries through a spreadsheet range error found by a graduate student in 2013.", "Sheets have been capped at 1,048,576 rows by 16,384 columns since Excel 2007."], "a": {"item_id": "3643597461dd", "text": "我们老板拿 Excel 管整个公司，他说这叫“轻资产”。翻译一下：舍不得买系统，又想当系统。\n\n全公司的命，就一个文件：《经营总表_最终版_改3_老板别动.xlsx》。财务看一眼，三十七个工作表，底下标签一路滚到右边，像在翻家谱。\n\n老板最骄傲的是他会 VLOOKUP。会一个 VLOOKUP，就觉得自己能当 CTO。结果有天实习生插了一列，全公司工资变成 #N/A。员工问我工资呢？我说你现在的身份是“找不到”。\n\n最惨的是人事，把员工身份证号一粘，全变成 4.40E+17。整个公司四百人，在 Excel 里是同一个人。\n\n公司倒闭那天，老板还在说：“别慌，Ctrl+Z。”\n\n兄弟，公司又不是单元格，撤销不了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "fdec8434c36d", "text": "你们公司有没有这种人？老板说系统太贵，他就用 Excel 管全公司。财务表、库存表、工资表、客户名单，四十个 sheet，每个都带合并单元格，像一座没有图纸的违章建筑。文件名叫\"最终版_最终版2_真的最终版.xlsx\"，打开一看，VLOOKUP 引用的还是前任小王电脑里的 D 盘路径。员工编号输进去变成 1.23E+15，身份证后三位全成了0，工资都算错了。他图什么？图省钱，图灵活，图老板一句\"这表你最懂\"。结果呢？他现在请不了年假，因为全公司只有他看得懂那个宏，他一休假，公司就停摆。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0583", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "crocs", "target_name": "Crocs 洞洞鞋", "facts": ["Founded in Boulder, Colorado, in 2002; the first clog debuted at the Fort Lauderdale boat show as a boating shoe.", "Made of 'Croslite', which Crocs describes as a proprietary closed-cell resin foam and which third parties identify as an injection-moulded EVA foam.", "Balenciaga sold a platform Crocs for about $850 in 2018.", "The stock fell more than 90% from its 2007 peak during the 2008 crisis; the company then boomed in the pandemic and gave away 860,000 pairs to healthcare workers in 2020.", "The holes are officially 'ventilation ports' and the charms that go in them are 'Jibbitz', a company Crocs bought in 2006."], "a": {"item_id": "0269f5e79bdf", "text": "穿洞洞鞋出门的人，开口都是同一句：“我就图个舒服。”行，那你解释一下鞋面上那一排小挂件。小熊、披萨、一个字母H，这哪是图舒服，这是脚上开了个朋友圈。\n\n还有那根后跟带，永远往后翻，他们管这叫“运动模式”。哥们，你是下楼取快递。真运动谁穿这个？这个模式唯一的功能，就是方便扶梯把你的鞋吃了。\n\n下场也都一样。夏天晒完，脚背上一块镂空格子，像被烤架翻过面。下雨天更惨，洞是通的，水进得特别积极，走一步，咕叽，咕叽，脚上养了两只青蛙。\n\n最后他还要补一句：“这个其实防臭。”没人问你。"}, "b": {"item_id": "c92c7ce6304b", "text": "我发现穿洞洞鞋出门的人，都有一套标准说辞：“丑是丑，但舒服啊。”舒服的东西多了，你怎么不裹着被窝去上班？\n\n嘴上说图舒服，转头往鞋洞里插满鞋花：十二只小恐龙、三块披萨、一个奥特曼。鞋八十，花四百。你这不是穿鞋，是脚上开了个小卖部。\n\n后跟那根带子，平时翻在脚背上叫“休闲模式”，一跑起来立马扳下去，叫“运动模式”。全身上下，就这根带子有档位。\n\n下场也很统一：一下雨，每个洞都是进水口，走一步“噗叽”一声；天一晴，一颗小石子钻进洞里怎么都甩不出来，只能当街单脚跳，像只刚学走路的火烈鸟。\n\n图舒服的人，最后全蹲在路边倒石子。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0584", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "windows-8", "target_name": "Windows 8", "facts": ["Released in October 2012; removed the Start button and booted into a full-screen grid of tiles designed for touch screens most PCs did not have.", "The 8.1 update in 2013 put a Start button back, Windows 10 (2015) restored the Start menu, and Microsoft skipped the name Windows 9 altogether.", "Microsoft dropped the 'Metro' name in August 2012 after reports that German retailer Metro AG had objected on trademark grounds; Microsoft called 'Metro' a code name and denied any litigation, and the style was afterwards known informally as 'Modern UI'.", "The 'Charms bar' hid Search, Share, and Settings behind an invisible swipe from the right edge.", "Windows RT, the ARM edition launched alongside it, could not run ordinary Windows programs; Microsoft wrote down $900 million of unsold Surface RT tablets in 2013."], "a": {"item_id": "a62b05dc7bff", "text": "给台式机装Windows 8的人，我特别好奇你图什么。图新鲜。结果开机，满屏彩色方块，我就想打开个文件夹，它给我弹出个\"体育\"。\n\n关机更绝。台式机，没有开始按钮，你得把鼠标甩到屏幕右下角，等侧边栏像幽灵一样滑出来，点设置，再点电源。我第一次关机找了十分钟，我怀疑这是微软的智力测试。\n\n而且这帮人的显示器根本不是触屏，可Windows 8整套设计就是让你拿手指划。所以你会看到一个成年人，坐在台式机前，手指悬在半空，犹豫要不要戳。\n\n最后下场都一样：第二天下载Classic Shell，把开始菜单请回来。花钱升级，就为了把它打扮成Windows 7。这不叫升级，这叫花钱请个演员，去演你的前任。"}, "b": {"item_id": "4dccc16b3e4c", "text": "我表哥就是那种人。2012年他给我家台式机装了Windows 8，理由就一句：“新的肯定好。”好什么？这系统是给平板设计的，满屏方块，你拿鼠标去戳，像拿筷子按电梯。开始按钮没了，他说不需要：“鼠标往右上角一甩，侧边栏就出来了。”我爸用了十年XP，现在为了关个机，得拿鼠标在屏幕边上来回招魂。最后我爸的关机方式改成了拔插头。表哥现在每周日被叫回来，不是吃饭，是找“我的电脑”去哪儿了。他还嘴硬：“等8.1就好了。”8.1来了，开始按钮真回来了，一点开，还是那屏方块。微软都认错了，他还没认。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0585", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "vim", "target_name": "Vim", "facts": ["Released by Bram Moolenaar in November 1991 as 'Vi IMitation', a vi clone he had begun writing for the Amiga in 1988 on top of Stevie; the expansion became 'Vi IMproved' with version 2.0 in December 1993.", "Licensed as charityware: the licence asks users to donate to ICCF Holland, a charity Moolenaar ran for children in Uganda.", "Stack Overflow's question 'How to exit the Vim editor?' passed one million visits in May 2017, which Stack Overflow marked with a blog post.", "Modal editing: in normal mode, typing letters runs commands rather than inserting text; :wq saves and quits, :q! quits and discards changes."], "a": {"item_id": "773f0cf90ece", "text": "我有个朋友用 Vim 写代码，这本来没什么，问题是他得让全世界知道。每天发朋友圈：\"今天 hjkl 走了八个小时，手没离开主键盘区。\"配图是一屏幕冒号和波浪号。底下点赞的全是同款人，互相夸：\"牛逼，我也是。\"他们图什么？图的就是别人看不懂还得点赞。结果上周他要退出文件，手一滑按了个 i，开始在文件里狂打字，还没反应过来。他想起有个 Esc，按了二十下，屏幕纹丝不动。最后他在群里喊：\"救命，我退不出去了！\"群友秒回一排：先按 Esc，再敲 :q!。他回：\"我已经发过朋友圈了，不能认输。\""}, "b": {"item_id": "304fbbdaf17e", "text": "咱公司有个哥们，从不说\"我在写代码\"，他说\"我在用 Vim 写代码\"。重点不是写，是 Vim。\n\n他图啥？图你路过他工位，看见他手指在键盘上弹钢琴，嘴里念着 hjkl hjkl，像在做法。你问他干嘛，他说\"移动光标\"。移一格十分钟，但\"不用离开主键区\"。省下摸方向键的半秒，代价是 .vimrc 调了三年。\n\n下场呢？有天重装系统，.vimrc 没备份。他对着裸奔的 Vim，像被扒了铠甲的骑士。第二天，他偷偷装了 VS Code，再装个 Vim 插件。\n\n现在他是：用 VS Code 假装用 Vim，还得让所有人知道。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0586", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "jackfruit", "target_name": "菠萝蜜", "facts": ["The largest fruit that grows on a tree; single fruits can weigh over 40 kg.", "The national fruit of Bangladesh and the state fruit of Kerala and Tamil Nadu.", "Unripe flesh is sold as a vegan 'pulled pork' substitute, although it has only 1-2 g of protein per 100 g.", "Cutting one releases a sticky white latex, so cooks oil their knives and hands first.", "A member of the mulberry family (Moraceae), a relative of breadfruit and figs."], "a": {"item_id": "b09e1a218e62", "text": "现在网红轻食店流行一道菜，叫“植物基手撕猪肉”，六十八一碗。我吃了一口，菠萝蜜。就是海南路边五块钱一斤、一个三十斤、浑身长刺、得抱着才能搬走的那个。\n\n老板图什么？图它没熟的时候能撕成一丝一丝的，看着像肉。可他没告诉你，切这玩意儿会流白胶，粘手上肥皂都洗不掉，得拿食用油搓。\n\n你想想这画面：一个天天喊“告别动物油脂”的素食老板，每天打烊第一件事，是在后厨拎一壶花生油搓手，搓得满手油光，跟刚偷吃完一盘红烧肉似的。\n\n最惨的是有一回他切到个熟的，整个店一股香蕉混榴莲的甜味儿。顾客举着筷子问：“老板，你这猪肉怎么一股水果糖味儿？”\n\n老板想了半天：“这是……吃水果长大的猪。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "292065870d49", "text": "菠萝蜜这玩意儿，长得像个带刺的炸弹，切开满手黏胶，洗手得用油。现在有人把它炖成\"手撕肉\"，印在菜单上叫\"植物基猪肉\"。我就想问，你们是没吃过猪肉，还是没吃过菠萝蜜？它被撕成丝是挺像，我承认。可你往嘴里一放，甜的！老板的算盘打得噼啪响：素食风口来了，成本便宜，还能加价三十。结果客人咬一口，愣住了：\"这肉怎么带着一股熟透了的水果味？\"老板说，这叫创新。创新完，一个月后店就没了，差评区全是\"猪肉被冒充，我的眼泪是甜的\"。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0587", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "pmma", "target_name": "PMMA 电子束光刻胶", "facts": ["The same polymer as Plexiglas and acrylic glass, dissolved in anisole or chlorobenzene and spun into a thin film.", "The oldest electron-beam resist still in everyday use; IBM researchers reported it as one in the late 1960s.", "Positive tone: the beam breaks polymer chains and the exposed region dissolves in a MIBK:IPA developer.", "Resolves below 10 nm but has poor sensitivity, so doses of hundreds to over a thousand µC/cm² (depending on voltage) make writing slow.", "Poor dry-etch resistance, so it is used mostly for lift-off rather than as an etch mask."], "a": {"item_id": "66f317caaa7a", "text": "干电子束光刻还在用 PMMA 的，基本都是博士生。图什么？图一句“PMMA 分辨率高，业内金标准”。金标准，对，这玩意儿跟你家鱼缸的亚克力、牙医给你做的牙托是一个东西。人家做牙托的，做完下班回家吃饭。咱们呢，拿它刻十纳米，电子枪在里面扫一整夜，剂量大得像给它做理疗，早上进实验室，机器还在写，你俩互相看着，谁也不好意思先走。\n\n好不容易写完了，MIBK 加 IPA 一泡，线条漂亮。结果一刻蚀，胶比我工资涨得还慢，消失得比导师的承诺还快。\n\n师弟问：“师兄，为什么不换个胶？”\n\n我说：“导师说，当年师兄就是用 950K 毕业的。”\n\n师兄毕业了，我还守着一块有机玻璃，等它给我一个答辩。"}, "b": {"item_id": "90a70ba10fe5", "text": "我有个哥们读纳米加工博士，电子束光刻到今天还用PMMA。PMMA是啥？有机玻璃。奶茶店拿它做招牌，他拿它做量子器件。为啥不换？导师说配方是师爷九十年代调的：950K叠495K，MIBK兑IPA一比三，显影四十五秒，一秒别动。图啥？便宜、稳，论文写一句\"standard PMMA process\"，审稿人都不问。代价是灵敏度低得像我爸回微信，一片芯片曝一整夜，机时按小时收钱，他就睡在机房门口。剂量给多了还交联，正胶活成负胶。最后剥离，丙酮泡一宿，金还粘着，一上超声，金掉了，片子也碎了。他捧着碎片说：\"至少线宽十纳米。\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0588", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "google-sheets", "target_name": "Google Sheets", "facts": ["Launched in June 2006 as Google Spreadsheets, built on XL2Web, a product Google acquired from 2Web Technologies.", "A spreadsheet is capped at 10 million cells (raised from 5 million in 2022).", "Functions such as IMPORTHTML, IMPORTXML, and GOOGLEFINANCE pull live web data straight into cells.", "Free with a Google account; files live in Google Drive, and collaborators edit the same sheet simultaneously with coloured cursors.", "In a browser, offline editing works only in Chrome or Edge, with the Google Docs Offline extension installed and offline access switched on while still connected; the Android and iOS apps work offline natively."], "a": {"item_id": "81690243de7c", "text": "每个团队都有这么一个人，说：“不用买Jira，太重了，我拿Google Sheets搞一下就行。”他不是图省钱，是图全公司只有他看得懂第七个标签页。\n\n三个月后，表里十四个标签页，条件格式红黄绿：红是延期，黄是风险，绿是“没人来骂我”。有个标签页叫“勿动”，全组都动过。\n\n直到有天，同事只选了B列，点了“A到Z排序”。从此任务和负责人各过各的，张三在干李四的活，李四在给一个叫“待定”的人打下手。\n\n最后他休年假，全组停摆，因为只有他知道IMPORTRANGE为什么报#REF!。他离职那天，交接文档就一个链接，点开写着“需要访问权限”，申请已自动发送给：他的个人邮箱。"}, "b": {"item_id": "5ceaecd3ab50", "text": "你们见过用 Google 表格管团队的人吗？这种人通常是行政出身，入职第一天就甩给大家一个链接，标题叫《项目总表》。图什么？图一眼看全局。结果他开了二十个标签页，第一个叫“总览”，第十七个叫“真的总览”，最后一个叫“别删”。每人一行，有人把单元格染成黄色，有人加了下拉框，选项从“进行中”一路改成“待定，等老板回”。周五他一打开，满屏#REF!，像看见自己的人生。翻版本记录更绝，凌晨三点有人把“张三”改成了“张三丰”。他终于明白，他管的不是团队，是一个会自己长脾气的表格。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0589", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "klingon", "target_name": "克林贡语", "facts": ["Created by linguist Marc Okrand for Star Trek III (1984), building on a few words the actor James Doohan improvised for the 1979 film.", "Deliberately alien grammar: object-verb-subject word order and no verb 'to be', so Hamlet's line became 'taH pagh taHbe'', 'continue or not continue'.", "The Klingon Language Institute (1992) published Hamlet 'in the original Klingon'; Bing Translator added Klingon in 2013 and Duolingo in 2018.", "Okrand's 1985 Klingon Dictionary listed roughly 1,500 words, and fluent speakers number only in the dozens: 20 to 30 in 2009, 30 to 50 by 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "1e0df73ad7c6", "text": "我认识一哥们，克林贡语说得贼溜。全世界能说流利的，据说也就几十个人，他算一个。\n\n你品品这是什么概念？这门语言里压根没有“你好”，见面只能说“nuqneH”，意思是“你想干嘛”。他花了十年，学会一门见面就吵架的语言，结果全世界能跟他吵的人，凑不齐一个班。\n\n他每年去参加克林贡语言研究所的年会，跟一屋子人朗读克林贡语版《哈姆雷特》。人家坚持说这才是原著，莎士比亚是翻译的。\n\n他还想从小教儿子说，孩子五岁就不肯接茬了。\n\n他这辈子唯一一次听异性跟他说克林贡语，是相亲。他说完特长，姑娘站起来，说了句“Qapla'”，就是“祝你成功”，然后走了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "c9277fcc0b88", "text": "练到流利克林贡语的人，我真心佩服。白天是会计，晚上披着披风，图什么？图终于有人配得上他的愤怒。结果呢，这门语言没有“你好”，打招呼只有一句“nuqneH”，意思是“你要干嘛？”。三年苦功，换来全世界几百个同好，见面第一句就是找茬。\n\n更惨的是语序。克林贡语是宾语、动词、主语，你骂人得先说被骂的是谁、骂了什么，最后才说是谁干的。一句狠话憋到末尾才亮出主语，气势全靠肺活量。\n\n回家对老婆表白，没有“谢谢”，没有“对不起”，这门语言压根没给你认错的词。想道歉？只能把对方打一顿再说“你赢了”。\n\n所以流利的下场就是：全世界会说的人聚在一个群里，用克林贡语吵架。吵了三个小时，谁也没听懂谁，因为谁都在等对方把主语说出来。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0590", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "canberra", "target_name": "堪培拉", "facts": ["Chosen as a compromise because neither Sydney nor Melbourne would accept the other as capital; Australia's constitution required the capital to be at least 100 miles from Sydney.", "Designed by the Chicago architects Walter Burley Griffin and Marion Mahony Griffin, who won an international competition in 1912.", "Its centrepiece, Lake Burley Griffin, is artificial, created by damming the Molonglo River in 1963-64.", "Parliament House (1988) is built into Capital Hill under a lawn meant for citizens to walk over their politicians; the lawn was fenced off in 2017 for security.", "Melbourne served as the capital until 1927 while Canberra was being built."], "a": {"item_id": "a93ae6b79980", "text": "有一种人我特别佩服：为了工作搬去堪培拉。问他图什么，他说：“稳定，公务员，铁饭碗。”行，铁饭碗，里面装的是空气。\n\n他们的周末是这样的：周六逛一遍Canberra Centre，周日再逛一遍，因为周六没买。想刺激一点？开车绕国会山的环岛，一圈，两圈，三圈，这就是全城夜生活。而且开错一个出口，你就出了首都领地，进新南威尔士了。\n\n第一年他说“我喜欢安静”；第二年开始对着格里芬湖说心里话；第三年每个月飞悉尼，不是玩，是想确认世界上还有陌生人。\n\n最惨的是升职。升到哪儿？换个部门，在马路对面。政府一换届，部门改名，他人没动，只是名片重印。这是堪培拉人一生里最大的变故。"}, "b": {"item_id": "7c175bf79c23", "text": "你们知道堪培拉为什么是首都吗？悉尼和墨尔本都不让对方当，吵了半天，最后选了个中间的地方，谁也不服，谁也不去。\n\n偏偏就有一群人，为了\"离权力近一点\"搬过来。处长、智库研究员、部长办公室的小助理，还有一堆咨询公司的。图什么？图升职，图简历上一个好看的头衔，图哪天上电视，站在国会山底下说一句\"我们正在研究\"。\n\n三年后呢？他们每个周五晚上开三个小时车去悉尼，周日傍晚再开三个小时回来，车里一句话不说，像押送回去上班的犯人。湖边散步走了一百圈，国会山看了一百遍，权力还是在别人手里。冬天早上零下五度，他们在环岛里绕圈，绕了三年，绕回了原点。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0591", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "almond-milk", "target_name": "杏仁奶", "facts": ["California grows about 80% of the world's almonds.", "A single almond takes about 1.1 US gallons (roughly 4 litres) of water to grow, a figure Mother Jones published in 2014 that spread during California's drought.", "A 2015 US class action alleged Almond Breeze contained only about 2% almonds; it partly survived a motion to dismiss, was stayed in 2017 pending a separate Blue Diamond labelling settlement, and the plaintiffs then dropped it.", "Pollinating California's almond orchards each February requires trucking in most of the commercial honeybee colonies in the United States.", "Medieval European cooks used almond milk as a Lenten substitute for dairy, centuries before the current fashion."], "a": {"item_id": "7d9582e4c68c", "text": "我有个同事，为了救地球，牛奶戒了，改喝杏仁奶。天天端着杯子说：“我在减少碳排放。”\n\n挺好。就是我查了一下：一颗杏仁，得喝掉好几升水。加州旱成那样，杏仁树还在那儿咕嘟咕嘟地喝。\n\n更绝的是他那盒杏仁奶，配料表上杏仁只占百分之二，剩下全是水。\n\n也就是说，他花三十块买了一盒“泡过杏仁的水”，那几颗杏仁还先喝光了一个村的水。他不是在救地球，他是在帮加州倒腾水。\n\n哦对，杏仁开花全靠租来的蜜蜂授粉，每年一季下来，蜜蜂成片成片地死。\n\n他现在特骄傲：“我从不碰动物制品。”\n\n兄弟，蜜蜂不算动物？"}, "b": {"item_id": "a3df26d73f36", "text": "你们身边有没有这种人？每天早上端一杯杏仁奶，说自己是为了地球。我问他：你知道做一杯杏仁奶要喝掉多少水吗？他说不知道，但包装上画着阳光下的草地，看着很安心。杏仁是出了名的费水作物，加州一棵杏树一年要喝好几百升水，他这杯“环保”，其实是拿加州的水库在泡杏仁。这人图什么？图个心安。图朋友圈里配一张杯子的照片，文案写“从我做起”。结果呢？牛奶钱没省下来，一瓶杏仁奶比牛奶贵一倍多。味道像加了两滴悔恨的白开水，倒进咖啡里直接分层，像一杯正在办离婚的饮料。上个月他开车去超市囤了三箱，车是烧油的，车钥匙还挂着一只环保袋。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0592", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "carbon-nanotubes", "target_name": "碳纳米管", "facts": ["Reported by Sumio Iijima in Nature in 1991.", "Promised as the cable for a space elevator; a 2006 analysis showed that ordinary defects cut the strength of a macroscopic cable far below the ideal value.", "Long, rigid multi-walled nanotubes behaved like asbestos fibres in a 2008 mouse study in Nature Nanotechnology.", "Sorting metallic from semiconducting tubes has blocked nanotube electronics for three decades; MIT's 16-bit RV16X-NANO processor (2019) is still a lab demonstration.", "Today they are sold mainly as a conductive additive in lithium-ion battery electrodes."], "a": {"item_id": "ef055d34e6aa", "text": "我认识一个博士，专门拿碳纳米管跟投资人讲故事。PPT第一页永远是太空电梯：“强度是钢的一百倍，一根拉到同步轨道！”投资人问现在能做多长，他说：“厘米级。”三万六千公里，差了也就九个数量级，四舍五入算到了。\n\n第二页讲替代硅芯片。难点只有一个：管子卷出来，一部分导电，一部分是半导体，还分不开。这好比开饭馆，菜一半能吃一半有毒，你还挑不出来，但你跟股东说下季度开连锁。\n\n这故事从1991年讲到现在，“再给十年”讲了三遍。最后他去哪了？给锂电池厂卖导电浆料，按吨算钱。PPT没删，太空电梯那页还在，标题改成了“愿景”。"}, "b": {"item_id": "0f999ec5f76f", "text": "有人拿碳纳米管去给投资人讲故事。碳纳米管什么概念？比头发细一万倍，比钢强一百倍。他的故事就一句话：用它搓一根绳子，从赤道挂到同步轨道，人坐电梯上太空。投资人眼睛都亮了。我就问一句：这根绳子现在多长？他说，实验室里最长的那根，比我的PPT还短。我又问：什么时候量产？他说，还差一点，就差把金属性和半导体性的管子分开，业内叫手性控制。投资人没听懂，但觉得这人太有学问了，当场打了三个亿。三年后项目黄了，他把剩下的管子磨成粉，改名叫\"碳管抗老精华\"，现在一瓶卖九百八。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0593", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "vision-pro", "target_name": "Apple Vision Pro", "facts": ["Released in February 2024 at $3,499, with an external battery pack on a cable rated for about two hours.", "'EyeSight' shows a rendering of the wearer's eyes on an outward-facing screen.", "Apple calls it 'spatial computing' and its App Store rules tell developers not to describe apps as AR, VR, XR, or MR.", "Reports during 2024 said Apple had cut production; analysts' first-year sales estimates were well under a million units.", "Weighs 600-650 g, before the battery."], "a": {"item_id": "af7b2649d37f", "text": "地铁上最好认的，就是戴Vision Pro的。三万块顶在脑门上，一根线连着裤兜里的电池，像刚从医院偷跑出来、还没拔输液管的病人。他图什么？图“空间计算”。人家早高峰挤成肉饼，他调出个月球环境。兄弟，你人在月球，屁股在十号线。最绝的是手势：眼睛一盯，两根手指一捏，旁边大姐以为他要掐她，回手就是一巴掌。外面那块屏还给你显示一双假眼睛，冲你深情地眨。下场呢？电池撑两小时，他坐过了站，脖子压出颈椎病。三万块买来的，是一种让全车厢都看得见你在独处的方式。"}, "b": {"item_id": "c894b12374a0", "text": "地铁上戴 Vision Pro 的人，我研究过了：花了两万九，最大的需求是让全车人知道。\n\n这玩意儿最大的卖点，是把外面的世界透传给你看。三万块，让你看见了你本来就在的这节车厢。我拿肉眼看，免费，还没延迟。\n\n他还得在空气里捏手指，一会儿放大，一会儿点击。旁边大妈以为他在捉苍蝇，再不然就是在隔空给她算命。\n\n最惨的是，前面那块屏幕专门显示他的眼睛，好让别人觉得他\"在场\"。可全车人透过那双假眼睛，看到的是一个被电池线拴在裤兜里、一动不敢动的人。\n\n电量撑两小时。他还没到站，已经先关机了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0594", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "vision-pro", "target_name": "Apple Vision Pro", "facts": ["Released in February 2024 at $3,499, with an external battery pack on a cable rated for about two hours.", "'EyeSight' shows a rendering of the wearer's eyes on an outward-facing screen.", "Apple calls it 'spatial computing' and its App Store rules tell developers not to describe apps as AR, VR, XR, or MR.", "Reports during 2024 said Apple had cut production; analysts' first-year sales estimates were well under a million units.", "Weighs 600-650 g, before the battery."], "a": {"item_id": "6311e32fb855", "text": "地铁上见过一哥们，戴着Vision Pro。三万块钱扣在脸上，跟把首付戴出门了似的。你图啥？显得有钱？有钱人不坐地铁啊兄弟！\n\n他还挺投入，对着空气捏手指，捏一下，旁边大姐退一步。人家以为他在掐诀，准备做法。\n\n最绝的是前面那块屏，会给你看他的“眼睛”。他一进沉浸模式看电影，那双眼睛就变成一团彩色雾，全车厢都知道：这人灵魂出窍了，肉身暂存二号线。\n\n地铁一刹车，画面飘了，他伸手去抓虚拟窗口，攥住了一个大爷的保温杯。\n\n然后电池没电了，线还从脸上一路连到裤兜里，跟刚做完手术没拔管子似的。续航两小时，刚好够他坐过站。"}, "b": {"item_id": "990fbbeab954", "text": "地铁上戴 Vision Pro 的人，我见过三类。第一类想显摆，结果他脸上扣着一块 3500 美元的玻璃，外屏还亮着他的眼睛，全车人都在和一双发光的眼睛对视，像鱼缸里的金鱼被人盯着看。第二类想沉浸式追剧，他在悬浮的大屏前对着空气捏了五次手指，每次都没对上，像在给一个看不见的人做心肺复苏。第三类最惨，他外接的电池线挂在脖子上，一只手在空中乱点，另一只手拎着电池，他把眼前的 4K 巨幕当成了整个世界，于是一头撞上了真的闸机，而那个闸机，连手势都不用捏。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0595", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "juicero", "target_name": "Juicero 榨汁机", "facts": ["A Wi-Fi-connected cold-press juicer launched in 2016 at $699, later cut to $399, that squeezed proprietary bags of chopped produce sold by subscription.", "Raised about $120 million from investors including Google Ventures and Kleiner Perkins.", "In April 2017, Bloomberg reporters squeezed the packs by hand and got 7.5 ounces of juice in 90 seconds, faster than the machine's 8 ounces in about two minutes.", "Each pack carried a QR code that the press scanned and checked online before squeezing; it refused expired packs and would not run at all unless connected to Wi-Fi.", "Shut down in September 2017 and offered refunds on the machine."], "a": {"item_id": "5d1ab9a2be91", "text": "买Juicero的人，我特别想认识一下。四百美元，买一台榨汁机，还得连Wi-Fi。你家果汁需要上网？它要先扫一下果蔬包上的二维码，确认这包菜没过期，才肯开榨。你花四百块，雇了个电子门卫，专门查蔬菜的身份证。\n\n这帮人图什么？不是图果汁，是图早上站在厨房，对着手机App，看机器庄严地压一包菜，那种\"我是住在未来的人\"的感觉。\n\n结果彭博社发现：不用机器，徒手捏那个袋子，果汁出得更快更多。也就是说，你买了一台输给你双手的机器。\n\n后来公司倒闭了，还好给你退了钱。但你的下场是：成了互联网上最出名的一批人。因为你把\"不会捏袋子\"，做成了一个融资一点二亿美元的生活方式。"}, "b": {"item_id": "2a181ea7d6cd", "text": "我花四百美金买了一台 Juicero。它的工作原理是：把预先切好的果蔬包塞进去，机器扫码确认是正版，然后用很大的压力把汁挤出来。后来有记者发现，那包东西用手就能捏出汁，不用扫码，也不用机器。所以我花四百块买的，其实是一副需要联网验证的手套。\n\n做这个的人我真想见见。一帮硅谷的人觉得榨汁缺一个\"数字化入口\"，拿了一亿多美元风投，就为了让你每周给一袋胡萝卜付订阅费。结果公司关门，机器打折甩卖。我那台现在是家里最贵的摆件，每次看到它，我都觉得它在等我扫码。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0596", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "monopoly", "target_name": "大富翁（Monopoly）", "facts": ["Descends from Lizzie Magie's The Landlord's Game, patented in 1904 to teach the evils of land monopoly in the spirit of Henry George.", "Charles Darrow sold his version to Parker Brothers in 1935 and was credited as the inventor; Parker bought Magie's patent for $500.", "The official rules require any unbought property to be auctioned, and 'Free Parking' pays nothing; the house rules that skip the auction and pile cash on Free Parking are why games drag.", "During the Second World War, British intelligence had maps, compasses, and real currency hidden in sets sent to prisoner-of-war camps."], "a": {"item_id": "a8f6340d1edd", "text": "过年一家人想图个团圆，掏出大富翁。这游戏设计得特别科学：专门把亲情换算成租金。\n\n我爸永远当银行，所以他既是央行又是裁判，还偷偷往自己那堆里放钱。我妈见一块买一块，一块不卖，说“留着传给你”，我说妈，这是纸。我弟更狠，四个火车站全买了，我每路过一次就交一次钱，对亲姐姐收费收得比春运还顺手。\n\n我走到他的酒店，一千五。我说我是你姐，他说：“那给你打个折，一千四。”\n\n最后我抵押了公用事业，进了监狱，全家居然都希望我多待几轮，因为我在里面不用交租。\n\n图的是团圆，落得的是游戏还没破产，亲戚关系先清算了。\n\n我一掀桌，我爸很冷静：“重来一局。还是我当银行。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "a7faf659e2b2", "text": "每年过年，总有一个人提议：“一家人玩个大富翁，热闹。”你想热闹？你想看你爸抢着当银行，发钱的时候顺手往自己那堆塞两张五百？你想看你妈踩了你的酒店，说“都是一家人，房租就免了”，轮到你踩她的南京路，她把地契往桌上一拍：“亲兄弟明算账。”你小姨夫拿两条破铁路换你的地，那个眼神，跟当年找你爸借三万块一模一样。玩到凌晨两点没人破产，因为奶奶偷偷往各家塞钱：“孩子进监狱了，快拿钱捞出来。”最后桌子一掀，三家人一年不说话。你图团圆？大富翁教会你一件事：一家人能坐一桌吃饭，是因为桌上没有地契。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0597", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "beanie-babies", "target_name": "Beanie Babies 豆豆娃", "facts": ["Launched by Ty Inc. in 1993 and sold only through small gift shops, with designs 'retired' to manufacture scarcity.", "At the height of the craze Beanie Babies accounted for about 10% of eBay's sales, and eBay's 1998 IPO filing said the site at times had over 30,000 Beanie Baby auctions running simultaneously in the quarter ending June 1998.", "In 1999 a divorcing couple divided their Beanie Baby collection one by one on a courtroom floor, in a photograph that ran worldwide.", "In 1999 Ty announced that Beanie Babies would be retired at the end of the year, then reversed itself after asking the public to vote and seeing an overwhelming vote to keep them.", "Most 'rare' Beanies that price guides valued in the thousands now sell for a few dollars."], "a": {"item_id": "a30c3de668a8", "text": "我表舅九十年代一口气囤了三百多只豆豆娃，每只屁股上的小吊牌都套三层塑料袋，连摸都不让摸。他说这叫抗通胀。后来厂家宣布\"退休\"，他激动得三天没睡，觉得自己是股神。结果呢？退休那批，现在二手平台一只八块包邮，买家还嫌吊牌有折痕。他家客厅的柜子搬到了阁楼，女儿问那是什么，他说：\"这是我的养老金。\"女儿说：\"爸，养老金能当饭吃吗？\""}, "b": {"item_id": "5b893c7de1a5", "text": "九十年代有一批美国大人，坚信一只塞满塑料颗粒的小熊能供孩子上大学。怎么投资呢？不让孩子碰。孩子想抱一下，爸妈拦住：别摸，一摸就贬值。这是养儿子还是供祖宗？他们还给心形吊牌单独买塑料保护壳，熊可以脏，牌不能折，那张纸才是资产。抢戴安娜王妃熊抢到打架，却没发现厂商一边喊“限量退休”，一边接着生产。你跟印钞的人比谁囤得多？后来有夫妻离婚，在法庭上分豆豆娃，分完发现整整一车，卖了不够付律师费。现在三百只堆在车库，挂网上五美元一只，没人要。孩子没上成大学，但学到一课：在爸爸心里，我排在一只带吊牌的熊后面。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0598", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "comic-sans", "target_name": "Comic Sans 字体", "facts": ["Designed by Vincent Connare at Microsoft in 1994, modeled on the comic-book lettering in The Dark Knight Returns and Watchmen.", "Made for the speech bubbles of Rover, the cartoon dog in Microsoft Bob; it missed Bob's release and shipped instead in the Windows 95 Plus! pack.", "CERN's July 2012 Higgs boson discovery announcement was presented on slides set in it.", "The 'Ban Comic Sans' campaign, started by designers Dave and Holly Combs in 1999, ran until 2019, when they retired it and launched usecomicsans.com."], "a": {"item_id": "130c2658ebda", "text": "我们小区物业有个行政大姐，发正式通知特别爱用Comic Sans。你知道这字体当年是干嘛的吗？微软设计给卡通小狗说话用的，专门往对话气泡里塞的。所以每回停水通知一贴，我都觉得是一只小狗在跟我说：“明天停水哦～”\n\n她图什么？图亲切。坏消息配这字体，就跟打针前先给你看张奥特曼贴纸一样。\n\n上个月涨物业费，她又用Comic Sans，那个“8”圆得像在冲你眨眼。全楼没一个当真，都以为是谁家小孩的恶作剧，撕下来折纸飞机了。年底一查，欠费率百分之八十。\n\n领导问她怎么回事，她理直气壮：“欧洲核子研究中心宣布发现希格斯玻色子都用这字体！”\n\n领导说：“对，所以全世界到现在还在笑他们。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "8384fe94668e", "text": "我们公司人事上周发裁员通知，用的Comic Sans。我问为什么，她说：“想柔和一点，减少冲击。”\n\n姐姐，这字体当年是给微软一只叫Rover的卡通狗设计的，专门用来写对话气泡。你这是让一条狗通知我下岗。\n\n结果呢？“因业务调整，您的岗位将被取消”，每个字都圆滚滚、歪歪扭扭，读起来就是“汪！你被开除啦～”\n\n冲击确实没有，因为根本没人信。有个哥们儿回复了一串“哈哈哈”，周一照常来上班，被保安拦在门口，还委屈：“你们那个通知不是在搞笑吗？”\n\n最惨的是人事自己，月底被优化了，通知也是Comic Sans。她盯着屏幕看了十分钟，说：“这肯定是恶作剧。”\n\n行，你的柔和，终于轮到你自己了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0599", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "crocs", "target_name": "Crocs 洞洞鞋", "facts": ["Founded in Boulder, Colorado, in 2002; the first clog debuted at the Fort Lauderdale boat show as a boating shoe.", "Made of 'Croslite', which Crocs describes as a proprietary closed-cell resin foam and which third parties identify as an injection-moulded EVA foam.", "Balenciaga sold a platform Crocs for about $850 in 2018.", "The stock fell more than 90% from its 2007 peak during the 2008 crisis; the company then boomed in the pandemic and gave away 860,000 pairs to healthcare workers in 2020.", "The holes are officially 'ventilation ports' and the charms that go in them are 'Jibbitz', a company Crocs bought in 2006."], "a": {"item_id": "d0de4b131b62", "text": "穿洞洞鞋出门的人，分两种：真懒的，和装懒的。装懒的最好认，鞋上插满智必星，小恐龙、小汉堡、小皮卡丘，一只脚背就是一个摊位。你图舒服我理解，你在鞋上开连锁店干什么？\n\n他们还有个绝招：把脑门上那根带子往后一掰，“我切换运动模式了”。兄弟，带子掰过去，你是能跑过谁？\n\n下场都一样。下雨天，鞋面上那十几个洞全是进水口，走一步咕叽一声。晴天更惨，小石子从洞里钻进去卡在脚心，又不能当街脱，只能单腿跳着甩，像在给鞋做心肺复苏。\n\n回家一看，智必星掉了三个。人是舒服了，资产缩水了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "061b4ce9b529", "text": "穿洞洞鞋出门的人，我大概知道是谁。嘴上说\"我就下楼拿个快递\"，结果一路走进商场、电影院，还有前任的婚礼。\n\n图什么呢？图舒服。舒服到觉得全世界都该给你的脚松绑。松绑还不够，还得在洞里插一排小鞋花：皮卡丘、小黄瓜、一个不知道哪来的西瓜。三十岁的人，脚上挂得像幼儿园书包。\n\n下场呢？下雨天，那些洞全是进水口，每走一步，脚底\"噗叽\"一声。上电扶梯，软塌塌的鞋帮被一口咬住，保安跑得比你妈都快。\n\n你图的是不用系鞋带的自由，换来的是单脚蹦着，在全商场的注视下，去捡一只挂着小黄瓜的鞋。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0600", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "google-sheets", "target_name": "Google Sheets", "facts": ["Launched in June 2006 as Google Spreadsheets, built on XL2Web, a product Google acquired from 2Web Technologies.", "A spreadsheet is capped at 10 million cells (raised from 5 million in 2022).", "Functions such as IMPORTHTML, IMPORTXML, and GOOGLEFINANCE pull live web data straight into cells.", "Free with a Google account; files live in Google Drive, and collaborators edit the same sheet simultaneously with coloured cursors.", "In a browser, offline editing works only in Chrome or Edge, with the Google Docs Offline extension installed and offline access switched on while still connected; the Android and iOS apps work offline natively."], "a": {"item_id": "81690243de7c", "text": "每个团队都有这么一个人，说：“不用买Jira，太重了，我拿Google Sheets搞一下就行。”他不是图省钱，是图全公司只有他看得懂第七个标签页。\n\n三个月后，表里十四个标签页，条件格式红黄绿：红是延期，黄是风险，绿是“没人来骂我”。有个标签页叫“勿动”，全组都动过。\n\n直到有天，同事只选了B列，点了“A到Z排序”。从此任务和负责人各过各的，张三在干李四的活，李四在给一个叫“待定”的人打下手。\n\n最后他休年假，全组停摆，因为只有他知道IMPORTRANGE为什么报#REF!。他离职那天，交接文档就一个链接，点开写着“需要访问权限”，申请已自动发送给：他的个人邮箱。"}, "b": {"item_id": "c5f3c3c4263e", "text": "用 Google Sheets 管团队的，一般是创业公司那位“运营兼 HR 兼老板小舅子”。图啥？图免费，图“实时协作，公开透明”。透明是真透明：你凌晨两点偷偷改自己的绩效，右上角冒出一只“匿名水豚”，光标就停在你那一行。全公司这个点还不睡的水豚，只有老板一个。\n\n更绝的是筛选。实习生按“已离职”一点，全公司的表同时只剩离职的人，老板以为昨晚裁员了。\n\n最后他用 IMPORTRANGE 把十二张表串成一张，终于实现了自动化：每天早上一打开，满屏 #REF!。他打开版本历史一条条往回翻，翻到最早那条：“由张伟创建”。那时候他还有头发。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0601", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "esperanto", "target_name": "世界语", "facts": ["Published in Warsaw in 1887 by L. L. Zamenhof, a Białystok-born eye doctor, under the pen name Doktoro Esperanto, 'one who hopes'.", "Speaker estimates range from 100,000 to two million; roughly a thousand people grow up with it as a native language.", "William Shatner starred in Incubus (1966), a horror film shot entirely in Esperanto, with pronunciation Esperantists still mock.", "Its Wikipedia has over 350,000 articles, and its Duolingo course for English speakers had 1.36 million learners by July 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "c6f136a1221b", "text": "我认识一个学世界语的。他跟我说，世界语只有十六条语法规则，一个月就能学会。没错，他一个月就学会了，然后花了十年找一个能跟他说上话的人。\n\n他图什么？图世界大同。柴门霍夫一八八七年发明它，是想让全人类共用一门第二语言。一百多年过去了，它还真成了所有人的第二语言：第二个“回头再说”的语言。\n\n他现在出国不住酒店，靠“护照服务”睡在别的世界语者家沙发上。俩人见面热泪盈眶，互称“同志”，聊了一宿。聊的是：怎么才能让更多人学世界语。\n\n我问他这辈子算不算失败。他说不算，世界语里没有“失败”，只有mal-成功。“成功”前面加个mal-，意思是成功的反面。\n\n哥们儿，翻译过来，那还是失败啊。"}, "b": {"item_id": "ff5e551b6c61", "text": "学世界语的人，我一般叫他们“希望者”。这词本来就是世界语自己起的，意思是“怀抱希望的人”，连名字都在提醒你别抱太大希望。这帮人多半是退休大爷，或者是想拯救世界的文艺中年。他们图啥？图全世界用一种语言聊天，不用再被英语六级追着跑。学了三年，名词加o，形容词加a，动词变位一次没错，十六条语法背得比谁都熟。然后呢？全世界会说世界语的人，凑一桌麻将都勉强。好不容易在周末的世界语角碰上一个外国人，是从匈牙利来的，两人聊了半小时，最后发现彼此都是看视频自学的，口音一个比一个离谱。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0602", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "almond-milk", "target_name": "杏仁奶", "facts": ["California grows about 80% of the world's almonds.", "A single almond takes about 1.1 US gallons (roughly 4 litres) of water to grow, a figure Mother Jones published in 2014 that spread during California's drought.", "A 2015 US class action alleged Almond Breeze contained only about 2% almonds; it partly survived a motion to dismiss, was stayed in 2017 pending a separate Blue Diamond labelling settlement, and the plaintiffs then dropped it.", "Pollinating California's almond orchards each February requires trucking in most of the commercial honeybee colonies in the United States.", "Medieval European cooks used almond milk as a Lenten substitute for dairy, centuries before the current fashion."], "a": {"item_id": "9b69772a0059", "text": "我有个朋友，为了环保，把牛奶戒了，改喝杏仁奶。天天端着一杯杏仁拿铁，跟我上课：“你知道吗？牛放屁，会导致全球变暖。”\n\n行，牛放屁。那你知道一颗杏仁要喝多少水吗？差不多四升。你每天一盒杏仁奶，等于亲自帮加州又旱了一个夏天。\n\n而且那一盒里，杏仁只占百分之二，剩下全是水。你花三十八块，买的是一瓶被杏仁路过的自来水。\n\n更惨的是，加州的杏仁全靠蜜蜂授粉。每年几百亿只蜜蜂被卡车拉过去打工，累死一大片。\n\n所以我这朋友，替地球省了一头牛的屁，搭进去好几个蜂群。他现在一端起拿铁就心虚，听见“嗡”一声，都想鞠躬道歉。"}, "b": {"item_id": "2a93fb2cbc64", "text": "我同事上个月宣布改喝杏仁奶，说是为了环保，为了少一头牛放屁。我就问他：你知道一颗杏仁要用四升水才能长出来吗？他说知道，但牛更费水。行，我又问：你这杯杏仁奶，杏仁占多少？他说，大部分吧。我说，百分之二。\n\n四十五块钱，买了一杯百分之九十八是水的东西，还是加州大旱那边的水。\n\n更绝的是，加州杏仁树要靠蜜蜂授粉，蜂群是卡车拉过去租的，累死一大片。所以他一个吃素的，为了不伤害牛，给蜜蜂送了终。\n\n最后，他在咖啡里一倒，杏仁奶当场分层，絮状物漂起来，像分手现场。他还是端着喝完了，发朋友圈：“为地球做了一点小事。”\n\n地球没拯救，拿铁凉了，蜜蜂走了，他还得去厕所。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0603", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "latex", "target_name": "LaTeX", "facts": ["Leslie Lamport wrote LaTeX in the early 1980s and released it in 1984 as a macro layer on Donald Knuth's TeX (1978), which Knuth built because he disliked how the galley proofs of the new edition of his own book looked.", "TeX's version number converges on π (3.141592653 since 2021), and Knuth's TeX bug bounty started at one hexadecimal dollar, $2.56, doubling yearly until frozen at $327.68; he stopped mailing real cheques in 2008 after fraud and now credits accounts at his fictional Bank of San Serriffe, and only 9 of the 275 cheques he sent from 2006 on were ever cashed.", "Its best-known warning is 'Underfull \\hbox (badness 10000)'; overfull lines are reported as 'Overfull \\hbox (Npt too wide)', and figures float to wherever the algorithm decides, not where the author put them.", "A single missing brace can produce an error reported pages away from the mistake, and a document can need three compiler passes to get its references right."], "a": {"item_id": "cc677fdb9115", "text": "领导让写个备忘录，内容就一句话：聚餐从周五改到周四。他说，Word？那是给没追求的人用的。打开Overleaf，先配ctex，再装十几个宏包，再调页边距。三小时后，这句话终于有了页眉、目录，还有参考文献，聚餐通知，引了两条。\n\n然后他在里面打了句“人均50$”，编译报错：Missing $ inserted。他的聚餐，被当成了数学公式。\n\n改完之后，“谢谢”两个字孤零零掉到第三页。他又花四十分钟调行距，就为了把这两个字塞回去。\n\n同事回复：“收到，能发个Word版吗？我想改个字。”\n\n他看着那个PDF，终于明白：他排的不是备忘录，是一座纪念碑，漂亮，工整，谁都不敢动。"}, "b": {"item_id": "8f8bc08e77f4", "text": "我们组有个哥们，博士毕业三年，还活在答辩那天。领导让他写两页备忘录，通知周五团建，他打开的是TeXstudio。\n\n图什么？他说：“Computer Modern字体，一看就是发过论文的。”兄弟，团建通知没有审稿人。\n\n开头先写十五行\\usepackage，中文还得装ctex，pdflatex不认，换xelatex。终于编译，报错：“Missing $ inserted”。因为他写了“人均预算$200”，LaTeX以为他要开始推公式了。\n\n改完再编，“经费超支20%，请自理”，逗号后面全没了，百分号把后半句当注释吃了。\n\n周五大家到了，饭店没订。备忘录上地址那栏写着：“详见第??页。”他忘了再编译一遍。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0604", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hawaiian-pizza", "target_name": "夏威夷披萨", "facts": ["Invented in 1962 by Sam Panopoulos, a Greek immigrant, at the Satellite Restaurant in Chatham, Ontario, Canada; named after the brand of canned pineapple he used.", "Iceland's president Guðni Th. Jóhannesson said in 2017 that he would ban pineapple on pizza if he could, then clarified that a president has no such power.", "Hawaii is no longer a major pineapple producer: Del Monte shut its Hawaiian pineapple operations in November 2006, two years ahead of its announced 2008 exit, the state's last cannery closed in 2007, and most canned pineapple now comes from Thailand, Indonesia, and the Philippines.", "Panopoulos, whose restaurant also served Chinese dishes, told the Toronto Sun in 2010 that 'in those days people didn't mix the tastes like sweet and sour, except for Chinese food'."], "a": {"item_id": "0878d1084313", "text": "每桌都有那么一个人，菜单一翻就说：“来个夏威夷的。”全桌瞬间安静，像有人宣布要在群里发起投票。\n\n他图什么？图人设。他想让你知道他不随大流，可他点的是每家店菜单上都有的第三个披萨。他心里还有本账：菠萝算水果，火腿算肉，一块下去，营养均衡。\n\n下场呢？别人点的都见底了，只剩他那盘，菠萝汁把饼底泡软，拎起来像拎一条热毛巾。他还硬撑：“真的好吃，你们不懂。”\n\n你知道夏威夷披萨是谁发明的吗？一个住在加拿大的希腊人。夏威夷不认，意大利不承认，全世界最后就剩你一个人替它出头。"}, "b": {"item_id": "2a083e43e4a9", "text": "点夏威夷披萨的人，我大概能猜出两种。一种是想显得自己没偏见，另一种是他妈从小就给他点这个。前一种会先举着手机拍那几块菠萝，配文“勇敢的人才吃”。吃到第三口，菠萝汁已经顺着饼底流进盘子，火腿片泡得发白，他还是硬着头皮说“层次很丰富”。层次确实丰富，甜、咸、油、软在嘴里打成一团，最后赢的是芝士，输的是他的胃。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0605", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "crocs", "target_name": "Crocs 洞洞鞋", "facts": ["Founded in Boulder, Colorado, in 2002; the first clog debuted at the Fort Lauderdale boat show as a boating shoe.", "Made of 'Croslite', which Crocs describes as a proprietary closed-cell resin foam and which third parties identify as an injection-moulded EVA foam.", "Balenciaga sold a platform Crocs for about $850 in 2018.", "The stock fell more than 90% from its 2007 peak during the 2008 crisis; the company then boomed in the pandemic and gave away 860,000 pairs to healthcare workers in 2020.", "The holes are officially 'ventilation ports' and the charms that go in them are 'Jibbitz', a company Crocs bought in 2006."], "a": {"item_id": "e55404ed5b22", "text": "你们知道穿洞洞鞋出门的都是什么人吗？一种是刚下飞机、防晒霜还没抹匀的人，另一种是觉得\"我就是不在乎形象\"的人。图什么？图脚舒服，图鞋上那些小挂件能挂满一圈，走路叮叮当当，像个移动的钥匙串。结果呢？去趟海边，洞洞里灌满沙子，走一步倒一把，像在给沙滩做漏斗。下雨天更惨，鞋底那几个孔等于给雨水开了后门，袜子从脚踝湿到脚趾。最要命的是全程还得踩着\"吧唧吧唧\"的声音进电梯，全电梯的人都知道你来了。后来他真的不出门了，因为他终于明白，别人不是看不见他，是听得见他。"}, "b": {"item_id": "c92c7ce6304b", "text": "我发现穿洞洞鞋出门的人，都有一套标准说辞：“丑是丑，但舒服啊。”舒服的东西多了，你怎么不裹着被窝去上班？\n\n嘴上说图舒服，转头往鞋洞里插满鞋花：十二只小恐龙、三块披萨、一个奥特曼。鞋八十，花四百。你这不是穿鞋，是脚上开了个小卖部。\n\n后跟那根带子，平时翻在脚背上叫“休闲模式”，一跑起来立马扳下去，叫“运动模式”。全身上下，就这根带子有档位。\n\n下场也很统一：一下雨，每个洞都是进水口，走一步“噗叽”一声；天一晴，一颗小石子钻进洞里怎么都甩不出来，只能当街单脚跳，像只刚学走路的火烈鸟。\n\n图舒服的人，最后全蹲在路边倒石子。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0606", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "nfts", "target_name": "NFT", "facts": ["Beeple's 'Everydays: The First 5000 Days' sold at Christie's for $69.3 million in March 2021.", "OpenSea, the largest NFT marketplace, processed a record $2.7 billion in trades on 1 May 2022 and just $9.34 million on 28 August 2022, a 99% drop, according to DappRadar data.", "A 2023 dappGambl study found that 95% of the NFT collections it examined had a market value of zero.", "The token usually records only a URL; the image itself sits on an ordinary server or IPFS, not on the blockchain.", "Collins Dictionary named 'NFT' its word of the year for 2021."], "a": {"item_id": "7d289cdaf821", "text": "2021年买NFT的哥们，我特别理解。他们不是想买画，是想买一张\"我比你早知道\"的门票。\n\n我有个朋友，花了几十万买了只戴水手帽的猴子。我说：\"这图我右键就能存啊。\"他很严肃地说：\"你存的是图，我拥有的是……\"他想了三秒，\"……一个指向图的链接。\"兄弟，你花几十万买了个超链接。\n\n买完他就入教了。头像换成猴子，天天在Discord里跟一个叫\"猿猴老王\"的陌生人互道\"gm\"，互相喊\"WAGMI\"，意思是我们都会发财。\n\n现在地板价跌了九成多，他想挂单卖，结果上架的gas费比成交价还贵。\n\n问他怎么办，他说：\"我是长期持有。\"\n\n不，你不是长期持有，你是没人接盘。你唯一的资产，是那个Discord群里还没退群的三个人。"}, "b": {"item_id": "812b8f18c10a", "text": "2021年买NFT的那批人，我太懂了。花九千块买一只无聊猴子的截图，换成头像，每天早上第一件事是看地板价。朋友问他买的啥，他说这是资产。问资产在哪，他指着手机说：在链上。我说那你右键另存为一下，他说那叫盗版，法律不允许。转一次账，手续费比猴子还贵，他还觉得这叫门槛。后来项目方拿着钱跑了，猴子还在，官网打不开了，他的头像成了全网最贵的一张\"此页面无法访问\"。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0607", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "crocs", "target_name": "Crocs 洞洞鞋", "facts": ["Founded in Boulder, Colorado, in 2002; the first clog debuted at the Fort Lauderdale boat show as a boating shoe.", "Made of 'Croslite', which Crocs describes as a proprietary closed-cell resin foam and which third parties identify as an injection-moulded EVA foam.", "Balenciaga sold a platform Crocs for about $850 in 2018.", "The stock fell more than 90% from its 2007 peak during the 2008 crisis; the company then boomed in the pandemic and gave away 860,000 pairs to healthcare workers in 2020.", "The holes are officially 'ventilation ports' and the charms that go in them are 'Jibbitz', a company Crocs bought in 2006."], "a": {"item_id": "c92c7ce6304b", "text": "我发现穿洞洞鞋出门的人，都有一套标准说辞：“丑是丑，但舒服啊。”舒服的东西多了，你怎么不裹着被窝去上班？\n\n嘴上说图舒服，转头往鞋洞里插满鞋花：十二只小恐龙、三块披萨、一个奥特曼。鞋八十，花四百。你这不是穿鞋，是脚上开了个小卖部。\n\n后跟那根带子，平时翻在脚背上叫“休闲模式”，一跑起来立马扳下去，叫“运动模式”。全身上下，就这根带子有档位。\n\n下场也很统一：一下雨，每个洞都是进水口，走一步“噗叽”一声；天一晴，一颗小石子钻进洞里怎么都甩不出来，只能当街单脚跳，像只刚学走路的火烈鸟。\n\n图舒服的人，最后全蹲在路边倒石子。"}, "b": {"item_id": "061b4ce9b529", "text": "穿洞洞鞋出门的人，我大概知道是谁。嘴上说\"我就下楼拿个快递\"，结果一路走进商场、电影院，还有前任的婚礼。\n\n图什么呢？图舒服。舒服到觉得全世界都该给你的脚松绑。松绑还不够，还得在洞里插一排小鞋花：皮卡丘、小黄瓜、一个不知道哪来的西瓜。三十岁的人，脚上挂得像幼儿园书包。\n\n下场呢？下雨天，那些洞全是进水口，每走一步，脚底\"噗叽\"一声。上电扶梯，软塌塌的鞋帮被一口咬住，保安跑得比你妈都快。\n\n你图的是不用系鞋带的自由，换来的是单脚蹦着，在全商场的注视下，去捡一只挂着小黄瓜的鞋。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0608", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "keurig", "target_name": "Keurig 胶囊咖啡机", "facts": ["John Sylvan invented the K-Cup in the early 1990s and sold his stake for about $50,000 in 1997; in 2015 he told The Atlantic, 'I feel bad sometimes that I ever did it.'", "Keurig 2.0 (2014) read an ink mark on the lid and rejected unlicensed pods; sales fell and workarounds spread online.", "By 2014 enough K-Cups were sold each year to circle the Earth more than ten times; the pods were mostly unrecyclable #7 plastic until a switch to polypropylene by 2020.", "Keurig Green Mountain merged with Dr Pepper Snapple in 2018 to form Keurig Dr Pepper."], "a": {"item_id": "6e62a676c319", "text": "买 Keurig 的人，特点是：嫌磨豆子麻烦，但不嫌贵。一颗胶囊里就十克咖啡粉，折下来一斤两百多块，他们管这叫“省事”。买就买了，还非买那种四十八颗混合装，榛果、焦糖、南瓜派。大清早喝一口南瓜派味的咖啡，你说你是在提神，还是在给自己过万圣节？喝完还得把那个塑料小杯子抠出来扔掉，一年下来垃圾桶里全是小杯子，像开了家微型保龄球馆。最离谱的是，这玩意儿的发明人自己都公开说后悔发明了它。你想想，你每天早上，一颗一颗地给一个连发明人都后悔的东西交钱，还觉得自己活得挺高效。"}, "b": {"item_id": "612741b2093f", "text": "你们办公室里有没有这种人？每天早上站在Keurig前面，按下去，等它“滋滋”响半分钟，端出一杯颜色像红茶、味道像热水的东西。他图什么？图的是“我也是讲究生活的人”。可这份讲究一个K杯一块多，一个月下来够买一台手冲壶了。更惨的是垃圾，那个小塑料杯带铝箔盖，还得掰开、分类，最后扔进去的还是塑料。他以为买的是咖啡，其实买的是“按一下就有”的错觉。结果一天一杯，喝到最后，他端着的是一杯加了热水的回忆。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0609", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "nuclear-fusion", "target_name": "可控核聚变", "facts": ["Has been 'about 30 years away' since the 1950s; at the 1955 Geneva atoms-for-peace conference, Homi Bhabha predicted fusion power within two decades.", "ITER, agreed in 2006 for first plasma in 2016, re-baselined in 2024 to start research operations in 2034 and deuterium-tritium burning in 2039, at a cost estimated above €20 billion.", "NIF's December 2022 'ignition' shot released 3.15 MJ from 2.05 MJ of laser energy; the lasers drew about 300 MJ from the grid to fire.", "Private fusion companies had raised more than $7 billion by 2024."], "a": {"item_id": "a3d17d6a7412", "text": "搞可控核聚变的人，是地球上唯一靠“还有十年”吃了一辈子饭的职业。五十年代说三十年，后来说五十年，现在说十年，你看，人家还在降价。\n\n图啥？图经费。“十年”这个数太精妙了：近到评审愿意批钱，远到项目负责人退休前不用验收。ITER在法国盖了快二十年，预算翻了好几倍。一亿度的等离子体他们说能控住，控不住的是工期。\n\n最后什么下场？老教授退休那天，把“还有十年”郑重交给博士生，跟传家宝似的。博士生一看：我二十五岁入行，原来是接了个祖传手艺。\n\n这炉子烧的不是氘和氚，是一代代人的头发。"}, "b": {"item_id": "46363a503661", "text": "“可控核聚变还有十年”，这句话是物理学界的传家宝。五十年代爷爷说，八十年代儿子说，现在孙子接着说，一个字都没改，比族谱还准。\n\n你以为他们在预测未来？不，他们在报经费周期。说三年，到期交不了差；说五十年，领导听完直接去开下一个会；说十年，刚好够干到退休，然后把这句话连同办公室钥匙一起交给学生。\n\n最惨的是什么？他们真把等离子体烧到了一亿度，比太阳核心还热好几倍，撑了几十秒，开发布会报喜。可他们自己的职称，在同一个岗位上冻了二十年。\n\n全人类只有这群人能造出一个小太阳，却暖不了自己的工资条。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0610", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "canberra", "target_name": "堪培拉", "facts": ["Chosen as a compromise because neither Sydney nor Melbourne would accept the other as capital; Australia's constitution required the capital to be at least 100 miles from Sydney.", "Designed by the Chicago architects Walter Burley Griffin and Marion Mahony Griffin, who won an international competition in 1912.", "Its centrepiece, Lake Burley Griffin, is artificial, created by damming the Molonglo River in 1963-64.", "Parliament House (1988) is built into Capital Hill under a lawn meant for citizens to walk over their politicians; the lawn was fenced off in 2017 for security.", "Melbourne served as the capital until 1927 while Canberra was being built."], "a": {"item_id": "46416cb1f80d", "text": "你身边肯定有这种人：为了“离权力近一点”，从悉尼搬去堪培拉。公务员、政策顾问、游说公司的，图的都是升职。三年后，他们每天开四十分钟车去一栋楼，写四十页报告，结论永远是“建议进一步研究”。周五下午，全城人开三小时车回悉尼，就剩他们守着伯利·格里芬湖。全城最大的娱乐是绕湖跑步，跑完一圈，正好跑回办公室。权力确实在附近，就在国会大厦里，可每次想见，都得先预约，还得等部长助理回邮件。离权力近了，离生活远了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "a93ae6b79980", "text": "有一种人我特别佩服：为了工作搬去堪培拉。问他图什么，他说：“稳定，公务员，铁饭碗。”行，铁饭碗，里面装的是空气。\n\n他们的周末是这样的：周六逛一遍Canberra Centre，周日再逛一遍，因为周六没买。想刺激一点？开车绕国会山的环岛，一圈，两圈，三圈，这就是全城夜生活。而且开错一个出口，你就出了首都领地，进新南威尔士了。\n\n第一年他说“我喜欢安静”；第二年开始对着格里芬湖说心里话；第三年每个月飞悉尼，不是玩，是想确认世界上还有陌生人。\n\n最惨的是升职。升到哪儿？换个部门，在马路对面。政府一换届，部门改名，他人没动，只是名片重印。这是堪培拉人一生里最大的变故。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0611", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "juicero", "target_name": "Juicero 榨汁机", "facts": ["A Wi-Fi-connected cold-press juicer launched in 2016 at $699, later cut to $399, that squeezed proprietary bags of chopped produce sold by subscription.", "Raised about $120 million from investors including Google Ventures and Kleiner Perkins.", "In April 2017, Bloomberg reporters squeezed the packs by hand and got 7.5 ounces of juice in 90 seconds, faster than the machine's 8 ounces in about two minutes.", "Each pack carried a QR code that the press scanned and checked online before squeezing; it refused expired packs and would not run at all unless connected to Wi-Fi.", "Shut down in September 2017 and offered refunds on the machine."], "a": {"item_id": "5d1ab9a2be91", "text": "买Juicero的人，我特别想认识一下。四百美元，买一台榨汁机，还得连Wi-Fi。你家果汁需要上网？它要先扫一下果蔬包上的二维码，确认这包菜没过期，才肯开榨。你花四百块，雇了个电子门卫，专门查蔬菜的身份证。\n\n这帮人图什么？不是图果汁，是图早上站在厨房，对着手机App，看机器庄严地压一包菜，那种\"我是住在未来的人\"的感觉。\n\n结果彭博社发现：不用机器，徒手捏那个袋子，果汁出得更快更多。也就是说，你买了一台输给你双手的机器。\n\n后来公司倒闭了，还好给你退了钱。但你的下场是：成了互联网上最出名的一批人。因为你把\"不会捏袋子\"，做成了一个融资一点二亿美元的生活方式。"}, "b": {"item_id": "90365272a695", "text": "花400美元买Juicero的都是什么人？就是那种觉得“我自己的手不够智能”的人。你买的根本不是榨汁机，是一台连WiFi的拳头。它这辈子唯一的工作，就是捏一包早就榨好的菜泥。彭博社记者徒手去捏，比它还快。想想那画面：硅谷精英每天早上站在厨房，等机器扫完二维码、联网确认这包菜没过期，它才肯开工。为了“健康生活”，每包再掏五六块，喝的其实是自己的智商汁。后来公司倒闭，机器连不上服务器，成了一块四百刀的案板。唯一还能用的，是你那双手。恭喜，绕了一大圈，你终于恢复了出厂设置。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0612", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "graphene", "target_name": "石墨烯", "facts": ["Isolated in 2004 by Andre Geim and Konstantin Novoselov at Manchester by peeling graphite with Scotch tape; Nobel Prize in Physics, 2010.", "The EU's Graphene Flagship, launched in 2013, committed €1 billion over ten years.", "A 2018 study in Advanced Materials tested 'graphene' from 60 producers and found none with more than 50% graphene content; most was graphite flakes.", "Sold in tennis rackets, running shoes, and headphones as a performance ingredient.", "Geim also won an Ig Nobel Prize in 2000 for levitating a frog with magnets."], "a": {"item_id": "20a7d109770e", "text": "我有个同学读材料的，逢人就说石墨烯要改变世界。每次开场一模一样：2004年，两个科学家拿胶带从铅笔芯上粘下一层碳，拿了诺贝尔奖。他讲得特别骄傲，好像那卷胶带是他家的。\n\n我问他：比钢硬，比铜导电，那你怎么还合租？他说量产有瓶颈。十年了，这瓶颈比石墨烯还硬。\n\n后来他创业，产品终于出来了：石墨烯保暖内衣。改变世界的材料，最后改变的是秋裤的价格。铅笔芯的成分，卖出羽绒服的价，还跟我说发热是黑科技。大哥，那是你自己急的。\n\n现在他融不到钱了，开始去我妈的养生群讲课。我妈听完说：小伙子，你这套跟我买的磁疗枕头是一个路数。"}, "b": {"item_id": "5c660764dbab", "text": "我有个表舅，逢人就说石墨烯要改变世界。他说这玩意儿就一个原子厚，比钢硬两百倍，导电导热全是第一。我问他：那你拿它干嘛了？他说：卖秋裤。石墨烯发热秋裤，一条六百八。\n\n表舅研究过，诺贝尔奖得主是拿透明胶带从铅笔芯上粘出来的。他一听，门槛不高啊，家里胶带有的是。二〇一五年，他把养老钱全押进了石墨烯概念股，从此真的改变了世界，先改变的是他自己的。\n\n现在他还在说。石墨烯什么都能干，就是出不了实验室；表舅什么都敢说，就是出不了我们家群。\n\n过年我问他，世界改变了吗？他把秋裤往上一拽：“你摸，热不热！”\n\n热。里头是电热丝。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0613", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "klingon", "target_name": "克林贡语", "facts": ["Created by linguist Marc Okrand for Star Trek III (1984), building on a few words the actor James Doohan improvised for the 1979 film.", "Deliberately alien grammar: object-verb-subject word order and no verb 'to be', so Hamlet's line became 'taH pagh taHbe'', 'continue or not continue'.", "The Klingon Language Institute (1992) published Hamlet 'in the original Klingon'; Bing Translator added Klingon in 2013 and Duolingo in 2018.", "Okrand's 1985 Klingon Dictionary listed roughly 1,500 words, and fluent speakers number only in the dozens: 20 to 30 in 2009, 30 to 50 by 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "c9277fcc0b88", "text": "练到流利克林贡语的人，我真心佩服。白天是会计，晚上披着披风，图什么？图终于有人配得上他的愤怒。结果呢，这门语言没有“你好”，打招呼只有一句“nuqneH”，意思是“你要干嘛？”。三年苦功，换来全世界几百个同好，见面第一句就是找茬。\n\n更惨的是语序。克林贡语是宾语、动词、主语，你骂人得先说被骂的是谁、骂了什么，最后才说是谁干的。一句狠话憋到末尾才亮出主语，气势全靠肺活量。\n\n回家对老婆表白，没有“谢谢”，没有“对不起”，这门语言压根没给你认错的词。想道歉？只能把对方打一顿再说“你赢了”。\n\n所以流利的下场就是：全世界会说的人聚在一个群里，用克林贡语吵架。吵了三个小时，谁也没听懂谁，因为谁都在等对方把主语说出来。"}, "b": {"item_id": "45f4bb0c382a", "text": "我朋友花三年把克林贡语练到了流利。流利到什么程度？他跟我吵架，全程用克林贡语。吵完我才发现，他是对着镜子练的，因为我一句都听不懂。他图什么？他说这语言够硬气，念起来一嗓子呼噜，像刚从战场上下来。结果他去参加同好聚会，满屋子都是练了三年的人，互相拍胸脯、喷口水打招呼，嗓子全哑了，喝口水都得用喉音。后来他女朋友跟他分手，留了一句话：你说你想战死的时候，能不能先问问我想不想吃晚饭？"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0614", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "nfts", "target_name": "NFT", "facts": ["Beeple's 'Everydays: The First 5000 Days' sold at Christie's for $69.3 million in March 2021.", "OpenSea, the largest NFT marketplace, processed a record $2.7 billion in trades on 1 May 2022 and just $9.34 million on 28 August 2022, a 99% drop, according to DappRadar data.", "A 2023 dappGambl study found that 95% of the NFT collections it examined had a market value of zero.", "The token usually records only a URL; the image itself sits on an ordinary server or IPFS, not on the blockchain.", "Collins Dictionary named 'NFT' its word of the year for 2021."], "a": {"item_id": "a8cfb67f2de5", "text": "2021年买NFT的人，我身边就有一个。以前连拼多多砍一刀都嫌麻烦，那年突然跟我说：我是数字艺术收藏家。我问收藏的啥，他给我看一只猴子，戴着墨镜，一脸没睡醒。十八万。\n\n我说这图我右键另存为就有了。他说：你那叫复制，我这叫所有权。我说所有权在哪？他说在区块链上。我说那你家里挂的是啥？他说：我挂在Twitter头像上。\n\n十八万，买个头像框。还得天天在群里跟一群头像也是猴子的陌生人说\"gm\"，互道早安。\n\n现在地板价跌了九成，他每天刷OpenSea，那只猴子挂出去半年，连个问价的都没有。他说他是钻石手，死拿不卖。\n\n兄弟，你不是钻石手，你是那只猴子唯一的粉丝。"}, "b": {"item_id": "36fed9141b46", "text": "我有个朋友，2021年花三万块买了一张猴子的图。无聊猿，戴墨镜叼烟斗那种。我问他图什么，他说这是数字资产，全世界独一无二。我说你右键另存为，它也能一模一样出现在你电脑里。他说不一样，我在链上有所有权。我问所有权是什么，他想了半天说：\"这只猴子是我的。\"光矿工费就够买台二手车。后来地板价跌了九成，群里一夜之间全是\"早期参与者\"，项目方带着钱消失了。他现在头像还是那只猴子，我看他是真的戴上了墨镜，不敢抬头见人。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0615", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "microsoft-word", "target_name": "Microsoft Word", "facts": ["Launched in 1983 as 'Multi-Tool Word' for Xenix and MS-DOS.", "Clippy, the animated paperclip assistant, arrived in Office 97 and was removed in Office 2007; Microsoft itself ran ads mocking it in 2001.", "Its .docx format, Office Open XML, was fast-tracked to ISO standard 29500 in 2008 as a roughly 6,000-page specification, amid accusations that national committees had been packed.", "Older spell checkers, Word's included, offered 'Cupertino' as a correction for a mistyped 'cooperation', and the resulting 'Cupertino effect' made it into published NATO, EU and UN-agency documents."], "a": {"item_id": "3173e42e100a", "text": "每年六月，全国几十万毕业生集体转行当排版工人。图什么？就图学校那份模板：摘要页码用罗马数字，正文从1开始。为了这个，他们学会了一个这辈子再也用不上的词：分节符。然后挨页去点掉“链接到前一节”，漏点一次，第三章的页眉就写着“致谢”。\n\n最惨的是插图。你把图往上挪了一毫米，后面二十页集体搬家，图3-2直接住进了参考文献。\n\n答辩完问他收获了什么，他说：收获了“论文_终稿_改3_打死不改.docx”。还有，千万别右键“更新域”，那是我目录最后一次完整的样子。"}, "b": {"item_id": "3536bc0676b0", "text": "有些人非要用 Word 排毕业论文。图什么？图一个\"一键生成目录\"。结果标题没用标题样式，手敲了个\"第一章\"，加粗了事。目录一更新，正文全变成了目录项。他去学分节符，学会了，又发现页码从第二节开始就断了，把\"链接到前一节\"点了十七次，点出一个新世界。凌晨三点，他按 Ctrl+A，对着域代码哭。答辩前一天，导师说：\"目录页码对不上。\"他按 F9，弹出一行\"错误！未找到目录项\"。那一刻他终于明白，Word 不是在排版，是在考他的耐心，而他的耐心已经交了白卷。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0616", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "luosifen", "target_name": "螺蛳粉", "facts": ["A rice-noodle dish from Liuzhou, Guangxi; the smell comes from pickled bamboo shoots (酸笋), not from the river snails, which only flavour the broth.", "The instant version boomed during the 2020 lockdowns; Liuzhou's government reported packaged luosifen sales of over 10 billion yuan that year.", "Added to China's national intangible cultural heritage list in 2021.", "Liuzhou opened a luosifen industry college in 2020 to train workers for the trade."], "a": {"item_id": "beb2c7971e47", "text": "合租房里煮螺蛳粉的，都是同一种人：把“闻着臭吃着香”当人生信条，但只要后半句，前半句全分给室友。\n\n他煮之前还特讲究，关门、开窗、开油烟机，跟拆炸弹似的。然后酸笋包一撕，全白搭。那味儿穿门缝、钻衣柜，第二天你穿着外套去面试，HR问你：“你家住垃圾站旁边？”\n\n他还觉得自己挺克制：“我没加臭，原味。”大哥，原味酸笋就是臭的底线，你跟我讲克制？\n\n最惨的是吃完那一刻。他满足了三分钟，剩下半锅红油汤倒哪？倒马桶，厕所臭三天；倒水池，整栋楼臭一周。\n\n最后合租群里没人骂他，就房东发了一句：“退房时，窗帘按新的赔。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "5c463dbea536", "text": "合租房里煮螺蛳粉的人，我太懂你们了。图什么？一包才八块钱，深更半夜饿了，想吃口有灵魂的。可你们不知道，那袋酸笋一拆开，整层楼都被你\"请\"来吃饭了。门缝底下往外冒的不是蒸汽，是战书。室友从卧室探出头，鼻子一抽，眼神像在看犯罪现场。第二天群里弹出一条消息：\"谁又煮螺蛳粉了？\"你把头埋进碗里，一句话不敢说。最后的下场是：窗户开成了通风道，外套挂三天还带着河沟味，押金一分不剩，房东在合同里加了一条\"禁止煮螺蛳粉\"。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0617", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "javascript", "target_name": "JavaScript", "facts": ["Written by Brendan Eich at Netscape in ten days in May 1995; named Mocha, then LiveScript, then JavaScript to ride Java's marketing, although the two are unrelated.", "typeof null returns 'object', a bug from the first version that has been kept for compatibility ever since.", "In March 2016 the author of left-pad, an 11-line npm package, unpublished it and broke builds across the industry, including Babel and React.", "[] + {} evaluates to '[object Object]'; {} + [] typed at a console evaluates to 0.", "Standardized by Ecma as 'ECMAScript' in 1997 because the name JavaScript is a trademark, first Sun's and now Oracle's."], "a": {"item_id": "3206715972e0", "text": "我同事学了三个月 JavaScript，现在看什么都像 JS。前两天他说要做个记事本，用 Electron。我说记事本？他说对，打出来 300 兆，因为里面自带一个 Chrome。我说你一个记事本为啥要带浏览器？他说：“我不想学第二门语言。”兄弟，你省的是学习时间，吃的是我的内存。\n\n他连判断奇偶数都不自己写，npm install is-even。装完 node_modules 两个G，他自己的代码三行。\n\n我考他：\"1\"+1 等于几？他说 11。\"1\"-1 呢？0。我说你就这么接受了？他说：“习惯了。”\n\n各位，这不是程序员，这是被 JavaScript 虐出斯德哥尔摩综合征的人。"}, "b": {"item_id": "0f8759744771", "text": "我朋友是前端，什么都用 JavaScript 写。写个脚本，用 Node。写个桌面软件，套个 Electron，一开就吃八百兆内存。他说这叫一套技术栈通吃。我说你那是通吃，还是通缉？\n\n上周他想写个开机自动备份，npm install 下来一千三百个包，其中一个叫 is-odd，专门判断一个数是不是奇数。备份脚本跑起来，先等一个包判断今天是不是单号，再等另一个包去找文件。备份成功了，电脑里也多了一个 node_modules，比他硬盘里所有照片加起来还大。\n\n现在他开始学 Rust，说要回归本质。我问他本质是什么，他说是 undefined。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0618", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "excel", "target_name": "Microsoft Excel", "facts": ["First shipped for the Macintosh in 1985; the Windows version followed in 1987.", "Silently converts gene names such as SEPT2 and MARCH1 into dates; by August 2020 the HUGO Gene Nomenclature Committee had renamed 27 human genes (MARCH1 became MARCHF1, SEPT2 became SEPTIN2) and published guidelines in Nature Genetics to stop it.", "In October 2020, Public Health England lost 15,841 COVID-19 cases when a legacy .xls file hit its 65,536-row limit.", "Reinhart and Rogoff's 2010 paper 'Growth in a Time of Debt', cited for austerity policies, omitted five countries through a spreadsheet range error found by a graduate student in 2013.", "Sheets have been capped at 1,048,576 rows by 16,384 columns since Excel 2007."], "a": {"item_id": "3643597461dd", "text": "我们老板拿 Excel 管整个公司，他说这叫“轻资产”。翻译一下：舍不得买系统，又想当系统。\n\n全公司的命，就一个文件：《经营总表_最终版_改3_老板别动.xlsx》。财务看一眼，三十七个工作表，底下标签一路滚到右边，像在翻家谱。\n\n老板最骄傲的是他会 VLOOKUP。会一个 VLOOKUP，就觉得自己能当 CTO。结果有天实习生插了一列，全公司工资变成 #N/A。员工问我工资呢？我说你现在的身份是“找不到”。\n\n最惨的是人事，把员工身份证号一粘，全变成 4.40E+17。整个公司四百人，在 Excel 里是同一个人。\n\n公司倒闭那天，老板还在说：“别慌，Ctrl+Z。”\n\n兄弟，公司又不是单元格，撤销不了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "7557f0ed30ba", "text": "见过拿Excel管整家公司的老板。问他怎么不上个系统，他说：\"Excel挺好用的。\"是挺好用，好用到库存、工资、客户全塞一张表里，VLOOKUP嵌了七层。这哪是做表，这是写遗嘱。\n\n图什么？图不用学新东西。结果为了不学新东西，公司专门养了个人来学这张表。刘姐，没职位，大家只说\"问刘姐\"。她请一天假，公司就瘫了，因为只有她知道D列不能动，一动，#REF!就像瘟疫一样往下传。\n\n下场更惨。上周我想打开订单表，弹窗：\"文件正在被张伟使用。\"张伟三年前就离职了。现在公司发工资，得等一个幽灵把文件保存关掉。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0619", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "luosifen", "target_name": "螺蛳粉", "facts": ["A rice-noodle dish from Liuzhou, Guangxi; the smell comes from pickled bamboo shoots (酸笋), not from the river snails, which only flavour the broth.", "The instant version boomed during the 2020 lockdowns; Liuzhou's government reported packaged luosifen sales of over 10 billion yuan that year.", "Added to China's national intangible cultural heritage list in 2021.", "Liuzhou opened a luosifen industry college in 2020 to train workers for the trade."], "a": {"item_id": "d1bc8bf17584", "text": "合租房里煮螺蛳粉的人，开口第一句永远是：“我开窗了。”你开窗？那是给酸笋开了条逃生通道，它出去之前，先把每个房间都参观一遍。\n\n他们图什么？图一口“闻着臭，吃着香”。这话是说给室友听的，意思是：你们负责闻，我负责香。\n\n而且螺蛳粉还是分期付款。红油先到，腐竹跟上，最后剪开酸笋包压轴登场，全屋观众不许退场。\n\n下场呢？第二天群里问“谁昨晚煮东西了”，没人认。但每个枕头都认。最后你被指认，不是靠监控，是你进电梯，旁边的人下意识看了一眼你的外套。\n\n你吃了十五分钟，穿了三天。"}, "b": {"item_id": "5d0789c353d9", "text": "合租房里煮螺蛳粉的人，我太懂了。他们总说：就煮一包，不碍事。图什么？图锅里飘着腐竹和木耳，图那口酸笋上头的快感，图自己在厨房吸溜得像个美食博主。结果呢？酸笋味顺着抽油烟机往外跑，跑不掉的全钻进了室友的被窝。第二天群里弹出一句：\"谁煮的螺蛳粉？\"没人认，但全屋人心里都有数。厨房门上很快贴了张纸，写着\"禁止螺蛳粉\"，他的押金被扣了两百，理由是\"除味费\"。最绝的是那个空料包，他打了三个结扔进垃圾桶，第二天整个单元楼的人都知道他吃了什么。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0620", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "almond-milk", "target_name": "杏仁奶", "facts": ["California grows about 80% of the world's almonds.", "A single almond takes about 1.1 US gallons (roughly 4 litres) of water to grow, a figure Mother Jones published in 2014 that spread during California's drought.", "A 2015 US class action alleged Almond Breeze contained only about 2% almonds; it partly survived a motion to dismiss, was stayed in 2017 pending a separate Blue Diamond labelling settlement, and the plaintiffs then dropped it.", "Pollinating California's almond orchards each February requires trucking in most of the commercial honeybee colonies in the United States.", "Medieval European cooks used almond milk as a Lenten substitute for dairy, centuries before the current fashion."], "a": {"item_id": "a3df26d73f36", "text": "你们身边有没有这种人？每天早上端一杯杏仁奶，说自己是为了地球。我问他：你知道做一杯杏仁奶要喝掉多少水吗？他说不知道，但包装上画着阳光下的草地，看着很安心。杏仁是出了名的费水作物，加州一棵杏树一年要喝好几百升水，他这杯“环保”，其实是拿加州的水库在泡杏仁。这人图什么？图个心安。图朋友圈里配一张杯子的照片，文案写“从我做起”。结果呢？牛奶钱没省下来，一瓶杏仁奶比牛奶贵一倍多。味道像加了两滴悔恨的白开水，倒进咖啡里直接分层，像一杯正在办离婚的饮料。上个月他开车去超市囤了三箱，车是烧油的，车钥匙还挂着一只环保袋。"}, "b": {"item_id": "2a93fb2cbc64", "text": "我同事上个月宣布改喝杏仁奶，说是为了环保，为了少一头牛放屁。我就问他：你知道一颗杏仁要用四升水才能长出来吗？他说知道，但牛更费水。行，我又问：你这杯杏仁奶，杏仁占多少？他说，大部分吧。我说，百分之二。\n\n四十五块钱，买了一杯百分之九十八是水的东西，还是加州大旱那边的水。\n\n更绝的是，加州杏仁树要靠蜜蜂授粉，蜂群是卡车拉过去租的，累死一大片。所以他一个吃素的，为了不伤害牛，给蜜蜂送了终。\n\n最后，他在咖啡里一倒，杏仁奶当场分层，絮状物漂起来，像分手现场。他还是端着喝完了，发朋友圈：“为地球做了一点小事。”\n\n地球没拯救，拿铁凉了，蜜蜂走了，他还得去厕所。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0621", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "papyrus", "target_name": "Papyrus 字体", "facts": ["Hand-drawn by Chris Costello in 1982 over about six months, when he was 23; he sold the rights to Letraset for $750.", "Bundled with macOS and with Microsoft Office, which is why it appears on so many menus and signs.", "James Cameron's Avatar (2009) used it for the film's logo and subtitles.", "A 2017 Saturday Night Live sketch starring Ryan Gosling is about a man unable to get over that choice."], "a": {"item_id": "02bcbf4a1359", "text": "开养生馆的老板选招牌，流程我都猜得到：打开Word，字体一路往下滑，滑到Papyrus，眼睛一亮——“哎，这个毛边，有古老东方的神秘感。”大哥，这字是个美国小伙二十三岁拿钢笔在纸上描出来的，你这“东方”是批发来的。更绝的是隔壁泰式按摩、墨西哥卷饼、埃及烤肉，全是同一个Papyrus，整条街像同一个古文明的遗址。他图什么？图Office自带，图不花钱，图“看着有文化”。结果呢？顾客一抬头，以为进的不是足疗店，是《阿凡达》片场。卡梅隆好歹花了几亿美元才用上这款字，你花了零块零毛，撞得一模一样，人家拿了奥斯卡，你拿了张差评：“技师手法一般，招牌像蓝色外星人开的。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "7a35ef193141", "text": "最离谱的是开养生馆、开餐馆的老板，招牌非要用Papyrus。这字体像用芦苇纸手撕出来的，笔画边缘毛毛躁躁，像法老陵墓门口那块石碑。他们图什么？图\"有调性\"，图\"古法养生\"，让客人一进门就觉得有故事。结果阿凡达的海报用的就是这个字，顾客抬头一看，以为要去潘多拉星球做足疗。隔壁烧烤店跟着学，招牌一亮，大家都在吃木乃伊烤串。老板很委屈：\"我要的是高级感。\"设计师说：\"是您自己在字体列表里一路往下划，点的这个。\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0622", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "graphene", "target_name": "石墨烯", "facts": ["Isolated in 2004 by Andre Geim and Konstantin Novoselov at Manchester by peeling graphite with Scotch tape; Nobel Prize in Physics, 2010.", "The EU's Graphene Flagship, launched in 2013, committed €1 billion over ten years.", "A 2018 study in Advanced Materials tested 'graphene' from 60 producers and found none with more than 50% graphene content; most was graphite flakes.", "Sold in tennis rackets, running shoes, and headphones as a performance ingredient.", "Geim also won an Ig Nobel Prize in 2000 for levitating a frog with magnets."], "a": {"item_id": "9b0a72d5be4f", "text": "饭桌上总有这种人，一开口就是：\"石墨烯要改变世界。\"你问他石墨烯是啥，他说就一层碳原子，导电好，导热快。行，那你告诉我，你脚下这双\"石墨烯暖宝宝\"鞋垫，里面到底有几层碳？他沉默三秒，说：\"暖和就行。\"这话我信，暖和是真的，毕竟它本质上就是个电热丝套了个新名字。前几年搞石墨烯概念的公司，股价一路涨，财报上研发投入写得比谁都大，产品呢，是往涂料里撒一把黑粉，再印上\"石墨烯\"三个字。投资人亏得哭，创始人转头去讲元宇宙了。世界没变，就他朋友圈头像换成了一张论文截图。"}, "b": {"item_id": "5c660764dbab", "text": "我有个表舅，逢人就说石墨烯要改变世界。他说这玩意儿就一个原子厚，比钢硬两百倍，导电导热全是第一。我问他：那你拿它干嘛了？他说：卖秋裤。石墨烯发热秋裤，一条六百八。\n\n表舅研究过，诺贝尔奖得主是拿透明胶带从铅笔芯上粘出来的。他一听，门槛不高啊，家里胶带有的是。二〇一五年，他把养老钱全押进了石墨烯概念股，从此真的改变了世界，先改变的是他自己的。\n\n现在他还在说。石墨烯什么都能干，就是出不了实验室；表舅什么都敢说，就是出不了我们家群。\n\n过年我问他，世界改变了吗？他把秋裤往上一拽：“你摸，热不热！”\n\n热。里头是电热丝。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0623", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "jackfruit", "target_name": "菠萝蜜", "facts": ["The largest fruit that grows on a tree; single fruits can weigh over 40 kg.", "The national fruit of Bangladesh and the state fruit of Kerala and Tamil Nadu.", "Unripe flesh is sold as a vegan 'pulled pork' substitute, although it has only 1-2 g of protein per 100 g.", "Cutting one releases a sticky white latex, so cooks oil their knives and hands first.", "A member of the mulberry family (Moraceae), a relative of breadfruit and figs."], "a": {"item_id": "73bd57e7ed48", "text": "有老板把青菠萝蜜撕成丝，当手撕猪肉卖。我就佩服，创业第一步，先跟自己的手结仇。青菠萝蜜那个胶，比前任还难甩，洗手液没用，得拿食用油搓。后厨剥一天，十根手指粘成一只爪子，出来跟客人握手，客人以为他入了什么帮派。\n\n图啥？图便宜。可你算算：猪肉切了就能炒，菠萝蜜得先拿刀砍一身刺，再对付一手胶，炖三小时，还得倒半瓶酱油才勉强有点肉味。人工加调料，够买半头猪了。\n\n最后下场，有食客点评：“这是我吃过最像菠萝蜜的猪肉。”一句话，比市监局还准。罚款单下来，老板终于明白，这生意他唯一卖出去的肉，是自己的肉疼。"}, "b": {"item_id": "292065870d49", "text": "菠萝蜜这玩意儿，长得像个带刺的炸弹，切开满手黏胶，洗手得用油。现在有人把它炖成\"手撕肉\"，印在菜单上叫\"植物基猪肉\"。我就想问，你们是没吃过猪肉，还是没吃过菠萝蜜？它被撕成丝是挺像，我承认。可你往嘴里一放，甜的！老板的算盘打得噼啪响：素食风口来了，成本便宜，还能加价三十。结果客人咬一口，愣住了：\"这肉怎么带着一股熟透了的水果味？\"老板说，这叫创新。创新完，一个月后店就没了，差评区全是\"猪肉被冒充，我的眼泪是甜的\"。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0624", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hsq", "target_name": "HSQ 电子束光刻胶", "facts": ["Hydrogen silsesquioxane, sold by Dow Corning as FOx ('flowable oxide') and designed as a spin-on dielectric for chip interconnects; lithographers adopted it as a negative-tone resist.", "Exposure converts its cage structure into a cross-linked, silica-like network, so the developed pattern is essentially SiO2.", "Holds the resolution records for electron-beam lithography: isolated features around 5 nm, dense lines below 10 nm half-pitch, with high-contrast 'salty' NaOH/NaCl development.", "Must be kept refrigerated (Dow Corning's datasheet gives a six-month shelf life at 5 °C); the solution ages into a jelly, and its effective sensitivity shifts with processing delays because cross-linking continues after exposure.", "DuPont, which inherited Dow Corning's FOx/XR-1541 HSQ line, discontinued it and ended production at the end of 2025; labs such as EPFL's CMi switched to third-party HSQ from EM Resist or DisChem, both formulated from AQM powder."], "a": {"item_id": "a38389497436", "text": "有些人为了分辨率，非要换HSQ。他们是谁？一群研究生，导师一句“线宽得压到十纳米以下”，就抱着一瓶胶去排电子束曝光机。这胶得放冰箱，过期会结胶，开了盖还得赶紧用。图的就是那几根细线，结果线是细了，一显影，线条齐刷刷倒向一边，像一排喝多了的人。更绝的是，曝光过的地方会固化成二氧化硅，等于他们在芯片上刻了一层玻璃，洗都洗不掉。毕业那天他们终于明白：分辨率没提上去，芯片上倒多了一块摘不下来的玻璃。"}, "b": {"item_id": "98b1aee74d1a", "text": "实验室里最危险的人，是那种盯着SEM说“PMMA不够细”的博士生。为了论文里一张“8纳米线宽”的图，他换了HSQ。从此他的人生就变了。\n\n先说钱，一小瓶贵得像茅台，还得冷藏，比他本人过得精致。开封了就开始老化，他每天看那瓶胶的眼神，比看他妈都焦虑。\n\n再说剂量，HSQ灵敏度低，曝一个芯片，电子束要写一整夜。别人约会，他守机台。\n\n显影还得加盐，学长说“盐显影对比度高”，他就真去称氯化钠，跟腌咸菜似的。\n\n最绝的是曝完它变成二氧化硅，洗不掉！想返工？上氢氟酸。\n\n最后图出来了，线是真细，倒了一片。导师一看：“挺好，再做一批。”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0625", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "jackfruit", "target_name": "菠萝蜜", "facts": ["The largest fruit that grows on a tree; single fruits can weigh over 40 kg.", "The national fruit of Bangladesh and the state fruit of Kerala and Tamil Nadu.", "Unripe flesh is sold as a vegan 'pulled pork' substitute, although it has only 1-2 g of protein per 100 g.", "Cutting one releases a sticky white latex, so cooks oil their knives and hands first.", "A member of the mulberry family (Moraceae), a relative of breadfruit and figs."], "a": {"item_id": "b09e1a218e62", "text": "现在网红轻食店流行一道菜，叫“植物基手撕猪肉”，六十八一碗。我吃了一口，菠萝蜜。就是海南路边五块钱一斤、一个三十斤、浑身长刺、得抱着才能搬走的那个。\n\n老板图什么？图它没熟的时候能撕成一丝一丝的，看着像肉。可他没告诉你，切这玩意儿会流白胶，粘手上肥皂都洗不掉，得拿食用油搓。\n\n你想想这画面：一个天天喊“告别动物油脂”的素食老板，每天打烊第一件事，是在后厨拎一壶花生油搓手，搓得满手油光，跟刚偷吃完一盘红烧肉似的。\n\n最惨的是有一回他切到个熟的，整个店一股香蕉混榴莲的甜味儿。顾客举着筷子问：“老板，你这猪肉怎么一股水果糖味儿？”\n\n老板想了半天：“这是……吃水果长大的猪。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "0c2c69874c8f", "text": "有人把菠萝蜜炖成“手撕肉”，标价三十八，招牌写着“植物蛋白，口感还原”。我点了一份，吃了一口，好家伙，是甜的。不是一般的甜，是榴莲没熟透、香蕉刚放软那种甜。我以为自己点的是红烧肉，结果是一碗加了纤维的糖水。老板挺自豪，说“你看这纹理，一丝一丝的，跟猪肉纤维一模一样”。我说老板，猪肉的纤维是肌肉，你这是树上挂的果囊，你是把一棵树的肚子撕开给我看。他说这叫创新。后来店关了，听说他转行卖菠萝蜜酥，说“不像肉了，但它甜”。我说你这才是找对了路子，肉没卖出去，至少糖分到位了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0626", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "risk", "target_name": "《战国风云》（Risk）", "facts": ["Invented in 1957 by Albert Lamorisse, the French director of The Red Balloon, as La Conquête du Monde; Parker Brothers published it in the United States in 1959.", "The map has 42 territories, among them Kamchatka, Irkutsk, and Yakutsk, while the entire Middle East is one territory.", "Holding Australia, which has a single entry point, is the standard opening strategy.", "A full game can run for hours, and players eliminated early have nothing to do but watch."], "a": {"item_id": "8083e937dc92", "text": "玩《战国风云》非要玩到底的人，都是觉得世界和平太无聊，想亲手重来一遍的。开局大家都说：“咱们友好点，随便玩玩。”四十分钟后，你的盟友从堪察加半岛给了你一刀。\n\n你还迷信澳洲：就一个入口，每回合白送两个兵，守住就能赢。六个小时后你真守住了，全世界就剩你缩在澳洲，对面三个骰子一扔，你的兵像袋鼠一样被撞飞。\n\n赢的人在欢呼，输的人在找外套，还有一个人早就退群了。整局唯一的和平提议，是凌晨两点那句“要不平局吧”。没人同意，因为每个人都觉得，再扔两把，自己就翻盘了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "49ce2b81452e", "text": "那种非要把《战国风云》玩到底的人，我是真服了。开局说好玩两小时，结果六个小时过去，桌上就剩他一个人还在操作，我们早就被他一个个灭了。他图什么？图那张世界地图全红的快感。他守住澳大利亚，吃下整个南美洲，最后手抖得像在拆炸弹，每掷一次骰子都要先深呼吸。我们问他饿不饿，他说再等等，还差阿拉斯加，他那二十个小兵正在阿拉斯加跟我们两个骑兵对峙。最后他赢了，全世界都是他的，只是红色塑料兵用光了，他只好拿薯片当兵。赢是赢了，第二天群里没人再约他。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0627", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hydrogen-cars", "target_name": "氢燃料电池汽车", "facts": ["Toyota launched the Mirai in 2014; 'mirai' means 'future' in Japanese.", "Nearly all US hydrogen filling stations are in California; Shell closed all seven of its California passenger-car stations in February 2024.", "More than 95% of the world's hydrogen is made from fossil fuels, mostly by steam-reforming natural gas.", "California pump prices reached about $36 per kilogram in 2023-24, over $200 for a Mirai fill; Toyota has handed buyers fuel cards worth $15,000.", "Mirai owners filed a class action against Toyota in July 2024 over hydrogen shortages and station outages; a second suit in 2025 sought at least $5.7 billion."], "a": {"item_id": "feb6bb90ad06", "text": "我有个朋友买了辆氢燃料电池车，逢人就说：“电动车充电一小时，我加氢只要三分钟。”对，三分钟，前提是你先开两百公里找到加氢站。全市就那么一座，还是给公交车队修的，他得排在十二辆大巴后面，等储罐压力回上来。人家加的是氢，他加的是耐心。\n\n他还爱说：“我这车唯一的排放是水。”是，排放是水，他眼里也是水。一公斤氢七十块，跑一百公里比油车还贵。\n\n最惨的是去年那座站关了。车现在停在楼下，他每天下去看一眼。他说他买的是未来。没错，未来还没到，二手车商的报价先到了：按废铁算，高压氢罐另收拆除费。"}, "b": {"item_id": "a7c3aeb313ce", "text": "我朋友非要买氢燃料电池车，说排出来的是水，环保。我说对，排的是水，你的钱也是一滴一滴没的，看不见。\n\n买这车的人分两种，一种是科技宅，觉得自己活在电影里；另一种是单位采购，花的是补贴，车是别人的。他图什么？图那句\"我开的是未来\"。结果未来在哪？在郊区一座加氢站，得提前预约，排队排到怀疑人生。站一关门，车就成了铁疙瘩，电堆里那点铂催化剂，比他的存款还金贵。\n\n最绝的是氢分子最小，能从70兆帕的罐子里漏出去。他的工资也一样，还没捂热，就从卡里漏没了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0628", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "canberra", "target_name": "堪培拉", "facts": ["Chosen as a compromise because neither Sydney nor Melbourne would accept the other as capital; Australia's constitution required the capital to be at least 100 miles from Sydney.", "Designed by the Chicago architects Walter Burley Griffin and Marion Mahony Griffin, who won an international competition in 1912.", "Its centrepiece, Lake Burley Griffin, is artificial, created by damming the Molonglo River in 1963-64.", "Parliament House (1988) is built into Capital Hill under a lawn meant for citizens to walk over their politicians; the lawn was fenced off in 2017 for security.", "Melbourne served as the capital until 1927 while Canberra was being built."], "a": {"item_id": "0d2a6bbab13d", "text": "我有个朋友，为了工作搬去堪培拉。他说：“联邦公务员，铁饭碗，三年升EL1。”兄弟，你才二十八岁，人生规划写得跟预算案附录一样。\n\n去了一年，他说话全成了缩写：“我在DFAT，赶上MOG调整，等APS6转正。”我问他周末干嘛，他说开两个多小时去贝特曼斯湾看海。你搬去一个城市，生活质量全看你多快能离开它。\n\n最惨的是，他现在会主动跟人说：“堪培拉其实挺好的，你得慢慢了解它。”这话我只在两种人嘴里听过：堪培拉人，和被关太久、开始同情绑匪的人质。"}, "b": {"item_id": "7c175bf79c23", "text": "你们知道堪培拉为什么是首都吗？悉尼和墨尔本都不让对方当，吵了半天，最后选了个中间的地方，谁也不服，谁也不去。\n\n偏偏就有一群人，为了\"离权力近一点\"搬过来。处长、智库研究员、部长办公室的小助理，还有一堆咨询公司的。图什么？图升职，图简历上一个好看的头衔，图哪天上电视，站在国会山底下说一句\"我们正在研究\"。\n\n三年后呢？他们每个周五晚上开三个小时车去悉尼，周日傍晚再开三个小时回来，车里一句话不说，像押送回去上班的犯人。湖边散步走了一百圈，国会山看了一百遍，权力还是在别人手里。冬天早上零下五度，他们在环岛里绕圈，绕了三年，绕回了原点。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0629", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "esperanto", "target_name": "世界语", "facts": ["Published in Warsaw in 1887 by L. L. Zamenhof, a Białystok-born eye doctor, under the pen name Doktoro Esperanto, 'one who hopes'.", "Speaker estimates range from 100,000 to two million; roughly a thousand people grow up with it as a native language.", "William Shatner starred in Incubus (1966), a horror film shot entirely in Esperanto, with pronunciation Esperantists still mock.", "Its Wikipedia has over 350,000 articles, and its Duolingo course for English speakers had 1.36 million learners by July 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "7911ad5f0fdc", "text": "学世界语的人，图的是全人类终于能好好说话。结果全人类没来，来的是同一拨人。这门语言的名字就叫\"希望者\"，学它的人等于从第一天就领了个外号。大会上他们互相寒暄：你哪国的？巴西。我韩国的。平时用世界语跟谁聊？跟你们俩啊。名词加-o，形容词加-a，宾格加-n，每句话都挂满尾巴，语法一个不错，朋友一个没有。"}, "b": {"item_id": "16da4448f632", "text": "我有个朋友学世界语，说是为了世界和平。这语言是1887年一个波兰眼科医生发明的，目标是让全人类说同一种话。一百三十多年过去了，全人类没统一，我朋友倒是跟全人类都说不上话了。\n\n他图什么呢？他说世界语圈有个\"护照服务\"，全球会说世界语的人家里都能免费住。我问他住过几家，他说一家，在匈牙利。两个人见面第一件事，就是互相纠正对方宾格结尾漏掉的那个\"-n\"。\n\n最绝的是，\"Esperanto\"这个词原意是\"心怀希望的人\"。你看，发明者早就想明白了：学这玩意儿的人，只能靠希望活着。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0630", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "las-vegas", "target_name": "拉斯维加斯", "facts": ["Nevada legalized casino gambling in 1931, the year construction of the Hoover Dam began nearby.", "Most of the Strip lies not in the city of Las Vegas but in the unincorporated town of Paradise.", "The Sphere, opened in 2023 at a cost of about $2.3 billion, is the largest spherical building in the world.", "'What happens here, stays here' was launched by the local convention and visitors authority in 2003.", "Lake Mead, its water source, fell in July 2022 to about 1,040 feet, its lowest since the reservoir first filled in 1937, and dropped below that mark again in August 2026."], "a": {"item_id": "dd338752f2cb", "text": "我有个朋友，看了三遍《决胜21点》，就觉得自己是MIT天才，揣着年终奖飞拉斯维加斯，说去“考察”。\n\n兄弟，你连工资条都算不明白，你去算牌？\n\n你也不想想，那是沙漠里凭空盖的一座城：没河、没矿、没工厂，贝拉吉奥的喷泉一天到晚往天上滋，钱哪来的？就是你们这种人！那每一滴水，都是“考察团”的眼泪。\n\n赌场里没钟没窗，他说好啊，能专心。专心到第三天，前台主动给他升了套房。他还挺骄傲，说这是VIP待遇。\n\n不是，那是人家算过了，你值这个价。\n\n回来他跟我说：“在拉斯维加斯发生的，就留在拉斯维加斯。”\n\n我说对，你的钱也是。"}, "b": {"item_id": "c54ee644ce17", "text": "去拉斯维加斯发财的人，最让我服气的是自信。个个都说“我研究过”。研究了啥？油管三分钟《21点必胜法》。人家赌场是几十亿砸出来的，你是三分钟视频，这叫博士打小学生。\n\n他们图什么？不图钱，图一句“我在维加斯赢过”。第一晚赢两百，感觉自己是邦德，服务员送免费酒，你以为是请客，那是预付款。喝到第三杯，你把全部押在红色，理由是“看着吉利”。\n\n下场很统一：赌场没窗户没钟，你以为晚上十点，其实周三了。最后站在ATM前，手续费十八块你都不心疼，已经输麻了。到机场，还有老虎机，你居然想“最后试一把”。\n\n回家有人问，你说：“我没输，我是去玩的。”对，你花四千块，买了一句“我没输”。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0631", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "las-vegas", "target_name": "拉斯维加斯", "facts": ["Nevada legalized casino gambling in 1931, the year construction of the Hoover Dam began nearby.", "Most of the Strip lies not in the city of Las Vegas but in the unincorporated town of Paradise.", "The Sphere, opened in 2023 at a cost of about $2.3 billion, is the largest spherical building in the world.", "'What happens here, stays here' was launched by the local convention and visitors authority in 2003.", "Lake Mead, its water source, fell in July 2022 to about 1,040 feet, its lowest since the reservoir first filled in 1937, and dropped below that mark again in August 2026."], "a": {"item_id": "dd338752f2cb", "text": "我有个朋友，看了三遍《决胜21点》，就觉得自己是MIT天才，揣着年终奖飞拉斯维加斯，说去“考察”。\n\n兄弟，你连工资条都算不明白，你去算牌？\n\n你也不想想，那是沙漠里凭空盖的一座城：没河、没矿、没工厂，贝拉吉奥的喷泉一天到晚往天上滋，钱哪来的？就是你们这种人！那每一滴水，都是“考察团”的眼泪。\n\n赌场里没钟没窗，他说好啊，能专心。专心到第三天，前台主动给他升了套房。他还挺骄傲，说这是VIP待遇。\n\n不是，那是人家算过了，你值这个价。\n\n回来他跟我说：“在拉斯维加斯发生的，就留在拉斯维加斯。”\n\n我说对，你的钱也是。"}, "b": {"item_id": "ade69157daa3", "text": "去拉斯维加斯发财的人，我真想问：你是觉得那些酒店是慈善机构吗？沙漠里一座城，没河没矿，全靠你们盖起来的。威尼斯人酒店里那条运河，是你的房贷挖的。\n\n出发前都说\"我就玩两百块\"，还说研究过策略，看了三小时视频：\"押红色，输了就翻倍。\"哥们，赌桌有下注上限，你翻到第七把，就剩尊严了。\n\n下场都一样：第一天赢八十，觉得自己是赌神；第二天在没有窗户没有钟的赌场里喝免费啤酒，不知道几点，也不知道自己是谁；第三天在机场，把回程的钱塞进老虎机。对，麦卡伦机场就有老虎机，怕你还没到市区就想通了。\n\n最后朋友圈发张照片：在猫王教堂，跟刚认识的人结婚了。发财没发成，倒是多了个人来分你剩下的。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0632", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "brasilia", "target_name": "巴西利亚", "facts": ["Inaugurated on 21 April 1960 after about 41 months of construction, moving the capital inland from Rio de Janeiro.", "Lúcio Costa's Plano Piloto is laid out like an airplane (or a bird, or a cross), with Oscar Niemeyer's government buildings along the fuselage.", "Listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1987, less than 30 years after it was built.", "Planned for 500,000 residents; the Federal District now has about 3 million, most in satellite towns outside the plan.", "Built for the car: wide avenues, few pedestrian crossings, and distances that make walking impractical."], "a": {"item_id": "326fa522889c", "text": "在巴西利亚靠两条腿出门的人，我得给他们立个碑。这帮人是谁？多半是刚搬来的新人，手机一打开，地图写着\"步行二十八分钟\"，他们就信了。图什么？图锻炼，图省钱，图不想等那趟十四分钟一班、还不一定来的公交。结果呢？整条Eixo Monumental上，人行道说没就没，只剩一条给卡车跑的高速路，他们只能贴着护栏，顶着尾气走。好不容易走到立交桥底下想抄近路，四个车道，红灯十秒。最后他站在W3大道中间那条绿化带上，左边是车，右边也是车，低头看手机，地图淡定地提示：\"您已到达目的地。\""}, "b": {"item_id": "83f9dfea8a34", "text": "在巴西利亚靠两条腿出门的，基本是两种人：建筑系学生，和刚下飞机的游客。学生是来朝圣的，说尼迈耶的建筑要\"用身体丈量\"。行，量吧。\n\n地图上，酒店到国会大厦一公里，看着像隔壁。你一走才发现，中间是八车道的Eixo Monumental，没有路口，只有立交桥，立交桥底下还有立交桥。你走了四十分钟，国会大厦一直在你正前方，又大又白，像吊在驴脑袋前面的胡萝卜，就是不让你靠近。\n\n走到第三十分钟，你终于崩溃，决定直接穿过草坪抄近道。恭喜，你踩出了一条\"欲望之路\"。\n\n最惨的是，你以为自己在反抗城市规划，其实你是在免费给规划师交作业。\n\n最后你浑身是汗，湿度百分之十，嘴唇干得像盖了章。走到门口，保安问你：您的车呢？"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0633", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "excel", "target_name": "Microsoft Excel", "facts": ["First shipped for the Macintosh in 1985; the Windows version followed in 1987.", "Silently converts gene names such as SEPT2 and MARCH1 into dates; by August 2020 the HUGO Gene Nomenclature Committee had renamed 27 human genes (MARCH1 became MARCHF1, SEPT2 became SEPTIN2) and published guidelines in Nature Genetics to stop it.", "In October 2020, Public Health England lost 15,841 COVID-19 cases when a legacy .xls file hit its 65,536-row limit.", "Reinhart and Rogoff's 2010 paper 'Growth in a Time of Debt', cited for austerity policies, omitted five countries through a spreadsheet range error found by a graduate student in 2013.", "Sheets have been capped at 1,048,576 rows by 16,384 columns since Excel 2007."], "a": {"item_id": "bee6f959fe81", "text": "有种老板，用 Excel 管整个公司。图什么？图省钱，还觉得自己特别“数据驱动”。结果公司里权力最大的不是他，是“表哥”，全公司唯一看得懂那张表的人。表里 VLOOKUP 套了七层，没人敢动，谁动谁 #REF!。发工资那天，财务像拆炸弹。员工身份证号被自动变成 1.23E+17，十八位全军覆没。想打开文件，弹窗说：“已被张三锁定”。张三在三亚休年假。最后表哥一跳槽，老板才明白：自己不是在管公司，是在给一个人打工。而那个人，把工作表保护密码带走了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "a27121aa4e43", "text": "有种老板，公司几百号人，ERP不肯上，他说：\"Excel就够了。\"图什么？省钱，还有一种\"所有数字都攥在我手里\"的快感。结果呢？销售总表传了四十个版本，最后一个叫\"最终版_真的不改了_2\"。财务有个VLOOKUP是前任写的，没人敢碰，一动利润表全是#N/A。行政把名单合并了单元格，想筛选，直接报错。最惨的是那个会写宏的小伙子，上个月离职，交接文档就是一个会自己弹窗的文件。现在公司每个月发工资，要靠一台谁都不敢关的电脑。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0634", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "google-glass", "target_name": "谷歌眼镜（Google Glass）", "facts": ["Unveiled at Google I/O 2012 with skydivers livestreaming their jump through it onto the Moscone Center roof.", "The Explorer Edition sold to invited users in 2013 for $1,500.", "Wearers were nicknamed 'Glassholes'; bars, cinemas, and casinos banned the device over its camera.", "The consumer programme ended in January 2015; an Enterprise Edition continued until Google stopped selling it in 2023.", "Its display was a 640-by-360 prism in the corner of one eye."], "a": {"item_id": "ccb8c9ae4f5c", "text": "戴谷歌眼镜进酒吧的人，我研究过，都是同一种人：花一千五百美元，就为了有人问他一句\"这是什么\"。他进门不先点酒，先点自己：\"OK Glass！\"全场安静，以为他在跟鬼说话。然后他说\"拍照\"，酒吧里所有人同时捂脸，你戴个眼镜，大家跟你上了安检。他还委屈：\"我就是想看看天气。\"哥们，在酒吧，天气就是头顶那盏灯。他右眼一直往上翻，看着像在回忆初恋，其实是在读促销邮件。英文里专门给这群人造了个词，叫Glasshole，得多招人烦，才能让一个词专门为你诞生。最后保安过来问：\"您在录像吗？\"他说：\"没有，它只是……亮着。\"被请出门，他对眼镜说\"导航回家\"，眼镜回：\"正在搜索信号。\"他站在路边，右眼是地图，左眼是现实，两只眼睛，过着两种人生。"}, "b": {"item_id": "e9562ca8081e", "text": "戴谷歌眼镜进酒吧的人，我见过。通常三十出头，帽衫上印着“改变世界”，花一千五百美金买了块架在眼前的小棱镜。图什么？图别人一进门先看见他，然后问一句“这是啥”，他就能顺势讲“这是未来”。结果呢？他对着镜腿说“OK Glass，拍下她”，那个小红灯一亮，姑娘直接把一杯啤酒泼在他脸上。保安把他请出去，顺手把眼镜摘了。第二天网上就给他们起了名字，叫“眼镜混蛋”。那玩意儿后来停产了，他也没改变世界，只把那晚的酒吧改成了一个全员提防他的地方。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0635", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "nfts", "target_name": "NFT", "facts": ["Beeple's 'Everydays: The First 5000 Days' sold at Christie's for $69.3 million in March 2021.", "OpenSea, the largest NFT marketplace, processed a record $2.7 billion in trades on 1 May 2022 and just $9.34 million on 28 August 2022, a 99% drop, according to DappRadar data.", "A 2023 dappGambl study found that 95% of the NFT collections it examined had a market value of zero.", "The token usually records only a URL; the image itself sits on an ordinary server or IPFS, not on the blockchain.", "Collins Dictionary named 'NFT' its word of the year for 2021."], "a": {"item_id": "7d289cdaf821", "text": "2021年买NFT的哥们，我特别理解。他们不是想买画，是想买一张\"我比你早知道\"的门票。\n\n我有个朋友，花了几十万买了只戴水手帽的猴子。我说：\"这图我右键就能存啊。\"他很严肃地说：\"你存的是图，我拥有的是……\"他想了三秒，\"……一个指向图的链接。\"兄弟，你花几十万买了个超链接。\n\n买完他就入教了。头像换成猴子，天天在Discord里跟一个叫\"猿猴老王\"的陌生人互道\"gm\"，互相喊\"WAGMI\"，意思是我们都会发财。\n\n现在地板价跌了九成多，他想挂单卖，结果上架的gas费比成交价还贵。\n\n问他怎么办，他说：\"我是长期持有。\"\n\n不，你不是长期持有，你是没人接盘。你唯一的资产，是那个Discord群里还没退群的三个人。"}, "b": {"item_id": "36fed9141b46", "text": "我有个朋友，2021年花三万块买了一张猴子的图。无聊猿，戴墨镜叼烟斗那种。我问他图什么，他说这是数字资产，全世界独一无二。我说你右键另存为，它也能一模一样出现在你电脑里。他说不一样，我在链上有所有权。我问所有权是什么，他想了半天说：\"这只猴子是我的。\"光矿工费就够买台二手车。后来地板价跌了九成，群里一夜之间全是\"早期参与者\"，项目方带着钱消失了。他现在头像还是那只猴子，我看他是真的戴上了墨镜，不敢抬头见人。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0636", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "ugg", "target_name": "UGG 雪地靴", "facts": ["Sheepskin boots Australian surfers wore to warm up after sessions; Brian Smith started selling them in California in 1978.", "Deckers Outdoor bought the brand in 1995 and holds 'UGG' as a trademark in the US and elsewhere; in Australia, 'ugg' is a generic word any bootmaker may use.", "In 2019, a US jury found an Australian maker liable for selling boots labelled 'ugg' into the United States.", "Oprah Winfrey featured them in her 'Favorite Things' lists repeatedly in the 2000s, which drove the first boom.", "UGG is Deckers' largest brand, with over $2 billion in annual sales."], "a": {"item_id": "52524a4d7862", "text": "有一类人，七月三十八度，脚上还蹬着UGG。你问她为啥，她说：“舒服，还不用穿袜子。”姐妹，那是羊毛内里替你把袜子穿了，还是加厚款。\n\n这鞋是澳洲人冲完浪上岸暖脚的。你在北京地铁里冲什么浪？冲的是汗。\n\n脚在里面，四周是羊毛，底下是羊皮，这不叫穿鞋，这叫铜锅：脚是肉，汗是汤。晚上去饭店要脱鞋，她一脱，全桌人当场不点羊肉了，已经闻饱了。\n\n最惨是下雨。UGG一沾水就硬，还掉色，所以夏天暴雨，她们在路口跳格子，跟拆雷一样。你以为她怕湿脚？她是在保护那一千多块的信仰。\n\n等到秋天，她还特骄傲：“你看，我终于穿对季节了。”对，那一个月，你和UGG的脚臭终于不是孤军奋战了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "3ebab0e81cc7", "text": "你们见过夏天三十八度还穿UGG的人吧？两只毛茸茸的大头，像刚从羊身上薅下来、还没下班的小动物。他们图什么？图一个\"我早就入冬了\"的仪式感。结果呢？走三百米，羊皮里子把脚焖成一锅卤鸭掌，袜子都不用穿，直接在桑拿房里逛街。到了十月，那层软塌塌的鞋底已经磨出了地图。最惨的是羊毛里子被汗压成一块毡，踩在地铁台阶上，像踩着两块泡过水的抹布。朋友圈里配文\"今天也很暖\"，照片里那双栗色的靴子，已经晒成了一条咸鱼。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0637", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "durian", "target_name": "榴莲", "facts": ["Banned on Singapore's MRT trains and in many Southeast Asian hotels because of the smell.", "A 2017 genome study in Nature Genetics traced the odour to a duplicated family of MGL genes that pump out volatile sulfur compounds.", "China buys most of the world's durian exports, chiefly from Thailand, in a trade worth billions of dollars a year.", "Alfred Russel Wallace wrote in 1856 that eating it was 'a new sensation worth a voyage to the East to experience'."], "a": {"item_id": "17f9f9e15e60", "text": "在高铁上吃榴莲的人，我一直想知道他图什么。图香？他鼻子早被腌入味了，闻不到。他以为自己在吃水果，其实全车厢都在被迫吃他的午饭。\n\n最绝的是他还特别坦荡，转头跟邻座说：\"你尝一口就懂了。\"大哥，我们早尝了，用鼻子，从郑州尝到武汉。\n\n下场也很统一。乘务员走过来，不骂你，就问一句：\"这是谁的？\"全车厢没人说话，但一百个鼻子同时转向你，这叫集体指认。\n\n你想销毁证据？壳带刺，塞包里扎手，包也成了证物。回家洗五遍手，老婆一开门：\"你今天去哪个案发现场了？\"晚上打个嗝，等于二次自首。"}, "b": {"item_id": "68573ec1558a", "text": "地铁上吃榴莲的人，我观察过，他们不是饿，是在传教。你一皱眉，他立刻甩出那句经典台词：“闻着臭，吃着香！”大哥，我又不吃，你光给我上半句，这跟只收定金不发货有什么区别？\n\n而且他还爱报价：“猫山王，八十一斤！”意思是这味儿是进口的，你得心怀感激。全车厢被迫消费，每人摊他二十块钱的味儿。\n\n下场呢？他到站下车，以为结束了。不，那味儿会住进他指甲缝里，洗三遍，晚上挠头都是猫山王。第二天同事全问他：“你昨天坐的几号线？”\n\n新加坡地铁明令禁止带榴莲。我建议咱们也补一条：禁止带吃过榴莲的人。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0638", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "windows-8", "target_name": "Windows 8", "facts": ["Released in October 2012; removed the Start button and booted into a full-screen grid of tiles designed for touch screens most PCs did not have.", "The 8.1 update in 2013 put a Start button back, Windows 10 (2015) restored the Start menu, and Microsoft skipped the name Windows 9 altogether.", "Microsoft dropped the 'Metro' name in August 2012 after reports that German retailer Metro AG had objected on trademark grounds; Microsoft called 'Metro' a code name and denied any litigation, and the style was afterwards known informally as 'Modern UI'.", "The 'Charms bar' hid Search, Share, and Settings behind an invisible swipe from the right edge.", "Windows RT, the ARM edition launched alongside it, could not run ordinary Windows programs; Microsoft wrote down $900 million of unsold Surface RT tablets in 2013."], "a": {"item_id": "4dea39aaf51b", "text": "我有个哥们儿，2012年非要给台式机装Windows 8，理由是“新的肯定比旧的好”。我说你这电脑连个触屏都没有，他说没事，“我用鼠标点磁贴”。\n\n装完那天他请我去看。开机，满屏花花绿绿的大方块，跟营业厅的叫号屏似的。他拿着鼠标在那儿横着拖，拖了半天才找到“桌面”这个磁贴。我说你装了个新系统，就为了点进去看旧桌面？\n\n最惨的是关机。开始按钮没了，他找了十分钟，最后鼠标往右上角一怼，蹦出一排竖条，设置，电源，关机。三步。他关一次机，比他的电脑开一次机还慢。\n\n现在他养成了个习惯：鼠标一闲下来，就往屏幕角落里甩。别人的鼠标是用来点东西的，他的鼠标是用来跟屏幕四个角打游击的。\n\n后来8.1把开始按钮还给他了，他激动得点了一下。结果点开的还是那屏大方块。"}, "b": {"item_id": "825a78e26940", "text": "给台式机装Windows 8的人，我太了解了。这种人，朋友聚会必说一句：\"我这个是尝鲜。\"尝鲜，你家台式机是个五斤重的铁箱子，插着鼠标键盘，哪来的\"鲜\"？\n\n图什么呢？图开机快，四秒。是，四秒就进去了，进去以后花四十分钟找关机。\n\n开始按钮没了，换成一整屏磁贴，天气、股票、应用商店，全对着你闪。他们对着显示器伸手去戳，显示器说：\"大哥，我不是触屏。\"\n\n关机更绝。鼠标得甩到右上角，等超级按钮栏冒出来，点设置，点电源，才能关。关个机像密室逃脱。\n\n下场呢？他们最后都去搜\"Windows 8 怎么把开始菜单弄回来\"，下载个第三方软件，把微软删掉的东西，亲手装回去。\n\n等到8.1出了，开始按钮回来了，他们热泪盈眶，一点：跳到磁贴屏。\n\n那不是按钮，那是个门铃，按了还是那帮磁贴来开门。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0639", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "jackfruit", "target_name": "菠萝蜜", "facts": ["The largest fruit that grows on a tree; single fruits can weigh over 40 kg.", "The national fruit of Bangladesh and the state fruit of Kerala and Tamil Nadu.", "Unripe flesh is sold as a vegan 'pulled pork' substitute, although it has only 1-2 g of protein per 100 g.", "Cutting one releases a sticky white latex, so cooks oil their knives and hands first.", "A member of the mulberry family (Moraceae), a relative of breadfruit and figs."], "a": {"item_id": "73bd57e7ed48", "text": "有老板把青菠萝蜜撕成丝，当手撕猪肉卖。我就佩服，创业第一步，先跟自己的手结仇。青菠萝蜜那个胶，比前任还难甩，洗手液没用，得拿食用油搓。后厨剥一天，十根手指粘成一只爪子，出来跟客人握手，客人以为他入了什么帮派。\n\n图啥？图便宜。可你算算：猪肉切了就能炒，菠萝蜜得先拿刀砍一身刺，再对付一手胶，炖三小时，还得倒半瓶酱油才勉强有点肉味。人工加调料，够买半头猪了。\n\n最后下场，有食客点评：“这是我吃过最像菠萝蜜的猪肉。”一句话，比市监局还准。罚款单下来，老板终于明白，这生意他唯一卖出去的肉，是自己的肉疼。"}, "b": {"item_id": "b09e1a218e62", "text": "现在网红轻食店流行一道菜，叫“植物基手撕猪肉”，六十八一碗。我吃了一口，菠萝蜜。就是海南路边五块钱一斤、一个三十斤、浑身长刺、得抱着才能搬走的那个。\n\n老板图什么？图它没熟的时候能撕成一丝一丝的，看着像肉。可他没告诉你，切这玩意儿会流白胶，粘手上肥皂都洗不掉，得拿食用油搓。\n\n你想想这画面：一个天天喊“告别动物油脂”的素食老板，每天打烊第一件事，是在后厨拎一壶花生油搓手，搓得满手油光，跟刚偷吃完一盘红烧肉似的。\n\n最惨的是有一回他切到个熟的，整个店一股香蕉混榴莲的甜味儿。顾客举着筷子问：“老板，你这猪肉怎么一股水果糖味儿？”\n\n老板想了半天：“这是……吃水果长大的猪。”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0640", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "emacs", "target_name": "Emacs", "facts": ["Began in 1976 as a set of macros for the TECO editor at MIT's AI Lab, written by Richard Stallman and others; GNU Emacs followed in 1985.", "Extensible in its own Lisp dialect, Emacs Lisp; the standing joke is that it is a fine operating system lacking only a good editor.", "Ships with a psychotherapist (M-x doctor), Tetris, a mail client, a calendar, and a web browser (eww).", "An old backronym is 'Eight Megabytes And Constantly Swapping', from when 8 MB was a lot of memory.", "'Emacs pinky' is the name for the hand strain blamed on its Control-key chords."], "a": {"item_id": "d38cb162db73", "text": "我有个朋友，把Emacs当操作系统用。邮件用mu4e，日程用org-mode，连窗口管理都交给EXWM。他的电脑开机只有一个目的：启动Emacs。\n\n我问他图什么，他说：“这样我一辈子都不用离开Emacs。”兄弟，这不叫自由，这叫软禁，门还是你自己拿elisp焊死的。\n\n他的init.el写了三千行，比他简历还长。上周升级了一个包，邮件收不了，日程没了，窗口也打不开，全家断电。查了一晚上，原因是少了一个括号。\n\n最惨的是他的小拇指。按了十年Ctrl，得了腱鞘炎。医生让他少按，他回家装了evil-mode。用Vim的键位，在Emacs里，躲Emacs。"}, "b": {"item_id": "f945ff696b4a", "text": "把 Emacs 当操作系统用的人，我见过。早上在 Emacs 里收邮件，中午在 org-mode 里排日程，晚上在 Emacs 里听音乐、写日记、看股票。他管这叫\"一站式工作流\"。\n\n有天他想关机，按了 C-x C-c，屏幕跳出一行字：\"Modified buffers exist; exit anyway? (yes or no)\"。他心想我又没改什么，一看，十四个 buffer 没保存，其中一个是他写了三年的日记。\n\n他盯着屏幕想了半分钟，最后打出了 yes。然后他意识到，这个操作系统唯一会问他要不要关机的时候，也是唯一不让他关机的时候。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0641", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "almond-milk", "target_name": "杏仁奶", "facts": ["California grows about 80% of the world's almonds.", "A single almond takes about 1.1 US gallons (roughly 4 litres) of water to grow, a figure Mother Jones published in 2014 that spread during California's drought.", "A 2015 US class action alleged Almond Breeze contained only about 2% almonds; it partly survived a motion to dismiss, was stayed in 2017 pending a separate Blue Diamond labelling settlement, and the plaintiffs then dropped it.", "Pollinating California's almond orchards each February requires trucking in most of the commercial honeybee colonies in the United States.", "Medieval European cooks used almond milk as a Lenten substitute for dairy, centuries before the current fashion."], "a": {"item_id": "9b69772a0059", "text": "我有个朋友，为了环保，把牛奶戒了，改喝杏仁奶。天天端着一杯杏仁拿铁，跟我上课：“你知道吗？牛放屁，会导致全球变暖。”\n\n行，牛放屁。那你知道一颗杏仁要喝多少水吗？差不多四升。你每天一盒杏仁奶，等于亲自帮加州又旱了一个夏天。\n\n而且那一盒里，杏仁只占百分之二，剩下全是水。你花三十八块，买的是一瓶被杏仁路过的自来水。\n\n更惨的是，加州的杏仁全靠蜜蜂授粉。每年几百亿只蜜蜂被卡车拉过去打工，累死一大片。\n\n所以我这朋友，替地球省了一头牛的屁，搭进去好几个蜂群。他现在一端起拿铁就心虚，听见“嗡”一声，都想鞠躬道歉。"}, "b": {"item_id": "096100e8c869", "text": "我朋友为了环保戒了牛奶，改喝杏仁奶。他说牛放屁排甲烷，地球受不了。行，有道理。然后我问他杏仁哪来的，他说加州。加州啊，那地方干得裂开，种一颗杏仁要喝掉好几升水，他一杯杏仁奶下去，等于替加州的杏树喝了半缸。更绝的是，加州的杏树开花得靠蜜蜂，蜂箱是用大卡车拉过去的，蜜蜂一路颠过去，授完粉累死一片。杏仁奶泡咖啡，三分钟就分层，像他的环保决心，看着坚定，一放就散。他现在每天喝两杯，花的钱是牛奶的三倍，说这是为地球付的。我说你付的是加州的水费，地球一分没收到。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0642", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "comic-sans", "target_name": "Comic Sans 字体", "facts": ["Designed by Vincent Connare at Microsoft in 1994, modeled on the comic-book lettering in The Dark Knight Returns and Watchmen.", "Made for the speech bubbles of Rover, the cartoon dog in Microsoft Bob; it missed Bob's release and shipped instead in the Windows 95 Plus! pack.", "CERN's July 2012 Higgs boson discovery announcement was presented on slides set in it.", "The 'Ban Comic Sans' campaign, started by designers Dave and Holly Combs in 1999, ran until 2019, when they retired it and launched usecomicsans.com."], "a": {"item_id": "8384fe94668e", "text": "我们公司人事上周发裁员通知，用的Comic Sans。我问为什么，她说：“想柔和一点，减少冲击。”\n\n姐姐，这字体当年是给微软一只叫Rover的卡通狗设计的，专门用来写对话气泡。你这是让一条狗通知我下岗。\n\n结果呢？“因业务调整，您的岗位将被取消”，每个字都圆滚滚、歪歪扭扭，读起来就是“汪！你被开除啦～”\n\n冲击确实没有，因为根本没人信。有个哥们儿回复了一串“哈哈哈”，周一照常来上班，被保安拦在门口，还委屈：“你们那个通知不是在搞笑吗？”\n\n最惨的是人事自己，月底被优化了，通知也是Comic Sans。她盯着屏幕看了十分钟，说：“这肯定是恶作剧。”\n\n行，你的柔和，终于轮到你自己了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "5f875c2be689", "text": "你们收过用Comic Sans写的正式通知吗？“本周六全小区停水”，底下配着那个圆滚滚的漫画体。发这种通知的，多半是物业或者HR，他们心里想的是：字圆一点，坏消息就没那么硬。可这字原本是微软给一个卡通小助手配气泡台词用的，骨子里就是为了“亲切”设计的。结果呢？停水像生日派对邀请函，扣工资像在发贴纸。大家看完第一反应不是慌，是笑：“这是哪个幼儿园发的？”通知被截图传遍业主群，发通知的人成了全小区的表情包。他本想做个好人，最后做成了段子手。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0643", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "javascript", "target_name": "JavaScript", "facts": ["Written by Brendan Eich at Netscape in ten days in May 1995; named Mocha, then LiveScript, then JavaScript to ride Java's marketing, although the two are unrelated.", "typeof null returns 'object', a bug from the first version that has been kept for compatibility ever since.", "In March 2016 the author of left-pad, an 11-line npm package, unpublished it and broke builds across the industry, including Babel and React.", "[] + {} evaluates to '[object Object]'; {} + [] typed at a console evaluates to 0.", "Standardized by Ecma as 'ECMAScript' in 1997 because the name JavaScript is a trademark, first Sun's and now Oracle's."], "a": {"item_id": "0f8759744771", "text": "我朋友是前端，什么都用 JavaScript 写。写个脚本，用 Node。写个桌面软件，套个 Electron，一开就吃八百兆内存。他说这叫一套技术栈通吃。我说你那是通吃，还是通缉？\n\n上周他想写个开机自动备份，npm install 下来一千三百个包，其中一个叫 is-odd，专门判断一个数是不是奇数。备份脚本跑起来，先等一个包判断今天是不是单号，再等另一个包去找文件。备份成功了，电脑里也多了一个 node_modules，比他硬盘里所有照片加起来还大。\n\n现在他开始学 Rust，说要回归本质。我问他本质是什么，他说是 undefined。"}, "b": {"item_id": "d6f1a64d2099", "text": "现在有一种人，什么都要拿 JavaScript 写。网页写完写后端，后端写完写桌面软件，现在在研究拿它控制烤箱。你问他图啥，他说：\"我不想再学一门新语言。\"\n\n兄弟，你为了不学一门语言，每周都在学一个新框架。\n\n他写的记事本用 Electron，打开占八百兆内存。因为里面塞了一整个浏览器，就为了让你输入\"买菜\"两个字。\n\n他还跟你解释：\"1\"加 1 等于 11，\"1\"减 1 等于 0，这叫灵活。灵活到最后，项目源码三千行，node_modules 两个 G。\n\n上线那天报错：undefined is not a function。他盯着屏幕，一脸茫然：\"到底是谁 undefined 了？\"\n\n我觉得是他的职业规划。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0644", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "oat-milk", "target_name": "燕麦奶", "facts": ["Oatly started in Sweden in the 1990s, built on Lund University research by Rickard Öste on lactose intolerance.", "Oatly's slogan 'It's like milk but made for humans' was banned in Sweden in 2015 after the dairy lobby LRF Mjölk sued; Oatly then published the lawsuit as advertising.", "Oatly listed on Nasdaq in May 2021 at a valuation near $10 billion; the shares later lost more than 90% of their value.", "Commercial oat milk is made by breaking oat starch into maltose with amylase enzymes, and many brands, Oatly among them, add rapeseed oil; the result has more sugar and a higher glycaemic index than almond or soy milk."], "a": {"item_id": "55ab352fe073", "text": "每个咖啡店都有这种人，站在柜台前深吸一口气：“拿铁，换燕麦奶。”语气像在宣布皈依。\n\n你问他乳糖不耐吗？不是。“就是想少点负担。”然后他加点了一块芝士蛋糕。\n\n负担是少了，钱包的负担没少，燕麦奶加五块。而且为了打出奶泡，燕麦里的淀粉会被水解成麦芽糖，升糖比牛奶还高。他花五块钱，把一杯奶换成了一杯液体麦片，还觉得自己在做减法。\n\n喝到一半，他拍照发朋友圈：“植物基生活。”\n\n最后一口，他皱着眉品了品：“嗯，有层次。”\n\n兄弟，那不是层次，那是你早餐的碗底。"}, "b": {"item_id": "e299ef76bfd2", "text": "有些人点拿铁，非要补一句：换燕麦奶。行，你换。店员一边敲单一边抬头看你，加五块。你其实不知道为什么换，你只知道办公室那个健身的同事换了。端上来喝一口，嗯，甜的，还有一股麦片泡在水里的味道。你心里一紧，觉得自己是不是喝出了健康。拍照的时候，奶泡塌得比你的表情还快。那半杯你放在工位上，等它自己慢慢变成一碗麦片粥。最后你还是回去点普通牛奶，还得跟同事解释：我不是不喜欢，我就是想试试。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0645", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "nuclear-fusion", "target_name": "可控核聚变", "facts": ["Has been 'about 30 years away' since the 1950s; at the 1955 Geneva atoms-for-peace conference, Homi Bhabha predicted fusion power within two decades.", "ITER, agreed in 2006 for first plasma in 2016, re-baselined in 2024 to start research operations in 2034 and deuterium-tritium burning in 2039, at a cost estimated above €20 billion.", "NIF's December 2022 'ignition' shot released 3.15 MJ from 2.05 MJ of laser energy; the lasers drew about 300 MJ from the grid to fire.", "Private fusion companies had raised more than $7 billion by 2024."], "a": {"item_id": "8315ced7f71d", "text": "可控核聚变还有十年，这话我爸上大学时就听过，是他老师说的。现在我听，说的人是他老师的学生。这行最稳定的不是等离子体，是那句“还有十年”，代代相传，比托卡马克里那团火命长多了。\n\n干这行的人图什么？你想啊，一个人一辈子的成果，是把一团一亿度的气体多关住了三十秒。开发布会，“创世界纪录！”台下鼓掌。他自己心里清楚，下个月还得写报告申请经费，因为磁约束线圈又烧了。\n\n最惨的是退休那天。学生问他：“老师，您干了四十年，离点火成功还有多远？”他想了想，特别诚实：“还有十年。”\n\n他入行那年也是这么说的。四十年过去，一天都没少。这才叫能量守恒。"}, "b": {"item_id": "0c1b7366a47b", "text": "搞可控核聚变的那帮人，我真佩服。每次申请经费，他们都拍着胸脯说：再给十年，就能把太阳装进罐子里。这话从上世纪五十年代说到现在，太阳没装进去，罐子倒是越造越大。ITER画图画了三十多年，工期一延再延，预算翻了好几倍，钱烧得比等离子体还热。你问他们图什么？图的是海水里那点氘，图的是一个不冒烟的未来。落得什么下场？当年的博士生，如今成了白头发的教授，还在给学生画同一张饼：就差最后一公里了。这最后一公里，比太阳的寿命还长。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0646", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "durian", "target_name": "榴莲", "facts": ["Banned on Singapore's MRT trains and in many Southeast Asian hotels because of the smell.", "A 2017 genome study in Nature Genetics traced the odour to a duplicated family of MGL genes that pump out volatile sulfur compounds.", "China buys most of the world's durian exports, chiefly from Thailand, in a trade worth billions of dollars a year.", "Alfred Russel Wallace wrote in 1856 that eating it was 'a new sensation worth a voyage to the East to experience'."], "a": {"item_id": "fd9d6c3fef4a", "text": "在地铁上开榴莲的人，我一直好奇你图什么？图那句“闻着臭吃着香”是吧？大哥，吃着香的只有你，闻着臭的是一整节车厢。你这不叫吃水果，叫一人点单，全车买单。\n\n而且这种人还特讲究，戴一次性手套，掰得小心翼翼，生怕刺扎手。你的手保住了，我们的鼻子谁管？\n\n他还要给你科普：“猫山王，一百多一斤！”哦，你花一百多，买了个毒气弹，还让我们免费帮你验货。\n\n下场也摆在那：车厢立刻空出一圈，全城最好找座位的人就是他。可惜手套白戴了，第二天一张嘴，全办公室都知道他昨晚吃的是猫山王。"}, "b": {"item_id": "17f9f9e15e60", "text": "在高铁上吃榴莲的人，我一直想知道他图什么。图香？他鼻子早被腌入味了，闻不到。他以为自己在吃水果，其实全车厢都在被迫吃他的午饭。\n\n最绝的是他还特别坦荡，转头跟邻座说：\"你尝一口就懂了。\"大哥，我们早尝了，用鼻子，从郑州尝到武汉。\n\n下场也很统一。乘务员走过来，不骂你，就问一句：\"这是谁的？\"全车厢没人说话，但一百个鼻子同时转向你，这叫集体指认。\n\n你想销毁证据？壳带刺，塞包里扎手，包也成了证物。回家洗五遍手，老婆一开门：\"你今天去哪个案发现场了？\"晚上打个嗝，等于二次自首。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0647", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "google-sheets", "target_name": "Google Sheets", "facts": ["Launched in June 2006 as Google Spreadsheets, built on XL2Web, a product Google acquired from 2Web Technologies.", "A spreadsheet is capped at 10 million cells (raised from 5 million in 2022).", "Functions such as IMPORTHTML, IMPORTXML, and GOOGLEFINANCE pull live web data straight into cells.", "Free with a Google account; files live in Google Drive, and collaborators edit the same sheet simultaneously with coloured cursors.", "In a browser, offline editing works only in Chrome or Edge, with the Google Docs Offline extension installed and offline access switched on while still connected; the Android and iOS apps work offline natively."], "a": {"item_id": "2a199d10637d", "text": "我特别佩服那种用Google Sheets管团队的领导。他们的口头禅是：“不用买系统，一个表格就够了。”省了一年三千块软件费，代价是自己变成全公司的人肉服务器。\n\n刚开始就一个标签页，三个月后四十七个，名字叫“总表”“总表2”“总表最终”“总表最终（别动）”。他还觉得自己是工程师，因为他会VLOOKUP。全组只有他会，别人看见公式栏，就像看见祖传药方。\n\n最离谱是开会。他共享屏幕，右上角蹦出个“匿名水豚正在查看”。全场安静，没人知道谁是水豚，只知道水豚刚把C列改成了红色。\n\n他请一天假，团队就瘫痪了，因为没人知道红色是“已完成”，还是“老板生气了”。\n\n后来他离职，交接文档就一句话：“有问题，看版本历史。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "5ceaecd3ab50", "text": "你们见过用 Google 表格管团队的人吗？这种人通常是行政出身，入职第一天就甩给大家一个链接，标题叫《项目总表》。图什么？图一眼看全局。结果他开了二十个标签页，第一个叫“总览”，第十七个叫“真的总览”，最后一个叫“别删”。每人一行，有人把单元格染成黄色，有人加了下拉框，选项从“进行中”一路改成“待定，等老板回”。周五他一打开，满屏#REF!，像看见自己的人生。翻版本记录更绝，凌晨三点有人把“张三”改成了“张三丰”。他终于明白，他管的不是团队，是一个会自己长脾气的表格。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0648", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "chicago-deep-dish", "target_name": "芝加哥深盘披萨", "facts": ["Created at Pizzeria Uno in Chicago in 1943; credit is disputed among founder Ike Sewell, his partner Ric Riccardo, and cook Rudy Malnati.", "Assembled upside down: sliced mozzarella on the dough, then toppings, then crushed tomatoes on top, so the cheese does not scorch during the long bake.", "Jon Stewart called it 'tomato soup in a bread bowl' and 'a casserole' on The Daily Show in 2013.", "Chicagoans' everyday pizza is thin 'tavern-style' cut into squares; GrubHub order data reported by WBEZ in 2013 showed city residents ordering non-deep-dish pizza about 10 to 1 over deep dish, with deep dish and stuffed making up under 9% of orders."], "a": {"item_id": "e091429db8dc", "text": "管芝加哥深盘叫“披萨”的人，主要就两种：芝加哥本地人，和刚在Giordano's门口排了一小时队的游客。\n\n本地人图什么？面子。你们那儿冬天零下二十度，总得有样东西能拿出去跟纽约吵架吧。\n\n游客图什么？沉没成本。排队一小时，再等四十五分钟烤，端上来番茄酱在最上面，奶酪压在底下，四周一圈黄油面墙。你不叫它披萨，就得承认自己花三十八刀排队吃了一锅砂锅。\n\n下场呢？拿刀叉吃“披萨”，一块就饱，剩下的打包，回酒店一躺，消化得比烤得还久。第二天照样发朋友圈：“正宗芝加哥披萨！”配图一看，一个蛋糕模具。"}, "b": {"item_id": "2802b3de89d7", "text": "管芝加哥深盘叫披萨的人，我见过三种。本地人嘴上说\"我们这儿就这么吃\"；网红拍完照发帖写\"正宗深盘披萨\"；还有一种最狠，纽约来的，吃第一口就开始跟服务员讲道理。图什么？图一个名分。可深盘是个铁锅里烤四十五分钟的芝士蛋糕，芝士垫在底下，番茄酱盖在最上面，你得拿刀拿叉，切到第三块，芝士还在拉丝。这帮人非要把它塞进\"披萨\"这个词里。结果那位纽约哥吃完，服务员问要不要打包，他说：\"不用，我要带一锅回家，当披萨。\"然后抱着一个烫手的铁锅挤地铁，全车人都以为他在搬家。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0649", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "carbon-nanotubes", "target_name": "碳纳米管", "facts": ["Reported by Sumio Iijima in Nature in 1991.", "Promised as the cable for a space elevator; a 2006 analysis showed that ordinary defects cut the strength of a macroscopic cable far below the ideal value.", "Long, rigid multi-walled nanotubes behaved like asbestos fibres in a 2008 mouse study in Nature Nanotechnology.", "Sorting metallic from semiconducting tubes has blocked nanotube electronics for three decades; MIT's 16-bit RV16X-NANO processor (2019) is still a lab demonstration.", "Today they are sold mainly as a conductive additive in lithium-ion battery electrodes."], "a": {"item_id": "c20ac403ef4f", "text": "你们见过拿碳纳米管融资的人吗？一般是个博士，PPT第一页就是太空电梯：“强度是钢的一百倍，一根线从地面拉到三万六千公里外！”投资人听得热泪盈眶。\n\n我就想问：你实验室里最长的一根有多长？“两厘米。”那你这不叫太空电梯，叫太空牙线。\n\n他图啥？图下一轮。电梯盖不盖得成不重要，估值得先上去。\n\n五年后再看，公司转型了，现在卖“导电剂”，就是往锂电池里掺的那点黑粉，一公斤赚几块钱。投资人问：“电梯呢？”博士说：“电梯……在电池里，形成了导电网络。”\n\n行，当年说要通天，现在是在电池里修了个楼梯间。"}, "b": {"item_id": "dc1d00e455ba", "text": "碳纳米管创业的，PPT第一页永远是太空电梯：比钢强一百倍，从地球拉根绳到同步轨道。投资人问：“多长了？”他说：“实验室最长做到半米。”离三万六千公里，还差三万五千九百九十九公里九百九十九米五。\n\n第二页讲替代硅：“摩尔定律到头了，我们接班！”问良率呢？他说：“有一个小问题，长出来三分之一是金属的，三分之二是半导体的，我们分不开。”大哥，你这叫“替代硅”？你这叫“开盲盒”。\n\n讲了二十年，一九九一年发现的东西，至今还在“即将改变世界”。\n\n最后公司怎么样了？活得挺好。不造电梯，不造芯片，一吨一吨往锂电池里卖导电剂，按公斤算钱。路演时说要通往太空，年报里写的是“主营业务：黑色粉末”。\n\n太空电梯没建成，但他确实上去了，坐的是电动车。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0650", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "javascript", "target_name": "JavaScript", "facts": ["Written by Brendan Eich at Netscape in ten days in May 1995; named Mocha, then LiveScript, then JavaScript to ride Java's marketing, although the two are unrelated.", "typeof null returns 'object', a bug from the first version that has been kept for compatibility ever since.", "In March 2016 the author of left-pad, an 11-line npm package, unpublished it and broke builds across the industry, including Babel and React.", "[] + {} evaluates to '[object Object]'; {} + [] typed at a console evaluates to 0.", "Standardized by Ecma as 'ECMAScript' in 1997 because the name JavaScript is a trademark, first Sun's and now Oracle's."], "a": {"item_id": "3206715972e0", "text": "我同事学了三个月 JavaScript，现在看什么都像 JS。前两天他说要做个记事本，用 Electron。我说记事本？他说对，打出来 300 兆，因为里面自带一个 Chrome。我说你一个记事本为啥要带浏览器？他说：“我不想学第二门语言。”兄弟，你省的是学习时间，吃的是我的内存。\n\n他连判断奇偶数都不自己写，npm install is-even。装完 node_modules 两个G，他自己的代码三行。\n\n我考他：\"1\"+1 等于几？他说 11。\"1\"-1 呢？0。我说你就这么接受了？他说：“习惯了。”\n\n各位，这不是程序员，这是被 JavaScript 虐出斯德哥尔摩综合征的人。"}, "b": {"item_id": "9255e820a06a", "text": "我有个朋友，信一句话：“凡是能用JavaScript写的，最终都会用JavaScript写。”他当预言，我当诅咒。\n\n他写了个计算器，用Electron打包，两百兆，自带一整个Chrome，就为了算一加一。一加一算对了，0.1加0.2出来0.30000000000000004。他说没事，装个库。一装，node_modules比他的人生还沉，里面有个包叫is-odd，专门判断奇数，它还依赖另一个包叫is-number。\n\n他图什么？图“前后端一门语言，不用学第二种”。结果为了这一门，React、Vue、Svelte轮着学，刚学会一个，下一个又出来了。\n\n现在他三十五，头发没了，问我他算个啥。我说你就是typeof null：明明啥也没有，还非说自己是个object。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0651", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "esperanto", "target_name": "世界语", "facts": ["Published in Warsaw in 1887 by L. L. Zamenhof, a Białystok-born eye doctor, under the pen name Doktoro Esperanto, 'one who hopes'.", "Speaker estimates range from 100,000 to two million; roughly a thousand people grow up with it as a native language.", "William Shatner starred in Incubus (1966), a horror film shot entirely in Esperanto, with pronunciation Esperantists still mock.", "Its Wikipedia has over 350,000 articles, and its Duolingo course for English speakers had 1.36 million learners by July 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "7911ad5f0fdc", "text": "学世界语的人，图的是全人类终于能好好说话。结果全人类没来，来的是同一拨人。这门语言的名字就叫\"希望者\"，学它的人等于从第一天就领了个外号。大会上他们互相寒暄：你哪国的？巴西。我韩国的。平时用世界语跟谁聊？跟你们俩啊。名词加-o，形容词加-a，宾格加-n，每句话都挂满尾巴，语法一个不错，朋友一个没有。"}, "b": {"item_id": "b6106e5169c7", "text": "学世界语的人图什么？图世界和平。发明人柴门霍夫说，人类打架是因为语言不通。结果一百多年过去了，学成的人飞去参加世界语大会，全世界同好聚一堂，你一开口“Saluton”，对方也“Saluton”，然后俩人大眼瞪小眼。因为你们发现，这门语言唯一的用途，就是找到另一个会这门语言的人，互相确认：对，咱俩都有病。\n\n“Esperanto”本意是“怀抱希望的人”，名字起得真诚实，没叫“已经实现的人”。\n\n最惨的是，他们的语法一条例外都没有，人生全是例外：没人跟他们说话。想练口语，只能住进另一个世界语者家里，沙发一睡三天，聊的全是“你为什么也学这个”。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0652", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "papyrus", "target_name": "Papyrus 字体", "facts": ["Hand-drawn by Chris Costello in 1982 over about six months, when he was 23; he sold the rights to Letraset for $750.", "Bundled with macOS and with Microsoft Office, which is why it appears on so many menus and signs.", "James Cameron's Avatar (2009) used it for the film's logo and subtitles.", "A 2017 Saturday Night Live sketch starring Ryan Gosling is about a man unable to get over that choice."], "a": {"item_id": "02bcbf4a1359", "text": "开养生馆的老板选招牌，流程我都猜得到：打开Word，字体一路往下滑，滑到Papyrus，眼睛一亮——“哎，这个毛边，有古老东方的神秘感。”大哥，这字是个美国小伙二十三岁拿钢笔在纸上描出来的，你这“东方”是批发来的。更绝的是隔壁泰式按摩、墨西哥卷饼、埃及烤肉，全是同一个Papyrus，整条街像同一个古文明的遗址。他图什么？图Office自带，图不花钱，图“看着有文化”。结果呢？顾客一抬头，以为进的不是足疗店，是《阿凡达》片场。卡梅隆好歹花了几亿美元才用上这款字，你花了零块零毛，撞得一模一样，人家拿了奥斯卡，你拿了张差评：“技师手法一般，招牌像蓝色外星人开的。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "1155b79dd841", "text": "我们这条街，艾灸馆、素食馆、足疗店，招牌全是Papyrus，就是那种笔画毛毛糙糙、像拿烧焦的树枝在牛皮纸上划出来的字体。\n\n老板们图什么？图\"古法\"\"天然\"\"千年底蕴\"。设计师报价三千，他一想：Word里自带的，免费，还自带古埃及气质，血赚。\n\n可《阿凡达》砸了二十多亿美元，片名用的就是Papyrus，被全世界笑了十几年。你一个六十平的刮痧店，凭什么觉得自己扛得住？\n\n现在客人走到门口，抬头一看\"古法艾灸\"，字还带着焦边，真不知道是进去放松，还是进去防腐。\n\n最惨的是素食馆老板，招牌、菜单、外卖袋全是Papyrus，一份麻婆豆腐，看着像刚从墓里刨出来的。他还纳闷生意怎么越来越淡。兄弟，你卖的是出土文物。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0653", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "pmma", "target_name": "PMMA 电子束光刻胶", "facts": ["The same polymer as Plexiglas and acrylic glass, dissolved in anisole or chlorobenzene and spun into a thin film.", "The oldest electron-beam resist still in everyday use; IBM researchers reported it as one in the late 1960s.", "Positive tone: the beam breaks polymer chains and the exposed region dissolves in a MIBK:IPA developer.", "Resolves below 10 nm but has poor sensitivity, so doses of hundreds to over a thousand µC/cm² (depending on voltage) make writing slow.", "Poor dry-etch resistance, so it is used mostly for lift-off rather than as an etch mask."], "a": {"item_id": "66f317caaa7a", "text": "干电子束光刻还在用 PMMA 的，基本都是博士生。图什么？图一句“PMMA 分辨率高，业内金标准”。金标准，对，这玩意儿跟你家鱼缸的亚克力、牙医给你做的牙托是一个东西。人家做牙托的，做完下班回家吃饭。咱们呢，拿它刻十纳米，电子枪在里面扫一整夜，剂量大得像给它做理疗，早上进实验室，机器还在写，你俩互相看着，谁也不好意思先走。\n\n好不容易写完了，MIBK 加 IPA 一泡，线条漂亮。结果一刻蚀，胶比我工资涨得还慢，消失得比导师的承诺还快。\n\n师弟问：“师兄，为什么不换个胶？”\n\n我说：“导师说，当年师兄就是用 950K 毕业的。”\n\n师兄毕业了，我还守着一块有机玻璃，等它给我一个答辩。"}, "b": {"item_id": "d0b057bba930", "text": "做电子束光刻的朋友，我是真佩服你们。别人都上EUV了，你们还抱着950K的PMMA不撒手。电子一打，分子链断成小段，拿MIBK兑异丙醇泡一泡，图形就显出来了，干净得很。问题是它怕刻蚀，刻到一半自己先塌了。你们图什么？图十纳米的线条画得漂亮。然后呢？做剥离，丙酮泡一晚上，金属毛边比头发丝还多。博士师兄熬了三个月，论文第三章就是一张拖尾的SEM照片。导师看了一眼说：线条挺好，就是没法用。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0654", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "beanie-babies", "target_name": "Beanie Babies 豆豆娃", "facts": ["Launched by Ty Inc. in 1993 and sold only through small gift shops, with designs 'retired' to manufacture scarcity.", "At the height of the craze Beanie Babies accounted for about 10% of eBay's sales, and eBay's 1998 IPO filing said the site at times had over 30,000 Beanie Baby auctions running simultaneously in the quarter ending June 1998.", "In 1999 a divorcing couple divided their Beanie Baby collection one by one on a courtroom floor, in a photograph that ran worldwide.", "In 1999 Ty announced that Beanie Babies would be retired at the end of the year, then reversed itself after asking the public to vote and seeing an overwhelming vote to keep them.", "Most 'rare' Beanies that price guides valued in the thousands now sell for a few dollars."], "a": {"item_id": "5b893c7de1a5", "text": "九十年代有一批美国大人，坚信一只塞满塑料颗粒的小熊能供孩子上大学。怎么投资呢？不让孩子碰。孩子想抱一下，爸妈拦住：别摸，一摸就贬值。这是养儿子还是供祖宗？他们还给心形吊牌单独买塑料保护壳，熊可以脏，牌不能折，那张纸才是资产。抢戴安娜王妃熊抢到打架，却没发现厂商一边喊“限量退休”，一边接着生产。你跟印钞的人比谁囤得多？后来有夫妻离婚，在法庭上分豆豆娃，分完发现整整一车，卖了不够付律师费。现在三百只堆在车库，挂网上五美元一只，没人要。孩子没上成大学，但学到一课：在爸爸心里，我排在一只带吊牌的熊后面。"}, "b": {"item_id": "4ba725269df8", "text": "我有个朋友，九十年代末拿全部积蓄囤豆豆娃，一只只买回来，吊牌小心翼翼套进塑料袋，供得跟菩萨似的。他说这玩意儿一宣布\"退役\"就涨价，所以他就等着它退役。结果厂家一年退役好几批，退役的比在售的还多，一只熊能退役三回。他那一屋子熊，现在二手平台十块钱包邮都没人要。他还在等那只戴安娜王妃纪念款的\"公主熊\"，说它一定能翻身。我说王妃的熊确实翻身了，你的积蓄没了。他说，吊牌还在，吊牌最值钱。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0655", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "vision-pro", "target_name": "Apple Vision Pro", "facts": ["Released in February 2024 at $3,499, with an external battery pack on a cable rated for about two hours.", "'EyeSight' shows a rendering of the wearer's eyes on an outward-facing screen.", "Apple calls it 'spatial computing' and its App Store rules tell developers not to describe apps as AR, VR, XR, or MR.", "Reports during 2024 said Apple had cut production; analysts' first-year sales estimates were well under a million units.", "Weighs 600-650 g, before the battery."], "a": {"item_id": "af7b2649d37f", "text": "地铁上最好认的，就是戴Vision Pro的。三万块顶在脑门上，一根线连着裤兜里的电池，像刚从医院偷跑出来、还没拔输液管的病人。他图什么？图“空间计算”。人家早高峰挤成肉饼，他调出个月球环境。兄弟，你人在月球，屁股在十号线。最绝的是手势：眼睛一盯，两根手指一捏，旁边大姐以为他要掐她，回手就是一巴掌。外面那块屏还给你显示一双假眼睛，冲你深情地眨。下场呢？电池撑两小时，他坐过了站，脖子压出颈椎病。三万块买来的，是一种让全车厢都看得见你在独处的方式。"}, "b": {"item_id": "990fbbeab954", "text": "地铁上戴 Vision Pro 的人，我见过三类。第一类想显摆，结果他脸上扣着一块 3500 美元的玻璃，外屏还亮着他的眼睛，全车人都在和一双发光的眼睛对视，像鱼缸里的金鱼被人盯着看。第二类想沉浸式追剧，他在悬浮的大屏前对着空气捏了五次手指，每次都没对上，像在给一个看不见的人做心肺复苏。第三类最惨，他外接的电池线挂在脖子上，一只手在空中乱点，另一只手拎着电池，他把眼前的 4K 巨幕当成了整个世界，于是一头撞上了真的闸机，而那个闸机，连手势都不用捏。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0656", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "slack", "target_name": "Slack", "facts": ["Spun out in 2013 from the internal chat tool of Tiny Speck's failed game Glitch; founder Stewart Butterfield had earlier spun Flickr out of another failed game.", "The name is a backronym: 'Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge'.", "Salesforce bought it for $27.7 billion in 2021.", "Filed an EU antitrust complaint against Microsoft in 2020 over the bundling of Teams with Office."], "a": {"item_id": "9ef25649a571", "text": "有一种老板，管公司全靠Slack。他图什么？图那种指挥作战的感觉。半夜两点在#general里@channel，三百个人的手机同时“咚”一声，他躺床上听着，跟听礼炮似的。\n\n他不开会，他回“👀”。你熬一礼拜的方案，换来一个眼睛表情，啥意思没人知道，你自己悟。\n\n他考勤看绿点。谁头像一灰，私信立马弹出一个“？”。于是全员网购了鼠标晃动器，公司最勤奋的员工，是十九块九包邮的小马达。\n\n下场呢？所有决策都埋在某条thread的第四十七楼，谁也翻不着。免费版只存九十天，这家公司的战略每三个月自动清空一次。他还挺得意：“看，我们多敏捷。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "62d910dd9864", "text": "有些老板觉得，把公司搬进 Slack 就叫数字化管理。他建了一百二十个频道，#general 底下永远是“早上好”和一排表情回应。他的理想是“全员透明”，结果大家学会了：发一句“收到”，状态设成“开会中”，然后去另一个频道划水。他半夜十一点在 #项目A-最终版-真的最终版 里@channel，回应他的只有三个🫡，还有一个Huddle邀请，是他自己发的。最后他终于明白：员工不是不看消息，是他把公司做成了一个未读红点比工资还多的地方。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0657", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "google-sheets", "target_name": "Google Sheets", "facts": ["Launched in June 2006 as Google Spreadsheets, built on XL2Web, a product Google acquired from 2Web Technologies.", "A spreadsheet is capped at 10 million cells (raised from 5 million in 2022).", "Functions such as IMPORTHTML, IMPORTXML, and GOOGLEFINANCE pull live web data straight into cells.", "Free with a Google account; files live in Google Drive, and collaborators edit the same sheet simultaneously with coloured cursors.", "In a browser, offline editing works only in Chrome or Edge, with the Google Docs Offline extension installed and offline access switched on while still connected; the Android and iOS apps work offline natively."], "a": {"item_id": "c5f3c3c4263e", "text": "用 Google Sheets 管团队的，一般是创业公司那位“运营兼 HR 兼老板小舅子”。图啥？图免费，图“实时协作，公开透明”。透明是真透明：你凌晨两点偷偷改自己的绩效，右上角冒出一只“匿名水豚”，光标就停在你那一行。全公司这个点还不睡的水豚，只有老板一个。\n\n更绝的是筛选。实习生按“已离职”一点，全公司的表同时只剩离职的人，老板以为昨晚裁员了。\n\n最后他用 IMPORTRANGE 把十二张表串成一张，终于实现了自动化：每天早上一打开，满屏 #REF!。他打开版本历史一条条往回翻，翻到最早那条：“由张伟创建”。那时候他还有头发。"}, "b": {"item_id": "62f3a7b081a2", "text": "干这事的，通常是刚学会\"共享\"按钮的 team lead。他图什么？图一眼看穿所有人，图能少开会。于是他建了个表：三十个标签页，一个叫\"总表\"，一个叫\"总表（最终版）\"，还有一个叫\"别用这个\"。\n\n每人一行，进度列设下拉框，红黄绿条件格式，颜色比过年还热闹。第一周很美。第二周，实习生在第三列手敲了个\"差不多了\"，下拉框直接失效，公式全报错，满屏井字号。第三周，他在批注里@全组，回复只有一句：\"我改的是哪一版？\"\n\n最后他关掉表格，开了个会。会上他说：\"我们需要一个更好的协作工具。\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0658", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "stinky-tofu", "target_name": "臭豆腐", "facts": ["Tofu soaked for hours to a day or two in a brine that has itself fermented for months from vegetables such as bamboo shoots and mustard greens, often with dried shrimp or meat; the smell is the brine's, not the bean's.", "Changsha's version is black, deep-fried and doused in chili; Taiwan's night-market version is golden and served with pickled cabbage; Shaoxing's, brined with amaranth stalks, is fried pale yellow or steamed with other 'stinky' pickles.", "Legend credits Wang Zhihe, a Qing-era exam candidate who left his unsold tofu too long in 1669; a Beijing fermented-bean-curd brand still carries his name.", "Street vendors are its main outlet, and the smell carries for a block."], "a": {"item_id": "5723ee516250", "text": "街边吃臭豆腐的人，我见过三种。第一种是下班的社畜，西装还没脱，站在摊子前左顾右盼，像在做贼。他图什么？图那口“香”。结果油锅一开，汤汁溅到白衬衫上，那味道从领口直接往上飘。地铁上三米之内全体让座，不是尊重他，是怕被熏晕。第二种是情侣。女生说“就尝一口”，男生陪着吃，两个人嘴里都是霉豆腐和辣酱的味儿，接吻的念头当场熄火。第三种最惨，是我室友。他一边吃一边说“这味儿才地道”，回宿舍后，全层楼的人都知道他吃了什么。他洗了三遍澡，衣服挂阳台上晾了一礼拜，他妈视频的时候还问他：你是不是去化工厂上班了？"}, "b": {"item_id": "0db2c726fd64", "text": "街边吃臭豆腐的人，我观察过，全是传教士。你路过，他嘴里正烫得直哈气，还要腾出一只手拦你：“闻着臭，吃着香！”大哥，这句话我从小听到大，你说得跟刚悟出来的一样。\n\n他图什么？图的就是那个反差。全世界都捂鼻子，就他看透了本质。一块发酵的豆腐，硬是吃出了顿悟的表情。\n\n然后呢？然后他上地铁了。五站路，整节车厢都在低头检查自己的鞋。他还挺得意：凡人，不懂。\n\n最惨的是那根牙签。一碗六块，就配一根，戳到最后一块，辣汤顺着手腕流进袖口。第二天开会他一抬手，领导皱眉：“小王，你家下水道是不是堵了？”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0659", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "papyrus", "target_name": "Papyrus 字体", "facts": ["Hand-drawn by Chris Costello in 1982 over about six months, when he was 23; he sold the rights to Letraset for $750.", "Bundled with macOS and with Microsoft Office, which is why it appears on so many menus and signs.", "James Cameron's Avatar (2009) used it for the film's logo and subtitles.", "A 2017 Saturday Night Live sketch starring Ryan Gosling is about a man unable to get over that choice."], "a": {"item_id": "d6bcba0a659e", "text": "开养生馆的老板选招牌字体，预算四百，又想显得有五千年底蕴，于是选了Papyrus，就是那个边儿像被猫挠过、笔画细得像没吃饱的字。他的逻辑是：这字一看就古埃及，古埃及一看就神秘，神秘就能卖六百八的艾灸套餐。我说艾灸是中国的，跟尼罗河有什么关系？他说：“反正是古的。”\n\n结果隔壁新开了家埃及烤肉店，也用Papyrus。两块招牌并排一挂，顾客全懵了：有人进养生馆问有没有鹰嘴豆泥，有人进烤肉店问能不能刮痧。设计师路过也不进门，就站门口拍照发朋友圈，配三个字：“又一个。”\n\n老板花四百块，买了个跟《阿凡达》同款的“独一无二”。"}, "b": {"item_id": "eb2c4af932ef", "text": "你们发现没有，开养生馆的老板，都觉得自己特有灵性。他要什么？“古朴、自然、带点异域。”然后他打开Word，字体往下拉，拉到P，眼睛一亮：Papyrus！边缘毛毛糙糙，像拿芦苇杆蘸墨写在莎草纸上。老板一拍大腿：就它，古埃及的灵性！\n\n大哥，你卖的是艾灸，不是木乃伊。\n\n隔壁泰国菜也这么想，楼上墨西哥卷饼也这么想。一条街的“异域风情”，全是电脑自带的同一款免费字体。\n\n最惨的是，卡梅隆花几个亿拍《阿凡达》，片名用的也是它。所以你推门进去，技师一抬头，你第一反应不是放松，是想问：“蓝色那位在几号房？”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0660", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "latex", "target_name": "LaTeX", "facts": ["Leslie Lamport wrote LaTeX in the early 1980s and released it in 1984 as a macro layer on Donald Knuth's TeX (1978), which Knuth built because he disliked how the galley proofs of the new edition of his own book looked.", "TeX's version number converges on π (3.141592653 since 2021), and Knuth's TeX bug bounty started at one hexadecimal dollar, $2.56, doubling yearly until frozen at $327.68; he stopped mailing real cheques in 2008 after fraud and now credits accounts at his fictional Bank of San Serriffe, and only 9 of the 275 cheques he sent from 2006 on were ever cashed.", "Its best-known warning is 'Underfull \\hbox (badness 10000)'; overfull lines are reported as 'Overfull \\hbox (Npt too wide)', and figures float to wherever the algorithm decides, not where the author put them.", "A single missing brace can produce an error reported pages away from the mistake, and a document can need three compiler passes to get its references right."], "a": {"item_id": "8f4ce41c713b", "text": "我见过一个人，写两页的部门备忘录，非要用LaTeX。不是不会Word，是觉得Word太俗，他图的是看上去像篇论文。他开Overleaf，写\\documentclass{article}，再\\usepackage{geometry}调页边距，又加个xeCJK调中文，第一次编译就报错，字体找不到，他查了一下午宏包版本。备忘录正文就一句话：周五下午三点开会，请带Q3数据。最后同事收到的PDF标题居中，页眉印着Memo，行距像拿尺子量过。老板回了一句：这个文件我改不了，能发个Word吗？他沉默了一会儿，删掉\\maketitle，把正文复制进了Word。"}, "b": {"item_id": "34dc34fd3a74", "text": "我特别佩服那种两页备忘录也要用LaTeX的人。通知内容就一句\"周五聚餐AA\"，他非得 \\documentclass 起手。图什么？图一个\"我不是Word那种凡人\"。你收到PDF，Computer Modern字体，一股刚证完黎曼猜想的气息，点开一看，火锅。\n\n下场呢？写十分钟，编译两小时。一个 Undefined control sequence，他在论坛翻到半夜，装了四个G的TeX Live，风扇转得像要起飞。好不容易编出来，满屏 Overfull \\hbox 警告，他一个都不肯放过。为了消掉一行0.3pt的溢出，他把\"聚餐\"改成了\"聚会\"。\n\n最后发出去，同事回：\"手机上看不清，你发文字行吗？\"\n\n他想了想，把PDF截了个图发过去。\n\n折腾一晚上，排版界的天花板，最后落成一张JPG。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0661", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "windows-8", "target_name": "Windows 8", "facts": ["Released in October 2012; removed the Start button and booted into a full-screen grid of tiles designed for touch screens most PCs did not have.", "The 8.1 update in 2013 put a Start button back, Windows 10 (2015) restored the Start menu, and Microsoft skipped the name Windows 9 altogether.", "Microsoft dropped the 'Metro' name in August 2012 after reports that German retailer Metro AG had objected on trademark grounds; Microsoft called 'Metro' a code name and denied any litigation, and the style was afterwards known informally as 'Modern UI'.", "The 'Charms bar' hid Search, Share, and Settings behind an invisible swipe from the right edge.", "Windows RT, the ARM edition launched alongside it, could not run ordinary Windows programs; Microsoft wrote down $900 million of unsold Surface RT tablets in 2013."], "a": {"item_id": "a62b05dc7bff", "text": "给台式机装Windows 8的人，我特别好奇你图什么。图新鲜。结果开机，满屏彩色方块，我就想打开个文件夹，它给我弹出个\"体育\"。\n\n关机更绝。台式机，没有开始按钮，你得把鼠标甩到屏幕右下角，等侧边栏像幽灵一样滑出来，点设置，再点电源。我第一次关机找了十分钟，我怀疑这是微软的智力测试。\n\n而且这帮人的显示器根本不是触屏，可Windows 8整套设计就是让你拿手指划。所以你会看到一个成年人，坐在台式机前，手指悬在半空，犹豫要不要戳。\n\n最后下场都一样：第二天下载Classic Shell，把开始菜单请回来。花钱升级，就为了把它打扮成Windows 7。这不叫升级，这叫花钱请个演员，去演你的前任。"}, "b": {"item_id": "09a19029d52d", "text": "有一种人，专门给台式机装 Windows 8。你问他图啥？他说，触屏才是未来，我得提前用上。问题是他这台机器是机箱加显示器加一个鼠标，屏幕根本摸不到。开机后满屏都是色块磁贴，像走进一家超市，货架上全是天气和新闻，唯独没有他要的开始菜单。他想关机，找了二十分钟，最后在屏幕右边缘一划，弹出一个叫\"超级按钮\"的东西。我很怀疑取这名字的人从来没关过机。最后他花九十块买了个第三方软件，把开始按钮请了回来。他现在逢人就说：\"我用的是 Windows 8，但我把它改回了 Windows 7。\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0662", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "google-glass", "target_name": "谷歌眼镜（Google Glass）", "facts": ["Unveiled at Google I/O 2012 with skydivers livestreaming their jump through it onto the Moscone Center roof.", "The Explorer Edition sold to invited users in 2013 for $1,500.", "Wearers were nicknamed 'Glassholes'; bars, cinemas, and casinos banned the device over its camera.", "The consumer programme ended in January 2015; an Enterprise Edition continued until Google stopped selling it in 2023.", "Its display was a 640-by-360 prism in the corner of one eye."], "a": {"item_id": "90035f33aea2", "text": "这批戴谷歌眼镜进酒吧的人，我是真佩服。一千五百美金，就为了让全场一眼看出你在录像。酒吧是什么地方？是大家喝多了说胡话的地方，你坐那儿，镜片上还亮着个小灯，全场立刻开始演《黑镜》。他们自称\"探索者\"，人家给他们起的外号叫\"Glasshole\"，直译过来就是\"眼镜混蛋\"。后来好多酒吧门口直接一句\"摘了再进\"，比谷歌自己撤产品还干脆。我就想问一句：你图什么？图对着酒杯喊\"OK Glass\"？那你是成功了，全场都在看你，只不过看的是一个对着空气打嗝的人。"}, "b": {"item_id": "3f70392c889f", "text": "戴谷歌眼镜进酒吧的人，我真想采访一下：你图什么？花一千五百美元，抢到一个\"探索者\"资格。注意，是资格，你得先被允许掏钱。然后戴着它进酒吧，就等人问一句：\"哥们，这是啥？\"\n\n结果没人问。大家只看你右眼上方那块小玻璃，心想：他是不是在录我？\n\n你想搭讪，张嘴就是\"OK Glass，拍照\"，姑娘以为你在对她说\"OK\"，转身走了。你想看个时间，还得仰头斜眼，盯着右上角，活像在找天花板上的WiFi。\n\n最后酒保说：\"把那个摘了。\"你说：\"这是未来。\"酒保说：\"未来请在门口等。\"\n\n你被请出去，站在马路边，对着空气喊\"OK Glass，叫车\"。它回你一句：没听清。\n\n一千五百美元，买了个让全酒吧都把你当偷拍狂的右眼显示器。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0663", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "excel", "target_name": "Microsoft Excel", "facts": ["First shipped for the Macintosh in 1985; the Windows version followed in 1987.", "Silently converts gene names such as SEPT2 and MARCH1 into dates; by August 2020 the HUGO Gene Nomenclature Committee had renamed 27 human genes (MARCH1 became MARCHF1, SEPT2 became SEPTIN2) and published guidelines in Nature Genetics to stop it.", "In October 2020, Public Health England lost 15,841 COVID-19 cases when a legacy .xls file hit its 65,536-row limit.", "Reinhart and Rogoff's 2010 paper 'Growth in a Time of Debt', cited for austerity policies, omitted five countries through a spreadsheet range error found by a graduate student in 2013.", "Sheets have been capped at 1,048,576 rows by 16,384 columns since Excel 2007."], "a": {"item_id": "3643597461dd", "text": "我们老板拿 Excel 管整个公司，他说这叫“轻资产”。翻译一下：舍不得买系统，又想当系统。\n\n全公司的命，就一个文件：《经营总表_最终版_改3_老板别动.xlsx》。财务看一眼，三十七个工作表，底下标签一路滚到右边，像在翻家谱。\n\n老板最骄傲的是他会 VLOOKUP。会一个 VLOOKUP，就觉得自己能当 CTO。结果有天实习生插了一列，全公司工资变成 #N/A。员工问我工资呢？我说你现在的身份是“找不到”。\n\n最惨的是人事，把员工身份证号一粘，全变成 4.40E+17。整个公司四百人，在 Excel 里是同一个人。\n\n公司倒闭那天，老板还在说：“别慌，Ctrl+Z。”\n\n兄弟，公司又不是单元格，撤销不了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "bee6f959fe81", "text": "有种老板，用 Excel 管整个公司。图什么？图省钱，还觉得自己特别“数据驱动”。结果公司里权力最大的不是他，是“表哥”，全公司唯一看得懂那张表的人。表里 VLOOKUP 套了七层，没人敢动，谁动谁 #REF!。发工资那天，财务像拆炸弹。员工身份证号被自动变成 1.23E+17，十八位全军覆没。想打开文件，弹窗说：“已被张三锁定”。张三在三亚休年假。最后表哥一跳槽，老板才明白：自己不是在管公司，是在给一个人打工。而那个人，把工作表保护密码带走了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0664", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "microsoft-word", "target_name": "Microsoft Word", "facts": ["Launched in 1983 as 'Multi-Tool Word' for Xenix and MS-DOS.", "Clippy, the animated paperclip assistant, arrived in Office 97 and was removed in Office 2007; Microsoft itself ran ads mocking it in 2001.", "Its .docx format, Office Open XML, was fast-tracked to ISO standard 29500 in 2008 as a roughly 6,000-page specification, amid accusations that national committees had been packed.", "Older spell checkers, Word's included, offered 'Cupertino' as a correction for a mistyped 'cooperation', and the resulting 'Cupertino effect' made it into published NATO, EU and UN-agency documents."], "a": {"item_id": "b80a1ea0ef97", "text": "用Word排毕业论文的人，我研究过，都是同一种人：觉得“不就是打字嘛”。他们图什么？图省两个小时，不想学“样式”和“分节符”。于是标题全靠手动：选中、加粗、三号、居中，四下。全文六十个标题，两百四十下。目录也是手打的，页码也是手填的，填得整整齐齐，看着特别像那么回事。\n\n然后导师说：第二章和第三章换个顺序。\n\n他只删了一个回车，第五十页那张图就像被召唤了一样，飞到了第一百一十二页，还顺手带走了摘要的罗马数字页码。\n\n他为了省两个小时，花了两百个小时。最后在打印店，用人家的电脑一打开，全篇错位，他蹲在那儿一页一页重新排。老板路过看了一眼：“同学，你这是论文还是拼图？”"}, "b": {"item_id": "b60d316d9566", "text": "有些同学非要用Word排毕业论文，图什么？图省事。学校要求\"标题三号黑体、正文小四宋体\"，他觉得点几下就能搞定。结果一章标题用了普通段落样式，目录里直接没有它；页眉一改，前面十页跟着变，\"链接到前一节\"这五个字他看了一整晚。最后目录页码差了三页，他按F9更新域，Word回他一句\"错误！未定义书签\"。你不是在写论文，你是在跟一个分节符谈恋爱，谈崩了它也不告诉你为什么。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0665", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "oat-milk", "target_name": "燕麦奶", "facts": ["Oatly started in Sweden in the 1990s, built on Lund University research by Rickard Öste on lactose intolerance.", "Oatly's slogan 'It's like milk but made for humans' was banned in Sweden in 2015 after the dairy lobby LRF Mjölk sued; Oatly then published the lawsuit as advertising.", "Oatly listed on Nasdaq in May 2021 at a valuation near $10 billion; the shares later lost more than 90% of their value.", "Commercial oat milk is made by breaking oat starch into maltose with amylase enzymes, and many brands, Oatly among them, add rapeseed oil; the result has more sugar and a higher glycaemic index than almond or soy milk."], "a": {"item_id": "e299ef76bfd2", "text": "有些人点拿铁，非要补一句：换燕麦奶。行，你换。店员一边敲单一边抬头看你，加五块。你其实不知道为什么换，你只知道办公室那个健身的同事换了。端上来喝一口，嗯，甜的，还有一股麦片泡在水里的味道。你心里一紧，觉得自己是不是喝出了健康。拍照的时候，奶泡塌得比你的表情还快。那半杯你放在工位上，等它自己慢慢变成一碗麦片粥。最后你还是回去点普通牛奶，还得跟同事解释：我不是不喜欢，我就是想试试。"}, "b": {"item_id": "f5f4ee009b3f", "text": "每个咖啡店都有这么一位，开口就是：\"拿铁，换燕麦奶。\"语气像在宣布退出某个组织。你问为什么，他说乳糖不耐。兄弟，你手边那块芝士蛋糕是自己长出来的吗？你这乳糖不耐很精准，只对咖啡里的牛奶不耐。\n\n再问，他说为了健康。燕麦奶，燕麦煮开后淀粉变成糖，热量比牛奶还高。所以他多花五块钱，多摄入一点糖，喝的是一杯稀释过的麦片粥汤，然后发朋友圈：\"今天也是植物基的一天。\"\n\n下场呢？咖啡一入口，满嘴湿麦片味，像早饭的碗没洗干净，被人顺手拿来接了咖啡。他还硬喝完，边喝边说：\"还挺顺滑的。\"\n\n对，是顺滑，因为你正在喝粥。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0666", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "nuclear-fusion", "target_name": "可控核聚变", "facts": ["Has been 'about 30 years away' since the 1950s; at the 1955 Geneva atoms-for-peace conference, Homi Bhabha predicted fusion power within two decades.", "ITER, agreed in 2006 for first plasma in 2016, re-baselined in 2024 to start research operations in 2034 and deuterium-tritium burning in 2039, at a cost estimated above €20 billion.", "NIF's December 2022 'ignition' shot released 3.15 MJ from 2.05 MJ of laser energy; the lasers drew about 300 MJ from the grid to fire.", "Private fusion companies had raised more than $7 billion by 2024."], "a": {"item_id": "8315ced7f71d", "text": "可控核聚变还有十年，这话我爸上大学时就听过，是他老师说的。现在我听，说的人是他老师的学生。这行最稳定的不是等离子体，是那句“还有十年”，代代相传，比托卡马克里那团火命长多了。\n\n干这行的人图什么？你想啊，一个人一辈子的成果，是把一团一亿度的气体多关住了三十秒。开发布会，“创世界纪录！”台下鼓掌。他自己心里清楚，下个月还得写报告申请经费，因为磁约束线圈又烧了。\n\n最惨的是退休那天。学生问他：“老师，您干了四十年，离点火成功还有多远？”他想了想，特别诚实：“还有十年。”\n\n他入行那年也是这么说的。四十年过去，一天都没少。这才叫能量守恒。"}, "b": {"item_id": "a3d17d6a7412", "text": "搞可控核聚变的人，是地球上唯一靠“还有十年”吃了一辈子饭的职业。五十年代说三十年，后来说五十年，现在说十年，你看，人家还在降价。\n\n图啥？图经费。“十年”这个数太精妙了：近到评审愿意批钱，远到项目负责人退休前不用验收。ITER在法国盖了快二十年，预算翻了好几倍。一亿度的等离子体他们说能控住，控不住的是工期。\n\n最后什么下场？老教授退休那天，把“还有十年”郑重交给博士生，跟传家宝似的。博士生一看：我二十五岁入行，原来是接了个祖传手艺。\n\n这炉子烧的不是氘和氚，是一代代人的头发。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0667", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "keurig", "target_name": "Keurig 胶囊咖啡机", "facts": ["John Sylvan invented the K-Cup in the early 1990s and sold his stake for about $50,000 in 1997; in 2015 he told The Atlantic, 'I feel bad sometimes that I ever did it.'", "Keurig 2.0 (2014) read an ink mark on the lid and rejected unlicensed pods; sales fell and workarounds spread online.", "By 2014 enough K-Cups were sold each year to circle the Earth more than ten times; the pods were mostly unrecyclable #7 plastic until a switch to polypropylene by 2020.", "Keurig Green Mountain merged with Dr Pepper Snapple in 2018 to form Keurig Dr Pepper."], "a": {"item_id": "45e5b40c4a0a", "text": "我同事天天用Keurig泡咖啡，理由是三十秒搞定。行，省事，不用磨豆，不用洗壶，按一下嗡地一声，像要起飞。结果出来一杯八盎司的热水，颜色像他的周报。每天撕一个锡箔盖、扔一个塑料杯，一个月下来，垃圾桶里的小杯子能堆成金字塔。上个月机器亮起\"请除垢\"，他查说明书，得买除垢剂，还得拿醋灌一遍。算下来，他的胶囊钱够买一台手冲壶和两袋豆子。最后他还是下楼买了杯星巴克，店员问要什么，他说：\"要一杯，有味儿的。\""}, "b": {"item_id": "f6e06d9fe87e", "text": "用 Keurig 胶囊的人，我太懂了：嫌磨豆子麻烦，嫌滤纸麻烦，嫌洗壶麻烦，一心要“按一下就有”。\n\n结果呢？一颗胶囊一块多，折合一磅咖啡粉五六十块，这哪是喝咖啡，这是喝首饰。喝的还都是什么？榛果奶油、南瓜派、焦糖玛奇朵，咖啡味的香水。\n\n更绝的是，为了省两分钟磨豆子，每天花三分钟撕锡纸、倒渣、把那个小塑料杯分类回收。一年三百六十五个小杯子，像在给咖啡办葬礼。\n\n最后机器亮灯：“请除垢。”翻开说明书，让你拿白醋泡机器。图省事的人，最后蹲在厨房里，给一台咖啡机泡醋。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0668", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "latex", "target_name": "LaTeX", "facts": ["Leslie Lamport wrote LaTeX in the early 1980s and released it in 1984 as a macro layer on Donald Knuth's TeX (1978), which Knuth built because he disliked how the galley proofs of the new edition of his own book looked.", "TeX's version number converges on π (3.141592653 since 2021), and Knuth's TeX bug bounty started at one hexadecimal dollar, $2.56, doubling yearly until frozen at $327.68; he stopped mailing real cheques in 2008 after fraud and now credits accounts at his fictional Bank of San Serriffe, and only 9 of the 275 cheques he sent from 2006 on were ever cashed.", "Its best-known warning is 'Underfull \\hbox (badness 10000)'; overfull lines are reported as 'Overfull \\hbox (Npt too wide)', and figures float to wherever the algorithm decides, not where the author put them.", "A single missing brace can produce an error reported pages away from the mistake, and a document can need three compiler passes to get its references right."], "a": {"item_id": "6b980d56eb4f", "text": "有人写两页备忘录，非要用 LaTeX 排。问他图什么？图那个 \\maketitle 出来的标题，图页边距精确到两厘米。结果开会前一小时，他对着 Overleaf 发呆：“! Missing $ inserted”，原因是他在“报价_终版”的下划线那儿忘了进数学模式。好不容易编译通过，领导看了一眼说：能不能发个 Word 版？他只好把 tabular 一行行拆回表格。这份备忘录全公司只有他一个人会改，而他本人，现在每次开会都得先说一句：源码我发你了，你别点开。"}, "b": {"item_id": "34dc34fd3a74", "text": "我特别佩服那种两页备忘录也要用LaTeX的人。通知内容就一句\"周五聚餐AA\"，他非得 \\documentclass 起手。图什么？图一个\"我不是Word那种凡人\"。你收到PDF，Computer Modern字体，一股刚证完黎曼猜想的气息，点开一看，火锅。\n\n下场呢？写十分钟，编译两小时。一个 Undefined control sequence，他在论坛翻到半夜，装了四个G的TeX Live，风扇转得像要起飞。好不容易编出来，满屏 Overfull \\hbox 警告，他一个都不肯放过。为了消掉一行0.3pt的溢出，他把\"聚餐\"改成了\"聚会\"。\n\n最后发出去，同事回：\"手机上看不清，你发文字行吗？\"\n\n他想了想，把PDF截了个图发过去。\n\n折腾一晚上，排版界的天花板，最后落成一张JPG。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0669", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hsq", "target_name": "HSQ 电子束光刻胶", "facts": ["Hydrogen silsesquioxane, sold by Dow Corning as FOx ('flowable oxide') and designed as a spin-on dielectric for chip interconnects; lithographers adopted it as a negative-tone resist.", "Exposure converts its cage structure into a cross-linked, silica-like network, so the developed pattern is essentially SiO2.", "Holds the resolution records for electron-beam lithography: isolated features around 5 nm, dense lines below 10 nm half-pitch, with high-contrast 'salty' NaOH/NaCl development.", "Must be kept refrigerated (Dow Corning's datasheet gives a six-month shelf life at 5 °C); the solution ages into a jelly, and its effective sensitivity shifts with processing delays because cross-linking continues after exposure.", "DuPont, which inherited Dow Corning's FOx/XR-1541 HSQ line, discontinued it and ended production at the end of 2025; labs such as EPFL's CMi switched to third-party HSQ from EM Resist or DisChem, both formulated from AQM powder."], "a": {"item_id": "650f7a5cc2ec", "text": "有个师兄为了分辨率，非要换HSQ。我问他图什么，他说十纳米以下的线条好看。好看是好看，可这玩意儿得冷藏，跟酸奶似的，放久了自己变稠，成了凝胶。旋涂的时候像往芯片上刷芝麻糊。显影还得用碱液，线条是立住了，一吹氮气，全倒成一排麦子。最惨的是曝光完它会变成二氧化硅，刻不掉，永久留在芯片上。他的论文发了，芯片留给了下一届，下一届一打开显微镜，满屏都是他的遗产。"}, "b": {"item_id": "e6a9d412465a", "text": "有人为了分辨率，把 PMMA 换成 HSQ。我就想问，你图啥？PMMA 便宜、好剥离、导师不骂你。HSQ 呢？得放冰箱，一开瓶就倒计时，跟生鲜外卖一个待遇。剂量要几千，写一小块图形，电子束扫一整夜，你在机房陪着，比陪对象都久。早上显影，一根 8 纳米的线，倒了，跟隔壁那根粘一块了。再来一轮，终于立住了，你想把多余的去掉？不好意思，曝光完它就是二氧化硅，想洗掉得上氢氟酸，连你的器件一块洗。最后论文里写“sub-10 nm”，那是三十张电镜图里挑出来的那一张，剩下二十九张，叫 preliminary data。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0670", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hsq", "target_name": "HSQ 电子束光刻胶", "facts": ["Hydrogen silsesquioxane, sold by Dow Corning as FOx ('flowable oxide') and designed as a spin-on dielectric for chip interconnects; lithographers adopted it as a negative-tone resist.", "Exposure converts its cage structure into a cross-linked, silica-like network, so the developed pattern is essentially SiO2.", "Holds the resolution records for electron-beam lithography: isolated features around 5 nm, dense lines below 10 nm half-pitch, with high-contrast 'salty' NaOH/NaCl development.", "Must be kept refrigerated (Dow Corning's datasheet gives a six-month shelf life at 5 °C); the solution ages into a jelly, and its effective sensitivity shifts with processing delays because cross-linking continues after exposure.", "DuPont, which inherited Dow Corning's FOx/XR-1541 HSQ line, discontinued it and ended production at the end of 2025; labs such as EPFL's CMi switched to third-party HSQ from EM Resist or DisChem, both formulated from AQM powder."], "a": {"item_id": "e6a9d412465a", "text": "有人为了分辨率，把 PMMA 换成 HSQ。我就想问，你图啥？PMMA 便宜、好剥离、导师不骂你。HSQ 呢？得放冰箱，一开瓶就倒计时，跟生鲜外卖一个待遇。剂量要几千，写一小块图形，电子束扫一整夜，你在机房陪着，比陪对象都久。早上显影，一根 8 纳米的线，倒了，跟隔壁那根粘一块了。再来一轮，终于立住了，你想把多余的去掉？不好意思，曝光完它就是二氧化硅，想洗掉得上氢氟酸，连你的器件一块洗。最后论文里写“sub-10 nm”，那是三十张电镜图里挑出来的那一张，剩下二十九张，叫 preliminary data。"}, "b": {"item_id": "a38389497436", "text": "有些人为了分辨率，非要换HSQ。他们是谁？一群研究生，导师一句“线宽得压到十纳米以下”，就抱着一瓶胶去排电子束曝光机。这胶得放冰箱，过期会结胶，开了盖还得赶紧用。图的就是那几根细线，结果线是细了，一显影，线条齐刷刷倒向一边，像一排喝多了的人。更绝的是，曝光过的地方会固化成二氧化硅，等于他们在芯片上刻了一层玻璃，洗都洗不掉。毕业那天他们终于明白：分辨率没提上去，芯片上倒多了一块摘不下来的玻璃。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0671", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "google-glass", "target_name": "谷歌眼镜（Google Glass）", "facts": ["Unveiled at Google I/O 2012 with skydivers livestreaming their jump through it onto the Moscone Center roof.", "The Explorer Edition sold to invited users in 2013 for $1,500.", "Wearers were nicknamed 'Glassholes'; bars, cinemas, and casinos banned the device over its camera.", "The consumer programme ended in January 2015; an Enterprise Edition continued until Google stopped selling it in 2023.", "Its display was a 640-by-360 prism in the corner of one eye."], "a": {"item_id": "90035f33aea2", "text": "这批戴谷歌眼镜进酒吧的人，我是真佩服。一千五百美金，就为了让全场一眼看出你在录像。酒吧是什么地方？是大家喝多了说胡话的地方，你坐那儿，镜片上还亮着个小灯，全场立刻开始演《黑镜》。他们自称\"探索者\"，人家给他们起的外号叫\"Glasshole\"，直译过来就是\"眼镜混蛋\"。后来好多酒吧门口直接一句\"摘了再进\"，比谷歌自己撤产品还干脆。我就想问一句：你图什么？图对着酒杯喊\"OK Glass\"？那你是成功了，全场都在看你，只不过看的是一个对着空气打嗝的人。"}, "b": {"item_id": "cea2da3417f8", "text": "你见过戴谷歌眼镜进酒吧的人吗？花一千五百美金，买了个“探索者”头衔，结果探索的是保安的耐心。他坐你对面，右眼上那块小棱镜一闪一闪，你根本分不清他是在看你，还是在拍你。你问他干嘛呢，他说：“查一下这杯酒的评分。”兄弟，你喝的是扎啤，评分就一个字：凉。最惨的是搭讪，他冲着美女深情地喊一声“OK Glass”，美女还以为在叫她，回头一看，他在让眼镜拍照。然后他就被请出去了。英语里专门给这种人造了个词，叫Glasshole。一千五百美金，买了个全世界都会念的外号。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0672", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "risk", "target_name": "《战国风云》（Risk）", "facts": ["Invented in 1957 by Albert Lamorisse, the French director of The Red Balloon, as La Conquête du Monde; Parker Brothers published it in the United States in 1959.", "The map has 42 territories, among them Kamchatka, Irkutsk, and Yakutsk, while the entire Middle East is one territory.", "Holding Australia, which has a single entry point, is the standard opening strategy.", "A full game can run for hours, and players eliminated early have nothing to do but watch."], "a": {"item_id": "5c5c0ab82bd3", "text": "你们见过真把一局《战国风云》玩到底的人吗？我见过，他到现在还单身。开局第一句话：“澳洲归我。”然后在暹罗堆了四十个兵，四个小时一动不动，跟守小区大门的保安一样。问他图什么，他说：“我在等卡。”凑齐三张一换，眼睛都亮了，好像换来的不是一把塑料小兵，是房本。凌晨三点，他终于从堪察加打进阿拉斯加，三颗骰子全是六，对面两个一。他站起来大喊：“世界是我的了！”没人理他。桌上就剩他一个，其他人早把国家互相送光，提前出局，打车回家了。他拿下了全世界，代价是再也没人接他电话。"}, "b": {"item_id": "8083e937dc92", "text": "玩《战国风云》非要玩到底的人，都是觉得世界和平太无聊，想亲手重来一遍的。开局大家都说：“咱们友好点，随便玩玩。”四十分钟后，你的盟友从堪察加半岛给了你一刀。\n\n你还迷信澳洲：就一个入口，每回合白送两个兵，守住就能赢。六个小时后你真守住了，全世界就剩你缩在澳洲，对面三个骰子一扔，你的兵像袋鼠一样被撞飞。\n\n赢的人在欢呼，输的人在找外套，还有一个人早就退群了。整局唯一的和平提议，是凌晨两点那句“要不平局吧”。没人同意，因为每个人都觉得，再扔两把，自己就翻盘了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0673", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "stinky-tofu", "target_name": "臭豆腐", "facts": ["Tofu soaked for hours to a day or two in a brine that has itself fermented for months from vegetables such as bamboo shoots and mustard greens, often with dried shrimp or meat; the smell is the brine's, not the bean's.", "Changsha's version is black, deep-fried and doused in chili; Taiwan's night-market version is golden and served with pickled cabbage; Shaoxing's, brined with amaranth stalks, is fried pale yellow or steamed with other 'stinky' pickles.", "Legend credits Wang Zhihe, a Qing-era exam candidate who left his unsold tofu too long in 1669; a Beijing fermented-bean-curd brand still carries his name.", "Street vendors are its main outlet, and the smell carries for a block."], "a": {"item_id": "416c9c06dab4", "text": "吃街边臭豆腐的人，都有一句口头禅：\"闻着臭，吃着香。\"这哪是说豆腐，这是他们的人生总结，意思是：我知道我有问题，但你们别管。\n\n他们排四十分钟队，站在油锅旁边，边熏边等，像报了个自费烟熏疗程。拿到手一碗黑乎乎的东西，插着一根牙签，还得再淋勺辣酱，撒把香菜，仪式感拉满，吃的时候还不能坐，必须站在马路边，对着车尾气，吃得热泪盈眶。\n\n吃完以为结束了？它跟你回家了。头发入味，外套入味，围巾直接入股。进电梯，全楼的人齐刷刷看你，你还得假装是旁边那位的味。\n\n最惨的是，你自己闻不到了，因为你已经被腌透了。\n\n你就是那块臭豆腐，区别是，没人愿意为你排四十分钟的队。"}, "b": {"item_id": "5723ee516250", "text": "街边吃臭豆腐的人，我见过三种。第一种是下班的社畜，西装还没脱，站在摊子前左顾右盼，像在做贼。他图什么？图那口“香”。结果油锅一开，汤汁溅到白衬衫上，那味道从领口直接往上飘。地铁上三米之内全体让座，不是尊重他，是怕被熏晕。第二种是情侣。女生说“就尝一口”，男生陪着吃，两个人嘴里都是霉豆腐和辣酱的味儿，接吻的念头当场熄火。第三种最惨，是我室友。他一边吃一边说“这味儿才地道”，回宿舍后，全层楼的人都知道他吃了什么。他洗了三遍澡，衣服挂阳台上晾了一礼拜，他妈视频的时候还问他：你是不是去化工厂上班了？"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0674", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hsq", "target_name": "HSQ 电子束光刻胶", "facts": ["Hydrogen silsesquioxane, sold by Dow Corning as FOx ('flowable oxide') and designed as a spin-on dielectric for chip interconnects; lithographers adopted it as a negative-tone resist.", "Exposure converts its cage structure into a cross-linked, silica-like network, so the developed pattern is essentially SiO2.", "Holds the resolution records for electron-beam lithography: isolated features around 5 nm, dense lines below 10 nm half-pitch, with high-contrast 'salty' NaOH/NaCl development.", "Must be kept refrigerated (Dow Corning's datasheet gives a six-month shelf life at 5 °C); the solution ages into a jelly, and its effective sensitivity shifts with processing delays because cross-linking continues after exposure.", "DuPont, which inherited Dow Corning's FOx/XR-1541 HSQ line, discontinued it and ended production at the end of 2025; labs such as EPFL's CMi switched to third-party HSQ from EM Resist or DisChem, both formulated from AQM powder."], "a": {"item_id": "650f7a5cc2ec", "text": "有个师兄为了分辨率，非要换HSQ。我问他图什么，他说十纳米以下的线条好看。好看是好看，可这玩意儿得冷藏，跟酸奶似的，放久了自己变稠，成了凝胶。旋涂的时候像往芯片上刷芝麻糊。显影还得用碱液，线条是立住了，一吹氮气，全倒成一排麦子。最惨的是曝光完它会变成二氧化硅，刻不掉，永久留在芯片上。他的论文发了，芯片留给了下一届，下一届一打开显微镜，满屏都是他的遗产。"}, "b": {"item_id": "98b1aee74d1a", "text": "实验室里最危险的人，是那种盯着SEM说“PMMA不够细”的博士生。为了论文里一张“8纳米线宽”的图，他换了HSQ。从此他的人生就变了。\n\n先说钱，一小瓶贵得像茅台，还得冷藏，比他本人过得精致。开封了就开始老化，他每天看那瓶胶的眼神，比看他妈都焦虑。\n\n再说剂量，HSQ灵敏度低，曝一个芯片，电子束要写一整夜。别人约会，他守机台。\n\n显影还得加盐，学长说“盐显影对比度高”，他就真去称氯化钠，跟腌咸菜似的。\n\n最绝的是曝完它变成二氧化硅，洗不掉！想返工？上氢氟酸。\n\n最后图出来了，线是真细，倒了一片。导师一看：“挺好，再做一批。”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0675", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "windows-8", "target_name": "Windows 8", "facts": ["Released in October 2012; removed the Start button and booted into a full-screen grid of tiles designed for touch screens most PCs did not have.", "The 8.1 update in 2013 put a Start button back, Windows 10 (2015) restored the Start menu, and Microsoft skipped the name Windows 9 altogether.", "Microsoft dropped the 'Metro' name in August 2012 after reports that German retailer Metro AG had objected on trademark grounds; Microsoft called 'Metro' a code name and denied any litigation, and the style was afterwards known informally as 'Modern UI'.", "The 'Charms bar' hid Search, Share, and Settings behind an invisible swipe from the right edge.", "Windows RT, the ARM edition launched alongside it, could not run ordinary Windows programs; Microsoft wrote down $900 million of unsold Surface RT tablets in 2013."], "a": {"item_id": "a62b05dc7bff", "text": "给台式机装Windows 8的人，我特别好奇你图什么。图新鲜。结果开机，满屏彩色方块，我就想打开个文件夹，它给我弹出个\"体育\"。\n\n关机更绝。台式机，没有开始按钮，你得把鼠标甩到屏幕右下角，等侧边栏像幽灵一样滑出来，点设置，再点电源。我第一次关机找了十分钟，我怀疑这是微软的智力测试。\n\n而且这帮人的显示器根本不是触屏，可Windows 8整套设计就是让你拿手指划。所以你会看到一个成年人，坐在台式机前，手指悬在半空，犹豫要不要戳。\n\n最后下场都一样：第二天下载Classic Shell，把开始菜单请回来。花钱升级，就为了把它打扮成Windows 7。这不叫升级，这叫花钱请个演员，去演你的前任。"}, "b": {"item_id": "f01853167432", "text": "你们这种人我见过：机箱插着显示器，鼠标线拖到桌子底下，就为了装 Windows 8。图什么？图那个开始屏幕，满屏方块，天气、股票、新闻全贴在墙上，像把手机桌面硬塞进显示器。结果呢？鼠标得滑到右上角，等一个叫\"超级按钮\"的东西弹出来，左下角的开始按钮没了。你找了半小时，按下 Win 键，跳出一整面磁贴，\"邮件\"那块还在显示三个月前的垃圾广告。最后你上网搜\"怎么把开始菜单还给我\"，第一条推荐的是收费软件。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0676", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "luosifen", "target_name": "螺蛳粉", "facts": ["A rice-noodle dish from Liuzhou, Guangxi; the smell comes from pickled bamboo shoots (酸笋), not from the river snails, which only flavour the broth.", "The instant version boomed during the 2020 lockdowns; Liuzhou's government reported packaged luosifen sales of over 10 billion yuan that year.", "Added to China's national intangible cultural heritage list in 2021.", "Liuzhou opened a luosifen industry college in 2020 to train workers for the trade."], "a": {"item_id": "beb2c7971e47", "text": "合租房里煮螺蛳粉的，都是同一种人：把“闻着臭吃着香”当人生信条，但只要后半句，前半句全分给室友。\n\n他煮之前还特讲究，关门、开窗、开油烟机，跟拆炸弹似的。然后酸笋包一撕，全白搭。那味儿穿门缝、钻衣柜，第二天你穿着外套去面试，HR问你：“你家住垃圾站旁边？”\n\n他还觉得自己挺克制：“我没加臭，原味。”大哥，原味酸笋就是臭的底线，你跟我讲克制？\n\n最惨的是吃完那一刻。他满足了三分钟，剩下半锅红油汤倒哪？倒马桶，厕所臭三天；倒水池，整栋楼臭一周。\n\n最后合租群里没人骂他，就房东发了一句：“退房时，窗帘按新的赔。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "d1bc8bf17584", "text": "合租房里煮螺蛳粉的人，开口第一句永远是：“我开窗了。”你开窗？那是给酸笋开了条逃生通道，它出去之前，先把每个房间都参观一遍。\n\n他们图什么？图一口“闻着臭，吃着香”。这话是说给室友听的，意思是：你们负责闻，我负责香。\n\n而且螺蛳粉还是分期付款。红油先到，腐竹跟上，最后剪开酸笋包压轴登场，全屋观众不许退场。\n\n下场呢？第二天群里问“谁昨晚煮东西了”，没人认。但每个枕头都认。最后你被指认，不是靠监控，是你进电梯，旁边的人下意识看了一眼你的外套。\n\n你吃了十五分钟，穿了三天。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0677", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "almond-milk", "target_name": "杏仁奶", "facts": ["California grows about 80% of the world's almonds.", "A single almond takes about 1.1 US gallons (roughly 4 litres) of water to grow, a figure Mother Jones published in 2014 that spread during California's drought.", "A 2015 US class action alleged Almond Breeze contained only about 2% almonds; it partly survived a motion to dismiss, was stayed in 2017 pending a separate Blue Diamond labelling settlement, and the plaintiffs then dropped it.", "Pollinating California's almond orchards each February requires trucking in most of the commercial honeybee colonies in the United States.", "Medieval European cooks used almond milk as a Lenten substitute for dairy, centuries before the current fashion."], "a": {"item_id": "096100e8c869", "text": "我朋友为了环保戒了牛奶，改喝杏仁奶。他说牛放屁排甲烷，地球受不了。行，有道理。然后我问他杏仁哪来的，他说加州。加州啊，那地方干得裂开，种一颗杏仁要喝掉好几升水，他一杯杏仁奶下去，等于替加州的杏树喝了半缸。更绝的是，加州的杏树开花得靠蜜蜂，蜂箱是用大卡车拉过去的，蜜蜂一路颠过去，授完粉累死一片。杏仁奶泡咖啡，三分钟就分层，像他的环保决心，看着坚定，一放就散。他现在每天喝两杯，花的钱是牛奶的三倍，说这是为地球付的。我说你付的是加州的水费，地球一分没收到。"}, "b": {"item_id": "7d9582e4c68c", "text": "我有个同事，为了救地球，牛奶戒了，改喝杏仁奶。天天端着杯子说：“我在减少碳排放。”\n\n挺好。就是我查了一下：一颗杏仁，得喝掉好几升水。加州旱成那样，杏仁树还在那儿咕嘟咕嘟地喝。\n\n更绝的是他那盒杏仁奶，配料表上杏仁只占百分之二，剩下全是水。\n\n也就是说，他花三十块买了一盒“泡过杏仁的水”，那几颗杏仁还先喝光了一个村的水。他不是在救地球，他是在帮加州倒腾水。\n\n哦对，杏仁开花全靠租来的蜜蜂授粉，每年一季下来，蜜蜂成片成片地死。\n\n他现在特骄傲：“我从不碰动物制品。”\n\n兄弟，蜜蜂不算动物？"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0678", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "windows-vista", "target_name": "Windows Vista", "facts": ["Released to consumers in January 2007, after the 'Longhorn' project was reset in 2004 and headline features such as the WinFS file system were dropped.", "User Account Control prompted so often that Apple's 'Get a Mac' campaign ran an ad about 'Cancel or Allow'.", "'Windows Vista Capable' stickers went on PCs that could only run Home Basic, without the Aero interface, and led to a class-action lawsuit.", "Windows 7 (2009), widely praised, was largely a tuned-up Vista on the same kernel line; Vista support ended in April 2017."], "a": {"item_id": "ebfddca0e411", "text": "第一天就升级Vista的人，你们公司肯定有一个，就是那个说“我对电脑懂一点”的。\n\n他图什么？图毛玻璃。Aero，窗口是半透明的，他觉得自己电脑一下子有钱了。结果他那台机器贴着“Vista Capable”的贴纸，意思是“能运行Vista”，但只能运行没有毛玻璃的Vista。花钱升级，就为了得到一个不透明的窗口。\n\n然后是UAC。他点一下，弹窗：“是否允许？”允许。又弹：“您确定吗？”确定。一天里被问“确定”的次数，比他求婚还多。\n\n打印机没驱动，声卡没驱动，最后他默默把XP装回去。同事问：“怎么样？”\n\n他说：“挺好，我体验了一下，什么叫花钱买降级。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "445c6f8771dc", "text": "有一种人，叫第一天就升级Vista的人。我问他图啥？他说要玻璃特效，要边栏小工具，要最新的系统。结果装完第一件事，是点了四十七次\"允许\"。不是我点的，是系统在问：您确定要复制这个文件吗？您确定要打开这个程序吗？您确定要关掉这个弹窗吗？像在跟一个多疑的保安办手续。\n\n复制一个文件，进度条走到99%停住，给你显示\"正在计算剩余时间\"，算完告诉你还要一个小时。他的显卡驱动？厂家说还在开发。打印机？厂家说用XP版就够了。最后他在论坛发帖，标题叫《Vista很好，是我的电脑不行》。电脑没问题，问题是他把\"最新\"当成了\"能用\"。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0679", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "brasilia", "target_name": "巴西利亚", "facts": ["Inaugurated on 21 April 1960 after about 41 months of construction, moving the capital inland from Rio de Janeiro.", "Lúcio Costa's Plano Piloto is laid out like an airplane (or a bird, or a cross), with Oscar Niemeyer's government buildings along the fuselage.", "Listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1987, less than 30 years after it was built.", "Planned for 500,000 residents; the Federal District now has about 3 million, most in satellite towns outside the plan.", "Built for the car: wide avenues, few pedestrian crossings, and distances that make walking impractical."], "a": {"item_id": "879d5b880e2f", "text": "在巴西利亚靠两条腿出门的，就三种人：刚考上公务员、工资还没到账的；捧着建筑系课本来朝圣尼迈耶的；还有一种，是前两种走到一半的样子。\n\n他们图什么？图“城市漫步”。可这城是照着飞机画的，你走路，等于在机翼上找人行道。地图上南翼308区和309区挨着，中间隔着一条六车道。想过去？去找那种叫“小剪刀”的立交，绕一圈下来，你从南翼走出了北翼的心情。\n\n旱季湿度百分之十，太阳毒得像在三权广场受审。认出他们很简单：草坪上那条被踩秃的土路，那不是小道，那是他们集体签的字。\n\n下场？第二个月，贷款买了辆二手菲亚特。"}, "b": {"item_id": "bb6fc598ef80", "text": "在巴西利亚靠两条腿出门的人，我服你们。你们都是什么神仙？大概三种：一种是退休大爷，每天走八公里，说是锻炼，其实是不想在家跟老伴大眼瞪小眼；一种是刚来的外地人，觉得\"这城市这么规整，走走就到了\"，二十分钟后他站在Eixão的六车道中间，像被冲上岸的海带；还有一种是非要证明自己有\"城市精神\"的人。图什么？图个\"我不开车也能活\"的面子。落得什么下场？你在308北和308南之间绕了一个小时，因为楼都长得一模一样。最后你走进一个部委大院，门卫问你找谁，你说找公交车站。公交车站？它在你身后三公里，中间隔着一条没有人行道的高速，隔着你的人生。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0680", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "esperanto", "target_name": "世界语", "facts": ["Published in Warsaw in 1887 by L. L. Zamenhof, a Białystok-born eye doctor, under the pen name Doktoro Esperanto, 'one who hopes'.", "Speaker estimates range from 100,000 to two million; roughly a thousand people grow up with it as a native language.", "William Shatner starred in Incubus (1966), a horror film shot entirely in Esperanto, with pronunciation Esperantists still mock.", "Its Wikipedia has over 350,000 articles, and its Duolingo course for English speakers had 1.36 million learners by July 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "16da4448f632", "text": "我有个朋友学世界语，说是为了世界和平。这语言是1887年一个波兰眼科医生发明的，目标是让全人类说同一种话。一百三十多年过去了，全人类没统一，我朋友倒是跟全人类都说不上话了。\n\n他图什么呢？他说世界语圈有个\"护照服务\"，全球会说世界语的人家里都能免费住。我问他住过几家，他说一家，在匈牙利。两个人见面第一件事，就是互相纠正对方宾格结尾漏掉的那个\"-n\"。\n\n最绝的是，\"Esperanto\"这个词原意是\"心怀希望的人\"。你看，发明者早就想明白了：学这玩意儿的人，只能靠希望活着。"}, "b": {"item_id": "3187bfd3bfb3", "text": "学世界语的人，我研究过，理想都很大：世界和平。方法也很简单：先让全世界都学世界语。目前进度，据说有一百万人会，而且这一百万人……基本互相认识。\n\n别人学外语是为了认识外国人，他们学是为了去参加世界语大会。四十个国家的人坐一屋，用一门没有任何国家说的语言，聊同一个话题：世界语为什么还没火。\n\n最离谱的是，1907年有人嫌它不够完美，搞了个改良版叫伊多语，成功把自己人分成了两派。\n\n人类还没统一呢，先把想统一人类的人给拆了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0681", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hydrogen-cars", "target_name": "氢燃料电池汽车", "facts": ["Toyota launched the Mirai in 2014; 'mirai' means 'future' in Japanese.", "Nearly all US hydrogen filling stations are in California; Shell closed all seven of its California passenger-car stations in February 2024.", "More than 95% of the world's hydrogen is made from fossil fuels, mostly by steam-reforming natural gas.", "California pump prices reached about $36 per kilogram in 2023-24, over $200 for a Mirai fill; Toyota has handed buyers fuel cards worth $15,000.", "Mirai owners filed a class action against Toyota in July 2024 over hydrogen shortages and station outages; a second suit in 2025 sought at least $5.7 billion."], "a": {"item_id": "7f3ef4cfecbe", "text": "我有个朋友买了氢燃料电池车，他说电动车太俗，他要“跳过一代，直接活在未来”。\n\n结果未来没来，他先搬家了。\n\n为啥？全市就俩加氢站，一个在开发区，一个在“维护中”。他现在挑对象第一句话不问工作，问：“你家离加氢站多远？”\n\n他最爱跟人吹：“我加满只要三分钟！”对，三分钟加满，四十分钟开过去，到了还得排在一溜儿氢能公交后面。\n\n他还特骄傲：“我这车尾气是水，能喝！”兄弟，你花三十万，就为了买台会开的净水器？\n\n前两天他想卖车，二手商看了一眼，说：“这车我收，但你得告诉我，买家去哪儿加氢。”\n\n他沉默了。他终于明白自己确实活在未来：在这片土地上，有这车的人还没出生。"}, "b": {"item_id": "c99d709097af", "text": "买氢燃料电池车的人，我研究过，就三种。\n\n第一种，环保人士。张口就是：“我这车尾气只有水。”兄弟，你这氢大部分是天然气炼的，污染在加氢站上游排完了，你在这儿排水。这就好比饭店说自己不产生垃圾，因为厨房在隔壁街。\n\n第二种，被销售一句“送三年氢气”忽悠来的。图的是免费。结果全市两座加氢站，一座检修，一座在隔壁区。他现在周末的娱乐活动就是去加氢，导航到了，门口贴着：“设备维护，复工时间待定”。\n\n下场更惨。氢气卡还没用完，加氢站先倒闭了。几十万的车，停小区里，邻居问：“这啥车？”他说：“氢的。”邻居点点头：“哦，那你现在确实挺空的。”\n\n最后，他开着车唯一的排放物，去浇了阳台那盆绿萝。他说：“至少我这车，能养花。”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0682", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "pmma", "target_name": "PMMA 电子束光刻胶", "facts": ["The same polymer as Plexiglas and acrylic glass, dissolved in anisole or chlorobenzene and spun into a thin film.", "The oldest electron-beam resist still in everyday use; IBM researchers reported it as one in the late 1960s.", "Positive tone: the beam breaks polymer chains and the exposed region dissolves in a MIBK:IPA developer.", "Resolves below 10 nm but has poor sensitivity, so doses of hundreds to over a thousand µC/cm² (depending on voltage) make writing slow.", "Poor dry-etch resistance, so it is used mostly for lift-off rather than as an etch mask."], "a": {"item_id": "90a70ba10fe5", "text": "我有个哥们读纳米加工博士，电子束光刻到今天还用PMMA。PMMA是啥？有机玻璃。奶茶店拿它做招牌，他拿它做量子器件。为啥不换？导师说配方是师爷九十年代调的：950K叠495K，MIBK兑IPA一比三，显影四十五秒，一秒别动。图啥？便宜、稳，论文写一句\"standard PMMA process\"，审稿人都不问。代价是灵敏度低得像我爸回微信，一片芯片曝一整夜，机时按小时收钱，他就睡在机房门口。剂量给多了还交联，正胶活成负胶。最后剥离，丙酮泡一宿，金还粘着，一上超声，金掉了，片子也碎了。他捧着碎片说：\"至少线宽十纳米。\""}, "b": {"item_id": "d0b057bba930", "text": "做电子束光刻的朋友，我是真佩服你们。别人都上EUV了，你们还抱着950K的PMMA不撒手。电子一打，分子链断成小段，拿MIBK兑异丙醇泡一泡，图形就显出来了，干净得很。问题是它怕刻蚀，刻到一半自己先塌了。你们图什么？图十纳米的线条画得漂亮。然后呢？做剥离，丙酮泡一晚上，金属毛边比头发丝还多。博士师兄熬了三个月，论文第三章就是一张拖尾的SEM照片。导师看了一眼说：线条挺好，就是没法用。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0683", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "slack", "target_name": "Slack", "facts": ["Spun out in 2013 from the internal chat tool of Tiny Speck's failed game Glitch; founder Stewart Butterfield had earlier spun Flickr out of another failed game.", "The name is a backronym: 'Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge'.", "Salesforce bought it for $27.7 billion in 2021.", "Filed an EU antitrust complaint against Microsoft in 2020 over the bundling of Teams with Office."], "a": {"item_id": "62d910dd9864", "text": "有些老板觉得，把公司搬进 Slack 就叫数字化管理。他建了一百二十个频道，#general 底下永远是“早上好”和一排表情回应。他的理想是“全员透明”，结果大家学会了：发一句“收到”，状态设成“开会中”，然后去另一个频道划水。他半夜十一点在 #项目A-最终版-真的最终版 里@channel，回应他的只有三个🫡，还有一个Huddle邀请，是他自己发的。最后他终于明白：员工不是不看消息，是他把公司做成了一个未读红点比工资还多的地方。"}, "b": {"item_id": "d768d460ddec", "text": "我见过一种老板，叫“Slack治国派”。创业第一天就宣布：咱们不发邮件，一切公开，扁平！三年后，公司一百人，四百个频道。#general没人敢说话，#random全是老板转发的文章。他最爱@channel，凌晨一点全公司一百部手机一起震，内容是：“大家觉得新logo咋样？”决策靠表情投票：三个👍，一个👀，通过。没人知道👀啥意思，包括发👀的人。\n\n最惨的是他自己。头像旁边那个绿点，他不敢让它灭，怕员工觉得他不在；员工也不敢让它灭，怕他觉得员工不在。全公司互相盯着一个绿点上班。\n\n他确实消灭了邮件。现在他想找一个决定，得先问一句：“这事是在哪个thread里定的？”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0684", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "emacs", "target_name": "Emacs", "facts": ["Began in 1976 as a set of macros for the TECO editor at MIT's AI Lab, written by Richard Stallman and others; GNU Emacs followed in 1985.", "Extensible in its own Lisp dialect, Emacs Lisp; the standing joke is that it is a fine operating system lacking only a good editor.", "Ships with a psychotherapist (M-x doctor), Tetris, a mail client, a calendar, and a web browser (eww).", "An old backronym is 'Eight Megabytes And Constantly Swapping', from when 8 MB was a lot of memory.", "'Emacs pinky' is the name for the hand strain blamed on its Control-key chords."], "a": {"item_id": "ce1edb495cdc", "text": "有一种人嫌操作系统太小，非要把Emacs装成操作系统。邮件在里面收，日程用org-mode排，网页用eww看，连音乐都在里面放。图什么？图一个界面管所有事。结果一启动，init.el跑了四十秒，我问他怎么退出，他说C-x C-c。我说那是退出，他说那是\"我还没想好要不要走\"。最惨的是他的小拇指，Ctrl和Meta全压在它身上。去年体检，医生问是不是长期弹钢琴，他说不是，我在写Lisp。现在他的操作系统还是没装好，但他已经能用一个快捷键，把自己的手指头按进了 *scratch* 缓冲区。"}, "b": {"item_id": "19ed8eac97e6", "text": "我认识一个把Emacs当操作系统的哥们。他用Emacs收邮件、看新闻、听歌、管日程。我问：“那你干嘛不直接装个操作系统？”他推了推眼镜：“Emacs就缺一个好编辑器。”说完笑得特别得意，那种全世界只有我懂的得意。\n\n他的待办用Org-mode管，第一条是“优化Org-mode配置”，已经挂了三年。init.el写了八千行，比他这辈子写过的业务代码都长。启动一次要四十七秒，所以他从不关，上次重启是因为搬家断电。\n\n小指因为按Ctrl已经变形了，医生问怎么弄的，他说“写东西”。女朋友说出来吃个饭，他说等我写个函数。函数写好了，女朋友没了。\n\n现在他唯一的倾诉对象是M-x doctor，一个七十年代的假心理医生。他说我好孤独，它说：“你为什么这么说？”\n\n他说，这是他用过最懂他的软件。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0685", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "macau", "target_name": "澳门", "facts": ["A Portuguese settlement from the 1550s until the handover to China in December 1999.", "Casino revenue passed the Las Vegas Strip's in 2006, and at its 2013 peak, about $45 billion, was roughly seven times the Strip's.", "One company, STDM, held the casino monopoly for four decades until licences opened to competition in 2002.", "Among the most densely populated places on Earth, at roughly 20,000 people per square kilometre.", "The Cotai Strip stands on land reclaimed from the sea between the islands of Taipa and Coloane."], "a": {"item_id": "be44032450bd", "text": "我特别佩服那种说\"我去澳门翻本\"的人。股票亏了三十万，他的方案是：去澳门，一晚上，赢回来。你想想，三十万是怎么亏的？靠判断力。现在他拿同一套判断力，去一个没有钟的地方翻本。\n\n到了威尼斯人，里面的天永远是下午四点，假的，就是让你觉得还早，还来得及。结果三十万变六十万，反方向的。出来一看，赌场对面就是当铺，灯亮着，贴心得像医院门口开殡仪馆。表当了，金链子当了，最后走回关闸，全部家当一个猪扒包。\n\n回来跟我说：\"差一点就翻了。\"哥，你差的不是一点，你差的是那三十万，加上另外三十万，再加上你那块表。"}, "b": {"item_id": "09aa41e46e5f", "text": "有种人我太熟了：上个月在澳门输了两万，发誓\"这次必须翻本\"。他怎么去的？早上七点坐大巴过拱北，在威尼斯人门口排队换筹码，图的就是那两万块能\"回家\"。结果他在百家乐桌前坐了一下午，翻本的钱全输给了赌场的冷气。傍晚他坐在大三巴牌坊底下啃葡挞，跟鸽子商量下个月的房租。你说他傻吗？不傻，他还算了账，每一笔都算得很认真。问题是，账算得再认真，庄家也是按规矩赢的。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0686", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "microsoft-word", "target_name": "Microsoft Word", "facts": ["Launched in 1983 as 'Multi-Tool Word' for Xenix and MS-DOS.", "Clippy, the animated paperclip assistant, arrived in Office 97 and was removed in Office 2007; Microsoft itself ran ads mocking it in 2001.", "Its .docx format, Office Open XML, was fast-tracked to ISO standard 29500 in 2008 as a roughly 6,000-page specification, amid accusations that national committees had been packed.", "Older spell checkers, Word's included, offered 'Cupertino' as a correction for a mistyped 'cooperation', and the resulting 'Cupertino effect' made it into published NATO, EU and UN-agency documents."], "a": {"item_id": "743026e864f3", "text": "我们宿舍有个兄弟，打死不学排版软件，说：“论文嘛，Word就够了，谁电脑里没个Word？”\n\n是，谁都有。所以全宿舍凌晨四点一起陪他看页码。\n\n他的诉求特别朴素：封面不要页码，摘要用罗马数字，正文从1开始。就这三个愿望，阿拉丁神灯都能办，Word办不了。他插了一晚上分节符，最后页眉上“第三章”出现在了致谢里，像个阴魂不散的前任。\n\n最狠的是插一张图。图一进来，后面二十页集体往下挪，表格跑到下一页，标题孤零零留在页底。他想拖回来，鼠标一碰，Word当场“未响应”，屏幕一白，跟他的人生一个颜色。\n\n现在他桌面上躺着：“论文终稿.docx”、“终稿改.docx”、“终稿真的不改了.docx”，还有一个“~$终稿.docx”，打不开也删不掉，跟他的学位一样。\n\n导师最后打开一看，用修订模式批了一句话：“格式有问题。”\n\n整篇变红。"}, "b": {"item_id": "b60d316d9566", "text": "有些同学非要用Word排毕业论文，图什么？图省事。学校要求\"标题三号黑体、正文小四宋体\"，他觉得点几下就能搞定。结果一章标题用了普通段落样式，目录里直接没有它；页眉一改，前面十页跟着变，\"链接到前一节\"这五个字他看了一整晚。最后目录页码差了三页，他按F9更新域，Word回他一句\"错误！未定义书签\"。你不是在写论文，你是在跟一个分节符谈恋爱，谈崩了它也不告诉你为什么。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0687", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "windows-8", "target_name": "Windows 8", "facts": ["Released in October 2012; removed the Start button and booted into a full-screen grid of tiles designed for touch screens most PCs did not have.", "The 8.1 update in 2013 put a Start button back, Windows 10 (2015) restored the Start menu, and Microsoft skipped the name Windows 9 altogether.", "Microsoft dropped the 'Metro' name in August 2012 after reports that German retailer Metro AG had objected on trademark grounds; Microsoft called 'Metro' a code name and denied any litigation, and the style was afterwards known informally as 'Modern UI'.", "The 'Charms bar' hid Search, Share, and Settings behind an invisible swipe from the right edge.", "Windows RT, the ARM edition launched alongside it, could not run ordinary Windows programs; Microsoft wrote down $900 million of unsold Surface RT tablets in 2013."], "a": {"item_id": "f01853167432", "text": "你们这种人我见过：机箱插着显示器，鼠标线拖到桌子底下，就为了装 Windows 8。图什么？图那个开始屏幕，满屏方块，天气、股票、新闻全贴在墙上，像把手机桌面硬塞进显示器。结果呢？鼠标得滑到右上角，等一个叫\"超级按钮\"的东西弹出来，左下角的开始按钮没了。你找了半小时，按下 Win 键，跳出一整面磁贴，\"邮件\"那块还在显示三个月前的垃圾广告。最后你上网搜\"怎么把开始菜单还给我\"，第一条推荐的是收费软件。"}, "b": {"item_id": "825a78e26940", "text": "给台式机装Windows 8的人，我太了解了。这种人，朋友聚会必说一句：\"我这个是尝鲜。\"尝鲜，你家台式机是个五斤重的铁箱子，插着鼠标键盘，哪来的\"鲜\"？\n\n图什么呢？图开机快，四秒。是，四秒就进去了，进去以后花四十分钟找关机。\n\n开始按钮没了，换成一整屏磁贴，天气、股票、应用商店，全对着你闪。他们对着显示器伸手去戳，显示器说：\"大哥，我不是触屏。\"\n\n关机更绝。鼠标得甩到右上角，等超级按钮栏冒出来，点设置，点电源，才能关。关个机像密室逃脱。\n\n下场呢？他们最后都去搜\"Windows 8 怎么把开始菜单弄回来\"，下载个第三方软件，把微软删掉的东西，亲手装回去。\n\n等到8.1出了，开始按钮回来了，他们热泪盈眶，一点：跳到磁贴屏。\n\n那不是按钮，那是个门铃，按了还是那帮磁贴来开门。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0688", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "jackfruit", "target_name": "菠萝蜜", "facts": ["The largest fruit that grows on a tree; single fruits can weigh over 40 kg.", "The national fruit of Bangladesh and the state fruit of Kerala and Tamil Nadu.", "Unripe flesh is sold as a vegan 'pulled pork' substitute, although it has only 1-2 g of protein per 100 g.", "Cutting one releases a sticky white latex, so cooks oil their knives and hands first.", "A member of the mulberry family (Moraceae), a relative of breadfruit and figs."], "a": {"item_id": "0c2c69874c8f", "text": "有人把菠萝蜜炖成“手撕肉”，标价三十八，招牌写着“植物蛋白，口感还原”。我点了一份，吃了一口，好家伙，是甜的。不是一般的甜，是榴莲没熟透、香蕉刚放软那种甜。我以为自己点的是红烧肉，结果是一碗加了纤维的糖水。老板挺自豪，说“你看这纹理，一丝一丝的，跟猪肉纤维一模一样”。我说老板，猪肉的纤维是肌肉，你这是树上挂的果囊，你是把一棵树的肚子撕开给我看。他说这叫创新。后来店关了，听说他转行卖菠萝蜜酥，说“不像肉了，但它甜”。我说你这才是找对了路子，肉没卖出去，至少糖分到位了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "17fe9d85a9f3", "text": "现在有些老板，把青菠萝蜜煮熟撕成丝，当手撕鸡卖。图啥？图便宜，青菠萝蜜几块钱一斤，一撕，能卖出肉的价。\n\n算盘打得响，但他没杀过菠萝蜜。这玩意儿一刀下去，流出来的不是汁，是胶。粘刀，粘手，粘案板，老板还没骗到顾客，先把自己焊在后厨了。为了切开它，得拿油抹刀，抹手，抹案板，一套流程下来，一颗菠萝蜜，三小时。他那不是做假肉，是跟一个长满刺的胖娃娃摔跤。\n\n成本一算：油、人工、调料、创可贴，比真鸡还贵。\n\n最后顾客咬一口：“老板，这鸡怎么有股没熟透的甜味？”老板还嘴硬：“土鸡，吃水果长大的。”\n\n现在他手上的胶还没洗掉，指纹全没了。骗人没骗成，倒是意外获得了一项新技能，可以去偷东西。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0689", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "juicero", "target_name": "Juicero 榨汁机", "facts": ["A Wi-Fi-connected cold-press juicer launched in 2016 at $699, later cut to $399, that squeezed proprietary bags of chopped produce sold by subscription.", "Raised about $120 million from investors including Google Ventures and Kleiner Perkins.", "In April 2017, Bloomberg reporters squeezed the packs by hand and got 7.5 ounces of juice in 90 seconds, faster than the machine's 8 ounces in about two minutes.", "Each pack carried a QR code that the press scanned and checked online before squeezing; it refused expired packs and would not run at all unless connected to Wi-Fi.", "Shut down in September 2017 and offered refunds on the machine."], "a": {"item_id": "c8e0492d10b9", "text": "你知道花400美元买Juicero的都是什么人吗？就是那种早上六点起床、先冥想再跑步、手机里装了十二个健康App的硅谷精英。他们不缺钱，缺的是“我连喝果汁都在用科技”的那份体面。\n\n结果呢？你花400块买了台联网的压榨机，再每袋七八美元买人家的果蔬包。机器还得先扫码、连Wi-Fi，确认你这包没过期，才肯开工。家里断网了？对不起，今天没有健康。\n\n然后彭博社的记者干了件特别缺德的事：直接上手捏那个袋子，挤出来的汁跟机器差不多，速度还更快。\n\n一群天天喊“颠覆传统”的人，被两只手颠覆了。\n\n最惨的是，后来公司倒闭了，给你退钱。你连“至少我还在用”都说不出口了。你最后得到了什么？一个教训：原来你最该投资的科技产品，是自己的手。"}, "b": {"item_id": "42eeb6dfde70", "text": "Juicero 要 400 美元，干的事是把一包切好的果蔬塞进去压成汁。问题是，那包东西你用手也能捏出来。做这台机器的是硅谷一帮创始人，想颠覆榨汁行业，融了一个多亿美元，图的是你每个月订阅他们的果蔬包，一包好几美元，还得联网认证。后来有记者拿它的包装袋直接用手挤，汁照样出来。投资人一看，哦，原来我们投的不是榨汁机，是一台给包装袋收月费的收费站。公司两年后就关门了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0690", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "microsoft-teams", "target_name": "Microsoft Teams", "facts": ["Launched in 2017, bundled at no extra charge into Office 365, and replaced Skype for Business.", "After Slack's antitrust complaint, Microsoft unbundled Teams from Office in the EU in 2023 and worldwide in 2024; the European Commission still sent a statement of objections in 2024.", "Microsoft reported 320 million monthly active users in 2023.", "Microsoft shut down consumer Skype in May 2025 and sent its users to Teams."], "a": {"item_id": "0c5f29fdb1c7", "text": "咱公司强推 Teams 的那位，我打听了，是采购。理由特别感人：“Office 365 套餐里本来就带着，不用另外花钱。”省了多少？年终述职写着呢：降本增效，三十万。\n\n三十万省下来了，然后呢？全公司每天问他同一个问题：“我昨天发的文件在哪？”他说：“Teams 里，‘文件’标签页。”其实在 SharePoint 里。他自己也是上周才知道 SharePoint 是什么。\n\n最惨的是，他在 Teams 里建了一个“团队”，团队里又建了一个“团队”，最后这个团队里只有他一个人。\n\n我们都在微信群里，聊他。"}, "b": {"item_id": "8605988b84fd", "text": "他们是谁？就是那个拍板\"全公司必须用Teams\"的行政总监。图什么？Office套餐自带，不花一分钱，还能在年终汇报上写\"数字化协同全面落地\"。结果呢？他自己的状态每天自动变成\"离开\"，因为他开会总忘了点\"举手\"；他撤回的消息显示\"此消息已被删除\"，大家早就截图了。后来Teams又崩了一次，IT被骂惨，他被调去当\"Teams使用培训官\"，每天负责回答\"为什么我进不了会议室\"。现在他的签名是：有事@我，别@所有人。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0691", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "risk", "target_name": "《战国风云》（Risk）", "facts": ["Invented in 1957 by Albert Lamorisse, the French director of The Red Balloon, as La Conquête du Monde; Parker Brothers published it in the United States in 1959.", "The map has 42 territories, among them Kamchatka, Irkutsk, and Yakutsk, while the entire Middle East is one territory.", "Holding Australia, which has a single entry point, is the standard opening strategy.", "A full game can run for hours, and players eliminated early have nothing to do but watch."], "a": {"item_id": "49ce2b81452e", "text": "那种非要把《战国风云》玩到底的人，我是真服了。开局说好玩两小时，结果六个小时过去，桌上就剩他一个人还在操作，我们早就被他一个个灭了。他图什么？图那张世界地图全红的快感。他守住澳大利亚，吃下整个南美洲，最后手抖得像在拆炸弹，每掷一次骰子都要先深呼吸。我们问他饿不饿，他说再等等，还差阿拉斯加，他那二十个小兵正在阿拉斯加跟我们两个骑兵对峙。最后他赢了，全世界都是他的，只是红色塑料兵用光了，他只好拿薯片当兵。赢是赢了，第二天群里没人再约他。"}, "b": {"item_id": "8c92129c3833", "text": "每个朋友圈里都有一个人，非要把一局《战国风云》打完。你们认识他：开局就缩进澳大利亚，守着暹罗那一个口子，像个在村口摆路障的村长。图什么？图每回合多拿那两个兵。别人在欧洲打成一锅粥，他在大洋洲养精蓄锐，一养就是四个小时。凌晨两点，屋里只剩他和堪察加。他攥着三张卡死等凑成一套换兵，眼神像股民在等解套。最后他赢了，世界是他的了。可赢给谁看？朋友早睡了，女朋友搬走了，房东在敲门。他把乌克兰上那四十个塑料小兵一个个收回盒子。那是他这辈子离统一天下最近的一次，也是最后一次有人喊他来玩桌游。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0692", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "php", "target_name": "PHP", "facts": ["Created by Rasmus Lerdorf in 1994 as 'Personal Home Page Tools', a set of C programs for tracking visitors to his own résumé page; the name now officially stands for the recursive 'PHP: Hypertext Preprocessor'.", "Lerdorf, in his 2007 MySQL Conference talk 'PHP on Hormones': 'I'm not a real programmer. I throw together things until it works then I move on.'", "Runs WordPress, which W3Techs counted on 39.4% of all websites (58.1% of sites with a known CMS) on 9 November 2026.", "Core function names are inconsistent (strpos, str_replace, htmlspecialchars, nl2br) partly because, as Lerdorf confirmed, early function names were chosen to balance a hash table keyed on string length.", "Until PHP 8 (2020), the comparison 0 == 'foo' was true because the string was silently converted to a number."], "a": {"item_id": "e721ec283ce4", "text": "干PHP吃饭的人，我认识三类。第一类是接外包的，客户说\"就改个按钮颜色\"，他打开一看，代码里还留着 mysql_connect，注释是2009年的，他知道这活要干到天亮。第二类是背框架的，嘴里全是Laravel，自己的小网站却还是 $_GET 直接拼进SQL，问他为啥，他说\"这叫传统\"。第三类图的就是那句\"语法简单，上手快\"，结果干了十年，还在查 \"0\" == false 为什么是真的。去年他投简历，HR问他精通什么，他写\"PHP\"，面试官问 == 和 === 有啥区别，他沉默三秒，把\"精通\"两个字划掉了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "e21dca174c4d", "text": "我有个朋友，靠写PHP吃饭。每次喝酒他都举杯：“PHP是世界上最好的语言！”全桌没人接话，因为全桌都是写PHP的。大家心里清楚，这是我们这行唯一能赢的一句台词。\n\n他图什么？图快。一个下午，织梦模板一套，宝塔面板一点，县城瓷砖城就有官网了。收三千，老板还嫌贵：“我侄子也会。”\n\n他说他天天跟钱打交道。确实，每个变量前面都挂着个美元符号。干了十年，那是他摸过最多的美元。\n\n现在他维护一套2009年的代码，mysql_query还在跑，他不敢动。一动，瓷砖城倒闭，他也失业。说白了，他跟那段代码是互相续命。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0693", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "keurig", "target_name": "Keurig 胶囊咖啡机", "facts": ["John Sylvan invented the K-Cup in the early 1990s and sold his stake for about $50,000 in 1997; in 2015 he told The Atlantic, 'I feel bad sometimes that I ever did it.'", "Keurig 2.0 (2014) read an ink mark on the lid and rejected unlicensed pods; sales fell and workarounds spread online.", "By 2014 enough K-Cups were sold each year to circle the Earth more than ten times; the pods were mostly unrecyclable #7 plastic until a switch to polypropylene by 2020.", "Keurig Green Mountain merged with Dr Pepper Snapple in 2018 to form Keurig Dr Pepper."], "a": {"item_id": "a23abc9807e4", "text": "用Keurig的人，就是想喝咖啡，但不想沾“做咖啡”的任何一个字。磨豆？累。烧水？累。你们的理想是按一下，然后盯着它嗡嗡响四十秒。这比烧壶水还久，但因为是按按钮，所以叫效率。\n\n算过账吗？一颗胶囊十克豆，折下来一斤两百多块。你付的是精品价，喝的是一根针扎穿锡纸挤出来的褐色热水。\n\n发明K杯那哥们儿自己都公开说后悔了，家里根本不用。发明人都退坑了，你们还在续费。\n\n最惨是那盒混合装，三个月后剩下的永远是那几颗榛子味。不喝，也不扔，就躺在抽屉里，跟你一样：方便，但没人要。"}, "b": {"item_id": "45e5b40c4a0a", "text": "我同事天天用Keurig泡咖啡，理由是三十秒搞定。行，省事，不用磨豆，不用洗壶，按一下嗡地一声，像要起飞。结果出来一杯八盎司的热水，颜色像他的周报。每天撕一个锡箔盖、扔一个塑料杯，一个月下来，垃圾桶里的小杯子能堆成金字塔。上个月机器亮起\"请除垢\"，他查说明书，得买除垢剂，还得拿醋灌一遍。算下来，他的胶囊钱够买一台手冲壶和两袋豆子。最后他还是下楼买了杯星巴克，店员问要什么，他说：\"要一杯，有味儿的。\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0694", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "windows-vista", "target_name": "Windows Vista", "facts": ["Released to consumers in January 2007, after the 'Longhorn' project was reset in 2004 and headline features such as the WinFS file system were dropped.", "User Account Control prompted so often that Apple's 'Get a Mac' campaign ran an ad about 'Cancel or Allow'.", "'Windows Vista Capable' stickers went on PCs that could only run Home Basic, without the Aero interface, and led to a class-action lawsuit.", "Windows 7 (2009), widely praised, was largely a tuned-up Vista on the same kernel line; Vista support ended in April 2017."], "a": {"item_id": "c62e9c0142f3", "text": "我有个朋友，2007年Vista首发当天零点就去排队，还非买旗舰版，三百九十九美元。图什么？广告说了：“Wow从现在开始。”\n\n确实，开机那一刻他就“哇”了：打印机没驱动，声卡没驱动，他那台贴着“Vista Capable”标签的电脑，连Aero玻璃特效都跑不动。他每点一下，屏幕就一黑：“Windows需要您的许可才能继续。”点“允许”，又黑一下。半年下来，他成了全世界被自己电脑审问最多的人。\n\n旗舰版承诺的“终极附加功能”，最后等来一副德州扑克。\n\n现在他逢人就推荐一个高级功能，叫“降级到XP”。"}, "b": {"item_id": "62ac4958dd62", "text": "有一种人，办公室里第一个喊\"我先装\"的，Vista一出来当天就升级。图什么？图的是半透明的Aero玻璃窗。结果开机六分钟，点开一个文件夹，弹窗问他\"您确定要打开吗\"，他点确定，又弹一个\"您真的确定吗\"。一天点了三百次确定，手指比他的工资还累。声卡没驱动，打印机没驱动，连鼠标滚轮都不认。最后他翻出XP安装盘，格式化重装，还在朋友圈发：我终于懂了什么叫成熟的系统。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0695", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "carbon-nanotubes", "target_name": "碳纳米管", "facts": ["Reported by Sumio Iijima in Nature in 1991.", "Promised as the cable for a space elevator; a 2006 analysis showed that ordinary defects cut the strength of a macroscopic cable far below the ideal value.", "Long, rigid multi-walled nanotubes behaved like asbestos fibres in a 2008 mouse study in Nature Nanotechnology.", "Sorting metallic from semiconducting tubes has blocked nanotube electronics for three decades; MIT's 16-bit RV16X-NANO processor (2019) is still a lab demonstration.", "Today they are sold mainly as a conductive additive in lithium-ion battery electrodes."], "a": {"item_id": "55f4d79ee616", "text": "我认识一个搞碳纳米管的博士，去路演，PPT第一页写：强度是钢的一百倍，重量只有钢的六分之一。投资人眼睛全亮了，有人举手问：能造太空电梯吗？他说理论上可以。全场鼓掌。\"理论上\"，是这圈子最值钱的三个字。他没讲的是，他那批管子里导电的和不导电的混在一起，像一锅分不清的挂面，想分开一根，得先烧掉一栋楼的钱。投资人也没问。后来他融了三轮，估值翻了几倍，公司改名叫\"某某量子新材料\"，实验室里那罐黑乎乎的粉，还是原来那罐。"}, "b": {"item_id": "dc1d00e455ba", "text": "碳纳米管创业的，PPT第一页永远是太空电梯：比钢强一百倍，从地球拉根绳到同步轨道。投资人问：“多长了？”他说：“实验室最长做到半米。”离三万六千公里，还差三万五千九百九十九公里九百九十九米五。\n\n第二页讲替代硅：“摩尔定律到头了，我们接班！”问良率呢？他说：“有一个小问题，长出来三分之一是金属的，三分之二是半导体的，我们分不开。”大哥，你这叫“替代硅”？你这叫“开盲盒”。\n\n讲了二十年，一九九一年发现的东西，至今还在“即将改变世界”。\n\n最后公司怎么样了？活得挺好。不造电梯，不造芯片，一吨一吨往锂电池里卖导电剂，按公斤算钱。路演时说要通往太空，年报里写的是“主营业务：黑色粉末”。\n\n太空电梯没建成，但他确实上去了，坐的是电动车。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0696", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "klingon", "target_name": "克林贡语", "facts": ["Created by linguist Marc Okrand for Star Trek III (1984), building on a few words the actor James Doohan improvised for the 1979 film.", "Deliberately alien grammar: object-verb-subject word order and no verb 'to be', so Hamlet's line became 'taH pagh taHbe'', 'continue or not continue'.", "The Klingon Language Institute (1992) published Hamlet 'in the original Klingon'; Bing Translator added Klingon in 2013 and Duolingo in 2018.", "Okrand's 1985 Klingon Dictionary listed roughly 1,500 words, and fluent speakers number only in the dozens: 20 to 30 in 2009, 30 to 50 by 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "45f4bb0c382a", "text": "我朋友花三年把克林贡语练到了流利。流利到什么程度？他跟我吵架，全程用克林贡语。吵完我才发现，他是对着镜子练的，因为我一句都听不懂。他图什么？他说这语言够硬气，念起来一嗓子呼噜，像刚从战场上下来。结果他去参加同好聚会，满屋子都是练了三年的人，互相拍胸脯、喷口水打招呼，嗓子全哑了，喝口水都得用喉音。后来他女朋友跟他分手，留了一句话：你说你想战死的时候，能不能先问问我想不想吃晚饭？"}, "b": {"item_id": "1e0df73ad7c6", "text": "我认识一哥们，克林贡语说得贼溜。全世界能说流利的，据说也就几十个人，他算一个。\n\n你品品这是什么概念？这门语言里压根没有“你好”，见面只能说“nuqneH”，意思是“你想干嘛”。他花了十年，学会一门见面就吵架的语言，结果全世界能跟他吵的人，凑不齐一个班。\n\n他每年去参加克林贡语言研究所的年会，跟一屋子人朗读克林贡语版《哈姆雷特》。人家坚持说这才是原著，莎士比亚是翻译的。\n\n他还想从小教儿子说，孩子五岁就不肯接茬了。\n\n他这辈子唯一一次听异性跟他说克林贡语，是相亲。他说完特长，姑娘站起来，说了句“Qapla'”，就是“祝你成功”，然后走了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0697", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "esperanto", "target_name": "世界语", "facts": ["Published in Warsaw in 1887 by L. L. Zamenhof, a Białystok-born eye doctor, under the pen name Doktoro Esperanto, 'one who hopes'.", "Speaker estimates range from 100,000 to two million; roughly a thousand people grow up with it as a native language.", "William Shatner starred in Incubus (1966), a horror film shot entirely in Esperanto, with pronunciation Esperantists still mock.", "Its Wikipedia has over 350,000 articles, and its Duolingo course for English speakers had 1.36 million learners by July 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "c6f136a1221b", "text": "我认识一个学世界语的。他跟我说，世界语只有十六条语法规则，一个月就能学会。没错，他一个月就学会了，然后花了十年找一个能跟他说上话的人。\n\n他图什么？图世界大同。柴门霍夫一八八七年发明它，是想让全人类共用一门第二语言。一百多年过去了，它还真成了所有人的第二语言：第二个“回头再说”的语言。\n\n他现在出国不住酒店，靠“护照服务”睡在别的世界语者家沙发上。俩人见面热泪盈眶，互称“同志”，聊了一宿。聊的是：怎么才能让更多人学世界语。\n\n我问他这辈子算不算失败。他说不算，世界语里没有“失败”，只有mal-成功。“成功”前面加个mal-，意思是成功的反面。\n\n哥们儿，翻译过来，那还是失败啊。"}, "b": {"item_id": "7911ad5f0fdc", "text": "学世界语的人，图的是全人类终于能好好说话。结果全人类没来，来的是同一拨人。这门语言的名字就叫\"希望者\"，学它的人等于从第一天就领了个外号。大会上他们互相寒暄：你哪国的？巴西。我韩国的。平时用世界语跟谁聊？跟你们俩啊。名词加-o，形容词加-a，宾格加-n，每句话都挂满尾巴，语法一个不错，朋友一个没有。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0698", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "php", "target_name": "PHP", "facts": ["Created by Rasmus Lerdorf in 1994 as 'Personal Home Page Tools', a set of C programs for tracking visitors to his own résumé page; the name now officially stands for the recursive 'PHP: Hypertext Preprocessor'.", "Lerdorf, in his 2007 MySQL Conference talk 'PHP on Hormones': 'I'm not a real programmer. I throw together things until it works then I move on.'", "Runs WordPress, which W3Techs counted on 39.4% of all websites (58.1% of sites with a known CMS) on 9 November 2026.", "Core function names are inconsistent (strpos, str_replace, htmlspecialchars, nl2br) partly because, as Lerdorf confirmed, early function names were chosen to balance a hash table keyed on string length.", "Until PHP 8 (2020), the comparison 0 == 'foo' was true because the string was silently converted to a number."], "a": {"item_id": "15733eca0d13", "text": "干PHP外包的，我见过一类人特别有意思。他们写的代码，每个变量前面都挂个$，像给每个变量别了张工牌。strpos的参数是针在前还是大海在前，他们闭着眼都能写对，因为早就不记了，改成背\"查一下手册\"。甲方说\"就改个按钮颜色\"，他们说好，打开一个三千行的文件，全局变量满天飞，一个函数里HTML、SQL、表单炖在一起，像一锅十年没洗过的火锅。他们图什么？图一个月能拿一万五。落得什么下场？三十五岁，简历上写着\"精通PHP\"，面试官问一句PHP 8有什么新特性，他沉默三秒，说：\"我们主要用的是宝塔。\""}, "b": {"item_id": "bcc6af302235", "text": "写PHP的人，我一直很佩服。这门语言每个变量前面都要加个美元符号：$name、$age、$salary。写了十年，满屏都是$，工资卡里一个都没见着。\n\n他们当初为什么入行？因为好上手。培训班三个月，出来就接单，接的全是老板表弟的商城、装修公司的官网。入行时都背过那句\"PHP是世界上最好的语言\"，十年过去，这话喊得最响的，永远是正在改前任祖传代码的人。\n\n下场更惨。每年都有人宣布PHP死了，他们不反驳，因为手上那个WordPress插件，作者三年前就跑路了。面试官问：\"你还在写PHP啊？\"他们只能说：\"我们现在升到8了，支持类型了。\"\n\n这就像有人说：我家一直漏雨，但是现在，我们有伞了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0699", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "latex", "target_name": "LaTeX", "facts": ["Leslie Lamport wrote LaTeX in the early 1980s and released it in 1984 as a macro layer on Donald Knuth's TeX (1978), which Knuth built because he disliked how the galley proofs of the new edition of his own book looked.", "TeX's version number converges on π (3.141592653 since 2021), and Knuth's TeX bug bounty started at one hexadecimal dollar, $2.56, doubling yearly until frozen at $327.68; he stopped mailing real cheques in 2008 after fraud and now credits accounts at his fictional Bank of San Serriffe, and only 9 of the 275 cheques he sent from 2006 on were ever cashed.", "Its best-known warning is 'Underfull \\hbox (badness 10000)'; overfull lines are reported as 'Overfull \\hbox (Npt too wide)', and figures float to wherever the algorithm decides, not where the author put them.", "A single missing brace can produce an error reported pages away from the mistake, and a document can need three compiler passes to get its references right."], "a": {"item_id": "6b980d56eb4f", "text": "有人写两页备忘录，非要用 LaTeX 排。问他图什么？图那个 \\maketitle 出来的标题，图页边距精确到两厘米。结果开会前一小时，他对着 Overleaf 发呆：“! Missing $ inserted”，原因是他在“报价_终版”的下划线那儿忘了进数学模式。好不容易编译通过，领导看了一眼说：能不能发个 Word 版？他只好把 tabular 一行行拆回表格。这份备忘录全公司只有他一个人会改，而他本人，现在每次开会都得先说一句：源码我发你了，你别点开。"}, "b": {"item_id": "cc677fdb9115", "text": "领导让写个备忘录，内容就一句话：聚餐从周五改到周四。他说，Word？那是给没追求的人用的。打开Overleaf，先配ctex，再装十几个宏包，再调页边距。三小时后，这句话终于有了页眉、目录，还有参考文献，聚餐通知，引了两条。\n\n然后他在里面打了句“人均50$”，编译报错：Missing $ inserted。他的聚餐，被当成了数学公式。\n\n改完之后，“谢谢”两个字孤零零掉到第三页。他又花四十分钟调行距，就为了把这两个字塞回去。\n\n同事回复：“收到，能发个Word版吗？我想改个字。”\n\n他看着那个PDF，终于明白：他排的不是备忘录，是一座纪念碑，漂亮，工整，谁都不敢动。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0700", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "google-glass", "target_name": "谷歌眼镜（Google Glass）", "facts": ["Unveiled at Google I/O 2012 with skydivers livestreaming their jump through it onto the Moscone Center roof.", "The Explorer Edition sold to invited users in 2013 for $1,500.", "Wearers were nicknamed 'Glassholes'; bars, cinemas, and casinos banned the device over its camera.", "The consumer programme ended in January 2015; an Enterprise Edition continued until Google stopped selling it in 2023.", "Its display was a 640-by-360 prism in the corner of one eye."], "a": {"item_id": "e9562ca8081e", "text": "戴谷歌眼镜进酒吧的人，我见过。通常三十出头，帽衫上印着“改变世界”，花一千五百美金买了块架在眼前的小棱镜。图什么？图别人一进门先看见他，然后问一句“这是啥”，他就能顺势讲“这是未来”。结果呢？他对着镜腿说“OK Glass，拍下她”，那个小红灯一亮，姑娘直接把一杯啤酒泼在他脸上。保安把他请出去，顺手把眼镜摘了。第二天网上就给他们起了名字，叫“眼镜混蛋”。那玩意儿后来停产了，他也没改变世界，只把那晚的酒吧改成了一个全员提防他的地方。"}, "b": {"item_id": "5674fb13d6e0", "text": "戴谷歌眼镜进酒吧的哥们儿，你图什么？花一千五百美元，给谷歌当测试员，钱还是你自己掏的。坐吧台边，右眼一直往右上角瞟，酒保问喝什么，他张嘴就是“OK Glass”。兄弟，人家不叫Glass。最绝的是这玩意儿眨眼就能拍照。以前在酒吧冲姑娘眨眼，顶多被泼一杯酒；现在姑娘得先琢磨：你是在撩我，还是在拍我？想完了，泼两杯。美国人还专门给这帮人造了个词：Glasshole。一个产品能把自己的用户骂成新单词，我是头回见。最后电池没撑过一杯长岛冰茶，他摘下眼镜一看：原来自己就是个一个人来喝闷酒的普通男人。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0701", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "oat-milk", "target_name": "燕麦奶", "facts": ["Oatly started in Sweden in the 1990s, built on Lund University research by Rickard Öste on lactose intolerance.", "Oatly's slogan 'It's like milk but made for humans' was banned in Sweden in 2015 after the dairy lobby LRF Mjölk sued; Oatly then published the lawsuit as advertising.", "Oatly listed on Nasdaq in May 2021 at a valuation near $10 billion; the shares later lost more than 90% of their value.", "Commercial oat milk is made by breaking oat starch into maltose with amylase enzymes, and many brands, Oatly among them, add rapeseed oil; the result has more sugar and a higher glycaemic index than almond or soy milk."], "a": {"item_id": "0f1dbb4a84d2", "text": "我有个同事，点拿铁必须换燕麦奶，加五块。问他为啥，他说乳糖不耐。上礼拜我亲眼看他干掉一整块芝士蛋糕，面不改色。乳糖不耐？我看你是乳糖不耐“便宜”。\n\n后来他又改口，说是为了健康、为了地球。哥，燕麦奶想打出奶泡，里面得加油，菜籽油。你每天早上花三十八，喝一杯“过了油的燕麦水”，还觉得自己在拯救地球。\n\n最惨的是，小时候你奶奶天天早上给你煮燕麦粥，你嫌弃，说“这是马吃的”。现在你多花五块钱，把粥兑进咖啡里，还拍照发朋友圈。\n\n马都没你讲究。马吃燕麦，至少不用排队。"}, "b": {"item_id": "55ab352fe073", "text": "每个咖啡店都有这种人，站在柜台前深吸一口气：“拿铁，换燕麦奶。”语气像在宣布皈依。\n\n你问他乳糖不耐吗？不是。“就是想少点负担。”然后他加点了一块芝士蛋糕。\n\n负担是少了，钱包的负担没少，燕麦奶加五块。而且为了打出奶泡，燕麦里的淀粉会被水解成麦芽糖，升糖比牛奶还高。他花五块钱，把一杯奶换成了一杯液体麦片，还觉得自己在做减法。\n\n喝到一半，他拍照发朋友圈：“植物基生活。”\n\n最后一口，他皱着眉品了品：“嗯，有层次。”\n\n兄弟，那不是层次，那是你早餐的碗底。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0702", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "comic-sans", "target_name": "Comic Sans 字体", "facts": ["Designed by Vincent Connare at Microsoft in 1994, modeled on the comic-book lettering in The Dark Knight Returns and Watchmen.", "Made for the speech bubbles of Rover, the cartoon dog in Microsoft Bob; it missed Bob's release and shipped instead in the Windows 95 Plus! pack.", "CERN's July 2012 Higgs boson discovery announcement was presented on slides set in it.", "The 'Ban Comic Sans' campaign, started by designers Dave and Holly Combs in 1999, ran until 2019, when they retired it and launched usecomicsans.com."], "a": {"item_id": "77e8416a8394", "text": "我们小区物业有位行政大哥，一心想跟业主拉近距离，所有通知一律用 Comic Sans。可他不知道，这字体根本没有中文。所以那张停水通知是这样的：“关于停水的通知”，宋体，庄严肃穆；后面“3月15日 8:00—18:00”，圆滚滚的，跟气球一样。一张纸上，中文在念悼词，数字在过六一。\n\n这字体当年是微软专门给一只卡通狗写对话框用的，大哥拿它通知全楼断水，等于派那只狗上门宣布：“汪！你家没水了！”\n\n结果没一个人当真。15号全楼照常洗澡，洗到一半水停了，一群人顶着泡沫下楼找他。大哥还挺委屈：“我通知得那么可爱，你们怎么不看呢？”"}, "b": {"item_id": "8384fe94668e", "text": "我们公司人事上周发裁员通知，用的Comic Sans。我问为什么，她说：“想柔和一点，减少冲击。”\n\n姐姐，这字体当年是给微软一只叫Rover的卡通狗设计的，专门用来写对话气泡。你这是让一条狗通知我下岗。\n\n结果呢？“因业务调整，您的岗位将被取消”，每个字都圆滚滚、歪歪扭扭，读起来就是“汪！你被开除啦～”\n\n冲击确实没有，因为根本没人信。有个哥们儿回复了一串“哈哈哈”，周一照常来上班，被保安拦在门口，还委屈：“你们那个通知不是在搞笑吗？”\n\n最惨的是人事自己，月底被优化了，通知也是Comic Sans。她盯着屏幕看了十分钟，说：“这肯定是恶作剧。”\n\n行，你的柔和，终于轮到你自己了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0703", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "latex", "target_name": "LaTeX", "facts": ["Leslie Lamport wrote LaTeX in the early 1980s and released it in 1984 as a macro layer on Donald Knuth's TeX (1978), which Knuth built because he disliked how the galley proofs of the new edition of his own book looked.", "TeX's version number converges on π (3.141592653 since 2021), and Knuth's TeX bug bounty started at one hexadecimal dollar, $2.56, doubling yearly until frozen at $327.68; he stopped mailing real cheques in 2008 after fraud and now credits accounts at his fictional Bank of San Serriffe, and only 9 of the 275 cheques he sent from 2006 on were ever cashed.", "Its best-known warning is 'Underfull \\hbox (badness 10000)'; overfull lines are reported as 'Overfull \\hbox (Npt too wide)', and figures float to wherever the algorithm decides, not where the author put them.", "A single missing brace can produce an error reported pages away from the mistake, and a document can need three compiler passes to get its references right."], "a": {"item_id": "4c2291ae9bb7", "text": "我们组有个哥们，通知周五聚餐，就两页备忘录，他非要用LaTeX排。这种人你们见过吧？他不是在写字，他是在“编译”。开头先敲四十行\\usepackage，正文一个字没有，导言区已经比来吃饭的人还多了。图什么？就图那个Computer Modern字体。PDF一打开，扑面而来一股“本人发过论文”的气息。结果呢？“周五晚七点”那一行报了个Overfull \\hbox，冒出页边三毫米，他调到凌晨两点。第二天大家都到了，就他没来。他在等BibTeX跑第二遍。我说一个吃饭通知，哪来的参考文献？他说：“我\\cite了菜单。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "8f4ce41c713b", "text": "我见过一个人，写两页的部门备忘录，非要用LaTeX。不是不会Word，是觉得Word太俗，他图的是看上去像篇论文。他开Overleaf，写\\documentclass{article}，再\\usepackage{geometry}调页边距，又加个xeCJK调中文，第一次编译就报错，字体找不到，他查了一下午宏包版本。备忘录正文就一句话：周五下午三点开会，请带Q3数据。最后同事收到的PDF标题居中，页眉印着Memo，行距像拿尺子量过。老板回了一句：这个文件我改不了，能发个Word吗？他沉默了一会儿，删掉\\maketitle，把正文复制进了Word。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0704", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "stinky-tofu", "target_name": "臭豆腐", "facts": ["Tofu soaked for hours to a day or two in a brine that has itself fermented for months from vegetables such as bamboo shoots and mustard greens, often with dried shrimp or meat; the smell is the brine's, not the bean's.", "Changsha's version is black, deep-fried and doused in chili; Taiwan's night-market version is golden and served with pickled cabbage; Shaoxing's, brined with amaranth stalks, is fried pale yellow or steamed with other 'stinky' pickles.", "Legend credits Wang Zhihe, a Qing-era exam candidate who left his unsold tofu too long in 1669; a Beijing fermented-bean-curd brand still carries his name.", "Street vendors are its main outlet, and the smell carries for a block."], "a": {"item_id": "083ba71caee5", "text": "街边吃臭豆腐的人，我观察过，全是一个表情：一边嚼一边皱眉，一边皱眉一边跟你说“闻着臭，吃着香”。哥，这句话你每吃一块都要念一遍，说明你自己也不太信，你这是在给自己做法。\n\n图什么？图那个味儿。可那个味儿你根本不用吃啊，你从摊前路过三米，它已经替你吃过一遍了。\n\n最惨的是吃完。人家吃烤串，擦擦嘴就走；你吃完，嘴擦干净了，味儿还在加班。进电梯，一电梯人开始互相怀疑；回到家，你妈第一句不是“吃饭没”，是“你刚从哪个厕所出来”。\n\n八块钱，买了一身洗不掉的嫌疑。还给你浇了辣椒。"}, "b": {"item_id": "416c9c06dab4", "text": "吃街边臭豆腐的人，都有一句口头禅：\"闻着臭，吃着香。\"这哪是说豆腐，这是他们的人生总结，意思是：我知道我有问题，但你们别管。\n\n他们排四十分钟队，站在油锅旁边，边熏边等，像报了个自费烟熏疗程。拿到手一碗黑乎乎的东西，插着一根牙签，还得再淋勺辣酱，撒把香菜，仪式感拉满，吃的时候还不能坐，必须站在马路边，对着车尾气，吃得热泪盈眶。\n\n吃完以为结束了？它跟你回家了。头发入味，外套入味，围巾直接入股。进电梯，全楼的人齐刷刷看你，你还得假装是旁边那位的味。\n\n最惨的是，你自己闻不到了，因为你已经被腌透了。\n\n你就是那块臭豆腐，区别是，没人愿意为你排四十分钟的队。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0705", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hydrogen-cars", "target_name": "氢燃料电池汽车", "facts": ["Toyota launched the Mirai in 2014; 'mirai' means 'future' in Japanese.", "Nearly all US hydrogen filling stations are in California; Shell closed all seven of its California passenger-car stations in February 2024.", "More than 95% of the world's hydrogen is made from fossil fuels, mostly by steam-reforming natural gas.", "California pump prices reached about $36 per kilogram in 2023-24, over $200 for a Mirai fill; Toyota has handed buyers fuel cards worth $15,000.", "Mirai owners filed a class action against Toyota in July 2024 over hydrogen shortages and station outages; a second suit in 2025 sought at least $5.7 billion."], "a": {"item_id": "6147a8dd376d", "text": "买氢燃料电池车的人，我研究过，就两种：一种是环保人士，一种是被送了三年氢气卡的。\n\n环保人士图什么？图尾管只排水。可那氢多半是拿天然气炼的，他花四十块一公斤，绕一大圈烧天然气，最后从屁股后面滴出一杯水，像在表演“我很干净”。\n\n下场更精彩：全城三个加氢站，一个周二检修，一个在郊区，一个前面排着三辆公交。他的日常就是刷加氢站App，像抢演唱会门票。抢到了，还得等储氢罐压力回上来。\n\n最后氢气卡到期那天，车一秒贬值一半。他挂二手车，文案写得特别诚恳：“车况良好，自带情怀，不含氢气。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "31458abe94e2", "text": "有一种人专门买氢燃料电池车。他们的理由很宏大：我要为人类的未来开车。结果未来还没到，先到了加氢站门口。这种站一般开在郊区，像个地下党接头点，营业时间比银行还短，还得提前在App上抢号，跟抢演唱会门票一个难度。好不容易排上了，一公斤氢六七十块，加满一箱，司机心疼得直喊\"这不是加氢，是放血\"。最讽刺的是，这车一天到晚排出来的是水，环保是真环保，就是你的钱全变成了水。等到加氢站哪天悄悄拆了，车就停进车库，成了全国最贵的加湿器，每天擦一擦，等着哪天氢气自己从天上掉下来。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0706", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "excel", "target_name": "Microsoft Excel", "facts": ["First shipped for the Macintosh in 1985; the Windows version followed in 1987.", "Silently converts gene names such as SEPT2 and MARCH1 into dates; by August 2020 the HUGO Gene Nomenclature Committee had renamed 27 human genes (MARCH1 became MARCHF1, SEPT2 became SEPTIN2) and published guidelines in Nature Genetics to stop it.", "In October 2020, Public Health England lost 15,841 COVID-19 cases when a legacy .xls file hit its 65,536-row limit.", "Reinhart and Rogoff's 2010 paper 'Growth in a Time of Debt', cited for austerity policies, omitted five countries through a spreadsheet range error found by a graduate student in 2013.", "Sheets have been capped at 1,048,576 rows by 16,384 columns since Excel 2007."], "a": {"item_id": "bee6f959fe81", "text": "有种老板，用 Excel 管整个公司。图什么？图省钱，还觉得自己特别“数据驱动”。结果公司里权力最大的不是他，是“表哥”，全公司唯一看得懂那张表的人。表里 VLOOKUP 套了七层，没人敢动，谁动谁 #REF!。发工资那天，财务像拆炸弹。员工身份证号被自动变成 1.23E+17，十八位全军覆没。想打开文件，弹窗说：“已被张三锁定”。张三在三亚休年假。最后表哥一跳槽，老板才明白：自己不是在管公司，是在给一个人打工。而那个人，把工作表保护密码带走了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "18732d8f011b", "text": "我们老板管公司就靠一个Excel，文件名叫“总表_最终版_改3_千万别动.xlsx”。他图什么？图省钱，图“一切尽在掌握”。掌握到什么程度？他一打开，全公司点进去都弹“文件已锁定，只读”。\n\n他以为自己在经营企业，其实他在养一个VLOOKUP。财务、库存、工资，全靠第三列去匹配第七列。上个月实习生手欠插了一列，全公司工资齐刷刷变成#N/A，那是我们第一次见识零元购。\n\n最惨的是，会写那串套了十一层IF的会计离职了。老板对着那行公式看了三天，像考古队在研究甲骨文。现在公司还活着，但谁都不敢点“排序”。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0707", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "emacs", "target_name": "Emacs", "facts": ["Began in 1976 as a set of macros for the TECO editor at MIT's AI Lab, written by Richard Stallman and others; GNU Emacs followed in 1985.", "Extensible in its own Lisp dialect, Emacs Lisp; the standing joke is that it is a fine operating system lacking only a good editor.", "Ships with a psychotherapist (M-x doctor), Tetris, a mail client, a calendar, and a web browser (eww).", "An old backronym is 'Eight Megabytes And Constantly Swapping', from when 8 MB was a lot of memory.", "'Emacs pinky' is the name for the hand strain blamed on its Control-key chords."], "a": {"item_id": "edb43822f3d2", "text": "我有个朋友，把 Emacs 当操作系统用。邮件在里面收，网页在里面看，连聊天都在里面聊。我问他图啥，他说：“不用切窗口，专注。”专注？他的 init.el 五千行，每个周末固定修配置，修的是上周为了省事写的配置。最绝的是，他最后装了 evil-mode，就是在 Emacs 里模拟 Vim 的按键。折腾十年，把全世界装进 Emacs，最后为了好用，把宿敌请了回来。现在他小指已经废了，他管这叫“Emacs 小指”，语气跟领了勋章似的。我说哥们，你这不是在用操作系统，是在给操作系统打工，还是不发工资的那种。"}, "b": {"item_id": "f945ff696b4a", "text": "把 Emacs 当操作系统用的人，我见过。早上在 Emacs 里收邮件，中午在 org-mode 里排日程，晚上在 Emacs 里听音乐、写日记、看股票。他管这叫\"一站式工作流\"。\n\n有天他想关机，按了 C-x C-c，屏幕跳出一行字：\"Modified buffers exist; exit anyway? (yes or no)\"。他心想我又没改什么，一看，十四个 buffer 没保存，其中一个是他写了三年的日记。\n\n他盯着屏幕想了半分钟，最后打出了 yes。然后他意识到，这个操作系统唯一会问他要不要关机的时候，也是唯一不让他关机的时候。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0708", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "macau", "target_name": "澳门", "facts": ["A Portuguese settlement from the 1550s until the handover to China in December 1999.", "Casino revenue passed the Las Vegas Strip's in 2006, and at its 2013 peak, about $45 billion, was roughly seven times the Strip's.", "One company, STDM, held the casino monopoly for four decades until licences opened to competition in 2002.", "Among the most densely populated places on Earth, at roughly 20,000 people per square kilometre.", "The Cotai Strip stands on land reclaimed from the sea between the islands of Taipa and Coloane."], "a": {"item_id": "56ba73fdce82", "text": "去澳门翻本的人，基本都是在内地被套急了的。股票亏了三十万，跟老婆说：“我去澳门，一把就回来。”一把！你在A股被按着打了三年，指望在百家乐桌上三分钟补回来？你这不叫翻本，你是去给澳门送第二茬本。\n\n他们还特别有原则：赢回三十万就走。结果赢到二十九万，心想，差一万，凑个整。威尼斯人里那片假天空永远是下午四点，你输到凌晨，抬头天还亮着，你以为自己才来一会儿。\n\n下场也统一：先当手表，赌场旁边那家当铺，老板比你老婆还了解你。最后口袋剩二十块，在大三巴买个蛋挞，边吃边发呆。\n\n回关闸的免费大巴上，一路没人说话。因为来的时候喊“一把回本”的那几个人，现在全在这辆车上。"}, "b": {"item_id": "09aa41e46e5f", "text": "有种人我太熟了：上个月在澳门输了两万，发誓\"这次必须翻本\"。他怎么去的？早上七点坐大巴过拱北，在威尼斯人门口排队换筹码，图的就是那两万块能\"回家\"。结果他在百家乐桌前坐了一下午，翻本的钱全输给了赌场的冷气。傍晚他坐在大三巴牌坊底下啃葡挞，跟鸽子商量下个月的房租。你说他傻吗？不傻，他还算了账，每一笔都算得很认真。问题是，账算得再认真，庄家也是按规矩赢的。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0709", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "windows-8", "target_name": "Windows 8", "facts": ["Released in October 2012; removed the Start button and booted into a full-screen grid of tiles designed for touch screens most PCs did not have.", "The 8.1 update in 2013 put a Start button back, Windows 10 (2015) restored the Start menu, and Microsoft skipped the name Windows 9 altogether.", "Microsoft dropped the 'Metro' name in August 2012 after reports that German retailer Metro AG had objected on trademark grounds; Microsoft called 'Metro' a code name and denied any litigation, and the style was afterwards known informally as 'Modern UI'.", "The 'Charms bar' hid Search, Share, and Settings behind an invisible swipe from the right edge.", "Windows RT, the ARM edition launched alongside it, could not run ordinary Windows programs; Microsoft wrote down $900 million of unsold Surface RT tablets in 2013."], "a": {"item_id": "825a78e26940", "text": "给台式机装Windows 8的人，我太了解了。这种人，朋友聚会必说一句：\"我这个是尝鲜。\"尝鲜，你家台式机是个五斤重的铁箱子，插着鼠标键盘，哪来的\"鲜\"？\n\n图什么呢？图开机快，四秒。是，四秒就进去了，进去以后花四十分钟找关机。\n\n开始按钮没了，换成一整屏磁贴，天气、股票、应用商店，全对着你闪。他们对着显示器伸手去戳，显示器说：\"大哥，我不是触屏。\"\n\n关机更绝。鼠标得甩到右上角，等超级按钮栏冒出来，点设置，点电源，才能关。关个机像密室逃脱。\n\n下场呢？他们最后都去搜\"Windows 8 怎么把开始菜单弄回来\"，下载个第三方软件，把微软删掉的东西，亲手装回去。\n\n等到8.1出了，开始按钮回来了，他们热泪盈眶，一点：跳到磁贴屏。\n\n那不是按钮，那是个门铃，按了还是那帮磁贴来开门。"}, "b": {"item_id": "4dccc16b3e4c", "text": "我表哥就是那种人。2012年他给我家台式机装了Windows 8，理由就一句：“新的肯定好。”好什么？这系统是给平板设计的，满屏方块，你拿鼠标去戳，像拿筷子按电梯。开始按钮没了，他说不需要：“鼠标往右上角一甩，侧边栏就出来了。”我爸用了十年XP，现在为了关个机，得拿鼠标在屏幕边上来回招魂。最后我爸的关机方式改成了拔插头。表哥现在每周日被叫回来，不是吃饭，是找“我的电脑”去哪儿了。他还嘴硬：“等8.1就好了。”8.1来了，开始按钮真回来了，一点开，还是那屏方块。微软都认错了，他还没认。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0710", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "microsoft-word", "target_name": "Microsoft Word", "facts": ["Launched in 1983 as 'Multi-Tool Word' for Xenix and MS-DOS.", "Clippy, the animated paperclip assistant, arrived in Office 97 and was removed in Office 2007; Microsoft itself ran ads mocking it in 2001.", "Its .docx format, Office Open XML, was fast-tracked to ISO standard 29500 in 2008 as a roughly 6,000-page specification, amid accusations that national committees had been packed.", "Older spell checkers, Word's included, offered 'Cupertino' as a correction for a mistyped 'cooperation', and the resulting 'Cupertino effect' made it into published NATO, EU and UN-agency documents."], "a": {"item_id": "b60d316d9566", "text": "有些同学非要用Word排毕业论文，图什么？图省事。学校要求\"标题三号黑体、正文小四宋体\"，他觉得点几下就能搞定。结果一章标题用了普通段落样式，目录里直接没有它；页眉一改，前面十页跟着变，\"链接到前一节\"这五个字他看了一整晚。最后目录页码差了三页，他按F9更新域，Word回他一句\"错误！未定义书签\"。你不是在写论文，你是在跟一个分节符谈恋爱，谈崩了它也不告诉你为什么。"}, "b": {"item_id": "74d77985a3e9", "text": "用Word排毕业论文的人，都信一句话：“所见即所得。”结果你看见的是第38页，导师打开是第41页，图还跑到上一章去了。\n\n这帮人从不用“样式”，标题全靠手动选中、加粗、调三号。目录也是手敲的，后面那一排点，是空格键一个一个敲出来的。\n\n答辩前一晚，导师说加一句话。他在第二章敲了个句号，整篇论文像多米诺骨牌，参考文献直接顶到封面上。\n\n他想设页码，摘要用罗马数字，正文用阿拉伯数字，找分节符找了三小时，最后决定：摘要不要页码了。\n\n文件名叫“终稿_改3_真的终稿_导师改后_最终.docx”。\n\n最后交上去的是PDF。这是他一辈子，第一次让论文别动。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0711", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hsq", "target_name": "HSQ 电子束光刻胶", "facts": ["Hydrogen silsesquioxane, sold by Dow Corning as FOx ('flowable oxide') and designed as a spin-on dielectric for chip interconnects; lithographers adopted it as a negative-tone resist.", "Exposure converts its cage structure into a cross-linked, silica-like network, so the developed pattern is essentially SiO2.", "Holds the resolution records for electron-beam lithography: isolated features around 5 nm, dense lines below 10 nm half-pitch, with high-contrast 'salty' NaOH/NaCl development.", "Must be kept refrigerated (Dow Corning's datasheet gives a six-month shelf life at 5 °C); the solution ages into a jelly, and its effective sensitivity shifts with processing delays because cross-linking continues after exposure.", "DuPont, which inherited Dow Corning's FOx/XR-1541 HSQ line, discontinued it and ended production at the end of 2025; labs such as EPFL's CMi switched to third-party HSQ from EM Resist or DisChem, both formulated from AQM powder."], "a": {"item_id": "e6a9d412465a", "text": "有人为了分辨率，把 PMMA 换成 HSQ。我就想问，你图啥？PMMA 便宜、好剥离、导师不骂你。HSQ 呢？得放冰箱，一开瓶就倒计时，跟生鲜外卖一个待遇。剂量要几千，写一小块图形，电子束扫一整夜，你在机房陪着，比陪对象都久。早上显影，一根 8 纳米的线，倒了，跟隔壁那根粘一块了。再来一轮，终于立住了，你想把多余的去掉？不好意思，曝光完它就是二氧化硅，想洗掉得上氢氟酸，连你的器件一块洗。最后论文里写“sub-10 nm”，那是三十张电镜图里挑出来的那一张，剩下二十九张，叫 preliminary data。"}, "b": {"item_id": "164876412c34", "text": "我们实验室有种人，组会上一听PMMA分辨率不够，当场就要换HSQ。图什么？图SEM照片里那根8纳米的线，能放进论文Figure 1。\n\n结果呢？HSQ灵敏度低，剂量往死里加。别人写一片半小时，他写一整晚，机时表上全是他的名字，像给电子束机守灵。\n\n显影还得用加盐的TMAH。人家做光刻，他在腌咸菜。\n\n最绝的是，这胶一曝光就变成二氧化硅。你辛辛苦苦图形化的不是胶，是玻璃。想去掉？请上氢氟酸。\n\n哦对，那瓶HSQ还得放冰箱，几个月就凝胶。最后他毕业延期了，胶先过期了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0712", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "oat-milk", "target_name": "燕麦奶", "facts": ["Oatly started in Sweden in the 1990s, built on Lund University research by Rickard Öste on lactose intolerance.", "Oatly's slogan 'It's like milk but made for humans' was banned in Sweden in 2015 after the dairy lobby LRF Mjölk sued; Oatly then published the lawsuit as advertising.", "Oatly listed on Nasdaq in May 2021 at a valuation near $10 billion; the shares later lost more than 90% of their value.", "Commercial oat milk is made by breaking oat starch into maltose with amylase enzymes, and many brands, Oatly among them, add rapeseed oil; the result has more sugar and a higher glycaemic index than almond or soy milk."], "a": {"item_id": "6af69cc6ef40", "text": "我发现点燕麦拿铁的人有个共同点：开口前先叹气。“不好意思，换燕麦奶，我乳糖不耐。”你不耐？上周火锅店你一个人干了三个冰淇淋球。\n\n他们图什么？图健康。可燕麦奶为了打出奶泡，要往里加植物油；为了喝着甜，得把燕麦酶解成麦芽糖。你多掏五块钱，买了一杯稀释燕麦粥，外加一勺油。\n\n图环保？说燕麦比奶牛省水。你省下那点水，转头就用纸巾擦了八遍杯口。\n\n下场呢？体检血糖偏高。医生问你早饭吃什么，你说只喝咖啡。医生摇头：“不对，你这是每天早上喝两碗粥，还嫌粥稀，花钱让人给你打了个泡。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "f5f4ee009b3f", "text": "每个咖啡店都有这么一位，开口就是：\"拿铁，换燕麦奶。\"语气像在宣布退出某个组织。你问为什么，他说乳糖不耐。兄弟，你手边那块芝士蛋糕是自己长出来的吗？你这乳糖不耐很精准，只对咖啡里的牛奶不耐。\n\n再问，他说为了健康。燕麦奶，燕麦煮开后淀粉变成糖，热量比牛奶还高。所以他多花五块钱，多摄入一点糖，喝的是一杯稀释过的麦片粥汤，然后发朋友圈：\"今天也是植物基的一天。\"\n\n下场呢？咖啡一入口，满嘴湿麦片味，像早饭的碗没洗干净，被人顺手拿来接了咖啡。他还硬喝完，边喝边说：\"还挺顺滑的。\"\n\n对，是顺滑，因为你正在喝粥。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0713", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "nuclear-fusion", "target_name": "可控核聚变", "facts": ["Has been 'about 30 years away' since the 1950s; at the 1955 Geneva atoms-for-peace conference, Homi Bhabha predicted fusion power within two decades.", "ITER, agreed in 2006 for first plasma in 2016, re-baselined in 2024 to start research operations in 2034 and deuterium-tritium burning in 2039, at a cost estimated above €20 billion.", "NIF's December 2022 'ignition' shot released 3.15 MJ from 2.05 MJ of laser energy; the lasers drew about 300 MJ from the grid to fire.", "Private fusion companies had raised more than $7 billion by 2024."], "a": {"item_id": "0c1b7366a47b", "text": "搞可控核聚变的那帮人，我真佩服。每次申请经费，他们都拍着胸脯说：再给十年，就能把太阳装进罐子里。这话从上世纪五十年代说到现在，太阳没装进去，罐子倒是越造越大。ITER画图画了三十多年，工期一延再延，预算翻了好几倍，钱烧得比等离子体还热。你问他们图什么？图的是海水里那点氘，图的是一个不冒烟的未来。落得什么下场？当年的博士生，如今成了白头发的教授，还在给学生画同一张饼：就差最后一公里了。这最后一公里，比太阳的寿命还长。"}, "b": {"item_id": "a3d17d6a7412", "text": "搞可控核聚变的人，是地球上唯一靠“还有十年”吃了一辈子饭的职业。五十年代说三十年，后来说五十年，现在说十年，你看，人家还在降价。\n\n图啥？图经费。“十年”这个数太精妙了：近到评审愿意批钱，远到项目负责人退休前不用验收。ITER在法国盖了快二十年，预算翻了好几倍。一亿度的等离子体他们说能控住，控不住的是工期。\n\n最后什么下场？老教授退休那天，把“还有十年”郑重交给博士生，跟传家宝似的。博士生一看：我二十五岁入行，原来是接了个祖传手艺。\n\n这炉子烧的不是氘和氚，是一代代人的头发。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0714", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "php", "target_name": "PHP", "facts": ["Created by Rasmus Lerdorf in 1994 as 'Personal Home Page Tools', a set of C programs for tracking visitors to his own résumé page; the name now officially stands for the recursive 'PHP: Hypertext Preprocessor'.", "Lerdorf, in his 2007 MySQL Conference talk 'PHP on Hormones': 'I'm not a real programmer. I throw together things until it works then I move on.'", "Runs WordPress, which W3Techs counted on 39.4% of all websites (58.1% of sites with a known CMS) on 9 November 2026.", "Core function names are inconsistent (strpos, str_replace, htmlspecialchars, nl2br) partly because, as Lerdorf confirmed, early function names were chosen to balance a hash table keyed on string length.", "Until PHP 8 (2020), the comparison 0 == 'foo' was true because the string was silently converted to a number."], "a": {"item_id": "8dd77ec7826a", "text": "我有个朋友，靠写PHP吃饭。你问他干啥的，他第一句永远是“PHP是世界上最好的语言”，说完自己先笑。这是他这行唯一的笑点，也是唯一的尊严。\n\n他图啥？图门槛低。2010年自学三天，用织梦给楼下火锅店搭了个网站，收八百，觉得自己要财务自由了。十五年过去，他还在搭站，还是八百。火锅店倒了，现在接的是美甲店。\n\n他的工作流程：FileZilla连上服务器，直接改线上文件，刷新，白屏；再改，再刷新，又白屏。他管这叫“敏捷开发”。\n\n他代码里全是美元符号，一个变量一个$，写了一辈子$，就没见过几张。\n\n最近他焦虑，说PHP要死了。我说你放心，它死不了，全世界四成网站还挂在WordPress上呢。会死的是你。"}, "b": {"item_id": "bcc6af302235", "text": "写PHP的人，我一直很佩服。这门语言每个变量前面都要加个美元符号：$name、$age、$salary。写了十年，满屏都是$，工资卡里一个都没见着。\n\n他们当初为什么入行？因为好上手。培训班三个月，出来就接单，接的全是老板表弟的商城、装修公司的官网。入行时都背过那句\"PHP是世界上最好的语言\"，十年过去，这话喊得最响的，永远是正在改前任祖传代码的人。\n\n下场更惨。每年都有人宣布PHP死了，他们不反驳，因为手上那个WordPress插件，作者三年前就跑路了。面试官问：\"你还在写PHP啊？\"他们只能说：\"我们现在升到8了，支持类型了。\"\n\n这就像有人说：我家一直漏雨，但是现在，我们有伞了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0715", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "almond-milk", "target_name": "杏仁奶", "facts": ["California grows about 80% of the world's almonds.", "A single almond takes about 1.1 US gallons (roughly 4 litres) of water to grow, a figure Mother Jones published in 2014 that spread during California's drought.", "A 2015 US class action alleged Almond Breeze contained only about 2% almonds; it partly survived a motion to dismiss, was stayed in 2017 pending a separate Blue Diamond labelling settlement, and the plaintiffs then dropped it.", "Pollinating California's almond orchards each February requires trucking in most of the commercial honeybee colonies in the United States.", "Medieval European cooks used almond milk as a Lenten substitute for dairy, centuries before the current fashion."], "a": {"item_id": "7d9582e4c68c", "text": "我有个同事，为了救地球，牛奶戒了，改喝杏仁奶。天天端着杯子说：“我在减少碳排放。”\n\n挺好。就是我查了一下：一颗杏仁，得喝掉好几升水。加州旱成那样，杏仁树还在那儿咕嘟咕嘟地喝。\n\n更绝的是他那盒杏仁奶，配料表上杏仁只占百分之二，剩下全是水。\n\n也就是说，他花三十块买了一盒“泡过杏仁的水”，那几颗杏仁还先喝光了一个村的水。他不是在救地球，他是在帮加州倒腾水。\n\n哦对，杏仁开花全靠租来的蜜蜂授粉，每年一季下来，蜜蜂成片成片地死。\n\n他现在特骄傲：“我从不碰动物制品。”\n\n兄弟，蜜蜂不算动物？"}, "b": {"item_id": "8cd05311eac8", "text": "我朋友为了环保戒了牛奶，改喝杏仁奶，说牛奶太费水。我说你知道一颗杏仁要喝四升水吗？加州的杏仁树比加州人还能喝。而且杏仁奶里杏仁只占百分之二，你花三十八块，买了一盒百分之九十八的水，外加一点杏仁味的遗憾。\n\n更绝的是，杏仁树要授粉，每年得用卡车把全美国的蜜蜂拉去加州打工，累死一大片。他不想伤害奶牛，结果当上了蜜蜂的包工头。\n\n最后咖啡一倒进去，絮状分层，像一场小型水土流失。他还端着杯子庄严地说：“我在拯救地球。”\n\n哥们，地球没让你救，它就想让你把这杯喝完，别发朋友圈。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0716", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "crocs", "target_name": "Crocs 洞洞鞋", "facts": ["Founded in Boulder, Colorado, in 2002; the first clog debuted at the Fort Lauderdale boat show as a boating shoe.", "Made of 'Croslite', which Crocs describes as a proprietary closed-cell resin foam and which third parties identify as an injection-moulded EVA foam.", "Balenciaga sold a platform Crocs for about $850 in 2018.", "The stock fell more than 90% from its 2007 peak during the 2008 crisis; the company then boomed in the pandemic and gave away 860,000 pairs to healthcare workers in 2020.", "The holes are officially 'ventilation ports' and the charms that go in them are 'Jibbitz', a company Crocs bought in 2006."], "a": {"item_id": "c92c7ce6304b", "text": "我发现穿洞洞鞋出门的人，都有一套标准说辞：“丑是丑，但舒服啊。”舒服的东西多了，你怎么不裹着被窝去上班？\n\n嘴上说图舒服，转头往鞋洞里插满鞋花：十二只小恐龙、三块披萨、一个奥特曼。鞋八十，花四百。你这不是穿鞋，是脚上开了个小卖部。\n\n后跟那根带子，平时翻在脚背上叫“休闲模式”，一跑起来立马扳下去，叫“运动模式”。全身上下，就这根带子有档位。\n\n下场也很统一：一下雨，每个洞都是进水口，走一步“噗叽”一声；天一晴，一颗小石子钻进洞里怎么都甩不出来，只能当街单脚跳，像只刚学走路的火烈鸟。\n\n图舒服的人，最后全蹲在路边倒石子。"}, "b": {"item_id": "15501877150c", "text": "你们见过那种穿卡骆驰洞洞鞋出门的人吗？他们的理由永远是“舒服”。舒服到什么程度？舒服到能穿着它去相亲，对方低头一看，两排塑料坑，脚趾在里面像泡温泉。他图什么？图不用系鞋带，图能下水、能进厨房、能上超市，等于把拖鞋、雨鞋、运动鞋一起省了。结果呢？去趟公园，脚底的洞灌进三粒沙子，一步一个噗嗤，全场都知道他来了。最后鞋帮上挂满小挂件，走起路来叮叮当当，像个移动的钥匙串，他还在朋友圈写：这才是生活的本质。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0717", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "beanie-babies", "target_name": "Beanie Babies 豆豆娃", "facts": ["Launched by Ty Inc. in 1993 and sold only through small gift shops, with designs 'retired' to manufacture scarcity.", "At the height of the craze Beanie Babies accounted for about 10% of eBay's sales, and eBay's 1998 IPO filing said the site at times had over 30,000 Beanie Baby auctions running simultaneously in the quarter ending June 1998.", "In 1999 a divorcing couple divided their Beanie Baby collection one by one on a courtroom floor, in a photograph that ran worldwide.", "In 1999 Ty announced that Beanie Babies would be retired at the end of the year, then reversed itself after asking the public to vote and seeing an overwhelming vote to keep them.", "Most 'rare' Beanies that price guides valued in the thousands now sell for a few dollars."], "a": {"item_id": "a30c3de668a8", "text": "我表舅九十年代一口气囤了三百多只豆豆娃，每只屁股上的小吊牌都套三层塑料袋，连摸都不让摸。他说这叫抗通胀。后来厂家宣布\"退休\"，他激动得三天没睡，觉得自己是股神。结果呢？退休那批，现在二手平台一只八块包邮，买家还嫌吊牌有折痕。他家客厅的柜子搬到了阁楼，女儿问那是什么，他说：\"这是我的养老金。\"女儿说：\"爸，养老金能当饭吃吗？\""}, "b": {"item_id": "e9aa38d24ee4", "text": "九十年代有一批人，觉得自己找到了下一个华尔街：一只塞满塑料粒的小熊。他们不看财报，看那颗红色心形吊牌。吊牌折个角，在他们眼里跟股市崩盘一样。孩子想抱一下？不行！先给吊牌套上塑料保护壳。宝贝，你的大学学费就在这只紫色戴安娜王妃熊身上，别流口水。Ty公司一宣布某款“退休”，他们连夜开车扫三家礼品店，比自己亲爹退休还上心。结果呢？拉斯维加斯真有一对夫妻离婚，法官让他们蹲在法庭地板上，一只一只轮流挑豆豆娃。这就是你的资产配置：二十年后，唯一升值的是你儿子的心理咨询费。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0718", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "pmma", "target_name": "PMMA 电子束光刻胶", "facts": ["The same polymer as Plexiglas and acrylic glass, dissolved in anisole or chlorobenzene and spun into a thin film.", "The oldest electron-beam resist still in everyday use; IBM researchers reported it as one in the late 1960s.", "Positive tone: the beam breaks polymer chains and the exposed region dissolves in a MIBK:IPA developer.", "Resolves below 10 nm but has poor sensitivity, so doses of hundreds to over a thousand µC/cm² (depending on voltage) make writing slow.", "Poor dry-etch resistance, so it is used mostly for lift-off rather than as an etch mask."], "a": {"item_id": "71032dd5e938", "text": "做电子束的人，你得理解，他们是一群对新材料过敏的人。你问为什么还在用PMMA，他一脸骄傲：“分辨率高，十纳米以下！”\n\n对，这玩意儿六十年代的，跟你家鱼缸、亚克力牙托是亲戚。他们拿牙托在刻十纳米。\n\n图什么？图稳。师兄留下的配方：950K，A4，4000转，180度烘三分钟，MIBK兑IPA一比三显影。没人知道为什么，但谁改谁延毕。\n\n下场呢？曝光剂量几百微库每平方厘米，一个图案写一晚上。第二天一看，束漂了，套刻对不上。好不容易写完去刻蚀，PMMA一见等离子体就没了，像纸糊的盾牌。\n\n最后博士五年读六年，论文里还得写：“PMMA以高分辨率著称。”\n\n是啊，胶是分辨率高、灵敏度低，人也是。对新材料，毫无反应。"}, "b": {"item_id": "90a70ba10fe5", "text": "我有个哥们读纳米加工博士，电子束光刻到今天还用PMMA。PMMA是啥？有机玻璃。奶茶店拿它做招牌，他拿它做量子器件。为啥不换？导师说配方是师爷九十年代调的：950K叠495K，MIBK兑IPA一比三，显影四十五秒，一秒别动。图啥？便宜、稳，论文写一句\"standard PMMA process\"，审稿人都不问。代价是灵敏度低得像我爸回微信，一片芯片曝一整夜，机时按小时收钱，他就睡在机房门口。剂量给多了还交联，正胶活成负胶。最后剥离，丙酮泡一宿，金还粘着，一上超声，金掉了，片子也碎了。他捧着碎片说：\"至少线宽十纳米。\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0719", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "slack", "target_name": "Slack", "facts": ["Spun out in 2013 from the internal chat tool of Tiny Speck's failed game Glitch; founder Stewart Butterfield had earlier spun Flickr out of another failed game.", "The name is a backronym: 'Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge'.", "Salesforce bought it for $27.7 billion in 2021.", "Filed an EU antitrust complaint against Microsoft in 2020 over the bundling of Teams with Office."], "a": {"item_id": "9ef25649a571", "text": "有一种老板，管公司全靠Slack。他图什么？图那种指挥作战的感觉。半夜两点在#general里@channel，三百个人的手机同时“咚”一声，他躺床上听着，跟听礼炮似的。\n\n他不开会，他回“👀”。你熬一礼拜的方案，换来一个眼睛表情，啥意思没人知道，你自己悟。\n\n他考勤看绿点。谁头像一灰，私信立马弹出一个“？”。于是全员网购了鼠标晃动器，公司最勤奋的员工，是十九块九包邮的小马达。\n\n下场呢？所有决策都埋在某条thread的第四十七楼，谁也翻不着。免费版只存九十天，这家公司的战略每三个月自动清空一次。他还挺得意：“看，我们多敏捷。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "b9535814ba64", "text": "有种老板，花三个月把公司搬到 Slack 上，说要\"扁平化、透明化、告别会议\"。结果建了四十七个频道：#general 没人说话，#random 全是老板自己转的领英鸡汤，#wins 里常年只有一条消息：\"我们建了 #wins\"。\n\n会议确实少了，因为全改成 huddle 了。那个小耳机图标一亮，意思是\"我们在聊，你怎么还不进来\"。\n\n员工回老板消息，只剩一个👀。老板还挺感动：\"看，大家都在关注。\"\n\n最绝的是他舍不得买付费版。九十天前的消息自动隐藏，上季度那个五百万的决定是谁拍的板，没人查得到。\n\n公司最后留下的唯一透明，是那行字：\"升级到专业版，查看更早的消息。\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0720", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "ugg", "target_name": "UGG 雪地靴", "facts": ["Sheepskin boots Australian surfers wore to warm up after sessions; Brian Smith started selling them in California in 1978.", "Deckers Outdoor bought the brand in 1995 and holds 'UGG' as a trademark in the US and elsewhere; in Australia, 'ugg' is a generic word any bootmaker may use.", "In 2019, a US jury found an Australian maker liable for selling boots labelled 'ugg' into the United States.", "Oprah Winfrey featured them in her 'Favorite Things' lists repeatedly in the 2000s, which drove the first boom.", "UGG is Deckers' largest brand, with over $2 billion in annual sales."], "a": {"item_id": "3ebab0e81cc7", "text": "你们见过夏天三十八度还穿UGG的人吧？两只毛茸茸的大头，像刚从羊身上薅下来、还没下班的小动物。他们图什么？图一个\"我早就入冬了\"的仪式感。结果呢？走三百米，羊皮里子把脚焖成一锅卤鸭掌，袜子都不用穿，直接在桑拿房里逛街。到了十月，那层软塌塌的鞋底已经磨出了地图。最惨的是羊毛里子被汗压成一块毡，踩在地铁台阶上，像踩着两块泡过水的抹布。朋友圈里配文\"今天也很暖\"，照片里那双栗色的靴子，已经晒成了一条咸鱼。"}, "b": {"item_id": "291bb4a33621", "text": "一年四季穿UGG的人，你仔细看，基本是同一种人：早上说“我就下楼拿个快递”，结果一路拿到了公司。\n\n问他图什么，他说：“不用系鞋带，一脚蹬。”对，UGG不是穿，是给脚办入住。\n\n夏天三十八度他还穿，问就是“里面是羊毛，透气”。对，羊也这么说，然后被剃了。\n\n下场特别统一。一场雨，一千多的靴子泡成两块发面馒头。到了办公室一脱鞋，羊毛负责把你全年的经历保存下来，同事都不用问你中午吃了啥。\n\n最惨的是冬天。你坚持了三季，终于等到主场，结果满大街都在穿，你的“个性”直接变成了“合群”。\n\n你说你这一年图什么？图了个脚，被焖成了刚出锅的馒头。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0721", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hsq", "target_name": "HSQ 电子束光刻胶", "facts": ["Hydrogen silsesquioxane, sold by Dow Corning as FOx ('flowable oxide') and designed as a spin-on dielectric for chip interconnects; lithographers adopted it as a negative-tone resist.", "Exposure converts its cage structure into a cross-linked, silica-like network, so the developed pattern is essentially SiO2.", "Holds the resolution records for electron-beam lithography: isolated features around 5 nm, dense lines below 10 nm half-pitch, with high-contrast 'salty' NaOH/NaCl development.", "Must be kept refrigerated (Dow Corning's datasheet gives a six-month shelf life at 5 °C); the solution ages into a jelly, and its effective sensitivity shifts with processing delays because cross-linking continues after exposure.", "DuPont, which inherited Dow Corning's FOx/XR-1541 HSQ line, discontinued it and ended production at the end of 2025; labs such as EPFL's CMi switched to third-party HSQ from EM Resist or DisChem, both formulated from AQM powder."], "a": {"item_id": "53375ff05c30", "text": "做纳米加工的博士生都有一个信仰：PMMA 分辨率不够，换 HSQ。换完你才发现，这不是换胶，是领养了一个早产儿。\n\n它得在冰箱里供着，用之前先回温，像伺候祖宗。旋完胶它就开始老化，你俩的关系是\"每天都不一样\"。显影用 25% 的 TMAH，有人为了对比度还往里加盐。头一回听说做精密加工往显影液里放盐的，厨子都没这么讲究。\n\n灵敏度还低，电子束机排队三个星期，一个小图案写一整晚。好不容易写出 8 纳米的线，一显影，塌了，粘一块儿，跟煮过头的面一样。\n\n就算没塌，HSQ 曝光完变成二氧化硅，洗不掉，永远留在你的器件上。你图啥？图论文里一张 SEM 图。导师看完说：\"挺好，再做个大面积的。\""}, "b": {"item_id": "164876412c34", "text": "我们实验室有种人，组会上一听PMMA分辨率不够，当场就要换HSQ。图什么？图SEM照片里那根8纳米的线，能放进论文Figure 1。\n\n结果呢？HSQ灵敏度低，剂量往死里加。别人写一片半小时，他写一整晚，机时表上全是他的名字，像给电子束机守灵。\n\n显影还得用加盐的TMAH。人家做光刻，他在腌咸菜。\n\n最绝的是，这胶一曝光就变成二氧化硅。你辛辛苦苦图形化的不是胶，是玻璃。想去掉？请上氢氟酸。\n\n哦对，那瓶HSQ还得放冰箱，几个月就凝胶。最后他毕业延期了，胶先过期了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0722", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hydrogen-cars", "target_name": "氢燃料电池汽车", "facts": ["Toyota launched the Mirai in 2014; 'mirai' means 'future' in Japanese.", "Nearly all US hydrogen filling stations are in California; Shell closed all seven of its California passenger-car stations in February 2024.", "More than 95% of the world's hydrogen is made from fossil fuels, mostly by steam-reforming natural gas.", "California pump prices reached about $36 per kilogram in 2023-24, over $200 for a Mirai fill; Toyota has handed buyers fuel cards worth $15,000.", "Mirai owners filed a class action against Toyota in July 2024 over hydrogen shortages and station outages; a second suit in 2025 sought at least $5.7 billion."], "a": {"item_id": "a7c3aeb313ce", "text": "我朋友非要买氢燃料电池车，说排出来的是水，环保。我说对，排的是水，你的钱也是一滴一滴没的，看不见。\n\n买这车的人分两种，一种是科技宅，觉得自己活在电影里；另一种是单位采购，花的是补贴，车是别人的。他图什么？图那句\"我开的是未来\"。结果未来在哪？在郊区一座加氢站，得提前预约，排队排到怀疑人生。站一关门，车就成了铁疙瘩，电堆里那点铂催化剂，比他的存款还金贵。\n\n最绝的是氢分子最小，能从70兆帕的罐子里漏出去。他的工资也一样，还没捂热，就从卡里漏没了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "c99d709097af", "text": "买氢燃料电池车的人，我研究过，就三种。\n\n第一种，环保人士。张口就是：“我这车尾气只有水。”兄弟，你这氢大部分是天然气炼的，污染在加氢站上游排完了，你在这儿排水。这就好比饭店说自己不产生垃圾，因为厨房在隔壁街。\n\n第二种，被销售一句“送三年氢气”忽悠来的。图的是免费。结果全市两座加氢站，一座检修，一座在隔壁区。他现在周末的娱乐活动就是去加氢，导航到了，门口贴着：“设备维护，复工时间待定”。\n\n下场更惨。氢气卡还没用完，加氢站先倒闭了。几十万的车，停小区里，邻居问：“这啥车？”他说：“氢的。”邻居点点头：“哦，那你现在确实挺空的。”\n\n最后，他开着车唯一的排放物，去浇了阳台那盆绿萝。他说：“至少我这车，能养花。”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0723", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "vim", "target_name": "Vim", "facts": ["Released by Bram Moolenaar in November 1991 as 'Vi IMitation', a vi clone he had begun writing for the Amiga in 1988 on top of Stevie; the expansion became 'Vi IMproved' with version 2.0 in December 1993.", "Licensed as charityware: the licence asks users to donate to ICCF Holland, a charity Moolenaar ran for children in Uganda.", "Stack Overflow's question 'How to exit the Vim editor?' passed one million visits in May 2017, which Stack Overflow marked with a blog post.", "Modal editing: in normal mode, typing letters runs commands rather than inserting text; :wq saves and quits, :q! quits and discards changes."], "a": {"item_id": "ca805bf868dc", "text": "我们组有个哥们儿用Vim。你怎么知道的？你不用知道，他会告诉你。开会共享屏幕，需求还没讲，先把鼠标往旁边一推：“我不用这个。”大哥，你推的是公司资产。\n\n他的.vimrc八百行，四十个插件：文件树、自动补全、调试器。我一看，这不就是VS Code吗？他说不一样，这是他亲手配的。哦，花三个月，手工拼了个免费软件。\n\n上礼拜他在服务器上改配置，突然不动了。全组盯着他，他说“我在思考”。思考了五分钟。后来才知道，那台机器上没有他的.vimrc。他进去了，出不来。\n\n现在他一紧张就按Esc。不是习惯，是创伤。"}, "b": {"item_id": "90b37ac793f9", "text": "有种人用 Vim 写代码，还非得让全世界知道。你问他用什么编辑器，他不直接说，停半秒，吐出两个字：\"Vim。\"那语气，像刚从战场上下来。他图什么？图你觉得他厉害。可你看他干活：左手按 ESC，右手敲 dd、yy、p，屏幕上东西一会儿没了，一会儿又冒出来，三个函数已经搬到别的文件里了，你连他光标在哪都没看清。同事问他怎么不用 IDE，他说：\"我在普通模式，你不懂。\"后来服务器出事，他被叫去救火，刚坐下就在群里喊：\"等我先退出一下。\"然后他对着屏幕敲了二十分钟 :q，纹丝不动，最后爬出来，第一句话是：\"谁能告诉我记事本怎么用？\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0724", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "nfts", "target_name": "NFT", "facts": ["Beeple's 'Everydays: The First 5000 Days' sold at Christie's for $69.3 million in March 2021.", "OpenSea, the largest NFT marketplace, processed a record $2.7 billion in trades on 1 May 2022 and just $9.34 million on 28 August 2022, a 99% drop, according to DappRadar data.", "A 2023 dappGambl study found that 95% of the NFT collections it examined had a market value of zero.", "The token usually records only a URL; the image itself sits on an ordinary server or IPFS, not on the blockchain.", "Collins Dictionary named 'NFT' its word of the year for 2021."], "a": {"item_id": "3a567b5377a2", "text": "2021年买NFT的朋友，我真佩服。花三十万买张猴子图，猴子叼着烟、一脸不想活，这是在提前预演自己的下场。\n\n你跟他说：“我右键另存为了啊。”他急了：“你存的是图，我拥有的是链上的哈希！”大哥，你花三十万，买了一张收据的收据。\n\n那年推特上全是六边形猴子头像，跟加入了邪教似的，天天在Discord里喊“WAGMI，我们都会发财”。项目方路线图写着“元宇宙乐园”，写完人就跑了。\n\n现在地板价跌成零头，当年付的Gas费都比猴子值钱。他还不卖，说这叫“长期持有”。兄弟，你那不叫长期持有，叫卖不掉。猴子叼着烟看着你，终于笑了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "7d289cdaf821", "text": "2021年买NFT的哥们，我特别理解。他们不是想买画，是想买一张\"我比你早知道\"的门票。\n\n我有个朋友，花了几十万买了只戴水手帽的猴子。我说：\"这图我右键就能存啊。\"他很严肃地说：\"你存的是图，我拥有的是……\"他想了三秒，\"……一个指向图的链接。\"兄弟，你花几十万买了个超链接。\n\n买完他就入教了。头像换成猴子，天天在Discord里跟一个叫\"猿猴老王\"的陌生人互道\"gm\"，互相喊\"WAGMI\"，意思是我们都会发财。\n\n现在地板价跌了九成多，他想挂单卖，结果上架的gas费比成交价还贵。\n\n问他怎么办，他说：\"我是长期持有。\"\n\n不，你不是长期持有，你是没人接盘。你唯一的资产，是那个Discord群里还没退群的三个人。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0725", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "juicero", "target_name": "Juicero 榨汁机", "facts": ["A Wi-Fi-connected cold-press juicer launched in 2016 at $699, later cut to $399, that squeezed proprietary bags of chopped produce sold by subscription.", "Raised about $120 million from investors including Google Ventures and Kleiner Perkins.", "In April 2017, Bloomberg reporters squeezed the packs by hand and got 7.5 ounces of juice in 90 seconds, faster than the machine's 8 ounces in about two minutes.", "Each pack carried a QR code that the press scanned and checked online before squeezing; it refused expired packs and would not run at all unless connected to Wi-Fi.", "Shut down in September 2017 and offered refunds on the machine."], "a": {"item_id": "42eeb6dfde70", "text": "Juicero 要 400 美元，干的事是把一包切好的果蔬塞进去压成汁。问题是，那包东西你用手也能捏出来。做这台机器的是硅谷一帮创始人，想颠覆榨汁行业，融了一个多亿美元，图的是你每个月订阅他们的果蔬包，一包好几美元，还得联网认证。后来有记者拿它的包装袋直接用手挤，汁照样出来。投资人一看，哦，原来我们投的不是榨汁机，是一台给包装袋收月费的收费站。公司两年后就关门了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "5d1ab9a2be91", "text": "买Juicero的人，我特别想认识一下。四百美元，买一台榨汁机，还得连Wi-Fi。你家果汁需要上网？它要先扫一下果蔬包上的二维码，确认这包菜没过期，才肯开榨。你花四百块，雇了个电子门卫，专门查蔬菜的身份证。\n\n这帮人图什么？不是图果汁，是图早上站在厨房，对着手机App，看机器庄严地压一包菜，那种\"我是住在未来的人\"的感觉。\n\n结果彭博社发现：不用机器，徒手捏那个袋子，果汁出得更快更多。也就是说，你买了一台输给你双手的机器。\n\n后来公司倒闭了，还好给你退了钱。但你的下场是：成了互联网上最出名的一批人。因为你把\"不会捏袋子\"，做成了一个融资一点二亿美元的生活方式。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0726", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "microsoft-teams", "target_name": "Microsoft Teams", "facts": ["Launched in 2017, bundled at no extra charge into Office 365, and replaced Skype for Business.", "After Slack's antitrust complaint, Microsoft unbundled Teams from Office in the EU in 2023 and worldwide in 2024; the European Commission still sent a statement of objections in 2024.", "Microsoft reported 320 million monthly active users in 2023.", "Microsoft shut down consumer Skype in May 2025 and sent its users to Teams."], "a": {"item_id": "fd671172ecb3", "text": "我们公司推Teams的是IT部老王。为啥选它？买Office 365送的，老王这辈子没拒绝过“送的”。培训会上他讲了四十分钟：“聊天”和“团队”不一样，“频道”里的“帖子”也不算聊天，文件其实存在SharePoint里。SharePoint是啥？别问。\n\n现在全公司最忙的就是老王，天天有人问他：文件呢？我电脑上怎么有两个Teams？\n\n最惨的是，老板发现了头像旁边那个小绿点，五分钟不动鼠标就变黄。现在老王桌上放着个自动晃鼠标的小玩意儿。他亲手装的监控，第一个抓的是他自己。"}, "b": {"item_id": "1fd1f04cdc79", "text": "逼我们用 Teams 的人，我研究过了，是 IT 总监。他的理由我听懂了：买了 Microsoft 365，Teams 免费送。他不是选了个软件，他是领了个赠品，还拿去年底述职：\"我给公司省了三十万！\"省的是软件钱，花的是我们的命。现在全公司表面在 Teams 上，实际都在微信群里问：\"那个会议链接到底点哪儿？\"最惨的是他自己，每次他一发言，头像都是黄色的\"离开\"，人明明坐在那儿，系统说他不在。推广一年，他终于被系统说中了：这个人，在公司，已经离开了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0727", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "beanie-babies", "target_name": "Beanie Babies 豆豆娃", "facts": ["Launched by Ty Inc. in 1993 and sold only through small gift shops, with designs 'retired' to manufacture scarcity.", "At the height of the craze Beanie Babies accounted for about 10% of eBay's sales, and eBay's 1998 IPO filing said the site at times had over 30,000 Beanie Baby auctions running simultaneously in the quarter ending June 1998.", "In 1999 a divorcing couple divided their Beanie Baby collection one by one on a courtroom floor, in a photograph that ran worldwide.", "In 1999 Ty announced that Beanie Babies would be retired at the end of the year, then reversed itself after asking the public to vote and seeing an overwhelming vote to keep them.", "Most 'rare' Beanies that price guides valued in the thousands now sell for a few dollars."], "a": {"item_id": "5b893c7de1a5", "text": "九十年代有一批美国大人，坚信一只塞满塑料颗粒的小熊能供孩子上大学。怎么投资呢？不让孩子碰。孩子想抱一下，爸妈拦住：别摸，一摸就贬值。这是养儿子还是供祖宗？他们还给心形吊牌单独买塑料保护壳，熊可以脏，牌不能折，那张纸才是资产。抢戴安娜王妃熊抢到打架，却没发现厂商一边喊“限量退休”，一边接着生产。你跟印钞的人比谁囤得多？后来有夫妻离婚，在法庭上分豆豆娃，分完发现整整一车，卖了不够付律师费。现在三百只堆在车库，挂网上五美元一只，没人要。孩子没上成大学，但学到一课：在爸爸心里，我排在一只带吊牌的熊后面。"}, "b": {"item_id": "e9aa38d24ee4", "text": "九十年代有一批人，觉得自己找到了下一个华尔街：一只塞满塑料粒的小熊。他们不看财报，看那颗红色心形吊牌。吊牌折个角，在他们眼里跟股市崩盘一样。孩子想抱一下？不行！先给吊牌套上塑料保护壳。宝贝，你的大学学费就在这只紫色戴安娜王妃熊身上，别流口水。Ty公司一宣布某款“退休”，他们连夜开车扫三家礼品店，比自己亲爹退休还上心。结果呢？拉斯维加斯真有一对夫妻离婚，法官让他们蹲在法庭地板上，一只一只轮流挑豆豆娃。这就是你的资产配置：二十年后，唯一升值的是你儿子的心理咨询费。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0728", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "slack", "target_name": "Slack", "facts": ["Spun out in 2013 from the internal chat tool of Tiny Speck's failed game Glitch; founder Stewart Butterfield had earlier spun Flickr out of another failed game.", "The name is a backronym: 'Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge'.", "Salesforce bought it for $27.7 billion in 2021.", "Filed an EU antitrust complaint against Microsoft in 2020 over the bundling of Teams with Office."], "a": {"item_id": "d768d460ddec", "text": "我见过一种老板，叫“Slack治国派”。创业第一天就宣布：咱们不发邮件，一切公开，扁平！三年后，公司一百人，四百个频道。#general没人敢说话，#random全是老板转发的文章。他最爱@channel，凌晨一点全公司一百部手机一起震，内容是：“大家觉得新logo咋样？”决策靠表情投票：三个👍，一个👀，通过。没人知道👀啥意思，包括发👀的人。\n\n最惨的是他自己。头像旁边那个绿点，他不敢让它灭，怕员工觉得他不在；员工也不敢让它灭，怕他觉得员工不在。全公司互相盯着一个绿点上班。\n\n他确实消灭了邮件。现在他想找一个决定，得先问一句：“这事是在哪个thread里定的？”"}, "b": {"item_id": "04d9a9a0c475", "text": "有一种老板，创业第一天就宣布：我们不开会，全员用 Slack，扁平、透明、异步。\n\n结果建了四十七个频道。#general 没人说话，#random 倒是热火朝天。真正的工作都在\"线程\"里，那地方跟地下室一样，进去就没人再见过。\n\n老板最爱 @channel。一发，三十个人手机同时一震，点开一看：周五记得填报销。然后三十个人回三十个 👍。他盯着那一排 👍，觉得这公司管得真好。\n\n他还盯着名字旁边那个绿点。员工呢，学会了每五分钟晃一下鼠标。公司就这样实现了\"全员在线，无人干活\"。\n\n说好不开会，最后他点了那个小耳机图标，叫 huddle。对，就是开会，只是名字可爱了点。\n\n最惨的是免费版，三个月前的消息自动消失。老板问：去年那个决定谁拍的板？\n\nSlack 说：什么决定？"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0729", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "almond-milk", "target_name": "杏仁奶", "facts": ["California grows about 80% of the world's almonds.", "A single almond takes about 1.1 US gallons (roughly 4 litres) of water to grow, a figure Mother Jones published in 2014 that spread during California's drought.", "A 2015 US class action alleged Almond Breeze contained only about 2% almonds; it partly survived a motion to dismiss, was stayed in 2017 pending a separate Blue Diamond labelling settlement, and the plaintiffs then dropped it.", "Pollinating California's almond orchards each February requires trucking in most of the commercial honeybee colonies in the United States.", "Medieval European cooks used almond milk as a Lenten substitute for dairy, centuries before the current fashion."], "a": {"item_id": "2a93fb2cbc64", "text": "我同事上个月宣布改喝杏仁奶，说是为了环保，为了少一头牛放屁。我就问他：你知道一颗杏仁要用四升水才能长出来吗？他说知道，但牛更费水。行，我又问：你这杯杏仁奶，杏仁占多少？他说，大部分吧。我说，百分之二。\n\n四十五块钱，买了一杯百分之九十八是水的东西，还是加州大旱那边的水。\n\n更绝的是，加州杏仁树要靠蜜蜂授粉，蜂群是卡车拉过去租的，累死一大片。所以他一个吃素的，为了不伤害牛，给蜜蜂送了终。\n\n最后，他在咖啡里一倒，杏仁奶当场分层，絮状物漂起来，像分手现场。他还是端着喝完了，发朋友圈：“为地球做了一点小事。”\n\n地球没拯救，拿铁凉了，蜜蜂走了，他还得去厕所。"}, "b": {"item_id": "096100e8c869", "text": "我朋友为了环保戒了牛奶，改喝杏仁奶。他说牛放屁排甲烷，地球受不了。行，有道理。然后我问他杏仁哪来的，他说加州。加州啊，那地方干得裂开，种一颗杏仁要喝掉好几升水，他一杯杏仁奶下去，等于替加州的杏树喝了半缸。更绝的是，加州的杏树开花得靠蜜蜂，蜂箱是用大卡车拉过去的，蜜蜂一路颠过去，授完粉累死一片。杏仁奶泡咖啡，三分钟就分层，像他的环保决心，看着坚定，一放就散。他现在每天喝两杯，花的钱是牛奶的三倍，说这是为地球付的。我说你付的是加州的水费，地球一分没收到。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0730", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "papyrus", "target_name": "Papyrus 字体", "facts": ["Hand-drawn by Chris Costello in 1982 over about six months, when he was 23; he sold the rights to Letraset for $750.", "Bundled with macOS and with Microsoft Office, which is why it appears on so many menus and signs.", "James Cameron's Avatar (2009) used it for the film's logo and subtitles.", "A 2017 Saturday Night Live sketch starring Ryan Gosling is about a man unable to get over that choice."], "a": {"item_id": "eb2c4af932ef", "text": "你们发现没有，开养生馆的老板，都觉得自己特有灵性。他要什么？“古朴、自然、带点异域。”然后他打开Word，字体往下拉，拉到P，眼睛一亮：Papyrus！边缘毛毛糙糙，像拿芦苇杆蘸墨写在莎草纸上。老板一拍大腿：就它，古埃及的灵性！\n\n大哥，你卖的是艾灸，不是木乃伊。\n\n隔壁泰国菜也这么想，楼上墨西哥卷饼也这么想。一条街的“异域风情”，全是电脑自带的同一款免费字体。\n\n最惨的是，卡梅隆花几个亿拍《阿凡达》，片名用的也是它。所以你推门进去，技师一抬头，你第一反应不是放松，是想问：“蓝色那位在几号房？”"}, "b": {"item_id": "7a35ef193141", "text": "最离谱的是开养生馆、开餐馆的老板，招牌非要用Papyrus。这字体像用芦苇纸手撕出来的，笔画边缘毛毛躁躁，像法老陵墓门口那块石碑。他们图什么？图\"有调性\"，图\"古法养生\"，让客人一进门就觉得有故事。结果阿凡达的海报用的就是这个字，顾客抬头一看，以为要去潘多拉星球做足疗。隔壁烧烤店跟着学，招牌一亮，大家都在吃木乃伊烤串。老板很委屈：\"我要的是高级感。\"设计师说：\"是您自己在字体列表里一路往下划，点的这个。\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0731", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "php", "target_name": "PHP", "facts": ["Created by Rasmus Lerdorf in 1994 as 'Personal Home Page Tools', a set of C programs for tracking visitors to his own résumé page; the name now officially stands for the recursive 'PHP: Hypertext Preprocessor'.", "Lerdorf, in his 2007 MySQL Conference talk 'PHP on Hormones': 'I'm not a real programmer. I throw together things until it works then I move on.'", "Runs WordPress, which W3Techs counted on 39.4% of all websites (58.1% of sites with a known CMS) on 9 November 2026.", "Core function names are inconsistent (strpos, str_replace, htmlspecialchars, nl2br) partly because, as Lerdorf confirmed, early function names were chosen to balance a hash table keyed on string length.", "Until PHP 8 (2020), the comparison 0 == 'foo' was true because the string was silently converted to a number."], "a": {"item_id": "bcc6af302235", "text": "写PHP的人，我一直很佩服。这门语言每个变量前面都要加个美元符号：$name、$age、$salary。写了十年，满屏都是$，工资卡里一个都没见着。\n\n他们当初为什么入行？因为好上手。培训班三个月，出来就接单，接的全是老板表弟的商城、装修公司的官网。入行时都背过那句\"PHP是世界上最好的语言\"，十年过去，这话喊得最响的，永远是正在改前任祖传代码的人。\n\n下场更惨。每年都有人宣布PHP死了，他们不反驳，因为手上那个WordPress插件，作者三年前就跑路了。面试官问：\"你还在写PHP啊？\"他们只能说：\"我们现在升到8了，支持类型了。\"\n\n这就像有人说：我家一直漏雨，但是现在，我们有伞了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "e721ec283ce4", "text": "干PHP吃饭的人，我认识三类。第一类是接外包的，客户说\"就改个按钮颜色\"，他打开一看，代码里还留着 mysql_connect，注释是2009年的，他知道这活要干到天亮。第二类是背框架的，嘴里全是Laravel，自己的小网站却还是 $_GET 直接拼进SQL，问他为啥，他说\"这叫传统\"。第三类图的就是那句\"语法简单，上手快\"，结果干了十年，还在查 \"0\" == false 为什么是真的。去年他投简历，HR问他精通什么，他写\"PHP\"，面试官问 == 和 === 有啥区别，他沉默三秒，把\"精通\"两个字划掉了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0732", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "durian", "target_name": "榴莲", "facts": ["Banned on Singapore's MRT trains and in many Southeast Asian hotels because of the smell.", "A 2017 genome study in Nature Genetics traced the odour to a duplicated family of MGL genes that pump out volatile sulfur compounds.", "China buys most of the world's durian exports, chiefly from Thailand, in a trade worth billions of dollars a year.", "Alfred Russel Wallace wrote in 1856 that eating it was 'a new sensation worth a voyage to the East to experience'."], "a": {"item_id": "fd9d6c3fef4a", "text": "在地铁上开榴莲的人，我一直好奇你图什么？图那句“闻着臭吃着香”是吧？大哥，吃着香的只有你，闻着臭的是一整节车厢。你这不叫吃水果，叫一人点单，全车买单。\n\n而且这种人还特讲究，戴一次性手套，掰得小心翼翼，生怕刺扎手。你的手保住了，我们的鼻子谁管？\n\n他还要给你科普：“猫山王，一百多一斤！”哦，你花一百多，买了个毒气弹，还让我们免费帮你验货。\n\n下场也摆在那：车厢立刻空出一圈，全城最好找座位的人就是他。可惜手套白戴了，第二天一张嘴，全办公室都知道他昨晚吃的是猫山王。"}, "b": {"item_id": "c1203c00cfe6", "text": "地铁上吃榴莲的人，我真的服。那味儿一进车厢，我以为哪个下水道盖子被掀了。更绝的是他还闭着眼，嘴角往上翘，一副在拍广告的样子。他图什么？图个\"我很会生活\"？行，你会生活，我们陪你一起窒息。下场也很精彩：全车人同时把口罩往上提，他下车时手里拎着带刺的壳，座位上留了一圈黏糊糊的黄纤维。第二天，他身上的味道洗三遍还在，同事开会时都默默把窗户打开了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0733", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "brasilia", "target_name": "巴西利亚", "facts": ["Inaugurated on 21 April 1960 after about 41 months of construction, moving the capital inland from Rio de Janeiro.", "Lúcio Costa's Plano Piloto is laid out like an airplane (or a bird, or a cross), with Oscar Niemeyer's government buildings along the fuselage.", "Listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1987, less than 30 years after it was built.", "Planned for 500,000 residents; the Federal District now has about 3 million, most in satellite towns outside the plan.", "Built for the car: wide avenues, few pedestrian crossings, and distances that make walking impractical."], "a": {"item_id": "7ded9e81696c", "text": "在巴西利亚走路的人，就两种：建筑系学生，和看地图觉得\"才两公里\"的游客。\n\n国会大厦就在前面，清清楚楚。你走二十分钟，它还在前面，一点没大。建筑师管这叫\"纪念性尺度\"，我管这叫海市蜃楼。\n\n图什么？图\"亲身体验世界遗产\"。结果这城是按飞机设计的，汽车安排得明明白白，行人？没设计。你只好在草地上踩出一条红泥路，当地人管这叫\"欲望之路\"，听着挺浪漫，其实就是一群跟你一样的人集体迷路的证据。\n\n下场都一样：裤腿红到膝盖，嘴唇在一千米海拔上裂开，最后卡在一座立交桥下，对面看得见，就是过不去。\n\n你叫了辆车，司机到了问：\"你怎么在这儿？\"\n\n你说：\"想感受一下城市。\"\n\n他说：\"这不是城市，这是飞机。你在机翼上散步。\""}, "b": {"item_id": "a632ffa6792b", "text": "在巴西利亚走路的，就三种人：刚下飞机的游客、还没买车的新公务员，和读过一本城市规划书的理想主义者。\n\n游客站在电视塔上看国会那两栋楼：“就在前面嘛。”对，中间隔着纪念轴十几条车道。尼迈耶给你设计了大教堂，没给你设计斑马线。\n\n理想主义者说要“用脚丈量城市”。卢西奥·科斯塔把城市画成一架飞机，你见过谁在机翼上散步？\n\n新公务员沿着部委大道走，十几栋一模一样的楼，走到第七栋就分不清自己是去上班还是在服刑。\n\n结局都一样：八月旱季，踩着前人在草地上踩出来的红土小道，鞋红了，裤脚红了，到门口保安问：“您车停哪儿了？”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0734", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "oat-milk", "target_name": "燕麦奶", "facts": ["Oatly started in Sweden in the 1990s, built on Lund University research by Rickard Öste on lactose intolerance.", "Oatly's slogan 'It's like milk but made for humans' was banned in Sweden in 2015 after the dairy lobby LRF Mjölk sued; Oatly then published the lawsuit as advertising.", "Oatly listed on Nasdaq in May 2021 at a valuation near $10 billion; the shares later lost more than 90% of their value.", "Commercial oat milk is made by breaking oat starch into maltose with amylase enzymes, and many brands, Oatly among them, add rapeseed oil; the result has more sugar and a higher glycaemic index than almond or soy milk."], "a": {"item_id": "0f1dbb4a84d2", "text": "我有个同事，点拿铁必须换燕麦奶，加五块。问他为啥，他说乳糖不耐。上礼拜我亲眼看他干掉一整块芝士蛋糕，面不改色。乳糖不耐？我看你是乳糖不耐“便宜”。\n\n后来他又改口，说是为了健康、为了地球。哥，燕麦奶想打出奶泡，里面得加油，菜籽油。你每天早上花三十八，喝一杯“过了油的燕麦水”，还觉得自己在拯救地球。\n\n最惨的是，小时候你奶奶天天早上给你煮燕麦粥，你嫌弃，说“这是马吃的”。现在你多花五块钱，把粥兑进咖啡里，还拍照发朋友圈。\n\n马都没你讲究。马吃燕麦，至少不用排队。"}, "b": {"item_id": "e299ef76bfd2", "text": "有些人点拿铁，非要补一句：换燕麦奶。行，你换。店员一边敲单一边抬头看你，加五块。你其实不知道为什么换，你只知道办公室那个健身的同事换了。端上来喝一口，嗯，甜的，还有一股麦片泡在水里的味道。你心里一紧，觉得自己是不是喝出了健康。拍照的时候，奶泡塌得比你的表情还快。那半杯你放在工位上，等它自己慢慢变成一碗麦片粥。最后你还是回去点普通牛奶，还得跟同事解释：我不是不喜欢，我就是想试试。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0735", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "comic-sans", "target_name": "Comic Sans 字体", "facts": ["Designed by Vincent Connare at Microsoft in 1994, modeled on the comic-book lettering in The Dark Knight Returns and Watchmen.", "Made for the speech bubbles of Rover, the cartoon dog in Microsoft Bob; it missed Bob's release and shipped instead in the Windows 95 Plus! pack.", "CERN's July 2012 Higgs boson discovery announcement was presented on slides set in it.", "The 'Ban Comic Sans' campaign, started by designers Dave and Holly Combs in 1999, ran until 2019, when they retired it and launched usecomicsans.com."], "a": {"item_id": "77e8416a8394", "text": "我们小区物业有位行政大哥，一心想跟业主拉近距离，所有通知一律用 Comic Sans。可他不知道，这字体根本没有中文。所以那张停水通知是这样的：“关于停水的通知”，宋体，庄严肃穆；后面“3月15日 8:00—18:00”，圆滚滚的，跟气球一样。一张纸上，中文在念悼词，数字在过六一。\n\n这字体当年是微软专门给一只卡通狗写对话框用的，大哥拿它通知全楼断水，等于派那只狗上门宣布：“汪！你家没水了！”\n\n结果没一个人当真。15号全楼照常洗澡，洗到一半水停了，一群人顶着泡沫下楼找他。大哥还挺委屈：“我通知得那么可爱，你们怎么不看呢？”"}, "b": {"item_id": "5f875c2be689", "text": "你们收过用Comic Sans写的正式通知吗？“本周六全小区停水”，底下配着那个圆滚滚的漫画体。发这种通知的，多半是物业或者HR，他们心里想的是：字圆一点，坏消息就没那么硬。可这字原本是微软给一个卡通小助手配气泡台词用的，骨子里就是为了“亲切”设计的。结果呢？停水像生日派对邀请函，扣工资像在发贴纸。大家看完第一反应不是慌，是笑：“这是哪个幼儿园发的？”通知被截图传遍业主群，发通知的人成了全小区的表情包。他本想做个好人，最后做成了段子手。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0736", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "excel", "target_name": "Microsoft Excel", "facts": ["First shipped for the Macintosh in 1985; the Windows version followed in 1987.", "Silently converts gene names such as SEPT2 and MARCH1 into dates; by August 2020 the HUGO Gene Nomenclature Committee had renamed 27 human genes (MARCH1 became MARCHF1, SEPT2 became SEPTIN2) and published guidelines in Nature Genetics to stop it.", "In October 2020, Public Health England lost 15,841 COVID-19 cases when a legacy .xls file hit its 65,536-row limit.", "Reinhart and Rogoff's 2010 paper 'Growth in a Time of Debt', cited for austerity policies, omitted five countries through a spreadsheet range error found by a graduate student in 2013.", "Sheets have been capped at 1,048,576 rows by 16,384 columns since Excel 2007."], "a": {"item_id": "fdec8434c36d", "text": "你们公司有没有这种人？老板说系统太贵，他就用 Excel 管全公司。财务表、库存表、工资表、客户名单，四十个 sheet，每个都带合并单元格，像一座没有图纸的违章建筑。文件名叫\"最终版_最终版2_真的最终版.xlsx\"，打开一看，VLOOKUP 引用的还是前任小王电脑里的 D 盘路径。员工编号输进去变成 1.23E+15，身份证后三位全成了0，工资都算错了。他图什么？图省钱，图灵活，图老板一句\"这表你最懂\"。结果呢？他现在请不了年假，因为全公司只有他看得懂那个宏，他一休假，公司就停摆。"}, "b": {"item_id": "bee6f959fe81", "text": "有种老板，用 Excel 管整个公司。图什么？图省钱，还觉得自己特别“数据驱动”。结果公司里权力最大的不是他，是“表哥”，全公司唯一看得懂那张表的人。表里 VLOOKUP 套了七层，没人敢动，谁动谁 #REF!。发工资那天，财务像拆炸弹。员工身份证号被自动变成 1.23E+17，十八位全军覆没。想打开文件，弹窗说：“已被张三锁定”。张三在三亚休年假。最后表哥一跳槽，老板才明白：自己不是在管公司，是在给一个人打工。而那个人，把工作表保护密码带走了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0737", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "klingon", "target_name": "克林贡语", "facts": ["Created by linguist Marc Okrand for Star Trek III (1984), building on a few words the actor James Doohan improvised for the 1979 film.", "Deliberately alien grammar: object-verb-subject word order and no verb 'to be', so Hamlet's line became 'taH pagh taHbe'', 'continue or not continue'.", "The Klingon Language Institute (1992) published Hamlet 'in the original Klingon'; Bing Translator added Klingon in 2013 and Duolingo in 2018.", "Okrand's 1985 Klingon Dictionary listed roughly 1,500 words, and fluent speakers number only in the dozens: 20 to 30 in 2009, 30 to 50 by 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "ab8685eebdf7", "text": "全世界能把克林贡语说流利的人，据说也就几十个。你想想，几十个人，凑一块儿连个婚礼都撑不满，还得从《星际迷航》剧组借人。\n\n这帮人图什么？图的是终于能用原文读《哈姆雷特》。对，有人真把莎士比亚翻成了克林贡语，理由是“莎士比亚本来就是克林贡人，被地球人偷了”。你说他疯吧，他还出了书。\n\n最狠的是有个语言学家，从儿子出生起只跟他说克林贡语，想培养世界上第一个克林贡母语者。结果孩子长到几岁，爸爸一开口说克林贡语，他就装听不见。你看，叛逆期都比别人早，还是外星文化先教会他的：荣誉第一，老爹靠边。\n\n而且这门语言根本不适合过日子。它连“你好”都没有，见面打招呼说的是“nuqneH”，意思是“你想干嘛？”你练了十年，终于能流利地跟人吵架了，问题是全世界能听懂你骂人的，也就那几十个，还都在同一个群里。"}, "b": {"item_id": "40a35f67e92f", "text": "我真佩服那些把克林贡语学到流利的人。流利啊，那是花了十年。这语言有多不适合社交？它没有\"你好\"。最接近的是 nuqneH，字面意思是：\"你要干嘛？\"也没有\"谢谢\"，因为克林贡人不感恩。\n\n所以你想想这帮人的处境：苦练十年，全世界能聊的就几百个同好。终于约到一个，见面第一句，你问他：\"你要干嘛？\"他回你：\"你要干嘛？\"然后俩人就僵在那儿了。\n\n想表达感情怎么办？只能用他们的方式，比如：\"今天是死的好日子。\"你女朋友说\"我想你了\"，你深情地回一句：\"今天是死的好日子。\"\n\n最惨的是什么？你辛辛苦苦练的是宾语—动词—主语的语序。你想说\"我爱你\"，得说成\"你，爱，我\"。听着像在被审问。\n\n最后他们图什么？图一个能用外星语言吵赢别人的机会。结果遇上另一个同样流利的，两人吵了三小时，谁也不会说\"对不起\"。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0738", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hydrogen-cars", "target_name": "氢燃料电池汽车", "facts": ["Toyota launched the Mirai in 2014; 'mirai' means 'future' in Japanese.", "Nearly all US hydrogen filling stations are in California; Shell closed all seven of its California passenger-car stations in February 2024.", "More than 95% of the world's hydrogen is made from fossil fuels, mostly by steam-reforming natural gas.", "California pump prices reached about $36 per kilogram in 2023-24, over $200 for a Mirai fill; Toyota has handed buyers fuel cards worth $15,000.", "Mirai owners filed a class action against Toyota in July 2024 over hydrogen shortages and station outages; a second suit in 2025 sought at least $5.7 billion."], "a": {"item_id": "31458abe94e2", "text": "有一种人专门买氢燃料电池车。他们的理由很宏大：我要为人类的未来开车。结果未来还没到，先到了加氢站门口。这种站一般开在郊区，像个地下党接头点，营业时间比银行还短，还得提前在App上抢号，跟抢演唱会门票一个难度。好不容易排上了，一公斤氢六七十块，加满一箱，司机心疼得直喊\"这不是加氢，是放血\"。最讽刺的是，这车一天到晚排出来的是水，环保是真环保，就是你的钱全变成了水。等到加氢站哪天悄悄拆了，车就停进车库，成了全国最贵的加湿器，每天擦一擦，等着哪天氢气自己从天上掉下来。"}, "b": {"item_id": "c99d709097af", "text": "买氢燃料电池车的人，我研究过，就三种。\n\n第一种，环保人士。张口就是：“我这车尾气只有水。”兄弟，你这氢大部分是天然气炼的，污染在加氢站上游排完了，你在这儿排水。这就好比饭店说自己不产生垃圾，因为厨房在隔壁街。\n\n第二种，被销售一句“送三年氢气”忽悠来的。图的是免费。结果全市两座加氢站，一座检修，一座在隔壁区。他现在周末的娱乐活动就是去加氢，导航到了，门口贴着：“设备维护，复工时间待定”。\n\n下场更惨。氢气卡还没用完，加氢站先倒闭了。几十万的车，停小区里，邻居问：“这啥车？”他说：“氢的。”邻居点点头：“哦，那你现在确实挺空的。”\n\n最后，他开着车唯一的排放物，去浇了阳台那盆绿萝。他说：“至少我这车，能养花。”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0739", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "luosifen", "target_name": "螺蛳粉", "facts": ["A rice-noodle dish from Liuzhou, Guangxi; the smell comes from pickled bamboo shoots (酸笋), not from the river snails, which only flavour the broth.", "The instant version boomed during the 2020 lockdowns; Liuzhou's government reported packaged luosifen sales of over 10 billion yuan that year.", "Added to China's national intangible cultural heritage list in 2021.", "Liuzhou opened a luosifen industry college in 2020 to train workers for the trade."], "a": {"item_id": "06a4c1a635ed", "text": "合租房里煮螺蛳粉的人，都信一句话：“闻着臭，吃着香。”这话是给吃的人用的。我们室友只拿到前半句。\n\n他煮十分钟，爽了。我在房间里闻三天，连根腐竹都没分到。\n\n他还特别讲礼貌，煮之前敲我门：“我煮螺蛳粉，你介意吗？”这哪是问，这是通知，跟飞机广播“前方有气流”一样，你介不介意，它都得颠。\n\n然后他开窗，开抽油烟机，觉得自己尽力了。兄弟，酸笋那玩意儿你开窗它不走，它是在考察房子，想签长租。\n\n结果第二天上班，同事凑过来：“你昨天是不是吃螺蛳粉了？”\n\n我没吃啊！\n\n我连汤都没喝上一口，就被螺蛳粉吃了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "2bf0b387fb70", "text": "合租房里煮螺蛳粉的，基本不是柳州人。柳州人懂分寸，在自己家吃。在合租房里煮的，都是刚入教的新信徒：一买就是一整箱袋装，每回还要额外多撕一包酸笋，说“原装的不够劲”。哥们，原装的已经够把燃气报警器熏醒了！\n\n你问他图什么，他永远就一句：“闻着臭，吃着香。”问题是吃的是你，闻的是我们仨。香你独享，臭我们均摊。这叫什么？这叫螺蛳粉式共享经济。\n\n最惨的还是他自己。吃了半年，鼻子早被酸笋腌入味了，啥也闻不出来。第二天去公司开会，一进门领导就抬头：“谁在会议室吃螺蛳粉？”他还跟着一起东张西望地找。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0740", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "ugg", "target_name": "UGG 雪地靴", "facts": ["Sheepskin boots Australian surfers wore to warm up after sessions; Brian Smith started selling them in California in 1978.", "Deckers Outdoor bought the brand in 1995 and holds 'UGG' as a trademark in the US and elsewhere; in Australia, 'ugg' is a generic word any bootmaker may use.", "In 2019, a US jury found an Australian maker liable for selling boots labelled 'ugg' into the United States.", "Oprah Winfrey featured them in her 'Favorite Things' lists repeatedly in the 2000s, which drove the first boom.", "UGG is Deckers' largest brand, with over $2 billion in annual sales."], "a": {"item_id": "52524a4d7862", "text": "有一类人，七月三十八度，脚上还蹬着UGG。你问她为啥，她说：“舒服，还不用穿袜子。”姐妹，那是羊毛内里替你把袜子穿了，还是加厚款。\n\n这鞋是澳洲人冲完浪上岸暖脚的。你在北京地铁里冲什么浪？冲的是汗。\n\n脚在里面，四周是羊毛，底下是羊皮，这不叫穿鞋，这叫铜锅：脚是肉，汗是汤。晚上去饭店要脱鞋，她一脱，全桌人当场不点羊肉了，已经闻饱了。\n\n最惨是下雨。UGG一沾水就硬，还掉色，所以夏天暴雨，她们在路口跳格子，跟拆雷一样。你以为她怕湿脚？她是在保护那一千多块的信仰。\n\n等到秋天，她还特骄傲：“你看，我终于穿对季节了。”对，那一个月，你和UGG的脚臭终于不是孤军奋战了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "3c6a30dd29f9", "text": "我有个朋友，一年四季穿UGG。三伏天，脚上套着两只羊。我问你不热吗？她说：“你不懂，UGG本来就是澳洲冲浪的人穿的。”姐，人家是冲完浪上岸暖脚，你是在地铁里给脚蒸桑拿。\n\n她图什么？图舒服，图不用系鞋带。确实不用系，穿到第三个月，靴筒自己就塌了，鞋跟往外磨成斜坡，她走路像一艘正在侧翻的船。下雨更惨，麂皮一沾水就是一圈白印，像脚踝上长了年轮，一看就知道这双鞋熬过几个夏天。\n\n最狠的是味道。羊毛内里，光脚，捂了一整个七月。她一脱鞋，全屋人都懂了：那只羊，没走远。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0741", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "vim", "target_name": "Vim", "facts": ["Released by Bram Moolenaar in November 1991 as 'Vi IMitation', a vi clone he had begun writing for the Amiga in 1988 on top of Stevie; the expansion became 'Vi IMproved' with version 2.0 in December 1993.", "Licensed as charityware: the licence asks users to donate to ICCF Holland, a charity Moolenaar ran for children in Uganda.", "Stack Overflow's question 'How to exit the Vim editor?' passed one million visits in May 2017, which Stack Overflow marked with a blog post.", "Modal editing: in normal mode, typing letters runs commands rather than inserting text; :wq saves and quits, :q! quits and discards changes."], "a": {"item_id": "025ba8683b8f", "text": "公司里总有一个用 Vim 的人，你不用问，他自己会说。入职第一天就官宣：\"我不用鼠标。\"没人问啊，我问的是厕所在哪。\n\n他图什么？图你从背后路过，看他光标嗖嗖飞三屏，你\"哇\"一声。就这一声，够他吃一个月。\n\n下场呢？.vimrc 调了六百行，花了三个周末，省下的时间还没调的时间多。最惨是帮你改代码，你电脑上开的是 VS Code，他自信满满先按 Esc，再敲 :wq，保存退出，一气呵成。结果文档里多出一行 :wq，像他的墓志铭。\n\n他还不认，说：\"你这编辑器，不行。\""}, "b": {"item_id": "90b37ac793f9", "text": "有种人用 Vim 写代码，还非得让全世界知道。你问他用什么编辑器，他不直接说，停半秒，吐出两个字：\"Vim。\"那语气，像刚从战场上下来。他图什么？图你觉得他厉害。可你看他干活：左手按 ESC，右手敲 dd、yy、p，屏幕上东西一会儿没了，一会儿又冒出来，三个函数已经搬到别的文件里了，你连他光标在哪都没看清。同事问他怎么不用 IDE，他说：\"我在普通模式，你不懂。\"后来服务器出事，他被叫去救火，刚坐下就在群里喊：\"等我先退出一下。\"然后他对着屏幕敲了二十分钟 :q，纹丝不动，最后爬出来，第一句话是：\"谁能告诉我记事本怎么用？\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0742", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "javascript", "target_name": "JavaScript", "facts": ["Written by Brendan Eich at Netscape in ten days in May 1995; named Mocha, then LiveScript, then JavaScript to ride Java's marketing, although the two are unrelated.", "typeof null returns 'object', a bug from the first version that has been kept for compatibility ever since.", "In March 2016 the author of left-pad, an 11-line npm package, unpublished it and broke builds across the industry, including Babel and React.", "[] + {} evaluates to '[object Object]'; {} + [] typed at a console evaluates to 0.", "Standardized by Ecma as 'ECMAScript' in 1997 because the name JavaScript is a trademark, first Sun's and now Oracle's."], "a": {"item_id": "c214f8db4fb9", "text": "我有个同事，什么都用 JavaScript 写。写脚本用 JS，写后台用 Node，连他家的烧水壶我都怀疑跑着 npm。他图什么？他说\"一门语言打天下\"。结果呢？一个 hello world 下了四百兆的 node_modules，八万个文件，他说这叫生态。上周他算 0.1 加 0.2，屏幕蹦出 0.30000000000000004，他盯了十分钟，说这是\"浮点的浪漫\"。昨天他 npm install 了一下，整个项目塌了，原因是有人从仓库里撤掉了一个十一行的小包，叫 left-pad。现在他分不清 null 和 undefined，我问他 typeof null 为什么是 object，他说这是历史遗留问题。我说，你才是。"}, "b": {"item_id": "9255e820a06a", "text": "我有个朋友，信一句话：“凡是能用JavaScript写的，最终都会用JavaScript写。”他当预言，我当诅咒。\n\n他写了个计算器，用Electron打包，两百兆，自带一整个Chrome，就为了算一加一。一加一算对了，0.1加0.2出来0.30000000000000004。他说没事，装个库。一装，node_modules比他的人生还沉，里面有个包叫is-odd，专门判断奇数，它还依赖另一个包叫is-number。\n\n他图什么？图“前后端一门语言，不用学第二种”。结果为了这一门，React、Vue、Svelte轮着学，刚学会一个，下一个又出来了。\n\n现在他三十五，头发没了，问我他算个啥。我说你就是typeof null：明明啥也没有，还非说自己是个object。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0743", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "luosifen", "target_name": "螺蛳粉", "facts": ["A rice-noodle dish from Liuzhou, Guangxi; the smell comes from pickled bamboo shoots (酸笋), not from the river snails, which only flavour the broth.", "The instant version boomed during the 2020 lockdowns; Liuzhou's government reported packaged luosifen sales of over 10 billion yuan that year.", "Added to China's national intangible cultural heritage list in 2021.", "Liuzhou opened a luosifen industry college in 2020 to train workers for the trade."], "a": {"item_id": "5d0789c353d9", "text": "合租房里煮螺蛳粉的人，我太懂了。他们总说：就煮一包，不碍事。图什么？图锅里飘着腐竹和木耳，图那口酸笋上头的快感，图自己在厨房吸溜得像个美食博主。结果呢？酸笋味顺着抽油烟机往外跑，跑不掉的全钻进了室友的被窝。第二天群里弹出一句：\"谁煮的螺蛳粉？\"没人认，但全屋人心里都有数。厨房门上很快贴了张纸，写着\"禁止螺蛳粉\"，他的押金被扣了两百，理由是\"除味费\"。最绝的是那个空料包，他打了三个结扔进垃圾桶，第二天整个单元楼的人都知道他吃了什么。"}, "b": {"item_id": "beb2c7971e47", "text": "合租房里煮螺蛳粉的，都是同一种人：把“闻着臭吃着香”当人生信条，但只要后半句，前半句全分给室友。\n\n他煮之前还特讲究，关门、开窗、开油烟机，跟拆炸弹似的。然后酸笋包一撕，全白搭。那味儿穿门缝、钻衣柜，第二天你穿着外套去面试，HR问你：“你家住垃圾站旁边？”\n\n他还觉得自己挺克制：“我没加臭，原味。”大哥，原味酸笋就是臭的底线，你跟我讲克制？\n\n最惨的是吃完那一刻。他满足了三分钟，剩下半锅红油汤倒哪？倒马桶，厕所臭三天；倒水池，整栋楼臭一周。\n\n最后合租群里没人骂他，就房东发了一句：“退房时，窗帘按新的赔。”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0744", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "almond-milk", "target_name": "杏仁奶", "facts": ["California grows about 80% of the world's almonds.", "A single almond takes about 1.1 US gallons (roughly 4 litres) of water to grow, a figure Mother Jones published in 2014 that spread during California's drought.", "A 2015 US class action alleged Almond Breeze contained only about 2% almonds; it partly survived a motion to dismiss, was stayed in 2017 pending a separate Blue Diamond labelling settlement, and the plaintiffs then dropped it.", "Pollinating California's almond orchards each February requires trucking in most of the commercial honeybee colonies in the United States.", "Medieval European cooks used almond milk as a Lenten substitute for dairy, centuries before the current fashion."], "a": {"item_id": "7d9582e4c68c", "text": "我有个同事，为了救地球，牛奶戒了，改喝杏仁奶。天天端着杯子说：“我在减少碳排放。”\n\n挺好。就是我查了一下：一颗杏仁，得喝掉好几升水。加州旱成那样，杏仁树还在那儿咕嘟咕嘟地喝。\n\n更绝的是他那盒杏仁奶，配料表上杏仁只占百分之二，剩下全是水。\n\n也就是说，他花三十块买了一盒“泡过杏仁的水”，那几颗杏仁还先喝光了一个村的水。他不是在救地球，他是在帮加州倒腾水。\n\n哦对，杏仁开花全靠租来的蜜蜂授粉，每年一季下来，蜜蜂成片成片地死。\n\n他现在特骄傲：“我从不碰动物制品。”\n\n兄弟，蜜蜂不算动物？"}, "b": {"item_id": "2a93fb2cbc64", "text": "我同事上个月宣布改喝杏仁奶，说是为了环保，为了少一头牛放屁。我就问他：你知道一颗杏仁要用四升水才能长出来吗？他说知道，但牛更费水。行，我又问：你这杯杏仁奶，杏仁占多少？他说，大部分吧。我说，百分之二。\n\n四十五块钱，买了一杯百分之九十八是水的东西，还是加州大旱那边的水。\n\n更绝的是，加州杏仁树要靠蜜蜂授粉，蜂群是卡车拉过去租的，累死一大片。所以他一个吃素的，为了不伤害牛，给蜜蜂送了终。\n\n最后，他在咖啡里一倒，杏仁奶当场分层，絮状物漂起来，像分手现场。他还是端着喝完了，发朋友圈：“为地球做了一点小事。”\n\n地球没拯救，拿铁凉了，蜜蜂走了，他还得去厕所。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0745", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "macau", "target_name": "澳门", "facts": ["A Portuguese settlement from the 1550s until the handover to China in December 1999.", "Casino revenue passed the Las Vegas Strip's in 2006, and at its 2013 peak, about $45 billion, was roughly seven times the Strip's.", "One company, STDM, held the casino monopoly for four decades until licences opened to competition in 2002.", "Among the most densely populated places on Earth, at roughly 20,000 people per square kilometre.", "The Cotai Strip stands on land reclaimed from the sea between the islands of Taipa and Coloane."], "a": {"item_id": "8dc5e4ad252d", "text": "我有个朋友，在老家输了二十万，说要去澳门翻本。你听听这逻辑：在小区棋牌室输的钱，要去全亚洲最专业的地方赢回来。这就像被村口的狗咬了，你决定去动物园找老虎报仇。\n\n他是坐赌场的免费接驳大巴进去的。记住，澳门最大方的就是这趟车，因为它只管送你进去，不管你怎么回来。三天后他给我发了张照片，大三巴牌坊，就剩前面一面墙，后头全空了。他配了一句：“你看，跟我一样。”\n\n最绝的是回来那天，他手上那块表押在了葡京旁边的当铺里。他说没事，等下回签注办下来就去赎。兄弟，你兜里那张不是当票，是下一趟的车票。"}, "b": {"item_id": "be44032450bd", "text": "我特别佩服那种说\"我去澳门翻本\"的人。股票亏了三十万，他的方案是：去澳门，一晚上，赢回来。你想想，三十万是怎么亏的？靠判断力。现在他拿同一套判断力，去一个没有钟的地方翻本。\n\n到了威尼斯人，里面的天永远是下午四点，假的，就是让你觉得还早，还来得及。结果三十万变六十万，反方向的。出来一看，赌场对面就是当铺，灯亮着，贴心得像医院门口开殡仪馆。表当了，金链子当了，最后走回关闸，全部家当一个猪扒包。\n\n回来跟我说：\"差一点就翻了。\"哥，你差的不是一点，你差的是那三十万，加上另外三十万，再加上你那块表。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0746", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "vim", "target_name": "Vim", "facts": ["Released by Bram Moolenaar in November 1991 as 'Vi IMitation', a vi clone he had begun writing for the Amiga in 1988 on top of Stevie; the expansion became 'Vi IMproved' with version 2.0 in December 1993.", "Licensed as charityware: the licence asks users to donate to ICCF Holland, a charity Moolenaar ran for children in Uganda.", "Stack Overflow's question 'How to exit the Vim editor?' passed one million visits in May 2017, which Stack Overflow marked with a blog post.", "Modal editing: in normal mode, typing letters runs commands rather than inserting text; :wq saves and quits, :q! quits and discards changes."], "a": {"item_id": "e21539084a9a", "text": "我们组有个哥们，用Vim。你怎么知道的？你不用知道，他会告诉你。开会时他说“我手从不离开键盘”，然后在投影上按了二十分钟hjkl，去找第三行。\n\n他的.vimrc写了八百行，装了四十个插件：文件树、自动补全、Git面板。兄弟，你花了三年，亲手把Vim配成了一个启动更慢的VS Code。\n\n他图什么？就图那一句：“你连怎么退出都不会吧？”\n\n可他的下场呢？给女朋友发微信：“我爱你:wq”。人家问：“你打错了吧？”他说：“没错，保存并退出。”\n\n她真退出了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "90b37ac793f9", "text": "有种人用 Vim 写代码，还非得让全世界知道。你问他用什么编辑器，他不直接说，停半秒，吐出两个字：\"Vim。\"那语气，像刚从战场上下来。他图什么？图你觉得他厉害。可你看他干活：左手按 ESC，右手敲 dd、yy、p，屏幕上东西一会儿没了，一会儿又冒出来，三个函数已经搬到别的文件里了，你连他光标在哪都没看清。同事问他怎么不用 IDE，他说：\"我在普通模式，你不懂。\"后来服务器出事，他被叫去救火，刚坐下就在群里喊：\"等我先退出一下。\"然后他对着屏幕敲了二十分钟 :q，纹丝不动，最后爬出来，第一句话是：\"谁能告诉我记事本怎么用？\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0747", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "jackfruit", "target_name": "菠萝蜜", "facts": ["The largest fruit that grows on a tree; single fruits can weigh over 40 kg.", "The national fruit of Bangladesh and the state fruit of Kerala and Tamil Nadu.", "Unripe flesh is sold as a vegan 'pulled pork' substitute, although it has only 1-2 g of protein per 100 g.", "Cutting one releases a sticky white latex, so cooks oil their knives and hands first.", "A member of the mulberry family (Moraceae), a relative of breadfruit and figs."], "a": {"item_id": "b09e1a218e62", "text": "现在网红轻食店流行一道菜，叫“植物基手撕猪肉”，六十八一碗。我吃了一口，菠萝蜜。就是海南路边五块钱一斤、一个三十斤、浑身长刺、得抱着才能搬走的那个。\n\n老板图什么？图它没熟的时候能撕成一丝一丝的，看着像肉。可他没告诉你，切这玩意儿会流白胶，粘手上肥皂都洗不掉，得拿食用油搓。\n\n你想想这画面：一个天天喊“告别动物油脂”的素食老板，每天打烊第一件事，是在后厨拎一壶花生油搓手，搓得满手油光，跟刚偷吃完一盘红烧肉似的。\n\n最惨的是有一回他切到个熟的，整个店一股香蕉混榴莲的甜味儿。顾客举着筷子问：“老板，你这猪肉怎么一股水果糖味儿？”\n\n老板想了半天：“这是……吃水果长大的猪。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "17fe9d85a9f3", "text": "现在有些老板，把青菠萝蜜煮熟撕成丝，当手撕鸡卖。图啥？图便宜，青菠萝蜜几块钱一斤，一撕，能卖出肉的价。\n\n算盘打得响，但他没杀过菠萝蜜。这玩意儿一刀下去，流出来的不是汁，是胶。粘刀，粘手，粘案板，老板还没骗到顾客，先把自己焊在后厨了。为了切开它，得拿油抹刀，抹手，抹案板，一套流程下来，一颗菠萝蜜，三小时。他那不是做假肉，是跟一个长满刺的胖娃娃摔跤。\n\n成本一算：油、人工、调料、创可贴，比真鸡还贵。\n\n最后顾客咬一口：“老板，这鸡怎么有股没熟透的甜味？”老板还嘴硬：“土鸡，吃水果长大的。”\n\n现在他手上的胶还没洗掉，指纹全没了。骗人没骗成，倒是意外获得了一项新技能，可以去偷东西。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0748", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "emacs", "target_name": "Emacs", "facts": ["Began in 1976 as a set of macros for the TECO editor at MIT's AI Lab, written by Richard Stallman and others; GNU Emacs followed in 1985.", "Extensible in its own Lisp dialect, Emacs Lisp; the standing joke is that it is a fine operating system lacking only a good editor.", "Ships with a psychotherapist (M-x doctor), Tetris, a mail client, a calendar, and a web browser (eww).", "An old backronym is 'Eight Megabytes And Constantly Swapping', from when 8 MB was a lot of memory.", "'Emacs pinky' is the name for the hand strain blamed on its Control-key chords."], "a": {"item_id": "ce1edb495cdc", "text": "有一种人嫌操作系统太小，非要把Emacs装成操作系统。邮件在里面收，日程用org-mode排，网页用eww看，连音乐都在里面放。图什么？图一个界面管所有事。结果一启动，init.el跑了四十秒，我问他怎么退出，他说C-x C-c。我说那是退出，他说那是\"我还没想好要不要走\"。最惨的是他的小拇指，Ctrl和Meta全压在它身上。去年体检，医生问是不是长期弹钢琴，他说不是，我在写Lisp。现在他的操作系统还是没装好，但他已经能用一个快捷键，把自己的手指头按进了 *scratch* 缓冲区。"}, "b": {"item_id": "45d1386d9957", "text": "我有个朋友，把Emacs当操作系统用。邮件用Gnus，上网用eww，终端开eshell，连窗口管理器都换成了EXWM。我问他图什么，他说：“图一辈子不用离开Emacs。”兄弟，这不叫生活，这叫软禁。他的init.el写了四千行，比毕业论文还长，每天上班第一件事，是修昨晚改坏的配置。一年下来，他就产出了俩东西：一个能用M-x点外卖的配置，一根被Ctrl键按废的小拇指。上礼拜Emacs一崩，他当场断网、断邮件、断社交，就像自己盖的房子塌了，人还埋在里头。现在他想开了，准备装Evil-mode改用Vim键位。好家伙，操作系统是齐了，就缺一个好用的编辑器。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0749", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "microsoft-word", "target_name": "Microsoft Word", "facts": ["Launched in 1983 as 'Multi-Tool Word' for Xenix and MS-DOS.", "Clippy, the animated paperclip assistant, arrived in Office 97 and was removed in Office 2007; Microsoft itself ran ads mocking it in 2001.", "Its .docx format, Office Open XML, was fast-tracked to ISO standard 29500 in 2008 as a roughly 6,000-page specification, amid accusations that national committees had been packed.", "Older spell checkers, Word's included, offered 'Cupertino' as a correction for a mistyped 'cooperation', and the resulting 'Cupertino effect' made it into published NATO, EU and UN-agency documents."], "a": {"item_id": "3536bc0676b0", "text": "有些人非要用 Word 排毕业论文。图什么？图一个\"一键生成目录\"。结果标题没用标题样式，手敲了个\"第一章\"，加粗了事。目录一更新，正文全变成了目录项。他去学分节符，学会了，又发现页码从第二节开始就断了，把\"链接到前一节\"点了十七次，点出一个新世界。凌晨三点，他按 Ctrl+A，对着域代码哭。答辩前一天，导师说：\"目录页码对不上。\"他按 F9，弹出一行\"错误！未找到目录项\"。那一刻他终于明白，Word 不是在排版，是在考他的耐心，而他的耐心已经交了白卷。"}, "b": {"item_id": "74d77985a3e9", "text": "用Word排毕业论文的人，都信一句话：“所见即所得。”结果你看见的是第38页，导师打开是第41页，图还跑到上一章去了。\n\n这帮人从不用“样式”，标题全靠手动选中、加粗、调三号。目录也是手敲的，后面那一排点，是空格键一个一个敲出来的。\n\n答辩前一晚，导师说加一句话。他在第二章敲了个句号，整篇论文像多米诺骨牌，参考文献直接顶到封面上。\n\n他想设页码，摘要用罗马数字，正文用阿拉伯数字，找分节符找了三小时，最后决定：摘要不要页码了。\n\n文件名叫“终稿_改3_真的终稿_导师改后_最终.docx”。\n\n最后交上去的是PDF。这是他一辈子，第一次让论文别动。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0750", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "brasilia", "target_name": "巴西利亚", "facts": ["Inaugurated on 21 April 1960 after about 41 months of construction, moving the capital inland from Rio de Janeiro.", "Lúcio Costa's Plano Piloto is laid out like an airplane (or a bird, or a cross), with Oscar Niemeyer's government buildings along the fuselage.", "Listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1987, less than 30 years after it was built.", "Planned for 500,000 residents; the Federal District now has about 3 million, most in satellite towns outside the plan.", "Built for the car: wide avenues, few pedestrian crossings, and distances that make walking impractical."], "a": {"item_id": "bb6fc598ef80", "text": "在巴西利亚靠两条腿出门的人，我服你们。你们都是什么神仙？大概三种：一种是退休大爷，每天走八公里，说是锻炼，其实是不想在家跟老伴大眼瞪小眼；一种是刚来的外地人，觉得\"这城市这么规整，走走就到了\"，二十分钟后他站在Eixão的六车道中间，像被冲上岸的海带；还有一种是非要证明自己有\"城市精神\"的人。图什么？图个\"我不开车也能活\"的面子。落得什么下场？你在308北和308南之间绕了一个小时，因为楼都长得一模一样。最后你走进一个部委大院，门卫问你找谁，你说找公交车站。公交车站？它在你身后三公里，中间隔着一条没有人行道的高速，隔着你的人生。"}, "b": {"item_id": "a632ffa6792b", "text": "在巴西利亚走路的，就三种人：刚下飞机的游客、还没买车的新公务员，和读过一本城市规划书的理想主义者。\n\n游客站在电视塔上看国会那两栋楼：“就在前面嘛。”对，中间隔着纪念轴十几条车道。尼迈耶给你设计了大教堂，没给你设计斑马线。\n\n理想主义者说要“用脚丈量城市”。卢西奥·科斯塔把城市画成一架飞机，你见过谁在机翼上散步？\n\n新公务员沿着部委大道走，十几栋一模一样的楼，走到第七栋就分不清自己是去上班还是在服刑。\n\n结局都一样：八月旱季，踩着前人在草地上踩出来的红土小道，鞋红了，裤脚红了，到门口保安问：“您车停哪儿了？”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0751", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "ugg", "target_name": "UGG 雪地靴", "facts": ["Sheepskin boots Australian surfers wore to warm up after sessions; Brian Smith started selling them in California in 1978.", "Deckers Outdoor bought the brand in 1995 and holds 'UGG' as a trademark in the US and elsewhere; in Australia, 'ugg' is a generic word any bootmaker may use.", "In 2019, a US jury found an Australian maker liable for selling boots labelled 'ugg' into the United States.", "Oprah Winfrey featured them in her 'Favorite Things' lists repeatedly in the 2000s, which drove the first boom.", "UGG is Deckers' largest brand, with over $2 billion in annual sales."], "a": {"item_id": "3ebab0e81cc7", "text": "你们见过夏天三十八度还穿UGG的人吧？两只毛茸茸的大头，像刚从羊身上薅下来、还没下班的小动物。他们图什么？图一个\"我早就入冬了\"的仪式感。结果呢？走三百米，羊皮里子把脚焖成一锅卤鸭掌，袜子都不用穿，直接在桑拿房里逛街。到了十月，那层软塌塌的鞋底已经磨出了地图。最惨的是羊毛里子被汗压成一块毡，踩在地铁台阶上，像踩着两块泡过水的抹布。朋友圈里配文\"今天也很暖\"，照片里那双栗色的靴子，已经晒成了一条咸鱼。"}, "b": {"item_id": "2b7b80053c20", "text": "我有个朋友，一年四季穿UGG。七月三十八度，她踩着两只羊上街。我问不热吗？她说：“羊毛，透气。”姐，羊自己热得都去剪毛了，你又给它穿回去？\n\n她图什么？图一脚蹬进去不用系鞋带，像回到妈妈怀里。代价呢？光脚闷一夏天，她一脱鞋，全办公室以为楼下烤串店开业了，还是羊肉串。\n\n穿到第三年，鞋跟往外撇，人往里倒，走路像两只企鹅抬着她。下场雨，麂皮全是水印，鞋面像世界地图。\n\n最讽刺的是，这叫雪地靴。她穿它蹚过三个夏天的水坑，唯独没见过雪：一到冬天，她嫌冷，换棉拖了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0752", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "google-glass", "target_name": "谷歌眼镜（Google Glass）", "facts": ["Unveiled at Google I/O 2012 with skydivers livestreaming their jump through it onto the Moscone Center roof.", "The Explorer Edition sold to invited users in 2013 for $1,500.", "Wearers were nicknamed 'Glassholes'; bars, cinemas, and casinos banned the device over its camera.", "The consumer programme ended in January 2015; an Enterprise Edition continued until Google stopped selling it in 2023.", "Its display was a 640-by-360 prism in the corner of one eye."], "a": {"item_id": "ccb8c9ae4f5c", "text": "戴谷歌眼镜进酒吧的人，我研究过，都是同一种人：花一千五百美元，就为了有人问他一句\"这是什么\"。他进门不先点酒，先点自己：\"OK Glass！\"全场安静，以为他在跟鬼说话。然后他说\"拍照\"，酒吧里所有人同时捂脸，你戴个眼镜，大家跟你上了安检。他还委屈：\"我就是想看看天气。\"哥们，在酒吧，天气就是头顶那盏灯。他右眼一直往上翻，看着像在回忆初恋，其实是在读促销邮件。英文里专门给这群人造了个词，叫Glasshole，得多招人烦，才能让一个词专门为你诞生。最后保安过来问：\"您在录像吗？\"他说：\"没有，它只是……亮着。\"被请出门，他对眼镜说\"导航回家\"，眼镜回：\"正在搜索信号。\"他站在路边，右眼是地图，左眼是现实，两只眼睛，过着两种人生。"}, "b": {"item_id": "90035f33aea2", "text": "这批戴谷歌眼镜进酒吧的人，我是真佩服。一千五百美金，就为了让全场一眼看出你在录像。酒吧是什么地方？是大家喝多了说胡话的地方，你坐那儿，镜片上还亮着个小灯，全场立刻开始演《黑镜》。他们自称\"探索者\"，人家给他们起的外号叫\"Glasshole\"，直译过来就是\"眼镜混蛋\"。后来好多酒吧门口直接一句\"摘了再进\"，比谷歌自己撤产品还干脆。我就想问一句：你图什么？图对着酒杯喊\"OK Glass\"？那你是成功了，全场都在看你，只不过看的是一个对着空气打嗝的人。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0753", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "graphene", "target_name": "石墨烯", "facts": ["Isolated in 2004 by Andre Geim and Konstantin Novoselov at Manchester by peeling graphite with Scotch tape; Nobel Prize in Physics, 2010.", "The EU's Graphene Flagship, launched in 2013, committed €1 billion over ten years.", "A 2018 study in Advanced Materials tested 'graphene' from 60 producers and found none with more than 50% graphene content; most was graphite flakes.", "Sold in tennis rackets, running shoes, and headphones as a performance ingredient.", "Geim also won an Ig Nobel Prize in 2000 for levitating a frog with magnets."], "a": {"item_id": "9b0a72d5be4f", "text": "饭桌上总有这种人，一开口就是：\"石墨烯要改变世界。\"你问他石墨烯是啥，他说就一层碳原子，导电好，导热快。行，那你告诉我，你脚下这双\"石墨烯暖宝宝\"鞋垫，里面到底有几层碳？他沉默三秒，说：\"暖和就行。\"这话我信，暖和是真的，毕竟它本质上就是个电热丝套了个新名字。前几年搞石墨烯概念的公司，股价一路涨，财报上研发投入写得比谁都大，产品呢，是往涂料里撒一把黑粉，再印上\"石墨烯\"三个字。投资人亏得哭，创始人转头去讲元宇宙了。世界没变，就他朋友圈头像换成了一张论文截图。"}, "b": {"item_id": "70e5e600bd85", "text": "我有个表舅，逢人就说石墨烯要改变世界：一层碳原子，比钢硬两百倍，导电比铜还快。你以为他是科学家？他是卖秋裤的。石墨烯秋裤，三百九一条，号称发热、抗菌、通经络。\n\n我问他世界啥时候改变？他说快了！二〇〇四年人家拿透明胶从铅笔芯上粘下来的，诺贝尔奖都发了。我说对啊，二十年了，奖人家领走了，透明胶还在卖，就你的秋裤卖不动。\n\n后来他把卖秋裤的钱全砸进石墨烯概念股，世界确实改变了，他的世界。现在他穿着自己那条秋裤跟人说：“石墨烯，薄啊，一层就一个原子那么薄。跟我家底一样。”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0754", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "emacs", "target_name": "Emacs", "facts": ["Began in 1976 as a set of macros for the TECO editor at MIT's AI Lab, written by Richard Stallman and others; GNU Emacs followed in 1985.", "Extensible in its own Lisp dialect, Emacs Lisp; the standing joke is that it is a fine operating system lacking only a good editor.", "Ships with a psychotherapist (M-x doctor), Tetris, a mail client, a calendar, and a web browser (eww).", "An old backronym is 'Eight Megabytes And Constantly Swapping', from when 8 MB was a lot of memory.", "'Emacs pinky' is the name for the hand strain blamed on its Control-key chords."], "a": {"item_id": "45d1386d9957", "text": "我有个朋友，把Emacs当操作系统用。邮件用Gnus，上网用eww，终端开eshell，连窗口管理器都换成了EXWM。我问他图什么，他说：“图一辈子不用离开Emacs。”兄弟，这不叫生活，这叫软禁。他的init.el写了四千行，比毕业论文还长，每天上班第一件事，是修昨晚改坏的配置。一年下来，他就产出了俩东西：一个能用M-x点外卖的配置，一根被Ctrl键按废的小拇指。上礼拜Emacs一崩，他当场断网、断邮件、断社交，就像自己盖的房子塌了，人还埋在里头。现在他想开了，准备装Evil-mode改用Vim键位。好家伙，操作系统是齐了，就缺一个好用的编辑器。"}, "b": {"item_id": "f945ff696b4a", "text": "把 Emacs 当操作系统用的人，我见过。早上在 Emacs 里收邮件，中午在 org-mode 里排日程，晚上在 Emacs 里听音乐、写日记、看股票。他管这叫\"一站式工作流\"。\n\n有天他想关机，按了 C-x C-c，屏幕跳出一行字：\"Modified buffers exist; exit anyway? (yes or no)\"。他心想我又没改什么，一看，十四个 buffer 没保存，其中一个是他写了三年的日记。\n\n他盯着屏幕想了半分钟，最后打出了 yes。然后他意识到，这个操作系统唯一会问他要不要关机的时候，也是唯一不让他关机的时候。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0755", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "durian", "target_name": "榴莲", "facts": ["Banned on Singapore's MRT trains and in many Southeast Asian hotels because of the smell.", "A 2017 genome study in Nature Genetics traced the odour to a duplicated family of MGL genes that pump out volatile sulfur compounds.", "China buys most of the world's durian exports, chiefly from Thailand, in a trade worth billions of dollars a year.", "Alfred Russel Wallace wrote in 1856 that eating it was 'a new sensation worth a voyage to the East to experience'."], "a": {"item_id": "17f9f9e15e60", "text": "在高铁上吃榴莲的人，我一直想知道他图什么。图香？他鼻子早被腌入味了，闻不到。他以为自己在吃水果，其实全车厢都在被迫吃他的午饭。\n\n最绝的是他还特别坦荡，转头跟邻座说：\"你尝一口就懂了。\"大哥，我们早尝了，用鼻子，从郑州尝到武汉。\n\n下场也很统一。乘务员走过来，不骂你，就问一句：\"这是谁的？\"全车厢没人说话，但一百个鼻子同时转向你，这叫集体指认。\n\n你想销毁证据？壳带刺，塞包里扎手，包也成了证物。回家洗五遍手，老婆一开门：\"你今天去哪个案发现场了？\"晚上打个嗝，等于二次自首。"}, "b": {"item_id": "aea5369accce", "text": "有些人在地铁上吃榴莲，图的就是一个\"我不在乎\"。你看他闭着眼，一脸品红酒的表情，可榴莲壳上的刺是扎手的，果肉是黏的，他一瓣一瓣抠出来，手指上全是黄色的汁。车厢里二十个人，被迫跟他一起品鉴。那味道先是甜，甜完一秒，洋葱、奶油、还有一股硫磺的冲劲，直接从你鼻孔顶到脑门。车门一开，他抱着半个带刺的壳下车，回头冲大家笑了一下。全车人都在看他，我猜他以为自己在过生活，其实他是今天全车唯一被记住的人，记住的还是那股味儿。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0756", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "nuclear-fusion", "target_name": "可控核聚变", "facts": ["Has been 'about 30 years away' since the 1950s; at the 1955 Geneva atoms-for-peace conference, Homi Bhabha predicted fusion power within two decades.", "ITER, agreed in 2006 for first plasma in 2016, re-baselined in 2024 to start research operations in 2034 and deuterium-tritium burning in 2039, at a cost estimated above €20 billion.", "NIF's December 2022 'ignition' shot released 3.15 MJ from 2.05 MJ of laser energy; the lasers drew about 300 MJ from the grid to fire.", "Private fusion companies had raised more than $7 billion by 2024."], "a": {"item_id": "04cdb6ae7f1b", "text": "搞可控核聚变的人，我是真佩服。他们能用磁场把一亿度的等离子体悬在半空，却没法把“还有十年”悬在十年。\n\n一个博士二十五岁入行，导师说还有三十年。他五十五岁带学生，说还有三十年。退休那天他把这句话传给徒弟，比传家宝还稳。\n\n图什么？图下一期经费。真搞成了，没人给你拨款；搞不成，永远有人拨。他们最怕的不是等离子体不稳定，是稳定了。\n\n前两年美国激光点火成功，全网欢呼，说输出能量比输入多。一算，激光器从电网吃的电是输出的一百多倍。这叫净增益，净亏本。\n\n下场呢？ITER从1985年谈到现在，预算翻了几番，一代人的青春，换来一句“明年的明年”。\n\n太阳烧了四十六亿年，从没交过项目申请。人造太阳，年年得写结题报告。"}, "b": {"item_id": "0c1b7366a47b", "text": "搞可控核聚变的那帮人，我真佩服。每次申请经费，他们都拍着胸脯说：再给十年，就能把太阳装进罐子里。这话从上世纪五十年代说到现在，太阳没装进去，罐子倒是越造越大。ITER画图画了三十多年，工期一延再延，预算翻了好几倍，钱烧得比等离子体还热。你问他们图什么？图的是海水里那点氘，图的是一个不冒烟的未来。落得什么下场？当年的博士生，如今成了白头发的教授，还在给学生画同一张饼：就差最后一公里了。这最后一公里，比太阳的寿命还长。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0757", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "google-glass", "target_name": "谷歌眼镜（Google Glass）", "facts": ["Unveiled at Google I/O 2012 with skydivers livestreaming their jump through it onto the Moscone Center roof.", "The Explorer Edition sold to invited users in 2013 for $1,500.", "Wearers were nicknamed 'Glassholes'; bars, cinemas, and casinos banned the device over its camera.", "The consumer programme ended in January 2015; an Enterprise Edition continued until Google stopped selling it in 2023.", "Its display was a 640-by-360 prism in the corner of one eye."], "a": {"item_id": "3f70392c889f", "text": "戴谷歌眼镜进酒吧的人，我真想采访一下：你图什么？花一千五百美元，抢到一个\"探索者\"资格。注意，是资格，你得先被允许掏钱。然后戴着它进酒吧，就等人问一句：\"哥们，这是啥？\"\n\n结果没人问。大家只看你右眼上方那块小玻璃，心想：他是不是在录我？\n\n你想搭讪，张嘴就是\"OK Glass，拍照\"，姑娘以为你在对她说\"OK\"，转身走了。你想看个时间，还得仰头斜眼，盯着右上角，活像在找天花板上的WiFi。\n\n最后酒保说：\"把那个摘了。\"你说：\"这是未来。\"酒保说：\"未来请在门口等。\"\n\n你被请出去，站在马路边，对着空气喊\"OK Glass，叫车\"。它回你一句：没听清。\n\n一千五百美元，买了个让全酒吧都把你当偷拍狂的右眼显示器。"}, "b": {"item_id": "e9562ca8081e", "text": "戴谷歌眼镜进酒吧的人，我见过。通常三十出头，帽衫上印着“改变世界”，花一千五百美金买了块架在眼前的小棱镜。图什么？图别人一进门先看见他，然后问一句“这是啥”，他就能顺势讲“这是未来”。结果呢？他对着镜腿说“OK Glass，拍下她”，那个小红灯一亮，姑娘直接把一杯啤酒泼在他脸上。保安把他请出去，顺手把眼镜摘了。第二天网上就给他们起了名字，叫“眼镜混蛋”。那玩意儿后来停产了，他也没改变世界，只把那晚的酒吧改成了一个全员提防他的地方。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0758", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "vision-pro", "target_name": "Apple Vision Pro", "facts": ["Released in February 2024 at $3,499, with an external battery pack on a cable rated for about two hours.", "'EyeSight' shows a rendering of the wearer's eyes on an outward-facing screen.", "Apple calls it 'spatial computing' and its App Store rules tell developers not to describe apps as AR, VR, XR, or MR.", "Reports during 2024 said Apple had cut production; analysts' first-year sales estimates were well under a million units.", "Weighs 600-650 g, before the battery."], "a": {"item_id": "c894b12374a0", "text": "地铁上戴 Vision Pro 的人，我研究过了：花了两万九，最大的需求是让全车人知道。\n\n这玩意儿最大的卖点，是把外面的世界透传给你看。三万块，让你看见了你本来就在的这节车厢。我拿肉眼看，免费，还没延迟。\n\n他还得在空气里捏手指，一会儿放大，一会儿点击。旁边大妈以为他在捉苍蝇，再不然就是在隔空给她算命。\n\n最惨的是，前面那块屏幕专门显示他的眼睛，好让别人觉得他\"在场\"。可全车人透过那双假眼睛，看到的是一个被电池线拴在裤兜里、一动不敢动的人。\n\n电量撑两小时。他还没到站，已经先关机了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "990fbbeab954", "text": "地铁上戴 Vision Pro 的人，我见过三类。第一类想显摆，结果他脸上扣着一块 3500 美元的玻璃，外屏还亮着他的眼睛，全车人都在和一双发光的眼睛对视，像鱼缸里的金鱼被人盯着看。第二类想沉浸式追剧，他在悬浮的大屏前对着空气捏了五次手指，每次都没对上，像在给一个看不见的人做心肺复苏。第三类最惨，他外接的电池线挂在脖子上，一只手在空中乱点，另一只手拎着电池，他把眼前的 4K 巨幕当成了整个世界，于是一头撞上了真的闸机，而那个闸机，连手势都不用捏。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0759", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "emacs", "target_name": "Emacs", "facts": ["Began in 1976 as a set of macros for the TECO editor at MIT's AI Lab, written by Richard Stallman and others; GNU Emacs followed in 1985.", "Extensible in its own Lisp dialect, Emacs Lisp; the standing joke is that it is a fine operating system lacking only a good editor.", "Ships with a psychotherapist (M-x doctor), Tetris, a mail client, a calendar, and a web browser (eww).", "An old backronym is 'Eight Megabytes And Constantly Swapping', from when 8 MB was a lot of memory.", "'Emacs pinky' is the name for the hand strain blamed on its Control-key chords."], "a": {"item_id": "19ed8eac97e6", "text": "我认识一个把Emacs当操作系统的哥们。他用Emacs收邮件、看新闻、听歌、管日程。我问：“那你干嘛不直接装个操作系统？”他推了推眼镜：“Emacs就缺一个好编辑器。”说完笑得特别得意，那种全世界只有我懂的得意。\n\n他的待办用Org-mode管，第一条是“优化Org-mode配置”，已经挂了三年。init.el写了八千行，比他这辈子写过的业务代码都长。启动一次要四十七秒，所以他从不关，上次重启是因为搬家断电。\n\n小指因为按Ctrl已经变形了，医生问怎么弄的，他说“写东西”。女朋友说出来吃个饭，他说等我写个函数。函数写好了，女朋友没了。\n\n现在他唯一的倾诉对象是M-x doctor，一个七十年代的假心理医生。他说我好孤独，它说：“你为什么这么说？”\n\n他说，这是他用过最懂他的软件。"}, "b": {"item_id": "d38cb162db73", "text": "我有个朋友，把Emacs当操作系统用。邮件用mu4e，日程用org-mode，连窗口管理都交给EXWM。他的电脑开机只有一个目的：启动Emacs。\n\n我问他图什么，他说：“这样我一辈子都不用离开Emacs。”兄弟，这不叫自由，这叫软禁，门还是你自己拿elisp焊死的。\n\n他的init.el写了三千行，比他简历还长。上周升级了一个包，邮件收不了，日程没了，窗口也打不开，全家断电。查了一晚上，原因是少了一个括号。\n\n最惨的是他的小拇指。按了十年Ctrl，得了腱鞘炎。医生让他少按，他回家装了evil-mode。用Vim的键位，在Emacs里，躲Emacs。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0760", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "juicero", "target_name": "Juicero 榨汁机", "facts": ["A Wi-Fi-connected cold-press juicer launched in 2016 at $699, later cut to $399, that squeezed proprietary bags of chopped produce sold by subscription.", "Raised about $120 million from investors including Google Ventures and Kleiner Perkins.", "In April 2017, Bloomberg reporters squeezed the packs by hand and got 7.5 ounces of juice in 90 seconds, faster than the machine's 8 ounces in about two minutes.", "Each pack carried a QR code that the press scanned and checked online before squeezing; it refused expired packs and would not run at all unless connected to Wi-Fi.", "Shut down in September 2017 and offered refunds on the machine."], "a": {"item_id": "e1fe9018b6c6", "text": "买 Juicero 的人我研究过：住旧金山，爱喝\"冷压\"，觉得自己动手捏个袋子太掉价，得花 400 美元雇一台联网的机器替他捏。\n\n这机器号称四吨压力。后来彭博社发现，徒手捏那个果汁包，出汁还更快。也就是说，这帮人花 400 块，买的是\"捏袋子需要 WiFi\"的权利。\n\n更绝的是，果汁包过期，机器直接罢工。它比你妈还关心你的健康。\n\n最后公司倒闭，全额退款。钱拿回来了，脸没拿回来。从此这帮人看自己的手，都带着一种复杂的情绪：这玩意儿，一直是免费的。"}, "b": {"item_id": "90365272a695", "text": "花400美元买Juicero的都是什么人？就是那种觉得“我自己的手不够智能”的人。你买的根本不是榨汁机，是一台连WiFi的拳头。它这辈子唯一的工作，就是捏一包早就榨好的菜泥。彭博社记者徒手去捏，比它还快。想想那画面：硅谷精英每天早上站在厨房，等机器扫完二维码、联网确认这包菜没过期，它才肯开工。为了“健康生活”，每包再掏五六块，喝的其实是自己的智商汁。后来公司倒闭，机器连不上服务器，成了一块四百刀的案板。唯一还能用的，是你那双手。恭喜，绕了一大圈，你终于恢复了出厂设置。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0761", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "pmma", "target_name": "PMMA 电子束光刻胶", "facts": ["The same polymer as Plexiglas and acrylic glass, dissolved in anisole or chlorobenzene and spun into a thin film.", "The oldest electron-beam resist still in everyday use; IBM researchers reported it as one in the late 1960s.", "Positive tone: the beam breaks polymer chains and the exposed region dissolves in a MIBK:IPA developer.", "Resolves below 10 nm but has poor sensitivity, so doses of hundreds to over a thousand µC/cm² (depending on voltage) make writing slow.", "Poor dry-etch resistance, so it is used mostly for lift-off rather than as an etch mask."], "a": {"item_id": "5266dfb33ccd", "text": "我认识一帮做纳米器件的博士，实验室一台电子束曝光机，几千万，往上涂的是PMMA。说白了就是亚克力，奶茶店吸管盒那个料。几千万的电子枪，天天给鱼缸刻字。\n\n问导师为啥不换，导师说：“分辨率好，十纳米！”好是好，就是灵敏度低，剂量得往死里加。机时按小时收钱，一块片子写一宿，学生睡机房门口，跟排专家号似的。\n\n显影液MIBK兑异丙醇一比三，配方七十年代传下来的，比他家族谱还稳。\n\n剂量加猛了它还交联，正胶变负胶。跟这帮博士一样，熬太久，人就反了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "71032dd5e938", "text": "做电子束的人，你得理解，他们是一群对新材料过敏的人。你问为什么还在用PMMA，他一脸骄傲：“分辨率高，十纳米以下！”\n\n对，这玩意儿六十年代的，跟你家鱼缸、亚克力牙托是亲戚。他们拿牙托在刻十纳米。\n\n图什么？图稳。师兄留下的配方：950K，A4，4000转，180度烘三分钟，MIBK兑IPA一比三显影。没人知道为什么，但谁改谁延毕。\n\n下场呢？曝光剂量几百微库每平方厘米，一个图案写一晚上。第二天一看，束漂了，套刻对不上。好不容易写完去刻蚀，PMMA一见等离子体就没了，像纸糊的盾牌。\n\n最后博士五年读六年，论文里还得写：“PMMA以高分辨率著称。”\n\n是啊，胶是分辨率高、灵敏度低，人也是。对新材料，毫无反应。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0762", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "las-vegas", "target_name": "拉斯维加斯", "facts": ["Nevada legalized casino gambling in 1931, the year construction of the Hoover Dam began nearby.", "Most of the Strip lies not in the city of Las Vegas but in the unincorporated town of Paradise.", "The Sphere, opened in 2023 at a cost of about $2.3 billion, is the largest spherical building in the world.", "'What happens here, stays here' was launched by the local convention and visitors authority in 2003.", "Lake Mead, its water source, fell in July 2022 to about 1,040 feet, its lowest since the reservoir first filled in 1937, and dropped below that mark again in August 2026."], "a": {"item_id": "c54ee644ce17", "text": "去拉斯维加斯发财的人，最让我服气的是自信。个个都说“我研究过”。研究了啥？油管三分钟《21点必胜法》。人家赌场是几十亿砸出来的，你是三分钟视频，这叫博士打小学生。\n\n他们图什么？不图钱，图一句“我在维加斯赢过”。第一晚赢两百，感觉自己是邦德，服务员送免费酒，你以为是请客，那是预付款。喝到第三杯，你把全部押在红色，理由是“看着吉利”。\n\n下场很统一：赌场没窗户没钟，你以为晚上十点，其实周三了。最后站在ATM前，手续费十八块你都不心疼，已经输麻了。到机场，还有老虎机，你居然想“最后试一把”。\n\n回家有人问，你说：“我没输，我是去玩的。”对，你花四千块，买了一句“我没输”。"}, "b": {"item_id": "bd02132185ac", "text": "我有个朋友，看了三遍《决胜21点》，觉得自己会算牌，买了张去拉斯维加斯的机票，说要去沙漠里挖金子。\n\n兄弟，人家能在沙漠里建一座城，靠的就是你这种来浇水的。\n\n他到了才发现，赌场里没窗户、没钟，酒还免费。你品，一个地方怕你知道几点、还主动把你灌醉，它是想让你赢吗？\n\n三天后他回来了，钱全没了，就剩一张自助餐券，外加一张小教堂的结婚证，证婚人是“猫王”，新娘是谁他想不起来。\n\n他还安慰自己：“在维加斯发生的，就留在维加斯。”\n\n对，你的钱也是这么想的。\n\n最绝的是候机的时候，他看见登机口旁边还有一排老虎机，又塞进去二十块。拉斯维加斯就是这样，连你走，都要再收一回过路费。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0763", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "php", "target_name": "PHP", "facts": ["Created by Rasmus Lerdorf in 1994 as 'Personal Home Page Tools', a set of C programs for tracking visitors to his own résumé page; the name now officially stands for the recursive 'PHP: Hypertext Preprocessor'.", "Lerdorf, in his 2007 MySQL Conference talk 'PHP on Hormones': 'I'm not a real programmer. I throw together things until it works then I move on.'", "Runs WordPress, which W3Techs counted on 39.4% of all websites (58.1% of sites with a known CMS) on 9 November 2026.", "Core function names are inconsistent (strpos, str_replace, htmlspecialchars, nl2br) partly because, as Lerdorf confirmed, early function names were chosen to balance a hash table keyed on string length.", "Until PHP 8 (2020), the comparison 0 == 'foo' was true because the string was silently converted to a number."], "a": {"item_id": "8dd77ec7826a", "text": "我有个朋友，靠写PHP吃饭。你问他干啥的，他第一句永远是“PHP是世界上最好的语言”，说完自己先笑。这是他这行唯一的笑点，也是唯一的尊严。\n\n他图啥？图门槛低。2010年自学三天，用织梦给楼下火锅店搭了个网站，收八百，觉得自己要财务自由了。十五年过去，他还在搭站，还是八百。火锅店倒了，现在接的是美甲店。\n\n他的工作流程：FileZilla连上服务器，直接改线上文件，刷新，白屏；再改，再刷新，又白屏。他管这叫“敏捷开发”。\n\n他代码里全是美元符号，一个变量一个$，写了一辈子$，就没见过几张。\n\n最近他焦虑，说PHP要死了。我说你放心，它死不了，全世界四成网站还挂在WordPress上呢。会死的是你。"}, "b": {"item_id": "e721ec283ce4", "text": "干PHP吃饭的人，我认识三类。第一类是接外包的，客户说\"就改个按钮颜色\"，他打开一看，代码里还留着 mysql_connect，注释是2009年的，他知道这活要干到天亮。第二类是背框架的，嘴里全是Laravel，自己的小网站却还是 $_GET 直接拼进SQL，问他为啥，他说\"这叫传统\"。第三类图的就是那句\"语法简单，上手快\"，结果干了十年，还在查 \"0\" == false 为什么是真的。去年他投简历，HR问他精通什么，他写\"PHP\"，面试官问 == 和 === 有啥区别，他沉默三秒，把\"精通\"两个字划掉了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0764", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "brasilia", "target_name": "巴西利亚", "facts": ["Inaugurated on 21 April 1960 after about 41 months of construction, moving the capital inland from Rio de Janeiro.", "Lúcio Costa's Plano Piloto is laid out like an airplane (or a bird, or a cross), with Oscar Niemeyer's government buildings along the fuselage.", "Listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1987, less than 30 years after it was built.", "Planned for 500,000 residents; the Federal District now has about 3 million, most in satellite towns outside the plan.", "Built for the car: wide avenues, few pedestrian crossings, and distances that make walking impractical."], "a": {"item_id": "326fa522889c", "text": "在巴西利亚靠两条腿出门的人，我得给他们立个碑。这帮人是谁？多半是刚搬来的新人，手机一打开，地图写着\"步行二十八分钟\"，他们就信了。图什么？图锻炼，图省钱，图不想等那趟十四分钟一班、还不一定来的公交。结果呢？整条Eixo Monumental上，人行道说没就没，只剩一条给卡车跑的高速路，他们只能贴着护栏，顶着尾气走。好不容易走到立交桥底下想抄近路，四个车道，红灯十秒。最后他站在W3大道中间那条绿化带上，左边是车，右边也是车，低头看手机，地图淡定地提示：\"您已到达目的地。\""}, "b": {"item_id": "7ded9e81696c", "text": "在巴西利亚走路的人，就两种：建筑系学生，和看地图觉得\"才两公里\"的游客。\n\n国会大厦就在前面，清清楚楚。你走二十分钟，它还在前面，一点没大。建筑师管这叫\"纪念性尺度\"，我管这叫海市蜃楼。\n\n图什么？图\"亲身体验世界遗产\"。结果这城是按飞机设计的，汽车安排得明明白白，行人？没设计。你只好在草地上踩出一条红泥路，当地人管这叫\"欲望之路\"，听着挺浪漫，其实就是一群跟你一样的人集体迷路的证据。\n\n下场都一样：裤腿红到膝盖，嘴唇在一千米海拔上裂开，最后卡在一座立交桥下，对面看得见，就是过不去。\n\n你叫了辆车，司机到了问：\"你怎么在这儿？\"\n\n你说：\"想感受一下城市。\"\n\n他说：\"这不是城市，这是飞机。你在机翼上散步。\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0765", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "nuclear-fusion", "target_name": "可控核聚变", "facts": ["Has been 'about 30 years away' since the 1950s; at the 1955 Geneva atoms-for-peace conference, Homi Bhabha predicted fusion power within two decades.", "ITER, agreed in 2006 for first plasma in 2016, re-baselined in 2024 to start research operations in 2034 and deuterium-tritium burning in 2039, at a cost estimated above €20 billion.", "NIF's December 2022 'ignition' shot released 3.15 MJ from 2.05 MJ of laser energy; the lasers drew about 300 MJ from the grid to fire.", "Private fusion companies had raised more than $7 billion by 2024."], "a": {"item_id": "8315ced7f71d", "text": "可控核聚变还有十年，这话我爸上大学时就听过，是他老师说的。现在我听，说的人是他老师的学生。这行最稳定的不是等离子体，是那句“还有十年”，代代相传，比托卡马克里那团火命长多了。\n\n干这行的人图什么？你想啊，一个人一辈子的成果，是把一团一亿度的气体多关住了三十秒。开发布会，“创世界纪录！”台下鼓掌。他自己心里清楚，下个月还得写报告申请经费，因为磁约束线圈又烧了。\n\n最惨的是退休那天。学生问他：“老师，您干了四十年，离点火成功还有多远？”他想了想，特别诚实：“还有十年。”\n\n他入行那年也是这么说的。四十年过去，一天都没少。这才叫能量守恒。"}, "b": {"item_id": "203425c81d14", "text": "\"可控核聚变还有十年\"，这句话我听了三十年，每次都是十年。干这行的是一群想把太阳关进罐子里的人，目标很朴素：让一亿度的等离子体乖乖悬在磁场里，别碰墙，别熄火。图什么？图经费，图论文，图退休前在新闻里看见自己的名字。结果呢？年年开会，年年说\"快了\"，PPT翻到第四十页，二〇三〇悄悄变成了二〇四〇。有个老教授等了一辈子，临退休还在改时间表，他说：\"太阳烧了四十亿年不用拨款，我们想关它一下，先得给罐子要钱。\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0766", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "vision-pro", "target_name": "Apple Vision Pro", "facts": ["Released in February 2024 at $3,499, with an external battery pack on a cable rated for about two hours.", "'EyeSight' shows a rendering of the wearer's eyes on an outward-facing screen.", "Apple calls it 'spatial computing' and its App Store rules tell developers not to describe apps as AR, VR, XR, or MR.", "Reports during 2024 said Apple had cut production; analysts' first-year sales estimates were well under a million units.", "Weighs 600-650 g, before the battery."], "a": {"item_id": "2c1f7e589f71", "text": "早高峰戴Vision Pro的哥们，我懂他。三万五买的，在家戴没人看见，只有地铁上观众够多。\n\n他图啥？图\"空间计算\"。结果一开机，透视模式：花三万五，看十号线，还带点延迟。\n\n更惨的是手势。人家在虚拟世界里优雅地捏一下就点开了，现实里就是对着空气一直捏，全车人以为他在抓蚊子。\n\n还有那根电池线，从脑袋连到裤兜。大爷一看：哟，新款拉环。一拽，当场表演\"摘头\"。\n\n最惨的是前面那块屏，专门显示他的眼睛，AI合成的，深情款款。他本人在看十号线，他的假眼睛在看你。你说他是在体验未来，还是在给未来当群演？"}, "b": {"item_id": "990fbbeab954", "text": "地铁上戴 Vision Pro 的人，我见过三类。第一类想显摆，结果他脸上扣着一块 3500 美元的玻璃，外屏还亮着他的眼睛，全车人都在和一双发光的眼睛对视，像鱼缸里的金鱼被人盯着看。第二类想沉浸式追剧，他在悬浮的大屏前对着空气捏了五次手指，每次都没对上，像在给一个看不见的人做心肺复苏。第三类最惨，他外接的电池线挂在脖子上，一只手在空中乱点，另一只手拎着电池，他把眼前的 4K 巨幕当成了整个世界，于是一头撞上了真的闸机，而那个闸机，连手势都不用捏。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0767", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hawaiian-pizza", "target_name": "夏威夷披萨", "facts": ["Invented in 1962 by Sam Panopoulos, a Greek immigrant, at the Satellite Restaurant in Chatham, Ontario, Canada; named after the brand of canned pineapple he used.", "Iceland's president Guðni Th. Jóhannesson said in 2017 that he would ban pineapple on pizza if he could, then clarified that a president has no such power.", "Hawaii is no longer a major pineapple producer: Del Monte shut its Hawaiian pineapple operations in November 2006, two years ahead of its announced 2008 exit, the state's last cannery closed in 2007, and most canned pineapple now comes from Thailand, Indonesia, and the Philippines.", "Panopoulos, whose restaurant also served Chinese dishes, told the Toronto Sun in 2010 that 'in those days people didn't mix the tastes like sweet and sour, except for Chinese food'."], "a": {"item_id": "5777117eb6e0", "text": "我发现，点夏威夷披萨的人有个共同点：菜单上二十种口味，他偏要带菠萝的。你问为什么，他说“想吃点水果”。兄弟，想吃水果，隔壁就是水果店！你这是把果盘塞进烤箱赎罪啊。\n\n他图什么？图个“我很包容”。他点一个夏威夷，全桌就得陪他吵半小时“菠萝能不能上披萨”，他的人设就立住了：全场只有他敢。\n\n下场呢？披萨一到，热菠萝一咬，汁“滋”地喷上颚，烫得他眼泪直流，还得笑着说“嗯，酸甜，有层次”。最惨的是，这玩意儿是加拿大人发明的，夏威夷人自己都不认。你为了一块夏威夷都不要的披萨，得罪了整个意大利。"}, "b": {"item_id": "273e9ae28d95", "text": "点夏威夷披萨的人，我一直想不通。你说你爱吃甜的，行啊，菠萝罐头买一罐，一勺一勺舀着吃，多痛快。你偏要把它摊在一张发面饼上，烤到边缘焦黄，再铺一层咸火腿，然后跟人说：\"这叫冲突之美。\"冲突你个头！菠萝一烤出汁，整张饼泡成了湿吐司。你咬一口，烫，甜，咸，三个味道在嘴里开会，谁也不服谁，散会的时候你只剩一嘴纸巾，和一句\"其实还行\"。这种人我见过，每次聚餐都点它，然后全桌人默默把菠萝挑到盘子边上，堆成一座小山，像一群赖着不走的客人。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0768", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "vision-pro", "target_name": "Apple Vision Pro", "facts": ["Released in February 2024 at $3,499, with an external battery pack on a cable rated for about two hours.", "'EyeSight' shows a rendering of the wearer's eyes on an outward-facing screen.", "Apple calls it 'spatial computing' and its App Store rules tell developers not to describe apps as AR, VR, XR, or MR.", "Reports during 2024 said Apple had cut production; analysts' first-year sales estimates were well under a million units.", "Weighs 600-650 g, before the battery."], "a": {"item_id": "2c1f7e589f71", "text": "早高峰戴Vision Pro的哥们，我懂他。三万五买的，在家戴没人看见，只有地铁上观众够多。\n\n他图啥？图\"空间计算\"。结果一开机，透视模式：花三万五，看十号线，还带点延迟。\n\n更惨的是手势。人家在虚拟世界里优雅地捏一下就点开了，现实里就是对着空气一直捏，全车人以为他在抓蚊子。\n\n还有那根电池线，从脑袋连到裤兜。大爷一看：哟，新款拉环。一拽，当场表演\"摘头\"。\n\n最惨的是前面那块屏，专门显示他的眼睛，AI合成的，深情款款。他本人在看十号线，他的假眼睛在看你。你说他是在体验未来，还是在给未来当群演？"}, "b": {"item_id": "6311e32fb855", "text": "地铁上见过一哥们，戴着Vision Pro。三万块钱扣在脸上，跟把首付戴出门了似的。你图啥？显得有钱？有钱人不坐地铁啊兄弟！\n\n他还挺投入，对着空气捏手指，捏一下，旁边大姐退一步。人家以为他在掐诀，准备做法。\n\n最绝的是前面那块屏，会给你看他的“眼睛”。他一进沉浸模式看电影，那双眼睛就变成一团彩色雾，全车厢都知道：这人灵魂出窍了，肉身暂存二号线。\n\n地铁一刹车，画面飘了，他伸手去抓虚拟窗口，攥住了一个大爷的保温杯。\n\n然后电池没电了，线还从脸上一路连到裤兜里，跟刚做完手术没拔管子似的。续航两小时，刚好够他坐过站。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0769", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "windows-vista", "target_name": "Windows Vista", "facts": ["Released to consumers in January 2007, after the 'Longhorn' project was reset in 2004 and headline features such as the WinFS file system were dropped.", "User Account Control prompted so often that Apple's 'Get a Mac' campaign ran an ad about 'Cancel or Allow'.", "'Windows Vista Capable' stickers went on PCs that could only run Home Basic, without the Aero interface, and led to a class-action lawsuit.", "Windows 7 (2009), widely praised, was largely a tuned-up Vista on the same kernel line; Vista support ended in April 2017."], "a": {"item_id": "ebfddca0e411", "text": "第一天就升级Vista的人，你们公司肯定有一个，就是那个说“我对电脑懂一点”的。\n\n他图什么？图毛玻璃。Aero，窗口是半透明的，他觉得自己电脑一下子有钱了。结果他那台机器贴着“Vista Capable”的贴纸，意思是“能运行Vista”，但只能运行没有毛玻璃的Vista。花钱升级，就为了得到一个不透明的窗口。\n\n然后是UAC。他点一下，弹窗：“是否允许？”允许。又弹：“您确定吗？”确定。一天里被问“确定”的次数，比他求婚还多。\n\n打印机没驱动，声卡没驱动，最后他默默把XP装回去。同事问：“怎么样？”\n\n他说：“挺好，我体验了一下，什么叫花钱买降级。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "c62e9c0142f3", "text": "我有个朋友，2007年Vista首发当天零点就去排队，还非买旗舰版，三百九十九美元。图什么？广告说了：“Wow从现在开始。”\n\n确实，开机那一刻他就“哇”了：打印机没驱动，声卡没驱动，他那台贴着“Vista Capable”标签的电脑，连Aero玻璃特效都跑不动。他每点一下，屏幕就一黑：“Windows需要您的许可才能继续。”点“允许”，又黑一下。半年下来，他成了全世界被自己电脑审问最多的人。\n\n旗舰版承诺的“终极附加功能”，最后等来一副德州扑克。\n\n现在他逢人就推荐一个高级功能，叫“降级到XP”。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0770", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "canberra", "target_name": "堪培拉", "facts": ["Chosen as a compromise because neither Sydney nor Melbourne would accept the other as capital; Australia's constitution required the capital to be at least 100 miles from Sydney.", "Designed by the Chicago architects Walter Burley Griffin and Marion Mahony Griffin, who won an international competition in 1912.", "Its centrepiece, Lake Burley Griffin, is artificial, created by damming the Molonglo River in 1963-64.", "Parliament House (1988) is built into Capital Hill under a lawn meant for citizens to walk over their politicians; the lawn was fenced off in 2017 for security.", "Melbourne served as the capital until 1927 while Canberra was being built."], "a": {"item_id": "0d2a6bbab13d", "text": "我有个朋友，为了工作搬去堪培拉。他说：“联邦公务员，铁饭碗，三年升EL1。”兄弟，你才二十八岁，人生规划写得跟预算案附录一样。\n\n去了一年，他说话全成了缩写：“我在DFAT，赶上MOG调整，等APS6转正。”我问他周末干嘛，他说开两个多小时去贝特曼斯湾看海。你搬去一个城市，生活质量全看你多快能离开它。\n\n最惨的是，他现在会主动跟人说：“堪培拉其实挺好的，你得慢慢了解它。”这话我只在两种人嘴里听过：堪培拉人，和被关太久、开始同情绑匪的人质。"}, "b": {"item_id": "46416cb1f80d", "text": "你身边肯定有这种人：为了“离权力近一点”，从悉尼搬去堪培拉。公务员、政策顾问、游说公司的，图的都是升职。三年后，他们每天开四十分钟车去一栋楼，写四十页报告，结论永远是“建议进一步研究”。周五下午，全城人开三小时车回悉尼，就剩他们守着伯利·格里芬湖。全城最大的娱乐是绕湖跑步，跑完一圈，正好跑回办公室。权力确实在附近，就在国会大厦里，可每次想见，都得先预约，还得等部长助理回邮件。离权力近了，离生活远了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0771", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "google-glass", "target_name": "谷歌眼镜（Google Glass）", "facts": ["Unveiled at Google I/O 2012 with skydivers livestreaming their jump through it onto the Moscone Center roof.", "The Explorer Edition sold to invited users in 2013 for $1,500.", "Wearers were nicknamed 'Glassholes'; bars, cinemas, and casinos banned the device over its camera.", "The consumer programme ended in January 2015; an Enterprise Edition continued until Google stopped selling it in 2023.", "Its display was a 640-by-360 prism in the corner of one eye."], "a": {"item_id": "90035f33aea2", "text": "这批戴谷歌眼镜进酒吧的人，我是真佩服。一千五百美金，就为了让全场一眼看出你在录像。酒吧是什么地方？是大家喝多了说胡话的地方，你坐那儿，镜片上还亮着个小灯，全场立刻开始演《黑镜》。他们自称\"探索者\"，人家给他们起的外号叫\"Glasshole\"，直译过来就是\"眼镜混蛋\"。后来好多酒吧门口直接一句\"摘了再进\"，比谷歌自己撤产品还干脆。我就想问一句：你图什么？图对着酒杯喊\"OK Glass\"？那你是成功了，全场都在看你，只不过看的是一个对着空气打嗝的人。"}, "b": {"item_id": "5674fb13d6e0", "text": "戴谷歌眼镜进酒吧的哥们儿，你图什么？花一千五百美元，给谷歌当测试员，钱还是你自己掏的。坐吧台边，右眼一直往右上角瞟，酒保问喝什么，他张嘴就是“OK Glass”。兄弟，人家不叫Glass。最绝的是这玩意儿眨眼就能拍照。以前在酒吧冲姑娘眨眼，顶多被泼一杯酒；现在姑娘得先琢磨：你是在撩我，还是在拍我？想完了，泼两杯。美国人还专门给这帮人造了个词：Glasshole。一个产品能把自己的用户骂成新单词，我是头回见。最后电池没撑过一杯长岛冰茶，他摘下眼镜一看：原来自己就是个一个人来喝闷酒的普通男人。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0772", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "windows-8", "target_name": "Windows 8", "facts": ["Released in October 2012; removed the Start button and booted into a full-screen grid of tiles designed for touch screens most PCs did not have.", "The 8.1 update in 2013 put a Start button back, Windows 10 (2015) restored the Start menu, and Microsoft skipped the name Windows 9 altogether.", "Microsoft dropped the 'Metro' name in August 2012 after reports that German retailer Metro AG had objected on trademark grounds; Microsoft called 'Metro' a code name and denied any litigation, and the style was afterwards known informally as 'Modern UI'.", "The 'Charms bar' hid Search, Share, and Settings behind an invisible swipe from the right edge.", "Windows RT, the ARM edition launched alongside it, could not run ordinary Windows programs; Microsoft wrote down $900 million of unsold Surface RT tablets in 2013."], "a": {"item_id": "4dea39aaf51b", "text": "我有个哥们儿，2012年非要给台式机装Windows 8，理由是“新的肯定比旧的好”。我说你这电脑连个触屏都没有，他说没事，“我用鼠标点磁贴”。\n\n装完那天他请我去看。开机，满屏花花绿绿的大方块，跟营业厅的叫号屏似的。他拿着鼠标在那儿横着拖，拖了半天才找到“桌面”这个磁贴。我说你装了个新系统，就为了点进去看旧桌面？\n\n最惨的是关机。开始按钮没了，他找了十分钟，最后鼠标往右上角一怼，蹦出一排竖条，设置，电源，关机。三步。他关一次机，比他的电脑开一次机还慢。\n\n现在他养成了个习惯：鼠标一闲下来，就往屏幕角落里甩。别人的鼠标是用来点东西的，他的鼠标是用来跟屏幕四个角打游击的。\n\n后来8.1把开始按钮还给他了，他激动得点了一下。结果点开的还是那屏大方块。"}, "b": {"item_id": "f01853167432", "text": "你们这种人我见过：机箱插着显示器，鼠标线拖到桌子底下，就为了装 Windows 8。图什么？图那个开始屏幕，满屏方块，天气、股票、新闻全贴在墙上，像把手机桌面硬塞进显示器。结果呢？鼠标得滑到右上角，等一个叫\"超级按钮\"的东西弹出来，左下角的开始按钮没了。你找了半小时，按下 Win 键，跳出一整面磁贴，\"邮件\"那块还在显示三个月前的垃圾广告。最后你上网搜\"怎么把开始菜单还给我\"，第一条推荐的是收费软件。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0773", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "juicero", "target_name": "Juicero 榨汁机", "facts": ["A Wi-Fi-connected cold-press juicer launched in 2016 at $699, later cut to $399, that squeezed proprietary bags of chopped produce sold by subscription.", "Raised about $120 million from investors including Google Ventures and Kleiner Perkins.", "In April 2017, Bloomberg reporters squeezed the packs by hand and got 7.5 ounces of juice in 90 seconds, faster than the machine's 8 ounces in about two minutes.", "Each pack carried a QR code that the press scanned and checked online before squeezing; it refused expired packs and would not run at all unless connected to Wi-Fi.", "Shut down in September 2017 and offered refunds on the machine."], "a": {"item_id": "5d1ab9a2be91", "text": "买Juicero的人，我特别想认识一下。四百美元，买一台榨汁机，还得连Wi-Fi。你家果汁需要上网？它要先扫一下果蔬包上的二维码，确认这包菜没过期，才肯开榨。你花四百块，雇了个电子门卫，专门查蔬菜的身份证。\n\n这帮人图什么？不是图果汁，是图早上站在厨房，对着手机App，看机器庄严地压一包菜，那种\"我是住在未来的人\"的感觉。\n\n结果彭博社发现：不用机器，徒手捏那个袋子，果汁出得更快更多。也就是说，你买了一台输给你双手的机器。\n\n后来公司倒闭了，还好给你退了钱。但你的下场是：成了互联网上最出名的一批人。因为你把\"不会捏袋子\"，做成了一个融资一点二亿美元的生活方式。"}, "b": {"item_id": "c8e0492d10b9", "text": "你知道花400美元买Juicero的都是什么人吗？就是那种早上六点起床、先冥想再跑步、手机里装了十二个健康App的硅谷精英。他们不缺钱，缺的是“我连喝果汁都在用科技”的那份体面。\n\n结果呢？你花400块买了台联网的压榨机，再每袋七八美元买人家的果蔬包。机器还得先扫码、连Wi-Fi，确认你这包没过期，才肯开工。家里断网了？对不起，今天没有健康。\n\n然后彭博社的记者干了件特别缺德的事：直接上手捏那个袋子，挤出来的汁跟机器差不多，速度还更快。\n\n一群天天喊“颠覆传统”的人，被两只手颠覆了。\n\n最惨的是，后来公司倒闭了，给你退钱。你连“至少我还在用”都说不出口了。你最后得到了什么？一个教训：原来你最该投资的科技产品，是自己的手。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0774", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "vim", "target_name": "Vim", "facts": ["Released by Bram Moolenaar in November 1991 as 'Vi IMitation', a vi clone he had begun writing for the Amiga in 1988 on top of Stevie; the expansion became 'Vi IMproved' with version 2.0 in December 1993.", "Licensed as charityware: the licence asks users to donate to ICCF Holland, a charity Moolenaar ran for children in Uganda.", "Stack Overflow's question 'How to exit the Vim editor?' passed one million visits in May 2017, which Stack Overflow marked with a blog post.", "Modal editing: in normal mode, typing letters runs commands rather than inserting text; :wq saves and quits, :q! quits and discards changes."], "a": {"item_id": "025ba8683b8f", "text": "公司里总有一个用 Vim 的人，你不用问，他自己会说。入职第一天就官宣：\"我不用鼠标。\"没人问啊，我问的是厕所在哪。\n\n他图什么？图你从背后路过，看他光标嗖嗖飞三屏，你\"哇\"一声。就这一声，够他吃一个月。\n\n下场呢？.vimrc 调了六百行，花了三个周末，省下的时间还没调的时间多。最惨是帮你改代码，你电脑上开的是 VS Code，他自信满满先按 Esc，再敲 :wq，保存退出，一气呵成。结果文档里多出一行 :wq，像他的墓志铭。\n\n他还不认，说：\"你这编辑器，不行。\""}, "b": {"item_id": "e21539084a9a", "text": "我们组有个哥们，用Vim。你怎么知道的？你不用知道，他会告诉你。开会时他说“我手从不离开键盘”，然后在投影上按了二十分钟hjkl，去找第三行。\n\n他的.vimrc写了八百行，装了四十个插件：文件树、自动补全、Git面板。兄弟，你花了三年，亲手把Vim配成了一个启动更慢的VS Code。\n\n他图什么？就图那一句：“你连怎么退出都不会吧？”\n\n可他的下场呢？给女朋友发微信：“我爱你:wq”。人家问：“你打错了吧？”他说：“没错，保存并退出。”\n\n她真退出了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0775", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "latex", "target_name": "LaTeX", "facts": ["Leslie Lamport wrote LaTeX in the early 1980s and released it in 1984 as a macro layer on Donald Knuth's TeX (1978), which Knuth built because he disliked how the galley proofs of the new edition of his own book looked.", "TeX's version number converges on π (3.141592653 since 2021), and Knuth's TeX bug bounty started at one hexadecimal dollar, $2.56, doubling yearly until frozen at $327.68; he stopped mailing real cheques in 2008 after fraud and now credits accounts at his fictional Bank of San Serriffe, and only 9 of the 275 cheques he sent from 2006 on were ever cashed.", "Its best-known warning is 'Underfull \\hbox (badness 10000)'; overfull lines are reported as 'Overfull \\hbox (Npt too wide)', and figures float to wherever the algorithm decides, not where the author put them.", "A single missing brace can produce an error reported pages away from the mistake, and a document can need three compiler passes to get its references right."], "a": {"item_id": "8f8bc08e77f4", "text": "我们组有个哥们，博士毕业三年，还活在答辩那天。领导让他写两页备忘录，通知周五团建，他打开的是TeXstudio。\n\n图什么？他说：“Computer Modern字体，一看就是发过论文的。”兄弟，团建通知没有审稿人。\n\n开头先写十五行\\usepackage，中文还得装ctex，pdflatex不认，换xelatex。终于编译，报错：“Missing $ inserted”。因为他写了“人均预算$200”，LaTeX以为他要开始推公式了。\n\n改完再编，“经费超支20%，请自理”，逗号后面全没了，百分号把后半句当注释吃了。\n\n周五大家到了，饭店没订。备忘录上地址那栏写着：“详见第??页。”他忘了再编译一遍。"}, "b": {"item_id": "34dc34fd3a74", "text": "我特别佩服那种两页备忘录也要用LaTeX的人。通知内容就一句\"周五聚餐AA\"，他非得 \\documentclass 起手。图什么？图一个\"我不是Word那种凡人\"。你收到PDF，Computer Modern字体，一股刚证完黎曼猜想的气息，点开一看，火锅。\n\n下场呢？写十分钟，编译两小时。一个 Undefined control sequence，他在论坛翻到半夜，装了四个G的TeX Live，风扇转得像要起飞。好不容易编出来，满屏 Overfull \\hbox 警告，他一个都不肯放过。为了消掉一行0.3pt的溢出，他把\"聚餐\"改成了\"聚会\"。\n\n最后发出去，同事回：\"手机上看不清，你发文字行吗？\"\n\n他想了想，把PDF截了个图发过去。\n\n折腾一晚上，排版界的天花板，最后落成一张JPG。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0776", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "slack", "target_name": "Slack", "facts": ["Spun out in 2013 from the internal chat tool of Tiny Speck's failed game Glitch; founder Stewart Butterfield had earlier spun Flickr out of another failed game.", "The name is a backronym: 'Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge'.", "Salesforce bought it for $27.7 billion in 2021.", "Filed an EU antitrust complaint against Microsoft in 2020 over the bundling of Teams with Office."], "a": {"item_id": "d768d460ddec", "text": "我见过一种老板，叫“Slack治国派”。创业第一天就宣布：咱们不发邮件，一切公开，扁平！三年后，公司一百人，四百个频道。#general没人敢说话，#random全是老板转发的文章。他最爱@channel，凌晨一点全公司一百部手机一起震，内容是：“大家觉得新logo咋样？”决策靠表情投票：三个👍，一个👀，通过。没人知道👀啥意思，包括发👀的人。\n\n最惨的是他自己。头像旁边那个绿点，他不敢让它灭，怕员工觉得他不在；员工也不敢让它灭，怕他觉得员工不在。全公司互相盯着一个绿点上班。\n\n他确实消灭了邮件。现在他想找一个决定，得先问一句：“这事是在哪个thread里定的？”"}, "b": {"item_id": "99cfffb47c21", "text": "有些老板非要用Slack管公司。开会太累，他说以后都在#general里说。结果一天九百条消息，他想每条都看，看到第三天放弃，给所有消息点了个小黄脸。他觉得这叫留痕，于是逢事必@channel，全公司手机同时震动，财务一边对账一边回了个\"收到\"的表情。频道越建越多：#项目A、#项目A-最终版、#项目A-真的最终版。员工每天花一小时在搜索框里找文件，找到的都是三个月前的讨论串，串里四十七条回复，最后一条是老板自己发的：\"这个先放放。\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0777", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "graphene", "target_name": "石墨烯", "facts": ["Isolated in 2004 by Andre Geim and Konstantin Novoselov at Manchester by peeling graphite with Scotch tape; Nobel Prize in Physics, 2010.", "The EU's Graphene Flagship, launched in 2013, committed €1 billion over ten years.", "A 2018 study in Advanced Materials tested 'graphene' from 60 producers and found none with more than 50% graphene content; most was graphite flakes.", "Sold in tennis rackets, running shoes, and headphones as a performance ingredient.", "Geim also won an Ig Nobel Prize in 2000 for levitating a frog with magnets."], "a": {"item_id": "70e5e600bd85", "text": "我有个表舅，逢人就说石墨烯要改变世界：一层碳原子，比钢硬两百倍，导电比铜还快。你以为他是科学家？他是卖秋裤的。石墨烯秋裤，三百九一条，号称发热、抗菌、通经络。\n\n我问他世界啥时候改变？他说快了！二〇〇四年人家拿透明胶从铅笔芯上粘下来的，诺贝尔奖都发了。我说对啊，二十年了，奖人家领走了，透明胶还在卖，就你的秋裤卖不动。\n\n后来他把卖秋裤的钱全砸进石墨烯概念股，世界确实改变了，他的世界。现在他穿着自己那条秋裤跟人说：“石墨烯，薄啊，一层就一个原子那么薄。跟我家底一样。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "8757c13cc756", "text": "我有个朋友，逢人就说石墨烯要改变世界。你问他石墨烯是什么，他说是碳原子排成六边形的单层，薄到只有一个原子厚。行，那你知道铅笔芯是什么吗？石墨。石墨就是一层层石墨烯叠起来的。你小学就天天在用它写字，世界怎么没变？他卖保暖内衣，吊牌上印着\"石墨烯含量\"，我让他拿检测报告，他说报告在另一个仓库。去年厂子抵给了银行。现在他改卖石墨烯足浴盆，广告写\"一泡，改变人生\"。我去他店里看了，人确实变了，老婆跑了，他还在那儿泡脚。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0778", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "microsoft-word", "target_name": "Microsoft Word", "facts": ["Launched in 1983 as 'Multi-Tool Word' for Xenix and MS-DOS.", "Clippy, the animated paperclip assistant, arrived in Office 97 and was removed in Office 2007; Microsoft itself ran ads mocking it in 2001.", "Its .docx format, Office Open XML, was fast-tracked to ISO standard 29500 in 2008 as a roughly 6,000-page specification, amid accusations that national committees had been packed.", "Older spell checkers, Word's included, offered 'Cupertino' as a correction for a mistyped 'cooperation', and the resulting 'Cupertino effect' made it into published NATO, EU and UN-agency documents."], "a": {"item_id": "743026e864f3", "text": "我们宿舍有个兄弟，打死不学排版软件，说：“论文嘛，Word就够了，谁电脑里没个Word？”\n\n是，谁都有。所以全宿舍凌晨四点一起陪他看页码。\n\n他的诉求特别朴素：封面不要页码，摘要用罗马数字，正文从1开始。就这三个愿望，阿拉丁神灯都能办，Word办不了。他插了一晚上分节符，最后页眉上“第三章”出现在了致谢里，像个阴魂不散的前任。\n\n最狠的是插一张图。图一进来，后面二十页集体往下挪，表格跑到下一页，标题孤零零留在页底。他想拖回来，鼠标一碰，Word当场“未响应”，屏幕一白，跟他的人生一个颜色。\n\n现在他桌面上躺着：“论文终稿.docx”、“终稿改.docx”、“终稿真的不改了.docx”，还有一个“~$终稿.docx”，打不开也删不掉，跟他的学位一样。\n\n导师最后打开一看，用修订模式批了一句话：“格式有问题。”\n\n整篇变红。"}, "b": {"item_id": "b80a1ea0ef97", "text": "用Word排毕业论文的人，我研究过，都是同一种人：觉得“不就是打字嘛”。他们图什么？图省两个小时，不想学“样式”和“分节符”。于是标题全靠手动：选中、加粗、三号、居中，四下。全文六十个标题，两百四十下。目录也是手打的，页码也是手填的，填得整整齐齐，看着特别像那么回事。\n\n然后导师说：第二章和第三章换个顺序。\n\n他只删了一个回车，第五十页那张图就像被召唤了一样，飞到了第一百一十二页，还顺手带走了摘要的罗马数字页码。\n\n他为了省两个小时，花了两百个小时。最后在打印店，用人家的电脑一打开，全篇错位，他蹲在那儿一页一页重新排。老板路过看了一眼：“同学，你这是论文还是拼图？”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0779", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "vim", "target_name": "Vim", "facts": ["Released by Bram Moolenaar in November 1991 as 'Vi IMitation', a vi clone he had begun writing for the Amiga in 1988 on top of Stevie; the expansion became 'Vi IMproved' with version 2.0 in December 1993.", "Licensed as charityware: the licence asks users to donate to ICCF Holland, a charity Moolenaar ran for children in Uganda.", "Stack Overflow's question 'How to exit the Vim editor?' passed one million visits in May 2017, which Stack Overflow marked with a blog post.", "Modal editing: in normal mode, typing letters runs commands rather than inserting text; :wq saves and quits, :q! quits and discards changes."], "a": {"item_id": "025ba8683b8f", "text": "公司里总有一个用 Vim 的人，你不用问，他自己会说。入职第一天就官宣：\"我不用鼠标。\"没人问啊，我问的是厕所在哪。\n\n他图什么？图你从背后路过，看他光标嗖嗖飞三屏，你\"哇\"一声。就这一声，够他吃一个月。\n\n下场呢？.vimrc 调了六百行，花了三个周末，省下的时间还没调的时间多。最惨是帮你改代码，你电脑上开的是 VS Code，他自信满满先按 Esc，再敲 :wq，保存退出，一气呵成。结果文档里多出一行 :wq，像他的墓志铭。\n\n他还不认，说：\"你这编辑器，不行。\""}, "b": {"item_id": "773f0cf90ece", "text": "我有个朋友用 Vim 写代码，这本来没什么，问题是他得让全世界知道。每天发朋友圈：\"今天 hjkl 走了八个小时，手没离开主键盘区。\"配图是一屏幕冒号和波浪号。底下点赞的全是同款人，互相夸：\"牛逼，我也是。\"他们图什么？图的就是别人看不懂还得点赞。结果上周他要退出文件，手一滑按了个 i，开始在文件里狂打字，还没反应过来。他想起有个 Esc，按了二十下，屏幕纹丝不动。最后他在群里喊：\"救命，我退不出去了！\"群友秒回一排：先按 Esc，再敲 :q!。他回：\"我已经发过朋友圈了，不能认输。\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0780", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "ugg", "target_name": "UGG 雪地靴", "facts": ["Sheepskin boots Australian surfers wore to warm up after sessions; Brian Smith started selling them in California in 1978.", "Deckers Outdoor bought the brand in 1995 and holds 'UGG' as a trademark in the US and elsewhere; in Australia, 'ugg' is a generic word any bootmaker may use.", "In 2019, a US jury found an Australian maker liable for selling boots labelled 'ugg' into the United States.", "Oprah Winfrey featured them in her 'Favorite Things' lists repeatedly in the 2000s, which drove the first boom.", "UGG is Deckers' largest brand, with over $2 billion in annual sales."], "a": {"item_id": "291bb4a33621", "text": "一年四季穿UGG的人，你仔细看，基本是同一种人：早上说“我就下楼拿个快递”，结果一路拿到了公司。\n\n问他图什么，他说：“不用系鞋带，一脚蹬。”对，UGG不是穿，是给脚办入住。\n\n夏天三十八度他还穿，问就是“里面是羊毛，透气”。对，羊也这么说，然后被剃了。\n\n下场特别统一。一场雨，一千多的靴子泡成两块发面馒头。到了办公室一脱鞋，羊毛负责把你全年的经历保存下来，同事都不用问你中午吃了啥。\n\n最惨的是冬天。你坚持了三季，终于等到主场，结果满大街都在穿，你的“个性”直接变成了“合群”。\n\n你说你这一年图什么？图了个脚，被焖成了刚出锅的馒头。"}, "b": {"item_id": "2b7b80053c20", "text": "我有个朋友，一年四季穿UGG。七月三十八度，她踩着两只羊上街。我问不热吗？她说：“羊毛，透气。”姐，羊自己热得都去剪毛了，你又给它穿回去？\n\n她图什么？图一脚蹬进去不用系鞋带，像回到妈妈怀里。代价呢？光脚闷一夏天，她一脱鞋，全办公室以为楼下烤串店开业了，还是羊肉串。\n\n穿到第三年，鞋跟往外撇，人往里倒，走路像两只企鹅抬着她。下场雨，麂皮全是水印，鞋面像世界地图。\n\n最讽刺的是，这叫雪地靴。她穿它蹚过三个夏天的水坑，唯独没见过雪：一到冬天，她嫌冷，换棉拖了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0781", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "pmma", "target_name": "PMMA 电子束光刻胶", "facts": ["The same polymer as Plexiglas and acrylic glass, dissolved in anisole or chlorobenzene and spun into a thin film.", "The oldest electron-beam resist still in everyday use; IBM researchers reported it as one in the late 1960s.", "Positive tone: the beam breaks polymer chains and the exposed region dissolves in a MIBK:IPA developer.", "Resolves below 10 nm but has poor sensitivity, so doses of hundreds to over a thousand µC/cm² (depending on voltage) make writing slow.", "Poor dry-etch resistance, so it is used mostly for lift-off rather than as an etch mask."], "a": {"item_id": "d0b057bba930", "text": "做电子束光刻的朋友，我是真佩服你们。别人都上EUV了，你们还抱着950K的PMMA不撒手。电子一打，分子链断成小段，拿MIBK兑异丙醇泡一泡，图形就显出来了，干净得很。问题是它怕刻蚀，刻到一半自己先塌了。你们图什么？图十纳米的线条画得漂亮。然后呢？做剥离，丙酮泡一晚上，金属毛边比头发丝还多。博士师兄熬了三个月，论文第三章就是一张拖尾的SEM照片。导师看了一眼说：线条挺好，就是没法用。"}, "b": {"item_id": "5266dfb33ccd", "text": "我认识一帮做纳米器件的博士，实验室一台电子束曝光机，几千万，往上涂的是PMMA。说白了就是亚克力，奶茶店吸管盒那个料。几千万的电子枪，天天给鱼缸刻字。\n\n问导师为啥不换，导师说：“分辨率好，十纳米！”好是好，就是灵敏度低，剂量得往死里加。机时按小时收钱，一块片子写一宿，学生睡机房门口，跟排专家号似的。\n\n显影液MIBK兑异丙醇一比三，配方七十年代传下来的，比他家族谱还稳。\n\n剂量加猛了它还交联，正胶变负胶。跟这帮博士一样，熬太久，人就反了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0782", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "las-vegas", "target_name": "拉斯维加斯", "facts": ["Nevada legalized casino gambling in 1931, the year construction of the Hoover Dam began nearby.", "Most of the Strip lies not in the city of Las Vegas but in the unincorporated town of Paradise.", "The Sphere, opened in 2023 at a cost of about $2.3 billion, is the largest spherical building in the world.", "'What happens here, stays here' was launched by the local convention and visitors authority in 2003.", "Lake Mead, its water source, fell in July 2022 to about 1,040 feet, its lowest since the reservoir first filled in 1937, and dropped below that mark again in August 2026."], "a": {"item_id": "ade69157daa3", "text": "去拉斯维加斯发财的人，我真想问：你是觉得那些酒店是慈善机构吗？沙漠里一座城，没河没矿，全靠你们盖起来的。威尼斯人酒店里那条运河，是你的房贷挖的。\n\n出发前都说\"我就玩两百块\"，还说研究过策略，看了三小时视频：\"押红色，输了就翻倍。\"哥们，赌桌有下注上限，你翻到第七把，就剩尊严了。\n\n下场都一样：第一天赢八十，觉得自己是赌神；第二天在没有窗户没有钟的赌场里喝免费啤酒，不知道几点，也不知道自己是谁；第三天在机场，把回程的钱塞进老虎机。对，麦卡伦机场就有老虎机，怕你还没到市区就想通了。\n\n最后朋友圈发张照片：在猫王教堂，跟刚认识的人结婚了。发财没发成，倒是多了个人来分你剩下的。"}, "b": {"item_id": "9c12f6e41e03", "text": "去拉斯维加斯发财的人，我见过三种。第一种是辞了职来的，行李箱一半是衣服，一半是对人生的最后指望。第二种是借钱来的，借的还是网贷，利息高得连老虎机的灯都嫉妒。第三种最可怕，他说就玩一小时，结果第二天中午你在自助餐厅找到他，正排第四趟帝王蟹腿，眼神像刚从战场上下来。外面四十度，他嫌出门热，就一直泡在赌场里。赢了几万，去兑换处，人家笑着说：您还有一张免费住宿券，欢迎下次光临。他拿着券上楼睡了一觉，醒来一看，钱没了，券还在，而且明天还有效。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0783", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "google-sheets", "target_name": "Google Sheets", "facts": ["Launched in June 2006 as Google Spreadsheets, built on XL2Web, a product Google acquired from 2Web Technologies.", "A spreadsheet is capped at 10 million cells (raised from 5 million in 2022).", "Functions such as IMPORTHTML, IMPORTXML, and GOOGLEFINANCE pull live web data straight into cells.", "Free with a Google account; files live in Google Drive, and collaborators edit the same sheet simultaneously with coloured cursors.", "In a browser, offline editing works only in Chrome or Edge, with the Google Docs Offline extension installed and offline access switched on while still connected; the Android and iOS apps work offline natively."], "a": {"item_id": "c795fe8e9fcf", "text": "每个拿Google Sheets管团队的人，都管这叫“极简”。其实就是舍不得那几十刀的Jira。\n\n一开始你说“一张表够了”。三个月后，十四个标签页，一个叫“总表”，一个叫“总表（真的）”。\n\n你开会最常说的一句：“谁开的筛选？”你筛一下，全公司跟着筛一下。右上角永远飘着三个头像，其中一个叫“匿名水豚”。你不知道它是谁，但它刚删了D列。\n\n下场呢？你成了公司唯一看得懂那条“VLOOKUP套IMPORTRANGE”的人。你不是经理了，你是这张表的人质。你休假那天，群里只有一句话：“老大，它又#REF!了。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "2a199d10637d", "text": "我特别佩服那种用Google Sheets管团队的领导。他们的口头禅是：“不用买系统，一个表格就够了。”省了一年三千块软件费，代价是自己变成全公司的人肉服务器。\n\n刚开始就一个标签页，三个月后四十七个，名字叫“总表”“总表2”“总表最终”“总表最终（别动）”。他还觉得自己是工程师，因为他会VLOOKUP。全组只有他会，别人看见公式栏，就像看见祖传药方。\n\n最离谱是开会。他共享屏幕，右上角蹦出个“匿名水豚正在查看”。全场安静，没人知道谁是水豚，只知道水豚刚把C列改成了红色。\n\n他请一天假，团队就瘫痪了，因为没人知道红色是“已完成”，还是“老板生气了”。\n\n后来他离职，交接文档就一句话：“有问题，看版本历史。”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0784", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "google-sheets", "target_name": "Google Sheets", "facts": ["Launched in June 2006 as Google Spreadsheets, built on XL2Web, a product Google acquired from 2Web Technologies.", "A spreadsheet is capped at 10 million cells (raised from 5 million in 2022).", "Functions such as IMPORTHTML, IMPORTXML, and GOOGLEFINANCE pull live web data straight into cells.", "Free with a Google account; files live in Google Drive, and collaborators edit the same sheet simultaneously with coloured cursors.", "In a browser, offline editing works only in Chrome or Edge, with the Google Docs Offline extension installed and offline access switched on while still connected; the Android and iOS apps work offline natively."], "a": {"item_id": "5ceaecd3ab50", "text": "你们见过用 Google 表格管团队的人吗？这种人通常是行政出身，入职第一天就甩给大家一个链接，标题叫《项目总表》。图什么？图一眼看全局。结果他开了二十个标签页，第一个叫“总览”，第十七个叫“真的总览”，最后一个叫“别删”。每人一行，有人把单元格染成黄色，有人加了下拉框，选项从“进行中”一路改成“待定，等老板回”。周五他一打开，满屏#REF!，像看见自己的人生。翻版本记录更绝，凌晨三点有人把“张三”改成了“张三丰”。他终于明白，他管的不是团队，是一个会自己长脾气的表格。"}, "b": {"item_id": "c795fe8e9fcf", "text": "每个拿Google Sheets管团队的人，都管这叫“极简”。其实就是舍不得那几十刀的Jira。\n\n一开始你说“一张表够了”。三个月后，十四个标签页，一个叫“总表”，一个叫“总表（真的）”。\n\n你开会最常说的一句：“谁开的筛选？”你筛一下，全公司跟着筛一下。右上角永远飘着三个头像，其中一个叫“匿名水豚”。你不知道它是谁，但它刚删了D列。\n\n下场呢？你成了公司唯一看得懂那条“VLOOKUP套IMPORTRANGE”的人。你不是经理了，你是这张表的人质。你休假那天，群里只有一句话：“老大，它又#REF!了。”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0785", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "crocs", "target_name": "Crocs 洞洞鞋", "facts": ["Founded in Boulder, Colorado, in 2002; the first clog debuted at the Fort Lauderdale boat show as a boating shoe.", "Made of 'Croslite', which Crocs describes as a proprietary closed-cell resin foam and which third parties identify as an injection-moulded EVA foam.", "Balenciaga sold a platform Crocs for about $850 in 2018.", "The stock fell more than 90% from its 2007 peak during the 2008 crisis; the company then boomed in the pandemic and gave away 860,000 pairs to healthcare workers in 2020.", "The holes are officially 'ventilation ports' and the charms that go in them are 'Jibbitz', a company Crocs bought in 2006."], "a": {"item_id": "d0de4b131b62", "text": "穿洞洞鞋出门的人，分两种：真懒的，和装懒的。装懒的最好认，鞋上插满智必星，小恐龙、小汉堡、小皮卡丘，一只脚背就是一个摊位。你图舒服我理解，你在鞋上开连锁店干什么？\n\n他们还有个绝招：把脑门上那根带子往后一掰，“我切换运动模式了”。兄弟，带子掰过去，你是能跑过谁？\n\n下场都一样。下雨天，鞋面上那十几个洞全是进水口，走一步咕叽一声。晴天更惨，小石子从洞里钻进去卡在脚心，又不能当街脱，只能单腿跳着甩，像在给鞋做心肺复苏。\n\n回家一看，智必星掉了三个。人是舒服了，资产缩水了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "15501877150c", "text": "你们见过那种穿卡骆驰洞洞鞋出门的人吗？他们的理由永远是“舒服”。舒服到什么程度？舒服到能穿着它去相亲，对方低头一看，两排塑料坑，脚趾在里面像泡温泉。他图什么？图不用系鞋带，图能下水、能进厨房、能上超市，等于把拖鞋、雨鞋、运动鞋一起省了。结果呢？去趟公园，脚底的洞灌进三粒沙子，一步一个噗嗤，全场都知道他来了。最后鞋帮上挂满小挂件，走起路来叮叮当当，像个移动的钥匙串，他还在朋友圈写：这才是生活的本质。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0786", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "chicago-deep-dish", "target_name": "芝加哥深盘披萨", "facts": ["Created at Pizzeria Uno in Chicago in 1943; credit is disputed among founder Ike Sewell, his partner Ric Riccardo, and cook Rudy Malnati.", "Assembled upside down: sliced mozzarella on the dough, then toppings, then crushed tomatoes on top, so the cheese does not scorch during the long bake.", "Jon Stewart called it 'tomato soup in a bread bowl' and 'a casserole' on The Daily Show in 2013.", "Chicagoans' everyday pizza is thin 'tavern-style' cut into squares; GrubHub order data reported by WBEZ in 2013 showed city residents ordering non-deep-dish pizza about 10 to 1 over deep dish, with deep dish and stuffed making up under 9% of orders."], "a": {"item_id": "54fda0729132", "text": "管芝加哥深盘叫披萨的，基本都是芝加哥人。你别跟他争，他已经争了一辈子了。他图什么？图个名分。番茄酱铺在最上面，芝士压在最底下，饼边砌得跟城墙一样高，这叫披萨？这叫一锅围了墙的炖菜。可他非得叫披萨，因为叫“砂锅”就没法跟纽约人吵架了。结果呢？进店点单，服务员说：“得烤四十五分钟。”他说：“没事，披萨嘛。”等完上桌，刀叉都给他配好了。他吃两块就扶着墙出门，嘴里还念叨：“这才是真正的披萨。”兄弟，真正的披萨，不用你吃完躺一下午。"}, "b": {"item_id": "31f74171df4b", "text": "总有人非要管芝加哥深盘叫披萨。这帮人是谁？就是去芝加哥待过三天、发过一张切面照的人，回来就成了\"披萨鉴赏家\"。图什么？图朋友圈里那句\"正宗美式披萨\"。结果端上来一口黑铁锅似的东西，番茄酱糊在最上面，芝士全锁在底下，等了四十分钟，用刀叉一戳，热气里拉出半米长的芝士丝。他边切边说\"这披萨真地道\"，旁边的芝加哥老哥头都没抬：\"兄弟，这叫pie，馅饼。\"他不服，掏手机去搜，第一条就是本地人的回答：披萨能用手拿着吃，这玩意你得先找把叉子。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0787", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "papyrus", "target_name": "Papyrus 字体", "facts": ["Hand-drawn by Chris Costello in 1982 over about six months, when he was 23; he sold the rights to Letraset for $750.", "Bundled with macOS and with Microsoft Office, which is why it appears on so many menus and signs.", "James Cameron's Avatar (2009) used it for the film's logo and subtitles.", "A 2017 Saturday Night Live sketch starring Ryan Gosling is about a man unable to get over that choice."], "a": {"item_id": "a1917bc26fbf", "text": "老板要开养生馆，设计师问他要什么字体，他一拍桌子：要古法、天然、有年头！设计师说那就Papyrus。招牌一立，我路过瞄了一眼，心想这店是不是刚从沙子里挖出来的。那几道毛边，像被雨淋过又晒干的纸巾，每个笔画都在掉渣。有人说这字像《阿凡达》的片头，我说那进去是不是要变蓝？老板很委屈：我这是回归自然！三个月后店关了，招牌还在。风一吹，那个\"养\"字的毛边翘起来，像在跟路人打招呼：我们也不太行了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "02bcbf4a1359", "text": "开养生馆的老板选招牌，流程我都猜得到：打开Word，字体一路往下滑，滑到Papyrus，眼睛一亮——“哎，这个毛边，有古老东方的神秘感。”大哥，这字是个美国小伙二十三岁拿钢笔在纸上描出来的，你这“东方”是批发来的。更绝的是隔壁泰式按摩、墨西哥卷饼、埃及烤肉，全是同一个Papyrus，整条街像同一个古文明的遗址。他图什么？图Office自带，图不花钱，图“看着有文化”。结果呢？顾客一抬头，以为进的不是足疗店，是《阿凡达》片场。卡梅隆好歹花了几亿美元才用上这款字，你花了零块零毛，撞得一模一样，人家拿了奥斯卡，你拿了张差评：“技师手法一般，招牌像蓝色外星人开的。”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0788", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "las-vegas", "target_name": "拉斯维加斯", "facts": ["Nevada legalized casino gambling in 1931, the year construction of the Hoover Dam began nearby.", "Most of the Strip lies not in the city of Las Vegas but in the unincorporated town of Paradise.", "The Sphere, opened in 2023 at a cost of about $2.3 billion, is the largest spherical building in the world.", "'What happens here, stays here' was launched by the local convention and visitors authority in 2003.", "Lake Mead, its water source, fell in July 2022 to about 1,040 feet, its lowest since the reservoir first filled in 1937, and dropped below that mark again in August 2026."], "a": {"item_id": "dd338752f2cb", "text": "我有个朋友，看了三遍《决胜21点》，就觉得自己是MIT天才，揣着年终奖飞拉斯维加斯，说去“考察”。\n\n兄弟，你连工资条都算不明白，你去算牌？\n\n你也不想想，那是沙漠里凭空盖的一座城：没河、没矿、没工厂，贝拉吉奥的喷泉一天到晚往天上滋，钱哪来的？就是你们这种人！那每一滴水，都是“考察团”的眼泪。\n\n赌场里没钟没窗，他说好啊，能专心。专心到第三天，前台主动给他升了套房。他还挺骄傲，说这是VIP待遇。\n\n不是，那是人家算过了，你值这个价。\n\n回来他跟我说：“在拉斯维加斯发生的，就留在拉斯维加斯。”\n\n我说对，你的钱也是。"}, "b": {"item_id": "9c12f6e41e03", "text": "去拉斯维加斯发财的人，我见过三种。第一种是辞了职来的，行李箱一半是衣服，一半是对人生的最后指望。第二种是借钱来的，借的还是网贷，利息高得连老虎机的灯都嫉妒。第三种最可怕，他说就玩一小时，结果第二天中午你在自助餐厅找到他，正排第四趟帝王蟹腿，眼神像刚从战场上下来。外面四十度，他嫌出门热，就一直泡在赌场里。赢了几万，去兑换处，人家笑着说：您还有一张免费住宿券，欢迎下次光临。他拿着券上楼睡了一觉，醒来一看，钱没了，券还在，而且明天还有效。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0789", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "carbon-nanotubes", "target_name": "碳纳米管", "facts": ["Reported by Sumio Iijima in Nature in 1991.", "Promised as the cable for a space elevator; a 2006 analysis showed that ordinary defects cut the strength of a macroscopic cable far below the ideal value.", "Long, rigid multi-walled nanotubes behaved like asbestos fibres in a 2008 mouse study in Nature Nanotechnology.", "Sorting metallic from semiconducting tubes has blocked nanotube electronics for three decades; MIT's 16-bit RV16X-NANO processor (2019) is still a lab demonstration.", "Today they are sold mainly as a conductive additive in lithium-ion battery electrodes."], "a": {"item_id": "ef055d34e6aa", "text": "我认识一个博士，专门拿碳纳米管跟投资人讲故事。PPT第一页永远是太空电梯：“强度是钢的一百倍，一根拉到同步轨道！”投资人问现在能做多长，他说：“厘米级。”三万六千公里，差了也就九个数量级，四舍五入算到了。\n\n第二页讲替代硅芯片。难点只有一个：管子卷出来，一部分导电，一部分是半导体，还分不开。这好比开饭馆，菜一半能吃一半有毒，你还挑不出来，但你跟股东说下季度开连锁。\n\n这故事从1991年讲到现在，“再给十年”讲了三遍。最后他去哪了？给锂电池厂卖导电浆料，按吨算钱。PPT没删，太空电梯那页还在，标题改成了“愿景”。"}, "b": {"item_id": "9252499a4c39", "text": "做碳纳米管创业的博士，路演第一页永远是太空电梯。说这玩意儿比钢强一百倍，重量才六分之一，投资人眼睛都亮了。他们图啥？图的是投资人别翻第二页。第二页写着：实验室里最长做到几十厘米，太空电梯需要三万六千公里，差一亿倍，他管这叫“早期阶段”。投资人说看看样品，他掏出一小瓶黑粉，说这就是未来。结果钱融到了，电梯没修成，产品卖给电池厂当导电剂，论公斤称，跟墨粉一个价位。最后他也活成了碳纳米管：单打独斗个个都强，凑一块儿就团聚，缠成一团，怎么都分不开。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0790", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hydrogen-cars", "target_name": "氢燃料电池汽车", "facts": ["Toyota launched the Mirai in 2014; 'mirai' means 'future' in Japanese.", "Nearly all US hydrogen filling stations are in California; Shell closed all seven of its California passenger-car stations in February 2024.", "More than 95% of the world's hydrogen is made from fossil fuels, mostly by steam-reforming natural gas.", "California pump prices reached about $36 per kilogram in 2023-24, over $200 for a Mirai fill; Toyota has handed buyers fuel cards worth $15,000.", "Mirai owners filed a class action against Toyota in July 2024 over hydrogen shortages and station outages; a second suit in 2025 sought at least $5.7 billion."], "a": {"item_id": "6147a8dd376d", "text": "买氢燃料电池车的人，我研究过，就两种：一种是环保人士，一种是被送了三年氢气卡的。\n\n环保人士图什么？图尾管只排水。可那氢多半是拿天然气炼的，他花四十块一公斤，绕一大圈烧天然气，最后从屁股后面滴出一杯水，像在表演“我很干净”。\n\n下场更精彩：全城三个加氢站，一个周二检修，一个在郊区，一个前面排着三辆公交。他的日常就是刷加氢站App，像抢演唱会门票。抢到了，还得等储氢罐压力回上来。\n\n最后氢气卡到期那天，车一秒贬值一半。他挂二手车，文案写得特别诚恳：“车况良好，自带情怀，不含氢气。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "7f3ef4cfecbe", "text": "我有个朋友买了氢燃料电池车，他说电动车太俗，他要“跳过一代，直接活在未来”。\n\n结果未来没来，他先搬家了。\n\n为啥？全市就俩加氢站，一个在开发区，一个在“维护中”。他现在挑对象第一句话不问工作，问：“你家离加氢站多远？”\n\n他最爱跟人吹：“我加满只要三分钟！”对，三分钟加满，四十分钟开过去，到了还得排在一溜儿氢能公交后面。\n\n他还特骄傲：“我这车尾气是水，能喝！”兄弟，你花三十万，就为了买台会开的净水器？\n\n前两天他想卖车，二手商看了一眼，说：“这车我收，但你得告诉我，买家去哪儿加氢。”\n\n他沉默了。他终于明白自己确实活在未来：在这片土地上，有这车的人还没出生。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0791", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "javascript", "target_name": "JavaScript", "facts": ["Written by Brendan Eich at Netscape in ten days in May 1995; named Mocha, then LiveScript, then JavaScript to ride Java's marketing, although the two are unrelated.", "typeof null returns 'object', a bug from the first version that has been kept for compatibility ever since.", "In March 2016 the author of left-pad, an 11-line npm package, unpublished it and broke builds across the industry, including Babel and React.", "[] + {} evaluates to '[object Object]'; {} + [] typed at a console evaluates to 0.", "Standardized by Ecma as 'ECMAScript' in 1997 because the name JavaScript is a trademark, first Sun's and now Oracle's."], "a": {"item_id": "5c3084898a09", "text": "我有个朋友，前端出身，人生信条就一句：“能用JavaScript写的，早晚都得用JavaScript写。”网页用它，后端上Node，桌面套Electron。给我写个计算器，装完四百兆，一开机比我家电暖气还烫。\n\n他图什么？图这辈子只学一门语言。结果呢？框架半年一换，他半年就得重学一遍，比考驾照还勤。\n\n上周他给我写了个记账软件。我存进去一块，再存一块，余额显示“11”。我说钱呢？他说别慌，换成三个等号再比一遍。\n\n现在他项目里的node_modules比他存款还大，删不掉也不敢动。万一哪个陌生人撤了一个包，全世界跟他一块儿塌。"}, "b": {"item_id": "0f8759744771", "text": "我朋友是前端，什么都用 JavaScript 写。写个脚本，用 Node。写个桌面软件，套个 Electron，一开就吃八百兆内存。他说这叫一套技术栈通吃。我说你那是通吃，还是通缉？\n\n上周他想写个开机自动备份，npm install 下来一千三百个包，其中一个叫 is-odd，专门判断一个数是不是奇数。备份脚本跑起来，先等一个包判断今天是不是单号，再等另一个包去找文件。备份成功了，电脑里也多了一个 node_modules，比他硬盘里所有照片加起来还大。\n\n现在他开始学 Rust，说要回归本质。我问他本质是什么，他说是 undefined。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0792", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "macau", "target_name": "澳门", "facts": ["A Portuguese settlement from the 1550s until the handover to China in December 1999.", "Casino revenue passed the Las Vegas Strip's in 2006, and at its 2013 peak, about $45 billion, was roughly seven times the Strip's.", "One company, STDM, held the casino monopoly for four decades until licences opened to competition in 2002.", "Among the most densely populated places on Earth, at roughly 20,000 people per square kilometre.", "The Cotai Strip stands on land reclaimed from the sea between the islands of Taipa and Coloane."], "a": {"item_id": "b300422ef6ef", "text": "我有个朋友，在家打麻将输了八千，说要去澳门翻本。这就像在小区泳池呛了口水，决定去太平洋练练。\n\n去的时候坐赌场免费大巴，人家专车接你进去，因为知道出来那趟，你得自己顺着港珠澳大桥走回去。\n\n三天后我在大三巴见到他，对着那面墙拍照。我说你拍它干嘛？他说：“亲切。教堂一把火烧光了，就剩个门脸撑着，跟我现在一模一样。”\n\n最后他在葡京边上的当铺把手表当了，买了一盒葡挞回家。他妈问输了多少，他说：“没输，我去吃葡挞了。一盒六个，四万一个。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "be44032450bd", "text": "我特别佩服那种说\"我去澳门翻本\"的人。股票亏了三十万，他的方案是：去澳门，一晚上，赢回来。你想想，三十万是怎么亏的？靠判断力。现在他拿同一套判断力，去一个没有钟的地方翻本。\n\n到了威尼斯人，里面的天永远是下午四点，假的，就是让你觉得还早，还来得及。结果三十万变六十万，反方向的。出来一看，赌场对面就是当铺，灯亮着，贴心得像医院门口开殡仪馆。表当了，金链子当了，最后走回关闸，全部家当一个猪扒包。\n\n回来跟我说：\"差一点就翻了。\"哥，你差的不是一点，你差的是那三十万，加上另外三十万，再加上你那块表。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0793", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "canberra", "target_name": "堪培拉", "facts": ["Chosen as a compromise because neither Sydney nor Melbourne would accept the other as capital; Australia's constitution required the capital to be at least 100 miles from Sydney.", "Designed by the Chicago architects Walter Burley Griffin and Marion Mahony Griffin, who won an international competition in 1912.", "Its centrepiece, Lake Burley Griffin, is artificial, created by damming the Molonglo River in 1963-64.", "Parliament House (1988) is built into Capital Hill under a lawn meant for citizens to walk over their politicians; the lawn was fenced off in 2017 for security.", "Melbourne served as the capital until 1927 while Canberra was being built."], "a": {"item_id": "e958e4adc831", "text": "有人为了工作搬去堪培拉，我真想问：你是想不开，还是想得太开？这帮人基本都是联邦公务员，图什么？图铁饭碗，图名片上印着个“部”字。去了才发现，这座城市就是个大办公室，周五下午四点人类集体消失，全开车去悉尼找活人。在那儿交朋友特别快：聊三句知道你哪个部，聊五句知道你在写什么简报，聊十句发现咱俩在写同一份。最惨是机构改革，部门被拆了、改名了，你人没动，老板换了，名片重印。五年后你攒够钱想回悉尼，一查，悉尼唯一对口的单位，总部在堪培拉。"}, "b": {"item_id": "cd30c7840947", "text": "你们知道谁会为了工作搬去堪培拉吗？二十三岁、学政治学、在悉尼租不起房的年轻人。面试官说：“APS4，稳定。”他听成了“国家需要你”。\n\n搬过去第一周，他在环岛里绕了六圈，因为这座城的路就是一个环岛接一个环岛。第二周，喜鹊开始俯冲啄他脑袋。连鸟都比他先熟悉这片土地。\n\n三年后他升到EL1，天天写部长简报，写完被删成一句话。终于在Gungahlin买了房，周末的娱乐是去湖边看别人骑车。\n\n回悉尼参加同学会，大家问他在干嘛。他说：“我在帮部长准备参议院听证会。”\n\n大家又问：“那你的人生呢？”\n\n他愣了三秒：“这个问题……我们会后书面答复。”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0794", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "juicero", "target_name": "Juicero 榨汁机", "facts": ["A Wi-Fi-connected cold-press juicer launched in 2016 at $699, later cut to $399, that squeezed proprietary bags of chopped produce sold by subscription.", "Raised about $120 million from investors including Google Ventures and Kleiner Perkins.", "In April 2017, Bloomberg reporters squeezed the packs by hand and got 7.5 ounces of juice in 90 seconds, faster than the machine's 8 ounces in about two minutes.", "Each pack carried a QR code that the press scanned and checked online before squeezing; it refused expired packs and would not run at all unless connected to Wi-Fi.", "Shut down in September 2017 and offered refunds on the machine."], "a": {"item_id": "2a181ea7d6cd", "text": "我花四百美金买了一台 Juicero。它的工作原理是：把预先切好的果蔬包塞进去，机器扫码确认是正版，然后用很大的压力把汁挤出来。后来有记者发现，那包东西用手就能捏出汁，不用扫码，也不用机器。所以我花四百块买的，其实是一副需要联网验证的手套。\n\n做这个的人我真想见见。一帮硅谷的人觉得榨汁缺一个\"数字化入口\"，拿了一亿多美元风投，就为了让你每周给一袋胡萝卜付订阅费。结果公司关门，机器打折甩卖。我那台现在是家里最贵的摆件，每次看到它，我都觉得它在等我扫码。"}, "b": {"item_id": "c8e0492d10b9", "text": "你知道花400美元买Juicero的都是什么人吗？就是那种早上六点起床、先冥想再跑步、手机里装了十二个健康App的硅谷精英。他们不缺钱，缺的是“我连喝果汁都在用科技”的那份体面。\n\n结果呢？你花400块买了台联网的压榨机，再每袋七八美元买人家的果蔬包。机器还得先扫码、连Wi-Fi，确认你这包没过期，才肯开工。家里断网了？对不起，今天没有健康。\n\n然后彭博社的记者干了件特别缺德的事：直接上手捏那个袋子，挤出来的汁跟机器差不多，速度还更快。\n\n一群天天喊“颠覆传统”的人，被两只手颠覆了。\n\n最惨的是，后来公司倒闭了，给你退钱。你连“至少我还在用”都说不出口了。你最后得到了什么？一个教训：原来你最该投资的科技产品，是自己的手。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0795", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "chicago-deep-dish", "target_name": "芝加哥深盘披萨", "facts": ["Created at Pizzeria Uno in Chicago in 1943; credit is disputed among founder Ike Sewell, his partner Ric Riccardo, and cook Rudy Malnati.", "Assembled upside down: sliced mozzarella on the dough, then toppings, then crushed tomatoes on top, so the cheese does not scorch during the long bake.", "Jon Stewart called it 'tomato soup in a bread bowl' and 'a casserole' on The Daily Show in 2013.", "Chicagoans' everyday pizza is thin 'tavern-style' cut into squares; GrubHub order data reported by WBEZ in 2013 showed city residents ordering non-deep-dish pizza about 10 to 1 over deep dish, with deep dish and stuffed making up under 9% of orders."], "a": {"item_id": "54fda0729132", "text": "管芝加哥深盘叫披萨的，基本都是芝加哥人。你别跟他争，他已经争了一辈子了。他图什么？图个名分。番茄酱铺在最上面，芝士压在最底下，饼边砌得跟城墙一样高，这叫披萨？这叫一锅围了墙的炖菜。可他非得叫披萨，因为叫“砂锅”就没法跟纽约人吵架了。结果呢？进店点单，服务员说：“得烤四十五分钟。”他说：“没事，披萨嘛。”等完上桌，刀叉都给他配好了。他吃两块就扶着墙出门，嘴里还念叨：“这才是真正的披萨。”兄弟，真正的披萨，不用你吃完躺一下午。"}, "b": {"item_id": "2c14d9c46ccf", "text": "最嘴硬的，是来芝加哥旅游的人。排队一小时，服务员说深盘要烤四十五分钟。你都等到这份上了，端上来什么你都得认它是披萨。结果奶酪在底下，酱在上面，还得用刀叉，这叫披萨？这叫面饼做的番茄肉酱焗饭。吃完一块，人瘫在椅子上，像刚做完开胸手术。可你还是要发朋友圈：“正宗芝加哥披萨！”因为你不能承认，自己花了两百美元，吃了顿砂锅。最讽刺的是本地人，他们平时吃的是切成小方块的薄底。深盘就跟全聚德一样，是留着带外地朋友去的。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0796", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "javascript", "target_name": "JavaScript", "facts": ["Written by Brendan Eich at Netscape in ten days in May 1995; named Mocha, then LiveScript, then JavaScript to ride Java's marketing, although the two are unrelated.", "typeof null returns 'object', a bug from the first version that has been kept for compatibility ever since.", "In March 2016 the author of left-pad, an 11-line npm package, unpublished it and broke builds across the industry, including Babel and React.", "[] + {} evaluates to '[object Object]'; {} + [] typed at a console evaluates to 0.", "Standardized by Ecma as 'ECMAScript' in 1997 because the name JavaScript is a trademark, first Sun's and now Oracle's."], "a": {"item_id": "0f8759744771", "text": "我朋友是前端，什么都用 JavaScript 写。写个脚本，用 Node。写个桌面软件，套个 Electron，一开就吃八百兆内存。他说这叫一套技术栈通吃。我说你那是通吃，还是通缉？\n\n上周他想写个开机自动备份，npm install 下来一千三百个包，其中一个叫 is-odd，专门判断一个数是不是奇数。备份脚本跑起来，先等一个包判断今天是不是单号，再等另一个包去找文件。备份成功了，电脑里也多了一个 node_modules，比他硬盘里所有照片加起来还大。\n\n现在他开始学 Rust，说要回归本质。我问他本质是什么，他说是 undefined。"}, "b": {"item_id": "9255e820a06a", "text": "我有个朋友，信一句话：“凡是能用JavaScript写的，最终都会用JavaScript写。”他当预言，我当诅咒。\n\n他写了个计算器，用Electron打包，两百兆，自带一整个Chrome，就为了算一加一。一加一算对了，0.1加0.2出来0.30000000000000004。他说没事，装个库。一装，node_modules比他的人生还沉，里面有个包叫is-odd，专门判断奇数，它还依赖另一个包叫is-number。\n\n他图什么？图“前后端一门语言，不用学第二种”。结果为了这一门，React、Vue、Svelte轮着学，刚学会一个，下一个又出来了。\n\n现在他三十五，头发没了，问我他算个啥。我说你就是typeof null：明明啥也没有，还非说自己是个object。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0797", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "comic-sans", "target_name": "Comic Sans 字体", "facts": ["Designed by Vincent Connare at Microsoft in 1994, modeled on the comic-book lettering in The Dark Knight Returns and Watchmen.", "Made for the speech bubbles of Rover, the cartoon dog in Microsoft Bob; it missed Bob's release and shipped instead in the Windows 95 Plus! pack.", "CERN's July 2012 Higgs boson discovery announcement was presented on slides set in it.", "The 'Ban Comic Sans' campaign, started by designers Dave and Holly Combs in 1999, ran until 2019, when they retired it and launched usecomicsans.com."], "a": {"item_id": "77e8416a8394", "text": "我们小区物业有位行政大哥，一心想跟业主拉近距离，所有通知一律用 Comic Sans。可他不知道，这字体根本没有中文。所以那张停水通知是这样的：“关于停水的通知”，宋体，庄严肃穆；后面“3月15日 8:00—18:00”，圆滚滚的，跟气球一样。一张纸上，中文在念悼词，数字在过六一。\n\n这字体当年是微软专门给一只卡通狗写对话框用的，大哥拿它通知全楼断水，等于派那只狗上门宣布：“汪！你家没水了！”\n\n结果没一个人当真。15号全楼照常洗澡，洗到一半水停了，一群人顶着泡沫下楼找他。大哥还挺委屈：“我通知得那么可爱，你们怎么不看呢？”"}, "b": {"item_id": "3c2c6cba7366", "text": "每个公司都有这么一位行政，觉得通知太严肃，大家会有压力，于是打开Word，把字体改成Comic Sans。图什么？图亲切，图“有温度”。\n\n结果上周公司发通知：“因业务调整，将优化百分之三十人员。”Comic Sans。全办公室第一反应：哟，团建？还有人回了句“收到，开心”。\n\n你知道这字体当初是给谁做的吗？一只卡通狗，专门给它的对话气泡用的。你拿一只狗的字体宣布裁员，狗看了都觉得不妥。\n\n最后，这位行政自己也在名单里。他去找HR，说能不能给我开个正式点的离职证明，宋体的。HR说：不用，已经打好了，Comic Sans，右下角还有个笑脸。\n\n他说：这笑脸不是我加的。\n\nHR说：对，是你上次培训时教我的。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0798", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "klingon", "target_name": "克林贡语", "facts": ["Created by linguist Marc Okrand for Star Trek III (1984), building on a few words the actor James Doohan improvised for the 1979 film.", "Deliberately alien grammar: object-verb-subject word order and no verb 'to be', so Hamlet's line became 'taH pagh taHbe'', 'continue or not continue'.", "The Klingon Language Institute (1992) published Hamlet 'in the original Klingon'; Bing Translator added Klingon in 2013 and Duolingo in 2018.", "Okrand's 1985 Klingon Dictionary listed roughly 1,500 words, and fluent speakers number only in the dozens: 20 to 30 in 2009, 30 to 50 by 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "ab8685eebdf7", "text": "全世界能把克林贡语说流利的人，据说也就几十个。你想想，几十个人，凑一块儿连个婚礼都撑不满，还得从《星际迷航》剧组借人。\n\n这帮人图什么？图的是终于能用原文读《哈姆雷特》。对，有人真把莎士比亚翻成了克林贡语，理由是“莎士比亚本来就是克林贡人，被地球人偷了”。你说他疯吧，他还出了书。\n\n最狠的是有个语言学家，从儿子出生起只跟他说克林贡语，想培养世界上第一个克林贡母语者。结果孩子长到几岁，爸爸一开口说克林贡语，他就装听不见。你看，叛逆期都比别人早，还是外星文化先教会他的：荣誉第一，老爹靠边。\n\n而且这门语言根本不适合过日子。它连“你好”都没有，见面打招呼说的是“nuqneH”，意思是“你想干嘛？”你练了十年，终于能流利地跟人吵架了，问题是全世界能听懂你骂人的，也就那几十个，还都在同一个群里。"}, "b": {"item_id": "c9277fcc0b88", "text": "练到流利克林贡语的人，我真心佩服。白天是会计，晚上披着披风，图什么？图终于有人配得上他的愤怒。结果呢，这门语言没有“你好”，打招呼只有一句“nuqneH”，意思是“你要干嘛？”。三年苦功，换来全世界几百个同好，见面第一句就是找茬。\n\n更惨的是语序。克林贡语是宾语、动词、主语，你骂人得先说被骂的是谁、骂了什么，最后才说是谁干的。一句狠话憋到末尾才亮出主语，气势全靠肺活量。\n\n回家对老婆表白，没有“谢谢”，没有“对不起”，这门语言压根没给你认错的词。想道歉？只能把对方打一顿再说“你赢了”。\n\n所以流利的下场就是：全世界会说的人聚在一个群里，用克林贡语吵架。吵了三个小时，谁也没听懂谁，因为谁都在等对方把主语说出来。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0799", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "windows-8", "target_name": "Windows 8", "facts": ["Released in October 2012; removed the Start button and booted into a full-screen grid of tiles designed for touch screens most PCs did not have.", "The 8.1 update in 2013 put a Start button back, Windows 10 (2015) restored the Start menu, and Microsoft skipped the name Windows 9 altogether.", "Microsoft dropped the 'Metro' name in August 2012 after reports that German retailer Metro AG had objected on trademark grounds; Microsoft called 'Metro' a code name and denied any litigation, and the style was afterwards known informally as 'Modern UI'.", "The 'Charms bar' hid Search, Share, and Settings behind an invisible swipe from the right edge.", "Windows RT, the ARM edition launched alongside it, could not run ordinary Windows programs; Microsoft wrote down $900 million of unsold Surface RT tablets in 2013."], "a": {"item_id": "4dccc16b3e4c", "text": "我表哥就是那种人。2012年他给我家台式机装了Windows 8，理由就一句：“新的肯定好。”好什么？这系统是给平板设计的，满屏方块，你拿鼠标去戳，像拿筷子按电梯。开始按钮没了，他说不需要：“鼠标往右上角一甩，侧边栏就出来了。”我爸用了十年XP，现在为了关个机，得拿鼠标在屏幕边上来回招魂。最后我爸的关机方式改成了拔插头。表哥现在每周日被叫回来，不是吃饭，是找“我的电脑”去哪儿了。他还嘴硬：“等8.1就好了。”8.1来了，开始按钮真回来了，一点开，还是那屏方块。微软都认错了，他还没认。"}, "b": {"item_id": "09a19029d52d", "text": "有一种人，专门给台式机装 Windows 8。你问他图啥？他说，触屏才是未来，我得提前用上。问题是他这台机器是机箱加显示器加一个鼠标，屏幕根本摸不到。开机后满屏都是色块磁贴，像走进一家超市，货架上全是天气和新闻，唯独没有他要的开始菜单。他想关机，找了二十分钟，最后在屏幕右边缘一划，弹出一个叫\"超级按钮\"的东西。我很怀疑取这名字的人从来没关过机。最后他花九十块买了个第三方软件，把开始按钮请了回来。他现在逢人就说：\"我用的是 Windows 8，但我把它改回了 Windows 7。\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0800", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "papyrus", "target_name": "Papyrus 字体", "facts": ["Hand-drawn by Chris Costello in 1982 over about six months, when he was 23; he sold the rights to Letraset for $750.", "Bundled with macOS and with Microsoft Office, which is why it appears on so many menus and signs.", "James Cameron's Avatar (2009) used it for the film's logo and subtitles.", "A 2017 Saturday Night Live sketch starring Ryan Gosling is about a man unable to get over that choice."], "a": {"item_id": "02bcbf4a1359", "text": "开养生馆的老板选招牌，流程我都猜得到：打开Word，字体一路往下滑，滑到Papyrus，眼睛一亮——“哎，这个毛边，有古老东方的神秘感。”大哥，这字是个美国小伙二十三岁拿钢笔在纸上描出来的，你这“东方”是批发来的。更绝的是隔壁泰式按摩、墨西哥卷饼、埃及烤肉，全是同一个Papyrus，整条街像同一个古文明的遗址。他图什么？图Office自带，图不花钱，图“看着有文化”。结果呢？顾客一抬头，以为进的不是足疗店，是《阿凡达》片场。卡梅隆好歹花了几亿美元才用上这款字，你花了零块零毛，撞得一模一样，人家拿了奥斯卡，你拿了张差评：“技师手法一般，招牌像蓝色外星人开的。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "d6bcba0a659e", "text": "开养生馆的老板选招牌字体，预算四百，又想显得有五千年底蕴，于是选了Papyrus，就是那个边儿像被猫挠过、笔画细得像没吃饱的字。他的逻辑是：这字一看就古埃及，古埃及一看就神秘，神秘就能卖六百八的艾灸套餐。我说艾灸是中国的，跟尼罗河有什么关系？他说：“反正是古的。”\n\n结果隔壁新开了家埃及烤肉店，也用Papyrus。两块招牌并排一挂，顾客全懵了：有人进养生馆问有没有鹰嘴豆泥，有人进烤肉店问能不能刮痧。设计师路过也不进门，就站门口拍照发朋友圈，配三个字：“又一个。”\n\n老板花四百块，买了个跟《阿凡达》同款的“独一无二”。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0801", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "microsoft-word", "target_name": "Microsoft Word", "facts": ["Launched in 1983 as 'Multi-Tool Word' for Xenix and MS-DOS.", "Clippy, the animated paperclip assistant, arrived in Office 97 and was removed in Office 2007; Microsoft itself ran ads mocking it in 2001.", "Its .docx format, Office Open XML, was fast-tracked to ISO standard 29500 in 2008 as a roughly 6,000-page specification, amid accusations that national committees had been packed.", "Older spell checkers, Word's included, offered 'Cupertino' as a correction for a mistyped 'cooperation', and the resulting 'Cupertino effect' made it into published NATO, EU and UN-agency documents."], "a": {"item_id": "74d77985a3e9", "text": "用Word排毕业论文的人，都信一句话：“所见即所得。”结果你看见的是第38页，导师打开是第41页，图还跑到上一章去了。\n\n这帮人从不用“样式”，标题全靠手动选中、加粗、调三号。目录也是手敲的，后面那一排点，是空格键一个一个敲出来的。\n\n答辩前一晚，导师说加一句话。他在第二章敲了个句号，整篇论文像多米诺骨牌，参考文献直接顶到封面上。\n\n他想设页码，摘要用罗马数字，正文用阿拉伯数字，找分节符找了三小时，最后决定：摘要不要页码了。\n\n文件名叫“终稿_改3_真的终稿_导师改后_最终.docx”。\n\n最后交上去的是PDF。这是他一辈子，第一次让论文别动。"}, "b": {"item_id": "3173e42e100a", "text": "每年六月，全国几十万毕业生集体转行当排版工人。图什么？就图学校那份模板：摘要页码用罗马数字，正文从1开始。为了这个，他们学会了一个这辈子再也用不上的词：分节符。然后挨页去点掉“链接到前一节”，漏点一次，第三章的页眉就写着“致谢”。\n\n最惨的是插图。你把图往上挪了一毫米，后面二十页集体搬家，图3-2直接住进了参考文献。\n\n答辩完问他收获了什么，他说：收获了“论文_终稿_改3_打死不改.docx”。还有，千万别右键“更新域”，那是我目录最后一次完整的样子。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0802", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "jackfruit", "target_name": "菠萝蜜", "facts": ["The largest fruit that grows on a tree; single fruits can weigh over 40 kg.", "The national fruit of Bangladesh and the state fruit of Kerala and Tamil Nadu.", "Unripe flesh is sold as a vegan 'pulled pork' substitute, although it has only 1-2 g of protein per 100 g.", "Cutting one releases a sticky white latex, so cooks oil their knives and hands first.", "A member of the mulberry family (Moraceae), a relative of breadfruit and figs."], "a": {"item_id": "73bd57e7ed48", "text": "有老板把青菠萝蜜撕成丝，当手撕猪肉卖。我就佩服，创业第一步，先跟自己的手结仇。青菠萝蜜那个胶，比前任还难甩，洗手液没用，得拿食用油搓。后厨剥一天，十根手指粘成一只爪子，出来跟客人握手，客人以为他入了什么帮派。\n\n图啥？图便宜。可你算算：猪肉切了就能炒，菠萝蜜得先拿刀砍一身刺，再对付一手胶，炖三小时，还得倒半瓶酱油才勉强有点肉味。人工加调料，够买半头猪了。\n\n最后下场，有食客点评：“这是我吃过最像菠萝蜜的猪肉。”一句话，比市监局还准。罚款单下来，老板终于明白，这生意他唯一卖出去的肉，是自己的肉疼。"}, "b": {"item_id": "0c2c69874c8f", "text": "有人把菠萝蜜炖成“手撕肉”，标价三十八，招牌写着“植物蛋白，口感还原”。我点了一份，吃了一口，好家伙，是甜的。不是一般的甜，是榴莲没熟透、香蕉刚放软那种甜。我以为自己点的是红烧肉，结果是一碗加了纤维的糖水。老板挺自豪，说“你看这纹理，一丝一丝的，跟猪肉纤维一模一样”。我说老板，猪肉的纤维是肌肉，你这是树上挂的果囊，你是把一棵树的肚子撕开给我看。他说这叫创新。后来店关了，听说他转行卖菠萝蜜酥，说“不像肉了，但它甜”。我说你这才是找对了路子，肉没卖出去，至少糖分到位了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0803", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "emacs", "target_name": "Emacs", "facts": ["Began in 1976 as a set of macros for the TECO editor at MIT's AI Lab, written by Richard Stallman and others; GNU Emacs followed in 1985.", "Extensible in its own Lisp dialect, Emacs Lisp; the standing joke is that it is a fine operating system lacking only a good editor.", "Ships with a psychotherapist (M-x doctor), Tetris, a mail client, a calendar, and a web browser (eww).", "An old backronym is 'Eight Megabytes And Constantly Swapping', from when 8 MB was a lot of memory.", "'Emacs pinky' is the name for the hand strain blamed on its Control-key chords."], "a": {"item_id": "ce1edb495cdc", "text": "有一种人嫌操作系统太小，非要把Emacs装成操作系统。邮件在里面收，日程用org-mode排，网页用eww看，连音乐都在里面放。图什么？图一个界面管所有事。结果一启动，init.el跑了四十秒，我问他怎么退出，他说C-x C-c。我说那是退出，他说那是\"我还没想好要不要走\"。最惨的是他的小拇指，Ctrl和Meta全压在它身上。去年体检，医生问是不是长期弹钢琴，他说不是，我在写Lisp。现在他的操作系统还是没装好，但他已经能用一个快捷键，把自己的手指头按进了 *scratch* 缓冲区。"}, "b": {"item_id": "d38cb162db73", "text": "我有个朋友，把Emacs当操作系统用。邮件用mu4e，日程用org-mode，连窗口管理都交给EXWM。他的电脑开机只有一个目的：启动Emacs。\n\n我问他图什么，他说：“这样我一辈子都不用离开Emacs。”兄弟，这不叫自由，这叫软禁，门还是你自己拿elisp焊死的。\n\n他的init.el写了三千行，比他简历还长。上周升级了一个包，邮件收不了，日程没了，窗口也打不开，全家断电。查了一晚上，原因是少了一个括号。\n\n最惨的是他的小拇指。按了十年Ctrl，得了腱鞘炎。医生让他少按，他回家装了evil-mode。用Vim的键位，在Emacs里，躲Emacs。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0804", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "stinky-tofu", "target_name": "臭豆腐", "facts": ["Tofu soaked for hours to a day or two in a brine that has itself fermented for months from vegetables such as bamboo shoots and mustard greens, often with dried shrimp or meat; the smell is the brine's, not the bean's.", "Changsha's version is black, deep-fried and doused in chili; Taiwan's night-market version is golden and served with pickled cabbage; Shaoxing's, brined with amaranth stalks, is fried pale yellow or steamed with other 'stinky' pickles.", "Legend credits Wang Zhihe, a Qing-era exam candidate who left his unsold tofu too long in 1669; a Beijing fermented-bean-curd brand still carries his name.", "Street vendors are its main outlet, and the smell carries for a block."], "a": {"item_id": "416c9c06dab4", "text": "吃街边臭豆腐的人，都有一句口头禅：\"闻着臭，吃着香。\"这哪是说豆腐，这是他们的人生总结，意思是：我知道我有问题，但你们别管。\n\n他们排四十分钟队，站在油锅旁边，边熏边等，像报了个自费烟熏疗程。拿到手一碗黑乎乎的东西，插着一根牙签，还得再淋勺辣酱，撒把香菜，仪式感拉满，吃的时候还不能坐，必须站在马路边，对着车尾气，吃得热泪盈眶。\n\n吃完以为结束了？它跟你回家了。头发入味，外套入味，围巾直接入股。进电梯，全楼的人齐刷刷看你，你还得假装是旁边那位的味。\n\n最惨的是，你自己闻不到了，因为你已经被腌透了。\n\n你就是那块臭豆腐，区别是，没人愿意为你排四十分钟的队。"}, "b": {"item_id": "0db2c726fd64", "text": "街边吃臭豆腐的人，我观察过，全是传教士。你路过，他嘴里正烫得直哈气，还要腾出一只手拦你：“闻着臭，吃着香！”大哥，这句话我从小听到大，你说得跟刚悟出来的一样。\n\n他图什么？图的就是那个反差。全世界都捂鼻子，就他看透了本质。一块发酵的豆腐，硬是吃出了顿悟的表情。\n\n然后呢？然后他上地铁了。五站路，整节车厢都在低头检查自己的鞋。他还挺得意：凡人，不懂。\n\n最惨的是那根牙签。一碗六块，就配一根，戳到最后一块，辣汤顺着手腕流进袖口。第二天开会他一抬手，领导皱眉：“小王，你家下水道是不是堵了？”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0805", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "vim", "target_name": "Vim", "facts": ["Released by Bram Moolenaar in November 1991 as 'Vi IMitation', a vi clone he had begun writing for the Amiga in 1988 on top of Stevie; the expansion became 'Vi IMproved' with version 2.0 in December 1993.", "Licensed as charityware: the licence asks users to donate to ICCF Holland, a charity Moolenaar ran for children in Uganda.", "Stack Overflow's question 'How to exit the Vim editor?' passed one million visits in May 2017, which Stack Overflow marked with a blog post.", "Modal editing: in normal mode, typing letters runs commands rather than inserting text; :wq saves and quits, :q! quits and discards changes."], "a": {"item_id": "ca805bf868dc", "text": "我们组有个哥们儿用Vim。你怎么知道的？你不用知道，他会告诉你。开会共享屏幕，需求还没讲，先把鼠标往旁边一推：“我不用这个。”大哥，你推的是公司资产。\n\n他的.vimrc八百行，四十个插件：文件树、自动补全、调试器。我一看，这不就是VS Code吗？他说不一样，这是他亲手配的。哦，花三个月，手工拼了个免费软件。\n\n上礼拜他在服务器上改配置，突然不动了。全组盯着他，他说“我在思考”。思考了五分钟。后来才知道，那台机器上没有他的.vimrc。他进去了，出不来。\n\n现在他一紧张就按Esc。不是习惯，是创伤。"}, "b": {"item_id": "773f0cf90ece", "text": "我有个朋友用 Vim 写代码，这本来没什么，问题是他得让全世界知道。每天发朋友圈：\"今天 hjkl 走了八个小时，手没离开主键盘区。\"配图是一屏幕冒号和波浪号。底下点赞的全是同款人，互相夸：\"牛逼，我也是。\"他们图什么？图的就是别人看不懂还得点赞。结果上周他要退出文件，手一滑按了个 i，开始在文件里狂打字，还没反应过来。他想起有个 Esc，按了二十下，屏幕纹丝不动。最后他在群里喊：\"救命，我退不出去了！\"群友秒回一排：先按 Esc，再敲 :q!。他回：\"我已经发过朋友圈了，不能认输。\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0806", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "windows-8", "target_name": "Windows 8", "facts": ["Released in October 2012; removed the Start button and booted into a full-screen grid of tiles designed for touch screens most PCs did not have.", "The 8.1 update in 2013 put a Start button back, Windows 10 (2015) restored the Start menu, and Microsoft skipped the name Windows 9 altogether.", "Microsoft dropped the 'Metro' name in August 2012 after reports that German retailer Metro AG had objected on trademark grounds; Microsoft called 'Metro' a code name and denied any litigation, and the style was afterwards known informally as 'Modern UI'.", "The 'Charms bar' hid Search, Share, and Settings behind an invisible swipe from the right edge.", "Windows RT, the ARM edition launched alongside it, could not run ordinary Windows programs; Microsoft wrote down $900 million of unsold Surface RT tablets in 2013."], "a": {"item_id": "4dea39aaf51b", "text": "我有个哥们儿，2012年非要给台式机装Windows 8，理由是“新的肯定比旧的好”。我说你这电脑连个触屏都没有，他说没事，“我用鼠标点磁贴”。\n\n装完那天他请我去看。开机，满屏花花绿绿的大方块，跟营业厅的叫号屏似的。他拿着鼠标在那儿横着拖，拖了半天才找到“桌面”这个磁贴。我说你装了个新系统，就为了点进去看旧桌面？\n\n最惨的是关机。开始按钮没了，他找了十分钟，最后鼠标往右上角一怼，蹦出一排竖条，设置，电源，关机。三步。他关一次机，比他的电脑开一次机还慢。\n\n现在他养成了个习惯：鼠标一闲下来，就往屏幕角落里甩。别人的鼠标是用来点东西的，他的鼠标是用来跟屏幕四个角打游击的。\n\n后来8.1把开始按钮还给他了，他激动得点了一下。结果点开的还是那屏大方块。"}, "b": {"item_id": "a62b05dc7bff", "text": "给台式机装Windows 8的人，我特别好奇你图什么。图新鲜。结果开机，满屏彩色方块，我就想打开个文件夹，它给我弹出个\"体育\"。\n\n关机更绝。台式机，没有开始按钮，你得把鼠标甩到屏幕右下角，等侧边栏像幽灵一样滑出来，点设置，再点电源。我第一次关机找了十分钟，我怀疑这是微软的智力测试。\n\n而且这帮人的显示器根本不是触屏，可Windows 8整套设计就是让你拿手指划。所以你会看到一个成年人，坐在台式机前，手指悬在半空，犹豫要不要戳。\n\n最后下场都一样：第二天下载Classic Shell，把开始菜单请回来。花钱升级，就为了把它打扮成Windows 7。这不叫升级，这叫花钱请个演员，去演你的前任。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0807", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "almond-milk", "target_name": "杏仁奶", "facts": ["California grows about 80% of the world's almonds.", "A single almond takes about 1.1 US gallons (roughly 4 litres) of water to grow, a figure Mother Jones published in 2014 that spread during California's drought.", "A 2015 US class action alleged Almond Breeze contained only about 2% almonds; it partly survived a motion to dismiss, was stayed in 2017 pending a separate Blue Diamond labelling settlement, and the plaintiffs then dropped it.", "Pollinating California's almond orchards each February requires trucking in most of the commercial honeybee colonies in the United States.", "Medieval European cooks used almond milk as a Lenten substitute for dairy, centuries before the current fashion."], "a": {"item_id": "a3df26d73f36", "text": "你们身边有没有这种人？每天早上端一杯杏仁奶，说自己是为了地球。我问他：你知道做一杯杏仁奶要喝掉多少水吗？他说不知道，但包装上画着阳光下的草地，看着很安心。杏仁是出了名的费水作物，加州一棵杏树一年要喝好几百升水，他这杯“环保”，其实是拿加州的水库在泡杏仁。这人图什么？图个心安。图朋友圈里配一张杯子的照片，文案写“从我做起”。结果呢？牛奶钱没省下来，一瓶杏仁奶比牛奶贵一倍多。味道像加了两滴悔恨的白开水，倒进咖啡里直接分层，像一杯正在办离婚的饮料。上个月他开车去超市囤了三箱，车是烧油的，车钥匙还挂着一只环保袋。"}, "b": {"item_id": "8cd05311eac8", "text": "我朋友为了环保戒了牛奶，改喝杏仁奶，说牛奶太费水。我说你知道一颗杏仁要喝四升水吗？加州的杏仁树比加州人还能喝。而且杏仁奶里杏仁只占百分之二，你花三十八块，买了一盒百分之九十八的水，外加一点杏仁味的遗憾。\n\n更绝的是，杏仁树要授粉，每年得用卡车把全美国的蜜蜂拉去加州打工，累死一大片。他不想伤害奶牛，结果当上了蜜蜂的包工头。\n\n最后咖啡一倒进去，絮状分层，像一场小型水土流失。他还端着杯子庄严地说：“我在拯救地球。”\n\n哥们，地球没让你救，它就想让你把这杯喝完，别发朋友圈。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0808", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "microsoft-teams", "target_name": "Microsoft Teams", "facts": ["Launched in 2017, bundled at no extra charge into Office 365, and replaced Skype for Business.", "After Slack's antitrust complaint, Microsoft unbundled Teams from Office in the EU in 2023 and worldwide in 2024; the European Commission still sent a statement of objections in 2024.", "Microsoft reported 320 million monthly active users in 2023.", "Microsoft shut down consumer Skype in May 2025 and sent its users to Teams."], "a": {"item_id": "1fd1f04cdc79", "text": "逼我们用 Teams 的人，我研究过了，是 IT 总监。他的理由我听懂了：买了 Microsoft 365，Teams 免费送。他不是选了个软件，他是领了个赠品，还拿去年底述职：\"我给公司省了三十万！\"省的是软件钱，花的是我们的命。现在全公司表面在 Teams 上，实际都在微信群里问：\"那个会议链接到底点哪儿？\"最惨的是他自己，每次他一发言，头像都是黄色的\"离开\"，人明明坐在那儿，系统说他不在。推广一年，他终于被系统说中了：这个人，在公司，已经离开了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "cfb198c08db3", "text": "我们公司那个力推全员用Teams的VP，我真想当面问他图啥。图啥？图一份\"数字化转型\"的PPT。结果呢？全公司每天三小时盯着那三个跳动的小圆点，等对面打完一个\"好\"字。他自己更惨，上周开会共享屏幕，忘了点\"允许\"，全场看他浏览器收藏夹看了十分钟。现在他每晚十一点在\"全体员工\"频道里@所有人：\"大家收到了吗？\"收到了老板，大家的状态早改成\"请勿打扰\"了，就是不想回。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0809", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "durian", "target_name": "榴莲", "facts": ["Banned on Singapore's MRT trains and in many Southeast Asian hotels because of the smell.", "A 2017 genome study in Nature Genetics traced the odour to a duplicated family of MGL genes that pump out volatile sulfur compounds.", "China buys most of the world's durian exports, chiefly from Thailand, in a trade worth billions of dollars a year.", "Alfred Russel Wallace wrote in 1856 that eating it was 'a new sensation worth a voyage to the East to experience'."], "a": {"item_id": "aea5369accce", "text": "有些人在地铁上吃榴莲，图的就是一个\"我不在乎\"。你看他闭着眼，一脸品红酒的表情，可榴莲壳上的刺是扎手的，果肉是黏的，他一瓣一瓣抠出来，手指上全是黄色的汁。车厢里二十个人，被迫跟他一起品鉴。那味道先是甜，甜完一秒，洋葱、奶油、还有一股硫磺的冲劲，直接从你鼻孔顶到脑门。车门一开，他抱着半个带刺的壳下车，回头冲大家笑了一下。全车人都在看他，我猜他以为自己在过生活，其实他是今天全车唯一被记住的人，记住的还是那股味儿。"}, "b": {"item_id": "68573ec1558a", "text": "地铁上吃榴莲的人，我观察过，他们不是饿，是在传教。你一皱眉，他立刻甩出那句经典台词：“闻着臭，吃着香！”大哥，我又不吃，你光给我上半句，这跟只收定金不发货有什么区别？\n\n而且他还爱报价：“猫山王，八十一斤！”意思是这味儿是进口的，你得心怀感激。全车厢被迫消费，每人摊他二十块钱的味儿。\n\n下场呢？他到站下车，以为结束了。不，那味儿会住进他指甲缝里，洗三遍，晚上挠头都是猫山王。第二天同事全问他：“你昨天坐的几号线？”\n\n新加坡地铁明令禁止带榴莲。我建议咱们也补一条：禁止带吃过榴莲的人。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0810", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "comic-sans", "target_name": "Comic Sans 字体", "facts": ["Designed by Vincent Connare at Microsoft in 1994, modeled on the comic-book lettering in The Dark Knight Returns and Watchmen.", "Made for the speech bubbles of Rover, the cartoon dog in Microsoft Bob; it missed Bob's release and shipped instead in the Windows 95 Plus! pack.", "CERN's July 2012 Higgs boson discovery announcement was presented on slides set in it.", "The 'Ban Comic Sans' campaign, started by designers Dave and Holly Combs in 1999, ran until 2019, when they retired it and launched usecomicsans.com."], "a": {"item_id": "5f875c2be689", "text": "你们收过用Comic Sans写的正式通知吗？“本周六全小区停水”，底下配着那个圆滚滚的漫画体。发这种通知的，多半是物业或者HR，他们心里想的是：字圆一点，坏消息就没那么硬。可这字原本是微软给一个卡通小助手配气泡台词用的，骨子里就是为了“亲切”设计的。结果呢？停水像生日派对邀请函，扣工资像在发贴纸。大家看完第一反应不是慌，是笑：“这是哪个幼儿园发的？”通知被截图传遍业主群，发通知的人成了全小区的表情包。他本想做个好人，最后做成了段子手。"}, "b": {"item_id": "3c2c6cba7366", "text": "每个公司都有这么一位行政，觉得通知太严肃，大家会有压力，于是打开Word，把字体改成Comic Sans。图什么？图亲切，图“有温度”。\n\n结果上周公司发通知：“因业务调整，将优化百分之三十人员。”Comic Sans。全办公室第一反应：哟，团建？还有人回了句“收到，开心”。\n\n你知道这字体当初是给谁做的吗？一只卡通狗，专门给它的对话气泡用的。你拿一只狗的字体宣布裁员，狗看了都觉得不妥。\n\n最后，这位行政自己也在名单里。他去找HR，说能不能给我开个正式点的离职证明，宋体的。HR说：不用，已经打好了，Comic Sans，右下角还有个笑脸。\n\n他说：这笑脸不是我加的。\n\nHR说：对，是你上次培训时教我的。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0811", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "javascript", "target_name": "JavaScript", "facts": ["Written by Brendan Eich at Netscape in ten days in May 1995; named Mocha, then LiveScript, then JavaScript to ride Java's marketing, although the two are unrelated.", "typeof null returns 'object', a bug from the first version that has been kept for compatibility ever since.", "In March 2016 the author of left-pad, an 11-line npm package, unpublished it and broke builds across the industry, including Babel and React.", "[] + {} evaluates to '[object Object]'; {} + [] typed at a console evaluates to 0.", "Standardized by Ecma as 'ECMAScript' in 1997 because the name JavaScript is a trademark, first Sun's and now Oracle's."], "a": {"item_id": "c214f8db4fb9", "text": "我有个同事，什么都用 JavaScript 写。写脚本用 JS，写后台用 Node，连他家的烧水壶我都怀疑跑着 npm。他图什么？他说\"一门语言打天下\"。结果呢？一个 hello world 下了四百兆的 node_modules，八万个文件，他说这叫生态。上周他算 0.1 加 0.2，屏幕蹦出 0.30000000000000004，他盯了十分钟，说这是\"浮点的浪漫\"。昨天他 npm install 了一下，整个项目塌了，原因是有人从仓库里撤掉了一个十一行的小包，叫 left-pad。现在他分不清 null 和 undefined，我问他 typeof null 为什么是 object，他说这是历史遗留问题。我说，你才是。"}, "b": {"item_id": "d6f1a64d2099", "text": "现在有一种人，什么都要拿 JavaScript 写。网页写完写后端，后端写完写桌面软件，现在在研究拿它控制烤箱。你问他图啥，他说：\"我不想再学一门新语言。\"\n\n兄弟，你为了不学一门语言，每周都在学一个新框架。\n\n他写的记事本用 Electron，打开占八百兆内存。因为里面塞了一整个浏览器，就为了让你输入\"买菜\"两个字。\n\n他还跟你解释：\"1\"加 1 等于 11，\"1\"减 1 等于 0，这叫灵活。灵活到最后，项目源码三千行，node_modules 两个 G。\n\n上线那天报错：undefined is not a function。他盯着屏幕，一脸茫然：\"到底是谁 undefined 了？\"\n\n我觉得是他的职业规划。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0812", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "monopoly", "target_name": "大富翁（Monopoly）", "facts": ["Descends from Lizzie Magie's The Landlord's Game, patented in 1904 to teach the evils of land monopoly in the spirit of Henry George.", "Charles Darrow sold his version to Parker Brothers in 1935 and was credited as the inventor; Parker bought Magie's patent for $500.", "The official rules require any unbought property to be auctioned, and 'Free Parking' pays nothing; the house rules that skip the auction and pile cash on Free Parking are why games drag.", "During the Second World War, British intelligence had maps, compasses, and real currency hidden in sets sent to prisoner-of-war camps."], "a": {"item_id": "d983ef681d05", "text": "我们家过年必玩大富翁，说是联络感情，其实是全家一起验证：血缘关系，扛不住房地产。\n\n我爸每次抢着当银行，他不是想为大家服务，他是想体验一下贪污。我妈只买橙色那一片，说吉利，结果整局靠收我的租活着。收的时候还特温柔：“妈妈收你的钱，你不会生气吧？”我说不会。她说：“那再加五十。”\n\n我九岁的表弟哭着求我把铁路卖给他，我心一软卖了。三圈以后，他拿着我的铁路，把我送进了监狱。\n\n我走投无路，找我爸借钱，他说：“行，按银行利率。”我说你是我爸！他说：“现在我是银行。”\n\n玩到凌晨，我爸有全部酒店，我妈有全部橙色，我手里只剩一张出狱卡。\n\n全家人公平分配了战果：他们赢了钱，我赢了离开这张桌子的权利。"}, "b": {"item_id": "9bfb2dd2da6e", "text": "每年过年，我家都要玩一局大富翁。说好了增进感情，结果每次都是战争。我舅非要当银行家，抽屉一拉，像在管国库，你借两百块买块地，他能跟你讲半小时利率。我爸图的是赢。他说这叫培养孩子的商业头脑，其实是六十岁了还在抢我十二岁妹妹的绿色地皮。我妈最绝，从不买地，专坐在\"免费停车\"那格收钱，说这叫稳健。最后我姐在紫色街区盖满酒店，我爸一脚踩上去，房租交得他当场抵押了电视机。我舅把银行的钱全押在自己身上，宣布：\"我破产了，但我是清白的。\"那局没人赢，大家都进了监狱。过年那顿饭，全家坐一桌，谁也不说话，桌上摆着一堆假钞。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0813", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "keurig", "target_name": "Keurig 胶囊咖啡机", "facts": ["John Sylvan invented the K-Cup in the early 1990s and sold his stake for about $50,000 in 1997; in 2015 he told The Atlantic, 'I feel bad sometimes that I ever did it.'", "Keurig 2.0 (2014) read an ink mark on the lid and rejected unlicensed pods; sales fell and workarounds spread online.", "By 2014 enough K-Cups were sold each year to circle the Earth more than ten times; the pods were mostly unrecyclable #7 plastic until a switch to polypropylene by 2020.", "Keurig Green Mountain merged with Dr Pepper Snapple in 2018 to form Keurig Dr Pepper."], "a": {"item_id": "612741b2093f", "text": "你们办公室里有没有这种人？每天早上站在Keurig前面，按下去，等它“滋滋”响半分钟，端出一杯颜色像红茶、味道像热水的东西。他图什么？图的是“我也是讲究生活的人”。可这份讲究一个K杯一块多，一个月下来够买一台手冲壶了。更惨的是垃圾，那个小塑料杯带铝箔盖，还得掰开、分类，最后扔进去的还是塑料。他以为买的是咖啡，其实买的是“按一下就有”的错觉。结果一天一杯，喝到最后，他端着的是一杯加了热水的回忆。"}, "b": {"item_id": "771391305fbe", "text": "用Keurig的人，我一眼就认得出来。他们会跟你说：“我不是懒，我是讲效率。”效率？大哥，你站在机器前面等它咕噜咕噜预热一分钟，然后对着三个按钮，6盎司、8盎司、10盎司，陷入人生最重大的抉择。最后你按了10盎司，因为“划算”。可胶囊里就那么一小撮粉！你花四块钱买了一杯咖啡味的热水，还觉得自己薅到了羊毛。\n\n办公室那盒混装胶囊更惨。一个月后好喝的全被抢光，只剩榛子味和低因。你盯着那盒剩货，就像盯着你的职业生涯：选择很多，没人想要。\n\n对了，发明K杯那哥们后来公开说他后悔了。亲爹都不认这孩子，你天天喂它。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0814", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "risk", "target_name": "《战国风云》（Risk）", "facts": ["Invented in 1957 by Albert Lamorisse, the French director of The Red Balloon, as La Conquête du Monde; Parker Brothers published it in the United States in 1959.", "The map has 42 territories, among them Kamchatka, Irkutsk, and Yakutsk, while the entire Middle East is one territory.", "Holding Australia, which has a single entry point, is the standard opening strategy.", "A full game can run for hours, and players eliminated early have nothing to do but watch."], "a": {"item_id": "407cba2981c5", "text": "有一种人，非要把《战国风云》玩到底。开局说好两小时吃完饭就散，结果凌晨三点，他还在盯着澳大利亚那块地，因为守它能多拿两个兵。他图什么？不图赢，图的是那句\"我占领了全世界\"。他掷三个六，对面掷两个一，他拍桌子喊\"天命所归\"。最后呢？他手里攥着整个北美，兵力只剩一颗，全世界就剩他和堪察加边上一个不肯投降的阿拉斯加。窗外天亮了，他还在算下一轮能不能凑齐一套骑兵卡。"}, "b": {"item_id": "5c5c0ab82bd3", "text": "你们见过真把一局《战国风云》玩到底的人吗？我见过，他到现在还单身。开局第一句话：“澳洲归我。”然后在暹罗堆了四十个兵，四个小时一动不动，跟守小区大门的保安一样。问他图什么，他说：“我在等卡。”凑齐三张一换，眼睛都亮了，好像换来的不是一把塑料小兵，是房本。凌晨三点，他终于从堪察加打进阿拉斯加，三颗骰子全是六，对面两个一。他站起来大喊：“世界是我的了！”没人理他。桌上就剩他一个，其他人早把国家互相送光，提前出局，打车回家了。他拿下了全世界，代价是再也没人接他电话。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0815", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "microsoft-teams", "target_name": "Microsoft Teams", "facts": ["Launched in 2017, bundled at no extra charge into Office 365, and replaced Skype for Business.", "After Slack's antitrust complaint, Microsoft unbundled Teams from Office in the EU in 2023 and worldwide in 2024; the European Commission still sent a statement of objections in 2024.", "Microsoft reported 320 million monthly active users in 2023.", "Microsoft shut down consumer Skype in May 2025 and sent its users to Teams."], "a": {"item_id": "04d98c0d62fd", "text": "我们公司换Teams，拍板的是IT总监老王。为什么选它？不是因为好用，是因为它“白送”，买Office 365附赠的。结果全公司天天在里面找文件：发在聊天里的跑进了OneDrive，发在频道里的又钻进了SharePoint。老王开会说“我共享一下屏幕”，然后整个部门盯着他的桌面看了五分钟。\n\n后来呢？欧盟说微软捆绑销售，Teams得拆开单买了。老王“白送”的东西开始收钱，他自己还天天被标成黄色“离开”，五分钟不动鼠标就算离开。现在他桌上插着个自动晃鼠标的小盒子。全公司最勤奋的那个绿点，是一块在原地抽搐的塑料。"}, "b": {"item_id": "cfb198c08db3", "text": "我们公司那个力推全员用Teams的VP，我真想当面问他图啥。图啥？图一份\"数字化转型\"的PPT。结果呢？全公司每天三小时盯着那三个跳动的小圆点，等对面打完一个\"好\"字。他自己更惨，上周开会共享屏幕，忘了点\"允许\"，全场看他浏览器收藏夹看了十分钟。现在他每晚十一点在\"全体员工\"频道里@所有人：\"大家收到了吗？\"收到了老板，大家的状态早改成\"请勿打扰\"了，就是不想回。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0816", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "graphene", "target_name": "石墨烯", "facts": ["Isolated in 2004 by Andre Geim and Konstantin Novoselov at Manchester by peeling graphite with Scotch tape; Nobel Prize in Physics, 2010.", "The EU's Graphene Flagship, launched in 2013, committed €1 billion over ten years.", "A 2018 study in Advanced Materials tested 'graphene' from 60 producers and found none with more than 50% graphene content; most was graphite flakes.", "Sold in tennis rackets, running shoes, and headphones as a performance ingredient.", "Geim also won an Ig Nobel Prize in 2000 for levitating a frog with magnets."], "a": {"item_id": "5c660764dbab", "text": "我有个表舅，逢人就说石墨烯要改变世界。他说这玩意儿就一个原子厚，比钢硬两百倍，导电导热全是第一。我问他：那你拿它干嘛了？他说：卖秋裤。石墨烯发热秋裤，一条六百八。\n\n表舅研究过，诺贝尔奖得主是拿透明胶带从铅笔芯上粘出来的。他一听，门槛不高啊，家里胶带有的是。二〇一五年，他把养老钱全押进了石墨烯概念股，从此真的改变了世界，先改变的是他自己的。\n\n现在他还在说。石墨烯什么都能干，就是出不了实验室；表舅什么都敢说，就是出不了我们家群。\n\n过年我问他，世界改变了吗？他把秋裤往上一拽：“你摸，热不热！”\n\n热。里头是电热丝。"}, "b": {"item_id": "8757c13cc756", "text": "我有个朋友，逢人就说石墨烯要改变世界。你问他石墨烯是什么，他说是碳原子排成六边形的单层，薄到只有一个原子厚。行，那你知道铅笔芯是什么吗？石墨。石墨就是一层层石墨烯叠起来的。你小学就天天在用它写字，世界怎么没变？他卖保暖内衣，吊牌上印着\"石墨烯含量\"，我让他拿检测报告，他说报告在另一个仓库。去年厂子抵给了银行。现在他改卖石墨烯足浴盆，广告写\"一泡，改变人生\"。我去他店里看了，人确实变了，老婆跑了，他还在那儿泡脚。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0817", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "google-sheets", "target_name": "Google Sheets", "facts": ["Launched in June 2006 as Google Spreadsheets, built on XL2Web, a product Google acquired from 2Web Technologies.", "A spreadsheet is capped at 10 million cells (raised from 5 million in 2022).", "Functions such as IMPORTHTML, IMPORTXML, and GOOGLEFINANCE pull live web data straight into cells.", "Free with a Google account; files live in Google Drive, and collaborators edit the same sheet simultaneously with coloured cursors.", "In a browser, offline editing works only in Chrome or Edge, with the Google Docs Offline extension installed and offline access switched on while still connected; the Android and iOS apps work offline natively."], "a": {"item_id": "62f3a7b081a2", "text": "干这事的，通常是刚学会\"共享\"按钮的 team lead。他图什么？图一眼看穿所有人，图能少开会。于是他建了个表：三十个标签页，一个叫\"总表\"，一个叫\"总表（最终版）\"，还有一个叫\"别用这个\"。\n\n每人一行，进度列设下拉框，红黄绿条件格式，颜色比过年还热闹。第一周很美。第二周，实习生在第三列手敲了个\"差不多了\"，下拉框直接失效，公式全报错，满屏井字号。第三周，他在批注里@全组，回复只有一句：\"我改的是哪一版？\"\n\n最后他关掉表格，开了个会。会上他说：\"我们需要一个更好的协作工具。\""}, "b": {"item_id": "c795fe8e9fcf", "text": "每个拿Google Sheets管团队的人，都管这叫“极简”。其实就是舍不得那几十刀的Jira。\n\n一开始你说“一张表够了”。三个月后，十四个标签页，一个叫“总表”，一个叫“总表（真的）”。\n\n你开会最常说的一句：“谁开的筛选？”你筛一下，全公司跟着筛一下。右上角永远飘着三个头像，其中一个叫“匿名水豚”。你不知道它是谁，但它刚删了D列。\n\n下场呢？你成了公司唯一看得懂那条“VLOOKUP套IMPORTRANGE”的人。你不是经理了，你是这张表的人质。你休假那天，群里只有一句话：“老大，它又#REF!了。”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0818", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "brasilia", "target_name": "巴西利亚", "facts": ["Inaugurated on 21 April 1960 after about 41 months of construction, moving the capital inland from Rio de Janeiro.", "Lúcio Costa's Plano Piloto is laid out like an airplane (or a bird, or a cross), with Oscar Niemeyer's government buildings along the fuselage.", "Listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1987, less than 30 years after it was built.", "Planned for 500,000 residents; the Federal District now has about 3 million, most in satellite towns outside the plan.", "Built for the car: wide avenues, few pedestrian crossings, and distances that make walking impractical."], "a": {"item_id": "7ded9e81696c", "text": "在巴西利亚走路的人，就两种：建筑系学生，和看地图觉得\"才两公里\"的游客。\n\n国会大厦就在前面，清清楚楚。你走二十分钟，它还在前面，一点没大。建筑师管这叫\"纪念性尺度\"，我管这叫海市蜃楼。\n\n图什么？图\"亲身体验世界遗产\"。结果这城是按飞机设计的，汽车安排得明明白白，行人？没设计。你只好在草地上踩出一条红泥路，当地人管这叫\"欲望之路\"，听着挺浪漫，其实就是一群跟你一样的人集体迷路的证据。\n\n下场都一样：裤腿红到膝盖，嘴唇在一千米海拔上裂开，最后卡在一座立交桥下，对面看得见，就是过不去。\n\n你叫了辆车，司机到了问：\"你怎么在这儿？\"\n\n你说：\"想感受一下城市。\"\n\n他说：\"这不是城市，这是飞机。你在机翼上散步。\""}, "b": {"item_id": "879d5b880e2f", "text": "在巴西利亚靠两条腿出门的，就三种人：刚考上公务员、工资还没到账的；捧着建筑系课本来朝圣尼迈耶的；还有一种，是前两种走到一半的样子。\n\n他们图什么？图“城市漫步”。可这城是照着飞机画的，你走路，等于在机翼上找人行道。地图上南翼308区和309区挨着，中间隔着一条六车道。想过去？去找那种叫“小剪刀”的立交，绕一圈下来，你从南翼走出了北翼的心情。\n\n旱季湿度百分之十，太阳毒得像在三权广场受审。认出他们很简单：草坪上那条被踩秃的土路，那不是小道，那是他们集体签的字。\n\n下场？第二个月，贷款买了辆二手菲亚特。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0819", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "oat-milk", "target_name": "燕麦奶", "facts": ["Oatly started in Sweden in the 1990s, built on Lund University research by Rickard Öste on lactose intolerance.", "Oatly's slogan 'It's like milk but made for humans' was banned in Sweden in 2015 after the dairy lobby LRF Mjölk sued; Oatly then published the lawsuit as advertising.", "Oatly listed on Nasdaq in May 2021 at a valuation near $10 billion; the shares later lost more than 90% of their value.", "Commercial oat milk is made by breaking oat starch into maltose with amylase enzymes, and many brands, Oatly among them, add rapeseed oil; the result has more sugar and a higher glycaemic index than almond or soy milk."], "a": {"item_id": "6af69cc6ef40", "text": "我发现点燕麦拿铁的人有个共同点：开口前先叹气。“不好意思，换燕麦奶，我乳糖不耐。”你不耐？上周火锅店你一个人干了三个冰淇淋球。\n\n他们图什么？图健康。可燕麦奶为了打出奶泡，要往里加植物油；为了喝着甜，得把燕麦酶解成麦芽糖。你多掏五块钱，买了一杯稀释燕麦粥，外加一勺油。\n\n图环保？说燕麦比奶牛省水。你省下那点水，转头就用纸巾擦了八遍杯口。\n\n下场呢？体检血糖偏高。医生问你早饭吃什么，你说只喝咖啡。医生摇头：“不对，你这是每天早上喝两碗粥，还嫌粥稀，花钱让人给你打了个泡。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "794cf9f48948", "text": "有一种人，点拿铁非要加一句“换燕麦奶”。你问为什么，他说“对身体好”。好在哪？他说不上来，但语气笃定，像刚发现了新大陆。\n\n这杯燕麦奶拿铁，奶泡两分钟就塌成一层白沫浮在上面。喝到最后，杯底是一层灰褐色的东西，像把麦片泡在水里忘了捞。\n\n每杯多收五块五，一个月下来一百六十多。他管这叫“投资健康”。\n\n体检报告出来，医生看着他说：“您血糖有点高。”他愣住：“我没加糖啊。”医生说：“燕麦奶本身就带甜，您那杯还加了两泵糖浆。”\n\n他沉默了一会儿，说：“我就是为了不喝糖，才换的燕麦奶。”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0820", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "keurig", "target_name": "Keurig 胶囊咖啡机", "facts": ["John Sylvan invented the K-Cup in the early 1990s and sold his stake for about $50,000 in 1997; in 2015 he told The Atlantic, 'I feel bad sometimes that I ever did it.'", "Keurig 2.0 (2014) read an ink mark on the lid and rejected unlicensed pods; sales fell and workarounds spread online.", "By 2014 enough K-Cups were sold each year to circle the Earth more than ten times; the pods were mostly unrecyclable #7 plastic until a switch to polypropylene by 2020.", "Keurig Green Mountain merged with Dr Pepper Snapple in 2018 to form Keurig Dr Pepper."], "a": {"item_id": "771391305fbe", "text": "用Keurig的人，我一眼就认得出来。他们会跟你说：“我不是懒，我是讲效率。”效率？大哥，你站在机器前面等它咕噜咕噜预热一分钟，然后对着三个按钮，6盎司、8盎司、10盎司，陷入人生最重大的抉择。最后你按了10盎司，因为“划算”。可胶囊里就那么一小撮粉！你花四块钱买了一杯咖啡味的热水，还觉得自己薅到了羊毛。\n\n办公室那盒混装胶囊更惨。一个月后好喝的全被抢光，只剩榛子味和低因。你盯着那盒剩货，就像盯着你的职业生涯：选择很多，没人想要。\n\n对了，发明K杯那哥们后来公开说他后悔了。亲爹都不认这孩子，你天天喂它。"}, "b": {"item_id": "6e62a676c319", "text": "买 Keurig 的人，特点是：嫌磨豆子麻烦，但不嫌贵。一颗胶囊里就十克咖啡粉，折下来一斤两百多块，他们管这叫“省事”。买就买了，还非买那种四十八颗混合装，榛果、焦糖、南瓜派。大清早喝一口南瓜派味的咖啡，你说你是在提神，还是在给自己过万圣节？喝完还得把那个塑料小杯子抠出来扔掉，一年下来垃圾桶里全是小杯子，像开了家微型保龄球馆。最离谱的是，这玩意儿的发明人自己都公开说后悔发明了它。你想想，你每天早上，一颗一颗地给一个连发明人都后悔的东西交钱，还觉得自己活得挺高效。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0821", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "beanie-babies", "target_name": "Beanie Babies 豆豆娃", "facts": ["Launched by Ty Inc. in 1993 and sold only through small gift shops, with designs 'retired' to manufacture scarcity.", "At the height of the craze Beanie Babies accounted for about 10% of eBay's sales, and eBay's 1998 IPO filing said the site at times had over 30,000 Beanie Baby auctions running simultaneously in the quarter ending June 1998.", "In 1999 a divorcing couple divided their Beanie Baby collection one by one on a courtroom floor, in a photograph that ran worldwide.", "In 1999 Ty announced that Beanie Babies would be retired at the end of the year, then reversed itself after asking the public to vote and seeing an overwhelming vote to keep them.", "Most 'rare' Beanies that price guides valued in the thousands now sell for a few dollars."], "a": {"item_id": "e9aa38d24ee4", "text": "九十年代有一批人，觉得自己找到了下一个华尔街：一只塞满塑料粒的小熊。他们不看财报，看那颗红色心形吊牌。吊牌折个角，在他们眼里跟股市崩盘一样。孩子想抱一下？不行！先给吊牌套上塑料保护壳。宝贝，你的大学学费就在这只紫色戴安娜王妃熊身上，别流口水。Ty公司一宣布某款“退休”，他们连夜开车扫三家礼品店，比自己亲爹退休还上心。结果呢？拉斯维加斯真有一对夫妻离婚，法官让他们蹲在法庭地板上，一只一只轮流挑豆豆娃。这就是你的资产配置：二十年后，唯一升值的是你儿子的心理咨询费。"}, "b": {"item_id": "4ba725269df8", "text": "我有个朋友，九十年代末拿全部积蓄囤豆豆娃，一只只买回来，吊牌小心翼翼套进塑料袋，供得跟菩萨似的。他说这玩意儿一宣布\"退役\"就涨价，所以他就等着它退役。结果厂家一年退役好几批，退役的比在售的还多，一只熊能退役三回。他那一屋子熊，现在二手平台十块钱包邮都没人要。他还在等那只戴安娜王妃纪念款的\"公主熊\"，说它一定能翻身。我说王妃的熊确实翻身了，你的积蓄没了。他说，吊牌还在，吊牌最值钱。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0822", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "google-glass", "target_name": "谷歌眼镜（Google Glass）", "facts": ["Unveiled at Google I/O 2012 with skydivers livestreaming their jump through it onto the Moscone Center roof.", "The Explorer Edition sold to invited users in 2013 for $1,500.", "Wearers were nicknamed 'Glassholes'; bars, cinemas, and casinos banned the device over its camera.", "The consumer programme ended in January 2015; an Enterprise Edition continued until Google stopped selling it in 2023.", "Its display was a 640-by-360 prism in the corner of one eye."], "a": {"item_id": "3f70392c889f", "text": "戴谷歌眼镜进酒吧的人，我真想采访一下：你图什么？花一千五百美元，抢到一个\"探索者\"资格。注意，是资格，你得先被允许掏钱。然后戴着它进酒吧，就等人问一句：\"哥们，这是啥？\"\n\n结果没人问。大家只看你右眼上方那块小玻璃，心想：他是不是在录我？\n\n你想搭讪，张嘴就是\"OK Glass，拍照\"，姑娘以为你在对她说\"OK\"，转身走了。你想看个时间，还得仰头斜眼，盯着右上角，活像在找天花板上的WiFi。\n\n最后酒保说：\"把那个摘了。\"你说：\"这是未来。\"酒保说：\"未来请在门口等。\"\n\n你被请出去，站在马路边，对着空气喊\"OK Glass，叫车\"。它回你一句：没听清。\n\n一千五百美元，买了个让全酒吧都把你当偷拍狂的右眼显示器。"}, "b": {"item_id": "5674fb13d6e0", "text": "戴谷歌眼镜进酒吧的哥们儿，你图什么？花一千五百美元，给谷歌当测试员，钱还是你自己掏的。坐吧台边，右眼一直往右上角瞟，酒保问喝什么，他张嘴就是“OK Glass”。兄弟，人家不叫Glass。最绝的是这玩意儿眨眼就能拍照。以前在酒吧冲姑娘眨眼，顶多被泼一杯酒；现在姑娘得先琢磨：你是在撩我，还是在拍我？想完了，泼两杯。美国人还专门给这帮人造了个词：Glasshole。一个产品能把自己的用户骂成新单词，我是头回见。最后电池没撑过一杯长岛冰茶，他摘下眼镜一看：原来自己就是个一个人来喝闷酒的普通男人。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0823", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "keurig", "target_name": "Keurig 胶囊咖啡机", "facts": ["John Sylvan invented the K-Cup in the early 1990s and sold his stake for about $50,000 in 1997; in 2015 he told The Atlantic, 'I feel bad sometimes that I ever did it.'", "Keurig 2.0 (2014) read an ink mark on the lid and rejected unlicensed pods; sales fell and workarounds spread online.", "By 2014 enough K-Cups were sold each year to circle the Earth more than ten times; the pods were mostly unrecyclable #7 plastic until a switch to polypropylene by 2020.", "Keurig Green Mountain merged with Dr Pepper Snapple in 2018 to form Keurig Dr Pepper."], "a": {"item_id": "45e5b40c4a0a", "text": "我同事天天用Keurig泡咖啡，理由是三十秒搞定。行，省事，不用磨豆，不用洗壶，按一下嗡地一声，像要起飞。结果出来一杯八盎司的热水，颜色像他的周报。每天撕一个锡箔盖、扔一个塑料杯，一个月下来，垃圾桶里的小杯子能堆成金字塔。上个月机器亮起\"请除垢\"，他查说明书，得买除垢剂，还得拿醋灌一遍。算下来，他的胶囊钱够买一台手冲壶和两袋豆子。最后他还是下楼买了杯星巴克，店员问要什么，他说：\"要一杯，有味儿的。\""}, "b": {"item_id": "771391305fbe", "text": "用Keurig的人，我一眼就认得出来。他们会跟你说：“我不是懒，我是讲效率。”效率？大哥，你站在机器前面等它咕噜咕噜预热一分钟，然后对着三个按钮，6盎司、8盎司、10盎司，陷入人生最重大的抉择。最后你按了10盎司，因为“划算”。可胶囊里就那么一小撮粉！你花四块钱买了一杯咖啡味的热水，还觉得自己薅到了羊毛。\n\n办公室那盒混装胶囊更惨。一个月后好喝的全被抢光，只剩榛子味和低因。你盯着那盒剩货，就像盯着你的职业生涯：选择很多，没人想要。\n\n对了，发明K杯那哥们后来公开说他后悔了。亲爹都不认这孩子，你天天喂它。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0824", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "juicero", "target_name": "Juicero 榨汁机", "facts": ["A Wi-Fi-connected cold-press juicer launched in 2016 at $699, later cut to $399, that squeezed proprietary bags of chopped produce sold by subscription.", "Raised about $120 million from investors including Google Ventures and Kleiner Perkins.", "In April 2017, Bloomberg reporters squeezed the packs by hand and got 7.5 ounces of juice in 90 seconds, faster than the machine's 8 ounces in about two minutes.", "Each pack carried a QR code that the press scanned and checked online before squeezing; it refused expired packs and would not run at all unless connected to Wi-Fi.", "Shut down in September 2017 and offered refunds on the machine."], "a": {"item_id": "90365272a695", "text": "花400美元买Juicero的都是什么人？就是那种觉得“我自己的手不够智能”的人。你买的根本不是榨汁机，是一台连WiFi的拳头。它这辈子唯一的工作，就是捏一包早就榨好的菜泥。彭博社记者徒手去捏，比它还快。想想那画面：硅谷精英每天早上站在厨房，等机器扫完二维码、联网确认这包菜没过期，它才肯开工。为了“健康生活”，每包再掏五六块，喝的其实是自己的智商汁。后来公司倒闭，机器连不上服务器，成了一块四百刀的案板。唯一还能用的，是你那双手。恭喜，绕了一大圈，你终于恢复了出厂设置。"}, "b": {"item_id": "42eeb6dfde70", "text": "Juicero 要 400 美元，干的事是把一包切好的果蔬塞进去压成汁。问题是，那包东西你用手也能捏出来。做这台机器的是硅谷一帮创始人，想颠覆榨汁行业，融了一个多亿美元，图的是你每个月订阅他们的果蔬包，一包好几美元，还得联网认证。后来有记者拿它的包装袋直接用手挤，汁照样出来。投资人一看，哦，原来我们投的不是榨汁机，是一台给包装袋收月费的收费站。公司两年后就关门了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0825", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "stinky-tofu", "target_name": "臭豆腐", "facts": ["Tofu soaked for hours to a day or two in a brine that has itself fermented for months from vegetables such as bamboo shoots and mustard greens, often with dried shrimp or meat; the smell is the brine's, not the bean's.", "Changsha's version is black, deep-fried and doused in chili; Taiwan's night-market version is golden and served with pickled cabbage; Shaoxing's, brined with amaranth stalks, is fried pale yellow or steamed with other 'stinky' pickles.", "Legend credits Wang Zhihe, a Qing-era exam candidate who left his unsold tofu too long in 1669; a Beijing fermented-bean-curd brand still carries his name.", "Street vendors are its main outlet, and the smell carries for a block."], "a": {"item_id": "0db2c726fd64", "text": "街边吃臭豆腐的人，我观察过，全是传教士。你路过，他嘴里正烫得直哈气，还要腾出一只手拦你：“闻着臭，吃着香！”大哥，这句话我从小听到大，你说得跟刚悟出来的一样。\n\n他图什么？图的就是那个反差。全世界都捂鼻子，就他看透了本质。一块发酵的豆腐，硬是吃出了顿悟的表情。\n\n然后呢？然后他上地铁了。五站路，整节车厢都在低头检查自己的鞋。他还挺得意：凡人，不懂。\n\n最惨的是那根牙签。一碗六块，就配一根，戳到最后一块，辣汤顺着手腕流进袖口。第二天开会他一抬手，领导皱眉：“小王，你家下水道是不是堵了？”"}, "b": {"item_id": "30b6f794ee22", "text": "街边吃臭豆腐的人，我观察过，都有点自我感动。排二十分钟队，就为了吃一句广告语：\"闻着臭，吃着香。\"这话翻译过来就是\"我人品不行，但我内在美\"，他们居然信了。\n\n你再看那个姿势：一手端纸碗，一手捏牙签，站在马路牙子上，腰弯九十度，对着一块豆腐鞠躬，生怕辣汤滴到白鞋上。\n\n最惨是吃完以后。你以为臭味留在嘴里？不，它搬进你外套里了。坐地铁，旁边的人默默挪开一个身位，你还觉得是自己今天气场强。\n\n回到家，女朋友一开门：\"你是不是路过了一场火灾？\"你说：\"没有，我吃了点好的。\"她说：\"那你把好的留在门口。\"\n\n人家豆腐是臭卤泡了一个月才臭的，你五分钟就追平了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0826", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "almond-milk", "target_name": "杏仁奶", "facts": ["California grows about 80% of the world's almonds.", "A single almond takes about 1.1 US gallons (roughly 4 litres) of water to grow, a figure Mother Jones published in 2014 that spread during California's drought.", "A 2015 US class action alleged Almond Breeze contained only about 2% almonds; it partly survived a motion to dismiss, was stayed in 2017 pending a separate Blue Diamond labelling settlement, and the plaintiffs then dropped it.", "Pollinating California's almond orchards each February requires trucking in most of the commercial honeybee colonies in the United States.", "Medieval European cooks used almond milk as a Lenten substitute for dairy, centuries before the current fashion."], "a": {"item_id": "096100e8c869", "text": "我朋友为了环保戒了牛奶，改喝杏仁奶。他说牛放屁排甲烷，地球受不了。行，有道理。然后我问他杏仁哪来的，他说加州。加州啊，那地方干得裂开，种一颗杏仁要喝掉好几升水，他一杯杏仁奶下去，等于替加州的杏树喝了半缸。更绝的是，加州的杏树开花得靠蜜蜂，蜂箱是用大卡车拉过去的，蜜蜂一路颠过去，授完粉累死一片。杏仁奶泡咖啡，三分钟就分层，像他的环保决心，看着坚定，一放就散。他现在每天喝两杯，花的钱是牛奶的三倍，说这是为地球付的。我说你付的是加州的水费，地球一分没收到。"}, "b": {"item_id": "8cd05311eac8", "text": "我朋友为了环保戒了牛奶，改喝杏仁奶，说牛奶太费水。我说你知道一颗杏仁要喝四升水吗？加州的杏仁树比加州人还能喝。而且杏仁奶里杏仁只占百分之二，你花三十八块，买了一盒百分之九十八的水，外加一点杏仁味的遗憾。\n\n更绝的是，杏仁树要授粉，每年得用卡车把全美国的蜜蜂拉去加州打工，累死一大片。他不想伤害奶牛，结果当上了蜜蜂的包工头。\n\n最后咖啡一倒进去，絮状分层，像一场小型水土流失。他还端着杯子庄严地说：“我在拯救地球。”\n\n哥们，地球没让你救，它就想让你把这杯喝完，别发朋友圈。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0827", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "slack", "target_name": "Slack", "facts": ["Spun out in 2013 from the internal chat tool of Tiny Speck's failed game Glitch; founder Stewart Butterfield had earlier spun Flickr out of another failed game.", "The name is a backronym: 'Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge'.", "Salesforce bought it for $27.7 billion in 2021.", "Filed an EU antitrust complaint against Microsoft in 2020 over the bundling of Teams with Office."], "a": {"item_id": "04d9a9a0c475", "text": "有一种老板，创业第一天就宣布：我们不开会，全员用 Slack，扁平、透明、异步。\n\n结果建了四十七个频道。#general 没人说话，#random 倒是热火朝天。真正的工作都在\"线程\"里，那地方跟地下室一样，进去就没人再见过。\n\n老板最爱 @channel。一发，三十个人手机同时一震，点开一看：周五记得填报销。然后三十个人回三十个 👍。他盯着那一排 👍，觉得这公司管得真好。\n\n他还盯着名字旁边那个绿点。员工呢，学会了每五分钟晃一下鼠标。公司就这样实现了\"全员在线，无人干活\"。\n\n说好不开会，最后他点了那个小耳机图标，叫 huddle。对，就是开会，只是名字可爱了点。\n\n最惨的是免费版，三个月前的消息自动消失。老板问：去年那个决定谁拍的板？\n\nSlack 说：什么决定？"}, "b": {"item_id": "9ef25649a571", "text": "有一种老板，管公司全靠Slack。他图什么？图那种指挥作战的感觉。半夜两点在#general里@channel，三百个人的手机同时“咚”一声，他躺床上听着，跟听礼炮似的。\n\n他不开会，他回“👀”。你熬一礼拜的方案，换来一个眼睛表情，啥意思没人知道，你自己悟。\n\n他考勤看绿点。谁头像一灰，私信立马弹出一个“？”。于是全员网购了鼠标晃动器，公司最勤奋的员工，是十九块九包邮的小马达。\n\n下场呢？所有决策都埋在某条thread的第四十七楼，谁也翻不着。免费版只存九十天，这家公司的战略每三个月自动清空一次。他还挺得意：“看，我们多敏捷。”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0828", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "php", "target_name": "PHP", "facts": ["Created by Rasmus Lerdorf in 1994 as 'Personal Home Page Tools', a set of C programs for tracking visitors to his own résumé page; the name now officially stands for the recursive 'PHP: Hypertext Preprocessor'.", "Lerdorf, in his 2007 MySQL Conference talk 'PHP on Hormones': 'I'm not a real programmer. I throw together things until it works then I move on.'", "Runs WordPress, which W3Techs counted on 39.4% of all websites (58.1% of sites with a known CMS) on 9 November 2026.", "Core function names are inconsistent (strpos, str_replace, htmlspecialchars, nl2br) partly because, as Lerdorf confirmed, early function names were chosen to balance a hash table keyed on string length.", "Until PHP 8 (2020), the comparison 0 == 'foo' was true because the string was silently converted to a number."], "a": {"item_id": "bcc6af302235", "text": "写PHP的人，我一直很佩服。这门语言每个变量前面都要加个美元符号：$name、$age、$salary。写了十年，满屏都是$，工资卡里一个都没见着。\n\n他们当初为什么入行？因为好上手。培训班三个月，出来就接单，接的全是老板表弟的商城、装修公司的官网。入行时都背过那句\"PHP是世界上最好的语言\"，十年过去，这话喊得最响的，永远是正在改前任祖传代码的人。\n\n下场更惨。每年都有人宣布PHP死了，他们不反驳，因为手上那个WordPress插件，作者三年前就跑路了。面试官问：\"你还在写PHP啊？\"他们只能说：\"我们现在升到8了，支持类型了。\"\n\n这就像有人说：我家一直漏雨，但是现在，我们有伞了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "e21dca174c4d", "text": "我有个朋友，靠写PHP吃饭。每次喝酒他都举杯：“PHP是世界上最好的语言！”全桌没人接话，因为全桌都是写PHP的。大家心里清楚，这是我们这行唯一能赢的一句台词。\n\n他图什么？图快。一个下午，织梦模板一套，宝塔面板一点，县城瓷砖城就有官网了。收三千，老板还嫌贵：“我侄子也会。”\n\n他说他天天跟钱打交道。确实，每个变量前面都挂着个美元符号。干了十年，那是他摸过最多的美元。\n\n现在他维护一套2009年的代码，mysql_query还在跑，他不敢动。一动，瓷砖城倒闭，他也失业。说白了，他跟那段代码是互相续命。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0829", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "las-vegas", "target_name": "拉斯维加斯", "facts": ["Nevada legalized casino gambling in 1931, the year construction of the Hoover Dam began nearby.", "Most of the Strip lies not in the city of Las Vegas but in the unincorporated town of Paradise.", "The Sphere, opened in 2023 at a cost of about $2.3 billion, is the largest spherical building in the world.", "'What happens here, stays here' was launched by the local convention and visitors authority in 2003.", "Lake Mead, its water source, fell in July 2022 to about 1,040 feet, its lowest since the reservoir first filled in 1937, and dropped below that mark again in August 2026."], "a": {"item_id": "c54ee644ce17", "text": "去拉斯维加斯发财的人，最让我服气的是自信。个个都说“我研究过”。研究了啥？油管三分钟《21点必胜法》。人家赌场是几十亿砸出来的，你是三分钟视频，这叫博士打小学生。\n\n他们图什么？不图钱，图一句“我在维加斯赢过”。第一晚赢两百，感觉自己是邦德，服务员送免费酒，你以为是请客，那是预付款。喝到第三杯，你把全部押在红色，理由是“看着吉利”。\n\n下场很统一：赌场没窗户没钟，你以为晚上十点，其实周三了。最后站在ATM前，手续费十八块你都不心疼，已经输麻了。到机场，还有老虎机，你居然想“最后试一把”。\n\n回家有人问，你说：“我没输，我是去玩的。”对，你花四千块，买了一句“我没输”。"}, "b": {"item_id": "9a99488eb663", "text": "去拉斯维加斯发财的人，我见过三种。第一种攒了半年假，信用卡一刷就来了，说今晚要翻身。第二种失业在家，把老婆的金项链拿去当铺，换了三千块，说这是最后的本钱。第三种最有意思，他不怎么玩，就盯着赌场大厅发呆。我问他看什么，他说这里没窗户、没钟，不知道外面是白天还是黑夜，所以觉得随时还能再来一把。结果呢？第一种回家路上只剩一张十九块九的自助餐券。第二种在一百一十度的沙漠太阳底下，被当铺老板问要不要把婚戒也估个价。第三种，赌场免费送的酒他喝到第五杯，才发现自己在这儿住了三天。拉斯维加斯真大方，免费的酒管够，唯独不给你看表。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0830", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "google-glass", "target_name": "谷歌眼镜（Google Glass）", "facts": ["Unveiled at Google I/O 2012 with skydivers livestreaming their jump through it onto the Moscone Center roof.", "The Explorer Edition sold to invited users in 2013 for $1,500.", "Wearers were nicknamed 'Glassholes'; bars, cinemas, and casinos banned the device over its camera.", "The consumer programme ended in January 2015; an Enterprise Edition continued until Google stopped selling it in 2023.", "Its display was a 640-by-360 prism in the corner of one eye."], "a": {"item_id": "cea2da3417f8", "text": "你见过戴谷歌眼镜进酒吧的人吗？花一千五百美金，买了个“探索者”头衔，结果探索的是保安的耐心。他坐你对面，右眼上那块小棱镜一闪一闪，你根本分不清他是在看你，还是在拍你。你问他干嘛呢，他说：“查一下这杯酒的评分。”兄弟，你喝的是扎啤，评分就一个字：凉。最惨的是搭讪，他冲着美女深情地喊一声“OK Glass”，美女还以为在叫她，回头一看，他在让眼镜拍照。然后他就被请出去了。英语里专门给这种人造了个词，叫Glasshole。一千五百美金，买了个全世界都会念的外号。"}, "b": {"item_id": "3f70392c889f", "text": "戴谷歌眼镜进酒吧的人，我真想采访一下：你图什么？花一千五百美元，抢到一个\"探索者\"资格。注意，是资格，你得先被允许掏钱。然后戴着它进酒吧，就等人问一句：\"哥们，这是啥？\"\n\n结果没人问。大家只看你右眼上方那块小玻璃，心想：他是不是在录我？\n\n你想搭讪，张嘴就是\"OK Glass，拍照\"，姑娘以为你在对她说\"OK\"，转身走了。你想看个时间，还得仰头斜眼，盯着右上角，活像在找天花板上的WiFi。\n\n最后酒保说：\"把那个摘了。\"你说：\"这是未来。\"酒保说：\"未来请在门口等。\"\n\n你被请出去，站在马路边，对着空气喊\"OK Glass，叫车\"。它回你一句：没听清。\n\n一千五百美元，买了个让全酒吧都把你当偷拍狂的右眼显示器。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0831", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "ugg", "target_name": "UGG 雪地靴", "facts": ["Sheepskin boots Australian surfers wore to warm up after sessions; Brian Smith started selling them in California in 1978.", "Deckers Outdoor bought the brand in 1995 and holds 'UGG' as a trademark in the US and elsewhere; in Australia, 'ugg' is a generic word any bootmaker may use.", "In 2019, a US jury found an Australian maker liable for selling boots labelled 'ugg' into the United States.", "Oprah Winfrey featured them in her 'Favorite Things' lists repeatedly in the 2000s, which drove the first boom.", "UGG is Deckers' largest brand, with over $2 billion in annual sales."], "a": {"item_id": "2b7b80053c20", "text": "我有个朋友，一年四季穿UGG。七月三十八度，她踩着两只羊上街。我问不热吗？她说：“羊毛，透气。”姐，羊自己热得都去剪毛了，你又给它穿回去？\n\n她图什么？图一脚蹬进去不用系鞋带，像回到妈妈怀里。代价呢？光脚闷一夏天，她一脱鞋，全办公室以为楼下烤串店开业了，还是羊肉串。\n\n穿到第三年，鞋跟往外撇，人往里倒，走路像两只企鹅抬着她。下场雨，麂皮全是水印，鞋面像世界地图。\n\n最讽刺的是，这叫雪地靴。她穿它蹚过三个夏天的水坑，唯独没见过雪：一到冬天，她嫌冷，换棉拖了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "52524a4d7862", "text": "有一类人，七月三十八度，脚上还蹬着UGG。你问她为啥，她说：“舒服，还不用穿袜子。”姐妹，那是羊毛内里替你把袜子穿了，还是加厚款。\n\n这鞋是澳洲人冲完浪上岸暖脚的。你在北京地铁里冲什么浪？冲的是汗。\n\n脚在里面，四周是羊毛，底下是羊皮，这不叫穿鞋，这叫铜锅：脚是肉，汗是汤。晚上去饭店要脱鞋，她一脱，全桌人当场不点羊肉了，已经闻饱了。\n\n最惨是下雨。UGG一沾水就硬，还掉色，所以夏天暴雨，她们在路口跳格子，跟拆雷一样。你以为她怕湿脚？她是在保护那一千多块的信仰。\n\n等到秋天，她还特骄傲：“你看，我终于穿对季节了。”对，那一个月，你和UGG的脚臭终于不是孤军奋战了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0832", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "microsoft-teams", "target_name": "Microsoft Teams", "facts": ["Launched in 2017, bundled at no extra charge into Office 365, and replaced Skype for Business.", "After Slack's antitrust complaint, Microsoft unbundled Teams from Office in the EU in 2023 and worldwide in 2024; the European Commission still sent a statement of objections in 2024.", "Microsoft reported 320 million monthly active users in 2023.", "Microsoft shut down consumer Skype in May 2025 and sent its users to Teams."], "a": {"item_id": "fd671172ecb3", "text": "我们公司推Teams的是IT部老王。为啥选它？买Office 365送的，老王这辈子没拒绝过“送的”。培训会上他讲了四十分钟：“聊天”和“团队”不一样，“频道”里的“帖子”也不算聊天，文件其实存在SharePoint里。SharePoint是啥？别问。\n\n现在全公司最忙的就是老王，天天有人问他：文件呢？我电脑上怎么有两个Teams？\n\n最惨的是，老板发现了头像旁边那个小绿点，五分钟不动鼠标就变黄。现在老王桌上放着个自动晃鼠标的小玩意儿。他亲手装的监控，第一个抓的是他自己。"}, "b": {"item_id": "8605988b84fd", "text": "他们是谁？就是那个拍板\"全公司必须用Teams\"的行政总监。图什么？Office套餐自带，不花一分钱，还能在年终汇报上写\"数字化协同全面落地\"。结果呢？他自己的状态每天自动变成\"离开\"，因为他开会总忘了点\"举手\"；他撤回的消息显示\"此消息已被删除\"，大家早就截图了。后来Teams又崩了一次，IT被骂惨，他被调去当\"Teams使用培训官\"，每天负责回答\"为什么我进不了会议室\"。现在他的签名是：有事@我，别@所有人。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0833", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "stinky-tofu", "target_name": "臭豆腐", "facts": ["Tofu soaked for hours to a day or two in a brine that has itself fermented for months from vegetables such as bamboo shoots and mustard greens, often with dried shrimp or meat; the smell is the brine's, not the bean's.", "Changsha's version is black, deep-fried and doused in chili; Taiwan's night-market version is golden and served with pickled cabbage; Shaoxing's, brined with amaranth stalks, is fried pale yellow or steamed with other 'stinky' pickles.", "Legend credits Wang Zhihe, a Qing-era exam candidate who left his unsold tofu too long in 1669; a Beijing fermented-bean-curd brand still carries his name.", "Street vendors are its main outlet, and the smell carries for a block."], "a": {"item_id": "30b6f794ee22", "text": "街边吃臭豆腐的人，我观察过，都有点自我感动。排二十分钟队，就为了吃一句广告语：\"闻着臭，吃着香。\"这话翻译过来就是\"我人品不行，但我内在美\"，他们居然信了。\n\n你再看那个姿势：一手端纸碗，一手捏牙签，站在马路牙子上，腰弯九十度，对着一块豆腐鞠躬，生怕辣汤滴到白鞋上。\n\n最惨是吃完以后。你以为臭味留在嘴里？不，它搬进你外套里了。坐地铁，旁边的人默默挪开一个身位，你还觉得是自己今天气场强。\n\n回到家，女朋友一开门：\"你是不是路过了一场火灾？\"你说：\"没有，我吃了点好的。\"她说：\"那你把好的留在门口。\"\n\n人家豆腐是臭卤泡了一个月才臭的，你五分钟就追平了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "5723ee516250", "text": "街边吃臭豆腐的人，我见过三种。第一种是下班的社畜，西装还没脱，站在摊子前左顾右盼，像在做贼。他图什么？图那口“香”。结果油锅一开，汤汁溅到白衬衫上，那味道从领口直接往上飘。地铁上三米之内全体让座，不是尊重他，是怕被熏晕。第二种是情侣。女生说“就尝一口”，男生陪着吃，两个人嘴里都是霉豆腐和辣酱的味儿，接吻的念头当场熄火。第三种最惨，是我室友。他一边吃一边说“这味儿才地道”，回宿舍后，全层楼的人都知道他吃了什么。他洗了三遍澡，衣服挂阳台上晾了一礼拜，他妈视频的时候还问他：你是不是去化工厂上班了？"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0834", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "risk", "target_name": "《战国风云》（Risk）", "facts": ["Invented in 1957 by Albert Lamorisse, the French director of The Red Balloon, as La Conquête du Monde; Parker Brothers published it in the United States in 1959.", "The map has 42 territories, among them Kamchatka, Irkutsk, and Yakutsk, while the entire Middle East is one territory.", "Holding Australia, which has a single entry point, is the standard opening strategy.", "A full game can run for hours, and players eliminated early have nothing to do but watch."], "a": {"item_id": "5c5c0ab82bd3", "text": "你们见过真把一局《战国风云》玩到底的人吗？我见过，他到现在还单身。开局第一句话：“澳洲归我。”然后在暹罗堆了四十个兵，四个小时一动不动，跟守小区大门的保安一样。问他图什么，他说：“我在等卡。”凑齐三张一换，眼睛都亮了，好像换来的不是一把塑料小兵，是房本。凌晨三点，他终于从堪察加打进阿拉斯加，三颗骰子全是六，对面两个一。他站起来大喊：“世界是我的了！”没人理他。桌上就剩他一个，其他人早把国家互相送光，提前出局，打车回家了。他拿下了全世界，代价是再也没人接他电话。"}, "b": {"item_id": "de1740709647", "text": "能把一局《战国风云》打到底的人，现实里什么都没征服过，所以才把野心全押在骰子上。\n\n开局必有一个人说：\"我就守澳洲，稳。\"稳个啥？澳洲就一个入口，你守的不是大陆，是自己的坟。\n\n然后有人说：\"咱俩结盟吧。\"三小时后，他在堪察加半岛捅了你一刀。你问：\"不是兄弟吗？\"他说：\"兄弟归兄弟，防守方平局算我赢。\"\n\n最惨的是第一个被淘汰的。不能走，不能说话，就坐那儿看别人扔骰子，一直看到凌晨两点。\n\n最后赢的那位统一了全世界，地图上全是他的颜色。一抬头，桌上一个活人都没了，微信里全是灰的。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0835", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "durian", "target_name": "榴莲", "facts": ["Banned on Singapore's MRT trains and in many Southeast Asian hotels because of the smell.", "A 2017 genome study in Nature Genetics traced the odour to a duplicated family of MGL genes that pump out volatile sulfur compounds.", "China buys most of the world's durian exports, chiefly from Thailand, in a trade worth billions of dollars a year.", "Alfred Russel Wallace wrote in 1856 that eating it was 'a new sensation worth a voyage to the East to experience'."], "a": {"item_id": "fd9d6c3fef4a", "text": "在地铁上开榴莲的人，我一直好奇你图什么？图那句“闻着臭吃着香”是吧？大哥，吃着香的只有你，闻着臭的是一整节车厢。你这不叫吃水果，叫一人点单，全车买单。\n\n而且这种人还特讲究，戴一次性手套，掰得小心翼翼，生怕刺扎手。你的手保住了，我们的鼻子谁管？\n\n他还要给你科普：“猫山王，一百多一斤！”哦，你花一百多，买了个毒气弹，还让我们免费帮你验货。\n\n下场也摆在那：车厢立刻空出一圈，全城最好找座位的人就是他。可惜手套白戴了，第二天一张嘴，全办公室都知道他昨晚吃的是猫山王。"}, "b": {"item_id": "aea5369accce", "text": "有些人在地铁上吃榴莲，图的就是一个\"我不在乎\"。你看他闭着眼，一脸品红酒的表情，可榴莲壳上的刺是扎手的，果肉是黏的，他一瓣一瓣抠出来，手指上全是黄色的汁。车厢里二十个人，被迫跟他一起品鉴。那味道先是甜，甜完一秒，洋葱、奶油、还有一股硫磺的冲劲，直接从你鼻孔顶到脑门。车门一开，他抱着半个带刺的壳下车，回头冲大家笑了一下。全车人都在看他，我猜他以为自己在过生活，其实他是今天全车唯一被记住的人，记住的还是那股味儿。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0836", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "latex", "target_name": "LaTeX", "facts": ["Leslie Lamport wrote LaTeX in the early 1980s and released it in 1984 as a macro layer on Donald Knuth's TeX (1978), which Knuth built because he disliked how the galley proofs of the new edition of his own book looked.", "TeX's version number converges on π (3.141592653 since 2021), and Knuth's TeX bug bounty started at one hexadecimal dollar, $2.56, doubling yearly until frozen at $327.68; he stopped mailing real cheques in 2008 after fraud and now credits accounts at his fictional Bank of San Serriffe, and only 9 of the 275 cheques he sent from 2006 on were ever cashed.", "Its best-known warning is 'Underfull \\hbox (badness 10000)'; overfull lines are reported as 'Overfull \\hbox (Npt too wide)', and figures float to wherever the algorithm decides, not where the author put them.", "A single missing brace can produce an error reported pages away from the mistake, and a document can need three compiler passes to get its references right."], "a": {"item_id": "34dc34fd3a74", "text": "我特别佩服那种两页备忘录也要用LaTeX的人。通知内容就一句\"周五聚餐AA\"，他非得 \\documentclass 起手。图什么？图一个\"我不是Word那种凡人\"。你收到PDF，Computer Modern字体，一股刚证完黎曼猜想的气息，点开一看，火锅。\n\n下场呢？写十分钟，编译两小时。一个 Undefined control sequence，他在论坛翻到半夜，装了四个G的TeX Live，风扇转得像要起飞。好不容易编出来，满屏 Overfull \\hbox 警告，他一个都不肯放过。为了消掉一行0.3pt的溢出，他把\"聚餐\"改成了\"聚会\"。\n\n最后发出去，同事回：\"手机上看不清，你发文字行吗？\"\n\n他想了想，把PDF截了个图发过去。\n\n折腾一晚上，排版界的天花板，最后落成一张JPG。"}, "b": {"item_id": "4c2291ae9bb7", "text": "我们组有个哥们，通知周五聚餐，就两页备忘录，他非要用LaTeX排。这种人你们见过吧？他不是在写字，他是在“编译”。开头先敲四十行\\usepackage，正文一个字没有，导言区已经比来吃饭的人还多了。图什么？就图那个Computer Modern字体。PDF一打开，扑面而来一股“本人发过论文”的气息。结果呢？“周五晚七点”那一行报了个Overfull \\hbox，冒出页边三毫米，他调到凌晨两点。第二天大家都到了，就他没来。他在等BibTeX跑第二遍。我说一个吃饭通知，哪来的参考文献？他说：“我\\cite了菜单。”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0837", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "pmma", "target_name": "PMMA 电子束光刻胶", "facts": ["The same polymer as Plexiglas and acrylic glass, dissolved in anisole or chlorobenzene and spun into a thin film.", "The oldest electron-beam resist still in everyday use; IBM researchers reported it as one in the late 1960s.", "Positive tone: the beam breaks polymer chains and the exposed region dissolves in a MIBK:IPA developer.", "Resolves below 10 nm but has poor sensitivity, so doses of hundreds to over a thousand µC/cm² (depending on voltage) make writing slow.", "Poor dry-etch resistance, so it is used mostly for lift-off rather than as an etch mask."], "a": {"item_id": "c782f4406d74", "text": "有人搞电子束光刻，到现在还用 PMMA。我认识一个博士，课题就是把 950K 的 PMMA 写出线条。他图什么？图稳，图他师兄三年前就是这么毕业的。结果呢？他做了个五乘五的剂量矩阵，每个格子写一遍，显影用 MIBK 兑异丙醇，实验室一股指甲油味。灵敏度低，写一个面要等一下午，机器都替他着急。答辩那天评委问他为什么不换化学放大胶，他说：\"我导师觉得 PMMA 最稳。\"台下沉默了三十秒，导师就坐在第一排，点了点头。"}, "b": {"item_id": "71032dd5e938", "text": "做电子束的人，你得理解，他们是一群对新材料过敏的人。你问为什么还在用PMMA，他一脸骄傲：“分辨率高，十纳米以下！”\n\n对，这玩意儿六十年代的，跟你家鱼缸、亚克力牙托是亲戚。他们拿牙托在刻十纳米。\n\n图什么？图稳。师兄留下的配方：950K，A4，4000转，180度烘三分钟，MIBK兑IPA一比三显影。没人知道为什么，但谁改谁延毕。\n\n下场呢？曝光剂量几百微库每平方厘米，一个图案写一晚上。第二天一看，束漂了，套刻对不上。好不容易写完去刻蚀，PMMA一见等离子体就没了，像纸糊的盾牌。\n\n最后博士五年读六年，论文里还得写：“PMMA以高分辨率著称。”\n\n是啊，胶是分辨率高、灵敏度低，人也是。对新材料，毫无反应。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0838", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "latex", "target_name": "LaTeX", "facts": ["Leslie Lamport wrote LaTeX in the early 1980s and released it in 1984 as a macro layer on Donald Knuth's TeX (1978), which Knuth built because he disliked how the galley proofs of the new edition of his own book looked.", "TeX's version number converges on π (3.141592653 since 2021), and Knuth's TeX bug bounty started at one hexadecimal dollar, $2.56, doubling yearly until frozen at $327.68; he stopped mailing real cheques in 2008 after fraud and now credits accounts at his fictional Bank of San Serriffe, and only 9 of the 275 cheques he sent from 2006 on were ever cashed.", "Its best-known warning is 'Underfull \\hbox (badness 10000)'; overfull lines are reported as 'Overfull \\hbox (Npt too wide)', and figures float to wherever the algorithm decides, not where the author put them.", "A single missing brace can produce an error reported pages away from the mistake, and a document can need three compiler passes to get its references right."], "a": {"item_id": "4c2291ae9bb7", "text": "我们组有个哥们，通知周五聚餐，就两页备忘录，他非要用LaTeX排。这种人你们见过吧？他不是在写字，他是在“编译”。开头先敲四十行\\usepackage，正文一个字没有，导言区已经比来吃饭的人还多了。图什么？就图那个Computer Modern字体。PDF一打开，扑面而来一股“本人发过论文”的气息。结果呢？“周五晚七点”那一行报了个Overfull \\hbox，冒出页边三毫米，他调到凌晨两点。第二天大家都到了，就他没来。他在等BibTeX跑第二遍。我说一个吃饭通知，哪来的参考文献？他说：“我\\cite了菜单。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "6b980d56eb4f", "text": "有人写两页备忘录，非要用 LaTeX 排。问他图什么？图那个 \\maketitle 出来的标题，图页边距精确到两厘米。结果开会前一小时，他对着 Overleaf 发呆：“! Missing $ inserted”，原因是他在“报价_终版”的下划线那儿忘了进数学模式。好不容易编译通过，领导看了一眼说：能不能发个 Word 版？他只好把 tabular 一行行拆回表格。这份备忘录全公司只有他一个人会改，而他本人，现在每次开会都得先说一句：源码我发你了，你别点开。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0839", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "oat-milk", "target_name": "燕麦奶", "facts": ["Oatly started in Sweden in the 1990s, built on Lund University research by Rickard Öste on lactose intolerance.", "Oatly's slogan 'It's like milk but made for humans' was banned in Sweden in 2015 after the dairy lobby LRF Mjölk sued; Oatly then published the lawsuit as advertising.", "Oatly listed on Nasdaq in May 2021 at a valuation near $10 billion; the shares later lost more than 90% of their value.", "Commercial oat milk is made by breaking oat starch into maltose with amylase enzymes, and many brands, Oatly among them, add rapeseed oil; the result has more sugar and a higher glycaemic index than almond or soy milk."], "a": {"item_id": "55ab352fe073", "text": "每个咖啡店都有这种人，站在柜台前深吸一口气：“拿铁，换燕麦奶。”语气像在宣布皈依。\n\n你问他乳糖不耐吗？不是。“就是想少点负担。”然后他加点了一块芝士蛋糕。\n\n负担是少了，钱包的负担没少，燕麦奶加五块。而且为了打出奶泡，燕麦里的淀粉会被水解成麦芽糖，升糖比牛奶还高。他花五块钱，把一杯奶换成了一杯液体麦片，还觉得自己在做减法。\n\n喝到一半，他拍照发朋友圈：“植物基生活。”\n\n最后一口，他皱着眉品了品：“嗯，有层次。”\n\n兄弟，那不是层次，那是你早餐的碗底。"}, "b": {"item_id": "794cf9f48948", "text": "有一种人，点拿铁非要加一句“换燕麦奶”。你问为什么，他说“对身体好”。好在哪？他说不上来，但语气笃定，像刚发现了新大陆。\n\n这杯燕麦奶拿铁，奶泡两分钟就塌成一层白沫浮在上面。喝到最后，杯底是一层灰褐色的东西，像把麦片泡在水里忘了捞。\n\n每杯多收五块五，一个月下来一百六十多。他管这叫“投资健康”。\n\n体检报告出来，医生看着他说：“您血糖有点高。”他愣住：“我没加糖啊。”医生说：“燕麦奶本身就带甜，您那杯还加了两泵糖浆。”\n\n他沉默了一会儿，说：“我就是为了不喝糖，才换的燕麦奶。”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0840", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "emacs", "target_name": "Emacs", "facts": ["Began in 1976 as a set of macros for the TECO editor at MIT's AI Lab, written by Richard Stallman and others; GNU Emacs followed in 1985.", "Extensible in its own Lisp dialect, Emacs Lisp; the standing joke is that it is a fine operating system lacking only a good editor.", "Ships with a psychotherapist (M-x doctor), Tetris, a mail client, a calendar, and a web browser (eww).", "An old backronym is 'Eight Megabytes And Constantly Swapping', from when 8 MB was a lot of memory.", "'Emacs pinky' is the name for the hand strain blamed on its Control-key chords."], "a": {"item_id": "ce1edb495cdc", "text": "有一种人嫌操作系统太小，非要把Emacs装成操作系统。邮件在里面收，日程用org-mode排，网页用eww看，连音乐都在里面放。图什么？图一个界面管所有事。结果一启动，init.el跑了四十秒，我问他怎么退出，他说C-x C-c。我说那是退出，他说那是\"我还没想好要不要走\"。最惨的是他的小拇指，Ctrl和Meta全压在它身上。去年体检，医生问是不是长期弹钢琴，他说不是，我在写Lisp。现在他的操作系统还是没装好，但他已经能用一个快捷键，把自己的手指头按进了 *scratch* 缓冲区。"}, "b": {"item_id": "edb43822f3d2", "text": "我有个朋友，把 Emacs 当操作系统用。邮件在里面收，网页在里面看，连聊天都在里面聊。我问他图啥，他说：“不用切窗口，专注。”专注？他的 init.el 五千行，每个周末固定修配置，修的是上周为了省事写的配置。最绝的是，他最后装了 evil-mode，就是在 Emacs 里模拟 Vim 的按键。折腾十年，把全世界装进 Emacs，最后为了好用，把宿敌请了回来。现在他小指已经废了，他管这叫“Emacs 小指”，语气跟领了勋章似的。我说哥们，你这不是在用操作系统，是在给操作系统打工，还是不发工资的那种。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0841", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "beanie-babies", "target_name": "Beanie Babies 豆豆娃", "facts": ["Launched by Ty Inc. in 1993 and sold only through small gift shops, with designs 'retired' to manufacture scarcity.", "At the height of the craze Beanie Babies accounted for about 10% of eBay's sales, and eBay's 1998 IPO filing said the site at times had over 30,000 Beanie Baby auctions running simultaneously in the quarter ending June 1998.", "In 1999 a divorcing couple divided their Beanie Baby collection one by one on a courtroom floor, in a photograph that ran worldwide.", "In 1999 Ty announced that Beanie Babies would be retired at the end of the year, then reversed itself after asking the public to vote and seeing an overwhelming vote to keep them.", "Most 'rare' Beanies that price guides valued in the thousands now sell for a few dollars."], "a": {"item_id": "0f9603e043d9", "text": "九十年代有一帮家长，坚信豆豆娃能攒出孩子的大学学费。这种人买玩具，第一件事是检查心形标签有没有折角。孩子伸手想摸，不行，\"这是你的学费，你摸学费干嘛？\"\n\n他们图的是\"绝版\"。厂家每隔几个月宣布一批豆豆娃\"退休\"，其实就是办完欢送会，转头再出新的。他们还不信邪，为了集齐麦当劳的迷你豆豆娃，一口气点二十份开心乐园餐，汉堡扔了，玩具供着。\n\n结果呢？二十年后，车库里三千只，每只市价两块钱。当年传说能换房的黛安娜紫熊，现在能换半杯奶茶。\n\n最后发财的只有一个人，就是做豆豆娃的老板。后来他因为海外藏钱逃税被抓了。你看，连他逃的税，都是你们的学费。"}, "b": {"item_id": "a30c3de668a8", "text": "我表舅九十年代一口气囤了三百多只豆豆娃，每只屁股上的小吊牌都套三层塑料袋，连摸都不让摸。他说这叫抗通胀。后来厂家宣布\"退休\"，他激动得三天没睡，觉得自己是股神。结果呢？退休那批，现在二手平台一只八块包邮，买家还嫌吊牌有折痕。他家客厅的柜子搬到了阁楼，女儿问那是什么，他说：\"这是我的养老金。\"女儿说：\"爸，养老金能当饭吃吗？\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0842", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "windows-vista", "target_name": "Windows Vista", "facts": ["Released to consumers in January 2007, after the 'Longhorn' project was reset in 2004 and headline features such as the WinFS file system were dropped.", "User Account Control prompted so often that Apple's 'Get a Mac' campaign ran an ad about 'Cancel or Allow'.", "'Windows Vista Capable' stickers went on PCs that could only run Home Basic, without the Aero interface, and led to a class-action lawsuit.", "Windows 7 (2009), widely praised, was largely a tuned-up Vista on the same kernel line; Vista support ended in April 2017."], "a": {"item_id": "445c6f8771dc", "text": "有一种人，叫第一天就升级Vista的人。我问他图啥？他说要玻璃特效，要边栏小工具，要最新的系统。结果装完第一件事，是点了四十七次\"允许\"。不是我点的，是系统在问：您确定要复制这个文件吗？您确定要打开这个程序吗？您确定要关掉这个弹窗吗？像在跟一个多疑的保安办手续。\n\n复制一个文件，进度条走到99%停住，给你显示\"正在计算剩余时间\"，算完告诉你还要一个小时。他的显卡驱动？厂家说还在开发。打印机？厂家说用XP版就够了。最后他在论坛发帖，标题叫《Vista很好，是我的电脑不行》。电脑没问题，问题是他把\"最新\"当成了\"能用\"。"}, "b": {"item_id": "0edc712dc233", "text": "2007年第一天就装Vista的人，我认识一个。他还买的旗舰版，两千多，就为了微软承诺的“独家福利”，最后等来一个德州扑克和一张会动的桌面。他电脑上贴着“Vista Capable”，意思是“能装”，没说“能用”。512兆内存硬开Aero毛玻璃，风扇响得像在替他喊冤。打印机没驱动，摄像头没驱动，就侧边栏那个小时钟特别准，每一秒都在提醒他后悔。最狠的是那个弹窗：“是否允许此程序更改？”微软问了他一千遍，他一千遍都点了“允许”，从来没想过点“取消”。现在他逢人就说自己是最早的尝鲜派。不，兄弟，你是最早降级回XP的那批。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0843", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "brasilia", "target_name": "巴西利亚", "facts": ["Inaugurated on 21 April 1960 after about 41 months of construction, moving the capital inland from Rio de Janeiro.", "Lúcio Costa's Plano Piloto is laid out like an airplane (or a bird, or a cross), with Oscar Niemeyer's government buildings along the fuselage.", "Listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1987, less than 30 years after it was built.", "Planned for 500,000 residents; the Federal District now has about 3 million, most in satellite towns outside the plan.", "Built for the car: wide avenues, few pedestrian crossings, and distances that make walking impractical."], "a": {"item_id": "879d5b880e2f", "text": "在巴西利亚靠两条腿出门的，就三种人：刚考上公务员、工资还没到账的；捧着建筑系课本来朝圣尼迈耶的；还有一种，是前两种走到一半的样子。\n\n他们图什么？图“城市漫步”。可这城是照着飞机画的，你走路，等于在机翼上找人行道。地图上南翼308区和309区挨着，中间隔着一条六车道。想过去？去找那种叫“小剪刀”的立交，绕一圈下来，你从南翼走出了北翼的心情。\n\n旱季湿度百分之十，太阳毒得像在三权广场受审。认出他们很简单：草坪上那条被踩秃的土路，那不是小道，那是他们集体签的字。\n\n下场？第二个月，贷款买了辆二手菲亚特。"}, "b": {"item_id": "83f9dfea8a34", "text": "在巴西利亚靠两条腿出门的，基本是两种人：建筑系学生，和刚下飞机的游客。学生是来朝圣的，说尼迈耶的建筑要\"用身体丈量\"。行，量吧。\n\n地图上，酒店到国会大厦一公里，看着像隔壁。你一走才发现，中间是八车道的Eixo Monumental，没有路口，只有立交桥，立交桥底下还有立交桥。你走了四十分钟，国会大厦一直在你正前方，又大又白，像吊在驴脑袋前面的胡萝卜，就是不让你靠近。\n\n走到第三十分钟，你终于崩溃，决定直接穿过草坪抄近道。恭喜，你踩出了一条\"欲望之路\"。\n\n最惨的是，你以为自己在反抗城市规划，其实你是在免费给规划师交作业。\n\n最后你浑身是汗，湿度百分之十，嘴唇干得像盖了章。走到门口，保安问你：您的车呢？"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0844", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "ugg", "target_name": "UGG 雪地靴", "facts": ["Sheepskin boots Australian surfers wore to warm up after sessions; Brian Smith started selling them in California in 1978.", "Deckers Outdoor bought the brand in 1995 and holds 'UGG' as a trademark in the US and elsewhere; in Australia, 'ugg' is a generic word any bootmaker may use.", "In 2019, a US jury found an Australian maker liable for selling boots labelled 'ugg' into the United States.", "Oprah Winfrey featured them in her 'Favorite Things' lists repeatedly in the 2000s, which drove the first boom.", "UGG is Deckers' largest brand, with over $2 billion in annual sales."], "a": {"item_id": "291bb4a33621", "text": "一年四季穿UGG的人，你仔细看，基本是同一种人：早上说“我就下楼拿个快递”，结果一路拿到了公司。\n\n问他图什么，他说：“不用系鞋带，一脚蹬。”对，UGG不是穿，是给脚办入住。\n\n夏天三十八度他还穿，问就是“里面是羊毛，透气”。对，羊也这么说，然后被剃了。\n\n下场特别统一。一场雨，一千多的靴子泡成两块发面馒头。到了办公室一脱鞋，羊毛负责把你全年的经历保存下来，同事都不用问你中午吃了啥。\n\n最惨的是冬天。你坚持了三季，终于等到主场，结果满大街都在穿，你的“个性”直接变成了“合群”。\n\n你说你这一年图什么？图了个脚，被焖成了刚出锅的馒头。"}, "b": {"item_id": "3c6a30dd29f9", "text": "我有个朋友，一年四季穿UGG。三伏天，脚上套着两只羊。我问你不热吗？她说：“你不懂，UGG本来就是澳洲冲浪的人穿的。”姐，人家是冲完浪上岸暖脚，你是在地铁里给脚蒸桑拿。\n\n她图什么？图舒服，图不用系鞋带。确实不用系，穿到第三个月，靴筒自己就塌了，鞋跟往外磨成斜坡，她走路像一艘正在侧翻的船。下雨更惨，麂皮一沾水就是一圈白印，像脚踝上长了年轮，一看就知道这双鞋熬过几个夏天。\n\n最狠的是味道。羊毛内里，光脚，捂了一整个七月。她一脱鞋，全屋人都懂了：那只羊，没走远。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0845", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "crocs", "target_name": "Crocs 洞洞鞋", "facts": ["Founded in Boulder, Colorado, in 2002; the first clog debuted at the Fort Lauderdale boat show as a boating shoe.", "Made of 'Croslite', which Crocs describes as a proprietary closed-cell resin foam and which third parties identify as an injection-moulded EVA foam.", "Balenciaga sold a platform Crocs for about $850 in 2018.", "The stock fell more than 90% from its 2007 peak during the 2008 crisis; the company then boomed in the pandemic and gave away 860,000 pairs to healthcare workers in 2020.", "The holes are officially 'ventilation ports' and the charms that go in them are 'Jibbitz', a company Crocs bought in 2006."], "a": {"item_id": "0269f5e79bdf", "text": "穿洞洞鞋出门的人，开口都是同一句：“我就图个舒服。”行，那你解释一下鞋面上那一排小挂件。小熊、披萨、一个字母H，这哪是图舒服，这是脚上开了个朋友圈。\n\n还有那根后跟带，永远往后翻，他们管这叫“运动模式”。哥们，你是下楼取快递。真运动谁穿这个？这个模式唯一的功能，就是方便扶梯把你的鞋吃了。\n\n下场也都一样。夏天晒完，脚背上一块镂空格子，像被烤架翻过面。下雨天更惨，洞是通的，水进得特别积极，走一步，咕叽，咕叽，脚上养了两只青蛙。\n\n最后他还要补一句：“这个其实防臭。”没人问你。"}, "b": {"item_id": "d0de4b131b62", "text": "穿洞洞鞋出门的人，分两种：真懒的，和装懒的。装懒的最好认，鞋上插满智必星，小恐龙、小汉堡、小皮卡丘，一只脚背就是一个摊位。你图舒服我理解，你在鞋上开连锁店干什么？\n\n他们还有个绝招：把脑门上那根带子往后一掰，“我切换运动模式了”。兄弟，带子掰过去，你是能跑过谁？\n\n下场都一样。下雨天，鞋面上那十几个洞全是进水口，走一步咕叽一声。晴天更惨，小石子从洞里钻进去卡在脚心，又不能当街脱，只能单腿跳着甩，像在给鞋做心肺复苏。\n\n回家一看，智必星掉了三个。人是舒服了，资产缩水了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0846", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "klingon", "target_name": "克林贡语", "facts": ["Created by linguist Marc Okrand for Star Trek III (1984), building on a few words the actor James Doohan improvised for the 1979 film.", "Deliberately alien grammar: object-verb-subject word order and no verb 'to be', so Hamlet's line became 'taH pagh taHbe'', 'continue or not continue'.", "The Klingon Language Institute (1992) published Hamlet 'in the original Klingon'; Bing Translator added Klingon in 2013 and Duolingo in 2018.", "Okrand's 1985 Klingon Dictionary listed roughly 1,500 words, and fluent speakers number only in the dozens: 20 to 30 in 2009, 30 to 50 by 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "c9277fcc0b88", "text": "练到流利克林贡语的人，我真心佩服。白天是会计，晚上披着披风，图什么？图终于有人配得上他的愤怒。结果呢，这门语言没有“你好”，打招呼只有一句“nuqneH”，意思是“你要干嘛？”。三年苦功，换来全世界几百个同好，见面第一句就是找茬。\n\n更惨的是语序。克林贡语是宾语、动词、主语，你骂人得先说被骂的是谁、骂了什么，最后才说是谁干的。一句狠话憋到末尾才亮出主语，气势全靠肺活量。\n\n回家对老婆表白，没有“谢谢”，没有“对不起”，这门语言压根没给你认错的词。想道歉？只能把对方打一顿再说“你赢了”。\n\n所以流利的下场就是：全世界会说的人聚在一个群里，用克林贡语吵架。吵了三个小时，谁也没听懂谁，因为谁都在等对方把主语说出来。"}, "b": {"item_id": "e66268bd7919", "text": "你们身边有没有那种人，把克林贡语学到流利？我认识一个。他周末不陪女朋友，说要练喉音。克林贡语一个词里有三个喉塞音，他说完一句话，像在下水道里给管道道歉。他图什么？他说等哪天遇见真正的克林贡人，就能用母语骂人。结果呢？他在地铁上对一个大爷说“nuqneH”，意思是“你要什么”，大爷以为他在要钱，给了他五块。他面试时用“Qapla'”回答“你的成功经验”，面试官以为他在清嗓子。现在他家里的小孩也学会了，写作业的时候对着卷子喊“ghobe'”，就是“不”。他老婆说，这是她这辈子听过最有骨气的一个“不”。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0847", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hawaiian-pizza", "target_name": "夏威夷披萨", "facts": ["Invented in 1962 by Sam Panopoulos, a Greek immigrant, at the Satellite Restaurant in Chatham, Ontario, Canada; named after the brand of canned pineapple he used.", "Iceland's president Guðni Th. Jóhannesson said in 2017 that he would ban pineapple on pizza if he could, then clarified that a president has no such power.", "Hawaii is no longer a major pineapple producer: Del Monte shut its Hawaiian pineapple operations in November 2006, two years ahead of its announced 2008 exit, the state's last cannery closed in 2007, and most canned pineapple now comes from Thailand, Indonesia, and the Philippines.", "Panopoulos, whose restaurant also served Chinese dishes, told the Toronto Sun in 2010 that 'in those days people didn't mix the tastes like sweet and sour, except for Chinese food'."], "a": {"item_id": "b9921996d500", "text": "每桌聚餐总有那么一个人，菜单翻到最后一页，清清嗓子：“要不……来个夏威夷？”\n\n这人图什么？图个性。他觉得自己在叛逆：“我不走寻常路。”兄弟，夏威夷披萨是全世界最出名的争议披萨，你点它，就像在摇滚圈穿皮衣，一点都不稀奇。而且它跟夏威夷没关系，发明它的是个住在加拿大的希腊人。你连“异域风情”都没沾上。\n\n下场呢？披萨一上桌，全桌人都去拿别的，只有它孤零零摆着。你只能说“我就爱这口”，然后一个人硬啃六块。菠萝出水，饼底泡成湿毛巾，火腿片在上面游泳。吃不完打包回家，第二天微波炉一转，咬一口，热乎乎的菠萝配咸火腿，就像前任突然发来一句“最近好吗”，说不上哪里不对，就是哪都不对。"}, "b": {"item_id": "2a083e43e4a9", "text": "点夏威夷披萨的人，我大概能猜出两种。一种是想显得自己没偏见，另一种是他妈从小就给他点这个。前一种会先举着手机拍那几块菠萝，配文“勇敢的人才吃”。吃到第三口，菠萝汁已经顺着饼底流进盘子，火腿片泡得发白，他还是硬着头皮说“层次很丰富”。层次确实丰富，甜、咸、油、软在嘴里打成一团，最后赢的是芝士，输的是他的胃。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0848", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "crocs", "target_name": "Crocs 洞洞鞋", "facts": ["Founded in Boulder, Colorado, in 2002; the first clog debuted at the Fort Lauderdale boat show as a boating shoe.", "Made of 'Croslite', which Crocs describes as a proprietary closed-cell resin foam and which third parties identify as an injection-moulded EVA foam.", "Balenciaga sold a platform Crocs for about $850 in 2018.", "The stock fell more than 90% from its 2007 peak during the 2008 crisis; the company then boomed in the pandemic and gave away 860,000 pairs to healthcare workers in 2020.", "The holes are officially 'ventilation ports' and the charms that go in them are 'Jibbitz', a company Crocs bought in 2006."], "a": {"item_id": "e55404ed5b22", "text": "你们知道穿洞洞鞋出门的都是什么人吗？一种是刚下飞机、防晒霜还没抹匀的人，另一种是觉得\"我就是不在乎形象\"的人。图什么？图脚舒服，图鞋上那些小挂件能挂满一圈，走路叮叮当当，像个移动的钥匙串。结果呢？去趟海边，洞洞里灌满沙子，走一步倒一把，像在给沙滩做漏斗。下雨天更惨，鞋底那几个孔等于给雨水开了后门，袜子从脚踝湿到脚趾。最要命的是全程还得踩着\"吧唧吧唧\"的声音进电梯，全电梯的人都知道你来了。后来他真的不出门了，因为他终于明白，别人不是看不见他，是听得见他。"}, "b": {"item_id": "061b4ce9b529", "text": "穿洞洞鞋出门的人，我大概知道是谁。嘴上说\"我就下楼拿个快递\"，结果一路走进商场、电影院，还有前任的婚礼。\n\n图什么呢？图舒服。舒服到觉得全世界都该给你的脚松绑。松绑还不够，还得在洞里插一排小鞋花：皮卡丘、小黄瓜、一个不知道哪来的西瓜。三十岁的人，脚上挂得像幼儿园书包。\n\n下场呢？下雨天，那些洞全是进水口，每走一步，脚底\"噗叽\"一声。上电扶梯，软塌塌的鞋帮被一口咬住，保安跑得比你妈都快。\n\n你图的是不用系鞋带的自由，换来的是单脚蹦着，在全商场的注视下，去捡一只挂着小黄瓜的鞋。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0849", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "jackfruit", "target_name": "菠萝蜜", "facts": ["The largest fruit that grows on a tree; single fruits can weigh over 40 kg.", "The national fruit of Bangladesh and the state fruit of Kerala and Tamil Nadu.", "Unripe flesh is sold as a vegan 'pulled pork' substitute, although it has only 1-2 g of protein per 100 g.", "Cutting one releases a sticky white latex, so cooks oil their knives and hands first.", "A member of the mulberry family (Moraceae), a relative of breadfruit and figs."], "a": {"item_id": "91e32b021f50", "text": "最近轻食店流行卖“植物基手撕猪肉”，一问，菠萝蜜。老板穿着亚麻衬衫，跟你聊碳排放，一盒六十八。你知道菠萝蜜在海南路边多少钱吗？一个三十斤，摊主拿它当板凳坐。他图什么？就图你没去过海南。\n\n但老天是公平的。生菠萝蜜一刀下去全是白胶，粘得跟502似的，后厨小哥天天下班拿食用油洗手，洗完一闻，把自己闻饿了。最惨的是哪天进货进成熟了，顾客咬一口“手撕猪肉”，满嘴甜得跟芒果味口香糖一样。老板只能硬着头皮解释：“这是我们的新品，果香黑猪。”\n\n你装了半年肉，一熟就露馅。这叫什么？瓜熟蒂落，人设也落。"}, "b": {"item_id": "292065870d49", "text": "菠萝蜜这玩意儿，长得像个带刺的炸弹，切开满手黏胶，洗手得用油。现在有人把它炖成\"手撕肉\"，印在菜单上叫\"植物基猪肉\"。我就想问，你们是没吃过猪肉，还是没吃过菠萝蜜？它被撕成丝是挺像，我承认。可你往嘴里一放，甜的！老板的算盘打得噼啪响：素食风口来了，成本便宜，还能加价三十。结果客人咬一口，愣住了：\"这肉怎么带着一股熟透了的水果味？\"老板说，这叫创新。创新完，一个月后店就没了，差评区全是\"猪肉被冒充，我的眼泪是甜的\"。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0850", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "canberra", "target_name": "堪培拉", "facts": ["Chosen as a compromise because neither Sydney nor Melbourne would accept the other as capital; Australia's constitution required the capital to be at least 100 miles from Sydney.", "Designed by the Chicago architects Walter Burley Griffin and Marion Mahony Griffin, who won an international competition in 1912.", "Its centrepiece, Lake Burley Griffin, is artificial, created by damming the Molonglo River in 1963-64.", "Parliament House (1988) is built into Capital Hill under a lawn meant for citizens to walk over their politicians; the lawn was fenced off in 2017 for security.", "Melbourne served as the capital until 1927 while Canberra was being built."], "a": {"item_id": "0d2a6bbab13d", "text": "我有个朋友，为了工作搬去堪培拉。他说：“联邦公务员，铁饭碗，三年升EL1。”兄弟，你才二十八岁，人生规划写得跟预算案附录一样。\n\n去了一年，他说话全成了缩写：“我在DFAT，赶上MOG调整，等APS6转正。”我问他周末干嘛，他说开两个多小时去贝特曼斯湾看海。你搬去一个城市，生活质量全看你多快能离开它。\n\n最惨的是，他现在会主动跟人说：“堪培拉其实挺好的，你得慢慢了解它。”这话我只在两种人嘴里听过：堪培拉人，和被关太久、开始同情绑匪的人质。"}, "b": {"item_id": "e958e4adc831", "text": "有人为了工作搬去堪培拉，我真想问：你是想不开，还是想得太开？这帮人基本都是联邦公务员，图什么？图铁饭碗，图名片上印着个“部”字。去了才发现，这座城市就是个大办公室，周五下午四点人类集体消失，全开车去悉尼找活人。在那儿交朋友特别快：聊三句知道你哪个部，聊五句知道你在写什么简报，聊十句发现咱俩在写同一份。最惨是机构改革，部门被拆了、改名了，你人没动，老板换了，名片重印。五年后你攒够钱想回悉尼，一查，悉尼唯一对口的单位，总部在堪培拉。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0851", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "nfts", "target_name": "NFT", "facts": ["Beeple's 'Everydays: The First 5000 Days' sold at Christie's for $69.3 million in March 2021.", "OpenSea, the largest NFT marketplace, processed a record $2.7 billion in trades on 1 May 2022 and just $9.34 million on 28 August 2022, a 99% drop, according to DappRadar data.", "A 2023 dappGambl study found that 95% of the NFT collections it examined had a market value of zero.", "The token usually records only a URL; the image itself sits on an ordinary server or IPFS, not on the blockchain.", "Collins Dictionary named 'NFT' its word of the year for 2021."], "a": {"item_id": "a8cfb67f2de5", "text": "2021年买NFT的人，我身边就有一个。以前连拼多多砍一刀都嫌麻烦，那年突然跟我说：我是数字艺术收藏家。我问收藏的啥，他给我看一只猴子，戴着墨镜，一脸没睡醒。十八万。\n\n我说这图我右键另存为就有了。他说：你那叫复制，我这叫所有权。我说所有权在哪？他说在区块链上。我说那你家里挂的是啥？他说：我挂在Twitter头像上。\n\n十八万，买个头像框。还得天天在群里跟一群头像也是猴子的陌生人说\"gm\"，互道早安。\n\n现在地板价跌了九成，他每天刷OpenSea，那只猴子挂出去半年，连个问价的都没有。他说他是钻石手，死拿不卖。\n\n兄弟，你不是钻石手，你是那只猴子唯一的粉丝。"}, "b": {"item_id": "3a567b5377a2", "text": "2021年买NFT的朋友，我真佩服。花三十万买张猴子图，猴子叼着烟、一脸不想活，这是在提前预演自己的下场。\n\n你跟他说：“我右键另存为了啊。”他急了：“你存的是图，我拥有的是链上的哈希！”大哥，你花三十万，买了一张收据的收据。\n\n那年推特上全是六边形猴子头像，跟加入了邪教似的，天天在Discord里喊“WAGMI，我们都会发财”。项目方路线图写着“元宇宙乐园”，写完人就跑了。\n\n现在地板价跌成零头，当年付的Gas费都比猴子值钱。他还不卖，说这叫“长期持有”。兄弟，你那不叫长期持有，叫卖不掉。猴子叼着烟看着你，终于笑了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0852", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "graphene", "target_name": "石墨烯", "facts": ["Isolated in 2004 by Andre Geim and Konstantin Novoselov at Manchester by peeling graphite with Scotch tape; Nobel Prize in Physics, 2010.", "The EU's Graphene Flagship, launched in 2013, committed €1 billion over ten years.", "A 2018 study in Advanced Materials tested 'graphene' from 60 producers and found none with more than 50% graphene content; most was graphite flakes.", "Sold in tennis rackets, running shoes, and headphones as a performance ingredient.", "Geim also won an Ig Nobel Prize in 2000 for levitating a frog with magnets."], "a": {"item_id": "20a7d109770e", "text": "我有个同学读材料的，逢人就说石墨烯要改变世界。每次开场一模一样：2004年，两个科学家拿胶带从铅笔芯上粘下一层碳，拿了诺贝尔奖。他讲得特别骄傲，好像那卷胶带是他家的。\n\n我问他：比钢硬，比铜导电，那你怎么还合租？他说量产有瓶颈。十年了，这瓶颈比石墨烯还硬。\n\n后来他创业，产品终于出来了：石墨烯保暖内衣。改变世界的材料，最后改变的是秋裤的价格。铅笔芯的成分，卖出羽绒服的价，还跟我说发热是黑科技。大哥，那是你自己急的。\n\n现在他融不到钱了，开始去我妈的养生群讲课。我妈听完说：小伙子，你这套跟我买的磁疗枕头是一个路数。"}, "b": {"item_id": "70e5e600bd85", "text": "我有个表舅，逢人就说石墨烯要改变世界：一层碳原子，比钢硬两百倍，导电比铜还快。你以为他是科学家？他是卖秋裤的。石墨烯秋裤，三百九一条，号称发热、抗菌、通经络。\n\n我问他世界啥时候改变？他说快了！二〇〇四年人家拿透明胶从铅笔芯上粘下来的，诺贝尔奖都发了。我说对啊，二十年了，奖人家领走了，透明胶还在卖，就你的秋裤卖不动。\n\n后来他把卖秋裤的钱全砸进石墨烯概念股，世界确实改变了，他的世界。现在他穿着自己那条秋裤跟人说：“石墨烯，薄啊，一层就一个原子那么薄。跟我家底一样。”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0853", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "canberra", "target_name": "堪培拉", "facts": ["Chosen as a compromise because neither Sydney nor Melbourne would accept the other as capital; Australia's constitution required the capital to be at least 100 miles from Sydney.", "Designed by the Chicago architects Walter Burley Griffin and Marion Mahony Griffin, who won an international competition in 1912.", "Its centrepiece, Lake Burley Griffin, is artificial, created by damming the Molonglo River in 1963-64.", "Parliament House (1988) is built into Capital Hill under a lawn meant for citizens to walk over their politicians; the lawn was fenced off in 2017 for security.", "Melbourne served as the capital until 1927 while Canberra was being built."], "a": {"item_id": "7c175bf79c23", "text": "你们知道堪培拉为什么是首都吗？悉尼和墨尔本都不让对方当，吵了半天，最后选了个中间的地方，谁也不服，谁也不去。\n\n偏偏就有一群人，为了\"离权力近一点\"搬过来。处长、智库研究员、部长办公室的小助理，还有一堆咨询公司的。图什么？图升职，图简历上一个好看的头衔，图哪天上电视，站在国会山底下说一句\"我们正在研究\"。\n\n三年后呢？他们每个周五晚上开三个小时车去悉尼，周日傍晚再开三个小时回来，车里一句话不说，像押送回去上班的犯人。湖边散步走了一百圈，国会山看了一百遍，权力还是在别人手里。冬天早上零下五度，他们在环岛里绕圈，绕了三年，绕回了原点。"}, "b": {"item_id": "e958e4adc831", "text": "有人为了工作搬去堪培拉，我真想问：你是想不开，还是想得太开？这帮人基本都是联邦公务员，图什么？图铁饭碗，图名片上印着个“部”字。去了才发现，这座城市就是个大办公室，周五下午四点人类集体消失，全开车去悉尼找活人。在那儿交朋友特别快：聊三句知道你哪个部，聊五句知道你在写什么简报，聊十句发现咱俩在写同一份。最惨是机构改革，部门被拆了、改名了，你人没动，老板换了，名片重印。五年后你攒够钱想回悉尼，一查，悉尼唯一对口的单位，总部在堪培拉。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0854", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "macau", "target_name": "澳门", "facts": ["A Portuguese settlement from the 1550s until the handover to China in December 1999.", "Casino revenue passed the Las Vegas Strip's in 2006, and at its 2013 peak, about $45 billion, was roughly seven times the Strip's.", "One company, STDM, held the casino monopoly for four decades until licences opened to competition in 2002.", "Among the most densely populated places on Earth, at roughly 20,000 people per square kilometre.", "The Cotai Strip stands on land reclaimed from the sea between the islands of Taipa and Coloane."], "a": {"item_id": "56ba73fdce82", "text": "去澳门翻本的人，基本都是在内地被套急了的。股票亏了三十万，跟老婆说：“我去澳门，一把就回来。”一把！你在A股被按着打了三年，指望在百家乐桌上三分钟补回来？你这不叫翻本，你是去给澳门送第二茬本。\n\n他们还特别有原则：赢回三十万就走。结果赢到二十九万，心想，差一万，凑个整。威尼斯人里那片假天空永远是下午四点，你输到凌晨，抬头天还亮着，你以为自己才来一会儿。\n\n下场也统一：先当手表，赌场旁边那家当铺，老板比你老婆还了解你。最后口袋剩二十块，在大三巴买个蛋挞，边吃边发呆。\n\n回关闸的免费大巴上，一路没人说话。因为来的时候喊“一把回本”的那几个人，现在全在这辆车上。"}, "b": {"item_id": "8dc5e4ad252d", "text": "我有个朋友，在老家输了二十万，说要去澳门翻本。你听听这逻辑：在小区棋牌室输的钱，要去全亚洲最专业的地方赢回来。这就像被村口的狗咬了，你决定去动物园找老虎报仇。\n\n他是坐赌场的免费接驳大巴进去的。记住，澳门最大方的就是这趟车，因为它只管送你进去，不管你怎么回来。三天后他给我发了张照片，大三巴牌坊，就剩前面一面墙，后头全空了。他配了一句：“你看，跟我一样。”\n\n最绝的是回来那天，他手上那块表押在了葡京旁边的当铺里。他说没事，等下回签注办下来就去赎。兄弟，你兜里那张不是当票，是下一趟的车票。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0855", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "emacs", "target_name": "Emacs", "facts": ["Began in 1976 as a set of macros for the TECO editor at MIT's AI Lab, written by Richard Stallman and others; GNU Emacs followed in 1985.", "Extensible in its own Lisp dialect, Emacs Lisp; the standing joke is that it is a fine operating system lacking only a good editor.", "Ships with a psychotherapist (M-x doctor), Tetris, a mail client, a calendar, and a web browser (eww).", "An old backronym is 'Eight Megabytes And Constantly Swapping', from when 8 MB was a lot of memory.", "'Emacs pinky' is the name for the hand strain blamed on its Control-key chords."], "a": {"item_id": "19ed8eac97e6", "text": "我认识一个把Emacs当操作系统的哥们。他用Emacs收邮件、看新闻、听歌、管日程。我问：“那你干嘛不直接装个操作系统？”他推了推眼镜：“Emacs就缺一个好编辑器。”说完笑得特别得意，那种全世界只有我懂的得意。\n\n他的待办用Org-mode管，第一条是“优化Org-mode配置”，已经挂了三年。init.el写了八千行，比他这辈子写过的业务代码都长。启动一次要四十七秒，所以他从不关，上次重启是因为搬家断电。\n\n小指因为按Ctrl已经变形了，医生问怎么弄的，他说“写东西”。女朋友说出来吃个饭，他说等我写个函数。函数写好了，女朋友没了。\n\n现在他唯一的倾诉对象是M-x doctor，一个七十年代的假心理医生。他说我好孤独，它说：“你为什么这么说？”\n\n他说，这是他用过最懂他的软件。"}, "b": {"item_id": "f945ff696b4a", "text": "把 Emacs 当操作系统用的人，我见过。早上在 Emacs 里收邮件，中午在 org-mode 里排日程，晚上在 Emacs 里听音乐、写日记、看股票。他管这叫\"一站式工作流\"。\n\n有天他想关机，按了 C-x C-c，屏幕跳出一行字：\"Modified buffers exist; exit anyway? (yes or no)\"。他心想我又没改什么，一看，十四个 buffer 没保存，其中一个是他写了三年的日记。\n\n他盯着屏幕想了半分钟，最后打出了 yes。然后他意识到，这个操作系统唯一会问他要不要关机的时候，也是唯一不让他关机的时候。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0856", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "windows-8", "target_name": "Windows 8", "facts": ["Released in October 2012; removed the Start button and booted into a full-screen grid of tiles designed for touch screens most PCs did not have.", "The 8.1 update in 2013 put a Start button back, Windows 10 (2015) restored the Start menu, and Microsoft skipped the name Windows 9 altogether.", "Microsoft dropped the 'Metro' name in August 2012 after reports that German retailer Metro AG had objected on trademark grounds; Microsoft called 'Metro' a code name and denied any litigation, and the style was afterwards known informally as 'Modern UI'.", "The 'Charms bar' hid Search, Share, and Settings behind an invisible swipe from the right edge.", "Windows RT, the ARM edition launched alongside it, could not run ordinary Windows programs; Microsoft wrote down $900 million of unsold Surface RT tablets in 2013."], "a": {"item_id": "09a19029d52d", "text": "有一种人，专门给台式机装 Windows 8。你问他图啥？他说，触屏才是未来，我得提前用上。问题是他这台机器是机箱加显示器加一个鼠标，屏幕根本摸不到。开机后满屏都是色块磁贴，像走进一家超市，货架上全是天气和新闻，唯独没有他要的开始菜单。他想关机，找了二十分钟，最后在屏幕右边缘一划，弹出一个叫\"超级按钮\"的东西。我很怀疑取这名字的人从来没关过机。最后他花九十块买了个第三方软件，把开始按钮请了回来。他现在逢人就说：\"我用的是 Windows 8，但我把它改回了 Windows 7。\""}, "b": {"item_id": "825a78e26940", "text": "给台式机装Windows 8的人，我太了解了。这种人，朋友聚会必说一句：\"我这个是尝鲜。\"尝鲜，你家台式机是个五斤重的铁箱子，插着鼠标键盘，哪来的\"鲜\"？\n\n图什么呢？图开机快，四秒。是，四秒就进去了，进去以后花四十分钟找关机。\n\n开始按钮没了，换成一整屏磁贴，天气、股票、应用商店，全对着你闪。他们对着显示器伸手去戳，显示器说：\"大哥，我不是触屏。\"\n\n关机更绝。鼠标得甩到右上角，等超级按钮栏冒出来，点设置，点电源，才能关。关个机像密室逃脱。\n\n下场呢？他们最后都去搜\"Windows 8 怎么把开始菜单弄回来\"，下载个第三方软件，把微软删掉的东西，亲手装回去。\n\n等到8.1出了，开始按钮回来了，他们热泪盈眶，一点：跳到磁贴屏。\n\n那不是按钮，那是个门铃，按了还是那帮磁贴来开门。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0857", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "vision-pro", "target_name": "Apple Vision Pro", "facts": ["Released in February 2024 at $3,499, with an external battery pack on a cable rated for about two hours.", "'EyeSight' shows a rendering of the wearer's eyes on an outward-facing screen.", "Apple calls it 'spatial computing' and its App Store rules tell developers not to describe apps as AR, VR, XR, or MR.", "Reports during 2024 said Apple had cut production; analysts' first-year sales estimates were well under a million units.", "Weighs 600-650 g, before the battery."], "a": {"item_id": "2c1f7e589f71", "text": "早高峰戴Vision Pro的哥们，我懂他。三万五买的，在家戴没人看见，只有地铁上观众够多。\n\n他图啥？图\"空间计算\"。结果一开机，透视模式：花三万五，看十号线，还带点延迟。\n\n更惨的是手势。人家在虚拟世界里优雅地捏一下就点开了，现实里就是对着空气一直捏，全车人以为他在抓蚊子。\n\n还有那根电池线，从脑袋连到裤兜。大爷一看：哟，新款拉环。一拽，当场表演\"摘头\"。\n\n最惨的是前面那块屏，专门显示他的眼睛，AI合成的，深情款款。他本人在看十号线，他的假眼睛在看你。你说他是在体验未来，还是在给未来当群演？"}, "b": {"item_id": "af7b2649d37f", "text": "地铁上最好认的，就是戴Vision Pro的。三万块顶在脑门上，一根线连着裤兜里的电池，像刚从医院偷跑出来、还没拔输液管的病人。他图什么？图“空间计算”。人家早高峰挤成肉饼，他调出个月球环境。兄弟，你人在月球，屁股在十号线。最绝的是手势：眼睛一盯，两根手指一捏，旁边大姐以为他要掐她，回手就是一巴掌。外面那块屏还给你显示一双假眼睛，冲你深情地眨。下场呢？电池撑两小时，他坐过了站，脖子压出颈椎病。三万块买来的，是一种让全车厢都看得见你在独处的方式。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0858", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "las-vegas", "target_name": "拉斯维加斯", "facts": ["Nevada legalized casino gambling in 1931, the year construction of the Hoover Dam began nearby.", "Most of the Strip lies not in the city of Las Vegas but in the unincorporated town of Paradise.", "The Sphere, opened in 2023 at a cost of about $2.3 billion, is the largest spherical building in the world.", "'What happens here, stays here' was launched by the local convention and visitors authority in 2003.", "Lake Mead, its water source, fell in July 2022 to about 1,040 feet, its lowest since the reservoir first filled in 1937, and dropped below that mark again in August 2026."], "a": {"item_id": "9a99488eb663", "text": "去拉斯维加斯发财的人，我见过三种。第一种攒了半年假，信用卡一刷就来了，说今晚要翻身。第二种失业在家，把老婆的金项链拿去当铺，换了三千块，说这是最后的本钱。第三种最有意思，他不怎么玩，就盯着赌场大厅发呆。我问他看什么，他说这里没窗户、没钟，不知道外面是白天还是黑夜，所以觉得随时还能再来一把。结果呢？第一种回家路上只剩一张十九块九的自助餐券。第二种在一百一十度的沙漠太阳底下，被当铺老板问要不要把婚戒也估个价。第三种，赌场免费送的酒他喝到第五杯，才发现自己在这儿住了三天。拉斯维加斯真大方，免费的酒管够，唯独不给你看表。"}, "b": {"item_id": "dd338752f2cb", "text": "我有个朋友，看了三遍《决胜21点》，就觉得自己是MIT天才，揣着年终奖飞拉斯维加斯，说去“考察”。\n\n兄弟，你连工资条都算不明白，你去算牌？\n\n你也不想想，那是沙漠里凭空盖的一座城：没河、没矿、没工厂，贝拉吉奥的喷泉一天到晚往天上滋，钱哪来的？就是你们这种人！那每一滴水，都是“考察团”的眼泪。\n\n赌场里没钟没窗，他说好啊，能专心。专心到第三天，前台主动给他升了套房。他还挺骄傲，说这是VIP待遇。\n\n不是，那是人家算过了，你值这个价。\n\n回来他跟我说：“在拉斯维加斯发生的，就留在拉斯维加斯。”\n\n我说对，你的钱也是。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0859", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "vision-pro", "target_name": "Apple Vision Pro", "facts": ["Released in February 2024 at $3,499, with an external battery pack on a cable rated for about two hours.", "'EyeSight' shows a rendering of the wearer's eyes on an outward-facing screen.", "Apple calls it 'spatial computing' and its App Store rules tell developers not to describe apps as AR, VR, XR, or MR.", "Reports during 2024 said Apple had cut production; analysts' first-year sales estimates were well under a million units.", "Weighs 600-650 g, before the battery."], "a": {"item_id": "c894b12374a0", "text": "地铁上戴 Vision Pro 的人，我研究过了：花了两万九，最大的需求是让全车人知道。\n\n这玩意儿最大的卖点，是把外面的世界透传给你看。三万块，让你看见了你本来就在的这节车厢。我拿肉眼看，免费，还没延迟。\n\n他还得在空气里捏手指，一会儿放大，一会儿点击。旁边大妈以为他在捉苍蝇，再不然就是在隔空给她算命。\n\n最惨的是，前面那块屏幕专门显示他的眼睛，好让别人觉得他\"在场\"。可全车人透过那双假眼睛，看到的是一个被电池线拴在裤兜里、一动不敢动的人。\n\n电量撑两小时。他还没到站，已经先关机了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "6311e32fb855", "text": "地铁上见过一哥们，戴着Vision Pro。三万块钱扣在脸上，跟把首付戴出门了似的。你图啥？显得有钱？有钱人不坐地铁啊兄弟！\n\n他还挺投入，对着空气捏手指，捏一下，旁边大姐退一步。人家以为他在掐诀，准备做法。\n\n最绝的是前面那块屏，会给你看他的“眼睛”。他一进沉浸模式看电影，那双眼睛就变成一团彩色雾，全车厢都知道：这人灵魂出窍了，肉身暂存二号线。\n\n地铁一刹车，画面飘了，他伸手去抓虚拟窗口，攥住了一个大爷的保温杯。\n\n然后电池没电了，线还从脸上一路连到裤兜里，跟刚做完手术没拔管子似的。续航两小时，刚好够他坐过站。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0860", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hsq", "target_name": "HSQ 电子束光刻胶", "facts": ["Hydrogen silsesquioxane, sold by Dow Corning as FOx ('flowable oxide') and designed as a spin-on dielectric for chip interconnects; lithographers adopted it as a negative-tone resist.", "Exposure converts its cage structure into a cross-linked, silica-like network, so the developed pattern is essentially SiO2.", "Holds the resolution records for electron-beam lithography: isolated features around 5 nm, dense lines below 10 nm half-pitch, with high-contrast 'salty' NaOH/NaCl development.", "Must be kept refrigerated (Dow Corning's datasheet gives a six-month shelf life at 5 °C); the solution ages into a jelly, and its effective sensitivity shifts with processing delays because cross-linking continues after exposure.", "DuPont, which inherited Dow Corning's FOx/XR-1541 HSQ line, discontinued it and ended production at the end of 2025; labs such as EPFL's CMi switched to third-party HSQ from EM Resist or DisChem, both formulated from AQM powder."], "a": {"item_id": "53375ff05c30", "text": "做纳米加工的博士生都有一个信仰：PMMA 分辨率不够，换 HSQ。换完你才发现，这不是换胶，是领养了一个早产儿。\n\n它得在冰箱里供着，用之前先回温，像伺候祖宗。旋完胶它就开始老化，你俩的关系是\"每天都不一样\"。显影用 25% 的 TMAH，有人为了对比度还往里加盐。头一回听说做精密加工往显影液里放盐的，厨子都没这么讲究。\n\n灵敏度还低，电子束机排队三个星期，一个小图案写一整晚。好不容易写出 8 纳米的线，一显影，塌了，粘一块儿，跟煮过头的面一样。\n\n就算没塌，HSQ 曝光完变成二氧化硅，洗不掉，永远留在你的器件上。你图啥？图论文里一张 SEM 图。导师看完说：\"挺好，再做个大面积的。\""}, "b": {"item_id": "a38389497436", "text": "有些人为了分辨率，非要换HSQ。他们是谁？一群研究生，导师一句“线宽得压到十纳米以下”，就抱着一瓶胶去排电子束曝光机。这胶得放冰箱，过期会结胶，开了盖还得赶紧用。图的就是那几根细线，结果线是细了，一显影，线条齐刷刷倒向一边，像一排喝多了的人。更绝的是，曝光过的地方会固化成二氧化硅，等于他们在芯片上刻了一层玻璃，洗都洗不掉。毕业那天他们终于明白：分辨率没提上去，芯片上倒多了一块摘不下来的玻璃。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0861", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "latex", "target_name": "LaTeX", "facts": ["Leslie Lamport wrote LaTeX in the early 1980s and released it in 1984 as a macro layer on Donald Knuth's TeX (1978), which Knuth built because he disliked how the galley proofs of the new edition of his own book looked.", "TeX's version number converges on π (3.141592653 since 2021), and Knuth's TeX bug bounty started at one hexadecimal dollar, $2.56, doubling yearly until frozen at $327.68; he stopped mailing real cheques in 2008 after fraud and now credits accounts at his fictional Bank of San Serriffe, and only 9 of the 275 cheques he sent from 2006 on were ever cashed.", "Its best-known warning is 'Underfull \\hbox (badness 10000)'; overfull lines are reported as 'Overfull \\hbox (Npt too wide)', and figures float to wherever the algorithm decides, not where the author put them.", "A single missing brace can produce an error reported pages away from the mistake, and a document can need three compiler passes to get its references right."], "a": {"item_id": "8f4ce41c713b", "text": "我见过一个人，写两页的部门备忘录，非要用LaTeX。不是不会Word，是觉得Word太俗，他图的是看上去像篇论文。他开Overleaf，写\\documentclass{article}，再\\usepackage{geometry}调页边距，又加个xeCJK调中文，第一次编译就报错，字体找不到，他查了一下午宏包版本。备忘录正文就一句话：周五下午三点开会，请带Q3数据。最后同事收到的PDF标题居中，页眉印着Memo，行距像拿尺子量过。老板回了一句：这个文件我改不了，能发个Word吗？他沉默了一会儿，删掉\\maketitle，把正文复制进了Word。"}, "b": {"item_id": "8f8bc08e77f4", "text": "我们组有个哥们，博士毕业三年，还活在答辩那天。领导让他写两页备忘录，通知周五团建，他打开的是TeXstudio。\n\n图什么？他说：“Computer Modern字体，一看就是发过论文的。”兄弟，团建通知没有审稿人。\n\n开头先写十五行\\usepackage，中文还得装ctex，pdflatex不认，换xelatex。终于编译，报错：“Missing $ inserted”。因为他写了“人均预算$200”，LaTeX以为他要开始推公式了。\n\n改完再编，“经费超支20%，请自理”，逗号后面全没了，百分号把后半句当注释吃了。\n\n周五大家到了，饭店没订。备忘录上地址那栏写着：“详见第??页。”他忘了再编译一遍。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0862", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "luosifen", "target_name": "螺蛳粉", "facts": ["A rice-noodle dish from Liuzhou, Guangxi; the smell comes from pickled bamboo shoots (酸笋), not from the river snails, which only flavour the broth.", "The instant version boomed during the 2020 lockdowns; Liuzhou's government reported packaged luosifen sales of over 10 billion yuan that year.", "Added to China's national intangible cultural heritage list in 2021.", "Liuzhou opened a luosifen industry college in 2020 to train workers for the trade."], "a": {"item_id": "d1bc8bf17584", "text": "合租房里煮螺蛳粉的人，开口第一句永远是：“我开窗了。”你开窗？那是给酸笋开了条逃生通道，它出去之前，先把每个房间都参观一遍。\n\n他们图什么？图一口“闻着臭，吃着香”。这话是说给室友听的，意思是：你们负责闻，我负责香。\n\n而且螺蛳粉还是分期付款。红油先到，腐竹跟上，最后剪开酸笋包压轴登场，全屋观众不许退场。\n\n下场呢？第二天群里问“谁昨晚煮东西了”，没人认。但每个枕头都认。最后你被指认，不是靠监控，是你进电梯，旁边的人下意识看了一眼你的外套。\n\n你吃了十五分钟，穿了三天。"}, "b": {"item_id": "2bf0b387fb70", "text": "合租房里煮螺蛳粉的，基本不是柳州人。柳州人懂分寸，在自己家吃。在合租房里煮的，都是刚入教的新信徒：一买就是一整箱袋装，每回还要额外多撕一包酸笋，说“原装的不够劲”。哥们，原装的已经够把燃气报警器熏醒了！\n\n你问他图什么，他永远就一句：“闻着臭，吃着香。”问题是吃的是你，闻的是我们仨。香你独享，臭我们均摊。这叫什么？这叫螺蛳粉式共享经济。\n\n最惨的还是他自己。吃了半年，鼻子早被酸笋腌入味了，啥也闻不出来。第二天去公司开会，一进门领导就抬头：“谁在会议室吃螺蛳粉？”他还跟着一起东张西望地找。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0863", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "slack", "target_name": "Slack", "facts": ["Spun out in 2013 from the internal chat tool of Tiny Speck's failed game Glitch; founder Stewart Butterfield had earlier spun Flickr out of another failed game.", "The name is a backronym: 'Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge'.", "Salesforce bought it for $27.7 billion in 2021.", "Filed an EU antitrust complaint against Microsoft in 2020 over the bundling of Teams with Office."], "a": {"item_id": "b9535814ba64", "text": "有种老板，花三个月把公司搬到 Slack 上，说要\"扁平化、透明化、告别会议\"。结果建了四十七个频道：#general 没人说话，#random 全是老板自己转的领英鸡汤，#wins 里常年只有一条消息：\"我们建了 #wins\"。\n\n会议确实少了，因为全改成 huddle 了。那个小耳机图标一亮，意思是\"我们在聊，你怎么还不进来\"。\n\n员工回老板消息，只剩一个👀。老板还挺感动：\"看，大家都在关注。\"\n\n最绝的是他舍不得买付费版。九十天前的消息自动隐藏，上季度那个五百万的决定是谁拍的板，没人查得到。\n\n公司最后留下的唯一透明，是那行字：\"升级到专业版，查看更早的消息。\""}, "b": {"item_id": "62d910dd9864", "text": "有些老板觉得，把公司搬进 Slack 就叫数字化管理。他建了一百二十个频道，#general 底下永远是“早上好”和一排表情回应。他的理想是“全员透明”，结果大家学会了：发一句“收到”，状态设成“开会中”，然后去另一个频道划水。他半夜十一点在 #项目A-最终版-真的最终版 里@channel，回应他的只有三个🫡，还有一个Huddle邀请，是他自己发的。最后他终于明白：员工不是不看消息，是他把公司做成了一个未读红点比工资还多的地方。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0864", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "microsoft-teams", "target_name": "Microsoft Teams", "facts": ["Launched in 2017, bundled at no extra charge into Office 365, and replaced Skype for Business.", "After Slack's antitrust complaint, Microsoft unbundled Teams from Office in the EU in 2023 and worldwide in 2024; the European Commission still sent a statement of objections in 2024.", "Microsoft reported 320 million monthly active users in 2023.", "Microsoft shut down consumer Skype in May 2025 and sent its users to Teams."], "a": {"item_id": "8605988b84fd", "text": "他们是谁？就是那个拍板\"全公司必须用Teams\"的行政总监。图什么？Office套餐自带，不花一分钱，还能在年终汇报上写\"数字化协同全面落地\"。结果呢？他自己的状态每天自动变成\"离开\"，因为他开会总忘了点\"举手\"；他撤回的消息显示\"此消息已被删除\"，大家早就截图了。后来Teams又崩了一次，IT被骂惨，他被调去当\"Teams使用培训官\"，每天负责回答\"为什么我进不了会议室\"。现在他的签名是：有事@我，别@所有人。"}, "b": {"item_id": "04d98c0d62fd", "text": "我们公司换Teams，拍板的是IT总监老王。为什么选它？不是因为好用，是因为它“白送”，买Office 365附赠的。结果全公司天天在里面找文件：发在聊天里的跑进了OneDrive，发在频道里的又钻进了SharePoint。老王开会说“我共享一下屏幕”，然后整个部门盯着他的桌面看了五分钟。\n\n后来呢？欧盟说微软捆绑销售，Teams得拆开单买了。老王“白送”的东西开始收钱，他自己还天天被标成黄色“离开”，五分钟不动鼠标就算离开。现在他桌上插着个自动晃鼠标的小盒子。全公司最勤奋的那个绿点，是一块在原地抽搐的塑料。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0865", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "google-sheets", "target_name": "Google Sheets", "facts": ["Launched in June 2006 as Google Spreadsheets, built on XL2Web, a product Google acquired from 2Web Technologies.", "A spreadsheet is capped at 10 million cells (raised from 5 million in 2022).", "Functions such as IMPORTHTML, IMPORTXML, and GOOGLEFINANCE pull live web data straight into cells.", "Free with a Google account; files live in Google Drive, and collaborators edit the same sheet simultaneously with coloured cursors.", "In a browser, offline editing works only in Chrome or Edge, with the Google Docs Offline extension installed and offline access switched on while still connected; the Android and iOS apps work offline natively."], "a": {"item_id": "81690243de7c", "text": "每个团队都有这么一个人，说：“不用买Jira，太重了，我拿Google Sheets搞一下就行。”他不是图省钱，是图全公司只有他看得懂第七个标签页。\n\n三个月后，表里十四个标签页，条件格式红黄绿：红是延期，黄是风险，绿是“没人来骂我”。有个标签页叫“勿动”，全组都动过。\n\n直到有天，同事只选了B列，点了“A到Z排序”。从此任务和负责人各过各的，张三在干李四的活，李四在给一个叫“待定”的人打下手。\n\n最后他休年假，全组停摆，因为只有他知道IMPORTRANGE为什么报#REF!。他离职那天，交接文档就一个链接，点开写着“需要访问权限”，申请已自动发送给：他的个人邮箱。"}, "b": {"item_id": "62f3a7b081a2", "text": "干这事的，通常是刚学会\"共享\"按钮的 team lead。他图什么？图一眼看穿所有人，图能少开会。于是他建了个表：三十个标签页，一个叫\"总表\"，一个叫\"总表（最终版）\"，还有一个叫\"别用这个\"。\n\n每人一行，进度列设下拉框，红黄绿条件格式，颜色比过年还热闹。第一周很美。第二周，实习生在第三列手敲了个\"差不多了\"，下拉框直接失效，公式全报错，满屏井字号。第三周，他在批注里@全组，回复只有一句：\"我改的是哪一版？\"\n\n最后他关掉表格，开了个会。会上他说：\"我们需要一个更好的协作工具。\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0866", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "macau", "target_name": "澳门", "facts": ["A Portuguese settlement from the 1550s until the handover to China in December 1999.", "Casino revenue passed the Las Vegas Strip's in 2006, and at its 2013 peak, about $45 billion, was roughly seven times the Strip's.", "One company, STDM, held the casino monopoly for four decades until licences opened to competition in 2002.", "Among the most densely populated places on Earth, at roughly 20,000 people per square kilometre.", "The Cotai Strip stands on land reclaimed from the sea between the islands of Taipa and Coloane."], "a": {"item_id": "09aa41e46e5f", "text": "有种人我太熟了：上个月在澳门输了两万，发誓\"这次必须翻本\"。他怎么去的？早上七点坐大巴过拱北，在威尼斯人门口排队换筹码，图的就是那两万块能\"回家\"。结果他在百家乐桌前坐了一下午，翻本的钱全输给了赌场的冷气。傍晚他坐在大三巴牌坊底下啃葡挞，跟鸽子商量下个月的房租。你说他傻吗？不傻，他还算了账，每一笔都算得很认真。问题是，账算得再认真，庄家也是按规矩赢的。"}, "b": {"item_id": "b300422ef6ef", "text": "我有个朋友，在家打麻将输了八千，说要去澳门翻本。这就像在小区泳池呛了口水，决定去太平洋练练。\n\n去的时候坐赌场免费大巴，人家专车接你进去，因为知道出来那趟，你得自己顺着港珠澳大桥走回去。\n\n三天后我在大三巴见到他，对着那面墙拍照。我说你拍它干嘛？他说：“亲切。教堂一把火烧光了，就剩个门脸撑着，跟我现在一模一样。”\n\n最后他在葡京边上的当铺把手表当了，买了一盒葡挞回家。他妈问输了多少，他说：“没输，我去吃葡挞了。一盒六个，四万一个。”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0867", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "windows-8", "target_name": "Windows 8", "facts": ["Released in October 2012; removed the Start button and booted into a full-screen grid of tiles designed for touch screens most PCs did not have.", "The 8.1 update in 2013 put a Start button back, Windows 10 (2015) restored the Start menu, and Microsoft skipped the name Windows 9 altogether.", "Microsoft dropped the 'Metro' name in August 2012 after reports that German retailer Metro AG had objected on trademark grounds; Microsoft called 'Metro' a code name and denied any litigation, and the style was afterwards known informally as 'Modern UI'.", "The 'Charms bar' hid Search, Share, and Settings behind an invisible swipe from the right edge.", "Windows RT, the ARM edition launched alongside it, could not run ordinary Windows programs; Microsoft wrote down $900 million of unsold Surface RT tablets in 2013."], "a": {"item_id": "f01853167432", "text": "你们这种人我见过：机箱插着显示器，鼠标线拖到桌子底下，就为了装 Windows 8。图什么？图那个开始屏幕，满屏方块，天气、股票、新闻全贴在墙上，像把手机桌面硬塞进显示器。结果呢？鼠标得滑到右上角，等一个叫\"超级按钮\"的东西弹出来，左下角的开始按钮没了。你找了半小时，按下 Win 键，跳出一整面磁贴，\"邮件\"那块还在显示三个月前的垃圾广告。最后你上网搜\"怎么把开始菜单还给我\"，第一条推荐的是收费软件。"}, "b": {"item_id": "4dccc16b3e4c", "text": "我表哥就是那种人。2012年他给我家台式机装了Windows 8，理由就一句：“新的肯定好。”好什么？这系统是给平板设计的，满屏方块，你拿鼠标去戳，像拿筷子按电梯。开始按钮没了，他说不需要：“鼠标往右上角一甩，侧边栏就出来了。”我爸用了十年XP，现在为了关个机，得拿鼠标在屏幕边上来回招魂。最后我爸的关机方式改成了拔插头。表哥现在每周日被叫回来，不是吃饭，是找“我的电脑”去哪儿了。他还嘴硬：“等8.1就好了。”8.1来了，开始按钮真回来了，一点开，还是那屏方块。微软都认错了，他还没认。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0868", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "esperanto", "target_name": "世界语", "facts": ["Published in Warsaw in 1887 by L. L. Zamenhof, a Białystok-born eye doctor, under the pen name Doktoro Esperanto, 'one who hopes'.", "Speaker estimates range from 100,000 to two million; roughly a thousand people grow up with it as a native language.", "William Shatner starred in Incubus (1966), a horror film shot entirely in Esperanto, with pronunciation Esperantists still mock.", "Its Wikipedia has over 350,000 articles, and its Duolingo course for English speakers had 1.36 million learners by July 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "3187bfd3bfb3", "text": "学世界语的人，我研究过，理想都很大：世界和平。方法也很简单：先让全世界都学世界语。目前进度，据说有一百万人会，而且这一百万人……基本互相认识。\n\n别人学外语是为了认识外国人，他们学是为了去参加世界语大会。四十个国家的人坐一屋，用一门没有任何国家说的语言，聊同一个话题：世界语为什么还没火。\n\n最离谱的是，1907年有人嫌它不够完美，搞了个改良版叫伊多语，成功把自己人分成了两派。\n\n人类还没统一呢，先把想统一人类的人给拆了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "c6f136a1221b", "text": "我认识一个学世界语的。他跟我说，世界语只有十六条语法规则，一个月就能学会。没错，他一个月就学会了，然后花了十年找一个能跟他说上话的人。\n\n他图什么？图世界大同。柴门霍夫一八八七年发明它，是想让全人类共用一门第二语言。一百多年过去了，它还真成了所有人的第二语言：第二个“回头再说”的语言。\n\n他现在出国不住酒店，靠“护照服务”睡在别的世界语者家沙发上。俩人见面热泪盈眶，互称“同志”，聊了一宿。聊的是：怎么才能让更多人学世界语。\n\n我问他这辈子算不算失败。他说不算，世界语里没有“失败”，只有mal-成功。“成功”前面加个mal-，意思是成功的反面。\n\n哥们儿，翻译过来，那还是失败啊。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0869", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "stinky-tofu", "target_name": "臭豆腐", "facts": ["Tofu soaked for hours to a day or two in a brine that has itself fermented for months from vegetables such as bamboo shoots and mustard greens, often with dried shrimp or meat; the smell is the brine's, not the bean's.", "Changsha's version is black, deep-fried and doused in chili; Taiwan's night-market version is golden and served with pickled cabbage; Shaoxing's, brined with amaranth stalks, is fried pale yellow or steamed with other 'stinky' pickles.", "Legend credits Wang Zhihe, a Qing-era exam candidate who left his unsold tofu too long in 1669; a Beijing fermented-bean-curd brand still carries his name.", "Street vendors are its main outlet, and the smell carries for a block."], "a": {"item_id": "0bded105eb6e", "text": "街边吃臭豆腐的人，我太懂他们了。他们是谁？三种：刚失恋的，想拍视频的，还有刚跟朋友吵完架、非要证明自己“敢吃”的。图什么？图那句“闻着臭，吃着香”。结果呢？香没吃出来，先吃到一滴红油，啪嗒，掉在白T恤上，像刚被人当街抽了一耳光。他拎着那袋酸菜站在公交站，三米之内没人敢靠近。上车，司机把窗全打开了，全车乘客齐刷刷看他，他举着手机说：“这条视频能火。”到家洗了三遍头，味儿还在。第二天约会，对方捏着鼻子问：“你身上是不是有股……”他说：“臭豆腐，昨天吃的。”对方沉默两秒：“哦，我还以为是你前任留下的。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "30b6f794ee22", "text": "街边吃臭豆腐的人，我观察过，都有点自我感动。排二十分钟队，就为了吃一句广告语：\"闻着臭，吃着香。\"这话翻译过来就是\"我人品不行，但我内在美\"，他们居然信了。\n\n你再看那个姿势：一手端纸碗，一手捏牙签，站在马路牙子上，腰弯九十度，对着一块豆腐鞠躬，生怕辣汤滴到白鞋上。\n\n最惨是吃完以后。你以为臭味留在嘴里？不，它搬进你外套里了。坐地铁，旁边的人默默挪开一个身位，你还觉得是自己今天气场强。\n\n回到家，女朋友一开门：\"你是不是路过了一场火灾？\"你说：\"没有，我吃了点好的。\"她说：\"那你把好的留在门口。\"\n\n人家豆腐是臭卤泡了一个月才臭的，你五分钟就追平了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0870", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "risk", "target_name": "《战国风云》（Risk）", "facts": ["Invented in 1957 by Albert Lamorisse, the French director of The Red Balloon, as La Conquête du Monde; Parker Brothers published it in the United States in 1959.", "The map has 42 territories, among them Kamchatka, Irkutsk, and Yakutsk, while the entire Middle East is one territory.", "Holding Australia, which has a single entry point, is the standard opening strategy.", "A full game can run for hours, and players eliminated early have nothing to do but watch."], "a": {"item_id": "407cba2981c5", "text": "有一种人，非要把《战国风云》玩到底。开局说好两小时吃完饭就散，结果凌晨三点，他还在盯着澳大利亚那块地，因为守它能多拿两个兵。他图什么？不图赢，图的是那句\"我占领了全世界\"。他掷三个六，对面掷两个一，他拍桌子喊\"天命所归\"。最后呢？他手里攥着整个北美，兵力只剩一颗，全世界就剩他和堪察加边上一个不肯投降的阿拉斯加。窗外天亮了，他还在算下一轮能不能凑齐一套骑兵卡。"}, "b": {"item_id": "8c92129c3833", "text": "每个朋友圈里都有一个人，非要把一局《战国风云》打完。你们认识他：开局就缩进澳大利亚，守着暹罗那一个口子，像个在村口摆路障的村长。图什么？图每回合多拿那两个兵。别人在欧洲打成一锅粥，他在大洋洲养精蓄锐，一养就是四个小时。凌晨两点，屋里只剩他和堪察加。他攥着三张卡死等凑成一套换兵，眼神像股民在等解套。最后他赢了，世界是他的了。可赢给谁看？朋友早睡了，女朋友搬走了，房东在敲门。他把乌克兰上那四十个塑料小兵一个个收回盒子。那是他这辈子离统一天下最近的一次，也是最后一次有人喊他来玩桌游。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0871", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "pmma", "target_name": "PMMA 电子束光刻胶", "facts": ["The same polymer as Plexiglas and acrylic glass, dissolved in anisole or chlorobenzene and spun into a thin film.", "The oldest electron-beam resist still in everyday use; IBM researchers reported it as one in the late 1960s.", "Positive tone: the beam breaks polymer chains and the exposed region dissolves in a MIBK:IPA developer.", "Resolves below 10 nm but has poor sensitivity, so doses of hundreds to over a thousand µC/cm² (depending on voltage) make writing slow.", "Poor dry-etch resistance, so it is used mostly for lift-off rather than as an etch mask."], "a": {"item_id": "66f317caaa7a", "text": "干电子束光刻还在用 PMMA 的，基本都是博士生。图什么？图一句“PMMA 分辨率高，业内金标准”。金标准，对，这玩意儿跟你家鱼缸的亚克力、牙医给你做的牙托是一个东西。人家做牙托的，做完下班回家吃饭。咱们呢，拿它刻十纳米，电子枪在里面扫一整夜，剂量大得像给它做理疗，早上进实验室，机器还在写，你俩互相看着，谁也不好意思先走。\n\n好不容易写完了，MIBK 加 IPA 一泡，线条漂亮。结果一刻蚀，胶比我工资涨得还慢，消失得比导师的承诺还快。\n\n师弟问：“师兄，为什么不换个胶？”\n\n我说：“导师说，当年师兄就是用 950K 毕业的。”\n\n师兄毕业了，我还守着一块有机玻璃，等它给我一个答辩。"}, "b": {"item_id": "5266dfb33ccd", "text": "我认识一帮做纳米器件的博士，实验室一台电子束曝光机，几千万，往上涂的是PMMA。说白了就是亚克力，奶茶店吸管盒那个料。几千万的电子枪，天天给鱼缸刻字。\n\n问导师为啥不换，导师说：“分辨率好，十纳米！”好是好，就是灵敏度低，剂量得往死里加。机时按小时收钱，一块片子写一宿，学生睡机房门口，跟排专家号似的。\n\n显影液MIBK兑异丙醇一比三，配方七十年代传下来的，比他家族谱还稳。\n\n剂量加猛了它还交联，正胶变负胶。跟这帮博士一样，熬太久，人就反了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0872", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "php", "target_name": "PHP", "facts": ["Created by Rasmus Lerdorf in 1994 as 'Personal Home Page Tools', a set of C programs for tracking visitors to his own résumé page; the name now officially stands for the recursive 'PHP: Hypertext Preprocessor'.", "Lerdorf, in his 2007 MySQL Conference talk 'PHP on Hormones': 'I'm not a real programmer. I throw together things until it works then I move on.'", "Runs WordPress, which W3Techs counted on 39.4% of all websites (58.1% of sites with a known CMS) on 9 November 2026.", "Core function names are inconsistent (strpos, str_replace, htmlspecialchars, nl2br) partly because, as Lerdorf confirmed, early function names were chosen to balance a hash table keyed on string length.", "Until PHP 8 (2020), the comparison 0 == 'foo' was true because the string was silently converted to a number."], "a": {"item_id": "15733eca0d13", "text": "干PHP外包的，我见过一类人特别有意思。他们写的代码，每个变量前面都挂个$，像给每个变量别了张工牌。strpos的参数是针在前还是大海在前，他们闭着眼都能写对，因为早就不记了，改成背\"查一下手册\"。甲方说\"就改个按钮颜色\"，他们说好，打开一个三千行的文件，全局变量满天飞，一个函数里HTML、SQL、表单炖在一起，像一锅十年没洗过的火锅。他们图什么？图一个月能拿一万五。落得什么下场？三十五岁，简历上写着\"精通PHP\"，面试官问一句PHP 8有什么新特性，他沉默三秒，说：\"我们主要用的是宝塔。\""}, "b": {"item_id": "e21dca174c4d", "text": "我有个朋友，靠写PHP吃饭。每次喝酒他都举杯：“PHP是世界上最好的语言！”全桌没人接话，因为全桌都是写PHP的。大家心里清楚，这是我们这行唯一能赢的一句台词。\n\n他图什么？图快。一个下午，织梦模板一套，宝塔面板一点，县城瓷砖城就有官网了。收三千，老板还嫌贵：“我侄子也会。”\n\n他说他天天跟钱打交道。确实，每个变量前面都挂着个美元符号。干了十年，那是他摸过最多的美元。\n\n现在他维护一套2009年的代码，mysql_query还在跑，他不敢动。一动，瓷砖城倒闭，他也失业。说白了，他跟那段代码是互相续命。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0873", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "chicago-deep-dish", "target_name": "芝加哥深盘披萨", "facts": ["Created at Pizzeria Uno in Chicago in 1943; credit is disputed among founder Ike Sewell, his partner Ric Riccardo, and cook Rudy Malnati.", "Assembled upside down: sliced mozzarella on the dough, then toppings, then crushed tomatoes on top, so the cheese does not scorch during the long bake.", "Jon Stewart called it 'tomato soup in a bread bowl' and 'a casserole' on The Daily Show in 2013.", "Chicagoans' everyday pizza is thin 'tavern-style' cut into squares; GrubHub order data reported by WBEZ in 2013 showed city residents ordering non-deep-dish pizza about 10 to 1 over deep dish, with deep dish and stuffed making up under 9% of orders."], "a": {"item_id": "e091429db8dc", "text": "管芝加哥深盘叫“披萨”的人，主要就两种：芝加哥本地人，和刚在Giordano's门口排了一小时队的游客。\n\n本地人图什么？面子。你们那儿冬天零下二十度，总得有样东西能拿出去跟纽约吵架吧。\n\n游客图什么？沉没成本。排队一小时，再等四十五分钟烤，端上来番茄酱在最上面，奶酪压在底下，四周一圈黄油面墙。你不叫它披萨，就得承认自己花三十八刀排队吃了一锅砂锅。\n\n下场呢？拿刀叉吃“披萨”，一块就饱，剩下的打包，回酒店一躺，消化得比烤得还久。第二天照样发朋友圈：“正宗芝加哥披萨！”配图一看，一个蛋糕模具。"}, "b": {"item_id": "2c14d9c46ccf", "text": "最嘴硬的，是来芝加哥旅游的人。排队一小时，服务员说深盘要烤四十五分钟。你都等到这份上了，端上来什么你都得认它是披萨。结果奶酪在底下，酱在上面，还得用刀叉，这叫披萨？这叫面饼做的番茄肉酱焗饭。吃完一块，人瘫在椅子上，像刚做完开胸手术。可你还是要发朋友圈：“正宗芝加哥披萨！”因为你不能承认，自己花了两百美元，吃了顿砂锅。最讽刺的是本地人，他们平时吃的是切成小方块的薄底。深盘就跟全聚德一样，是留着带外地朋友去的。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0874", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "nuclear-fusion", "target_name": "可控核聚变", "facts": ["Has been 'about 30 years away' since the 1950s; at the 1955 Geneva atoms-for-peace conference, Homi Bhabha predicted fusion power within two decades.", "ITER, agreed in 2006 for first plasma in 2016, re-baselined in 2024 to start research operations in 2034 and deuterium-tritium burning in 2039, at a cost estimated above €20 billion.", "NIF's December 2022 'ignition' shot released 3.15 MJ from 2.05 MJ of laser energy; the lasers drew about 300 MJ from the grid to fire.", "Private fusion companies had raised more than $7 billion by 2024."], "a": {"item_id": "203425c81d14", "text": "\"可控核聚变还有十年\"，这句话我听了三十年，每次都是十年。干这行的是一群想把太阳关进罐子里的人，目标很朴素：让一亿度的等离子体乖乖悬在磁场里，别碰墙，别熄火。图什么？图经费，图论文，图退休前在新闻里看见自己的名字。结果呢？年年开会，年年说\"快了\"，PPT翻到第四十页，二〇三〇悄悄变成了二〇四〇。有个老教授等了一辈子，临退休还在改时间表，他说：\"太阳烧了四十亿年不用拨款，我们想关它一下，先得给罐子要钱。\""}, "b": {"item_id": "a3d17d6a7412", "text": "搞可控核聚变的人，是地球上唯一靠“还有十年”吃了一辈子饭的职业。五十年代说三十年，后来说五十年，现在说十年，你看，人家还在降价。\n\n图啥？图经费。“十年”这个数太精妙了：近到评审愿意批钱，远到项目负责人退休前不用验收。ITER在法国盖了快二十年，预算翻了好几倍。一亿度的等离子体他们说能控住，控不住的是工期。\n\n最后什么下场？老教授退休那天，把“还有十年”郑重交给博士生，跟传家宝似的。博士生一看：我二十五岁入行，原来是接了个祖传手艺。\n\n这炉子烧的不是氘和氚，是一代代人的头发。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0875", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "nuclear-fusion", "target_name": "可控核聚变", "facts": ["Has been 'about 30 years away' since the 1950s; at the 1955 Geneva atoms-for-peace conference, Homi Bhabha predicted fusion power within two decades.", "ITER, agreed in 2006 for first plasma in 2016, re-baselined in 2024 to start research operations in 2034 and deuterium-tritium burning in 2039, at a cost estimated above €20 billion.", "NIF's December 2022 'ignition' shot released 3.15 MJ from 2.05 MJ of laser energy; the lasers drew about 300 MJ from the grid to fire.", "Private fusion companies had raised more than $7 billion by 2024."], "a": {"item_id": "0c1b7366a47b", "text": "搞可控核聚变的那帮人，我真佩服。每次申请经费，他们都拍着胸脯说：再给十年，就能把太阳装进罐子里。这话从上世纪五十年代说到现在，太阳没装进去，罐子倒是越造越大。ITER画图画了三十多年，工期一延再延，预算翻了好几倍，钱烧得比等离子体还热。你问他们图什么？图的是海水里那点氘，图的是一个不冒烟的未来。落得什么下场？当年的博士生，如今成了白头发的教授，还在给学生画同一张饼：就差最后一公里了。这最后一公里，比太阳的寿命还长。"}, "b": {"item_id": "46363a503661", "text": "“可控核聚变还有十年”，这句话是物理学界的传家宝。五十年代爷爷说，八十年代儿子说，现在孙子接着说，一个字都没改，比族谱还准。\n\n你以为他们在预测未来？不，他们在报经费周期。说三年，到期交不了差；说五十年，领导听完直接去开下一个会；说十年，刚好够干到退休，然后把这句话连同办公室钥匙一起交给学生。\n\n最惨的是什么？他们真把等离子体烧到了一亿度，比太阳核心还热好几倍，撑了几十秒，开发布会报喜。可他们自己的职称，在同一个岗位上冻了二十年。\n\n全人类只有这群人能造出一个小太阳，却暖不了自己的工资条。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0876", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "durian", "target_name": "榴莲", "facts": ["Banned on Singapore's MRT trains and in many Southeast Asian hotels because of the smell.", "A 2017 genome study in Nature Genetics traced the odour to a duplicated family of MGL genes that pump out volatile sulfur compounds.", "China buys most of the world's durian exports, chiefly from Thailand, in a trade worth billions of dollars a year.", "Alfred Russel Wallace wrote in 1856 that eating it was 'a new sensation worth a voyage to the East to experience'."], "a": {"item_id": "68573ec1558a", "text": "地铁上吃榴莲的人，我观察过，他们不是饿，是在传教。你一皱眉，他立刻甩出那句经典台词：“闻着臭，吃着香！”大哥，我又不吃，你光给我上半句，这跟只收定金不发货有什么区别？\n\n而且他还爱报价：“猫山王，八十一斤！”意思是这味儿是进口的，你得心怀感激。全车厢被迫消费，每人摊他二十块钱的味儿。\n\n下场呢？他到站下车，以为结束了。不，那味儿会住进他指甲缝里，洗三遍，晚上挠头都是猫山王。第二天同事全问他：“你昨天坐的几号线？”\n\n新加坡地铁明令禁止带榴莲。我建议咱们也补一条：禁止带吃过榴莲的人。"}, "b": {"item_id": "8c929753db61", "text": "在地铁上吃榴莲的人，我一直想知道他图什么。饿吗？榴莲得先撬壳、戴手套、对付一身刺，这不是饿，这是办仪式。他图的是效果：车厢瞬间清出半径三米的圆，他站在圆心，像刚召唤出神兽的法师。\n\n这帮人还有统一台词：\"闻着臭，吃着香。\"兄弟，你是吃着香，我们只分到了前半句。\n\n最惨是下场。吃完了，肉没了，壳还在。地铁里没有榴莲壳专用垃圾桶，他只能抱着那个刺球站五站，表情像抱着自己的罪证。回家后手上的味道三天不散，老婆跟他说话，隔着一张餐桌，还要开窗。\n\n都说榴莲是水果之王，他倒好，吃出了一个王国，人口一个。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0877", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hsq", "target_name": "HSQ 电子束光刻胶", "facts": ["Hydrogen silsesquioxane, sold by Dow Corning as FOx ('flowable oxide') and designed as a spin-on dielectric for chip interconnects; lithographers adopted it as a negative-tone resist.", "Exposure converts its cage structure into a cross-linked, silica-like network, so the developed pattern is essentially SiO2.", "Holds the resolution records for electron-beam lithography: isolated features around 5 nm, dense lines below 10 nm half-pitch, with high-contrast 'salty' NaOH/NaCl development.", "Must be kept refrigerated (Dow Corning's datasheet gives a six-month shelf life at 5 °C); the solution ages into a jelly, and its effective sensitivity shifts with processing delays because cross-linking continues after exposure.", "DuPont, which inherited Dow Corning's FOx/XR-1541 HSQ line, discontinued it and ended production at the end of 2025; labs such as EPFL's CMi switched to third-party HSQ from EM Resist or DisChem, both formulated from AQM powder."], "a": {"item_id": "98b1aee74d1a", "text": "实验室里最危险的人，是那种盯着SEM说“PMMA不够细”的博士生。为了论文里一张“8纳米线宽”的图，他换了HSQ。从此他的人生就变了。\n\n先说钱，一小瓶贵得像茅台，还得冷藏，比他本人过得精致。开封了就开始老化，他每天看那瓶胶的眼神，比看他妈都焦虑。\n\n再说剂量，HSQ灵敏度低，曝一个芯片，电子束要写一整夜。别人约会，他守机台。\n\n显影还得加盐，学长说“盐显影对比度高”，他就真去称氯化钠，跟腌咸菜似的。\n\n最绝的是曝完它变成二氧化硅，洗不掉！想返工？上氢氟酸。\n\n最后图出来了，线是真细，倒了一片。导师一看：“挺好，再做一批。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "53375ff05c30", "text": "做纳米加工的博士生都有一个信仰：PMMA 分辨率不够，换 HSQ。换完你才发现，这不是换胶，是领养了一个早产儿。\n\n它得在冰箱里供着，用之前先回温，像伺候祖宗。旋完胶它就开始老化，你俩的关系是\"每天都不一样\"。显影用 25% 的 TMAH，有人为了对比度还往里加盐。头一回听说做精密加工往显影液里放盐的，厨子都没这么讲究。\n\n灵敏度还低，电子束机排队三个星期，一个小图案写一整晚。好不容易写出 8 纳米的线，一显影，塌了，粘一块儿，跟煮过头的面一样。\n\n就算没塌，HSQ 曝光完变成二氧化硅，洗不掉，永远留在你的器件上。你图啥？图论文里一张 SEM 图。导师看完说：\"挺好，再做个大面积的。\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0878", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "durian", "target_name": "榴莲", "facts": ["Banned on Singapore's MRT trains and in many Southeast Asian hotels because of the smell.", "A 2017 genome study in Nature Genetics traced the odour to a duplicated family of MGL genes that pump out volatile sulfur compounds.", "China buys most of the world's durian exports, chiefly from Thailand, in a trade worth billions of dollars a year.", "Alfred Russel Wallace wrote in 1856 that eating it was 'a new sensation worth a voyage to the East to experience'."], "a": {"item_id": "c1203c00cfe6", "text": "地铁上吃榴莲的人，我真的服。那味儿一进车厢，我以为哪个下水道盖子被掀了。更绝的是他还闭着眼，嘴角往上翘，一副在拍广告的样子。他图什么？图个\"我很会生活\"？行，你会生活，我们陪你一起窒息。下场也很精彩：全车人同时把口罩往上提，他下车时手里拎着带刺的壳，座位上留了一圈黏糊糊的黄纤维。第二天，他身上的味道洗三遍还在，同事开会时都默默把窗户打开了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "8c929753db61", "text": "在地铁上吃榴莲的人，我一直想知道他图什么。饿吗？榴莲得先撬壳、戴手套、对付一身刺，这不是饿，这是办仪式。他图的是效果：车厢瞬间清出半径三米的圆，他站在圆心，像刚召唤出神兽的法师。\n\n这帮人还有统一台词：\"闻着臭，吃着香。\"兄弟，你是吃着香，我们只分到了前半句。\n\n最惨是下场。吃完了，肉没了，壳还在。地铁里没有榴莲壳专用垃圾桶，他只能抱着那个刺球站五站，表情像抱着自己的罪证。回家后手上的味道三天不散，老婆跟他说话，隔着一张餐桌，还要开窗。\n\n都说榴莲是水果之王，他倒好，吃出了一个王国，人口一个。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0879", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "slack", "target_name": "Slack", "facts": ["Spun out in 2013 from the internal chat tool of Tiny Speck's failed game Glitch; founder Stewart Butterfield had earlier spun Flickr out of another failed game.", "The name is a backronym: 'Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge'.", "Salesforce bought it for $27.7 billion in 2021.", "Filed an EU antitrust complaint against Microsoft in 2020 over the bundling of Teams with Office."], "a": {"item_id": "99cfffb47c21", "text": "有些老板非要用Slack管公司。开会太累，他说以后都在#general里说。结果一天九百条消息，他想每条都看，看到第三天放弃，给所有消息点了个小黄脸。他觉得这叫留痕，于是逢事必@channel，全公司手机同时震动，财务一边对账一边回了个\"收到\"的表情。频道越建越多：#项目A、#项目A-最终版、#项目A-真的最终版。员工每天花一小时在搜索框里找文件，找到的都是三个月前的讨论串，串里四十七条回复，最后一条是老板自己发的：\"这个先放放。\""}, "b": {"item_id": "b9535814ba64", "text": "有种老板，花三个月把公司搬到 Slack 上，说要\"扁平化、透明化、告别会议\"。结果建了四十七个频道：#general 没人说话，#random 全是老板自己转的领英鸡汤，#wins 里常年只有一条消息：\"我们建了 #wins\"。\n\n会议确实少了，因为全改成 huddle 了。那个小耳机图标一亮，意思是\"我们在聊，你怎么还不进来\"。\n\n员工回老板消息，只剩一个👀。老板还挺感动：\"看，大家都在关注。\"\n\n最绝的是他舍不得买付费版。九十天前的消息自动隐藏，上季度那个五百万的决定是谁拍的板，没人查得到。\n\n公司最后留下的唯一透明，是那行字：\"升级到专业版，查看更早的消息。\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0880", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "nfts", "target_name": "NFT", "facts": ["Beeple's 'Everydays: The First 5000 Days' sold at Christie's for $69.3 million in March 2021.", "OpenSea, the largest NFT marketplace, processed a record $2.7 billion in trades on 1 May 2022 and just $9.34 million on 28 August 2022, a 99% drop, according to DappRadar data.", "A 2023 dappGambl study found that 95% of the NFT collections it examined had a market value of zero.", "The token usually records only a URL; the image itself sits on an ordinary server or IPFS, not on the blockchain.", "Collins Dictionary named 'NFT' its word of the year for 2021."], "a": {"item_id": "6da2286dc224", "text": "2021年买NFT那帮兄弟，平时买杯奶茶都要比三家团购，结果花八万块，买了一张猴子图的“所有权”。注意，不是图，是所有权。图谁都能右键另存为，你那张跟我存的那张一个像素都不差，区别是你那张会心疼。\n\n图什么？图头像。八万块设成微信头像，开口先让人看猴。天天在Discord里喊“WAGMI”，我们都会成功。后来确实一起了，地板价从八万掉到八百。\n\n最惨的是光Gas费就交了两千，比猴现在还贵。问他猴呢？在钱包里。钱包呢？助记词抄在纸上。纸呢？被他妈当废纸卖了。全家最懂Web3的，是收废品的大爷。"}, "b": {"item_id": "7d289cdaf821", "text": "2021年买NFT的哥们，我特别理解。他们不是想买画，是想买一张\"我比你早知道\"的门票。\n\n我有个朋友，花了几十万买了只戴水手帽的猴子。我说：\"这图我右键就能存啊。\"他很严肃地说：\"你存的是图，我拥有的是……\"他想了三秒，\"……一个指向图的链接。\"兄弟，你花几十万买了个超链接。\n\n买完他就入教了。头像换成猴子，天天在Discord里跟一个叫\"猿猴老王\"的陌生人互道\"gm\"，互相喊\"WAGMI\"，意思是我们都会发财。\n\n现在地板价跌了九成多，他想挂单卖，结果上架的gas费比成交价还贵。\n\n问他怎么办，他说：\"我是长期持有。\"\n\n不，你不是长期持有，你是没人接盘。你唯一的资产，是那个Discord群里还没退群的三个人。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0881", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "nfts", "target_name": "NFT", "facts": ["Beeple's 'Everydays: The First 5000 Days' sold at Christie's for $69.3 million in March 2021.", "OpenSea, the largest NFT marketplace, processed a record $2.7 billion in trades on 1 May 2022 and just $9.34 million on 28 August 2022, a 99% drop, according to DappRadar data.", "A 2023 dappGambl study found that 95% of the NFT collections it examined had a market value of zero.", "The token usually records only a URL; the image itself sits on an ordinary server or IPFS, not on the blockchain.", "Collins Dictionary named 'NFT' its word of the year for 2021."], "a": {"item_id": "a8cfb67f2de5", "text": "2021年买NFT的人，我身边就有一个。以前连拼多多砍一刀都嫌麻烦，那年突然跟我说：我是数字艺术收藏家。我问收藏的啥，他给我看一只猴子，戴着墨镜，一脸没睡醒。十八万。\n\n我说这图我右键另存为就有了。他说：你那叫复制，我这叫所有权。我说所有权在哪？他说在区块链上。我说那你家里挂的是啥？他说：我挂在Twitter头像上。\n\n十八万，买个头像框。还得天天在群里跟一群头像也是猴子的陌生人说\"gm\"，互道早安。\n\n现在地板价跌了九成，他每天刷OpenSea，那只猴子挂出去半年，连个问价的都没有。他说他是钻石手，死拿不卖。\n\n兄弟，你不是钻石手，你是那只猴子唯一的粉丝。"}, "b": {"item_id": "812b8f18c10a", "text": "2021年买NFT的那批人，我太懂了。花九千块买一只无聊猴子的截图，换成头像，每天早上第一件事是看地板价。朋友问他买的啥，他说这是资产。问资产在哪，他指着手机说：在链上。我说那你右键另存为一下，他说那叫盗版，法律不允许。转一次账，手续费比猴子还贵，他还觉得这叫门槛。后来项目方拿着钱跑了，猴子还在，官网打不开了，他的头像成了全网最贵的一张\"此页面无法访问\"。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0882", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hydrogen-cars", "target_name": "氢燃料电池汽车", "facts": ["Toyota launched the Mirai in 2014; 'mirai' means 'future' in Japanese.", "Nearly all US hydrogen filling stations are in California; Shell closed all seven of its California passenger-car stations in February 2024.", "More than 95% of the world's hydrogen is made from fossil fuels, mostly by steam-reforming natural gas.", "California pump prices reached about $36 per kilogram in 2023-24, over $200 for a Mirai fill; Toyota has handed buyers fuel cards worth $15,000.", "Mirai owners filed a class action against Toyota in July 2024 over hydrogen shortages and station outages; a second suit in 2025 sought at least $5.7 billion."], "a": {"item_id": "feb6bb90ad06", "text": "我有个朋友买了辆氢燃料电池车，逢人就说：“电动车充电一小时，我加氢只要三分钟。”对，三分钟，前提是你先开两百公里找到加氢站。全市就那么一座，还是给公交车队修的，他得排在十二辆大巴后面，等储罐压力回上来。人家加的是氢，他加的是耐心。\n\n他还爱说：“我这车唯一的排放是水。”是，排放是水，他眼里也是水。一公斤氢七十块，跑一百公里比油车还贵。\n\n最惨的是去年那座站关了。车现在停在楼下，他每天下去看一眼。他说他买的是未来。没错，未来还没到，二手车商的报价先到了：按废铁算，高压氢罐另收拆除费。"}, "b": {"item_id": "6147a8dd376d", "text": "买氢燃料电池车的人，我研究过，就两种：一种是环保人士，一种是被送了三年氢气卡的。\n\n环保人士图什么？图尾管只排水。可那氢多半是拿天然气炼的，他花四十块一公斤，绕一大圈烧天然气，最后从屁股后面滴出一杯水，像在表演“我很干净”。\n\n下场更精彩：全城三个加氢站，一个周二检修，一个在郊区，一个前面排着三辆公交。他的日常就是刷加氢站App，像抢演唱会门票。抢到了，还得等储氢罐压力回上来。\n\n最后氢气卡到期那天，车一秒贬值一半。他挂二手车，文案写得特别诚恳：“车况良好，自带情怀，不含氢气。”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0883", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "comic-sans", "target_name": "Comic Sans 字体", "facts": ["Designed by Vincent Connare at Microsoft in 1994, modeled on the comic-book lettering in The Dark Knight Returns and Watchmen.", "Made for the speech bubbles of Rover, the cartoon dog in Microsoft Bob; it missed Bob's release and shipped instead in the Windows 95 Plus! pack.", "CERN's July 2012 Higgs boson discovery announcement was presented on slides set in it.", "The 'Ban Comic Sans' campaign, started by designers Dave and Holly Combs in 1999, ran until 2019, when they retired it and launched usecomicsans.com."], "a": {"item_id": "130c2658ebda", "text": "我们小区物业有个行政大姐，发正式通知特别爱用Comic Sans。你知道这字体当年是干嘛的吗？微软设计给卡通小狗说话用的，专门往对话气泡里塞的。所以每回停水通知一贴，我都觉得是一只小狗在跟我说：“明天停水哦～”\n\n她图什么？图亲切。坏消息配这字体，就跟打针前先给你看张奥特曼贴纸一样。\n\n上个月涨物业费，她又用Comic Sans，那个“8”圆得像在冲你眨眼。全楼没一个当真，都以为是谁家小孩的恶作剧，撕下来折纸飞机了。年底一查，欠费率百分之八十。\n\n领导问她怎么回事，她理直气壮：“欧洲核子研究中心宣布发现希格斯玻色子都用这字体！”\n\n领导说：“对，所以全世界到现在还在笑他们。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "3c2c6cba7366", "text": "每个公司都有这么一位行政，觉得通知太严肃，大家会有压力，于是打开Word，把字体改成Comic Sans。图什么？图亲切，图“有温度”。\n\n结果上周公司发通知：“因业务调整，将优化百分之三十人员。”Comic Sans。全办公室第一反应：哟，团建？还有人回了句“收到，开心”。\n\n你知道这字体当初是给谁做的吗？一只卡通狗，专门给它的对话气泡用的。你拿一只狗的字体宣布裁员，狗看了都觉得不妥。\n\n最后，这位行政自己也在名单里。他去找HR，说能不能给我开个正式点的离职证明，宋体的。HR说：不用，已经打好了，Comic Sans，右下角还有个笑脸。\n\n他说：这笑脸不是我加的。\n\nHR说：对，是你上次培训时教我的。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0884", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "oat-milk", "target_name": "燕麦奶", "facts": ["Oatly started in Sweden in the 1990s, built on Lund University research by Rickard Öste on lactose intolerance.", "Oatly's slogan 'It's like milk but made for humans' was banned in Sweden in 2015 after the dairy lobby LRF Mjölk sued; Oatly then published the lawsuit as advertising.", "Oatly listed on Nasdaq in May 2021 at a valuation near $10 billion; the shares later lost more than 90% of their value.", "Commercial oat milk is made by breaking oat starch into maltose with amylase enzymes, and many brands, Oatly among them, add rapeseed oil; the result has more sugar and a higher glycaemic index than almond or soy milk."], "a": {"item_id": "f5f4ee009b3f", "text": "每个咖啡店都有这么一位，开口就是：\"拿铁，换燕麦奶。\"语气像在宣布退出某个组织。你问为什么，他说乳糖不耐。兄弟，你手边那块芝士蛋糕是自己长出来的吗？你这乳糖不耐很精准，只对咖啡里的牛奶不耐。\n\n再问，他说为了健康。燕麦奶，燕麦煮开后淀粉变成糖，热量比牛奶还高。所以他多花五块钱，多摄入一点糖，喝的是一杯稀释过的麦片粥汤，然后发朋友圈：\"今天也是植物基的一天。\"\n\n下场呢？咖啡一入口，满嘴湿麦片味，像早饭的碗没洗干净，被人顺手拿来接了咖啡。他还硬喝完，边喝边说：\"还挺顺滑的。\"\n\n对，是顺滑，因为你正在喝粥。"}, "b": {"item_id": "794cf9f48948", "text": "有一种人，点拿铁非要加一句“换燕麦奶”。你问为什么，他说“对身体好”。好在哪？他说不上来，但语气笃定，像刚发现了新大陆。\n\n这杯燕麦奶拿铁，奶泡两分钟就塌成一层白沫浮在上面。喝到最后，杯底是一层灰褐色的东西，像把麦片泡在水里忘了捞。\n\n每杯多收五块五，一个月下来一百六十多。他管这叫“投资健康”。\n\n体检报告出来，医生看着他说：“您血糖有点高。”他愣住：“我没加糖啊。”医生说：“燕麦奶本身就带甜，您那杯还加了两泵糖浆。”\n\n他沉默了一会儿，说：“我就是为了不喝糖，才换的燕麦奶。”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0885", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "vim", "target_name": "Vim", "facts": ["Released by Bram Moolenaar in November 1991 as 'Vi IMitation', a vi clone he had begun writing for the Amiga in 1988 on top of Stevie; the expansion became 'Vi IMproved' with version 2.0 in December 1993.", "Licensed as charityware: the licence asks users to donate to ICCF Holland, a charity Moolenaar ran for children in Uganda.", "Stack Overflow's question 'How to exit the Vim editor?' passed one million visits in May 2017, which Stack Overflow marked with a blog post.", "Modal editing: in normal mode, typing letters runs commands rather than inserting text; :wq saves and quits, :q! quits and discards changes."], "a": {"item_id": "ca805bf868dc", "text": "我们组有个哥们儿用Vim。你怎么知道的？你不用知道，他会告诉你。开会共享屏幕，需求还没讲，先把鼠标往旁边一推：“我不用这个。”大哥，你推的是公司资产。\n\n他的.vimrc八百行，四十个插件：文件树、自动补全、调试器。我一看，这不就是VS Code吗？他说不一样，这是他亲手配的。哦，花三个月，手工拼了个免费软件。\n\n上礼拜他在服务器上改配置，突然不动了。全组盯着他，他说“我在思考”。思考了五分钟。后来才知道，那台机器上没有他的.vimrc。他进去了，出不来。\n\n现在他一紧张就按Esc。不是习惯，是创伤。"}, "b": {"item_id": "025ba8683b8f", "text": "公司里总有一个用 Vim 的人，你不用问，他自己会说。入职第一天就官宣：\"我不用鼠标。\"没人问啊，我问的是厕所在哪。\n\n他图什么？图你从背后路过，看他光标嗖嗖飞三屏，你\"哇\"一声。就这一声，够他吃一个月。\n\n下场呢？.vimrc 调了六百行，花了三个周末，省下的时间还没调的时间多。最惨是帮你改代码，你电脑上开的是 VS Code，他自信满满先按 Esc，再敲 :wq，保存退出，一气呵成。结果文档里多出一行 :wq，像他的墓志铭。\n\n他还不认，说：\"你这编辑器，不行。\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0886", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hawaiian-pizza", "target_name": "夏威夷披萨", "facts": ["Invented in 1962 by Sam Panopoulos, a Greek immigrant, at the Satellite Restaurant in Chatham, Ontario, Canada; named after the brand of canned pineapple he used.", "Iceland's president Guðni Th. Jóhannesson said in 2017 that he would ban pineapple on pizza if he could, then clarified that a president has no such power.", "Hawaii is no longer a major pineapple producer: Del Monte shut its Hawaiian pineapple operations in November 2006, two years ahead of its announced 2008 exit, the state's last cannery closed in 2007, and most canned pineapple now comes from Thailand, Indonesia, and the Philippines.", "Panopoulos, whose restaurant also served Chinese dishes, told the Toronto Sun in 2010 that 'in those days people didn't mix the tastes like sweet and sour, except for Chinese food'."], "a": {"item_id": "0878d1084313", "text": "每桌都有那么一个人，菜单一翻就说：“来个夏威夷的。”全桌瞬间安静，像有人宣布要在群里发起投票。\n\n他图什么？图人设。他想让你知道他不随大流，可他点的是每家店菜单上都有的第三个披萨。他心里还有本账：菠萝算水果，火腿算肉，一块下去，营养均衡。\n\n下场呢？别人点的都见底了，只剩他那盘，菠萝汁把饼底泡软，拎起来像拎一条热毛巾。他还硬撑：“真的好吃，你们不懂。”\n\n你知道夏威夷披萨是谁发明的吗？一个住在加拿大的希腊人。夏威夷不认，意大利不承认，全世界最后就剩你一个人替它出头。"}, "b": {"item_id": "6ec4abe781be", "text": "点夏威夷披萨的人，骨子里都想当和事佬：甜的也要，咸的也要，菠萝火腿往饼上一铺，谁都不得罪。结果得罪了全世界。意大利人想报警，夏威夷人也想报警，因为这玩意儿是加拿大人发明的，跟夏威夷唯一的关系就是一听菠萝罐头。\n\n最惨是聚餐。八个人点四盒，他非要加一盒夏威夷的：“你们试试，真的好吃！”最后另外三盒全空了，就剩他那盒，八块，一块没动。他一个人坐那儿一块一块往下吃，吃到最后菠萝凉了，汁儿全渗进饼底，软塌塌的。跟他在这个饭局上的地位，一模一样。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0887", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "beanie-babies", "target_name": "Beanie Babies 豆豆娃", "facts": ["Launched by Ty Inc. in 1993 and sold only through small gift shops, with designs 'retired' to manufacture scarcity.", "At the height of the craze Beanie Babies accounted for about 10% of eBay's sales, and eBay's 1998 IPO filing said the site at times had over 30,000 Beanie Baby auctions running simultaneously in the quarter ending June 1998.", "In 1999 a divorcing couple divided their Beanie Baby collection one by one on a courtroom floor, in a photograph that ran worldwide.", "In 1999 Ty announced that Beanie Babies would be retired at the end of the year, then reversed itself after asking the public to vote and seeing an overwhelming vote to keep them.", "Most 'rare' Beanies that price guides valued in the thousands now sell for a few dollars."], "a": {"item_id": "5b893c7de1a5", "text": "九十年代有一批美国大人，坚信一只塞满塑料颗粒的小熊能供孩子上大学。怎么投资呢？不让孩子碰。孩子想抱一下，爸妈拦住：别摸，一摸就贬值。这是养儿子还是供祖宗？他们还给心形吊牌单独买塑料保护壳，熊可以脏，牌不能折，那张纸才是资产。抢戴安娜王妃熊抢到打架，却没发现厂商一边喊“限量退休”，一边接着生产。你跟印钞的人比谁囤得多？后来有夫妻离婚，在法庭上分豆豆娃，分完发现整整一车，卖了不够付律师费。现在三百只堆在车库，挂网上五美元一只，没人要。孩子没上成大学，但学到一课：在爸爸心里，我排在一只带吊牌的熊后面。"}, "b": {"item_id": "4798ef5c2a37", "text": "九十年代有一批人，真心相信养老金能靠一只塞满塑料粒的熊。他们给豆豆娃的心形吊牌套上塑料保护壳。娃可以脏，吊牌不能折，折了就从“遗产”降级成“玩具”。孩子想抱一下？不行，那是你的大学学费！Ty公司一宣布某款“退休”，他们连夜开车跑三家礼品店，跟抢金条似的。戴安娜王妃一走，他们抢那只紫色王妃熊，觉得王妃没了，熊必涨。结果呢？拉斯维加斯真有对夫妻离婚，分不清这堆熊，法官让俩人坐在法庭地板上，一人一只轮流挑。二十多年过去，那些熊还在阁楼里，吊牌完好，崭新得像从没被爱过。跟他们的回报率一样。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0888", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "monopoly", "target_name": "大富翁（Monopoly）", "facts": ["Descends from Lizzie Magie's The Landlord's Game, patented in 1904 to teach the evils of land monopoly in the spirit of Henry George.", "Charles Darrow sold his version to Parker Brothers in 1935 and was credited as the inventor; Parker bought Magie's patent for $500.", "The official rules require any unbought property to be auctioned, and 'Free Parking' pays nothing; the house rules that skip the auction and pile cash on Free Parking are why games drag.", "During the Second World War, British intelligence had maps, compasses, and real currency hidden in sets sent to prisoner-of-war camps."], "a": {"item_id": "d983ef681d05", "text": "我们家过年必玩大富翁，说是联络感情，其实是全家一起验证：血缘关系，扛不住房地产。\n\n我爸每次抢着当银行，他不是想为大家服务，他是想体验一下贪污。我妈只买橙色那一片，说吉利，结果整局靠收我的租活着。收的时候还特温柔：“妈妈收你的钱，你不会生气吧？”我说不会。她说：“那再加五十。”\n\n我九岁的表弟哭着求我把铁路卖给他，我心一软卖了。三圈以后，他拿着我的铁路，把我送进了监狱。\n\n我走投无路，找我爸借钱，他说：“行，按银行利率。”我说你是我爸！他说：“现在我是银行。”\n\n玩到凌晨，我爸有全部酒店，我妈有全部橙色，我手里只剩一张出狱卡。\n\n全家人公平分配了战果：他们赢了钱，我赢了离开这张桌子的权利。"}, "b": {"item_id": "a7faf659e2b2", "text": "每年过年，总有一个人提议：“一家人玩个大富翁，热闹。”你想热闹？你想看你爸抢着当银行，发钱的时候顺手往自己那堆塞两张五百？你想看你妈踩了你的酒店，说“都是一家人，房租就免了”，轮到你踩她的南京路，她把地契往桌上一拍：“亲兄弟明算账。”你小姨夫拿两条破铁路换你的地，那个眼神，跟当年找你爸借三万块一模一样。玩到凌晨两点没人破产，因为奶奶偷偷往各家塞钱：“孩子进监狱了，快拿钱捞出来。”最后桌子一掀，三家人一年不说话。你图团圆？大富翁教会你一件事：一家人能坐一桌吃饭，是因为桌上没有地契。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0889", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "google-sheets", "target_name": "Google Sheets", "facts": ["Launched in June 2006 as Google Spreadsheets, built on XL2Web, a product Google acquired from 2Web Technologies.", "A spreadsheet is capped at 10 million cells (raised from 5 million in 2022).", "Functions such as IMPORTHTML, IMPORTXML, and GOOGLEFINANCE pull live web data straight into cells.", "Free with a Google account; files live in Google Drive, and collaborators edit the same sheet simultaneously with coloured cursors.", "In a browser, offline editing works only in Chrome or Edge, with the Google Docs Offline extension installed and offline access switched on while still connected; the Android and iOS apps work offline natively."], "a": {"item_id": "2a199d10637d", "text": "我特别佩服那种用Google Sheets管团队的领导。他们的口头禅是：“不用买系统，一个表格就够了。”省了一年三千块软件费，代价是自己变成全公司的人肉服务器。\n\n刚开始就一个标签页，三个月后四十七个，名字叫“总表”“总表2”“总表最终”“总表最终（别动）”。他还觉得自己是工程师，因为他会VLOOKUP。全组只有他会，别人看见公式栏，就像看见祖传药方。\n\n最离谱是开会。他共享屏幕，右上角蹦出个“匿名水豚正在查看”。全场安静，没人知道谁是水豚，只知道水豚刚把C列改成了红色。\n\n他请一天假，团队就瘫痪了，因为没人知道红色是“已完成”，还是“老板生气了”。\n\n后来他离职，交接文档就一句话：“有问题，看版本历史。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "62f3a7b081a2", "text": "干这事的，通常是刚学会\"共享\"按钮的 team lead。他图什么？图一眼看穿所有人，图能少开会。于是他建了个表：三十个标签页，一个叫\"总表\"，一个叫\"总表（最终版）\"，还有一个叫\"别用这个\"。\n\n每人一行，进度列设下拉框，红黄绿条件格式，颜色比过年还热闹。第一周很美。第二周，实习生在第三列手敲了个\"差不多了\"，下拉框直接失效，公式全报错，满屏井字号。第三周，他在批注里@全组，回复只有一句：\"我改的是哪一版？\"\n\n最后他关掉表格，开了个会。会上他说：\"我们需要一个更好的协作工具。\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0890", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "luosifen", "target_name": "螺蛳粉", "facts": ["A rice-noodle dish from Liuzhou, Guangxi; the smell comes from pickled bamboo shoots (酸笋), not from the river snails, which only flavour the broth.", "The instant version boomed during the 2020 lockdowns; Liuzhou's government reported packaged luosifen sales of over 10 billion yuan that year.", "Added to China's national intangible cultural heritage list in 2021.", "Liuzhou opened a luosifen industry college in 2020 to train workers for the trade."], "a": {"item_id": "5c463dbea536", "text": "合租房里煮螺蛳粉的人，我太懂你们了。图什么？一包才八块钱，深更半夜饿了，想吃口有灵魂的。可你们不知道，那袋酸笋一拆开，整层楼都被你\"请\"来吃饭了。门缝底下往外冒的不是蒸汽，是战书。室友从卧室探出头，鼻子一抽，眼神像在看犯罪现场。第二天群里弹出一条消息：\"谁又煮螺蛳粉了？\"你把头埋进碗里，一句话不敢说。最后的下场是：窗户开成了通风道，外套挂三天还带着河沟味，押金一分不剩，房东在合同里加了一条\"禁止煮螺蛳粉\"。"}, "b": {"item_id": "2bf0b387fb70", "text": "合租房里煮螺蛳粉的，基本不是柳州人。柳州人懂分寸，在自己家吃。在合租房里煮的，都是刚入教的新信徒：一买就是一整箱袋装，每回还要额外多撕一包酸笋，说“原装的不够劲”。哥们，原装的已经够把燃气报警器熏醒了！\n\n你问他图什么，他永远就一句：“闻着臭，吃着香。”问题是吃的是你，闻的是我们仨。香你独享，臭我们均摊。这叫什么？这叫螺蛳粉式共享经济。\n\n最惨的还是他自己。吃了半年，鼻子早被酸笋腌入味了，啥也闻不出来。第二天去公司开会，一进门领导就抬头：“谁在会议室吃螺蛳粉？”他还跟着一起东张西望地找。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0891", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "monopoly", "target_name": "大富翁（Monopoly）", "facts": ["Descends from Lizzie Magie's The Landlord's Game, patented in 1904 to teach the evils of land monopoly in the spirit of Henry George.", "Charles Darrow sold his version to Parker Brothers in 1935 and was credited as the inventor; Parker bought Magie's patent for $500.", "The official rules require any unbought property to be auctioned, and 'Free Parking' pays nothing; the house rules that skip the auction and pile cash on Free Parking are why games drag.", "During the Second World War, British intelligence had maps, compasses, and real currency hidden in sets sent to prisoner-of-war camps."], "a": {"item_id": "a8f6340d1edd", "text": "过年一家人想图个团圆，掏出大富翁。这游戏设计得特别科学：专门把亲情换算成租金。\n\n我爸永远当银行，所以他既是央行又是裁判，还偷偷往自己那堆里放钱。我妈见一块买一块，一块不卖，说“留着传给你”，我说妈，这是纸。我弟更狠，四个火车站全买了，我每路过一次就交一次钱，对亲姐姐收费收得比春运还顺手。\n\n我走到他的酒店，一千五。我说我是你姐，他说：“那给你打个折，一千四。”\n\n最后我抵押了公用事业，进了监狱，全家居然都希望我多待几轮，因为我在里面不用交租。\n\n图的是团圆，落得的是游戏还没破产，亲戚关系先清算了。\n\n我一掀桌，我爸很冷静：“重来一局。还是我当银行。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "9bfb2dd2da6e", "text": "每年过年，我家都要玩一局大富翁。说好了增进感情，结果每次都是战争。我舅非要当银行家，抽屉一拉，像在管国库，你借两百块买块地，他能跟你讲半小时利率。我爸图的是赢。他说这叫培养孩子的商业头脑，其实是六十岁了还在抢我十二岁妹妹的绿色地皮。我妈最绝，从不买地，专坐在\"免费停车\"那格收钱，说这叫稳健。最后我姐在紫色街区盖满酒店，我爸一脚踩上去，房租交得他当场抵押了电视机。我舅把银行的钱全押在自己身上，宣布：\"我破产了，但我是清白的。\"那局没人赢，大家都进了监狱。过年那顿饭，全家坐一桌，谁也不说话，桌上摆着一堆假钞。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0892", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "nfts", "target_name": "NFT", "facts": ["Beeple's 'Everydays: The First 5000 Days' sold at Christie's for $69.3 million in March 2021.", "OpenSea, the largest NFT marketplace, processed a record $2.7 billion in trades on 1 May 2022 and just $9.34 million on 28 August 2022, a 99% drop, according to DappRadar data.", "A 2023 dappGambl study found that 95% of the NFT collections it examined had a market value of zero.", "The token usually records only a URL; the image itself sits on an ordinary server or IPFS, not on the blockchain.", "Collins Dictionary named 'NFT' its word of the year for 2021."], "a": {"item_id": "812b8f18c10a", "text": "2021年买NFT的那批人，我太懂了。花九千块买一只无聊猴子的截图，换成头像，每天早上第一件事是看地板价。朋友问他买的啥，他说这是资产。问资产在哪，他指着手机说：在链上。我说那你右键另存为一下，他说那叫盗版，法律不允许。转一次账，手续费比猴子还贵，他还觉得这叫门槛。后来项目方拿着钱跑了，猴子还在，官网打不开了，他的头像成了全网最贵的一张\"此页面无法访问\"。"}, "b": {"item_id": "3a567b5377a2", "text": "2021年买NFT的朋友，我真佩服。花三十万买张猴子图，猴子叼着烟、一脸不想活，这是在提前预演自己的下场。\n\n你跟他说：“我右键另存为了啊。”他急了：“你存的是图，我拥有的是链上的哈希！”大哥，你花三十万，买了一张收据的收据。\n\n那年推特上全是六边形猴子头像，跟加入了邪教似的，天天在Discord里喊“WAGMI，我们都会发财”。项目方路线图写着“元宇宙乐园”，写完人就跑了。\n\n现在地板价跌成零头，当年付的Gas费都比猴子值钱。他还不卖，说这叫“长期持有”。兄弟，你那不叫长期持有，叫卖不掉。猴子叼着烟看着你，终于笑了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0893", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "crocs", "target_name": "Crocs 洞洞鞋", "facts": ["Founded in Boulder, Colorado, in 2002; the first clog debuted at the Fort Lauderdale boat show as a boating shoe.", "Made of 'Croslite', which Crocs describes as a proprietary closed-cell resin foam and which third parties identify as an injection-moulded EVA foam.", "Balenciaga sold a platform Crocs for about $850 in 2018.", "The stock fell more than 90% from its 2007 peak during the 2008 crisis; the company then boomed in the pandemic and gave away 860,000 pairs to healthcare workers in 2020.", "The holes are officially 'ventilation ports' and the charms that go in them are 'Jibbitz', a company Crocs bought in 2006."], "a": {"item_id": "15501877150c", "text": "你们见过那种穿卡骆驰洞洞鞋出门的人吗？他们的理由永远是“舒服”。舒服到什么程度？舒服到能穿着它去相亲，对方低头一看，两排塑料坑，脚趾在里面像泡温泉。他图什么？图不用系鞋带，图能下水、能进厨房、能上超市，等于把拖鞋、雨鞋、运动鞋一起省了。结果呢？去趟公园，脚底的洞灌进三粒沙子，一步一个噗嗤，全场都知道他来了。最后鞋帮上挂满小挂件，走起路来叮叮当当，像个移动的钥匙串，他还在朋友圈写：这才是生活的本质。"}, "b": {"item_id": "061b4ce9b529", "text": "穿洞洞鞋出门的人，我大概知道是谁。嘴上说\"我就下楼拿个快递\"，结果一路走进商场、电影院，还有前任的婚礼。\n\n图什么呢？图舒服。舒服到觉得全世界都该给你的脚松绑。松绑还不够，还得在洞里插一排小鞋花：皮卡丘、小黄瓜、一个不知道哪来的西瓜。三十岁的人，脚上挂得像幼儿园书包。\n\n下场呢？下雨天，那些洞全是进水口，每走一步，脚底\"噗叽\"一声。上电扶梯，软塌塌的鞋帮被一口咬住，保安跑得比你妈都快。\n\n你图的是不用系鞋带的自由，换来的是单脚蹦着，在全商场的注视下，去捡一只挂着小黄瓜的鞋。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0894", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "carbon-nanotubes", "target_name": "碳纳米管", "facts": ["Reported by Sumio Iijima in Nature in 1991.", "Promised as the cable for a space elevator; a 2006 analysis showed that ordinary defects cut the strength of a macroscopic cable far below the ideal value.", "Long, rigid multi-walled nanotubes behaved like asbestos fibres in a 2008 mouse study in Nature Nanotechnology.", "Sorting metallic from semiconducting tubes has blocked nanotube electronics for three decades; MIT's 16-bit RV16X-NANO processor (2019) is still a lab demonstration.", "Today they are sold mainly as a conductive additive in lithium-ion battery electrodes."], "a": {"item_id": "c20ac403ef4f", "text": "你们见过拿碳纳米管融资的人吗？一般是个博士，PPT第一页就是太空电梯：“强度是钢的一百倍，一根线从地面拉到三万六千公里外！”投资人听得热泪盈眶。\n\n我就想问：你实验室里最长的一根有多长？“两厘米。”那你这不叫太空电梯，叫太空牙线。\n\n他图啥？图下一轮。电梯盖不盖得成不重要，估值得先上去。\n\n五年后再看，公司转型了，现在卖“导电剂”，就是往锂电池里掺的那点黑粉，一公斤赚几块钱。投资人问：“电梯呢？”博士说：“电梯……在电池里，形成了导电网络。”\n\n行，当年说要通天，现在是在电池里修了个楼梯间。"}, "b": {"item_id": "55f4d79ee616", "text": "我认识一个搞碳纳米管的博士，去路演，PPT第一页写：强度是钢的一百倍，重量只有钢的六分之一。投资人眼睛全亮了，有人举手问：能造太空电梯吗？他说理论上可以。全场鼓掌。\"理论上\"，是这圈子最值钱的三个字。他没讲的是，他那批管子里导电的和不导电的混在一起，像一锅分不清的挂面，想分开一根，得先烧掉一栋楼的钱。投资人也没问。后来他融了三轮，估值翻了几倍，公司改名叫\"某某量子新材料\"，实验室里那罐黑乎乎的粉，还是原来那罐。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0895", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "windows-vista", "target_name": "Windows Vista", "facts": ["Released to consumers in January 2007, after the 'Longhorn' project was reset in 2004 and headline features such as the WinFS file system were dropped.", "User Account Control prompted so often that Apple's 'Get a Mac' campaign ran an ad about 'Cancel or Allow'.", "'Windows Vista Capable' stickers went on PCs that could only run Home Basic, without the Aero interface, and led to a class-action lawsuit.", "Windows 7 (2009), widely praised, was largely a tuned-up Vista on the same kernel line; Vista support ended in April 2017."], "a": {"item_id": "445c6f8771dc", "text": "有一种人，叫第一天就升级Vista的人。我问他图啥？他说要玻璃特效，要边栏小工具，要最新的系统。结果装完第一件事，是点了四十七次\"允许\"。不是我点的，是系统在问：您确定要复制这个文件吗？您确定要打开这个程序吗？您确定要关掉这个弹窗吗？像在跟一个多疑的保安办手续。\n\n复制一个文件，进度条走到99%停住，给你显示\"正在计算剩余时间\"，算完告诉你还要一个小时。他的显卡驱动？厂家说还在开发。打印机？厂家说用XP版就够了。最后他在论坛发帖，标题叫《Vista很好，是我的电脑不行》。电脑没问题，问题是他把\"最新\"当成了\"能用\"。"}, "b": {"item_id": "d1b5347e5ac6", "text": "第一天就升级 Vista 的人，我研究过，都是那种等不及第一个补丁的人。他们图什么？图那个毛玻璃窗口。Aero，半透明的，能隐约看到后面的桌面，就像前任的 QQ 空间，什么都看不清，但就是忍不住盯着。\n\n结果装完发现：打印机没驱动，声卡没驱动，连他那个二手摄像头也认不出他了。电脑配置 1G 内存，风扇转得像要起飞，机身还贴着“Vista Ready”的贴纸，Ready 的是风扇。\n\n最惨的是那个弹窗：“Windows 需要您的许可才能继续。”他想删个文件，系统问他：“你确定吗？”他点“允许”。系统又问：“你真的确定吗？”他再点“允许”。这哪是操作系统，这是我妈。\n\n三天后，他的 MSN 签名改了：“Vista 用户，求 XP 安装盘，在线等，挺急的。”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0896", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "keurig", "target_name": "Keurig 胶囊咖啡机", "facts": ["John Sylvan invented the K-Cup in the early 1990s and sold his stake for about $50,000 in 1997; in 2015 he told The Atlantic, 'I feel bad sometimes that I ever did it.'", "Keurig 2.0 (2014) read an ink mark on the lid and rejected unlicensed pods; sales fell and workarounds spread online.", "By 2014 enough K-Cups were sold each year to circle the Earth more than ten times; the pods were mostly unrecyclable #7 plastic until a switch to polypropylene by 2020.", "Keurig Green Mountain merged with Dr Pepper Snapple in 2018 to form Keurig Dr Pepper."], "a": {"item_id": "6e62a676c319", "text": "买 Keurig 的人，特点是：嫌磨豆子麻烦，但不嫌贵。一颗胶囊里就十克咖啡粉，折下来一斤两百多块，他们管这叫“省事”。买就买了，还非买那种四十八颗混合装，榛果、焦糖、南瓜派。大清早喝一口南瓜派味的咖啡，你说你是在提神，还是在给自己过万圣节？喝完还得把那个塑料小杯子抠出来扔掉，一年下来垃圾桶里全是小杯子，像开了家微型保龄球馆。最离谱的是，这玩意儿的发明人自己都公开说后悔发明了它。你想想，你每天早上，一颗一颗地给一个连发明人都后悔的东西交钱，还觉得自己活得挺高效。"}, "b": {"item_id": "a23abc9807e4", "text": "用Keurig的人，就是想喝咖啡，但不想沾“做咖啡”的任何一个字。磨豆？累。烧水？累。你们的理想是按一下，然后盯着它嗡嗡响四十秒。这比烧壶水还久，但因为是按按钮，所以叫效率。\n\n算过账吗？一颗胶囊十克豆，折下来一斤两百多块。你付的是精品价，喝的是一根针扎穿锡纸挤出来的褐色热水。\n\n发明K杯那哥们儿自己都公开说后悔了，家里根本不用。发明人都退坑了，你们还在续费。\n\n最惨是那盒混合装，三个月后剩下的永远是那几颗榛子味。不喝，也不扔，就躺在抽屉里，跟你一样：方便，但没人要。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0897", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "chicago-deep-dish", "target_name": "芝加哥深盘披萨", "facts": ["Created at Pizzeria Uno in Chicago in 1943; credit is disputed among founder Ike Sewell, his partner Ric Riccardo, and cook Rudy Malnati.", "Assembled upside down: sliced mozzarella on the dough, then toppings, then crushed tomatoes on top, so the cheese does not scorch during the long bake.", "Jon Stewart called it 'tomato soup in a bread bowl' and 'a casserole' on The Daily Show in 2013.", "Chicagoans' everyday pizza is thin 'tavern-style' cut into squares; GrubHub order data reported by WBEZ in 2013 showed city residents ordering non-deep-dish pizza about 10 to 1 over deep dish, with deep dish and stuffed making up under 9% of orders."], "a": {"item_id": "54fda0729132", "text": "管芝加哥深盘叫披萨的，基本都是芝加哥人。你别跟他争，他已经争了一辈子了。他图什么？图个名分。番茄酱铺在最上面，芝士压在最底下，饼边砌得跟城墙一样高，这叫披萨？这叫一锅围了墙的炖菜。可他非得叫披萨，因为叫“砂锅”就没法跟纽约人吵架了。结果呢？进店点单，服务员说：“得烤四十五分钟。”他说：“没事，披萨嘛。”等完上桌，刀叉都给他配好了。他吃两块就扶着墙出门，嘴里还念叨：“这才是真正的披萨。”兄弟，真正的披萨，不用你吃完躺一下午。"}, "b": {"item_id": "2802b3de89d7", "text": "管芝加哥深盘叫披萨的人，我见过三种。本地人嘴上说\"我们这儿就这么吃\"；网红拍完照发帖写\"正宗深盘披萨\"；还有一种最狠，纽约来的，吃第一口就开始跟服务员讲道理。图什么？图一个名分。可深盘是个铁锅里烤四十五分钟的芝士蛋糕，芝士垫在底下，番茄酱盖在最上面，你得拿刀拿叉，切到第三块，芝士还在拉丝。这帮人非要把它塞进\"披萨\"这个词里。结果那位纽约哥吃完，服务员问要不要打包，他说：\"不用，我要带一锅回家，当披萨。\"然后抱着一个烫手的铁锅挤地铁，全车人都以为他在搬家。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0898", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "comic-sans", "target_name": "Comic Sans 字体", "facts": ["Designed by Vincent Connare at Microsoft in 1994, modeled on the comic-book lettering in The Dark Knight Returns and Watchmen.", "Made for the speech bubbles of Rover, the cartoon dog in Microsoft Bob; it missed Bob's release and shipped instead in the Windows 95 Plus! pack.", "CERN's July 2012 Higgs boson discovery announcement was presented on slides set in it.", "The 'Ban Comic Sans' campaign, started by designers Dave and Holly Combs in 1999, ran until 2019, when they retired it and launched usecomicsans.com."], "a": {"item_id": "5f56efde52fb", "text": "你们有没有收到过这种通知？标题是《关于本周五停电的通知》，字体是Comic Sans。我一看，以为是小区要办儿童节派对，结果是停水停电，还要求业主自带水桶。\n\n干这事的人我大概知道是谁，物业行政，或者公司HR。他们想表达的是\"我们很亲切，咱们是一家人\"。结果字一出来，就像在给你发一张蜡笔画的请柬。\n\n这字体本来是微软1994年给一个卡通式聊天软件Microsoft Bob做的，用在漫画气泡里的。一份要你立即整改的文件，最后看上去像是从对话框里蹦出来的。\n\n然后呢？全公司把它截图发进群，配文：\"这是谁的生日会通知？\"领导第二天在会上说要严肃点，底下大家一边点头，一边憋笑。"}, "b": {"item_id": "77e8416a8394", "text": "我们小区物业有位行政大哥，一心想跟业主拉近距离，所有通知一律用 Comic Sans。可他不知道，这字体根本没有中文。所以那张停水通知是这样的：“关于停水的通知”，宋体，庄严肃穆；后面“3月15日 8:00—18:00”，圆滚滚的，跟气球一样。一张纸上，中文在念悼词，数字在过六一。\n\n这字体当年是微软专门给一只卡通狗写对话框用的，大哥拿它通知全楼断水，等于派那只狗上门宣布：“汪！你家没水了！”\n\n结果没一个人当真。15号全楼照常洗澡，洗到一半水停了，一群人顶着泡沫下楼找他。大哥还挺委屈：“我通知得那么可爱，你们怎么不看呢？”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0899", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hsq", "target_name": "HSQ 电子束光刻胶", "facts": ["Hydrogen silsesquioxane, sold by Dow Corning as FOx ('flowable oxide') and designed as a spin-on dielectric for chip interconnects; lithographers adopted it as a negative-tone resist.", "Exposure converts its cage structure into a cross-linked, silica-like network, so the developed pattern is essentially SiO2.", "Holds the resolution records for electron-beam lithography: isolated features around 5 nm, dense lines below 10 nm half-pitch, with high-contrast 'salty' NaOH/NaCl development.", "Must be kept refrigerated (Dow Corning's datasheet gives a six-month shelf life at 5 °C); the solution ages into a jelly, and its effective sensitivity shifts with processing delays because cross-linking continues after exposure.", "DuPont, which inherited Dow Corning's FOx/XR-1541 HSQ line, discontinued it and ended production at the end of 2025; labs such as EPFL's CMi switched to third-party HSQ from EM Resist or DisChem, both formulated from AQM powder."], "a": {"item_id": "a38389497436", "text": "有些人为了分辨率，非要换HSQ。他们是谁？一群研究生，导师一句“线宽得压到十纳米以下”，就抱着一瓶胶去排电子束曝光机。这胶得放冰箱，过期会结胶，开了盖还得赶紧用。图的就是那几根细线，结果线是细了，一显影，线条齐刷刷倒向一边，像一排喝多了的人。更绝的是，曝光过的地方会固化成二氧化硅，等于他们在芯片上刻了一层玻璃，洗都洗不掉。毕业那天他们终于明白：分辨率没提上去，芯片上倒多了一块摘不下来的玻璃。"}, "b": {"item_id": "164876412c34", "text": "我们实验室有种人，组会上一听PMMA分辨率不够，当场就要换HSQ。图什么？图SEM照片里那根8纳米的线，能放进论文Figure 1。\n\n结果呢？HSQ灵敏度低，剂量往死里加。别人写一片半小时，他写一整晚，机时表上全是他的名字，像给电子束机守灵。\n\n显影还得用加盐的TMAH。人家做光刻，他在腌咸菜。\n\n最绝的是，这胶一曝光就变成二氧化硅。你辛辛苦苦图形化的不是胶，是玻璃。想去掉？请上氢氟酸。\n\n哦对，那瓶HSQ还得放冰箱，几个月就凝胶。最后他毕业延期了，胶先过期了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0900", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hydrogen-cars", "target_name": "氢燃料电池汽车", "facts": ["Toyota launched the Mirai in 2014; 'mirai' means 'future' in Japanese.", "Nearly all US hydrogen filling stations are in California; Shell closed all seven of its California passenger-car stations in February 2024.", "More than 95% of the world's hydrogen is made from fossil fuels, mostly by steam-reforming natural gas.", "California pump prices reached about $36 per kilogram in 2023-24, over $200 for a Mirai fill; Toyota has handed buyers fuel cards worth $15,000.", "Mirai owners filed a class action against Toyota in July 2024 over hydrogen shortages and station outages; a second suit in 2025 sought at least $5.7 billion."], "a": {"item_id": "31458abe94e2", "text": "有一种人专门买氢燃料电池车。他们的理由很宏大：我要为人类的未来开车。结果未来还没到，先到了加氢站门口。这种站一般开在郊区，像个地下党接头点，营业时间比银行还短，还得提前在App上抢号，跟抢演唱会门票一个难度。好不容易排上了，一公斤氢六七十块，加满一箱，司机心疼得直喊\"这不是加氢，是放血\"。最讽刺的是，这车一天到晚排出来的是水，环保是真环保，就是你的钱全变成了水。等到加氢站哪天悄悄拆了，车就停进车库，成了全国最贵的加湿器，每天擦一擦，等着哪天氢气自己从天上掉下来。"}, "b": {"item_id": "feb6bb90ad06", "text": "我有个朋友买了辆氢燃料电池车，逢人就说：“电动车充电一小时，我加氢只要三分钟。”对，三分钟，前提是你先开两百公里找到加氢站。全市就那么一座，还是给公交车队修的，他得排在十二辆大巴后面，等储罐压力回上来。人家加的是氢，他加的是耐心。\n\n他还爱说：“我这车唯一的排放是水。”是，排放是水，他眼里也是水。一公斤氢七十块，跑一百公里比油车还贵。\n\n最惨的是去年那座站关了。车现在停在楼下，他每天下去看一眼。他说他买的是未来。没错，未来还没到，二手车商的报价先到了：按废铁算，高压氢罐另收拆除费。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0901", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "excel", "target_name": "Microsoft Excel", "facts": ["First shipped for the Macintosh in 1985; the Windows version followed in 1987.", "Silently converts gene names such as SEPT2 and MARCH1 into dates; by August 2020 the HUGO Gene Nomenclature Committee had renamed 27 human genes (MARCH1 became MARCHF1, SEPT2 became SEPTIN2) and published guidelines in Nature Genetics to stop it.", "In October 2020, Public Health England lost 15,841 COVID-19 cases when a legacy .xls file hit its 65,536-row limit.", "Reinhart and Rogoff's 2010 paper 'Growth in a Time of Debt', cited for austerity policies, omitted five countries through a spreadsheet range error found by a graduate student in 2013.", "Sheets have been capped at 1,048,576 rows by 16,384 columns since Excel 2007."], "a": {"item_id": "a27121aa4e43", "text": "有种老板，公司几百号人，ERP不肯上，他说：\"Excel就够了。\"图什么？省钱，还有一种\"所有数字都攥在我手里\"的快感。结果呢？销售总表传了四十个版本，最后一个叫\"最终版_真的不改了_2\"。财务有个VLOOKUP是前任写的，没人敢碰，一动利润表全是#N/A。行政把名单合并了单元格，想筛选，直接报错。最惨的是那个会写宏的小伙子，上个月离职，交接文档就是一个会自己弹窗的文件。现在公司每个月发工资，要靠一台谁都不敢关的电脑。"}, "b": {"item_id": "7557f0ed30ba", "text": "见过拿Excel管整家公司的老板。问他怎么不上个系统，他说：\"Excel挺好用的。\"是挺好用，好用到库存、工资、客户全塞一张表里，VLOOKUP嵌了七层。这哪是做表，这是写遗嘱。\n\n图什么？图不用学新东西。结果为了不学新东西，公司专门养了个人来学这张表。刘姐，没职位，大家只说\"问刘姐\"。她请一天假，公司就瘫了，因为只有她知道D列不能动，一动，#REF!就像瘟疫一样往下传。\n\n下场更惨。上周我想打开订单表，弹窗：\"文件正在被张伟使用。\"张伟三年前就离职了。现在公司发工资，得等一个幽灵把文件保存关掉。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0902", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "comic-sans", "target_name": "Comic Sans 字体", "facts": ["Designed by Vincent Connare at Microsoft in 1994, modeled on the comic-book lettering in The Dark Knight Returns and Watchmen.", "Made for the speech bubbles of Rover, the cartoon dog in Microsoft Bob; it missed Bob's release and shipped instead in the Windows 95 Plus! pack.", "CERN's July 2012 Higgs boson discovery announcement was presented on slides set in it.", "The 'Ban Comic Sans' campaign, started by designers Dave and Holly Combs in 1999, ran until 2019, when they retired it and launched usecomicsans.com."], "a": {"item_id": "130c2658ebda", "text": "我们小区物业有个行政大姐，发正式通知特别爱用Comic Sans。你知道这字体当年是干嘛的吗？微软设计给卡通小狗说话用的，专门往对话气泡里塞的。所以每回停水通知一贴，我都觉得是一只小狗在跟我说：“明天停水哦～”\n\n她图什么？图亲切。坏消息配这字体，就跟打针前先给你看张奥特曼贴纸一样。\n\n上个月涨物业费，她又用Comic Sans，那个“8”圆得像在冲你眨眼。全楼没一个当真，都以为是谁家小孩的恶作剧，撕下来折纸飞机了。年底一查，欠费率百分之八十。\n\n领导问她怎么回事，她理直气壮：“欧洲核子研究中心宣布发现希格斯玻色子都用这字体！”\n\n领导说：“对，所以全世界到现在还在笑他们。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "5f875c2be689", "text": "你们收过用Comic Sans写的正式通知吗？“本周六全小区停水”，底下配着那个圆滚滚的漫画体。发这种通知的，多半是物业或者HR，他们心里想的是：字圆一点，坏消息就没那么硬。可这字原本是微软给一个卡通小助手配气泡台词用的，骨子里就是为了“亲切”设计的。结果呢？停水像生日派对邀请函，扣工资像在发贴纸。大家看完第一反应不是慌，是笑：“这是哪个幼儿园发的？”通知被截图传遍业主群，发通知的人成了全小区的表情包。他本想做个好人，最后做成了段子手。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0903", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hawaiian-pizza", "target_name": "夏威夷披萨", "facts": ["Invented in 1962 by Sam Panopoulos, a Greek immigrant, at the Satellite Restaurant in Chatham, Ontario, Canada; named after the brand of canned pineapple he used.", "Iceland's president Guðni Th. Jóhannesson said in 2017 that he would ban pineapple on pizza if he could, then clarified that a president has no such power.", "Hawaii is no longer a major pineapple producer: Del Monte shut its Hawaiian pineapple operations in November 2006, two years ahead of its announced 2008 exit, the state's last cannery closed in 2007, and most canned pineapple now comes from Thailand, Indonesia, and the Philippines.", "Panopoulos, whose restaurant also served Chinese dishes, told the Toronto Sun in 2010 that 'in those days people didn't mix the tastes like sweet and sour, except for Chinese food'."], "a": {"item_id": "0878d1084313", "text": "每桌都有那么一个人，菜单一翻就说：“来个夏威夷的。”全桌瞬间安静，像有人宣布要在群里发起投票。\n\n他图什么？图人设。他想让你知道他不随大流，可他点的是每家店菜单上都有的第三个披萨。他心里还有本账：菠萝算水果，火腿算肉，一块下去，营养均衡。\n\n下场呢？别人点的都见底了，只剩他那盘，菠萝汁把饼底泡软，拎起来像拎一条热毛巾。他还硬撑：“真的好吃，你们不懂。”\n\n你知道夏威夷披萨是谁发明的吗？一个住在加拿大的希腊人。夏威夷不认，意大利不承认，全世界最后就剩你一个人替它出头。"}, "b": {"item_id": "273e9ae28d95", "text": "点夏威夷披萨的人，我一直想不通。你说你爱吃甜的，行啊，菠萝罐头买一罐，一勺一勺舀着吃，多痛快。你偏要把它摊在一张发面饼上，烤到边缘焦黄，再铺一层咸火腿，然后跟人说：\"这叫冲突之美。\"冲突你个头！菠萝一烤出汁，整张饼泡成了湿吐司。你咬一口，烫，甜，咸，三个味道在嘴里开会，谁也不服谁，散会的时候你只剩一嘴纸巾，和一句\"其实还行\"。这种人我见过，每次聚餐都点它，然后全桌人默默把菠萝挑到盘子边上，堆成一座小山，像一群赖着不走的客人。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0904", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "windows-vista", "target_name": "Windows Vista", "facts": ["Released to consumers in January 2007, after the 'Longhorn' project was reset in 2004 and headline features such as the WinFS file system were dropped.", "User Account Control prompted so often that Apple's 'Get a Mac' campaign ran an ad about 'Cancel or Allow'.", "'Windows Vista Capable' stickers went on PCs that could only run Home Basic, without the Aero interface, and led to a class-action lawsuit.", "Windows 7 (2009), widely praised, was largely a tuned-up Vista on the same kernel line; Vista support ended in April 2017."], "a": {"item_id": "ebfddca0e411", "text": "第一天就升级Vista的人，你们公司肯定有一个，就是那个说“我对电脑懂一点”的。\n\n他图什么？图毛玻璃。Aero，窗口是半透明的，他觉得自己电脑一下子有钱了。结果他那台机器贴着“Vista Capable”的贴纸，意思是“能运行Vista”，但只能运行没有毛玻璃的Vista。花钱升级，就为了得到一个不透明的窗口。\n\n然后是UAC。他点一下，弹窗：“是否允许？”允许。又弹：“您确定吗？”确定。一天里被问“确定”的次数，比他求婚还多。\n\n打印机没驱动，声卡没驱动，最后他默默把XP装回去。同事问：“怎么样？”\n\n他说：“挺好，我体验了一下，什么叫花钱买降级。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "62ac4958dd62", "text": "有一种人，办公室里第一个喊\"我先装\"的，Vista一出来当天就升级。图什么？图的是半透明的Aero玻璃窗。结果开机六分钟，点开一个文件夹，弹窗问他\"您确定要打开吗\"，他点确定，又弹一个\"您真的确定吗\"。一天点了三百次确定，手指比他的工资还累。声卡没驱动，打印机没驱动，连鼠标滚轮都不认。最后他翻出XP安装盘，格式化重装，还在朋友圈发：我终于懂了什么叫成熟的系统。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0905", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "luosifen", "target_name": "螺蛳粉", "facts": ["A rice-noodle dish from Liuzhou, Guangxi; the smell comes from pickled bamboo shoots (酸笋), not from the river snails, which only flavour the broth.", "The instant version boomed during the 2020 lockdowns; Liuzhou's government reported packaged luosifen sales of over 10 billion yuan that year.", "Added to China's national intangible cultural heritage list in 2021.", "Liuzhou opened a luosifen industry college in 2020 to train workers for the trade."], "a": {"item_id": "06a4c1a635ed", "text": "合租房里煮螺蛳粉的人，都信一句话：“闻着臭，吃着香。”这话是给吃的人用的。我们室友只拿到前半句。\n\n他煮十分钟，爽了。我在房间里闻三天，连根腐竹都没分到。\n\n他还特别讲礼貌，煮之前敲我门：“我煮螺蛳粉，你介意吗？”这哪是问，这是通知，跟飞机广播“前方有气流”一样，你介不介意，它都得颠。\n\n然后他开窗，开抽油烟机，觉得自己尽力了。兄弟，酸笋那玩意儿你开窗它不走，它是在考察房子，想签长租。\n\n结果第二天上班，同事凑过来：“你昨天是不是吃螺蛳粉了？”\n\n我没吃啊！\n\n我连汤都没喝上一口，就被螺蛳粉吃了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "5d0789c353d9", "text": "合租房里煮螺蛳粉的人，我太懂了。他们总说：就煮一包，不碍事。图什么？图锅里飘着腐竹和木耳，图那口酸笋上头的快感，图自己在厨房吸溜得像个美食博主。结果呢？酸笋味顺着抽油烟机往外跑，跑不掉的全钻进了室友的被窝。第二天群里弹出一句：\"谁煮的螺蛳粉？\"没人认，但全屋人心里都有数。厨房门上很快贴了张纸，写着\"禁止螺蛳粉\"，他的押金被扣了两百，理由是\"除味费\"。最绝的是那个空料包，他打了三个结扔进垃圾桶，第二天整个单元楼的人都知道他吃了什么。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0906", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "graphene", "target_name": "石墨烯", "facts": ["Isolated in 2004 by Andre Geim and Konstantin Novoselov at Manchester by peeling graphite with Scotch tape; Nobel Prize in Physics, 2010.", "The EU's Graphene Flagship, launched in 2013, committed €1 billion over ten years.", "A 2018 study in Advanced Materials tested 'graphene' from 60 producers and found none with more than 50% graphene content; most was graphite flakes.", "Sold in tennis rackets, running shoes, and headphones as a performance ingredient.", "Geim also won an Ig Nobel Prize in 2000 for levitating a frog with magnets."], "a": {"item_id": "20a7d109770e", "text": "我有个同学读材料的，逢人就说石墨烯要改变世界。每次开场一模一样：2004年，两个科学家拿胶带从铅笔芯上粘下一层碳，拿了诺贝尔奖。他讲得特别骄傲，好像那卷胶带是他家的。\n\n我问他：比钢硬，比铜导电，那你怎么还合租？他说量产有瓶颈。十年了，这瓶颈比石墨烯还硬。\n\n后来他创业，产品终于出来了：石墨烯保暖内衣。改变世界的材料，最后改变的是秋裤的价格。铅笔芯的成分，卖出羽绒服的价，还跟我说发热是黑科技。大哥，那是你自己急的。\n\n现在他融不到钱了，开始去我妈的养生群讲课。我妈听完说：小伙子，你这套跟我买的磁疗枕头是一个路数。"}, "b": {"item_id": "9b0a72d5be4f", "text": "饭桌上总有这种人，一开口就是：\"石墨烯要改变世界。\"你问他石墨烯是啥，他说就一层碳原子，导电好，导热快。行，那你告诉我，你脚下这双\"石墨烯暖宝宝\"鞋垫，里面到底有几层碳？他沉默三秒，说：\"暖和就行。\"这话我信，暖和是真的，毕竟它本质上就是个电热丝套了个新名字。前几年搞石墨烯概念的公司，股价一路涨，财报上研发投入写得比谁都大，产品呢，是往涂料里撒一把黑粉，再印上\"石墨烯\"三个字。投资人亏得哭，创始人转头去讲元宇宙了。世界没变，就他朋友圈头像换成了一张论文截图。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0907", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "microsoft-teams", "target_name": "Microsoft Teams", "facts": ["Launched in 2017, bundled at no extra charge into Office 365, and replaced Skype for Business.", "After Slack's antitrust complaint, Microsoft unbundled Teams from Office in the EU in 2023 and worldwide in 2024; the European Commission still sent a statement of objections in 2024.", "Microsoft reported 320 million monthly active users in 2023.", "Microsoft shut down consumer Skype in May 2025 and sent its users to Teams."], "a": {"item_id": "0c5f29fdb1c7", "text": "咱公司强推 Teams 的那位，我打听了，是采购。理由特别感人：“Office 365 套餐里本来就带着，不用另外花钱。”省了多少？年终述职写着呢：降本增效，三十万。\n\n三十万省下来了，然后呢？全公司每天问他同一个问题：“我昨天发的文件在哪？”他说：“Teams 里，‘文件’标签页。”其实在 SharePoint 里。他自己也是上周才知道 SharePoint 是什么。\n\n最惨的是，他在 Teams 里建了一个“团队”，团队里又建了一个“团队”，最后这个团队里只有他一个人。\n\n我们都在微信群里，聊他。"}, "b": {"item_id": "fd671172ecb3", "text": "我们公司推Teams的是IT部老王。为啥选它？买Office 365送的，老王这辈子没拒绝过“送的”。培训会上他讲了四十分钟：“聊天”和“团队”不一样，“频道”里的“帖子”也不算聊天，文件其实存在SharePoint里。SharePoint是啥？别问。\n\n现在全公司最忙的就是老王，天天有人问他：文件呢？我电脑上怎么有两个Teams？\n\n最惨的是，老板发现了头像旁边那个小绿点，五分钟不动鼠标就变黄。现在老王桌上放着个自动晃鼠标的小玩意儿。他亲手装的监控，第一个抓的是他自己。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0908", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "brasilia", "target_name": "巴西利亚", "facts": ["Inaugurated on 21 April 1960 after about 41 months of construction, moving the capital inland from Rio de Janeiro.", "Lúcio Costa's Plano Piloto is laid out like an airplane (or a bird, or a cross), with Oscar Niemeyer's government buildings along the fuselage.", "Listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1987, less than 30 years after it was built.", "Planned for 500,000 residents; the Federal District now has about 3 million, most in satellite towns outside the plan.", "Built for the car: wide avenues, few pedestrian crossings, and distances that make walking impractical."], "a": {"item_id": "326fa522889c", "text": "在巴西利亚靠两条腿出门的人，我得给他们立个碑。这帮人是谁？多半是刚搬来的新人，手机一打开，地图写着\"步行二十八分钟\"，他们就信了。图什么？图锻炼，图省钱，图不想等那趟十四分钟一班、还不一定来的公交。结果呢？整条Eixo Monumental上，人行道说没就没，只剩一条给卡车跑的高速路，他们只能贴着护栏，顶着尾气走。好不容易走到立交桥底下想抄近路，四个车道，红灯十秒。最后他站在W3大道中间那条绿化带上，左边是车，右边也是车，低头看手机，地图淡定地提示：\"您已到达目的地。\""}, "b": {"item_id": "a632ffa6792b", "text": "在巴西利亚走路的，就三种人：刚下飞机的游客、还没买车的新公务员，和读过一本城市规划书的理想主义者。\n\n游客站在电视塔上看国会那两栋楼：“就在前面嘛。”对，中间隔着纪念轴十几条车道。尼迈耶给你设计了大教堂，没给你设计斑马线。\n\n理想主义者说要“用脚丈量城市”。卢西奥·科斯塔把城市画成一架飞机，你见过谁在机翼上散步？\n\n新公务员沿着部委大道走，十几栋一模一样的楼，走到第七栋就分不清自己是去上班还是在服刑。\n\n结局都一样：八月旱季，踩着前人在草地上踩出来的红土小道，鞋红了，裤脚红了，到门口保安问：“您车停哪儿了？”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0909", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "microsoft-teams", "target_name": "Microsoft Teams", "facts": ["Launched in 2017, bundled at no extra charge into Office 365, and replaced Skype for Business.", "After Slack's antitrust complaint, Microsoft unbundled Teams from Office in the EU in 2023 and worldwide in 2024; the European Commission still sent a statement of objections in 2024.", "Microsoft reported 320 million monthly active users in 2023.", "Microsoft shut down consumer Skype in May 2025 and sent its users to Teams."], "a": {"item_id": "cfb198c08db3", "text": "我们公司那个力推全员用Teams的VP，我真想当面问他图啥。图啥？图一份\"数字化转型\"的PPT。结果呢？全公司每天三小时盯着那三个跳动的小圆点，等对面打完一个\"好\"字。他自己更惨，上周开会共享屏幕，忘了点\"允许\"，全场看他浏览器收藏夹看了十分钟。现在他每晚十一点在\"全体员工\"频道里@所有人：\"大家收到了吗？\"收到了老板，大家的状态早改成\"请勿打扰\"了，就是不想回。"}, "b": {"item_id": "fd671172ecb3", "text": "我们公司推Teams的是IT部老王。为啥选它？买Office 365送的，老王这辈子没拒绝过“送的”。培训会上他讲了四十分钟：“聊天”和“团队”不一样，“频道”里的“帖子”也不算聊天，文件其实存在SharePoint里。SharePoint是啥？别问。\n\n现在全公司最忙的就是老王，天天有人问他：文件呢？我电脑上怎么有两个Teams？\n\n最惨的是，老板发现了头像旁边那个小绿点，五分钟不动鼠标就变黄。现在老王桌上放着个自动晃鼠标的小玩意儿。他亲手装的监控，第一个抓的是他自己。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0910", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "keurig", "target_name": "Keurig 胶囊咖啡机", "facts": ["John Sylvan invented the K-Cup in the early 1990s and sold his stake for about $50,000 in 1997; in 2015 he told The Atlantic, 'I feel bad sometimes that I ever did it.'", "Keurig 2.0 (2014) read an ink mark on the lid and rejected unlicensed pods; sales fell and workarounds spread online.", "By 2014 enough K-Cups were sold each year to circle the Earth more than ten times; the pods were mostly unrecyclable #7 plastic until a switch to polypropylene by 2020.", "Keurig Green Mountain merged with Dr Pepper Snapple in 2018 to form Keurig Dr Pepper."], "a": {"item_id": "6e62a676c319", "text": "买 Keurig 的人，特点是：嫌磨豆子麻烦，但不嫌贵。一颗胶囊里就十克咖啡粉，折下来一斤两百多块，他们管这叫“省事”。买就买了，还非买那种四十八颗混合装，榛果、焦糖、南瓜派。大清早喝一口南瓜派味的咖啡，你说你是在提神，还是在给自己过万圣节？喝完还得把那个塑料小杯子抠出来扔掉，一年下来垃圾桶里全是小杯子，像开了家微型保龄球馆。最离谱的是，这玩意儿的发明人自己都公开说后悔发明了它。你想想，你每天早上，一颗一颗地给一个连发明人都后悔的东西交钱，还觉得自己活得挺高效。"}, "b": {"item_id": "45e5b40c4a0a", "text": "我同事天天用Keurig泡咖啡，理由是三十秒搞定。行，省事，不用磨豆，不用洗壶，按一下嗡地一声，像要起飞。结果出来一杯八盎司的热水，颜色像他的周报。每天撕一个锡箔盖、扔一个塑料杯，一个月下来，垃圾桶里的小杯子能堆成金字塔。上个月机器亮起\"请除垢\"，他查说明书，得买除垢剂，还得拿醋灌一遍。算下来，他的胶囊钱够买一台手冲壶和两袋豆子。最后他还是下楼买了杯星巴克，店员问要什么，他说：\"要一杯，有味儿的。\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0911", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "excel", "target_name": "Microsoft Excel", "facts": ["First shipped for the Macintosh in 1985; the Windows version followed in 1987.", "Silently converts gene names such as SEPT2 and MARCH1 into dates; by August 2020 the HUGO Gene Nomenclature Committee had renamed 27 human genes (MARCH1 became MARCHF1, SEPT2 became SEPTIN2) and published guidelines in Nature Genetics to stop it.", "In October 2020, Public Health England lost 15,841 COVID-19 cases when a legacy .xls file hit its 65,536-row limit.", "Reinhart and Rogoff's 2010 paper 'Growth in a Time of Debt', cited for austerity policies, omitted five countries through a spreadsheet range error found by a graduate student in 2013.", "Sheets have been capped at 1,048,576 rows by 16,384 columns since Excel 2007."], "a": {"item_id": "a27121aa4e43", "text": "有种老板，公司几百号人，ERP不肯上，他说：\"Excel就够了。\"图什么？省钱，还有一种\"所有数字都攥在我手里\"的快感。结果呢？销售总表传了四十个版本，最后一个叫\"最终版_真的不改了_2\"。财务有个VLOOKUP是前任写的，没人敢碰，一动利润表全是#N/A。行政把名单合并了单元格，想筛选，直接报错。最惨的是那个会写宏的小伙子，上个月离职，交接文档就是一个会自己弹窗的文件。现在公司每个月发工资，要靠一台谁都不敢关的电脑。"}, "b": {"item_id": "18732d8f011b", "text": "我们老板管公司就靠一个Excel，文件名叫“总表_最终版_改3_千万别动.xlsx”。他图什么？图省钱，图“一切尽在掌握”。掌握到什么程度？他一打开，全公司点进去都弹“文件已锁定，只读”。\n\n他以为自己在经营企业，其实他在养一个VLOOKUP。财务、库存、工资，全靠第三列去匹配第七列。上个月实习生手欠插了一列，全公司工资齐刷刷变成#N/A，那是我们第一次见识零元购。\n\n最惨的是，会写那串套了十一层IF的会计离职了。老板对着那行公式看了三天，像考古队在研究甲骨文。现在公司还活着，但谁都不敢点“排序”。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0912", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "juicero", "target_name": "Juicero 榨汁机", "facts": ["A Wi-Fi-connected cold-press juicer launched in 2016 at $699, later cut to $399, that squeezed proprietary bags of chopped produce sold by subscription.", "Raised about $120 million from investors including Google Ventures and Kleiner Perkins.", "In April 2017, Bloomberg reporters squeezed the packs by hand and got 7.5 ounces of juice in 90 seconds, faster than the machine's 8 ounces in about two minutes.", "Each pack carried a QR code that the press scanned and checked online before squeezing; it refused expired packs and would not run at all unless connected to Wi-Fi.", "Shut down in September 2017 and offered refunds on the machine."], "a": {"item_id": "2a181ea7d6cd", "text": "我花四百美金买了一台 Juicero。它的工作原理是：把预先切好的果蔬包塞进去，机器扫码确认是正版，然后用很大的压力把汁挤出来。后来有记者发现，那包东西用手就能捏出汁，不用扫码，也不用机器。所以我花四百块买的，其实是一副需要联网验证的手套。\n\n做这个的人我真想见见。一帮硅谷的人觉得榨汁缺一个\"数字化入口\"，拿了一亿多美元风投，就为了让你每周给一袋胡萝卜付订阅费。结果公司关门，机器打折甩卖。我那台现在是家里最贵的摆件，每次看到它，我都觉得它在等我扫码。"}, "b": {"item_id": "e1fe9018b6c6", "text": "买 Juicero 的人我研究过：住旧金山，爱喝\"冷压\"，觉得自己动手捏个袋子太掉价，得花 400 美元雇一台联网的机器替他捏。\n\n这机器号称四吨压力。后来彭博社发现，徒手捏那个果汁包，出汁还更快。也就是说，这帮人花 400 块，买的是\"捏袋子需要 WiFi\"的权利。\n\n更绝的是，果汁包过期，机器直接罢工。它比你妈还关心你的健康。\n\n最后公司倒闭，全额退款。钱拿回来了，脸没拿回来。从此这帮人看自己的手，都带着一种复杂的情绪：这玩意儿，一直是免费的。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0913", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "klingon", "target_name": "克林贡语", "facts": ["Created by linguist Marc Okrand for Star Trek III (1984), building on a few words the actor James Doohan improvised for the 1979 film.", "Deliberately alien grammar: object-verb-subject word order and no verb 'to be', so Hamlet's line became 'taH pagh taHbe'', 'continue or not continue'.", "The Klingon Language Institute (1992) published Hamlet 'in the original Klingon'; Bing Translator added Klingon in 2013 and Duolingo in 2018.", "Okrand's 1985 Klingon Dictionary listed roughly 1,500 words, and fluent speakers number only in the dozens: 20 to 30 in 2009, 30 to 50 by 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "ab8685eebdf7", "text": "全世界能把克林贡语说流利的人，据说也就几十个。你想想，几十个人，凑一块儿连个婚礼都撑不满，还得从《星际迷航》剧组借人。\n\n这帮人图什么？图的是终于能用原文读《哈姆雷特》。对，有人真把莎士比亚翻成了克林贡语，理由是“莎士比亚本来就是克林贡人，被地球人偷了”。你说他疯吧，他还出了书。\n\n最狠的是有个语言学家，从儿子出生起只跟他说克林贡语，想培养世界上第一个克林贡母语者。结果孩子长到几岁，爸爸一开口说克林贡语，他就装听不见。你看，叛逆期都比别人早，还是外星文化先教会他的：荣誉第一，老爹靠边。\n\n而且这门语言根本不适合过日子。它连“你好”都没有，见面打招呼说的是“nuqneH”，意思是“你想干嘛？”你练了十年，终于能流利地跟人吵架了，问题是全世界能听懂你骂人的，也就那几十个，还都在同一个群里。"}, "b": {"item_id": "45f4bb0c382a", "text": "我朋友花三年把克林贡语练到了流利。流利到什么程度？他跟我吵架，全程用克林贡语。吵完我才发现，他是对着镜子练的，因为我一句都听不懂。他图什么？他说这语言够硬气，念起来一嗓子呼噜，像刚从战场上下来。结果他去参加同好聚会，满屋子都是练了三年的人，互相拍胸脯、喷口水打招呼，嗓子全哑了，喝口水都得用喉音。后来他女朋友跟他分手，留了一句话：你说你想战死的时候，能不能先问问我想不想吃晚饭？"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0914", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "monopoly", "target_name": "大富翁（Monopoly）", "facts": ["Descends from Lizzie Magie's The Landlord's Game, patented in 1904 to teach the evils of land monopoly in the spirit of Henry George.", "Charles Darrow sold his version to Parker Brothers in 1935 and was credited as the inventor; Parker bought Magie's patent for $500.", "The official rules require any unbought property to be auctioned, and 'Free Parking' pays nothing; the house rules that skip the auction and pile cash on Free Parking are why games drag.", "During the Second World War, British intelligence had maps, compasses, and real currency hidden in sets sent to prisoner-of-war camps."], "a": {"item_id": "d983ef681d05", "text": "我们家过年必玩大富翁，说是联络感情，其实是全家一起验证：血缘关系，扛不住房地产。\n\n我爸每次抢着当银行，他不是想为大家服务，他是想体验一下贪污。我妈只买橙色那一片，说吉利，结果整局靠收我的租活着。收的时候还特温柔：“妈妈收你的钱，你不会生气吧？”我说不会。她说：“那再加五十。”\n\n我九岁的表弟哭着求我把铁路卖给他，我心一软卖了。三圈以后，他拿着我的铁路，把我送进了监狱。\n\n我走投无路，找我爸借钱，他说：“行，按银行利率。”我说你是我爸！他说：“现在我是银行。”\n\n玩到凌晨，我爸有全部酒店，我妈有全部橙色，我手里只剩一张出狱卡。\n\n全家人公平分配了战果：他们赢了钱，我赢了离开这张桌子的权利。"}, "b": {"item_id": "7f9957efd2c9", "text": "过年我舅非要拉全家玩大富翁，说就玩一把。我告诉你，这一把从吃完饺子玩到天亮。我舅抢着当银行，发钱的时候笑得比真银行还真。轮到他自己，橙色那条街先买下，盖三栋房子，再升级成旅馆。我表姐踩上去交了一大笔过路费，脸比她手里的绿房子还绿。我爸更惨，一圈转到监狱，他说我就路过。人家说那叫Just Visiting，你是进去了。没钱交保释金，只能眼睁睁看着我舅把他的地一块块收走。最后免费停车场那堆钱全被我舅拿走，他抱着钱说：这就是亲情。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0915", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "keurig", "target_name": "Keurig 胶囊咖啡机", "facts": ["John Sylvan invented the K-Cup in the early 1990s and sold his stake for about $50,000 in 1997; in 2015 he told The Atlantic, 'I feel bad sometimes that I ever did it.'", "Keurig 2.0 (2014) read an ink mark on the lid and rejected unlicensed pods; sales fell and workarounds spread online.", "By 2014 enough K-Cups were sold each year to circle the Earth more than ten times; the pods were mostly unrecyclable #7 plastic until a switch to polypropylene by 2020.", "Keurig Green Mountain merged with Dr Pepper Snapple in 2018 to form Keurig Dr Pepper."], "a": {"item_id": "612741b2093f", "text": "你们办公室里有没有这种人？每天早上站在Keurig前面，按下去，等它“滋滋”响半分钟，端出一杯颜色像红茶、味道像热水的东西。他图什么？图的是“我也是讲究生活的人”。可这份讲究一个K杯一块多，一个月下来够买一台手冲壶了。更惨的是垃圾，那个小塑料杯带铝箔盖，还得掰开、分类，最后扔进去的还是塑料。他以为买的是咖啡，其实买的是“按一下就有”的错觉。结果一天一杯，喝到最后，他端着的是一杯加了热水的回忆。"}, "b": {"item_id": "f6e06d9fe87e", "text": "用 Keurig 胶囊的人，我太懂了：嫌磨豆子麻烦，嫌滤纸麻烦，嫌洗壶麻烦，一心要“按一下就有”。\n\n结果呢？一颗胶囊一块多，折合一磅咖啡粉五六十块，这哪是喝咖啡，这是喝首饰。喝的还都是什么？榛果奶油、南瓜派、焦糖玛奇朵，咖啡味的香水。\n\n更绝的是，为了省两分钟磨豆子，每天花三分钟撕锡纸、倒渣、把那个小塑料杯分类回收。一年三百六十五个小杯子，像在给咖啡办葬礼。\n\n最后机器亮灯：“请除垢。”翻开说明书，让你拿白醋泡机器。图省事的人，最后蹲在厨房里，给一台咖啡机泡醋。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0916", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "php", "target_name": "PHP", "facts": ["Created by Rasmus Lerdorf in 1994 as 'Personal Home Page Tools', a set of C programs for tracking visitors to his own résumé page; the name now officially stands for the recursive 'PHP: Hypertext Preprocessor'.", "Lerdorf, in his 2007 MySQL Conference talk 'PHP on Hormones': 'I'm not a real programmer. I throw together things until it works then I move on.'", "Runs WordPress, which W3Techs counted on 39.4% of all websites (58.1% of sites with a known CMS) on 9 November 2026.", "Core function names are inconsistent (strpos, str_replace, htmlspecialchars, nl2br) partly because, as Lerdorf confirmed, early function names were chosen to balance a hash table keyed on string length.", "Until PHP 8 (2020), the comparison 0 == 'foo' was true because the string was silently converted to a number."], "a": {"item_id": "15733eca0d13", "text": "干PHP外包的，我见过一类人特别有意思。他们写的代码，每个变量前面都挂个$，像给每个变量别了张工牌。strpos的参数是针在前还是大海在前，他们闭着眼都能写对，因为早就不记了，改成背\"查一下手册\"。甲方说\"就改个按钮颜色\"，他们说好，打开一个三千行的文件，全局变量满天飞，一个函数里HTML、SQL、表单炖在一起，像一锅十年没洗过的火锅。他们图什么？图一个月能拿一万五。落得什么下场？三十五岁，简历上写着\"精通PHP\"，面试官问一句PHP 8有什么新特性，他沉默三秒，说：\"我们主要用的是宝塔。\""}, "b": {"item_id": "8dd77ec7826a", "text": "我有个朋友，靠写PHP吃饭。你问他干啥的，他第一句永远是“PHP是世界上最好的语言”，说完自己先笑。这是他这行唯一的笑点，也是唯一的尊严。\n\n他图啥？图门槛低。2010年自学三天，用织梦给楼下火锅店搭了个网站，收八百，觉得自己要财务自由了。十五年过去，他还在搭站，还是八百。火锅店倒了，现在接的是美甲店。\n\n他的工作流程：FileZilla连上服务器，直接改线上文件，刷新，白屏；再改，再刷新，又白屏。他管这叫“敏捷开发”。\n\n他代码里全是美元符号，一个变量一个$，写了一辈子$，就没见过几张。\n\n最近他焦虑，说PHP要死了。我说你放心，它死不了，全世界四成网站还挂在WordPress上呢。会死的是你。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0917", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "papyrus", "target_name": "Papyrus 字体", "facts": ["Hand-drawn by Chris Costello in 1982 over about six months, when he was 23; he sold the rights to Letraset for $750.", "Bundled with macOS and with Microsoft Office, which is why it appears on so many menus and signs.", "James Cameron's Avatar (2009) used it for the film's logo and subtitles.", "A 2017 Saturday Night Live sketch starring Ryan Gosling is about a man unable to get over that choice."], "a": {"item_id": "1155b79dd841", "text": "我们这条街，艾灸馆、素食馆、足疗店，招牌全是Papyrus，就是那种笔画毛毛糙糙、像拿烧焦的树枝在牛皮纸上划出来的字体。\n\n老板们图什么？图\"古法\"\"天然\"\"千年底蕴\"。设计师报价三千，他一想：Word里自带的，免费，还自带古埃及气质，血赚。\n\n可《阿凡达》砸了二十多亿美元，片名用的就是Papyrus，被全世界笑了十几年。你一个六十平的刮痧店，凭什么觉得自己扛得住？\n\n现在客人走到门口，抬头一看\"古法艾灸\"，字还带着焦边，真不知道是进去放松，还是进去防腐。\n\n最惨的是素食馆老板，招牌、菜单、外卖袋全是Papyrus，一份麻婆豆腐，看着像刚从墓里刨出来的。他还纳闷生意怎么越来越淡。兄弟，你卖的是出土文物。"}, "b": {"item_id": "7a35ef193141", "text": "最离谱的是开养生馆、开餐馆的老板，招牌非要用Papyrus。这字体像用芦苇纸手撕出来的，笔画边缘毛毛躁躁，像法老陵墓门口那块石碑。他们图什么？图\"有调性\"，图\"古法养生\"，让客人一进门就觉得有故事。结果阿凡达的海报用的就是这个字，顾客抬头一看，以为要去潘多拉星球做足疗。隔壁烧烤店跟着学，招牌一亮，大家都在吃木乃伊烤串。老板很委屈：\"我要的是高级感。\"设计师说：\"是您自己在字体列表里一路往下划，点的这个。\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0918", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "microsoft-teams", "target_name": "Microsoft Teams", "facts": ["Launched in 2017, bundled at no extra charge into Office 365, and replaced Skype for Business.", "After Slack's antitrust complaint, Microsoft unbundled Teams from Office in the EU in 2023 and worldwide in 2024; the European Commission still sent a statement of objections in 2024.", "Microsoft reported 320 million monthly active users in 2023.", "Microsoft shut down consumer Skype in May 2025 and sent its users to Teams."], "a": {"item_id": "1fd1f04cdc79", "text": "逼我们用 Teams 的人，我研究过了，是 IT 总监。他的理由我听懂了：买了 Microsoft 365，Teams 免费送。他不是选了个软件，他是领了个赠品，还拿去年底述职：\"我给公司省了三十万！\"省的是软件钱，花的是我们的命。现在全公司表面在 Teams 上，实际都在微信群里问：\"那个会议链接到底点哪儿？\"最惨的是他自己，每次他一发言，头像都是黄色的\"离开\"，人明明坐在那儿，系统说他不在。推广一年，他终于被系统说中了：这个人，在公司，已经离开了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "04d98c0d62fd", "text": "我们公司换Teams，拍板的是IT总监老王。为什么选它？不是因为好用，是因为它“白送”，买Office 365附赠的。结果全公司天天在里面找文件：发在聊天里的跑进了OneDrive，发在频道里的又钻进了SharePoint。老王开会说“我共享一下屏幕”，然后整个部门盯着他的桌面看了五分钟。\n\n后来呢？欧盟说微软捆绑销售，Teams得拆开单买了。老王“白送”的东西开始收钱，他自己还天天被标成黄色“离开”，五分钟不动鼠标就算离开。现在他桌上插着个自动晃鼠标的小盒子。全公司最勤奋的那个绿点，是一块在原地抽搐的塑料。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0919", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "microsoft-teams", "target_name": "Microsoft Teams", "facts": ["Launched in 2017, bundled at no extra charge into Office 365, and replaced Skype for Business.", "After Slack's antitrust complaint, Microsoft unbundled Teams from Office in the EU in 2023 and worldwide in 2024; the European Commission still sent a statement of objections in 2024.", "Microsoft reported 320 million monthly active users in 2023.", "Microsoft shut down consumer Skype in May 2025 and sent its users to Teams."], "a": {"item_id": "8605988b84fd", "text": "他们是谁？就是那个拍板\"全公司必须用Teams\"的行政总监。图什么？Office套餐自带，不花一分钱，还能在年终汇报上写\"数字化协同全面落地\"。结果呢？他自己的状态每天自动变成\"离开\"，因为他开会总忘了点\"举手\"；他撤回的消息显示\"此消息已被删除\"，大家早就截图了。后来Teams又崩了一次，IT被骂惨，他被调去当\"Teams使用培训官\"，每天负责回答\"为什么我进不了会议室\"。现在他的签名是：有事@我，别@所有人。"}, "b": {"item_id": "1fd1f04cdc79", "text": "逼我们用 Teams 的人，我研究过了，是 IT 总监。他的理由我听懂了：买了 Microsoft 365，Teams 免费送。他不是选了个软件，他是领了个赠品，还拿去年底述职：\"我给公司省了三十万！\"省的是软件钱，花的是我们的命。现在全公司表面在 Teams 上，实际都在微信群里问：\"那个会议链接到底点哪儿？\"最惨的是他自己，每次他一发言，头像都是黄色的\"离开\"，人明明坐在那儿，系统说他不在。推广一年，他终于被系统说中了：这个人，在公司，已经离开了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0920", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "stinky-tofu", "target_name": "臭豆腐", "facts": ["Tofu soaked for hours to a day or two in a brine that has itself fermented for months from vegetables such as bamboo shoots and mustard greens, often with dried shrimp or meat; the smell is the brine's, not the bean's.", "Changsha's version is black, deep-fried and doused in chili; Taiwan's night-market version is golden and served with pickled cabbage; Shaoxing's, brined with amaranth stalks, is fried pale yellow or steamed with other 'stinky' pickles.", "Legend credits Wang Zhihe, a Qing-era exam candidate who left his unsold tofu too long in 1669; a Beijing fermented-bean-curd brand still carries his name.", "Street vendors are its main outlet, and the smell carries for a block."], "a": {"item_id": "0db2c726fd64", "text": "街边吃臭豆腐的人，我观察过，全是传教士。你路过，他嘴里正烫得直哈气，还要腾出一只手拦你：“闻着臭，吃着香！”大哥，这句话我从小听到大，你说得跟刚悟出来的一样。\n\n他图什么？图的就是那个反差。全世界都捂鼻子，就他看透了本质。一块发酵的豆腐，硬是吃出了顿悟的表情。\n\n然后呢？然后他上地铁了。五站路，整节车厢都在低头检查自己的鞋。他还挺得意：凡人，不懂。\n\n最惨的是那根牙签。一碗六块，就配一根，戳到最后一块，辣汤顺着手腕流进袖口。第二天开会他一抬手，领导皱眉：“小王，你家下水道是不是堵了？”"}, "b": {"item_id": "0bded105eb6e", "text": "街边吃臭豆腐的人，我太懂他们了。他们是谁？三种：刚失恋的，想拍视频的，还有刚跟朋友吵完架、非要证明自己“敢吃”的。图什么？图那句“闻着臭，吃着香”。结果呢？香没吃出来，先吃到一滴红油，啪嗒，掉在白T恤上，像刚被人当街抽了一耳光。他拎着那袋酸菜站在公交站，三米之内没人敢靠近。上车，司机把窗全打开了，全车乘客齐刷刷看他，他举着手机说：“这条视频能火。”到家洗了三遍头，味儿还在。第二天约会，对方捏着鼻子问：“你身上是不是有股……”他说：“臭豆腐，昨天吃的。”对方沉默两秒：“哦，我还以为是你前任留下的。”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0921", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "brasilia", "target_name": "巴西利亚", "facts": ["Inaugurated on 21 April 1960 after about 41 months of construction, moving the capital inland from Rio de Janeiro.", "Lúcio Costa's Plano Piloto is laid out like an airplane (or a bird, or a cross), with Oscar Niemeyer's government buildings along the fuselage.", "Listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1987, less than 30 years after it was built.", "Planned for 500,000 residents; the Federal District now has about 3 million, most in satellite towns outside the plan.", "Built for the car: wide avenues, few pedestrian crossings, and distances that make walking impractical."], "a": {"item_id": "83f9dfea8a34", "text": "在巴西利亚靠两条腿出门的，基本是两种人：建筑系学生，和刚下飞机的游客。学生是来朝圣的，说尼迈耶的建筑要\"用身体丈量\"。行，量吧。\n\n地图上，酒店到国会大厦一公里，看着像隔壁。你一走才发现，中间是八车道的Eixo Monumental，没有路口，只有立交桥，立交桥底下还有立交桥。你走了四十分钟，国会大厦一直在你正前方，又大又白，像吊在驴脑袋前面的胡萝卜，就是不让你靠近。\n\n走到第三十分钟，你终于崩溃，决定直接穿过草坪抄近道。恭喜，你踩出了一条\"欲望之路\"。\n\n最惨的是，你以为自己在反抗城市规划，其实你是在免费给规划师交作业。\n\n最后你浑身是汗，湿度百分之十，嘴唇干得像盖了章。走到门口，保安问你：您的车呢？"}, "b": {"item_id": "bb6fc598ef80", "text": "在巴西利亚靠两条腿出门的人，我服你们。你们都是什么神仙？大概三种：一种是退休大爷，每天走八公里，说是锻炼，其实是不想在家跟老伴大眼瞪小眼；一种是刚来的外地人，觉得\"这城市这么规整，走走就到了\"，二十分钟后他站在Eixão的六车道中间，像被冲上岸的海带；还有一种是非要证明自己有\"城市精神\"的人。图什么？图个\"我不开车也能活\"的面子。落得什么下场？你在308北和308南之间绕了一个小时，因为楼都长得一模一样。最后你走进一个部委大院，门卫问你找谁，你说找公交车站。公交车站？它在你身后三公里，中间隔着一条没有人行道的高速，隔着你的人生。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0922", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "windows-vista", "target_name": "Windows Vista", "facts": ["Released to consumers in January 2007, after the 'Longhorn' project was reset in 2004 and headline features such as the WinFS file system were dropped.", "User Account Control prompted so often that Apple's 'Get a Mac' campaign ran an ad about 'Cancel or Allow'.", "'Windows Vista Capable' stickers went on PCs that could only run Home Basic, without the Aero interface, and led to a class-action lawsuit.", "Windows 7 (2009), widely praised, was largely a tuned-up Vista on the same kernel line; Vista support ended in April 2017."], "a": {"item_id": "0edc712dc233", "text": "2007年第一天就装Vista的人，我认识一个。他还买的旗舰版，两千多，就为了微软承诺的“独家福利”，最后等来一个德州扑克和一张会动的桌面。他电脑上贴着“Vista Capable”，意思是“能装”，没说“能用”。512兆内存硬开Aero毛玻璃，风扇响得像在替他喊冤。打印机没驱动，摄像头没驱动，就侧边栏那个小时钟特别准，每一秒都在提醒他后悔。最狠的是那个弹窗：“是否允许此程序更改？”微软问了他一千遍，他一千遍都点了“允许”，从来没想过点“取消”。现在他逢人就说自己是最早的尝鲜派。不，兄弟，你是最早降级回XP的那批。"}, "b": {"item_id": "ebfddca0e411", "text": "第一天就升级Vista的人，你们公司肯定有一个，就是那个说“我对电脑懂一点”的。\n\n他图什么？图毛玻璃。Aero，窗口是半透明的，他觉得自己电脑一下子有钱了。结果他那台机器贴着“Vista Capable”的贴纸，意思是“能运行Vista”，但只能运行没有毛玻璃的Vista。花钱升级，就为了得到一个不透明的窗口。\n\n然后是UAC。他点一下，弹窗：“是否允许？”允许。又弹：“您确定吗？”确定。一天里被问“确定”的次数，比他求婚还多。\n\n打印机没驱动，声卡没驱动，最后他默默把XP装回去。同事问：“怎么样？”\n\n他说：“挺好，我体验了一下，什么叫花钱买降级。”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0923", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "nfts", "target_name": "NFT", "facts": ["Beeple's 'Everydays: The First 5000 Days' sold at Christie's for $69.3 million in March 2021.", "OpenSea, the largest NFT marketplace, processed a record $2.7 billion in trades on 1 May 2022 and just $9.34 million on 28 August 2022, a 99% drop, according to DappRadar data.", "A 2023 dappGambl study found that 95% of the NFT collections it examined had a market value of zero.", "The token usually records only a URL; the image itself sits on an ordinary server or IPFS, not on the blockchain.", "Collins Dictionary named 'NFT' its word of the year for 2021."], "a": {"item_id": "36fed9141b46", "text": "我有个朋友，2021年花三万块买了一张猴子的图。无聊猿，戴墨镜叼烟斗那种。我问他图什么，他说这是数字资产，全世界独一无二。我说你右键另存为，它也能一模一样出现在你电脑里。他说不一样，我在链上有所有权。我问所有权是什么，他想了半天说：\"这只猴子是我的。\"光矿工费就够买台二手车。后来地板价跌了九成，群里一夜之间全是\"早期参与者\"，项目方带着钱消失了。他现在头像还是那只猴子，我看他是真的戴上了墨镜，不敢抬头见人。"}, "b": {"item_id": "6da2286dc224", "text": "2021年买NFT那帮兄弟，平时买杯奶茶都要比三家团购，结果花八万块，买了一张猴子图的“所有权”。注意，不是图，是所有权。图谁都能右键另存为，你那张跟我存的那张一个像素都不差，区别是你那张会心疼。\n\n图什么？图头像。八万块设成微信头像，开口先让人看猴。天天在Discord里喊“WAGMI”，我们都会成功。后来确实一起了，地板价从八万掉到八百。\n\n最惨的是光Gas费就交了两千，比猴现在还贵。问他猴呢？在钱包里。钱包呢？助记词抄在纸上。纸呢？被他妈当废纸卖了。全家最懂Web3的，是收废品的大爷。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0924", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "papyrus", "target_name": "Papyrus 字体", "facts": ["Hand-drawn by Chris Costello in 1982 over about six months, when he was 23; he sold the rights to Letraset for $750.", "Bundled with macOS and with Microsoft Office, which is why it appears on so many menus and signs.", "James Cameron's Avatar (2009) used it for the film's logo and subtitles.", "A 2017 Saturday Night Live sketch starring Ryan Gosling is about a man unable to get over that choice."], "a": {"item_id": "1155b79dd841", "text": "我们这条街，艾灸馆、素食馆、足疗店，招牌全是Papyrus，就是那种笔画毛毛糙糙、像拿烧焦的树枝在牛皮纸上划出来的字体。\n\n老板们图什么？图\"古法\"\"天然\"\"千年底蕴\"。设计师报价三千，他一想：Word里自带的，免费，还自带古埃及气质，血赚。\n\n可《阿凡达》砸了二十多亿美元，片名用的就是Papyrus，被全世界笑了十几年。你一个六十平的刮痧店，凭什么觉得自己扛得住？\n\n现在客人走到门口，抬头一看\"古法艾灸\"，字还带着焦边，真不知道是进去放松，还是进去防腐。\n\n最惨的是素食馆老板，招牌、菜单、外卖袋全是Papyrus，一份麻婆豆腐，看着像刚从墓里刨出来的。他还纳闷生意怎么越来越淡。兄弟，你卖的是出土文物。"}, "b": {"item_id": "eb2c4af932ef", "text": "你们发现没有，开养生馆的老板，都觉得自己特有灵性。他要什么？“古朴、自然、带点异域。”然后他打开Word，字体往下拉，拉到P，眼睛一亮：Papyrus！边缘毛毛糙糙，像拿芦苇杆蘸墨写在莎草纸上。老板一拍大腿：就它，古埃及的灵性！\n\n大哥，你卖的是艾灸，不是木乃伊。\n\n隔壁泰国菜也这么想，楼上墨西哥卷饼也这么想。一条街的“异域风情”，全是电脑自带的同一款免费字体。\n\n最惨的是，卡梅隆花几个亿拍《阿凡达》，片名用的也是它。所以你推门进去，技师一抬头，你第一反应不是放松，是想问：“蓝色那位在几号房？”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0925", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "stinky-tofu", "target_name": "臭豆腐", "facts": ["Tofu soaked for hours to a day or two in a brine that has itself fermented for months from vegetables such as bamboo shoots and mustard greens, often with dried shrimp or meat; the smell is the brine's, not the bean's.", "Changsha's version is black, deep-fried and doused in chili; Taiwan's night-market version is golden and served with pickled cabbage; Shaoxing's, brined with amaranth stalks, is fried pale yellow or steamed with other 'stinky' pickles.", "Legend credits Wang Zhihe, a Qing-era exam candidate who left his unsold tofu too long in 1669; a Beijing fermented-bean-curd brand still carries his name.", "Street vendors are its main outlet, and the smell carries for a block."], "a": {"item_id": "5723ee516250", "text": "街边吃臭豆腐的人，我见过三种。第一种是下班的社畜，西装还没脱，站在摊子前左顾右盼，像在做贼。他图什么？图那口“香”。结果油锅一开，汤汁溅到白衬衫上，那味道从领口直接往上飘。地铁上三米之内全体让座，不是尊重他，是怕被熏晕。第二种是情侣。女生说“就尝一口”，男生陪着吃，两个人嘴里都是霉豆腐和辣酱的味儿，接吻的念头当场熄火。第三种最惨，是我室友。他一边吃一边说“这味儿才地道”，回宿舍后，全层楼的人都知道他吃了什么。他洗了三遍澡，衣服挂阳台上晾了一礼拜，他妈视频的时候还问他：你是不是去化工厂上班了？"}, "b": {"item_id": "083ba71caee5", "text": "街边吃臭豆腐的人，我观察过，全是一个表情：一边嚼一边皱眉，一边皱眉一边跟你说“闻着臭，吃着香”。哥，这句话你每吃一块都要念一遍，说明你自己也不太信，你这是在给自己做法。\n\n图什么？图那个味儿。可那个味儿你根本不用吃啊，你从摊前路过三米，它已经替你吃过一遍了。\n\n最惨的是吃完。人家吃烤串，擦擦嘴就走；你吃完，嘴擦干净了，味儿还在加班。进电梯，一电梯人开始互相怀疑；回到家，你妈第一句不是“吃饭没”，是“你刚从哪个厕所出来”。\n\n八块钱，买了一身洗不掉的嫌疑。还给你浇了辣椒。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0926", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "oat-milk", "target_name": "燕麦奶", "facts": ["Oatly started in Sweden in the 1990s, built on Lund University research by Rickard Öste on lactose intolerance.", "Oatly's slogan 'It's like milk but made for humans' was banned in Sweden in 2015 after the dairy lobby LRF Mjölk sued; Oatly then published the lawsuit as advertising.", "Oatly listed on Nasdaq in May 2021 at a valuation near $10 billion; the shares later lost more than 90% of their value.", "Commercial oat milk is made by breaking oat starch into maltose with amylase enzymes, and many brands, Oatly among them, add rapeseed oil; the result has more sugar and a higher glycaemic index than almond or soy milk."], "a": {"item_id": "0f1dbb4a84d2", "text": "我有个同事，点拿铁必须换燕麦奶，加五块。问他为啥，他说乳糖不耐。上礼拜我亲眼看他干掉一整块芝士蛋糕，面不改色。乳糖不耐？我看你是乳糖不耐“便宜”。\n\n后来他又改口，说是为了健康、为了地球。哥，燕麦奶想打出奶泡，里面得加油，菜籽油。你每天早上花三十八，喝一杯“过了油的燕麦水”，还觉得自己在拯救地球。\n\n最惨的是，小时候你奶奶天天早上给你煮燕麦粥，你嫌弃，说“这是马吃的”。现在你多花五块钱，把粥兑进咖啡里，还拍照发朋友圈。\n\n马都没你讲究。马吃燕麦，至少不用排队。"}, "b": {"item_id": "794cf9f48948", "text": "有一种人，点拿铁非要加一句“换燕麦奶”。你问为什么，他说“对身体好”。好在哪？他说不上来，但语气笃定，像刚发现了新大陆。\n\n这杯燕麦奶拿铁，奶泡两分钟就塌成一层白沫浮在上面。喝到最后，杯底是一层灰褐色的东西，像把麦片泡在水里忘了捞。\n\n每杯多收五块五，一个月下来一百六十多。他管这叫“投资健康”。\n\n体检报告出来，医生看着他说：“您血糖有点高。”他愣住：“我没加糖啊。”医生说：“燕麦奶本身就带甜，您那杯还加了两泵糖浆。”\n\n他沉默了一会儿，说：“我就是为了不喝糖，才换的燕麦奶。”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0927", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "juicero", "target_name": "Juicero 榨汁机", "facts": ["A Wi-Fi-connected cold-press juicer launched in 2016 at $699, later cut to $399, that squeezed proprietary bags of chopped produce sold by subscription.", "Raised about $120 million from investors including Google Ventures and Kleiner Perkins.", "In April 2017, Bloomberg reporters squeezed the packs by hand and got 7.5 ounces of juice in 90 seconds, faster than the machine's 8 ounces in about two minutes.", "Each pack carried a QR code that the press scanned and checked online before squeezing; it refused expired packs and would not run at all unless connected to Wi-Fi.", "Shut down in September 2017 and offered refunds on the machine."], "a": {"item_id": "e1fe9018b6c6", "text": "买 Juicero 的人我研究过：住旧金山，爱喝\"冷压\"，觉得自己动手捏个袋子太掉价，得花 400 美元雇一台联网的机器替他捏。\n\n这机器号称四吨压力。后来彭博社发现，徒手捏那个果汁包，出汁还更快。也就是说，这帮人花 400 块，买的是\"捏袋子需要 WiFi\"的权利。\n\n更绝的是，果汁包过期，机器直接罢工。它比你妈还关心你的健康。\n\n最后公司倒闭，全额退款。钱拿回来了，脸没拿回来。从此这帮人看自己的手，都带着一种复杂的情绪：这玩意儿，一直是免费的。"}, "b": {"item_id": "42eeb6dfde70", "text": "Juicero 要 400 美元，干的事是把一包切好的果蔬塞进去压成汁。问题是，那包东西你用手也能捏出来。做这台机器的是硅谷一帮创始人，想颠覆榨汁行业，融了一个多亿美元，图的是你每个月订阅他们的果蔬包，一包好几美元，还得联网认证。后来有记者拿它的包装袋直接用手挤，汁照样出来。投资人一看，哦，原来我们投的不是榨汁机，是一台给包装袋收月费的收费站。公司两年后就关门了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0928", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "risk", "target_name": "《战国风云》（Risk）", "facts": ["Invented in 1957 by Albert Lamorisse, the French director of The Red Balloon, as La Conquête du Monde; Parker Brothers published it in the United States in 1959.", "The map has 42 territories, among them Kamchatka, Irkutsk, and Yakutsk, while the entire Middle East is one territory.", "Holding Australia, which has a single entry point, is the standard opening strategy.", "A full game can run for hours, and players eliminated early have nothing to do but watch."], "a": {"item_id": "407cba2981c5", "text": "有一种人，非要把《战国风云》玩到底。开局说好两小时吃完饭就散，结果凌晨三点，他还在盯着澳大利亚那块地，因为守它能多拿两个兵。他图什么？不图赢，图的是那句\"我占领了全世界\"。他掷三个六，对面掷两个一，他拍桌子喊\"天命所归\"。最后呢？他手里攥着整个北美，兵力只剩一颗，全世界就剩他和堪察加边上一个不肯投降的阿拉斯加。窗外天亮了，他还在算下一轮能不能凑齐一套骑兵卡。"}, "b": {"item_id": "8083e937dc92", "text": "玩《战国风云》非要玩到底的人，都是觉得世界和平太无聊，想亲手重来一遍的。开局大家都说：“咱们友好点，随便玩玩。”四十分钟后，你的盟友从堪察加半岛给了你一刀。\n\n你还迷信澳洲：就一个入口，每回合白送两个兵，守住就能赢。六个小时后你真守住了，全世界就剩你缩在澳洲，对面三个骰子一扔，你的兵像袋鼠一样被撞飞。\n\n赢的人在欢呼，输的人在找外套，还有一个人早就退群了。整局唯一的和平提议，是凌晨两点那句“要不平局吧”。没人同意，因为每个人都觉得，再扔两把，自己就翻盘了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0929", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hydrogen-cars", "target_name": "氢燃料电池汽车", "facts": ["Toyota launched the Mirai in 2014; 'mirai' means 'future' in Japanese.", "Nearly all US hydrogen filling stations are in California; Shell closed all seven of its California passenger-car stations in February 2024.", "More than 95% of the world's hydrogen is made from fossil fuels, mostly by steam-reforming natural gas.", "California pump prices reached about $36 per kilogram in 2023-24, over $200 for a Mirai fill; Toyota has handed buyers fuel cards worth $15,000.", "Mirai owners filed a class action against Toyota in July 2024 over hydrogen shortages and station outages; a second suit in 2025 sought at least $5.7 billion."], "a": {"item_id": "31458abe94e2", "text": "有一种人专门买氢燃料电池车。他们的理由很宏大：我要为人类的未来开车。结果未来还没到，先到了加氢站门口。这种站一般开在郊区，像个地下党接头点，营业时间比银行还短，还得提前在App上抢号，跟抢演唱会门票一个难度。好不容易排上了，一公斤氢六七十块，加满一箱，司机心疼得直喊\"这不是加氢，是放血\"。最讽刺的是，这车一天到晚排出来的是水，环保是真环保，就是你的钱全变成了水。等到加氢站哪天悄悄拆了，车就停进车库，成了全国最贵的加湿器，每天擦一擦，等着哪天氢气自己从天上掉下来。"}, "b": {"item_id": "7f3ef4cfecbe", "text": "我有个朋友买了氢燃料电池车，他说电动车太俗，他要“跳过一代，直接活在未来”。\n\n结果未来没来，他先搬家了。\n\n为啥？全市就俩加氢站，一个在开发区，一个在“维护中”。他现在挑对象第一句话不问工作，问：“你家离加氢站多远？”\n\n他最爱跟人吹：“我加满只要三分钟！”对，三分钟加满，四十分钟开过去，到了还得排在一溜儿氢能公交后面。\n\n他还特骄傲：“我这车尾气是水，能喝！”兄弟，你花三十万，就为了买台会开的净水器？\n\n前两天他想卖车，二手商看了一眼，说：“这车我收，但你得告诉我，买家去哪儿加氢。”\n\n他沉默了。他终于明白自己确实活在未来：在这片土地上，有这车的人还没出生。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0930", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hsq", "target_name": "HSQ 电子束光刻胶", "facts": ["Hydrogen silsesquioxane, sold by Dow Corning as FOx ('flowable oxide') and designed as a spin-on dielectric for chip interconnects; lithographers adopted it as a negative-tone resist.", "Exposure converts its cage structure into a cross-linked, silica-like network, so the developed pattern is essentially SiO2.", "Holds the resolution records for electron-beam lithography: isolated features around 5 nm, dense lines below 10 nm half-pitch, with high-contrast 'salty' NaOH/NaCl development.", "Must be kept refrigerated (Dow Corning's datasheet gives a six-month shelf life at 5 °C); the solution ages into a jelly, and its effective sensitivity shifts with processing delays because cross-linking continues after exposure.", "DuPont, which inherited Dow Corning's FOx/XR-1541 HSQ line, discontinued it and ended production at the end of 2025; labs such as EPFL's CMi switched to third-party HSQ from EM Resist or DisChem, both formulated from AQM powder."], "a": {"item_id": "650f7a5cc2ec", "text": "有个师兄为了分辨率，非要换HSQ。我问他图什么，他说十纳米以下的线条好看。好看是好看，可这玩意儿得冷藏，跟酸奶似的，放久了自己变稠，成了凝胶。旋涂的时候像往芯片上刷芝麻糊。显影还得用碱液，线条是立住了，一吹氮气，全倒成一排麦子。最惨的是曝光完它会变成二氧化硅，刻不掉，永久留在芯片上。他的论文发了，芯片留给了下一届，下一届一打开显微镜，满屏都是他的遗产。"}, "b": {"item_id": "53375ff05c30", "text": "做纳米加工的博士生都有一个信仰：PMMA 分辨率不够，换 HSQ。换完你才发现，这不是换胶，是领养了一个早产儿。\n\n它得在冰箱里供着，用之前先回温，像伺候祖宗。旋完胶它就开始老化，你俩的关系是\"每天都不一样\"。显影用 25% 的 TMAH，有人为了对比度还往里加盐。头一回听说做精密加工往显影液里放盐的，厨子都没这么讲究。\n\n灵敏度还低，电子束机排队三个星期，一个小图案写一整晚。好不容易写出 8 纳米的线，一显影，塌了，粘一块儿，跟煮过头的面一样。\n\n就算没塌，HSQ 曝光完变成二氧化硅，洗不掉，永远留在你的器件上。你图啥？图论文里一张 SEM 图。导师看完说：\"挺好，再做个大面积的。\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0931", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "las-vegas", "target_name": "拉斯维加斯", "facts": ["Nevada legalized casino gambling in 1931, the year construction of the Hoover Dam began nearby.", "Most of the Strip lies not in the city of Las Vegas but in the unincorporated town of Paradise.", "The Sphere, opened in 2023 at a cost of about $2.3 billion, is the largest spherical building in the world.", "'What happens here, stays here' was launched by the local convention and visitors authority in 2003.", "Lake Mead, its water source, fell in July 2022 to about 1,040 feet, its lowest since the reservoir first filled in 1937, and dropped below that mark again in August 2026."], "a": {"item_id": "ade69157daa3", "text": "去拉斯维加斯发财的人，我真想问：你是觉得那些酒店是慈善机构吗？沙漠里一座城，没河没矿，全靠你们盖起来的。威尼斯人酒店里那条运河，是你的房贷挖的。\n\n出发前都说\"我就玩两百块\"，还说研究过策略，看了三小时视频：\"押红色，输了就翻倍。\"哥们，赌桌有下注上限，你翻到第七把，就剩尊严了。\n\n下场都一样：第一天赢八十，觉得自己是赌神；第二天在没有窗户没有钟的赌场里喝免费啤酒，不知道几点，也不知道自己是谁；第三天在机场，把回程的钱塞进老虎机。对，麦卡伦机场就有老虎机，怕你还没到市区就想通了。\n\n最后朋友圈发张照片：在猫王教堂，跟刚认识的人结婚了。发财没发成，倒是多了个人来分你剩下的。"}, "b": {"item_id": "bd02132185ac", "text": "我有个朋友，看了三遍《决胜21点》，觉得自己会算牌，买了张去拉斯维加斯的机票，说要去沙漠里挖金子。\n\n兄弟，人家能在沙漠里建一座城，靠的就是你这种来浇水的。\n\n他到了才发现，赌场里没窗户、没钟，酒还免费。你品，一个地方怕你知道几点、还主动把你灌醉，它是想让你赢吗？\n\n三天后他回来了，钱全没了，就剩一张自助餐券，外加一张小教堂的结婚证，证婚人是“猫王”，新娘是谁他想不起来。\n\n他还安慰自己：“在维加斯发生的，就留在维加斯。”\n\n对，你的钱也是这么想的。\n\n最绝的是候机的时候，他看见登机口旁边还有一排老虎机，又塞进去二十块。拉斯维加斯就是这样，连你走，都要再收一回过路费。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0932", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "macau", "target_name": "澳门", "facts": ["A Portuguese settlement from the 1550s until the handover to China in December 1999.", "Casino revenue passed the Las Vegas Strip's in 2006, and at its 2013 peak, about $45 billion, was roughly seven times the Strip's.", "One company, STDM, held the casino monopoly for four decades until licences opened to competition in 2002.", "Among the most densely populated places on Earth, at roughly 20,000 people per square kilometre.", "The Cotai Strip stands on land reclaimed from the sea between the islands of Taipa and Coloane."], "a": {"item_id": "b300422ef6ef", "text": "我有个朋友，在家打麻将输了八千，说要去澳门翻本。这就像在小区泳池呛了口水，决定去太平洋练练。\n\n去的时候坐赌场免费大巴，人家专车接你进去，因为知道出来那趟，你得自己顺着港珠澳大桥走回去。\n\n三天后我在大三巴见到他，对着那面墙拍照。我说你拍它干嘛？他说：“亲切。教堂一把火烧光了，就剩个门脸撑着，跟我现在一模一样。”\n\n最后他在葡京边上的当铺把手表当了，买了一盒葡挞回家。他妈问输了多少，他说：“没输，我去吃葡挞了。一盒六个，四万一个。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "56ba73fdce82", "text": "去澳门翻本的人，基本都是在内地被套急了的。股票亏了三十万，跟老婆说：“我去澳门，一把就回来。”一把！你在A股被按着打了三年，指望在百家乐桌上三分钟补回来？你这不叫翻本，你是去给澳门送第二茬本。\n\n他们还特别有原则：赢回三十万就走。结果赢到二十九万，心想，差一万，凑个整。威尼斯人里那片假天空永远是下午四点，你输到凌晨，抬头天还亮着，你以为自己才来一会儿。\n\n下场也统一：先当手表，赌场旁边那家当铺，老板比你老婆还了解你。最后口袋剩二十块，在大三巴买个蛋挞，边吃边发呆。\n\n回关闸的免费大巴上，一路没人说话。因为来的时候喊“一把回本”的那几个人，现在全在这辆车上。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0933", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "nfts", "target_name": "NFT", "facts": ["Beeple's 'Everydays: The First 5000 Days' sold at Christie's for $69.3 million in March 2021.", "OpenSea, the largest NFT marketplace, processed a record $2.7 billion in trades on 1 May 2022 and just $9.34 million on 28 August 2022, a 99% drop, according to DappRadar data.", "A 2023 dappGambl study found that 95% of the NFT collections it examined had a market value of zero.", "The token usually records only a URL; the image itself sits on an ordinary server or IPFS, not on the blockchain.", "Collins Dictionary named 'NFT' its word of the year for 2021."], "a": {"item_id": "6da2286dc224", "text": "2021年买NFT那帮兄弟，平时买杯奶茶都要比三家团购，结果花八万块，买了一张猴子图的“所有权”。注意，不是图，是所有权。图谁都能右键另存为，你那张跟我存的那张一个像素都不差，区别是你那张会心疼。\n\n图什么？图头像。八万块设成微信头像，开口先让人看猴。天天在Discord里喊“WAGMI”，我们都会成功。后来确实一起了，地板价从八万掉到八百。\n\n最惨的是光Gas费就交了两千，比猴现在还贵。问他猴呢？在钱包里。钱包呢？助记词抄在纸上。纸呢？被他妈当废纸卖了。全家最懂Web3的，是收废品的大爷。"}, "b": {"item_id": "a8cfb67f2de5", "text": "2021年买NFT的人，我身边就有一个。以前连拼多多砍一刀都嫌麻烦，那年突然跟我说：我是数字艺术收藏家。我问收藏的啥，他给我看一只猴子，戴着墨镜，一脸没睡醒。十八万。\n\n我说这图我右键另存为就有了。他说：你那叫复制，我这叫所有权。我说所有权在哪？他说在区块链上。我说那你家里挂的是啥？他说：我挂在Twitter头像上。\n\n十八万，买个头像框。还得天天在群里跟一群头像也是猴子的陌生人说\"gm\"，互道早安。\n\n现在地板价跌了九成，他每天刷OpenSea，那只猴子挂出去半年，连个问价的都没有。他说他是钻石手，死拿不卖。\n\n兄弟，你不是钻石手，你是那只猴子唯一的粉丝。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0934", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hydrogen-cars", "target_name": "氢燃料电池汽车", "facts": ["Toyota launched the Mirai in 2014; 'mirai' means 'future' in Japanese.", "Nearly all US hydrogen filling stations are in California; Shell closed all seven of its California passenger-car stations in February 2024.", "More than 95% of the world's hydrogen is made from fossil fuels, mostly by steam-reforming natural gas.", "California pump prices reached about $36 per kilogram in 2023-24, over $200 for a Mirai fill; Toyota has handed buyers fuel cards worth $15,000.", "Mirai owners filed a class action against Toyota in July 2024 over hydrogen shortages and station outages; a second suit in 2025 sought at least $5.7 billion."], "a": {"item_id": "7f3ef4cfecbe", "text": "我有个朋友买了氢燃料电池车，他说电动车太俗，他要“跳过一代，直接活在未来”。\n\n结果未来没来，他先搬家了。\n\n为啥？全市就俩加氢站，一个在开发区，一个在“维护中”。他现在挑对象第一句话不问工作，问：“你家离加氢站多远？”\n\n他最爱跟人吹：“我加满只要三分钟！”对，三分钟加满，四十分钟开过去，到了还得排在一溜儿氢能公交后面。\n\n他还特骄傲：“我这车尾气是水，能喝！”兄弟，你花三十万，就为了买台会开的净水器？\n\n前两天他想卖车，二手商看了一眼，说：“这车我收，但你得告诉我，买家去哪儿加氢。”\n\n他沉默了。他终于明白自己确实活在未来：在这片土地上，有这车的人还没出生。"}, "b": {"item_id": "a7c3aeb313ce", "text": "我朋友非要买氢燃料电池车，说排出来的是水，环保。我说对，排的是水，你的钱也是一滴一滴没的，看不见。\n\n买这车的人分两种，一种是科技宅，觉得自己活在电影里；另一种是单位采购，花的是补贴，车是别人的。他图什么？图那句\"我开的是未来\"。结果未来在哪？在郊区一座加氢站，得提前预约，排队排到怀疑人生。站一关门，车就成了铁疙瘩，电堆里那点铂催化剂，比他的存款还金贵。\n\n最绝的是氢分子最小，能从70兆帕的罐子里漏出去。他的工资也一样，还没捂热，就从卡里漏没了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0935", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "monopoly", "target_name": "大富翁（Monopoly）", "facts": ["Descends from Lizzie Magie's The Landlord's Game, patented in 1904 to teach the evils of land monopoly in the spirit of Henry George.", "Charles Darrow sold his version to Parker Brothers in 1935 and was credited as the inventor; Parker bought Magie's patent for $500.", "The official rules require any unbought property to be auctioned, and 'Free Parking' pays nothing; the house rules that skip the auction and pile cash on Free Parking are why games drag.", "During the Second World War, British intelligence had maps, compasses, and real currency hidden in sets sent to prisoner-of-war camps."], "a": {"item_id": "f719efc18290", "text": "每年过年，总有亲戚提议：“一家人玩个大富翁，热闹热闹。”热闹？这游戏的胜利条件是：让你亲妈破产。\n\n图什么？平时不好意思跟你爸算的账，全在这块板上算了。我爸一辈子没买过房，一摸骰子就开始囤地，一条街全拿下，盖满酒店，等我路过，笑得像个开发商。我妈当银行，银行行长自己也下场玩，这在现实里叫什么？叫进去。\n\n我舅连着三轮进监狱，出来第一句：“谁欠我过路费？”\n\n玩到凌晨一点，没人赢，全在抵押。最后我弟把桌子一掀，地契满天飞。第二天早饭，没人说话。团圆了，账还没算完。"}, "b": {"item_id": "9bfb2dd2da6e", "text": "每年过年，我家都要玩一局大富翁。说好了增进感情，结果每次都是战争。我舅非要当银行家，抽屉一拉，像在管国库，你借两百块买块地，他能跟你讲半小时利率。我爸图的是赢。他说这叫培养孩子的商业头脑，其实是六十岁了还在抢我十二岁妹妹的绿色地皮。我妈最绝，从不买地，专坐在\"免费停车\"那格收钱，说这叫稳健。最后我姐在紫色街区盖满酒店，我爸一脚踩上去，房租交得他当场抵押了电视机。我舅把银行的钱全押在自己身上，宣布：\"我破产了，但我是清白的。\"那局没人赢，大家都进了监狱。过年那顿饭，全家坐一桌，谁也不说话，桌上摆着一堆假钞。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0936", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "php", "target_name": "PHP", "facts": ["Created by Rasmus Lerdorf in 1994 as 'Personal Home Page Tools', a set of C programs for tracking visitors to his own résumé page; the name now officially stands for the recursive 'PHP: Hypertext Preprocessor'.", "Lerdorf, in his 2007 MySQL Conference talk 'PHP on Hormones': 'I'm not a real programmer. I throw together things until it works then I move on.'", "Runs WordPress, which W3Techs counted on 39.4% of all websites (58.1% of sites with a known CMS) on 9 November 2026.", "Core function names are inconsistent (strpos, str_replace, htmlspecialchars, nl2br) partly because, as Lerdorf confirmed, early function names were chosen to balance a hash table keyed on string length.", "Until PHP 8 (2020), the comparison 0 == 'foo' was true because the string was silently converted to a number."], "a": {"item_id": "8dd77ec7826a", "text": "我有个朋友，靠写PHP吃饭。你问他干啥的，他第一句永远是“PHP是世界上最好的语言”，说完自己先笑。这是他这行唯一的笑点，也是唯一的尊严。\n\n他图啥？图门槛低。2010年自学三天，用织梦给楼下火锅店搭了个网站，收八百，觉得自己要财务自由了。十五年过去，他还在搭站，还是八百。火锅店倒了，现在接的是美甲店。\n\n他的工作流程：FileZilla连上服务器，直接改线上文件，刷新，白屏；再改，再刷新，又白屏。他管这叫“敏捷开发”。\n\n他代码里全是美元符号，一个变量一个$，写了一辈子$，就没见过几张。\n\n最近他焦虑，说PHP要死了。我说你放心，它死不了，全世界四成网站还挂在WordPress上呢。会死的是你。"}, "b": {"item_id": "0e4fd995e750", "text": "写PHP的人，都是被\"好找活\"骗进来的。全世界四成网站是WordPress，意味着永远有活。就像殡仪馆，永远有生意，但没人羡慕。\n\n入行第一天，每个变量前面都得带个美元符号。你以为是兆头，结果是嘲讽：满屏的$，一分不进兜。\n\n接手的全是前任留下的，一个文件三千行，HTML里插SQL，SQL里插HTML，中间一行注释：\"别动，会炸。\"写注释的人早跑了，你接手的不是项目，是文物，还得原地保护。\n\n每年还有人宣布\"PHP已死\"，他们都练出来了，一边点头一边给来宾递茶。\n\n最惨的是记函数。in_array先写要找的针，strpos先写草堆。干了十年，每次还得去查文档。\n\n别人的职业生涯叫积累，PHP程序员的叫：十年了，我还在大海里捞针，而且先放哪个，我还得查。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0937", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "pmma", "target_name": "PMMA 电子束光刻胶", "facts": ["The same polymer as Plexiglas and acrylic glass, dissolved in anisole or chlorobenzene and spun into a thin film.", "The oldest electron-beam resist still in everyday use; IBM researchers reported it as one in the late 1960s.", "Positive tone: the beam breaks polymer chains and the exposed region dissolves in a MIBK:IPA developer.", "Resolves below 10 nm but has poor sensitivity, so doses of hundreds to over a thousand µC/cm² (depending on voltage) make writing slow.", "Poor dry-etch resistance, so it is used mostly for lift-off rather than as an etch mask."], "a": {"item_id": "71032dd5e938", "text": "做电子束的人，你得理解，他们是一群对新材料过敏的人。你问为什么还在用PMMA，他一脸骄傲：“分辨率高，十纳米以下！”\n\n对，这玩意儿六十年代的，跟你家鱼缸、亚克力牙托是亲戚。他们拿牙托在刻十纳米。\n\n图什么？图稳。师兄留下的配方：950K，A4，4000转，180度烘三分钟，MIBK兑IPA一比三显影。没人知道为什么，但谁改谁延毕。\n\n下场呢？曝光剂量几百微库每平方厘米，一个图案写一晚上。第二天一看，束漂了，套刻对不上。好不容易写完去刻蚀，PMMA一见等离子体就没了，像纸糊的盾牌。\n\n最后博士五年读六年，论文里还得写：“PMMA以高分辨率著称。”\n\n是啊，胶是分辨率高、灵敏度低，人也是。对新材料，毫无反应。"}, "b": {"item_id": "d0b057bba930", "text": "做电子束光刻的朋友，我是真佩服你们。别人都上EUV了，你们还抱着950K的PMMA不撒手。电子一打，分子链断成小段，拿MIBK兑异丙醇泡一泡，图形就显出来了，干净得很。问题是它怕刻蚀，刻到一半自己先塌了。你们图什么？图十纳米的线条画得漂亮。然后呢？做剥离，丙酮泡一晚上，金属毛边比头发丝还多。博士师兄熬了三个月，论文第三章就是一张拖尾的SEM照片。导师看了一眼说：线条挺好，就是没法用。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0938", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "chicago-deep-dish", "target_name": "芝加哥深盘披萨", "facts": ["Created at Pizzeria Uno in Chicago in 1943; credit is disputed among founder Ike Sewell, his partner Ric Riccardo, and cook Rudy Malnati.", "Assembled upside down: sliced mozzarella on the dough, then toppings, then crushed tomatoes on top, so the cheese does not scorch during the long bake.", "Jon Stewart called it 'tomato soup in a bread bowl' and 'a casserole' on The Daily Show in 2013.", "Chicagoans' everyday pizza is thin 'tavern-style' cut into squares; GrubHub order data reported by WBEZ in 2013 showed city residents ordering non-deep-dish pizza about 10 to 1 over deep dish, with deep dish and stuffed making up under 9% of orders."], "a": {"item_id": "66023dd37676", "text": "芝加哥人为了证明深盘是披萨，付出了巨大的代价。你问他，他拍着胸脯说：“这就是披萨！”行，那披萨怎么要等四十五分钟？披萨是外卖，你这是预约手术。上来还得拿刀叉，芝士压在底下，酱糊在最上面，一个厚面壳兜着一锅东西。这不叫披萨，这叫往派里倒了碗番茄汤。\n\n他们图什么？就图跟纽约人吵架。纽约人说一句“那是砂锅”，芝加哥人当场气得再加点一个。结果呢，一块下去，人就饱了。剩下七块在冰箱里陪你三天。\n\n所以你看，这帮人为了捍卫深盘，每次都得亲自吃一遍。吃完瘫在沙发上，连吵架的力气都没了。纽约人单手折着一片，边走边吃，已经赢了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "e091429db8dc", "text": "管芝加哥深盘叫“披萨”的人，主要就两种：芝加哥本地人，和刚在Giordano's门口排了一小时队的游客。\n\n本地人图什么？面子。你们那儿冬天零下二十度，总得有样东西能拿出去跟纽约吵架吧。\n\n游客图什么？沉没成本。排队一小时，再等四十五分钟烤，端上来番茄酱在最上面，奶酪压在底下，四周一圈黄油面墙。你不叫它披萨，就得承认自己花三十八刀排队吃了一锅砂锅。\n\n下场呢？拿刀叉吃“披萨”，一块就饱，剩下的打包，回酒店一躺，消化得比烤得还久。第二天照样发朋友圈：“正宗芝加哥披萨！”配图一看，一个蛋糕模具。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0939", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "stinky-tofu", "target_name": "臭豆腐", "facts": ["Tofu soaked for hours to a day or two in a brine that has itself fermented for months from vegetables such as bamboo shoots and mustard greens, often with dried shrimp or meat; the smell is the brine's, not the bean's.", "Changsha's version is black, deep-fried and doused in chili; Taiwan's night-market version is golden and served with pickled cabbage; Shaoxing's, brined with amaranth stalks, is fried pale yellow or steamed with other 'stinky' pickles.", "Legend credits Wang Zhihe, a Qing-era exam candidate who left his unsold tofu too long in 1669; a Beijing fermented-bean-curd brand still carries his name.", "Street vendors are its main outlet, and the smell carries for a block."], "a": {"item_id": "0bded105eb6e", "text": "街边吃臭豆腐的人，我太懂他们了。他们是谁？三种：刚失恋的，想拍视频的，还有刚跟朋友吵完架、非要证明自己“敢吃”的。图什么？图那句“闻着臭，吃着香”。结果呢？香没吃出来，先吃到一滴红油，啪嗒，掉在白T恤上，像刚被人当街抽了一耳光。他拎着那袋酸菜站在公交站，三米之内没人敢靠近。上车，司机把窗全打开了，全车乘客齐刷刷看他，他举着手机说：“这条视频能火。”到家洗了三遍头，味儿还在。第二天约会，对方捏着鼻子问：“你身上是不是有股……”他说：“臭豆腐，昨天吃的。”对方沉默两秒：“哦，我还以为是你前任留下的。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "416c9c06dab4", "text": "吃街边臭豆腐的人，都有一句口头禅：\"闻着臭，吃着香。\"这哪是说豆腐，这是他们的人生总结，意思是：我知道我有问题，但你们别管。\n\n他们排四十分钟队，站在油锅旁边，边熏边等，像报了个自费烟熏疗程。拿到手一碗黑乎乎的东西，插着一根牙签，还得再淋勺辣酱，撒把香菜，仪式感拉满，吃的时候还不能坐，必须站在马路边，对着车尾气，吃得热泪盈眶。\n\n吃完以为结束了？它跟你回家了。头发入味，外套入味，围巾直接入股。进电梯，全楼的人齐刷刷看你，你还得假装是旁边那位的味。\n\n最惨的是，你自己闻不到了，因为你已经被腌透了。\n\n你就是那块臭豆腐，区别是，没人愿意为你排四十分钟的队。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0940", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "ugg", "target_name": "UGG 雪地靴", "facts": ["Sheepskin boots Australian surfers wore to warm up after sessions; Brian Smith started selling them in California in 1978.", "Deckers Outdoor bought the brand in 1995 and holds 'UGG' as a trademark in the US and elsewhere; in Australia, 'ugg' is a generic word any bootmaker may use.", "In 2019, a US jury found an Australian maker liable for selling boots labelled 'ugg' into the United States.", "Oprah Winfrey featured them in her 'Favorite Things' lists repeatedly in the 2000s, which drove the first boom.", "UGG is Deckers' largest brand, with over $2 billion in annual sales."], "a": {"item_id": "0ef8b911f7d9", "text": "我表哥一年四季穿UGG雪地靴。夏天三十五度，他穿着那双栗色的羊皮内里靴挤地铁，脚底跟焖在蒸笼里一样。我问他不热吗，他说这是态度。我说态度能当空调用？他说UGG要的就是这份松弛感。结果今年夏天，他那双靴子的羊毛内里全板结了，一股酸味，地铁上的人自动给他让出三米。他图的是别人觉得他不怕冷，结果大家只觉得他不怕臭。"}, "b": {"item_id": "52524a4d7862", "text": "有一类人，七月三十八度，脚上还蹬着UGG。你问她为啥，她说：“舒服，还不用穿袜子。”姐妹，那是羊毛内里替你把袜子穿了，还是加厚款。\n\n这鞋是澳洲人冲完浪上岸暖脚的。你在北京地铁里冲什么浪？冲的是汗。\n\n脚在里面，四周是羊毛，底下是羊皮，这不叫穿鞋，这叫铜锅：脚是肉，汗是汤。晚上去饭店要脱鞋，她一脱，全桌人当场不点羊肉了，已经闻饱了。\n\n最惨是下雨。UGG一沾水就硬，还掉色，所以夏天暴雨，她们在路口跳格子，跟拆雷一样。你以为她怕湿脚？她是在保护那一千多块的信仰。\n\n等到秋天，她还特骄傲：“你看，我终于穿对季节了。”对，那一个月，你和UGG的脚臭终于不是孤军奋战了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0941", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "keurig", "target_name": "Keurig 胶囊咖啡机", "facts": ["John Sylvan invented the K-Cup in the early 1990s and sold his stake for about $50,000 in 1997; in 2015 he told The Atlantic, 'I feel bad sometimes that I ever did it.'", "Keurig 2.0 (2014) read an ink mark on the lid and rejected unlicensed pods; sales fell and workarounds spread online.", "By 2014 enough K-Cups were sold each year to circle the Earth more than ten times; the pods were mostly unrecyclable #7 plastic until a switch to polypropylene by 2020.", "Keurig Green Mountain merged with Dr Pepper Snapple in 2018 to form Keurig Dr Pepper."], "a": {"item_id": "a23abc9807e4", "text": "用Keurig的人，就是想喝咖啡，但不想沾“做咖啡”的任何一个字。磨豆？累。烧水？累。你们的理想是按一下，然后盯着它嗡嗡响四十秒。这比烧壶水还久，但因为是按按钮，所以叫效率。\n\n算过账吗？一颗胶囊十克豆，折下来一斤两百多块。你付的是精品价，喝的是一根针扎穿锡纸挤出来的褐色热水。\n\n发明K杯那哥们儿自己都公开说后悔了，家里根本不用。发明人都退坑了，你们还在续费。\n\n最惨是那盒混合装，三个月后剩下的永远是那几颗榛子味。不喝，也不扔，就躺在抽屉里，跟你一样：方便，但没人要。"}, "b": {"item_id": "612741b2093f", "text": "你们办公室里有没有这种人？每天早上站在Keurig前面，按下去，等它“滋滋”响半分钟，端出一杯颜色像红茶、味道像热水的东西。他图什么？图的是“我也是讲究生活的人”。可这份讲究一个K杯一块多，一个月下来够买一台手冲壶了。更惨的是垃圾，那个小塑料杯带铝箔盖，还得掰开、分类，最后扔进去的还是塑料。他以为买的是咖啡，其实买的是“按一下就有”的错觉。结果一天一杯，喝到最后，他端着的是一杯加了热水的回忆。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0942", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "klingon", "target_name": "克林贡语", "facts": ["Created by linguist Marc Okrand for Star Trek III (1984), building on a few words the actor James Doohan improvised for the 1979 film.", "Deliberately alien grammar: object-verb-subject word order and no verb 'to be', so Hamlet's line became 'taH pagh taHbe'', 'continue or not continue'.", "The Klingon Language Institute (1992) published Hamlet 'in the original Klingon'; Bing Translator added Klingon in 2013 and Duolingo in 2018.", "Okrand's 1985 Klingon Dictionary listed roughly 1,500 words, and fluent speakers number only in the dozens: 20 to 30 in 2009, 30 to 50 by 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "40a35f67e92f", "text": "我真佩服那些把克林贡语学到流利的人。流利啊，那是花了十年。这语言有多不适合社交？它没有\"你好\"。最接近的是 nuqneH，字面意思是：\"你要干嘛？\"也没有\"谢谢\"，因为克林贡人不感恩。\n\n所以你想想这帮人的处境：苦练十年，全世界能聊的就几百个同好。终于约到一个，见面第一句，你问他：\"你要干嘛？\"他回你：\"你要干嘛？\"然后俩人就僵在那儿了。\n\n想表达感情怎么办？只能用他们的方式，比如：\"今天是死的好日子。\"你女朋友说\"我想你了\"，你深情地回一句：\"今天是死的好日子。\"\n\n最惨的是什么？你辛辛苦苦练的是宾语—动词—主语的语序。你想说\"我爱你\"，得说成\"你，爱，我\"。听着像在被审问。\n\n最后他们图什么？图一个能用外星语言吵赢别人的机会。结果遇上另一个同样流利的，两人吵了三小时，谁也不会说\"对不起\"。"}, "b": {"item_id": "45f4bb0c382a", "text": "我朋友花三年把克林贡语练到了流利。流利到什么程度？他跟我吵架，全程用克林贡语。吵完我才发现，他是对着镜子练的，因为我一句都听不懂。他图什么？他说这语言够硬气，念起来一嗓子呼噜，像刚从战场上下来。结果他去参加同好聚会，满屋子都是练了三年的人，互相拍胸脯、喷口水打招呼，嗓子全哑了，喝口水都得用喉音。后来他女朋友跟他分手，留了一句话：你说你想战死的时候，能不能先问问我想不想吃晚饭？"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0943", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hawaiian-pizza", "target_name": "夏威夷披萨", "facts": ["Invented in 1962 by Sam Panopoulos, a Greek immigrant, at the Satellite Restaurant in Chatham, Ontario, Canada; named after the brand of canned pineapple he used.", "Iceland's president Guðni Th. Jóhannesson said in 2017 that he would ban pineapple on pizza if he could, then clarified that a president has no such power.", "Hawaii is no longer a major pineapple producer: Del Monte shut its Hawaiian pineapple operations in November 2006, two years ahead of its announced 2008 exit, the state's last cannery closed in 2007, and most canned pineapple now comes from Thailand, Indonesia, and the Philippines.", "Panopoulos, whose restaurant also served Chinese dishes, told the Toronto Sun in 2010 that 'in those days people didn't mix the tastes like sweet and sour, except for Chinese food'."], "a": {"item_id": "b9921996d500", "text": "每桌聚餐总有那么一个人，菜单翻到最后一页，清清嗓子：“要不……来个夏威夷？”\n\n这人图什么？图个性。他觉得自己在叛逆：“我不走寻常路。”兄弟，夏威夷披萨是全世界最出名的争议披萨，你点它，就像在摇滚圈穿皮衣，一点都不稀奇。而且它跟夏威夷没关系，发明它的是个住在加拿大的希腊人。你连“异域风情”都没沾上。\n\n下场呢？披萨一上桌，全桌人都去拿别的，只有它孤零零摆着。你只能说“我就爱这口”，然后一个人硬啃六块。菠萝出水，饼底泡成湿毛巾，火腿片在上面游泳。吃不完打包回家，第二天微波炉一转，咬一口，热乎乎的菠萝配咸火腿，就像前任突然发来一句“最近好吗”，说不上哪里不对，就是哪都不对。"}, "b": {"item_id": "6ec4abe781be", "text": "点夏威夷披萨的人，骨子里都想当和事佬：甜的也要，咸的也要，菠萝火腿往饼上一铺，谁都不得罪。结果得罪了全世界。意大利人想报警，夏威夷人也想报警，因为这玩意儿是加拿大人发明的，跟夏威夷唯一的关系就是一听菠萝罐头。\n\n最惨是聚餐。八个人点四盒，他非要加一盒夏威夷的：“你们试试，真的好吃！”最后另外三盒全空了，就剩他那盒，八块，一块没动。他一个人坐那儿一块一块往下吃，吃到最后菠萝凉了，汁儿全渗进饼底，软塌塌的。跟他在这个饭局上的地位，一模一样。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0944", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "papyrus", "target_name": "Papyrus 字体", "facts": ["Hand-drawn by Chris Costello in 1982 over about six months, when he was 23; he sold the rights to Letraset for $750.", "Bundled with macOS and with Microsoft Office, which is why it appears on so many menus and signs.", "James Cameron's Avatar (2009) used it for the film's logo and subtitles.", "A 2017 Saturday Night Live sketch starring Ryan Gosling is about a man unable to get over that choice."], "a": {"item_id": "eb2c4af932ef", "text": "你们发现没有，开养生馆的老板，都觉得自己特有灵性。他要什么？“古朴、自然、带点异域。”然后他打开Word，字体往下拉，拉到P，眼睛一亮：Papyrus！边缘毛毛糙糙，像拿芦苇杆蘸墨写在莎草纸上。老板一拍大腿：就它，古埃及的灵性！\n\n大哥，你卖的是艾灸，不是木乃伊。\n\n隔壁泰国菜也这么想，楼上墨西哥卷饼也这么想。一条街的“异域风情”，全是电脑自带的同一款免费字体。\n\n最惨的是，卡梅隆花几个亿拍《阿凡达》，片名用的也是它。所以你推门进去，技师一抬头，你第一反应不是放松，是想问：“蓝色那位在几号房？”"}, "b": {"item_id": "a1917bc26fbf", "text": "老板要开养生馆，设计师问他要什么字体，他一拍桌子：要古法、天然、有年头！设计师说那就Papyrus。招牌一立，我路过瞄了一眼，心想这店是不是刚从沙子里挖出来的。那几道毛边，像被雨淋过又晒干的纸巾，每个笔画都在掉渣。有人说这字像《阿凡达》的片头，我说那进去是不是要变蓝？老板很委屈：我这是回归自然！三个月后店关了，招牌还在。风一吹，那个\"养\"字的毛边翘起来，像在跟路人打招呼：我们也不太行了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0945", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "graphene", "target_name": "石墨烯", "facts": ["Isolated in 2004 by Andre Geim and Konstantin Novoselov at Manchester by peeling graphite with Scotch tape; Nobel Prize in Physics, 2010.", "The EU's Graphene Flagship, launched in 2013, committed €1 billion over ten years.", "A 2018 study in Advanced Materials tested 'graphene' from 60 producers and found none with more than 50% graphene content; most was graphite flakes.", "Sold in tennis rackets, running shoes, and headphones as a performance ingredient.", "Geim also won an Ig Nobel Prize in 2000 for levitating a frog with magnets."], "a": {"item_id": "9b0a72d5be4f", "text": "饭桌上总有这种人，一开口就是：\"石墨烯要改变世界。\"你问他石墨烯是啥，他说就一层碳原子，导电好，导热快。行，那你告诉我，你脚下这双\"石墨烯暖宝宝\"鞋垫，里面到底有几层碳？他沉默三秒，说：\"暖和就行。\"这话我信，暖和是真的，毕竟它本质上就是个电热丝套了个新名字。前几年搞石墨烯概念的公司，股价一路涨，财报上研发投入写得比谁都大，产品呢，是往涂料里撒一把黑粉，再印上\"石墨烯\"三个字。投资人亏得哭，创始人转头去讲元宇宙了。世界没变，就他朋友圈头像换成了一张论文截图。"}, "b": {"item_id": "e2b2b3137823", "text": "我表哥2013年起逢人就说，石墨烯要改变世界。理由是：一层原子厚，比钢硬两百倍，诺贝尔奖就是拿胶带从铅笔芯上粘出来的。他听完热血沸腾，当场把攒的二十万全买了石墨烯概念股。你看，人家诺奖是粘下来的，他是被粘住了。\n\n十年过去，世界没变，他变了：从\"石墨烯取代硅\"，变成\"石墨烯发热内衣，第二件半价\"。股票被ST，库存剩三百件内衣，现在全家一到秋冬，都在替石墨烯做临床试验。\n\n最惨的是他还跟我说\"再等十年\"。\n\n哥，这材料最牛的地方，是只剩一层原子也扛得住。你呢？被剥了十年，也就剩一层了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0946", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "brasilia", "target_name": "巴西利亚", "facts": ["Inaugurated on 21 April 1960 after about 41 months of construction, moving the capital inland from Rio de Janeiro.", "Lúcio Costa's Plano Piloto is laid out like an airplane (or a bird, or a cross), with Oscar Niemeyer's government buildings along the fuselage.", "Listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1987, less than 30 years after it was built.", "Planned for 500,000 residents; the Federal District now has about 3 million, most in satellite towns outside the plan.", "Built for the car: wide avenues, few pedestrian crossings, and distances that make walking impractical."], "a": {"item_id": "83f9dfea8a34", "text": "在巴西利亚靠两条腿出门的，基本是两种人：建筑系学生，和刚下飞机的游客。学生是来朝圣的，说尼迈耶的建筑要\"用身体丈量\"。行，量吧。\n\n地图上，酒店到国会大厦一公里，看着像隔壁。你一走才发现，中间是八车道的Eixo Monumental，没有路口，只有立交桥，立交桥底下还有立交桥。你走了四十分钟，国会大厦一直在你正前方，又大又白，像吊在驴脑袋前面的胡萝卜，就是不让你靠近。\n\n走到第三十分钟，你终于崩溃，决定直接穿过草坪抄近道。恭喜，你踩出了一条\"欲望之路\"。\n\n最惨的是，你以为自己在反抗城市规划，其实你是在免费给规划师交作业。\n\n最后你浑身是汗，湿度百分之十，嘴唇干得像盖了章。走到门口，保安问你：您的车呢？"}, "b": {"item_id": "a632ffa6792b", "text": "在巴西利亚走路的，就三种人：刚下飞机的游客、还没买车的新公务员，和读过一本城市规划书的理想主义者。\n\n游客站在电视塔上看国会那两栋楼：“就在前面嘛。”对，中间隔着纪念轴十几条车道。尼迈耶给你设计了大教堂，没给你设计斑马线。\n\n理想主义者说要“用脚丈量城市”。卢西奥·科斯塔把城市画成一架飞机，你见过谁在机翼上散步？\n\n新公务员沿着部委大道走，十几栋一模一样的楼，走到第七栋就分不清自己是去上班还是在服刑。\n\n结局都一样：八月旱季，踩着前人在草地上踩出来的红土小道，鞋红了，裤脚红了，到门口保安问：“您车停哪儿了？”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0947", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "vim", "target_name": "Vim", "facts": ["Released by Bram Moolenaar in November 1991 as 'Vi IMitation', a vi clone he had begun writing for the Amiga in 1988 on top of Stevie; the expansion became 'Vi IMproved' with version 2.0 in December 1993.", "Licensed as charityware: the licence asks users to donate to ICCF Holland, a charity Moolenaar ran for children in Uganda.", "Stack Overflow's question 'How to exit the Vim editor?' passed one million visits in May 2017, which Stack Overflow marked with a blog post.", "Modal editing: in normal mode, typing letters runs commands rather than inserting text; :wq saves and quits, :q! quits and discards changes."], "a": {"item_id": "ca805bf868dc", "text": "我们组有个哥们儿用Vim。你怎么知道的？你不用知道，他会告诉你。开会共享屏幕，需求还没讲，先把鼠标往旁边一推：“我不用这个。”大哥，你推的是公司资产。\n\n他的.vimrc八百行，四十个插件：文件树、自动补全、调试器。我一看，这不就是VS Code吗？他说不一样，这是他亲手配的。哦，花三个月，手工拼了个免费软件。\n\n上礼拜他在服务器上改配置，突然不动了。全组盯着他，他说“我在思考”。思考了五分钟。后来才知道，那台机器上没有他的.vimrc。他进去了，出不来。\n\n现在他一紧张就按Esc。不是习惯，是创伤。"}, "b": {"item_id": "304fbbdaf17e", "text": "咱公司有个哥们，从不说\"我在写代码\"，他说\"我在用 Vim 写代码\"。重点不是写，是 Vim。\n\n他图啥？图你路过他工位，看见他手指在键盘上弹钢琴，嘴里念着 hjkl hjkl，像在做法。你问他干嘛，他说\"移动光标\"。移一格十分钟，但\"不用离开主键区\"。省下摸方向键的半秒，代价是 .vimrc 调了三年。\n\n下场呢？有天重装系统，.vimrc 没备份。他对着裸奔的 Vim，像被扒了铠甲的骑士。第二天，他偷偷装了 VS Code，再装个 Vim 插件。\n\n现在他是：用 VS Code 假装用 Vim，还得让所有人知道。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0948", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "google-sheets", "target_name": "Google Sheets", "facts": ["Launched in June 2006 as Google Spreadsheets, built on XL2Web, a product Google acquired from 2Web Technologies.", "A spreadsheet is capped at 10 million cells (raised from 5 million in 2022).", "Functions such as IMPORTHTML, IMPORTXML, and GOOGLEFINANCE pull live web data straight into cells.", "Free with a Google account; files live in Google Drive, and collaborators edit the same sheet simultaneously with coloured cursors.", "In a browser, offline editing works only in Chrome or Edge, with the Google Docs Offline extension installed and offline access switched on while still connected; the Android and iOS apps work offline natively."], "a": {"item_id": "81690243de7c", "text": "每个团队都有这么一个人，说：“不用买Jira，太重了，我拿Google Sheets搞一下就行。”他不是图省钱，是图全公司只有他看得懂第七个标签页。\n\n三个月后，表里十四个标签页，条件格式红黄绿：红是延期，黄是风险，绿是“没人来骂我”。有个标签页叫“勿动”，全组都动过。\n\n直到有天，同事只选了B列，点了“A到Z排序”。从此任务和负责人各过各的，张三在干李四的活，李四在给一个叫“待定”的人打下手。\n\n最后他休年假，全组停摆，因为只有他知道IMPORTRANGE为什么报#REF!。他离职那天，交接文档就一个链接，点开写着“需要访问权限”，申请已自动发送给：他的个人邮箱。"}, "b": {"item_id": "c795fe8e9fcf", "text": "每个拿Google Sheets管团队的人，都管这叫“极简”。其实就是舍不得那几十刀的Jira。\n\n一开始你说“一张表够了”。三个月后，十四个标签页，一个叫“总表”，一个叫“总表（真的）”。\n\n你开会最常说的一句：“谁开的筛选？”你筛一下，全公司跟着筛一下。右上角永远飘着三个头像，其中一个叫“匿名水豚”。你不知道它是谁，但它刚删了D列。\n\n下场呢？你成了公司唯一看得懂那条“VLOOKUP套IMPORTRANGE”的人。你不是经理了，你是这张表的人质。你休假那天，群里只有一句话：“老大，它又#REF!了。”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0949", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "ugg", "target_name": "UGG 雪地靴", "facts": ["Sheepskin boots Australian surfers wore to warm up after sessions; Brian Smith started selling them in California in 1978.", "Deckers Outdoor bought the brand in 1995 and holds 'UGG' as a trademark in the US and elsewhere; in Australia, 'ugg' is a generic word any bootmaker may use.", "In 2019, a US jury found an Australian maker liable for selling boots labelled 'ugg' into the United States.", "Oprah Winfrey featured them in her 'Favorite Things' lists repeatedly in the 2000s, which drove the first boom.", "UGG is Deckers' largest brand, with over $2 billion in annual sales."], "a": {"item_id": "0ef8b911f7d9", "text": "我表哥一年四季穿UGG雪地靴。夏天三十五度，他穿着那双栗色的羊皮内里靴挤地铁，脚底跟焖在蒸笼里一样。我问他不热吗，他说这是态度。我说态度能当空调用？他说UGG要的就是这份松弛感。结果今年夏天，他那双靴子的羊毛内里全板结了，一股酸味，地铁上的人自动给他让出三米。他图的是别人觉得他不怕冷，结果大家只觉得他不怕臭。"}, "b": {"item_id": "2b7b80053c20", "text": "我有个朋友，一年四季穿UGG。七月三十八度，她踩着两只羊上街。我问不热吗？她说：“羊毛，透气。”姐，羊自己热得都去剪毛了，你又给它穿回去？\n\n她图什么？图一脚蹬进去不用系鞋带，像回到妈妈怀里。代价呢？光脚闷一夏天，她一脱鞋，全办公室以为楼下烤串店开业了，还是羊肉串。\n\n穿到第三年，鞋跟往外撇，人往里倒，走路像两只企鹅抬着她。下场雨，麂皮全是水印，鞋面像世界地图。\n\n最讽刺的是，这叫雪地靴。她穿它蹚过三个夏天的水坑，唯独没见过雪：一到冬天，她嫌冷，换棉拖了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0950", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "crocs", "target_name": "Crocs 洞洞鞋", "facts": ["Founded in Boulder, Colorado, in 2002; the first clog debuted at the Fort Lauderdale boat show as a boating shoe.", "Made of 'Croslite', which Crocs describes as a proprietary closed-cell resin foam and which third parties identify as an injection-moulded EVA foam.", "Balenciaga sold a platform Crocs for about $850 in 2018.", "The stock fell more than 90% from its 2007 peak during the 2008 crisis; the company then boomed in the pandemic and gave away 860,000 pairs to healthcare workers in 2020.", "The holes are officially 'ventilation ports' and the charms that go in them are 'Jibbitz', a company Crocs bought in 2006."], "a": {"item_id": "0269f5e79bdf", "text": "穿洞洞鞋出门的人，开口都是同一句：“我就图个舒服。”行，那你解释一下鞋面上那一排小挂件。小熊、披萨、一个字母H，这哪是图舒服，这是脚上开了个朋友圈。\n\n还有那根后跟带，永远往后翻，他们管这叫“运动模式”。哥们，你是下楼取快递。真运动谁穿这个？这个模式唯一的功能，就是方便扶梯把你的鞋吃了。\n\n下场也都一样。夏天晒完，脚背上一块镂空格子，像被烤架翻过面。下雨天更惨，洞是通的，水进得特别积极，走一步，咕叽，咕叽，脚上养了两只青蛙。\n\n最后他还要补一句：“这个其实防臭。”没人问你。"}, "b": {"item_id": "e55404ed5b22", "text": "你们知道穿洞洞鞋出门的都是什么人吗？一种是刚下飞机、防晒霜还没抹匀的人，另一种是觉得\"我就是不在乎形象\"的人。图什么？图脚舒服，图鞋上那些小挂件能挂满一圈，走路叮叮当当，像个移动的钥匙串。结果呢？去趟海边，洞洞里灌满沙子，走一步倒一把，像在给沙滩做漏斗。下雨天更惨，鞋底那几个孔等于给雨水开了后门，袜子从脚踝湿到脚趾。最要命的是全程还得踩着\"吧唧吧唧\"的声音进电梯，全电梯的人都知道你来了。后来他真的不出门了，因为他终于明白，别人不是看不见他，是听得见他。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0951", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "las-vegas", "target_name": "拉斯维加斯", "facts": ["Nevada legalized casino gambling in 1931, the year construction of the Hoover Dam began nearby.", "Most of the Strip lies not in the city of Las Vegas but in the unincorporated town of Paradise.", "The Sphere, opened in 2023 at a cost of about $2.3 billion, is the largest spherical building in the world.", "'What happens here, stays here' was launched by the local convention and visitors authority in 2003.", "Lake Mead, its water source, fell in July 2022 to about 1,040 feet, its lowest since the reservoir first filled in 1937, and dropped below that mark again in August 2026."], "a": {"item_id": "c54ee644ce17", "text": "去拉斯维加斯发财的人，最让我服气的是自信。个个都说“我研究过”。研究了啥？油管三分钟《21点必胜法》。人家赌场是几十亿砸出来的，你是三分钟视频，这叫博士打小学生。\n\n他们图什么？不图钱，图一句“我在维加斯赢过”。第一晚赢两百，感觉自己是邦德，服务员送免费酒，你以为是请客，那是预付款。喝到第三杯，你把全部押在红色，理由是“看着吉利”。\n\n下场很统一：赌场没窗户没钟，你以为晚上十点，其实周三了。最后站在ATM前，手续费十八块你都不心疼，已经输麻了。到机场，还有老虎机，你居然想“最后试一把”。\n\n回家有人问，你说：“我没输，我是去玩的。”对，你花四千块，买了一句“我没输”。"}, "b": {"item_id": "9c12f6e41e03", "text": "去拉斯维加斯发财的人，我见过三种。第一种是辞了职来的，行李箱一半是衣服，一半是对人生的最后指望。第二种是借钱来的，借的还是网贷，利息高得连老虎机的灯都嫉妒。第三种最可怕，他说就玩一小时，结果第二天中午你在自助餐厅找到他，正排第四趟帝王蟹腿，眼神像刚从战场上下来。外面四十度，他嫌出门热，就一直泡在赌场里。赢了几万，去兑换处，人家笑着说：您还有一张免费住宿券，欢迎下次光临。他拿着券上楼睡了一觉，醒来一看，钱没了，券还在，而且明天还有效。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0952", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "risk", "target_name": "《战国风云》（Risk）", "facts": ["Invented in 1957 by Albert Lamorisse, the French director of The Red Balloon, as La Conquête du Monde; Parker Brothers published it in the United States in 1959.", "The map has 42 territories, among them Kamchatka, Irkutsk, and Yakutsk, while the entire Middle East is one territory.", "Holding Australia, which has a single entry point, is the standard opening strategy.", "A full game can run for hours, and players eliminated early have nothing to do but watch."], "a": {"item_id": "de1740709647", "text": "能把一局《战国风云》打到底的人，现实里什么都没征服过，所以才把野心全押在骰子上。\n\n开局必有一个人说：\"我就守澳洲，稳。\"稳个啥？澳洲就一个入口，你守的不是大陆，是自己的坟。\n\n然后有人说：\"咱俩结盟吧。\"三小时后，他在堪察加半岛捅了你一刀。你问：\"不是兄弟吗？\"他说：\"兄弟归兄弟，防守方平局算我赢。\"\n\n最惨的是第一个被淘汰的。不能走，不能说话，就坐那儿看别人扔骰子，一直看到凌晨两点。\n\n最后赢的那位统一了全世界，地图上全是他的颜色。一抬头，桌上一个活人都没了，微信里全是灰的。"}, "b": {"item_id": "8c92129c3833", "text": "每个朋友圈里都有一个人，非要把一局《战国风云》打完。你们认识他：开局就缩进澳大利亚，守着暹罗那一个口子，像个在村口摆路障的村长。图什么？图每回合多拿那两个兵。别人在欧洲打成一锅粥，他在大洋洲养精蓄锐，一养就是四个小时。凌晨两点，屋里只剩他和堪察加。他攥着三张卡死等凑成一套换兵，眼神像股民在等解套。最后他赢了，世界是他的了。可赢给谁看？朋友早睡了，女朋友搬走了，房东在敲门。他把乌克兰上那四十个塑料小兵一个个收回盒子。那是他这辈子离统一天下最近的一次，也是最后一次有人喊他来玩桌游。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0953", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "jackfruit", "target_name": "菠萝蜜", "facts": ["The largest fruit that grows on a tree; single fruits can weigh over 40 kg.", "The national fruit of Bangladesh and the state fruit of Kerala and Tamil Nadu.", "Unripe flesh is sold as a vegan 'pulled pork' substitute, although it has only 1-2 g of protein per 100 g.", "Cutting one releases a sticky white latex, so cooks oil their knives and hands first.", "A member of the mulberry family (Moraceae), a relative of breadfruit and figs."], "a": {"item_id": "73bd57e7ed48", "text": "有老板把青菠萝蜜撕成丝，当手撕猪肉卖。我就佩服，创业第一步，先跟自己的手结仇。青菠萝蜜那个胶，比前任还难甩，洗手液没用，得拿食用油搓。后厨剥一天，十根手指粘成一只爪子，出来跟客人握手，客人以为他入了什么帮派。\n\n图啥？图便宜。可你算算：猪肉切了就能炒，菠萝蜜得先拿刀砍一身刺，再对付一手胶，炖三小时，还得倒半瓶酱油才勉强有点肉味。人工加调料，够买半头猪了。\n\n最后下场，有食客点评：“这是我吃过最像菠萝蜜的猪肉。”一句话，比市监局还准。罚款单下来，老板终于明白，这生意他唯一卖出去的肉，是自己的肉疼。"}, "b": {"item_id": "91e32b021f50", "text": "最近轻食店流行卖“植物基手撕猪肉”，一问，菠萝蜜。老板穿着亚麻衬衫，跟你聊碳排放，一盒六十八。你知道菠萝蜜在海南路边多少钱吗？一个三十斤，摊主拿它当板凳坐。他图什么？就图你没去过海南。\n\n但老天是公平的。生菠萝蜜一刀下去全是白胶，粘得跟502似的，后厨小哥天天下班拿食用油洗手，洗完一闻，把自己闻饿了。最惨的是哪天进货进成熟了，顾客咬一口“手撕猪肉”，满嘴甜得跟芒果味口香糖一样。老板只能硬着头皮解释：“这是我们的新品，果香黑猪。”\n\n你装了半年肉，一熟就露馅。这叫什么？瓜熟蒂落，人设也落。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0954", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "php", "target_name": "PHP", "facts": ["Created by Rasmus Lerdorf in 1994 as 'Personal Home Page Tools', a set of C programs for tracking visitors to his own résumé page; the name now officially stands for the recursive 'PHP: Hypertext Preprocessor'.", "Lerdorf, in his 2007 MySQL Conference talk 'PHP on Hormones': 'I'm not a real programmer. I throw together things until it works then I move on.'", "Runs WordPress, which W3Techs counted on 39.4% of all websites (58.1% of sites with a known CMS) on 9 November 2026.", "Core function names are inconsistent (strpos, str_replace, htmlspecialchars, nl2br) partly because, as Lerdorf confirmed, early function names were chosen to balance a hash table keyed on string length.", "Until PHP 8 (2020), the comparison 0 == 'foo' was true because the string was silently converted to a number."], "a": {"item_id": "15733eca0d13", "text": "干PHP外包的，我见过一类人特别有意思。他们写的代码，每个变量前面都挂个$，像给每个变量别了张工牌。strpos的参数是针在前还是大海在前，他们闭着眼都能写对，因为早就不记了，改成背\"查一下手册\"。甲方说\"就改个按钮颜色\"，他们说好，打开一个三千行的文件，全局变量满天飞，一个函数里HTML、SQL、表单炖在一起，像一锅十年没洗过的火锅。他们图什么？图一个月能拿一万五。落得什么下场？三十五岁，简历上写着\"精通PHP\"，面试官问一句PHP 8有什么新特性，他沉默三秒，说：\"我们主要用的是宝塔。\""}, "b": {"item_id": "0e4fd995e750", "text": "写PHP的人，都是被\"好找活\"骗进来的。全世界四成网站是WordPress，意味着永远有活。就像殡仪馆，永远有生意，但没人羡慕。\n\n入行第一天，每个变量前面都得带个美元符号。你以为是兆头，结果是嘲讽：满屏的$，一分不进兜。\n\n接手的全是前任留下的，一个文件三千行，HTML里插SQL，SQL里插HTML，中间一行注释：\"别动，会炸。\"写注释的人早跑了，你接手的不是项目，是文物，还得原地保护。\n\n每年还有人宣布\"PHP已死\"，他们都练出来了，一边点头一边给来宾递茶。\n\n最惨的是记函数。in_array先写要找的针，strpos先写草堆。干了十年，每次还得去查文档。\n\n别人的职业生涯叫积累，PHP程序员的叫：十年了，我还在大海里捞针，而且先放哪个，我还得查。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0955", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "jackfruit", "target_name": "菠萝蜜", "facts": ["The largest fruit that grows on a tree; single fruits can weigh over 40 kg.", "The national fruit of Bangladesh and the state fruit of Kerala and Tamil Nadu.", "Unripe flesh is sold as a vegan 'pulled pork' substitute, although it has only 1-2 g of protein per 100 g.", "Cutting one releases a sticky white latex, so cooks oil their knives and hands first.", "A member of the mulberry family (Moraceae), a relative of breadfruit and figs."], "a": {"item_id": "91e32b021f50", "text": "最近轻食店流行卖“植物基手撕猪肉”，一问，菠萝蜜。老板穿着亚麻衬衫，跟你聊碳排放，一盒六十八。你知道菠萝蜜在海南路边多少钱吗？一个三十斤，摊主拿它当板凳坐。他图什么？就图你没去过海南。\n\n但老天是公平的。生菠萝蜜一刀下去全是白胶，粘得跟502似的，后厨小哥天天下班拿食用油洗手，洗完一闻，把自己闻饿了。最惨的是哪天进货进成熟了，顾客咬一口“手撕猪肉”，满嘴甜得跟芒果味口香糖一样。老板只能硬着头皮解释：“这是我们的新品，果香黑猪。”\n\n你装了半年肉，一熟就露馅。这叫什么？瓜熟蒂落，人设也落。"}, "b": {"item_id": "0c2c69874c8f", "text": "有人把菠萝蜜炖成“手撕肉”，标价三十八，招牌写着“植物蛋白，口感还原”。我点了一份，吃了一口，好家伙，是甜的。不是一般的甜，是榴莲没熟透、香蕉刚放软那种甜。我以为自己点的是红烧肉，结果是一碗加了纤维的糖水。老板挺自豪，说“你看这纹理，一丝一丝的，跟猪肉纤维一模一样”。我说老板，猪肉的纤维是肌肉，你这是树上挂的果囊，你是把一棵树的肚子撕开给我看。他说这叫创新。后来店关了，听说他转行卖菠萝蜜酥，说“不像肉了，但它甜”。我说你这才是找对了路子，肉没卖出去，至少糖分到位了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0956", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "vision-pro", "target_name": "Apple Vision Pro", "facts": ["Released in February 2024 at $3,499, with an external battery pack on a cable rated for about two hours.", "'EyeSight' shows a rendering of the wearer's eyes on an outward-facing screen.", "Apple calls it 'spatial computing' and its App Store rules tell developers not to describe apps as AR, VR, XR, or MR.", "Reports during 2024 said Apple had cut production; analysts' first-year sales estimates were well under a million units.", "Weighs 600-650 g, before the battery."], "a": {"item_id": "7a31cad715fb", "text": "在地铁上戴 Vision Pro 的人，我真是服了。他们是谁？通常是刚花三万买了设备的程序员，或者想在朋友圈证明自己是\"未来人\"的部门主管。图什么？图全车人都看他。结果呢，他戴着个大眼罩，坐在车厢里对着空气划拉手指，像在跟一个看不见的前女友视频通话。旁边大爷以为他在看病，主动给他让座。最惨的是，他脸上那块外屏还亮着他自己的眼睛，一闪一闪的，全车人跟他对视，感觉被一只戴泳镜的大眼怪盯上了。电池包的线还拖在兜里，两个小时就没电。他摘下来一看，自己还在五号线上，被挤成了沙丁鱼。"}, "b": {"item_id": "af7b2649d37f", "text": "地铁上最好认的，就是戴Vision Pro的。三万块顶在脑门上，一根线连着裤兜里的电池，像刚从医院偷跑出来、还没拔输液管的病人。他图什么？图“空间计算”。人家早高峰挤成肉饼，他调出个月球环境。兄弟，你人在月球，屁股在十号线。最绝的是手势：眼睛一盯，两根手指一捏，旁边大姐以为他要掐她，回手就是一巴掌。外面那块屏还给你显示一双假眼睛，冲你深情地眨。下场呢？电池撑两小时，他坐过了站，脖子压出颈椎病。三万块买来的，是一种让全车厢都看得见你在独处的方式。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0957", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "almond-milk", "target_name": "杏仁奶", "facts": ["California grows about 80% of the world's almonds.", "A single almond takes about 1.1 US gallons (roughly 4 litres) of water to grow, a figure Mother Jones published in 2014 that spread during California's drought.", "A 2015 US class action alleged Almond Breeze contained only about 2% almonds; it partly survived a motion to dismiss, was stayed in 2017 pending a separate Blue Diamond labelling settlement, and the plaintiffs then dropped it.", "Pollinating California's almond orchards each February requires trucking in most of the commercial honeybee colonies in the United States.", "Medieval European cooks used almond milk as a Lenten substitute for dairy, centuries before the current fashion."], "a": {"item_id": "9b69772a0059", "text": "我有个朋友，为了环保，把牛奶戒了，改喝杏仁奶。天天端着一杯杏仁拿铁，跟我上课：“你知道吗？牛放屁，会导致全球变暖。”\n\n行，牛放屁。那你知道一颗杏仁要喝多少水吗？差不多四升。你每天一盒杏仁奶，等于亲自帮加州又旱了一个夏天。\n\n而且那一盒里，杏仁只占百分之二，剩下全是水。你花三十八块，买的是一瓶被杏仁路过的自来水。\n\n更惨的是，加州的杏仁全靠蜜蜂授粉。每年几百亿只蜜蜂被卡车拉过去打工，累死一大片。\n\n所以我这朋友，替地球省了一头牛的屁，搭进去好几个蜂群。他现在一端起拿铁就心虚，听见“嗡”一声，都想鞠躬道歉。"}, "b": {"item_id": "a3df26d73f36", "text": "你们身边有没有这种人？每天早上端一杯杏仁奶，说自己是为了地球。我问他：你知道做一杯杏仁奶要喝掉多少水吗？他说不知道，但包装上画着阳光下的草地，看着很安心。杏仁是出了名的费水作物，加州一棵杏树一年要喝好几百升水，他这杯“环保”，其实是拿加州的水库在泡杏仁。这人图什么？图个心安。图朋友圈里配一张杯子的照片，文案写“从我做起”。结果呢？牛奶钱没省下来，一瓶杏仁奶比牛奶贵一倍多。味道像加了两滴悔恨的白开水，倒进咖啡里直接分层，像一杯正在办离婚的饮料。上个月他开车去超市囤了三箱，车是烧油的，车钥匙还挂着一只环保袋。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0958", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "durian", "target_name": "榴莲", "facts": ["Banned on Singapore's MRT trains and in many Southeast Asian hotels because of the smell.", "A 2017 genome study in Nature Genetics traced the odour to a duplicated family of MGL genes that pump out volatile sulfur compounds.", "China buys most of the world's durian exports, chiefly from Thailand, in a trade worth billions of dollars a year.", "Alfred Russel Wallace wrote in 1856 that eating it was 'a new sensation worth a voyage to the East to experience'."], "a": {"item_id": "17f9f9e15e60", "text": "在高铁上吃榴莲的人，我一直想知道他图什么。图香？他鼻子早被腌入味了，闻不到。他以为自己在吃水果，其实全车厢都在被迫吃他的午饭。\n\n最绝的是他还特别坦荡，转头跟邻座说：\"你尝一口就懂了。\"大哥，我们早尝了，用鼻子，从郑州尝到武汉。\n\n下场也很统一。乘务员走过来，不骂你，就问一句：\"这是谁的？\"全车厢没人说话，但一百个鼻子同时转向你，这叫集体指认。\n\n你想销毁证据？壳带刺，塞包里扎手，包也成了证物。回家洗五遍手，老婆一开门：\"你今天去哪个案发现场了？\"晚上打个嗝，等于二次自首。"}, "b": {"item_id": "c1203c00cfe6", "text": "地铁上吃榴莲的人，我真的服。那味儿一进车厢，我以为哪个下水道盖子被掀了。更绝的是他还闭着眼，嘴角往上翘，一副在拍广告的样子。他图什么？图个\"我很会生活\"？行，你会生活，我们陪你一起窒息。下场也很精彩：全车人同时把口罩往上提，他下车时手里拎着带刺的壳，座位上留了一圈黏糊糊的黄纤维。第二天，他身上的味道洗三遍还在，同事开会时都默默把窗户打开了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0959", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "macau", "target_name": "澳门", "facts": ["A Portuguese settlement from the 1550s until the handover to China in December 1999.", "Casino revenue passed the Las Vegas Strip's in 2006, and at its 2013 peak, about $45 billion, was roughly seven times the Strip's.", "One company, STDM, held the casino monopoly for four decades until licences opened to competition in 2002.", "Among the most densely populated places on Earth, at roughly 20,000 people per square kilometre.", "The Cotai Strip stands on land reclaimed from the sea between the islands of Taipa and Coloane."], "a": {"item_id": "b300422ef6ef", "text": "我有个朋友，在家打麻将输了八千，说要去澳门翻本。这就像在小区泳池呛了口水，决定去太平洋练练。\n\n去的时候坐赌场免费大巴，人家专车接你进去，因为知道出来那趟，你得自己顺着港珠澳大桥走回去。\n\n三天后我在大三巴见到他，对着那面墙拍照。我说你拍它干嘛？他说：“亲切。教堂一把火烧光了，就剩个门脸撑着，跟我现在一模一样。”\n\n最后他在葡京边上的当铺把手表当了，买了一盒葡挞回家。他妈问输了多少，他说：“没输，我去吃葡挞了。一盒六个，四万一个。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "49f31f62d85b", "text": "去澳门翻本的人，我太懂了。前一晚借了三千块，第二天凌晨五点坐大巴，车上睡得比谁都香，他说今天是转运日。到了威尼斯人，水晶吊灯晃得他眼花，他说这地方风水好。风水好？这地方是把你的钱吸进去，再把你的时间吸出来。他图什么？图一把翻身，图回家能在群里发一句\"又赢了\"。结果早上赢了两万，中午输光，下午去找人借钱，晚上在金光大道上抽烟，手机里老婆的未接来电排到了第九个。大巴司机喊上车，他拎着一袋葡式蛋挞走上去。全场唯一赢回来的，就是这袋还热着的蛋挞。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0960", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "nuclear-fusion", "target_name": "可控核聚变", "facts": ["Has been 'about 30 years away' since the 1950s; at the 1955 Geneva atoms-for-peace conference, Homi Bhabha predicted fusion power within two decades.", "ITER, agreed in 2006 for first plasma in 2016, re-baselined in 2024 to start research operations in 2034 and deuterium-tritium burning in 2039, at a cost estimated above €20 billion.", "NIF's December 2022 'ignition' shot released 3.15 MJ from 2.05 MJ of laser energy; the lasers drew about 300 MJ from the grid to fire.", "Private fusion companies had raised more than $7 billion by 2024."], "a": {"item_id": "04cdb6ae7f1b", "text": "搞可控核聚变的人，我是真佩服。他们能用磁场把一亿度的等离子体悬在半空，却没法把“还有十年”悬在十年。\n\n一个博士二十五岁入行，导师说还有三十年。他五十五岁带学生，说还有三十年。退休那天他把这句话传给徒弟，比传家宝还稳。\n\n图什么？图下一期经费。真搞成了，没人给你拨款；搞不成，永远有人拨。他们最怕的不是等离子体不稳定，是稳定了。\n\n前两年美国激光点火成功，全网欢呼，说输出能量比输入多。一算，激光器从电网吃的电是输出的一百多倍。这叫净增益，净亏本。\n\n下场呢？ITER从1985年谈到现在，预算翻了几番，一代人的青春，换来一句“明年的明年”。\n\n太阳烧了四十六亿年，从没交过项目申请。人造太阳，年年得写结题报告。"}, "b": {"item_id": "203425c81d14", "text": "\"可控核聚变还有十年\"，这句话我听了三十年，每次都是十年。干这行的是一群想把太阳关进罐子里的人，目标很朴素：让一亿度的等离子体乖乖悬在磁场里，别碰墙，别熄火。图什么？图经费，图论文，图退休前在新闻里看见自己的名字。结果呢？年年开会，年年说\"快了\"，PPT翻到第四十页，二〇三〇悄悄变成了二〇四〇。有个老教授等了一辈子，临退休还在改时间表，他说：\"太阳烧了四十亿年不用拨款，我们想关它一下，先得给罐子要钱。\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0961", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "vision-pro", "target_name": "Apple Vision Pro", "facts": ["Released in February 2024 at $3,499, with an external battery pack on a cable rated for about two hours.", "'EyeSight' shows a rendering of the wearer's eyes on an outward-facing screen.", "Apple calls it 'spatial computing' and its App Store rules tell developers not to describe apps as AR, VR, XR, or MR.", "Reports during 2024 said Apple had cut production; analysts' first-year sales estimates were well under a million units.", "Weighs 600-650 g, before the battery."], "a": {"item_id": "7a31cad715fb", "text": "在地铁上戴 Vision Pro 的人，我真是服了。他们是谁？通常是刚花三万买了设备的程序员，或者想在朋友圈证明自己是\"未来人\"的部门主管。图什么？图全车人都看他。结果呢，他戴着个大眼罩，坐在车厢里对着空气划拉手指，像在跟一个看不见的前女友视频通话。旁边大爷以为他在看病，主动给他让座。最惨的是，他脸上那块外屏还亮着他自己的眼睛，一闪一闪的，全车人跟他对视，感觉被一只戴泳镜的大眼怪盯上了。电池包的线还拖在兜里，两个小时就没电。他摘下来一看，自己还在五号线上，被挤成了沙丁鱼。"}, "b": {"item_id": "2c1f7e589f71", "text": "早高峰戴Vision Pro的哥们，我懂他。三万五买的，在家戴没人看见，只有地铁上观众够多。\n\n他图啥？图\"空间计算\"。结果一开机，透视模式：花三万五，看十号线，还带点延迟。\n\n更惨的是手势。人家在虚拟世界里优雅地捏一下就点开了，现实里就是对着空气一直捏，全车人以为他在抓蚊子。\n\n还有那根电池线，从脑袋连到裤兜。大爷一看：哟，新款拉环。一拽，当场表演\"摘头\"。\n\n最惨的是前面那块屏，专门显示他的眼睛，AI合成的，深情款款。他本人在看十号线，他的假眼睛在看你。你说他是在体验未来，还是在给未来当群演？"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0962", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "esperanto", "target_name": "世界语", "facts": ["Published in Warsaw in 1887 by L. L. Zamenhof, a Białystok-born eye doctor, under the pen name Doktoro Esperanto, 'one who hopes'.", "Speaker estimates range from 100,000 to two million; roughly a thousand people grow up with it as a native language.", "William Shatner starred in Incubus (1966), a horror film shot entirely in Esperanto, with pronunciation Esperantists still mock.", "Its Wikipedia has over 350,000 articles, and its Duolingo course for English speakers had 1.36 million learners by July 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "ff5e551b6c61", "text": "学世界语的人，我一般叫他们“希望者”。这词本来就是世界语自己起的，意思是“怀抱希望的人”，连名字都在提醒你别抱太大希望。这帮人多半是退休大爷，或者是想拯救世界的文艺中年。他们图啥？图全世界用一种语言聊天，不用再被英语六级追着跑。学了三年，名词加o，形容词加a，动词变位一次没错，十六条语法背得比谁都熟。然后呢？全世界会说世界语的人，凑一桌麻将都勉强。好不容易在周末的世界语角碰上一个外国人，是从匈牙利来的，两人聊了半小时，最后发现彼此都是看视频自学的，口音一个比一个离谱。"}, "b": {"item_id": "b6106e5169c7", "text": "学世界语的人图什么？图世界和平。发明人柴门霍夫说，人类打架是因为语言不通。结果一百多年过去了，学成的人飞去参加世界语大会，全世界同好聚一堂，你一开口“Saluton”，对方也“Saluton”，然后俩人大眼瞪小眼。因为你们发现，这门语言唯一的用途，就是找到另一个会这门语言的人，互相确认：对，咱俩都有病。\n\n“Esperanto”本意是“怀抱希望的人”，名字起得真诚实，没叫“已经实现的人”。\n\n最惨的是，他们的语法一条例外都没有，人生全是例外：没人跟他们说话。想练口语，只能住进另一个世界语者家里，沙发一睡三天，聊的全是“你为什么也学这个”。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0963", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "stinky-tofu", "target_name": "臭豆腐", "facts": ["Tofu soaked for hours to a day or two in a brine that has itself fermented for months from vegetables such as bamboo shoots and mustard greens, often with dried shrimp or meat; the smell is the brine's, not the bean's.", "Changsha's version is black, deep-fried and doused in chili; Taiwan's night-market version is golden and served with pickled cabbage; Shaoxing's, brined with amaranth stalks, is fried pale yellow or steamed with other 'stinky' pickles.", "Legend credits Wang Zhihe, a Qing-era exam candidate who left his unsold tofu too long in 1669; a Beijing fermented-bean-curd brand still carries his name.", "Street vendors are its main outlet, and the smell carries for a block."], "a": {"item_id": "0bded105eb6e", "text": "街边吃臭豆腐的人，我太懂他们了。他们是谁？三种：刚失恋的，想拍视频的，还有刚跟朋友吵完架、非要证明自己“敢吃”的。图什么？图那句“闻着臭，吃着香”。结果呢？香没吃出来，先吃到一滴红油，啪嗒，掉在白T恤上，像刚被人当街抽了一耳光。他拎着那袋酸菜站在公交站，三米之内没人敢靠近。上车，司机把窗全打开了，全车乘客齐刷刷看他，他举着手机说：“这条视频能火。”到家洗了三遍头，味儿还在。第二天约会，对方捏着鼻子问：“你身上是不是有股……”他说：“臭豆腐，昨天吃的。”对方沉默两秒：“哦，我还以为是你前任留下的。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "083ba71caee5", "text": "街边吃臭豆腐的人，我观察过，全是一个表情：一边嚼一边皱眉，一边皱眉一边跟你说“闻着臭，吃着香”。哥，这句话你每吃一块都要念一遍，说明你自己也不太信，你这是在给自己做法。\n\n图什么？图那个味儿。可那个味儿你根本不用吃啊，你从摊前路过三米，它已经替你吃过一遍了。\n\n最惨的是吃完。人家吃烤串，擦擦嘴就走；你吃完，嘴擦干净了，味儿还在加班。进电梯，一电梯人开始互相怀疑；回到家，你妈第一句不是“吃饭没”，是“你刚从哪个厕所出来”。\n\n八块钱，买了一身洗不掉的嫌疑。还给你浇了辣椒。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0964", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "google-glass", "target_name": "谷歌眼镜（Google Glass）", "facts": ["Unveiled at Google I/O 2012 with skydivers livestreaming their jump through it onto the Moscone Center roof.", "The Explorer Edition sold to invited users in 2013 for $1,500.", "Wearers were nicknamed 'Glassholes'; bars, cinemas, and casinos banned the device over its camera.", "The consumer programme ended in January 2015; an Enterprise Edition continued until Google stopped selling it in 2023.", "Its display was a 640-by-360 prism in the corner of one eye."], "a": {"item_id": "e9562ca8081e", "text": "戴谷歌眼镜进酒吧的人，我见过。通常三十出头，帽衫上印着“改变世界”，花一千五百美金买了块架在眼前的小棱镜。图什么？图别人一进门先看见他，然后问一句“这是啥”，他就能顺势讲“这是未来”。结果呢？他对着镜腿说“OK Glass，拍下她”，那个小红灯一亮，姑娘直接把一杯啤酒泼在他脸上。保安把他请出去，顺手把眼镜摘了。第二天网上就给他们起了名字，叫“眼镜混蛋”。那玩意儿后来停产了，他也没改变世界，只把那晚的酒吧改成了一个全员提防他的地方。"}, "b": {"item_id": "cea2da3417f8", "text": "你见过戴谷歌眼镜进酒吧的人吗？花一千五百美金，买了个“探索者”头衔，结果探索的是保安的耐心。他坐你对面，右眼上那块小棱镜一闪一闪，你根本分不清他是在看你，还是在拍你。你问他干嘛呢，他说：“查一下这杯酒的评分。”兄弟，你喝的是扎啤，评分就一个字：凉。最惨的是搭讪，他冲着美女深情地喊一声“OK Glass”，美女还以为在叫她，回头一看，他在让眼镜拍照。然后他就被请出去了。英语里专门给这种人造了个词，叫Glasshole。一千五百美金，买了个全世界都会念的外号。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0965", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "graphene", "target_name": "石墨烯", "facts": ["Isolated in 2004 by Andre Geim and Konstantin Novoselov at Manchester by peeling graphite with Scotch tape; Nobel Prize in Physics, 2010.", "The EU's Graphene Flagship, launched in 2013, committed €1 billion over ten years.", "A 2018 study in Advanced Materials tested 'graphene' from 60 producers and found none with more than 50% graphene content; most was graphite flakes.", "Sold in tennis rackets, running shoes, and headphones as a performance ingredient.", "Geim also won an Ig Nobel Prize in 2000 for levitating a frog with magnets."], "a": {"item_id": "e2b2b3137823", "text": "我表哥2013年起逢人就说，石墨烯要改变世界。理由是：一层原子厚，比钢硬两百倍，诺贝尔奖就是拿胶带从铅笔芯上粘出来的。他听完热血沸腾，当场把攒的二十万全买了石墨烯概念股。你看，人家诺奖是粘下来的，他是被粘住了。\n\n十年过去，世界没变，他变了：从\"石墨烯取代硅\"，变成\"石墨烯发热内衣，第二件半价\"。股票被ST，库存剩三百件内衣，现在全家一到秋冬，都在替石墨烯做临床试验。\n\n最惨的是他还跟我说\"再等十年\"。\n\n哥，这材料最牛的地方，是只剩一层原子也扛得住。你呢？被剥了十年，也就剩一层了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "70e5e600bd85", "text": "我有个表舅，逢人就说石墨烯要改变世界：一层碳原子，比钢硬两百倍，导电比铜还快。你以为他是科学家？他是卖秋裤的。石墨烯秋裤，三百九一条，号称发热、抗菌、通经络。\n\n我问他世界啥时候改变？他说快了！二〇〇四年人家拿透明胶从铅笔芯上粘下来的，诺贝尔奖都发了。我说对啊，二十年了，奖人家领走了，透明胶还在卖，就你的秋裤卖不动。\n\n后来他把卖秋裤的钱全砸进石墨烯概念股，世界确实改变了，他的世界。现在他穿着自己那条秋裤跟人说：“石墨烯，薄啊，一层就一个原子那么薄。跟我家底一样。”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0966", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "beanie-babies", "target_name": "Beanie Babies 豆豆娃", "facts": ["Launched by Ty Inc. in 1993 and sold only through small gift shops, with designs 'retired' to manufacture scarcity.", "At the height of the craze Beanie Babies accounted for about 10% of eBay's sales, and eBay's 1998 IPO filing said the site at times had over 30,000 Beanie Baby auctions running simultaneously in the quarter ending June 1998.", "In 1999 a divorcing couple divided their Beanie Baby collection one by one on a courtroom floor, in a photograph that ran worldwide.", "In 1999 Ty announced that Beanie Babies would be retired at the end of the year, then reversed itself after asking the public to vote and seeing an overwhelming vote to keep them.", "Most 'rare' Beanies that price guides valued in the thousands now sell for a few dollars."], "a": {"item_id": "0f9603e043d9", "text": "九十年代有一帮家长，坚信豆豆娃能攒出孩子的大学学费。这种人买玩具，第一件事是检查心形标签有没有折角。孩子伸手想摸，不行，\"这是你的学费，你摸学费干嘛？\"\n\n他们图的是\"绝版\"。厂家每隔几个月宣布一批豆豆娃\"退休\"，其实就是办完欢送会，转头再出新的。他们还不信邪，为了集齐麦当劳的迷你豆豆娃，一口气点二十份开心乐园餐，汉堡扔了，玩具供着。\n\n结果呢？二十年后，车库里三千只，每只市价两块钱。当年传说能换房的黛安娜紫熊，现在能换半杯奶茶。\n\n最后发财的只有一个人，就是做豆豆娃的老板。后来他因为海外藏钱逃税被抓了。你看，连他逃的税，都是你们的学费。"}, "b": {"item_id": "4798ef5c2a37", "text": "九十年代有一批人，真心相信养老金能靠一只塞满塑料粒的熊。他们给豆豆娃的心形吊牌套上塑料保护壳。娃可以脏，吊牌不能折，折了就从“遗产”降级成“玩具”。孩子想抱一下？不行，那是你的大学学费！Ty公司一宣布某款“退休”，他们连夜开车跑三家礼品店，跟抢金条似的。戴安娜王妃一走，他们抢那只紫色王妃熊，觉得王妃没了，熊必涨。结果呢？拉斯维加斯真有对夫妻离婚，分不清这堆熊，法官让俩人坐在法庭地板上，一人一只轮流挑。二十多年过去，那些熊还在阁楼里，吊牌完好，崭新得像从没被爱过。跟他们的回报率一样。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0967", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "esperanto", "target_name": "世界语", "facts": ["Published in Warsaw in 1887 by L. L. Zamenhof, a Białystok-born eye doctor, under the pen name Doktoro Esperanto, 'one who hopes'.", "Speaker estimates range from 100,000 to two million; roughly a thousand people grow up with it as a native language.", "William Shatner starred in Incubus (1966), a horror film shot entirely in Esperanto, with pronunciation Esperantists still mock.", "Its Wikipedia has over 350,000 articles, and its Duolingo course for English speakers had 1.36 million learners by July 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "ff5e551b6c61", "text": "学世界语的人，我一般叫他们“希望者”。这词本来就是世界语自己起的，意思是“怀抱希望的人”，连名字都在提醒你别抱太大希望。这帮人多半是退休大爷，或者是想拯救世界的文艺中年。他们图啥？图全世界用一种语言聊天，不用再被英语六级追着跑。学了三年，名词加o，形容词加a，动词变位一次没错，十六条语法背得比谁都熟。然后呢？全世界会说世界语的人，凑一桌麻将都勉强。好不容易在周末的世界语角碰上一个外国人，是从匈牙利来的，两人聊了半小时，最后发现彼此都是看视频自学的，口音一个比一个离谱。"}, "b": {"item_id": "3187bfd3bfb3", "text": "学世界语的人，我研究过，理想都很大：世界和平。方法也很简单：先让全世界都学世界语。目前进度，据说有一百万人会，而且这一百万人……基本互相认识。\n\n别人学外语是为了认识外国人，他们学是为了去参加世界语大会。四十个国家的人坐一屋，用一门没有任何国家说的语言，聊同一个话题：世界语为什么还没火。\n\n最离谱的是，1907年有人嫌它不够完美，搞了个改良版叫伊多语，成功把自己人分成了两派。\n\n人类还没统一呢，先把想统一人类的人给拆了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0968", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "risk", "target_name": "《战国风云》（Risk）", "facts": ["Invented in 1957 by Albert Lamorisse, the French director of The Red Balloon, as La Conquête du Monde; Parker Brothers published it in the United States in 1959.", "The map has 42 territories, among them Kamchatka, Irkutsk, and Yakutsk, while the entire Middle East is one territory.", "Holding Australia, which has a single entry point, is the standard opening strategy.", "A full game can run for hours, and players eliminated early have nothing to do but watch."], "a": {"item_id": "de1740709647", "text": "能把一局《战国风云》打到底的人，现实里什么都没征服过，所以才把野心全押在骰子上。\n\n开局必有一个人说：\"我就守澳洲，稳。\"稳个啥？澳洲就一个入口，你守的不是大陆，是自己的坟。\n\n然后有人说：\"咱俩结盟吧。\"三小时后，他在堪察加半岛捅了你一刀。你问：\"不是兄弟吗？\"他说：\"兄弟归兄弟，防守方平局算我赢。\"\n\n最惨的是第一个被淘汰的。不能走，不能说话，就坐那儿看别人扔骰子，一直看到凌晨两点。\n\n最后赢的那位统一了全世界，地图上全是他的颜色。一抬头，桌上一个活人都没了，微信里全是灰的。"}, "b": {"item_id": "407cba2981c5", "text": "有一种人，非要把《战国风云》玩到底。开局说好两小时吃完饭就散，结果凌晨三点，他还在盯着澳大利亚那块地，因为守它能多拿两个兵。他图什么？不图赢，图的是那句\"我占领了全世界\"。他掷三个六，对面掷两个一，他拍桌子喊\"天命所归\"。最后呢？他手里攥着整个北美，兵力只剩一颗，全世界就剩他和堪察加边上一个不肯投降的阿拉斯加。窗外天亮了，他还在算下一轮能不能凑齐一套骑兵卡。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0969", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "pmma", "target_name": "PMMA 电子束光刻胶", "facts": ["The same polymer as Plexiglas and acrylic glass, dissolved in anisole or chlorobenzene and spun into a thin film.", "The oldest electron-beam resist still in everyday use; IBM researchers reported it as one in the late 1960s.", "Positive tone: the beam breaks polymer chains and the exposed region dissolves in a MIBK:IPA developer.", "Resolves below 10 nm but has poor sensitivity, so doses of hundreds to over a thousand µC/cm² (depending on voltage) make writing slow.", "Poor dry-etch resistance, so it is used mostly for lift-off rather than as an etch mask."], "a": {"item_id": "66f317caaa7a", "text": "干电子束光刻还在用 PMMA 的，基本都是博士生。图什么？图一句“PMMA 分辨率高，业内金标准”。金标准，对，这玩意儿跟你家鱼缸的亚克力、牙医给你做的牙托是一个东西。人家做牙托的，做完下班回家吃饭。咱们呢，拿它刻十纳米，电子枪在里面扫一整夜，剂量大得像给它做理疗，早上进实验室，机器还在写，你俩互相看着，谁也不好意思先走。\n\n好不容易写完了，MIBK 加 IPA 一泡，线条漂亮。结果一刻蚀，胶比我工资涨得还慢，消失得比导师的承诺还快。\n\n师弟问：“师兄，为什么不换个胶？”\n\n我说：“导师说，当年师兄就是用 950K 毕业的。”\n\n师兄毕业了，我还守着一块有机玻璃，等它给我一个答辩。"}, "b": {"item_id": "c782f4406d74", "text": "有人搞电子束光刻，到现在还用 PMMA。我认识一个博士，课题就是把 950K 的 PMMA 写出线条。他图什么？图稳，图他师兄三年前就是这么毕业的。结果呢？他做了个五乘五的剂量矩阵，每个格子写一遍，显影用 MIBK 兑异丙醇，实验室一股指甲油味。灵敏度低，写一个面要等一下午，机器都替他着急。答辩那天评委问他为什么不换化学放大胶，他说：\"我导师觉得 PMMA 最稳。\"台下沉默了三十秒，导师就坐在第一排，点了点头。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0970", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "excel", "target_name": "Microsoft Excel", "facts": ["First shipped for the Macintosh in 1985; the Windows version followed in 1987.", "Silently converts gene names such as SEPT2 and MARCH1 into dates; by August 2020 the HUGO Gene Nomenclature Committee had renamed 27 human genes (MARCH1 became MARCHF1, SEPT2 became SEPTIN2) and published guidelines in Nature Genetics to stop it.", "In October 2020, Public Health England lost 15,841 COVID-19 cases when a legacy .xls file hit its 65,536-row limit.", "Reinhart and Rogoff's 2010 paper 'Growth in a Time of Debt', cited for austerity policies, omitted five countries through a spreadsheet range error found by a graduate student in 2013.", "Sheets have been capped at 1,048,576 rows by 16,384 columns since Excel 2007."], "a": {"item_id": "fdec8434c36d", "text": "你们公司有没有这种人？老板说系统太贵，他就用 Excel 管全公司。财务表、库存表、工资表、客户名单，四十个 sheet，每个都带合并单元格，像一座没有图纸的违章建筑。文件名叫\"最终版_最终版2_真的最终版.xlsx\"，打开一看，VLOOKUP 引用的还是前任小王电脑里的 D 盘路径。员工编号输进去变成 1.23E+15，身份证后三位全成了0，工资都算错了。他图什么？图省钱，图灵活，图老板一句\"这表你最懂\"。结果呢？他现在请不了年假，因为全公司只有他看得懂那个宏，他一休假，公司就停摆。"}, "b": {"item_id": "18732d8f011b", "text": "我们老板管公司就靠一个Excel，文件名叫“总表_最终版_改3_千万别动.xlsx”。他图什么？图省钱，图“一切尽在掌握”。掌握到什么程度？他一打开，全公司点进去都弹“文件已锁定，只读”。\n\n他以为自己在经营企业，其实他在养一个VLOOKUP。财务、库存、工资，全靠第三列去匹配第七列。上个月实习生手欠插了一列，全公司工资齐刷刷变成#N/A，那是我们第一次见识零元购。\n\n最惨的是，会写那串套了十一层IF的会计离职了。老板对着那行公式看了三天，像考古队在研究甲骨文。现在公司还活着，但谁都不敢点“排序”。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0971", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "oat-milk", "target_name": "燕麦奶", "facts": ["Oatly started in Sweden in the 1990s, built on Lund University research by Rickard Öste on lactose intolerance.", "Oatly's slogan 'It's like milk but made for humans' was banned in Sweden in 2015 after the dairy lobby LRF Mjölk sued; Oatly then published the lawsuit as advertising.", "Oatly listed on Nasdaq in May 2021 at a valuation near $10 billion; the shares later lost more than 90% of their value.", "Commercial oat milk is made by breaking oat starch into maltose with amylase enzymes, and many brands, Oatly among them, add rapeseed oil; the result has more sugar and a higher glycaemic index than almond or soy milk."], "a": {"item_id": "e299ef76bfd2", "text": "有些人点拿铁，非要补一句：换燕麦奶。行，你换。店员一边敲单一边抬头看你，加五块。你其实不知道为什么换，你只知道办公室那个健身的同事换了。端上来喝一口，嗯，甜的，还有一股麦片泡在水里的味道。你心里一紧，觉得自己是不是喝出了健康。拍照的时候，奶泡塌得比你的表情还快。那半杯你放在工位上，等它自己慢慢变成一碗麦片粥。最后你还是回去点普通牛奶，还得跟同事解释：我不是不喜欢，我就是想试试。"}, "b": {"item_id": "6af69cc6ef40", "text": "我发现点燕麦拿铁的人有个共同点：开口前先叹气。“不好意思，换燕麦奶，我乳糖不耐。”你不耐？上周火锅店你一个人干了三个冰淇淋球。\n\n他们图什么？图健康。可燕麦奶为了打出奶泡，要往里加植物油；为了喝着甜，得把燕麦酶解成麦芽糖。你多掏五块钱，买了一杯稀释燕麦粥，外加一勺油。\n\n图环保？说燕麦比奶牛省水。你省下那点水，转头就用纸巾擦了八遍杯口。\n\n下场呢？体检血糖偏高。医生问你早饭吃什么，你说只喝咖啡。医生摇头：“不对，你这是每天早上喝两碗粥，还嫌粥稀，花钱让人给你打了个泡。”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0972", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "latex", "target_name": "LaTeX", "facts": ["Leslie Lamport wrote LaTeX in the early 1980s and released it in 1984 as a macro layer on Donald Knuth's TeX (1978), which Knuth built because he disliked how the galley proofs of the new edition of his own book looked.", "TeX's version number converges on π (3.141592653 since 2021), and Knuth's TeX bug bounty started at one hexadecimal dollar, $2.56, doubling yearly until frozen at $327.68; he stopped mailing real cheques in 2008 after fraud and now credits accounts at his fictional Bank of San Serriffe, and only 9 of the 275 cheques he sent from 2006 on were ever cashed.", "Its best-known warning is 'Underfull \\hbox (badness 10000)'; overfull lines are reported as 'Overfull \\hbox (Npt too wide)', and figures float to wherever the algorithm decides, not where the author put them.", "A single missing brace can produce an error reported pages away from the mistake, and a document can need three compiler passes to get its references right."], "a": {"item_id": "cc677fdb9115", "text": "领导让写个备忘录，内容就一句话：聚餐从周五改到周四。他说，Word？那是给没追求的人用的。打开Overleaf，先配ctex，再装十几个宏包，再调页边距。三小时后，这句话终于有了页眉、目录，还有参考文献，聚餐通知，引了两条。\n\n然后他在里面打了句“人均50$”，编译报错：Missing $ inserted。他的聚餐，被当成了数学公式。\n\n改完之后，“谢谢”两个字孤零零掉到第三页。他又花四十分钟调行距，就为了把这两个字塞回去。\n\n同事回复：“收到，能发个Word版吗？我想改个字。”\n\n他看着那个PDF，终于明白：他排的不是备忘录，是一座纪念碑，漂亮，工整，谁都不敢动。"}, "b": {"item_id": "8f4ce41c713b", "text": "我见过一个人，写两页的部门备忘录，非要用LaTeX。不是不会Word，是觉得Word太俗，他图的是看上去像篇论文。他开Overleaf，写\\documentclass{article}，再\\usepackage{geometry}调页边距，又加个xeCJK调中文，第一次编译就报错，字体找不到，他查了一下午宏包版本。备忘录正文就一句话：周五下午三点开会，请带Q3数据。最后同事收到的PDF标题居中，页眉印着Memo，行距像拿尺子量过。老板回了一句：这个文件我改不了，能发个Word吗？他沉默了一会儿，删掉\\maketitle，把正文复制进了Word。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0973", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "ugg", "target_name": "UGG 雪地靴", "facts": ["Sheepskin boots Australian surfers wore to warm up after sessions; Brian Smith started selling them in California in 1978.", "Deckers Outdoor bought the brand in 1995 and holds 'UGG' as a trademark in the US and elsewhere; in Australia, 'ugg' is a generic word any bootmaker may use.", "In 2019, a US jury found an Australian maker liable for selling boots labelled 'ugg' into the United States.", "Oprah Winfrey featured them in her 'Favorite Things' lists repeatedly in the 2000s, which drove the first boom.", "UGG is Deckers' largest brand, with over $2 billion in annual sales."], "a": {"item_id": "0ef8b911f7d9", "text": "我表哥一年四季穿UGG雪地靴。夏天三十五度，他穿着那双栗色的羊皮内里靴挤地铁，脚底跟焖在蒸笼里一样。我问他不热吗，他说这是态度。我说态度能当空调用？他说UGG要的就是这份松弛感。结果今年夏天，他那双靴子的羊毛内里全板结了，一股酸味，地铁上的人自动给他让出三米。他图的是别人觉得他不怕冷，结果大家只觉得他不怕臭。"}, "b": {"item_id": "3c6a30dd29f9", "text": "我有个朋友，一年四季穿UGG。三伏天，脚上套着两只羊。我问你不热吗？她说：“你不懂，UGG本来就是澳洲冲浪的人穿的。”姐，人家是冲完浪上岸暖脚，你是在地铁里给脚蒸桑拿。\n\n她图什么？图舒服，图不用系鞋带。确实不用系，穿到第三个月，靴筒自己就塌了，鞋跟往外磨成斜坡，她走路像一艘正在侧翻的船。下雨更惨，麂皮一沾水就是一圈白印，像脚踝上长了年轮，一看就知道这双鞋熬过几个夏天。\n\n最狠的是味道。羊毛内里，光脚，捂了一整个七月。她一脱鞋，全屋人都懂了：那只羊，没走远。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0974", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "jackfruit", "target_name": "菠萝蜜", "facts": ["The largest fruit that grows on a tree; single fruits can weigh over 40 kg.", "The national fruit of Bangladesh and the state fruit of Kerala and Tamil Nadu.", "Unripe flesh is sold as a vegan 'pulled pork' substitute, although it has only 1-2 g of protein per 100 g.", "Cutting one releases a sticky white latex, so cooks oil their knives and hands first.", "A member of the mulberry family (Moraceae), a relative of breadfruit and figs."], "a": {"item_id": "17fe9d85a9f3", "text": "现在有些老板，把青菠萝蜜煮熟撕成丝，当手撕鸡卖。图啥？图便宜，青菠萝蜜几块钱一斤，一撕，能卖出肉的价。\n\n算盘打得响，但他没杀过菠萝蜜。这玩意儿一刀下去，流出来的不是汁，是胶。粘刀，粘手，粘案板，老板还没骗到顾客，先把自己焊在后厨了。为了切开它，得拿油抹刀，抹手，抹案板，一套流程下来，一颗菠萝蜜，三小时。他那不是做假肉，是跟一个长满刺的胖娃娃摔跤。\n\n成本一算：油、人工、调料、创可贴，比真鸡还贵。\n\n最后顾客咬一口：“老板，这鸡怎么有股没熟透的甜味？”老板还嘴硬：“土鸡，吃水果长大的。”\n\n现在他手上的胶还没洗掉，指纹全没了。骗人没骗成，倒是意外获得了一项新技能，可以去偷东西。"}, "b": {"item_id": "292065870d49", "text": "菠萝蜜这玩意儿，长得像个带刺的炸弹，切开满手黏胶，洗手得用油。现在有人把它炖成\"手撕肉\"，印在菜单上叫\"植物基猪肉\"。我就想问，你们是没吃过猪肉，还是没吃过菠萝蜜？它被撕成丝是挺像，我承认。可你往嘴里一放，甜的！老板的算盘打得噼啪响：素食风口来了，成本便宜，还能加价三十。结果客人咬一口，愣住了：\"这肉怎么带着一股熟透了的水果味？\"老板说，这叫创新。创新完，一个月后店就没了，差评区全是\"猪肉被冒充，我的眼泪是甜的\"。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0975", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "macau", "target_name": "澳门", "facts": ["A Portuguese settlement from the 1550s until the handover to China in December 1999.", "Casino revenue passed the Las Vegas Strip's in 2006, and at its 2013 peak, about $45 billion, was roughly seven times the Strip's.", "One company, STDM, held the casino monopoly for four decades until licences opened to competition in 2002.", "Among the most densely populated places on Earth, at roughly 20,000 people per square kilometre.", "The Cotai Strip stands on land reclaimed from the sea between the islands of Taipa and Coloane."], "a": {"item_id": "49f31f62d85b", "text": "去澳门翻本的人，我太懂了。前一晚借了三千块，第二天凌晨五点坐大巴，车上睡得比谁都香，他说今天是转运日。到了威尼斯人，水晶吊灯晃得他眼花，他说这地方风水好。风水好？这地方是把你的钱吸进去，再把你的时间吸出来。他图什么？图一把翻身，图回家能在群里发一句\"又赢了\"。结果早上赢了两万，中午输光，下午去找人借钱，晚上在金光大道上抽烟，手机里老婆的未接来电排到了第九个。大巴司机喊上车，他拎着一袋葡式蛋挞走上去。全场唯一赢回来的，就是这袋还热着的蛋挞。"}, "b": {"item_id": "56ba73fdce82", "text": "去澳门翻本的人，基本都是在内地被套急了的。股票亏了三十万，跟老婆说：“我去澳门，一把就回来。”一把！你在A股被按着打了三年，指望在百家乐桌上三分钟补回来？你这不叫翻本，你是去给澳门送第二茬本。\n\n他们还特别有原则：赢回三十万就走。结果赢到二十九万，心想，差一万，凑个整。威尼斯人里那片假天空永远是下午四点，你输到凌晨，抬头天还亮着，你以为自己才来一会儿。\n\n下场也统一：先当手表，赌场旁边那家当铺，老板比你老婆还了解你。最后口袋剩二十块，在大三巴买个蛋挞，边吃边发呆。\n\n回关闸的免费大巴上，一路没人说话。因为来的时候喊“一把回本”的那几个人，现在全在这辆车上。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0976", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "risk", "target_name": "《战国风云》（Risk）", "facts": ["Invented in 1957 by Albert Lamorisse, the French director of The Red Balloon, as La Conquête du Monde; Parker Brothers published it in the United States in 1959.", "The map has 42 territories, among them Kamchatka, Irkutsk, and Yakutsk, while the entire Middle East is one territory.", "Holding Australia, which has a single entry point, is the standard opening strategy.", "A full game can run for hours, and players eliminated early have nothing to do but watch."], "a": {"item_id": "49ce2b81452e", "text": "那种非要把《战国风云》玩到底的人，我是真服了。开局说好玩两小时，结果六个小时过去，桌上就剩他一个人还在操作，我们早就被他一个个灭了。他图什么？图那张世界地图全红的快感。他守住澳大利亚，吃下整个南美洲，最后手抖得像在拆炸弹，每掷一次骰子都要先深呼吸。我们问他饿不饿，他说再等等，还差阿拉斯加，他那二十个小兵正在阿拉斯加跟我们两个骑兵对峙。最后他赢了，全世界都是他的，只是红色塑料兵用光了，他只好拿薯片当兵。赢是赢了，第二天群里没人再约他。"}, "b": {"item_id": "de1740709647", "text": "能把一局《战国风云》打到底的人，现实里什么都没征服过，所以才把野心全押在骰子上。\n\n开局必有一个人说：\"我就守澳洲，稳。\"稳个啥？澳洲就一个入口，你守的不是大陆，是自己的坟。\n\n然后有人说：\"咱俩结盟吧。\"三小时后，他在堪察加半岛捅了你一刀。你问：\"不是兄弟吗？\"他说：\"兄弟归兄弟，防守方平局算我赢。\"\n\n最惨的是第一个被淘汰的。不能走，不能说话，就坐那儿看别人扔骰子，一直看到凌晨两点。\n\n最后赢的那位统一了全世界，地图上全是他的颜色。一抬头，桌上一个活人都没了，微信里全是灰的。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0977", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "las-vegas", "target_name": "拉斯维加斯", "facts": ["Nevada legalized casino gambling in 1931, the year construction of the Hoover Dam began nearby.", "Most of the Strip lies not in the city of Las Vegas but in the unincorporated town of Paradise.", "The Sphere, opened in 2023 at a cost of about $2.3 billion, is the largest spherical building in the world.", "'What happens here, stays here' was launched by the local convention and visitors authority in 2003.", "Lake Mead, its water source, fell in July 2022 to about 1,040 feet, its lowest since the reservoir first filled in 1937, and dropped below that mark again in August 2026."], "a": {"item_id": "ade69157daa3", "text": "去拉斯维加斯发财的人，我真想问：你是觉得那些酒店是慈善机构吗？沙漠里一座城，没河没矿，全靠你们盖起来的。威尼斯人酒店里那条运河，是你的房贷挖的。\n\n出发前都说\"我就玩两百块\"，还说研究过策略，看了三小时视频：\"押红色，输了就翻倍。\"哥们，赌桌有下注上限，你翻到第七把，就剩尊严了。\n\n下场都一样：第一天赢八十，觉得自己是赌神；第二天在没有窗户没有钟的赌场里喝免费啤酒，不知道几点，也不知道自己是谁；第三天在机场，把回程的钱塞进老虎机。对，麦卡伦机场就有老虎机，怕你还没到市区就想通了。\n\n最后朋友圈发张照片：在猫王教堂，跟刚认识的人结婚了。发财没发成，倒是多了个人来分你剩下的。"}, "b": {"item_id": "9a99488eb663", "text": "去拉斯维加斯发财的人，我见过三种。第一种攒了半年假，信用卡一刷就来了，说今晚要翻身。第二种失业在家，把老婆的金项链拿去当铺，换了三千块，说这是最后的本钱。第三种最有意思，他不怎么玩，就盯着赌场大厅发呆。我问他看什么，他说这里没窗户、没钟，不知道外面是白天还是黑夜，所以觉得随时还能再来一把。结果呢？第一种回家路上只剩一张十九块九的自助餐券。第二种在一百一十度的沙漠太阳底下，被当铺老板问要不要把婚戒也估个价。第三种，赌场免费送的酒他喝到第五杯，才发现自己在这儿住了三天。拉斯维加斯真大方，免费的酒管够，唯独不给你看表。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0978", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "beanie-babies", "target_name": "Beanie Babies 豆豆娃", "facts": ["Launched by Ty Inc. in 1993 and sold only through small gift shops, with designs 'retired' to manufacture scarcity.", "At the height of the craze Beanie Babies accounted for about 10% of eBay's sales, and eBay's 1998 IPO filing said the site at times had over 30,000 Beanie Baby auctions running simultaneously in the quarter ending June 1998.", "In 1999 a divorcing couple divided their Beanie Baby collection one by one on a courtroom floor, in a photograph that ran worldwide.", "In 1999 Ty announced that Beanie Babies would be retired at the end of the year, then reversed itself after asking the public to vote and seeing an overwhelming vote to keep them.", "Most 'rare' Beanies that price guides valued in the thousands now sell for a few dollars."], "a": {"item_id": "4ba725269df8", "text": "我有个朋友，九十年代末拿全部积蓄囤豆豆娃，一只只买回来，吊牌小心翼翼套进塑料袋，供得跟菩萨似的。他说这玩意儿一宣布\"退役\"就涨价，所以他就等着它退役。结果厂家一年退役好几批，退役的比在售的还多，一只熊能退役三回。他那一屋子熊，现在二手平台十块钱包邮都没人要。他还在等那只戴安娜王妃纪念款的\"公主熊\"，说它一定能翻身。我说王妃的熊确实翻身了，你的积蓄没了。他说，吊牌还在，吊牌最值钱。"}, "b": {"item_id": "4798ef5c2a37", "text": "九十年代有一批人，真心相信养老金能靠一只塞满塑料粒的熊。他们给豆豆娃的心形吊牌套上塑料保护壳。娃可以脏，吊牌不能折，折了就从“遗产”降级成“玩具”。孩子想抱一下？不行，那是你的大学学费！Ty公司一宣布某款“退休”，他们连夜开车跑三家礼品店，跟抢金条似的。戴安娜王妃一走，他们抢那只紫色王妃熊，觉得王妃没了，熊必涨。结果呢？拉斯维加斯真有对夫妻离婚，分不清这堆熊，法官让俩人坐在法庭地板上，一人一只轮流挑。二十多年过去，那些熊还在阁楼里，吊牌完好，崭新得像从没被爱过。跟他们的回报率一样。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0979", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "beanie-babies", "target_name": "Beanie Babies 豆豆娃", "facts": ["Launched by Ty Inc. in 1993 and sold only through small gift shops, with designs 'retired' to manufacture scarcity.", "At the height of the craze Beanie Babies accounted for about 10% of eBay's sales, and eBay's 1998 IPO filing said the site at times had over 30,000 Beanie Baby auctions running simultaneously in the quarter ending June 1998.", "In 1999 a divorcing couple divided their Beanie Baby collection one by one on a courtroom floor, in a photograph that ran worldwide.", "In 1999 Ty announced that Beanie Babies would be retired at the end of the year, then reversed itself after asking the public to vote and seeing an overwhelming vote to keep them.", "Most 'rare' Beanies that price guides valued in the thousands now sell for a few dollars."], "a": {"item_id": "0f9603e043d9", "text": "九十年代有一帮家长，坚信豆豆娃能攒出孩子的大学学费。这种人买玩具，第一件事是检查心形标签有没有折角。孩子伸手想摸，不行，\"这是你的学费，你摸学费干嘛？\"\n\n他们图的是\"绝版\"。厂家每隔几个月宣布一批豆豆娃\"退休\"，其实就是办完欢送会，转头再出新的。他们还不信邪，为了集齐麦当劳的迷你豆豆娃，一口气点二十份开心乐园餐，汉堡扔了，玩具供着。\n\n结果呢？二十年后，车库里三千只，每只市价两块钱。当年传说能换房的黛安娜紫熊，现在能换半杯奶茶。\n\n最后发财的只有一个人，就是做豆豆娃的老板。后来他因为海外藏钱逃税被抓了。你看，连他逃的税，都是你们的学费。"}, "b": {"item_id": "e9aa38d24ee4", "text": "九十年代有一批人，觉得自己找到了下一个华尔街：一只塞满塑料粒的小熊。他们不看财报，看那颗红色心形吊牌。吊牌折个角，在他们眼里跟股市崩盘一样。孩子想抱一下？不行！先给吊牌套上塑料保护壳。宝贝，你的大学学费就在这只紫色戴安娜王妃熊身上，别流口水。Ty公司一宣布某款“退休”，他们连夜开车扫三家礼品店，比自己亲爹退休还上心。结果呢？拉斯维加斯真有一对夫妻离婚，法官让他们蹲在法庭地板上，一只一只轮流挑豆豆娃。这就是你的资产配置：二十年后，唯一升值的是你儿子的心理咨询费。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0980", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "google-glass", "target_name": "谷歌眼镜（Google Glass）", "facts": ["Unveiled at Google I/O 2012 with skydivers livestreaming their jump through it onto the Moscone Center roof.", "The Explorer Edition sold to invited users in 2013 for $1,500.", "Wearers were nicknamed 'Glassholes'; bars, cinemas, and casinos banned the device over its camera.", "The consumer programme ended in January 2015; an Enterprise Edition continued until Google stopped selling it in 2023.", "Its display was a 640-by-360 prism in the corner of one eye."], "a": {"item_id": "cea2da3417f8", "text": "你见过戴谷歌眼镜进酒吧的人吗？花一千五百美金，买了个“探索者”头衔，结果探索的是保安的耐心。他坐你对面，右眼上那块小棱镜一闪一闪，你根本分不清他是在看你，还是在拍你。你问他干嘛呢，他说：“查一下这杯酒的评分。”兄弟，你喝的是扎啤，评分就一个字：凉。最惨的是搭讪，他冲着美女深情地喊一声“OK Glass”，美女还以为在叫她，回头一看，他在让眼镜拍照。然后他就被请出去了。英语里专门给这种人造了个词，叫Glasshole。一千五百美金，买了个全世界都会念的外号。"}, "b": {"item_id": "ccb8c9ae4f5c", "text": "戴谷歌眼镜进酒吧的人，我研究过，都是同一种人：花一千五百美元，就为了有人问他一句\"这是什么\"。他进门不先点酒，先点自己：\"OK Glass！\"全场安静，以为他在跟鬼说话。然后他说\"拍照\"，酒吧里所有人同时捂脸，你戴个眼镜，大家跟你上了安检。他还委屈：\"我就是想看看天气。\"哥们，在酒吧，天气就是头顶那盏灯。他右眼一直往上翻，看着像在回忆初恋，其实是在读促销邮件。英文里专门给这群人造了个词，叫Glasshole，得多招人烦，才能让一个词专门为你诞生。最后保安过来问：\"您在录像吗？\"他说：\"没有，它只是……亮着。\"被请出门，他对眼镜说\"导航回家\"，眼镜回：\"正在搜索信号。\"他站在路边，右眼是地图，左眼是现实，两只眼睛，过着两种人生。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0981", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "comic-sans", "target_name": "Comic Sans 字体", "facts": ["Designed by Vincent Connare at Microsoft in 1994, modeled on the comic-book lettering in The Dark Knight Returns and Watchmen.", "Made for the speech bubbles of Rover, the cartoon dog in Microsoft Bob; it missed Bob's release and shipped instead in the Windows 95 Plus! pack.", "CERN's July 2012 Higgs boson discovery announcement was presented on slides set in it.", "The 'Ban Comic Sans' campaign, started by designers Dave and Holly Combs in 1999, ran until 2019, when they retired it and launched usecomicsans.com."], "a": {"item_id": "3c2c6cba7366", "text": "每个公司都有这么一位行政，觉得通知太严肃，大家会有压力，于是打开Word，把字体改成Comic Sans。图什么？图亲切，图“有温度”。\n\n结果上周公司发通知：“因业务调整，将优化百分之三十人员。”Comic Sans。全办公室第一反应：哟，团建？还有人回了句“收到，开心”。\n\n你知道这字体当初是给谁做的吗？一只卡通狗，专门给它的对话气泡用的。你拿一只狗的字体宣布裁员，狗看了都觉得不妥。\n\n最后，这位行政自己也在名单里。他去找HR，说能不能给我开个正式点的离职证明，宋体的。HR说：不用，已经打好了，Comic Sans，右下角还有个笑脸。\n\n他说：这笑脸不是我加的。\n\nHR说：对，是你上次培训时教我的。"}, "b": {"item_id": "5f56efde52fb", "text": "你们有没有收到过这种通知？标题是《关于本周五停电的通知》，字体是Comic Sans。我一看，以为是小区要办儿童节派对，结果是停水停电，还要求业主自带水桶。\n\n干这事的人我大概知道是谁，物业行政，或者公司HR。他们想表达的是\"我们很亲切，咱们是一家人\"。结果字一出来，就像在给你发一张蜡笔画的请柬。\n\n这字体本来是微软1994年给一个卡通式聊天软件Microsoft Bob做的，用在漫画气泡里的。一份要你立即整改的文件，最后看上去像是从对话框里蹦出来的。\n\n然后呢？全公司把它截图发进群，配文：\"这是谁的生日会通知？\"领导第二天在会上说要严肃点，底下大家一边点头，一边憋笑。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0982", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "papyrus", "target_name": "Papyrus 字体", "facts": ["Hand-drawn by Chris Costello in 1982 over about six months, when he was 23; he sold the rights to Letraset for $750.", "Bundled with macOS and with Microsoft Office, which is why it appears on so many menus and signs.", "James Cameron's Avatar (2009) used it for the film's logo and subtitles.", "A 2017 Saturday Night Live sketch starring Ryan Gosling is about a man unable to get over that choice."], "a": {"item_id": "a1917bc26fbf", "text": "老板要开养生馆，设计师问他要什么字体，他一拍桌子：要古法、天然、有年头！设计师说那就Papyrus。招牌一立，我路过瞄了一眼，心想这店是不是刚从沙子里挖出来的。那几道毛边，像被雨淋过又晒干的纸巾，每个笔画都在掉渣。有人说这字像《阿凡达》的片头，我说那进去是不是要变蓝？老板很委屈：我这是回归自然！三个月后店关了，招牌还在。风一吹，那个\"养\"字的毛边翘起来，像在跟路人打招呼：我们也不太行了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "d6bcba0a659e", "text": "开养生馆的老板选招牌字体，预算四百，又想显得有五千年底蕴，于是选了Papyrus，就是那个边儿像被猫挠过、笔画细得像没吃饱的字。他的逻辑是：这字一看就古埃及，古埃及一看就神秘，神秘就能卖六百八的艾灸套餐。我说艾灸是中国的，跟尼罗河有什么关系？他说：“反正是古的。”\n\n结果隔壁新开了家埃及烤肉店，也用Papyrus。两块招牌并排一挂，顾客全懵了：有人进养生馆问有没有鹰嘴豆泥，有人进烤肉店问能不能刮痧。设计师路过也不进门，就站门口拍照发朋友圈，配三个字：“又一个。”\n\n老板花四百块，买了个跟《阿凡达》同款的“独一无二”。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0983", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "javascript", "target_name": "JavaScript", "facts": ["Written by Brendan Eich at Netscape in ten days in May 1995; named Mocha, then LiveScript, then JavaScript to ride Java's marketing, although the two are unrelated.", "typeof null returns 'object', a bug from the first version that has been kept for compatibility ever since.", "In March 2016 the author of left-pad, an 11-line npm package, unpublished it and broke builds across the industry, including Babel and React.", "[] + {} evaluates to '[object Object]'; {} + [] typed at a console evaluates to 0.", "Standardized by Ecma as 'ECMAScript' in 1997 because the name JavaScript is a trademark, first Sun's and now Oracle's."], "a": {"item_id": "c214f8db4fb9", "text": "我有个同事，什么都用 JavaScript 写。写脚本用 JS，写后台用 Node，连他家的烧水壶我都怀疑跑着 npm。他图什么？他说\"一门语言打天下\"。结果呢？一个 hello world 下了四百兆的 node_modules，八万个文件，他说这叫生态。上周他算 0.1 加 0.2，屏幕蹦出 0.30000000000000004，他盯了十分钟，说这是\"浮点的浪漫\"。昨天他 npm install 了一下，整个项目塌了，原因是有人从仓库里撤掉了一个十一行的小包，叫 left-pad。现在他分不清 null 和 undefined，我问他 typeof null 为什么是 object，他说这是历史遗留问题。我说，你才是。"}, "b": {"item_id": "5c3084898a09", "text": "我有个朋友，前端出身，人生信条就一句：“能用JavaScript写的，早晚都得用JavaScript写。”网页用它，后端上Node，桌面套Electron。给我写个计算器，装完四百兆，一开机比我家电暖气还烫。\n\n他图什么？图这辈子只学一门语言。结果呢？框架半年一换，他半年就得重学一遍，比考驾照还勤。\n\n上周他给我写了个记账软件。我存进去一块，再存一块，余额显示“11”。我说钱呢？他说别慌，换成三个等号再比一遍。\n\n现在他项目里的node_modules比他存款还大，删不掉也不敢动。万一哪个陌生人撤了一个包，全世界跟他一块儿塌。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0984", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "google-sheets", "target_name": "Google Sheets", "facts": ["Launched in June 2006 as Google Spreadsheets, built on XL2Web, a product Google acquired from 2Web Technologies.", "A spreadsheet is capped at 10 million cells (raised from 5 million in 2022).", "Functions such as IMPORTHTML, IMPORTXML, and GOOGLEFINANCE pull live web data straight into cells.", "Free with a Google account; files live in Google Drive, and collaborators edit the same sheet simultaneously with coloured cursors.", "In a browser, offline editing works only in Chrome or Edge, with the Google Docs Offline extension installed and offline access switched on while still connected; the Android and iOS apps work offline natively."], "a": {"item_id": "c5f3c3c4263e", "text": "用 Google Sheets 管团队的，一般是创业公司那位“运营兼 HR 兼老板小舅子”。图啥？图免费，图“实时协作，公开透明”。透明是真透明：你凌晨两点偷偷改自己的绩效，右上角冒出一只“匿名水豚”，光标就停在你那一行。全公司这个点还不睡的水豚，只有老板一个。\n\n更绝的是筛选。实习生按“已离职”一点，全公司的表同时只剩离职的人，老板以为昨晚裁员了。\n\n最后他用 IMPORTRANGE 把十二张表串成一张，终于实现了自动化：每天早上一打开，满屏 #REF!。他打开版本历史一条条往回翻，翻到最早那条：“由张伟创建”。那时候他还有头发。"}, "b": {"item_id": "5ceaecd3ab50", "text": "你们见过用 Google 表格管团队的人吗？这种人通常是行政出身，入职第一天就甩给大家一个链接，标题叫《项目总表》。图什么？图一眼看全局。结果他开了二十个标签页，第一个叫“总览”，第十七个叫“真的总览”，最后一个叫“别删”。每人一行，有人把单元格染成黄色，有人加了下拉框，选项从“进行中”一路改成“待定，等老板回”。周五他一打开，满屏#REF!，像看见自己的人生。翻版本记录更绝，凌晨三点有人把“张三”改成了“张三丰”。他终于明白，他管的不是团队，是一个会自己长脾气的表格。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0985", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "durian", "target_name": "榴莲", "facts": ["Banned on Singapore's MRT trains and in many Southeast Asian hotels because of the smell.", "A 2017 genome study in Nature Genetics traced the odour to a duplicated family of MGL genes that pump out volatile sulfur compounds.", "China buys most of the world's durian exports, chiefly from Thailand, in a trade worth billions of dollars a year.", "Alfred Russel Wallace wrote in 1856 that eating it was 'a new sensation worth a voyage to the East to experience'."], "a": {"item_id": "aea5369accce", "text": "有些人在地铁上吃榴莲，图的就是一个\"我不在乎\"。你看他闭着眼，一脸品红酒的表情，可榴莲壳上的刺是扎手的，果肉是黏的，他一瓣一瓣抠出来，手指上全是黄色的汁。车厢里二十个人，被迫跟他一起品鉴。那味道先是甜，甜完一秒，洋葱、奶油、还有一股硫磺的冲劲，直接从你鼻孔顶到脑门。车门一开，他抱着半个带刺的壳下车，回头冲大家笑了一下。全车人都在看他，我猜他以为自己在过生活，其实他是今天全车唯一被记住的人，记住的还是那股味儿。"}, "b": {"item_id": "8c929753db61", "text": "在地铁上吃榴莲的人，我一直想知道他图什么。饿吗？榴莲得先撬壳、戴手套、对付一身刺，这不是饿，这是办仪式。他图的是效果：车厢瞬间清出半径三米的圆，他站在圆心，像刚召唤出神兽的法师。\n\n这帮人还有统一台词：\"闻着臭，吃着香。\"兄弟，你是吃着香，我们只分到了前半句。\n\n最惨是下场。吃完了，肉没了，壳还在。地铁里没有榴莲壳专用垃圾桶，他只能抱着那个刺球站五站，表情像抱着自己的罪证。回家后手上的味道三天不散，老婆跟他说话，隔着一张餐桌，还要开窗。\n\n都说榴莲是水果之王，他倒好，吃出了一个王国，人口一个。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0986", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "vim", "target_name": "Vim", "facts": ["Released by Bram Moolenaar in November 1991 as 'Vi IMitation', a vi clone he had begun writing for the Amiga in 1988 on top of Stevie; the expansion became 'Vi IMproved' with version 2.0 in December 1993.", "Licensed as charityware: the licence asks users to donate to ICCF Holland, a charity Moolenaar ran for children in Uganda.", "Stack Overflow's question 'How to exit the Vim editor?' passed one million visits in May 2017, which Stack Overflow marked with a blog post.", "Modal editing: in normal mode, typing letters runs commands rather than inserting text; :wq saves and quits, :q! quits and discards changes."], "a": {"item_id": "304fbbdaf17e", "text": "咱公司有个哥们，从不说\"我在写代码\"，他说\"我在用 Vim 写代码\"。重点不是写，是 Vim。\n\n他图啥？图你路过他工位，看见他手指在键盘上弹钢琴，嘴里念着 hjkl hjkl，像在做法。你问他干嘛，他说\"移动光标\"。移一格十分钟，但\"不用离开主键区\"。省下摸方向键的半秒，代价是 .vimrc 调了三年。\n\n下场呢？有天重装系统，.vimrc 没备份。他对着裸奔的 Vim，像被扒了铠甲的骑士。第二天，他偷偷装了 VS Code，再装个 Vim 插件。\n\n现在他是：用 VS Code 假装用 Vim，还得让所有人知道。"}, "b": {"item_id": "e21539084a9a", "text": "我们组有个哥们，用Vim。你怎么知道的？你不用知道，他会告诉你。开会时他说“我手从不离开键盘”，然后在投影上按了二十分钟hjkl，去找第三行。\n\n他的.vimrc写了八百行，装了四十个插件：文件树、自动补全、Git面板。兄弟，你花了三年，亲手把Vim配成了一个启动更慢的VS Code。\n\n他图什么？就图那一句：“你连怎么退出都不会吧？”\n\n可他的下场呢？给女朋友发微信：“我爱你:wq”。人家问：“你打错了吧？”他说：“没错，保存并退出。”\n\n她真退出了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0987", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "risk", "target_name": "《战国风云》（Risk）", "facts": ["Invented in 1957 by Albert Lamorisse, the French director of The Red Balloon, as La Conquête du Monde; Parker Brothers published it in the United States in 1959.", "The map has 42 territories, among them Kamchatka, Irkutsk, and Yakutsk, while the entire Middle East is one territory.", "Holding Australia, which has a single entry point, is the standard opening strategy.", "A full game can run for hours, and players eliminated early have nothing to do but watch."], "a": {"item_id": "8c92129c3833", "text": "每个朋友圈里都有一个人，非要把一局《战国风云》打完。你们认识他：开局就缩进澳大利亚，守着暹罗那一个口子，像个在村口摆路障的村长。图什么？图每回合多拿那两个兵。别人在欧洲打成一锅粥，他在大洋洲养精蓄锐，一养就是四个小时。凌晨两点，屋里只剩他和堪察加。他攥着三张卡死等凑成一套换兵，眼神像股民在等解套。最后他赢了，世界是他的了。可赢给谁看？朋友早睡了，女朋友搬走了，房东在敲门。他把乌克兰上那四十个塑料小兵一个个收回盒子。那是他这辈子离统一天下最近的一次，也是最后一次有人喊他来玩桌游。"}, "b": {"item_id": "8083e937dc92", "text": "玩《战国风云》非要玩到底的人，都是觉得世界和平太无聊，想亲手重来一遍的。开局大家都说：“咱们友好点，随便玩玩。”四十分钟后，你的盟友从堪察加半岛给了你一刀。\n\n你还迷信澳洲：就一个入口，每回合白送两个兵，守住就能赢。六个小时后你真守住了，全世界就剩你缩在澳洲，对面三个骰子一扔，你的兵像袋鼠一样被撞飞。\n\n赢的人在欢呼，输的人在找外套，还有一个人早就退群了。整局唯一的和平提议，是凌晨两点那句“要不平局吧”。没人同意，因为每个人都觉得，再扔两把，自己就翻盘了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0988", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "chicago-deep-dish", "target_name": "芝加哥深盘披萨", "facts": ["Created at Pizzeria Uno in Chicago in 1943; credit is disputed among founder Ike Sewell, his partner Ric Riccardo, and cook Rudy Malnati.", "Assembled upside down: sliced mozzarella on the dough, then toppings, then crushed tomatoes on top, so the cheese does not scorch during the long bake.", "Jon Stewart called it 'tomato soup in a bread bowl' and 'a casserole' on The Daily Show in 2013.", "Chicagoans' everyday pizza is thin 'tavern-style' cut into squares; GrubHub order data reported by WBEZ in 2013 showed city residents ordering non-deep-dish pizza about 10 to 1 over deep dish, with deep dish and stuffed making up under 9% of orders."], "a": {"item_id": "66023dd37676", "text": "芝加哥人为了证明深盘是披萨，付出了巨大的代价。你问他，他拍着胸脯说：“这就是披萨！”行，那披萨怎么要等四十五分钟？披萨是外卖，你这是预约手术。上来还得拿刀叉，芝士压在底下，酱糊在最上面，一个厚面壳兜着一锅东西。这不叫披萨，这叫往派里倒了碗番茄汤。\n\n他们图什么？就图跟纽约人吵架。纽约人说一句“那是砂锅”，芝加哥人当场气得再加点一个。结果呢，一块下去，人就饱了。剩下七块在冰箱里陪你三天。\n\n所以你看，这帮人为了捍卫深盘，每次都得亲自吃一遍。吃完瘫在沙发上，连吵架的力气都没了。纽约人单手折着一片，边走边吃，已经赢了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "2802b3de89d7", "text": "管芝加哥深盘叫披萨的人，我见过三种。本地人嘴上说\"我们这儿就这么吃\"；网红拍完照发帖写\"正宗深盘披萨\"；还有一种最狠，纽约来的，吃第一口就开始跟服务员讲道理。图什么？图一个名分。可深盘是个铁锅里烤四十五分钟的芝士蛋糕，芝士垫在底下，番茄酱盖在最上面，你得拿刀拿叉，切到第三块，芝士还在拉丝。这帮人非要把它塞进\"披萨\"这个词里。结果那位纽约哥吃完，服务员问要不要打包，他说：\"不用，我要带一锅回家，当披萨。\"然后抱着一个烫手的铁锅挤地铁，全车人都以为他在搬家。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0989", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "slack", "target_name": "Slack", "facts": ["Spun out in 2013 from the internal chat tool of Tiny Speck's failed game Glitch; founder Stewart Butterfield had earlier spun Flickr out of another failed game.", "The name is a backronym: 'Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge'.", "Salesforce bought it for $27.7 billion in 2021.", "Filed an EU antitrust complaint against Microsoft in 2020 over the bundling of Teams with Office."], "a": {"item_id": "99cfffb47c21", "text": "有些老板非要用Slack管公司。开会太累，他说以后都在#general里说。结果一天九百条消息，他想每条都看，看到第三天放弃，给所有消息点了个小黄脸。他觉得这叫留痕，于是逢事必@channel，全公司手机同时震动，财务一边对账一边回了个\"收到\"的表情。频道越建越多：#项目A、#项目A-最终版、#项目A-真的最终版。员工每天花一小时在搜索框里找文件，找到的都是三个月前的讨论串，串里四十七条回复，最后一条是老板自己发的：\"这个先放放。\""}, "b": {"item_id": "04d9a9a0c475", "text": "有一种老板，创业第一天就宣布：我们不开会，全员用 Slack，扁平、透明、异步。\n\n结果建了四十七个频道。#general 没人说话，#random 倒是热火朝天。真正的工作都在\"线程\"里，那地方跟地下室一样，进去就没人再见过。\n\n老板最爱 @channel。一发，三十个人手机同时一震，点开一看：周五记得填报销。然后三十个人回三十个 👍。他盯着那一排 👍，觉得这公司管得真好。\n\n他还盯着名字旁边那个绿点。员工呢，学会了每五分钟晃一下鼠标。公司就这样实现了\"全员在线，无人干活\"。\n\n说好不开会，最后他点了那个小耳机图标，叫 huddle。对，就是开会，只是名字可爱了点。\n\n最惨的是免费版，三个月前的消息自动消失。老板问：去年那个决定谁拍的板？\n\nSlack 说：什么决定？"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0990", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "durian", "target_name": "榴莲", "facts": ["Banned on Singapore's MRT trains and in many Southeast Asian hotels because of the smell.", "A 2017 genome study in Nature Genetics traced the odour to a duplicated family of MGL genes that pump out volatile sulfur compounds.", "China buys most of the world's durian exports, chiefly from Thailand, in a trade worth billions of dollars a year.", "Alfred Russel Wallace wrote in 1856 that eating it was 'a new sensation worth a voyage to the East to experience'."], "a": {"item_id": "8c929753db61", "text": "在地铁上吃榴莲的人，我一直想知道他图什么。饿吗？榴莲得先撬壳、戴手套、对付一身刺，这不是饿，这是办仪式。他图的是效果：车厢瞬间清出半径三米的圆，他站在圆心，像刚召唤出神兽的法师。\n\n这帮人还有统一台词：\"闻着臭，吃着香。\"兄弟，你是吃着香，我们只分到了前半句。\n\n最惨是下场。吃完了，肉没了，壳还在。地铁里没有榴莲壳专用垃圾桶，他只能抱着那个刺球站五站，表情像抱着自己的罪证。回家后手上的味道三天不散，老婆跟他说话，隔着一张餐桌，还要开窗。\n\n都说榴莲是水果之王，他倒好，吃出了一个王国，人口一个。"}, "b": {"item_id": "fd9d6c3fef4a", "text": "在地铁上开榴莲的人，我一直好奇你图什么？图那句“闻着臭吃着香”是吧？大哥，吃着香的只有你，闻着臭的是一整节车厢。你这不叫吃水果，叫一人点单，全车买单。\n\n而且这种人还特讲究，戴一次性手套，掰得小心翼翼，生怕刺扎手。你的手保住了，我们的鼻子谁管？\n\n他还要给你科普：“猫山王，一百多一斤！”哦，你花一百多，买了个毒气弹，还让我们免费帮你验货。\n\n下场也摆在那：车厢立刻空出一圈，全城最好找座位的人就是他。可惜手套白戴了，第二天一张嘴，全办公室都知道他昨晚吃的是猫山王。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0991", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "keurig", "target_name": "Keurig 胶囊咖啡机", "facts": ["John Sylvan invented the K-Cup in the early 1990s and sold his stake for about $50,000 in 1997; in 2015 he told The Atlantic, 'I feel bad sometimes that I ever did it.'", "Keurig 2.0 (2014) read an ink mark on the lid and rejected unlicensed pods; sales fell and workarounds spread online.", "By 2014 enough K-Cups were sold each year to circle the Earth more than ten times; the pods were mostly unrecyclable #7 plastic until a switch to polypropylene by 2020.", "Keurig Green Mountain merged with Dr Pepper Snapple in 2018 to form Keurig Dr Pepper."], "a": {"item_id": "f6e06d9fe87e", "text": "用 Keurig 胶囊的人，我太懂了：嫌磨豆子麻烦，嫌滤纸麻烦，嫌洗壶麻烦，一心要“按一下就有”。\n\n结果呢？一颗胶囊一块多，折合一磅咖啡粉五六十块，这哪是喝咖啡，这是喝首饰。喝的还都是什么？榛果奶油、南瓜派、焦糖玛奇朵，咖啡味的香水。\n\n更绝的是，为了省两分钟磨豆子，每天花三分钟撕锡纸、倒渣、把那个小塑料杯分类回收。一年三百六十五个小杯子，像在给咖啡办葬礼。\n\n最后机器亮灯：“请除垢。”翻开说明书，让你拿白醋泡机器。图省事的人，最后蹲在厨房里，给一台咖啡机泡醋。"}, "b": {"item_id": "771391305fbe", "text": "用Keurig的人，我一眼就认得出来。他们会跟你说：“我不是懒，我是讲效率。”效率？大哥，你站在机器前面等它咕噜咕噜预热一分钟，然后对着三个按钮，6盎司、8盎司、10盎司，陷入人生最重大的抉择。最后你按了10盎司，因为“划算”。可胶囊里就那么一小撮粉！你花四块钱买了一杯咖啡味的热水，还觉得自己薅到了羊毛。\n\n办公室那盒混装胶囊更惨。一个月后好喝的全被抢光，只剩榛子味和低因。你盯着那盒剩货，就像盯着你的职业生涯：选择很多，没人想要。\n\n对了，发明K杯那哥们后来公开说他后悔了。亲爹都不认这孩子，你天天喂它。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0992", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "windows-vista", "target_name": "Windows Vista", "facts": ["Released to consumers in January 2007, after the 'Longhorn' project was reset in 2004 and headline features such as the WinFS file system were dropped.", "User Account Control prompted so often that Apple's 'Get a Mac' campaign ran an ad about 'Cancel or Allow'.", "'Windows Vista Capable' stickers went on PCs that could only run Home Basic, without the Aero interface, and led to a class-action lawsuit.", "Windows 7 (2009), widely praised, was largely a tuned-up Vista on the same kernel line; Vista support ended in April 2017."], "a": {"item_id": "62ac4958dd62", "text": "有一种人，办公室里第一个喊\"我先装\"的，Vista一出来当天就升级。图什么？图的是半透明的Aero玻璃窗。结果开机六分钟，点开一个文件夹，弹窗问他\"您确定要打开吗\"，他点确定，又弹一个\"您真的确定吗\"。一天点了三百次确定，手指比他的工资还累。声卡没驱动，打印机没驱动，连鼠标滚轮都不认。最后他翻出XP安装盘，格式化重装，还在朋友圈发：我终于懂了什么叫成熟的系统。"}, "b": {"item_id": "0edc712dc233", "text": "2007年第一天就装Vista的人，我认识一个。他还买的旗舰版，两千多，就为了微软承诺的“独家福利”，最后等来一个德州扑克和一张会动的桌面。他电脑上贴着“Vista Capable”，意思是“能装”，没说“能用”。512兆内存硬开Aero毛玻璃，风扇响得像在替他喊冤。打印机没驱动，摄像头没驱动，就侧边栏那个小时钟特别准，每一秒都在提醒他后悔。最狠的是那个弹窗：“是否允许此程序更改？”微软问了他一千遍，他一千遍都点了“允许”，从来没想过点“取消”。现在他逢人就说自己是最早的尝鲜派。不，兄弟，你是最早降级回XP的那批。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0993", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "pmma", "target_name": "PMMA 电子束光刻胶", "facts": ["The same polymer as Plexiglas and acrylic glass, dissolved in anisole or chlorobenzene and spun into a thin film.", "The oldest electron-beam resist still in everyday use; IBM researchers reported it as one in the late 1960s.", "Positive tone: the beam breaks polymer chains and the exposed region dissolves in a MIBK:IPA developer.", "Resolves below 10 nm but has poor sensitivity, so doses of hundreds to over a thousand µC/cm² (depending on voltage) make writing slow.", "Poor dry-etch resistance, so it is used mostly for lift-off rather than as an etch mask."], "a": {"item_id": "c782f4406d74", "text": "有人搞电子束光刻，到现在还用 PMMA。我认识一个博士，课题就是把 950K 的 PMMA 写出线条。他图什么？图稳，图他师兄三年前就是这么毕业的。结果呢？他做了个五乘五的剂量矩阵，每个格子写一遍，显影用 MIBK 兑异丙醇，实验室一股指甲油味。灵敏度低，写一个面要等一下午，机器都替他着急。答辩那天评委问他为什么不换化学放大胶，他说：\"我导师觉得 PMMA 最稳。\"台下沉默了三十秒，导师就坐在第一排，点了点头。"}, "b": {"item_id": "5266dfb33ccd", "text": "我认识一帮做纳米器件的博士，实验室一台电子束曝光机，几千万，往上涂的是PMMA。说白了就是亚克力，奶茶店吸管盒那个料。几千万的电子枪，天天给鱼缸刻字。\n\n问导师为啥不换，导师说：“分辨率好，十纳米！”好是好，就是灵敏度低，剂量得往死里加。机时按小时收钱，一块片子写一宿，学生睡机房门口，跟排专家号似的。\n\n显影液MIBK兑异丙醇一比三，配方七十年代传下来的，比他家族谱还稳。\n\n剂量加猛了它还交联，正胶变负胶。跟这帮博士一样，熬太久，人就反了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0994", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "beanie-babies", "target_name": "Beanie Babies 豆豆娃", "facts": ["Launched by Ty Inc. in 1993 and sold only through small gift shops, with designs 'retired' to manufacture scarcity.", "At the height of the craze Beanie Babies accounted for about 10% of eBay's sales, and eBay's 1998 IPO filing said the site at times had over 30,000 Beanie Baby auctions running simultaneously in the quarter ending June 1998.", "In 1999 a divorcing couple divided their Beanie Baby collection one by one on a courtroom floor, in a photograph that ran worldwide.", "In 1999 Ty announced that Beanie Babies would be retired at the end of the year, then reversed itself after asking the public to vote and seeing an overwhelming vote to keep them.", "Most 'rare' Beanies that price guides valued in the thousands now sell for a few dollars."], "a": {"item_id": "0f9603e043d9", "text": "九十年代有一帮家长，坚信豆豆娃能攒出孩子的大学学费。这种人买玩具，第一件事是检查心形标签有没有折角。孩子伸手想摸，不行，\"这是你的学费，你摸学费干嘛？\"\n\n他们图的是\"绝版\"。厂家每隔几个月宣布一批豆豆娃\"退休\"，其实就是办完欢送会，转头再出新的。他们还不信邪，为了集齐麦当劳的迷你豆豆娃，一口气点二十份开心乐园餐，汉堡扔了，玩具供着。\n\n结果呢？二十年后，车库里三千只，每只市价两块钱。当年传说能换房的黛安娜紫熊，现在能换半杯奶茶。\n\n最后发财的只有一个人，就是做豆豆娃的老板。后来他因为海外藏钱逃税被抓了。你看，连他逃的税，都是你们的学费。"}, "b": {"item_id": "4ba725269df8", "text": "我有个朋友，九十年代末拿全部积蓄囤豆豆娃，一只只买回来，吊牌小心翼翼套进塑料袋，供得跟菩萨似的。他说这玩意儿一宣布\"退役\"就涨价，所以他就等着它退役。结果厂家一年退役好几批，退役的比在售的还多，一只熊能退役三回。他那一屋子熊，现在二手平台十块钱包邮都没人要。他还在等那只戴安娜王妃纪念款的\"公主熊\"，说它一定能翻身。我说王妃的熊确实翻身了，你的积蓄没了。他说，吊牌还在，吊牌最值钱。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0995", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "microsoft-word", "target_name": "Microsoft Word", "facts": ["Launched in 1983 as 'Multi-Tool Word' for Xenix and MS-DOS.", "Clippy, the animated paperclip assistant, arrived in Office 97 and was removed in Office 2007; Microsoft itself ran ads mocking it in 2001.", "Its .docx format, Office Open XML, was fast-tracked to ISO standard 29500 in 2008 as a roughly 6,000-page specification, amid accusations that national committees had been packed.", "Older spell checkers, Word's included, offered 'Cupertino' as a correction for a mistyped 'cooperation', and the resulting 'Cupertino effect' made it into published NATO, EU and UN-agency documents."], "a": {"item_id": "b60d316d9566", "text": "有些同学非要用Word排毕业论文，图什么？图省事。学校要求\"标题三号黑体、正文小四宋体\"，他觉得点几下就能搞定。结果一章标题用了普通段落样式，目录里直接没有它；页眉一改，前面十页跟着变，\"链接到前一节\"这五个字他看了一整晚。最后目录页码差了三页，他按F9更新域，Word回他一句\"错误！未定义书签\"。你不是在写论文，你是在跟一个分节符谈恋爱，谈崩了它也不告诉你为什么。"}, "b": {"item_id": "3173e42e100a", "text": "每年六月，全国几十万毕业生集体转行当排版工人。图什么？就图学校那份模板：摘要页码用罗马数字，正文从1开始。为了这个，他们学会了一个这辈子再也用不上的词：分节符。然后挨页去点掉“链接到前一节”，漏点一次，第三章的页眉就写着“致谢”。\n\n最惨的是插图。你把图往上挪了一毫米，后面二十页集体搬家，图3-2直接住进了参考文献。\n\n答辩完问他收获了什么，他说：收获了“论文_终稿_改3_打死不改.docx”。还有，千万别右键“更新域”，那是我目录最后一次完整的样子。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0996", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hawaiian-pizza", "target_name": "夏威夷披萨", "facts": ["Invented in 1962 by Sam Panopoulos, a Greek immigrant, at the Satellite Restaurant in Chatham, Ontario, Canada; named after the brand of canned pineapple he used.", "Iceland's president Guðni Th. Jóhannesson said in 2017 that he would ban pineapple on pizza if he could, then clarified that a president has no such power.", "Hawaii is no longer a major pineapple producer: Del Monte shut its Hawaiian pineapple operations in November 2006, two years ahead of its announced 2008 exit, the state's last cannery closed in 2007, and most canned pineapple now comes from Thailand, Indonesia, and the Philippines.", "Panopoulos, whose restaurant also served Chinese dishes, told the Toronto Sun in 2010 that 'in those days people didn't mix the tastes like sweet and sour, except for Chinese food'."], "a": {"item_id": "2a083e43e4a9", "text": "点夏威夷披萨的人，我大概能猜出两种。一种是想显得自己没偏见，另一种是他妈从小就给他点这个。前一种会先举着手机拍那几块菠萝，配文“勇敢的人才吃”。吃到第三口，菠萝汁已经顺着饼底流进盘子，火腿片泡得发白，他还是硬着头皮说“层次很丰富”。层次确实丰富，甜、咸、油、软在嘴里打成一团，最后赢的是芝士，输的是他的胃。"}, "b": {"item_id": "5777117eb6e0", "text": "我发现，点夏威夷披萨的人有个共同点：菜单上二十种口味，他偏要带菠萝的。你问为什么，他说“想吃点水果”。兄弟，想吃水果，隔壁就是水果店！你这是把果盘塞进烤箱赎罪啊。\n\n他图什么？图个“我很包容”。他点一个夏威夷，全桌就得陪他吵半小时“菠萝能不能上披萨”，他的人设就立住了：全场只有他敢。\n\n下场呢？披萨一到，热菠萝一咬，汁“滋”地喷上颚，烫得他眼泪直流，还得笑着说“嗯，酸甜，有层次”。最惨的是，这玩意儿是加拿大人发明的，夏威夷人自己都不认。你为了一块夏威夷都不要的披萨，得罪了整个意大利。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0997", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "microsoft-word", "target_name": "Microsoft Word", "facts": ["Launched in 1983 as 'Multi-Tool Word' for Xenix and MS-DOS.", "Clippy, the animated paperclip assistant, arrived in Office 97 and was removed in Office 2007; Microsoft itself ran ads mocking it in 2001.", "Its .docx format, Office Open XML, was fast-tracked to ISO standard 29500 in 2008 as a roughly 6,000-page specification, amid accusations that national committees had been packed.", "Older spell checkers, Word's included, offered 'Cupertino' as a correction for a mistyped 'cooperation', and the resulting 'Cupertino effect' made it into published NATO, EU and UN-agency documents."], "a": {"item_id": "743026e864f3", "text": "我们宿舍有个兄弟，打死不学排版软件，说：“论文嘛，Word就够了，谁电脑里没个Word？”\n\n是，谁都有。所以全宿舍凌晨四点一起陪他看页码。\n\n他的诉求特别朴素：封面不要页码，摘要用罗马数字，正文从1开始。就这三个愿望，阿拉丁神灯都能办，Word办不了。他插了一晚上分节符，最后页眉上“第三章”出现在了致谢里，像个阴魂不散的前任。\n\n最狠的是插一张图。图一进来，后面二十页集体往下挪，表格跑到下一页，标题孤零零留在页底。他想拖回来，鼠标一碰，Word当场“未响应”，屏幕一白，跟他的人生一个颜色。\n\n现在他桌面上躺着：“论文终稿.docx”、“终稿改.docx”、“终稿真的不改了.docx”，还有一个“~$终稿.docx”，打不开也删不掉，跟他的学位一样。\n\n导师最后打开一看，用修订模式批了一句话：“格式有问题。”\n\n整篇变红。"}, "b": {"item_id": "74d77985a3e9", "text": "用Word排毕业论文的人，都信一句话：“所见即所得。”结果你看见的是第38页，导师打开是第41页，图还跑到上一章去了。\n\n这帮人从不用“样式”，标题全靠手动选中、加粗、调三号。目录也是手敲的，后面那一排点，是空格键一个一个敲出来的。\n\n答辩前一晚，导师说加一句话。他在第二章敲了个句号，整篇论文像多米诺骨牌，参考文献直接顶到封面上。\n\n他想设页码，摘要用罗马数字，正文用阿拉伯数字，找分节符找了三小时，最后决定：摘要不要页码了。\n\n文件名叫“终稿_改3_真的终稿_导师改后_最终.docx”。\n\n最后交上去的是PDF。这是他一辈子，第一次让论文别动。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0998", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "macau", "target_name": "澳门", "facts": ["A Portuguese settlement from the 1550s until the handover to China in December 1999.", "Casino revenue passed the Las Vegas Strip's in 2006, and at its 2013 peak, about $45 billion, was roughly seven times the Strip's.", "One company, STDM, held the casino monopoly for four decades until licences opened to competition in 2002.", "Among the most densely populated places on Earth, at roughly 20,000 people per square kilometre.", "The Cotai Strip stands on land reclaimed from the sea between the islands of Taipa and Coloane."], "a": {"item_id": "8dc5e4ad252d", "text": "我有个朋友，在老家输了二十万，说要去澳门翻本。你听听这逻辑：在小区棋牌室输的钱，要去全亚洲最专业的地方赢回来。这就像被村口的狗咬了，你决定去动物园找老虎报仇。\n\n他是坐赌场的免费接驳大巴进去的。记住，澳门最大方的就是这趟车，因为它只管送你进去，不管你怎么回来。三天后他给我发了张照片，大三巴牌坊，就剩前面一面墙，后头全空了。他配了一句：“你看，跟我一样。”\n\n最绝的是回来那天，他手上那块表押在了葡京旁边的当铺里。他说没事，等下回签注办下来就去赎。兄弟，你兜里那张不是当票，是下一趟的车票。"}, "b": {"item_id": "49f31f62d85b", "text": "去澳门翻本的人，我太懂了。前一晚借了三千块，第二天凌晨五点坐大巴，车上睡得比谁都香，他说今天是转运日。到了威尼斯人，水晶吊灯晃得他眼花，他说这地方风水好。风水好？这地方是把你的钱吸进去，再把你的时间吸出来。他图什么？图一把翻身，图回家能在群里发一句\"又赢了\"。结果早上赢了两万，中午输光，下午去找人借钱，晚上在金光大道上抽烟，手机里老婆的未接来电排到了第九个。大巴司机喊上车，他拎着一袋葡式蛋挞走上去。全场唯一赢回来的，就是这袋还热着的蛋挞。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-0999", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "las-vegas", "target_name": "拉斯维加斯", "facts": ["Nevada legalized casino gambling in 1931, the year construction of the Hoover Dam began nearby.", "Most of the Strip lies not in the city of Las Vegas but in the unincorporated town of Paradise.", "The Sphere, opened in 2023 at a cost of about $2.3 billion, is the largest spherical building in the world.", "'What happens here, stays here' was launched by the local convention and visitors authority in 2003.", "Lake Mead, its water source, fell in July 2022 to about 1,040 feet, its lowest since the reservoir first filled in 1937, and dropped below that mark again in August 2026."], "a": {"item_id": "bd02132185ac", "text": "我有个朋友，看了三遍《决胜21点》，觉得自己会算牌，买了张去拉斯维加斯的机票，说要去沙漠里挖金子。\n\n兄弟，人家能在沙漠里建一座城，靠的就是你这种来浇水的。\n\n他到了才发现，赌场里没窗户、没钟，酒还免费。你品，一个地方怕你知道几点、还主动把你灌醉，它是想让你赢吗？\n\n三天后他回来了，钱全没了，就剩一张自助餐券，外加一张小教堂的结婚证，证婚人是“猫王”，新娘是谁他想不起来。\n\n他还安慰自己：“在维加斯发生的，就留在维加斯。”\n\n对，你的钱也是这么想的。\n\n最绝的是候机的时候，他看见登机口旁边还有一排老虎机，又塞进去二十块。拉斯维加斯就是这样，连你走，都要再收一回过路费。"}, "b": {"item_id": "9a99488eb663", "text": "去拉斯维加斯发财的人，我见过三种。第一种攒了半年假，信用卡一刷就来了，说今晚要翻身。第二种失业在家，把老婆的金项链拿去当铺，换了三千块，说这是最后的本钱。第三种最有意思，他不怎么玩，就盯着赌场大厅发呆。我问他看什么，他说这里没窗户、没钟，不知道外面是白天还是黑夜，所以觉得随时还能再来一把。结果呢？第一种回家路上只剩一张十九块九的自助餐券。第二种在一百一十度的沙漠太阳底下，被当铺老板问要不要把婚戒也估个价。第三种，赌场免费送的酒他喝到第五杯，才发现自己在这儿住了三天。拉斯维加斯真大方，免费的酒管够，唯独不给你看表。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1000", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "canberra", "target_name": "堪培拉", "facts": ["Chosen as a compromise because neither Sydney nor Melbourne would accept the other as capital; Australia's constitution required the capital to be at least 100 miles from Sydney.", "Designed by the Chicago architects Walter Burley Griffin and Marion Mahony Griffin, who won an international competition in 1912.", "Its centrepiece, Lake Burley Griffin, is artificial, created by damming the Molonglo River in 1963-64.", "Parliament House (1988) is built into Capital Hill under a lawn meant for citizens to walk over their politicians; the lawn was fenced off in 2017 for security.", "Melbourne served as the capital until 1927 while Canberra was being built."], "a": {"item_id": "cd30c7840947", "text": "你们知道谁会为了工作搬去堪培拉吗？二十三岁、学政治学、在悉尼租不起房的年轻人。面试官说：“APS4，稳定。”他听成了“国家需要你”。\n\n搬过去第一周，他在环岛里绕了六圈，因为这座城的路就是一个环岛接一个环岛。第二周，喜鹊开始俯冲啄他脑袋。连鸟都比他先熟悉这片土地。\n\n三年后他升到EL1，天天写部长简报，写完被删成一句话。终于在Gungahlin买了房，周末的娱乐是去湖边看别人骑车。\n\n回悉尼参加同学会，大家问他在干嘛。他说：“我在帮部长准备参议院听证会。”\n\n大家又问：“那你的人生呢？”\n\n他愣了三秒：“这个问题……我们会后书面答复。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "7c175bf79c23", "text": "你们知道堪培拉为什么是首都吗？悉尼和墨尔本都不让对方当，吵了半天，最后选了个中间的地方，谁也不服，谁也不去。\n\n偏偏就有一群人，为了\"离权力近一点\"搬过来。处长、智库研究员、部长办公室的小助理，还有一堆咨询公司的。图什么？图升职，图简历上一个好看的头衔，图哪天上电视，站在国会山底下说一句\"我们正在研究\"。\n\n三年后呢？他们每个周五晚上开三个小时车去悉尼，周日傍晚再开三个小时回来，车里一句话不说，像押送回去上班的犯人。湖边散步走了一百圈，国会山看了一百遍，权力还是在别人手里。冬天早上零下五度，他们在环岛里绕圈，绕了三年，绕回了原点。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1001", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "esperanto", "target_name": "世界语", "facts": ["Published in Warsaw in 1887 by L. L. Zamenhof, a Białystok-born eye doctor, under the pen name Doktoro Esperanto, 'one who hopes'.", "Speaker estimates range from 100,000 to two million; roughly a thousand people grow up with it as a native language.", "William Shatner starred in Incubus (1966), a horror film shot entirely in Esperanto, with pronunciation Esperantists still mock.", "Its Wikipedia has over 350,000 articles, and its Duolingo course for English speakers had 1.36 million learners by July 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "ff5e551b6c61", "text": "学世界语的人，我一般叫他们“希望者”。这词本来就是世界语自己起的，意思是“怀抱希望的人”，连名字都在提醒你别抱太大希望。这帮人多半是退休大爷，或者是想拯救世界的文艺中年。他们图啥？图全世界用一种语言聊天，不用再被英语六级追着跑。学了三年，名词加o，形容词加a，动词变位一次没错，十六条语法背得比谁都熟。然后呢？全世界会说世界语的人，凑一桌麻将都勉强。好不容易在周末的世界语角碰上一个外国人，是从匈牙利来的，两人聊了半小时，最后发现彼此都是看视频自学的，口音一个比一个离谱。"}, "b": {"item_id": "16da4448f632", "text": "我有个朋友学世界语，说是为了世界和平。这语言是1887年一个波兰眼科医生发明的，目标是让全人类说同一种话。一百三十多年过去了，全人类没统一，我朋友倒是跟全人类都说不上话了。\n\n他图什么呢？他说世界语圈有个\"护照服务\"，全球会说世界语的人家里都能免费住。我问他住过几家，他说一家，在匈牙利。两个人见面第一件事，就是互相纠正对方宾格结尾漏掉的那个\"-n\"。\n\n最绝的是，\"Esperanto\"这个词原意是\"心怀希望的人\"。你看，发明者早就想明白了：学这玩意儿的人，只能靠希望活着。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1002", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "risk", "target_name": "《战国风云》（Risk）", "facts": ["Invented in 1957 by Albert Lamorisse, the French director of The Red Balloon, as La Conquête du Monde; Parker Brothers published it in the United States in 1959.", "The map has 42 territories, among them Kamchatka, Irkutsk, and Yakutsk, while the entire Middle East is one territory.", "Holding Australia, which has a single entry point, is the standard opening strategy.", "A full game can run for hours, and players eliminated early have nothing to do but watch."], "a": {"item_id": "5c5c0ab82bd3", "text": "你们见过真把一局《战国风云》玩到底的人吗？我见过，他到现在还单身。开局第一句话：“澳洲归我。”然后在暹罗堆了四十个兵，四个小时一动不动，跟守小区大门的保安一样。问他图什么，他说：“我在等卡。”凑齐三张一换，眼睛都亮了，好像换来的不是一把塑料小兵，是房本。凌晨三点，他终于从堪察加打进阿拉斯加，三颗骰子全是六，对面两个一。他站起来大喊：“世界是我的了！”没人理他。桌上就剩他一个，其他人早把国家互相送光，提前出局，打车回家了。他拿下了全世界，代价是再也没人接他电话。"}, "b": {"item_id": "49ce2b81452e", "text": "那种非要把《战国风云》玩到底的人，我是真服了。开局说好玩两小时，结果六个小时过去，桌上就剩他一个人还在操作，我们早就被他一个个灭了。他图什么？图那张世界地图全红的快感。他守住澳大利亚，吃下整个南美洲，最后手抖得像在拆炸弹，每掷一次骰子都要先深呼吸。我们问他饿不饿，他说再等等，还差阿拉斯加，他那二十个小兵正在阿拉斯加跟我们两个骑兵对峙。最后他赢了，全世界都是他的，只是红色塑料兵用光了，他只好拿薯片当兵。赢是赢了，第二天群里没人再约他。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1003", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "chicago-deep-dish", "target_name": "芝加哥深盘披萨", "facts": ["Created at Pizzeria Uno in Chicago in 1943; credit is disputed among founder Ike Sewell, his partner Ric Riccardo, and cook Rudy Malnati.", "Assembled upside down: sliced mozzarella on the dough, then toppings, then crushed tomatoes on top, so the cheese does not scorch during the long bake.", "Jon Stewart called it 'tomato soup in a bread bowl' and 'a casserole' on The Daily Show in 2013.", "Chicagoans' everyday pizza is thin 'tavern-style' cut into squares; GrubHub order data reported by WBEZ in 2013 showed city residents ordering non-deep-dish pizza about 10 to 1 over deep dish, with deep dish and stuffed making up under 9% of orders."], "a": {"item_id": "66023dd37676", "text": "芝加哥人为了证明深盘是披萨，付出了巨大的代价。你问他，他拍着胸脯说：“这就是披萨！”行，那披萨怎么要等四十五分钟？披萨是外卖，你这是预约手术。上来还得拿刀叉，芝士压在底下，酱糊在最上面，一个厚面壳兜着一锅东西。这不叫披萨，这叫往派里倒了碗番茄汤。\n\n他们图什么？就图跟纽约人吵架。纽约人说一句“那是砂锅”，芝加哥人当场气得再加点一个。结果呢，一块下去，人就饱了。剩下七块在冰箱里陪你三天。\n\n所以你看，这帮人为了捍卫深盘，每次都得亲自吃一遍。吃完瘫在沙发上，连吵架的力气都没了。纽约人单手折着一片，边走边吃，已经赢了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "31f74171df4b", "text": "总有人非要管芝加哥深盘叫披萨。这帮人是谁？就是去芝加哥待过三天、发过一张切面照的人，回来就成了\"披萨鉴赏家\"。图什么？图朋友圈里那句\"正宗美式披萨\"。结果端上来一口黑铁锅似的东西，番茄酱糊在最上面，芝士全锁在底下，等了四十分钟，用刀叉一戳，热气里拉出半米长的芝士丝。他边切边说\"这披萨真地道\"，旁边的芝加哥老哥头都没抬：\"兄弟，这叫pie，馅饼。\"他不服，掏手机去搜，第一条就是本地人的回答：披萨能用手拿着吃，这玩意你得先找把叉子。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1004", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "almond-milk", "target_name": "杏仁奶", "facts": ["California grows about 80% of the world's almonds.", "A single almond takes about 1.1 US gallons (roughly 4 litres) of water to grow, a figure Mother Jones published in 2014 that spread during California's drought.", "A 2015 US class action alleged Almond Breeze contained only about 2% almonds; it partly survived a motion to dismiss, was stayed in 2017 pending a separate Blue Diamond labelling settlement, and the plaintiffs then dropped it.", "Pollinating California's almond orchards each February requires trucking in most of the commercial honeybee colonies in the United States.", "Medieval European cooks used almond milk as a Lenten substitute for dairy, centuries before the current fashion."], "a": {"item_id": "8cd05311eac8", "text": "我朋友为了环保戒了牛奶，改喝杏仁奶，说牛奶太费水。我说你知道一颗杏仁要喝四升水吗？加州的杏仁树比加州人还能喝。而且杏仁奶里杏仁只占百分之二，你花三十八块，买了一盒百分之九十八的水，外加一点杏仁味的遗憾。\n\n更绝的是，杏仁树要授粉，每年得用卡车把全美国的蜜蜂拉去加州打工，累死一大片。他不想伤害奶牛，结果当上了蜜蜂的包工头。\n\n最后咖啡一倒进去，絮状分层，像一场小型水土流失。他还端着杯子庄严地说：“我在拯救地球。”\n\n哥们，地球没让你救，它就想让你把这杯喝完，别发朋友圈。"}, "b": {"item_id": "9b69772a0059", "text": "我有个朋友，为了环保，把牛奶戒了，改喝杏仁奶。天天端着一杯杏仁拿铁，跟我上课：“你知道吗？牛放屁，会导致全球变暖。”\n\n行，牛放屁。那你知道一颗杏仁要喝多少水吗？差不多四升。你每天一盒杏仁奶，等于亲自帮加州又旱了一个夏天。\n\n而且那一盒里，杏仁只占百分之二，剩下全是水。你花三十八块，买的是一瓶被杏仁路过的自来水。\n\n更惨的是，加州的杏仁全靠蜜蜂授粉。每年几百亿只蜜蜂被卡车拉过去打工，累死一大片。\n\n所以我这朋友，替地球省了一头牛的屁，搭进去好几个蜂群。他现在一端起拿铁就心虚，听见“嗡”一声，都想鞠躬道歉。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1005", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "jackfruit", "target_name": "菠萝蜜", "facts": ["The largest fruit that grows on a tree; single fruits can weigh over 40 kg.", "The national fruit of Bangladesh and the state fruit of Kerala and Tamil Nadu.", "Unripe flesh is sold as a vegan 'pulled pork' substitute, although it has only 1-2 g of protein per 100 g.", "Cutting one releases a sticky white latex, so cooks oil their knives and hands first.", "A member of the mulberry family (Moraceae), a relative of breadfruit and figs."], "a": {"item_id": "91e32b021f50", "text": "最近轻食店流行卖“植物基手撕猪肉”，一问，菠萝蜜。老板穿着亚麻衬衫，跟你聊碳排放，一盒六十八。你知道菠萝蜜在海南路边多少钱吗？一个三十斤，摊主拿它当板凳坐。他图什么？就图你没去过海南。\n\n但老天是公平的。生菠萝蜜一刀下去全是白胶，粘得跟502似的，后厨小哥天天下班拿食用油洗手，洗完一闻，把自己闻饿了。最惨的是哪天进货进成熟了，顾客咬一口“手撕猪肉”，满嘴甜得跟芒果味口香糖一样。老板只能硬着头皮解释：“这是我们的新品，果香黑猪。”\n\n你装了半年肉，一熟就露馅。这叫什么？瓜熟蒂落，人设也落。"}, "b": {"item_id": "17fe9d85a9f3", "text": "现在有些老板，把青菠萝蜜煮熟撕成丝，当手撕鸡卖。图啥？图便宜，青菠萝蜜几块钱一斤，一撕，能卖出肉的价。\n\n算盘打得响，但他没杀过菠萝蜜。这玩意儿一刀下去，流出来的不是汁，是胶。粘刀，粘手，粘案板，老板还没骗到顾客，先把自己焊在后厨了。为了切开它，得拿油抹刀，抹手，抹案板，一套流程下来，一颗菠萝蜜，三小时。他那不是做假肉，是跟一个长满刺的胖娃娃摔跤。\n\n成本一算：油、人工、调料、创可贴，比真鸡还贵。\n\n最后顾客咬一口：“老板，这鸡怎么有股没熟透的甜味？”老板还嘴硬：“土鸡，吃水果长大的。”\n\n现在他手上的胶还没洗掉，指纹全没了。骗人没骗成，倒是意外获得了一项新技能，可以去偷东西。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1006", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hawaiian-pizza", "target_name": "夏威夷披萨", "facts": ["Invented in 1962 by Sam Panopoulos, a Greek immigrant, at the Satellite Restaurant in Chatham, Ontario, Canada; named after the brand of canned pineapple he used.", "Iceland's president Guðni Th. Jóhannesson said in 2017 that he would ban pineapple on pizza if he could, then clarified that a president has no such power.", "Hawaii is no longer a major pineapple producer: Del Monte shut its Hawaiian pineapple operations in November 2006, two years ahead of its announced 2008 exit, the state's last cannery closed in 2007, and most canned pineapple now comes from Thailand, Indonesia, and the Philippines.", "Panopoulos, whose restaurant also served Chinese dishes, told the Toronto Sun in 2010 that 'in those days people didn't mix the tastes like sweet and sour, except for Chinese food'."], "a": {"item_id": "5777117eb6e0", "text": "我发现，点夏威夷披萨的人有个共同点：菜单上二十种口味，他偏要带菠萝的。你问为什么，他说“想吃点水果”。兄弟，想吃水果，隔壁就是水果店！你这是把果盘塞进烤箱赎罪啊。\n\n他图什么？图个“我很包容”。他点一个夏威夷，全桌就得陪他吵半小时“菠萝能不能上披萨”，他的人设就立住了：全场只有他敢。\n\n下场呢？披萨一到，热菠萝一咬，汁“滋”地喷上颚，烫得他眼泪直流，还得笑着说“嗯，酸甜，有层次”。最惨的是，这玩意儿是加拿大人发明的，夏威夷人自己都不认。你为了一块夏威夷都不要的披萨，得罪了整个意大利。"}, "b": {"item_id": "b9921996d500", "text": "每桌聚餐总有那么一个人，菜单翻到最后一页，清清嗓子：“要不……来个夏威夷？”\n\n这人图什么？图个性。他觉得自己在叛逆：“我不走寻常路。”兄弟，夏威夷披萨是全世界最出名的争议披萨，你点它，就像在摇滚圈穿皮衣，一点都不稀奇。而且它跟夏威夷没关系，发明它的是个住在加拿大的希腊人。你连“异域风情”都没沾上。\n\n下场呢？披萨一上桌，全桌人都去拿别的，只有它孤零零摆着。你只能说“我就爱这口”，然后一个人硬啃六块。菠萝出水，饼底泡成湿毛巾，火腿片在上面游泳。吃不完打包回家，第二天微波炉一转，咬一口，热乎乎的菠萝配咸火腿，就像前任突然发来一句“最近好吗”，说不上哪里不对，就是哪都不对。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1007", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hsq", "target_name": "HSQ 电子束光刻胶", "facts": ["Hydrogen silsesquioxane, sold by Dow Corning as FOx ('flowable oxide') and designed as a spin-on dielectric for chip interconnects; lithographers adopted it as a negative-tone resist.", "Exposure converts its cage structure into a cross-linked, silica-like network, so the developed pattern is essentially SiO2.", "Holds the resolution records for electron-beam lithography: isolated features around 5 nm, dense lines below 10 nm half-pitch, with high-contrast 'salty' NaOH/NaCl development.", "Must be kept refrigerated (Dow Corning's datasheet gives a six-month shelf life at 5 °C); the solution ages into a jelly, and its effective sensitivity shifts with processing delays because cross-linking continues after exposure.", "DuPont, which inherited Dow Corning's FOx/XR-1541 HSQ line, discontinued it and ended production at the end of 2025; labs such as EPFL's CMi switched to third-party HSQ from EM Resist or DisChem, both formulated from AQM powder."], "a": {"item_id": "164876412c34", "text": "我们实验室有种人，组会上一听PMMA分辨率不够，当场就要换HSQ。图什么？图SEM照片里那根8纳米的线，能放进论文Figure 1。\n\n结果呢？HSQ灵敏度低，剂量往死里加。别人写一片半小时，他写一整晚，机时表上全是他的名字，像给电子束机守灵。\n\n显影还得用加盐的TMAH。人家做光刻，他在腌咸菜。\n\n最绝的是，这胶一曝光就变成二氧化硅。你辛辛苦苦图形化的不是胶，是玻璃。想去掉？请上氢氟酸。\n\n哦对，那瓶HSQ还得放冰箱，几个月就凝胶。最后他毕业延期了，胶先过期了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "650f7a5cc2ec", "text": "有个师兄为了分辨率，非要换HSQ。我问他图什么，他说十纳米以下的线条好看。好看是好看，可这玩意儿得冷藏，跟酸奶似的，放久了自己变稠，成了凝胶。旋涂的时候像往芯片上刷芝麻糊。显影还得用碱液，线条是立住了，一吹氮气，全倒成一排麦子。最惨的是曝光完它会变成二氧化硅，刻不掉，永久留在芯片上。他的论文发了，芯片留给了下一届，下一届一打开显微镜，满屏都是他的遗产。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1008", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "chicago-deep-dish", "target_name": "芝加哥深盘披萨", "facts": ["Created at Pizzeria Uno in Chicago in 1943; credit is disputed among founder Ike Sewell, his partner Ric Riccardo, and cook Rudy Malnati.", "Assembled upside down: sliced mozzarella on the dough, then toppings, then crushed tomatoes on top, so the cheese does not scorch during the long bake.", "Jon Stewart called it 'tomato soup in a bread bowl' and 'a casserole' on The Daily Show in 2013.", "Chicagoans' everyday pizza is thin 'tavern-style' cut into squares; GrubHub order data reported by WBEZ in 2013 showed city residents ordering non-deep-dish pizza about 10 to 1 over deep dish, with deep dish and stuffed making up under 9% of orders."], "a": {"item_id": "31f74171df4b", "text": "总有人非要管芝加哥深盘叫披萨。这帮人是谁？就是去芝加哥待过三天、发过一张切面照的人，回来就成了\"披萨鉴赏家\"。图什么？图朋友圈里那句\"正宗美式披萨\"。结果端上来一口黑铁锅似的东西，番茄酱糊在最上面，芝士全锁在底下，等了四十分钟，用刀叉一戳，热气里拉出半米长的芝士丝。他边切边说\"这披萨真地道\"，旁边的芝加哥老哥头都没抬：\"兄弟，这叫pie，馅饼。\"他不服，掏手机去搜，第一条就是本地人的回答：披萨能用手拿着吃，这玩意你得先找把叉子。"}, "b": {"item_id": "2c14d9c46ccf", "text": "最嘴硬的，是来芝加哥旅游的人。排队一小时，服务员说深盘要烤四十五分钟。你都等到这份上了，端上来什么你都得认它是披萨。结果奶酪在底下，酱在上面，还得用刀叉，这叫披萨？这叫面饼做的番茄肉酱焗饭。吃完一块，人瘫在椅子上，像刚做完开胸手术。可你还是要发朋友圈：“正宗芝加哥披萨！”因为你不能承认，自己花了两百美元，吃了顿砂锅。最讽刺的是本地人，他们平时吃的是切成小方块的薄底。深盘就跟全聚德一样，是留着带外地朋友去的。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1009", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "luosifen", "target_name": "螺蛳粉", "facts": ["A rice-noodle dish from Liuzhou, Guangxi; the smell comes from pickled bamboo shoots (酸笋), not from the river snails, which only flavour the broth.", "The instant version boomed during the 2020 lockdowns; Liuzhou's government reported packaged luosifen sales of over 10 billion yuan that year.", "Added to China's national intangible cultural heritage list in 2021.", "Liuzhou opened a luosifen industry college in 2020 to train workers for the trade."], "a": {"item_id": "beb2c7971e47", "text": "合租房里煮螺蛳粉的，都是同一种人：把“闻着臭吃着香”当人生信条，但只要后半句，前半句全分给室友。\n\n他煮之前还特讲究，关门、开窗、开油烟机，跟拆炸弹似的。然后酸笋包一撕，全白搭。那味儿穿门缝、钻衣柜，第二天你穿着外套去面试，HR问你：“你家住垃圾站旁边？”\n\n他还觉得自己挺克制：“我没加臭，原味。”大哥，原味酸笋就是臭的底线，你跟我讲克制？\n\n最惨的是吃完那一刻。他满足了三分钟，剩下半锅红油汤倒哪？倒马桶，厕所臭三天；倒水池，整栋楼臭一周。\n\n最后合租群里没人骂他，就房东发了一句：“退房时，窗帘按新的赔。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "06a4c1a635ed", "text": "合租房里煮螺蛳粉的人，都信一句话：“闻着臭，吃着香。”这话是给吃的人用的。我们室友只拿到前半句。\n\n他煮十分钟，爽了。我在房间里闻三天，连根腐竹都没分到。\n\n他还特别讲礼貌，煮之前敲我门：“我煮螺蛳粉，你介意吗？”这哪是问，这是通知，跟飞机广播“前方有气流”一样，你介不介意，它都得颠。\n\n然后他开窗，开抽油烟机，觉得自己尽力了。兄弟，酸笋那玩意儿你开窗它不走，它是在考察房子，想签长租。\n\n结果第二天上班，同事凑过来：“你昨天是不是吃螺蛳粉了？”\n\n我没吃啊！\n\n我连汤都没喝上一口，就被螺蛳粉吃了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1010", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "papyrus", "target_name": "Papyrus 字体", "facts": ["Hand-drawn by Chris Costello in 1982 over about six months, when he was 23; he sold the rights to Letraset for $750.", "Bundled with macOS and with Microsoft Office, which is why it appears on so many menus and signs.", "James Cameron's Avatar (2009) used it for the film's logo and subtitles.", "A 2017 Saturday Night Live sketch starring Ryan Gosling is about a man unable to get over that choice."], "a": {"item_id": "a1917bc26fbf", "text": "老板要开养生馆，设计师问他要什么字体，他一拍桌子：要古法、天然、有年头！设计师说那就Papyrus。招牌一立，我路过瞄了一眼，心想这店是不是刚从沙子里挖出来的。那几道毛边，像被雨淋过又晒干的纸巾，每个笔画都在掉渣。有人说这字像《阿凡达》的片头，我说那进去是不是要变蓝？老板很委屈：我这是回归自然！三个月后店关了，招牌还在。风一吹，那个\"养\"字的毛边翘起来，像在跟路人打招呼：我们也不太行了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "1155b79dd841", "text": "我们这条街，艾灸馆、素食馆、足疗店，招牌全是Papyrus，就是那种笔画毛毛糙糙、像拿烧焦的树枝在牛皮纸上划出来的字体。\n\n老板们图什么？图\"古法\"\"天然\"\"千年底蕴\"。设计师报价三千，他一想：Word里自带的，免费，还自带古埃及气质，血赚。\n\n可《阿凡达》砸了二十多亿美元，片名用的就是Papyrus，被全世界笑了十几年。你一个六十平的刮痧店，凭什么觉得自己扛得住？\n\n现在客人走到门口，抬头一看\"古法艾灸\"，字还带着焦边，真不知道是进去放松，还是进去防腐。\n\n最惨的是素食馆老板，招牌、菜单、外卖袋全是Papyrus，一份麻婆豆腐，看着像刚从墓里刨出来的。他还纳闷生意怎么越来越淡。兄弟，你卖的是出土文物。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1011", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "nfts", "target_name": "NFT", "facts": ["Beeple's 'Everydays: The First 5000 Days' sold at Christie's for $69.3 million in March 2021.", "OpenSea, the largest NFT marketplace, processed a record $2.7 billion in trades on 1 May 2022 and just $9.34 million on 28 August 2022, a 99% drop, according to DappRadar data.", "A 2023 dappGambl study found that 95% of the NFT collections it examined had a market value of zero.", "The token usually records only a URL; the image itself sits on an ordinary server or IPFS, not on the blockchain.", "Collins Dictionary named 'NFT' its word of the year for 2021."], "a": {"item_id": "3a567b5377a2", "text": "2021年买NFT的朋友，我真佩服。花三十万买张猴子图，猴子叼着烟、一脸不想活，这是在提前预演自己的下场。\n\n你跟他说：“我右键另存为了啊。”他急了：“你存的是图，我拥有的是链上的哈希！”大哥，你花三十万，买了一张收据的收据。\n\n那年推特上全是六边形猴子头像，跟加入了邪教似的，天天在Discord里喊“WAGMI，我们都会发财”。项目方路线图写着“元宇宙乐园”，写完人就跑了。\n\n现在地板价跌成零头，当年付的Gas费都比猴子值钱。他还不卖，说这叫“长期持有”。兄弟，你那不叫长期持有，叫卖不掉。猴子叼着烟看着你，终于笑了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "36fed9141b46", "text": "我有个朋友，2021年花三万块买了一张猴子的图。无聊猿，戴墨镜叼烟斗那种。我问他图什么，他说这是数字资产，全世界独一无二。我说你右键另存为，它也能一模一样出现在你电脑里。他说不一样，我在链上有所有权。我问所有权是什么，他想了半天说：\"这只猴子是我的。\"光矿工费就够买台二手车。后来地板价跌了九成，群里一夜之间全是\"早期参与者\"，项目方带着钱消失了。他现在头像还是那只猴子，我看他是真的戴上了墨镜，不敢抬头见人。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1012", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "macau", "target_name": "澳门", "facts": ["A Portuguese settlement from the 1550s until the handover to China in December 1999.", "Casino revenue passed the Las Vegas Strip's in 2006, and at its 2013 peak, about $45 billion, was roughly seven times the Strip's.", "One company, STDM, held the casino monopoly for four decades until licences opened to competition in 2002.", "Among the most densely populated places on Earth, at roughly 20,000 people per square kilometre.", "The Cotai Strip stands on land reclaimed from the sea between the islands of Taipa and Coloane."], "a": {"item_id": "09aa41e46e5f", "text": "有种人我太熟了：上个月在澳门输了两万，发誓\"这次必须翻本\"。他怎么去的？早上七点坐大巴过拱北，在威尼斯人门口排队换筹码，图的就是那两万块能\"回家\"。结果他在百家乐桌前坐了一下午，翻本的钱全输给了赌场的冷气。傍晚他坐在大三巴牌坊底下啃葡挞，跟鸽子商量下个月的房租。你说他傻吗？不傻，他还算了账，每一笔都算得很认真。问题是，账算得再认真，庄家也是按规矩赢的。"}, "b": {"item_id": "8dc5e4ad252d", "text": "我有个朋友，在老家输了二十万，说要去澳门翻本。你听听这逻辑：在小区棋牌室输的钱，要去全亚洲最专业的地方赢回来。这就像被村口的狗咬了，你决定去动物园找老虎报仇。\n\n他是坐赌场的免费接驳大巴进去的。记住，澳门最大方的就是这趟车，因为它只管送你进去，不管你怎么回来。三天后他给我发了张照片，大三巴牌坊，就剩前面一面墙，后头全空了。他配了一句：“你看，跟我一样。”\n\n最绝的是回来那天，他手上那块表押在了葡京旁边的当铺里。他说没事，等下回签注办下来就去赎。兄弟，你兜里那张不是当票，是下一趟的车票。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1013", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "microsoft-teams", "target_name": "Microsoft Teams", "facts": ["Launched in 2017, bundled at no extra charge into Office 365, and replaced Skype for Business.", "After Slack's antitrust complaint, Microsoft unbundled Teams from Office in the EU in 2023 and worldwide in 2024; the European Commission still sent a statement of objections in 2024.", "Microsoft reported 320 million monthly active users in 2023.", "Microsoft shut down consumer Skype in May 2025 and sent its users to Teams."], "a": {"item_id": "cfb198c08db3", "text": "我们公司那个力推全员用Teams的VP，我真想当面问他图啥。图啥？图一份\"数字化转型\"的PPT。结果呢？全公司每天三小时盯着那三个跳动的小圆点，等对面打完一个\"好\"字。他自己更惨，上周开会共享屏幕，忘了点\"允许\"，全场看他浏览器收藏夹看了十分钟。现在他每晚十一点在\"全体员工\"频道里@所有人：\"大家收到了吗？\"收到了老板，大家的状态早改成\"请勿打扰\"了，就是不想回。"}, "b": {"item_id": "0c5f29fdb1c7", "text": "咱公司强推 Teams 的那位，我打听了，是采购。理由特别感人：“Office 365 套餐里本来就带着，不用另外花钱。”省了多少？年终述职写着呢：降本增效，三十万。\n\n三十万省下来了，然后呢？全公司每天问他同一个问题：“我昨天发的文件在哪？”他说：“Teams 里，‘文件’标签页。”其实在 SharePoint 里。他自己也是上周才知道 SharePoint 是什么。\n\n最惨的是，他在 Teams 里建了一个“团队”，团队里又建了一个“团队”，最后这个团队里只有他一个人。\n\n我们都在微信群里，聊他。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1014", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "emacs", "target_name": "Emacs", "facts": ["Began in 1976 as a set of macros for the TECO editor at MIT's AI Lab, written by Richard Stallman and others; GNU Emacs followed in 1985.", "Extensible in its own Lisp dialect, Emacs Lisp; the standing joke is that it is a fine operating system lacking only a good editor.", "Ships with a psychotherapist (M-x doctor), Tetris, a mail client, a calendar, and a web browser (eww).", "An old backronym is 'Eight Megabytes And Constantly Swapping', from when 8 MB was a lot of memory.", "'Emacs pinky' is the name for the hand strain blamed on its Control-key chords."], "a": {"item_id": "edb43822f3d2", "text": "我有个朋友，把 Emacs 当操作系统用。邮件在里面收，网页在里面看，连聊天都在里面聊。我问他图啥，他说：“不用切窗口，专注。”专注？他的 init.el 五千行，每个周末固定修配置，修的是上周为了省事写的配置。最绝的是，他最后装了 evil-mode，就是在 Emacs 里模拟 Vim 的按键。折腾十年，把全世界装进 Emacs，最后为了好用，把宿敌请了回来。现在他小指已经废了，他管这叫“Emacs 小指”，语气跟领了勋章似的。我说哥们，你这不是在用操作系统，是在给操作系统打工，还是不发工资的那种。"}, "b": {"item_id": "d38cb162db73", "text": "我有个朋友，把Emacs当操作系统用。邮件用mu4e，日程用org-mode，连窗口管理都交给EXWM。他的电脑开机只有一个目的：启动Emacs。\n\n我问他图什么，他说：“这样我一辈子都不用离开Emacs。”兄弟，这不叫自由，这叫软禁，门还是你自己拿elisp焊死的。\n\n他的init.el写了三千行，比他简历还长。上周升级了一个包，邮件收不了，日程没了，窗口也打不开，全家断电。查了一晚上，原因是少了一个括号。\n\n最惨的是他的小拇指。按了十年Ctrl，得了腱鞘炎。医生让他少按，他回家装了evil-mode。用Vim的键位，在Emacs里，躲Emacs。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1015", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "vision-pro", "target_name": "Apple Vision Pro", "facts": ["Released in February 2024 at $3,499, with an external battery pack on a cable rated for about two hours.", "'EyeSight' shows a rendering of the wearer's eyes on an outward-facing screen.", "Apple calls it 'spatial computing' and its App Store rules tell developers not to describe apps as AR, VR, XR, or MR.", "Reports during 2024 said Apple had cut production; analysts' first-year sales estimates were well under a million units.", "Weighs 600-650 g, before the battery."], "a": {"item_id": "6311e32fb855", "text": "地铁上见过一哥们，戴着Vision Pro。三万块钱扣在脸上，跟把首付戴出门了似的。你图啥？显得有钱？有钱人不坐地铁啊兄弟！\n\n他还挺投入，对着空气捏手指，捏一下，旁边大姐退一步。人家以为他在掐诀，准备做法。\n\n最绝的是前面那块屏，会给你看他的“眼睛”。他一进沉浸模式看电影，那双眼睛就变成一团彩色雾，全车厢都知道：这人灵魂出窍了，肉身暂存二号线。\n\n地铁一刹车，画面飘了，他伸手去抓虚拟窗口，攥住了一个大爷的保温杯。\n\n然后电池没电了，线还从脸上一路连到裤兜里，跟刚做完手术没拔管子似的。续航两小时，刚好够他坐过站。"}, "b": {"item_id": "7a31cad715fb", "text": "在地铁上戴 Vision Pro 的人，我真是服了。他们是谁？通常是刚花三万买了设备的程序员，或者想在朋友圈证明自己是\"未来人\"的部门主管。图什么？图全车人都看他。结果呢，他戴着个大眼罩，坐在车厢里对着空气划拉手指，像在跟一个看不见的前女友视频通话。旁边大爷以为他在看病，主动给他让座。最惨的是，他脸上那块外屏还亮着他自己的眼睛，一闪一闪的，全车人跟他对视，感觉被一只戴泳镜的大眼怪盯上了。电池包的线还拖在兜里，两个小时就没电。他摘下来一看，自己还在五号线上，被挤成了沙丁鱼。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1016", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "canberra", "target_name": "堪培拉", "facts": ["Chosen as a compromise because neither Sydney nor Melbourne would accept the other as capital; Australia's constitution required the capital to be at least 100 miles from Sydney.", "Designed by the Chicago architects Walter Burley Griffin and Marion Mahony Griffin, who won an international competition in 1912.", "Its centrepiece, Lake Burley Griffin, is artificial, created by damming the Molonglo River in 1963-64.", "Parliament House (1988) is built into Capital Hill under a lawn meant for citizens to walk over their politicians; the lawn was fenced off in 2017 for security.", "Melbourne served as the capital until 1927 while Canberra was being built."], "a": {"item_id": "cd30c7840947", "text": "你们知道谁会为了工作搬去堪培拉吗？二十三岁、学政治学、在悉尼租不起房的年轻人。面试官说：“APS4，稳定。”他听成了“国家需要你”。\n\n搬过去第一周，他在环岛里绕了六圈，因为这座城的路就是一个环岛接一个环岛。第二周，喜鹊开始俯冲啄他脑袋。连鸟都比他先熟悉这片土地。\n\n三年后他升到EL1，天天写部长简报，写完被删成一句话。终于在Gungahlin买了房，周末的娱乐是去湖边看别人骑车。\n\n回悉尼参加同学会，大家问他在干嘛。他说：“我在帮部长准备参议院听证会。”\n\n大家又问：“那你的人生呢？”\n\n他愣了三秒：“这个问题……我们会后书面答复。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "46416cb1f80d", "text": "你身边肯定有这种人：为了“离权力近一点”，从悉尼搬去堪培拉。公务员、政策顾问、游说公司的，图的都是升职。三年后，他们每天开四十分钟车去一栋楼，写四十页报告，结论永远是“建议进一步研究”。周五下午，全城人开三小时车回悉尼，就剩他们守着伯利·格里芬湖。全城最大的娱乐是绕湖跑步，跑完一圈，正好跑回办公室。权力确实在附近，就在国会大厦里，可每次想见，都得先预约，还得等部长助理回邮件。离权力近了，离生活远了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1017", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "chicago-deep-dish", "target_name": "芝加哥深盘披萨", "facts": ["Created at Pizzeria Uno in Chicago in 1943; credit is disputed among founder Ike Sewell, his partner Ric Riccardo, and cook Rudy Malnati.", "Assembled upside down: sliced mozzarella on the dough, then toppings, then crushed tomatoes on top, so the cheese does not scorch during the long bake.", "Jon Stewart called it 'tomato soup in a bread bowl' and 'a casserole' on The Daily Show in 2013.", "Chicagoans' everyday pizza is thin 'tavern-style' cut into squares; GrubHub order data reported by WBEZ in 2013 showed city residents ordering non-deep-dish pizza about 10 to 1 over deep dish, with deep dish and stuffed making up under 9% of orders."], "a": {"item_id": "2c14d9c46ccf", "text": "最嘴硬的，是来芝加哥旅游的人。排队一小时，服务员说深盘要烤四十五分钟。你都等到这份上了，端上来什么你都得认它是披萨。结果奶酪在底下，酱在上面，还得用刀叉，这叫披萨？这叫面饼做的番茄肉酱焗饭。吃完一块，人瘫在椅子上，像刚做完开胸手术。可你还是要发朋友圈：“正宗芝加哥披萨！”因为你不能承认，自己花了两百美元，吃了顿砂锅。最讽刺的是本地人，他们平时吃的是切成小方块的薄底。深盘就跟全聚德一样，是留着带外地朋友去的。"}, "b": {"item_id": "2802b3de89d7", "text": "管芝加哥深盘叫披萨的人，我见过三种。本地人嘴上说\"我们这儿就这么吃\"；网红拍完照发帖写\"正宗深盘披萨\"；还有一种最狠，纽约来的，吃第一口就开始跟服务员讲道理。图什么？图一个名分。可深盘是个铁锅里烤四十五分钟的芝士蛋糕，芝士垫在底下，番茄酱盖在最上面，你得拿刀拿叉，切到第三块，芝士还在拉丝。这帮人非要把它塞进\"披萨\"这个词里。结果那位纽约哥吃完，服务员问要不要打包，他说：\"不用，我要带一锅回家，当披萨。\"然后抱着一个烫手的铁锅挤地铁，全车人都以为他在搬家。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1018", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "crocs", "target_name": "Crocs 洞洞鞋", "facts": ["Founded in Boulder, Colorado, in 2002; the first clog debuted at the Fort Lauderdale boat show as a boating shoe.", "Made of 'Croslite', which Crocs describes as a proprietary closed-cell resin foam and which third parties identify as an injection-moulded EVA foam.", "Balenciaga sold a platform Crocs for about $850 in 2018.", "The stock fell more than 90% from its 2007 peak during the 2008 crisis; the company then boomed in the pandemic and gave away 860,000 pairs to healthcare workers in 2020.", "The holes are officially 'ventilation ports' and the charms that go in them are 'Jibbitz', a company Crocs bought in 2006."], "a": {"item_id": "e55404ed5b22", "text": "你们知道穿洞洞鞋出门的都是什么人吗？一种是刚下飞机、防晒霜还没抹匀的人，另一种是觉得\"我就是不在乎形象\"的人。图什么？图脚舒服，图鞋上那些小挂件能挂满一圈，走路叮叮当当，像个移动的钥匙串。结果呢？去趟海边，洞洞里灌满沙子，走一步倒一把，像在给沙滩做漏斗。下雨天更惨，鞋底那几个孔等于给雨水开了后门，袜子从脚踝湿到脚趾。最要命的是全程还得踩着\"吧唧吧唧\"的声音进电梯，全电梯的人都知道你来了。后来他真的不出门了，因为他终于明白，别人不是看不见他，是听得见他。"}, "b": {"item_id": "d0de4b131b62", "text": "穿洞洞鞋出门的人，分两种：真懒的，和装懒的。装懒的最好认，鞋上插满智必星，小恐龙、小汉堡、小皮卡丘，一只脚背就是一个摊位。你图舒服我理解，你在鞋上开连锁店干什么？\n\n他们还有个绝招：把脑门上那根带子往后一掰，“我切换运动模式了”。兄弟，带子掰过去，你是能跑过谁？\n\n下场都一样。下雨天，鞋面上那十几个洞全是进水口，走一步咕叽一声。晴天更惨，小石子从洞里钻进去卡在脚心，又不能当街脱，只能单腿跳着甩，像在给鞋做心肺复苏。\n\n回家一看，智必星掉了三个。人是舒服了，资产缩水了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1019", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "windows-8", "target_name": "Windows 8", "facts": ["Released in October 2012; removed the Start button and booted into a full-screen grid of tiles designed for touch screens most PCs did not have.", "The 8.1 update in 2013 put a Start button back, Windows 10 (2015) restored the Start menu, and Microsoft skipped the name Windows 9 altogether.", "Microsoft dropped the 'Metro' name in August 2012 after reports that German retailer Metro AG had objected on trademark grounds; Microsoft called 'Metro' a code name and denied any litigation, and the style was afterwards known informally as 'Modern UI'.", "The 'Charms bar' hid Search, Share, and Settings behind an invisible swipe from the right edge.", "Windows RT, the ARM edition launched alongside it, could not run ordinary Windows programs; Microsoft wrote down $900 million of unsold Surface RT tablets in 2013."], "a": {"item_id": "4dea39aaf51b", "text": "我有个哥们儿，2012年非要给台式机装Windows 8，理由是“新的肯定比旧的好”。我说你这电脑连个触屏都没有，他说没事，“我用鼠标点磁贴”。\n\n装完那天他请我去看。开机，满屏花花绿绿的大方块，跟营业厅的叫号屏似的。他拿着鼠标在那儿横着拖，拖了半天才找到“桌面”这个磁贴。我说你装了个新系统，就为了点进去看旧桌面？\n\n最惨的是关机。开始按钮没了，他找了十分钟，最后鼠标往右上角一怼，蹦出一排竖条，设置，电源，关机。三步。他关一次机，比他的电脑开一次机还慢。\n\n现在他养成了个习惯：鼠标一闲下来，就往屏幕角落里甩。别人的鼠标是用来点东西的，他的鼠标是用来跟屏幕四个角打游击的。\n\n后来8.1把开始按钮还给他了，他激动得点了一下。结果点开的还是那屏大方块。"}, "b": {"item_id": "09a19029d52d", "text": "有一种人，专门给台式机装 Windows 8。你问他图啥？他说，触屏才是未来，我得提前用上。问题是他这台机器是机箱加显示器加一个鼠标，屏幕根本摸不到。开机后满屏都是色块磁贴，像走进一家超市，货架上全是天气和新闻，唯独没有他要的开始菜单。他想关机，找了二十分钟，最后在屏幕右边缘一划，弹出一个叫\"超级按钮\"的东西。我很怀疑取这名字的人从来没关过机。最后他花九十块买了个第三方软件，把开始按钮请了回来。他现在逢人就说：\"我用的是 Windows 8，但我把它改回了 Windows 7。\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1020", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hsq", "target_name": "HSQ 电子束光刻胶", "facts": ["Hydrogen silsesquioxane, sold by Dow Corning as FOx ('flowable oxide') and designed as a spin-on dielectric for chip interconnects; lithographers adopted it as a negative-tone resist.", "Exposure converts its cage structure into a cross-linked, silica-like network, so the developed pattern is essentially SiO2.", "Holds the resolution records for electron-beam lithography: isolated features around 5 nm, dense lines below 10 nm half-pitch, with high-contrast 'salty' NaOH/NaCl development.", "Must be kept refrigerated (Dow Corning's datasheet gives a six-month shelf life at 5 °C); the solution ages into a jelly, and its effective sensitivity shifts with processing delays because cross-linking continues after exposure.", "DuPont, which inherited Dow Corning's FOx/XR-1541 HSQ line, discontinued it and ended production at the end of 2025; labs such as EPFL's CMi switched to third-party HSQ from EM Resist or DisChem, both formulated from AQM powder."], "a": {"item_id": "98b1aee74d1a", "text": "实验室里最危险的人，是那种盯着SEM说“PMMA不够细”的博士生。为了论文里一张“8纳米线宽”的图，他换了HSQ。从此他的人生就变了。\n\n先说钱，一小瓶贵得像茅台，还得冷藏，比他本人过得精致。开封了就开始老化，他每天看那瓶胶的眼神，比看他妈都焦虑。\n\n再说剂量，HSQ灵敏度低，曝一个芯片，电子束要写一整夜。别人约会，他守机台。\n\n显影还得加盐，学长说“盐显影对比度高”，他就真去称氯化钠，跟腌咸菜似的。\n\n最绝的是曝完它变成二氧化硅，洗不掉！想返工？上氢氟酸。\n\n最后图出来了，线是真细，倒了一片。导师一看：“挺好，再做一批。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "e6a9d412465a", "text": "有人为了分辨率，把 PMMA 换成 HSQ。我就想问，你图啥？PMMA 便宜、好剥离、导师不骂你。HSQ 呢？得放冰箱，一开瓶就倒计时，跟生鲜外卖一个待遇。剂量要几千，写一小块图形，电子束扫一整夜，你在机房陪着，比陪对象都久。早上显影，一根 8 纳米的线，倒了，跟隔壁那根粘一块了。再来一轮，终于立住了，你想把多余的去掉？不好意思，曝光完它就是二氧化硅，想洗掉得上氢氟酸，连你的器件一块洗。最后论文里写“sub-10 nm”，那是三十张电镜图里挑出来的那一张，剩下二十九张，叫 preliminary data。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1021", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "graphene", "target_name": "石墨烯", "facts": ["Isolated in 2004 by Andre Geim and Konstantin Novoselov at Manchester by peeling graphite with Scotch tape; Nobel Prize in Physics, 2010.", "The EU's Graphene Flagship, launched in 2013, committed €1 billion over ten years.", "A 2018 study in Advanced Materials tested 'graphene' from 60 producers and found none with more than 50% graphene content; most was graphite flakes.", "Sold in tennis rackets, running shoes, and headphones as a performance ingredient.", "Geim also won an Ig Nobel Prize in 2000 for levitating a frog with magnets."], "a": {"item_id": "20a7d109770e", "text": "我有个同学读材料的，逢人就说石墨烯要改变世界。每次开场一模一样：2004年，两个科学家拿胶带从铅笔芯上粘下一层碳，拿了诺贝尔奖。他讲得特别骄傲，好像那卷胶带是他家的。\n\n我问他：比钢硬，比铜导电，那你怎么还合租？他说量产有瓶颈。十年了，这瓶颈比石墨烯还硬。\n\n后来他创业，产品终于出来了：石墨烯保暖内衣。改变世界的材料，最后改变的是秋裤的价格。铅笔芯的成分，卖出羽绒服的价，还跟我说发热是黑科技。大哥，那是你自己急的。\n\n现在他融不到钱了，开始去我妈的养生群讲课。我妈听完说：小伙子，你这套跟我买的磁疗枕头是一个路数。"}, "b": {"item_id": "8757c13cc756", "text": "我有个朋友，逢人就说石墨烯要改变世界。你问他石墨烯是什么，他说是碳原子排成六边形的单层，薄到只有一个原子厚。行，那你知道铅笔芯是什么吗？石墨。石墨就是一层层石墨烯叠起来的。你小学就天天在用它写字，世界怎么没变？他卖保暖内衣，吊牌上印着\"石墨烯含量\"，我让他拿检测报告，他说报告在另一个仓库。去年厂子抵给了银行。现在他改卖石墨烯足浴盆，广告写\"一泡，改变人生\"。我去他店里看了，人确实变了，老婆跑了，他还在那儿泡脚。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1022", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "luosifen", "target_name": "螺蛳粉", "facts": ["A rice-noodle dish from Liuzhou, Guangxi; the smell comes from pickled bamboo shoots (酸笋), not from the river snails, which only flavour the broth.", "The instant version boomed during the 2020 lockdowns; Liuzhou's government reported packaged luosifen sales of over 10 billion yuan that year.", "Added to China's national intangible cultural heritage list in 2021.", "Liuzhou opened a luosifen industry college in 2020 to train workers for the trade."], "a": {"item_id": "06a4c1a635ed", "text": "合租房里煮螺蛳粉的人，都信一句话：“闻着臭，吃着香。”这话是给吃的人用的。我们室友只拿到前半句。\n\n他煮十分钟，爽了。我在房间里闻三天，连根腐竹都没分到。\n\n他还特别讲礼貌，煮之前敲我门：“我煮螺蛳粉，你介意吗？”这哪是问，这是通知，跟飞机广播“前方有气流”一样，你介不介意，它都得颠。\n\n然后他开窗，开抽油烟机，觉得自己尽力了。兄弟，酸笋那玩意儿你开窗它不走，它是在考察房子，想签长租。\n\n结果第二天上班，同事凑过来：“你昨天是不是吃螺蛳粉了？”\n\n我没吃啊！\n\n我连汤都没喝上一口，就被螺蛳粉吃了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "5c463dbea536", "text": "合租房里煮螺蛳粉的人，我太懂你们了。图什么？一包才八块钱，深更半夜饿了，想吃口有灵魂的。可你们不知道，那袋酸笋一拆开，整层楼都被你\"请\"来吃饭了。门缝底下往外冒的不是蒸汽，是战书。室友从卧室探出头，鼻子一抽，眼神像在看犯罪现场。第二天群里弹出一条消息：\"谁又煮螺蛳粉了？\"你把头埋进碗里，一句话不敢说。最后的下场是：窗户开成了通风道，外套挂三天还带着河沟味，押金一分不剩，房东在合同里加了一条\"禁止煮螺蛳粉\"。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1023", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "slack", "target_name": "Slack", "facts": ["Spun out in 2013 from the internal chat tool of Tiny Speck's failed game Glitch; founder Stewart Butterfield had earlier spun Flickr out of another failed game.", "The name is a backronym: 'Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge'.", "Salesforce bought it for $27.7 billion in 2021.", "Filed an EU antitrust complaint against Microsoft in 2020 over the bundling of Teams with Office."], "a": {"item_id": "99cfffb47c21", "text": "有些老板非要用Slack管公司。开会太累，他说以后都在#general里说。结果一天九百条消息，他想每条都看，看到第三天放弃，给所有消息点了个小黄脸。他觉得这叫留痕，于是逢事必@channel，全公司手机同时震动，财务一边对账一边回了个\"收到\"的表情。频道越建越多：#项目A、#项目A-最终版、#项目A-真的最终版。员工每天花一小时在搜索框里找文件，找到的都是三个月前的讨论串，串里四十七条回复，最后一条是老板自己发的：\"这个先放放。\""}, "b": {"item_id": "9ef25649a571", "text": "有一种老板，管公司全靠Slack。他图什么？图那种指挥作战的感觉。半夜两点在#general里@channel，三百个人的手机同时“咚”一声，他躺床上听着，跟听礼炮似的。\n\n他不开会，他回“👀”。你熬一礼拜的方案，换来一个眼睛表情，啥意思没人知道，你自己悟。\n\n他考勤看绿点。谁头像一灰，私信立马弹出一个“？”。于是全员网购了鼠标晃动器，公司最勤奋的员工，是十九块九包邮的小马达。\n\n下场呢？所有决策都埋在某条thread的第四十七楼，谁也翻不着。免费版只存九十天，这家公司的战略每三个月自动清空一次。他还挺得意：“看，我们多敏捷。”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1024", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hawaiian-pizza", "target_name": "夏威夷披萨", "facts": ["Invented in 1962 by Sam Panopoulos, a Greek immigrant, at the Satellite Restaurant in Chatham, Ontario, Canada; named after the brand of canned pineapple he used.", "Iceland's president Guðni Th. Jóhannesson said in 2017 that he would ban pineapple on pizza if he could, then clarified that a president has no such power.", "Hawaii is no longer a major pineapple producer: Del Monte shut its Hawaiian pineapple operations in November 2006, two years ahead of its announced 2008 exit, the state's last cannery closed in 2007, and most canned pineapple now comes from Thailand, Indonesia, and the Philippines.", "Panopoulos, whose restaurant also served Chinese dishes, told the Toronto Sun in 2010 that 'in those days people didn't mix the tastes like sweet and sour, except for Chinese food'."], "a": {"item_id": "5777117eb6e0", "text": "我发现，点夏威夷披萨的人有个共同点：菜单上二十种口味，他偏要带菠萝的。你问为什么，他说“想吃点水果”。兄弟，想吃水果，隔壁就是水果店！你这是把果盘塞进烤箱赎罪啊。\n\n他图什么？图个“我很包容”。他点一个夏威夷，全桌就得陪他吵半小时“菠萝能不能上披萨”，他的人设就立住了：全场只有他敢。\n\n下场呢？披萨一到，热菠萝一咬，汁“滋”地喷上颚，烫得他眼泪直流，还得笑着说“嗯，酸甜，有层次”。最惨的是，这玩意儿是加拿大人发明的，夏威夷人自己都不认。你为了一块夏威夷都不要的披萨，得罪了整个意大利。"}, "b": {"item_id": "0878d1084313", "text": "每桌都有那么一个人，菜单一翻就说：“来个夏威夷的。”全桌瞬间安静，像有人宣布要在群里发起投票。\n\n他图什么？图人设。他想让你知道他不随大流，可他点的是每家店菜单上都有的第三个披萨。他心里还有本账：菠萝算水果，火腿算肉，一块下去，营养均衡。\n\n下场呢？别人点的都见底了，只剩他那盘，菠萝汁把饼底泡软，拎起来像拎一条热毛巾。他还硬撑：“真的好吃，你们不懂。”\n\n你知道夏威夷披萨是谁发明的吗？一个住在加拿大的希腊人。夏威夷不认，意大利不承认，全世界最后就剩你一个人替它出头。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1025", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "crocs", "target_name": "Crocs 洞洞鞋", "facts": ["Founded in Boulder, Colorado, in 2002; the first clog debuted at the Fort Lauderdale boat show as a boating shoe.", "Made of 'Croslite', which Crocs describes as a proprietary closed-cell resin foam and which third parties identify as an injection-moulded EVA foam.", "Balenciaga sold a platform Crocs for about $850 in 2018.", "The stock fell more than 90% from its 2007 peak during the 2008 crisis; the company then boomed in the pandemic and gave away 860,000 pairs to healthcare workers in 2020.", "The holes are officially 'ventilation ports' and the charms that go in them are 'Jibbitz', a company Crocs bought in 2006."], "a": {"item_id": "0269f5e79bdf", "text": "穿洞洞鞋出门的人，开口都是同一句：“我就图个舒服。”行，那你解释一下鞋面上那一排小挂件。小熊、披萨、一个字母H，这哪是图舒服，这是脚上开了个朋友圈。\n\n还有那根后跟带，永远往后翻，他们管这叫“运动模式”。哥们，你是下楼取快递。真运动谁穿这个？这个模式唯一的功能，就是方便扶梯把你的鞋吃了。\n\n下场也都一样。夏天晒完，脚背上一块镂空格子，像被烤架翻过面。下雨天更惨，洞是通的，水进得特别积极，走一步，咕叽，咕叽，脚上养了两只青蛙。\n\n最后他还要补一句：“这个其实防臭。”没人问你。"}, "b": {"item_id": "15501877150c", "text": "你们见过那种穿卡骆驰洞洞鞋出门的人吗？他们的理由永远是“舒服”。舒服到什么程度？舒服到能穿着它去相亲，对方低头一看，两排塑料坑，脚趾在里面像泡温泉。他图什么？图不用系鞋带，图能下水、能进厨房、能上超市，等于把拖鞋、雨鞋、运动鞋一起省了。结果呢？去趟公园，脚底的洞灌进三粒沙子，一步一个噗嗤，全场都知道他来了。最后鞋帮上挂满小挂件，走起路来叮叮当当，像个移动的钥匙串，他还在朋友圈写：这才是生活的本质。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1026", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "canberra", "target_name": "堪培拉", "facts": ["Chosen as a compromise because neither Sydney nor Melbourne would accept the other as capital; Australia's constitution required the capital to be at least 100 miles from Sydney.", "Designed by the Chicago architects Walter Burley Griffin and Marion Mahony Griffin, who won an international competition in 1912.", "Its centrepiece, Lake Burley Griffin, is artificial, created by damming the Molonglo River in 1963-64.", "Parliament House (1988) is built into Capital Hill under a lawn meant for citizens to walk over their politicians; the lawn was fenced off in 2017 for security.", "Melbourne served as the capital until 1927 while Canberra was being built."], "a": {"item_id": "cd30c7840947", "text": "你们知道谁会为了工作搬去堪培拉吗？二十三岁、学政治学、在悉尼租不起房的年轻人。面试官说：“APS4，稳定。”他听成了“国家需要你”。\n\n搬过去第一周，他在环岛里绕了六圈，因为这座城的路就是一个环岛接一个环岛。第二周，喜鹊开始俯冲啄他脑袋。连鸟都比他先熟悉这片土地。\n\n三年后他升到EL1，天天写部长简报，写完被删成一句话。终于在Gungahlin买了房，周末的娱乐是去湖边看别人骑车。\n\n回悉尼参加同学会，大家问他在干嘛。他说：“我在帮部长准备参议院听证会。”\n\n大家又问：“那你的人生呢？”\n\n他愣了三秒：“这个问题……我们会后书面答复。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "a93ae6b79980", "text": "有一种人我特别佩服：为了工作搬去堪培拉。问他图什么，他说：“稳定，公务员，铁饭碗。”行，铁饭碗，里面装的是空气。\n\n他们的周末是这样的：周六逛一遍Canberra Centre，周日再逛一遍，因为周六没买。想刺激一点？开车绕国会山的环岛，一圈，两圈，三圈，这就是全城夜生活。而且开错一个出口，你就出了首都领地，进新南威尔士了。\n\n第一年他说“我喜欢安静”；第二年开始对着格里芬湖说心里话；第三年每个月飞悉尼，不是玩，是想确认世界上还有陌生人。\n\n最惨的是升职。升到哪儿？换个部门，在马路对面。政府一换届，部门改名，他人没动，只是名片重印。这是堪培拉人一生里最大的变故。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1027", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "windows-vista", "target_name": "Windows Vista", "facts": ["Released to consumers in January 2007, after the 'Longhorn' project was reset in 2004 and headline features such as the WinFS file system were dropped.", "User Account Control prompted so often that Apple's 'Get a Mac' campaign ran an ad about 'Cancel or Allow'.", "'Windows Vista Capable' stickers went on PCs that could only run Home Basic, without the Aero interface, and led to a class-action lawsuit.", "Windows 7 (2009), widely praised, was largely a tuned-up Vista on the same kernel line; Vista support ended in April 2017."], "a": {"item_id": "d1b5347e5ac6", "text": "第一天就升级 Vista 的人，我研究过，都是那种等不及第一个补丁的人。他们图什么？图那个毛玻璃窗口。Aero，半透明的，能隐约看到后面的桌面，就像前任的 QQ 空间，什么都看不清，但就是忍不住盯着。\n\n结果装完发现：打印机没驱动，声卡没驱动，连他那个二手摄像头也认不出他了。电脑配置 1G 内存，风扇转得像要起飞，机身还贴着“Vista Ready”的贴纸，Ready 的是风扇。\n\n最惨的是那个弹窗：“Windows 需要您的许可才能继续。”他想删个文件，系统问他：“你确定吗？”他点“允许”。系统又问：“你真的确定吗？”他再点“允许”。这哪是操作系统，这是我妈。\n\n三天后，他的 MSN 签名改了：“Vista 用户，求 XP 安装盘，在线等，挺急的。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "0edc712dc233", "text": "2007年第一天就装Vista的人，我认识一个。他还买的旗舰版，两千多，就为了微软承诺的“独家福利”，最后等来一个德州扑克和一张会动的桌面。他电脑上贴着“Vista Capable”，意思是“能装”，没说“能用”。512兆内存硬开Aero毛玻璃，风扇响得像在替他喊冤。打印机没驱动，摄像头没驱动，就侧边栏那个小时钟特别准，每一秒都在提醒他后悔。最狠的是那个弹窗：“是否允许此程序更改？”微软问了他一千遍，他一千遍都点了“允许”，从来没想过点“取消”。现在他逢人就说自己是最早的尝鲜派。不，兄弟，你是最早降级回XP的那批。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1028", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "carbon-nanotubes", "target_name": "碳纳米管", "facts": ["Reported by Sumio Iijima in Nature in 1991.", "Promised as the cable for a space elevator; a 2006 analysis showed that ordinary defects cut the strength of a macroscopic cable far below the ideal value.", "Long, rigid multi-walled nanotubes behaved like asbestos fibres in a 2008 mouse study in Nature Nanotechnology.", "Sorting metallic from semiconducting tubes has blocked nanotube electronics for three decades; MIT's 16-bit RV16X-NANO processor (2019) is still a lab demonstration.", "Today they are sold mainly as a conductive additive in lithium-ion battery electrodes."], "a": {"item_id": "ef055d34e6aa", "text": "我认识一个博士，专门拿碳纳米管跟投资人讲故事。PPT第一页永远是太空电梯：“强度是钢的一百倍，一根拉到同步轨道！”投资人问现在能做多长，他说：“厘米级。”三万六千公里，差了也就九个数量级，四舍五入算到了。\n\n第二页讲替代硅芯片。难点只有一个：管子卷出来，一部分导电，一部分是半导体，还分不开。这好比开饭馆，菜一半能吃一半有毒，你还挑不出来，但你跟股东说下季度开连锁。\n\n这故事从1991年讲到现在，“再给十年”讲了三遍。最后他去哪了？给锂电池厂卖导电浆料，按吨算钱。PPT没删，太空电梯那页还在，标题改成了“愿景”。"}, "b": {"item_id": "c20ac403ef4f", "text": "你们见过拿碳纳米管融资的人吗？一般是个博士，PPT第一页就是太空电梯：“强度是钢的一百倍，一根线从地面拉到三万六千公里外！”投资人听得热泪盈眶。\n\n我就想问：你实验室里最长的一根有多长？“两厘米。”那你这不叫太空电梯，叫太空牙线。\n\n他图啥？图下一轮。电梯盖不盖得成不重要，估值得先上去。\n\n五年后再看，公司转型了，现在卖“导电剂”，就是往锂电池里掺的那点黑粉，一公斤赚几块钱。投资人问：“电梯呢？”博士说：“电梯……在电池里，形成了导电网络。”\n\n行，当年说要通天，现在是在电池里修了个楼梯间。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1029", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "oat-milk", "target_name": "燕麦奶", "facts": ["Oatly started in Sweden in the 1990s, built on Lund University research by Rickard Öste on lactose intolerance.", "Oatly's slogan 'It's like milk but made for humans' was banned in Sweden in 2015 after the dairy lobby LRF Mjölk sued; Oatly then published the lawsuit as advertising.", "Oatly listed on Nasdaq in May 2021 at a valuation near $10 billion; the shares later lost more than 90% of their value.", "Commercial oat milk is made by breaking oat starch into maltose with amylase enzymes, and many brands, Oatly among them, add rapeseed oil; the result has more sugar and a higher glycaemic index than almond or soy milk."], "a": {"item_id": "f5f4ee009b3f", "text": "每个咖啡店都有这么一位，开口就是：\"拿铁，换燕麦奶。\"语气像在宣布退出某个组织。你问为什么，他说乳糖不耐。兄弟，你手边那块芝士蛋糕是自己长出来的吗？你这乳糖不耐很精准，只对咖啡里的牛奶不耐。\n\n再问，他说为了健康。燕麦奶，燕麦煮开后淀粉变成糖，热量比牛奶还高。所以他多花五块钱，多摄入一点糖，喝的是一杯稀释过的麦片粥汤，然后发朋友圈：\"今天也是植物基的一天。\"\n\n下场呢？咖啡一入口，满嘴湿麦片味，像早饭的碗没洗干净，被人顺手拿来接了咖啡。他还硬喝完，边喝边说：\"还挺顺滑的。\"\n\n对，是顺滑，因为你正在喝粥。"}, "b": {"item_id": "0f1dbb4a84d2", "text": "我有个同事，点拿铁必须换燕麦奶，加五块。问他为啥，他说乳糖不耐。上礼拜我亲眼看他干掉一整块芝士蛋糕，面不改色。乳糖不耐？我看你是乳糖不耐“便宜”。\n\n后来他又改口，说是为了健康、为了地球。哥，燕麦奶想打出奶泡，里面得加油，菜籽油。你每天早上花三十八，喝一杯“过了油的燕麦水”，还觉得自己在拯救地球。\n\n最惨的是，小时候你奶奶天天早上给你煮燕麦粥，你嫌弃，说“这是马吃的”。现在你多花五块钱，把粥兑进咖啡里，还拍照发朋友圈。\n\n马都没你讲究。马吃燕麦，至少不用排队。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1030", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "carbon-nanotubes", "target_name": "碳纳米管", "facts": ["Reported by Sumio Iijima in Nature in 1991.", "Promised as the cable for a space elevator; a 2006 analysis showed that ordinary defects cut the strength of a macroscopic cable far below the ideal value.", "Long, rigid multi-walled nanotubes behaved like asbestos fibres in a 2008 mouse study in Nature Nanotechnology.", "Sorting metallic from semiconducting tubes has blocked nanotube electronics for three decades; MIT's 16-bit RV16X-NANO processor (2019) is still a lab demonstration.", "Today they are sold mainly as a conductive additive in lithium-ion battery electrodes."], "a": {"item_id": "55f4d79ee616", "text": "我认识一个搞碳纳米管的博士，去路演，PPT第一页写：强度是钢的一百倍，重量只有钢的六分之一。投资人眼睛全亮了，有人举手问：能造太空电梯吗？他说理论上可以。全场鼓掌。\"理论上\"，是这圈子最值钱的三个字。他没讲的是，他那批管子里导电的和不导电的混在一起，像一锅分不清的挂面，想分开一根，得先烧掉一栋楼的钱。投资人也没问。后来他融了三轮，估值翻了几倍，公司改名叫\"某某量子新材料\"，实验室里那罐黑乎乎的粉，还是原来那罐。"}, "b": {"item_id": "9252499a4c39", "text": "做碳纳米管创业的博士，路演第一页永远是太空电梯。说这玩意儿比钢强一百倍，重量才六分之一，投资人眼睛都亮了。他们图啥？图的是投资人别翻第二页。第二页写着：实验室里最长做到几十厘米，太空电梯需要三万六千公里，差一亿倍，他管这叫“早期阶段”。投资人说看看样品，他掏出一小瓶黑粉，说这就是未来。结果钱融到了，电梯没修成，产品卖给电池厂当导电剂，论公斤称，跟墨粉一个价位。最后他也活成了碳纳米管：单打独斗个个都强，凑一块儿就团聚，缠成一团，怎么都分不开。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1031", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "stinky-tofu", "target_name": "臭豆腐", "facts": ["Tofu soaked for hours to a day or two in a brine that has itself fermented for months from vegetables such as bamboo shoots and mustard greens, often with dried shrimp or meat; the smell is the brine's, not the bean's.", "Changsha's version is black, deep-fried and doused in chili; Taiwan's night-market version is golden and served with pickled cabbage; Shaoxing's, brined with amaranth stalks, is fried pale yellow or steamed with other 'stinky' pickles.", "Legend credits Wang Zhihe, a Qing-era exam candidate who left his unsold tofu too long in 1669; a Beijing fermented-bean-curd brand still carries his name.", "Street vendors are its main outlet, and the smell carries for a block."], "a": {"item_id": "083ba71caee5", "text": "街边吃臭豆腐的人，我观察过，全是一个表情：一边嚼一边皱眉，一边皱眉一边跟你说“闻着臭，吃着香”。哥，这句话你每吃一块都要念一遍，说明你自己也不太信，你这是在给自己做法。\n\n图什么？图那个味儿。可那个味儿你根本不用吃啊，你从摊前路过三米，它已经替你吃过一遍了。\n\n最惨的是吃完。人家吃烤串，擦擦嘴就走；你吃完，嘴擦干净了，味儿还在加班。进电梯，一电梯人开始互相怀疑；回到家，你妈第一句不是“吃饭没”，是“你刚从哪个厕所出来”。\n\n八块钱，买了一身洗不掉的嫌疑。还给你浇了辣椒。"}, "b": {"item_id": "30b6f794ee22", "text": "街边吃臭豆腐的人，我观察过，都有点自我感动。排二十分钟队，就为了吃一句广告语：\"闻着臭，吃着香。\"这话翻译过来就是\"我人品不行，但我内在美\"，他们居然信了。\n\n你再看那个姿势：一手端纸碗，一手捏牙签，站在马路牙子上，腰弯九十度，对着一块豆腐鞠躬，生怕辣汤滴到白鞋上。\n\n最惨是吃完以后。你以为臭味留在嘴里？不，它搬进你外套里了。坐地铁，旁边的人默默挪开一个身位，你还觉得是自己今天气场强。\n\n回到家，女朋友一开门：\"你是不是路过了一场火灾？\"你说：\"没有，我吃了点好的。\"她说：\"那你把好的留在门口。\"\n\n人家豆腐是臭卤泡了一个月才臭的，你五分钟就追平了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1032", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "latex", "target_name": "LaTeX", "facts": ["Leslie Lamport wrote LaTeX in the early 1980s and released it in 1984 as a macro layer on Donald Knuth's TeX (1978), which Knuth built because he disliked how the galley proofs of the new edition of his own book looked.", "TeX's version number converges on π (3.141592653 since 2021), and Knuth's TeX bug bounty started at one hexadecimal dollar, $2.56, doubling yearly until frozen at $327.68; he stopped mailing real cheques in 2008 after fraud and now credits accounts at his fictional Bank of San Serriffe, and only 9 of the 275 cheques he sent from 2006 on were ever cashed.", "Its best-known warning is 'Underfull \\hbox (badness 10000)'; overfull lines are reported as 'Overfull \\hbox (Npt too wide)', and figures float to wherever the algorithm decides, not where the author put them.", "A single missing brace can produce an error reported pages away from the mistake, and a document can need three compiler passes to get its references right."], "a": {"item_id": "cc677fdb9115", "text": "领导让写个备忘录，内容就一句话：聚餐从周五改到周四。他说，Word？那是给没追求的人用的。打开Overleaf，先配ctex，再装十几个宏包，再调页边距。三小时后，这句话终于有了页眉、目录，还有参考文献，聚餐通知，引了两条。\n\n然后他在里面打了句“人均50$”，编译报错：Missing $ inserted。他的聚餐，被当成了数学公式。\n\n改完之后，“谢谢”两个字孤零零掉到第三页。他又花四十分钟调行距，就为了把这两个字塞回去。\n\n同事回复：“收到，能发个Word版吗？我想改个字。”\n\n他看着那个PDF，终于明白：他排的不是备忘录，是一座纪念碑，漂亮，工整，谁都不敢动。"}, "b": {"item_id": "4c2291ae9bb7", "text": "我们组有个哥们，通知周五聚餐，就两页备忘录，他非要用LaTeX排。这种人你们见过吧？他不是在写字，他是在“编译”。开头先敲四十行\\usepackage，正文一个字没有，导言区已经比来吃饭的人还多了。图什么？就图那个Computer Modern字体。PDF一打开，扑面而来一股“本人发过论文”的气息。结果呢？“周五晚七点”那一行报了个Overfull \\hbox，冒出页边三毫米，他调到凌晨两点。第二天大家都到了，就他没来。他在等BibTeX跑第二遍。我说一个吃饭通知，哪来的参考文献？他说：“我\\cite了菜单。”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1033", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "pmma", "target_name": "PMMA 电子束光刻胶", "facts": ["The same polymer as Plexiglas and acrylic glass, dissolved in anisole or chlorobenzene and spun into a thin film.", "The oldest electron-beam resist still in everyday use; IBM researchers reported it as one in the late 1960s.", "Positive tone: the beam breaks polymer chains and the exposed region dissolves in a MIBK:IPA developer.", "Resolves below 10 nm but has poor sensitivity, so doses of hundreds to over a thousand µC/cm² (depending on voltage) make writing slow.", "Poor dry-etch resistance, so it is used mostly for lift-off rather than as an etch mask."], "a": {"item_id": "c782f4406d74", "text": "有人搞电子束光刻，到现在还用 PMMA。我认识一个博士，课题就是把 950K 的 PMMA 写出线条。他图什么？图稳，图他师兄三年前就是这么毕业的。结果呢？他做了个五乘五的剂量矩阵，每个格子写一遍，显影用 MIBK 兑异丙醇，实验室一股指甲油味。灵敏度低，写一个面要等一下午，机器都替他着急。答辩那天评委问他为什么不换化学放大胶，他说：\"我导师觉得 PMMA 最稳。\"台下沉默了三十秒，导师就坐在第一排，点了点头。"}, "b": {"item_id": "90a70ba10fe5", "text": "我有个哥们读纳米加工博士，电子束光刻到今天还用PMMA。PMMA是啥？有机玻璃。奶茶店拿它做招牌，他拿它做量子器件。为啥不换？导师说配方是师爷九十年代调的：950K叠495K，MIBK兑IPA一比三，显影四十五秒，一秒别动。图啥？便宜、稳，论文写一句\"standard PMMA process\"，审稿人都不问。代价是灵敏度低得像我爸回微信，一片芯片曝一整夜，机时按小时收钱，他就睡在机房门口。剂量给多了还交联，正胶活成负胶。最后剥离，丙酮泡一宿，金还粘着，一上超声，金掉了，片子也碎了。他捧着碎片说：\"至少线宽十纳米。\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1034", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "google-sheets", "target_name": "Google Sheets", "facts": ["Launched in June 2006 as Google Spreadsheets, built on XL2Web, a product Google acquired from 2Web Technologies.", "A spreadsheet is capped at 10 million cells (raised from 5 million in 2022).", "Functions such as IMPORTHTML, IMPORTXML, and GOOGLEFINANCE pull live web data straight into cells.", "Free with a Google account; files live in Google Drive, and collaborators edit the same sheet simultaneously with coloured cursors.", "In a browser, offline editing works only in Chrome or Edge, with the Google Docs Offline extension installed and offline access switched on while still connected; the Android and iOS apps work offline natively."], "a": {"item_id": "c5f3c3c4263e", "text": "用 Google Sheets 管团队的，一般是创业公司那位“运营兼 HR 兼老板小舅子”。图啥？图免费，图“实时协作，公开透明”。透明是真透明：你凌晨两点偷偷改自己的绩效，右上角冒出一只“匿名水豚”，光标就停在你那一行。全公司这个点还不睡的水豚，只有老板一个。\n\n更绝的是筛选。实习生按“已离职”一点，全公司的表同时只剩离职的人，老板以为昨晚裁员了。\n\n最后他用 IMPORTRANGE 把十二张表串成一张，终于实现了自动化：每天早上一打开，满屏 #REF!。他打开版本历史一条条往回翻，翻到最早那条：“由张伟创建”。那时候他还有头发。"}, "b": {"item_id": "2a199d10637d", "text": "我特别佩服那种用Google Sheets管团队的领导。他们的口头禅是：“不用买系统，一个表格就够了。”省了一年三千块软件费，代价是自己变成全公司的人肉服务器。\n\n刚开始就一个标签页，三个月后四十七个，名字叫“总表”“总表2”“总表最终”“总表最终（别动）”。他还觉得自己是工程师，因为他会VLOOKUP。全组只有他会，别人看见公式栏，就像看见祖传药方。\n\n最离谱是开会。他共享屏幕，右上角蹦出个“匿名水豚正在查看”。全场安静，没人知道谁是水豚，只知道水豚刚把C列改成了红色。\n\n他请一天假，团队就瘫痪了，因为没人知道红色是“已完成”，还是“老板生气了”。\n\n后来他离职，交接文档就一句话：“有问题，看版本历史。”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1035", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "graphene", "target_name": "石墨烯", "facts": ["Isolated in 2004 by Andre Geim and Konstantin Novoselov at Manchester by peeling graphite with Scotch tape; Nobel Prize in Physics, 2010.", "The EU's Graphene Flagship, launched in 2013, committed €1 billion over ten years.", "A 2018 study in Advanced Materials tested 'graphene' from 60 producers and found none with more than 50% graphene content; most was graphite flakes.", "Sold in tennis rackets, running shoes, and headphones as a performance ingredient.", "Geim also won an Ig Nobel Prize in 2000 for levitating a frog with magnets."], "a": {"item_id": "e2b2b3137823", "text": "我表哥2013年起逢人就说，石墨烯要改变世界。理由是：一层原子厚，比钢硬两百倍，诺贝尔奖就是拿胶带从铅笔芯上粘出来的。他听完热血沸腾，当场把攒的二十万全买了石墨烯概念股。你看，人家诺奖是粘下来的，他是被粘住了。\n\n十年过去，世界没变，他变了：从\"石墨烯取代硅\"，变成\"石墨烯发热内衣，第二件半价\"。股票被ST，库存剩三百件内衣，现在全家一到秋冬，都在替石墨烯做临床试验。\n\n最惨的是他还跟我说\"再等十年\"。\n\n哥，这材料最牛的地方，是只剩一层原子也扛得住。你呢？被剥了十年，也就剩一层了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "8757c13cc756", "text": "我有个朋友，逢人就说石墨烯要改变世界。你问他石墨烯是什么，他说是碳原子排成六边形的单层，薄到只有一个原子厚。行，那你知道铅笔芯是什么吗？石墨。石墨就是一层层石墨烯叠起来的。你小学就天天在用它写字，世界怎么没变？他卖保暖内衣，吊牌上印着\"石墨烯含量\"，我让他拿检测报告，他说报告在另一个仓库。去年厂子抵给了银行。现在他改卖石墨烯足浴盆，广告写\"一泡，改变人生\"。我去他店里看了，人确实变了，老婆跑了，他还在那儿泡脚。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1036", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "monopoly", "target_name": "大富翁（Monopoly）", "facts": ["Descends from Lizzie Magie's The Landlord's Game, patented in 1904 to teach the evils of land monopoly in the spirit of Henry George.", "Charles Darrow sold his version to Parker Brothers in 1935 and was credited as the inventor; Parker bought Magie's patent for $500.", "The official rules require any unbought property to be auctioned, and 'Free Parking' pays nothing; the house rules that skip the auction and pile cash on Free Parking are why games drag.", "During the Second World War, British intelligence had maps, compasses, and real currency hidden in sets sent to prisoner-of-war camps."], "a": {"item_id": "7f9957efd2c9", "text": "过年我舅非要拉全家玩大富翁，说就玩一把。我告诉你，这一把从吃完饺子玩到天亮。我舅抢着当银行，发钱的时候笑得比真银行还真。轮到他自己，橙色那条街先买下，盖三栋房子，再升级成旅馆。我表姐踩上去交了一大笔过路费，脸比她手里的绿房子还绿。我爸更惨，一圈转到监狱，他说我就路过。人家说那叫Just Visiting，你是进去了。没钱交保释金，只能眼睁睁看着我舅把他的地一块块收走。最后免费停车场那堆钱全被我舅拿走，他抱着钱说：这就是亲情。"}, "b": {"item_id": "f719efc18290", "text": "每年过年，总有亲戚提议：“一家人玩个大富翁，热闹热闹。”热闹？这游戏的胜利条件是：让你亲妈破产。\n\n图什么？平时不好意思跟你爸算的账，全在这块板上算了。我爸一辈子没买过房，一摸骰子就开始囤地，一条街全拿下，盖满酒店，等我路过，笑得像个开发商。我妈当银行，银行行长自己也下场玩，这在现实里叫什么？叫进去。\n\n我舅连着三轮进监狱，出来第一句：“谁欠我过路费？”\n\n玩到凌晨一点，没人赢，全在抵押。最后我弟把桌子一掀，地契满天飞。第二天早饭，没人说话。团圆了，账还没算完。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1037", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "carbon-nanotubes", "target_name": "碳纳米管", "facts": ["Reported by Sumio Iijima in Nature in 1991.", "Promised as the cable for a space elevator; a 2006 analysis showed that ordinary defects cut the strength of a macroscopic cable far below the ideal value.", "Long, rigid multi-walled nanotubes behaved like asbestos fibres in a 2008 mouse study in Nature Nanotechnology.", "Sorting metallic from semiconducting tubes has blocked nanotube electronics for three decades; MIT's 16-bit RV16X-NANO processor (2019) is still a lab demonstration.", "Today they are sold mainly as a conductive additive in lithium-ion battery electrodes."], "a": {"item_id": "dc1d00e455ba", "text": "碳纳米管创业的，PPT第一页永远是太空电梯：比钢强一百倍，从地球拉根绳到同步轨道。投资人问：“多长了？”他说：“实验室最长做到半米。”离三万六千公里，还差三万五千九百九十九公里九百九十九米五。\n\n第二页讲替代硅：“摩尔定律到头了，我们接班！”问良率呢？他说：“有一个小问题，长出来三分之一是金属的，三分之二是半导体的，我们分不开。”大哥，你这叫“替代硅”？你这叫“开盲盒”。\n\n讲了二十年，一九九一年发现的东西，至今还在“即将改变世界”。\n\n最后公司怎么样了？活得挺好。不造电梯，不造芯片，一吨一吨往锂电池里卖导电剂，按公斤算钱。路演时说要通往太空，年报里写的是“主营业务：黑色粉末”。\n\n太空电梯没建成，但他确实上去了，坐的是电动车。"}, "b": {"item_id": "9252499a4c39", "text": "做碳纳米管创业的博士，路演第一页永远是太空电梯。说这玩意儿比钢强一百倍，重量才六分之一，投资人眼睛都亮了。他们图啥？图的是投资人别翻第二页。第二页写着：实验室里最长做到几十厘米，太空电梯需要三万六千公里，差一亿倍，他管这叫“早期阶段”。投资人说看看样品，他掏出一小瓶黑粉，说这就是未来。结果钱融到了，电梯没修成，产品卖给电池厂当导电剂，论公斤称，跟墨粉一个价位。最后他也活成了碳纳米管：单打独斗个个都强，凑一块儿就团聚，缠成一团，怎么都分不开。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1038", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hawaiian-pizza", "target_name": "夏威夷披萨", "facts": ["Invented in 1962 by Sam Panopoulos, a Greek immigrant, at the Satellite Restaurant in Chatham, Ontario, Canada; named after the brand of canned pineapple he used.", "Iceland's president Guðni Th. Jóhannesson said in 2017 that he would ban pineapple on pizza if he could, then clarified that a president has no such power.", "Hawaii is no longer a major pineapple producer: Del Monte shut its Hawaiian pineapple operations in November 2006, two years ahead of its announced 2008 exit, the state's last cannery closed in 2007, and most canned pineapple now comes from Thailand, Indonesia, and the Philippines.", "Panopoulos, whose restaurant also served Chinese dishes, told the Toronto Sun in 2010 that 'in those days people didn't mix the tastes like sweet and sour, except for Chinese food'."], "a": {"item_id": "b9921996d500", "text": "每桌聚餐总有那么一个人，菜单翻到最后一页，清清嗓子：“要不……来个夏威夷？”\n\n这人图什么？图个性。他觉得自己在叛逆：“我不走寻常路。”兄弟，夏威夷披萨是全世界最出名的争议披萨，你点它，就像在摇滚圈穿皮衣，一点都不稀奇。而且它跟夏威夷没关系，发明它的是个住在加拿大的希腊人。你连“异域风情”都没沾上。\n\n下场呢？披萨一上桌，全桌人都去拿别的，只有它孤零零摆着。你只能说“我就爱这口”，然后一个人硬啃六块。菠萝出水，饼底泡成湿毛巾，火腿片在上面游泳。吃不完打包回家，第二天微波炉一转，咬一口，热乎乎的菠萝配咸火腿，就像前任突然发来一句“最近好吗”，说不上哪里不对，就是哪都不对。"}, "b": {"item_id": "273e9ae28d95", "text": "点夏威夷披萨的人，我一直想不通。你说你爱吃甜的，行啊，菠萝罐头买一罐，一勺一勺舀着吃，多痛快。你偏要把它摊在一张发面饼上，烤到边缘焦黄，再铺一层咸火腿，然后跟人说：\"这叫冲突之美。\"冲突你个头！菠萝一烤出汁，整张饼泡成了湿吐司。你咬一口，烫，甜，咸，三个味道在嘴里开会，谁也不服谁，散会的时候你只剩一嘴纸巾，和一句\"其实还行\"。这种人我见过，每次聚餐都点它，然后全桌人默默把菠萝挑到盘子边上，堆成一座小山，像一群赖着不走的客人。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1039", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "javascript", "target_name": "JavaScript", "facts": ["Written by Brendan Eich at Netscape in ten days in May 1995; named Mocha, then LiveScript, then JavaScript to ride Java's marketing, although the two are unrelated.", "typeof null returns 'object', a bug from the first version that has been kept for compatibility ever since.", "In March 2016 the author of left-pad, an 11-line npm package, unpublished it and broke builds across the industry, including Babel and React.", "[] + {} evaluates to '[object Object]'; {} + [] typed at a console evaluates to 0.", "Standardized by Ecma as 'ECMAScript' in 1997 because the name JavaScript is a trademark, first Sun's and now Oracle's."], "a": {"item_id": "c214f8db4fb9", "text": "我有个同事，什么都用 JavaScript 写。写脚本用 JS，写后台用 Node，连他家的烧水壶我都怀疑跑着 npm。他图什么？他说\"一门语言打天下\"。结果呢？一个 hello world 下了四百兆的 node_modules，八万个文件，他说这叫生态。上周他算 0.1 加 0.2，屏幕蹦出 0.30000000000000004，他盯了十分钟，说这是\"浮点的浪漫\"。昨天他 npm install 了一下，整个项目塌了，原因是有人从仓库里撤掉了一个十一行的小包，叫 left-pad。现在他分不清 null 和 undefined，我问他 typeof null 为什么是 object，他说这是历史遗留问题。我说，你才是。"}, "b": {"item_id": "3206715972e0", "text": "我同事学了三个月 JavaScript，现在看什么都像 JS。前两天他说要做个记事本，用 Electron。我说记事本？他说对，打出来 300 兆，因为里面自带一个 Chrome。我说你一个记事本为啥要带浏览器？他说：“我不想学第二门语言。”兄弟，你省的是学习时间，吃的是我的内存。\n\n他连判断奇偶数都不自己写，npm install is-even。装完 node_modules 两个G，他自己的代码三行。\n\n我考他：\"1\"+1 等于几？他说 11。\"1\"-1 呢？0。我说你就这么接受了？他说：“习惯了。”\n\n各位，这不是程序员，这是被 JavaScript 虐出斯德哥尔摩综合征的人。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1040", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "oat-milk", "target_name": "燕麦奶", "facts": ["Oatly started in Sweden in the 1990s, built on Lund University research by Rickard Öste on lactose intolerance.", "Oatly's slogan 'It's like milk but made for humans' was banned in Sweden in 2015 after the dairy lobby LRF Mjölk sued; Oatly then published the lawsuit as advertising.", "Oatly listed on Nasdaq in May 2021 at a valuation near $10 billion; the shares later lost more than 90% of their value.", "Commercial oat milk is made by breaking oat starch into maltose with amylase enzymes, and many brands, Oatly among them, add rapeseed oil; the result has more sugar and a higher glycaemic index than almond or soy milk."], "a": {"item_id": "55ab352fe073", "text": "每个咖啡店都有这种人，站在柜台前深吸一口气：“拿铁，换燕麦奶。”语气像在宣布皈依。\n\n你问他乳糖不耐吗？不是。“就是想少点负担。”然后他加点了一块芝士蛋糕。\n\n负担是少了，钱包的负担没少，燕麦奶加五块。而且为了打出奶泡，燕麦里的淀粉会被水解成麦芽糖，升糖比牛奶还高。他花五块钱，把一杯奶换成了一杯液体麦片，还觉得自己在做减法。\n\n喝到一半，他拍照发朋友圈：“植物基生活。”\n\n最后一口，他皱着眉品了品：“嗯，有层次。”\n\n兄弟，那不是层次，那是你早餐的碗底。"}, "b": {"item_id": "6af69cc6ef40", "text": "我发现点燕麦拿铁的人有个共同点：开口前先叹气。“不好意思，换燕麦奶，我乳糖不耐。”你不耐？上周火锅店你一个人干了三个冰淇淋球。\n\n他们图什么？图健康。可燕麦奶为了打出奶泡，要往里加植物油；为了喝着甜，得把燕麦酶解成麦芽糖。你多掏五块钱，买了一杯稀释燕麦粥，外加一勺油。\n\n图环保？说燕麦比奶牛省水。你省下那点水，转头就用纸巾擦了八遍杯口。\n\n下场呢？体检血糖偏高。医生问你早饭吃什么，你说只喝咖啡。医生摇头：“不对，你这是每天早上喝两碗粥，还嫌粥稀，花钱让人给你打了个泡。”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1041", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "slack", "target_name": "Slack", "facts": ["Spun out in 2013 from the internal chat tool of Tiny Speck's failed game Glitch; founder Stewart Butterfield had earlier spun Flickr out of another failed game.", "The name is a backronym: 'Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge'.", "Salesforce bought it for $27.7 billion in 2021.", "Filed an EU antitrust complaint against Microsoft in 2020 over the bundling of Teams with Office."], "a": {"item_id": "62d910dd9864", "text": "有些老板觉得，把公司搬进 Slack 就叫数字化管理。他建了一百二十个频道，#general 底下永远是“早上好”和一排表情回应。他的理想是“全员透明”，结果大家学会了：发一句“收到”，状态设成“开会中”，然后去另一个频道划水。他半夜十一点在 #项目A-最终版-真的最终版 里@channel，回应他的只有三个🫡，还有一个Huddle邀请，是他自己发的。最后他终于明白：员工不是不看消息，是他把公司做成了一个未读红点比工资还多的地方。"}, "b": {"item_id": "04d9a9a0c475", "text": "有一种老板，创业第一天就宣布：我们不开会，全员用 Slack，扁平、透明、异步。\n\n结果建了四十七个频道。#general 没人说话，#random 倒是热火朝天。真正的工作都在\"线程\"里，那地方跟地下室一样，进去就没人再见过。\n\n老板最爱 @channel。一发，三十个人手机同时一震，点开一看：周五记得填报销。然后三十个人回三十个 👍。他盯着那一排 👍，觉得这公司管得真好。\n\n他还盯着名字旁边那个绿点。员工呢，学会了每五分钟晃一下鼠标。公司就这样实现了\"全员在线，无人干活\"。\n\n说好不开会，最后他点了那个小耳机图标，叫 huddle。对，就是开会，只是名字可爱了点。\n\n最惨的是免费版，三个月前的消息自动消失。老板问：去年那个决定谁拍的板？\n\nSlack 说：什么决定？"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1042", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "carbon-nanotubes", "target_name": "碳纳米管", "facts": ["Reported by Sumio Iijima in Nature in 1991.", "Promised as the cable for a space elevator; a 2006 analysis showed that ordinary defects cut the strength of a macroscopic cable far below the ideal value.", "Long, rigid multi-walled nanotubes behaved like asbestos fibres in a 2008 mouse study in Nature Nanotechnology.", "Sorting metallic from semiconducting tubes has blocked nanotube electronics for three decades; MIT's 16-bit RV16X-NANO processor (2019) is still a lab demonstration.", "Today they are sold mainly as a conductive additive in lithium-ion battery electrodes."], "a": {"item_id": "c20ac403ef4f", "text": "你们见过拿碳纳米管融资的人吗？一般是个博士，PPT第一页就是太空电梯：“强度是钢的一百倍，一根线从地面拉到三万六千公里外！”投资人听得热泪盈眶。\n\n我就想问：你实验室里最长的一根有多长？“两厘米。”那你这不叫太空电梯，叫太空牙线。\n\n他图啥？图下一轮。电梯盖不盖得成不重要，估值得先上去。\n\n五年后再看，公司转型了，现在卖“导电剂”，就是往锂电池里掺的那点黑粉，一公斤赚几块钱。投资人问：“电梯呢？”博士说：“电梯……在电池里，形成了导电网络。”\n\n行，当年说要通天，现在是在电池里修了个楼梯间。"}, "b": {"item_id": "0f999ec5f76f", "text": "有人拿碳纳米管去给投资人讲故事。碳纳米管什么概念？比头发细一万倍，比钢强一百倍。他的故事就一句话：用它搓一根绳子，从赤道挂到同步轨道，人坐电梯上太空。投资人眼睛都亮了。我就问一句：这根绳子现在多长？他说，实验室里最长的那根，比我的PPT还短。我又问：什么时候量产？他说，还差一点，就差把金属性和半导体性的管子分开，业内叫手性控制。投资人没听懂，但觉得这人太有学问了，当场打了三个亿。三年后项目黄了，他把剩下的管子磨成粉，改名叫\"碳管抗老精华\"，现在一瓶卖九百八。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1043", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "emacs", "target_name": "Emacs", "facts": ["Began in 1976 as a set of macros for the TECO editor at MIT's AI Lab, written by Richard Stallman and others; GNU Emacs followed in 1985.", "Extensible in its own Lisp dialect, Emacs Lisp; the standing joke is that it is a fine operating system lacking only a good editor.", "Ships with a psychotherapist (M-x doctor), Tetris, a mail client, a calendar, and a web browser (eww).", "An old backronym is 'Eight Megabytes And Constantly Swapping', from when 8 MB was a lot of memory.", "'Emacs pinky' is the name for the hand strain blamed on its Control-key chords."], "a": {"item_id": "19ed8eac97e6", "text": "我认识一个把Emacs当操作系统的哥们。他用Emacs收邮件、看新闻、听歌、管日程。我问：“那你干嘛不直接装个操作系统？”他推了推眼镜：“Emacs就缺一个好编辑器。”说完笑得特别得意，那种全世界只有我懂的得意。\n\n他的待办用Org-mode管，第一条是“优化Org-mode配置”，已经挂了三年。init.el写了八千行，比他这辈子写过的业务代码都长。启动一次要四十七秒，所以他从不关，上次重启是因为搬家断电。\n\n小指因为按Ctrl已经变形了，医生问怎么弄的，他说“写东西”。女朋友说出来吃个饭，他说等我写个函数。函数写好了，女朋友没了。\n\n现在他唯一的倾诉对象是M-x doctor，一个七十年代的假心理医生。他说我好孤独，它说：“你为什么这么说？”\n\n他说，这是他用过最懂他的软件。"}, "b": {"item_id": "45d1386d9957", "text": "我有个朋友，把Emacs当操作系统用。邮件用Gnus，上网用eww，终端开eshell，连窗口管理器都换成了EXWM。我问他图什么，他说：“图一辈子不用离开Emacs。”兄弟，这不叫生活，这叫软禁。他的init.el写了四千行，比毕业论文还长，每天上班第一件事，是修昨晚改坏的配置。一年下来，他就产出了俩东西：一个能用M-x点外卖的配置，一根被Ctrl键按废的小拇指。上礼拜Emacs一崩，他当场断网、断邮件、断社交，就像自己盖的房子塌了，人还埋在里头。现在他想开了，准备装Evil-mode改用Vim键位。好家伙，操作系统是齐了，就缺一个好用的编辑器。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1044", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hawaiian-pizza", "target_name": "夏威夷披萨", "facts": ["Invented in 1962 by Sam Panopoulos, a Greek immigrant, at the Satellite Restaurant in Chatham, Ontario, Canada; named after the brand of canned pineapple he used.", "Iceland's president Guðni Th. Jóhannesson said in 2017 that he would ban pineapple on pizza if he could, then clarified that a president has no such power.", "Hawaii is no longer a major pineapple producer: Del Monte shut its Hawaiian pineapple operations in November 2006, two years ahead of its announced 2008 exit, the state's last cannery closed in 2007, and most canned pineapple now comes from Thailand, Indonesia, and the Philippines.", "Panopoulos, whose restaurant also served Chinese dishes, told the Toronto Sun in 2010 that 'in those days people didn't mix the tastes like sweet and sour, except for Chinese food'."], "a": {"item_id": "2a083e43e4a9", "text": "点夏威夷披萨的人，我大概能猜出两种。一种是想显得自己没偏见，另一种是他妈从小就给他点这个。前一种会先举着手机拍那几块菠萝，配文“勇敢的人才吃”。吃到第三口，菠萝汁已经顺着饼底流进盘子，火腿片泡得发白，他还是硬着头皮说“层次很丰富”。层次确实丰富，甜、咸、油、软在嘴里打成一团，最后赢的是芝士，输的是他的胃。"}, "b": {"item_id": "6ec4abe781be", "text": "点夏威夷披萨的人，骨子里都想当和事佬：甜的也要，咸的也要，菠萝火腿往饼上一铺，谁都不得罪。结果得罪了全世界。意大利人想报警，夏威夷人也想报警，因为这玩意儿是加拿大人发明的，跟夏威夷唯一的关系就是一听菠萝罐头。\n\n最惨是聚餐。八个人点四盒，他非要加一盒夏威夷的：“你们试试，真的好吃！”最后另外三盒全空了，就剩他那盒，八块，一块没动。他一个人坐那儿一块一块往下吃，吃到最后菠萝凉了，汁儿全渗进饼底，软塌塌的。跟他在这个饭局上的地位，一模一样。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1045", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "vim", "target_name": "Vim", "facts": ["Released by Bram Moolenaar in November 1991 as 'Vi IMitation', a vi clone he had begun writing for the Amiga in 1988 on top of Stevie; the expansion became 'Vi IMproved' with version 2.0 in December 1993.", "Licensed as charityware: the licence asks users to donate to ICCF Holland, a charity Moolenaar ran for children in Uganda.", "Stack Overflow's question 'How to exit the Vim editor?' passed one million visits in May 2017, which Stack Overflow marked with a blog post.", "Modal editing: in normal mode, typing letters runs commands rather than inserting text; :wq saves and quits, :q! quits and discards changes."], "a": {"item_id": "90b37ac793f9", "text": "有种人用 Vim 写代码，还非得让全世界知道。你问他用什么编辑器，他不直接说，停半秒，吐出两个字：\"Vim。\"那语气，像刚从战场上下来。他图什么？图你觉得他厉害。可你看他干活：左手按 ESC，右手敲 dd、yy、p，屏幕上东西一会儿没了，一会儿又冒出来，三个函数已经搬到别的文件里了，你连他光标在哪都没看清。同事问他怎么不用 IDE，他说：\"我在普通模式，你不懂。\"后来服务器出事，他被叫去救火，刚坐下就在群里喊：\"等我先退出一下。\"然后他对着屏幕敲了二十分钟 :q，纹丝不动，最后爬出来，第一句话是：\"谁能告诉我记事本怎么用？\""}, "b": {"item_id": "304fbbdaf17e", "text": "咱公司有个哥们，从不说\"我在写代码\"，他说\"我在用 Vim 写代码\"。重点不是写，是 Vim。\n\n他图啥？图你路过他工位，看见他手指在键盘上弹钢琴，嘴里念着 hjkl hjkl，像在做法。你问他干嘛，他说\"移动光标\"。移一格十分钟，但\"不用离开主键区\"。省下摸方向键的半秒，代价是 .vimrc 调了三年。\n\n下场呢？有天重装系统，.vimrc 没备份。他对着裸奔的 Vim，像被扒了铠甲的骑士。第二天，他偷偷装了 VS Code，再装个 Vim 插件。\n\n现在他是：用 VS Code 假装用 Vim，还得让所有人知道。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1046", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "klingon", "target_name": "克林贡语", "facts": ["Created by linguist Marc Okrand for Star Trek III (1984), building on a few words the actor James Doohan improvised for the 1979 film.", "Deliberately alien grammar: object-verb-subject word order and no verb 'to be', so Hamlet's line became 'taH pagh taHbe'', 'continue or not continue'.", "The Klingon Language Institute (1992) published Hamlet 'in the original Klingon'; Bing Translator added Klingon in 2013 and Duolingo in 2018.", "Okrand's 1985 Klingon Dictionary listed roughly 1,500 words, and fluent speakers number only in the dozens: 20 to 30 in 2009, 30 to 50 by 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "40a35f67e92f", "text": "我真佩服那些把克林贡语学到流利的人。流利啊，那是花了十年。这语言有多不适合社交？它没有\"你好\"。最接近的是 nuqneH，字面意思是：\"你要干嘛？\"也没有\"谢谢\"，因为克林贡人不感恩。\n\n所以你想想这帮人的处境：苦练十年，全世界能聊的就几百个同好。终于约到一个，见面第一句，你问他：\"你要干嘛？\"他回你：\"你要干嘛？\"然后俩人就僵在那儿了。\n\n想表达感情怎么办？只能用他们的方式，比如：\"今天是死的好日子。\"你女朋友说\"我想你了\"，你深情地回一句：\"今天是死的好日子。\"\n\n最惨的是什么？你辛辛苦苦练的是宾语—动词—主语的语序。你想说\"我爱你\"，得说成\"你，爱，我\"。听着像在被审问。\n\n最后他们图什么？图一个能用外星语言吵赢别人的机会。结果遇上另一个同样流利的，两人吵了三小时，谁也不会说\"对不起\"。"}, "b": {"item_id": "e66268bd7919", "text": "你们身边有没有那种人，把克林贡语学到流利？我认识一个。他周末不陪女朋友，说要练喉音。克林贡语一个词里有三个喉塞音，他说完一句话，像在下水道里给管道道歉。他图什么？他说等哪天遇见真正的克林贡人，就能用母语骂人。结果呢？他在地铁上对一个大爷说“nuqneH”，意思是“你要什么”，大爷以为他在要钱，给了他五块。他面试时用“Qapla'”回答“你的成功经验”，面试官以为他在清嗓子。现在他家里的小孩也学会了，写作业的时候对着卷子喊“ghobe'”，就是“不”。他老婆说，这是她这辈子听过最有骨气的一个“不”。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1047", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "durian", "target_name": "榴莲", "facts": ["Banned on Singapore's MRT trains and in many Southeast Asian hotels because of the smell.", "A 2017 genome study in Nature Genetics traced the odour to a duplicated family of MGL genes that pump out volatile sulfur compounds.", "China buys most of the world's durian exports, chiefly from Thailand, in a trade worth billions of dollars a year.", "Alfred Russel Wallace wrote in 1856 that eating it was 'a new sensation worth a voyage to the East to experience'."], "a": {"item_id": "c1203c00cfe6", "text": "地铁上吃榴莲的人，我真的服。那味儿一进车厢，我以为哪个下水道盖子被掀了。更绝的是他还闭着眼，嘴角往上翘，一副在拍广告的样子。他图什么？图个\"我很会生活\"？行，你会生活，我们陪你一起窒息。下场也很精彩：全车人同时把口罩往上提，他下车时手里拎着带刺的壳，座位上留了一圈黏糊糊的黄纤维。第二天，他身上的味道洗三遍还在，同事开会时都默默把窗户打开了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "68573ec1558a", "text": "地铁上吃榴莲的人，我观察过，他们不是饿，是在传教。你一皱眉，他立刻甩出那句经典台词：“闻着臭，吃着香！”大哥，我又不吃，你光给我上半句，这跟只收定金不发货有什么区别？\n\n而且他还爱报价：“猫山王，八十一斤！”意思是这味儿是进口的，你得心怀感激。全车厢被迫消费，每人摊他二十块钱的味儿。\n\n下场呢？他到站下车，以为结束了。不，那味儿会住进他指甲缝里，洗三遍，晚上挠头都是猫山王。第二天同事全问他：“你昨天坐的几号线？”\n\n新加坡地铁明令禁止带榴莲。我建议咱们也补一条：禁止带吃过榴莲的人。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1048", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "esperanto", "target_name": "世界语", "facts": ["Published in Warsaw in 1887 by L. L. Zamenhof, a Białystok-born eye doctor, under the pen name Doktoro Esperanto, 'one who hopes'.", "Speaker estimates range from 100,000 to two million; roughly a thousand people grow up with it as a native language.", "William Shatner starred in Incubus (1966), a horror film shot entirely in Esperanto, with pronunciation Esperantists still mock.", "Its Wikipedia has over 350,000 articles, and its Duolingo course for English speakers had 1.36 million learners by July 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "7911ad5f0fdc", "text": "学世界语的人，图的是全人类终于能好好说话。结果全人类没来，来的是同一拨人。这门语言的名字就叫\"希望者\"，学它的人等于从第一天就领了个外号。大会上他们互相寒暄：你哪国的？巴西。我韩国的。平时用世界语跟谁聊？跟你们俩啊。名词加-o，形容词加-a，宾格加-n，每句话都挂满尾巴，语法一个不错，朋友一个没有。"}, "b": {"item_id": "3187bfd3bfb3", "text": "学世界语的人，我研究过，理想都很大：世界和平。方法也很简单：先让全世界都学世界语。目前进度，据说有一百万人会，而且这一百万人……基本互相认识。\n\n别人学外语是为了认识外国人，他们学是为了去参加世界语大会。四十个国家的人坐一屋，用一门没有任何国家说的语言，聊同一个话题：世界语为什么还没火。\n\n最离谱的是，1907年有人嫌它不够完美，搞了个改良版叫伊多语，成功把自己人分成了两派。\n\n人类还没统一呢，先把想统一人类的人给拆了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1049", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hawaiian-pizza", "target_name": "夏威夷披萨", "facts": ["Invented in 1962 by Sam Panopoulos, a Greek immigrant, at the Satellite Restaurant in Chatham, Ontario, Canada; named after the brand of canned pineapple he used.", "Iceland's president Guðni Th. Jóhannesson said in 2017 that he would ban pineapple on pizza if he could, then clarified that a president has no such power.", "Hawaii is no longer a major pineapple producer: Del Monte shut its Hawaiian pineapple operations in November 2006, two years ahead of its announced 2008 exit, the state's last cannery closed in 2007, and most canned pineapple now comes from Thailand, Indonesia, and the Philippines.", "Panopoulos, whose restaurant also served Chinese dishes, told the Toronto Sun in 2010 that 'in those days people didn't mix the tastes like sweet and sour, except for Chinese food'."], "a": {"item_id": "6ec4abe781be", "text": "点夏威夷披萨的人，骨子里都想当和事佬：甜的也要，咸的也要，菠萝火腿往饼上一铺，谁都不得罪。结果得罪了全世界。意大利人想报警，夏威夷人也想报警，因为这玩意儿是加拿大人发明的，跟夏威夷唯一的关系就是一听菠萝罐头。\n\n最惨是聚餐。八个人点四盒，他非要加一盒夏威夷的：“你们试试，真的好吃！”最后另外三盒全空了，就剩他那盒，八块，一块没动。他一个人坐那儿一块一块往下吃，吃到最后菠萝凉了，汁儿全渗进饼底，软塌塌的。跟他在这个饭局上的地位，一模一样。"}, "b": {"item_id": "273e9ae28d95", "text": "点夏威夷披萨的人，我一直想不通。你说你爱吃甜的，行啊，菠萝罐头买一罐，一勺一勺舀着吃，多痛快。你偏要把它摊在一张发面饼上，烤到边缘焦黄，再铺一层咸火腿，然后跟人说：\"这叫冲突之美。\"冲突你个头！菠萝一烤出汁，整张饼泡成了湿吐司。你咬一口，烫，甜，咸，三个味道在嘴里开会，谁也不服谁，散会的时候你只剩一嘴纸巾，和一句\"其实还行\"。这种人我见过，每次聚餐都点它，然后全桌人默默把菠萝挑到盘子边上，堆成一座小山，像一群赖着不走的客人。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1050", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hydrogen-cars", "target_name": "氢燃料电池汽车", "facts": ["Toyota launched the Mirai in 2014; 'mirai' means 'future' in Japanese.", "Nearly all US hydrogen filling stations are in California; Shell closed all seven of its California passenger-car stations in February 2024.", "More than 95% of the world's hydrogen is made from fossil fuels, mostly by steam-reforming natural gas.", "California pump prices reached about $36 per kilogram in 2023-24, over $200 for a Mirai fill; Toyota has handed buyers fuel cards worth $15,000.", "Mirai owners filed a class action against Toyota in July 2024 over hydrogen shortages and station outages; a second suit in 2025 sought at least $5.7 billion."], "a": {"item_id": "feb6bb90ad06", "text": "我有个朋友买了辆氢燃料电池车，逢人就说：“电动车充电一小时，我加氢只要三分钟。”对，三分钟，前提是你先开两百公里找到加氢站。全市就那么一座，还是给公交车队修的，他得排在十二辆大巴后面，等储罐压力回上来。人家加的是氢，他加的是耐心。\n\n他还爱说：“我这车唯一的排放是水。”是，排放是水，他眼里也是水。一公斤氢七十块，跑一百公里比油车还贵。\n\n最惨的是去年那座站关了。车现在停在楼下，他每天下去看一眼。他说他买的是未来。没错，未来还没到，二手车商的报价先到了：按废铁算，高压氢罐另收拆除费。"}, "b": {"item_id": "c99d709097af", "text": "买氢燃料电池车的人，我研究过，就三种。\n\n第一种，环保人士。张口就是：“我这车尾气只有水。”兄弟，你这氢大部分是天然气炼的，污染在加氢站上游排完了，你在这儿排水。这就好比饭店说自己不产生垃圾，因为厨房在隔壁街。\n\n第二种，被销售一句“送三年氢气”忽悠来的。图的是免费。结果全市两座加氢站，一座检修，一座在隔壁区。他现在周末的娱乐活动就是去加氢，导航到了，门口贴着：“设备维护，复工时间待定”。\n\n下场更惨。氢气卡还没用完，加氢站先倒闭了。几十万的车，停小区里，邻居问：“这啥车？”他说：“氢的。”邻居点点头：“哦，那你现在确实挺空的。”\n\n最后，他开着车唯一的排放物，去浇了阳台那盆绿萝。他说：“至少我这车，能养花。”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1051", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "beanie-babies", "target_name": "Beanie Babies 豆豆娃", "facts": ["Launched by Ty Inc. in 1993 and sold only through small gift shops, with designs 'retired' to manufacture scarcity.", "At the height of the craze Beanie Babies accounted for about 10% of eBay's sales, and eBay's 1998 IPO filing said the site at times had over 30,000 Beanie Baby auctions running simultaneously in the quarter ending June 1998.", "In 1999 a divorcing couple divided their Beanie Baby collection one by one on a courtroom floor, in a photograph that ran worldwide.", "In 1999 Ty announced that Beanie Babies would be retired at the end of the year, then reversed itself after asking the public to vote and seeing an overwhelming vote to keep them.", "Most 'rare' Beanies that price guides valued in the thousands now sell for a few dollars."], "a": {"item_id": "a30c3de668a8", "text": "我表舅九十年代一口气囤了三百多只豆豆娃，每只屁股上的小吊牌都套三层塑料袋，连摸都不让摸。他说这叫抗通胀。后来厂家宣布\"退休\"，他激动得三天没睡，觉得自己是股神。结果呢？退休那批，现在二手平台一只八块包邮，买家还嫌吊牌有折痕。他家客厅的柜子搬到了阁楼，女儿问那是什么，他说：\"这是我的养老金。\"女儿说：\"爸，养老金能当饭吃吗？\""}, "b": {"item_id": "4798ef5c2a37", "text": "九十年代有一批人，真心相信养老金能靠一只塞满塑料粒的熊。他们给豆豆娃的心形吊牌套上塑料保护壳。娃可以脏，吊牌不能折，折了就从“遗产”降级成“玩具”。孩子想抱一下？不行，那是你的大学学费！Ty公司一宣布某款“退休”，他们连夜开车跑三家礼品店，跟抢金条似的。戴安娜王妃一走，他们抢那只紫色王妃熊，觉得王妃没了，熊必涨。结果呢？拉斯维加斯真有对夫妻离婚，分不清这堆熊，法官让俩人坐在法庭地板上，一人一只轮流挑。二十多年过去，那些熊还在阁楼里，吊牌完好，崭新得像从没被爱过。跟他们的回报率一样。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1052", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "klingon", "target_name": "克林贡语", "facts": ["Created by linguist Marc Okrand for Star Trek III (1984), building on a few words the actor James Doohan improvised for the 1979 film.", "Deliberately alien grammar: object-verb-subject word order and no verb 'to be', so Hamlet's line became 'taH pagh taHbe'', 'continue or not continue'.", "The Klingon Language Institute (1992) published Hamlet 'in the original Klingon'; Bing Translator added Klingon in 2013 and Duolingo in 2018.", "Okrand's 1985 Klingon Dictionary listed roughly 1,500 words, and fluent speakers number only in the dozens: 20 to 30 in 2009, 30 to 50 by 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "ab8685eebdf7", "text": "全世界能把克林贡语说流利的人，据说也就几十个。你想想，几十个人，凑一块儿连个婚礼都撑不满，还得从《星际迷航》剧组借人。\n\n这帮人图什么？图的是终于能用原文读《哈姆雷特》。对，有人真把莎士比亚翻成了克林贡语，理由是“莎士比亚本来就是克林贡人，被地球人偷了”。你说他疯吧，他还出了书。\n\n最狠的是有个语言学家，从儿子出生起只跟他说克林贡语，想培养世界上第一个克林贡母语者。结果孩子长到几岁，爸爸一开口说克林贡语，他就装听不见。你看，叛逆期都比别人早，还是外星文化先教会他的：荣誉第一，老爹靠边。\n\n而且这门语言根本不适合过日子。它连“你好”都没有，见面打招呼说的是“nuqneH”，意思是“你想干嘛？”你练了十年，终于能流利地跟人吵架了，问题是全世界能听懂你骂人的，也就那几十个，还都在同一个群里。"}, "b": {"item_id": "e66268bd7919", "text": "你们身边有没有那种人，把克林贡语学到流利？我认识一个。他周末不陪女朋友，说要练喉音。克林贡语一个词里有三个喉塞音，他说完一句话，像在下水道里给管道道歉。他图什么？他说等哪天遇见真正的克林贡人，就能用母语骂人。结果呢？他在地铁上对一个大爷说“nuqneH”，意思是“你要什么”，大爷以为他在要钱，给了他五块。他面试时用“Qapla'”回答“你的成功经验”，面试官以为他在清嗓子。现在他家里的小孩也学会了，写作业的时候对着卷子喊“ghobe'”，就是“不”。他老婆说，这是她这辈子听过最有骨气的一个“不”。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1053", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "microsoft-word", "target_name": "Microsoft Word", "facts": ["Launched in 1983 as 'Multi-Tool Word' for Xenix and MS-DOS.", "Clippy, the animated paperclip assistant, arrived in Office 97 and was removed in Office 2007; Microsoft itself ran ads mocking it in 2001.", "Its .docx format, Office Open XML, was fast-tracked to ISO standard 29500 in 2008 as a roughly 6,000-page specification, amid accusations that national committees had been packed.", "Older spell checkers, Word's included, offered 'Cupertino' as a correction for a mistyped 'cooperation', and the resulting 'Cupertino effect' made it into published NATO, EU and UN-agency documents."], "a": {"item_id": "3536bc0676b0", "text": "有些人非要用 Word 排毕业论文。图什么？图一个\"一键生成目录\"。结果标题没用标题样式，手敲了个\"第一章\"，加粗了事。目录一更新，正文全变成了目录项。他去学分节符，学会了，又发现页码从第二节开始就断了，把\"链接到前一节\"点了十七次，点出一个新世界。凌晨三点，他按 Ctrl+A，对着域代码哭。答辩前一天，导师说：\"目录页码对不上。\"他按 F9，弹出一行\"错误！未找到目录项\"。那一刻他终于明白，Word 不是在排版，是在考他的耐心，而他的耐心已经交了白卷。"}, "b": {"item_id": "743026e864f3", "text": "我们宿舍有个兄弟，打死不学排版软件，说：“论文嘛，Word就够了，谁电脑里没个Word？”\n\n是，谁都有。所以全宿舍凌晨四点一起陪他看页码。\n\n他的诉求特别朴素：封面不要页码，摘要用罗马数字，正文从1开始。就这三个愿望，阿拉丁神灯都能办，Word办不了。他插了一晚上分节符，最后页眉上“第三章”出现在了致谢里，像个阴魂不散的前任。\n\n最狠的是插一张图。图一进来，后面二十页集体往下挪，表格跑到下一页，标题孤零零留在页底。他想拖回来，鼠标一碰，Word当场“未响应”，屏幕一白，跟他的人生一个颜色。\n\n现在他桌面上躺着：“论文终稿.docx”、“终稿改.docx”、“终稿真的不改了.docx”，还有一个“~$终稿.docx”，打不开也删不掉，跟他的学位一样。\n\n导师最后打开一看，用修订模式批了一句话：“格式有问题。”\n\n整篇变红。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1054", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "nuclear-fusion", "target_name": "可控核聚变", "facts": ["Has been 'about 30 years away' since the 1950s; at the 1955 Geneva atoms-for-peace conference, Homi Bhabha predicted fusion power within two decades.", "ITER, agreed in 2006 for first plasma in 2016, re-baselined in 2024 to start research operations in 2034 and deuterium-tritium burning in 2039, at a cost estimated above €20 billion.", "NIF's December 2022 'ignition' shot released 3.15 MJ from 2.05 MJ of laser energy; the lasers drew about 300 MJ from the grid to fire.", "Private fusion companies had raised more than $7 billion by 2024."], "a": {"item_id": "04cdb6ae7f1b", "text": "搞可控核聚变的人，我是真佩服。他们能用磁场把一亿度的等离子体悬在半空，却没法把“还有十年”悬在十年。\n\n一个博士二十五岁入行，导师说还有三十年。他五十五岁带学生，说还有三十年。退休那天他把这句话传给徒弟，比传家宝还稳。\n\n图什么？图下一期经费。真搞成了，没人给你拨款；搞不成，永远有人拨。他们最怕的不是等离子体不稳定，是稳定了。\n\n前两年美国激光点火成功，全网欢呼，说输出能量比输入多。一算，激光器从电网吃的电是输出的一百多倍。这叫净增益，净亏本。\n\n下场呢？ITER从1985年谈到现在，预算翻了几番，一代人的青春，换来一句“明年的明年”。\n\n太阳烧了四十六亿年，从没交过项目申请。人造太阳，年年得写结题报告。"}, "b": {"item_id": "46363a503661", "text": "“可控核聚变还有十年”，这句话是物理学界的传家宝。五十年代爷爷说，八十年代儿子说，现在孙子接着说，一个字都没改，比族谱还准。\n\n你以为他们在预测未来？不，他们在报经费周期。说三年，到期交不了差；说五十年，领导听完直接去开下一个会；说十年，刚好够干到退休，然后把这句话连同办公室钥匙一起交给学生。\n\n最惨的是什么？他们真把等离子体烧到了一亿度，比太阳核心还热好几倍，撑了几十秒，开发布会报喜。可他们自己的职称，在同一个岗位上冻了二十年。\n\n全人类只有这群人能造出一个小太阳，却暖不了自己的工资条。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "ladder-frame-zh-1055", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "windows-vista", "target_name": "Windows Vista", "facts": ["Released to consumers in January 2007, after the 'Longhorn' project was reset in 2004 and headline features such as the WinFS file system were dropped.", "User Account Control prompted so often that Apple's 'Get a Mac' campaign ran an ad about 'Cancel or Allow'.", "'Windows Vista Capable' stickers went on PCs that could only run Home Basic, without the Aero interface, and led to a class-action lawsuit.", "Windows 7 (2009), widely praised, was largely a tuned-up Vista on the same kernel line; Vista support ended in April 2017."], "a": {"item_id": "c62e9c0142f3", "text": "我有个朋友，2007年Vista首发当天零点就去排队，还非买旗舰版，三百九十九美元。图什么？广告说了：“Wow从现在开始。”\n\n确实，开机那一刻他就“哇”了：打印机没驱动，声卡没驱动，他那台贴着“Vista Capable”标签的电脑，连Aero玻璃特效都跑不动。他每点一下，屏幕就一黑：“Windows需要您的许可才能继续。”点“允许”，又黑一下。半年下来，他成了全世界被自己电脑审问最多的人。\n\n旗舰版承诺的“终极附加功能”，最后等来一副德州扑克。\n\n现在他逢人就推荐一个高级功能，叫“降级到XP”。"}, "b": {"item_id": "d1b5347e5ac6", "text": "第一天就升级 Vista 的人，我研究过，都是那种等不及第一个补丁的人。他们图什么？图那个毛玻璃窗口。Aero，半透明的，能隐约看到后面的桌面，就像前任的 QQ 空间，什么都看不清，但就是忍不住盯着。\n\n结果装完发现：打印机没驱动，声卡没驱动，连他那个二手摄像头也认不出他了。电脑配置 1G 内存，风扇转得像要起飞，机身还贴着“Vista Ready”的贴纸，Ready 的是风扇。\n\n最惨的是那个弹窗：“Windows 需要您的许可才能继续。”他想删个文件，系统问他：“你确定吗？”他点“允许”。系统又问：“你真的确定吗？”他再点“允许”。这哪是操作系统，这是我妈。\n\n三天后，他的 MSN 签名改了：“Vista 用户，求 XP 安装盘，在线等，挺急的。”"}, "gold": null}]};
